		end 'throwing'

		// ⭐ Record which SOURCE parameters are bare inner aliases (P1.6-C), so the parser's per-instance
		// argument check can re-qualify them with a concrete receiver's instance alias at the call site. Only
		// inside a generic type that declares inner aliases, and only a method/factory that HAS such a parameter
		// gets an entry (the common case costs none). This is the ONE place the whole-program index learns a
		// method's inner-alias parameters — the return-type read above SKIPS the parameter list.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THE GATE IS THE DENOTATION DOOR'S OWN CONDITION, NOT THE BODY WALK'S SET, AND THE DIFFERENCE WAS
		// A MISSING REFUSAL (F10).** In an `extension` body a bare member resolves against the whole program
		// (`namesInnerAliasHere`), so the set below is EMPTY for a method that names a member some OTHER body
		// declared — and this gate then skipped the walk, the index learned no inner-alias parameter, and the
		// per-instance check had nothing to compare. **MEASURED, both spellings, on a program the type-body form
		// refuses: `WA.Idx` handed to a `WB` method compiled clean and returned 1** where the identical program
		// with the alias in `type Wrapper`'s own body is `E3005 … expected 'WB.Idx', got 'WA.Idx'`. It was
		// already true of TWO `extension` bodies in ONE file, so it is older than the cross-file rule that made
		// it reachable a second way. Both cases are `error.*-inner-alias-is-per-instance`.
		if self.enclosingInnerAliases.count() != 0 or self.innerAliasesFromIndex 'maybeInnerAliasParams'
			let innerAliasInfo = self.innerAliasParamsIn(paramsStart)
			if innerAliasInfo.hasInnerAliasMember() 'hasInnerAliasParam'
				methodInnerAliasParamOut.push(MethodInnerAliasParamEntry.create(funcName, info: innerAliasInfo))
			end 'hasInnerAliasParam'
		end 'maybeInnerAliasParams'

		// The cursor is now at the body start. Scan the body (peek-only, cursor untouched) for parameters
		// this function moves into durable storage — its CONSUME/FEED set.
		//
		// ⚠ **A RECEIVER-LESS function** (top-level function, `static` method) is scanned for BOTH the direct
		// struct-field store (`Self{f: p}`) and, inside a generic type, the type-parameter FEED; its source
		// parameter list already IS its call-site parameter list.
		//
		// ⚠ **AN INSTANCE method** (`push(item Element)`) prepends `self` as parameter 0 (`parseFunction`), so
		// the recorded facts must be CALL-ALIGNED — `SelfParamName` is prepended by the walk above so
		// `feeds[i]`/`bits[i]` name the same slot the receiver-prepending call site (`parseCallNamed`) does. A
		// direct `Self{f: p}` store in an instance method does not have to be scanned for
		// (`detectFieldStores: false`), because such a store never CONSUMES `p`.
		// ⚠ **THE CONCLUSION IS UNCHANGED BUT ITS REASON EXPIRED ON 2026-08-04, so do not re-derive the old
		// one.** It used to hold because a borrowed value in that position was REFUSED outright
		// (`moveInPayload`'s clean E2015), which made the case vacuous. The transitive-consume ruling replaced
		// that refusal with CO-OWNERSHIP, so the store now succeeds — and it is still not a consume, because
		// `coOwnBorrowedForConsume` takes a second reference and deliberately does NOT poison the source
		// ("AND IT IS NOT A MOVE", at its own definition). ⇒ the parameter's consume facts are the same either
		// way. A future change that made this position POISON its source would invalidate the skip.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE OPAQUE-`T` FEED SHAPES ARE SCANNED FOR ON BOTH SIDES, and W10 is why.** They used to be probed
		// only under a literal `self` token, which made them an instance method's business by construction; a
		// `static` that pushes its type-parameter argument into a LOCAL `Array with T` and hands that array to
		// the `Self` it returns feeds just as durably, so `scanBodyFacts` probes for them whichever kind of
		// declaration it was handed.
		//
		// ⚠ **ONE CALL, NOT A BRANCH, AND THE NON-GENERIC INSTANCE METHOD IS COVERED BY ITS EMPTY NAME LIST
		// RATHER THAN BY A GATE.** `scanBodyFacts` returns immediately on an empty `paramNames`, and that is
		// exactly the declaration shape the walk above declines to read — so the two spellings of "do not scan
		// this one" have become one.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND PBR-2's BY-REFERENCE COLUMNS COME OUT OF THE SAME WALK, OFF A SECOND NAME LIST.** They must
		// be read for an INSTANCE method too — a method that forwards a parameter into a reassigning one is as
		// much a forwarder as a free function is, and three of `specs/pass-by-reference.md`'s transitive cases
		// are exactly that program — whereas the consume half deliberately declines to read one. Hence two
		// lists into one walk rather than a second walk of the same tokens.
		self.scanBodyFacts(funcName, funcBareName: nameTok.value, paramNames: scannedParamNames, byRefParamNames: self.byRefScanParamNames(scannedParamNames, sourceParamNames: sourceParamNames, takesReceiver: takesReceiver), typeParamPositions: scannedTypeParamPositions, bodyStart: self.pos, paramConsumes: paramConsumes, paramReassigns: paramReassigns, globalAliasReturns: globalAliasReturns, divergingDecls: divergingDecls, declaresThrows: declaresThrows, detectFieldStores: not takesReceiver, takesReceiver: takesReceiver)
	end 'recordScannedSignature'

	// ⭐ **THE CALL-ALIGNED PARAMETER NAMES PBR-2's SCAN IS INDEXED BY.** For everything the consume scan
	// already reads names for — a receiver-less declaration, a generic type's method — that list IS the
	// call-aligned one and is handed straight back, so the common declaration costs nothing extra.
	//
	// The one shape that needs aligning here is a NON-GENERIC INSTANCE METHOD, which the consume scan skips
	// (see there) and which therefore arrives with the shared EMPTY list. Its SOURCE names come from the one
	// parameter-list walk `recordScannedSignature` makes for every shape — this used to re-walk the list for
	// itself — and a method with no user parameters is left with an empty list, which ends the walk at
	// `scanBodyFacts`' own guard rather than pushing `__self` alone through it.
	function byRefScanParamNames(scannedParamNames ByteArrayArray, sourceParamNames ByteArrayArray, takesReceiver bool) returns ByteArrayArray
		if not takesReceiver or scannedParamNames.count() != 0 'alreadyCallAligned'
			return scannedParamNames
		end 'alreadyCallAligned'

		if sourceParamNames.count() == 0 'nothingToForward'
			return sourceParamNames
		end 'nothingToForward'

		return callAlignedFrom(sourceParamNames)
	end 'byRefScanParamNames'

	// ⭐ **THE DECLARATION'S OPAQUE-PARAMETER RECORD, OR THE SHARED "NONE" (ARRR)** — see `OpaqueParamInfo`
