			self.recordValueType(MaxonType.integer)
			self.artifact.recordParamOrigin((paramCount - witnessParamCount + w) as ValueId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
		end 'seedWitnessParam'

		// Create the entry block (registered in func.blockRefs). It also displaces the detached
		// placeholder `currentBlock` was created with — from here on, every op the parser emits goes to a
		// block that belongs to a real function.
		var entry = self.artifact.module.addBlockAt(func, label: b"entry", filePath: self.filePath.toString(), firstLine: nameTok.line)
		self.currentBlock = entry

		// ⭐ **THE FIELD ALIASES ARE DECLARED BEFORE THE PARAMETERS, AND THE ORDER IS THE E3006
		// CONTRACT.** Both a parameter and a body local are bound through `declareValueBinding`,
		// which refuses to displace a self-field alias — so declaring the aliases into the
		// just-`reset` scope FIRST is what puts every colliding declaration on the guarded side of
		// the collision, with its own token in hand to anchor the diagnostic on. Reversed, the
		// parameter would be in place first and the ALIAS would be the one refused, leaving the
		// error to be reported against a field declaration that is not what needs to change.
		// ⚠ An `enum`/`union` receiver contributes NO aliases: an enum has no fields, and
		// `declareSelfFieldAliases` would ask `enclosingLayout` for a `StructLayout` no enum declares —
		// which now REFUSES with a position (the D1 review found it panicking there through the explicit
		// `self.x` spelling instead). So a bare name in an enum method's body is an ordinary local or an
		// undefined variable, never a field — which is also what makes the E3006 collision contract below
		// vacuous here.
		// ⚠ **A PRIMITIVE RECEIVER IS THE SECOND SUCH DECLARATION (W133), AND IT IS THE SAME SENTENCE FOR A
		// DIFFERENT REASON.** `extension int`'s methods take a receiver and the enclosing type is `int`, which
		// no struct registry holds — a primitive IS its value, so there is nothing for a layout to describe.
		// MEASURED before this clause: every method of `extension int` took the compiler down at
		// `enclosingLayout`, blaming the declaration sweep for a `type int` no program wrote.
		// ⛔⛔ **AN INTERFACE EXTENSION'S BODY IS THE THIRD SUCH DECLARATION, AND IT IS THE ONE WHOSE ABSENCE
		// TOLD A USER TO RENAME A LOCAL THEY DID NOT WRITE (W166).** `extension Iterable` is re-parsed once per
		// CONFORMER (`parseExtensionDeclaration`), with `enclosingType` set to the conformer — so the
		// conformer's FIELDS were installed as bare aliases into a body written in another file, against the
		// INTERFACE, by an author who cannot see them. A `type Bag uses Element implements Iterable` whose
		// field is spelled `item` therefore collided with `map`'s own `for item in self`, and the diagnostic
		// was **`error E3006: stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:201:7: local 'item' shadows self field 'Bag.item'`** — a
		// refusal quoting a library line, naming a library local, on a program whose author's only mistake was
		// choosing a field name. Every local in every `extension Iterable` method was a reserved word for
		// conformers.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE ALIASES ARE WITHHELD RATHER THAN THE COLLISION EXCUSED, AND THAT IS THE NARROWER FIX OF THE
		// TWO.** Permitting the shadow would let the local win and stop the refusal, but it leaves the other
		// half standing: a bare name in an extension body that does NOT collide with a local would still
		// resolve to the conformer's private field, so the body's meaning would depend on storage the interface
		// does not expose and a different conformer does not have. **An interface declares a contract, not
		// storage** — so there is nothing here for a bare name to denote, and `self.<field>` remains the
		// explicit spelling for a body that has some other reason to reach one. It is the enum clause's
		// sentence for a different reason: a bare name in such a body is an ordinary local or an undefined
		// variable, never a field.
		if takesReceiver and not self.enclosingTypeIsEnum and primitiveTagOfTypeName(self.enclosingType) == ValueTypeTag.unresolved and self.extensionInterfaceName.isEmpty() 'selfFieldAliases'
			try self.declareSelfFieldAliases(nameTok)
		end 'selfFieldAliases'

		// Bind each parameter name to its reserved ValueId (i for parameter i), so a
		// body reference resolves straight to the parameter's SSA value — the same
		// value-substitution model `let`/`var` bindings use. Parameters are immutable
		// (a reassignment is E2013); mutating a parameter needs a local shadow. Runs after the push +
		// `setCurrentFuncIndex` above so each parameter's `recordParamOrigin` is filed under this
		// function's real index.
		// ⚠ `sourceName`, not `name`: this asks the whole-program DECLARATION SWEEP about the parameters'
		// consume/feed facts, and the sweep publishes them under the name the SOURCE wrote — it never sees an
		// overload's mangled registration name. `overloadRegistrationNameFor` refuses an overload set whose
		// name has consume facts at all, precisely so that this per-name answer cannot be one sibling's read
		// as another's.
		// ⭐ PBR-2: which of this body's local names need a cell, decided from tokens before the body is parsed
		// (see `collectCellLocalNames` for why it is one walk per body and not one per declaration).
		self.collectCellLocalNames(self.pos)
		try self.bindParameters(func, funcName: sourceName, paramNames: paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, paramSurfaces: paramSurfaces, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts)

		self.skipNewlines()
		// ⭐⭐ **A STDLIB BODY NO PATH FROM `main` REACHES IS SKIPPED WHOLE — the ops, and with them every
		// diagnostic that is a property of the ops.** See `Parser.bodyDemand` for the measurement and for why
		// the DECLARATION above this line is never skipped.
		if not self.buildsBodies 'bodyNotReached'
			self.skipUnreachedFunctionBody(entry, nameTok: nameTok)
		end 'bodyNotReached' else 'bodyReached'
			// parseStatements threads a "current block" that control-flow statements can
			// move forward (an `if` leaves subsequent statements in its continuation block),
			// so the block it RETURNS — not `entry` — is the one control falls out of.
			var bodyEnd = try self.parseStatements(func, block: entry)
			try self.closeFunctionBody(func, bodyEnd: bodyEnd, nameTok: nameTok)
			try self.requireDescriptorForOpaqueDrops(nameTok.value, line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			// ⚠ The BARE name off the token, not `sourceName` and not `name`: the interface-requirement waiver
			// matches against the requirement an `interface` body spells, which is the method name alone.
			// `sourceName` is already qualified for a method (`Silent.greet` — `declaredMethodName`) and `name`
			// may be mangled on top of that for an overload; either would match no contract, silently, and the
			// waiver would simply never fire (measured: `interface-method-local-var-still-errors` reported the
			// contract's own `volume`).
			// E3012 first, E3077 only when it found nothing — the precedence, stated as the order of the two
			// scans over one list. See `reportVarShouldBeLet`.
			if not self.reportUnusedBindings(nameTok.value) 'nothingUnused'
				self.reportVarShouldBeLet()
			end 'nothingUnused'
		end 'bodyReached'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.end)
		self.skipOptionalLabel()

		self.publishExistentialInstances(func)
		self.artifact.recordFuncReturnType(name, returnType: returnType, declaredSurface: declaredReturnSurface)
		self.artifact.recordFuncSignature(name, signature: FuncSignature.create(paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramAggregateNames: self.paramAggregateNamesOf(paramTypes), paramDeclaredNames: self.paramDeclaredNamesOf(paramTypes), returnAggregateName: self.erasedAggregateNameOfReturn(returnType), paramExistentialSites: self.paramExistentialSitesOf(paramTypes, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts), returnExistentialSites: self.returnExistentialSitesOf(returnType, returnTypeStart: returnTypeStart), extensionConstraints: self.enclosingExtensionConstraints), nameLine: nameTok.line, nameColumn: nameTok.column, sourceFilePath: self.filePath, synthesized: false)
		self.commitRangeChecks(name)
	end 'parseFunction'

	// ⛔ **A BODY THAT DROPS AN OPAQUE `T` MUST CARRY THE LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR THAT DROP READS (W10).** Asked once
	// the whole body has been emitted, because "was a drop emitted" is a fact about the WHOLE body — and asked
	// HERE rather than at the drop, because the descriptor slot is reserved before the body is parsed and a late
	// discovery can only refuse, never reserve.
	//
	// The reservation (`scanDescriptorNeedsInto`' `dropsFeed`) rests on a token pre-scan concluding that a
	// STRAIGHT-LINE body always MOVES its feed parameter. That holds when the fed receiver is a real opaque
	// `Array`, which always takes ownership; it does not when the receiver is a USER type whose `push`/`set`/
	// `insert` merely BORROWS, and the two are the same token shape one receiver TYPE apart, which the pre-scan
	// (running before any body is parsed) cannot resolve. So the over-detected case is refused here.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THAT IS ITS ORIGINATING CASE AND NOT ITS REACH, AND THE MESSAGE USED TO ASSERT THE CASE AS THE CAUSE**
	// (corrected at review, both measured on this tip):
	//
	//   • `static shrink(xs ElementArray)` doing `xs.pop()` — a drop of a MOVED-OUT element, no `push` anywhere.
	//     `movesOutContainerElement` carries `not isStatic`, so the static reserves nothing. On `cad4cf30d` this
	//     ABORTED in `LowerMaxonToStd.appendDropTypeParamDescriptor`; here it is refused with a position.
	//   • a conditional `items.push(item)` inside an `extension Container` on a generic type — a real opaque array,
	//     a real move-in, and the RESERVATION side is what is missing: the descriptor-need fixpoint ran for a
	//     `type` body and never for an EXTENSION's. On `cad4cf30d` this **compiles and exits 0**, so this guard
	//     does turn one previously-compiling program red. It is a refusal rather than a wrong answer, and the gap
	//     under it (extension-scope descriptor needs) is filed rather than closed here.
	//   • a CURSOR `for` element in a shared generic body (W57) — `for item in source` over a value held at a
	//     parameterized interface. Its element is `current()`'s result, which is the enclosing type's own
	//     parameter and is OWNED (the impl retains before returning), so the trip owes a drop; the pre-scan
	//     reserves for the STORE, so a loop that stores into an `Array with <type parameter>` is served and one
	//     that merely reads is refused here.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THAT THIRD SHAPE IS NOW TWO SHAPES, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS STILL HERE (W159).** What separates
	// them is the SOURCE, and with it the dispatch the loop emits:
	//
	//   • **`for … in self` — SERVED, since W159.** The receiver is the enclosing instance, so the protocol
	//     calls are DIRECT (`emitDeclaredProtocolCall`) into the conformer's own shared bodies and the
	//     descriptor the drop reads is the one this method already carries. `scanDescriptorSignalsIn`'s
	//     `walksReceiverAsACursor` reserves it, gated on the cursor genuinely yielding a bare type parameter
	//     (`receiverCursorElementIsOpaque`). This is what `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable`
	//     needs — its `contains` reads its element and stores it nowhere, so it is the one traversal in the
	//     library that DROPS rather than moves, and it is re-parsed onto every conformer. Before W159 every
	//     listed conformer escaped by accident (`Set`/`Array` contest `contains`, `Map`'s element is a tuple,
	//     `Range`/`String` have concrete elements), so a user's `type Bag uses Element implements Iterable`
	//     was the first thing in the language to reach it.
	//
	//   • **any OTHER source — STILL REFUSED, and the refusal is load-bearing.** A parameter, a field or a
	//     local may be a value held at a PARAMETERIZED INTERFACE or at a `where`-constrained type parameter,
	//     and the pre-scan resolves no receiver. Reserving a descriptor would let such a body compile, and the
	//     dispatch it then emits — `current()` through a witness table into a GENERIC conformer's impl —
	//     passes no layout descriptor at all, because the witness ABI has no slot for one. MEASURED
	//     byte-identical on two tips: **0xC0000005**. Lifting THAT belongs with the ABI slot; `self` is the
	//     one spelling that can never be an existential, which is why it could be split off ahead of it. See
	//     `forStatementWalksBareReceiverAt`, whose positive test for what follows `self` is what keeps
	//     `for v in self.item` — a `where T is Seq` parameter — on this side of the line.
	//
	// So the message states the FACT it is certain of — a drop with no descriptor to drop through — and offers the
	// causes as causes.
	function requireDescriptorForOpaqueDrops(owner ByteArray, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		try self.requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores(owner, line: line, column: column)
		if not self.functionDroppedOpaqueWithoutDescriptor 'nothingOwed'
			return
		end 'nothingOwed'
		throw ParseError.unsupported("'{String.from(owner)}' owns an opaque type-parameter value it must release on some path, but the method reserves no layout descriptor to release it through — the shared generic body compiles once for every instantiation, so the value's destructor is read from the enclosing instance's descriptor at run time, and the parameter carrying it is reserved only for the method shapes that are known ahead of the body to need one. Three shapes reach this: a type-parameter argument handed to a `push`/`set`/`insert` on something that is NOT an `Array` and so never takes ownership of it (move it into an `Array with <type parameter>` or a type-parameter field instead); a `pop`/`remove`/`removeFirst` of an opaque element in a `static function` (do it on an instance method, which can source the descriptor from `self`); a `for … in` over a value held at a PARAMETERIZED interface, whose element is the enclosing type's own parameter and is owned per trip (store it into an `Array with <type parameter>`, which reserves the descriptor, or iterate in a method of a concrete instantiation); and a CLOSURE written inside any of them, whose lifted body is a function of its own and reserves no descriptor however well served the method around it is (do the owning work in the method and hand the closure a value it need not release)", line: line, column: column)
	end 'requireDescriptorForOpaqueDrops'

	// ⛔ **A BODY THAT STORES A BORROWED OPAQUE `T` MUST CARRY THE DESCRIPTOR THAT REFERENCE IS TAKEN THROUGH
	// (W41-opaque)** — the store-side sibling of the guard above, asked at the same moment and for the same
	// reason: the slot is reserved before the body is parsed, so a late discovery can only refuse.
	//
	// The reservation (`scanDescriptorNeedsInto`'s `buildsRecordFromOpaqueSource`) rests on a token pre-scan
	// finding BOTH a record construction and a borrowing read of an opaque value in the same body. Both halves
	// are over-approximations that cost only an unused parameter — but the pre-scan cannot resolve a RECEIVER,
	// so a body whose only record construction is a `Self{…}` in a `static` reserves nothing (a static generic
	// constructor has no descriptor to source, `dropsFeed`'s stated bound), and such a body storing a borrowed
	// opaque value is refused here rather than aborting the compiler in `appendTypeParamDescriptor`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A SECOND SHAPE REACHES IT SINCE W41-fix, AND I WROTE THE OPPOSITE HERE BEFORE MEASURING IT.**
	// `handleEscapingBorrowFeed` takes a reference through this same descriptor when a body FORWARDS a borrowed
	// opaque `T` to a sibling that stores it, so such a body owes a descriptor too — and the claim that *"the
	// descriptor-need fixpoint already gives a caller whatever its self-callee needs"* is FALSE for exactly
	// this case: the storing sibling MOVES its parameter, so it needs no descriptor itself, so there is no need
	// to propagate and the forwarding caller is left with none. MEASURED on a `relay(i)` doing
	// `stash(try items.get(i) otherwise panic(…))` — refused here, where the oracle runs it.
	//
	// ⭐ That refusal REPLACES A MISCOMPILE rather than a working program: before W41-fix the same body handed
	// the sibling a borrow it moved into durable storage, which is the `Map.grow()` double free itself. So the
	// direction is sound and the shape is simply not built yet — a descriptor seed for "forwards a borrowed
	// opaque value to a storing sibling" is what would let it compile, and the token pre-scan cannot see that
	// forward (the value is an expression, not a parameter, so the feed-edge scanner does not record it
	// either). Measured corpus reach: ZERO — the suite is 4771/0 and every case builds the whitelisted stdlib.
	// The MESSAGE therefore names both shapes rather than asserting the `static` one as the cause.
	function requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores(owner ByteArray, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		if not self.functionStoredOpaqueWithoutDescriptor 'nothingOwed'
			return
		end 'nothingOwed'
		throw ParseError.unsupported("'{String.from(owner)}' takes a reference to a borrowed type-parameter value, but the method reserves no layout descriptor to take that reference through — the shared generic body compiles once for every instantiation, so how a `T` is referenced (a copy for a `String`, an incref for a struct, nothing for an `int`) is read from the enclosing instance's descriptor at run time, and the parameter carrying it is reserved only for the method shapes that are known ahead of the body to need one. Two shapes reach this: a `static function` that builds a record out of a borrowed `T`, which has no `self` to source the descriptor from (build the record in an instance method instead); and a method that FORWARDS a borrowed `T` — a `get`/`first`/`last`, a `for` element, an opaque field read — to a sibling that moves it into durable storage, which is not built yet (read the value in the sibling itself, or pass it a value this method owns); and a CLOSURE written inside any method at all, whose lifted body reserves no descriptor of its own (take the reference in the method and hand the closure the result)", line: line, column: column)
	end 'requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores'

	// Commit this function's accumulated range-check sites to the artifact (P1.9), keyed by `name`.
	// Files nothing when the function has no cast into a ranged alias AND no ranged return — the
	// common case — so the registry stays proportional to the checks a program actually contains.
	function commitRangeChecks(name ByteArray)
		// P1.6-C rides this per-function channel too (`FunctionRangeChecks.valueInstanceAliases`), so a
		// function with NO range check but a generic-instance construction still files an entry. The presence
		// gate reads `count()` (no allocation); only a function that actually constructs an instance packages a
		// fresh map, every other filed entry carries the SHARED empty one.
		let hasInstanceAliases = self.valueInstanceAlias.count() != 0
		// ⚠ A RANGED PARAMETER IS ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, even for a function with no site of its own: the entry is
		// how this function's CALLERS learn what its parameters were declared as (`paramSites`) and where its own
		// entry guard goes, and the caller's site names it by the name this map is keyed by.
		if self.rangeCheckSites.isEmpty() and self.rangeCheckArgSites.isEmpty() and self.rangeCheckReturnSites.isEmpty() and self.rangeCheckParamSites.isEmpty() and not hasInstanceAliases 'noSideData'
			return
		end 'noSideData'
		let instanceAliases = self.packagedInstanceAliases() if hasInstanceAliases else sharedEmptyInstanceAliasNames
		self.artifact.recordFunctionRangeChecks(name, checks: FunctionRangeChecks.create(self.filePath, sites: self.rangeCheckSites, argSites: self.rangeCheckArgSites, returnAliasName: self.rangeCheckReturnAlias, returnSites: self.rangeCheckReturnSites, paramSites: self.rangeCheckParamSites, valueInstanceAliases: instanceAliases))
	end 'commitRangeChecks'

	// Package the parser's per-value instance-alias provenance (ValueId ⇒ interned alias id, file-local) into
	// the NAME-keyed map SemanticCheck reads (P1.6-C) — names travel the merge with no id remap. Called only
	// when the function actually constructed an instance (`commitRangeChecks` gates on `count()`).
	function packagedInstanceAliases() returns ValueInstanceAliasNameMap
		var out = ValueInstanceAliasNameMap.create()
		for (value, aliasId) in self.valueInstanceAlias 'eachInstanceAlias'
			out.upsert(value, value: self.internedName(aliasId))
		end 'eachInstanceAlias'
		return out
	end 'packagedInstanceAliases'

	// The name of the function's return type IF it is a ranged alias — integer or float — else EMPTY
	// (`ExitCode`, a bare primitive, void: none is a user ranged alias). Asked once per function, off
	// the DECLARED return type, before `resolveTypes` collapses the alias away — which is the whole
	// reason the pass reads a side table rather than the resolved return type.
	//
	// ⭐ A FLOAT ALIAS USED TO BE EXCLUDED HERE, and removing that exclusion is what closed #19. The
	// carve-out was the ONLY thing keeping float bit patterns away from `InsertRangeChecks`' signed
	// integer compares — which made the pass's correctness rest on a caller's discretion. The pass now
	// carries its own float arm (`aliasIsFloat` and the predicates it forks), so the domain is decided
	// where the bounds are read rather than where the site is recorded.
	function rangedReturnAliasName(returnType MaxonReturnType) returns ByteArray
		return match returnType 'r'
			void gives EmptySourceText
			value(inner) gives self.rangedAliasName(maxonTypeTag(inner), nameId: typeNameIdOf(inner))
		end 'r'
	end 'rangedReturnAliasName'

	// ⭐ **THE PARAMETER TWIN OF THE LINE ABOVE — one site per RANGED-ALIAS parameter, and a THROW when none
	// of them is.** Asked once per function, off the declared parameter types, at the one point they still
	// carry their names.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT USED TO ANSWER "NONE" WITH AN EMPTY ARRAY, AND THAT SENTINEL IS WHAT COST THIS FILE AN
	// APPARATUS.** Returning the empty list meant returning SOMETHING, and the cheapest something was a
	// module-level shared array — which then had to be laundered through a `var` binding, guarded by an
	// `is`-identity test and privatised by a free function before any `push`, an invariant held by nothing
	// but a comment. See `RangedParamError` for why the absence is an error instead, and
	// `sharedEmptyParamSites` for what is left of the array once nothing writes it.
	//
	// It exists because a CALL ARGUMENT cannot be checked where it is written. Every other position holds
	// its own declared type; a call argument's type belongs to the callee, which may be declared in a file
	// this one is parsed independently of — the whole-program `ProgramSignatures` sweep carries return
	// types, not parameter types — so the alias has to be PUBLISHED here and read back by the whole-program
	// pass. See `RangeCheckArgSite`; and A1f made the same record the anchor of the entry guard that
	// enforces the range on an argument no constant folds (`RangeCheckParamSite`), which is why the site
	// carries the parameter NAME TOKEN's line and column.
	//
	// ⚠ It walks the DECLARED list, not the reserved one: `paramTypes` holds exactly the user's
	// parameters, while the ValueId space `parseFunction` reserves also carries closure-env companions, the
	// layout descriptor and the witness slots. `argSlotPosition` maps an argument to a position in the
	// USER list, and a user parameter's ValueId IS that position, so indexing anything wider would slide
	// every alias by the count of hidden slots.
	//
	// The array is built lazily — most functions take no ranged parameter at all, and one shared empty
	// array is the difference between that and one allocation per function in the program.
	function rangedParamSites(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray) returns RangeCheckParamSiteArray throws RangedParamError
		if not self.declaresARangedParam(paramTypes) 'noneRanged'
			throw RangedParamError.noRangedParameters
		end 'noneRanged'

		var sites = RangeCheckParamSiteArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("rangedParamSites: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			let aliasName = self.rangedAliasName(maxonTypeTag(paramType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(paramType))
			if aliasName.isEmpty() 'notRanged'
				continue
			end 'notRanged'

			let nameRange = try paramRanges.get(i) otherwise panic("rangedParamSites: paramRanges.get OOB at {i} — one range is pushed per parameter name token, so the two lists have the same length")
			sites.push(RangeCheckParamSite.create(i, aliasName: aliasName, line: nameRange.line, column: nameRange.column))
		end 'eachParam'
		return sites
	end 'rangedParamSites'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS PARAMETER LIST DECLARE A RANGED ALIAS AT ALL** — the question `rangedParamSites` must
	// answer BEFORE it builds anything, so that a function with none allocates no list to report it with.
	//
	// It answers a `bool` rather than the first ranged index, and that is the same rule this pair exists to
	// obey: an index would need a *"none"* spelling, and the only ones available are a sentinel past the end
	// or a second error — one sentinel traded for another. The cost is that the fill loop below re-probes
	// from 0; that is `count()` extra `rangedAliasName` lookups on the RARE path where a ranged parameter
	// exists, against zero allocations on the common path where none does.
	function declaresARangedParam(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray) returns bool
		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("declaresARangedParam: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			if not self.rangedAliasName(maxonTypeTag(paramType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(paramType)).isEmpty() 'ranged'
				return true
			end 'ranged'
		end 'eachParam'

		return false
	end 'declaresARangedParam'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A `MaxonType` IS ASKED WHETHER IT DENOTES A RANGED ALIAS.** Every reader of that
	// question goes through here — the return path above, and the `as`-cast path's TWO readers (which
	// alias a narrowing check names, and what that alias's range says about the shift fill).
	//
	// It hands back the ALIAS rather than its name so a caller that needs both answers pays for one
	// lookup: `signatures.aliasOf` is a whole-program registry probe that CONSTRUCTS its `DeclaredAlias`,
	// so a second call is a second allocation and not a second field read. `applyCast` is that caller.
	function declaredAliasOfType(t MaxonType) returns DeclaredAlias
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.declaredAliasOfNameId(typeNameIdOf(t))
	end 'declaredAliasOfType'

	// The same question asked of a VALUE rather than of a written type — the shape the `try … otherwise`
	// check needs, where what is in hand is the try's result and not the callee's declared return type.
	// A ranged-int alias value carries tag `named` and its alias name in the parallel name column (see
	// `aggregateNameOf`), so the two routes reach the identical registry probe below.
	function declaredAliasOfValue(value ValueId) returns DeclaredAlias
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.declaredAliasOfNameId(self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'declaredAliasOfValue'

	// The registry probe both spellings above end in, written once: a file-local interner id becomes the
	// name, and the name becomes this FILE's answer for it (the alias registry is scoped per declaring
	// file, so the reader's path is not optional).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CANNOT TELL THE FILLER ID FROM A REAL ONE, AND NOTHING HERE CAN.** `UnnamedTypeId` is 0 and
	// `TypeNameInterner` starts empty, so id 0 is the first name a file mentions; a guard here would
	// refuse that name. The obligation is on the MINT — a value tagged `named` carries a real id — and
	// `parseUnary`'s `-` arm records what it cost when one did not.
	function declaredAliasOfNameId(nameId TypeNameId) returns DeclaredAlias
		return self.signatures.aliasOf(self.internedName(nameId), readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'declaredAliasOfNameId'

	// ⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION ASKED OF AN INDEX-SPACE TYPE** — one read out of `ProgramSignatures` (a
	// container's element, an instance's substituted argument) rather than minted by this file's parse. Same
	// registry probe, DIFFERENT INTERNER, and the difference is not cosmetic: the id means one name in the
	// index's table and whatever name that NUMBER happens to hold in this file's. `indexSpaceTypeName` carries
	// the general measurement; the concrete one here is that `[10 as Byte, 20 as Byte, 30 as Byte]` panicked
	// `internedName` outright the moment the element door was routed through `declaredAliasOfType` above.
	function declaredAliasOfIndexSpaceType(indexSpaceType MaxonType) returns DeclaredAlias
		if maxonTypeTag(indexSpaceType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'spellsNoAlias'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'spellsNoAlias'

		return self.signatures.aliasOf(self.signatures.indexSpaceTypeName(indexSpaceType), readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'declaredAliasOfIndexSpaceType'

	// The ranged-alias name a `(tag, nameId)` pair denotes, else EMPTY — the spelling of the question above
	// for the two readers that want a NAME rather than the alias: the range-check sites, which store one
	// (`commitRangeChecks`), and the EXPECTED side of an argument refusal (`declaredSlotDisplayName`), which
	// prints one. It takes the scalar pair rather than a `MaxonType` because half its callers hold a
	// RESOLVED tag (`floatResolvedTag`) that no written type carries.
	function rangedAliasName(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notNamed'

		return match self.declaredAliasOfNameId(nameId) 'a'
			declared(alias) gives alias.name
			undeclared gives EmptySourceText
		end 'a'
	end 'rangedAliasName'

	// Close a function body. `bodyEnd` is the block its last statement left control in;
	// when that block has no terminator, control can FALL OFF THE END of the function
	// and the parser must supply what the source did not:
	//
	//   • VOID function — the implicit `return`. This is why `function doNothing()
	//     end 'doNothing'` compiles (specs/empty-block.md), and it is what every void
	//     function needs, not just an empty one: no void body writes a trailing
	//     `return`. Without it the block reaches the backend in `Terminator.unset`.
	//   • VALUE-returning function — an error: control would leave with no value to
	//     return. E3013, positioned at the function's name (specs/missing-return-error.md).
	//
	// An already-terminated `bodyEnd` covers the UNREACHABLE case for free: an `if`
	// whose branches all return leaves its continuation marked `dead`, which counts as
	// terminated — so `main` ending in a fully-returning `if`/`else` is not a missing
	// return. The synthetic `retVoid` is anchored on the function's name for the same
	// reason E3013 is: no source token spawned it, and the name is where a reader looks
	// for "what does this function return?".
	//
	// Reads the return type off `func` rather than taking it as a parameter — the same
	// place `parseReturnStatement` reads it (see there). `nameTok` still rides along: it
	// is the SPAN the synthetic `retVoid` and the E3013 are anchored on, and a span is
	// not something `IrFunction` carries.
	function closeFunctionBody(func IrFunction, bodyEnd IrBlock, nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		if bodyEnd.isTerminated() 'terminated'
			return
		end 'terminated'

		if not maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(func.maxonReturnType) 'fallsOffEnd'
			throw ParseError.missingReturn(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'fallsOffEnd'

		// ⭐ **THE IMPLICIT RETURN IS A RETURN, AND IT OWES THE SAME DROPS** — this is the THIRD
		// exit path a function has, and it was the one that leaked. `emitScopeDrops` hung only off
		// `parseReturnStatement`, so a void function that simply ENDS —
		//
		//     function helper()
		//         let p = Point.create(1, y: 2)
		//     end 'helper'
		//
		// — never dropped `p` and reported exit 101 on a perfectly valid program. It is exactly the
		// case this routine exists for: "no void body writes a trailing `return`" is the sentence
		// three lines up, and it is precisely why the drop cannot live only where `return` is
		// WRITTEN. A VALUE function cannot reach here (E3013 above), so the leak was void-only,
		// which is why no acceptance case saw it.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE DROPS AND THE TERMINATOR GO TO THE SAME BLOCK, AND IT IS `bodyEnd`.** This used to
		// claim "`bodyEnd` IS `self.currentBlock`" and emit the drops through the latter. It is not: a
		// body ending in an `if`/`while` leaves `currentBlock` at that construct's BODY, so the decrefs
		// landed on one path of a branch whose `retVoid` was on another. See `emitScopeDrops`.
		self.emitScopeDrops(bodyEnd, floor: WholeFunctionScopeFloor, anchor: nameTok)
		self.artifact.emitTerminator(bodyEnd, op: MaxonOp.retVoid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
	end 'closeFunctionBody'

	// ⭐⭐ **WALK THE CURSOR PAST A BODY THIS PARSE IS NOT BUILDING, leaving it ON the function's own `end`**
	// — exactly where `parseStatements` leaves it, so the caller's `consume(TokenKind.end)` and its optional
	// label are untouched and this is invisible to everything after the call.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A THIRD READER OF THE BLOCK RULE, NOT A THIRD COPY OF IT.** Maxon's block structure is
	// written in `parseStatement`/`parseBlockBody` and re-derived once in `opensBlockAt`/`closesBlockAt`;
	// this asks those predicates, exactly as `ensureSiblingReceivers` does, so a construct added to the
	// grammar is learned here through the same door. The `functionDeclarationAt` term is that walk's
	// documented +1: a NAMED function opens a block `opensBlockAt` does not count (it knows
	// `if`/`while`/`match`/`try`), while a CLOSURE is `function(` — no name, no `end` — so it needs none.
	//
	// ⚠ The entry block still needs a terminator or the module is malformed, and `retVoid` is what it gets
	// whatever the declared return type is. That is sound ONLY because a skipped body is by construction one
	// no path from `main` reaches: `queryAllModule`'s fixpoint demands every body the reachability walk
	// touches, and `requireEveryReachedStdlibBodyWasBuilt` turns a disagreement into a panic rather than
	// letting a `retVoid` stand in for a value the program would have read.
	function skipUnreachedFunctionBody(entry IrBlock, nameTok Token)
		var depth = 0 as BlockDepth
		while self.kindAt(self.pos) != TokenKind.eof 'scan'
			if self.functionDeclarationAt(self.pos) or self.opensBlockAt(self.pos) 'opens'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'opens' else if self.closesBlockAt(self.pos) 'closes'
				if depth == 0 'ownEnd'
					break
				end 'ownEnd'
				depth = depth - 1
			end 'closes'

			self.advance()
		end 'scan'

		self.artifact.emitTerminator(entry, op: MaxonOp.retVoid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
	end 'skipUnreachedFunctionBody'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3012 — THE FIRST BINDING THIS FUNCTION DECLARED AND NEVER MENTIONED.** Called at the `end` of
	// a function, once the whole body has been seen, because "never mentioned" is a fact about the WHOLE
	// body and about no smaller part of it. A candidate is a PARAMETER, a `for` binding (A4g) or a body
	// `var`, which is why the list is walked with a `continue` and its kind is asked at the waiver below
	// rather than once before the loop.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **BODY `var`s ARE IN, AND THE NOTE SAYING NO LOCAL COULD BE WAS WRONG ABOUT ITS REASON.** It read:
	// the local rule "is not separable from two refusals shv2 lacks — `let _ = expr` is E2001 and
	// `_ = <pure call>` is E3064 — until all three land together, a borrowed element that may be neither
	// bound, nor discarded, nor used has no legal spelling at all." That premise is the ORACLE's and does
	// not transfer: **shv2's `_ =` DOOR CONSULTS NO PURITY VERDICT**, so `_ = <any call>` is already the
	// discard spelling here (`parseAssignment`'s `DiscardBindingName` arm) and such a value HAS a legal
	// spelling before this rule is written. MEASURED, not reasoned: `_ = f()` on a non-mutating call compiles
	// and runs. E2001 and E3064 are not blockers, and neither is anything else the note names.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REASON WAS "shv2 has no purity analysis" AND THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE** —
	// `SemanticCheck.buildEffectFreeSummary` landed with the tuple-assign port. The CONCLUSION is unchanged
	// because it never rested on the analysis being absent, only on this door not asking it, and this door
	// still does not.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT IS REAL IS A COST THE NOTE NEVER MENTIONED — the unused-`let` half costs 82 cases of this
	// suite, in EXPRESSIVENESS rather than tidiness.** See `UnusedBindingKind.mutableLocal`, which carries
	// the measurement and says which spec owns the rung. A body `var` rides now because E3077 owes it.
	//
	// ⇒ **it returns a VERDICT, because E3077 rides behind it** (`reportVarShouldBeLet`): "an unused `var`
	// reports as UNUSED, not as should-be-let" is the two scans' ORDER over one list, which is how both
	// reference compilers state it (v1's `checkPendingUnusedVars` runs the same three checks in the same
	// order behind the same "has anything fired yet" flag).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **"REFERENCED" IS A FACT ABOUT THE NAME, NOT ABOUT THE BINDING**, so the verdict asks TWO questions
	// and the second is the one a per-binding bit cannot answer: two sequential `for i` loops mint two
	// `VarInfo`s, and the oracle ACCEPTS the pair when only the second reads `i` (its `_referencedVars` is a
	// per-function `HashSet<string>`). See `referencedNames`, which is where that half lives, and
	// `specs-shv2/unused-variable-name-scope.md`, which pins it.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE DIAGNOSTIC PER FUNCTION, AND IT IS THE EARLIEST DECLARATION.** `unusedCandidates` is in
	// declaration order — parameters as `bindParameters` mints them, then each `for` binding and each body
	// `var` as the body reaches it — so a forward scan reports the leftmost unused one, which is what both
	// reference compilers produce, because both THROW on the first one they find. The scan stops for the
	// same reason they do: the rest are the same fact about the same edit, and `multiple-unused` (two
	// unused parameters) pins exactly one line.
	//
	// ⚠ **PARAMETERS DO NOT ALL PRECEDE LOCALS IN THE ORACLE, AND HERE THEY DO — a deliberate difference
	// with nothing to pin it.** v1 pushes every parameter, THEN every local, so an unused parameter outranks
	// an unused local declared above it; shv2's list is in true source order. No case in either suite
	// declares an unused parameter and an unused local in one function, so the two agree everywhere the
	// corpus looks, and source order is the rule the header above already states.
	//
	// ⚠ **A FUNCTION WHOSE BODY FAILED TO PARSE NEVER REACHES HERE**, which is the suppression v1 has to
	// spend a diagnostic scan on (`functionHasPriorDiagnostic`): a `ParseError` unwinds past this call, so a
	// body with a real error never also reports its unused names as cascade noise. shv2 gets it structurally
	// and needs no rule for it.
	function reportUnusedBindings(methodName ByteArray) returns bool
		for candidate in self.unusedCandidates 'eachCandidate'
			if self.bindingNameWasMentioned(candidate.binding) 'wasMentioned'
				continue
			end 'wasMentioned'

			// ⭐ **THE WAIVER IS ABOUT PARAMETERS AND MUST NOT REACH A LOOP BINDING OR A LOCAL.** MEASURED on
			// the oracle: an unread `for i in 0 upto limit 'l'` inside an interface-dictated method is still
			// `error E3012: unused variable: 'i'`, and so is an unread body `let`. The oracle draws the same
			// line structurally — its `skipParamCheck` skips the parameter loop and never the locals loop.
			//
			// A `continue` rather than the `return` this used to be: with three candidate kinds in one list, a
			// waived parameter must not suppress a later declaration the contract has no say over. Asked
			// here rather than before the loop because it walks two registries, and the overwhelming majority
			// of functions never reach it; `and` short-circuits, so a non-parameter never pays for it.
			let waivable = match candidate.kind 'candidateKind'
				parameter gives true
				loopVariable gives false
				mutableLocal to immutableLocal gives false
				// A divergent `otherwise`'s `(e)` is the author's own and OPTIONAL — no contract can force it
				// to be declared, so there is nothing for the interface waiver to excuse.
				divergentHandlerBinding gives false
			end 'candidateKind'
			if waivable and self.parametersAreInterfaceDictated(methodName) 'contractDictatedTheList'
				continue
			end 'contractDictatedTheList'

			self.recordBindingHygieneDiagnostic(ErrorCode.semanticUnusedVariable, message: "unused variable: '{String.from(candidate.binding.name)}'", range: candidate.nameRange)
			return true
		end 'eachCandidate'

		return false
	end 'reportUnusedBindings'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3077 — THE FIRST `var` THIS FUNCTION DECLARED AND NEVER NEEDED WRITABLE.** The twin of the scan
	// above, over the SAME candidate list, run only when that one reported nothing — which is the whole of
	// "E3012 takes precedence" (`unused-takes-precedence`: `var x = 10` + `return 0` is the unused
	// diagnostic, not this one). Both reference compilers order the two exactly this way.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CANDIDATE TEST IS `binding.mutable`, WHICH IS EXACTLY "THE AUTHOR WROTE `var`"** — not a kind
	// match, and deliberately: a parameter is immutable (`ParametersAreMutable` is false) and a `for`
	// element is bound `mutable: false`, so the property already separates the kinds and cannot drift from
	// them the way an enumerated list of exempt kinds would. A future mutable binding kind is checked the
	// day it becomes mutable, which is the day the rule starts applying to it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `var` NOTHING MENTIONED IS SKIPPED RATHER THAN REPORTED, and that is not the same as the
	// precedence above.** The precedence covers one function reaching both rules; this covers the SECOND
	// unused declaration, which E3012 deliberately did not report (one diagnostic per function). Reporting
	// it here would say "use `let`" about a binding whose real fix is deletion. v1 skips it on the identical
	// `referenced` test, for the identical reason.
	function reportVarShouldBeLet()
		for candidate in self.unusedCandidates 'eachCandidate'
			if not candidate.binding.mutable 'notAVar'
				continue
			end 'notAVar'

			if not self.bindingNameWasMentioned(candidate.binding) 'unusedIsE3012sToReport'
				continue
			end 'unusedIsE3012sToReport'

			if candidate.binding.mutabilityExercised or self.nameIsListed(self.mutabilityExercisedNames, name: candidate.binding.name) 'neededTheMutability'
				continue
			end 'neededTheMutability'

			self.recordBindingHygieneDiagnostic(ErrorCode.semanticVarShouldBeLet, message: "variable '{String.from(candidate.binding.name)}' is never reassigned; use 'let' instead of 'var'", range: candidate.nameRange)
			return
		end 'eachCandidate'
	end 'reportVarShouldBeLet'

	// Was the NAME this binding declared mentioned anywhere in the function being parsed? The per-binding
	// bit first, then the name-scoped half — see `referencedNames` for why the second question exists and
	// why a per-binding bit structurally cannot answer it. The scan is reached only by a declaration one of
	// the two scans above is about to report or excuse, so a function whose bindings are all used never
	// walks the list at all.
	function bindingNameWasMentioned(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return binding.referenced or self.nameIsListed(self.referencedNames, name: binding.name)
	end 'bindingNameWasMentioned'

	// ⭐ **THE SINGLE WRITER OF E3012'S EVIDENCE** — the per-binding bit and the per-function name list,
	// together. Three doors call it (`requireBindingLive`, `parseAssignment`, `emitCaptureRead`); see
	// `VarInfo.referenced` for why a name can be mentioned in exactly three places none of the others sees.
	//
	// The bit is the DEDUP: a name reaches the list once per binding rather than once per mention, so a hot
	// read path pays one already-set test and nothing else.
	function markBindingReferenced(binding VarInfo)
		if binding.referenced 'alreadyRecorded'
			return
		end 'alreadyRecorded'

		binding.referenced = true
		self.referencedNames.push(binding.name)
	end 'markBindingReferenced'

	// ⭐ **THE SINGLE WRITER OF E3077'S EVIDENCE**, the exact twin of `markBindingReferenced` above and
	// deduped the same way. Its doors are the ones that REQUIRE a binding to be writable and then let the
	// program through — see `VarInfo.mutabilityExercised` for the list and for why a receiver and an
	// argument are marked without asking whether the callee actually writes.
	function markMutabilityExercised(binding VarInfo)
		if binding.mutabilityExercised 'alreadyRecorded'
			return
		end 'alreadyRecorded'

		binding.mutabilityExercised = true
		self.mutabilityExercisedNames.push(binding.name)
	end 'markMutabilityExercised'

	// The same mark reached by NAME, for the doors that hold a name rather than the binding — a call
	// ARGUMENT, a method RECEIVER and a `match` scrutinee, all of which carry the storage's name because
	// E3019, E3070 and the payload write-back already needed it.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE GUARD IS `innermostMutableVarPosition`, NOT `scope.contains`, and it is the QUESTION rather
	// than a cheap proxy for it**: only a `var` in scope can ever be an E3077 candidate, so a name that is
	// not one — a `let`, a parameter, a global, an undeclared token the sweep happened to walk past — has
	// nothing to mark. It is also what makes this affordable on the parse path: the index is the SHARED
	// EMPTY map for any function that declares no `var` (`sharedEmptyMutableVarInnermost`), so such a
	// function pays one lookup that cannot hit, and the argument sweep never reaches this at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BINDING IT THEN MARKS MAY BE A `let` THAT SHADOWS THAT `var`, AND THAT IS CORRECT.** The
	// verdict is name-scoped (see `mutabilityExercisedNames`), so what matters is that the NAME lands in
	// the list — which excuses the outer `var`, exactly as both references' per-name sets do.
	// ⚠⚠ **THE GATE ASKS THE SCOPE, NOT `mutableVars`, AND PBR-2 IS WHY.** It used to ask
	// `innermostMutableVarPosition` — membership in the loop-phi tracking list — as a cheap stand-in for "is
	// this name a `var`?". A CELL-RESIDENT `var` is deliberately never pushed into that list (its pointer is
	// loop-invariant, so it must mint no phi — see `declareCellLocal`), so the stand-in started answering NO
	// for exactly the bindings pass-by-reference exists to serve: MEASURED as E3077 *"variable 'n' is never
	// reassigned; use 'let' instead of 'var'"* on `var n = 0` + `setTo99(n)`, on nine cases at once.
	//
	// The scope answers the question that was always being asked, and answers it for both representations. It
	// is equivalent for every binding the old test admitted: a `let`, a parameter and a self-field alias are
	// all `mutable: false`, and a top-level `var` is not in scope here at all.
	function markNameMutabilityExercised(name ByteArray)
		if not self.scope.contains(name) 'namesNoLocalBinding'
			return
		end 'namesNoLocalBinding'

		let binding = self.scope.getInfo(name)
		if not binding.mutable 'namesNoVarInScope'
			return
		end 'namesNoVarInScope'

		self.markMutabilityExercised(binding)
	end 'markNameMutabilityExercised'

	// Does `names` hold this name? A linear scan because these lists are short (one entry per binding that
	// recorded the fact) and each is consulted only for a declaration E3012 or E3077 is about to report or
	// excuse — a map would cost a hash of every name on the write path to save a walk on a path that runs a
	// handful of times per compile.
	//
	// ONE scan for the two lists rather than one function each: they answer different questions
	// (`referencedNames`, `mutabilityExercisedNames`) and are read through the two predicates that name
	// those questions, but "is this name in that list" is a single fact and a second copy of the walk would
	// be a second place for the name-scoped rule to be got wrong.
	function nameIsListed(names ByteArrayArray, name ByteArray) returns bool
		for listed in names 'eachListed'
			if listed.equals(name) 'sameName'
				return true
			end 'sameName'
		end 'eachListed'

		return false
	end 'nameIsListed'

	// ⭐ **DOES AN INTERFACE THIS TYPE IMPLEMENTS REQUIRE A METHOD OF THIS NAME?** — the E3012 parameter
	// exemption. An implementer is forced to declare every parameter the contract names, even one this
	// particular implementation has no use for, so the unused check may not be applied to a method that
	// satisfies a requirement.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE WAIVER IS PER METHOD, NOT PER TYPE.** A sibling `function helper(unused Integer)` on the same
	// conforming type is nobody's requirement and is still checked — the two cases sit one beside the other
	// in `specs-shv2/interface-conformance.md`. And it waives the PARAMETERS only: a `for` binding inside an
	// interface method is the author's own, so it stays checked.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT USED TO BE STRUCTURAL AND IS NOW A CONDITION SOMETHING HAS TO ASK (A4g).** While a parameter
	// was the only candidate kind, "waives the parameters only" needed no code — the drain never saw
	// anything else, and it could `return` on the first waived one. `unusedCandidates` now carries two kinds,
	// so the caller tests `candidate.kind` and `continue`s: a waived parameter must not end the scan before a
	// loop binding the contract has no say over. `interface-conformance.md`'s
	// `interface-method-loop-variable-still-errors` is red both ways round.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ANSWERED AT PARSE TIME, AND THAT IS A DEPARTURE FROM v1 WITH A REASON.** v1 has to
	// RE-evaluate its parser's flag whole-program (`checkPendingUnusedVars`), because its parse-time answer
	// was a false negative whenever the implemented interface was parsed after the conformer. shv2's
	// declaration sweep completes before any body is parsed, so `signatures.interfaceMap()` is already the
	// WHOLE program here — the same store `requireInterfaceForParse` resolves a `where` constraint through —
	// and file order cannot move the answer. Its contribution to the parse memo's key is already carried:
	// `recordInterfaceDeclaration` mixes every requirement's name into the signature hash.
	//
	// Requirements are collected TRANSITIVELY through `extends` (`witnessSlotsOf`, the memoized walk the
	// witness dispatch already uses), so a method satisfying an inherited requirement is waived too.
	//
	// A free function or a closure has no enclosing type, and an `enum`/`union` records no `ConformanceDecl`
	// at all (its `implements` clause files names on the layout — see `ConformanceCheck`'s note, an enum has
	// no methods for a requirement to be checked against), so both answer `false` after one `isEmpty` test.
	function parametersAreInterfaceDictated(methodName ByteArray) returns bool
		if self.enclosingType.isEmpty() 'notAMethod'
			return false
		end 'notAMethod'

		for conformance in self.artifact.conformances 'eachDecl'
			if not conformance.typeName.equals(self.enclosingType) 'anotherType'
				continue
			end 'anotherType'

			for entry in conformance.interfaces 'eachInterface'
				let slots = self.requirementSlotsOf(entry.interfaceName)
				for slot in slots 'eachRequirement'
					if slot.method.name.equals(methodName) 'thisMethodIsRequired'
						return true
					end 'thisMethodIsRequired'
				end 'eachRequirement'
			end 'eachInterface'
		end 'eachDecl'

		return false
	end 'parametersAreInterfaceDictated'

	// The TRANSITIVE requirement list of `interfaceName` — the memoized walk a witness dispatch already
	// takes (`witnessSlotsOf`), so a method satisfying a requirement inherited through `extends` is seen.
	//
	// An `implements` naming something no file declares requires nothing, and that name is E3015's to
	// report (`ConformanceCheck`), not this reader's to guess about — so a miss is the SHARED empty list
	// rather than an allocation or a refusal.
	function requirementSlotsOf(interfaceName ByteArray) returns CollectedInterfaceMethodArray
		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: interfaceName) 'resolve'
			found(iface) then return self.witnessSlotsOf(iface)
			notFound then return sharedEmptyRequirementSlots
		end 'resolve'
	end 'requirementSlotsOf'

	// Parse a comma-separated parameter list `name type[, name type]*` into the
	// parallel `paramNames`/`paramTypes` lists (empty when the list is empty). Each
	// parameter is `name type` — a bare identifier followed by a type reference. A
	// `= <default>` after the type is captured for `owner` (see `ParamDefaultOwner`).
	// The caller has already consumed the `(` and consumes the closing `)`.
	//
	// ⭐ **`paramSurfaces` IS THE PRE-ERASURE SPELLING COLUMN (A2j / A2m)**, the shape
	// `FuncSignature.paramAggregateNames` already established for a union parameter's name: a fact the
	// declaration holds and `parseTypeReference` is about to destroy, captured at the last moment it is
	// readable and carried alongside the columns it describes. It needs no signature-index entry, because the
	// only reader is `bindParameters` — and both live inside one `parseFunction`.
	function parseFunctionParameters(paramNames ByteArrayArray, paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray, paramSurfaces DeclaredSurfaceArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexColumn, owner ParamDefaultOwner, paramTypeHint ClosureParamTypeHint) throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'noParams'
			return
		end 'noParams'

		// The offer as it stands for the parameter about to be read — the caller's hint for the first one,
		// `containerElementTaken` for every one after it. See the spend below, beside the type read.
		var offeredType = paramTypeHint

		while true 'paramLoop'
			// ⭐ A KEYWORD MAY BE A PARAMETER'S NAME (D8) — `function identity(type Integer)`, the shape
			// `keyword-parameter-names.md` is written for. A parameter list admits nothing but `name type` in
			// this position, so the keyword is a name; `keywordIsAName` learns the same fact for the token
			// scans, or a `function f(end Integer)` would read as a block CLOSE that never opened.
			let nameTok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)
			try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
			// Asked at the cursor, which sits on the type's first token — the last instant the spelling
			// `__ManagedMemory` still exists, at any depth (`declaredSurfaceAt`). `slotElementsFromAliases:
			// true`: the tolerant sweep skips a parameter list wholesale (`skipBalancedParens`), so this door
			// is the REAL parse's alone, the whole-program alias registry is complete, and the answer is
			// consumed by `bindParameters` in this same pass rather than stored for another one.
			let declaredSurface = self.declaredSurfaceAt(self.pos, slotElementsFromAliases: true)
			// ⭐ **AND THE TYPE'S OWN FIRST TOKEN, FOR THE SAME REASON ONE LINE UP (W57).** A parameter declared at a
			// PARAMETERIZED interface type is resolved to the bare `interfaceRef(Base)` by `instanceDenotedType`,
			// which is where its `with` arguments stop existing — so the position that still spells them is captured
			// here and read by `bindParameters`, exactly as `paramSurfaces` is. An index rather than the resolved
			// instance because an OMITTED type resolves nothing at all and there is no absent-instance to invent.
			let paramTypeStart = self.pos
			let paramType = try self.parameterTypeOrHinted(offeredType, nameTok: nameTok)

			// ⭐ **SPEND THE CALL POSITION'S OFFER — one offer, one parameter** (see `ClosureParamTypeHint`).
			// Done whether or not THIS parameter took it: a hinted closure that declares its first parameter's
			// type explicitly has still had the one position that types anything, so `function(a Integer, b)`
			// is refused exactly as `function(a, b)` is. Only the offer is spent; `none` stays `none`, so an
			// ordinary function's later untyped parameter keeps the refusal that names the one construct which
			// does infer.
			match offeredType 'spendOffer'
				fromContainerElement then offeredType = ClosureParamTypeHint.containerElementTaken
				containerElementTaken then break 'spendOffer'
				none then break 'spendOffer'
			end 'spendOffer'

			// ⭐ **A DEFAULT VALUE IS CAPTURED, NOT PARSED HERE (BATCH2 slice 4)** — its expression becomes the
			// whole body of a synthesized nullary helper, parsed at the END of the file by
			// `parseDefaultHelpers`, and the call site emits a call to that helper for every argument it
			// omits (see `SignatureIndex.ParamDefaultInfo` for the mechanism and why it is a function).
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS DEFERRED TO END-OF-FILE RATHER THAN PARSED IN PLACE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON
			// THIS RUNG NEEDS NO STATE SAVE/RESTORE.** A helper is a FUNCTION: parsing one needs the ~25
			// per-function columns (scope, value numbering, blocks, drop sets, range-check accumulators)
			// swapped to fresh ones. Done HERE, in the middle of a declaration, that is
			// `parseClosureExpression`'s save/restore dance — whose header records that its ~25 live
			// `savedX` aliases crashed the bootstrap's refcount runtime when they were factored into a
			// method. Drained between top-level declarations there is nothing live to alias: the enclosing
			// function has closed, so the helper simply re-initializes the columns as `parseFunction` does
			// at its own entry and nothing has to be put back.
			//
			// It ALSO gives the default the scope reset it needs for free (v1 does this explicitly): drained
			// outside every declaration, the helper's body cannot see the callee's parameters or locals, nor
			// its enclosing `type`. A default is evaluated at the CALL site and belongs to neither frame.
			// ⭐ **`_` IS EXEMPT, AND IT IS THE ONLY EXEMPTION.** Two `_` parameters DO collide in `Scope.vars` —
			// `bindParameters` installs each through `declareParameterBinding` like any other parameter, and
			// `Scope.install` upserts, so the second displaces the first. What makes that harmless is not that
			// the collision is absent but that NOTHING CAN OBSERVE WHICH ONE WON: both denote the same discard,
			// no body may name `_` to read it, and neither is an E3012 candidate. A signature needs one `_` per
			// parameter it does not want, and
			// `function make(_ Integer = 1, _ String = "n", item T)` is the ordinary spelling of "two defaults
			// ahead of the one that matters". MEASURED on the oracle: that declaration compiles and runs, while
			// shv2 refused it `E2014 duplicate parameter name '_'`.
			//
			// Keyed on the name being EXACTLY `_`: an underscore PREFIX is an ordinary identifier, so two `_x`
			// parameters are still E2014, as is any repeat of a real name.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE AMBIGUITY THIS SEEMS TO OPEN IS CAUGHT AT THE CALL, WHICH IS WHERE IT ACTUALLY OCCURS.**
			// `_` IS a legal argument label (measured: `make(6, _: 3)` runs), so two `_` parameters mean a
			// label that no longer names one position. `slotCallArgs` already refuses a slot filled twice
			// (`ArgSlotResult.duplicateArg`) — and the oracle reports that same program at that same point
			// (`E3005 … parameter '_' already has a value (passed positionally)`), never at the declaration.
			// So the declaration-time check was never what protected the slotting; see `argSlotPosition`.
			if not nameTok.value.equals(DiscardBindingName) and namesInclude(paramNames, name: nameTok.value) 'duplicate'
				throw ParseError.duplicateParameter(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'duplicate'

			// After the duplicate check, so a declaration already refused captures nothing — and BEFORE the
			// pushes below, because `paramNames.count()` is this parameter's own CALL-ALIGNED position only
			// while its own name is still absent from the list.
			if self.at(TokenKind.equals) 'hasDefault'
				try self.captureParamDefault(owner, paramName: nameTok.value, paramIndex: paramNames.count(), paramType: paramType)
			end 'hasDefault'

			paramNames.push(nameTok.value)
			paramTypes.push(paramType)
			paramRanges.push(SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
			paramSurfaces.push(declaredSurface)
			paramTypeStarts.push(paramTypeStart)

			// ⚠ NO COUNT CHECK HERE. There used to be one, testing `paramNames.count()` against the old
			// register cap — a SECOND, and WEAKER, spelling of the ceiling: it counted only the parameters the
			// source writes, while the ABI also passes a companion environment per function-typed parameter,
			// a layout descriptor and one witness per constraint. Two sites, two different counts, one
			// notional rule — the shape this project keeps getting bitten by. The ceiling is now asked exactly
			// once, of the ABI count, by `requireAbiParamCount`; both callers of this routine
			// (`parseFunction`, `parseClosureExpression`) ask it after they know their own full count.
			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endParams'
				break
			end 'endParams'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'paramLoop'
	end 'parseFunctionParameters'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `Base with Args` INSTANCE THE DECLARED TYPE AT `index` NAMES (W57)** — the fact
	// `ProgramSignatures.instanceDenotedType` destroys when it collapses an interface instance to the bare
	// `interfaceRef(Base)` the value is typed with, recovered from the one thing that survives: the tokens.
	// `MapError.keyNotFound` for a type that names no generic alias, which is every ordinary declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO NAME SHAPES ARE `parseTypeReference`'s TWO ALIAS ARMS, IN ITS ORDER, AND THEY MUST STAY
	// THAT ORDER.** An INNER alias resolves under its base-qualified name (`Array.ElementIterable`) and a
	// file-scope one under its bare name, and the inner one wins — a nested `typealias` shadows a top-level
	// one of the same spelling. Asking bare-first would silently type a shared generic body's iteration
	// against some other file's instantiation. This is the only restatement of that rule; the JOIN it reaches
	// (`genericAliasDenotedType`'s two calls) is shared rather than spelled again.
	//
	// ⚠ It declines a non-identifier by TOKEN KIND, which is what lets the receiver's slot hold the
	// signature's `(` rather than an index whose meaning depends on what a name happens to denote.
	function declaredExistentialInstanceAt(index TokenIndex) returns GenericInstanceId throws MapError
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.identifier 'namesNoTypeAtAll'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoTypeAtAll'

		let name = self.tokenAt(index).value
		if self.namesInnerAliasHere(name) 'innerAliasOfTheEnclosingType'
			return try self.declaredGenericAliasInstance(joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: name))
		end 'innerAliasOfTheEnclosingType'

		return try self.declaredGenericAliasInstance(name)
	end 'declaredExistentialInstanceAt'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SIGNATURE'S HALF OF THE SAME FACT (W58)** — which PARAMETERS were declared at a parameterized
	// existential, and at which instance. `bindParameters` records this per VALUE, for the callee's own body
	// (W57); the caller asking *"may this concrete argument widen into that parameter?"* stands in a different
	// function and a different SSA space, so it reads the SIGNATURE. Both derive it from the same
	// `paramTypeStarts` column through the same `declaredExistentialInstanceAt`, which is what keeps one
	// parameter from being read two ways.
	//
	// The `interfaceRef` gate is the same one `bindParameters` applies and for the same reason: only an
	// existential can have had `with` arguments to lose, and a generic alias over a STRUCT base keeps its
	// arguments on the type where every other door already reads them.
	//
	// ⚠ The walk's list is allocated up front and COLLAPSED to the shared empty when nothing claimed, rather
	// than materialized copy-on-write. Copy-on-write would mean reassigning the walk's own binding from
	// inside a branch, which is the measured `E9001 … does not dominate` shape — see
	// `materializedPendingDefaultHelpers`, the one helper still built around it — and the claim here is
	// discovered inside an `if let` that no `continue` guard can flatten without asking
	// `declaredExistentialInstanceAt` twice. The cost is one empty array per parsed function, which is
	// strictly less than the full-length column `paramAggregateNamesOf` builds beside it.
	function paramExistentialSitesOf(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexColumn) returns ParamExistentialSiteArray
		var sites = ParamExistentialSiteArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("paramExistentialSitesOf: paramTypes.get OOB at {i} below the count just read from the same array")
			if maxonTypeTag(paramType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
				continue
			end 'notAnExistential'

			let start = try paramTypeStarts.get(i) otherwise panic("paramExistentialSitesOf: paramTypeStarts.get OOB at {i} — a parameter's columns are built in lockstep")
			if let instance = try self.declaredExistentialInstanceAt(start) 'namesAnInstantiation'
				sites.push(ParamExistentialSite.create(i, instance: instance))
			end 'namesAnInstantiation'
		end 'eachParam'

		return sharedEmptyParamExistentialSites if sites.count() == 0 else sites
	end 'paramExistentialSitesOf'

	// The RETURN slot's half — at most one instance, so an EMPTY column is "this function's return names no
	// instantiation" and there is no absent-value to invent. A `void` return and a non-existential one leave it
	// empty through the same predicate, exactly as a non-existential parameter is skipped above.
	function returnExistentialSitesOf(returnType MaxonReturnType, returnTypeStart TokenIndex) returns GenericInstanceIdArray
		if maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'returnsNothing'
			return sharedEmptyReturnExistentialSites
		end 'returnsNothing'

		if maxonReturnTypeTag(returnType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
			return sharedEmptyReturnExistentialSites
		end 'notAnExistential'

		var sites = GenericInstanceIdArray.create()
		if let instance = try self.declaredExistentialInstanceAt(returnTypeStart) 'namesAnInstantiation'
			sites.push(instance)
		end 'namesAnInstantiation'

		return sharedEmptyReturnExistentialSites if sites.count() == 0 else sites
	end 'returnExistentialSitesOf'

	// ⭐ **THE TOKEN INDEX OF THE DECLARED RETURN TYPE'S OWN NAME** — `self.pos` is the `returns` keyword and
	// the type follows it. Spelled once because two readers want it: `returnDeclaredSurface` reads the buffer
	// spelling off it and `returnExistentialSitesOf` reads the `with` arguments, and a drift between the two
	// would have them describing different tokens of one declaration.
	//
	// With NO return clause it answers `self.pos` itself, which is the newline or the `throws` keyword — a slot
	// that names no type BY TOKEN KIND, which is the same guarantee the receiver's slot rests on (see
	// `parseFunction`'s `receiverParam`) rather than a claim about what some identifier means.
	function returnTypeTokenIndex() returns TokenIndex
		return self.pos if not self.at(TokenKind.returns) else (self.pos + 1) as TokenIndex
	end 'returnTypeTokenIndex'

	// The instance a `typealias` bound `name` to; `MapError.keyNotFound` for a name no generic alias declares.
	// The throwing half of `genericAliasDenotedType`'s join, for the one caller that wants the INSTANCE rather
	// than what it denotes — see that function for why the two are not one door.
	function declaredGenericAliasInstance(name ByteArray) returns GenericInstanceId throws MapError
		if not self.signatures.isGenericAlias(name) 'namesNoInstance'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'namesNoInstance'

		return self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'declaredGenericAliasInstance'

	// ⭐ **THE DECLARED TYPE, OR THE ONE THE CALL POSITION SUPPLIED (`function(p) gives …`).** An OMITTED type
	// is recognized by the parameter list's own punctuation standing where a type would begin — the `,` before
	// the next parameter or the `)` that closes the list — which is the whole test, and it needs no lookahead:
	// no type reference starts with either token. `parseTypeReference` used to meet those two and report
	// "expected a type", which was the correct interim answer while nothing could supply one.
	//
	// A hint reaches here only from a container `map`'s transform argument (see `ClosureParamTypeHint`), so
	// every other omission — including one in a NAMED function's parameter list, which no position types —
	// still refuses, and says which construct does infer.
	//
	// ⭐ The two refusals are different mistakes and say so. `containerElementTaken` means a position DID
	// supply a type and this parameter is past the one it supplies for — the reference bootstrap's E2003,
	// worded as the reference words it, because the fix is the same one it names. `none` means no position
	// supplies anything here, and names the one construct that does.
	function parameterTypeOrHinted(hint ClosureParamTypeHint, nameTok Token) returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		if not (self.at(TokenKind.comma) or self.at(TokenKind.rightParen)) 'declaredType'
			return try self.parseTypeReference()
		end 'declaredType'

		match hint 'hinted'
			fromContainerElement(elementType) then return elementType
			containerElementTaken then throw ParseError.uninferrableClosureParam(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			none then break 'hinted'
		end 'hinted'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("parameter '{String.from(nameTok.value)}' with no type — shv2 infers an omitted parameter type in exactly one position, a closure passed to a container's `{String.from(MapMethod)}`, whose transform takes the container's own element; every other parameter declares its type", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
	end 'parameterTypeOrHinted'

	// Capture one parameter's `= <default>` for its owning declaration and leave the cursor on the token
	// that ENDS it (the `,` before the next parameter, or the list's `)`), so the parameter loop resumes
	// exactly as it would have without a default. The cursor arrives ON the `=`.
	//
	// The two non-function owners are refused here rather than in their own callers, because the refusal is
	// a property of the PARAMETER LIST's owner and this is the one routine that reads a parameter list —
	// stated in either caller it would be a rule about a construct written somewhere other than where the
	// construct is parsed, and the third caller would silently not have it.
	function captureParamDefault(owner ParamDefaultOwner, paramName ByteArray, paramIndex ParamCount, paramType MaxonType) throws ParseError
		let eqTok = self.current()
		match owner 'owner'
			closureLiteral then throw ParseError.unsupported("a default value on closure parameter '{String.from(paramName)}' — a closure is called INDIRECTLY, through a function value, so no call site has a callee name to look the default up by. Give the closure's caller the value explicitly", line: eqTok.line, column: eqTok.column)
			interfaceRequirement then throw ParseError.unsupported("a default value on interface requirement parameter '{String.from(paramName)}' — a witness dispatch slots its arguments against the INTERFACE's formals, which are in no signature registry, so no dispatch site can read a default from the requirement. Declare the default on each conforming type's method", line: eqTok.line, column: eqTok.column)
			declaredFunction then break 'owner'
		end 'owner'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD REFUSAL: THIS NAME'S DECLARATIONS DO NOT DESCRIBE ONE DEFAULT SHAPE, SO NO CALL CAN
		// BE FILLED FOR ALL OF THEM.** The whole-program sweep publishes defaults under the name the SOURCE
		// wrote, and a short call is filled while it is PARSED — a whole pass before `resolveOverloadedCalls`
		// picks a member. An OVERLOAD SET whose members publish the IDENTICAL shape is filled the same way
		// whichever member wins and needs no refusal (W74; the resolved member's own default EXPRESSION is
		// supplied by `SemanticCheck.repointDefaultedArgHelpers`). One whose declarations disagree — a
		// different parameter list, a different set of defaulted positions, or a sibling that defaults nothing
		// at all — has no one answer to fill from, and is refused HERE, at the `=`, with a position.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT ALSO COVERS THE CASE THAT IS NOT AN OVERLOAD SET: the same `function f` written in two
		// files.** That is E3006, and E3006 is reported only once every file has parsed; until then the losing
		// file's own drain reads the WINNER's shape out of the by-name registry and finds no helper at its
		// parameter's position, which reached `requireSweptHelperName`'s panic — MEASURED as a compiler crash
		// with no diagnostic and no source position, where the identical program without the defaults answers
		// a clean E3006. Two files whose declarations happen to AGREE fall through to that clean E3006 now,
		// which is the better diagnostic and was always the one the program deserved.
		let ownerName = paramDefaultOwnerName(owner)

		// ✅ **THE FIFTH REFUSAL IS GONE (W75), AND WHAT RETIRED IT IS A KEY RATHER THAN A BETTER TEST.** A
		// `static` member and an instance member of one type are two registration keys — `m` and `m#__static`
		// — and the sweep used to file BOTH members' defaults under the one name the source wrote, so `T.m()`
		// asked the defaults registry for the static key, missed, filled nothing, and was refused
		// `E3036: 'T.m#__static' expects 1 argument(s) but 0 were provided`: a symbol no source wrote, blamed
		// on a call the author got right. The by-name folds now key by the member each entry belongs to
		// (`ProgramSignatures.sweepRegistrationKey`), so each half of a pair has a shape of its own and there
		// is nothing left for a call to be unable to tell apart. `ownerName` above IS that key.

		if self.signatures.paramDefaultsCollide(ownerName) 'declarationsDisagree'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a default value on parameter '{String.from(paramName)}' of '{String.from(ownerName)}' — that name is declared more than once in this program and the declarations do not agree about their defaults: they differ in their parameter list, in which positions default, or in whether they default anything at all. The whole-program declaration sweep publishes defaults under the name the source wrote and a short call is filled when it is PARSED, a whole pass before the overload is resolved, so a call that omits an argument cannot be told which declaration's shape to fill. Give every declaration of this name the same parameters and the same defaults, or drop the default", line: eqTok.line, column: eqTok.column)
		end 'declarationsDisagree'

		// ⚠ **THE HELPER'S NAME IS NOT MINTED HERE — IT IS READ BACK FROM THE SWEEP AT THE DRAIN**
		// (`parseDefaultHelper`). The sweep is the PARAMETER mint's one home, because it is the mint a CALL
		// SITE in another file must be able to see; a second one here would be the same name derived twice,
		// and the only input the two could disagree on — the parameter's index — is exactly what they did
		// disagree on when this was written that way. Reading it turns the agreement into a lookup that
		// either finds the sweep's answer or says so. (A FIELD default's name IS minted at its capture, and
		// safely — see `DefaultHelperOwner` for the property a field name has that a position does not.)
		let exprEnd = self.skipParamDefault(self.pos)

		// ⭐⭐ **A CALLER-LOCATION DEFAULT SYNTHESIZES NOTHING, SO IT CAPTURES NOTHING (W72).** There is no
		// expression for a helper to return: the sweep published `callerLine`/`callerFile` at this slot from
		// this same classifier, and `fillArgsFromParamDefaults` emits the constant at each call. Pushing a
		// pending helper here would declare a function under a name the sweep never minted — the exact
		// disagreement `requireSweptHelperName` panics on.
		if isCallerLocationDefault(self.callerLocationDefaultAt(self.pos, exprEnd: exprEnd)) 'materializedAtEachCallSite'
			self.pos = exprEnd
			return
		end 'materializedAtEachCallSite'

		try self.requireNoEmbeddedCallerLocation(self.pos, exprEnd: exprEnd)
		self.pendingDefaultHelpers = materializedPendingDefaultHelpers(self.pendingDefaultHelpers)
		self.pendingDefaultHelpers.push(PendingDefaultHelper.create(DefaultHelperOwner.parameter(ownerName, registrationName: ownerName, paramIndex: paramIndex), declaredType: paramType, exprStart: (self.pos + 1) as TokenIndex, exprEnd: exprEnd, enclosingType: self.enclosingType))
		self.pos = exprEnd
	end 'captureParamDefault'

	// The declared name a `declaredFunction` owner carries. It panics on the other two arms because
	// `captureParamDefault` has already thrown for them — the extraction exists only because a `match`
	// binding cannot be used past the arm that binds it.
	function paramDefaultOwnerName(owner ParamDefaultOwner) returns ByteArray
		match owner 'owner'
			declaredFunction(name) then return name
			closureLiteral then panic("paramDefaultOwnerName: a closure literal owner is refused by captureParamDefault before this is asked")
			interfaceRequirement then panic("paramDefaultOwnerName: an interface requirement owner is refused by captureParamDefault before this is asked")
		end 'owner'
	end 'paramDefaultOwnerName'

	// ⭐ **PARSE EVERY CAPTURED DEFAULT AS ITS OWN FUNCTION** (BATCH2 slice 4; fields from S2f) — the drain,
	// run once at the end of `parseModule`, when the last top-level declaration has closed and NO function
	// is open. That timing is the whole design: see `parseFunctionParameters`' capture site for why parsing
	// a helper in place would be `parseClosureExpression`'s ~25-alias save/restore, and why draining here
	// needs none of it. A FIELD default needs it for a second reason of its own: a `Self{}` written ABOVE
	// the field it defaults must still get that default (`field-defaults.method-before-field`), and a drain
	// at end-of-file is the only point at which declaration ORDER inside a type body cannot matter.
	//
	// It runs BEFORE `reportUnusedTypeAliases`, because a default expression can be the only USE of an alias
	// in its file (`items IntArray = [1, 2, 3]` names `IntArray` in the type, but `let n = Count.max` in a
	// default names one nothing else need mention). Reported first, that alias would be called unused on the
	// strength of code the parser had not read yet.
	//
	// ⚠ The list cannot grow while it is drained. A parameter default can only reach a parameter list
	// through a CLOSURE literal, and `captureParamDefault` refuses a default there; a field default can only
	// be captured from a `type` body, and an expression declares no type.
	function parseDefaultHelpers() throws ParseError
		if self.pendingDefaultHelpers.isEmpty() 'noDefaults'
			return
		end 'noDefaults'

		let resume = self.pos
		for pending in self.pendingDefaultHelpers 'eachDefault'
			try self.parseDefaultHelper(pending)
		end 'eachDefault'
		self.pos = resume
		// The last helper drained left ITS declaration's scope open (W43, `restoreDeclaringTypeScope`), and
		// the module is at file scope from here on — so it is closed here rather than left for whatever reads
		// the parser next to be surprised by.
		self.closeTypeParamScope()
	end 'parseDefaultHelpers'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAME THIS DECLARATION'S OWN DEFAULT HELPER IS EMITTED UNDER (W74)** — the swept name for the
	// declaration that keeps its source spelling, and the REGISTRATION name's own for an overload member that
	// does not.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SWEPT LOOKUP RUNS EITHER WAY, and that is the point rather than a leftover.** Its panic is the
	// standing check that the sweep and the real parse walked one parameter list to the same conclusion — that
	// this slot holds a default at all, that it sits where both think it does, and that it is not a
	// caller-location keyword (`requireSweptHelperName` names all three). A member that mints its own name
	// still owes that agreement: what an overload changes is the SYMBOL a helper is published as, never
	// whether the two walks agree about the parameter list.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE MINT IS `paramDefaultHelperName`, THE SWEEP'S OWN JOIN.** One speller, called with two
	// different bases, is not two derivations of one name — it is the same construction the N1c refile already
	// uses when it re-points a contested free function's helpers at the directory-qualified spelling. What is
	// NOT allowed is a second join written here, and the reason is measured: a name derived in two places
	// disagreed about the parameter's index and the disagreement surfaced as a backend `call to unknown
	// function` with no source position.
	function declaredParamDefaultHelperName(sourceName ByteArray, registrationName ByteArray, paramIndex ParamCount) returns ByteArray
		let swept = self.sweptParamDefaultHelperName(sourceName, paramIndex: paramIndex)
		if registrationName.equals(sourceName) 'theFirstDeclarationOfThisName'
			return swept
		end 'theFirstDeclarationOfThisName'

		return paramDefaultHelperName(registrationName, paramIndex: paramIndex)
	end 'declaredParamDefaultHelperName'

	// ⭐ **RE-POINT THE DEFAULTS THIS DECLARATION'S PARAMETER LIST JUST CAPTURED AT THE REGISTRATION NAME IT
	// CLAIMED (W74).** Called by `parseFunction` at the one moment both halves exist: the parameter list is
	// read (so the pendings are pushed) and `overloadRegistrationNameFor` has answered. `firstPending` is the
	// list's height taken BEFORE the parameter list was read, so this touches this declaration's own captures
	// and no earlier one's.
	//
	// A declaration that keeps its source spelling — every function in every program that does not overload a
	// defaulted name — is left alone, so the common path writes nothing.
	function claimDefaultHelpersForRegistrationName(firstPending ParamCount, sourceName ByteArray, registrationName ByteArray)
		if registrationName.equals(sourceName) 'theFirstDeclarationOfThisName'
			return
		end 'theFirstDeclarationOfThisName'

		for i in firstPending upto self.pendingDefaultHelpers.count() 'eachCaptureOfThisDeclaration'
			var pending = try self.pendingDefaultHelpers.get(i) otherwise panic("claimDefaultHelpersForRegistrationName: pendingDefaultHelpers.get OOB at {i} inside its own count()")
			match pending.owner 'owner'
				parameter(capturedSource, _, paramIndex) then pending.owner = DefaultHelperOwner.parameter(capturedSource, registrationName: registrationName, paramIndex: paramIndex)
				// A FIELD default is captured from a `type` body and never from a parameter list, so none can
				// have been pushed between this declaration's two ends.
				field then panic("claimDefaultHelpersForRegistrationName: a FIELD default was captured while '{String.from(sourceName)}'s parameter list was read — a field default is only captured from a `type` body, and a parameter list declares no field")
			end 'owner'
		end 'eachCaptureOfThisDeclaration'
	end 'claimDefaultHelpersForRegistrationName'

	// The name the SWEEP published for this captured default — the mechanism's one mint, read back rather
	// than re-derived (see `captureParamDefault`). It panics rather than falling back to a fresh mint,
	// because a fallback would emit a function under a name no call site can be calling: the callers were
	// compiled against the sweep's answer, so a disagreement is a missing symbol at LINK time with no source
	// position, which is precisely how this was found.
	//
	// It is asked at the DRAIN and not at the capture, deliberately. The sweep is tolerant and abandons a
	// declaration it cannot read (a malformed return type, which it reads AFTER the parameter list the real
	// parse has already captured from); asked at the capture, that would panic ahead of the positioned
	// diagnostic the real parse is about to raise for the same malformed declaration. Asked here, a file
	// whose parse throws never reaches the drain at all.
	function sweptParamDefaultHelperName(ownerName ByteArray, paramIndex ParamCount) returns ByteArray
		match self.signatures.defaultsOf(ownerName) 'swept'
			allRequired then break 'swept'
			defaults(info) then return self.requireSweptHelperName(info.producerFor(paramIndex), ownerName: ownerName, paramIndex: paramIndex)
		end 'swept'

		return self.requireSweptHelperName(ParamDefaultProducer.required, ownerName: ownerName, paramIndex: paramIndex)
	end 'sweptParamDefaultHelperName'

	// The panic the lookup above owes when the sweep published no HELPER at the position the parse captured
	// one for — one sentence for all three ways that happens (no entry for the function at all, an entry
	// with no default at this position, or an entry calling it a CALLER-LOCATION slot), because they are one
	// defect: the two walks of one parameter list have come apart. The third way is W72's and is named
	// separately in the sentence, because `callerLocationDefaultAt` is what the two would have disagreed on.
	function requireSweptHelperName(producer ParamDefaultProducer, ownerName ByteArray, paramIndex ParamCount) returns ByteArray
		match producer 'produces'
			required to callerFile then panic("sweptParamDefaultHelperName: '{String.from(ownerName)}' declares a default on CALL-ALIGNED parameter {paramIndex} that the real parse captured as an expression, and the declaration sweep published no helper at that position — `recordScannedParamDefaults` and `parseFunctionParameters` walk the same parameter list and have come apart on whether it holds a `=`, on where the parameter sits in it, or on whether `callerLocationDefaultAt` calls that default a caller-location keyword")
			helper(name) then return name
		end 'produces'
	end 'requireSweptHelperName'

	// ⭐⭐ **RE-OPEN THE DECLARATION SCOPE A CAPTURED DEFAULT WAS WRITTEN INSIDE (W43)** — the drain runs at
	// the end of `parseModule`, where the type that declared the default closed long ago, so every scope
	// `enterTypeScope`/`openTypeParamScope` install has to be put back or the expression is re-parsed as if
	// it had been written at top level.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONLY THE NAME TRAVELS; EVERYTHING ELSE IS READ BACK OFF THE INDEX.** A type's `uses` parameters
	// and its `where` constraints are ON its `StructLayout`, whole-program and complete by the drain, so
	// capturing copies of them at the declaration would be two records of one fact.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ONE SCOPE THAT CANNOT BE READ BACK IS `enclosingInnerAliases`** — it is built by the BODY WALK
	// and is not a column of anything — which is why `innerAliasesFromIndex` exists: inside a drained default
	// the same question is answered from the three whole-program alias registries instead
	// (`namesInnerAliasHere`, the one home of it). That is not a widening: `declaresInnerAlias` is keyed
	// `<Type>.<member>`, so it answers about exactly the declarations the body walk would have recorded.
	//
	// ⚠ An INTERFACE EXTENSION's associated-type substitution is deliberately NOT restored — it is a
	// property of the (extension, conformer) PAIR rather than of the conformer, and neither the pending
	// record nor the layout names the extension. A default expression inside an extension method that writes
	// the interface's associated type therefore reports `E3011 Unknown type`, which is a refusal with a
	// position and not a wrong type.
	function restoreDeclaringTypeScope(declaringType ByteArray)
		if declaringType.isEmpty() 'writtenAtFileScope'
			self.closeTypeParamScope()
			return
		end 'writtenAtFileScope'

		self.enterTypeScope(declaringType, isEnum: self.signatures.containsEnum(declaringType))
		// An ENUM declares no `uses` clause and no `where` clause, and an undeclared name reaches here only
		// from a program the real parse is already refusing — both leave the empty scopes `enterTypeScope`
		// just installed, which is exactly what those declarations have.
		match self.signatures.structOf(declaringType) 'declaringType'
			declared(layout) then self.installDeclaringTypeScope(layout)
			undeclared then break 'declaringType'
		end 'declaringType'
		self.innerAliasesFromIndex = true
	end 'restoreDeclaringTypeScope'

	// The two per-declaration scopes a `StructLayout` carries, installed together because `enterTypeScope`
	// resets them together. The lists are the layout's own — the same aliasing `openTypeParamScope` already
	// creates between the parser field and the layout it stamps.
	function installDeclaringTypeScope(layout StructLayout)
		self.enclosingTypeParams = layout.typeParamNames
		self.enclosingWhereConstraints = layout.whereConstraints
		// A TYPE's own declaration carries no extension clause; the extension half is the extension's alone.
		self.enclosingExtensionConstraints = sharedEmptyWhereConstraints
	end 'installDeclaringTypeScope'

	// ⭐ **THE NAME AND THE TIER — the ONLY two things a parameter default's helper and a field default's
	// helper differ in.** Everything else about them is one function (`parseDefaultHelperBody`), which is
	// what "a default value is a synthesized nullary function" means when it is said once instead of twice.
	function parseDefaultHelper(pending PendingDefaultHelper) throws ParseError
		match pending.owner 'owner'
			// ⭐ GLOBAL: a defaulted parameter of an `export function` is omitted by call sites in OTHER
			// files, and each of them emits a call to this helper by name.
			parameter(sourceName, registrationName, paramIndex) then try self.parseDefaultHelperBody(self.declaredParamDefaultHelperName(sourceName, registrationName: registrationName, paramIndex: paramIndex), visibility: Visibility.global, pending: pending)
			// ⭐ FILE, and the difference from the parameter tier is a real fact rather than caution: a struct
			// literal may only be written inside the type's OWN body (`requireConstructible`'s E3076), so
			// every call to a field default's helper is in the file that declares the type. A lifted closure
			// is published the same way for the same reason.
			field(helperName) then try self.parseDefaultHelperBody(helperName, visibility: Visibility.file, pending: pending)
		end 'owner'
	end 'parseDefaultHelper'

	// One helper: `function <the minted name>() returns <the declared type>` whose entire body is
	// `return <the default expression>`.
	//
	// Every per-function column is re-created exactly as `parseFunction` re-creates it at its own entry, and
	// for its stated reason — a stray entry carried in from the previous function would name a value in that
	// function's SSA space. Nothing is saved: the caller restores only the CURSOR, because between top-level
	// declarations no other per-function state is live to restore.
	//
	// The body goes through `parseCheckedValueReturn`, so the default meets every rule a `return` meets —
	// which is what makes `name String = 42` an ordinary return-type mismatch and `factor Number = 2` an
	// ordinary widening, with no rule written twice. That is exactly as true of a FIELD's `as Integer =
	// "x"`, which is why the field door needed no type rule of its own.
	function parseDefaultHelperBody(helperName ByteArray, visibility Visibility, pending PendingDefaultHelper) throws ParseError
		self.pos = pending.exprStart
		let anchor = self.current()
		// The DECLARATION scope the default was written inside, re-opened before a token of it is read (W43).
		// See `PendingDefaultHelper.enclosingType` for the measurement.
		self.restoreDeclaringTypeScope(pending.enclosingType)
		// Resolved HERE rather than at the capture: a `named` declared type is re-tagged `structRef` only
		// once the whole-program struct registry is complete, which at capture time — mid-declaration, and
		// possibly above the `type` being named — it is not.
		let returnType = self.resolveNamedStructReturn(MaxonReturnType.value(pending.declaredType))

		// ⭐ **A HELPER OF A GENERIC TYPE CARRIES THE HIDDEN LAYOUT-DESCRIPTOR PARAMETER ON EXACTLY THE TERMS
		// A METHOD DOES (W43b)** — `var keys as KeyArray = KeyArray.create()` constructs an OPAQUE
		// `Array with Key`, which reads the enclosing instance's descriptor. The need is the same
		// whole-program fixpoint's, keyed by the helper's own minted name
		// (`Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds` → `recordFieldDefaultDescriptorNeeds`), and the slot is reserved here
		// — before the body's values are numbered — for `parseFunction`'s stated reason: its ValueId must land
		// inside `[0, paramCount)` so the backend's param-capture clobber guard covers it.
		// A helper takes no receiver and no user parameter, so the descriptor is ValueId 0 and `paramCount` is
		// what `LowerMaxonToStd.functionHasDescriptorParam` reads it back off — minus the count slot, which since
		// W190 that routine subtracts by NAME because two optional slots cannot both be read out of one total.
		let needsLayoutParam = self.signatures.anyFunctionNeedsLayoutDescriptor() and self.signatures.functionNeedsLayoutDescriptor(helperName)
		// A field default may read `countof(Self)` (`var slots as Int = countof(Self)`), so it reserves the count
		// slot on the same terms and out of the same fixpoint (W190).
		let needsCountParam = self.signatures.anyFunctionReadsFixedElementCount() and self.signatures.functionReadsFixedElementCount(helperName)
		self.functionCarriesLayoutParam = needsLayoutParam
		self.functionCarriesFixedElementCountParam = needsCountParam
		self.functionDroppedOpaqueWithoutDescriptor = false
		self.functionStoredOpaqueWithoutDescriptor = false
		// Named apart from the total, because it is the total MINUS this that places the layout slot — the
		// `[layout][count]` step `layoutDescriptorSlotValueId` takes, spelled here the way `parseFunction`
		// spells it.
		let countParamCount = 1 if needsCountParam else 0
		let helperParamCount = (1 if needsLayoutParam else 0) + countParamCount
		// A helper takes no receiver, no user parameter and no constraint witness, so the two optional slots are
		// the whole list — but WHICH ValueId each lands on is still the ABI's order, and it is read here through
		// the one home `parseFunction` reserves through (`fixedElementCountSlotValueId`), not restated.
		self.fixedElementCountParamValueId = 0
		if needsCountParam 'countSlot'
			self.fixedElementCountParamValueId = fixedElementCountSlotValueId(helperParamCount, witnessParamCount: 0)
		end 'countSlot'

		self.scope = Scope.create()
		self.valueIds = ValueIdRef.create(helperParamCount)
		self.mutableVars = VarInfoArray.create()
		self.mutableVarShadowed = sharedEmptyMutableVarShadowed
		self.mutableVarInnermost = sharedEmptyMutableVarInnermost
		self.ownedBindings = VarInfoArray.create()
		self.pendingTempDrops = ValueIdArray.create()
		self.lastFieldReadValid = false
		self.loops = LoopContextArray.create()
		// Fresh per function, with its companion flag — see the `tryBlocks` field declaration for why.
		self.tryBlocks = TryBlockContextArray.create()
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = false
		self.matches = MatchContextArray.create()
		self.iterationLockedBindings = sharedEmptyIterationLocks
		self.valueTags = ValueTypeTagArray.create()
		self.valueNameIds = TypeNameIdArray.create()
		self.valueConsts = ParsedIntArray.create()
		self.valueConstDomains = ConstPayloadDomainArray.create()
		self.valueOwnsHeap = BoolArray.create()
		self.valueOwnsHeapAlone = BoolArray.create()
		self.valuePromiseMarks = BoolArray.create()
		self.nodeHandleMintedFrom = ValueIdArray.create()
		self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks = BoolArray.create()
		self.valueIsCapturingClosure = BoolArray.create()
		self.valueClosureEnv = ValueIdArray.create()
		self.valueWitnessIds = ValueIdArray.create()
		// A synthesized helper is a real function of its own and reserves no witness parameters, so a dispatch
		// inside one has none to name — `witnessParamValueId` says so loudly rather than indexing past the
		// parameter block. ⚠ A LIFTED CLOSURE is NOT this case and its body deliberately leaves the pair
		// standing (W58); see `parseClosureExpression`'s reset block.
		self.declaringWitnessParamBase = 0
		self.declaringWitnessParamCount = 0
		self.valueInstanceAlias = sharedEmptyInstanceAliasMap
		self.valueExistentialInstance = sharedEmptyExistentialInstanceMap
		self.valueDeclaredAlias = sharedEmptyDeclaredAliasMap
		self.charLiteralOps = sharedEmptyCharLiteralOps
		self.stringBytesViewOps = sharedEmptyStringBytesViewOps
		self.immutableGlobalReads = sharedEmptyImmutableGlobalReads
		self.mergeImmutableBlame = sharedEmptyMergeBlames
		// ⭐⭐ **W117, ASKED OF THE HELPER'S OWN MINTED NAME — WHICH IS WHAT SEPARATES THE LANGUAGE'S TWO
		// DEFAULT DOORS HERE, AND THE SEPARATION IS THE KEY RATHER THAN A CASE.** A PARAMETER default's helper
		// is published by the sweep (`recordDefaultHelperGlobalAliasReturn`), so `f(p T = A)` is admitted and
		// its call site marks the argument exactly as the written-out `f(A)` is marked. A FIELD default's is
		// NOT published, so this answers false and `ownedFormOfBorrowedValue` keeps the E2015 at its return.
		//
		// ⛔ **THAT ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE AND IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT TO TIDY UP.** The parameter door hands the
		// record to a CALL ARGUMENT, which is where the mark is READ
		// (`SemanticCheck.checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam`) and where a mutating callee is refused. A field
		// default hands it to a struct SLOT, and the mark does not survive a field store: MEASURED on the tip,
		// `var h = Holder.make(G)` storing a global's record and then `h.names.push(1)` compiles clean on shv2
		// and mutates `G`.
		//
		// ⚠ **AND THE ORACLE IS NOT STRICTER HERE — it prints the same `g=1`**, so this is a hole in W117 as a
		// whole rather than an shv2-only defect. That is exactly why the door stays shut rather than being
		// opened to match the oracle: W117 is DELIBERATELY stricter than the bootstrap about a global's record
		// reaching a mutating position (`grow(shared())` is E3019 here and legal there), and publishing the
		// field helper would open a route into the one part of that rule which cannot yet follow the record.
		// A refusal is the direction this rule may err in; a silent acceptance is not. It comes off the day a
		// field store carries the mark.
		self.enclosingFunctionMayReturnAGlobalAlias = self.functionMayReturnAGlobalAlias(helperName)
		self.capturedImmutableGlobalReads = sharedEmptyImmutableGlobalReads
		// W41's mark crosses the same boundary and for the same reason — see `rebindableSlotReads`.
		self.rebindableSlotReads = sharedEmptyRebindableSlotReads
		self.constructorFeedParams = sharedEmptyConstructorFeedParams
		self.recordsOwningOpaqueSlots = sharedEmptyRecordsOwningOpaqueSlots
		self.recordsHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlots = sharedEmptyReferencedOpaqueRecords
		self.callReferencedAnOpaqueFeedSlot = false
		// ⭐ **ALL THREE, for `parseFunction`'s stated reason — "they describe one value space" (BATCH22
		// review).** This site reset `bufferSurfaceValues` alone, and the other two crossed the boundary: a
		// default helper is a whole FUNCTION drained at the end of `parseModule`, so what reaches it is the
		// PREVIOUS function's own private map, which `markBufferSurfaceSlots`' copy-on-write detach cannot
		// help with — the detach protects the shared module-level anchor, not the parser field. MEASURED:
		// `function useBufferPair(p (__ManagedMemory, Int))` last in the file, then a default expression
		// reading `.0.length()` off a `(ByteArray, Int)` call, COMPILED AND RAN — both are ValueId 0.
		self.bufferSurfaceValues = sharedEmptyBufferSurfaceValues
		self.bufferSurfaceSlots = sharedEmptyBufferSurfaceSlots
		self.bufferSurfaceElements = sharedEmptyBufferSurfaceElements
		self.sizedContainerBufferValues = sharedEmptySizedContainerBufferValues
		self.inMatchArmBody = false
		self.rangeCheckSites = RangeCheckSiteArray.create()
		self.rangeCheckArgSites = RangeCheckArgSiteArray.create()
		self.rangeCheckReturnSites = RangeCheckReturnSiteArray.create()
		// A ranged-alias declared type makes the helper a ranged-RETURN function, so the default is guarded
		// by the P1.9 entry check exactly as `return <expr>` from such a function is — which is precisely the
		// check the parameter (or the field) itself would otherwise owe.
		self.rangeCheckReturnAlias = self.rangedReturnAliasName(returnType)
		self.rangeCheckParamSites = sharedEmptyParamSites

		var helper = IrFunction.create(helperName, maxonReturnType: returnType, paramCount: helperParamCount, maxonParamTypes: MaxonTypeArray.create(), valueTags: self.valueTags, valueNameIds: self.valueNameIds, valueClosureEnv: self.valueClosureEnv, valueIsCapturingClosure: self.valueIsCapturingClosure, valueWitnessIds: self.valueWitnessIds, returnLine: anchor.line, declaredAt: SourcePosition.declaredAt(self.filePath, nameTok: anchor), throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.none)
		// The tier the OWNER decided (`parseDefaultHelper`), which is the one fact about a helper that is not
		// the same for both doors: a parameter's is reachable from every file, a field's from exactly one.
		helper.visibility = visibility

		let helperIndex = self.artifact.module.funcCount()
		self.artifact.module.pushFunc(helper)
		self.artifact.setCurrentFuncIndex(helperIndex)

		// The hidden layout slot's value columns, seeded exactly as `parseFunction` seeds its own and at the
		// same point in the sequence — after `setCurrentFuncIndex`, so the origin is filed under THIS function
		// (W43b; see the block in `parseFunction` for the abort a misfiled hidden origin produced). An i64
		// pointer, unnamed — nothing binds it, only the opaque-`Array` create and the `sizeof(T)` lowering read
		// it, by position — with the default expression's first token as its span, since no source token
		// declares it. A helper has no user parameter, so this IS ValueId 0 and the column stays dense.
		if needsLayoutParam 'seedLayoutParam'
			self.recordValueType(MaxonType.integer)
			self.artifact.recordParamOrigin(layoutDescriptorSlotValueId(helperParamCount, witnessParamCount: 0, fixedElementCountSlots: countParamCount), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'seedLayoutParam'

		// The hidden count slot's columns, seeded on the same terms one line up and in the same ABI order
		// (`[layout][count]`, W190). Unlike the layout slot it is READ AS A VALUE — a default expression may
		// write `countof(Self)` — so its recorded type is the `integer` the expression machinery will find.
		if needsCountParam 'seedCountParam'
			self.recordValueType(MaxonType.integer)
			self.artifact.recordParamOrigin(self.fixedElementCountParamValueId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'seedCountParam'

		let entry = self.artifact.module.addBlockAt(helper, label: b"entry", filePath: self.filePath.toString(), firstLine: anchor.line)
		self.currentBlock = entry

		try self.parseCheckedValueReturn(helper, block: entry, anchor: anchor)

		// ⭐ THE DEFAULT EXPRESSION MUST CONSUME EVERYTHING THE CAPTURE SKIPPED. Everywhere a statement
		// is read this is free — the parser meets a newline and says so, and `let x = 1 zzz` has never
		// been legal — but the helper's body is one expression lifted out of a token region, and what
		// the expression did not reach was simply abandoned: `b Integer = 7 zzz` defaulted to 7, and
		// `b Real = 1e100` defaulted to 1, because `1e100` is not a float literal (one must contain a
		// decimal point) so `e100` lexed as an identifier and vanished — a number a hundred orders of
		// magnitude wrong, silently. The bootstrap refused neither and now refuses both, at its own two
		// injected streams; a rule enforced by one compiler and not the other would make a program's
		// meaning depend on which one read it.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND A FIELD DEFAULT INHERITS IT WHOLE (S2f)**, which is why the field door built no check of
		// its own: `var v as Integer = 7 zzz` had the identical silent drop with the identical cure, and
		// `field-defaults.error.trailing-tokens` is the case that pins it here.
		//
		// ⛔ **THE STRAY TOKEN IS NAMED BY ITS TEXT, NOT BY ITS KIND, AND IT SAID `'identifier'` UNTIL S2f.**
		// The reader has to FIND the token this refuses, and `zzz` is in the file where `identifier` is not
		// — the D11c defect class exactly, in a message rather than a callee. `tokenDisplayText` is the one
		// door that answers "how does a diagnostic name this token?" (the kind's spelling only where the
		// token carries no text of its own), so this asks it rather than inlining half of it. MEASURED
		// against the oracle on the identical program: the bootstrap says `but got 'zzz'`, and
		// `specs/default-values.md` — the canonical definition, for the PARAMETER door — pins `'zzz'` and
		// `'e100'`. shv2's own `param-default-refusals.md` had encoded the wrong answer as its expectation.
		if self.pos != pending.exprEnd 'trailingTokens'
			let stray = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unexpectedToken("end of default value", got: tokenDisplayText(stray), line: stray.line, column: stray.column)
		end 'trailingTokens'

		self.artifact.recordFuncReturnType(helperName, returnType: returnType, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)
		// A default-value helper takes NO parameters and its return type is the parameter's own already-resolved
		// declared type (`pending.declaredType`), not a spelling this parse is standing on — so neither
		// existential column has a token to read and both stay shared-empty. A parameter declared at a
		// parameterized existential cannot have a default: `E3121`/`E2010` aside, a default is an EXPRESSION and
		// there is no literal of an interface type to write.
		self.publishExistentialInstances(helper)
		self.artifact.recordFuncSignature(helperName, signature: FuncSignature.create(ByteArrayArray.create(), paramTypes: MaxonTypeArray.create(), paramAggregateNames: ByteArrayArray.create(), paramDeclaredNames: ByteArrayArray.create(), returnAggregateName: self.erasedAggregateNameOfReturn(returnType), paramExistentialSites: sharedEmptyParamExistentialSites, returnExistentialSites: sharedEmptyReturnExistentialSites, extensionConstraints: sharedEmptyWhereConstraints), nameLine: anchor.line, nameColumn: anchor.column, sourceFilePath: self.filePath, synthesized: true)
		self.commitRangeChecks(helperName)
	end 'parseDefaultHelperBody'

	// Bind each parameter name to its reserved SSA ValueId in the fresh function
	// scope: parameter `i` is ValueId `i` (the ids 0..paramCount-1 the value
	// numbering reserved). Bound as an immutable value binding, so a body reference
	// resolves to the parameter value and a reassignment is rejected as immutable.
	//
	// A parameter of an instance method may COLLIDE with a field of the enclosing type —
	// `function bump(count Integer)` inside a `type Counter` that declares `count`. The aliases are
	// already in scope by now (see `parseFunction`), so `declareValueBinding` refuses it and this
	// is where that refusal becomes E3006, anchored on the PARAMETER's own name token, which is
	// the declaration that has to change. The bootstrap reports the identical program at the
	// identical token.
	// Install one parameter's binding — cell-resident when it arrives by reference, an ordinary value binding
	// otherwise. One fork, at the one site that binds a parameter, so "what kind of binding is a parameter?"
	// has a single answer and the `ScopeError` both kinds can raise is caught once at the caller.
	function declareParameterOrCellBinding(paramName ByteArray, paramType MaxonType, paramIsMutable bool, paramIsByRef bool, paramValue ValueId) returns VarInfo throws ScopeError
		if paramIsByRef 'arrivesAsACellAddress'
			// ⚠ **THE FLOAT ALIAS IS COLLAPSED HERE AND NOWHERE LATER.** A `var` local's `varType` is the type
			// of its INITIALIZER, which is already resolved; a parameter's is the DECLARED spelling, so a
			// `typealias Float = float(…)` parameter arrives as `named(Float)`. Every reader of a cell
			// binding's type — the load's mint, the store's width, the assignment's coercion — needs the
			// machine class, and `x = 99.0` on such a parameter was E3009 ("cannot implicitly convert 'float'
			// to 'int'") until this resolved it. A ranged INT alias stays `named` on purpose: its NAME is what
			// carries the signedness `valueIsUnsigned` reads.
			return try self.scope.declareCellBinding(paramName, varType: self.resolveFloatAliasType(paramType), mutable: paramIsMutable, cell: paramValue, isParameter: true)
		end 'arrivesAsACellAddress'

		return try self.scope.declareParameterBinding(paramName, varType: paramType, mutable: paramIsMutable, value: paramValue)
	end 'declareParameterOrCellBinding'

	function bindParameters(func IrFunction, funcName ByteArray, paramNames ByteArrayArray, paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray, paramSurfaces DeclaredSurfaceArray, paramTypeStarts TokenIndexColumn) throws ParseError
		for i in 0 upto paramNames.count() 'eachParam'
			let paramName = try paramNames.get(i) otherwise panic("bindParameters: paramNames.get OOB at {i}")
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("bindParameters: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			let paramRange = try paramRanges.get(i) otherwise panic("bindParameters: paramRanges.get OOB at {i}")

			// ⚠ THE LOOP-PHI TOKEN SCAN DEPENDS ON THIS BEING FALSE, AND NOTHING ELSE SAYS SO.
			//
			// `assignedBindingsIn` decides which vars a loop carries a phi for by scanning TOKENS for
			// `identifier =` (`assignsAt`). That is exhaustive only while EVERY write to a mutable
			// binding wears that shape — and a `var` (mutating) PARAMETER does not: its write is
			// `mutate(p)` at the CALL SITE, with no `=` anywhere in the loop for the scan to find.
			// The loop would mint no phi for `p`, and `p` would silently RESET to its entry value
			// on every iteration. A wrong answer, and one no drift guard can catch: unlike a
			// `loopBodyEndIndex` mismatch, the loop's EXTENT would still be perfectly correct.
			//
			// **The assert is on the FLAG ACTUALLY BEING PASSED, at the site that passes it** — not
			// on the constant somewhere else. A real `var`-parameter feature is per-parameter, so it
			// replaces the right-hand side here with `paramIsVar.get(i)`; a tripwire that tested
			// `ParametersAreMutable` would simply stop being read at that moment and never fire,
			// shipping the reset it exists to stop. This one trips on any spelling, because whatever
			// is bound mutable has to come through this line.
			//
			// The other write forms are covered differently: one that IS a statement (`a.b = x`,
			// `x += 1`) has to be taught to `parseStatement`, and `assignsAt`'s comment is the
			// standing instruction to teach the scan in the same edit. A mutating parameter is the
			// case that instruction cannot reach, because it is not a statement at all.
			// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS PARAMETER PASSED BY REFERENCE (PBR-2)?** The whole-program answer, asked of the
			// declaration sweep under the name the SOURCE wrote — exactly as the consume facts below are, and for
			// the identical reason: the sweep never sees an overload's mangled registration name.
			// `requireOverloadableName` refuses an overload set that states by-reference facts at all, so this
			// per-name answer can never be one sibling's read as another's.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED AT POSITION `i` OF THE CALL-ALIGNED LIST, WHICH IS THIS LOOP'S OWN INDEX.** An
			// instance method's `paramNames` already carries `__self` at 0 by the time it reaches here
			// (`parseFunction` prepends it), which is the same alignment the sweep recorded and the same one a
			// receiver-prepending call site slots against.
			// ⚡ **THE SCALAR GATE IS ASKED FIRST, AND THAT IS THE MEASURED SHAPE THIS FILE ALREADY WARNS
			// ABOUT ONE COLUMN OVER.** `paramIsByRef` is a whole-program map lookup that returns a UNION, and
			// `paramFeedOf`'s header records what that costs when asked per parameter per function: two
			// allocations EVERY TIME, on a corpus that uses none of the feature. `programTakesAParamByRef` is
			// a `count()` on a set that is EMPTY for every program written before this rung, so the
			// overwhelmingly common function pays one integer comparison per parameter and nothing else.
			let paramIsByRef = self.signatures.programTakesAParamByRef() and self.signatures.paramIsByRef(funcName, index: i)
			if paramIsByRef 'arrivesAsACellAddress'
				func.reassignedParamMask = maskWithParam(func.reassignedParamMask, param: i)
			end 'arrivesAsACellAddress'

			// ⭐⭐ **E3019's SEED FOR A PLAIN REASSIGNMENT (PBR-2).** `writtenParamMask` answers *"does passing
			// an immutable binding HERE make it an error?"*, and a body that REBINDS the name is the plainest
			// possible yes: `let n = 5` handed to a `setTo99(x)` whose body is `x = 99` must be refused, which
			// is `pass-by-reference.let-to-mutating-param-error`. From there the existing machinery does the
			// rest — `buildParamMutationSummary` closes it over the call graph and
			// `checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam` reports it — so this rung adds a SEED and no new check.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE *DIRECT* COLUMN, NEVER `paramIsByRef`.** A forwarder takes its parameter by reference
			// without writing anything of its own, and the fixpoint is what carries the callee's write back to
			// it; seeding from the wider column would refuse a `let` at every forwarding position in the
			// program. It is the same asymmetry PBR-1 established at the other door — a FIELD WRITE through a
			// parameter deliberately does NOT feed this column, because the oracle accepts
			// `let s = State.make()` handed to a `scan(state)` that writes `state.flag`.
			// Gated on `paramIsByRef` rather than asked on its own: a DIRECT reassignment implies by-reference
			// by construction (`isByRef` returns true on the direct column alone), so a parameter that failed
			// the test above cannot pass this one — and the gate spares every other parameter in the program a
			// second union-returning lookup.
			if paramIsByRef and self.signatures.paramIsDirectlyReassigned(funcName, index: i) 'rebindsThisParameter'
				func.writtenParamMask = maskWithParam(func.writtenParamMask, param: i)
			end 'rebindsThisParameter'
			// ⚠ **THE VALUE COLUMN KEEPS THE DECLARED TYPE, DELIBERATELY.** ValueId `i` physically holds a
			// cell's ADDRESS, and an earlier cut of this rung retyped it to `integer` to say so — which made
			// every tier above the lowering read a `Node` parameter as an `int`, and `SemanticCheck.checkArgTypes`
			// refused all five transitive cases with `expected 'Node', got 'int'`. The representation is the
			// LOWERING's to know (`LowerMaxonToStd.stdTypeOf`, off `reassignedParamMask`); the type is
			// everyone else's.

			// ⚠ THE LOOP-PHI TOKEN SCAN STILL DEPENDS ON THIS BEING FALSE, AND PBR-2 DID NOT CHANGE THAT — IT
			// REMOVED THE NEED TO.
			//
			// A by-reference parameter is NOT bound mutable. It is bound CELL-RESIDENT (below), which takes it
			// out of the phi-tracked class altogether: its name denotes a heap cell whose POINTER is
			// loop-invariant, and its value is read out of memory at each use rather than carried by a block
			// argument. So the phi `assignedBindingsIn` cannot mint for `mutate(p)` at a call site is a phi
			// nothing wants, and the hazard below is answered by construction instead of by widening the scan.
			// That is why the tripwire stands unchanged: it still catches the thing it was written to catch, and
			// a real `var` parameter would still be wrong for exactly the reason it says.
			let paramIsMutable = ParametersAreMutable
			if paramIsMutable 'writesThisScanCannotSee'
				panic("bindParameters: parameter '{String.from(paramName)}' is being bound MUTABLE, but `assignedBindingsIn` finds a loop's carried vars by scanning tokens for `identifier =` — and a `var` parameter is written by `mutate(p)` at the CALL SITE, leaving no `=` for that scan to find. The loop would mint no phi and the parameter would silently reset to its entry value every iteration. Teach `assignsAt`/`assignedBindingsIn` the new write form before binding a parameter mutable.")
			end 'writesThisScanCannotSee'

			// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD SIGN-EXTENSION DOOR: A PARAMETER MAY ARRIVE RAW OUT OF A NARROW SIGNED SLOT
			// (`enum-narrow-storage`).** The other two answer *"a value coming OUT of a call"* —
			// `emitContainerElementAccessor` for a compiler-emitted element read,
			// `retypeOpaqueReturnValue` for one a corpus body hands back. Neither stands in front of the
			// third shape: a shared corpus body reads a slot with the zero-extending element load and passes
			// the raw word INTO user code as an ARGUMENT, through a function value or a witness call. The
			// body is compiled once against an opaque `Element` and cannot extend it; the caller cannot
			// reach inside the loop that does the read. **The callee's own entry is the one place that can,
			// and it is COMPLETE rather than an enumeration: every value that enters a function from
			// somewhere other than its own reads is a parameter.**
			//
			// ⚠ **MEASURED, and the wrong answer was not only a printed number.** `a.sort(cmpSig)` over
			// `Signal { minus = -1 }` — `Array.sort`'s comparator overload carries no conformance
			// constraint, so it is the cheapest reach — had `driftQuicksort`'s `managed.get(scan)` hand
			// `-1` to the user's comparator as **255**, so the comparator saw a value no case of its own
			// type has AND the resulting ORDER was wrong. `stdlib/helpers/sort/*` reaches this shape at
			// five sites and `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `contains` reaches it through witness dispatch.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS SAFE ON A PARAMETER THAT WAS ALREADY CORRECT** — the extension is the identity on
			// every value of the type, because the slot was chosen to hold every one of them
			// (`ProgramSignatures.narrowSignedSlotOf`'s header). So this needs no test for where the
			// argument came from, which is exactly what makes one door enough for all the routes.
			//
			// ⚠ The SLOT keeps ValueId `i` and only the NAME binds to the extended value: the surface mark,
			// the existential instance and the declaration span below are facts about the parameter's own
			// slot, and a narrow signed enum can be none of the things they describe.
			//
			// ⚠ Gated on `buildsBodies` because an UNREACHED stdlib body is skipped whole a few lines after
			// this returns (`skipUnreachedFunctionBody`), and emitting into its entry block would leave an
			// op in a body nothing parses — the same byte-neutrality rule `lowerMaxonToStd` and
			// `insertRangeChecks` both shut their doors onto.
			// ⚠ **A BY-REFERENCE PARAMETER TAKES ITS RAW SLOT AND SKIPS THE SIGN EXTENSION, WHICH IS NOT AN
			// OPTIMIZATION.** The extension exists to widen a value that arrived out of a NARROW SIGNED SLOT;
			// a by-reference parameter carries an ADDRESS, so extending it would sign-extend a pointer. It is
			// also unnecessary: the value is read out of a cell that stores a full machine word
			// (`wordSlotStorageType`), so nothing narrows it on the way in.
			let paramValue = i if paramIsByRef else self.narrowSignedParamValue(i as ValueId, paramType: paramType, range: paramRange)
			// ⭐⭐ **A BY-REFERENCE PARAMETER'S VALUE *IS* THE CELL** — the caller allocated it and the caller owns
			// it, so this frame binds the name to that address and never drops it. Reads become `loadIndirect`
			// through it and the rebind a `storeIndirect`, which is what makes the caller observe the write.
			// `isParameter` still rides: it answers "may the container this name denotes be written?", which is
			// a question a by-reference parameter answers exactly as any other parameter does.
			let binding = try self.declareParameterOrCellBinding(paramName, paramType: paramType, paramIsMutable: paramIsMutable, paramIsByRef: paramIsByRef, paramValue: paramValue) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(paramName), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: paramRange.line, column: paramRange.column)
			// ⭐ **E3012 CANDIDATE: A DECLARED PARAMETER MUST BE READ** (`reportUnusedBindings`). Two names are
			// not candidates and neither is a policy choice: `__self` is the RECEIVER the parser itself pushed,
			// which no source token declares and a bare field read never mentions, so every instance method
			// would report it; and `_` is the language's discard, which says in as many words that the author
			// wants the slot and not the name.
			if not paramName.equals(SelfParamName) and not paramName.equals(DiscardBindingName) 'checkedForUse'
				self.unusedCandidates.push(UnusedBindingCandidate.create(binding, nameRange: paramRange, kind: UnusedBindingKind.parameter))
			end 'checkedForUse'
			// ⭐ **A PARAMETER THAT NAMED `__ManagedMemory` DENOTES THE BUFFER THERE (A2j / A2m)** — the spelling
			// was read at the declaration (`parseFunctionParameters`) and this is the site that BINDS the value it
			// named, so this is where the fact becomes the marks `dispatchArrayMethod` and `emitFieldLoad` read.
			// Parameter `i` owns ValueId `i`, which is what makes the column's index the value's.
			self.markDeclaredSurface(i as ValueId, declared: try paramSurfaces.get(i) otherwise panic("bindParameters: paramSurfaces.get OOB at {i} — a parameter's columns are built in lockstep"))
			// ⛔⛔ **NO SIZED-CONTAINER MARK IS WRITTEN HERE, AND THE ATTEMPT TO IS RECORDED BECAUSE IT WAS A
			// MEASURED OVER-REFUSAL (W192 review).** A parameter spelled `ElementMemory` inside the sized
			// container's declaration looks like the record and is not necessarily one: `managed.slice(0, 2)`
			// mints a fresh VIEW whose type is the SAME `__ManagedMemory with Element`, so a declaration-keyed
			// mark cannot tell the two apart and marked the view as well. MEASURED: passing a slice to a
			// `Vector` helper that calls `clear()` on it was refused *"this buffer is a `Vector`'s own
			// record"* — a legal program refused by a FALSE sentence, while the identical `s.clear()` written
			// in place compiled and ran (exit 30). ⇒ The fact is CARRIED, never inferred: the mark lives on
			// the value that `bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord` minted, and the escape is refused at the two
			// points where that mark still exists (`refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping`).
			// ⭐ **AND THE OTHER FACT THE DECLARATION HELD AND THE TYPE COLUMNS CANNOT (W57)** — a parameter
			// declared at a PARAMETERIZED interface type carries `interfaceRef(Base)` and nothing about what
			// `Base`'s associated positions were bound to, which is what a dispatch on it needs to type an
			// associated-type result. Gated on the tag so no other parameter pays a lookup: only an existential
			// can have `with` arguments to have lost.
			if maxonTypeTag(paramType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'declaredAtAnInterface'
				let paramTypeStart = try paramTypeStarts.get(i) otherwise panic("bindParameters: paramTypeStarts.get OOB at {i} — a parameter's columns are built in lockstep")
				if let instance = try self.declaredExistentialInstanceAt(paramTypeStart) 'namesAnInstantiation'
					self.recordValueExistentialInstance(i, instance: instance)
				end 'namesAnInstantiation'
			end 'declaredAtAnInterface'
			// Parameter `i` owns ValueId `i`; record its declaration span so the register-pressure
			// diagnostic can resolve the parameter to source (it is minted by no op — see
			// ParamOriginTable — so it has no `emitOp` origin).
			self.artifact.recordParamOrigin(i as ValueId, range: paramRange)

			// ⭐ **A CONSUMED MANAGED PARAMETER IS ENROLLED AS OWNED** (P1.4a wave 2). The consume analysis
			// marked it moved-into-durable-storage, so the caller transfers ownership at the call site and
			// this function OWNS the value: it is enrolled exactly as an owned local, so `moveInPayload`
			// accepts moving it into a field (an owned bare-local reference is poisoned), and if it is NOT
			// moved on some path `emitScopeDrops` drops it once at function exit — sound whether the analysis
			// was exact or over-approximated. A SCALAR consumed param owns no heap and is left borrowed (its
			// consume bit is inert), so it is never enrolled — a decref of an integer would corrupt the count.
			//
			// ⚠ **CO-OWNED, NOT SOLE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A SEGFAULT THIS FRAME CANNOT SEE.** The caller
			// transfers a consumed argument either by MOVING an owned value or by `coOwnBorrowedForConsume`'s
			// `__mm_incref` of a borrowed one, and which it chose is a fact about the CALL SITE, not about this
			// signature — the same boundary the transitive-consume fixpoint sits on. So a `match` on a consumed
			// union parameter must RETAIN its payload rather than null the slot, or an incref-transferring caller
			// re-reads a hole (measured on the sibling shapes; see `OwnedHeapExclusivity`).
			// ⚠ **A BY-REFERENCE PARAMETER IS NEVER ENROLLED OWNED, AND THE `not paramIsByRef` IS LOAD-BEARING
			// RATHER THAN BELT-AND-BRACES.** The cell belongs to the CALLER's frame; dropping it here would free
			// the caller's storage out from under it. The retype above already makes `valueIsManagedHeap` answer
			// false for it — the value is an `integer` now — but that is a consequence of how the address is
			// typed, not a statement of the ownership rule, and a future retype would silently take the guard with
			// it. The rule is stated where it is decided.
			if not paramIsByRef and self.signatures.paramIsConsumed(funcName, index: i) and self.valueIsManagedHeap(i as ValueId) 'consumedManaged'
				self.markValueOwnsHeap(i as ValueId, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
				self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
			end 'consumedManaged'

			// ⭐ **A CONSUMED OPAQUE TYPE-PARAMETER FEED PARAMETER IS ENROLLED AS OWNED** (P1.7 slice 3b-v). A
			// feed parameter (`push(item Element)` moving `item` into `self.items`, or a sibling forwarder the
			// transitive fixpoint marked) is CONSUMED at its concrete call site — the callee OWNS the value — but
			// its `typeParameter` tag fails `valueIsManagedHeap` (opaque here), so the branch above never enrols
			// it. Enrol it now so the SHARED body move-tracks it: a `self.items.push(item)` / consuming
			// `self.N(item)` forward MOVES it (poisons the binding, a second use → E3102), and if it is left LIVE
			// on some path `emitScopeDrops` / the join reconciliation drop it once through the descriptor-gated
			// `__drop_type_param` (0 destructor for a trivial instantiation → inert). An always-moved feed
			// (`push`) is `movedFrom` at exit, so no drop is emitted and the shared body's golden is unchanged —
			// only a method that can drop reserves the descriptor (`Queries.foldDescriptorNeeds`).
			//
			// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS THE FEED'S SINK, AND IT USED TO ASK WHETHER THE FUNCTION HAD A RECEIVER (W10).** A
			// `Self{value: v}` CONSTRUCTOR feed is settled entirely at the concrete call site — the value the call
			// RETURNS owns it — so enrolling one here would drop it a second time, through a `__drop_type_param`
			// whose descriptor a non-array generic program does not even install. `isInstanceMethod` stood in for
			// that distinction and was exact only while the recognizer saw `self`-headed stores alone; a STATIC
			// that pushes its argument into a local `Array with T` and hands that array to the `Self` it returns
			// feeds CALLEE STORAGE with no receiver at all, and reading the receiver there leaves the caller
			// transferring a value nobody drops (a leak). The sink is recorded, so this reads it.
			//
			// Gated on the opaque `typeParameter` tag as well: a feed parameter is type-parameter-typed by
			// construction (`markOpaqueContainerMutatorFeeds` marks only such a parameter), so the tag test
			// is redundant with the sink — but it makes this branch PROVABLY exclusive of the managed branch above
			// (which needs `valueIsManagedHeap`, false for a `typeParameter`), so no parameter is ever enrolled
			// twice, and it pins the drop router's `typeParameter` arm to exactly what is enrolled here.
			//
			// ⚡ **THE TAG IS ASKED FIRST, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE OPTIMIZATION.** `tagOf` is a dense
			// per-value array read; `paramFeedOf` is a whole-program map lookup that returns two unions, and
			// MEASURED it costs 2 allocations EVERY TIME IT IS ASKED — +2 per single-parameter function and
			// +6 per three-parameter one, on a corpus with no generics anywhere in it. The predecessor kept it
			// off that path by accident: `isInstanceMethod` was a per-FUNCTION boolean hoisted out of this loop,
			// and it short-circuited every receiverless function's parameters before the lookup. W10 replaced it
			// with the right question and lost the guard with the wrong one. The tag is the guard that belongs
			// here — it is local, it is exact (a feed parameter is type-parameter-typed by construction, stated
			// two paragraphs up), and it is already a required conjunct, so asking it first adds no test at all.
			// Co-owned for the branch above's reason, which does not depend on the tag: a feed parameter's
			// concrete call site may have increfed a borrow to pay for the slot.
			if self.tagOf(i as ValueId) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter and self.signatures.paramFeedOf(funcName, index: i).sinkIsCalleeStorage() 'opaqueFeed'
				self.markValueOwnsHeap(i as ValueId, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
				self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
			end 'opaqueFeed'

			// ⭐ **A `constructedValue` FEED IS RECORDED RATHER THAN ENROLLED (W41-opaque).** The paragraph above
			// says why it must not be enrolled owned — the value the call RETURNS owns it, so a drop here would
			// be the second. But the STORE that puts it there must equally not take a reference for it: the
			// concrete call site already consumed the argument to pay for that slot. So the fact is recorded for
			// `moveOpaqueValueInto` to read, which is the one place that has to tell this parameter from a
			// borrow the body itself produced. Guarded by the same tag test, first, for its measured reason.
			if self.tagOf(i as ValueId) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter and self.signatures.paramFeedOf(funcName, index: i).isFeed() 'anyOpaqueFeed'
				self.constructorFeedParams = privateValueMarkSet(self.constructorFeedParams, anchor: sharedEmptyConstructorFeedParams)
				self.constructorFeedParams.insert(i as ValueId)
			end 'anyOpaqueFeed'
		end 'eachParam'
	end 'bindParameters'

	// ⭐ **SEED AND WIRE THE COMPANION ENVIRONMENT PARAMETERS (P1.5-A2b-2).** Under the uniform ABI a
	// function-typed PARAMETER travels with an environment: the caller supplies the (fn ptr, env) pair, and
	// this function's own `f(x)` call threads that env. The companions are APPENDED after the user parameters
	// (ValueIds `userParamCount`..), each an i64 heap pointer, and each function-typed user parameter's
	// `valueClosureEnv` is pointed at its companion. Appending — rather than v1's interleave, which puts the
	// env immediately after its fn parameter — keeps every USER parameter at its own ABI position, so the call
	// machinery needs no source-slot→ABI-slot remap; multiple function parameters compose (their companions
	// append in declaration order, which the caller's `appendCalleeEnvArgs` matches). A companion carries no
	// source name (it is never referenced by the body, only by the indirect call THROUGH the function
	// parameter, via `valueClosureEnv`), so `bindParameters` does not bind it. Its ValueId's value columns are
	// seeded here so the columns stay dense; `recordParamOrigin` gives it the declaring parameter's span for
	// the register-pressure diagnostic.
	function bindClosureEnvParams(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray, userParamCount ParamCount)
		var envSlot = userParamCount
		for i in 0 upto userParamCount 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("bindClosureEnvParams: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			if maxonTypeTag(paramType) != ValueTypeTag.function 'notFunction'
				continue
			end 'notFunction'
			// Seed the companion's value columns (an i64 pointer) at ValueId `envSlot` — the next push — then
			// point the function parameter at it. Seeding pushes to every value column, so it must precede the set.
			self.recordValueType(MaxonType.integer)
			try self.valueClosureEnv.set(i, value: envSlot) otherwise panic("bindClosureEnvParams: function parameter v{i} has no closure-env slot — the value columns are seeded before this")
			let paramRange = try paramRanges.get(i) otherwise panic("bindClosureEnvParams: paramRanges.get OOB at {i}")
			self.artifact.recordParamOrigin(envSlot, range: paramRange)
			envSlot = envSlot + 1
		end 'eachParam'
	end 'bindClosureEnvParams'

	// ⭐⭐ **SEED AND WIRE THE COMPANION WITNESS PARAMETERS (P1.7a-existentials slice 1)** —
	// `bindClosureEnvParams`' exact twin, and deliberately so: a function-typed parameter travels with an
	// ENVIRONMENT and an interface-typed one travels with a WITNESS, and both are "one hidden machine word per
	// qualifying user parameter, appended as a block, pointed at from the user parameter's own dense column".
	//
	// ⚠ **THEY ARE APPENDED AFTER THE ENV BLOCK, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE ABI:**
	// `[user][env][existentialWitness][layout][constraintWitness]`. Every hidden parameter BELOW this one is
	// addressed from the END (`paramCount - witnessParamCount - 1` for the layout slot, `paramCount -
	// witnessParamCount + i` for a constraint witness), so inserting a block here moves neither — and the env
	// block is addressed from the START, so this block must come after it rather than before.
	//
	// The companion is UNNAMED: `bindParameters` never binds it, and only the widening (caller side) and the
	// dispatch (callee side) read it, by position.
	function bindExistentialWitnessParams(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray, userParamCount ParamCount, envParamCount ParamCount)
		var witnessSlot = userParamCount + envParamCount
		for i in 0 upto userParamCount 'eachParam'
			let paramType = try paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("bindExistentialWitnessParams: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
			if maxonTypeTag(paramType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notExistential'
				continue
			end 'notExistential'
			// Seeding pushes to every value column, so it must precede the pairing set — `bindClosureEnvParams`'
			// ordering, for its reason.
			self.recordValueType(MaxonType.integer)
			self.pairInterfaceWitness(i, witness: witnessSlot)
			let paramRange = try paramRanges.get(i) otherwise panic("bindExistentialWitnessParams: paramRanges.get OOB at {i}")
			self.artifact.recordParamOrigin(witnessSlot, range: paramRange)
			witnessSlot = witnessSlot + 1
		end 'eachParam'
	end 'bindExistentialWitnessParams'

	// Is `value` a managed HEAP value — a struct box, a String, or a boxed union — the kinds a consumed
	// parameter can own (and this function must drop) and a consumed argument must transfer? A ranged-alias /
	// `int` / `bool` owns no heap. This is the one owned-temp adoption test, so `enrolOwnedCallTemp` routes
	// through it rather than re-spelling the three tags. A managed heap value is a BYTE RECORD (a String or a
	// Character) or a non-text aggregate, so it is derived from `valueIsNonTextAggregate` to keep the
	// aggregate half stated once.
	//
	// ⚠ **`tagIsByteRecord`, NOT `tagIsText` — THIS IS THE LEAK SITE.** It is the single test that decides
	// whether a freshly minted value is ADOPTED as an owned temporary and therefore ever dropped. Spelled
	// `tagIsText` it answers "no" for a Character, and every Character a program produces — every trip of
	// `for c in s` — is allocated and never freed (measured by sabotage: exit 101).
	function valueIsManagedHeap(value ValueId) returns bool
		return tagIsByteRecord(self.tagOf(value)) or self.valueIsNonTextAggregate(value)
	end 'valueIsManagedHeap'

	// Is `value` a managed heap AGGREGATE — a struct box or a boxed union — but NOT a String? An aggregate
	// has no cheap owning COPY (unlike a String, which promotes with the fused-record blit
	// `promoteToOwnedString`), so a borrowed one becomes owned only by CO-OWNERSHIP — an `__mm_retain`
	// incref of the shared box (`retainBorrowedAggregate`, OPEN #40).
	//
	// ⭐ **ALL THREE DOORS NOW SPEND IT (S5), and the two that did not were held back by a wrong premise.**
	// The `var q = p` binding always did. The two HAND-OFF sites — `return <borrowed aggregate>`
	// (`emitOwnedValueReturn`) and a borrowed-aggregate `gives`/ternary arm merged into an owned result phi
	// (`promoteBorrowedGive`) — refused, on the reasoning that co-owning ACROSS a call boundary needed the
	// cross-call consume. It does not, and the refcount is what makes the difference: the callee increfs
	// before the `ret`, the caller adopts and decrefs once, and the borrow's own owner never notices — the
	// same balance the in-frame binding gets, with the two halves in different frames. What genuinely needs
	// the cross-call consume is a MOVE, where the source must be poisoned so exactly one owner remains
	// (`moveManagedValueInto`, still a clean refusal for a borrowed aggregate).
	// ⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL IS ONE (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).** Its VALUE half is a conformer's record, which
	// has no cheap owning copy either — a fat pointer cannot be deep-copied without knowing the dynamic type
	// — so it becomes owned by exactly the same CO-OWNERSHIP the aggregates above use, and `retainBorrowedAggregate`
	// carries its witness across the retain. Reading it as a trivial scalar is what left a consumed
	// existential parameter unenrolled (`bindParameters`), so the callee owned a reference nothing dropped.
	function valueIsNonTextAggregate(value ValueId) returns bool
		let tag = self.tagOf(value)
		// A generic INSTANCE (`Sizer with bool`, P1.6-B1) is a heap box exactly like a plain `structRef`: a
		// pointer to a refcounted record with a baked destructor. It carries the SAME managed-aggregate
		// obligations — owned, dropped once at scope exit, never handed off borrowed-as-owned — so it joins
		// the boundary here rather than being read as a trivial scalar and leaked.
		return tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance or tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef or self.valueIsBoxedUnion(value)
	end 'valueIsNonTextAggregate'

	// `returns <Type>` or nothing (void). The return type flows to the registry
	// (for SemanticCheck) and to lowering (via the function signature).
	function parseOptionalReturnType() returns MaxonReturnType throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.returns) 'noReturns'
			return MaxonReturnType.void
		end 'noReturns'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.returns)
		return MaxonReturnType.value(try self.parseTypeReference())
	end 'parseOptionalReturnType'

	// The `throws E` clause that follows a function's return type (P1.4b). `E` is a bare enum name — a
	// payload-free enum conforming to `Error` (validated later, in SemanticCheck; the sweep and the real
	// parse are tolerant). Read by BOTH the declaration sweep (`recordScannedSignature`, whole-program) and
	// the real parse (`parseFunction`, which stamps `func.throwsTypeName`), so the throws fact is captured
	// once and shared, exactly as `parseOptionalReturnType` is.
	function parseOptionalThrowsClause() returns ThrowsClause throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.throws) 'noThrows'
			return ThrowsClause.none
		end 'noThrows'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.throws)
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(nameTok.value))
	end 'parseOptionalThrowsClause'

	// ⛔ **A FUNCTION SIGNATURE ENDS AT THE END OF ITS LINE.** Both clauses that may follow the
	// parameter list are optional and both have just been offered (`returns T`, then `throws E`), so a
	// token still standing here belongs to no part of the header — and the body has not started, because
	// a body starts on the next line. `function foo() int` is the shape this names: the `returns` keyword
	// omitted, which the two optional readers cannot see, since "no `returns`" is also how a VOID function
	// is spelled.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REASON IT IS A CHECK HERE AND NOT A REFUSAL INSIDE `parseOptionalReturnType`:** that reader
	// runs BEFORE the `throws` clause, so to leave `function f() throws E` alone it would have to know the
	// follow set of `)` — the grammar's order, written down a second time, in the one routine that is not
	// reading it. It is also shared with the TOLERANT declaration sweep, whose `otherwise return` would
	// swallow the refusal and report nothing; the sweep's contract is to record what it can, and a
	// judgement it can only discard does not belong in it. The real parse holds the position and reports.
	//
	// Without it the `int` fell through to the statement dispatcher and was rejected as `E2015 Unsupported:
	// int statement` — a true sentence about the wrong thing, pointing at a body that does not exist and
	// never naming the missing keyword. EOF is admitted so a source truncated mid-declaration keeps
	// reaching the missing-`end` diagnostic, which says more about it than "unexpected token".
	function requireSignatureLineEnd() throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.newline) or self.isEof() 'headerComplete'
			return
		end 'headerComplete'

		let tok = self.current()
		throw ParseError.strayToken(tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'requireSignatureLineEnd'

	// The optional `implements <Interface>` clause on an enum/union declaration — error enums declare
	// `implements Error` (P1.4b). Shared by the tolerant declaration sweep and the real parse, exactly as
	// `parseOptionalThrowsClause` is, so the two record the same conformance.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT USED TO CONSUME AND DISCARD, AND THAT WAS THE HOLE (A1s wave 2).** Its predecessor
	// (`skipOptionalImplementsClause`) called the payload-free-enum conforms-to-`Error` check "a follow-up",
	// which was true while nothing asked — but wave 1's abstract-`throws` narrowing began asking, and with
	// no record to consult it narrowed `throws Error` to ANY enum. See `EnumLayout.conformsTo`.
	//
	// A COMMA LIST, matching `parseTypeImplementsClause`'s: `enum E implements Error, Debuggable` names two.
	// An enum that implements nothing returns the SHARED empty, so the common case allocates nothing.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ALSO READS THE `with <TypeArgs>` OF EVERY NAME THAT CARRIES ONE** (conditional extensions). The
	// bindings are what a conditional extension's `where` clause is evaluated against
	// (`StructLayout.conformanceBindings`), and they are read through `parseConformanceWithArgs` — the SAME
	// reader the real parse's `parseOneConformance` uses — rather than a second walk of the same grammar.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **WHERE ONE `with` ENDS IS THE INTERFACE'S `uses` ARITY, SO THE CLAUSE'S EXTENT IS A WHOLE-PROGRAM
	// FACT — AND THAT IS WHY THIS READER STOPS EARLY IN THE SWEEP AND NOWHERE ELSE (W95).** Whether the comma
	// after `with Score` opens a second ARGUMENT or a second INTERFACE is decided by `Score`'s interface's
	// arity (R7), which only the complete index holds. Two of this function's three callers run with that
	// index whole (the real enum parse, and `Queries.foldConformanceClauses`); the DECLARATION SWEEP is the
	// one that does not, because the sweep is what builds it.
	//
	// So the loop asks the fact itself rather than being told which pass it is in: it continues past a `with`
	// exactly when the boundary is UNAMBIGUOUS — the arguments were parenthesized (the `)` ends them whatever
	// the arity is), or the arity RESOLVED. In the sweep no arity resolves, so an unparenthesized `with` ends
	// the clause exactly as it always did; a misspelled interface ends it in every pass, which is the same
	// bounded outcome and a program the whole-program `E3015 … implements unknown interface` already names.
	//
	// ⛔ **CONTINUING WITHOUT THE ARITY WOULD TRADE A MISSING CONFORMANCE FOR A WRONG ONE**, which is strictly
	// worse: `implements Pair with A, B` against `interface Pair uses First, Second` would file `B` as an
	// INTERFACE and produce a spurious `E3015 … implements unknown interface 'B'` on a legal program — R5's
	// measured bug, in the other direction. The deferral (`ConformanceClauseDeclSite`) exists precisely so the
	// sweep never has to guess.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **STOPPING EARLY IS ABOUT MEANING, NEVER ABOUT THE CURSOR — the remainder is always CONSUMED
	// (`skipRestOfImplementsClause`).** It used to be left standing, and on an `enum` that was a WRONG ANSWER
	// rather than a missing one: `recordScannedEnum` resumes its CASE walk at whatever token this stops on, so
	// `enum Slow implements Tagged with Code, Error` had its case list read out of the clause's own tail.
	// ⛔ MEASURED: `error E3034: unknown enum case: 'tooSmall'` on a program whose only fault was the ORDER of
	// its clause — `implements Error, Tagged with Code` compiled and ran. `recordScannedType` survived it only
	// because a stray identifier at a type's top level is skipped rather than read as a member.
	function readOptionalImplementsClause() returns ScannedConformanceClause throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.implements) 'noImplements'
			return ScannedConformanceClause.declaringNothing()
		end 'noImplements'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.implements)

		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
		// One entry per NAME, so the two columns pair by index (`StructLayout.conformanceBindings`). Built
		// eagerly rather than on the first `with`: the clause that binds nothing at all is the one that never
		// reaches here, and it takes the shared empty from `declaringNothing` above.
		var bindings = ConformanceBindingArray.create()
		while true 'moreInterfaces'
			let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
			names.push(nameTok.value)

			if self.at(TokenKind.with) 'bindsAssociatedTypes'
				try self.consume(TokenKind.with)
				// Read BEFORE the arguments, because the reader consumes the `(` that is the answer.
				let delimited = self.at(TokenKind.leftParen)
				bindings.push(try self.parseConformanceWithArgs(nameTok.value, spelling: ConformanceArgSpelling.asWritten))

				if not delimited and not self.conformanceArityResolves(nameTok.value) 'theArityIsNotKnownYet'
					self.skipRestOfImplementsClause()
					break
				end 'theArityIsNotKnownYet'
			end 'bindsAssociatedTypes' else 'bindsNothing'
				bindings.push(sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances)
			end 'bindsNothing'

			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'lastInterface'
				break
			end 'lastInterface'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'moreInterfaces'

		return ScannedConformanceClause.create(names, bindings: bindings)
	end 'readOptionalImplementsClause'

	// ⭐⭐ **CONSUME THE CLAUSE ITEMS THE READER ABOVE COULD NOT INTERPRET — the half of "stop here" that is
	// about the CURSOR (W95).** Every caller resumes at whatever token the clause read leaves, and one of them
	// (`recordScannedEnum`) reads the next token as a CASE, so a clause tail left standing becomes part of the
	// declaration. See the header above for the measured `E3034`.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS EXACT WITHOUT THE ARITY, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON A SKIP CAN GO WHERE A READ CANNOT.** The
	// two readings of `… with A, B` disagree about what `B` MEANS — a second argument or a second interface —
	// and agree exactly on the TOKENS it occupies: a comma, then a name, then an optional `with` and its
	// arguments. So this walks that shape and cannot be wrong either way.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ADVANCES ONLY WHILE IT IS ON A COMMA**, which is what keeps it out of the declaration's body: a
	// case written on the header's own line (`enum E implements A with X  red green end`) follows no comma, so
	// the walk stops on `red` and the case walk reads it, exactly as it would after a fully interpreted clause.
	function skipRestOfImplementsClause()
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'anotherClauseItem'
			self.advance()
			if not self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'namesNothing'
				return
			end 'namesNothing'
			self.advance()

			if not self.at(TokenKind.with) 'bindsNothing'
				continue
			end 'bindsNothing'
			self.advance()
			self.skipOneConformanceArgument()
		end 'anotherClauseItem'
	end 'skipRestOfImplementsClause'

	// The ONE argument that follows a `with` in the skip above — a parenthesized group, or a leaf type
	// reference and its dotted chain. A FURTHER argument, if the interface takes one, arrives as the comma the
	// caller's loop is already written around, so the two spellings of a multi-argument `with` need no
	// distinction here either.
	function skipOneConformanceArgument()
		if self.skipBalancedParens() 'delimited'
			return
		end 'delimited'

		self.advance()
		while self.at(TokenKind.dot) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.identifier 'dottedChain'
			self.advance()
			self.advance()
		end 'dottedChain'
	end 'skipOneConformanceArgument'

	// Does the program state `interfaceName`'s `uses` arity YET? — the one question that decides whether an
	// unparenthesized `with` has a knowable end (see `readOptionalImplementsClause`). It is the ARITY DOOR's
	// own union read for its other half: `interfaceUsesArity` wants the number and substitutes a default when
	// there is none, while this wants to know whether there IS one, and folding the two would hide the
	// default's presence behind a count that looks like an answer.
	function conformanceArityResolves(interfaceName ByteArray) returns bool
		match self.conformanceUsesArity(interfaceName) 'arity'
			declared then return true
			unresolvable then return false
		end 'arity'
	end 'conformanceArityResolves'

	// A type reference. M1: a bare identifier (a named type like `ExitCode`,
	// interned into the artifact's local interner) or a bare primitive keyword.
	// Ranged/generic/qualified type syntax grows at M2+.
	//
	// ⭐ **`Self` — AND THE ENCLOSING TYPE'S OWN NAME — RESOLVE TO THE ENCLOSING DECLARATION'S OWN TYPE
	// HERE, SYNTACTICALLY** (`enclosingSelfType`: a `structRef` inside a `type`, a `named` inside an
	// `enum`/`union`). Every other identifier stays `named` and is resolved later, by `TypeResolution`
	// against the project registry. The asymmetry is not a shortcut, it is the only thing that WORKS:
	//
	//   • **This routine runs in BOTH passes.** The tolerant declaration sweep reads a signature
	//     through it (`recordScannedSignature`) while the whole-program index is still being BUILT,
	//     so it cannot ask "is `Point` a declared type?" — the answer depends on how far the sweep
	//     has got, which would make a declaration's recorded return type depend on its POSITION.
	//   • **`Self` needs no index.** It means the type whose body encloses it, which is a fact about
	//     the TOKENS, so both passes answer it identically and neither has to look anything up.
	//
	// So `static function create(…) returns Self` is `structRef(Point)` in the sweep and in the
	// parse, and a call's result is typed exactly (`mintCallResult` reads the sweep). That is what
	// `let p = Point.create(3, y: 4)` needs to know `p` is a Point at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **`returns Point` from OUTSIDE `type Point` is the gap, and it is LOUD.** The signature
	// resolves to `structRef` (TypeResolution) while the call site's tag comes from the sweep's
	// `named` — so `let p = make()` binds `p` as a ranged int and `p.x` reports "is not a struct".
	// A wrong REJECTION of a correct program, never a wrong answer, and the program it rejects is
	// `struct-return` — still `disabled-test:`, deferred to P1.4's ownership ruling, which is what
	// a struct crossing a call boundary needs before it can be right for any other reason.
	function parseTypeReference() returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		// A TUPLE TYPE (P1.10) — `(Integer, Integer)`. Asked first because `(` opens nothing else in a type
		// position: the one other parenthesized type form, `function(…)`, is introduced by its own keyword and
		// is read by `readFunctionTypeAlias` before this is ever reached.
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'tupleType'
			return try self.parseTupleTypeReference()
		end 'tupleType'

		if self.at(TokenKind.selfType) 'selfType'
			let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.selfType)
			return try self.enclosingSelfType(tok)
		end 'selfType'

		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'named'
			// The head's INDEX, taken before the cursor moves: the dotted-chain walk below is indexed from
			// the head, exactly as the CALL door's is, so both ask `dottedChainLengthAt` the same question
			// about the same token.
			let headIndex = self.pos
			let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
			// Read before the substitution below, because that substitution is for BARE names only — see it.
			let qualifierDots = self.dottedChainLengthAt(headIndex)

			// ⭐⭐ **AN INTERFACE EXTENSION'S ASSOCIATED TYPE IS THE CONFORMER'S BINDING FOR IT, SUBSTITUTED
			// HERE AS A NAME AND RESOLVED BY EVERY ARM BELOW UNCHANGED (conditional extensions).** `extension
			// HasItems where Element is Equatable` writing `function has(target Element)` is parsed once per
			// conformer, and `IntList implements HasItems with Integer` binds `Element` to `Integer` — so what
			// the body means by `Element` is exactly what it would have meant by `Integer`, and the honest way
			// to say that is to resolve the name the binding names.
			//
			// ⚠⚠ **IT IS A NAME SUBSTITUTION AND *NOT* AN ENTRY IN `enclosingTypeParams`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS
			// A SILENT WRONG TYPE.** Installing the interface's associated names as type parameters would make
			// `Element` resolve to `typeParameter(0)` — position 0 of the CONFORMER's own `uses` list, a
			// different type entirely, and one nothing downstream would question. Substituting the name instead
			// means the arms below answer for `Integer` / `HashItem` / `NotEq` exactly as they answer for a
			// hand-written annotation, so an alias stays a ranged alias and a struct stays a struct.
			//
			// ⚠ **BARE NAMES ONLY.** A dotted chain's head is a namespace or an instance
			// (`lib.fmt.Score`, `StrWrapper.Idx`), never an associated type, and rewriting one would retarget a
			// qualified reference at a name that has nothing to do with it. A TYPE extension installs no
			// bindings at all (its parameters are per-instantiation, and `enclosingTypeParams` is the right
			// mechanism there) — see `openExtensionBodyScope`.
			let name = tok.value if qualifierDots > 0 else self.extensionAssociatedTypeBinding(tok.value)

			// Recorded HERE, above every arm, rather than in the arm that finally decides what the name
			// denotes: a name written in a type position is a USE whichever way it resolves, and hooking
			// the arms instead would need one hook per arm and would silently miss the next one added.
			// It records the SUBSTITUTED name, because that is the declaration a use of `Element` keeps alive.
			self.recordTypeNameUse(name)

			// ⭐ A QUALIFIED PER-INSTANCE ALIAS REFERENCE (P1.6-C) — `StrWrapper.Idx` in a type position. An
			// identifier followed by `.` and another identifier is `Instance.Member`, the ONE dotted type form,
			// so this peek precedes every bare-identifier arm below (the `.` is what tells a qualified reference
			// from a plain one). It resolves to `named(join)` in BOTH passes — the SHAPE is enough, needing no
			// registry, so the tolerant sweep and the real parse answer identically while the index is still
			// building. TypeResolution erases a registered per-instance `X.Y` to `integer` (keeping the nominal
			// name in the value column) and reports an unregistered one as E3011.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **THE WALK IS GREEDY AND CONSUMES EVERY `.identifier` PAIR (N1b), which is what admits a
			// MULTI-SEGMENT NAMESPACE — `lib.fmt.Score`.** It stays purely SYNTACTIC, asking no registry, so
			// the tolerant sweep and the real parse still answer identically; `TypeResolution` decides what the
			// assembled name denotes (a per-instance `X.Y`, a directory-qualified alias, or E3011).
			//
			// ⚠ **GREEDY CANNOT CHANGE A PROGRAM THAT ALREADY COMPILED**, and that is why no lookahead gate is
			// needed: the one-dot version already consumed a dot unconditionally, so the only inputs this
			// touches are chains of THREE or more segments — and every one of those was already a parse that
			// minted `named("a.b")` and then met a dangling `.c` with no rule for it. There is no type position
			// in the grammar where a type reference may legally be followed by `.<name>`: an `as` cast is
			// not a postfix operator here (`parseCast` loops only on another `as`), and a return clause, a
			// parameter, a field and a generic argument are each closed by their own follower.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE *SAME* WALK THE CALL DOOR USES — ONE `dottedChainLengthAt`, ONE
			// `assembleDottedChainAt` (N1b review), AND IT WAS TWO.** A hand-rolled loop here demanded
			// `TokenKind.identifier` of every segment while the call walk asked `tokenCanBeAName`, and the two
			// spelled the same rule differently with nothing to make them agree. ⛔ MEASURED on the divergence:
			// with a module directory `lib/from/`, `lib.from.helper()` COMPILED AND RAN while
			// `21 as lib.from.Score` was refused `E3011: Unknown type 'lib'` — a wrong REJECTION quoting a
			// fragment of the name, for a declaration `pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias` had filed under exactly the
			// key the author wrote. A directory name is a FILESYSTEM name and owes the lexer nothing, so the
			// segments of a namespace are `tokenCanBeAName`'s question in BOTH positions or the mirror is
			// reachable from one of them only. Pinned by
			// `namespace-qualified-resolution/keyword-named-directory-segment-resolves-in-both-positions`.
			if qualifierDots > 0 'qualifiedMember'
				let qualified = self.assembleDottedChainAt(headIndex, dots: qualifierDots)

				// Stops PAST the final segment, unlike `parseNamespaceQualifiedCall`'s twin loop: nothing
				// downstream of a type reference consumes that token, where a call hands it to `parseCallNamed`.
				// `advance` and not `consume(identifier)` — a segment may be a keyword standing as a name (D8),
				// which is the whole point of sharing the walk above.
				for _ in 0 upto qualifierDots 'eachSegment'
					try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
					self.advance()
				end 'eachSegment'

				// ⭐ **THE FINAL SEGMENT IS THE TYPE NAME, AND THE RECORDING ABOVE CAUGHT ONLY THE HEAD.**
				// `recordTypeNameUse` runs before the chain is assembled, so `api.Score` recorded `api` — the
				// DIRECTORY — and never `Score`. E3062 could not see the difference (a namespace is not one of
				// this file's own typealiases either way), but `checkUnusedExports` asks which DECLARATION was
				// named, and a declaration is registered under its bare name: MEASURED, seven cases across
				// `typealias-collision` and `namespace-qualified-resolution` reported `export typealias 'Score'`
				// as unreferenced while their whole subject is that the qualified reference reaches it.
				match splitQualifiedName(qualified) 'member'
					notQualified then panic("parseTypeReference: assembleDottedChainAt built a chain from {qualifierDots} dot(s) that splitQualifiedName reads as unqualified")
					parts(_, member) then self.recordTypeNameUse(member)
				end 'member'

				return MaxonType.named(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(qualified))
			end 'qualifiedMember'

			// The `String` and `Character` BUILTINS — recognized SYNTACTICALLY, like `Self` below, so they
			// need no registry and both the declaration sweep and the real parse answer them identically.
			// Each resolves to its OWN `MaxonType` arm (`string` / `character`, NEVER `named`), which is what
			// tags a `String`/`Character` parameter's value at parse time and keeps every `named`-reading rule
			// from treating a record pointer as a ranged int.
			//
			// ⭐ The two names are ONE TABLE (`byteRecordTagOfTypeName`) rather than an arm each, because
			// `enclosingSelfType` must reach the SAME answer for the SAME name and for a rung did not — see
			// there, and see the table for the 25 wrong rejections that cost.
			let byteRecord = byteRecordTagOfTypeName(name)
			if byteRecord != ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'builtinByteRecord'
				return maxonTypeOfTag(byteRecord, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
			end 'builtinByteRecord'
			// ⭐⭐ **`CharacterSet` HAD AN ARM HERE, AND W115 DELETED IT: THE BARE NAME NOW FALLS THROUGH TO THE
			// ORDINARY STRUCT REGISTRY, BECAUSE `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` IS WHAT PUTS IT THERE.** The arm
			// answered `structRef(__CharacterSet)` — the reserved spelling the compiler registered its own
			// layout under — and W17's header called that *"one layout, the compiler's, reachable under the
			// name the corpus writes"*, recording that it was also what kept the corpus module OFF the
			// whitelist: two layouts under two keys was the hazard. Listing the module settles it the other
			// way round, and the arm had to go WITH the listing rather than after it — left in place, every
			// `chars CharacterSet` parameter in `stdlib/String.maxon` kept resolving to `__CharacterSet` while
			// the corpus statics returned the declared `CharacterSet`, which is **`E3005 expected
			// 'CharacterSet', got '__CharacterSet'`** on `"  x  ".trim()`.
			// The type's own name inside its own body means what `Self` means — for an `enum Toggle`'s
			// `function flip() returns Toggle` exactly as for a `type Point`'s `returns Point`. Both
			// spellings are legal Maxon and they must not resolve differently, because a struct's own name
			// left `named` would type `create`'s result as an integer and `p.x` would fail against the very
			// type that declared it.
			if self.namesEnclosingType(name) 'ownName'
				return try self.enclosingSelfType(tok)
			end 'ownName'

			// A GENERIC TYPE PARAMETER (P1.6-A) — a bare `T` inside `type Box uses T`. Recognized by NAME
			// against the enclosing type's `uses` list, so the tolerant sweep and the real parse answer it
			// identically without a registry (like `Self`). It resolves to an opaque i64 — NEVER `named`, for
			// the reason `structRef`/`string`/`function` are their own arms: the rules that read `named` treat
			// it as a ranged int, and a `T` is neither numeric nor a declared type. There is no shadowing
			// question at this slice: a type param name equal to a declared type name is not in the corpus,
			// and the param wins here (it is the innermost scope).
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **THE IDENTITY IS `(THIS DECLARATION, THIS NAME)` AND NOT THE POSITION (W14).** A position
			// made `HoldA`'s first parameter and `HoldB`'s the same value, so `Array with U` and `Array with V`
			// interned as one instance — see `ProgramSignatures.typeParamTokenFor` for the measured wrong
			// answer that produced. **`self.enclosingType` IS the owner at all three of the doors that install
			// a parameter scope**, and that is a property of the doors rather than an assumption here: a
			// `type`'s own body (`openTypeParamScope`) and an `interface`'s (`readInterfaceDeclaration`) are
			// each inside the declaration that names the parameters, and a TYPE EXTENSION's body
			// (`openTypeExtensionBodyScope`) installs the TARGET's list under `enterTypeScope(conformer)` —
			// where the conformer of a `typeExtension` IS the extended type (`extensionTargetOf` builds the
			// conformer list as the target alone), which is exactly what makes an extension's `T` the same `T`
			// as the type's own. An INTERFACE extension's body (`openExtensionBodyScope`) is the fourth door
			// and it installs the CONFORMER's list under `enterTypeScope(conformer)` — never the interface's
			// associated names, which reach a body as a NAME SUBSTITUTION and are resolved by whichever arm
			// the substituted name lands on.
			//
			// ⚠ The digest is a pure function of the two names, so the tolerant sweep and the real parse
			// compute the SAME token with no registry between them — which is the property this arm's
			// "without a registry" sentence has always rested on and which an intern-order id would break.
			if self.typeParamPositionOf(name) < self.enclosingTypeParams.count() 'typeParam'
				return MaxonType.typeParameter(typeParamTokenFor(self.enclosingType, paramName: name))
			end 'typeParam'

			// A BARE INNER ALIAS (P1.6-C / P1.7 slice 3b-i) — `Idx` OR `ElementArray` inside `type Wrapper`.
			// Recognized by NAME against `enclosingInnerAliases` (populated while THIS type's body is parsed, so
			// the sweep and the real parse agree without the whole-program registries, which the sweep has not
			// folded yet). It resolves under its BASE-QUALIFIED name (`Wrapper.Idx`), NEVER the bare member (which
			// collides with a same-named inner alias of another generic type). Two shapes: a GENERIC-INSTANCE
			// inner alias (`ElementArray = Array with T`) resolves to `genericInstance(gid)` in the REAL parse
			// (the index is complete), the read-side twin of the top-level generic-alias arm below — so the field
			// type is a genericInstance TypeResolution leaves concrete, not a `named` it would reject as E3011. A
			// RANGED inner alias stays `named("Wrapper.Idx")` (TypeResolution erases it to `integer`, and the
			// method-return retype re-qualifies it at the call site). During the SWEEP both stay `named`, on the
			// `allFilesFolded` gate the block below argues for — and the sweep's field type is re-tagged at
			// every read. ⚠ Here the gate is a RESTATEMENT rather than a change: an inner alias is folded with
			// its enclosing type at `foldFile`, i.e. when the file it is DECLARED IN ends, and this arm only
			// fires inside that same type's body — so the registry already answered no throughout the sweep of
			// every WELL-FORMED program. What the gate removes is the one way it could have answered yes: a
			// SECOND file declaring a type of the same name, which the tolerant sweep folds without complaint
			// and the real parse rejects later.
			if self.namesInnerAliasHere(name) 'innerAlias'
				let qualifiedInner = joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: name)
				if self.signatures.allFilesFolded and self.signatures.isGenericAlias(qualifiedInner) 'genericInnerAlias'
					return self.genericAliasDenotedType(qualifiedInner)
				end 'genericInnerAlias'
				// A TUPLE inner alias (W43) — `Entry` inside `type Map uses Key, Value`. It resolves STRAIGHT
				// THROUGH to the interned tuple's own `structRef`, exactly as the file-scope tuple-alias arm far
				// below does, which is what makes an `Entry`-typed slot and a `(Key, Value)`-typed one one type.
				// UNGATED by `allFilesFolded` for that arm's stated reason: `recordTupleAlias` writes DURING the
				// walk, so the resolved answer is the common case here and the bare `named` the rare one.
				if self.signatures.isTupleAlias(qualifiedInner) 'tupleInnerAlias'
					return MaxonType.structRef(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.signatures.tupleAliasTarget(qualifiedInner)))
				end 'tupleInnerAlias'
				return MaxonType.named(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(qualifiedInner))
			end 'innerAlias'

			// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-FILE ALIAS REGISTRIES ARE READ IN THE REAL PARSE ONLY, AND `allFilesFolded` IS WHAT
			// MAKES THAT TRUE RATHER THAN MERELY CLAIMED (A3e).** Both arms below ask a registry the declaration
			// SWEEP is still filling, and the sweep reaches both: `foldDeclaredSignaturesInto` parses no BODY,
			// but it reads declared TYPES — a struct field, a return clause, a generic alias's arguments —
			// through this very routine, holding the `ProgramSignatures` it is filling (`Queries.foldOneFileInto`).
			//
			// ⚠ **THE ANSWER WAS THEREFORE A PROPERTY OF THE DISK, NOT OF THE PROGRAM.** Both registries are
			// written by `foldFile`, which runs when a file's whole token walk ENDS — so an alias declared in
			// the file being swept always reads as undeclared (the arm's original comment), but one declared in
			// a file the walk reached EARLIER is already registered. The walk's order is the filesystem's and
			// the loader deliberately does not sort it (`StdlibLoader`'s header, user ruling 2026-07-24).
			//
			// ⇒ Gated, the sweep answers from the TOKENS ALONE and records the same bare `named` whatever the
			// walk's order — which is the answer the SAME-FILE case already had, so the gate deletes the
			// cross-file exception rather than introducing a new path. A swept `named` is exactly what the READ
			// doors exist to repair (`ProgramSignatures.declaredSlotType` for a field / array element / tuple
			// element, `resolveNamedAlias` for a call result), and repairing it is now the only route rather
			// than one of two that happened to agree.
			//
			// ⚠ **MEASURED, A3e** — the same two-file program staged in both walk orders, compiled with the
			// same output path and compared BYTE FOR BYTE: a generic alias and a function alias at a struct
			// field, a method return and a free return were already byte-identical, so what the gate removes is
			// a structural coincidence rather than a wrong answer. It is a coincidence that had already been
			// spent once: this arm's comment asserted the sweep never reached it and that a generic alias is
			// "never a swept value type", and both halves were false.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE TUPLE-ALIAS ARM BELOW IS DELIBERATELY OUTSIDE THIS GATE — the asymmetry is in WHEN the
			// registry is written, and it is measured.** `recordTupleAlias` writes DURING the walk, not at
			// `foldFile`, so for a tuple alias the sweep's RESOLVED answer is the common case and the bare
			// `named` is the rare cross-file one. Gating it would move every `Array with <a tuple alias>` onto
			// that rare spelling, and a generic instance's stored ARGUMENT is read RAW by seven index doors
			// (element size, mangled name, canonical name, owned-ness, the instantiation-kind gates) — measured,
			// it changed the emitted element size in `tuples/value-tuple-escaping-into-array-stays-heap` and
			// the answer of `tuples/array-of-managed-element-tuples-drops-each`. Its cross-file order dependence
			// is real and is fixed at the READ doors instead; see `ProgramSignatures.declaredSlotType`.
			//
			// ⚠ Every OTHER reader of these registries runs in the real parse only — a call base, a cast
			// target, an instantiation check — so it needs no gate; a gate there would assert something that
			// cannot be false.
			// ⭐⭐ **AN `interface` NAME IN A TYPE POSITION IS AN EXISTENTIAL (P1.7a-existentials slice 1)** —
			// `function useIt(b Backend)`, `let t as Tagged`, `returns Producer`. It resolves to
			// `interfaceRef`, the fat pointer, and NOT to the bare `named` the fallthrough would give: a
			// `named` reads as a ranged INT everywhere the type rules ask (`TypeRules.typeClassOf`'s stated
			// theorem), which is what made an interface-typed field *"cannot assign 'struct' to variable 'b'
			// of type 'int'"* before this arm.
			//
			// ⚠ **GATED ON `allFilesFolded` FOR THE BLOCK BELOW'S REASON, AND WITH A SHARPER EDGE OF ITS OWN.**
			// `foldInterfaceDeclarations` runs at the very END of `queryProgramSignatures` — after the flag is
			// set — so during the declaration SWEEP this registry is not merely incomplete, it is EMPTY. A
			// swept annotation is therefore always a bare `named`, whatever the file order, which is exactly
			// the order-independence the block below argues for; `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` is the READ-door
			// repair that turns those back into `interfaceRef` once the registry is whole, the same
			// arrangement `declaredSlotType`'s re-tags live under.
			//
			// ⚠ It cannot answer differently for the ONE parse that runs while the registry is still filling —
			// `readInterfaceDeclaration` itself, re-seeking each `interface` site — because an interface
			// method's types are stored as RENDERED SOURCE STRINGS and `maxonTypeName` spells `named(id)` and
			// `interfaceRef(id)` identically. A requirement's rendered signature is therefore the same bytes
			// whichever arm produced it, which is what conformance compares.
			//
			// `lookupInterface` rather than a bare map probe: it is the ONE lookup rule, and it is what puts
			// the SYNTHESIZED protocol interfaces (`Stringable`, `Hashable`, …) on the same footing as the
			// user-declared ones — a `function show(s Stringable)` is an existential exactly as a
			// `function run(b Backend)` is.
			// ⚠ `isInterfaceName`, NOT `lookupInterface`: this runs on EVERY type annotation in the program and
			// only needs the ANSWER, and a union return is heap-boxed even for its `notFound` case — measured
			// at +24,396 allocations in `phase:parse` at the ladder's rung 5, doubling per rung, on a corpus
			// with no interface in it. See `builtinInterfaceKind`.
			if self.signatures.allFilesFolded and isInterfaceName(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: name) 'wholeProgramInterfaceRegistry'
				return MaxonType.interfaceRef(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(name))
			end 'wholeProgramInterfaceRegistry'

			if self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'wholeProgramAliasRegistries'
				// A GENERIC-INSTANCE ALIAS (P1.6-A) — `IntBox` where `typealias IntBox = Box with Integer`.
				// It resolves to `genericInstance(giid)`, the read-side twin of the function-alias arm below —
				// or, over an `interface` base, to the EXISTENTIAL that instance denotes. `instanceDenotedType`
				// is the one place that split is made; the arm ABOVE is the bare-name half of the same fact.
				if self.signatures.isGenericAlias(name) 'genericAlias'
					return self.genericAliasDenotedType(name)
				end 'genericAlias'

				// A FUNCTION typealias name resolves to a `function` type, NEVER a `named` one (P1.5-A1) — so
				// a param/field/return declared with a function alias carries the `function` tag, which is what
				// makes a function value assignable to it and an indirect call reach it.
				if self.signatures.isFunctionAlias(name) 'functionAlias'
					// ⭐⭐ **INSIDE A TYPE BODY, THAT BODY'S OWN NESTED DECLARATION WINS (W49b)** — the innermost
					// scope, exactly as `namesInnerAliasHere` gives the three per-instance forms above. It is what
					// makes `ElementTransform` mean THIS conformer's `function(Element) returns Element` in a
					// `extension Iterable` body read once per conformer, instead of whichever fold wrote the bare
					// key last; see `publishNestedFunctionAlias` for the eleven-signature measurement.
					//
					// ⚠ **THE BARE HIT IS THE GATE, AND IT IS EXACT RATHER THAN AN APPROXIMATION** — a nested
					// declaration files BOTH keys, so a qualified entry cannot exist without its bare twin and
					// nothing is missed by asking second. It is also what keeps the join OFF the hot path: every
					// type annotation in the program reaches this arm, and only the handful of spellings that are
					// already function aliases pay `joinQualifiedName`'s allocation.
					if not self.enclosingType.isEmpty() 'maybeDeclaredInThisBody'
						let qualifiedInner = joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: name)
						if self.signatures.isFunctionAlias(qualifiedInner) 'thisBodysOwn'
							return MaxonType.function(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(qualifiedInner))
						end 'thisBodysOwn'
					end 'maybeDeclaredInThisBody'

					return MaxonType.function(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(name))
				end 'functionAlias'
			end 'wholeProgramAliasRegistries'

			// A TUPLE typealias (P1.10) — `Pair` where `typealias Pair = (Num, Num)`. It resolves STRAIGHT
			// THROUGH to the tuple's own `structRef`, minting no identity of its own, which is what makes a
			// `Pair`-typed slot and a `(Num, Num)`-typed one the same type. `tupleAliasTarget` canonicalizes,
			// so a target the SWEEP registered under the source spelling answers under the canonical one.
			// Ungated, for the reason the block above states.
			if self.signatures.isTupleAlias(name) 'tupleAlias'
				return MaxonType.structRef(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.signatures.tupleAliasTarget(name)))
			end 'tupleAlias'

			// ⭐⭐ **A BARE GENERIC TYPE NAME INSIDE A TYPE-PARAMETER SCOPE IS THAT TYPE AT THIS SCOPE'S
			// PARAMETERS** — `returns Inner` inside `type Outer uses T` is `Inner with Outer.T`, and
			// `WithIterIterator with Iter, Element` inside an interface extension specialized for a generic
			// conformer reaches this arm at its ARGUMENTS. It sits below every arm that binds a name more
			// tightly — the enclosing type's own name, a type PARAMETER, an inner alias, a generic/function/tuple
			// alias — because each of those is a nearer scope and this one only answers a name none of them
			// claimed. It stays ABOVE the `named` fallthrough because that is the DECLARATION view (a type whose
			// parameters nothing bound), which is precisely the reading this arm exists to replace.
			//
			// The `count()` gate is `bareGenericInstanceInScope`'s cost obligation, stated at that door: every
			// type annotation in the program passes this line, and outside a generic body there is no scope for
			// the question to have an answer in.
			if self.enclosingTypeParams.count() != 0 'insideATypeParameterScope'
				match self.bareGenericInstanceInScope(name) 'scoped'
					instance(gid) then return MaxonType.genericInstance(gid)
					none then break 'scoped'
				end 'scoped'
			end 'insideATypeParameterScope'

			// ⭐⭐ **THE LAST ARM IS WHERE A BARE RANGED-ALIAS NAME LANDS, SO IT IS WHERE E3063 IS DECIDED (N1c).**
			try self.requireUnambiguousTypeName(name, anchor: tok)

			return MaxonType.named(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(name))
		end 'named'
		if self.at(TokenKind.int) 'primInt'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.int)
			return MaxonType.integer
		end 'primInt'
		if self.at(TokenKind.bool) 'primBool'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.bool)
			return MaxonType.boolean
		end 'primBool'
		if self.at(TokenKind.float) 'primFloat'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.float)
			return MaxonType.float
		end 'primFloat'
		// ⭐⭐ **`cstring` — A NUL-TERMINATED BYTE POINTER, AND IT RESOLVES TO A MACHINE WORD RATHER THAN TO A
		// TAG OF ITS OWN.** The Lexer has carried the keyword since P1.2 (its help text already tells an author
		// to *"produce one with `String.cstr()` or `__ManagedMemory.toCString()`"*) while `parseTypeReference`
		// had no arm for it, so the one declaration in the corpus that names it —
		// `stdlib/String.maxon:194`'s `export function cstr() returns cstring` — was `E2010 Expected 'a type'`.
		//
		// ⚠ **WHY A WORD AND NOT A `ValueTypeTag` OF ITS OWN, STATED SO THE NEXT READER CAN OVERTURN IT.** A
		// `cstring` IS a raw address: it carries no length, no capacity and no ownership, and shv2 already
		// types every such address `integer` — `buffer@0` out of a record, `parent@32`, a `functionRef`'s code
		// pointer. A distinct tag would buy one thing, which is refusing `cstr() + 1`; it would cost an arm in
		// every exhaustive `ValueTypeTag` match across the three backends, for a type whose ONLY consumer is a
		// `__Builtins.*` parameter that immediately `strlen`s it. **What it does NOT buy is safety**: the
		// pointer's lifetime is the buffer's either way, and no tag expresses that.
		// ⇒ The distinction is deliberately not modelled, and the day a builtin declares a `cstring` PARAMETER
		// that must reject an ordinary int is the day this becomes a tag.
		//
		// ⚠ **THAT DAY HAS ARRIVED AND THE DECISION IS OPEN — the canonical corpus now carries the three cases
		// that ask for it.** `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s listing brought `__Builtins.subprocessResolveOnPath`
		// (a `cstring` parameter) and `__Builtins.subprocessGetPid` (an `i64` one) within reach of user code,
		// and `specs-shv2/builtin-type-checking.md`'s three `error-subprocess-*` cases pin exactly the pair of
		// refusals this arm cannot give: MEASURED, `subprocessResolveOnPath(42)` and `subprocessGetPid(s.cstr())`
		// both COMPILE CLEAN under shv2 and are refused by the bootstrap. They are disabled at that file with
		// this arm named as the reason; the fourth, `subprocess-resolve-on-path-cstring`, passes.
		if self.at(TokenKind.cstring) 'cstringPointer'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.cstring)
			return MaxonType.integer
		end 'cstringPointer'
		let tok = self.current()
		throw ParseError.unexpectedToken("a type", got: tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'parseTypeReference'

	// The type naming the declaration currently being read, for `Self` and for its own name.
	// `anchor` is only the token a diagnostic points at.
	//
	// ⭐ **A STRUCT'S IS `structRef`, AN ENUM'S IS `named` — the SAME split `TypeResolution` makes** (a
	// declared `type` keeps its nominal id, a declared `enum`/`union` erases to `integer`, because an enum
	// value IS its tag or its box pointer and nothing downstream needs to know which enum it was). The two
	// must agree: a `named` receiver is what `dispatchMethodOnBinding` routes an enum method call on and
	// what `parseMatchCommon` recognizes a `match self` scrutinee by, while a `structRef` one would be sent
	// to `requireStructBase` to resolve a `StructLayout` no enum declares.
	//
	// The kind is read off `enclosingTypeIsEnum` rather than the signature index for that field's stated
	// reason: this routine answers in the tolerant SWEEP too, where the enum being declared is not folded.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A BYTE RECORD IS NEITHER, AND ASKING FIRST IS THE WHOLE POINT (W17).** The corpus's `type String`
	// and `type Character` declare the two types the compiler already owns, so `Self` — and the implicit
	// receiver every instance method of those bodies carries — is the BUILTIN, not a struct that happens to
	// bear its name. `parseTypeReference` settles the written spelling `String` that way one routine over
	// (`byteRecordTagOfTypeName`, which this asks so the two cannot answer one name two ways); without this
	// arm `self` reached a `s String` parameter as a `structRef` and was refused against its own type. The
	// table carries the measurement.
	function enclosingSelfType(anchor Token) returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		let name = try self.requireEnclosingTypeName(anchor)

		// ⭐⭐ **A PRIMITIVE IS THE THIRD KIND OF BUILTIN VALUE TYPE `Self` CAN NAME (W133), AND IT JOINS THE
		// BYTE RECORDS' ARM RATHER THAN GETTING ONE OF ITS OWN.** `extension int` enters this body with
		// `enclosingType` = `int`, so `equals(other Self)`, `compare(other Self)` and `clone() returns Self` all
		// resolve through here — and the `structRef` below would hand every one of them a reference to a type no
		// registry holds. That is the defect the self-hosted compiler recorded and fixed
		// (`maxon-selfhosted/ROADMAP.md:472`), one door over: there `internMaxonType`'s primitive mapping
		// answered `MaxonType.named(…)`, so an `extension float` body declared its `__self` as a NAMED Float
		// rather than the float primitive and `"{self}"` interpolation broke.
		//
		// The two tables stay APART — four other doors need "is this name a BYTE RECORD?" and must not start
		// admitting `int` — while the QUESTION is asked once, because what this reader wants is neither table
		// but their union: *"does this name denote a builtin value type, and which tag?"*
		let byteRecord = byteRecordTagOfTypeName(name)
		let builtinValueTag = primitiveTagOfTypeName(name) if byteRecord == ValueTypeTag.unresolved else byteRecord
		if builtinValueTag != ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'theBuiltinValueType'
			return maxonTypeOfTag(builtinValueTag, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		end 'theBuiltinValueType'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERIC TWIN OF THE ARM ABOVE (W57): `Self` INSIDE `type Array` IS THE INSTANCE.** A
		// non-generic byte record answers with its TAG for the reason `ProgramSignatures.ownRecordSelfInstance`
		// states in full — a value of it is the compiler's own record, not a box built from declared fields —
		// and `Array` is that same fact with a type parameter on it. The base `structRef` below is right only
		// for a declaration that HAS a base box; `Array with T` is its `__ManagedMemory with T`, so a
		// `structRef(Array)` would give `clone()`'s result a struct cascade over the buffer record.
		// ⚠ **AND THE SIZED CONTAINER IS THE SAME FACT WITH A SECOND COORDINATE ON IT (W189).** `Self` inside
		// `stdlib/Vector.maxon` is the declaration's own instance for exactly the reason above — a `Vector`'s
		// value IS the compiler's fused record — and answering `structRef(Vector)` gave `init`'s
		// `return Self{managed: managed}` **`E3005 Cannot return 'Vector_T…' from function declared to return
		// 'Vector'`**: the literal produced the instance, the declared return produced the base box, and the two
		// are the same type under two spellings. The countless instance it answers with is what
		// `substitutedFixedSize` then gives the receiver's SIZE at a call.
		if self.signatures.declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord(name) 'theCompilersOwnGenericRecord'
			return MaxonType.genericInstance(self.signatures.ownRecordSelfInstance(name))
		end 'theCompilersOwnGenericRecord'

		let id = self.artifact.typeNames.intern(name)
		return MaxonType.named(id) if self.enclosingTypeIsEnum else MaxonType.structRef(id)
	end 'enclosingSelfType'

	// **THE NAME `Self` DENOTES** — the enclosing declaration's own. One reader for both positions the
	// keyword stands in: the TYPE position above, and the EXPRESSION position (`typeBaseTokenAt`, D9). They
	// must not disagree, since `returns Self` and `Self.create(…)` in one body name one type.
	// `anchor` is only the token a diagnostic points at.
	//
	// A `Self` at FILE SCOPE has no type to refer to. It is a positioned `unsupported` rather than a
	// panic because the source can perfectly well write one, and it is not `unexpectedToken` because
	// `Self` is a type reference — it simply names nothing here.
	function requireEnclosingTypeName(anchor Token) returns ByteArray throws ParseError
		if self.enclosingType.isEmpty() 'outsideAType'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("'{anchor.kind.rawValue}' outside a type declaration (`Self` names the type whose body encloses it, and there is none here)", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'outsideAType'
		return self.enclosingType
	end 'requireEnclosingTypeName'

	// Does `name` name the type currently being declared? False at file scope, where there is no
	// enclosing type for any name to be — which is what makes the `isEmpty` test load-bearing
	// rather than an optimization: without it a file-scope `EmptySourceText` would match nothing
	// anyway, but only by accident of no identifier being empty.
	function namesEnclosingType(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return not self.enclosingType.isEmpty() and self.enclosingType.equals(name)
	end 'namesEnclosingType'

	// The number of hidden witness parameters a function of the enclosing type carries (P1.7a slice 2a) — one
	// per `where` constraint of a constrained generic type, for every method that has somewhere to source them
	// FROM. The reservation is uniform (no per-method need analysis), so this is a pure function of the
	// declaration's shape and the type's constraint count.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND A `Self`-RETURNING STATIC HAS SOMEWHERE, EXACTLY AS IT DOES FOR THE LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR (W43b).**
	// A static has no `self`, which is why the block used to be gated on `takesReceiver` alone — but a static
	// that returns the enclosing type builds the concrete instance, and the parser retypes its RESULT to that
	// instance (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`), so the call site can materialize each witness table from
	// the result's type argument. It is the SAME source `materializeStaticConstructorLayout` reads the
	// descriptor off, and the two are now reserved on the same terms rather than one of them alone.
	// **MEASURED before this: `panic at LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1411: forwardCallerWitness: caller 'Map.init'
	// carries 0 witness parameter(s) and was asked to forward slot 0 to 'Map.upsert'`** — `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s
	// static `init`, which builds a `Self{…}` and calls a key-hashing method on it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SUPPLY SIDE IS `LowerMaxonToStd.witnessParamCountForCallee`, AND BOTH ASK ONE DOOR**
	// (`ProgramSignatures.calleeReturnsItsDeclaringType`). A witness-slot count is ABI: a reservation and a
	// supply that disagree is an argument-register mismatch nothing downstream can notice
	// (`witnessConstraintsOfMethod`'s header records what two such disagreements cost), so the `Self`-return
	// test is asked in ONE place off the whole-program declared return type rather than spelled on each side.
	function witnessParamCountFor(takesReceiver bool, registrationName ByteArray) returns ParamCount
		if self.enclosingWhereConstraints.isEmpty() 'unconstrainedType'
			return 0
		end 'unconstrainedType'
		if not takesReceiver and not self.signatures.calleeReturnsItsDeclaringType(registrationName) 'noWitnessSource'
			return 0
		end 'noWitnessSource'
		return self.enclosingWhereConstraints.count()
	end 'witnessParamCountFor'

	// The POSITION of `name` in the enclosing type's `uses` list (P1.6-A), or `count()` when it is not
	// a type parameter — the door `parseTypeReference` recognizes a bare `T` through. ⚠ Since W14 the
	// answer is spent there as a MEMBERSHIP test and nothing more: what the arm mints is
	// `typeParamTokenFor(enclosingType, paramName: name)`, an identity, not this index. It is still the
	// position `readWhereClause` records a constraint against, which is why it survives as a position.
	// A linear scan, for `StructLayout.indexOfField`'s reason: a type
	// declares a handful of parameters, so a per-parse name→index map would be a heap object bought to
	// serve two or three lookups.
	function typeParamPositionOf(name ByteArray) returns TypeParamPosition
		for i in 0 upto self.enclosingTypeParams.count() 'each'
			let paramName = try self.enclosingTypeParams.get(i) otherwise panic("typeParamPositionOf: enclosingTypeParams.get OOB at {i}")
			if paramName.equals(name) 'hit'
				return i
			end 'hit'
		end 'each'
		return self.enclosingTypeParams.count()
	end 'typeParamPositionOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE INSTANCE A BARE GENERIC TYPE NAME DENOTES AT THE CURSOR, or `none`** — `Inner` written
	// inside `type Outer uses T` means `Inner with Outer.T`, at a TYPE reference (`returns Inner`,
	// `var v as Inner`, a `with` ARGUMENT), at a STATIC CALL base (`Inner.make(items)`), and — through the
	// first of those — in an `implements … with (…)` clause. All of them used to read the name as a plain
	// `named("Inner")`, the base struct's DECLARATION view: a type whose parameters nothing had bound, so
	// `Inner.make`'s `Array with Inner.T` parameter and the caller's `Array with Outer.T` argument were two
	// instances with no relation and the legal program was **`E3005: expected 'Inner.TArr', got 'Outer.OArr'`**.
	//
	// The RULE is `ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericInstanceOf`'s, shared with the two read doors that ask it of
	// a different scope; what this adds is only which scope the CURSOR is in, and the `ConstructorInstance` the
	// call door speaks in.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CALLER GATES ON `enclosingTypeParams` BEING NON-EMPTY**, and that gate is why this may be asked
	// at every type annotation in the program: a union return is heap-boxed even for its payload-free arm
	// (see `parseTypeReference`'s `isInterfaceName` note, measured at +24,396 allocations), so the whole
	// generics-free corpus — and every non-generic body in a generic program — must not reach the call at all.
	function bareGenericInstanceInScope(name ByteArray) returns ConstructorInstance
		match self.signatures.scopedGenericInstanceOf(name, declaringTypeName: self.enclosingType, scopeParamNames: self.enclosingTypeParams) 'scoped'
			notInScope then return ConstructorInstance.none
			instance(gid) then return ConstructorInstance.instance(gid)
		end 'scoped'
	end 'bareGenericInstanceInScope'

	// ⭐⭐ **A CALL RESULT NAMING A BARE GENERIC TYPE IS READ IN THE CALLEE'S OWN SCOPE** — the READ-DOOR
	// repair of a swept return type, and the reason the type-reference arm alone is not enough.
	//
	// ⚠ **`mintCallResult` TYPES A CALL FROM THE TOKEN SWEEP — `overloadedDeclsOf` where the name is overloaded
	// and its declarations disagree, `returnTypeOf` otherwise — NEVER FROM THE REAL PARSE**, and
	// the sweep answers every bare generic name as a plain `named` — deliberately, on the `allFilesFolded`
	// gate `parseTypeReference` argues for at length, because a registry answer during the sweep is a
	// property of the loader's walk order. So `MyMap.createIterator() returns MyMapIterator` reaches its CALL
	// SITE as `MyMapIterator`, the base, and everything the receiver's instance could have fixed about it is
	// already gone. That is exactly the split v1 pays for with a second copy of the algorithm.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SCOPE IS THE CALLEE'S DECLARING TYPE, NOT THE CALLER'S** — `MyMapIterator` written in `MyMap`'s
	// body means `MyMapIterator with (MyMap.Key, MyMap.Value)` whoever is calling, and the caller's own
	// parameters have nothing to do with it. `retypeInstanceMethodResult` then fixes THAT with the receiver's
	// concrete instance, which is the second half and a different question.
	function promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope(result ValueId, receiver ValueId)
		let resultTag = self.tagOf(result)
		if resultTag != ValueTypeTag.named and not tagIsStructRef(resultTag) 'namesNoDeclaredType'
			return
		end 'namesNoDeclaredType'

		var declaringType = EmptySourceText
		match self.methodSurfaceOf(receiver) 'receiverSurface'
			// No method surface at all — nothing was dispatched, so there is no callee scope to read in.
			none then return
			// ⚠ **A DISPATCHED RECEIVER HAS NO CALLEE SCOPE, AND THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS HAVING NONE.** Its
			// result was typed off the INTERFACE REQUIREMENT (`mintWitnessDispatchResult`), which is already
			// concrete — there is no shared generic body whose parameters this could bind, and an interface's
			// name is not a struct any type-parameter registry answers for.
			witnessDispatched then return
			declared(typeName, _) then declaringType = typeName
		end 'receiverSurface'

		// ⚠ **THE COST GATE, and it is the same one `ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf` spells for its own door**:
		// this runs at every method call in the program whose result names a declared type, and the answer is a
		// UNION Maxon heap-boxes even for its payload-free arm. A NON-GENERIC declaring type binds nothing, so
		// every call in the generics-free corpus leaves here rather than allocating to be told so.
		let scopeParamNames = self.signatures.structTypeParamsOf(declaringType)
		if scopeParamNames.isEmpty() 'calleeDeclaresNoTypeParameters'
			return
		end 'calleeDeclaresNoTypeParameters'

		match self.signatures.scopedGenericInstanceOf(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(result)), declaringTypeName: declaringType, scopeParamNames: scopeParamNames) 'scoped'
			notInScope then break 'scoped'
			instance(gid) then self.retypeValue(result, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(gid))
		end 'scoped'
	end 'promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE WRITTEN NAME `name` DENOTE AN INNER `typealias` OF THE BODY BEING PARSED — the ONE home
	// of that question (S2i review).** THREE doors ask it and they must not answer one written name with two
	// different types: `parseTypeReference`'s inner-alias arm (a field type, a return clause), the STRUCT
	// LITERAL door (`parseStructLiteral`) and the STATIC CALL door (`parseQualifiedCall`).
	//
	// ⛔ **THE ENCLOSING TYPE'S OWN NAME AND ITS TYPE PARAMETERS BOTH WIN OVER AN ALIAS SHADOWING THEM**, and
	// that is `parseTypeReference`'s ARM ORDER read out as a predicate rather than a precedence chosen here:
	// that routine answers the type's own name (`enclosingSelfType`) and then a bare `T`
	// (`typeParameter(token)`) BEFORE it consults `enclosingInnerAliases`. So `type Holder uses Element`
	// declaring `typealias Element = Array with ExitCode` means the PARAMETER at `var items as Element`, and
	// it has to mean the parameter at `Element{}` and `Element.create()` too.
	//
	// MEASURED with the two value doors deciding for themselves: `Element{}` built an ARRAY and its record
	// was accepted into a `typeParameter` field, so one declaration denoted two types in one body and the
	// only complaint came at the READ (`self.items.count()` refused for a method the parameter's constraints
	// do not provide — a diagnostic about the slot, not about the store). Neither reference compiler offers
	// an acceptance to match: the bootstrap CRASHES on the shadowed literal (an unhandled
	// `IrTypeParameterType`→`IrStructType` cast inside `ParseStructLiteral`, reported as `E9001`), and for
	// the type parameter it does NOT crash on — a plain `T{}` with no alias in sight — it answers the same
	// E3076 shv2 answers, which is what this predicate hands the shadowed spelling back to.
	//
	// ⚠ At `parseTypeReference` the first two tests are already settled by the arms above it and are asked
	// again only so that the three doors share ONE predicate: a fourth clause added here reaches every door
	// that resolves a written name, which is exactly what the arm order could not guarantee.
	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS WRITTEN SPELLING DENOTE THE `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER? — THE LITERAL NAME, A FILE-SCOPE
	// ALIAS, *OR* AN INNER ONE.** The third is what `stdlib/Character.maxon:172` needs: its
	// `typealias ByteMemory = __ManagedMemory with Byte` is declared INSIDE `type Character`, so the registry
	// holds it under `Character.ByteMemory` while the call site writes the bare `ByteMemory`, and a lookup of
	// the bare name misses. `ByteMemory.create(1, 1)` then routed to `parseArrayStaticCall` and met
	// `E2010 Expected ')' but got '1'` against `Array.create()`'s no arguments.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT QUALIFIES THE SAME WAY `parseTypeReference` DOES, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT** — one
	// construction of an inner alias's registry key (`joinQualifiedName(enclosingType, member:)`), gated by
	// the one predicate that says a bare name IS an inner alias here. A second spelling of that join is how a
	// type position and a call position come to disagree about what one written word denotes.
	// ⚠ **THE INNER-ALIAS PROBE IS GATED ON `enclosingType` BEING NON-EMPTY, NOT ON `namesInnerAliasHere`.**
	// The bare probe comes first because a file-scope alias is the common case; the join is skipped at file
	// scope, where `enclosingType` is empty and the key it would build cannot exist. `namesInnerAliasHere`
	// would be the stricter test and is the wrong one HERE: it consults the body walk's live alias set, which
	// a DECLARATION position (a parameter, a return clause) is read outside of.
	// ⚠⚠ **THE ALIAS REGISTRIES ARE READ IN THE REAL PARSE ONLY, ON `allFilesFolded` (W49) — the gate
	// `parseTypeReference`'s own alias arms sit behind, arriving here for the same two reasons.**
	//   1. **IT WAS A PROPERTY OF THE DISK.** Both sets are written by `recordGenericAlias`, which `foldFile`
	//      calls when a file's token walk ENDS — so during the sweep an alias declared in the file being swept
	//      always read as absent while one declared in a file the walk reached EARLIER did not, and the SWEEP's
	//      answer is the one the whole-program column stores.
	//   2. **AND THE STORED ANSWER IS NOW REDUNDANT FOR AN ALIAS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE GATE FREE.** A2m's
	//      warning against suppressing the root here was measured BEFORE A3e gated `parseTypeReference` on
	//      `allFilesFolded`; since that gate, a swept alias-typed position keeps its bare `named` in EVERY file
	//      order, so `ProgramSignatures.derivedAliasSurfaceBits` answers for it at the read door in every
	//      order. What the sweep stores for an alias could therefore only ever AGREE with the derivation or be
	//      WRONG — and it was wrong, because it read a whole-program set for a FILE-SCOPED name.
	// ⇒ The sweep answers from the TOKENS ALONE (the reserved spelling, which is a token fact and needs no
	// registry), and the doors only the real parse reaches — a PARAMETER, a static call — ask the registries
	// where they are complete and can be scoped.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE READ IS SCOPED TO THIS FILE.** A plain `typealias` is file-local, so two files may each
	// declare `B` and mean different things; these sets are keyed by the bare name with no file in the key.
	// MEASURED in both file orders: with `typealias B = __ManagedMemory with Byte` in one file and
	// `typealias B = Array with Int` in another, the second file's own `B` wore the BUFFER's surface — its
	// parameter refused `count`, and its `B.create()` routed to the buffer's two-argument static.
	function spellingDenotesBufferSurface(spelling ByteArray) returns bool
		if spelling.equals(ManagedMemoryTypeName) 'spelledTheReservedNameItself'
			return true
		end 'spelledTheReservedNameItself'

		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'sweepAnswersFromTokensAlone'
			return false
		end 'sweepAnswersFromTokensAlone'

		if self.signatures.aliasDenotesBufferSurface(spelling, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'aFileScopeAlias'
			return true
		end 'aFileScopeAlias'

		return not self.enclosingType.isEmpty() and self.signatures.aliasDenotesBufferSurface(joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: spelling), readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'spellingDenotesBufferSurface'

	function namesInnerAliasHere(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if self.namesEnclosingType(name) 'ownName'
			return false
		end 'ownName'

		if self.typeParamPositionOf(name) < self.enclosingTypeParams.count() 'typeParam'
			return false
		end 'typeParam'

		// ⭐⭐ **TWO POSITIONS ANSWER FROM THE WHOLE-PROGRAM REGISTRIES INSTEAD OF FROM THIS BODY'S OWN WALK, AND
		// THEY ARE ONE MECHANISM (F10).** Both ask the identical `<Type>.<member>` question, so they widen
		// nothing: `declaresInnerAlias` is keyed by the enclosing type, and it answers about exactly the
		// declarations a body walk would have recorded.
		//   • A drained DEFAULT expression — the set below no longer exists, because the body walk that built it
		//     closed with its declaration (`restoreDeclaringTypeScope`).
		//   • An EXTENSION body — the set below holds only what THIS body declared, and a nested `typealias` is a
		//     MEMBER of the extended type, so every `extension` over that type may spell it whatever file it
		//     lives in. `stdlib/helpers/sort/` is the case: six files write `export extension Array`,
		//     `insertionSort.maxon:14` declares `SortIndex` and the other five spell it bare.
		//
		// ⛔ **BOTH REFERENCE COMPILERS MAKE THESE NAMES FLAT AND PROGRAM-WIDE, AND shv2 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.**
		// The bootstrap writes an extension's `typealias` into `module.TypeDefs` under its BARE name
		// (`0-Compiler.cs:1199`, `2-Parser.cs:5325` keeping an `OwnerTypeName` it never puts in a key), so
		// `SortIndex` is nameable from any file at file scope; v1 stores it on `Array.innerAliases` but then
		// keeps three bare-name global fallbacks over it (`Project.structInnerAliasNames`,
		// `functionInnerAliasCache`, `TypeResolution.tryAnyStructInnerAlias`, whose own comment records the
		// assumption *"inner-alias names are unique across the stdlib … no ambiguity"*). Neither can diagnose
		// two types declaring one member name. shv2 keys `<Type>.<member>` for exactly that reason
		// (`qualifiedInnerGenericAlias`), and the whole of this widening is the ENCLOSING TYPE's own members —
		// so `Array.SortIndex` reaches an `extension Array` in any file and reaches nothing else anywhere.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TYPE'S OWN BODY IS NOT GIVEN THIS, AND THAT IS THE PASS STRUCTURE RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.**
		// A `type` body's signatures are read during the per-file declaration sweep, which runs BEFORE
		// `Queries.foldExtensionDeclarations` publishes any extension's members — so a `type Foo` body granted
		// the probe would resolve a written name one way in the sweep and another in the real parse, which is
		// one declaration recorded under two spellings. An extension body is read only by the two passes that
		// both run after the pre-pass, so for it the two readings agree by construction.
		if self.innerAliasesFromIndex and self.signatures.declaresInnerAlias(joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: name)) 'aMemberDeclaredElsewhere'
			return true
		end 'aMemberDeclaredElsewhere'

		return self.enclosingInnerAliases.contains(name)
	end 'namesInnerAliasHere'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE-PROGRAM `genericAliases` KEY A WRITTEN BASE NAME DENOTES — the one home of "which
	// registration is this spelling?" for the static-call doors (W7).** A file-scope generic alias is keyed
	// under its own name (`IntBox`); an INNER one under the enclosing type's, base-qualified
	// (`Plain.IntPair`) — `recordScannedType` and `foldExtensionDeclarationInto` both re-key it through
	// `qualifiedInnerGenericAlias`, because a bare member collides with another type's same-named alias.
	// A caller here holds the name the AUTHOR wrote, which is the bare member for both.
	//
	// ⛔ **ASKING THE BARE NAME WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, NOT A REFUSAL.** `parseQualifiedCall` makes four
	// alias-driven decisions and every one of them missed for an inner alias over a USER generic base, so
	// `IntPair.create(9)` mangled onto a callee no file declares and `mintCallResult` typed the value
	// `unresolved`. The program was then refused for a field access on `unknown` — one door past E3076's own
	// advice to write exactly that call, which is why `parseInnerAliasLiteral` had recorded the failure in
	// prose while a comment two arms below it called the same cure "real". MEASURED: the bootstrap compiles
	// and runs that program (exit 9).
	//
	// A name that is not an inner alias here, and one the declaration sweep did not key, are both handed back
	// unchanged — the callers' file-scope reading, and the tolerant sweep's `no` for every name.
	function genericAliasKeyFor(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if not self.namesInnerAliasHere(name) 'notInnerAlias'
			return name
		end 'notInnerAlias'

		let qualified = joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: name)
		if not self.signatures.isGenericAlias(qualified) 'notKeyedWholeProgram'
			return name
		end 'notKeyedWholeProgram'

		return qualified
	end 'genericAliasKeyFor'

	// ⭐ **RESOLVE A `named` STRUCT REFERENCE TO `structRef` — the parse-time half of what `resolveTypes`
	// does to a signature.** `parseTypeReference` mints `structRef` SYNTACTICALLY for `Self` and the
	// enclosing type's own name (they need no registry), but leaves every OTHER type name `named`,
	// because it runs in the tolerant declaration sweep too — where "is `Vec2` a struct?" has no answer.
	// This runs in the REAL parse ONLY (`self.signatures` is the complete whole-program index), so it CAN
	// ask, and it re-tags a `named` that denotes a declared `type` as `structRef` — which is what makes a
	// struct PARAMETER (`a Vec2`) a struct base for `a.x`, a struct RETURN (`returns Pair`) agree with the
	// `structRef` a factory hands back, and a struct CALL RESULT (`let p = makePair()`) bind a struct.
	//
	// A `named` that denotes a UNION/ENUM stays `named` — a union value carries the `named` tag
	// (`enumMatchFor` reads it) — and a ranged int ALIAS stays `named` (it IS the integer). `structRef`
	// (a `Self`/own-name reference) and every non-`named` type pass through untouched, so this is
	// idempotent and safe to fold over an already-partly-resolved list. `resolveTypes`'s `structRef` arm
	// then finds the signature already resolved and leaves it, so the two cannot drift.
	//
	// The `containsStruct` guard, not `structOf`, for the reason its header states: this is asked of
	// EVERY `named` parameter and return type, most of them int aliases, and `structOf` would box a
	// throwaway `DeclaredStruct.undeclared` for each.
	function resolveNamedStruct(t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return t
		end 'notNamed'

		let id = typeNameIdOf(t)
		let name = try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("resolveNamedStruct: a `named` type's id is not in this file's interner — `parseTypeReference` interned it from a name in the same table")
		if not self.signatures.containsStruct(name) 'notStruct'
			return t
		end 'notStruct'

		return MaxonType.structRef(id)
	end 'resolveNamedStruct'

	// ⭐ **RESOLVE A `named` FUNCTION-ALIAS REFERENCE TO `function`** (P1.5-A1) — the read-side twin of
	// `resolveNamedStruct`, for the types the WHOLE-PROGRAM signature index hands back as `named` because
	// the SWEEP that recorded them ran before the function alias was known: a call RESULT
	// (`returnTypeOf` → `let f = pickDouble()`) and a struct-FIELD read (`fieldTypeOf` → `let f = h.op`).
	// `parseTypeReference` already re-tags a PARAMETER/return/annotation directly (the sweep is complete
	// by the real parse), so this catches only the sweep-sourced types. A `named` that is neither a function
	// alias (`function`) NOR a generic-instance alias (`genericInstance(gid)`, P1.6-B2 — `returns StrBox`, so
	// `let b = make()` binds a real generic instance with its type-arg managed drop, not an opaque `named` a
	// field access rejects) NOR a TUPLE alias (`structRef`, A3e), and every non-`named` type, pass through
	// untouched. The SWEEP records a return type before the index knows its aliases, so all three arrive here
	// `named`; a name is a struct, a float alias, a function alias, a generic alias, a tuple alias or a ranged
	// int — NEVER two — so ONE `nameOf` decides which, idempotent and foldable alongside
	// `resolveNamedStruct`/`resolveFloatAliasType`.
	function resolveNamedAlias(t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return t
		end 'notNamed'

		let id = typeNameIdOf(t)
		let name = try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("resolveNamedAlias: a `named` type's id is not in this file's interner — `parseTypeReference`/`fieldTypeOf` interned it from a name in the same table")
		if self.signatures.isFunctionAlias(name) 'fa'
			return MaxonType.function(id)
		end 'fa'
		if self.signatures.isGenericAlias(name) 'genericAlias'
			return self.genericAliasDenotedType(name)
		end 'genericAlias'
		// ⭐ A TUPLE-ALIAS return (`returns Pair`) — the `named` the sweep recorded resolved to the tuple's own
		// CANONICAL `structRef`, so `let t = make()` binds a struct the caller adopts and `t.0` resolves.
		// ⚠ MEASURED, A3e: without it the very same program was `E3011 Unknown type 'Pair'` whenever the walk
		// reached the alias's file after the caller's, and ran when it reached it first.
		if self.signatures.isTupleAlias(name) 'tupleAlias'
			return MaxonType.structRef(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.signatures.tupleAliasTarget(name)))
		end 'tupleAlias'
		return t
	end 'resolveNamedAlias'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A GENERIC-ALIAS NAME DENOTES IN A TYPE POSITION** — the instance it was bound to, resolved
	// through the one door that decides whether that instance is a BOX or an EXISTENTIAL
	// (`instanceDenotedType`). Three type-resolution arms spelled this same two-call join — the inner-alias
	// arm, the file-scope alias arm and the sweep repair — which is three chances for one of them to keep
	// reading a `genericInstance` after the split moved.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `isGenericAlias` GATE STAYS AT EACH ARM RATHER THAN MOVING IN HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED
	// CHOICE.** This runs on the path `parseTypeReference` takes for every type annotation in the program; a
	// door answering "or nothing" would return a union, and a union return is heap-boxed even for its miss
	// case — the same +24,396 allocations at rung 5 that `isInterfaceName` is spelled the narrow way for, one
	// arm above. The gate is a bool and costs nothing; only the JOIN is shared.
	function genericAliasDenotedType(name ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		return self.signatures.instanceDenotedType(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath), into: self.artifact.typeNames)
	end 'genericAliasDenotedType'

	// `resolveNamedStruct` folded over a parameter-type list — the whole struct-`named` → `structRef`
	// rewrite for one function's parameters, done ONCE after they are parsed so every downstream reader
	// (the value-tag seeding, the binding types, the recorded signature) sees the same resolved list.
	function resolveNamedStructAll(types MaxonTypeArray) returns MaxonTypeArray
		var resolved = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for t in types 'each'
			resolved.push(self.resolveNamedStruct(t))
		end 'each'
		return resolved
	end 'resolveNamedStructAll'

	// `resolveNamedStruct` for a return type: a `returns Pair` resolves its inner struct-`named` to
	// `structRef` so `parseReturnStatement`'s declared-type check agrees with the `structRef` a factory's
	// `return Self{…}` hands back, and so the recorded signature matches the call site's adopted result.
	function resolveNamedStructReturn(rt MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonReturnType
		return match rt 'r'
			void gives rt
			value(inner) gives MaxonReturnType.value(self.resolveNamedStruct(inner))
		end 'r'
	end 'resolveNamedStructReturn'

	// Does `returnType` name the ENCLOSING type — `Self`, the base struct, `structRef` after
	// `resolveNamedStructReturn` (P1.7 slice 3b-i)? It is the return a receiverless layout-needing constructor
	// must have: only then does the call's RESULT get retyped to the concrete instance
	// (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`) the caller reads the layout descriptor off. Compared by interned
	// id in `self.artifact.typeNames` — the interner both the resolved return type and `self.enclosingType`
	// live in — so no `nameOf` round-trip is needed.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND A `Self` THAT RESOLVED TO AN INSTANCE ANSWERS TRUE TOO (W57).** `enclosingSelfType` gives a
	// container whose record the compiler owns the INSTANCE over its own parameters rather than the base
	// `structRef`, so `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `static function from(source) returns Self` arrived here as a
	// `genericInstance` and was told a static returning `Self` has no descriptor source — a refusal naming
	// the very cure the declaration already wrote. The question this asks is unchanged; only the two
	// spellings `Self` can now take have to be read, and an ORDINARY generic still cannot reach the second
	// one, so nothing else's answer moves.
	function returnsEnclosingType(returnType MaxonReturnType) returns bool
		return match returnType 'r'
			void gives false
			value(inner) gives self.typeIsEnclosingType(inner)
		end 'r'
	end 'returnsEnclosingType'

	// The two spellings of "this type IS the enclosing declaration", split out so the `match` above stays one
	// expression per arm. The instance half compares BASE NAMES: a `genericInstance` id lives in the
	// signature index's own space, not in `self.artifact.typeNames`, so there is no id to compare.
	function typeIsEnclosingType(t MaxonType) returns bool
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'theBaseBox'
			return typeNameIdOf(t) == self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.enclosingType)
		end 'theBaseBox'

		return tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(typeNameIdOf(t)).equals(self.enclosingType)
	end 'typeIsEnclosingType'

	// Statement list until the closing `end`, emitting into `block`. Returns the block
	// the LAST statement left control in — the same `block` for straight-line code, but
	// an `if` moves control into its continuation block, so subsequent statements (and
	// the caller) must follow it. `func` is threaded so control-flow statements can
	// create successor blocks. Blank lines separate statements.
	function parseStatements(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		var current = block
		// ⭐⭐ **E3071 LIVES HERE BECAUSE THIS IS THE ONE STATEMENT-LIST LOOP** — a function body and every
		// nested block body (`parseBlockBody`) both come through it, so the dead-code rule is stated once for
		// the whole language. See `statementEndsItsBlockAt` for the roster and `PrecedingTerminator` for why
		// the keyword's own bytes are what travels.
		var preceding = PrecedingTerminator.none
		while true 'stmts'
			self.skipNewlines()
			if self.at(TokenKind.end) 'endBlock'
				break
			end 'endBlock'
			if self.isEof() 'eof'
				let tok = self.current()
				throw ParseError.unexpectedEof(tok.line, column: tok.column)
			end 'eof'

			// A real statement follows a terminator in THIS block, so control can never reach it (E3071).
			// Asked after the `end` and EOF tests, both of which are the block ending rather than a statement:
			// a terminator as the block's LAST statement is the ordinary shape, and a truncated body is
			// `unexpectedEof`'s to report, not this one's.
			let stmtTok = self.current()
			match preceding 'dead'
				none then break 'dead'
				keyword(name) then throw ParseError.unreachableCode(String.from(name), line: stmtTok.line, column: stmtTok.column)
			end 'dead'

			// Recorded BEFORE the statement is parsed, off its opening token, so the next iteration can
			// refuse whatever follows it.
			preceding = PrecedingTerminator.keyword(stmtTok.value) if statementEndsItsBlockAt(stmtTok.kind) else PrecedingTerminator.none

			// ⭐⭐ **E3070: AN ELEMENT ACCESSOR NO NAME TOOK DIES WITH ITS STATEMENT** — the watermark is taken
			// here and the claim list is cut back to it below. See `dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows` for why this
			// loop is the only place that may do it, and what it cost when nothing did.
			let pendingBorrowMark = func.borrowFacts.pending.count()
			current = try self.parseStatement(func, block: current)
			self.dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows(func, mark: pendingBorrowMark)
		end 'stmts'
		return current
	end 'parseStatements'

	// One statement. Returns the block subsequent statements continue in.
	//
	// EVERY arm returns `self.currentBlock`, not `block`, and that is the change P1.0d.2
	// forces: a short-circuit `and`/`or` anywhere inside a statement's EXPRESSION leaves
	// control in a fresh merge block, so even a "straight-line" statement — a `let`, an
	// assignment, a bare call — can move it. Returning the incoming `block` would send the
	// next statement's ops to a block control has already left, which reads as a wild
	// miscompile (ops stranded on a dead path) rather than as a parse error.
	//
	// `break`/`continue` are the two that genuinely cannot move it: neither takes an
	// expression. They still route through the same return so there is ONE rule here rather
	// than an exception to remember.
	function parseStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block

		// The statement's first token — the anchor `drainPendingTemps` gives the drops it emits (no source
		// token spawns a drop). Captured before parsing advances the cursor.
		let stmtTok = self.current()

		// E3067, before the dispatch and before any op is emitted: this statement's TOKENS may already say
		// it does nothing. See `rejectDegenerateAssignment` for the rule and for what it deliberately lets
		// through — it is asked here, once, rather than by each of the three assignment arms it spans.
		try self.rejectDegenerateAssignment(self.pos)

		if self.at(TokenKind.return) 'ret'
			try self.parseReturnStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'ret' else if self.at(TokenKind.let) 'letDecl'
			try self.parseBinding(func, block: block, mutable: false, heapAnnotated: false)
		end 'letDecl' else if self.at(TokenKind.var) 'varDecl'
			try self.parseBinding(func, block: block, mutable: true, heapAnnotated: false)
		end 'varDecl' else if self.at(TokenKind.at) 'annotatedDecl'
			// `@heap let p = Point.create(1, y: 2)` — an ANNOTATED binding. The `@` is the only token that opens
			// one and it opens nothing else, so the arm sits beside the two bindings it decorates.
			try self.parseAnnotatedBinding(func, block: block)
		end 'annotatedDecl' else if self.at(TokenKind.if) 'ifStmt'
			return try self.parseIfStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'ifStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.while) 'whileStmt'
			return try self.parseWhileStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'whileStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.for) 'forStmt'
			return try self.parseForStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'forStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.match) 'matchStmt'
			return try self.parseMatchStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'matchStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.break) 'breakStmt'
			try self.parseBreakStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'breakStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.continue) 'continueStmt'
			try self.parseContinueStatement(block)
		end 'continueStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.throw) 'throwStmt'
			try self.parseThrowStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'throwStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.try) 'tryStmt'
			// The BLOCK form (`try 'label' … end 'label' otherwise (e) 'h' … end 'h'`) is a statement and
			// nothing else — it groups statements and yields no value — so it forks HERE, off the same
			// label lookahead the extent scan reads (`tryOpensBlockAt`, which is also where the label-vs-
			// character-receiver rule lives). `parseTry` keeps its own refusal of the form for the EXPRESSION
			// position that still cannot take it.
			if self.tryOpensBlockAt(self.pos) 'blockForm'
				return try self.parseTryBlockStatement(func, block: block)
			end 'blockForm'

			// A `try` in statement position — its value is discarded; the forms are the propagate,
			// the control-flow `otherwise`s, `otherwise ignore`, and the block handlers.
			_ = try self.parseTry(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'tryStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.panic) 'panicStmt'
			try self.parsePanicStatement(func, block: block)
		end 'panicStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.await) 'awaitStmt'
			// ⭐ **`await p` ON A LINE OF ITS OWN** — a VOID async awaited for its EFFECT. The await IS the
			// synchronization point and there is nothing to bind: a void async's await yields a void-tagged
			// value, so no binding and no expression position can host it. That is the same "this is the
			// position that wants no value" argument every bare-call arm above makes, and it is the reason a
			// void `async` was spawnable but not awaitable — `async-await.void` and `async-await.nested-void`
			// died here as `E2001: unexpected token: 'await'` while the spawn one line above them compiled.
			//
			// A NON-void result written here is discarded like any other bare call's, and the statement's
			// `drainPendingTemps` tail releases it if it owns heap. `try await p otherwise …` is NOT this arm:
			// it opens with `try` and `tryStmt` above claims it.
			_ = try self.parseAwait(func)
		end 'awaitStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.otherwise) 'strayOtherwise'
			// An `otherwise` with no `try` to attach to (`let v = f() otherwise 0`). Reaches here as a
			// statement starter after the preceding expression completed. Renders as E3058.
			let otherwiseTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.otherwiseWithoutTry(otherwiseTok.line, column: otherwiseTok.column)
		end 'strayOtherwise' else if self.tupleAssignEqualsAt(self.pos) != self.pos 'tupleAssign'
			// `(x, let y) = f()` — a statement-level TUPLE ASSIGNMENT (`specs/tuple-assign.md`): one
			// right-hand side, evaluated once, distributed positionally over targets that rebind, declare or
			// discard. It is the only statement in the grammar that opens with `(`, and the predicate — not
			// this arm — is where that claim is stated and defended. See `tupleAssignEqualsAt`.
			try self.parseTupleAssignment(func, block: block)
		end 'tupleAssign' else if self.assignsAt(self.pos) 'assign'
			// An identifier followed by `=` is a reassignment. The two-token lookahead
			// keeps a bare identifier from being mis-parsed as an incomplete assignment.
			// Through `assignsAt`, which is also what decides a loop's carried vars — see there.
			try self.parseAssignment(func, block: block)
		end 'assign' else if self.selfFieldAssignsAt(self.pos) 'selfFieldAssign'
			// `self.count = 5` — the explicit spelling of a self-field store. Its own predicate for
			// `fieldAssignsAt`'s reason: it assigns no BINDING, so `assignedBindingsIn` must not see it.
			// (The BARE spelling, `count = 5`, matches `assignsAt` and is routed above — see there
			// for why the scan finding `count` is harmless.)
			try self.parseExplicitSelfFieldAssignment(func, block: block)
		end 'selfFieldAssign' else if self.staticMemberAssignsAt(self.pos) 'staticMemberAssign'
			// `Counter.count = 42` — a store to a static member's module storage (W65). Its own arm, ahead of
			// `fieldAssignsAt`, whose token shape it shares EXACTLY: what tells the two apart is that this base
			// names a TYPE and that one a VALUE, which is the same separation `methodCallsAt` draws between
			// `c.increment()` and `Point.create()`. Left to the field arm it reaches `requireStructBase` and is
			// refused as a chain through a binding nothing declared.
			//
			// `assignedBindingsIn` reads only `assignsAt`, and must not see this one for `fieldAssignsAt`'s
			// reason: it assigns no BINDING, so a loop writing a static must mint no header phi for it — every
			// read of the slot is a fresh `loadIndirect` (`emitGlobalLoad`), exactly as for a file-scope `var`.
			try self.parseStaticMemberAssignment(func, block: block)
		end 'staticMemberAssign' else if self.fieldAssignsAt(self.pos) 'fieldAssign'
			// `p.x = 5` — a store THROUGH a binding rather than a rebinding of one. Its own
			// predicate, and `assignedBindingsIn` reads only `assignsAt`: see `assignsAt` for why
			// widening that one instead would have minted the loop's phi for the FIELD's name.
			try self.parseFieldAssignment(func, block: block)
		end 'fieldAssign' else if self.methodCallsAt(self.pos) or self.arrayManagedMemberCallAt(self.pos) 'methodCallStmt'
			// `c.increment()` / `self.increment()` — a method call for its EFFECT. Void methods are
			// most of what a type declares, and this is the only position that wants no value, so
			// `resultUsed: false` is what makes them callable at all.
			//
			// A FUNCTION-TYPED FIELD reached the same way (`h.op()`, `self.op()`) is NOT a method (#97):
			// `dispatchMethodOnBase` LOADS the field (a `function` value) and leaves the trailing `(args)` for the
			// postfix loop, exactly as it does in expression position. `parsePostfix` applies it as an INDIRECT call
			// (the #68/#78 lowering), so the trailing `(` is not left to be misread as a `( statement` on the next
			// iteration. A real method call consumes its own `(args)`, so `parsePostfix` then sees no trailing `(`
			// and is a no-op — the emitted IR of every existing method-call statement is unchanged.
			_ = try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parseMethodCall(func, resultUsed: false), resultUsed: false)
		end 'methodCallStmt' else if self.fieldChainMethodCallAt(self.pos) 'fieldChainMethodStmt'
			// `b.ops.push(1)` / `o.inner.op()` — a call reached through a ≥2-hop field chain, on an ARRAY FIELD's
			// method or a FUNCTION-typed FIELD (#97). `methodCallsAt` covers only the one-hop `b.push(…)` / `h.op()`;
			// this is the chain case, dispatched for effect (`resultUsed: false`). A function field's trailing
			// `(args)` goes through `parsePostfix` exactly as the one-hop `methodCallStmt` above.
			_ = try self.parseFieldChainCall(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'fieldChainMethodStmt' else if self.staticMemberCallsAt(self.pos) 'staticMemberCallStmt'
			// `Names.items.push(9)` — a method called for its EFFECT on a static member's record (W65). The
			// expression door reaches the same reader through `parseDottedPrimary`'s static arm; a statement
			// has no arm that claims a dotted form, so it needs one of its own — exactly as
			// `methodCallStmt` and `fieldChainMethodStmt` each do. Without it the canonical spelling of the
			// only mutation a managed static supports died as `E2015: Unsupported: identifier statement`.
			_ = try self.parseStaticMemberAccess(func, baseTok: try self.typeBaseTokenAt(self.pos), resultUsed: false)
		end 'staticMemberCallStmt' else if self.voidStaticCallsAt(self.pos) 'voidStaticStmt'
			// ⭐ `__Builtins.sleep(ms)` / `__ManagedFile.statFree(buffer)` — A VOID COMPILER-OWNED STATIC
			// CALLED FOR ITS EFFECT, and `statFree` is the case that makes the position load-bearing rather
			// than a convenience: it is the ONLY thing that can release the raw block `stat` hands back, and
			// it is void and non-throwing, so a statement is the only position it can be written in. Without
			// this arm the canonical spelling (`stdlib/File.maxon:186`) died as
			// `E2015: Unsupported: identifier statement` and every successful `stat` leaked 48 bytes — exit
			// 101, measured (found in review, R4.2). The void-and-THROWING pair (`delete`/`rename`) never
			// needed it: a `try` statement is their own form.
			//
			// The reason a
			// STATIC call is otherwise not a statement does not apply to either. `Point.create()` on a line of
			// its own is refused because it discards a box nothing would then free (`methodCallsAt`); an
			// intrinsic allocates nothing, so a value-returning one written here simply has its result
			// discarded, exactly as every other bare-call statement's is.
			//
			// This is the position `stdlib/Sleep.maxon` needs — the module every source-level `sleep(ms)`
			// now resolves to: its whole body is `__Builtins.sleep(milliseconds)` on a line of its own,
			// so without this arm the module does not compile. Until it existed, the three
			// recognized intrinsics all RETURNED a value, so every one of them arrived in expression
			// position and the recognizer was never asked from here — a void intrinsic died as
			// `E2015: Unsupported: identifier statement`, pointing at the `__Builtins` token.
			//
			// A member the table does NOT recognize falls through `parseCallNamed` to `emitCall`, whose
			// reserved-callee rejection reports the positioned E3004 (`requireCalleeIsNotReservedName`) —
			// so widening the statement door widens no name, it only routes them to the same answer
			// expression position already gives.
			_ = try self.parseQualifiedCall(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'voidStaticStmt' else if self.namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt(self.pos, minimumDots: MethodQualifierDots) > 0 'namespaceQualifiedCallStmt'
			// ⭐ **`utils.shout()` / `lib.inner.shout()` ON A LINE OF ITS OWN (N1b)** — a FREE call written with
			// a directory qualifier, and a free call is exactly what `callStmt` below already admits: the
			// result is discarded, `resultUsed: false` is what makes a VOID one legal here, and the
			// statement's `drainPendingTemps` tail releases an owned result nothing took. Without this arm the
			// canonical spelling died as `E2015: Unsupported: identifier statement` — `callStmt`'s lookahead
			// is `identifier (`, which a qualifier's `.` fails — so a namespaced module could declare a void
			// function nobody outside its own directory could call. Found by probing this rung's own
			// mechanism; pinned by `namespaces/namespace-qualified-void-call-statement`.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `Point.create()`'s CASE, AND THE RECOGNIZER IS WHAT KEEPS THEM APART.** A static
			// call as a statement stays refused (it would discard a box nothing then frees — see
			// `methodCallsAt`), because `resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction` declines any chain a
			// `Type.method` key already wears and this arm never sees it. This is also why ONE dot is admitted
			// here and not in expression position: no arm at this door owns the shape.
			//
			// Through `parsePostfix` for `directCallStmt`'s reason: a call whose RESULT is a function value can
			// carry a trailing `(args)`, which the postfix loop applies as an indirect call. A plain direct
			// call leaves none, so this is a no-op for every ordinary namespace-qualified statement.
			_ = try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parseNamespaceQualifiedCall(func, resultUsed: false), resultUsed: false)
		end 'namespaceQualifiedCallStmt' else if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftParen 'callStmt'
			// An identifier followed by `(` is a bare-call statement — the call is evaluated for its
			// effect and its result discarded (M5). `resultUsed: false` is what makes a VOID call legal
			// HERE and nowhere else: this is the position that wants no value.
			//
			// A FUNCTION-TYPED LOCAL called here (`let cb = doIt; cb()`) is an INDIRECT call through the
			// value — the SAME diversion `parsePrimary` makes in expression position (P1.5-A1, #68).
			// Resolve it as a variable reference and let `parsePostfix` apply the trailing `(args)` as an
			// indirect call, exactly as the expression path does; its result — void, or a value discarded
			// here like any other bare-call statement — is dropped by the statement's `drainPendingTemps`
			// tail when it owns heap. Treating `cb` as a direct call instead is what misreported it E3004.
			if self.calleeIsFunctionTypedLocal() 'functionLocalCallStmt'
				_ = try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parseVariableReference(), resultUsed: false)
			end 'functionLocalCallStmt' else 'directCallStmt'
				// A trailing `(` on the call's RESULT is a call-result callee (`getFn()()`) — the result is itself a
				// `function` value, applied by `parsePostfix` as an indirect call, the statement-position twin of the
				// value-position `pick()(21)` (#97). A plain direct call leaves no trailing `(`, so `parsePostfix` is a
				// no-op and every existing direct-call statement lowers to the same IR.
				let calleeTok = self.current()
				let callTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
				_ = try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parseCall(func, resultUsed: false), resultUsed: false)
				self.terminateAfterDivergingCall(func, calleeTok: calleeTok, tempFloor: callTempFloor)
			end 'directCallStmt'
		end 'callStmt' else if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'memberExprStmt'
			// `h.op` / `arr.managed` / `Point.create()` / `Self.make(1)` — a MEMBER ACCESS written on a line
			// of its own. Every arm above has already declined it (no `=` to make it a store, no `(args)`
			// shape any of the call dispatchers claims), and what is left is a WELL-FORMED EXPRESSION refused
			// for a reason that belongs to statement position alone: a value-returning static discards a box
			// nothing would then free, and a field read is a BORROW with no one here to borrow for.
			//
			// ⚠ The guard asks only what FOLLOWS, deliberately, where the `callStmt` arm above also pins the
			// current token to an identifier. A member access's receiver is not always one — `"abc".foo()`
			// and `Self.make(1)` both arrive here — so keying on the receiver's kind would sort a literal
			// receiver into the arm below and change a verdict this port has no case for. Reading the
			// lookahead alone makes the split CONSERVATIVE by construction: every token followed by `.`
			// keeps the exact sentence it had before this arm existed, and only tokens that are not
			// move.
			//
			// That is why this arm keeps E2015 while the one below does not. Both refusals are in force today,
			// but only this one is PROVISIONAL: it stands until the ownership rung decides what a discarded
			// box or an unclaimed borrow costs, so "a shape the compiler does not lower HERE YET" is literally
			// what E2015 means for it — where the arm below is refused permanently, which E2015 cannot say.
			// Four ported specs pin this sentence
			// (`first-class-functions`, `managed-memory-methods`, `self-type-member-expression`,
			// `static-method-statement`) and its category-not-text wording is theirs, not this port's.
			let memberTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("{memberTok.kind.rawValue} statement", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'memberExprStmt' else 'strayStatementToken'
			// A bare `foo` on a line of its own, a `retur x` typo, a `test` that meant `test()`. Every arm
			// above has declined the token and it opens no construct of its own — and no future feature makes
			// any of these legal, which is the one verdict E2015 ("a construct this compiler does not
			// implement yet") cannot carry. E2001 is the code the registry already claims for a token the
			// grammar has no position for. Same fault and same fix as `dispatchTopLevel`'s fall-through
			// (806bc0260) one scope up, and the same second defect with it: the message rendered
			// `tok.kind.rawValue`, the token's CATEGORY, which is not a string any reader can search the file
			// for. `tokenDisplayText` is the one reader that resolves a token's text — see its definition
			// (`Lexer.maxon`) for why reading `.value` directly is not the same thing.
			let tok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.strayToken(tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'strayStatementToken'

		// ⭐ **DROP THE STATEMENT'S UNCONSUMED OWNED TEMPORARIES.** `print("{x}")` created an interpolation
		// result that `print` only BORROWED; under static single-owner it is owned by this statement and
		// dies here. A binding transferred its result out already (`parseBinding` cleared it), so a `let s =
		// "{x}"` drains nothing — `s` drops at scope exit instead.
		//
		// ⚠ Guarded on the block NOT being terminated, and that is the fail-safe: a terminating statement
		// has already ended the block, and a `__mm_decref` emitted after a terminator would sit on a dead
		// path — which is NOT merely a leak. The release lands in the NEXT block the parser opens, and if
		// the temporary's definitions do not dominate that block the program is WRONG: measured on
		// `panic("v{x}")` inside an `if`, where x64 spilled the String and decref'd an UNINITIALIZED SLOT on
		// the path the `if` did not take, and arm64 kept it in a register and the allocator refused it
		// (`seedInUse`, several passes later). See `drainPendingTemps`' region list.
		//
		// ⇒ **THE OBLIGATION THIS SKIP HANDS TO EVERY TERMINATING STATEMENT THAT CARRIES AN EXPRESSION: DRAIN
		// YOUR OWN TEMPORARIES BEFORE YOUR TERMINATOR.** All FIVE do, and the list is the whole argument, so
		// keep it complete when a sixth arrives:
		//   • `return wrap("v{x}")` — `parseReturnStatement` → `emitHandoffExitDrops`, ahead of the `ret`.
		//   • `throw E.p(len("v{x}"))` — `emitThrowError` → the same routine, ahead of the `throwError`.
		//   • `panic("v{x}")` — `parsePanicStatement`, ahead of the abort op. NOT via `emitHandoffExitDrops`:
		//     it drains from the statement's own FLOOR (a match arm's scrutinee temporary sits below it and
		//     must survive) and emits no scope drops at all, control never leaving `mrt_panic`.
		//   • `abort("v{x}")` where `abort` NEVER RETURNS (G17) — `terminateAfterDivergingCall`, on
		//     `panic`'s own terms and for its reasons: the statement's own floor, and no scope drops.
		//   • `break`/`continue` — nothing to drain: they carry no expression, so they build no temporary.
		// The A3n bug was this list being written down as `break`/`continue` alone, with `panic` missing.
		//
		// The `count() > 0` half is what keeps a statement that produced NO temporary — every statement in a
		// program with no interpolation — from paying anything: `drainPendingTemps` reallocates the list, so
		// calling it per statement would be one allocation on every line of the whole compiler. Almost no
		// statement enrolls a temporary, so the common path skips the call entirely.
		if not self.currentBlock.isTerminated() and self.pendingTempDrops.count() > 0 'dropTemps'
			self.drainPendingTemps(WholeStatementTempFloor, anchor: stmtTok)
		end 'dropTemps'

		return self.currentBlock
	end 'parseStatement'

	// ⭐⭐ **`@heap let p = Point.create(1, y: 2)` — THE HEAP ANNOTATION (`specs/ownership.md`), WHICH SHV2
	// ACCEPTS, VALIDATES, AND THEN LOWERS AS A PLAIN BINDING.** It asks the compiler to heap-allocate the
	// decorated local instead of promoting it to the stack — a request shv2 has nothing to do about, because
	// **every struct in shv2 IS a heap box** (`Self{…}` emits an `__mm_alloc`; see `valueIsNonTextAggregate`)
	// and there is no stack-promotion pass to opt out of. So the annotation is honoured by construction, and
	// the four `ownership.md` cases that carry it (`local-struct-freed`, `nested-block-frees`,
	// `if-then-block-cleanup`, `while-loop-iteration-cleanup`) were MEASURED to pass with the annotation
	// stripped — same stdout, exit 0, no leak — which is what says a no-op lowering is honest rather than
	// merely sufficient. v1 reached the same conclusion for the same reason (`Parser.maxon:10817`).
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT IS NOT A NO-OP IS THE VALIDATION.** An unrecognized annotation, an `@heap` decorating something
	// that is not a binding, and an `@heap` on a value that owns no heap are all refused — see the three
	// `ParseError` arms. Accepting the token and ignoring what follows it would make `@hepa let x = 5` a
	// silently-honoured typo.
	function parseAnnotatedBinding(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let atTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.at)

		let annotationTok = self.current()
		if annotationTok.kind != TokenKind.identifier or not annotationTok.value.equals(HeapAnnotationName) 'unknownAnnotation'
			throw ParseError.unknownAnnotation(tokenDisplayText(annotationTok), line: atTok.line, column: atTok.column)
		end 'unknownAnnotation'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

		if not self.at(TokenKind.var) and not self.at(TokenKind.let) 'notABinding'
			throw ParseError.heapAnnotationWithoutBinding(atTok.line, column: atTok.column)
		end 'notABinding'

		try self.parseBinding(func, block: block, mutable: self.at(TokenKind.var), heapAnnotated: true)
	end 'parseAnnotatedBinding'

	// Parse a `let`/`var` binding: `let <name> = <expr>`. M2 rejects a type
	// annotation — a `:` after the name makes the `=` consume fail with E2010
	// (`Expected '=' but got ':'`), which IS the `variables.md` type-annotation
	// contract, so no explicit `:` check is needed. The initializer's SSA value is
	// bound to the name in the scope, so a later reference resolves straight to it
	// (no slot, no load/store — see Scope.declareValueBinding). `mutable`
	// distinguishes `var` from `let`: only a `var` may be reassigned, and only a `var`
	// carries a loop-carried phi.
	//
	// The binding lands in the INNERMOST open scope frame, so a declaration inside an
	// `if`/`while` body is gone at its `end` — and may legally SHADOW an outer binding
	// of the same name while it lives (specs/block-scoping.md).
	// The binding takes the initializer's INFERRED type, not a hardcoded `integer`. That was
	// harmless while every value was an int; it is not any more — `let flag = a > b` binds a
	// `bool`, and `flag and other` short-circuits only if the parser knows so.
	//
	// `heapAnnotated` is true only for the `@heap` spelling (`parseAnnotatedBinding` above), and its whole
	// effect is the refusal below — the annotation changes no IR.
	function parseBinding(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, mutable bool, heapAnnotated bool) throws ParseError
		let keywordTok = self.current()
		if mutable 'isVar'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.var)
		end 'isVar' else 'isLet'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.let)
		end 'isLet'

		// ⭐ **`let (x, y) = <a tuple>` — DESTRUCTURING (P1.10).** Recognized by the `(` where a name belongs:
		// no other binding form opens with one, so the two grammars are disjoint at the first token.
		//
		// `@heap` owes this path no check: a destructuring initializer must BE a tuple (`requireDestructurableTuple`),
		// a tuple is a heap record, so the annotation is satisfied by construction and the refusal below could
		// never fire here. The bootstrap accepts the same shape (measured on `@heap let (a, b) = (1, 2)`).
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'destructure'
			try self.parseDestructuringBinding(func, block: block, mutable: mutable, keywordTok: keywordTok)
			return
		end 'destructure'

		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)
		let rhsStart = self.pos
		let value = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)

		// ⭐ **`@heap` ON A VALUE THAT OWNS NO HEAP IS REFUSED** — the annotation asks for a box, and for an
		// `int` there is none to force. See the `heapAnnotationOnNonRecord` union arm for why the admissible
		// set is `valueIsManagedHeap`'s and not the bootstrap's narrower "struct literals".
		if heapAnnotated and not self.valueIsManagedHeap(value) 'annotatedNonRecord'
			throw ParseError.heapAnnotationOnNonRecord(String.from(nameTok.value), tag: typeTagName(self.tagOf(value)), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'annotatedNonRecord'

		// WHOSE storage did the initializer reach into — `let u = t` (the binding itself) or `let u = t.x`
		// (a field of it) — and empty for every other RHS, which mints a fresh value owned by nobody.
		// Captured here, off the token position, so neither the move poison nor E3078 runs on a consume
		// (see `rhsRootLocalName`).
		let sourceName = self.rhsRootLocalName(rhsStart)
		let sourceReadsAField = self.rhsSpanReadsAField(rhsStart)

		// ⭐⭐ **E3078 IS ASKED AT THE DOORS THAT HOLD A NAME THE AUTHOR WROTE — this one and the
		// destructuring ELEMENT — and never inside `declareInitializedBinding`.** That door also declares
		// the destructuring TEMP, a binding the compiler mints (`__destructure_<line>_<col>`) and hands the
		// author's keyword although its mutability is inert; a mutability-keyed rule asked there refused
		// `var (a, b) = t` from an immutable `t` outright and blamed a name that appears nowhere in the
		// program. Asked here, the sentence can only ever name something the author can go and change.
		// The ordering the rule needs is unaffected: this still runs before the borrowed-managed promotion
		// that door performs, which is the aliasing being refused (see `requireMutableBindingSource`).
		try self.requireMutableBindingSource(nameTok, value: value, sourceName: sourceName, sourceReadsAField: sourceReadsAField, mutable: mutable)

		try self.declareInitializedBinding(func, nameTok: nameTok, value: value, sourceName: sourceName, sourceReadsAField: sourceReadsAField, mutable: mutable)
		self.noteBodyBindingDeclared(nameTok, mutable: mutable)
	end 'parseBinding'

	// ⭐ **E3077 / E3012 CANDIDATE: A BODY `var` THE AUTHOR WROTE.** Enrolled at the SAME two doors E3078 is
	// asked at — this one and the destructuring ELEMENT — and for the same reason spelled there:
	// `declareInitializedBinding`'s third caller declares the destructuring TEMP, a name the compiler mints,
	// and no diagnostic telling an author what to change may name it.
	//
	// ⚠ A body `let` IS enrolled since the 2026-08-19 ruling — see `UnusedBindingKind.mutableLocal` for the
	// blocker that stood here and the IR measurement that refuted it.
	//
	// ⭐ `_` is the discard and is exempt, exactly as it is in a `for` header (`declareLoopVariable`) and as
	// v1 filters it out of its parameter candidates. It binds a name the source never intends to read.
	//
	// The binding is read back out of the scope rather than threaded out of the declaration: the declaration
	// door has more callers than this rule has candidates, so a return value would make the others carry a
	// binding nothing reads.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ENROLMENT DOORS ARE NARROWER THAN THE DECLARATION DOORS, and the third one is not the temp.**
	// `parseBoundThenBody` declares `if var x = try f()`'s binding through `declareInitializedBinding`
	// directly, so a bound-then `var` is a candidate for NEITHER rule. That is the safe direction — a missed
	// diagnostic, never a refused program — and it is written down here because "two doors" reads like an
	// exhaustive claim otherwise.
	function noteBodyBindingDeclared(nameTok Token, mutable bool)
		if nameTok.value.equals(DiscardBindingName) 'notACandidateName'
			return
		end 'notACandidateName'

		let kind = UnusedBindingKind.mutableLocal if mutable else UnusedBindingKind.immutableLocal
		self.unusedCandidates.push(UnusedBindingCandidate.create(self.scope.getInfo(nameTok.value), nameRange: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok), kind: kind))
	end 'noteBodyBindingDeclared'

	// ⭐ **BIND ONE NAME TO ONE ALREADY-PARSED INITIALIZER — the whole declaration half of a `let`/`var`.**
	// Split out of `parseBinding` when destructuring landed (P1.10): `let (x, y) = t` declares N names from
	// N field loads and every one of them owes exactly what a single binding owes — the borrowed-managed
	// promotion, the self-field shadow check, the pending-borrow attachment, the mutable-var enrolment, the
	// owned/promise enrolment and the move-on-bind poison. A second copy of that list is not a duplication
	// risk in the abstract; it is the concrete way a destructured `String` element comes to be dropped twice
	// or not at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **E3078 IS THE CALLER'S, AND IT MUST ALREADY HAVE BEEN ASKED.** The borrowed-managed promotion below
	// IS the aliasing that rule refuses — it retains an immutable source's box and hands it to a writable
	// name — so the refusal has to run BEFORE this door rather than inside it. It is asked at the two callers
	// that hold a name the AUTHOR WROTE (`parseBinding`, and the destructuring ELEMENT) and at neither of the
	// other two: the `if let` binding names no source at all, and the destructuring TEMP does name one but is
	// a binding the compiler MINTED, which no diagnostic may tell an author to change. `sourceName` reaches
	// this door for the MOVE poison, which is a different question. See `requireMutableBindingSource`.
	function declareInitializedBinding(func IrFunction, nameTok Token, value ValueId, sourceName ByteArray, sourceReadsAField bool, mutable bool) throws ParseError
		// A bare read of a local is the only initializer that can MOVE an owned value out of an existing
		// binding — a field read of one mints a fresh borrowed pointer that is the value of no binding.
		let rhsWasBareLocal = not sourceName.isEmpty() and not sourceReadsAField
		// ⭐ **A MUTABLE BORROWED MANAGED BINDING IS PROMOTED TO OWNED AT ITS DECLARATION.** A `var` whose
		// initializer is a BORROWED managed value (a `"literal"` rdata String, or a struct/union PARAMETER —
		// both hold a managed tag yet own nothing) is made to own a droppable value here, at ITS OWN scope:
		//
		//   • a String COPIES into a writable owned heap record — `var s = "hi"` binds an immortal READ-ONLY
		//     rdata record `s.append(…)` cannot grow in place, so it is copied (value semantics).
		//   • a struct/union INCREFS the shared box (OPEN #40) — an aggregate has no cheap owning copy and its
		//     alias is observable (`q.x = 100` must show through to the caller), so it stays the SAME box with
		//     one more owner (reference semantics).
		//
		// Promoting at the DECLARATION is what enrols the binding as owned at the depth it is declared. Left
		// borrowed, a later `q = <owned>` inside a NESTED block would be the FIRST thing to make it own a
		// drop, and `reassignBindingValue`'s `borrowedToOwned` would enrol it at the INNER block's depth — so
		// the fresh box would drop at that block's `end` while the binding (declared outer) is read past it: a
		// use-after-free (the String append-in-nested-loop hazard, and its aggregate twin OPEN #40). The
		// retain/copy balances on every path: a reassignment decrefs/drops the promoted value, and an untaken
		// branch leaves the scope-exit drop to reclaim it — net zero, so the caller stays the owner of an
		// aggregate box. An already-OWNED initializer (`var s = "{x}"`, `var p = Point.create(…)`, a move
		// `var q = t`) is left untouched by the `not valueIsOwnedHeap` guard. The promotion makes the binding
		// a fresh CONSUME, not a bare-local move.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND A `let` NEEDS IT TOO WHEN THE VALUE CAME OUT OF A REBINDABLE SLOT (W41).** The `mutable`
		// gate alone rested on "a `let` is never reassigned", which answers the wrong half: the hazard is not
		// this BINDING being rebound, it is the STORAGE the value was read out of being rebound underneath it.
		// A mutable field or `var` global is exactly that storage, its store drops the old occupant, and the
		// immutable binding then names freed memory — the segfault `Map.grow()`'s four `let old… = <field>`
		// binds hit, where the runnable oracle increfs at the same point and prints the answer. The two gates
		// are ONE question asked of two things — "can the value under this name be dropped while the name is
		// live" — so they share the one promotion door and the one `not valueIsOwnedHeap` guard.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE MARK IS NOT THE AUTHORITY ON WHAT PROMOTION MEANS.** `promoteBorrowedToOwned` keeps that:
		// a String COPIES (an `__mm_retain` of a byte record is the measured wrong cure — an immortal `.rdata`
		// literal admits no incref), an aggregate INCREFS, an existential goes through its witness's
		// `retainFunc@16`. This decides only WHETHER a promotion is owed.
		var boundValue = value
		var boundBareLocal = rhsWasBareLocal
		if (mutable or self.rebindableSlotReads.contains(value)) and self.valueIsManagedHeap(value) and not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value) 'promoteBorrowedBinding'
			boundValue = try self.promoteBorrowedToOwned(value, door: BorrowLaunderDoor.binding, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
			boundBareLocal = false
		end 'promoteBorrowedBinding'

		// ⭐⭐ **A `var` ANOTHER FRAME MUST BE ABLE TO WRITE LIVES IN A CELL FROM ITS DECLARATION (PBR-2).**
		// Taken here, before the ordinary value binding, because the cell has to dominate every read of the
		// name — there is no later point at which the representation could be changed, since the reads
		// already emitted would be reading an SSA value nobody writes to.
		//
		// ⚠ A `let` never qualifies and the gate is not merely an optimization: an immutable binding cannot
		// be reassigned by its own frame, and passing one to a parameter a callee reassigns is E3019 — so a
		// cell for it would be storage nothing could ever legally write.
		if mutable and self.cellLocalNames.contains(nameTok.value) 'livesInACell'
			try self.declareCellLocal(func, nameTok: nameTok, boundValue: boundValue, rhsWasBareLocal: boundBareLocal)
			return
		end 'livesInACell'

		// A local taking the name of a field of the enclosing type is E3006, not a shadow — see
		// `Scope.declareValueBinding`. Anchored on the LOCAL's name token: the field is fine, the
		// local is what has to be renamed.
		var binding = try self.scope.declareValueBinding(nameTok.value, varType: self.typeOf(boundValue), mutable: mutable, value: boundValue) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(nameTok.value), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		// ⭐ E3070: the initializer's outstanding element borrows become THIS binding's, activated HERE — see
		// `attachPendingBorrows` for why the binding and not the accessor is the activation point.
		self.attachPendingBorrows(func, borrower: binding, boundValue: boundValue, liveUntil: self.pos)

		if mutable 'trackVar'
			// Record `var`s in declaration order so loop headers can mint phis for every
			// mutable binding in scope (M4b on-the-fly SSA). `let`s never join — they
			// cannot be reassigned, so they carry no loop-carried phi.
			self.pushMutableVar(binding)
		end 'trackVar'

		// ⭐ **A BINDING OWNS A DROP IFF ITS VALUE OWNS DROPPABLE HEAP** (`valueIsOwnedHeap` — a struct
		// box, a boxed union, an owned interpolation/promotion String, an adopted call result), so it
		// joins the list every scope exit drops (see `ownedBindings`). `let` and `var` alike: ownership is
		// a property of the VALUE, never the mutability — `var p = Point.create(…)` owns exactly what a
		// `let` one does.
		//
		// It keys on the PROVENANCE BIT, not the type tag, and that is what tells an owned box from a
		// BORROWED one: `let s = "hi"` (an immortal rdata literal) and `let q = a` where `a` is a struct
		// PARAMETER (a borrowed pointer) both hold a struct/String tag yet own nothing — no bit, so the
		// binding is borrowed and never dropped. `let u = t` from an already-owned binding DOES own (the
		// bit rides the value), and MOVES it (below). The day a struct arrives from somewhere other than a
		// factory call, its producer set the same bit, and this asks no new question.
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(boundValue) 'trackOwned'
			// ⭐⭐ **AN IMMUTABLE REBIND IS A SECOND REFERENCE, NOT A MOVE (the ownership ruling, 2026-08-04).**
			// Maxon is single-ownership and everything is a reference; `clone()` is the only copy. What makes
			// `let b = a` SAFE is mutability, not linearity — neither name can be written through, so the two
			// cannot observe each other changing and the value is simply reachable by two names. So `b` binds
			// `a`'s value, `a` is NOT poisoned, and both stay readable: `specs/ownership.md`'s `local-alias`.
			//
			// ⭐ **ONE DROP, AND IT IS STILL THE OWNER'S — no refcount traffic.** The alias is NOT enrolled in
			// `ownedBindings`, so `emitScopeDrops` never sees it and the ONE drop stays where it already was:
			// at the owner's scope exit. That is sound without any liveness analysis because a binding can only
			// be declared AFTER the name it reads, in that name's scope or one nested inside it — so the owner
			// always outlives every alias of it, structurally. It is the same shape a borrowed binding
			// (`let q = <a struct parameter>`) already had; what changed is which initializers reach it.
			//
			// ⚠ **A MUTABLE SOURCE STILL MOVES**, and that is the ruling's own rationale read the other way: if
			// `a` is a `var`, a second name for its value CAN observe a write through the first, so there is no
			// aliasing to be safe. `a = <other>` would then drop the box this binding still names. The refusal
			// on the mirror shape — a `var` made FROM an immutable name — is E3078 above, so by here a mutable
			// binding of an owned bare local implies a mutable source.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT ASKS `mutable`, NOT `bindingStorageIsWritable`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.**
			// E3078 asks whether a WRITE could reach storage its owner declared read-only, so it exempts a
			// PARAMETER (a borrowed reference the callee may mutate). This asks something else: can the source
			// LOSE its value while the alias still names it? Only a rebind can do that, and only a `var` can be
			// rebound — a parameter is E2013 — so a consumed owned parameter is a perfectly safe thing to
			// alias. Two questions, two predicates; collapsing them would either leak a rebound box or refuse
			// a legal alias.
			if boundBareLocal and self.rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias(sourceName) 'aliasImmutableSource'
				// `poisonMoveSource` must now consider more than the owned set: a consume of this value
				// (`f(b)`, `return b`) poisons its OWNER, and this name would go on reading a value the callee
				// took. The flag keeps that scan off every function that mints no alias — the overwhelming
				// majority — rather than paying a symbol-table walk per move everywhere.
				self.functionMintedValueAliases = true
			end 'aliasImmutableSource' else 'takeOwnership'
				// ⭐ **MOVE-ON-BIND.** If the initializer was a bare reference to an ALREADY-owned MUTABLE
				// binding (`var t` + `let u = t`), `boundValue` is that binding's value: it MOVES to this new
				// one. Poison the source so a later read of it is use-after-move and only the new owner drops
				// the value. A fresh owned temp (`build()`, `"{x}"`, a promoted mutable-string copy) is held by
				// no binding, so nothing is poisoned — that is a CONSUME (move-in), not a source-poisoning
				// move. Gated on `boundBareLocal`, so a consume never pays the owned-set scan; run BEFORE the
				// push below, so the scan cannot find this new binding (it aliases the same value).
				if boundBareLocal 'moveOnBind'
					try self.poisonMoveSource(boundValue, anchor: nameTok)
				end 'moveOnBind'
				self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
				// Ownership TRANSFERS from the statement to the binding, so the statement must no longer drop
				// it — a second drop of the box the binding now owns is a double-free. A struct box is never in
				// the pending list (only `emitStringInterp` enrolls); an owned interpolation result is, so this
				// is what moves its drop from statement-end to scope-exit.
				self.removeFromPendingTemps(boundValue)
			end 'takeOwnership'
		end 'trackOwned'

		// ⭐ **A PROMISE BINDING OWNS A GREEN THREAD (P1.5-B2 #88), the dual of the E3100 linear-await check.**
		// `let/var p = async f()` enrols the spawn as an owned value, so the SAME scope-exit / re-arm / branch-
		// reconciliation machinery that drops an owned heap box (`emitScopeDrops`, `reconcileMovesAtMerge`,
		// `reassignBindingValue`) drops (and cancels) the thread — through `__gt_promise_drop` — on every path
		// that does not `await` it. `await` marks it moved (`consumePromiseThread`), so an awaited path skips it.
		//
		// ⚠ **AN ALIAS (`let q = p`, `boundBareLocal`) IS NOT A SECOND OWNER.** A promise ALIASES rather than
		// moves (linearity keys on the shared thread ValueId — `await p; await q` is one thread awaited twice),
		// so `q` binds `p`'s value verbatim and is NOT enrolled: exactly the spawn binding drops the one thread
		// once. This is why promises are enrolled separately from `valueIsOwnedHeap` (which move-poisons a bare-
		// local RHS) rather than folded into it. A promise is never `valueIsOwnedHeap`, so the two are disjoint.
		if self.valueOwnsGreenThread(boundValue) and not boundBareLocal 'trackPromise'
			self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(boundValue)
		end 'trackPromise'
	end 'declareInitializedBinding'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3078 — A MUTABLE NAME MAY NOT BE MADE FOR AN IMMUTABLE NAME'S REFERENCE VALUE.**
	//
	// The rule the whole ownership model rests on: Maxon is SINGLE-OWNERSHIP and everything is a reference,
	// so what makes `let b = a` safe is not linearity but MUTABILITY — neither name can be written through,
	// so two names for one value cannot observe each other. `var b = a` breaks exactly that premise: `b.x = 9`
	// would show through `a`, which the author wrote `let` to prevent. The author keeps `b` immutable, or
	// takes an independent copy with `clone()`. Both reference compilers refuse it (the bootstrap at its
	// `ParseVarOrLetDecl`, v1 deferred to `checkVarFromImmutableForEntry`); shv2 simply never had the rule,
	// and its move-on-bind accidentally COVERED the hole — after `var b = a` poisoned `a`, the alias was
	// unreachable rather than refused. Making an immutable rebind an alias removes that cover, which is why
	// the two halves land together.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT RUNS BEFORE THE BORROWED-MANAGED PROMOTION, because the promotion IS the aliasing.**
	// `declareInitializedBinding` retains a borrowed box for a `var` (`retainBorrowedAggregate`), so a
	// `var q = <an immutable struct parameter>` would co-own the caller's box under a writable name. Asked
	// first, that shape never reaches the retain.
	//
	// ⭐ **ITS TWO ASKERS ARE THE TWO DOORS THAT HOLD AN AUTHOR-WRITTEN NAME** — `parseBinding`, and the
	// destructuring ELEMENT (`parseDestructuringBinding`), which asks it of a field of the same source. It is
	// deliberately NOT asked inside `declareInitializedBinding`, whose third caller declares the destructuring
	// TEMP: a name the compiler mints cannot be the subject of a sentence telling the author what to change,
	// and the temp's mutability is inert anyway (see there).
	//
	// ⚠ **A REFERENCE TYPE HERE IS A VALUE THAT NAMES STORAGE — asked of the VALUE, not of a type registry.**
	// `valueIsManagedHeap` is the compiler's existing answer to "is there a record behind this?" (struct box,
	// boxed union, String/Character record, generic instance, existential), and a FUNCTION value is added
	// because `specs/var-should-be-let.md` pins it: it is a code pointer, so the copy is harmless, but both
	// references refuse it and the rule reads better as "everything that is not a number".
	//
	// ⚠ **DIVERGENCE, DELIBERATE: a plain ENUM is NOT a reference type in shv2.** The bootstrap refuses
	// `var d = c` for an enum-typed `c` (its `MaxonValueKind.Enum` arm) because its enums are heap-backed.
	// shv2 lowers a payload-free enum to its ORDINAL — an integer in a register, with no storage for a second
	// name to reach — so the refusal would name a hazard the compiler does not have, and `var-should-be-let`
	// pins no enum case. A union with a payload IS boxed and IS refused, through `valueIsManagedHeap`.
	function requireMutableBindingSource(nameTok Token, value ValueId, sourceName ByteArray, sourceReadsAField bool, mutable bool) throws ParseError
		if not mutable or sourceName.isEmpty() 'notAMutableBindOfANamedSource'
			return
		end 'notAMutableBindOfANamedSource'

		// ⭐ **ASKED FIRST, AND THE ORDER IS E3077'S RATHER THAN E3078's.** A scalar copy is an independent
		// value: neither the shape this refuses, nor a reason the SOURCE had to be writable. Both arms below
		// merely `return`, so moving this above the writability test moves nothing observable for E3078 —
		// what it buys is that the mark below fires only where a mutable name really did reach the source's
		// storage, so `var y = x` over an `int` does not silently excuse `x`.
		if not self.valueIsManagedHeap(value) and self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.function 'independentCopy'
			return
		end 'independentCopy'

		// A source whose storage is already writable is not the shape this refuses. A `var` is the move
		// model's own case — it gives the value up, so no second live name aliases it. A PARAMETER is a
		// borrowed reference to the caller's record, which the callee may mutate; that exemption is
		// `bindingStorageIsWritable`'s, shared with E3019, and MEASURED on the oracle.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS E3077's SEVENTH DOOR — the one that was missing, and whose absence left a correct
		// program with NO LEGAL SPELLING.** `bindingStorageIsWritable`'s header states the invariant these
		// two rules rest on and must not drift from; E3077's evidence was recorded on the E3019 side only,
		// so `var i = o.inner` did not count as a use of `o`'s mutability. MEASURED, all four spellings of
		// the canonical `var-from-immutable-struct-field` program, each following the previous diagnostic's
		// own advice: `var o`/`var i` was **E3077** on `o`, `let o`/`var i` **E3078** on `i`, `let o`/`let i`
		// **E2013** at the write, and the `clone()` E3078 advertises **E2015** on a struct-field read. Four
		// refusals and no way through, for "take a mutable handle into a field of a local struct".
		//
		// Marked HERE rather than at a second `markMutabilityExercised` call site, because the exemption and
		// the mark are the same fact: the source had to be writable for this binding to be legal.
		if self.bindingStorageIsWritable(self.scope.getInfo(sourceName)) 'sourceIsWritableToo'
			self.markNameMutabilityExercised(sourceName)
			return
		end 'sourceIsWritableToo'

		throw ParseError.varFromImmutable(self.immutableSourceSubject(sourceName, sourceReadsAField: sourceReadsAField), binding: String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
	end 'requireMutableBindingSource'

	// The clause E3078's sentence blames the source with. A BARE source is blamed by name; a FIELD CHAIN
	// (`var i = o.inner`) is not, because the value the mutable name would reach is the field's storage and
	// the field has no binding of its own to name — the root `o` is the immutable thing, and saying `o` would
	// point the author at a name their `var` line does not mention. Both spellings are the reference
	// compilers' verbatim (v1 `checkVarFromImmutableForEntry`'s two arms).
	function immutableSourceSubject(sourceName ByteArray, sourceReadsAField bool) returns String
		return "from immutable variable" if sourceReadsAField else "immutable variable '{String.from(sourceName)}'"
	end 'immutableSourceSubject'

	// ⭐⭐ **`let (x, y) = <a tuple>` — DESTRUCTURING, DESUGARED TO A HIDDEN BINDING PLUS ONE FIELD LOAD PER
	// NAME (P1.10).** Both references lower it exactly this way (v1 `parseTupleDestructureVarDecl`,
	// `Parser.maxon:11004-11063`; bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:10165-10305`), and shv2 has no reason to differ:
	// there is nothing here a `let tmp = t` followed by `let x = tmp._0` does not already mean.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE HIDDEN BINDING IS AN OWNERSHIP OBLIGATION, NOT A CONVENIENCE.** The RHS is a freshly built
	// tuple BOX that some binding has to drop; the element names cannot, because a `_N` field read is a
	// BORROW (`emitFieldLoad` never marks the loaded value owned-heap) and N of them would drop the same
	// String N times. So the box is bound to a name the source cannot spell, that name enters the ordinary
	// owned-binding list, and the box dies at scope exit exactly as `let t = (…)` makes it die — while the
	// elements stay borrows that are valid for precisely as long.
	//
	// It runs the SAME `declareInitializedBinding` an ordinary `let` does — for the temp and for every
	// element — so nothing about a destructured binding is special downstream.

	// ⭐ **`( x , y , … )` — THE NAME LIST OF A DESTRUCTURING PATTERN, cursor on the `(`.** Two constructs
	// spell it: a `let`/`var` binding and a `for … in` loop variable (`for (key, value) in m`), and they are
	// one pattern rather than two — `_` discards a position in both, every name is checked against the
	// reserved set in both, and both refuse a one-name pattern with the same sentence.
	//
	// The ARITY is refused here, before the source is even parsed, so `let (x) = …` and `for (x) in …` are
	// each read as the malformed destructure they are rather than as a type error about whatever follows.
	// The sentence names `let`, which is the spelling every reader of it has written; a `for` header that
	// wanted one name simply omits the parens.
	function parseDestructuringNames(keywordTok Token) returns TokenArray throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		var nameToks = TokenArray.create()
		while not self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'collectNames'
			let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
			try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
			nameToks.push(nameTok)
			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'lastName'
				break
			end 'lastName'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'collectNames'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		if nameToks.count() < MinTupleArity 'tooFewNames'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a destructuring binding of {nameToks.count()} name(s) (a tuple has at least {MinTupleArity} elements, so `let (x) = …` names no destructurable shape — write `let x = …`)", line: keywordTok.line, column: keywordTok.column)
		end 'tooFewNames'

		return nameToks
	end 'parseDestructuringNames'

	function parseDestructuringBinding(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, mutable bool, keywordTok Token) throws ParseError
		let nameToks = try self.parseDestructuringNames(keywordTok)

		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)
		let rhsStart = self.pos
		let value = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)
		let layout = try self.requireDestructurableTuple(value, nameToks: nameToks, keywordTok: keywordTok, patternSubject: BindingPatternSubject, sourceNoun: BindingPatternSourceNoun, needsClause: BindingPatternNeedsClause)

		// WHICH local the initializer reached into, read once for the whole destructure — the temp's move
		// source, and the root every ELEMENT reaches a field of (see the E3078 ask below).
		let sourceName = self.rhsRootLocalName(rhsStart)

		// The hidden binding takes the SOURCE's keyword: a `var (x, y) = …` element may be reassigned, and a
		// reassignment of an element rebinds only that name — the box itself is never written through, so the
		// temp's mutability is inert and simply matches what the author wrote.
		//
		// ⚠ **AND BECAUSE IT IS INERT, THE TEMP IS NOT WHAT E3078 MAY BLAME.** Its name is minted here and
		// appears in no source file, so a refusal keyed on its mutability reported a binding the author never
		// wrote — and refused `var (a, b) = t` for a tuple of ints, where nothing is aliased at all. The rule
		// belongs to the ELEMENT names, below, which are the names a write would actually reach storage
		// through. The temp still supplies the MOVE source, which is a different question (`boundBareLocal`).
		let tempTok = Token.create(TokenKind.identifier, line: keywordTok.line, column: keywordTok.column, value: "{DestructureTempPrefix}{keywordTok.line}_{keywordTok.column}".toByteArray())
		try self.declareInitializedBinding(func, nameTok: tempTok, value: value, sourceName: sourceName, sourceReadsAField: self.rhsSpanReadsAField(rhsStart), mutable: mutable)

		for i in 0 upto nameToks.count() 'eachName'
			let nameTok = try nameToks.get(i) otherwise panic("parseDestructuringBinding: nameToks.get OOB at {i} below count {nameToks.count()}")
			try self.declareDestructuredElement(func, nameTok: nameTok, element: self.emitFieldLoad(value, layout: layout, index: i, range: SourceRange.between(keywordTok, last: nameTok)), sourceName: sourceName, mutable: mutable)
		end 'eachName'
	end 'parseDestructuringBinding'

	// ⭐ **ONE DESTRUCTURED ELEMENT, BOUND TO THE NAME THAT NAMES IT** — shared by the DECLARING pattern
	// (`let (x, y) = t`, once per name) and by a tuple ASSIGNMENT's `let`/`var` target (`(x, let y) = f()`),
	// which are the same three obligations in the same order. Written twice, an element of one form would
	// come to be enrolled for a rule the other's is not.
	function declareDestructuredElement(func IrFunction, nameTok Token, element ValueId, sourceName ByteArray, mutable bool) throws ParseError
		// ⭐⭐ **E3078 IS ASKED PER ELEMENT, AND `sourceReadsAField` IS UNCONDITIONALLY TRUE HERE** — an
		// element IS a field of the source, whatever the initializer's own span was, so `var (p, q) = t`
		// from an immutable `t` is exactly the `var p = t.0` the desugaring means and gets that program's
		// answer and that program's sentence, blaming the name the author wrote. A managed element would
		// otherwise be promoted (retained) into a writable name and `p.x = 9` would show through `t` —
		// MEASURED, and refused by both reference compilers in the longhand spelling.
		try self.requireMutableBindingSource(nameTok, value: element, sourceName: sourceName, sourceReadsAField: true, mutable: mutable)

		// No source name for the DECLARATION: a field load is a fresh borrowed value held by no binding,
		// so there is nothing for the move poison to find.
		try self.declareInitializedBinding(func, nameTok: nameTok, value: element, sourceName: EmptySourceText, sourceReadsAField: false, mutable: mutable)
		self.noteBodyBindingDeclared(nameTok, mutable: mutable)
	end 'declareDestructuredElement'

	// ⭐⭐ **`(x, let y) = makePair(10, b: 32)` — STATEMENT-LEVEL TUPLE ASSIGNMENT (`specs/tuple-assign.md`),
	// lowered as ONE evaluation of the right-hand side plus one field read per target.** It is the
	// DECLARING destructure with the per-position question widened: each target either REBINDS a binding
	// already in scope, DECLARES a fresh one, or DISCARDS its element. Everything else it does —  the hidden
	// owner of the box, the borrowed field reads, the per-element declaration — is `parseDestructuringBinding`'s,
	// shared rather than restated.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE IS EVALUATED EXACTLY ONCE, AND THE HIDDEN BINDING IS WHAT MAKES THAT TRUE
	// RATHER THAN MERELY INTENDED.** Every target reads a FIELD of the one value the expression produced, so
	// `(a, b) = step(a)` inside a loop calls `step` once per trip whatever the arity —  and `a` on the
	// right-hand side is read before any target is written, because the whole expression is parsed and
	// emitted ahead of the distribution loop below.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE HIDDEN BINDING IS AN OWNERSHIP OBLIGATION, exactly as it is for the declaring form.** The
	// right-hand side is a tuple BOX somebody has to drop; the targets cannot, because a `_N` field read is a
	// BORROW (`emitFieldLoad` never marks the loaded value owned-heap) and N of them would drop one managed
	// element N times. So the box is bound to a name the source cannot spell, which drops it at scope exit —
	// and that is also why a `_` DISCARD owes nothing at all: it reads no field, and the element it declined
	// to read was never its to release.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TEMP IS DECLARED IMMUTABLE, where the declaring form hands it the author's keyword.** There is
	// no author keyword here — the mutability is per TARGET — and the temp's own is inert either way (nothing
	// rebinds it; the box is never written through). Immutable is the honest spelling of "nothing rebinds
	// this", and it is the same value `let (x, y) = …` passes.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **"THE BOX HAS AN OWNER" IS NOT THE WHOLE OWNERSHIP QUESTION HERE, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE IT IS
	// NOT — CAN A TARGET OUTLIVE THAT OWNER?** The declaring form never has to ask: its names are declared in
	// the same scope as its temp, so they die together. An ASSIGNMENT can name a binding declared in an
	// ENCLOSING scope, and the temp dies at the END of the block the statement sits in — so a target holding a
	// borrow of the temp's box would be read after the box was released. MEASURED, on the shape that would
	// expose it (a loop rebinding an outer `var s` from a `(String, String)` per trip, then 40 further
	// allocations to REUSE any freed slab before `s` is printed): **the right string, exit 0, no 101** — and
	// identical to the bootstrap on the un-churned form.
	//
	// It is right because a REBINDING target does not hold a borrow at all: `completeLocalRebind` →
	// `reassignBindingValue` gives the binding a value it owns (a borrowed managed element is promoted — a
	// String COPIES, an aggregate increfs), which is the same promotion `s = <a field read>` performs. The
	// borrow is consumed inside the statement; nothing of the temp's survives it. A DECLARING target's name is
	// scoped with the temp, exactly as in the declaring form.
	function parseTupleAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let openTok = self.current()
		let openIndex = self.pos
		let equalsIndex = self.tupleAssignEqualsAt(openIndex)
		let targetIndices = self.tupleAssignTargetIndices(openIndex, equalsIndex: equalsIndex)

		// Past `( … ) =` in one step. The predicate has already read every token of the head and the walk
		// above has already split it into targets, so consuming them one at a time here would be that same
		// grammar walked a third time — and the third copy is the one that gets taught a new target shape
		// last.
		self.pos = (equalsIndex + 1) as TokenIndex

		// ONE pass over the targets before the right-hand side exists, answering both questions that have to be
		// settled before it is parsed: the reserved-name refusal (so `(x, let __y) = f()` is refused as the bad
		// declaration it is, rather than after a call was emitted) and whether EVERY target discards (E3064's
		// door, below). A rebinding target names a binding that was checked at its own declaration.
		var everyTargetDiscards = true
		for k in 0 upto targetIndices.count() 'eachTargetBeforeTheRhs'
			let nameIndex = try targetIndices.get(k) otherwise panic("parseTupleAssignment: targetIndices.get OOB at {k} below count {targetIndices.count()}")
			let target = self.tupleAssignTargetAt(nameIndex)
			if tupleAssignTargetDiscards(target) 'discarded'
				continue
			end 'discarded'

			everyTargetDiscards = false
			if tupleAssignTargetDeclares(target) 'declaringTarget'
				let nameTok = self.tokenAt(nameIndex)
				try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
			end 'declaringTarget'
		end 'eachTargetBeforeTheRhs'

		let rhsStart = self.pos
		// ⭐ **A MODULE-WIDE `OpIndex`, NOT A PER-BLOCK COUNT — the genuine analogue of
		// `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`'s `targetOpFloor`.** The producer probe compares against the block the
		// expression ENDED on, which a `try` or a ternary may have forked; two different blocks' op COUNTS
		// compare to nothing at all, and the fresh block's small count silently skipped the enrolment. Op
		// indices are global to the module, so this floor means the same thing on whatever block the
		// expression left the cursor.
		let opFloor = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		let value = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)

		// ⚠ **PROBED IMMEDIATELY, RECORDED LATER.** The producer is "the last op this statement emitted", and
		// the temp's declaration below can emit one of its own (a borrowed-managed promotion) — which would
		// put a `__mm_retain` where the call is expected. So the callee is read here and the pending site is
		// filed only once the statement is known to be well formed, below: a program the arity check is about
		// to refuse must not leave an E3064 site behind in the artifact.
		var discardedCallee = DiscardedCallee.notACall
		if everyTargetDiscards 'nothingTookAnElement'
			discardedCallee = try self.directCallProducing(value, opFloor: opFloor)
		end 'nothingTookAnElement'

		let layout = try self.requireTupleLayout(value, keywordTok: openTok, patternSubject: AssignPatternSubject, sourceNoun: AssignPatternSourceNoun, needsClause: AssignPatternNeedsClause)

		// E3005, positioned on the `(` — where the author decided how many targets to write. It is NOT the
		// E2015 the two DECLARING patterns raise for the same shape of mistake (`requireTupleLayout` says
		// why): this sentence and this code are `specs/tuple-assign.md`'s, byte for byte.
		if layout.fieldCount() != targetIndices.count() 'arityMismatch'
			throw ParseError.tupleAssignArityMismatch(layout.fieldCount(), targets: targetIndices.count(), line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column)
		end 'arityMismatch'

		match discardedCallee 'usedNothing'
			named(callee) then self.artifact.recordDiscardedResult(PendingDiscardedResult.create(callee, line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column, filePath: self.filePath))
			notACall then break 'usedNothing'
		end 'usedNothing'

		// WHICH local the right-hand side reached into, read once for the whole statement — the temp's move
		// source, and the root every DECLARING target reaches a field of (see `declareDestructuredElement`).
		let sourceName = self.rhsRootLocalName(rhsStart)

		let tempTok = Token.create(TokenKind.identifier, line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column, value: "{DestructureTempPrefix}{openTok.line}_{openTok.column}".toByteArray())
		try self.declareInitializedBinding(func, nameTok: tempTok, value: value, sourceName: sourceName, sourceReadsAField: self.rhsSpanReadsAField(rhsStart), mutable: false)

		for i in 0 upto targetIndices.count() 'eachTarget'
			let nameIndex = try targetIndices.get(i) otherwise panic("parseTupleAssignment: targetIndices.get OOB at {i} below count {targetIndices.count()}")
			let target = self.tupleAssignTargetAt(nameIndex)
			let nameTok = self.tokenAt(nameIndex)

			// Asked BEFORE the read, which is the whole of what a discard costs: no field load, no store, no
			// binding — see the ownership paragraph in this function's header.
			if tupleAssignTargetDiscards(target) 'discarded'
				continue
			end 'discarded'

			let element = self.emitFieldLoad(value, layout: layout, index: i, range: SourceRange.between(openTok, last: nameTok))
			match target 'kind'
				declares(mutable) then try self.declareDestructuredElement(func, nameTok: nameTok, element: element, sourceName: sourceName, mutable: mutable)
				rebinds then try self.rebindTupleAssignTarget(func, nameTok: nameTok, nameIndex: nameIndex, element: element)
				discards then panic("parseTupleAssignment: `tupleAssignTargetDiscards` already continued past every discard, so this arm names a case that cannot reach it")
			end 'kind'
		end 'eachTarget'
	end 'parseTupleAssignment'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH CALL PRODUCED THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE — the half of E3064's door the PARSER can answer.**
	// `(_, _) = makePair(10, b: 32)` uses nothing the call produced, so the statement is a call written for an
	// effect; whether the callee HAS one is a whole-program question, judged by
	// `SemanticCheck.checkDiscardedResults` from the site the caller files (see `PendingDiscardedResult`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A RIGHT-HAND SIDE THAT IS NOT A DIRECT CALL ANSWERS `notACall`, and that is the rule rather than a
	// limitation.** `(_, _) = (1, 2)` builds a tuple and drops it: wasteful, but there is no callee whose
	// result went unused and so nothing E3064 can be about. The producer is identified the way the `try`
	// rewrite identifies its target — the last op this statement emitted, required to DEFINE the value the
	// expression handed back — so a `+`, a group, or a bare name all decline it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `tryCall` IS NOT MATCHED** (`directCallDefining` lists it with the non-call ops), so
	// `(_, _) = try f() otherwise (1, 2)` records nothing. Deliberate and in the safe direction: a throwing
	// callee is never effect-free by this analysis anyway (`opShowsAnEffect` treats a throw as an effect), so
	// the site it would file could never fire. Stated because "the last op is the call" reads like it covers
	// both spellings.
	//
	// ⚠ It reads `self.currentBlock` FRESH rather than the block it was handed: an expression may have forked
	// (a `try`, a ternary), and the op that produced the value is on the block the expression ENDED on. What
	// makes that safe is the floor being a module-wide `OpIndex` — see the caller.
	function directCallProducing(value ValueId, opFloor OpIndex) returns DiscardedCallee throws ParseError
		let refCount = self.currentBlock.opRefs.count()
		if refCount == 0 'theBlockHoldsNoOp'
			return DiscardedCallee.notACall
		end 'theBlockHoldsNoOp'

		let opIdx = try self.currentBlock.opRefs.get(refCount - 1) otherwise panic("directCallProducing: opRefs.get OOB on a block whose count was just read as non-zero")
		if opIdx < opFloor 'olderThanThisStatement'
			return DiscardedCallee.notACall
		end 'olderThanThisStatement'

		return try self.directCallDefining(opIdx, value: value)
	end 'directCallProducing'

	// The identifier token of each target of a confirmed list, in source order. Every identifier in the span
	// IS a target's name and nothing else is one — a qualifier is a KEYWORD and a separator is a comma — so
	// this is the walk in full, and the k-th identifier is the k-th tuple element's target.
	function tupleAssignTargetIndices(openIndex TokenIndex, equalsIndex TokenIndex) returns TokenIndexArray
		var indices = TokenIndexArray.create()
		var i = (openIndex + 1) as TokenIndex
		while i < equalsIndex 'eachToken'
			if self.kindAt(i) == TokenKind.identifier 'targetName'
				indices.push(i)
			end 'targetName'

			i = (i + 1) as TokenIndex
		end 'eachToken'
		return indices
	end 'tupleAssignTargetIndices'

	// ⭐ **A BARE TARGET IS THE ASSIGNMENT `x = <that element>`, and it goes through the same door.** Everything
	// a rebind owes — the E3012 mention, the mutability refusal (E2013), E3070's rebind note, the type and
	// aggregate-identity checks, the cell store and the payload write-back — is `parseAssignment`'s and is
	// asked here through the same two routines, in the same order.
	//
	// ⚠ **`rhsWasBareLocal` IS FALSE, and that is a fact rather than a simplification.** The value is a FIELD
	// LOAD — a fresh borrowed pointer that is the value of no binding — so there is no source binding for the
	// move poison to find, exactly as `declareDestructuredElement` passes no source name for the same reason.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND `integerizedOperand` IS NOT ASKED, where `parseAssignment` asks it.** That coercion turns a
	// CHARACTER LITERAL right-hand side into its codepoint for an integral target (`b = '0'`), and it is keyed
	// on the RHS's TOKEN SPAN. A tuple element has no span of its own — it is a field of an already-built box,
	// and a tuple element is never a bare character literal at this point — so there is nothing here for it to
	// convert. Stated because its absence otherwise reads as an omission.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NAME REPEATED ACROSS TARGETS (`(x, x) = f()`) IS NOT DIAGNOSED: both stores run, the last wins.**
	// Consistent rather than overlooked — `x = a` then `x = b` on two lines is legal Maxon and means the same
	// thing — and neither reference compiler refuses the tuple spelling either.
	//
	// ⛔ **A TARGET THAT DOES NOT NAME A LOCAL IS REFUSED, NOT QUIETLY REDIRECTED.** A self field
	// (`count = 1` inside a method) and a module-scope `var` are each written through a DIFFERENT store —
	// `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` and `emitCheckedGlobalStore` — and each of those parses its own right-hand
	// side, which a tuple assignment has already evaluated. Routing such a name to the local rebind instead
	// would silently rebind the field's ALIAS and never write the receiver's storage: a wrong answer, not a
	// missing feature. E2015 is exactly "a shape this compiler does not lower HERE yet"; the name that
	// resolves to nothing at all keeps the E2004/E2013 answer a plain `x = …` gives it.
	function rebindTupleAssignTarget(func IrFunction, nameTok Token, nameIndex TokenIndex, element ValueId) throws ParseError
		if not self.scope.contains(nameTok.value) 'notALocal'
			match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(nameTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
				found then throw ParseError.unsupported("a tuple-assignment target naming the module-scope binding '{String.from(nameTok.value)}' (a module binding is written through a different store, which parses its own right-hand side; assign the element to a local and store that)", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
				notFound then try self.rejectNonGlobalAssignment(nameTok)
			end 'global'
		end 'notALocal'

		var binding = self.scope.getInfo(nameTok.value)
		self.markBindingReferenced(binding)

		if binding.isSelfField 'selfField'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a tuple-assignment target naming the field '{String.from(nameTok.value)}' of the enclosing type (a field is written through the receiver by a different store, which parses its own right-hand side; assign the element to a local and store that)", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'selfField'

		try self.requireMutableBinding(binding, nameTok: nameTok)
		self.noteLocalRebind(func, nameIndex: nameIndex, anchor: nameTok)
		try self.completeLocalRebind(func, binding: binding, nameTok: nameTok, value: element, rhsBareLocalName: EmptySourceText)
	end 'rebindTupleAssignTarget'

	// The tuple layout `value` must have for a destructuring pattern over `nameToks` to mean anything: a tuple
	// (never a declared struct — its fields have names the author would have to write) whose element count is
	// the name count. Both refusals are positioned on the construct's keyword, which is where the author
	// decides how many names to write.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONSTRUCT'S NOUNS ARE THE CALLER'S, exactly as `parseHashTableColumnArg` takes its subject** —
	// because only the caller knows which construct is speaking. `for (a, b) in xs` over an int array reaches
	// the first sentence, and hard-coded it read *"a destructuring BINDING of a 'int' INITIALIZER … needs a
	// RIGHT-HAND SIDE"* about a header that has no binding, no initializer and no right-hand side. The nouns
	// are the two triples above; the `let` triple keeps both sentences byte-identical to their committed pins.
	function requireDestructurableTuple(value ValueId, nameToks TokenArray, keywordTok Token, patternSubject String, sourceNoun String, needsClause String) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		let layout = try self.requireTupleLayout(value, keywordTok: keywordTok, patternSubject: patternSubject, sourceNoun: sourceNoun, needsClause: needsClause)
		if layout.fieldCount() != nameToks.count() 'arityMismatch'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("{patternSubject} of {nameToks.count()} names against a tuple of {layout.fieldCount()} elements (every element must be named)", line: keywordTok.line, column: keywordTok.column)
		end 'arityMismatch'
		return layout
	end 'requireDestructurableTuple'

	// ⭐ **THE "IT MUST BE A TUPLE" HALF, ON ITS OWN, because a THIRD construct spells the pattern and does
	// NOT share the arity sentence.** A statement-level tuple ASSIGNMENT (`(x, y) = f()`) asks exactly this
	// question of its right-hand side, and then asks a different arity question: the canonical
	// `specs/tuple-assign.md` pins its count mismatch as **E3005** with its own wording, where the two
	// DECLARING patterns above pin E2015 with theirs (`specs-shv2/tuples.md`). Two committed sentences under
	// two committed codes, so the split is where they actually differ and nowhere else — the non-tuple
	// refusal stays one sentence assembled from the caller's nouns.
	function requireTupleLayout(value ValueId, keywordTok Token, patternSubject String, sourceNoun String, needsClause String) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		if not tagIsStructRef(self.tagOf(value)) or not isTupleTypeName(self.valueTypeName(value)) 'notATuple'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("{patternSubject} of a '{typeTagName(self.tagOf(value))}' {sourceNoun} (only a TUPLE can be destructured — {needsClause})", line: keywordTok.line, column: keywordTok.column)
		end 'notATuple'

		return self.tupleLayoutOfValue(value)
	end 'requireTupleLayout'

	// ⭐ **IS `value` A TUPLE THIS RUNG MAY COPY?** — a tuple `structRef` none of whose elements owns heap.
	// THE one gate on the value-semantics copy, so its askers cannot come to disagree about which tuples have
	// value semantics — a disagreement that would not be a missed case but a record freed twice or not at all.
	// Its askers are the durable-storage move-in (`moveManagedValueInto`) and `promoteBorrowedToOwned`, which
	// asks it for the whole HAND-OFF family at once (a `return`, a closure `gives`, a `match`/ternary arm, a
	// `try … otherwise` fallback). ⚠ It used to say "BOTH of its sites … and the return", naming the return
	// because that is where the hand-off copy was spelled — and while it was spelled THERE rather than here,
	// the merge doors silently increfed a borrowed tuple instead (see `borrowLaunderDoorIsHandOff`).
	// ⚠ `transferConsumedArg` is a THIRD borrowed-managed decider that does NOT ask this and REFUSES a
	// borrowed trivial tuple at a consuming argument position, where the move-in one call away copies it.
	// Pre-existing, and a refusal rather than a wrong answer, so it is filed rather than widened here.
	//
	// The managed probe is asked through `structHasManagedField`, the SAME predicate the destructor synthesis
	// gates on, so "this tuple needs no deep clone" and "this tuple needs no drop cascade" are one answer.
	function valueIsCopyableTuple(value ValueId) returns bool
		if not tagIsStructRef(self.tagOf(value)) 'notAStruct'
			return false
		end 'notAStruct'

		let name = self.valueTypeName(value)
		// ⛔⛔ **A SLOT STANDING AT A BARE TYPE PARAMETER IS NOT A TRIVIAL ELEMENT, AND `structHasManagedField`
		// CANNOT SEE IT (W183).** `typeIsManaged` answers `false` for a `typeParameter`, so a `(K, V)` tuple
		// read as copyable — and the shallow copy below then took a SECOND pointer to a reference the source
		// record owns. **MEASURED on the mm-trace: `Map.map` pushed the copy into its result array, the
		// original's dictionary destructor released the key it had CLONED (`retainFunc@64` is `__str_clone`
		// for a String argument), and the array's element was left pointing at a freed 58-byte record.** The
		// rule this restores is the one this predicate's own header states — only a TRIVIAL-element tuple may
		// be shallow-copied — over the one element kind the managed test is blind to.
		return isTupleTypeName(name) and not self.signatures.structHasManagedField(name) and not self.recordRestsAnOpaqueSlot(name)
	end 'valueIsCopyableTuple'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE LOOKUP THE TWO OPAQUE-SLOT QUESTIONS SHARE.** `recordOpaqueSlotDeclaration` panics for a
	// name that denotes no record, so the guard belongs with it rather than at each asker — written twice it
	// was two copies of one precondition, free to be dropped from one of them.
	function recordOpaqueSlotOwnerOf(name ByteArray) returns OpaqueSlotDeclaration
		if not self.signatures.containsStruct(name) 'namesNoRecord'
			return OpaqueSlotDeclaration.none
		end 'namesNoRecord'

		return self.signatures.recordOpaqueSlotDeclaration(name)
	end 'recordOpaqueSlotOwnerOf'

	// Does `name` denote a record with a slot standing at a bare type parameter — ANY declaration's? The
	// type-level half of `recordRestsThisBodysOpaqueSlot`, for the callers that care only THAT such a slot
	// exists and not whose `uses` list it indexes into. The two differ in exactly the line below and nothing
	// else, which is why they read one lookup.
	function recordRestsAnOpaqueSlot(name ByteArray) returns bool
		match self.recordOpaqueSlotOwnerOf(name) 'restsASlot'
			none then return false
			declaredBy then return true
		end 'restsASlot'
	end 'recordRestsAnOpaqueSlot'

	// A fresh record holding the same elements as `value` — the copy `valueIsCopyableTuple` licenses. It is
	// `emitStructLiteral`'s box, filled from a load per element instead of from parsed values, so the
	// allocation, the size and the per-slot offsets come from the one layout both routines read. The result
	// is an OWNED temporary of the statement, like any freshly built box.
	function copyTupleValue(value ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let layout = self.tupleLayoutOfValue(value)
		let box = self.emitOwnedStructBox(layout, range: range)

		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachElement'
			self.emitFieldStore(box, layout: layout, index: i, value: self.emitFieldLoad(value, layout: layout, index: i, range: range), range: range)
		end 'eachElement'
		return box
	end 'copyTupleValue'

	// The layout of the tuple `value` is typed with. Precondition: the value carries a tuple `structRef`,
	// which is why a miss is a panic — every tuple type in the compiler is registered by `internTupleLayout`
	// before anything is typed with it.
	function tupleLayoutOfValue(value ValueId) returns StructLayout
		match self.signatures.structOf(self.valueTypeName(value)) 'registered'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("tupleLayoutOfValue: the tuple `{String.from(self.valueTypeName(value))}` a value carries is not registered — every tuple `structRef` was minted by `internTupleLayout`, which registers the layout before it hands the name back")
		end 'registered'
	end 'tupleLayoutOfValue'

	// The interned TYPE NAME a `structRef`/`named`-tagged value carries — the pair `internedName` and
	// `valueNameId` spell together, wherever a value's NOMINAL identity is what decides something.
	function valueTypeName(value ValueId) returns ByteArray
		return self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'valueTypeName'

	// Open a lexical block — an `if` / `else` / `while` body. THREE things are scoped to
	// it and must be opened and closed TOGETHER: the Scope frame its declarations land
	// in, the height of the mutable-binding list, and the height of the owned-binding
	// list. A `var` that leaves scope must also leave the first, or the next merge
	// snapshot would mint a phi for a binding no source line can name; a struct binding
	// that leaves scope must leave the second, or a later `return` would emit a
	// `__mm_decref` for a name that is gone — a DOUBLE FREE if the block already
	// returned through it. Returns the mark `closeBlock` unwinds to.
	//
	// ONE mark serves both lists, and that is sound rather than a shortcut: both are
	// append-only stacks pushed at a declaration and drained here, so their heights move
	// together with the block structure. It is not one mark serving two INDICES — each
	// list is drained to its own recorded height (see `BlockMark`).
	function openBlock() returns BlockMark
		self.scope.pushScope()
		return BlockMark.create(self.mutableVars.count(), ownedCount: self.ownedBindings.count())
	end 'openBlock'

	// Close a lexical block: drop its declarations out of scope (restoring anything they
	// shadowed), its `var`s off the mutable-binding list, and its owned bindings off the
	// owned list.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT EMITS NO DROP, AND THAT IS NOW CORRECT RATHER THAN A DEFERRAL.** The `__mm_decref` for a
	// block-local owned binding — `if c 'b' let s = build(1) end 'b'` — is emitted by the code that
	// makes control LEAVE the block, keyed on HOW it leaves: a fall-through drops it in `parseBlockBody`
	// (before this pops it), a `break`/`continue` in `parseBreakStatement`/`parseContinueStatement`
	// (down to the enclosing loop's height), a `return` in `emitScopeDrops` (the whole scope). That
	// placement is what avoids the double-free/use-after-free this comment once warned the drop-here
	// approach could not: each edge that leaves the block alive drops exactly once, and a terminated
	// edge has already dropped, so this routine only unwinds the TRACKING lists and emits nothing.
	function closeBlock(mark BlockMark)
		self.scope.popScope()
		while self.mutableVars.count() > mark.mutableCount 'drainVars'
			self.popMutableVar()
		end 'drainVars'
		while self.ownedBindings.count() > mark.ownedCount 'drainOwned'
			_ = try self.ownedBindings.pop() otherwise panic("closeBlock: ownedBindings.pop failed above the mark {mark.ownedCount}")
		end 'drainOwned'
	end 'closeBlock'

	// Push a `var` onto the mutable-binding stack, and onto the two structures that index it.
	// THE ONE PLACE ALL THREE GROW, so they cannot grow apart: the shadow column is written before
	// the name index, because the position it records is the one the name index is about to lose.
	function pushMutableVar(binding VarInfo)
		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empties before the first push — see
		// `sharedEmptyMutableVarShadowed`. One guard covers both because both are written right below,
		// and a function that declares no `var` keeps the shared pair for its whole life.
		if self.mutableVarShadowed is sharedEmptyMutableVarShadowed 'firstVarInThisFunction'
			self.mutableVarShadowed = MutableVarPositionArray.create()
			self.mutableVarInnermost = MutableVarPositionByName.create()
		end 'firstVarInThisFunction'

		self.mutableVarShadowed.push(self.innermostMutableVarPosition(binding.name))
		self.mutableVarInnermost.upsert(binding.name, value: self.mutableVars.count() as MutableVarPosition)
		self.mutableVars.push(binding)
	end 'pushMutableVar'

	// Pop the innermost `var` off all three, restoring whatever name it shadowed. The twin of
	// `pushMutableVar` and the ONE place all three shrink.
	//
	// ⚠ It relies on the stack being STRICTLY LIFO — the popped binding's name must be the one the
	// index currently maps to this position. `openBlock`/`closeBlock` are the only things that move
	// the height, and they nest, so it is; a mid-stack removal (the shape `removeFromOwnedBindings`
	// deliberately avoids for the drop marks) would desync the index instead.
	function popMutableVar()
		let dropped = try self.mutableVars.pop() otherwise panic("popMutableVar: mutableVars.pop failed on a non-empty stack")
		let shadowed = try self.mutableVarShadowed.pop() otherwise panic("popMutableVar: the shadow column ran out before `mutableVars` did — the two grew apart")
		if shadowed == NoMutableVar 'nothingShadowed'
			_ = self.mutableVarInnermost.remove(dropped.name)
			return
		end 'nothingShadowed'

		self.mutableVarInnermost.upsert(dropped.name, value: shadowed)
	end 'popMutableVar'

	// Where the innermost mutable binding named `name` sits in `mutableVars`, or `NoMutableVar` when
	// no `var` of that name is in scope (a `let`, a self-field, a payload binding, an undeclared name
	// — none of which can carry a phi).
	function innermostMutableVarPosition(name ByteArray) returns MutableVarPosition
		return try self.mutableVarInnermost.get(name) otherwise NoMutableVar
	end 'innermostMutableVarPosition'

	// Parse a lexical block's statements into `block`, through its closing `end [label]`.
	// The ONE place a block body is parsed, so the scope frame is opened and closed in
	// one place too: an `if` body, an `else` body, a `while`/`for` body and a block-form
	// `otherwise` handler all come through here. The caller has already consumed the
	// block's opening label and passes it as `label`.
	//
	// Returns the block the body's last statement left control in (which is `block`
	// itself only for straight-line code — a nested `if` moves control into its
	// continuation).
	//
	// ⭐ **AN EMPTY LABELLED BLOCK IS E3082**, and it is checked HERE because this is the one routine
	// that sees a NESTED block's statements — which is also exactly why a FUNCTION body is exempt
	// (`function doNothing() end 'doNothing'` is legal, `empty-block.md`'s `valid-empty-function`).
	// The exemption is therefore structural rather than a carve-out anyone has to remember. The check
	// runs before the scope frame opens, so the throw leaves no half-opened block behind, and it is
	// anchored on the closing `end` — where the missing statements would have gone. A body holding
	// only a COMMENT reads as empty, because the lexer emits no token for one; the spec's own
	// documentation example is exactly that program.
	function parseBlockBody(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		self.skipNewlines()
		if self.at(TokenKind.end) 'emptyBlock'
			let endTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.emptyBlock(String.from(label), line: endTok.line, column: endTok.column)
		end 'emptyBlock'

		let mark = self.openBlock()
		let bodyEnd = try self.parseStatements(func, block: block)

		// ⭐ **DROP THE BLOCK'S OWN OWNED BINDINGS ON FALL-THROUGH** — a `let s = build(i)` inside an
		// `if`/`while` body dies at the block's `end`, so a loop body drops per iteration. Only the
		// bindings the block itself declared (above `mark.ownedCount`); the outer ones drop when THEIR
		// block closes. Emitted into `bodyEnd` — which IS `self.currentBlock`, as `parseStatements`
		// threads it — BEFORE `closeBlock` pops them off the list, and before the caller
		// (`parseIf`/`parseWhile`) emits the block's exit jump, so the drops precede the continuation
		// branch / back-edge.
		//
		// ⚠ Skipped on a TERMINATED `bodyEnd`: a `return`/`break`/`continue` on every path has already
		// emitted its own drops (the whole scope, or down to the enclosing loop), so dropping again here
		// would double-free — the exact hazard `closeBlock`'s old comment named. `isTerminated()` is the
		// guard that makes this an addition rather than that bug.
		if not bodyEnd.isTerminated() 'dropBlockOwned'
			// ⚠ **TWO THINGS HAPPEN HERE AND THEY ARE NOT ONE THING** — which is what this line looked
			// like when the destination was implicit. `emitScopeDrops` is told WHERE THE DROPS GO by its
			// parameter; this assignment is the PARSER'S POSITION, and the header's claim that "`bodyEnd`
			// IS `self.currentBlock`, as `parseStatements` threads it" is not true of every body: a block
			// whose last statement is an `if` leaves `currentBlock` behind at that `if`'s continuation, and
			// this re-sync is what put it back. It was invisible while one assignment served both roles —
			// MEASURED when `emitScopeDrops` began restoring what it borrowed: `block-condition-temporaries`'s
			// `if-inside-a-try-handler` left its `ifcont` with no terminator at all, because the code that
			// branches a fallen-through handler to the merge reads `currentBlock`.
			self.currentBlock = bodyEnd
			self.emitScopeDrops(bodyEnd, floor: mark.ownedCount, anchor: self.current())
		end 'dropBlockOwned'

		// Published for `parseWhileStatement`'s drift guard — the cursor is ON the `end` here,
		// which is the one place the block's closing token index is known.
		self.lastBlockBodyEnd = self.pos
		try self.consume(TokenKind.end)
		self.skipOptionalLabel()
		self.closeBlock(mark)
		return bodyEnd
	end 'parseBlockBody'

	// Parse a reassignment `<name> = <expr>` (M4b). The target must be a declared
	// `var`; a `let` target is E2013, an undeclared name is `undefinedVariable`. The
	// RHS is evaluated FIRST (so `x = x + 1` reads the old `x`), then the binding's
	// current SSA value is rebound to the result via `Scope.setValue` — the read-
	// after-write model that makes a later reference see the new value. No slot,
	// no store op: the value model is on-the-fly SSA (the reassignment is just a
	// rebinding of the variable's current ValueId, and the loop machinery inserts
	// phis where the rebinding crosses a control-flow merge).
	//
	// **THE ASSIGNED VALUE MUST AGREE WITH THE BINDING'S DECLARED TYPE, AND THAT CHECK IS WHAT
	// MAKES EVERY MERGE PHI'S TYPE TRUE.** A binding's type is fixed where it is declared
	// (`var x = 5` is an int) and `VarInfo.varType` holds it; a reassignment does not redeclare
	// it. There are therefore TWO records of a value's type — the per-VALUE column
	// (`self.valueTags`, which follows the value) and the per-BINDING one (`varType`, frozen at
	// the declaration) — and they meet in exactly one place: `mintPhiForBinding`, which stamps
	// a merge phi with the BINDING's type while the phi carries the REASSIGNED value.
	//
	// So without this check the binding type is a LIE that the phi launders into the value
	// column, and every rule downstream reads the lie and is satisfied:
	//
	//     var x = 5
	//     if c 'b'
	//         x = false        // unchecked: the binding still claims `int`
	//     end 'b'
	//     return x + 1         // the phi is tagged `integer`, so `+` sees two ints — and adds
	//                          // a bool. THE ENTIRE RUNG, defeated by one `if`.
	//
	// Checking the ASSIGNMENT — one statement — makes all three phi mint sites (if-merge, loop
	// header, loop exit) sound BY CONSTRUCTION, with no per-edge check anywhere. Deriving each
	// phi's tag from its incoming values instead cannot work: the loop-header phi is minted
	// EAGERLY, before the body that writes those values has been parsed.
	//
	// AUTHORED, not ported — like the `if <int>` rule, and for the same reason. The C# bootstrap
	// has no assignment type check at all: it accepts `var x = 5` / `x = true` outright, and
	// catches the laundered value only when it is USED, in its Maxon→Std lowering, as an
	// INTERNAL error (E9001, a .NET cast failure with a stack trace and no source position). It
	// does not consider such a program valid — it just has nowhere to say so. shv2 says so here,
	// at the statement that introduces the contradiction.
	function parseAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		// The target's own token index, captured before the cursor leaves it, so the E3070 subject below comes
		// through the shared `borrowSubjectNameAt` rather than off `nameTok.value` — one derivation of "which
		// storage does this write go through", asked identically at every door. It is handed on to
		// `parseTopLevelAssignment` as well: the module-storage rebind is the same write one anchoring out,
		// and deriving it there off the token's TEXT would be that one derivation written down twice.
		let nameIndex = self.pos
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)

		// `_ = <expr>` — a DISCARD assignment: evaluate the RHS for its effect and throw the value away. `_`
		// binds nothing (`DiscardBindingName`, the same discard the match-payload `walk(_)` uses), so at file
		// scope and in any function declaring no `_` parameter it would otherwise reach `undefinedVariable`.
		// ⚠ It IS a scope binding inside a function that declares one — a `_` parameter is installed like any
		// other (`bindParameters`) — so this test runs FIRST for a reason: reaching `scope.contains` would make
		// `_ = f()` a rebinding of that parameter (E2013, a parameter is immutable) instead of the discard the
		// language says it is. The arm is the rule, not a fallback. An owned RHS temporary drops at
		// statement end through `drainPendingTemps`, exactly as a bare-call statement's result does. This is
		// the value-position twin of the bare-call statement, reached by `_ = try arr.pop() otherwise 0` and
		// any `_ = f(…)`.
		if nameTok.value.equals(DiscardBindingName) 'discardResult'
			_ = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)
			return
		end 'discardResult'

		if not self.scope.contains(nameTok.value) 'undeclared'
			// Not a local. It may still name a top-level binding — a `var` (a STORE) or a `let` (an
			// error, and a DIFFERENT error from "undefined"). LOCAL FIRST, exactly as
			// `parseVariableReference` resolves a read: a local `var` shadows a top-level one, so the
			// top level is consulted only once the name has failed to resolve as a local.
			try self.parseTopLevelAssignment(func, block: block, nameTok: nameTok)
			return
		end 'undeclared'

		// `var`, because the write is RECORDED on the binding below — a `let` here is a store through an
		// immutable local (E2013).
		var binding = self.scope.getInfo(nameTok.value)

		// ⭐ **A WRITE IS A MENTION, AND E3012 COUNTS MENTIONS.** MEASURED against the runnable oracle, both
		// directions on the same three-line program: `var x = 1` alone is `unused variable: 'x'`, and
		// `var x = 1` + `x = 2` compiles and exits 0. So a reassignment satisfies the check even though it
		// reads nothing — which is the honest rule, because the author DID have to name the binding, and the
		// diagnostic's job is to find declarations nothing in the body refers to.
		//
		// It is written here rather than in `requireBindingLive` because a write must NOT go through that
		// guard: an assignment REVIVES a moved-from binding, and routing it there would reject `t = <expr>`
		// after a move as use-after-move (see `parseVariableReference`'s note that the READ site is the only
		// caller). Third door, same chokepoint — see `VarInfo.referenced`.
		self.markBindingReferenced(binding)

		// A bare field name inside an instance method — `count = count + 1`. It is a STORE through
		// the receiver, not a rebinding of anything, so it asks the field's mutability rather than
		// this binding's (see `parseSelfFieldAssignment`) and never reaches `setValue` below.
		if binding.isSelfField 'selfField'
			try self.parseSelfFieldAssignment(func, block: block, nameTok: nameTok, binding: binding)
			return
		end 'selfField'

		try self.requireMutableBinding(binding, nameTok: nameTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **E3070's REBIND DOOR — and shv2 must REFUSE where the oracle merely forgets the borrow.**
		// `arr = other` drops the record `arr` held (`reassignBindingValue` decrefs it), so every element
		// borrowed out of it is freed: MEASURED **0xC0000005** on `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise ""` +
		// `arr = StringArray.create()` + `print("{s}")`, where the oracle prints the string and exits 0. It
		// gets away with it because it RETAINS on `get` and so simply KILLS the borrow at a reassignment
		// (`BorrowCheckPass.cs:129-132`); shv2 takes no reference there, so killing the borrow would be
		// unsound and the only two answers left are retain-on-get or refuse. This rung IS the refusal.
		self.noteLocalRebind(func, nameIndex: nameIndex, anchor: nameTok)

		let rhsStart = self.pos
		let parsedValue = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)
		// WHICH local the RHS was a bare reference to (`s = t`), empty for every other RHS. Only that shape
		// can MOVE an owned value out of a source binding; every other RHS is a consume. The NAME rather
		// than the bool, because the rebind asks two questions of it — may this poison anything, and was
		// that local declared `let` (`rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias`) — and one walk answers both. Captured
		// off the token position, before any further token is consumed (see `rhsBareLocalSourceName`).
		let rhsBareLocalName = self.rhsBareLocalSourceName(rhsStart)

		// ⭐ AN ASSIGNMENT IS AN INTEGER-EXPECTING POSITION when the binding declares an integral type, so a
		// character literal RHS coerces to its codepoint — `b = '0'` into a `var b Byte`. A `Character` binding
		// needs nothing: the literal already IS one. See `integerizedOperand`.
		let declaredTag = maxonTypeTag(binding.varType)
		let value = try self.integerizedOperand(parsedValue, expected: declaredTag, start: rhsStart, pastEnd: self.pos)

		try self.completeLocalRebind(func, binding: binding, nameTok: nameTok, value: value, rhsBareLocalName: rhsBareLocalName)
	end 'parseAssignment'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3070's LOCAL REBIND DOOR — the guard and the subject derivation, in one place because TWO
	// statements open it.** `x = <expr>` and a tuple assignment's BARE target (`(x, y) = f()`) rebind the
	// same storage under the same name token, so they owe the same note; the two sibling doors
	// (`storeToGlobal`, `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore`) keep their own because their SUBJECT is derived
	// differently, and that difference is exactly what each of their headers is about.
	function noteLocalRebind(func IrFunction, nameIndex TokenIndex, anchor Token)
		if self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'mayConflictWithABorrow'
			self.noteBorrowSubjectRebind(func, subjectName: self.borrowSubjectNameAt(nameIndex), anchor: anchor)
		end 'mayConflictWithABorrow'
	end 'noteLocalRebind'

	// ⭐⭐ **A LOCAL REBIND, FROM THE TYPE CHECK TO THE STORE — everything `x = <expr>` still owes once its
	// value exists.** SHARED with a tuple assignment's bare target, which arrives here holding an element of
	// an already-evaluated tuple rather than a freshly parsed expression. A second copy of this list is not
	// a duplication risk in the abstract: it is the concrete way a rebind comes to skip the aggregate-retype
	// refusal, or to store past a cell, or to leave a mutated payload unwritten.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CHECKS THAT RUN *BEFORE* THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE ARE DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE** —
	// `requireMutableBinding` and `noteLocalRebind` are asked by each caller at ITS OWN point. For
	// `parseAssignment` that point is before the RHS is parsed, and moving it after would reorder two true
	// complaints about one statement (`let x = 0` / `x = nosuch()` reports E2013 today, the RHS's error
	// afterwards). A tuple assignment cannot ask them there — its RHS is evaluated once, ahead of every
	// target — so it asks them per target, in target order, which is the order its own pins read in.
	function completeLocalRebind(func IrFunction, binding VarInfo, nameTok Token, value ValueId, rhsBareLocalName ByteArray) throws ParseError
		let declaredTag = maxonTypeTag(binding.varType)
		let valueTag = self.tagOf(value)

		// ⭐ The binding KEEPS ITS DECLARED TYPE ACROSS A REBIND, so `x = 3` into a `var x = 0.0` rebinds
		// to a WIDENED float and not to the raw integer. Without the coercion the binding silently became
		// an int: `x == 3.0` on the next line was E3005 ("cannot compare int with float") against a
		// perfectly legal program, and a rebind INSIDE A LOOP fed an i64 and an f64 into one header phi —
		// measured, a cross-register-file panic in the x64 emitter.
		let assigned = try self.assignableValue(nameTok, value: value, declared: declaredTag, declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(binding.varType), door: FunctionTypeDoor.variable, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.assignedPlace)

		// ⭐ OPEN #54/#59: reassigning a `var` to the WRONG named aggregate — a DIFFERENT struct/union, OR
		// an aggregate where the binding declares a SCALAR — is a retype the tag check above cannot see
		// (`structRef == structRef` for any two structs; a boxed union and a ranged-int alias share
		// `named`); the overwritten box would later be dropped under the binding's declared destructor —
		// a wild free / leak. `assignableValue`'s verdict stays tag-based (its scalar-coercion job is, and
		// it is SHARED with the global-store and field callers). Identity is the interned name, EXACT.
		// Un-gated — both names are computed unconditionally, empty for a scalar with no allocation — and
		// `displayTypeNameOf` prints the scalar side's primitive, not an empty name.
		//
		// It reads the PARSED value, not the coerced one: a widening only ever produces a float scalar, so
		// both aggregate names are empty on that path and the question is the same one either way.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TWO NAMES DECIDE THE VERDICT AND DO NOT SPELL IT** (user ruling, 2026-08-04). They are
		// IDENTITIES — for a generic instance, the canonical `Box_Leaf` two values are compared on — and
		// reusing one as a spelling put a name no source line holds into the sentence. The message asks
		// `displayTypeNameOf`, which is the same door the ARGUMENT voice already renders through, so the
		// author's `LeafBox` is what both say.
		let declaredAggregate = self.aggregateNameOf(declaredTag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(binding.varType))
		let assignedAggregate = self.aggregateNameOf(valueTag, nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
		if self.signatures.aggregatesConflict(declaredAggregate, expectedTag: declaredTag, expectedNameId: typeNameIdOf(binding.varType), got: assignedAggregate, gotTag: valueTag, gotNameId: self.valueNameId(value)) 'aggregateReassignMismatch'
			throw ParseError.assignTypeMismatch(assignedPlacePhrase(FunctionTypeDoor.variable, name: nameTok.value, owner: EmptySourceText), got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), expected: self.displayTypeNameOf(declaredTag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(binding.varType)), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'aggregateReassignMismatch'

		// ⭐⭐ **A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING IS REBOUND BY STORING, NOT BY `Scope.setValue` (PBR-2).** It never
		// reaches `reassignBindingValue`: that routine's whole job is to move a binding from one SSA value to
		// another and release what the old one owned, and a cell binding holds no SSA value to move — its
		// contents live in memory, where a later read will find them. The release of the displaced occupant is
		// the same obligation, discharged against the memory rather than against a value id.
		if binding.isCellBinding 'livesInACell'
			try self.reassignCellBinding(binding, nameTok: nameTok, value: assigned, rhsBareLocalName: rhsBareLocalName)
			return
		end 'livesInACell'

		try self.reassignBindingValue(func, binding: binding, nameTok: nameTok, value: assigned, rhsBareLocalName: rhsBareLocalName)

		// ⭐ A MATCH-ARM PAYLOAD BINDING IS ALSO A PLACE IN THE SCRUTINEE'S BOX, so the rebind above is only
		// half of the write — the other half is the store that makes a later `match` read the new value.
		// AFTER the rebind, deliberately: the box takes its reference to the value the BINDING ended up
		// holding, which for a borrowed RHS is the owned copy `reassignBindingValue` promoted it to.
		if binding.isPayloadBinding 'writeBackIntoTheBox'
			try self.writeBackPayload(func, binding: binding, nameTok: nameTok)
		end 'writeBackIntoTheBox'
	end 'completeLocalRebind'

	// ⭐⭐ **WRITE A MUTATED PAYLOAD BINDING BACK INTO THE SCRUTINEE'S BOX** — the exact inverse of
	// `emitUnionPayloadLoad`, and the whole of `specs/mutable-enums.md`. It stores through
	// `emitIndirectStore` (THE one `storeIndirect` emit) at the offset and width the LOAD committed to,
	// carried on the binding rather than re-derived (see `VarInfo.isPayloadBinding`).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BOX TAKES ITS OWN REFERENCE; THE BINDING KEEPS ITS OWN.** After the store the value has TWO
	// owners — the box, whose `__destruct_<U>` cascade releases the slot, and the binding, which
	// `dropArmScopedOwned` releases at the arm's exit — so a MANAGED payload is increfed here. It is the
	// same unconditional, structural refcount `retainBorrowedPayload` takes for the mirror-image case (a
	// binding retained out of a box somebody else owns), and it balances for the same reason: shv2's
	// release is structural, so an unconditional acquire is unconditionally released.
	//
	// ⚠ **v1's TRAP DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND ITS COMMENT SAYS SO FROM THE OTHER SIDE.** v1 DELETED the manual
	// incref/decref triple at its own payload store (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:12594-12632` — the
	// "mutable-enums garbage-header over-release") because its `InsertRefcounts` dataflow pass supplied the
	// same pair and the two DOUBLED. shv2 has no such pass; the ops emitted here are the only accounting
	// there is, so omitting them would leak the displaced value and free the new one twice.
	//
	// ⚠ **"MANAGED" IS READ FROM `ownedBindings` MEMBERSHIP, not from a bit copied onto the binding.** The
	// binding is in the owned set iff its payload is managed (`ownPayloadBinding` is the only enroller, and
	// a reassignment cannot change the answer — a scalar binding's declared type refuses a heap RHS), so
	// membership IS the fact, already single-homed in `bindingIsOwned`. A second column would be free to
	// disagree with the set that actually emits the drop.
	function writeBackPayload(func IrFunction, binding VarInfo, nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		// E3064's seed, as at `emitFieldWrite`: this stores into the SCRUTINEE's box, an aggregate that already
		// existed and that the caller may still be holding. The second of the two assignment emitters.
		func.assignsIntoAnExistingAggregate = true

		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)
		let value = binding.boundValue
		let managed = self.bindingIsOwned(binding)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN ESCAPE ROUTE OF ITS OWN, GUARDED HERE BECAUSE IT IS *THIS STORE* THAT ESCAPES.** A
		// payload binding looks like a plain local — assigning a capturing closure to a real local is legal and
		// must stay legal — so the refusal cannot live at the assignment. What makes this one an escape is the
		// line below: the environment block lives in THIS frame and the box outlives it. Sitting on the emitter,
		// the guard cannot be routed around by a second assignment path.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SHELVED CASE'S DIVERGENCE ARGUMENT DIED WHEN THE WRITE-BACK WAS BUILT.** It read *"shv2
		// payload bindings are IMMUTABLE frame-local COPIES … shv2 never writes back through a payload binding,
		// so the E3099 escape route does not exist here"*, and E2013 was doing the refusing. `31c4435de` built
		// mutable payload bindings with exactly that write-back (`specs/mutable-enums.md`), which made the
		// premise false and the route real — the store this function emits IS the alias into the heap box the
		// note said shv2 has not got.
		//
		// It is anchored at the BINDING, not at the closure literal: SemanticCheck's whole-program backstop
		// already catches the raw `storeIndirect` and words it *"into memory that outlives this frame"* at the
		// RHS, which names nothing the author can act on. This is the same relationship every other route has
		// with that backstop — the specific voice first, the generic one only for stores no parser door owns.
		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(value, message: "cannot store a closure that captures in payload binding '{String.from(binding.name)}': {CapturingClosureEscapeSuffix}", tok: nameTok)

		// ⛔ **THE PREMISE, STATED LOUDLY: A MANAGED PAYLOAD REACHING A WRITE-BACK WAS MOVED OUT.** The store
		// below overwrites the slot without releasing what it held, which is sound ONLY because a move-out
		// nulled it. `declarePayloadBindings` guarantees that by making a RETAINED managed payload read-only
		// — and it did not, until a measured exit-101 leak said so (see the `borrowedRetain` arm). A third
		// acquisition path, or a mutability rule that drifts from the acquisition again, lands here; it must
		// stop here rather than emit a store that strands the box's reference.
		if managed and not binding.payloadSlotVacated 'retainedManagedWrite'
			panic("writeBackPayload: managed payload binding '{String.from(binding.name)}' is writable but its slot was never vacated — a retained payload's box still owns the slot, so this store would strand that reference; `declarePayloadBindings` must not mint such a binding mutable")
		end 'retainedManagedWrite'

		if managed 'boxBecomesASecondOwner'
			self.emitIncref(value, range: range)
		end 'boxBecomesASecondOwner'

		self.emitIndirectStore(binding.payloadScrutBox, offset: binding.payloadSlotOffset, value: value, storageType: binding.payloadStorage, range: range)

		// ⭐ **THE SLOT IS WHOLE AGAIN, so the scrutinee may stop being partially moved — BUT ONLY IF NO
		// OTHER BINDING OF THE SAME BOX IS STILL HOLDING A HOLE.** `values(a String, b String)` with only
		// `a` assigned leaves `b`'s slot null; clearing the bit there would let the code after the match
		// re-bind `b` and dereference a null payload. So the bit is RECOMPUTED from the live bindings
		// rather than assumed, and `mutate-string-payload`'s second `match n` is legal because that
		// recomputation genuinely finds no hole left.
		if binding.payloadSlotVacated 'holeFilled'
			binding.payloadSlotVacated = false
			self.setBindingPartiallyMoved(binding.payloadScrutBox, value: self.boxHasVacatedPayloadSlot(binding.payloadScrutBox))
		end 'holeFilled'
	end 'writeBackPayload'

	// Does any LIVE payload binding still have its slot in `box` nulled? The honest half of the
	// `partiallyMoved` recomputation above. It scans `ownedBindings` because that is exactly where a
	// vacated binding lives — only a MANAGED payload is moved out, and only a managed payload binding is
	// enrolled (`ownPayloadBinding`) — and those entries are popped at the arm's own exit
	// (`dropArmScopedOwned`), which is also the last point a write-back can be parsed.
	function boxHasVacatedPayloadSlot(box ValueId) returns bool
		for owned in self.ownedBindings 'each'
			if owned.isPayloadBinding and owned.payloadSlotVacated and owned.payloadScrutBox == box 'stillEmpty'
				return true
			end 'stillEmpty'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'boxHasVacatedPayloadSlot'

	// ⭐ **REBIND A `var` TO A NEW VALUE, RELEASING WHAT IT OWNED.** The rebinding half is one line —
	// `Scope.setValue` — but a `var` that owns a heap allocation (an owned interpolation String, a
	// struct box) must DROP that allocation as it is overwritten, or it leaks: a binding's scope-exit
	// drop only ever reaches the LAST value it was assigned, so every earlier one is lost.
	//
	// The bug is MASKED at this rung and stops being masked the moment `__mm_free` reclaims: today the
	// bump allocator recycles nothing, and the leak counter nets to zero by coincidence — the
	// overwritten value that leaks and the last value (drained as a statement temporary AND dropped at
	// scope exit) that double-frees cancel one-for-one. With a real free list the same miscompile is a
	// leak, a double-free and a use-after-free at once.
	//
	// ⚠ **OWNERSHIP IS BINDING-LEVEL: `wasOwned` reads membership in `ownedBindings` (`bindingIsOwned`),
	// NEVER `valueIsOwnedHeap(oldValue)`.** A loop-carried `var`'s current value is a header phi, and a
	// phi carries no owned-heap provenance bit (the bit rides the value flowing INTO the merge, not the
	// merge) — so the value column reads `false` for a binding the loop genuinely owns, and the
	// overwritten allocation would leak every iteration. Membership is the only record that survives the
	// phi. `newIsOwned` is `valueIsOwnedHeap` — the SAME predicate `parseBinding` enrols a fresh binding by —
	// so the reassign and the declaration cannot drift on what "owned" means.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN OWNED BINDING STAYS OWNED — a borrowed RHS is PROMOTED to an owned copy rather than the
	// binding being removed from `ownedBindings`.** Removal would be a middle-of-stack delete, and
	// `ownedBindings` is a STACK whose heights the block/loop drop marks (`BlockMark.ownedCount`,
	// `LoopContext.ownedFloor`) index into: reassigning an OUTER owned `var` to a literal inside a
	// nested block would delete a below-mark entry, shift every block-local drop down one slot, and
	// strand a block-local binding to be dropped at function exit on a path where its value is not even
	// defined (a register-allocator "use dominates def" panic, verified). Promoting the literal to an
	// owned heap copy — the SAME fused-record copy a borrowed `return` makes (`promoteToOwnedString`) —
	// keeps every reassignment of an owned binding a uniform owned→owned: the binding never moves, and
	// an `if`/loop merge is handled by the phi machinery exactly as for any other carried value.
	// ⭐ **ALLOCATE A CELL — one machine word on the heap, with the trivial destructor every shv2 box carries.**
	// The ONE allocation site, shared by a cell-resident LOCAL and an r-value SCRATCH cell, so the two cannot
	// come to disagree about a cell's size or its drop.
	//
	// ⚠ **HEAP AND NOT A STACK SLOT, WHICH IS THE RUNG'S RULED DESIGN AND NOT A CONVENIENCE.** shv2 has no
	// addressable locals — a binding is an SSA value, there is no `stackAddr` and no mem2reg — so there is no
	// stack address to hand a callee; and `wasm32-wasi` cannot take the address of a local at all, so both
	// reference compilers' stack-slot design would have to box into linear memory there anyway.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CELL VALUE IS TYPED AS WHAT IT HOLDS, NOT AS THE POINTER IT IS — AND THAT IS THE DECISION
	// THAT KEEPS EVERY TYPE RULE IN THE COMPILER WORKING ON A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING.** The cell stands for the
	// binding, and the binding's type is its declared one, so `SemanticCheck.checkArgTypes` compares a `Node`
	// against a `Node` at a by-reference argument, `enumAccessorFieldAt` still sees a `named` value where an
	// enum accessor is legal, and `requireStructBase` still finds a layout. MEASURED as the alternative:
	// tagged `integer`, every one of those read the ADDRESS's type and answered about the wrong thing —
	// `argument type mismatch for 'cur': expected 'Node', got 'int'` on six cases and
	// `a field access on 'c', which is declared 'int'` on a seventh.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ONE READER THAT MUST NOT BELIEVE IT IS THE DROP ROUTER, AND IT IS HANDLED WHERE IT LIVES.**
	// `decrefCalleeFor` routes on the tag, so a `Node`-tagged cell would be freed through `__destruct_Node` —
	// running a struct's cascade over an 8-byte box. `emitScopeDrops`' cell arm therefore frees the box
	// EXPLICITLY (`__mm_decref`) instead of asking the router, and releases the occupant through the router
	// by LOADING it first — which is the value the router is actually right about.
	//
	// The ABI is unaffected: which parameters are addresses is `IrFunction.reassignedParamMask`'s answer, and
	// the lowering reads that rather than a tag.
	function allocateCell(declared MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		return self.emitBoxAlloc(self.emitLiteral(CellPayloadBytes, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), resultTag: maxonTypeTag(declared), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(declared), range: range, tag: MmTag.ParameterCell)
	end 'allocateCell'

	// Move `value` into a freshly allocated cell and hand the cell back — the shared body of the two things
	// that put a value into one for the first time: a cell-resident LOCAL's initializer and an r-value
	// SCRATCH cell's spill. Both owe the identical three steps (allocate, take ownership, store), and the
	// ownership step is the one that must not be written twice: a managed value moved into durable storage
	// has to be de-listed from the statement's pending drops, or the statement end frees what the cell holds.
	function fillFreshCell(value ValueId, declared MaxonType, rhsWasBareLocal bool, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let cell = self.allocateCell(declared, range: range)
		var stored = value
		if self.signatures.typeIsManaged(declared, from: self.artifact.typeNames) 'cellTakesOwnership'
			stored = try self.moveManagedValueInto(value, argBareLocal: rhsWasBareLocal, isString: tagIsByteRecord(maxonTypeTag(declared)), range: range)
		end 'cellTakesOwnership'

		self.emitIndirectStore(cell, offset: CellContentOffset, value: stored, storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(self.resolveFloatAliasType(declared)), range: range)
		// The FIRST value into a cell hands it W117's mark exactly as a later rebind does (`emitCellStore`) —
		// asked of `value` rather than of `stored`, because the ownership move above mints the occupant and it
		// is the incoming value the promotion marked.
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: value, to: cell)
		return cell
	end 'fillFreshCell'

	// ⭐⭐ **DECLARE A CELL-RESIDENT LOCAL (PBR-2).** The cell is this frame's: it is enrolled in
	// `ownedBindings`, so `emitScopeDrops` releases what it holds and then frees it — the two-step drop that
	// header describes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT PUSHED INTO `mutableVars`, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE DESIGN.** A
	// loop header mints a phi for every mutable binding in scope that the token scan sees assigned; a cell
	// binding must mint NONE, because its name denotes a pointer that never changes and its value lives in
	// memory. Tracking it would hand `installLoopPhi` a binding whose `boundValue` is the CELL and let the
	// header rebind that — turning the address into a phi of addresses, one per iteration, of which only the
	// first is real.
	//
	// ⚠ The E3012 candidacy and the pending-borrow attachment are owed exactly as they are by a value
	// binding: it is the same declaration wearing a different representation, and neither diagnostic asks
	// anything about how a name is stored.
	// ⚠ It returns NOTHING, deliberately. It used to hand the binding back and its one caller — a VOID
	// function — wrote `return try self.declareCellLocal(…)`, which produced an owned `VarInfo` that nothing
	// dropped: MEASURED as `MM leak: 7 allocation(s) remain` in the COMPILER, on every program that declared a
	// cell-resident local. Nothing wants the binding back; the scope and `ownedBindings` both hold it.
	function declareCellLocal(func IrFunction, nameTok Token, boundValue ValueId, rhsWasBareLocal bool) throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)
		let declared = self.typeOf(boundValue)
		let cell = try self.fillFreshCell(boundValue, declared: declared, rhsWasBareLocal: rhsWasBareLocal, range: range)
		var binding = try self.scope.declareCellBinding(nameTok.value, varType: declared, mutable: true, cell: cell, isParameter: false) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(nameTok.value), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		self.attachPendingBorrows(func, borrower: binding, boundValue: boundValue, liveUntil: self.pos)

		// `sole` — a fresh `__mm_alloc` three lines up whose pointer nothing else has been handed. The BOX is
		// what this frame owns; what it holds is released through it (`emitScopeDrops`).
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(cell, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
	end 'declareCellLocal'

	// ⭐⭐ **SPILL AN R-VALUE INTO A SCRATCH CELL FOR A BY-REFERENCE ARGUMENT (PBR-2)** — `reassign(41)`,
	// `reassign(makeVal())`, `reassign(Node.create(1))`. An r-value has no caller-side binding and therefore no
	// cell, but the callee's ABI still expects an address, so the caller materializes one.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SLOT IS AN ANONYMOUS CELL BINDING, NOT A THIRD KIND OF TEMPORARY**, which is what gives it a
	// correct drop for free: `emitScopeDrops` already releases a cell's occupant and then the cell, so the
	// FINAL occupant — whether the callee replaced it or not — is released exactly once. That is precisely the
	// discipline `specs/refcount-byref-rvalue-scratch-slot.md` describes, and both of v1's bugs there were in
	// the machinery this shape does not need: it identifies the slot by WHAT IT IS rather than by an id
	// ordering (`isByRefRvalueScratchSlot`'s `slotId >= slotMaxonTypes.count()`), so there is no sweep to
	// widen and no uninitialised stack to decref — the cell is written by the spill before anything can read
	// it, and it is a fresh allocation rather than a slot the entry zero-init loop might or might not cover.
	// `buildClosureEnv`'s env block is the same "anonymous owned thing on the binding stack" shape.
	//
	// ⚠ It drops at the enclosing BLOCK's exit rather than at the statement's, which is later than strictly
	// needed and is the conservative direction: the callee may have stored the argument somewhere the caller
	// still reads through this statement, and a block is the smallest region the drop machinery indexes.
	function materializeByRefScratchCell(value ValueId, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(anchor)
		let declared = self.typeOf(value)
		// An r-value is not a bare local reference by construction — the call site only reaches here when the
		// argument did NOT resolve to a cell-resident binding — so it moves nothing out of another name.
		let cell = try self.fillFreshCell(value, declared: declared, rhsWasBareLocal: false, range: range)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(cell, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		self.ownedBindings.push(VarInfo.createCellBinding(EmptySourceText, varType: declared, mutable: false, cell: cell, isParameter: false))
		return cell
	end 'materializeByRefScratchCell'

	// ⭐⭐ **REBIND A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING (PBR-2) — THE WRITE A CALLER OBSERVES.** The exact shape
	// `emitCheckedGlobalStore` already uses for module storage, one storage kind over, and for the same reason:
	// a durable slot's write is THREE things and not one — take ownership of the new value, RELEASE the record
	// the slot was holding, then store.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.** The release must come AFTER the new value is settled
	// (`n = Node.create(n.value + 1)` reads the old occupant while building the new one) and BEFORE the store
	// overwrites the only pointer to it. `reassignBindingValue`'s own header states the same rule for a `var`:
	// *"release the overwritten allocation BEFORE the rebind, while `oldValue` still names it"*.
	//
	// ⚠ **UNLIKE THE GLOBAL STORE, THE DISPLACED VALUE IS LOADED RATHER THAN REMEMBERED.** A cell's occupant
	// may have been written by a CALLEE through the very pointer this binding holds, so an SSA value the parser
	// captured earlier is not what the cell contains — releasing that would free a record the cell no longer
	// holds and leak the one it does. The load is what the cell actually holds at this instant, which is the
	// only thing a release may act on. (`emitOpaqueSlotReleases` reaches the identical conclusion for a
	// writable tuple slot.)
	//
	// ⚠ **A SCALAR CELL IS A BARE STORE.** No release is owed and none is emitted, so a by-reference `int`,
	// `float`, `bool`, byte or enum parameter costs exactly one `storeIndirect` per rebind.
	function reassignCellBinding(binding VarInfo, nameTok Token, value ValueId, rhsBareLocalName ByteArray) throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)
		if not self.signatures.typeIsManaged(binding.varType, from: self.artifact.typeNames) 'scalarCell'
			self.emitCellStore(binding, value: value, range: range)
			return
		end 'scalarCell'

		// The cell is DURABLE STORAGE, exactly as a global's slot or a struct field is, so the new value is
		// stored through the one door all of them share: a borrowed RHS (an element read out of a container, a
		// String literal) is promoted to something the cell may own and drop, and an owned temporary is de-listed
		// from the statement's pending drops so the statement end does not free what the cell now holds.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND AN OWNED VALUE A LIVE BINDING KEEPS IS CO-OWNED — SO THIS IS THE ONE REBIND THAT IS NOT A
		// MOVE (⚖ user ruling, 2026-08-12).** `byRefParam = t` reads as a rebind and is a durable STORE, because
		// the cell is the CALLER's storage and outlives this frame: the caller will read what is written here
		// long after `t`'s scope has ended. So the cell takes its own reference and `t` stays live with its own
		// — where a plain local rebind (`s = t`, `reassignBindingValue`) still MOVES, the new name inheriting the
		// drop the old one owed. `moveManagedValueInto`'s header states the rule and names this exception; the
		// two are stated together because "a rebind moves" is true of one door and false of this one.
		let owned = try self.moveManagedValueInto(value, argBareLocal: not rhsBareLocalName.isEmpty(), isString: tagIsByteRecord(maxonTypeTag(binding.varType)), range: range)
		self.emitDecref(self.emitCellLoad(binding.varType, box: binding.boundValue, range: range), range: range)
		self.emitCellStore(binding, value: owned, range: range)
	end 'reassignCellBinding'

	function reassignBindingValue(func IrFunction, binding VarInfo, nameTok Token, value ValueId, rhsBareLocalName ByteArray) throws ParseError
		let oldValue = binding.boundValue
		let rhsWasBareLocal = not rhsBareLocalName.isEmpty()

		// ⭐⭐ **AN IMMUTABLE SOURCE ALIASES WHERE A MUTABLE ONE MOVES (the ownership ruling, 2026-08-04),
		// AND THIS DOOR IS WHERE THE DECLARATION ONE'S EXEMPTION WAS MISSING.** Read once, here, because the
		// two halves of what it means are spent far apart: the poison below is SKIPPED, and the reference
		// that skip makes this binding owe is taken at the promotion point.
		let aliasesImmutableSource = rhsWasBareLocal and self.rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias(rhsBareLocalName)

		// A SELF-REBIND OF A LIVE BINDING — it is assigned the value it already holds AND still owns, so
		// the value and its ownership are both unchanged: nothing to drop and nothing to reclassify.
		// Guarding here is also what keeps a loop self-assign — where `oldValue` is a provenance-free phi —
		// from being misread and re-tracked (the phi carries no owned-heap bit, so the general path would
		// `promoteBorrowed` it), and prevents a drop-then-keep (decref'ing a value the binding still holds).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TEST IS VALUE IDENTITY, NOT SPELLING, AND THE SPELLING `s = s` NO LONGER REACHES IT** —
		// it is refused at the door as E3067 (see `rejectDegenerateAssignment`).
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **BUT VALUE IDENTITY ALONE IS THE WRONG QUESTION WHEN THE BINDING IS MOVED-FROM — THAT IS
		// OWNERSHIP BEING HANDED BACK, THE EXACT OPPOSITE OF A NO-OP.** A move does not mint a value, it
		// re-homes one: `var b = a` gives `b` `a`'s very ValueId, so `a = b` satisfies `oldValue == value`
		// while meaning *`a` owns it again and `b` must not*. Eliding it left BOTH halves undone — `a`
		// still poisoned (E3102 on the next read of it) and `b` still live — i.e. a statement with no
		// effect whatsoever. `specs/optimizer-refcount.md`'s whole-program baseline is the pin.
		//
		// The gate sends only that shape down the general path below, which already does the entire job
		// and adds no refcount traffic (the value genuinely does not change): `moveOnAssign` poisons the
		// source, `wasOwned` is false so nothing is decref'd, `inOwnedSet` is true so the revived binding
		// is not re-enrolled, and the revive clears the bit — one box, still exactly one drop, now owed by
		// this binding instead of the source's.
		if oldValue == value and not binding.movedFrom 'selfAssign'
			self.scope.setValue(nameTok.value, value: value)
			return
		end 'selfAssign'

		// ⭐ **RE-ARM A PROMISE BINDING (P1.5-B2 #88).** A promise is a scalar green-thread handle with a
		// bespoke drop (`__gt_promise_drop`), never COW-promoted — so a binding that owns (or receives) a
		// thread MUST be handled here, before the heap move/promotion path below (which would `promoteBorrowed`
		// a scalar and panic).
		let oldIsPromise = self.valueOwnsGreenThread(oldValue)
		let newIsPromise = self.valueOwnsGreenThread(value)
		if oldIsPromise or newIsPromise 'promiseReassign'
			// ⚠ **"STILL OWNS A LIVE THREAD" IS READ FROM MEMBERSHIP, NOT `valueOwnsGreenThread(oldValue)`.** A
			// loop-header / if-merge phi carries no promise mark, so the value bit is phi-BLIND — a re-armed
			// `var p` across a merge holds that unmarked phi as `oldValue`. Membership is the SAME source the
			// heap path reads for exactly this reason (`wasOwned` below), which single-sources the drop
			// decision so promise- and heap-provenance cannot drift across a phi again (Finding 1).
			let ownsLiveThread = not binding.movedFrom and self.bindingIsOwned(binding)

			// `p = q` — rebinding a promise binding to an ALIAS is refused: it would give one thread two owners
			// (a double drop) or orphan this binding's own thread. A fresh `async` spawn is the only legal
			// promise RHS; `await` one name before re-binding.
			if newIsPromise and rhsWasBareLocal 'aliasRebind'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("a promise binding ('{String.from(nameTok.value)}') cannot be re-armed from another promise binding — only a fresh `async` spawn may re-arm it; `await` one name before re-binding", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'aliasRebind'

			// ⭐ **FINDING 2 — re-arming would DROP a thread another name still ALIASES.** `let q = p` gives
			// this binding's thread a second name without enrolling it (a promise aliases, it does not move);
			// re-arming `p` here drops the thread while `q` still names it, so a later `await q` reclaims freed
			// memory — a UAF E3100 cannot catch (it is not a double await). Refuse it (a compile error, not a
			// miscompile). Gated on `ownsLiveThread`, so a re-arm AFTER the thread was awaited (the committed
			// `double-await-alias-outlives-rebind`, whose re-arm drops nothing) stays a linearity check, not this.
			if ownsLiveThread and self.promiseThreadHasLiveAlias(func, reArmedName: nameTok.value, thread: oldValue) 'aliasedThreadRearm'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("cannot re-arm the promise binding ('{String.from(nameTok.value)}'): its green thread is still named by an alias, so dropping it here would leave that alias dangling — `await` through one name before re-arming", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'aliasedThreadRearm'

			// Drop (cancel) the old thread iff this binding still OWNS a live one — membership-based, so it
			// fires even when `oldValue` is a phi (a re-armed `var` across a loop / branch). A binding whose
			// thread an `await` consumed (`movedFrom`) owns nothing, so reassigning it to a scalar is fine and
			// drops nothing (Finding 4). `emitDecref` routes an owned promise to `__gt_promise_drop`.
			if ownsLiveThread 'dropOldThread'
				self.emitDecref(oldValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
			end 'dropOldThread'

			let alreadyInSet = self.bindingIsOwned(binding) or binding.movedFrom
			self.scope.setValue(nameTok.value, value: value)
			if newIsPromise 'revivePromise'
				// The binding owns the fresh thread — revive it for reads and its scope-exit drop, and enrol
				// once (a re-armed promise is already in the set as a moved ghost; a non-promise binding first
				// taking a promise — `var x = 5; x = async f()` — joins now).
				binding.movedFrom = false
				binding.partiallyMoved = false
				if not alreadyInSet 'enrolPromise'
					self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
				end 'enrolPromise'
			end 'revivePromise' else 'becameScalar'
				// A non-promise value (`p = 5` after a consuming `await`, or after the live thread was dropped
				// just above): the binding now owns nothing droppable. It STAYS in `ownedBindings` (mid-stack
				// removal would desync the block/loop drop marks), so it is marked MOVED to make `emitScopeDrops`
				// skip it — a bare scalar must not be `__mm_decref`'d. Reads still work: `requireBindingLive`
				// admits any scalar-tagged binding (a scalar is never an unsafely-moved heap value).
				binding.movedFrom = true
				binding.partiallyMoved = false
			end 'becameScalar'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(value)
			return
		end 'promiseReassign'

		// ⭐ **MOVE-ON-ASSIGN.** A bare reference to an ALREADY-owned MUTABLE binding as the RHS (`s = t`)
		// MOVES that value: poison the source so a later read of it is use-after-move and only the new owner
		// drops the value. A fresh owned temp is held by no binding — a CONSUME, not a move. Gated on
		// `rhsWasBareLocal`, so a consume never pays the owned-set scan; run BEFORE `setValue` below, so
		// the scan matches the SOURCE's value rather than this binding's (which `setValue` is about to
		// overwrite with the moved value).
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AN IMMUTABLE SOURCE IS EXEMPT, EXACTLY AS IT IS AT THE DECLARATION DOOR.** `a = t` over a
		// `let t` is a second REFERENCE (`rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias`), so nothing is poisoned and every
		// name stays readable — the loop the harness's `parseProgramArgs` is built from hands ONE
		// per-iteration `let next` to two outer cursors and only ever reads all three. It also drops the
		// loop-escape refusal `poisonOwningBinding` raises, and that is the refusal becoming inapplicable
		// rather than being suppressed: it exists because a MOVE across a back edge has no reconciliation,
		// and an alias has nothing to reconcile — each iteration takes one reference and releases the one
		// the previous iteration took.
		if rhsWasBareLocal and not aliasesImmutableSource 'moveOnAssign'
			try self.poisonMoveSource(value, anchor: nameTok)
		end 'moveOnAssign'

		// Is `binding` a LIVE owner (owes a drop of `oldValue`), and/or already enrolled in
		// `ownedBindings`? `binding.movedFrom` is read DIRECTLY, not via `bindingIsOwned`: a moved-from
		// binding shares its stale `boundValue` with the binding it moved to, so a value-keyed membership
		// test would find that new owner and wrongly report the moved-from binding as still owning a
		// drop. A moved-from binding owns NOTHING (its value belongs to its new owner), so it drops
		// nothing here — but it IS still in `ownedBindings`, so a revive must not re-enrol it.
		let wasMovedFrom = binding.movedFrom
		let wasOwned = not wasMovedFrom and self.bindingIsOwned(binding)
		let inOwnedSet = wasOwned or wasMovedFrom

		// Promote a borrowed value assigned to a binding that is (or stays) in the owned set to an owned
		// value, so the binding keeps owning a droppable one rather than being deleted from the middle of
		// the owned-binding stack (see the header). Two shapes reach here: a borrowed String
		// (owned→borrowed `s = "literal"`, or a moved-from String revived with a literal), COPIED; and a
		// borrowed struct/union (`q = p2`, reassigning a retained OPEN #40 aggregate var to another borrowed
		// parameter — same aggregate type, so `requireAssignable` admits it), INCREF'd. The same
		// `promoteBorrowedToOwned` the declaration routes through, so the two sites cannot answer the
		// String-vs-aggregate question differently: string-promoting a borrowed struct here blits its fields
		// as a fused String record, a silent miscompile.
		let sourceValueIsOwned = self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value)
		var newValue = value
		var newIsOwned = sourceValueIsOwned
		if inOwnedSet and not newIsOwned 'promoteBorrowed'
			newValue = try self.promoteBorrowedToOwned(value, door: BorrowLaunderDoor.binding, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
			newIsOwned = true
		end 'promoteBorrowed'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE REFERENCE THE SKIPPED POISON LEFT THIS BINDING OWING.** An immutable source keeps the
		// drop it has always owed, so this binding — enrolled below, or already in the owned set — cannot
		// INHERIT one and must hold its own (`coOwnImmutableRebindSource`). Two names, two references, two
		// releases: without it the alias and its source both drop one box, which the leak gate reports as
		// exit 101, and the destination may be the LONGER-LIVED of the two (an outer `var` taking a
		// loop-body `let`), so nothing here can lean on the source outliving it the way the declaration
		// door does.
		//
		// ⚠ It reads the SOURCE value's ownership, not `newIsOwned`, which is what makes it exclusive with
		// the promotion above by construction rather than by ordering: that arm runs only on a BORROWED
		// value and has already minted a reference this binding owns outright, so a retain on top of its
		// result would leak exactly one box.
		if aliasesImmutableSource and sourceValueIsOwned 'coOwnImmutableSource'
			newValue = self.coOwnImmutableRebindSource(value, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
		end 'coOwnImmutableSource'

		// Release the overwritten allocation BEFORE the rebind, while `oldValue` still names it. Only a
		// LIVE owner has one to release: a moved-from binding's old value belongs to the binding it moved
		// to, which drops it — decref-ing it here would double-free.
		if wasOwned 'dropOld'
			self.emitDecref(oldValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
		end 'dropOld'

		self.scope.setValue(nameTok.value, value: newValue)

		// A WRITE REVIVES the binding: it owns `newValue` now, so a later read of it is legal again. Both
		// move states are cleared — `partiallyMoved` no less than `movedFrom`, because a reassignment gives
		// the binding a WHOLE fresh value (no moved-out slots), so a scrutinee consumed by an earlier
		// binding-match is fully live once overwritten. Clearing only `movedFrom` left `requireBindingLive`
		// (which rejects on EITHER flag) refusing every use of a reassigned `var` union — an over-rejection
		// of a valid program.
		binding.movedFrom = false
		binding.partiallyMoved = false

		if newIsOwned 'trackNew'
			if not inOwnedSet 'borrowedToOwned'
				// A genuinely borrowed binding (`var s = ""; s = build(1)`) joins the owned set. A binding
				// already in the set — a live owner, or a moved-from ghost being revived — must NOT be
				// pushed again, or scope exit would decref `newValue` twice.
				self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
			end 'borrowedToOwned'
			// The new owned value is the binding's to drop at scope exit, so the STATEMENT must not also
			// drop it: a call/interpolation/promotion result is enrolled as a pending temp, and draining
			// it too would be a double-free (and, before the scope-exit drop, a use-after-free).
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(newValue)
		end 'trackNew'
	end 'reassignBindingValue'

	// Assign to a name that is not a local: a top-level `var` (a STORE to its `.data` slot), a
	// top-level `let` (E2013 — assigning to a constant), or nothing (E2004).
	//
	// **THE `let` ARM IS WHY THE CONSTANT IS PROBED AT ALL.** Without it a top-level `let` would
	// resolve as neither a local nor a var and be reported "undefined variable: 'origin'" — naming a
	// name that IS defined, three lines up, which is a diagnostic that sends the reader looking for a
	// typo instead of at the `let` they meant to write `var`. `specs/static-variables.md`'s
	// `top-level-let-struct-reassign-error` pins the honest one (E2013).
	//
	// The type check the local path applies is applied here too, and through the same
	// `typesAgree`/`assignTypeMismatch` pair — a global is no less able to be handed a bool where it
	// declared an int, and a laundered value in a `.data` slot is exactly as wrong as one in a
	// register. The check happens BEFORE the store is emitted, so a rejected program emits no op.
	//
	// ⚠ **HAVING A SLOT IS NOT PERMISSION TO WRITE ONE** (P1.7 slice 2b). `globalDeclFor` answers "which slot
	// does this name denote?", and an array `let` has an answer to that — its value is a heap record, so it
	// is STORED like a `var` and read like one. Whether it may be WRITTEN is the separate question this
	// statement is the only asker of, so the `mutable` bit rides the lookup and the refusal happens here.
	// Without it `let xs = [1, 2]` would silently accept `xs = [3, 4]` — an accepted write that should have
	// been refused, which is a wrong answer rather than a compile error.
	// ⭐ **AND A STATIC MEMBER'S STORE IS THE SAME STATEMENT (W65)**, reached with a name token spelling
	// `Counter.count` (`staticMemberNameToken`). Every arm below is the answer it needs: a `static var` finds
	// its slot, a `static let` is an inlined constant and gets the E2013 a top-level `let` gets, and a
	// misspelled member is undefined. Nothing here distinguishes the two spellings, which is the point.
	function parseTopLevelAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(nameTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
			found(label, valueType, mutable) then try self.storeToGlobal(func, block: block, nameTok: nameTok, label: label, valueType: valueType, mutable: mutable)
			notFound then try self.rejectNonGlobalAssignment(nameTok)
		end 'global'
	end 'parseTopLevelAssignment'

	// `Counter.count = 42` — consume the `<Type> . <member> =` head and hand the store to the module-storage
	// writer under the qualified name (W65). The base is read through `typeBaseTokenAt`, the ONE reader of a
	// type-naming base, so `Self.count = 1` inside a static method is the same statement written the other
	// way and needs no arm of its own.
	//
	// `staticMemberAssignsAt` has already established the shape and the resolution, so the reads below cannot
	// fail — the `otherwise panic` says so rather than inventing a second refusal for a program the
	// dispatcher would never have sent here.
	function parseStaticMemberAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let baseTok = try self.typeBaseTokenAt(self.pos) otherwise panic("parseStaticMemberAssignment: the base at the cursor does not name a type, but `staticMemberAssignsAt` read it as one to resolve this member")
		let nameTok = self.staticMemberNameToken(baseTok, memberTok: self.tokenAt(self.pos + TokensPerQualifierSegment))

		// Past `<base> . <member>` — the base token, then one qualifier segment.
		self.pos = (self.pos + TokensPerQualifierSegment + 1) as TokenIndex
		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)

		try self.parseTopLevelAssignment(func, block: block, nameTok: nameTok)
	end 'parseStaticMemberAssignment'

	// Write a top-level global, or refuse because it is immutable — an array `let`, the one binding that has
	// a slot and still refuses the write (P1.7 slice 2b).
	//
	// It raises E2013 HERE rather than through `rejectNonGlobalAssignment` below, because the two know
	// different things and the difference is the whole diagnostic: that one is reached having established
	// the name is NOT a stored global, so it must go and ask whether the name exists at all before it can
	// choose between E2013 and E2004. This one is holding the resolution that proves the name is declared,
	// so the question is already answered and re-asking it through a kind-filtered door would report a
	// declared name as undefined.
	function storeToGlobal(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, nameTok Token, label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, mutable bool) throws ParseError
		if not mutable 'immutableGlobal'
			throw assignToImmutableError(nameTok)
		end 'immutableGlobal'

		// ⭐ **E3070's REBIND DOOR FOR MODULE STORAGE — the same write as `arr = other`, one anchoring out.**
		// The store below DECREFS the record the slot was holding (`emitCheckedGlobalStore`), so every element
		// borrowed out of it is freed: MEASURED **0xC0000005** on `let s = try g.get(0) otherwise ""` +
		// `g = StringArray.create()` + `print("{s}")`.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SUBJECT IS THE TARGET'S STORAGE NAME, TAKEN OFF THE RESOLVED NAME RATHER THAN OFF A TOKEN
		// INDEX (W65).** A static member's target is `Counter.count`, which no single token spells — a
		// token-index derivation (`borrowSubjectNameAt`) would read the BASE and answer `Counter`, which names
		// no storage, so the note would be silently dropped and every borrow taken out of a managed static
		// would be left unguarded. Both callers reach this door having already established the name is not a
		// local, which is the only thing the index-based derivation adds.
		if self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'mayConflictWithABorrow'
			self.noteBorrowSubjectRebind(func, subjectName: self.moduleStorageSubjectName(nameTok.value), anchor: nameTok)
		end 'mayConflictWithABorrow'

		try self.emitCheckedGlobalStore(func, block: block, nameTok: nameTok, label: label, valueType: valueType)
	end 'storeToGlobal'

	// Parse the right-hand side, check it against the global's declared type, and store it.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A MANAGED GLOBAL'S WRITE IS THREE THINGS, NOT ONE** (P1.7 slice 2a): take ownership of the new
	// value, RELEASE the record the slot was holding, then store. The order matters in both directions —
	// the release must come after the new value is settled (it may be built from the old one) and before
	// the store overwrites the only pointer to it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OLD-VALUE DECREF IS UNCONDITIONAL, WITH NO NULL TEST.** Both reference compilers guard it
	// (`EmitDecrefValueIfNonnull`, `__mm_decref_maybenull_helper`) and then have to special-case the
	// initializer function BY NAME to suppress it, because in their design a global's first store IS an
	// ordinary store into a slot that is still zero. shv2's initializing store is not on this path at all —
	// it is `ModuleInit`'s own emission — so every store reaching HERE finds a slot that is already filled.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND WHAT MAKES THAT TRUE IS NO LONGER `__module_init`, WHICH IS WHY THIS PARAGRAPH WAS REWRITTEN**
	// (spec-port `lazy-static`, §6 review finding 4). It used to read *"every store reaching HERE is a
	// re-assignment of a slot `__module_init` already filled"*, and for a `static` member that is now FALSE:
	// its slot is filled on FIRST ACCESS, and this store may BE the first access. The decref below is safe
	// because **`emitGlobalAddr` forces the initializer first** — it is the single door every global read and
	// write opens with, and it emits the `__lazy_init#<label>` call ahead of the address
	// (`emitLazyStaticInitGuard`). ⚠ So that guard call is LOAD-BEARING FOR THIS DECREF, not merely for the
	// value's freshness: remove it and a first-access assignment decrefs a null slot. A reader trusting the
	// old sentence would have concluded exactly the opposite.
	function emitCheckedGlobalStore(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, nameTok Token, label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType) throws ParseError
		let valueStart = self.pos
		let value = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)

		// The slot keeps the type its declaration gave it, so `g = 3` into a `var g = 0.0` stores a WIDENED
		// f64 and not the integer's raw bytes — the same coercion the local rebind above performs, through
		// the same door.
		let stored = try self.assignableValue(nameTok, value: value, declared: maxonTypeTag(valueType), declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType), door: FunctionTypeDoor.global, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.assignedPlace)

		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)
		// `from:` names the interner `valueType`'s ids would live in — this file's own. A global's type comes
		// from its INITIALIZER (`constantValueType` / `managedConstValueType` / `constArrayValueType`), none of
		// which mint a `named`, so nothing is resolved through it today; it is stated because the alternative
		// is a query whose answer depends on which table the caller happened to hold, which is precisely the
		// cross-file misclassification T1 fixed one door along.
		if not self.signatures.typeIsManaged(valueType, from: self.artifact.typeNames) 'scalarGlobal'
			self.emitGlobalStore(label, valueType: valueType, value: stored, range: range)
			return
		end 'scalarGlobal'

		// The slot is DURABLE STORAGE, exactly as a struct field or a union payload is, so the new value
		// arrives through the one door that decides copy-vs-co-own-vs-adopt for all of them: a borrowed String
		// is COPIED into a fresh owned record, an owned value held by a LIVE BINDING is CO-OWNED (the slot takes
		// its own reference and the binding keeps its own — the 2026-08-12 durable-store ruling), and an owned
		// TEMPORARY is ADOPTED, its pending statement-end drop cancelled. A second spelling here would be free
		// to leak the temporary or double-free the source.
		let owned = try self.moveManagedValueInto(stored, argBareLocal: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart), isString: tagIsByteRecord(maxonTypeTag(valueType)), range: range)

		self.emitDecref(self.emitGlobalLoad(label, valueType: valueType, range: range), range: range)
		self.emitGlobalStore(label, valueType: valueType, value: owned, range: range)
	end 'emitCheckedGlobalStore'

	// **THE MUTABILITY RULE FOR A LOCAL BINDING — TWO DOORS, BECAUSE THE TWO STATEMENTS THAT WRITE A
	// BINDING WRITE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS.** The reassignment `x = 5` gives the NAME a new value; the field
	// store `p.x = 5` leaves the name alone and writes the RECORD it denotes. They agree about every
	// binding kind but one, and they are kept together here so the disagreement is READ rather than
	// rediscovered.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DISAGREEMENT IS A PARAMETER, AND IT IS A RULING RATHER THAN A RELAXATION** (⚖ user,
	// 2026-07-18, `PLAN.md`): **parameters BORROW by default, and a borrow's POINTEE is writable even
	// though the binding itself is not rebindable.** A struct parameter holds a pointer to the CALLER's
	// box (`LowerMaxonToStd`), so `state.flag = 7` inside `function scan(state State)` writes the record
	// the caller handed over — which is what by-reference means and what the runnable oracle does
	// (measured, all four cells: `state.flag = 7` on a PARAMETER compiles and the caller sees the write,
	// while `s.flag = 7` on a `let` LOCAL is E2013 in BOTH compilers). shv2 used to refuse both from one
	// door, conflating *"cannot rebind the reference"* with *"cannot write through it"*.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE REBIND IS NOT RELAXED WITH IT, AND MUST NOT BE.** `p = Point.create(…)` on a parameter stays
	// E2013: rebinding needs a per-call cell for the name, which shv2 does not have, and `bindParameters`
	// PANICS on `ParametersAreMutable = true` because `assignedBindingsIn`'s loop-phi token scan is
	// exhaustive only while parameters are immutable. So the split lives in the PERMISSION, per operation
	// — which is precisely the split the bootstrap never had to make (it declares every parameter
	// `Mutable = true` outright, `2-Parser.cs:8097-8125`, and gets both writes from one flag).
	//
	// ⚠ What the two doors DO share is single-sourced rather than restated: the E2013 mint
	// (`assignToImmutableError`), the E3077 evidence (`markMutabilityExercised`) and the iteration lock a
	// `for … in` imposes on either write. A future answer to what "writable" means — a captured closure
	// variable, a moved-from binding under static ownership — belongs in the predicate both read, never in
	// one door. The bootstrap is the cautionary tale: it spells this rule at FOUR sites and reads
	// `VarInfo.Mutable` in three unrelated places.
	function requireMutableBinding(binding VarInfo, nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE REBIND RANK IS THE INSTANCE RANK PLUS ONE CONJUNCT, AND IT IS WRITTEN THAT WAY SO THE
		// LOCK CANNOT BE TAUGHT TO ONE DOOR AND NOT THE OTHER.** Every reason a record may not be written
		// through is also a reason its name may not be rebound — an enclosing `for … in` iterating the
		// binding is today's (the loop walks the record it captured in its preheader, and a rebind drops it
		// via `reassignBindingValue` while the header still reads its length) — so this asks the shared
		// `bindingStorageIsWritableHere` and adds only what a REBIND additionally needs: a `var`
		// declaration. Spelling the lock out here instead left `bindingStorageIsWritableHere`'s header
		// claiming three doors while a fourth read `bindingIsIterationLocked` on its own.
		//
		// `binding.mutable` implies `bindingStorageIsWritable`, so the conjunction is exactly the
		// "declared `var` and not iteration-locked" this door has always asked.
		// ⭐⭐ **AND PBR-2 ADDS THE CELL RANK BESIDE THE `var` ONE, WHICH IS A THIRD ANSWER RATHER THAN A
		// RELAXATION OF THE SECOND.** A by-reference parameter is bound IMMUTABLE — `ParametersAreMutable` is
		// still false and the `bindParameters` tripwire still stands — yet `x = 99` on it is exactly what the
		// language means by pass-by-reference. It is legal because the write is not a REBIND of an SSA name at
		// all: it is a `storeIndirect` into the caller's cell, so the loop-phi exhaustiveness argument
		// `binding.mutable` protects is not engaged.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ITERATION LOCK STILL BINDS IT, AND THAT IS WHY THE CONJUNCT IS ADDED INSIDE AND NOT AROUND.**
		// `bindingStorageIsWritableHere` is asked of a cell binding exactly as of any other — a `for … in`
		// walking the record a name denotes refuses a write through it whatever the name's representation, and
		// `for-iterated-self-field`'s four E2013 pins are that rule.
		//
		// A LOCAL cell is always a `var`, so it satisfies both conjuncts and this changes nothing for it; the
		// only binding the second conjunct admits that the first did not is a by-reference PARAMETER.
		if not (self.bindingStorageIsWritableHere(binding) and (binding.mutable or binding.isCellBinding)) 'immutable'
			throw assignToImmutableError(nameTok)
		end 'immutable'

		// ⭐ **E3077's FIRST DOOR, and it is this one BECAUSE it is the one that refuses.** A write that gets
		// past here is a write a `let` would not have survived, which is exactly the fact E3077 needs — so
		// the rule reads the enforcement rather than re-deriving from the syntax which statements are writes.
		self.markMutabilityExercised(binding)
	end 'requireMutableBinding'

	// **THE SECOND DOOR: MAY THE RECORD THIS NAME DENOTES BE WRITTEN THROUGH?** — the INSTANCE rank of
	// `p.x = 5` (see `parseFieldAssignment` for the rank order the whole permission band obeys). It differs
	// from the rebind above in exactly the parameter, and it asks that through
	// `bindingStorageIsWritableHere` rather than restating it — the same predicate the mutating-METHOD
	// receiver asks, because `p.push(9)` and `p.x = 5` are one write of one record under two spellings.
	//
	// ⚠ It marks E3077's evidence on the same terms the rebind door does, and for a PARAMETER that mark is
	// inert rather than special-cased: only a `var` DECLARATION is ever an E3077 candidate, and the
	// receiver door already marks a parameter receiver unconditionally (`dispatchMethodOnBinding`), so a
	// second rule here would be this one fact answered twice.
	function requireWritableInstance(binding VarInfo, nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⭐⭐ **THE TAINT DOOR (W117), AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN SENTENCE RATHER THAN E2013's.** `p.x = 5` through a
		// value that aliases a `let`-declared global is refused on the same terms the mutating-METHOD receiver
		// one over is (`immutableReceiverNameOf`), off the same one predicate — but E2013 renders "cannot
		// assign to immutable variable: 'a'", which is FALSE of the `var` this most often is and would send the
		// author to change the wrong keyword. The remedy here is the global's own declaration, so the message
		// names that.
		if self.bindingAliasesAnImmutableGlobal(binding) 'aliasesAnImmutableGlobal'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(immutableGlobalWriteRefusal(String.from(nameTok.value)), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'aliasesAnImmutableGlobal'

		if not self.bindingStorageIsWritableHere(binding) 'immutableInstance'
			throw assignToImmutableError(nameTok)
		end 'immutableInstance'

		self.markMutabilityExercised(binding)
	end 'requireWritableInstance'

	// **WHAT A USER MAY NOT DECLARE, IN ONE PLACE.** Two reservations, both raised as E2051 at the declared
	// name — the token the user has to change:
	//   • a name starting with the prefix Maxon reserves for compiler internals (`ReservedNamePrefix`);
	//   • the implicit receiver's own name, `self` (`ReceiverSourceSpelling`, and see `bindsInto` for the
	//     one namespace it is legal in).
	// They are one DOOR and two RULES: every binding site asks "may this declaration take this name?" once,
	// and what the answer is made of lives here rather than at the sites.
	//
	// ⚠ It is called at **17** binding sites — function/method name, parameter, top-level and body
	// `let`/`var`, destructuring names, loop-variable pattern, `if` binder, caught-error binders,
	// `type` name, struct field, union payload field, `typealias` name, interface name, enum/union case —
	// and it is ONE call at each, never 17 copies of `bytesStartWith(…, b"__")` + the message. That the rule
	// reads once is the whole point: 17 copies is this project's signature bug, and a reservation that held
	// at 16 sites and lapsed at the 17th would be a SILENT hole (a user `__`-name accepted there), not a
	// compile error — exactly the class of defect this door exists to close.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND IT LAPSED AT EXACTLY TWO, WHICH IS WHY THE COUNT IS WRITTEN DOWN AND WHY IT IS CHECKED.** This
	// header said EIGHT while the tree had 15 calls, and the two CAUGHT-ERROR binders — `otherwise (e)` and
	// `end 'c' else (e)` — took `consume(TokenKind.identifier)` straight to `declareCaughtError` without ever
	// asking. MEASURED before the two calls below were added: `try arr.get(0) otherwise (__x) 'h'` COMPILED
	// AND RAN on shv2 (exit 42) while the oracle refuses it, `E2051 … identifier '__x' is reserved`, and the
	// `else (__e)` form was the same both ways. A stale census is not a documentation defect here — it is the
	// only thing that would have shown the hole, since nothing else about those two sites looks wrong.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **`declaredName` IS PASSED AND IS *NOT* `nameTok.value`, AND THAT SPLIT IS A HOLE THIS DOOR ONCE
	// HAD.** For every site but one the two are the same bytes, which is exactly why reading the token's own
	// text looked safe. An IMPLICITLY BACKED enum case breaks it: `enum E { "\x5f\x5fx" }` DECLARES the case
	// `__x` (the decoded content — what `EnumLayout.addCase` stores and every `indexOfCase` matches) while the
	// token's text is `\x5f\x5fx`, which does not start with the prefix. Reading the token would have waved a
	// reserved declaration through. The rule is about the name being DECLARED, so the declared name is what
	// the caller states; the token is here for the POSITION only, and the message names what was declared
	// rather than how it was spelled.
	//
	// ⭐ It sees only USER SOURCE TOKENS, which is why it does not — and structurally CANNOT — reject
	// the compiler's own `__` functions: `__mm_alloc`, `__print_string`, `__destruct_*` are built by
	// `IrFunction.createFromStd` and never pass through the parser. That is what keeps the compiler's own
	// names out of `funcSignatures`, which is the fact `MmRuntime.isSignaturelessCompilerCallee` reads to
	// license the "skip the user-function checks" shortcut — a registry answer, not a naming convention,
	// because the exemption below means the prefix alone no longer decides it.
	function requireUnreservedName(nameTok Token, declaredName ByteArray, bindsInto DeclaredNameNamespace = DeclaredNameNamespace.shadowsTheReceiver) throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE ONE FILE THAT DECLARES THE RESERVED SPACE IS EXEMPT (D6)** — `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`,
		// whose whole content is the type-system bridge to compiler intrinsics: the error enums a builtin
		// throws (`__ManagedFileError`) and the parse helpers `int.fromString` rewrites to
		// (`__int_fromString`). Nothing else in `stdlib/` may, which is what keeps the paragraph above
		// true for every other file: the reservation still holds everywhere a user can write.
		//
		// The exemption is on the FILE (`declaresCompilerInternalNames`) and not on a per-name allowlist,
		// which is the reference's shape too (bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:24672-24673` exempts three stdlib
		// files by basename). A name list would have to be kept in lockstep with a file it does not live
		// in — and the compiler already learns each of these names by parsing the declaration, so a second
		// copy could only ever be the stale one.
		if self.declaresCompilerInternalNames 'theReservedSpacesOwnFile'
			// ⛔ **THE ONE THING AN EXEMPT MODULE STILL MAY NOT DECLARE (W72).** `__line__` and `__file__` are
			// reserved WORDS, not merely reserved-prefix names — `specs/source-location-defaults.md`: "no
			// program can declare or shadow them" — and the file exemption above is precisely the hole that
			// sentence would otherwise acquire the day a second module was listed. It costs a byte compare per
			// declared name in the exempt files ALONE, which is why it sits inside the branch rather than
			// above it: every other file already refuses both names on the prefix rule below.
			try requireNotACallerLocationDefault(declaredName, at: nameTok)
			return
		end 'theReservedSpacesOwnFile'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE SECOND RESERVATION AT THIS DOOR: THE IMPLICIT RECEIVER'S OWN NAME (`reserved-self.md`).**
		// `self` is not a `__` name, so it cannot ride the prefix test below and would be waved through by its
		// early-out; it is a separate rule over a separate name, asked here because "may a user declaration
		// take this name" is ONE question and this is the one place every binding site asks it. Before this,
		// `function configure(self int)` PARSED and the fault surfaced two ways downstream, both in the wrong
		// place and for the wrong reason: `E3012 unused variable: 'self'` if the body never read it, and
		// `E2001 'self' can only be used inside instance methods` if it did.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT SITS BELOW THE FILE EXEMPTION, ON THE SAME SIDE AS THE `__` RULE** — the reference draws
		// exactly one boundary in this door (`2-Parser.cs:27418`: the `self` throw is inside the same function
		// the stdlib-file exemption returns from), and a second boundary here would be one door with two
		// shapes. Nothing shv2 parses rides on the choice: MEASURED across every module
		// `whitelistedStdlibModules` lists and the exempt modules besides (`stdlib/Builtins.maxon`, and
		// `stdlib/Testing.maxon` since W72, which reach the parser as exempt files rather than as listed ones),
		// ZERO declare a binding named `self`.
		//
		// ⛔ **AND THE FILE THAT WOULD — `stdlib/Internals.maxon`, 24 of them, `__destruct___ManagedMemory(self
		// MachineWord)` and its siblings — IS NOT EXEMPT IN shv2 AND MUST NOT BE ASSUMED TO BE.** The bootstrap
		// exempts three BASENAMES (`2-Parser.cs:27401-27402`: `Builtins.maxon`, `Internals.maxon`,
		// `Testing.maxon`); shv2's `declaresCompilerInternalNames` is an identity compare against
		// `Builtins.maxon` and `Testing.maxon` — two of that three, and W72 added the second.
		// `whitelistedStdlibModules` does not list `Internals.maxon`, so shv2 never
		// parses it and the gap costs nothing today — but whoever lists it inherits NOTHING from the reference:
		// it goes red on the `__` rule first (every one of those function NAMES is `__`-prefixed), and widening
		// the exemption is what that day's work is. This rule rides along with that decision; it does not
		// depend on it.
		if namespaceShadowsTheReceiver(bindsInto) and declaredName.equals(ReceiverSourceSpelling) 'theImplicitReceiversName'
			throw ParseError.reservedReceiverName(nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'theImplicitReceiversName'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE RULE'S OWN TEST COMES FIRST, AND THAT ORDER IS A COST CURVE RATHER THAN A STYLE (A1s
		// optimizer).** This function is called at every binding site (see the header's census), so it runs
		// once per declared name in every file — and the exemption below is a LINEAR SCAN of the rename table
		// (`isMintedStdlibShadowName`, whose own header argues why a scan is the right shape for a table that
		// is empty in the whole existing corpus). Asked BEFORE this test, that scan ran at every stdlib
		// declaration site whether or not the name could possibly be a mint, which is a `sites x renames`
		// PRODUCT — and it ALLOCATES, because iterating the table does.
		//
		// MEASURED on `stdlib/`'s eight listed modules, one binary swapped into one path, a program whose
		// contested-name count is swept 0 → 15: `phase:parse` allocations grew **+393 per contested name**
		// (57,180 at one name → 62,683 at fifteen) and are now **FLAT at +12** (56,388 → 56,539) — −6,144
		// allocations at fifteen names, −6,876 on the two-round maximal contest, and 7/7 interleaved pairs
		// faster on CPU (−5.2% on `phase:parse`, min-of-7). The scale ladder cannot see any of this: its
		// corpus contests NOTHING, so the product is `sites x 0` there and reads a flat zero.
		//
		// ⚠ **AND THE EARLY-OUT IS CORRECT FOR ANY MINT PREFIX, NOT MERELY FOR TODAY'S EQUAL ONES.** It does
		// not assume `SignatureIndex.ReservedTypeNamePrefix` still equals `ReservedNamePrefix` — the three
		// module-local copies of the `__` rule are deliberate (see this constant's header) and are free to
		// drift. A name that fails THIS test is one this door was never going to refuse, so there is nothing
		// for an exemption to rescue: whatever the mint is spelled as, an unreserved name leaves here
		// accepted either way. The exemption can therefore only ever matter on the reserved side of it.
		if not bytesStartWith(declaredName, prefix: ReservedNamePrefix) 'notReserved'
			return
		end 'notReserved'

		// ⭐ **A NAME THE *COMPILER* MINTED FOR THIS COMPILE IS NOT A NAME THE AUTHOR WROTE (A1s wave 2).**
		// A contested stdlib declaration is moved into the reserved space, and the move is applied to the
		// file's identifier TOKENS — so what reaches this check is `__Clock` at `stdlib/Clock.maxon`'s own
		// `type Clock`, a `__` name no source contains. MEASURED before this arm: `E2051 … identifier
		// '__Clock' is reserved` reported against a line whose text reads `type Clock`.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A WIDENING OF THE `__` RESERVATION, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE MINT.** The exemption
		// is not "a stdlib file may declare `__` names" — it is "this exact name is one THIS COMPILE minted",
		// asked of the rename table itself. A stdlib file writing its own `__Foo` is still E2051 unless it is
		// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`, because nothing minted `__Foo`.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS SCOPED TO STDLIB SOURCE, BECAUSE A MINT IS ONLY EVER *WRITTEN* INTO STDLIB TOKENS
		// (A1s wave 2 review).** `shadowRenamedTokens` rewrites the stdlib half and only the stdlib half, so a
		// minted name reaching this check from a USER file is not the compiler's own mint — it is the author
		// literally having typed `__Clock`, which E2051 exists to refuse. MEASURED without this conjunct: a
		// user file declaring BOTH `type __Clock` and `type Clock` had its E2051 SUPPRESSED and got
		// `E3006 … stdlib/Clock.maxon:10:13: duplicate definition of '__Clock'` instead — the mint's own
		// collision, reported inside a file the author never opened. The mint is now disjoint from a user's
		// `__` declarations too (`ProgramSignatures.shadowRenameIsTaken`), so with both halves in place that
		// program gets exactly the one diagnostic it is owed.
		if self.isStdlibSource and self.signatures.isMintedStdlibShadowName(declaredName) 'theCompilersOwnMint'
			return
		end 'theCompilersOwnMint'

		throw ParseError.reservedIdentifier(String.from(declaredName), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
	end 'requireUnreservedName'

	// ⭐ **A DECLARATION MAY NOT GIVE A COMPILER-OWNED TYPE NAME A *NOMINAL* IDENTITY (P1.7a slice 2b-ii).**
	// `requireUnreservedName`'s sibling, and the two are separate rules over separate name sets rather than one
	// combined check, because they govern different things: `__` is reserved at EVERY declaration (a local, a
	// field, a function, an enum case), while `Ordering`/`HashValue`/`ExitCode` are reserved only where a
	// declaration would mint a TYPE IDENTITY under the name — `let Ordering = 5` binds a value and collides
	// with nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOMINAL is the precise line, and a RANGED typealias is deliberately on the legal side of it.** A
	// `type`, an `enum`/`union`, a FUNCTION typealias and a GENERIC-INSTANCE typealias each make the name mean
	// a distinct thing the parser re-tags a `named` reference INTO (`structRef`, an enum layout, `function`,
	// `genericInstance`) — and that is precisely where the parser and `resolveNamedType` come to disagree about
	// a compiler-owned name (see `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName` for the measured 0xC0000005). A
	// RANGED alias mints no identity at all: it ERASES to `integer`/`float`, which is what the builtin does
	// too, so the two answers agree and there is nothing to refuse. `specs-shv2/generic-type-nested-array-
	// typealias.md` declares `typealias ExitCode = int(0 to 125)` in all 27 of its cases and is correct to.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CORPUS MAY DECLARE THESE NAMES — IT IS WHERE THEY COME FROM (W3).** The reservation says a
	// name has ONE meaning, not that the compiler must be the one to write it down: `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`
	// declares `Ordering` and `IterationError`, and the compiler now READS those declarations rather than
	// carrying a second transcription of them (`Project.declaredBuiltinEnum`, the `__Managed*Error` precedent
	// from A1s). Refusing the corpus here would refuse the very file the layout is read from.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BIT IS `livesUnderStdlibDirectory`, NOT `isStdlibSource`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A COMMAND THE
	// WORKSTREAM RUNS.** `isStdlibSource` answers a VISIBILITY question and deliberately hands a project ROOTED
	// under `stdlib/` its own files back as the USER's, so gated on it, `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`
	// — the readiness probe `StdlibLoader`'s collision rule prescribes — would refuse the module it is checking.
	// D6 drew this distinction for `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` and the stdlib-only `String` gate paid for missing
	// it; being the declaring site of a compiler-owned name is a property of the FILE, never of how a command
	// line spelled the project.
	//
	// ⚠ It admits stdlib source WHOLESALE rather than mapping each name to one module, and that is exact rather
	// than loose: a second listed module declaring the same name is an ordinary duplicate definition (E3006,
	// `commitFuncSignatures`' twin for types), which is a LOUDER answer than a per-name allowlist could give and
	// one that already exists. What this door owes is only that a USER file may not mint the identity.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKS THE ONE PREDICATE INSTEAD OF RE-SPELLING THE CONJUNCTION, AND THIS DOOR IS *WHY* THAT
	// PREDICATE EXISTS (W16 review).** Three sites decide the identical question — this one, which
	// DIAGNOSES, and the two registries that DROP (`Project.upsertDeclaredEnum`,
	// `ProgramSignatures.recordStruct`). W16 unified the two registries after the disagreement between THIS
	// door and one of them cost two reachable compiler PANICS: it admitted `stdlib/String.maxon`'s
	// declaration while `recordStruct` threw the layout away, so the parse walked into a body whose layout
	// no longer existed. Leaving `isCompilerOwnedTypeName(…) and not <corpus>` written out a third time here
	// would leave the rule with two homes again — and the next edit to either one is the same crash. **The
	// BIT is this parser's** (`livesUnderStdlibDirectory`, for the reason two paragraphs up); **the RULE
	// over it is `Project.declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName`'s, and only its.**
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS A CONTEST ONLY IF THE DECLARATION *DISAGREES*** (spec-port `lazy-static`). The caller
	// states what its declaration makes the name MEAN, and the rule compares that against the meaning the
	// compiler already holds — see `Project.declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName` and `DeclaredTypeMeaning`.
	// Three of the four doors below mint a NOMINAL identity and so can never agree; the generic-instance
	// alias can, and is the one that had been refusing `typealias CharSet = Set with Character` — the
	// reference's own line.
	function requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned(nameTok Token, declares DeclaredTypeMeaning) throws ParseError
		if not declarationContestsACompilerOwnedName(nameTok.value, declaredByTheCorpus: self.livesUnderStdlibDirectory, declares: declares) 'notAContest'
			return
		end 'notAContest'

		throw ParseError.declarationTakesCompilerOwnedTypeName(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
	end 'requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE PARSER-TIER COERCION, PAID.** `TypeRules.widensIntToFloat` states the obligation an `ok`
	// verdict leaves behind; this is the ONE place the parser discharges it for an SSA value, and every
	// parser-tier coercion site routes through here rather than restating the rule. That is the whole
	// shape of the bug it closes: six doors each asked `checkDeclaredType`, accepted `ok`, and then went
	// on using the value they already held — an i64 in a GPR meeting a slot declared f64.
	//
	// `promoteToFloat` is the primitive (a `cvtsi2sd`, via `MaxonUnaryOp.promoteToFloat`) and it is
	// idempotent, so the guard is about the DECLARED side, not the value's: without it a `return 3` from
	// a `returns int` would convert too.
	//
	// ⚠ It is the PARSER's promotion and deliberately not the Std tier's. shv2 has exactly two promotion
	// primitives and the split is forced: `LowerMaxonToStd.floatDomainCrossedArg` converts a call ARGUMENT,
	// whose callee signature is a whole-program fact the parser may not have; every site below is intra-file —
	// a `return` against its own function's declared type, a field against a layout already in hand, a
	// binding against its own declaration — so all of them promote HERE, off the `valueTags` column, with
	// no second table.
	function coerceToDeclared(value ValueId, declared ValueTypeTag, anchor Token) returns ValueId
		if not widensIntToFloat(self.tagOf(value), declared: declared) 'noWidening'
			return value
		end 'noWidening'

		// ⚠⚠ **THE RULE CANNOT TELL THE TWO `named` KINDS APART, AND ONLY AN INVARIANT HELD ELSEWHERE DOES.**
		// `widensIntToFloat` answers TRUE for a `named` value meeting a `float`, because `tagIsIntegral`
		// answers TRUE for every `named`. That is exactly RIGHT for a ranged INT alias — `var z = 0.0` then
		// `z = aCount` must convert — and CATASTROPHIC for a ranged FLOAT alias, whose value is already an
		// f64: a `cvtsi2sd` over its bit pattern is a silent wrong answer by ~300 orders of magnitude, not a
		// diagnostic. One tag, two representations, and the RULE sees only the tag.
		//
		// What actually keeps them apart is that no float-alias VALUE ever carries a `named` tag: every
		// producer lifts it to `float` first — `resolveFloatAliasType` at a parameter binding, a call result
		// and a closure capture, `ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf`'s float-alias arm at a field. So a `named`
		// float alias arriving HERE means a producer was added without that lift, which is a compiler bug and
		// never a program's.
		//
		// It is a PANIC and not a comment because the comment is what failed: `widensIntToFloat`'s own header
		// offered the `ok` verdict as the safeguard, and the verdict is no safeguard at all —
		// `checkDeclaredType(named, float)` answers `ok` and hands the conversion straight through. The next
		// rung to mint a float-alias value gets a named site instead of a number.
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.named and self.namedIsFloatAlias(self.valueNameId(value)) 'unliftedFloatAlias'
			panic("Parser.coerceToDeclared: value v{value} is tagged `named` for the ranged FLOAT alias '{String.from(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)))}' and reached the int->float widening — every producer of a float-alias value must lift its tag through `resolveFloatAliasType` or `ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf` first, or this emits a `cvtsi2sd` over an f64 bit pattern")
		end 'unliftedFloatAlias'

		return self.promoteToFloat(value, opTok: anchor)
	end 'coerceToDeclared'

	// **CHECK AN ASSIGNED VALUE AGAINST ITS DECLARED SLOT AND HAND BACK THE VALUE THAT SLOT WANTS** — the
	// verdict and the coercion in ONE call, so a caller cannot take the `ok` and forget the conversion.
	// `voice` is the SENTENCE the refusal is told in and nothing else — see `SlotMismatchVoice`.
	// Every assignment-shaped door in the parser goes through it: a local `var` rebind, a top-level global
	// store, a struct-literal field, a field write, a self-field write.
	//
	// It returns a value rather than nothing precisely because the caller must REBIND: the promoted id is
	// a different SSA value from the one that was parsed, and a caller that stored the original would
	// store the raw integer bits — which is what all five of them did.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY ARGUMENT IT TAKES IS A SCALAR, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN STYLISTIC.** This is one of
	// the hottest paths in the parser — every `var` rebind, every struct-literal field, every field write
	// and every global store — so what crosses the boundary is what the program pays per assignment. The
	// declared type arrives as the `(tag, nameId)` PAIR every other type-deciding door in this file takes
	// (`aggregateNameOf`, `floatResolvedTag`, `maxonTypeOfTag`), and the place arrives as the door ENUM
	// rather than the noun STRING it used to: threading that string on into the function-type door cost
	// **one allocation per call, +1,852 in `phase:parse` at scale rung 0**, for a record almost every call
	// then discarded. See `FunctionTypeDoor`.
	//
	// ⚠ **`placeOwner` IS THE ONE NON-SCALAR, AND IT IS A BORROWED NAME THAT ALREADY EXISTS — never a built
	// one.** A FIELD is worded `field 'x' of 'Point'`, so the declaring type is part of the place and the
	// field doors pass `layout.name`; every other door passes `EmptySourceText`. **MEASURED against a
	// control built from the same tree without it: `phase:parse` allocations went DOWN at every rung —
	// -588 at rung 0 and -8,834 at rung 5** — because the same change stopped the payload and
	// container-element doors building a `String.from(...)` place EAGERLY on paths whose voice quotes no
	// place at all. The saving grows with the corpus because it is one discarded allocation per such site.
	function assignableValue(nameTok Token, value ValueId, declared ValueTypeTag, declaredNameId TypeNameId, door FunctionTypeDoor, placeOwner ByteArray, voice SlotMismatchVoice) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let got = self.tagOf(value)
		try self.requireAssignable(nameTok, value: got, declared: declared, declaredNameId: declaredNameId, door: door, placeOwner: placeOwner, voice: voice)
		if isFunctionTypeDoor(declared, got: got) 'functionTypeDoor'
			self.recordFunctionTypeDoor(declaredNameId, gotNameId: self.valueNameId(value), door: door, placeName: nameTok.value, anchor: nameTok)
		end 'functionTypeDoor'
		return self.coerceToDeclared(value, declared: declared, anchor: nameTok)
	end 'assignableValue'

	// The VERDICT rule for an ASSIGNMENT — the local path, the global one, the struct-literal field, the
	// field write and the field DEFAULT all ask it, and none of them restates it. A global is no less able
	// to be handed a bool where it declared an int, or a float where it declared an int, than a local is;
	// every one of them anchors the diagnostic on the assigned NAME and names its own PLACE (the `door`,
	// whose noun `functionTypeDoorNoun` renders — and renders only where a diagnostic is built).
	//
	// ⚠ It is the verdict ALONE, and it is asked DIRECTLY by exactly one caller: the site holding a
	// parse-time CONSTANT rather than an SSA value (a struct field's literal default), which pays the
	// widening the verdict licenses in the constant's own representation. Every site holding a VALUE asks
	// `assignableValue` — this plus that conversion, in one call, so the two halves cannot come apart.
	function requireAssignable(nameTok Token, value ValueTypeTag, declared ValueTypeTag, declaredNameId TypeNameId, door FunctionTypeDoor, placeOwner ByteArray, voice SlotMismatchVoice) throws ParseError
		match checkDeclaredType(value, declared: declared) 'declared'
			ok then break 'declared'
			mismatch then throw self.slotMismatchError(voice, placeDoor: door, placeName: nameTok.value, placeOwner: placeOwner, got: typeTagName(value), gotTag: value, resolvedExpected: typeTagName(declared), declared: declared, declaredNameId: declaredNameId, line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			narrowsFloatToInt then throw ParseError.implicitNarrowing(typeTagName(value), targetType: typeTagName(declared), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			// A FUNCTION VALUE into a NON-function place (P1.5-A1) — the spec's specific E3005 ("cannot
			// assign a value of type 'function' to {noun} '{name}', which holds '{held}'"). The noun comes
			// from the DOOR the caller named, so this site and the function-type check word one place alike.
			functionIntoNonFunction then throw ParseError.functionValueIntoNonFunction(functionTypeDoorNoun(door), name: String.from(nameTok.value), heldType: typeTagName(declared), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'declared'
	end 'requireAssignable'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE E3005 A STORAGE-SLOT MISMATCH IS RAISED AS, GIVEN THE VOICE ITS PLACE SPEAKS IN.** Both
	// verdict doors — the trivial `checkDeclaredType` one above and the managed `requireManagedValueMatches`
	// one — end here, so a container element and a struct field cannot come to explain one rejection two
	// ways beyond the one difference `SlotMismatchVoice` names. Everything that varies with the voice varies
	// HERE, in one `match`: which `ParseError` variant, which name is quoted, and how the EXPECTED side is
	// spelled.
	//
	// `resolvedExpected` is the assignment voice's answer and arrives already rendered, because the two
	// verdict doors resolve it differently — a bare tag name where two TAGS disagree (there is no name to
	// print: the value and the slot are not even the same kind of thing), a NAME where two aggregates of one
	// tag are told apart — and neither may have that decision taken away from it.
	//
	// ⚠ **`assignedPlace` IS THE PLACE THE VALUE WAS ENTERING, AND THE VOICE DECIDES WHETHER THE SENTENCE
	// QUOTES IT.** The assignment voice does — a local, a global, a field, a union payload all name
	// themselves. The argument voice does not: at a container mutator the anchor and the quoted name part
	// company, and both halves are the reference's (`specs/type-checking.md` anchors
	// `method-call-wrong-element-type` on the `push` token of `arr.push("hello")` while naming the mutator's
	// `value` PARAMETER, which is what an author would have to change). `ArrayValueLabel` is that parameter —
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares `push(value Element)`, `set(index, value)` and `insert(at, value)`, so the
	// label a two-argument mutator REQUIRES and the name its element parameter CARRIES are one fact with one
	// home. **The selection is made HERE and not by the callers** (review, 2026-08-04): it was a caller
	// convention, correct at its one site and structurally free to stop being, and a `voice` paired with the
	// wrong name is precisely the "one refusal, two sentences" defect this enum exists to end.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHEREVER A NAME IS PRINTED AT ALL, BOTH VOICES PRINT `displayTypeNameOf`'s** (user ruling,
	// 2026-08-04: a diagnostic names a type by the `typealias` the author wrote, never by the compiler's
	// mint) — so a struct, a union, an interface and a GENERIC INSTANCE read the author's way in either
	// sentence. The one remaining divergence is the extra probe `declaredSlotDisplayName` makes for the
	// argument voice, documented there rather than restated here — which is why `gotTag` is threaded down: the
	// probe's answer depends on what the OTHER half of the sentence turned out to be.
	function slotMismatchError(voice SlotMismatchVoice, placeDoor FunctionTypeDoor, placeName ByteArray, placeOwner ByteArray, got String, gotTag ValueTypeTag, resolvedExpected String, declared ValueTypeTag, declaredNameId TypeNameId, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) returns ParseError
		return match voice 'v'
			assignedPlace gives ParseError.assignTypeMismatch(assignedPlacePhrase(placeDoor, name: placeName, owner: placeOwner), got: got, expected: resolvedExpected, line: line, column: column)
			containerElementArgument gives ParseError.builtinArgumentType(String.from(ArrayValueLabel), expected: self.declaredSlotDisplayName(declared, nameId: declaredNameId, gotTag: gotTag), got: got, line: line, column: column)
			// The payload voice quotes no name at all (there is none to quote) and spells the EXPECTED side
			// through `declaredSlotDisplayName`, the same probe the argument voice makes — which is what puts
			// `enum-full.md`'s `expected Integer` (a ranged alias) and `union-cases.md`'s `expected Color` (an
			// enum) under one rule instead of two.
			payloadArgument gives ParseError.typeMismatchExpectedGot(self.declaredSlotDisplayName(declared, nameId: declaredNameId, gotTag: gotTag), got: got, line: line, column: column)
		end 'v'
	end 'slotMismatchError'

	// ⭐ **HOW A DECLARED SLOT'S TYPE IS SPELLED WHERE THE SENTENCE NAMES WHAT WAS *EXPECTED* — the parser's
	// half of `SemanticCheck.paramDisplayName`, under the same ruling and with the same gate.**
	// `displayTypeNameOf` already answers for every tag that carries a written name (a struct, an interface, a
	// registered union/enum, a generic instance's author-written alias). The ONE thing it cannot answer for is
	// a RANGED-INT ALIAS: its `named` tag is deliberately not an aggregate (`aggregateNameOf` returns empty
	// for it, because it is numeric), so it falls through to the bare primitive `int` — and
	// `typealias Int = int(i64.min to i64.max)` is exactly what `specs/type-checking.md`'s element cases
	// declare. The alias REGISTRY is what still holds the spelling, and `declaredAliasOfNameId` is its one
	// probe.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE ALIAS IS WORTH PRINTING ONLY WHEN THE OTHER HALF OF THE SENTENCE NAMES A TYPE** — the rule
	// `SemanticCheck.paramDisplayName` states in full, from the two canonical specs that pin the SAME shape
	// two ways (`type-checking.md`'s `expected 'Integer', got 'String'` against
	// `implicit-type-conversion.md`'s `expected 'int', got 'bool'`). **MEASURED before this gate existed
	// (review, 2026-08-04): `arr.push(true)` on an `Array with Integer` said `expected 'Integer', got 'bool'`
	// where `takesInt(true)` on the identical `x Integer` said `expected 'int'` — one rule, two answers,
	// decided by which door the value entered, and the bootstrap answers `'int'` at BOTH.** It is the very
	// defect `SlotMismatchVoice`'s header records one tier down, so the gate belongs on both sides of it.
	function declaredSlotDisplayName(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, gotTag ValueTypeTag) returns String
		let rangedAlias = self.rangedAliasName(tag, nameId: nameId)
		if rangedAlias.isEmpty() or tagAdmitsNoAggregate(gotTag) 'bothHalvesAreClassWords'
			return self.displayTypeNameOf(tag, nameId: nameId)
		end 'bothHalvesAreClassWords'

		return String.from(rangedAlias)
	end 'declaredSlotDisplayName'

	// ⭐⭐ **A DECLARED PLACE THAT CANNOT HOLD A FAT POINTER YET — THE ONE REFUSAL FOR EVERY SUCH PLACE**
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1, grown by its reviews). An existential is TWO machine words, and it is
	// supplied only where there is somewhere to put the second one: a PARAMETER of a NAMED function (an
	// adjacent hidden argument), a LOCAL (a second SSA value), and — since slice 2 — a STRUCT FIELD (a second
	// SLOT, `Project.fieldSlotCount`). Every place `DeclaredStoragePosition` names lacks that room, and each
	// was a COMPILER PANIC, a SEGFAULT or a silently degraded type on a program the front end accepted until
	// its arm existed — measured, every one:
	//
	//   • a UNION PAYLOAD — `classifyUnionPayload` panicked naming this very function, which had been designed
	//     and never written. A payload slot is one machine word and, unlike a struct field, cannot widen (every
	//     case shares one slot region), so a payload bound out of a `match` arm would dispatch through whatever
	//     the next slot happened to hold.
	//   • the RETURN positions that CANNOT read the second return register, which since the return ABI
	//     landed are no longer the same case as the one that can. A DIRECT call's return writes it (P1.7a
	//     slice 2); a THROWING function's is already spending it on the error flag; a function TYPE's and a
	//     REQUIREMENT's are dispatched through call ops that declare one result. ⚠ v1 hits the identical wall
	//     on the throwing case and SILENTLY SKIPS its return-witness path (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:13545`);
	//     shv2 refuses instead, because silence is the one unacceptable outcome — and before any of this it
	//     did neither, it panicked at the first dispatch on the result.
	//   • a CONTAINER ELEMENT — an element slot is one machine word.
	//   • the three PARAMETER kinds with no adjacent slot — a closure's and a function TYPE's (the uniform
	//     `(userargs, env)` indirect ABI) and an interface REQUIREMENT's (a witness call). MEASURED: exit 139
	//     on x64 with a proper conformer, and a wasm trap even where the parameter was never dispatched on.
	//   • a TUPLE ELEMENT — a tuple is a synthesized struct, and this one reached the BACKEND: it panicked in
	//     `LayoutDescriptor.primitiveTypeTagName`, on an assertion that the front end had already refused it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED AT THE DECLARATION AND NOT AT THE USE.** The panics all fired at a USE — a field store, a
	// method call on a returned value — where the mechanism is already missing and the author has nothing left
	// to change. Refusing where the type is WRITTEN names the line that has to move.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TOLERANT READERS ARE KEPT OUT THREE DIFFERENT WAYS, AND ONLY ONE IS "BY CONSTRUCTION".** This
	// used to claim the single structural reason for all of them, which stopped being true as the positions
	// grew:
	//   • the PAYLOAD/ELEMENT readers, by construction — during `foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`
	//     `parseTypeReference` cannot mint an `interfaceRef` at all (its arm is gated on `allFilesFolded`, and
	//     the interface registry is not filled until the sweep ends), so a swept declaration is a bare `named`.
	//   • the FUNCTION-TYPE reader, by a FLAG — `readFunctionTypeAlias`'s `recordSignature`, which the sweep
	//     passes `false`.
	//   • the INTERFACE reader, by DEFERRAL — it notes rather than throws, because its other caller
	//     (`foldInterfaceDeclarationInto`) swallows a `ParseError` and would silently drop the declaration.
	//     That one is why "one call per position" is not literally true either: the requirement positions
	//     reach the message through `requireNoUnstorableRequirementType` instead of through this function.
	function requireStorableDeclaredType(declared MaxonType, position DeclaredStoragePosition, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
			return
		end 'notAnExistential'

		throw ParseError.unsupported(interfaceStoragePositionMessage(position, interfaceName: self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(declared))), line: line, column: column)
	end 'requireStorableDeclaredType'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE `Base with Args` ARGUMENT MAY BE — the two-armed door `parseGenericArgNode` asks, and
	// the ONE place a fat pointer is admitted rather than refused.**
	//
	// A CONTAINER ELEMENT is the ordinary storage question, unchanged: an element slot is one machine word.
	//
	// An ASSOCIATED-TYPE BINDING naming an interface is something else entirely — it is not a value being
	// stored anywhere, it is the program DECLARING that it holds that associated position existentially
	// (`ProgramSignatures.settleAssociatedTypeHoldings`). Admitted where the associated type is only ever a
	// requirement's RESULT, because the second table then rides in each conformer's own witness table; refused
	// where a requirement TAKES it, because there the table would have to come from the caller and no static
	// slot can be right about it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARITY GUARDS ARE `checkGenericInstance`'s AND E3016's, NOT THIS DOOR'S** — an argument past the
	// interface's `uses` names, and an interface no file declares readably, both ADMIT here and are reported
	// where they belong. Refusing on them would report an arity mistake as an ABI one.
	function requireCarryableGenericArg(argType MaxonType, slot GenericArgSlot, position FieldIndex, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		match slot 'which'
			containerElement then try self.requireStorableDeclaredType(argType, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.containerElement, line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
			associatedTypeBinding(interfaceName) then try self.requireCarryableAssociatedBinding(argType, interfaceName: interfaceName, position: position, argTok: argTok)
		end 'which'
	end 'requireCarryableGenericArg'

	// The binding arm of the door above. A CONCRETE argument is not this check's business at all — it is a
	// claim the conformances settle (E3125) — so only an interface-typed one is looked at.
	function requireCarryableAssociatedBinding(argType MaxonType, interfaceName ByteArray, position FieldIndex, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		if maxonTypeTag(argType) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'aConcreteBinding'
			return
		end 'aConcreteBinding'

		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: interfaceName) 'base'
			notFound then return
			found(iface) then try self.requireHeldAssociatedTypeIsOnlyReturned(iface, position: position, heldAt: self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(argType)), argTok: argTok)
		end 'base'
	end 'requireCarryableAssociatedBinding'

	// Refuse a holding whose associated type reaches a requirement's PARAMETER — see
	// `DeclaredStoragePosition.associatedTypeBinding` for why that direction alone is unsupplyable.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF THE TRANSITIVE REQUIREMENT LIST** (`requirementTakesType`, the same door E3119/E3120
	// read), so a parameter projected in through `extends` is refused too. A scan of `iface.methods` alone was
	// a measured escape there and would be the identical one here.
	function requireHeldAssociatedTypeIsOnlyReturned(iface IrInterface, position FieldIndex, heldAt ByteArray, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		if position >= iface.associatedTypeNames.count() 'pastThisInterfacesUsesNames'
			return
		end 'pastThisInterfacesUsesNames'

		let associated = try iface.associatedTypeNames.get(position) otherwise panic("requireHeldAssociatedTypeIsOnlyReturned: associatedTypeNames.get OOB at {position} below count {iface.associatedTypeNames.count()} — the guard above excludes it")
		if not requirementTakesType(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), iface: iface, name: associated) 'onlyEverAResult'
			return
		end 'onlyEverAResult'

		throw ParseError.unsupported(interfaceStoragePositionMessage(DeclaredStoragePosition.associatedTypeBinding, interfaceName: heldAt), line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
	end 'requireHeldAssociatedTypeIsOnlyReturned'

	// The interned NAME behind a `named`/`structRef` id, from THIS file's interner — where every id a
	// type check sees was minted: the value during this parse, a declared return/field/binding type
	// from this file. A miss is a compiler invariant break, never user input (see TypeNameInterner).
	function internedName(id TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
		return try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("Parser.internedName: type-name id {id} is not in this file's interner — every `named`/`structRef` id is minted from it")
	end 'internedName'

	// The interned type-name id of an SSA value, read straight from the dense `valueNameIds` column —
	// the id `typeOf` and `valueIsUnsigned` both need, and a scalar (no `MaxonType` materialized). A
	// miss is a parser invariant break: the two type columns are minted in lockstep for every value.
	function valueNameId(value ValueId) returns TypeNameId
		return try self.valueNameIds.get(value) otherwise panic("Parser.valueNameId: value v{value} has no recorded type name — the two type columns fell out of lockstep")
	end 'valueNameId'

	// Record that `value` (a generic instance) came from instance alias `aliasName` (P1.6-C) — the
	// provenance the shared `GenericInstanceId` cannot carry (`WrapperA` and `WrapperB` share one gid). An
	// EMPTY `aliasName` records nothing: a value with no statically-known alias DECAYS (its `Idx` results and
	// arguments lose per-instance identity), which matches the oracle.
	function recordValueInstanceAlias(value ValueId, aliasName ByteArray)
		if aliasName.isEmpty() 'noAlias'
			return
		end 'noAlias'
		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty map: a function that constructs an instance gets its own map here,
		// on first write, so the generics-free corpus never allocates one (the reset points at the shared one).
		if self.valueInstanceAlias is sharedEmptyInstanceAliasMap 'stillShared'
			self.valueInstanceAlias = ValueInstanceAliasMap.create()
		end 'stillShared'
		self.valueInstanceAlias.upsert(value, value: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(aliasName))
	end 'recordValueInstanceAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **HAND THIS FUNCTION'S EXISTENTIAL-INSTANCE COLUMN TO THE `IrFunction`, ONCE, AFTER THE BODY (W59).**
	// Every sibling value column is handed over at `IrFunction.create` and grown in place afterwards; this one
	// is REPLACED by the copy-on-write below rather than grown, so the reference `create` was given is the
	// shared empty for exactly the functions that later record something. Called at the end of all THREE parses
	// that mint an `IrFunction` — a declared function, a default-value helper and a lifted closure — each at the
	// point its own `recordFuncSignature` runs, which is the one place per parse where the body is complete and
	// the parser's field still points at the map that body filled (a closure's body SWAPS the field, and swaps
	// it back, so the enclosing function's publish must happen after the swap has been undone — which the end of
	// its body is).
	function publishExistentialInstances(func IrFunction)
		func.valueExistentialInstances = self.valueExistentialInstance
		// ⭐ **W117's per-value marks travel to SemanticCheck the same way, at the same moment, for the same
		// reason**: they name ids in THIS function's SSA space, so they must be handed over while that space is
		// still the current one. The call-site half of the rule cannot run in the parser — whether `bump(a)` is
		// legal depends on `bump`'s BODY, which may be in another file — which is precisely the split E3019
		// already has. The shared-empty set rides across unchanged for a function that marked nothing.
		func.immutableGlobalAliasValues = self.immutableGlobalReads
	end 'publishExistentialInstances'

	// ⭐⭐ **RECORD THE `with` ARGUMENTS AN EXISTENTIAL VALUE'S DECLARED TYPE NAMED (W57)** — see
	// `ValueExistentialInstanceMap` for what the column is for and why the fact cannot ride on the type. Two
	// producers write it and they are the two ends of one chain: the PARAMETER whose declared type is a
	// generic alias over an interface base, and a dispatch RESULT whose associated position that parameter's
	// instance holds at another interface (`mintHeldAssociatedWitnessResult`) — so `for item in source` reaches
	// `current()` with the source's own binding still in hand.
	function recordValueExistentialInstance(value ValueId, instance GenericInstanceId)
		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty map, exactly as `recordValueInstanceAlias` does and for its
		// reason: a function with no parameterized-interface value never allocates one.
		if self.valueExistentialInstance is sharedEmptyExistentialInstanceMap 'stillShared'
			self.valueExistentialInstance = ValueExistentialInstanceMap.create()
		end 'stillShared'
		self.valueExistentialInstance.upsert(value, value: instance)
	end 'recordValueExistentialInstance'

	// ⭐ **RECORD THE RANGED TYPEALIAS A VALUE'S DECLARED TYPE NAMES** (see `ValueDeclaredAliasMap` for what
	// this map is for and why it exists beside the two type columns). An `undeclared` alias records nothing:
	// a cast to `ExitCode`, to a bare primitive or to a struct names no ranged alias, and a value with no
	// entry simply falls back to its own `named` tag (`valueDenotedAlias`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CALLER OWNS THE ALIASING QUESTION.** This writes the value it is given, so a caller holding a
	// value that another BINDING also names must not call it — see `ValueDeclaredAliasMap`'s gate and
	// `applyCast`, the one door where that can happen.
	function recordValueDeclaredAlias(value ValueId, alias DeclaredAlias)
		match alias 'a'
			undeclared then break 'a'
			declared(declaredAlias) then self.recordValueDeclaredAliasName(value, aliasName: declaredAlias.name)
		end 'a'
	end 'recordValueDeclaredAlias'

	// The write itself, off the alias's NAME — split out because `mintOwnedCallResult` reaches it holding a
	// `MaxonType` rather than a resolved `DeclaredAlias`, and both must land the same entry.
	function recordValueDeclaredAliasName(value ValueId, aliasName ByteArray)
		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty map, exactly as `recordValueInstanceAlias` does and for its
		// reason: a function with no float-alias value and no `as` cast never allocates one.
		if self.valueDeclaredAlias is sharedEmptyDeclaredAliasMap 'stillShared'
			self.valueDeclaredAlias = ValueDeclaredAliasMap.create()
		end 'stillShared'
		self.valueDeclaredAlias.upsert(value, value: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(aliasName))
	end 'recordValueDeclaredAliasName'

	// ⭐ **THE RANGED TYPEALIAS AN EXPRESSION'S VALUE DENOTES** — the SOURCE half of every containment
	// question in this file: E3010's *"is the written cast unneeded?"*, and (A4f) every range-check door's
	// *"is the runtime guard provably dead?"*. They are one question with two consumers, so the value's
	// answer is read in one place; it was named for the cast when the cast was its only caller.
	//
	// The recorded entry WINS over the value's own `named` tag, because it is the LATER and more specific
	// statement about the same value: `identity(42) as Byte` hands back the call result untouched, and that
	// result's type column still says `Integer` while the expression now denotes a `Byte`.
	function valueDenotedAlias(value ValueId) returns DeclaredAlias
		let recorded = self.recordedCastAlias(value)

		return match recorded 'r'
			declared gives recorded
			undeclared gives self.provenAliasOfValue(value)
		end 'r'
	end 'valueDenotedAlias'

	// ⭐ **THE ALIAS A CAST RECORDED ON THIS VALUE, OR `undeclared`** — the map read the two denoted-alias
	// readers share, spelled once. They differ ONLY in what they fall back to when nothing was recorded,
	// and that difference is the whole of why there are two (see `domainDeclaringAlias`); the lookup
	// itself is one fact and lives in one place.
	function recordedCastAlias(value ValueId) returns DeclaredAlias
		let aliasId = try self.valueDeclaredAlias.get(value) otherwise return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		return self.declaredAliasOfNameId(aliasId)
	end 'recordedCastAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE RANGED ALIAS AN INTEGER VALUE'S DECLARED TYPE NAMES — `valueDenotedAlias` WITH THE OTHER
	// FALLBACK, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A MEASURED ONE (X10 review).** Same map, same precedence; where
	// nothing was recorded this reads the value's DECLARED type (`declaredAliasOfValue`) and not its
	// PROVEN one. It is what `integerValueDomain` — and so both consumers of that domain, the divide
	// and the decimal renderer — resolves a value's signedness through.
	//
	// ⚠ **A PHI'S DECLARED ALIAS IS WITHHELD FROM THE ZERO PROOF AND MUST NOT BE WITHHELD FROM THE
	// SIGNEDNESS RULE.** G14's withhold exists because a merge can carry a `0` its declared `NonZero`
	// never proved, and a wrong `neverZero` is an UNCATCHABLE hardware fault. Signedness has no such
	// asymmetry: it selects an INSTRUCTION from the type the program WROTE, which is exactly what the
	// bootstrap oracle reads, and `valueIsUnsigned` — the shift rule, the other half of the same
	// question — has always read the plain declared type. MEASURED: routing this through
	// `provenAliasOfValue` turned stdlib's float printer back to `cqo`+`idiv` on a loop-carried
	// `int(0 to u64.max)`, silently undoing X10 on ten fragments.
	function domainDeclaringAlias(value ValueId) returns DeclaredAlias
		let recorded = self.recordedCastAlias(value)

		return match recorded 'r'
			declared gives recorded
			undeclared gives self.declaredAliasOfValue(value)
		end 'r'
	end 'domainDeclaringAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **`declaredAliasOfValue` MINUS THE MERGES NOTHING PROVED — the reader for every question that
	// spends the alias as a PROOF, as opposed to reading it as the value's DECLARED type (G14).** The type-name
	// column answers both questions and they are not the same question: for a phi it states the type the merge
	// was declared with, which no incoming edge need satisfy (see `phiAliasUnprovenMarks`).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SPLIT IS THE POINT — `declaredAliasOfValue` KEEPS ITS TWO HONEST READERS.**
	// `requireOtherwiseInRangedReturn` asks what a try-result's type IS, to refuse an out-of-range literal
	// fallback against it, and `elementPackingOfValue` asks how a value would be STORED as an array element.
	// Both are properties of the DECLARATION and stay right for a merge; routing them through here would
	// delete a compile-time diagnostic and silently change an element stride. The two PROOF readers are this
	// function's: `valueDenotedAlias` (E3010 and A4f's guard-death test) and `divisorProof`.
	//
	// ⚠ It is consulted only as `valueDenotedAlias`'s FALLBACK, never ahead of `valueDeclaredAlias`: a
	// recorded cast target IS a proof — the `as` emits the guard — so `<phi> as Num` legitimately denotes
	// `Num` afterwards even though the phi alone does not.
	function provenAliasOfValue(value ValueId) returns DeclaredAlias
		if self.phiAliasIsUnproven(value) 'mergeProvesNothing'
			return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
		end 'mergeProvesNothing'

		return self.declaredAliasOfValue(value)
	end 'provenAliasOfValue'

	// Is `value` a merge whose declared ranged alias nothing proved? Read in the one place that spends the
	// column (`provenAliasOfValue`), written in two (`mintPhi`, `recordMergeAliasProofAgainst`).
	function phiAliasIsUnproven(value ValueId) returns bool
		return try self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks.get(value) otherwise panic("phiAliasIsUnproven: v{value} has no merge-alias slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
	end 'phiAliasIsUnproven'

	// The instance alias NAME `value` came from (`WrapperA`), or `EmptySourceText` when it has none — a
	// `self` receiver, a value merged across branches, or any non-instance value (P1.6-C).
	function instanceAliasNameOfValue(value ValueId) returns ByteArray
		let aliasId = try self.valueInstanceAlias.get(value) otherwise return EmptySourceText
		return self.internedName(aliasId)
	end 'instanceAliasNameOfValue'

	// ⭐ **THE INTERNED NAME OF THE DECLARED AGGREGATE A (tag, nameId) PAIR DENOTES, OR `EmptySourceText`
	// FOR ANYTHING THAT IS NOT ONE** — a scalar, a ranged-int alias, a String. The parser-tier half of
	// the OPEN #54 identity check, ported from v1's `namedStructOrEnumName` (SemanticCheck.maxon:2687),
	// which tells a struct/enum name apart from a numeric alias by the same registry lookup.
	//
	// ⚠ **KEEP IN LOCKSTEP WITH ITS SemanticCheck TWIN `aggregateNameFor`** — the two answer the SAME
	// question (which tags name an aggregate, and how) on the two interners the two tiers hold (this one
	// on the file-local `self.artifact.typeNames` + `self.signatures`; the other on the merged
	// `project.typeNames` + `project.structTypes`/`enumTypes`). The classification MUST match — teach a
	// new aggregate tag to one and not the other and a value the parser names an aggregate would slip the
	// call-argument check as a scalar. They cannot share a call (different tier, different interner), so
	// the safety net is the specs on BOTH sides: `error.return-wrong-union` guards this one,
	// `error.callarg-wrong-union-*` guards the twin.
	//
	// It takes the SCALAR (tag, nameId) pair, never a `MaxonType`, so a coercion site reads it off the
	// `valueTags`/`valueNameIds` columns (or a declared type's tag/id) and materializes no heap union —
	// the discipline TypeRules' header states for every type rule, and the reason `structRef`-returning
	// is checked without an allocation on the common scalar-returning path.
	//
	//   • `structRef` is ALWAYS a declared struct — its name, no registry lookup (the parser mints the
	//     arm only for a declared `type`, so `structRef` cannot name anything else).
	//   • `named` is a boxed union/enum HERE at the parser (TypeResolution erases it to an integer only
	//     later), so it is an aggregate exactly when the signature index registers its name — a
	//     ranged-int alias is `named` too and is numeric, NOT an aggregate (the theorem `typeClassOf`
	//     rests on).
	//   • every other tag names no aggregate.
	//
	// The "none" answer is `EmptySourceText`, matching this file's `enclosingType` idiom: a real
	// aggregate name is never empty, so `isEmpty()` distinguishes it exactly (`namedAggregatesConflict`).
	function aggregateNameOf(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'struct'
			return self.internedName(nameId)
		end 'struct'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL IS A NOMINAL AGGREGATE, IDENTIFIED BY ITS INTERFACE (P1.7a-existentials slice 1
		// review), AND WITHOUT THIS ARM IT WAS A MEASURED SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** Two existentials share the
		// `interfaceRef` tag and therefore `TypeClass.interfaceValue`, so `checkDeclaredType` — the tag-only
		// verdict every coercion site opens with — AGREES for any two interfaces. Nothing else then asks:
		// this function is where the parser's assignment, return, field-store, global-store and
		// container-element doors get their nominal identity, and an empty answer means "no identity to
		// compare".
		//
		// **MEASURED**, on `interface Derived extends Base` with `var t = <a Base>` then `t = <a Derived>`:
		// the program COMPILED and returned **2** where the answer is **1**. `t.base()` numbered slot 0 of
		// `Base`'s table through `__witness_Impl.Derived`, whose slot 0 is `derived()` —
		// `interfaceWitnessSlots` puts an interface's OWN requirements before its parents'. A wrong function
		// pointer, no diagnostic, and the one thing this rung's own header says the refusal exists to stop.
		//
		// ⚠ **NAME IDENTITY IS EXACT HERE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT THE `extends`-TRANSITIVE RULE
		// `ConformanceCheck.typeConformsTo` USES.** A CONCRETE `Impl implements Derived` reaching a `Base`
		// place is a widening the argument door performs by MATERIALIZING `__witness_Impl.Base`; a `Derived`
		// EXISTENTIAL reaching one is not, because its witness half already addresses `Derived`'s table and
		// there is no conversion between two tables with two slot orders. Transitive conformance answers the
		// first question; exact identity answers this one.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			return self.internedName(nameId)
		end 'existential'

		// ⭐ A GENERIC INSTANCE IS A NOMINAL AGGREGATE, IDENTIFIED BY ITS COMPILED NAME (P1.7). `Box with
		// Leaf` and `Box with Other` are both `genericInstance`-tagged pointers to a base `Box` box, so the
		// tag check reads them as one type and the wrong one, stored where the other is declared, is later
		// dropped by the wrong `__destruct_<instance>` — the identical wild free two distinct STRUCTS sharing
		// `structRef` would be, one abstraction up. The id a `genericInstance` carries is a
		// `GenericInstanceId`, which is whole-program (not a file-local interner id), so it needs no
		// `internedName` — and its name is a pure READ here, pre-derived by `queryProgramSignatures`.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'genericInstance'
			return self.signatures.canonicalInstanceName(nameId)
		end 'genericInstance'

		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.namedAggregateName(self.internedName(nameId))
	end 'aggregateNameOf'

	// The `named` arm's whole rule, given the already-interned name: which NAMES carry a nominal identity, and
	// which are numeric. Named apart from the tag dispatch above so that the question *"is this NAME nominal?"*
	// — the only arm of `aggregateNameOf` that consults a registry rather than reading the tag — is stated once,
	// where a second spelling of it is exactly what would let a signature's stored identity and a live value's
	// disagree about the same name. Every reader arrives through `aggregateNameOf`, including the pre-erasure
	// columns, which reach it through `erasedAggregateNameOf`.
	function namedAggregateName(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if self.signatures.containsStruct(name) or self.signatures.containsEnum(name) 'registeredAggregate'
			return name
		end 'registeredAggregate'

		// ⭐ A REGISTERED PER-INSTANCE ALIAS ("IntWrapper.Idx") is a nominal aggregate too (P1.6-C): a
		// `named` value carrying one has an IDENTITY the tag check cannot see (two per-instance aliases share
		// the `named` tag and the `integer` width). A BASE-TEMPLATE inner alias ("Wrapper.Idx") and a plain
		// ranged alias are NOT nominal — `isPerInstanceAlias` answers false for both — so they stay structural.
		if self.signatures.isPerInstanceAlias(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'perInstanceAlias'
			return name
		end 'perInstanceAlias'

		return EmptySourceText
	end 'namedAggregateName'

	// ⭐ **RECORD A FUNCTION VALUE MEETING A FUNCTION-TYPED PLACE — the FUNCTION twin of the nominal
	// `aggregatesConflict` identity check every door beside this one already makes.** A `structRef` and a
	// `function` are both aggregates the tag check reads as one type: two different structs share
	// `structRef`, and two different function TYPES share `function`. The struct half is decided in place,
	// because a struct's identity is its name and the parser holds both names. The function half cannot be:
	// a function value's own shape is its parameter list, which lives in the whole-program `funcSignatures`
	// the parser does not have, and the shapes are compared RESOLVED, which is not true until
	// `resolveTypes`. So this records the two governing NAMES and
	// `SemanticCheck.checkFunctionTypeDoors` decides.
	//
	// A NO-OP unless BOTH sides are function-tagged: a function value meeting a non-function place is
	// `DeclaredTypeMatch.functionIntoNonFunction` (already reported, with its own advice), and a
	// non-function value meeting a function place is an ordinary class mismatch. Only function-meets-function
	// reaches a rule that has nothing left to compare.
	// ⚠ **PRECONDITION: `isFunctionTypeDoor` ANSWERED TRUE** — the gate is the CALLER's, so a call that is
	// not a door evaluates nothing at all. That matters here more than at a typical helper: two of the three
	// doors sit on the hottest paths in the parser (`assignableValue` runs at every assignment,
	// `parseReturnStatement` at every `return`) and almost none of them is a function meeting a function.
	//
	// ⚠ Placing the gate here was NOT what made the cost go away, and the difference is worth recording
	// because it was measured the wrong way round first: with the gate inside, at the call site, or with the
	// declared type passed as a `MaxonType`, the ladder read the SAME +1,852 allocations at rung 0 to the
	// digit. What actually cost that was the place NOUN — a `String` threaded on from `assignableValue` —
	// and it went to ZERO the moment the noun became a scalar (`FunctionTypeDoor`). Every argument this
	// takes is now a scalar or a shared reference, which is why `phase:parse` is allocation-identical to
	// the parent.
	function recordFunctionTypeDoor(declaredNameId TypeNameId, gotNameId TypeNameId, door FunctionTypeDoor, placeName ByteArray, anchor Token)
		self.recordFunctionTypeDoorByName(self.internedName(declaredNameId), gotName: self.internedName(gotNameId), door: door, placeName: placeName, anchor: anchor)
	end 'recordFunctionTypeDoor'

	// ⭐ **THE RECORD ITSELF — the door sites that already hold NAMES rather than ids.** A
	// `PendingFunctionTypeDoor` travels as two names (see there: interner-free, so the record survives the
	// merge unremapped), so the id-taking entry point above is a convenience for the sites whose two sides
	// are SSA/type-column ids, and this is the same fact with the un-interning already done.
	//
	// ⚠ **SPLIT RATHER THAN COPIED, and the copy is what it replaced.** A function-backed enum case holds its
	// two sides as `ByteArray` (a case's raw value is bytes on the layout), so it hand-built the
	// `PendingFunctionTypeDoor` — becoming the one site that knew `filePath` belongs on the record and how an
	// anchor decomposes. Two sites spelling one record is how the two come to disagree about it.
	function recordFunctionTypeDoorByName(declaredName ByteArray, gotName ByteArray, door FunctionTypeDoor, placeName ByteArray, anchor Token)
		self.artifact.recordFunctionTypeDoor(PendingFunctionTypeDoor.create(gotName, declaredName: declaredName, door: door, placeName: placeName, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column, filePath: self.filePath))
	end 'recordFunctionTypeDoorByName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE IDENTITY `resolveTypes` IS ABOUT TO DESTROY — the ONE extraction EVERY pre-erasure column
	// fills through (OPEN #54 Slice B2, widened to the function-TYPE tier by BATCH18-b).** The interned name
	// of a declared boxed union/enum (or a per-instance ranged alias), and `EmptySourceText` for everything
	// else. Captured at the real parse because this is the last point such a type still knows its name:
	// `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType` collapses it to bare `integer`, leaving every whole-program check
	// nothing to compare.
	//
	// ⚠ **`resolveNamedStruct` IS ASKED FIRST, AND THAT LINE IS THE WHOLE UNIFICATION.** The rule this column
	// states is *"the identity that does NOT survive resolution"*, so a STRUCT must answer empty — its
	// `structRef(id)` survives and every reader takes its name live off the type. But the two callers hand in
	// types at DIFFERENT stages of exactly that rewrite: `parseFunction`/`parseClosureExpression` have already
	// folded `resolveNamedStructAll` over their parameter list, while `readFunctionTypeAlias` has not (a
	// function typealias stores what `parseTypeReference` minted, and a struct is still `named` there). Asking
	// the idempotent re-tag here is what makes both answer alike; without it a `function(Point)` ALIAS carried
	// "Point" while a `function(p Point)` FUNCTION carried nothing, and `functionShapesAgree` would have
	// refused the legal program that passes one to the other. A near-mirror that is not exact is this
	// project's signature bug — so the two sides share the extraction, not merely the comparison.
	//
	// ⚠ **CALLABLE ONLY FROM THE REAL PARSE**, for `resolveNamedStruct`/`aggregateNameOf`'s own reason: both
	// consult `self.signatures`, which the tolerant declaration SWEEP is still building, so a name the sweep
	// has not reached yet would classify differently there. Every caller is inside a `recordSignature`-gated
	// or real-parse-only path.
	function erasedAggregateNameOf(t MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		let resolved = self.resolveNamedStruct(t)
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(resolved)
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'survivesResolution'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'survivesResolution'

		return self.aggregateNameOf(tag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(resolved))
	end 'erasedAggregateNameOf'

	// The same extraction for a RETURN slot — a `void` return carries no type and so no identity. Its column
	// is a single `ByteArray` rather than an array because a function type returns exactly one thing.
	function erasedAggregateNameOfReturn(rt MaxonReturnType) returns ByteArray
		return match rt 'r'
			void gives EmptySourceText
			value(inner) gives self.erasedAggregateNameOf(inner)
		end 'r'
	end 'erasedAggregateNameOfReturn'

	// `erasedAggregateNameOf` folded over a parameter list — `FuncSignature.paramAggregateNames` for a
	// declared function or a lifted closure, one entry per parameter and in lockstep with `paramTypes`.
	function paramAggregateNamesOf(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for pt in paramTypes 'each'
			names.push(self.erasedAggregateNameOf(pt))
		end 'each'
		return names
	end 'paramAggregateNamesOf'

	// ⭐ **THE PRE-ERASURE *SPELLING* COLUMN A SIGNATURE CARRIES (user ruling, 2026-08-04)** — the interned
	// name of every `named` parameter, `EmptySourceText` for anything else. Its sibling
	// `paramAggregateNamesOf` above walks the SAME `paramTypes`, position for position, so the two columns
	// cannot come to disagree about which parameter is which. See `FuncSignature.paramDeclaredNames` for why
	// they are different facts — a SPELLING to print versus an IDENTITY to compare.
	//
	// Captured HERE, at the real parse, for `paramAggregateNamesOf`'s reason: this is the last point a `named`
	// parameter still knows its name, because `resolveTypes` erases both a union/enum and a ranged alias to
	// bare `integer` (TypeResolution.resolveNamedType). A STRUCT / interface / generic instance keeps its own
	// tag through resolution and is read straight off `paramTypes`, so its slot stays empty and no name is
	// stored twice.
	function paramDeclaredNamesOf(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
		for pt in paramTypes 'each'
			let tag = maxonTypeTag(pt)
			if tag == ValueTypeTag.named 'erasedByResolution'
				names.push(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(pt)))
			end 'erasedByResolution' else 'keepsItsOwnTag'
				names.push(EmptySourceText)
			end 'keepsItsOwnTag'
		end 'each'
		return names
	end 'paramDeclaredNamesOf'

	// ⭐ **THE PRIMITIVE TAG THE RANGED ALIAS `nameId` REDUCES TO**, or `named` when no ranged alias
	// declares that name. It is decided through the SAME whole-program `aliasOf` door
	// `valueIsUnsigned`/`fieldStorageType` already ask, so an alias declared in ANY file resolves — and
	// NEVER a String/struct/union `named` (`aliasOf` answers `undeclared` for those, and the name keeps
	// its `named` tag). The parse-time twin of `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType`'s alias arm.
	//
	// ⚠ The caller must know the tag is `named` before asking: `nameId` for a non-`named` type is
	// `UnnamedTypeId`, which is not in the interner (`internedName` would panic). `floatResolvedTag` /
	// `resolveFloatAliasType` / `builtinConformerOfType` are the gated doors, and every reader goes
	// through one of them.
	//
	// TWO facts are read off this ONE reduction, and they must not become two lookups: whether a `named`
	// is a FLOAT alias (F2a — the tag `named` was too coarse to carry), and whether it is an INT alias
	// (the builtin-conformance receiver classifier, which must see `Byte` and `Integer` as `int` exactly
	// as `ProgramSignatures.conformerTypeArgName` does for a witness type argument).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REGISTRY IS NOT THE WHOLE ALIAS SET, AND THE ONE NAME OUTSIDE IT IS THIS RUNG'S OWN RESULT
	// TYPE.** `HashValue` — what `hash()` returns — is a ranged int alias the COMPILER declares, so it is in
	// no registry `aliasOf` can see (`isSynthesizedIntAliasName`). Asked through the registry alone it
	// answered `named`, and `i.hash().hash()` — an int method on an int, which the header of
	// `builtinConformerOfType` claims is one conformer with `Integer`/`Byte`/`int` — was E2015 while
	// `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType` had erased the very same name to `integer` since slice 2a.
	function rangedAliasTag(nameId TypeNameId) returns ValueTypeTag
		return self.rangedAliasTagOfName(self.internedName(nameId))
	end 'rangedAliasTag'

	// The rule above, asked of the NAME rather than of an interner id — for the one caller that holds no
	// id: the top-level constant evaluator, which "emits no ops and touches no artifact" and so has no
	// interner of its own (`requireConstCastTargetKind` passes `UnnamedTypeId` as a filler). Both spellings
	// of an `as` cast must decide a float-alias target the same way, so the decision is written once here
	// and the id-keyed form above only resolves the id.
	function rangedAliasTagOfName(name ByteArray) returns ValueTypeTag
		if isSynthesizedIntAliasName(name) 'compilerDeclaredIntAlias'
			return ValueTypeTag.integer
		end 'compilerDeclaredIntAlias'

		return match self.signatures.aliasOf(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'a'
			declared(alias) gives maxonTypeTag(alias.underlying)
			undeclared gives ValueTypeTag.named
		end 'a'
	end 'rangedAliasTagOfName'

	// Does `nameId` name a ranged FLOAT alias? A `float(low to high)` alias
	// (`Parser.readTypeAliasDeclaration`) is the ONE `named` type whose VALUE is a genuine float; an
	// `int(low to high)` alias is `named` too and is an int.
	function namedIsFloatAlias(nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return tagIsFloat(self.rangedAliasTag(nameId))
	end 'namedIsFloatAlias'

	// A `named` TAG that names a ranged float alias resolves to `float`; every other tag passes through —
	// an INT alias stays `named` (`checkDeclaredType` reads it identically, and its name still carries the
	// signedness `valueIsUnsigned` needs), a scalar/struct/String carries no alias. THE F2a resolution for
	// a DECLARED type met at a coercion site: `checkDeclaredType(3.14's float, Float)` must ask
	// `checkDeclaredType(float, float)` and answer `ok`, not read `Float`'s overloaded `named` tag as an
	// integer and report a false narrowing (E3009). `resolveFloatAliasType` is its twin for a produced
	// VALUE's type; both delegate the alias lookup to `namedIsFloatAlias`.
	function floatResolvedTag(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ValueTypeTag
		// ⚠ The guard is repeated below rather than delegated to, and it is load-bearing HERE: callers hand
		// this the filler `UnnamedTypeId` beside a concrete tag all the time, and `internedName` PANICS on an
		// id its interner never minted. Only a `named` tag has a name to resolve.
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return tag
		end 'notNamed'
		return self.floatResolvedTagOfName(tag, name: self.internedName(nameId))
	end 'floatResolvedTag'

	// The resolution above, asked of the NAME — the form the constant evaluator's cast target needs (see
	// `rangedAliasTagOfName`). ONE statement of "a `named` tag over a ranged FLOAT alias is a `float`", so
	// the body cast and the top-level-`let` cast cannot come to disagree about which types a cast may cross
	// (`castHasNoLegalConversion`'s float-resolved precondition) or about what a rejection names.
	function floatResolvedTagOfName(tag ValueTypeTag, name ByteArray) returns ValueTypeTag
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return tag
		end 'notNamed'
		return ValueTypeTag.float if tagIsFloat(self.rangedAliasTagOfName(name)) else tag
	end 'floatResolvedTagOfName'

	// A `named` tag REDUCED to whatever its ranged alias underlies — `Integer` to `integer`, `Percent`
	// to `float` — with every other tag passing through. The general form of the two functions above,
	// and it exists because they are deliberately NOT general: `floatResolvedTag` leaves an INT alias
	// `named` (its callers still need the name's signedness), which makes it useless to a caller whose
	// question is "is this an integer?".
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS NOT `tagIsIntegral` EITHER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON IT EXISTS. `tagIsIntegral`
	// answers `true` for EVERY `named` tag, float aliases included, and its own comment says so and says
	// why it is sound where it is used. At `emitBitsToFloat` it would not be: it would admit a
	// `Percent`-typed operand through the front-end gate and hand `bitcastI64ToF64` an f64, which
	// `assertUnaryOperandDomain` answers with a PANIC. A gate whose failure mode is a compiler crash
	// on a legal-looking program has to resolve the name, not approximate it.
	//
	// The alias lookup itself is not restated — it is `rangedAliasTag`'s, exactly as the two above.
	// An UNDECLARED name reduces to `named` and therefore matches no primitive question, which is the
	// truthful answer: the program is being rejected for the missing type anyway (E3011).
	function rangedAliasResolvedTag(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ValueTypeTag
		// The guard `floatResolvedTag`'s carries, and load-bearing for the same reason: a non-`named`
		// tag's `nameId` is the filler `UnnamedTypeId`, which `internedName` PANICS on.
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return tag
		end 'notNamed'
		return self.rangedAliasTag(nameId)
	end 'rangedAliasResolvedTag'

	// Resolve a float-alias `named` TYPE to `MaxonType.float` — the produced-VALUE twin of
	// `floatResolvedTag`. A `returns Float` CALL RESULT is a genuine float value, so its tag must be
	// `float` (matching the XMM class the value-class column gives a float return from the callee's f64
	// Std return type), or `trunc(x)` reads the `named` tag as already-integral and returns `x` unchanged
	// and `return x` moves an XMM value through a GPR. An int alias keeps its `named` type (its name
	// carries signedness); a struct/scalar/String passes through.
	function resolveFloatAliasType(t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return t
		end 'notNamed'
		return MaxonType.float if self.namedIsFloatAlias(typeNameIdOf(t)) else t
	end 'resolveFloatAliasType'

	// ⭐ **THE BUILTIN CONFORMER A `(tag, nameId)` TYPE DISPATCHES ITS `.hash()`/`.equals()`/`.compare()`
	// UNDER (P1.7a direct dispatch)** — THE one classifier behind both spellings of a direct dispatch (a
	// binding receiver in `parseMethodCall`, a call-result receiver in `parsePostfix`), so the two cannot
	// come to disagree about which values carry a builtin conformance.
	//
	// A ranged INT alias reduces to `integer` FIRST, which is what makes `Integer`, `Byte`, `HashValue` and
	// a bare `int` one conformer rather than four: `Byte.compare` does not exist and never will — the
	// witness path already collapses a ranged type ARGUMENT the same way
	// (`ProgramSignatures.conformerTypeArgName` → `canonicalizeRangedAlias`), so both dispatch paths key the
	// same `int.compare` symbol off the same type. A ranged FLOAT alias reduces to `float` by the very same
	// step, so `Percent.compare` is `float.compare`.
	//
	// Every tag with no synthesized impl answers `notAConformer` (`builtinConformerOfTag`), which is what
	// keeps a struct receiver on the struct path.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A `string` TAG IS NO LONGER ONE OF THOSE, AND NOTHING HERE IS WHAT KEEPS A `String` RECEIVER OFF
	// THE CONFORMER ARM — THE ARM *ORDER* IS** (BATCH2 slice 2). This paragraph used to read "which is what
	// keeps a `String`-valued receiver out of here", and that sentence inverted the day `String` gained its
	// synthesized `hash`/`equals`: this classifier now answers `stringConformer` for a string tag, and
	// `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` routes a String receiver to `dispatchStringMethod` SEVEN arms above the
	// conformer arm — which is the load-bearing fact that header states, and the only one. Reading the
	// guarantee off this function instead would be reading it off the wrong mechanism, and the wrong one
	// stopped holding.
	//
	// ⇒ what the `string` answer is actually FOR is the `Self`-ARGUMENT check
	// (`requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs` → `valueIsConformer`), which the receiver split cannot reach:
	// `"ab".equals("ac")` hands a String to a `Self` formal and is admitted here, once.
	//
	// ⚠ It answers with the CONFORMER and not its name, and that is a measured decision rather than a
	// stylistic one — see `BuiltinConformer`'s header for the 16-byte box this used to allocate at every
	// method-call site in every program, feature or no feature.
	function builtinConformerOfType(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns BuiltinConformer
		return builtinConformerOfTag(tag if tag != ValueTypeTag.named else self.rangedAliasTag(nameId))
	end 'builtinConformerOfType'

	// The classifier above, asked of an SSA VALUE's own type — the form the postfix loop and the
	// `Self`-argument check need (a call result and a method argument are values, not bindings).
	function builtinConformerOfValue(value ValueId) returns BuiltinConformer
		return self.builtinConformerOfType(self.tagOf(value), nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'builtinConformerOfValue'

	// Is `value` a value of the conformer `conformer`? The concrete-receiver twin of
	// `isReceiverTypeParamValue`: what a `Self`-typed formal accepts. Compared as CONFORMERS rather than as
	// names, so a `Self` argument agrees with its receiver by the same classifier the dispatch was chosen by
	// and no name is materialized to answer a question about types.
	function valueIsConformer(value ValueId, conformer BuiltinConformer) returns bool
		return self.builtinConformerOfValue(value) == conformer
	end 'valueIsConformer'

	// The name is not a local and not a top-level binding WITH STORAGE. Say which of the two remaining things
	// it is: an INLINED top-level constant (E2013) or nothing at all (E2004).
	//
	// A stored-but-immutable binding — an array `let` (P1.7 slice 2b) — never reaches here: it resolves
	// through `globalDeclFor` like any other global, and `storeToGlobal` refuses it there, holding the
	// resolution that proves the name is declared. So the constant probe below is the whole remaining
	// question, exactly as it was before that binding existed.
	function rejectNonGlobalAssignment(nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.constantValueOf(nameTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'constant'
			// Either kind of constant is immutable — assigning to a managed `let` is the same mistake as
			// assigning to a scalar one, and gets the same diagnostic.
			found to foundManaged then throw assignToImmutableError(nameTok)
			notFound then throw ParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'constant'
	end 'rejectNonGlobalAssignment'

	// `return [<expr>]`. Sets the block's terminator directly, emitting the distinct
	// `retVoid` variant for a void return rather than a `ret` carrying some absent-value
	// sentinel (see MaxonDialect's `retVoid`).
	//
	// WHICH variant is decided by `func`'s DECLARED return type — never by the token that
	// follows `return`. The two are not interchangeable, and the token alone is the wrong
	// question: it says what the source WROTE, not what the function PROMISED, and when
	// they differ it is the promise the whole backend was built around. Deriving the
	// answer from the token stream instead gave the compiler two sources of truth for
	// "is this function void", with nothing to make them agree — and they disagreed on
	// exactly two programs, each of which was a bug no later pass could catch:
	//
	//   • `return <expr>` in a VOID function reached the x64 tier as a value `ret`, where
	//     `emitPrimaryReturnMove` has no return register to move the value into — a
	//     compiler PANIC on a program the reference compiler accepts.
	//   • a BARE `return` in a value-returning function reached it as `retVoid`, which
	//     emits the `ret` without ever writing R8 — a SILENT MISCOMPILE handing the caller
	//     whatever the last op happened to leave in the return register.
	//
	// `func.maxonReturnType` is not a copy of the contract kept beside it: it IS the
	// field the rest of the pipeline reads — it flows to `funcReturnTypes`, through
	// lowering, and becomes the backend's `StdReturnType`. Reading it here means the
	// parser and the tier that must honour it cannot be looking at different answers.
	//
	// So the statement's shape is checked AGAINST the contract rather than used to infer
	// it, and a `return` that cannot honour it is rejected here — where a source position
	// still exists — instead of reaching a tier that can only panic or emit the wrong op.
	function parseReturnStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let retTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.return)
		let bare = self.statementEndsAt(self.pos)

		if maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(func.maxonReturnType) 'voidFunction'
			if not bare 'valueFromVoid'
				throw ParseError.returnValueFromVoid(String.from(func.name), line: retTok.line, column: retTok.column)
			end 'valueFromVoid'

			self.emitScopeDrops(block, floor: WholeFunctionScopeFloor, anchor: retTok)
			self.artifact.emitTerminator(block, op: MaxonOp.retVoid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(retTok))
			return
		end 'voidFunction'

		// Value-returning: a bare `return` has nothing to return, and there is no value
		// the compiler may invent for it.
		if bare 'noValue'
			throw ParseError.returnWithoutValue(String.from(func.name), line: retTok.line, column: retTok.column)
		end 'noValue'

		try self.parseCheckedValueReturn(func, block: block, anchor: retTok)
	end 'parseReturnStatement'

	// ⭐ **PARSE AND RETURN ONE VALUE EXPRESSION, WITH EVERY RULE A `return` OWES** — the character-expecting
	// coercion, the escaping-closure reject, the declared-type check, the aggregate-identity check, the
	// widening coercion, the owned hand-off and the ranged-return site. The cursor sits ON the expression's
	// first token; `anchor` is what a diagnostic and the emitted span point at.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS SHARED BECAUSE A PARAMETER DEFAULT *IS* A RETURN** (BATCH2 slice 4). A default is
	// compiled as the whole body of a synthesized nullary helper whose declared return type is the
	// parameter's declared type (`parseDefaultHelperBody`), so `name String = 42` has to be refused with
	// exactly the diagnostic `return 42` earns from a `returns String` function, and `factor Number = 2` has
	// to widen exactly as `return 2` does. Every one of those rules lives here, once: a second copy at the
	// default site would be a second opinion about what a value must satisfy to leave a function.
	function parseCheckedValueReturn(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		// The terminator goes on the block the EXPRESSION ended in, not the one it started
		// in. `return a or b` evaluates the `or` across three blocks and leaves control in
		// the merge; terminating `block` instead would leave the merge unterminated (a
		// backend panic) and put the `ret` on a path the value it returns does not reach.
		// ⭐ A `return` IS AN INTEGER-EXPECTING POSITION when the function declares an integral type, so a
		// character literal coerces to its codepoint there. `return 'a'` from `returns Character` needs
		// nothing: the literal already IS one. See `integerizedOperand`.
		let retStart = self.pos
		let retVal = try self.integerizedOperand(try self.parseExpression(func, block: block), expected: maxonReturnTypeTag(func.maxonReturnType), start: retStart, pastEnd: self.pos)

		// ⭐ **ESCAPE (P1.5-A2b): a capturing closure may not be RETURNED** — the env it carries is dropped at
		// this frame's scope exit, so handing it back would leave the caller a closure whose environment is
		// gone. This is the route people actually write (`makeAdder`), and the frame guard is structural: the
		// closure mark is a per-function column, so `retVal` can only be a capturing closure of THIS frame. It
		// rides the stable `boundValue`, so a `return f` from a nested block refuses just as one from the top
		// does — the value crossing the block boundary is the same SSA id, still marked.
		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(retVal, message: "cannot return a closure that captures: {CapturingClosureEscapeSuffix}", tok: anchor)

		// The value must satisfy what the function PROMISED — `checkDeclaredType`, the same
		// COERCION-SITE rule the call arguments and the assignments ask, and deliberately not the
		// symmetric `typesAgree` an operator's two operands ask. `return flag` from a function
		// declared `returns Integer` is the argument-position bug in reverse: the bool's 1 payload
		// would be handed back through the return register as the integer 1. And `return 3.7` from
		// one is the LOSSY half — the classes agree, so only a one-sided rule can see it.
		//
		// The declared type here is the RAW parse-time one (a `named` alias, not yet resolved by
		// `TypeResolution`, which runs after the parse). A ranged INT alias's `named` tag is what
		// `typeClassOf`/`tagIsIntegral` are built to read; a ranged FLOAT alias's is not — `named` is
		// `tagIsIntegral`, so `checkDeclaredType(3.14's float, Float)` would read the alias as an int and
		// report a false narrowing (E3009) on `return 3.14`. `floatResolvedTag` resolves a float alias to
		// `float` HERE, at the call site, leaving the shared `named`-is-int rules untouched: `named` still
		// means int everywhere those rules read it, and only a proven float alias is lifted out of it.
		// `typeTagName` then prints `float` for the float alias and `int` for the int one, both the
		// resolved primitive the bootstrap prints.
		let retTag = self.tagOf(retVal)
		let declaredNameId = maxonReturnTypeNameId(func.maxonReturnType)
		let declaredTag = self.floatResolvedTag(maxonReturnTypeTag(func.maxonReturnType), nameId: declaredNameId)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INTERFACE-TYPED RETURN IS A WIDENING POSITION, NOT AN AGREEMENT ONE (P1.7a slice 2, the
		// return ABI).** It is the SECOND widening position the language has, and it takes exactly the shape
		// the first one took at a call ARGUMENT (`SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType`): a conforming concrete
		// value gains its witness half on the way out and leaves as a fat pointer, so `TypeClass.interfaceValue`
		// deliberately agrees with nothing and BOTH checks below would refuse every legal return — the tag
		// check on the class, the aggregate-identity check on two nominal names that are never the same name.
		// It returns rather than falling through for that second reason, exactly as `checkOneArgType` does.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE VERDICT IS NOT TAKEN HERE, AND CANNOT BE.** Whether the returned type CONFORMS is
		// `ConformanceCheck.valueWidensToInterface`, which takes the whole `Project` — and the parser holds
		// none, because a conformer's `implements` clause is recorded when its OWN file is parsed and the two
		// files have no ordering. `SemanticCheck.checkOneReturnedValue` asks it whole-program through the
		// SAME door the argument position asks, so the two widening positions cannot come to disagree about
		// what conforms — nor about a `float`, which conforms and still has no way into the fat pointer.
		//
		// The P1.9 ranged-return site below is not skipped by returning early: it is gated on
		// `rangeCheckReturnAlias`, which is the declared return type's RANGED-ALIAS name, and an interface is
		// not one — so it is empty on every path that reaches here.
		if declaredTag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialReturn'
			try self.emitOwnedValueReturn(retVal, anchor: anchor)
			return
		end 'existentialReturn'

		// The tag check runs FIRST: a genuine CLASS mismatch (a bool, or a struct, where an int is
		// declared) keeps its tag-based message, and the aggregate-name check below only NEWLY catches
		// the case the tag check WRONGLY AGREES on — a boxed union returned where a scalar is declared,
		// where `checkDeclaredType(named, named)` agrees because `ValueTypeTag.named` is overloaded.
		match checkDeclaredType(retTag, declared: declaredTag) 'declared'
			ok then break 'declared'
			mismatch then throw ParseError.returnTypeMismatch(typeTagName(retTag), expected: typeTagName(declaredTag), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			narrowsFloatToInt then throw ParseError.implicitNarrowing(typeTagName(retTag), targetType: typeTagName(declaredTag), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			// A FUNCTION VALUE returned where a NON-function type is declared (P1.5-A1) — the spec's
			// specific E3005 ("Cannot return 'function' from function declared to return '{declared}': …").
			// A function returned from a function-typed return (`return double` from `returns UnaryOp`)
			// agrees and never reaches this.
			functionIntoNonFunction then throw ParseError.functionValueReturned(typeTagName(declaredTag), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'declared'

		// ⭐ OPEN #54/#59: a `return` of the WRONG named aggregate — a DIFFERENT struct/union, OR an
		// aggregate where a SCALAR is declared — is handed back and dropped under the DECLARED return
		// type's destructor: a wild free / leak the tag check above cannot see (two structs share the
		// `structRef` tag; a boxed union and a ranged-int alias share `named`). Identity is the interned
		// name, EXACT (no subtyping). Un-gated — both names are computed unconditionally, `aggregateNameOf`
		// returning the shared empty for a scalar with no allocation — and `displayTypeNameOf` prints the
		// scalar side's primitive (`int`), not an empty name.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TWO NAMES DECIDE THE VERDICT AND DO NOT SPELL IT** — `parseAssignment`'s reassign check and
		// `requireManagedValueMatches` carry the identical note for the identical reason (user ruling,
		// 2026-08-04). MEASURED before the RETURN voice was converted, on a program declaring both aliases:
		// `Cannot return 'Array_String' from function declared to return 'Array_Integer'`, where the author
		// wrote `Strs` and `Ints` and no line of the program holds either mint.
		// The FUNCTION twin of the aggregate identity check below — a function value returned where a
		// function type is declared agrees on the tag and can still be a different function type entirely.
		// See `recordFunctionTypeDoor` for why it is recorded rather than decided here.
		if isFunctionTypeDoor(declaredTag, got: retTag) 'functionTypeDoor'
			self.recordFunctionTypeDoor(declaredNameId, gotNameId: self.valueNameId(retVal), door: FunctionTypeDoor.returned, placeName: EmptySourceText, anchor: anchor)
		end 'functionTypeDoor'

		let declaredReturnName = self.aggregateNameOf(declaredTag, nameId: declaredNameId)
		let returnedName = self.aggregateNameOf(retTag, nameId: self.valueNameId(retVal))
		if self.signatures.aggregatesConflict(declaredReturnName, expectedTag: declaredTag, expectedNameId: declaredNameId, got: returnedName, gotTag: retTag, gotNameId: self.valueNameId(retVal)) 'aggregateReturnMismatch'
			throw ParseError.returnTypeMismatch(self.valueDisplayTypeName(retVal), expected: self.displayTypeNameOf(declaredTag, nameId: declaredNameId), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'aggregateReturnMismatch'

		// ⭐ **THE `ok` VERDICT ABOVE LICENSED A WIDENING, AND SOMETHING HAS TO PERFORM IT.** `return 3`
		// from a `returns float` function agreed (both numeric, nothing lost) and then handed the raw i64
		// straight to `emitOwnedValueReturn` — which put a GPR where the f64 return register is, and the
		// x64 emitter panicked on the cross-register-file move it was asked for. The same door every other
		// coercion site uses; `retVal` is already a float when the source wrote one, so this is a no-op for
		// every program that compiled before.
		let returned = self.coerceToDeclared(retVal, declared: declaredTag, anchor: anchor)

		try self.emitOwnedValueReturn(returned, anchor: anchor)

		// P1.9: a `return` in a ranged-int-alias-returning function is a narrowing site. `returned` is the
		// scalar the `ret` terminator carries (a ranged int return is never an owned value, so
		// `emitOwnedValueReturn` passes it through unwrapped), so recording it here matches the id the
		// Std pass finds on the `ret` op. Anchored on the `return` keyword (the E3005 position). A ranged
		// INT alias never widens, so this is the value the coercion returned untouched.
		//
		// ⚠ **RECORDED AFTER THE `ret` IS EMITTED, BECAUSE THE BLOCK IS HALF THE SITE** (see
		// `RangeCheckReturnSite.block`): the guard belongs in front of THIS site's own `ret`, and the
		// block that terminator went into is `currentBlock` as `emitOwnedValueReturn` left it. The
		// hand-off's scope drops run first and may branch, so the block the `return` keyword was parsed
		// in is not reliably the one holding the `ret`.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND A `return` WHOSE VALUE THE DECLARED TYPE PROVABLY ADMITS IS NOT A SITE (A4f) — THE
		// DOOR THIS DEFECT WAS FOUND AT.** `return b` from a `returns ExitCode` function, `b` a
		// `Byte = int(0 to u8.max)`, emitted a `0 ≤ x ≤ 4294967295` cascade with an `mrt_panic` arm on a
		// value that cannot fail it — while the SAME compile reported `b as ExitCode` as E3010 *"'Byte'
		// already fits in 'ExitCode'"*, which is that cascade's own impossibility, proved. The alias-named
		// doors ask this inside `recordRangeCheckSite`; a `return` asks it through `returnGuardIsDead`
		// because its destination is the FUNCTION's declared type rather than one written at the site.
		if not self.rangeCheckReturnAlias.isEmpty() and not self.returnGuardIsDead(returned, declaredNameId: declaredNameId) 'rangedReturn'
			self.rangeCheckReturnSites.push(RangeCheckReturnSite.create(returned, block: self.currentBlock.id, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column))
		end 'rangedReturn'
	end 'parseCheckedValueReturn'

	// ⭐ **IS THE `return` DOOR'S GUARD PROVABLY DEAD? — the destination unwrapped, then the one shared
	// containment (A4f).** It is reached only behind `rangeCheckReturnAlias` being non-empty, which is the
	// tag-checked answer to *"does the declared return type name a ranged alias?"* (`rangedReturnAliasName`)
	// — so `undeclared` here is the SAME name failing to resolve twice and cannot mean the return type is
	// unranged. It answers `false` for it all the same, because a destination with no bounds proves nothing
	// and the safe direction is to keep the check.
	function returnGuardIsDead(returned ValueId, declaredNameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return match self.declaredAliasOfNameId(declaredNameId) 'returnAlias'
			undeclared gives false
			declared(alias) gives rangeCoversDenotedRange(alias, source: self.valueDenotedAlias(returned))
		end 'returnAlias'
	end 'returnGuardIsDead'

	// ⭐ **EMIT A VALUE `ret` THAT HANDS ITS VALUE TO THE CALLER AS AN OWNED ONE** — the shared tail of a
	// value `return` and a non-capturing closure body's `gives <expr>` (P1.5-A2a). A closure body IS a
	// value return (its expression is the lifted function's returned value), so both go through here rather
	// than each spelling the promotion/reject/drop dance. `anchor` is the token a diagnostic and the span
	// point at (`return`'s keyword, or the closure's `gives`).
	//
	// **A `returns String` FUNCTION HANDS BACK A UNIFORMLY OWNED HEAP String**, and the caller drops that
	// result unconditionally (`enrolOwnedCallTemp`). So the callee must return something OWNED and must not
	// drop it — two adjustments, in this order, BEFORE the scope drops:
	//
	//   • PROMOTE a borrowed return. `return "hi"` is an immortal rdata literal (capacity == -2), not owned
	//     heap; handed back as-is, the caller would `__mm_decref` read-only data. Copying its bytes into a
	//     fresh owned record — the same single-`__mm_alloc` fused-record path an interpolation builds, with
	//     the source as its one part — gives the caller a box it may safely drop. `return "{x}"` and
	//     `return t` are already owned, so they pass through here. This half is return-specific and stays
	//     here; the MOVE below is the shared hand-off cleanup.
	//   • MOVE the owned return OUT of both drop sets — the shared `emitHandoffExitDrops` (the throw twin
	//     performs the SAME move). It belongs to the caller now: out of `ownedBindings` so `emitScopeDrops`
	//     does not double-free `return t`, and out of `pendingTempDrops` so the interp/promoted temporary is
	//     not dropped on the dead path the `ret` leaves behind. The `ownedBindings` removal is SCOPED to
	//     this return's drops and UNDONE after them (the helper's `savedOwnedBindings`), because
	//     `ownedBindings` OUTLIVES the `return` and the returned binding may sit in an enclosing block with
	//     other LIVE exit edges whose drops are emitted LATER in the parse and still own it.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A RETURNED STRUCT / BOXED UNION MUST BE OWNED, AND A BORROWED ONE IS CO-OWNED RATHER THAN REFUSED
	// (S5).** An OWNED box carries the provenance bit and is moved out, so exactly one owner drops it. A
	// BORROWED aggregate — `return self`, a struct/union parameter, a re-borrow, a struct-typed field read —
	// has an owner ELSEWHERE, and handing the box back untouched would make two owners free one box. It is
	// therefore INCREF'd on the way out, through the one `promoteBorrowedToOwned` door a `var q = p` binding
	// already uses: the caller adopts a real second reference and decrefs it once, the borrow's own owner is
	// untouched, and the box is freed exactly once on every path.
	//
	// This used to be a clean REJECT whose sentence deferred the fix to "P1.4b" — a milestone that had long
	// since shipped, and a mechanism (`__mm_retain`, OPEN #40) that was already in the tree and already wired
	// to the binding door. The refusal cost `stdlib/FilePath.maxon` its two `return self` sites, which is the
	// whole of what stood between that module and compiling.
	//
	// A String is COPIED rather than increfed (value semantics) and a trivial TUPLE gets its own record, both
	// through the same door; a scalar or payload-free enum carries no box and is always fine.
	//
	// **THE DROPS GO AFTER THE VALUE, BEFORE THE TERMINATOR.** AFTER the value, because `return p.x + p.y`
	// READS the box; dropping first would free the memory the loads are about to read. BEFORE the
	// terminator, because a `ret` ENDS the block. `returned` is already in a register by then.
	function emitOwnedValueReturn(retVal ValueId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		// ⭐⭐ **W2′ — THE SIZED CONTAINER'S RECORD MAY NOT BE RETURNED (W192 review).** Asked on `retVal`
		// BEFORE the promotion below, which mints a fresh id and would carry the mark onto it
		// (`carryDeclaredSurface`): the value the SOURCE named is the one the sentence is about.
		//
		// ⚠ **UNSCOPED, AND THE MISSING "…from a member of the sized container" CONJUNCT IS REDUNDANT RATHER
		// THAN FORGOTTEN.** The mark's only producer is `bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord`, reached only by an
		// `emitFieldLoad` of the sized container's own inline `managed` — visible nowhere but inside that
		// declaration (`v.managed` from outside is E3014) — and the marks reset per function, so a marked
		// value at a `return` proves the enclosing function is one of the declaration's own. Its ARGUMENT twin
		// IS scoped, and that asymmetry is real: a foreign callee is deliberately excluded there.
		try self.refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping(retVal, escape: SizedContainerRecordEscape.returned, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)

		var returned = retVal

		// The ONE promotion door (see the header): a borrowed String COPIES, a borrowed TRIVIAL TUPLE gets its
		// own record, a borrowed struct/union/instance INCREFS. An already-owned value and a scalar pass
		// straight through. The tuple half used to be asked HERE, one branch ahead of the shared door, which
		// made it the `returned` door's private rule — and the `merged` door, opening onto the same shared door
		// in S5, increfed a borrowed tuple instead and then handed the return an alias wearing the owned bit.
		if self.valueIsManagedHeap(returned) and not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(returned) 'promoteBorrowed'
			returned = try self.promoteBorrowedToOwned(returned, door: BorrowLaunderDoor.returned, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'promoteBorrowed'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME PROMOTION FOR THE ONE TYPE THE BODY CANNOT NAME (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-a).** A shared
		// generic body's `returns T` obeys the convention above unchanged — hand the caller a `+1` — and the
		// door directly above declines for it, because `valueIsManagedHeap` reads the TAG and `typeParameter` is
		// neither a byte record nor an aggregate. So a BORROWED opaque `T` takes its reference the only way a
		// body compiled once for every instantiation can: through the enclosing instance's descriptor, whose
		// `retainFunc@64` is `__str_clone` for a byte record, `__mm_retain` for a managed aggregate and 0 — a
		// no-op — for an instantiation that owns nothing. An ALREADY-OWNED opaque value (an element `pop` moved
		// out of an `Array with T` field) passes straight through and is moved out of the drop sets below,
		// exactly as an owned String does.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **THIS WAS A CLEAN `E2015` UNTIL THIS SLICE, AND THE REFUSAL'S OWN REASONING NAMED THE WRONG
		// PARTY.** It said the caller "neither adopts nor drops" an opaque return, which was true — the caller
		// took its own reference instead, on the premise that a shared body hands one back UNREFERENCED. That
		// premise is read off the DECLARED type and the obligation belongs to the RETURNED VALUE, so a body
		// returning a value it already owned handed over a real `+1` that the caller's own `+1` then leaked:
		// `return fresh()` through a `returns Element` printed the right answer and exited 101, with no `pop`,
		// no array and no function alias anywhere in it. There is no convention under which the CALLER can take
		// the reference — a body that returns a value it CREATED has nowhere to put the one it holds, and
		// releasing it before the `ret` frees the record the caller is about to retain — so the callee
		// discharges the hand-off for every shape and the caller simply adopts (`enrolOwnedCallTemp`). It is
		// also what v1 does: `planReturnedBorrowRetains` / `planReturnIncrefs` retain a returned borrow in the
		// CALLEE, through `mm_retain_type_param(value, layout)`.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS UNGATED, AND `moveOpaqueValueInto`'s SIBLING STORE IS NOT — the two doors are reserved by
		// different signals and each is written to match its own.** A store's reservation
		// (`buildsRecordFromOpaqueSource`) is a body-shape guess and declines for a type no `with` ever names,
		// so the store asks `typeParamOwnerIsInstantiated` and declines with it. A RETURN's reservation is read
		// off the DECLARATION (`calleeReturnsBareTypeParameter`) and declines for nothing, because an
		// instantiation the pre-scan cannot see is one the body's own parse can — see `scanDescriptorNeedsInto`
		// for the 38 `map/*` cases that measured exactly that disagreement.
		if self.tagOf(returned) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter and not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(returned) 'borrowedOpaqueReturn'
			returned = self.coOwnBorrowedOpaque(returned, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'borrowedOpaqueReturn'

		self.emitHandoffExitDrops(returned, anchor: anchor)

		self.artifact.emitTerminator(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.ret(returned), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'emitOwnedValueReturn'

	// ============================================================================
	// P1.4b — scalar error handling: throw / try / otherwise / panic
	// ============================================================================

	// `throw E.case` or `throw <e>` — leave the enclosing ERROR SCOPE, which is the FUNCTION unless a
	// block-form `try` body encloses this statement, in which case it is that block's shared handler (see
	// `emitThrowError`, which owns the fork, and `routeBareThrowToTryBlock`). The thrown expression evaluates
	// to a `named(E)` enum value carrying the case's tag (a literal for `throw E.case`, the caught `(e)`
	// binding for `throw e`); the flag encode is the same at both destinations. Owned locals are dropped
	// before control leaves — the whole frame's on the function edge, the block's own on the routed one.
	function parseThrowStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block
		let throwTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.throw)
		try self.emitThrowError(func, anchor: throwTok)
	end 'parseThrowStatement'

	// Parse the thrown enum expression and send the error on its way — the `throwError` terminator out of the
	// function, or a branch to the enclosing block-form `try`'s shared handler. Shared by the `throw <e>`
	// statement and the match `default throws <E.case>` arm (which differ only in the keyword their caller
	// consumed — `throw` vs `throws`), so both spellings route identically inside a `try` block.
	function emitThrowError(func IrFunction, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **WHERE THIS THROW GOES IS DECIDED FIRST, BECAUSE IT DECIDES WHAT THE THROW OWES.** A ROUTED throw
		// does not leave the function — it branches to the enclosing block-form `try`'s shared handler — so it
		// is measured against no enclosing `throws` clause (there may not be one: `main` may hold a `try` block
		// whose body throws) and it releases only what the BLOCK owns, not the whole frame. Read off exactly
		// the stack the old refusal read, so the set of programs that route is the set that used to be refused.
		let routed = self.tryBlocks.count() > 0
		let errValue = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		let errorType = self.typeOf(errValue)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE THROWN VALUE'S TYPE IS WHAT MAKES THE NEXT LINE AGREE WITH THE CATCH SITE (A1s-throwsbox
		// review).** `errorIsBoxed` below is derived from the VALUE; the catch derives the same fact from the
		// enclosing function's DECLARED CLAUSE (`tryErrorEnumType` → the same predicate over the clause name).
		// Two derivations of ONE fact, and until this check nothing made them agree — the same shape, and the
		// same door, the propagate form has guarded with E3059 since P1.4b.
		//
		try self.requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing(func, errorType: errorType, routed: routed, anchor: anchor)

		let errorIsBoxed = self.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion(errorType)

		// ⭐ **A THROWN BOX MUST BE OWNED BY THIS FRAME — a box owned elsewhere is MOVED OUT of an OWNED-LOCAL
		// container, RETAINED out of a BORROWED one, or refused.** A boxed error is handed to the caller through
		// the flag register, which ADOPTS and drops it once. An OWNED box (a fresh `throw U.case(x)` or a caught
		// `(e)` binding) carries the provenance bit and is moved out below. A box that is NOT owned here splits
		// three ways:
		//   • a boxed-union FIELD read whose CONTAINER this frame is the SOLE owner of — the throw TRANSFERS
		//     it out by MOVE: `moveOutThrownField` nulls the field slot (so the container's `__destruct_<T>`
		//     cascade skips it) and marks the value owned, so the same owned hand-off below transfers it. Sound
		//     because a solely-owned container dies with this frame — no surviving owner can read the nulled
		//     slot, and the null is cheaper than an incref that the container's own drop would only cancel (#64).
		//     ⚠ **THE GATE IS `valueIsSoleHeapOwner` AND IT USED TO BE `valueIsOwnedHeap`, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT
		//     QUESTION AND A MEASURED SEGFAULT.** A CO-OWNED container — `let c = g.inner`, whose read out of a
		//     mutable field is promoted by `__mm_retain` (W41) — carries the owned bit while `g.inner` still
		//     points at the same box, so the null store stole the field from a live owner and `g.inner.e` read a
		//     hole. Its match-side twin is `declarePayloadBindings`; see `OwnedHeapExclusivity`.
		//   • a boxed-union FIELD of a container this frame does not solely own (a `self`-field in an instance
		//     method, a borrowed struct/union PARAM's field, a co-owned local) — the throw TRANSFERS it out by
		//     RETAIN (#64): `retainThrownField` increfs the box so the thrown reference is a SECOND owner, and
		//     the slot is LEFT INTACT so a caller that catches and re-reads the field still sees a live box. The
		//     caller's container keeps its own reference; the catch consumes the thrown one; the refcount
		//     balances at one free. This applies the #40 retain-promotion thesis to the throw error channel,
		//     sidestepping the cross-call move-tracking a null-move would have needed to tell a re-reading caller
		//     from a tearing-down one.
		//   • anything else NOT owned here — a `throw <union param>` (or a re-borrow of one), a WHOLE borrowed
		//     union value with no owned home in this frame. It takes the SAME RETAIN, for the same reason: the
		//     thrown reference is a second owner, the catch consumes it, the borrow's own owner drops its own,
		//     and the refcount balances at one free.
		//     ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO BE REFUSED "until cross-call consume", AND THAT PREMISE WAS ALREADY RETIRED IN
		//     THIS FILE.** See `valueIsNonTextAggregate`'s header (S5): the two HAND-OFF doors — `return
		//     <borrowed aggregate>` and a borrowed arm merged into an owned phi — refused on exactly that
		//     reasoning, and it is wrong. *"The callee increfs before the `ret`, the caller adopts and decrefs
		//     once, and the borrow's own owner never notices … What genuinely needs the cross-call consume is a
		//     MOVE, where the source must be poisoned so exactly one owner remains."* A `throw` is a HAND-OFF,
		//     not a move: nothing is poisoned, so nothing needs the consume. The refusal outlived its reason,
		//     and the retain it now takes is the two lines the field case beside it was already spending.
		if errorIsBoxed and not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(errValue) 'borrowedThrow'
			let thrownIsFieldRead = self.lastFieldReadValid and self.lastFieldReadValue == errValue
			if thrownIsFieldRead and self.valueIsSoleHeapOwner(self.lastFieldReadBox) 'fieldThrow'
				self.moveOutThrownField(errValue, anchor: anchor)
			end 'fieldThrow' else 'coOwnTheThrownBox'
				self.retainThrownValue(errValue, anchor: anchor)
			end 'coOwnTheThrownBox'
		end 'borrowedThrow'

		// ⭐ **THROW TRANSFERS THE BOX OUT OF THE LOCAL DROP SETS — BEFORE the scope drops.** The box is
		// handed to the caller, so the scope cleanup releases every OTHER owned local on the throw edge but
		// not the box in flight — else it is freed here AND adopted by the caller (a double free, OPEN #16
		// family). This is the SAME hand-off cleanup a `return <value>` performs (`emitHandoffExitDrops`):
		// the thrown box leaves the local drop sets, the throw expression's leftover temporaries and every
		// other owned binding drop, and the owned list is restored for a sibling exit edge that still owns
		// it. A SCALAR error owns no heap, so its move-out is inert — the Wave-1 behaviour, unchanged.
		if routed 'toTheBlockHandler'
			self.routeBareThrowToTryBlock(errValue, errorType: errorType, errorIsBoxed: errorIsBoxed, anchor: anchor)
			return
		end 'toTheBlockHandler'

		self.emitHandoffExitDrops(errValue, anchor: anchor)

		self.artifact.emitTerminator(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.throwError(errValue, errorIsBoxed: errorIsBoxed), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'emitThrowError'

	// ⭐⭐ **ROUTE A BARE `throw` TO THE ENCLOSING BLOCK-FORM `try`'s SHARED HANDLER — the THROW twin of
	// `routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock`, and the whole of what that construct's body means by "a throw".**
	// Both references route it and both record that NOT routing it is a correctness bug (`routeBareThrowToTryBlock`
	// at `2-Parser.cs:25146`, `routeBareThrowToActiveTryBlock` at `maxon-selfhosted/Parser.maxon:13342`), and
	// shv2 MEASURED the same wrong answer before it was refused: a body holding one routed call and one
	// `throw AErr.beta` answered 3 where the bootstrap answered 6 — the throw neither reached the handler nor
	// left the function.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ONE THING THAT HAD TO BE BUILT FOR IT IS A VALUE FORM OF THE ERROR-FLAG ENCODE**, which is now
	// `MaxonOp.errorFlagEncode` — lowered by the SAME `encodeScalarErrorFlag` the `throwError` terminator is,
	// so the flag a routed throw hands the handler is bit-identical to the one a returned throw hands a caller.
	// The refusal this replaces was right that spelling the encode a second time in the parser was the wrong
	// answer; it was the op, not the parser, that was missing.
	//
	// ⭐ **THREE THINGS ARE SHARED VERBATIM WITH THE CALL ROUTER, and each is a guard that construct paid for**:
	// the error edge's args are the flag FIRST and every carried binding's value RIGHT HERE (positional against
	// the phis, `edgeArgsFor(preLoop: false)`); the edge goes through `tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget`, so this site's
	// own live temporaries and the body's owned bindings are released in a landing pad rather than leaked over
	// the body's `end`; and `recordRoutedEdge` settles the handler's error TYPE and the ownership state the
	// handler is entered with. Nothing here is a second opinion about any of them.
	//
	// ⚠ **BUT THE BOX IN FLIGHT LEAVES THE DROP SETS FIRST, WHICH IS THE CALL ROUTER'S OWN HAZARD WEARING THE
	// THROW'S CLOTHES.** A `throw U.case(x)` builds an owned box and hands it to the handler, which adopts it
	// (`enrollCaughtBox`); left in `pendingTempDrops`/`ownedBindings` the landing pad would release it on the
	// very edge that transfers it — a double free. `emitHandoffExitDrops` performs exactly this move-out for
	// the function-exit form, and its save/restore of `ownedBindings` is kept for its reason: a SIBLING edge
	// (another arm, the path that did not throw) still owns whatever this one gave away.
	function routeBareThrowToTryBlock(errValue ValueId, errorType MaxonType, errorIsBoxed bool, anchor Token)
		var ctx = try self.tryBlocks.get(self.tryBlocks.count() - 1) otherwise panic("routeBareThrowToTryBlock: tryBlocks.get failed on a stack the caller has already tested is non-empty")
		let member = self.recordRoutedEdge(ctx, clause: self.thrownTypeClause(errorType))

		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.errorFlagEncode(flag, errorValue: errValue, errorIsBoxed: errorIsBoxed), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		let fused = self.routedEdgeFusedValue(ctx, member: member, flag: flag)

		let errArgs = self.tryBlockErrorEdgeArgs(ctx, flag: flag)
		let savedOwnedBindings = self.ownedBindings
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(errValue) 'boxInFlight'
			self.removeFromOwnedBindings(errValue)
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(errValue)
		end 'boxInFlight'

		let errTargetId = self.tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget(ctx, errArgs: errArgs, flag: flag, member: member, fusedValue: fused, anchor: anchor)
		self.ownedBindings = savedOwnedBindings

		self.emitBranch(self.currentBlock, targetId: errTargetId)
	end 'routeBareThrowToTryBlock'

	// ⭐ **THE `ThrowsClause` A THROWN VALUE'S TYPE NAMES — the THROW door's projection, where a CALL's is
	// `signatures.throwsOf(callee)`.** Both routed edges (a bare throwing call, a bare `throw`) hand
	// `recordRoutedEdge` one of these, so the block's error type is settled from ONE kind of answer whichever
	// door produced it. Its two readers — this and `requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing` — are why it is a function
	// rather than two spellings of `nameOf(typeNameIdOf(...))`.
	//
	// It PANICS on a non-`named` type rather than answering `none`: every caller has already run
	// `requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing`'s first test, which is the one place "that is not an error type at all"
	// is reported, and a `none` here would be that refusal silently re-decided as "this block routed nothing".
	function thrownTypeClause(errorType MaxonType) returns ThrowsClause
		if maxonTypeTag(errorType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notAnErrorType'
			panic("thrownTypeClause: a thrown value's type is not `named` — `requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing` reports that as E3062 before either caller reaches here")
		end 'notAnErrorType'

		let name = try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(errorType)) otherwise panic("thrownTypeClause: a `named` MaxonType's TypeNameId did not resolve in the artifact's interner — every such id was minted by `intern` against this same interner")
		return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(name))
	end 'thrownTypeClause'

	// ⭐ **MOVE A THROWN BOXED-UNION FIELD OUT OF ITS OWNED-LOCAL CONTAINER.** The field READ borrowed the
	// box (its container owns it); throwing it transfers ownership to the caller. The transfer is a MOVE:
	// NULL the slot the value was loaded from (recorded by `emitFieldLoad` in the
	// `lastFieldRead*` fields), so the container's `__destruct_<T>` cascade's null-guard SKIPS it — else the
	// container would free the box the caller has just adopted (a double free). Marking the value OWNED then
	// routes it through the SAME hand-off + `throwError` transfer a fresh `throw U.case(x)` takes. This is the
	// throw twin of match's `moveOutManagedPayload`, one field-load out. **The caller GATES this on a container
	// this frame SOLELY owns** (`valueIsSoleHeapOwner(lastFieldReadBox)`): such a container dies with this frame,
	// so its own scope-exit drop honours the null and no surviving owner can read the nulled slot — and the null
	// is cheaper than an incref its own drop would only cancel. Anything else — a BORROWED container (a
	// `self`-field, a borrowed param's field) or a CO-OWNED one (a `let c = g.inner` the field read's `__mm_retain`
	// promoted, whose other owner is still `g.inner`) — RETAINS instead (`retainThrownField`, #64): that owner may
	// re-read the field, so the slot is left intact and the box increfed rather than nulled.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE THROWN VALUE IS CO-OWNED, AND THE `sole` THAT STOOD HERE WAS F1'S INFERENCE ONE DOOR OVER.** It
	// read *"sole because the gate has just established that the container was"* — which derives a claim about
	// the box in the SLOT from a claim about the box HOLDING the slot, and that step is exactly the one
	// `moveOutManagedPayload` measured two segfaults for. A sole container may hold a co-owned field:
	// `Self{pending: <a borrowed union>}` co-owns it in (`moveManagedValueInto`'s borrowed arm), so nulling the
	// container's slot transfers THAT slot's reference and says nothing about the allocation.
	//
	// ⚠ **UNREACHABLE TODAY, AND FIXED ANYWAY FOR `markValueCoOwnsHeap`'s STATED REASON.** `valueIsSoleHeapOwner`
	// has exactly two readers — a match scrutinee and a field-read base — and control leaves this frame on the
	// terminator two lines down, so nothing here can read the claim. It costs nothing to be honest and it stops
	// the sentence being an invitation to the edit that makes it matter. What the caller needs is unaffected:
	// adopt-and-drop-once is `valueIsOwnedHeap`'s business, and the catch enrols its own box co-owned regardless
	// (`enrollCaughtBox`).
	//
	// The null store is an i64 pointer write — a boxed-union field slot holds a machine-word box address
	// (`fieldStorageType` gives a boxed-union field `integer`), the same width `moveOutManagedPayload` nulls
	// a match-scrutinee slot at.
	function moveOutThrownField(value ValueId, anchor Token)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(anchor)
		let zero = self.emitLiteral(0 as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.emitIndirectStore(self.lastFieldReadBox, offset: self.lastFieldReadOffset, value: zero, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(value, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
	end 'moveOutThrownField'

	// ⭐ **RETAIN A THROWN BOXED-UNION FIELD OF A BORROWED CONTAINER (#64).** The field READ borrowed the box
	// (the container — a borrowed `self`, or a borrowed struct/union PARAM — owns it, and that owner is the
	// CALLER's local). Throwing it transfers an owned reference to the caller, but unlike `moveOutThrownField`
	// the slot must NOT be nulled: the caller still owns the container and may re-read the field after
	// catching. So instead of a MOVE this RETAINS — `__mm_incref` raises the box's refcount so the thrown
	// reference is a SECOND owner while the field keeps pointing at a live box. The caller's container keeps
	// its own reference (dropped when the container dies), the thrown box is consumed by the catch (`otherwise
	// (e)` skips its incref, no-binding `otherwise` decrefs once), and the refcount balances at exactly one
	// free. Marking the value OWNED then routes it through the SAME hand-off + `throwError` transfer the
	// move-out and a fresh `throw U.case(x)` take, so the box the retain produces is indistinguishable
	// downstream from any other owned thrown box. The RETAIN twin of `moveOutThrownField`, applying the #40
	// retain-promotion thesis (`retainBorrowedAggregate`) to the throw error channel — a bounded refcount
	// exception that sidesteps the cross-call move-tracking a null-move would have needed to tell a re-reading
	// caller from a tearing-down one.
	//
	// The thrown value is CO-OWNED, which is the whole content of the paragraph above said once in the type
	// system: the container keeps its reference, so a callee that catches this box and matches it must retain its
	// payload rather than null the slot the container still reads (`OwnedHeapExclusivity`).
	// ⭐ **CO-OWN THE THROWN BOX.** Two lines, and neither is about a field — the name said `Field` while it
	// served every borrowed box, which is why the non-field case looked like it needed a mechanism it did not.
	function retainThrownValue(value ValueId, anchor Token)
		self.emitIncref(value, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(value, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
	end 'retainThrownValue'

	// ⭐ **`panic("message")` — ABORT, PRINTING `panic at <file>:<line>: <message>` AND A BACKTRACE, EXIT 1.**
	// Emits a `panicAbort`/`panicAbortValue` body op carrying the message, followed by a dead return
	// terminator (control never returns, so the return is unreachable but keeps the block well-formed).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE MESSAGE IS ASSEMBLED HERE BECAUSE TWO OF ITS THREE PARTS ONLY EXIST HERE** — the source
	// BASENAME and the LINE of the `panic` keyword. The format itself is `panicMessageLine`'s (and, for an
	// interpolation, its two halves), shared with the P1.9 range-check guard so the runtime prints one shape
	// whichever producer assembled it. This is v1's arrangement
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.parsePanicStatement` interns a location-prefixed message at parse
	// time) and it is kept for v1's reason, which shv2 does not improve on — except where it cannot apply,
	// a message whose text is not a parse-time constant (`PanicMessage`).
	//
	// ⚠ **`panic` IS A TERMINATING STATEMENT THAT CARRIES AN EXPRESSION, WHICH IS WHY IT DRAINS ITS OWN
	// TEMPORARIES.** `parseStatement`'s post-statement drain is skipped once the block is terminated, on the
	// stated argument that a terminating statement drops its temporaries BEFORE its terminator (as
	// `parseReturnStatement` does) and that `break`/`continue` carry no expression to build one. `panic` was
	// the case that argument did not cover: an interpolated message builds an owned String, and left in
	// `pendingTempDrops` it was released by the NEXT statement's drain — which, for a `panic` inside an
	// `if`, is a block the interpolation's definitions do not dominate. That is the exact shape
	// `drainPendingTemps`' region list warns about: on x64 the value happened to be spilled and the release
	// read an UNINITIALIZED SLOT on the path the `if` did not take (a silent wrong free), and on arm64 it
	// stayed in a register and the register allocator reported it, several passes later, as
	// `seedInUse: … a use dominates its def`.
	function parsePanicStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block
		let panicTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.panic)
		let messageTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let message = try self.parsePanicMessage(func, panicTok: panicTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		if self.pendingTempDrops.count() > messageTempFloor 'dropMessageTemps'
			self.drainPendingTemps(messageTempFloor, anchor: panicTok)
		end 'dropMessageTemps'

		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(panicTok)
		match message 'form'
			baked(line) then self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.panicAbort(line), range: range)
			rendered(linePtr) then self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.panicAbortValue(linePtr), range: range)
		end 'form'

		self.emitDeadReturn(func, anchor: panicTok)
	end 'parsePanicStatement'

	// The `panic at <file>:<line>: <text>\n` line a `panic(…)` hands the runtime, having consumed its
	// argument — BAKED for a plain literal, RENDERED for an interpolation (see `PanicMessage`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENT MUST BE A STRING *LITERAL* (interpolated or not), NOT MERELY A `String`** — the
	// ORACLE's rule, measured: `panic(m)` on a `String` binding is `E3005 panic requires a string argument`
	// from the bootstrap, and shv2 used to accept it (and then exit with no message at all, because there
	// was nothing to bake). The requirement is not an implementation limit dressed as a rule: it is what
	// makes the message's SHAPE a compile-time fact, so a plain one can live in `.rdata` and print from a
	// process whose heap may be the thing that failed. An interpolation necessarily allocates to render its
	// holes — but only on the path that is already aborting.
	function parsePanicMessage(func IrFunction, panicTok Token) returns PanicMessage throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'notALiteral'
			let argTok = self.current()
			let arg = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			throw ParseError.builtinOperandType("panic", required: StringLiteralRequirement, got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(arg), line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
		end 'notALiteral'

		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.stringInterpStart 'interpolated'
			return PanicMessage.rendered(try self.renderedPanicMessagePointer(func, panicTok: panicTok))
		end 'interpolated'

		let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.stringLiteral)
		let text = String.from(try self.decodedStringLiteralBytes(tok))
		return PanicMessage.baked(panicMessageLine(self.filePath, line: panicTok.line, text: text))
	end 'parsePanicMessage'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN INTERPOLATED PANIC ASSEMBLES ITS WHOLE LINE AT RUN TIME — LOCATION PREFIX AND TRAILING
	// NEWLINE INCLUDED — AND HANDS THE RUNTIME THE ADDRESS OF THE BYTES.** `panic("value is {x}")` cannot
	// bake a line: `{x}` is not known until the program runs. So the two constant halves become extra
	// literal FRAGMENTS of an ordinary interpolation (`panicMessageLinePrefix`, `PanicMessageLineTerminator`
	// — the same two `panicMessageLine` bakes, so the format is spelled once for both producers) and the
	// existing interpolation machinery assembles the rest.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT MAKES THIS COST NO NEW RUNTIME: A FUSED INTERPOLATION BUFFER IS ALREADY NUL-TERMINATED.**
	// `emitStringInterp` allocates the record + the bytes + one more, and the slab zeroes what it hands
	// back, so `buffer[totalLen]` IS the NUL `mrt_panic` scans for. What reaches the runtime is therefore a
	// C string of exactly the shape the interned `.rdata` blob is — one entry point, one contract, two ways
	// of producing the bytes.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RECORD LEAVES THE PENDING LIST WITHOUT A DROP, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** Its bytes are what
	// the runtime reads, so a `__mm_decref` before the call would free the message being printed; and there
	// is no "after the call", because `mrt_panic` exits the process. This is `emitHandoffExitDrops`' shape
	// — a value handed away stops being the statement's to release — with the hand-off going to a runtime
	// rather than to a caller. Every OTHER temporary the message expression built (a `toString()` result an
	// interpolation spliced in) stays enrolled and is drained by `parsePanicStatement`, in the block its
	// definition dominates.
	function renderedPanicMessagePointer(func IrFunction, panicTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let record = try self.parseStringInterpolation(func, prefix: panicMessageLinePrefix(self.filePath, line: panicTok.line), suffix: PanicMessageLineTerminator)
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(record)
		return self.emitLoadStringField(record, offset: StringBufferOffset, range: SourceRange.ofToken(panicTok))
	end 'renderedPanicMessagePointer'

	// The dead return terminator emitted after a diverging op (`panicAbort`): a `retVoid` for a void
	// function, else a zero. Unreachable — the preceding op never returns — but every block must commit
	// to a terminator.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **ITS BITS ARE DEAD; ITS REGISTER FILE IS NOT.** The comment here used to say the type "need not
	// match the declared return … lowering moves it to the return register like any other `ret`", and the
	// second half is exactly why the first is false: `ret` moves the value into the return register, which
	// for a float-returning function is XMM0/d0, so an INTEGER zero made the x64 emitter panic —
	// *"a register-to-register move from rax to xmm0 crosses register files"*. MEASURED on a function whose
	// whole body is `if x < 0.0 … panic("neg") … end / return x`, with no `try` in sight. The value is dead
	// either way; the CLASS the allocator colours it in is not, and no diagnostic exists for it because the
	// program is perfectly legal.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS IS THE THROW EDGE'S BUG, ONE DOOR OVER, AND ITS FIX IS THE SAME FIX.** `LowerMaxonToStd`'s
	// `emitZeroConstOfReturnType` already states the rule for `errorReturn`'s family-default value, quoting
	// that same panic — but a `panic()` statement never reaches it, because the parser commits this
	// terminator at the MAXON tier. Two producers of "the dead value a diverging path returns", and only one
	// of them knew.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FLOAT TEST GOES THROUGH `floatResolvedTag`, NOT THE BARE TAG.** A `returns ParsedFloat` — the
	// declared return of `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s `__float_fromString` — is a `named` ranged alias at parse
	// time (aliases erase in `resolveTypes`, which runs later), and a `named` that reduces to `float` occupies
	// XMM exactly as a bare `float` does. Reading the bare tag classes it GPR and reproduces the panic on the
	// one declaration this most matters for.
	function emitDeadReturn(func IrFunction, anchor Token)
		if maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(func.maxonReturnType) 'void'
			self.artifact.emitTerminator(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.retVoid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
			return
		end 'void'
		let zero = self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: self.deadReturnZeroType(func), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		self.artifact.emitTerminator(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.ret(zero), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'emitDeadReturn'

	// ⭐⭐ **A BARE-CALL STATEMENT TO A FUNCTION THAT NEVER RETURNS ENDS ITS BLOCK (G17)** — the same
	// `emitDeadReturn` a `panic` statement written in its place would reach, because it is the same fact:
	// control does not continue past this line. This is the ONLY consumer of `functionNeverReturns`, and it
	// is deliberately ONE consumer: the fact reaches every construct that asks `isTerminated()` — an
	// `otherwise` handler's fall-through gate (E3059), a function body's missing-return gate (E3013), an
	// `if`/`match` arm's merge — through the block's terminator rather than through a reader of its own.
	//
	// What ending the block then COSTS — the drain this statement owes, the absent scope drops, the fresh dead
	// block the tail is parsed into — is `endBlockAfterUnreachableTail`'s, shared with G18's `try` door and
	// stated there once. This statement is the FIFTH holder of the drain obligation `parseStatement`'s
	// terminated-block skip hands out; the list is at that skip.
	//
	// ⚠ **A short-circuit `and`/`or` in an ARGUMENT leaves control in a fresh merge block**, which is where the
	// call landed and so which one gets terminated — never the block this statement opened in.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DOOR IS THE BARE-NAME CALL STATEMENT, AND THE OTHER CALL SHAPES ARE OUT BY A BOUNDARY RATHER
	// THAN BY OVERSIGHT.** A METHOD call's key is `Type.method`, which needs the receiver's type and so is not
	// knowable from the statement's first token; a NAMESPACE-QUALIFIED call's key is assembled by
	// `parseNamespaceQualifiedCall` from the contest state. Each would be a second reader of the same fact
	// deriving the key a second way — and every uncertainty about the key must resolve to "it returns", which
	// is exactly what not asking gives. A shape left out here compiles as it did before; one asked with the
	// WRONG key could answer yes for a declaration that comes back.
	function terminateAfterDivergingCall(func IrFunction, calleeTok Token, tempFloor TempDropMark)
		// ⚡ A single `Set.contains` on a set that is EMPTY for every program declaring no `panic` helper,
		// asked once per bare-call statement.
		if not self.signatures.functionNeverReturns(calleeTok.value) 'theCalleeComesBack'
			return
		end 'theCalleeComesBack'

		self.endBlockAfterUnreachableTail(func, anchor: calleeTok, tempFloor: tempFloor)
	end 'terminateAfterDivergingCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A STATEMENT DOES ONCE CONTROL PROVABLY DOES NOT CONTINUE PAST IT — the three steps, written
	// once for the two doors that reach them** (G17's bare call to a `panic` helper, G18's propagating `try` on
	// an always-throwing callee). The two doors differ only in the QUESTION they ask the signature index; what
	// they then do to the block is the same, and a second copy of it is a second chance to omit the drain.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DRAIN IS NOT OPTIONAL, because `parseStatement`'s tail drain SKIPS a terminated block.** This
	// statement's own temporaries are released here or by nobody. The floor is the STATEMENT's, never the whole
	// list: an enclosing construct's temporary (a match scrutinee, a `for` element) sits below it, is not this
	// statement's to release, and taking it off the pending list would leak it on every OTHER path.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO SCOPE DROPS, EXACTLY AS `panic` EMITS NONE.** Control never reaches the rest of this frame, so its
	// owned bindings are never released and never can be; a drop emitted here would be code on a path that does
	// not exist.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT FOLLOWS GOES INTO A FRESH `dead` BLOCK, AND THAT IS THE PRECEDENT RATHER THAN AN INVENTION.**
	// `finalizeIfElse` does exactly this for the continuation of an `if` whose branches all terminate: the tail
	// is parsed for syntax and emits into a block nothing reaches. It is also what keeps a second terminator off
	// THIS block — `emitTerminator` panics on one, and a `return` written under such a statement is one unless
	// the parser has moved on. E3071 is not widened to refuse that tail: its roster is KEYWORDS at a statement's
	// first token (`statementEndsItsBlockAt`), neither a call nor a `try` is one, and a statement after a
	// fully-terminating `if`/`else` is accepted today for the identical reason.
	function endBlockAfterUnreachableTail(func IrFunction, anchor Token, tempFloor TempDropMark)
		// The block this statement's work landed in cannot ordinarily be terminated — its ops were just
		// appended — but a `dead` one can arrive here from an unreachable tail, and `Terminator.dead` is not an
		// op `emitTerminator` may replace.
		if self.currentBlock.isTerminated() 'nothingReachesThisStatementAnyway'
			return
		end 'nothingReachesThisStatementAnyway'

		if self.pendingTempDrops.count() > tempFloor 'dropStatementTemps'
			self.drainPendingTemps(tempFloor, anchor: anchor)
		end 'dropStatementTemps'

		self.emitDeadReturn(func, anchor: anchor)

		self.currentBlock = self.newDeadBlock(func, label: b"afterdiverge", line: anchor.line)
	end 'endBlockAfterUnreachableTail'

	// Does this `try` target name a callee that can only ever throw (G18)? Only a DIRECT call has a declaration
	// to ask about: a witness dispatch resolves to a conformer this call site does not know, and an awaited
	// promise names a storage type rather than a body — and every uncertainty here must resolve to "it comes
	// back", which is exactly what those two arms answering `false` gives.
	function tryTargetAlwaysThrows(target TryTarget) returns bool
		return match target 'shape'
			named(callee) gives self.signatures.functionAlwaysThrows(callee)
			witness gives false
			promise gives false
		end 'shape'
	end 'tryTargetAlwaysThrows'

	// The type a dead return's zero is born with — `float` when the declared return occupies XMM, `integer`
	// otherwise. Every non-float return type keeps the integer zero it always had, so no existing golden
	// moves: a String / struct / boxed-union return is a POINTER, which is a GPR value exactly as an `int`
	// is, and the bits are dead on every one of them.
	function deadReturnZeroType(func IrFunction) returns MaxonType
		let declared = func.maxonReturnType
		if tagIsFloat(self.floatResolvedTag(maxonReturnTypeTag(declared), nameId: maxonReturnTypeNameId(declared))) 'floatReturn'
			return MaxonType.float
		end 'floatReturn'

		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'deadReturnZeroType'

	// ⭐ **THE try/otherwise DESUGAR.** Parses `try <call> [otherwise <handler>]`, emitting a `tryCall`
	// (the call rewritten to define a second `errorFlag` value), a `compare(flag != 0)` + `condBranch`
	// two-way split, the error handler, and the merge. Returns the try's RESULT value (used in expression
	// position; discarded as a statement). `resultUsed` is true in value position — it decides the
	// void-callee (E3059) and `otherwise ignore` (E3059) rejects.
	function parseTry(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let tryTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.try)

		// ⭐ **A BLOCK-FORM `try` REACHING HERE IS IN AN EXPRESSION POSITION**, and that is the only way it
		// can: `parseStatement` forks the statement spelling to `parseTryBlockStatement` before this routine
		// is entered (both read the same `tryOpensBlockAt` label lookahead). The construct GROUPS statements
		// and yields nothing, so there is no value for the enclosing expression to take — the same rule that
		// makes an `if` a statement and a `<t> if <c> else <f>` the expression spelling.
		if self.charLiteralOpensTryBlockAt(self.pos) 'blockFormInValuePosition'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("a block-form `try 'label' … end` groups statements and yields no value, so it cannot stand where a value is expected — use a single-statement `try call()` with an `otherwise <value>` fallback", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'blockFormInValuePosition'

		// ⭐⭐ **WHERE THE TARGET'S OWN OPS BEGIN — the fact that makes "the LAST op in the block" mean "the
		// TARGET'S last op".** `module.ops` is append-only for the whole parse (nothing truncates it, and a
		// rewrite mutates in place), so an op index below this floor was appended before the target was
		// parsed and cannot be the target's however the target is spelled.
		//
		// ⚠ It is a FLOOR on the op INDEX rather than a count of `currentBlock.opRefs`, because the target
		// may legally leave `currentBlock` behind: a nested `try` (`try consume(try get() otherwise 0)`)
		// desugars into a fork and the outer call lands in the merge block, so a per-block count would be
		// measured against a block that is no longer current.
		let targetOpFloor = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		// This statement's own temporary floor, captured before the target is parsed so the target's operands
		// sit above it — what `endBlockAfterUnreachableTail` drains if the ok edge turns out to be unreachable
		// (G18). Read only on that path; every other `try` ignores it.
		let tempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		let result = try self.parseTryCallTarget(func)
		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		let target = try self.rewriteLastCallToTryCall(func, flag: flag, tryTok: tryTok, targetOpFloor: targetOpFloor)

		try self.requireThrowingTryTarget(target, tryTok: tryTok)

		// A value-position `try` on a VOID callee has no value to yield (E3059).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SAME ENGLISH SENTENCE IS ALSO E2004's** (`Queries.voidCallResultUsed`, *"Function 'x' does not
		// return a value"*), and the two are deliberately NOT merged: this one is the `try` spelling, refused from
		// the result TAG at the try's own position, while E2004 is the BARE spelling, refused by each dispatch
		// arm's `resultUsed` guard a pass earlier. Two codes, two checks, two positions — `void-call-result.md`
		// pins both halves of every void mutator, because a guard lost from either arm passes on the other's case.
		if resultUsed and self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.void 'voidInValue'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("'{String.from(tryTargetNoun(target, signatures: self.signatures))}' does not return a value", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'voidInValue'

		return try self.desugarTry(func, tryTok: tryTok, result: result, flag: flag, target: target, resultUsed: resultUsed, tempFloor: tempFloor)
	end 'parseTry'

	// Parse the throwing call target after `try` — a primary call form followed by its WHOLE POSTFIX CHAIN
	// (`try make().slice(1, endIndex: 3)`, `try [10,20,30].get(0)`, `try pick()(21)`). Emits an ordinary
	// `call` op that `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` then converts.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE `try` BINDS TO THE OUTERMOST CALL, WHICH IS WHY THE CHAIN MUST BE PARSED TO ITS END.**
	// `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` converts the LAST op the target emitted, so stopping at the primary hands it
	// the WRONG call: `try make().slice(…)` left `.slice(…)` unconsumed and rewrote `make`'s call, then
	// rejected the whole thing as "'make' does not throw" (OPEN #107) — a program the reference compiler
	// accepts and runs. The reference reaches the same rule from the other end: its `try` parses a whole
	// primary (postfix included) and rewrites the tail op. `try f(g())` is unaffected and must stay so — `g`
	// is an ARGUMENT, emitted BEFORE `f`'s call, so the tail op is still `f`.
	//
	// `resultUsed: false` at every arm, and that is the one thing the try target decides differently from an
	// ordinary expression: whether the try's VALUE is usable is settled ONCE, by `parseTry`, from the try's
	// own position (the void-callee E3059). A second copy of that question inside the target answers it from
	// the wrong place — reporting "this call's value cannot be used" for a `try f().push(1)` STATEMENT, whose
	// value is not used at all — where the real fault is that `push` cannot throw (E3055).
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ROUTINE BOTH `try` TARGETS FUNNEL THROUGH, WHICH IS WHY THE BLOCK-FORM SUPPRESSION SITS
	// HERE.** The call this parses is about to be rewritten into a `tryCall` by the caller's own desugar, so
	// the block-form router must not claim it first: a `try f() otherwise 0` written INSIDE a `try 'l' … end`
	// body catches its own error and the enclosing block never sees it — the bootstrap's `_inTryContext`
	// (`2-Parser.cs:23844`) and v1's op-kind exclusion (`Parser.maxon:13233`, which skips a `tryCall`) both
	// draw the same line. Its two callers are the EXPRESSION form (`parseTry`) and the `if try` form
	// (`parseCatchingCondition`), and setting the flag in each would be the same fact written twice.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT THE ONLY SITE THAT SUPPRESSES, AND SAYING OTHERWISE ROTS.** The `for … in <cursor>`
	// advance (`emitCursorAdvance`) claims a call for `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` WITHOUT parsing a try target
	// — it synthesizes the call itself, so there is no target grammar for it to come through here for — and
	// the synthesized ownership calls suppress for a different reason entirely (`emitOwnershipRuntimeCall`).
	// What all of them share is the FLAG, whose own header enumerates them; this routine is one setter of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A `ParseError` FROM THE TARGET SKIPS THE RESTORE, AND THAT IS HARMLESS BY CONSTRUCTION**: the flag
	// is re-seeded per function beside `tryBlocks`, and a parse error ends this file's parse regardless.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SUPPRESSION COVERS THE TARGET'S ARGUMENTS TOO, WHICH IS A NARROWING WORTH KNOWING.** A bare
	// throwing call in an explicit try target's argument list inside a block body — `try g(h())`, `h`
	// throwing — is not routed and stays a plain `call`, so it is the ordinary E3057 telling the author to
	// `try` it. That is what a bare `h()` gets everywhere else in the language today; the block form does not
	// make it worse, it simply does not yet make it better.
	//
	function parseTryCallTarget(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let savedRoutingSuppressed = self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = true
		let target = try self.parseTryTargetExpression(func)
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = savedRoutingSuppressed
		return target
	end 'parseTryCallTarget'

	// ⭐⭐ **TAKE THE OPS A BLOCK HAS GAINED SINCE `mark` OFF IT, AND HAND THEM BACK.** A ternary's TRUE ARM is
	// parsed onto the unconditional path before the `if` announces the form (`parseTernaryExpression`), and is
	// then re-attached inside a block only ONE edge reaches. The ops keep their `module.ops` indices — only the
	// block referencing them changes.
	//
	// ⚠ It had a SECOND caller until the opaque-`T` return convention moved the `+1` into the callee: a
	// substituted `T` result's caller-side co-own was emitted onto the block a `try` was about to fork, and had
	// to be lifted onto the ok edge. Nothing is emitted after such a call any more, so there is nothing to lift
	// — see `emitOwnedValueReturn` for where the reference is taken now.
	//
	// ⚠ **COLLECT AND TRUNCATE ARE ONE OPERATION, WHICH IS WHY THEY ARE ONE FUNCTION.** A collect without the
	// truncate leaves the ops running on the path that was supposed to lose them — a `+1` (or a whole arm) on
	// both edges of a fork, which is a leak on one of them and no diagnostic on either.
	function detachOpRefsAbove(block IrBlock, mark ElementCount) returns OpIndexArray
		var detached = OpIndexArray.create()
		for i in mark upto block.opRefs.count() 'collect'
			detached.push(try block.opRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("detachOpRefsAbove: block {block.id} opRefs.get OOB at {i}"))
		end 'collect'
		while block.opRefs.count() > mark 'truncate'
			_ = try block.opRefs.pop() otherwise panic("detachOpRefsAbove: block {block.id} opRefs.pop above the mark {mark} failed")
		end 'truncate'

		return detached
	end 'detachOpRefsAbove'

	// The target's GRAMMAR, split from the state above so the flag brackets every path through it.
	function parseTryTargetExpression(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// P1.5-B2b: `try await p` — the awaited THROWING promise is the try target (the AWAIT is the throwing
		// operation). Emit the `await` op via the SHARED `emitAwaitOp`, WITHOUT the plain-await E3057 reject:
		// catching the thrown error is exactly what `try await` does. `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` then converts
		// the `await` into a `tryAwait`, and `parseTry`'s E3055 check refuses a NON-throwing promise here.
		//
		// TERMINAL — no postfix chain is applied to the awaited value, and deliberately so: `emitAwaitOp` was
		// entered WITHOUT the E3057 reject on the promise, so a trailing `.method(…)` would make the METHOD the
		// try target and leave the throwing await unguarded, silently dropping its error flag. `parseUnary`
		// already consumed any postfix on the PROMISE operand itself, so nothing legal is lost here.
		if self.at(TokenKind.await) 'awaitTarget'
			let awaitTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.await)
			let promise = try self.parseUnary(func)
			return try self.emitAwaitOp(func, promise: promise, awaitTok: awaitTok)
		end 'awaitTarget'

		return try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parseTryCallReceiver(func), resultUsed: false)
	end 'parseTryTargetExpression'

	// The PRIMARY a `try` target's postfix chain starts from — a free call `f(…)`, a method/qualified call
	// `obj.m(…)` / `Type.m(…)` / `Self.m(…)`, a `self.m(…)`, a field-chain call, or an array / byte-string
	// LITERAL receiver.
	// Split from `parseTryCallTarget` so the postfix continuation is applied to EVERY form in one place rather
	// than per arm (it was applied to the two literal arms only, which is how the free-call arm came to stop
	// at the first callee — OPEN #107).
	function parseTryCallReceiver(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `try bag.items.get(0) otherwise …` / `try self.nodes.get(i) otherwise …` — a throwing method on an
		// ARRAY reached through a 2+-hop field chain (P1.7 slice 3). Checked FIRST: a single-hop `self.m(…)` /
		// `obj.m(…)` is not a field chain (`fieldChainMethodCallAt` needs 2+ hops) and falls to the arms below;
		// a 2+-hop chain resolves to the array field and applies the method, leaving the `call __managed_get` as the
		// last op for `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`.
		// `try arr.managed.get(0) otherwise …` — asked BEFORE the field chain, which would otherwise claim the
		// two-hop shape and resolve `.managed` as a field of a record that has none (see `arrayManagedFieldAt`).
		// The CHAINED predicate: a `try` target must be a throwing CALL, and a bare `try arr.managed` is not one.
		if self.arrayManagedMemberCallAt(self.pos) 'managedFieldTarget'
			return try self.parseMethodCall(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'managedFieldTarget'
		if self.fieldChainMethodCallAt(self.pos) 'fieldChainTarget'
			return try self.parseFieldChainCall(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'fieldChainTarget'
		if self.at(TokenKind.self) 'selfCall'
			return try self.parseSelfPrimary(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'selfCall'
		// `try Self.check(v) otherwise …` — the `Self.` spelling of the qualified static the arm above serves
		// (D9). `Self` is its own keyword token, so it cannot ride that arm; and it opens the DOTTED form ONLY,
		// which is why it is not simply folded into it — there is no `Self(…)` free call for the sibling
		// sub-arm to serve. `parseDottedPrimary` resolves the base, so nothing here learns what `Self` means.
		if self.at(TokenKind.selfType) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'selfDottedTarget'
			return try self.parseDottedPrimary(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'selfDottedTarget'
		// `try int.fromString(s) otherwise 0` — the primitive-static spelling of the qualified static above
		// (A1s-prim), and the position that MATTERS for it: all four `__<prim>_fromString` declarations throw,
		// so `try` is the only way a program calls one. `int`/`float`/`bool` are keywords, so they cannot ride
		// the `named` arm; the SHAPE gate is `parsePrimary`'s, asked from one place so the try target and the
		// expression admit the same set. `byte.fromString(…)` is an identifier and is served by `named` above.
		if self.primitiveStaticCallAt(self.pos) 'primitiveStaticTarget'
			return try self.parseDottedPrimary(func, resultUsed: false)
		end 'primitiveStaticTarget'
		// `try [10,20,30].get(0) otherwise …` — a throwing method on an array LITERAL (P1.7 slice 1), and its
		// exact byte-string analogue `try b"…".get(0)` (P1.7 slice 2). Each parses to an owned array temp that
		// the postfix chain applies its method to; the temp is a FORK temporary, which `desugarTry` suspends
		// across the handler so every ABANDONING path releases it and the merging ones keep it.
		// Both literal kinds are enrolled together — accepting one and not the other would
		// reject a legal `try b"…".get(…)` as "not a call".
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'arrayLiteralTarget'
			return try self.parseArrayLiteral(func, element: ArrayLiteralElementSource.inferred)
		end 'arrayLiteralTarget'
		if self.at(TokenKind.byteStringLiteral) 'byteStringLiteralTarget'
			return try self.parseByteStringLiteral()
		end 'byteStringLiteralTarget'
		// ⭐ **`try "abc".findFirst("b") otherwise …` — A STRING LITERAL RECEIVER (BATCH2 slice 6), AND THE ARM
		// THE SENTENCE ABOVE ALREADY ARGUED FOR.** That note says the two literal kinds are enrolled together
		// because "accepting one and not the other would reject a legal `try b\"…\".get(…)` as 'not a call'" —
		// and a `String` has THREE throwing methods a program may reach on a literal (`findFirst`, `findLast`,
		// `indexAfter`), so the same argument had a third case it did not cover. MEASURED before this arm:
		// `try "abc".findFirst("b") otherwise "abc".startIndex()` is `E2015 … (got 'string literal')` under
		// shv2 while the reference compiles it and answers 1 — a legal program refused, the OPEN #107 shape
		// one literal kind over.
		//
		// ⚠ It calls `parseStringLiteral`, not `consumeStringLiteralValue`, so an INTERPOLATED receiver
		// (`try "a{x}".findFirst("b")`) is enrolled too. That result is an OWNED heap String rather than an
		// immortal rdata one, which needs no special handling here for the array literal's reason: the postfix
		// chain applies its method to the temp and `desugarTry` suspends it across the handler.
		if self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'stringLiteralTarget'
			return try self.parseStringLiteral(func)
		end 'stringLiteralTarget'

		// ⭐ **`try 'A'.asciiValue() otherwise 0` — A CHARACTER LITERAL RECEIVER (A5m-ab), THE FOURTH CASE OF
		// THE ARGUMENT THE TWO NOTES ABOVE ALREADY MAKE.** `Character.asciiValue()` is the type's one throwing
		// member, so `try` is the only way a program calls it, and a literal is the receiver the oracle prints
		// **65** for. Reaching this arm at all is the other half of the same rung: the block-form router used to
		// claim the token first, and `tryOpensBlockAt` now tells a block LABEL from a receiver by the `.` behind
		// it — which is also why nothing here needs to re-ask, a bare `try 'x'` never gets past `parseTry`.
		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'characterLiteralTarget'
			return try self.parseCharLiteral()
		end 'characterLiteralTarget'

		// ⭐ **`try (a / b) otherwise …` — A PARENTHESIZED EXPRESSION AS THE TRY TARGET (A1).** The only
		// throwing operation in the language written as an OPERATOR rather than a call, so it is the only one
		// whose `try` cannot name a callee: `stdlib/Math.maxon`, `Json.maxon` and `helpers/sort/driftsort.maxon`
		// spell it this way at eighteen sites, and `specs/arithmetic-operators.md:37` documents it as the form.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THIS DOES NOT OPEN `try` TO ARBITRARY PARENTHESIZED EXPRESSIONS.** The group is parsed as an
		// ordinary expression, and `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` then looks at the LAST op the target emitted: a
		// possibly-zero divide left a `call __checked_div` and is converted, while `try (4 + 5)` left a
		// `binOp` and falls to `rejectTryTarget`. So the accepted set is exactly "the group's outermost
		// operation is fallible", derived from the one rule that already decides it for every other try shape
		// rather than from a second division-shaped test here. The oracle reaches the same boundary from the
		// same direction — `try (12 / d)` over a PROVABLY non-zero `d` is its own E2001, because a bare
		// divide is not a call there either.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **BUT IT DID NEED ONE NEW FACT, AND SAYING OTHERWISE WAS FALSE UNTIL A REVIEW MEASURED IT.**
		// "The last op the target emitted" is only the target's while the target is guaranteed to have
		// emitted one, and this is the FIRST try form that can emit nothing: `(v)` over a bare name is a
		// lookup. `parseTry`'s `targetOpFloor` is that fact — see `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`, and
		// `safety.md`'s two `error.try-over-a-parenthesized-…` cases for the wrong answer it stops.
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'parenthesizedTarget'
			return try self.parseParenthesizedExpression(func)
		end 'parenthesizedTarget'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERAL NAMED TARGET, AND IT IS LAST ON PURPOSE.** `try f(…)` and `try obj.method(…)` where
		// the head is any token that can stand as a NAME — which since D8 includes a KEYWORD, because a
		// function and a parameter may be declared with one. `try from.get(k)` on a parameter named `from`
		// (`IR/CsrGraph.maxon:75`) is the shape, and it was `E2015 "try must be applied to a call … (got
		// 'from')"` on source the bootstrap compiles — this arm asked `TokenKind.identifier`, which a keyword
		// is not.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT MOVED DOWN RATHER THAN GAINING AN EXCLUSION LIST, and the difference is the one this file
		// keeps paying for.** Widened in place it would have claimed `Self.check(v)` and `int.fromString(s)`
		// out from under the two arms above that exist to serve them — the `Self` arm's own header says it
		// cannot ride the named arm, and a primitive static reaches `parseDottedPrimary` through neither. The
		// alternative was `tokenCanBeAName(…) and not at(selfType) and not primitiveStaticCallAt(…)`, an
		// enumerated exclusion that goes stale the next time a keyword-headed target earns an arm. Ordering
		// IS the rule here — the router's own header says the field-chain arm is "checked FIRST" for exactly
		// this reason — so the general case goes last and every specific one keeps its claim by standing
		// above it.
		if tokenCanBeAName(self.current()) 'named'
			if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftParen 'freeCall'
				return try self.parseCall(func, resultUsed: false)
			end 'freeCall'
			if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'dotted'
				return try self.parseDottedPrimary(func, resultUsed: false)
			end 'dotted'
		end 'named'

		let tok = self.current()
		throw ParseError.unsupported("`try` must be applied to a call — `try f(…)`, `try obj.method(…)`, `try (a / b)`, or `try await p` (got '{tok.kind.rawValue}')", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'parseTryCallReceiver'

	// Rewrite the just-emitted `call` op in `currentBlock` into a `tryCall` defining `errorFlag` as its
	// second result. Returns the `TryTarget` the desugar asks its two questions of. v1 rewrites the terminal
	// call in place for the same reason: it makes nested tries (`try consume(try get() otherwise 0)`)
	// work — each inner try rewrites its OWN call, and the outer rewrites the last op after the whole
	// parse, which is its own call.
	// The op a `try` target's rewrite belongs on — `lastIdx`, or the CALL one step behind it when `lastIdx` is
	// the element reinterpretation that call's float result is read through. See the caller for why one step
	// and not a scan.
	//
	// ⚠ It requires the reinterpretation's operand to be the previous op's OWN result, so a `bitsToFloat` the
	// author wrote (`__Builtins.bitsToFloat(x)` — a call, not a bare op) and a reinterpretation over anything
	// but the call in front of it both leave the index alone.
	function tryTargetCallOpIndex(lastIdx OpIndex, refCount OpIndex, targetOpFloor OpIndex) returns OpIndex throws ParseError
		if refCount < 2 'noOpBehindIt'
			return lastIdx
		end 'noOpBehindIt'

		let prevIdx = try self.currentBlock.opRefs.get(refCount - 2) otherwise panic("tryTargetCallOpIndex: opRefs.get OOB at {refCount - 2} on a block with {refCount} ops")
		if prevIdx < targetOpFloor 'beforeTheTarget'
			return lastIdx
		end 'beforeTheTarget'

		return prevIdx if try self.opReinterpretsResultOf(lastIdx, producer: prevIdx) else lastIdx
	end 'tryTargetCallOpIndex'

	// Is op `idx` a REINTERPRETATION of the value the CALL at `producer` defines — the one shape
	// `tryTargetCallOpIndex` steps back over. Both halves are required: a reinterpretation over anything
	// else, and a reinterpretation sitting behind a non-call, leave the try target where it was.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO OPS QUALIFY, AND WHAT MAKES THEM QUALIFY IS THAT A USER CANNOT SPELL EITHER.** A float
	// element's `bitsToFloat` and a narrow signed element's sign extension are both emitted by
	// `emitContainerElementAccessor` as part of producing the call's result, so `try v.get(0) otherwise 0.0`
	// and `try arr.get(0) otherwise Signal.zero` are each exactly one call. `try f() + 1` is NOT, and the
	// only thing separating the two is that its `binOp` is the author's — which is why this is a membership
	// test over compiler-only ops and not a shape test over the op graph. (`__Builtins.bitsToFloat(x)` is a
	// CALL, not a bare op, so the author's spelling of the reinterpretation is not in the set either.)
	function opReinterpretsResultOf(idx OpIndex, producer OpIndex) returns bool throws ParseError
		return match try self.artifact.module.ops.get(idx) otherwise panic("opReinterpretsResultOf: opRef {idx} not in module.ops") 'reinterpretation'
			unaryOp(_, operand, op) gives unaryOpProducesACallResult(op) and discardedCalleeIsACall(try self.directCallDefining(producer, value: operand))
			call to binOp gives false
			compare to panicAbortValue gives false
			// The error-flag encode is emitted by the ROUTER, never by a call's result production, so it can never be
			// the reinterpretation sitting on top of one.
			errorFlagEncode gives false
		end 'reinterpretation'
	end 'opReinterpretsResultOf'

	// The unary ops that are part of a CALL's RESULT PRODUCTION rather than an expression applied to it —
	// the membership `opReinterpretsResultOf` tests. Exhaustive, so a unary op the compiler starts inserting
	// after a call has to be classified here rather than silently breaking a `try` over that call.
	function unaryOpProducesACallResult(op MaxonUnaryOp) returns bool
		return match op 'o'
			neg to floatToBits gives false
			bitsToFloat gives true
			signExtendI8 to signExtendI32 gives true
		end 'o'
	end 'unaryOpProducesACallResult'

	// ⭐ **WHICH DIRECT CALL DEFINED `value`, if the op at `idx` is one.** ONE enumeration of the op union
	// serves both askers — the `try` rewrite, which only wants to know THAT a call defines its operand
	// (`discardedCalleeIsACall`), and the discarded-result probe, which wants the callee by NAME — so a newly
	// appended op is classified here once and cannot be classified two ways.
	function directCallDefining(idx OpIndex, value ValueId) returns DiscardedCallee throws ParseError
		return match try self.artifact.module.ops.get(idx) otherwise panic("directCallDefining: opRef {idx} not in module.ops") 'producer'
			call(result, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) gives DiscardedCallee.named(callee) if result == value else DiscardedCallee.notACall
			literal to panicAbortValue gives DiscardedCallee.notACall
			// The error-flag encode defines a value, but never a CALL's result — this test is about a direct call's.
			errorFlagEncode gives DiscardedCallee.notACall
		end 'producer'
	end 'directCallDefining'

	function rewriteLastCallToTryCall(func IrFunction, flag ValueId, tryTok Token, targetOpFloor OpIndex) returns TryTarget throws ParseError
		let refCount = self.currentBlock.opRefs.count()
		if refCount == 0 'noOp'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`try` must be applied to a call — the expression after `try` emitted no call", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'noOp'
		// ⭐⭐ **A TRAILING ELEMENT REINTERPRETATION BELONGS TO THE CALL THAT PRODUCED IT.** A container whose
		// element is a FLOAT reads its slot as 64 BITS — the runtime returns in a general-purpose register —
		// and reinterprets them with a `bitsToFloat` op right after the call (`emitContainerElementAccessor`).
		// That op, not the call, is then the last one the target emitted, and `try v.get(0) otherwise 0.0`
		// would be refused as *"`try` must be applied to a call"* on a program that is exactly one.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A BACKWARD SCAN, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT.** It steps back over
		// ONE op, only when that op is a reinterpretation whose operand is the immediately preceding op's own
		// result, and never below `targetOpFloor` — so `try f() + 1` (a `binOp`) and `try (a)` (no op of the
		// target's at all) keep the verdicts the paragraph below describes, and nothing can reach a previous
		// statement's call. The step is what makes the reinterpretation part of the call's RESULT PRODUCTION
		// rather than a second expression the `try` was applied to.
		let opIdx = try self.tryTargetCallOpIndex(try self.currentBlock.opRefs.get(refCount - 1) otherwise panic("rewriteLastCallToTryCall: opRefs.get OOB on a non-empty block"), refCount: refCount, targetOpFloor: targetOpFloor)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE LAST OP MUST BE ONE THE TARGET EMITTED (see `targetOpFloor`).** Every try form but one
		// is guaranteed to emit its call, so this never fired until `try (…)` opened the door to a group
		// whose inner expression is a bare NAME — a lookup, which emits nothing — and the "last op the
		// target emitted" became the last op the PREVIOUS STATEMENT emitted. That is not a cosmetic blame
		// slip: `let a = arr.get(2)` then `try (a) otherwise 99` REWROTE the array accessor two statements
		// up, so the handler caught the ARRAY's error, `w` took 99 instead of `a`, and the E3057 that bare
		// accessor owed vanished with the `call` it would have been reported against.
		//
		// It routes to `rejectTryTarget` rather than wording its own sentence: to the author, `try (a)` and
		// `try (a + b)` are one mistake — the thing after `try` is not a call — and one sentence is what
		// keeps the two spellings from drifting apart.
		if opIdx < targetOpFloor 'notTheTargetsOp'
			return try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
		end 'notTheTargetsOp'

		let op = try self.artifact.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteLastCallToTryCall: last opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		return match op 'rewrite'
			call(result, callee, args, argLabels, argRanges, argImmutableNames, argDefaultSlots, mint) gives TryTarget.named(self.commitTryCallRewrite(opIdx, result: result, errorFlag: flag, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, argDefaultSlots: argDefaultSlots, mint: mint))
			// P1.5-B2b: `try await p` — rewrite the `await` op into a `tryAwait` defining the error flag,
			// exactly as `call` → `tryCall`. The awaited callee (recovered from the promise) is returned for
			// the desugar's E3055/E3059 checks and its `otherwise` error-type binding.
			await (result, promise) gives self.commitTryAwaitRewrite(func, opIdx: opIdx, result: result, flag: flag, promise: promise)
			// Only a DIRECT `call` or an `await` can be `try`ed. An `indirectCall`/`functionRef` (P1.5-A1) is
			// never throwing — a throwing function cannot be a function value (E3101) — an `asyncCall` (a spawn)
			// is not the throwing operation (the AWAIT is), and a `tryAwait` cannot be a fresh try target. All
			// reject `try` exactly as the other non-call ops do.
			literal to asyncCall gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			tryAwait gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			sizeofType gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			constArrayLiteral gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			switch gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			// A throwing WITNESS dispatch (`try self.item.digest()`, P1.7a slice 2b-v) — rewritten into the
			// dual-register `witnessTryDispatch` defining the error flag, exactly as `call` becomes `tryCall`.
			// Routed through a helper that RE-MATCHES the op in a SMALL match so `args` is bound in a
			// single-call arm, never in this big one (the tail-slot-Array decref corruption `witnessDispatch`
			// documents).
			witnessDispatch gives self.commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite(opIdx, flag: flag)
			// A `witnessTryDispatch` in the last slot means an inner `try` already claimed this dispatch; it is
			// no more a fresh try target than a `tryAwait` is.
			witnessTryDispatch gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			// A panic is not a call and cannot throw — it ends the process — so it rejects `try` exactly as
			// every other non-call op does.
			panicAbortValue gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
			// A `try` cannot be applied to the error-flag encode: the parser emits it, no source expression is it.
			errorFlagEncode gives try self.rejectTryTarget(tryTok)
		end 'rewrite'
	end 'rewriteLastCallToTryCall'

	// Replace the `witnessDispatch` op at `opIdx` with a `witnessTryDispatch` defining the second `errorFlag`
	// result, and return the target the desugar reads — the interface method's NAME and its declared `throws`.
	// The op carries NEITHER (both are parse-time facts of the constraint resolution, and an op field for
	// them would be paid for by every dispatch to answer a question only a `try` asks), so the dispatch
	// RECORDS them as it emits and this reads them back. The recording is exact: a dispatch records at the
	// instant it emits its op, and this runs on the op in the LAST opRef slot — which is that same op,
	// because nothing else can have emitted since. A non-throwing method rewritten here is E3055, reported
	// by `requireThrowingTryTarget` off the `none` clause this hands it: the same route a non-throwing
	// direct callee takes.
	function commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite(opIdx OpIndex, flag ValueId) returns TryTarget
		// CHECKED BEFORE THE REWRITE, not after: a stale record would mean the target's name and throws clause
		// belong to some OTHER dispatch, and rewriting first would leave a half-converted op behind the panic.
		if self.lastWitnessDispatchIndex != opIdx 'staleRecord'
			panic("commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite: the `witnessDispatch` at {opIdx} is not the one `emitWitnessDispatch` recorded ({self.lastWitnessDispatchIndex}) — every witness dispatch must be emitted through that one door so its method name and throws clause reach the `try` desugar")
		end 'staleRecord'

		match try self.artifact.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops") 'w'
			witnessDispatch(result, args, witness, methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex, isVoid, _) then try self.artifact.module.ops.set(opIdx, value: MaxonOp.witnessTryDispatch(result, errorFlag: flag, args: args, witness: witness, methodIndex: methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex: interfaceDeclIndex, isVoid: isVoid)) otherwise panic("commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite: module.ops.set OOB at {opIdx}")
			default panic("commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite: the op at {opIdx} is not a `witnessDispatch` — `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`'s match routes only that arm here")
		end 'w'
		return TryTarget.witness(self.lastWitnessDispatchName, throwsClause: self.lastWitnessDispatchThrows)
	end 'commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite'

	// Replace the `call` op at `opIdx` with a `tryCall` defining the second `errorFlag` result, preserving
	// every call field, and return the callee name.
	// `mint` crosses the rewrite with every other operand: a `try int.fromString(s)` is the SAME call, and
	// the noun its E3036 would name does not change because the error flag became a second result.
	function commitTryCallRewrite(opIdx OpIndex, result ValueId, errorFlag ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, argLabels ByteArrayArray, argRanges SourceRangeArray, argImmutableNames ByteArrayArray, argDefaultSlots ParamCountArray, mint CalleeMint) returns ByteArray
		try self.artifact.module.ops.set(opIdx, value: MaxonOp.tryCall(result, errorFlag: errorFlag, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, argDefaultSlots: argDefaultSlots, mint: mint)) otherwise panic("commitTryCallRewrite: module.ops.set OOB at {opIdx}")
		return callee
	end 'commitTryCallRewrite'

	// Replace the `await` op at `opIdx` with a `tryAwait` defining the `flag` second result (P1.5-B2b), and
	// return the TARGET the desugar asks its two questions of — the awaited callee for a spawn, the storage
	// TYPE for a promise out of a container (P1.5-B2c). Both come from `awaitedPromiseFacts`, so the target a
	// `try await` is desugared against and the result type its `await` was minted at are one derivation.
	function commitTryAwaitRewrite(func IrFunction, opIdx OpIndex, result ValueId, flag ValueId, promise ValueId) returns TryTarget
		try self.artifact.module.ops.set(opIdx, value: MaxonOp.tryAwait(result, errorFlag: flag, promise: promise)) otherwise panic("commitTryAwaitRewrite: module.ops.set OOB at {opIdx}")
		return self.awaitedPromiseFacts(func, promise: promise).tryTarget
	end 'commitTryAwaitRewrite'

	// The AWAITED callee name a promise handle carries — recovered from the `asyncCall` op that minted it, by
	// scanning this function's emitted blocks. A promise ValueId is minted by exactly ONE `asyncCall` in its
	// OWN function (the promise mark is per-function, so the search never crosses a frame), so the first match
	// is THE one. `none` means the value is not an async spawn's result — the caller reports its own
	// "not a promise" / invariant diagnostic. Read at the `try await`/plain-`await` sites (rare), so an
	// O(function ops) scan costs nothing on the await-free program and keeps the promise → callee fact off
	// every value (no per-value column that the scale corpus, which has no async, would pay for).
	//
	// ⭐ **A RE-ARMED `var`'s PROMISE IS A MERGE PHI (P1.5-B2 #88, Finding 1)** — no `asyncCall` defines it
	// directly, so the direct scan misses and the resolution TRACES the phi's incoming edge values to a
	// defining `asyncCall`. A promise var's phi merges promises on every reaching edge (all from `async
	// <callee>` of the same consistently-typed binding), so the first one reached answers for the awaited
	// result type and the throws check. A `Set` of visited values breaks loop back-edge cycles.
	function asyncCallCalleeOf(func IrFunction, promise ValueId) returns AsyncCalleeLookup
		var visited = AsyncCalleeVisited.create()
		return self.resolveAsyncCallee(func, promise: promise, visited: visited)
	end 'asyncCallCalleeOf'

	function resolveAsyncCallee(func IrFunction, promise ValueId, visited AsyncCalleeVisited) returns AsyncCalleeLookup
		if visited.contains(promise) 'seen'
			return AsyncCalleeLookup.none
		end 'seen'
		visited.insert(promise)
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try self.artifact.module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("resolveAsyncCallee: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				match asyncCalleeIfMatch(try self.artifact.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("resolveAsyncCallee: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), promise: promise) 'm'
					found(callee) then return AsyncCalleeLookup.found(callee)
					none then break 'm'
				end 'm'
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
		// Not defined by an `asyncCall` directly — trace a merge phi's incoming values.
		for incoming in self.phiIncomingValues(func, phi: promise) 'eachIncoming'
			match self.resolveAsyncCallee(func, promise: incoming, visited: visited) 'r'
				found(callee) then return AsyncCalleeLookup.found(callee)
				none then break 'r'
			end 'r'
		end 'eachIncoming'
		return AsyncCalleeLookup.none
	end 'resolveAsyncCallee'

	// The values that flow INTO `phi` on each predecessor edge, if `phi` is a merge block-arg — else empty. A
	// ValueId is a block-arg of exactly ONE block (SSA), so the first block carrying it is THE one; the phi's
	// slot in that block's `blockArgs` indexes each incoming edge's `argIds` (recorded in lockstep — an edge
	// exists in `branchEdges` iff its target carries block-args, so every edge into this block has one).
	function phiIncomingValues(func IrFunction, phi ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
		var incoming = ValueIdArray.create()
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try self.artifact.module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("phiIncomingValues: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for slot in 0 upto block.blockArgs.count() 'eachArg'
				let arg = try block.blockArgs.get(slot) otherwise panic("phiIncomingValues: blockArgs.get OOB at {slot}")
				if arg.valueId == phi 'isPhi'
					for predRef in func.blockRefs 'eachPred'
						let pred = try self.artifact.module.blocks.get(predRef) otherwise panic("phiIncomingValues: predRef not in module.blocks")
						for edge in pred.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
							if edge.targetBlockId == block.id 'intoBlock'
								incoming.push(try edge.argIds.get(slot) otherwise panic("phiIncomingValues: an edge into block {block.id} carries {edge.argIds.count()} args but the phi is at slot {slot} — edge args must be lockstep with block args"))
							end 'intoBlock'
						end 'eachEdge'
					end 'eachPred'
					return incoming
				end 'isPhi'
			end 'eachArg'
		end 'eachBlock'
		return incoming
	end 'phiIncomingValues'

	// **E3055 — the `try` target's terminal call must be one that CAN throw.** Anchored on the `try` token,
	// which is why it lives here and not in SemanticCheck (see `ParseError.tryOnNonThrowing`).
	//
	// ⭐ **THERE ARE TWO CALLEE CLASSES, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS BEING ASKED.** A COMPILER-EMITTED callee
	// (`__managed_count`, `__print_string`, `__managed_append`) is in no signature registry, so `declaresCallee`
	// answers `false` for it and the check used to wave every non-throwing builtin through: `try arr.count()`
	// and `try print("x")` compiled, and the desugar then branched on an error-flag register the callee never
	// wrote — a `try` that takes its error path on whatever the ABI left in R10. The reference compiler
	// rejects both. `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` is THE ONE home of "which builtin throws" (a throwing array
	// accessor, or `__Builtins.runProcess`), and this is the exact mirror of `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall`, which reads
	// the SAME predicate to report the opposite mistake (E3057 — a throwing builtin called WITHOUT `try`).
	// Neither can drift from the other.
	//
	// A callee NO file declares is left to SemanticCheck's E3004: a `try <typo>` is an unknown function, not a
	// non-throwing one.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE DECLARED CALLEE IS ASKED FIRST, BECAUSE "COMPILER-INTERNAL" NO LONGER IMPLIES
	// "UNDECLARED" (A1r).** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declares `__int_fromString(…) throws ParseError` and now
	// CALLS it, and a `__`-first order answered that call's question from the builtin roster — which of course
	// does not list it — so the module's own `try __int_fromString(input)` was refused as *"this builtin call
	// cannot fail"* (E3055, MEASURED at `:322` as the very next blocker once the call door opened). A DECLARED
	// callee's `throws` clause is written in the program; nothing about the shape of its name can outrank it.
	// The two arms are still disjoint for every callee that existed before A1r, because a `__` name could not
	// be declared at all — so this order changes no answer it did not have to.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT ASKS `declaresCallee`, WHICH IS WHERE A PER-CALL PREDICATE BELONGS.** `returnTypeOf` answers
	// the same question by ADOPTING the return type into this file's interner, so it allocates a `CalleeReturn`
	// per ask; in front of the builtin arm that lands on every `try` over an array accessor or a checked divide
	// in the program — MEASURED on the scale ladder as +52 allocations at rung 0 doubling to +1,664 at rung 5,
	// a per-site constant, which is what a `Map.contains` half of that pair exists to avoid. It is also the one
	// `requireCalleeIsNotReservedName` asks, so the door that ADMITS a reserved call and the check that reads
	// its `throws` clause cannot disagree about whether the name has a declaration.
	//
	// A WITNESS target answers from its OWN clause, and it has no "undeclared" escape. An interface method is
	// in NO signature registry, so routing it through `declaresCallee` would answer `false` and wave every
	// non-throwing interface method through — this very hole, arriving from the other side. Its `throws` is a
	// fact the constraint resolution already established, so the test is unconditional.
	function requireThrowingTryTarget(target TryTarget, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		match target 'kind'
			named(callee) then try self.requireThrowingNamedTryTarget(callee, tryTok: tryTok)
			witness(methodName, throwsClause) then try requireThrowingClauseTryTarget(methodName, throwsClause: throwsClause, tryTok: tryTok)
			promise(storageName, throwsClause) then try requireThrowingClauseTryTarget(storageName, throwsClause: throwsClause, tryTok: tryTok)
		end 'kind'
	end 'requireThrowingTryTarget'

	// The NAMED half of the check above — a direct callee or an awaited one, asked of the signature index.
	function requireThrowingNamedTryTarget(callee ByteArray, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.declaresCallee(callee) 'declared'
			if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(self.signatures.throwsOf(callee)) 'nonThrowing'
				throw ParseError.tryOnNonThrowing(String.from(callee), line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
			end 'nonThrowing'
			return
		end 'declared'

		if isCompilerInternalCallee(callee) 'builtin'
			if isThrowingRuntimeCallee(callee) 'throwingAccessor'
				return
			end 'throwingAccessor'

			// ⚠ **A DIVISION ANSWERS AS A DIVISION (A1x).** A1x's total remainder is the one non-throwing callee
			// the author did not write the NAME of — they wrote an operator — so the builtin sentence below would
			// describe their `mod` as "this builtin call". Asked before it, and only for a division the compiler
			// expanded: every other `__` callee here is a method or a `print` the author DID spell.
			let divisionKind = divisionKindOf(callee)
			match divisionKind 'totalDivision'
				guardedRemainder then throw ParseError.tryOnTotalDivision(divisionOperatorSpellingForKind(divisionKind), line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
				// The two `__checked_*` names are throwing and returned above; `notCheckedDivision` is every other
				// builtin, which the sentence below is for.
				quotient to remainder then panic("requireThrowingNamedTryTarget: '{String.from(callee)}' is a THROWING division, so `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` must have accepted the `try` already — reaching here means `divisionKindThrows` and that predicate disagree")
				notCheckedDivision then break 'totalDivision'
			end 'totalDivision'

			throw ParseError.tryOnNonThrowingBuiltin(tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'builtin'
	end 'requireThrowingNamedTryTarget'

	// The expression after `try` was not a call. Typed `returns TryTarget` so it slots into
	// `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`'s `match`, but it always throws.
	function rejectTryTarget(tryTok Token) returns TryTarget throws ParseError
		throw ParseError.unsupported("`try` must be applied to a call — `try f(…)` or `try obj.method(…)`; the expression after `try` is not a call", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
	end 'rejectTryTarget'

	// Build the flag split + handler + merge for a parsed `tryCall`. `result` is the success value,
	// `flag` its error flag, both defined by the `tryCall` in `currentBlock`.
	function desugarTry(func IrFunction, tryTok Token, result ValueId, flag ValueId, target TryTarget, resultUsed bool, tempFloor TempDropMark) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let entryBlock = self.currentBlock
		// `NoErrorFlag`, not a bare `0`: this is the dual-register ABI's "no error in flight" value, the one
		// the throw ENCODE's bias reserves, and it is read here and by the block form's own flag test.
		let noError = self.emitLiteral(NoErrorFlag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tryTok))
		let isErr = try self.emitCompare(flag, rhs: noError, op: MaxonCmpOp.ne, opTok: tryTok)

		var okBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"tryok", line: tryTok.line)
		let errBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"tryerr", line: tryTok.line)

		// ⭐ **A MANAGED tryCall RESULT IS OWNED ONLY ON THE OK EDGE — pull it out of the ENTRY-block drop
		// set before the handler parses.** `enrolOwnedCallTemp` tracked the result as an owned pending
		// temp in the entry block, which flows to BOTH `tryok` and `tryerr`; on the `tryerr` edge the
		// result register is NULL (a throw zeroes it). An error handler that drains the pending temps
		// (`otherwise return`/`throw`, whose exit runs `emitHandoffExitDrops`) would then decref that NULL —
		// a segfault. Removing it here confines every touch of the result to the ok path; each finish path
		// re-scopes the still-owned value (the owned-heap bit is untouched, so `valueIsOwnedHeap` still
		// answers true) to the ok block, the phi, or an ok-block drop. A scalar result owns no heap, is in
		// no drop set, and this is a no-op for it — the Wave-1 behaviour, unchanged.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT RUNS BEFORE `takeForkTemps`, AND THAT ORDER IS WHAT KEEPS THE SEGFAULT ABOVE FIXED.** The
		// suspension below enrols everything the fork still owes as an OWNED BINDING, and an owned binding is
		// released by every abandoning exit — so a `result` left on the list would be decref'd on the error
		// edge, where its register is exactly the NULL this paragraph is about.
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) 'managedResultOffEntry'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(result)
		end 'managedResultOffEntry'

		// ⛔⛔ **THE FORK'S TEMPORARIES ARE STILL LIVE ON THE OK EDGE, AND DROPPING THEM THERE WAS A
		// USE-AFTER-FREE.** They belong to the ENCLOSING EXPRESSION, which is only half-built when the `try`
		// forks — so on any path that reaches the rest of that expression they are values it has yet to
		// CONSUME, not garbage it has finished with. MEASURED, on a program with no stdlib in it at all
		// (`specs-shv2/try-fork-pending-temporaries.md`): `Conf{a: StrArray.create(), n: try mayFail(1)
		// otherwise panic("x")}` released the array at the head of `tryok` and then STORED it into the
		// struct three ops later, and the reference bootstrap answers `1` where this segfaulted.
		//
		// ⇒ THE RULE: **a path that goes on to finish the enclosing expression keeps them; a path that
		// ABANDONS it releases them on its way out.** The ok edge always finishes it, and so does an error
		// edge whose handler yields a value or falls through — both merge into the continuation, where the
		// ordinary statement-end drain releases them once for the merged path.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **AND "ABANDONS" IS A PROPERTY OF A PATH, NOT OF THE HANDLER — THIS ROUTINE CLASSIFIED THE
		// HANDLER, AND LEAKED.** A `fellThrough` block reaches the merge at its `end` and can STILL leave by
		// `return`/`throw`/`break`/`continue` on an INNER path, so one answer per handler is a lie about the
		// other half of it. MEASURED (found by review): a handler falling through at its `end` with an inner
		// `return` was **exit 101** where the bootstrap answers 7.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **SO THE RELEASE IS NOT PLACED BY THIS ROUTINE AT ALL — IT IS DELEGATED TO THE MACHINERY THAT
		// ALREADY KNOWS WHAT A PATH LEAVING EARLY OWES.** For the duration of the handler the temporaries are
		// enrolled as ANONYMOUS OWNED BINDINGS — the enrolment `parseOtherwiseHandler` already makes one level
		// in for the caught error box, for the reason it states there: *"so a terminating form's own
		// `emitScopeDrops` releases it"*. Every exit is then covered with no new knowledge anywhere:
		//   • `return` / `throw` — `emitHandoffExitDrops`, which drops the whole in-scope list and RESTORES
		//     it, so two SIBLING exits each release them on their own path and neither steals the other's;
		//   • a bare propagating `try` — `parseOtherwiseHandler`'s `propagate` arm already emits the same
		//     whole-function scope drops before its terminator;
		//   • `break` / `continue` — `dropTargetBodyOwned(ctx.ownedFloor)`, and **the destination decides the
		//     answer for free**: a target OUTSIDE the `try` recorded its floor before this enrolment, so these
		//     are released; a loop opened INSIDE the handler recorded its floor above them, so they are not.
		//     That question needed no answer of its own, which is the whole argument for this mechanism;
		//   • `panic` — releases no owned binding of any kind and aborts the process, exactly as it already
		//     does not release the caught box;
		//   • the handler falling through its `end` — `closeBlock` pops only above the block's own mark, which
		//     is above this enrolment, so nothing is released and the merged path still owns them.
		//
		// ⇒ **THE TWO `try` FORMS ANSWER THIS FROM ONE RULE.** `tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget` gives each throwing
		// site of the BLOCK form its own release and leaves the list intact for the paths that did not throw;
		// this is that rule — release per ABANDONING PATH, never per construct — reached through the
		// scope-drop registry rather than the pending-temp one, because a suspended operand outlives the
		// statement boundaries the pending-temp list is drained at and the scope registry is the one whose
		// floors already survive a nested block.
		//
		// The temporaries do come OFF `pendingTempDrops` while the handler parses, and that half is what the
		// enrolment replaces rather than repeats: a handler's own statement-end drains read the whole list
		// with a whole-statement floor, so left in place they would sweep the enclosing expression's operands
		// and FORGET them, leaking on the ok path. `resumeForkTemps` hands them back once both edges are wired.
		let forkTemps = self.takeForkTemps()
		let suspendedMark = self.suspendForkTempsAsOwned(forkTemps)

		self.emitCondBranch(entryBlock, cond: isErr, thenId: errBlock.id, elseId: okBlock.id, ifTok: tryTok)

		// Values entering both branches: the ok path leaves the carried vars unchanged, so `entryVals`
		// is what it carries; the error handler may reassign them (`otherwise 'err' result = 42`).
		//
		// ⚠ Both snapshots are taken AFTER the suspension above, and that order is load-bearing:
		// `restoreMoveMark` PANICS unless the owned-binding height it rewinds to is the one it captured, so
		// an enrolment made between the two would abort the compiler rather than misbehave quietly.
		let entryVals = self.snapshotBindings(self.mutableVars)
		// ⭐ **AND THE SAME SNAPSHOT FOR THE DROP-GATING BIT.** The ok edge leaves this fork having moved
		// nothing (the handler is the only thing parsed between here and the merge), so `entryMoveMark` IS
		// the ok edge's move state — which makes it both the state to rewind to when the handler terminates
		// and the ok edge's half of the reconciliation when it does not. `parseIfStatement` captures its
		// `entryMoveMark` at the identical point for the identical pair of uses.
		let entryMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()

		// ⭐⭐ **G18: IS THIS `try`'s SUCCESS EDGE REACHABLE AT ALL?** Read HERE, at the one point the cursor
		// still stands on the token that decides the form and the target is already resolved — and passed to
		// `parseOtherwiseHandler` rather than re-read there, so the two cannot come to disagree about which form
		// this is. All three conditions are the boundary of the widening, not a filter on it:
		//
		//   • **PROPAGATING** — an `otherwise` CATCHES the error, so the handler runs and control comes back;
		//     the call is not a terminator and the handler is not dead code;
		//   • **STATEMENT position** (`not resultUsed`) — a value-position `let x = try f()` is left alone: its
		//     binding is unreachable either way, and terminating there would put a definition in a block nothing
		//     enters, for no gain to any reader;
		//   • the callee can only ever THROW, so the flag test below is decided and the `tryok` edge is a branch
		//     control never takes.
		let propagates = not self.at(TokenKind.otherwise)
		let okEdgeIsUnreachable = propagates and not resultUsed and self.tryTargetAlwaysThrows(target)

		self.currentBlock = errBlock
		let handler = try self.parseOtherwiseHandler(func, result: result, flag: flag, target: target, resultUsed: resultUsed, propagates: propagates, tryTok: tryTok)
		var errEnd = self.currentBlock
		let errMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()

		let merged = match handler 'h'
			terminated gives self.finishTerminatedTry(okBlock, entryVals: entryVals, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, result: result)
			value(otherwiseValue, blame) gives try self.finishValueTry(func, okBlock: okBlock, errEnd: errEnd, result: result, otherwiseValue: otherwiseValue, otherwiseBlame: blame, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, errMoveMark: errMoveMark, tryTok: tryTok)
			fellThrough gives try self.finishFellThroughTry(func, okBlock: okBlock, errEnd: errEnd, entryVals: entryVals, result: result, resultUsed: resultUsed, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, errMoveMark: errMoveMark, tryTok: tryTok)
		end 'h'

		// LAST, and after the finish: `finishValueTry` drains the FALLBACK's own temporaries on the error edge
		// with a whole-statement floor, and would sweep these with them if they were back on the list.
		self.resumeForkTemps(forkTemps, suspendedMark: suspendedMark)

		// ⭐ **AND ONLY NOW MAY THE UNREACHABLE OK EDGE BE CLOSED (G18).** `finishTerminatedTry` has left control
		// in `okBlock` and re-enrolled a managed result there, and `resumeForkTemps` has handed the fork's
		// temporaries back — so this is the first point at which the pending list is the one the drain must clear.
		// Closing it earlier would strand both.
		if okEdgeIsUnreachable 'theCalleeNeverComesBack'
			self.endBlockAfterUnreachableTail(func, anchor: tryTok, tempFloor: tempFloor)
		end 'theCalleeNeverComesBack'

		return merged
	end 'desugarTry'

	// ⭐⭐ **RECONCILE A `try`'s TWO LIVE EDGES' MOVE STATE — the ok edge, which moved nothing since the fork,
	// and the handler's, which may have.** A binding moved on one edge and live on the other is dropped on
	// the live edge and marked uniformly moved past the merge, so the later scope-exit drop is correct on
	// both paths. Shared by the two `desugarTry` finishes that HAVE two live edges (`finishValueTry`,
	// `finishFellThroughTry`), because the reconciliation is the same three lines and the same reason at
	// both, and the terminated finish has one edge and rewinds instead.
	//
	// Called BEFORE the edges' branches are emitted: `reconcileMovesAtMerge` appends each decref to the
	// edge block that owes it, and that has to land ahead of that block's jump to the continuation.
	function reconcileTryMergeMoves(okBlock IrBlock, errEnd IrBlock, entryMoveMark MoveMark, errMoveMark MoveMark, tryTok Token)
		var moveEdges = ReachingMoveEdgeArray.create()
		moveEdges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(okBlock, mark: entryMoveMark))
		moveEdges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(errEnd, mark: errMoveMark))
		self.reconcileMovesAtMerge(moveEdges, anchor: tryTok)
	end 'reconcileTryMergeMoves'

	// The error handler TERMINATED (propagate / return / break / continue / throw / panic): only the ok
	// block reaches the continuation. Restore the carried vars AND the drop-gating move state to their
	// pre-try values (neither the error path's reassignments nor its MOVES reach here — it dropped its own
	// live values on the way out) and continue in the ok block.
	function finishTerminatedTry(okBlock IrBlock, entryVals ValueIdArray, entryMoveMark MoveMark, result ValueId) returns ValueId
		self.restoreBindings(self.mutableVars, snapshot: entryVals)
		self.restoreMoveMark(entryMoveMark)
		self.currentBlock = okBlock

		// ⭐ **RE-ENROL A MANAGED RESULT AS AN OWNED TEMP ON THE OK PATH.** The error handler terminated, so
		// only `okBlock` continues, and here the managed result is a live owned box — exactly a plain managed
		// call result. `desugarTry` pulled it off the entry drop set (it is NULL on the dead error edge); put
		// it back so the ok continuation binds it (`let s = …` → `parseBinding` moves it to a scope-exit drop)
		// or drops it (a discarded `try f()` → the statement drain), on the ok path only. The owned-heap bit
		// survived the removal, so only the pending enrolment is restored; a scalar result owns no heap and is
		// re-enrolled in nothing.
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) 'reenrollManagedResult'
			self.pendingTempDrops.push(result)
		end 'reenrollManagedResult'

		return result
	end 'finishTerminatedTry'

	// `otherwise <expr>` — both edges reach a fresh continuation carrying a result phi of `result` (ok)
	// and `otherwiseValue` (err). No carried-var phis: an `otherwise` VALUE is an expression, which
	// assigns no binding, so the ok and err edges agree on every carried var.
	function finishValueTry(func IrFunction, okBlock IrBlock, errEnd IrBlock, result ValueId, otherwiseValue ValueId, otherwiseBlame ByteArray, entryMoveMark MoveMark, errMoveMark MoveMark, tryTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// CONSUMER GUARD for the void-result invariant OPEN #55 fixed at the producer: a void `try`-result
		// has no width, so a value merge is ill-formed and `mintPhi` below would build a void phi block-arg
		// lowering can't type. `parseUnboundOtherwiseForm`'s value-form guard already rejects it (E3059) —
		// it is the ONE `TryHandler.value` producer and runs strictly before here — so this can only trip on
		// a FUTURE value-handler producer that forgets the check, and it names that invariant rather than
		// deferring the crash to the confusing `valueTagToStdType` lowering panic it used to be.
		if self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.void 'voidResultPhi'
			panic("finishValueTry: a void try-result reached the value merge — parseUnboundOtherwiseForm's value-form guard (OPEN #55) must reject `try <voidCall> otherwise <value>` before here")
		end 'voidResultPhi'

		// ⭐ **REJECT AN OPAQUE-`T` TRY-MERGE THAT WOULD NEED A DESCRIPTOR-GATED INCREF/COPY (P1.7 slice
		// 3b-vi-a).** The owned reconciliation below assumes a CONCRETE managed pointer: it increfs the borrowed
		// OK result (`__mm_incref`), copies a borrowed String fallback (`promoteToOwnedString`), or increfs an
		// aliased owned fallback (`transferFallbackToPhi`). For an OPAQUE `typeParameter` element every one of
		// those is WRONG — a trivial instantiation's word is a RAW SCALAR a plain `__mm_incref` faults on
		// (access violation), and a managed one needs the enclosing instance's descriptor to pick the retain. So
		// a `try <opaqueArr>.get/first/last(…) otherwise <owned>` (a borrow merged with an owned fallback) or a
		// `try <opaqueArr>.pop/remove(…) otherwise <value>` (an owned move-out merged with a fallback value) is a
		// clean E2015 until a descriptor-gated reconciliation lands (a later slice). A DIVERGING otherwise
		// (`otherwise return`/`throw`/`panic`) routes to `finishTerminatedTry` and never reaches here — that plain
		// borrow / move-out is exactly what this slice supports. Only the opaque array accessors produce a
		// `typeParameter` try-result at this slice (a function cannot yet return an opaque `T`), so the tag is the
		// whole test; a value fallback that must be reconciled is signalled by either side owning heap.
		if self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter and (self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) or self.valueIsOwnedHeap(otherwiseValue)) 'opaqueTryMerge'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a `try` on an opaque type-parameter array accessor (`get`/`first`/`last`/`pop`/`remove` on an `Array with <type parameter>` field) with a VALUE `otherwise <expr>` — reconciling the borrowed/moved-out element with the fallback at the `try` continuation needs a descriptor-gated incref/copy the shared body cannot pick statically (the element is a raw scalar for a trivial instantiation and a managed pointer for another), a distinct future slice. Use a DIVERGING `otherwise` (`otherwise return`/`throw`/`panic`) instead — the plain borrow (`get`/`first`/`last`) and move-out (`pop`/`remove`) are supported that way (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-a).", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'opaqueTryMerge'

		try self.refuseExistentialValueMerge(self.tagOf(result), what: "the result of a `try … otherwise <expr>`", tok: tryTok)

		var cont = self.newBlock(func, label: b"trycont", line: tryTok.line)
		let phiId = self.mintPhi(cont, tag: self.tagOf(result), nameId: try self.valueNameIds.get(result) otherwise panic("finishValueTry: result has no name column"))

		// ⭐ E3070: a `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise …` binds THIS phi, not the accessor's result, so an
		// element borrow riding the ok edge has to move onto it or the binding would claim nothing. Done
		// where the phi is minted — the one place both values are in hand (`retargetPendingBorrow`).
		self.retargetPendingBorrow(func, from: result, to: phiId)

		// ⭐ **A MANAGED RESULT PHI IS OWNED — reconcile both edges to owned before wiring them in.** When the
		// try-result owns heap, the phi is the sole owner of whichever value reaches the merge and is dropped
		// once at its scope exit, so each edge's contribution must transfer ONE owned reference IN: the ok
		// `result` was already pulled off the entry drop set (`desugarTry`), and the err `otherwiseValue` is
		// reconciled here. `parseUnboundOtherwiseForm` already refused a fallback whose TYPE could not merge.
		// A SCALAR result keeps the borrowed phi the Wave-1 path built — nothing is owned, nothing is dropped.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE TWO KINDS OF FALLBACK ARE RECONCILED BY DIFFERENT DOORS, AND THE SPLIT IS WHAT KEEPS THE
		// COUNT AT ONE (S5).** A BORROWED fallback is minted a reference of its own by
		// `promoteBorrowedMergeEdge` — the SAME helper a `match`/ternary arm's borrowed give goes through, which
		// is where the steps that keeps the count at one (and the reason running `transferFallbackToPhi` on top
		// would leak) are written down. An ALREADY-OWNED fallback has an existing owner to settle with instead,
		// which is `transferFallbackToPhi`'s job (move a temp, incref a live binding). The error edge IS
		// `currentBlock` here, which is the block the promotion must land in.
		var errEdgeValue = otherwiseValue
		var phiOwned = false
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) 'managedResultPhi'
			if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(otherwiseValue) 'promoteBorrowedFallback'
				errEdgeValue = try self.promoteBorrowedMergeEdge(otherwiseValue, edgeBlock: self.currentBlock, anchor: tryTok)
			end 'promoteBorrowedFallback' else 'ownedFallback'
				self.transferFallbackToPhi(errEdgeValue, tryTok: tryTok)
			end 'ownedFallback'
			phiOwned = true
		end 'managedResultPhi' else 'borrowResult'
			// ⭐ **A BORROW try-result merged with an OWNED fallback** (`try arr.get(i) otherwise Item.create(…)`,
			// P1.7 slice 3). A managed-array `get`/`first`/`last` yields a BORROW — the array owns the element
			// and its `__managed_decref` walk drops it — while the `otherwise` fallback is a FRESH OWNED box. The
			// two edges merge into ONE binding, which has ONE drop discipline, so they MUST agree on ownership.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE OK BORROW IS INCREF'D SO THE PHI IS OWNED — do NOT compensate for the fallback with an
			// error-edge drop.** Making the phi a borrow (never dropped) leaks the owned fallback; the earlier
			// cure — `emitDecref(otherwiseValue)` on the error edge — freed the fallback BEFORE the merged
			// binding was read, a use-after-free the non-reclaiming bump allocator (#108) hid until a poisoning
			// `__mm_free` exposed it (four `array-slots` reads came back `0x3F3F…`). Instead the ok edge takes a
			// SECOND owner of the borrowed element with `__mm_incref`, so the phi owns a reference on BOTH edges
			// and is dropped exactly ONCE at the binding's scope exit — after the last read, on every path.
			// incref and every managed drop (`__mm_decref`/`__str_decref`/`__managed_decref`/`__destruct_*`) share the
			// one header-refcount protocol (`emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner`), so the array's own decref walk and
			// this scope-exit drop balance regardless of which runs last: the element ends at refcount 0 with
			// exactly one free. The incref goes into `okBlock` (single-threaded, before its branch to `cont`);
			// `currentBlock` is `errEnd` and is restored after.
			//
			// The ERROR edge owes the phi a reference too, by the SAME rule: `transferFallbackToPhi` moves an
			// owned temp or increfs an aliased binding, so a `try arr.get(i) otherwise fallbackBinding` (the
			// binding read again after the try) no longer double-frees the box the phi and the binding share.
			//
			// A SCALAR result with a scalar fallback owns nothing on either edge, so the guard is false and the
			// Wave-1 borrowed phi stands — nothing incref'd, nothing dropped.
			if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(otherwiseValue) 'ownedFallbackIntoBorrow'
				let resumeBlock = self.currentBlock
				self.currentBlock = okBlock
				self.emitIncref(result, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tryTok))
				self.currentBlock = resumeBlock

				self.transferFallbackToPhi(otherwiseValue, tryTok: tryTok)
				phiOwned = true
			end 'ownedFallbackIntoBorrow'
		end 'borrowResult'

		// ⭐ **THE FALLBACK EXPRESSION'S ABANDONED TEMPORARIES DIE ON THE ERROR EDGE — the `otherwise <value>`
		// region, the one fork this rung's per-region drop list omitted.** The fallback runs on the error edge
		// ALONE, so a temporary it built and did not hand back (`[1,2]` in `otherwise [1,2].count()`) is exactly a
		// ternary/match arm's abandoned temp, one construct over: releasing it at the shared statement tail —
		// which sits in `cont`, reached from the ok edge too — releases a value that edge never built, the
		// register allocator's "a use dominates its def" (a compiler panic on a valid program). `currentBlock` is
		// `errEnd` (or the block the fallback left control in), which dominates the err→`cont` edge, so the drops
		// ride the single-threaded error path. The floor is `WholeStatementTempFloor`: `desugarTry` cleared the
		// pending list at the fork, so everything enrolled since is the fallback's own, and the fallback VALUE was
		// pulled out just above (into the phi) — the phi is enrolled AFTER this drain so it is not swept here.
		//
		// ⚠ SKIPPED when the phi is a BORROW of a still-live source (`phiOwned` false AND the fallback is a
		// managed pointer): such a fallback can BORROW into one of these temporaries (`otherwise makeX().field`),
		// and dropping the temporary would free what the phi reads. That shape needs the source incref'd, not
		// dropped — a narrower case left for its own change; draining here would trade the pre-existing panic for
		// a use-after-free. A scalar fallback borrows nothing, so the common `otherwise <expr>.count()` is safe.
		if phiOwned or not self.valueIsManagedHeap(otherwiseValue) 'dropFallbackTemps'
			self.drainPendingTemps(WholeStatementTempFloor, anchor: tryTok)
		end 'dropFallbackTemps'

		// ⭐ **THE TWO EDGE VALUES THAT REACH `cont`, BUILT ONCE FOR BOTH READERS.** The exclusivity join and
		// `recordMergeAliasProof` below ask DIFFERENT questions of the SAME pair, and both only iterate it, so
		// one list serves both — the comment used to say "the same pair `recordMergeAliasProof` reads below"
		// while each site built its own, which is one `ValueIdArray` per value-`try` merge for nothing. Hoisted
		// above the `phiOwned` gate deliberately: the alias reader is ungated, so the list is needed on every
		// path anyway and the branch would only decide WHICH site paid for it.
		let contEdges = pairArgs(result, second: errEdgeValue)

		// The phi is only as strong as its weakest path. Both branches above already downgraded what they
		// increfed (`emitIncref` marks its operand co-owned), so this reads the state they left rather than
		// re-deriving which branch ran.
		if phiOwned 'enrolPhi'
			self.trackOwnedTemp(phiId, exclusivity: self.mergedHeapExclusivity(contEdges))
		end 'enrolPhi'

		// ⭐ **THE THIRD MERGE'S SHARE OF THE E3019 BLAME** — `finalizeMatchMerge`'s rule, one construct over,
		// ungated for its measured reason: a fallback naming an immutable binding is refused whatever the phi's
		// ownership turns out to be, because the oracle refuses both shapes.
		self.recordMergeBlame(phiId, blamed: otherwiseBlame)

		// A VALUE fallback is an expression, but an expression can still CONSUME (`otherwise takeIt(s)`), so
		// the two edges are reconciled here for the same reason the fell-through form reconciles.
		self.reconcileTryMergeMoves(okBlock, errEnd: errEnd, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, errMoveMark: errMoveMark, tryTok: tryTok)

		var okArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		okArgs.push(result)
		self.recordBranchEdge(okBlock, targetId: cont.id, argIds: okArgs)
		self.emitBranch(okBlock, targetId: cont.id)

		var errArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		errArgs.push(errEdgeValue)
		self.recordBranchEdge(errEnd, targetId: cont.id, argIds: errArgs)
		self.emitBranch(errEnd, targetId: cont.id)

		// ⭐ **BOTH EDGES ARE FINAL HERE, SO THE MERGE MAY ASK FOR ITS ALIAS CLAIM BACK (G14).** The ok edge is
		// the callee's result, guarded by the callee's own ranged `return`; the err edge is the fallback, which
		// proves the range when it is an in-range literal or a value already denoting one, and proves nothing
		// when it is neither. `recordMergeAliasProof` decides from the values rather than from the shape, which
		// is what keeps `otherwise 0` eliding while `otherwise <wider var>` no longer does.
		self.recordMergeAliasProof(phiId, incoming: contEdges)

		// ⭐ **AND THE BUFFER-SURFACE FAMILY — THE THIRD MERGE'S SHARE OF IT (W192 second review).** Asked of
		// `contEdges` for `recordMergeAliasProof`'s stated reason one line up: both edges are final here, and
		// the err edge in particular is only settled after the ownership reconciliation above. MEASURED as the
		// route this closes: `try managed.slice(0, 99) otherwise managed` inside an `extension Vector` cleared
		// the vector's own record through the phi.
		self.propagateBufferSurfaceToPhi(phiId, gives: contEdges)

		self.currentBlock = cont
		return phiId
	end 'finishValueTry'

	// ⭐ **HAND THE MERGE PHI EXACTLY ONE OWNED REFERENCE FROM THE ERROR EDGE.** `finishValueTry` drops the
	// phi once at the binding's scope exit, so the value flowing in on the error edge must give the phi a
	// reference it can own outright — and the two fallback shapes owe that reference differently:
	//   • an unconsumed owned TEMPORARY (`otherwise Slot.create(…)`) already owns its one reference and is
	//     handed off — MOVE it into the phi by dropping it from the pending list; nothing else drops it.
	//   • a live owned BINDING or consumed param (`otherwise fallback`, aliased and read again after the
	//     `try`) keeps its OWN reference and drops it at its own scope exit — so the phi needs a SECOND
	//     reference, taken with `__mm_incref` on the error edge (`currentBlock` is `errEnd` at both callers).
	// Getting it backwards is a wrong answer either way: increfing a temp LEAKS (its extra reference is
	// dropped by nobody — no binding owns it), moving a binding DOUBLE-FREES (the phi and the binding each
	// drop the one box, the leak count goes negative → exit 101). `pendingTempsContain` is the discriminator:
	// the two owned homes are disjoint by construction (a transfer to a binding calls `removeFromPendingTemps`
	// first), so a value still in the pending list is owned by no binding. This is the error-edge twin of the
	// ok-edge borrow incref above.
	function transferFallbackToPhi(errEdgeValue ValueId, tryTok Token)
		if self.pendingTempsContain(errEdgeValue) 'movedTemp'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(errEdgeValue)
		end 'movedTemp' else 'aliasedBinding'
			self.emitIncref(errEdgeValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tryTok))
		end 'aliasedBinding'
	end 'transferFallbackToPhi'

	// `otherwise ignore` or a block handler that FELL THROUGH — both edges reach a fresh continuation
	// with no try-value. Merge the carried vars a block handler may have reassigned (`mergeAtContinuation`
	// mints a phi only where the two edges differ). The returned `result` is the discarded statement value.
	function finishFellThroughTry(func IrFunction, okBlock IrBlock, errEnd IrBlock, entryVals ValueIdArray, result ValueId, resultUsed bool, entryMoveMark MoveMark, errMoveMark MoveMark, tryTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **A FELL-THROUGH HANDLER CARRIES NO VALUE, SO IT CANNOT STAND IN A VALUE POSITION.** `otherwise
		// ignore` and a handler block that runs off its end both catch the error and produce nothing, so the
		// error edge reaches the merge with no value while a value position needs one on BOTH edges — and for
		// a managed result the ok edge is about to DROP that result below, so binding it would read freed heap
		// (a leak/UAF, not merely an undefined scalar). Rejected HERE, the ONE point at which both spellings
		// have collapsed to the `fellThrough` signal, so the rule is stated once rather than at each spelling's
		// parse site (`otherwise ignore` used to reject itself, and a fall-through block went unchecked — the
		// two drifted, and the block case leaked).
		if resultUsed 'valuelessHandlerInValue'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("a `try` used for its value needs a value on the error path too, but this `otherwise` handler catches the error without producing one (`otherwise ignore`, or a handler block that runs off its end) — give it a fallback value with `otherwise <expr>`, or make every path of the handler terminate (`return`/`throw`/`break`/`continue`)", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'valuelessHandlerInValue'

		var cont = self.newBlock(func, label: b"trycont", line: tryTok.line)
		let errVals = self.snapshotBindings(self.mutableVars)

		// A block handler is statements, so it can move an outer owned binding (`otherwise (e) 'h'
		// consume(s) end 'h'`) where the ok edge left it live — reconciled across the two edges before
		// either branches. See `reconcileTryMergeMoves`.
		self.reconcileTryMergeMoves(okBlock, errEnd: errEnd, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, errMoveMark: errMoveMark, tryTok: tryTok)

		self.mergeAtContinuation(okBlock, predB: errEnd, cont: cont, bindings: self.mutableVars, valsA: entryVals, valsB: errVals)

		// ⭐ **A DISCARDED MANAGED RESULT DROPS ON THE OK EDGE, IN `okBlock`.** A fell-through handler
		// (`otherwise ignore`, or a block that ran off its end) always discards the try's value, and a managed
		// result is a live owned box on the ok edge but NULL on the error edge. Its drop goes into `okBlock`,
		// BEFORE its branch to the shared `cont` — never in `cont` (reached by both edges, so a drop there
		// would decref the NULL error-edge register) and never on the error edge. `desugarTry` already pulled
		// it off the pending list, so the post-statement drain never sees it and this is its only drop.
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) 'dropManagedResultOnOk'
			self.currentBlock = okBlock
			self.emitDecref(result, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tryTok))
		end 'dropManagedResultOnOk'

		self.emitBranch(okBlock, targetId: cont.id)
		self.emitBranch(errEnd, targetId: cont.id)
		self.currentBlock = cont
		return result
	end 'finishFellThroughTry'

	// Parse the `otherwise` handler (or its absence — a PROPAGATE) into the current (error) block, and
	// report how it affects the merge. On entry `currentBlock` is the error block.
	//
	// `propagates` is the absence of an `otherwise`, read by `desugarTry` off the same cursor position this
	// routine would have read it at and handed down rather than asked twice — it decides the ok edge's
	// reachability there (G18) and the handler's shape here, and those two must be one answer.
	function parseOtherwiseHandler(func IrFunction, result ValueId, flag ValueId, target TryTarget, resultUsed bool, propagates bool, tryTok Token) returns TryHandler throws ParseError
		if propagates 'propagate'
			// Uncaught `try f()` / `try await p` — re-publish the callee's flag to this function's caller. The
			// flag is re-emitted VERBATIM, so the propagated error type MUST match this function's `throws`
			// type or the caller decodes one enum's ordinals as another's (E3059, checked here).
			try self.checkPropagateType(func, throwsClause: self.tryTargetThrows(target), tryTok: tryTok)
			self.emitScopeDrops(self.currentBlock, floor: WholeFunctionScopeFloor, anchor: tryTok)
			self.artifact.emitTerminator(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.propagateError(flag), range: SourceRange.ofToken(tryTok))
			return TryHandler.terminated
		end 'propagate'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.otherwise)

		// Block forms own their caught box end-to-end (`parseOtherwiseBlock` enrols, binds and drops it).
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'bindingForm'
			return try self.parseOtherwiseBinding(func, flag: flag, errorType: self.tryErrorEnumType(self.tryTargetThrows(target)), form: CaughtErrorForm.singleCall)
		end 'bindingForm'

		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'blockForm'
			return try self.parseOtherwiseBlock(func, bindingName: EmptySourceText, hasBinding: false, flag: flag, errorType: self.tryErrorEnumType(self.tryTargetThrows(target)), form: CaughtErrorForm.singleCall)
		end 'blockForm'

		// ⭐ **THE NON-BLOCK FORMS DISCARD THE CAUGHT ERROR, SO ITS BOX MUST DROP HERE.** `otherwise ignore`,
		// a fallback VALUE, and the single-statement `return`/`break`/`continue`/`throw <fresh>` each catch a
		// boxed error and then throw it away — so its box, owned by this try, drops exactly once on the error
		// edge. Enrol it (ANONYMOUS — these forms name no `e`) so a terminating form's own `emitScopeDrops`
		// releases it, then drop it here for the non-terminating forms (`dropArmScopedOwned` frees it on a live
		// exit, drains it either way). A SCALAR error owns no box, so `boxed` is false and this is inert — the
		// existing scalar forms are untouched. (`otherwise panic` aborts the process before any free matters.)
		let eType = self.tryErrorEnumType(self.tryTargetThrows(target))
		let ownedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()
		if self.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion(eType) 'enrolBox'
			self.enrollCaughtBox(flag, eType: eType, binding: VarInfo.createValueBinding(EmptySourceText, varType: eType, mutable: false, boundValue: flag))
		end 'enrolBox'

		let handler = try self.parseUnboundOtherwiseForm(func, result: result, resultUsed: resultUsed, tryTok: tryTok)
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(ownedFloor, exitBlock: self.currentBlock, anchor: tryTok)
		return handler
	end 'parseOtherwiseHandler'

	// The `otherwise` forms that bind no `e` and open no block: `ignore`, a single-statement `return`/`break`/
	// `continue`/`throw`/`panic`, or a fallback VALUE. Split out of `parseOtherwiseHandler` so its caller can
	// bracket them with the caught-box enrol/drop every one of them shares — each DISCARDS the caught error.
	// Returns how the form affects the merge; on entry `currentBlock` is the error block.
	function parseUnboundOtherwiseForm(func IrFunction, result ValueId, resultUsed bool, tryTok Token) returns TryHandler throws ParseError
		// `otherwise ignore` catches the error and discards the value — a fell-through (statement) form. Its
		// use in a value position is refused uniformly with a fall-through block, at the one `fellThrough`
		// gate in `finishFellThroughTry`; here it only reports HOW the merge behaves.
		if self.at(TokenKind.ignore) 'ignoreForm'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.ignore)
			return TryHandler.fellThrough
		end 'ignoreForm'

		// The single-statement CONTROL-FLOW forms terminate the error block; their own scope drops release
		// the caught box the caller enrolled.
		if self.at(TokenKind.return) or self.at(TokenKind.break) or self.at(TokenKind.continue) 'controlFlow'
			_ = try self.parseStatement(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			return TryHandler.terminated
		end 'controlFlow'
		if self.at(TokenKind.throw) 'rethrow'
			try self.parseThrowStatement(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			return TryHandler.terminated
		end 'rethrow'
		if self.at(TokenKind.panic) 'panicForm'
			try self.parsePanicStatement(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			return TryHandler.terminated
		end 'panicForm'

		// `otherwise <expr>` — a fallback VALUE that merges with the success value.
		//
		// A void `try` (its call returns nothing) has no success value for a fallback VALUE to stand in
		// for, so `otherwise <expr>` is ill-formed here — `finishValueTry` would mint a void phi that
		// lowering cannot type (OPEN #55). The valueless forms a void `try` DOES take (`ignore`, a block,
		// a single-statement return/break/continue/throw/panic) are all handled above, so reaching here
		// means a genuine value. This is the statement-position twin of `parseTry`'s value-position void
		// reject; both are E3059. (A value-position void `try` is rejected before the handler parses, so
		// `result` is void here only in statement position.)
		if self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.void 'voidTryOtherwiseValue'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("a void `try` (its call returns nothing) cannot take an `otherwise <value>` fallback — the success path yields no value for the fallback to stand in for; use `otherwise ignore`, an `otherwise 'block' … end`, or a single-statement `otherwise return`/`throw`", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'voidTryOtherwiseValue'

		let otherwiseTok = self.current()
		// The token index the fallback expression starts at, kept so the range check below can ask what
		// the expression parser ACTUALLY consumed — see `requireOtherwiseInRangedReturn`.
		let otherwiseStart = self.pos
		let otherwiseValue = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.requireOtherwiseInRangedReturn(result, from: otherwiseStart, to: self.pos, tryTok: tryTok)

		// ⭐ **MERGE (P1.5-A2b): a capturing closure may not be an `otherwise <value>` fallback** — it merges
		// with the try's success value through one result phi (the code pointer alone), and the env rides a
		// side column a phi cannot carry, so on the error edge the merged closure would be called with env=0
		// and nil-deref. The same merge hazard as a match-expression arm, refused through the same E3099 gate.
		// (The try's SUCCESS value cannot itself be a capturing closure — a function may not RETURN one, so the
		// callee is refused at its own `return` — leaving only the fallback to guard here.)
		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(otherwiseValue, message: "cannot use a closure that captures as the value of an `otherwise` fallback: {CapturingClosureMergeSuffix}", tok: otherwiseTok)

		if resultUsed and tagIsFloat(self.tagOf(otherwiseValue)) != tagIsFloat(self.tagOf(result)) 'typeMismatch'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("otherwise type '{typeTagName(self.tagOf(otherwiseValue))}' does not match expected type '{typeTagName(self.tagOf(result))}'", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'typeMismatch'

		// ⭐ OPEN #54/#59: an `otherwise` fallback of the WRONG named aggregate — a DIFFERENT struct/union,
		// OR an aggregate where the try-result is a SCALAR (or the reverse) — merges into the try's owned
		// result phi and is dropped under the RESULT's destructor: a wild free / leak the float check above
		// cannot see (two structs share the `structRef` tag; a boxed union and a ranged-int alias share
		// `named`). Runs regardless of `resultUsed`: the phi is dropped on the discard path too (see the
		// managed-result handling below). Identity is the interned name, EXACT. Un-gated — both names are
		// computed unconditionally, empty for a scalar with no allocation — and `displayTypeNameOf` prints
		// the scalar side's primitive, not an empty name.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TWO NAMES DECIDE THE VERDICT AND DO NOT SPELL IT** (user ruling, 2026-08-04) — the same
		// note the return and reassignment doors carry. MEASURED before this voice was converted:
		// `otherwise type 'Array_String' does not match expected type 'Array_Integer'` on a program whose
		// author wrote `Strs` and `Ints`.
		let resultAggregate = self.aggregateNameOf(self.tagOf(result), nameId: self.valueNameId(result))
		let fallbackAggregate = self.aggregateNameOf(self.tagOf(otherwiseValue), nameId: self.valueNameId(otherwiseValue))
		if self.signatures.aggregatesConflict(resultAggregate, expectedTag: self.tagOf(result), expectedNameId: self.valueNameId(result), got: fallbackAggregate, gotTag: self.tagOf(otherwiseValue), gotNameId: self.valueNameId(otherwiseValue)) 'aggregateOtherwiseMismatch'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("otherwise type '{self.valueDisplayTypeName(otherwiseValue)}' does not match expected type '{self.valueDisplayTypeName(result)}'", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'aggregateOtherwiseMismatch'

		// ⭐ **A MANAGED try-result and its `otherwise` fallback merge through an OWNED result phi**
		// (`finishValueTry`), dropped once at its scope exit — so both edges must contribute an OWNED value
		// of the SAME managed type. The TAG must therefore agree: a `String` result with an `int` fallback
		// would have the phi's drop decref an integer as a String pointer, a wild free. Refused HERE, where
		// the fallback's type is in hand, and regardless of `resultUsed` (the phi is dropped on the discard
		// path too).
		//
		// ⭐ **A BORROWED fallback is PROMOTED at the merge, not refused (S5).** It used to be a second
		// refusal here, worded *"consuming a borrowed aggregate arrives with cross-call consume"* — the same
		// premise `emitOwnedValueReturn` and `promoteBorrowedGive` were refusing on, and the same one that
		// was false: a borrowed String COPIES and a borrowed struct/union INCREFS, either way giving the phi
		// one owned reference of its own (`finishValueTry` → `promoteBorrowedToOwned`). The value oracle runs
		// the program this refused (`try risky() otherwise <borrowed Item param>`, exit 12).
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) and self.tagOf(otherwiseValue) != self.tagOf(result) 'managedOtherwiseTypeMismatch'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("otherwise type '{typeTagName(self.tagOf(otherwiseValue))}' does not match expected type '{typeTagName(self.tagOf(result))}'", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'managedOtherwiseTypeMismatch'

		return TryHandler.value(otherwiseValue, blame: self.expressionImmutableBlame(otherwiseTok, valueStart: otherwiseStart, value: otherwiseValue))
	end 'parseUnboundOtherwiseForm'

	// ⭐ **AN `otherwise` FALLBACK LITERAL MUST LIE INSIDE THE TRY RESULT'S RANGED TYPE.** The fallback
	// merges with the success value through one result phi typed by the callee's return type, so an
	// out-of-range default binds a value wearing a type whose declared range excludes it — the one
	// invariant a ranged type exists to carry, broken on exactly the path the author wrote the fallback
	// for. Refused at compile time, as an out-of-range literal is at a cast and at a ranged `return`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT READS THE TOKENS THE EXPRESSION PARSER ACTUALLY CONSUMED, which is why it takes a SPAN and
	// does not peek ahead.** "Was this fallback a plain integer literal?" is a question about what was
	// PARSED, and only `parseExpression` knows where its expression ended; a look-ahead here would be a
	// second reading of the grammar, free to disagree with the first about where a fallback stops. The
	// literal's text becomes a number through `intLiteralValue`, the one decoder every literal uses.
	//
	// A non-literal fallback — a call, an arithmetic expression, a variable — is deliberately NOT
	// checked, matching the reference oracle (2-Parser.cs:9833, which requires a literal too). shv2 has
	// no compile-time value for one here, and no guard site to hang a runtime check on: the pass that
	// emits those (`InsertRangeChecks`) guards CASTS and RETURNS, and a fallback is neither.
	function requireOtherwiseInRangedReturn(result ValueId, from TokenIndex, to TokenIndex, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.declaredAliasOfValue(result) 'a'
			undeclared then return
			declared(alias) then try self.requireOtherwiseLiteralInRange(alias, from: from, to: to, tryTok: tryTok)
		end 'a'
	end 'requireOtherwiseInRangedReturn'

	// The half above that has an alias in hand. A FLOAT alias is skipped: its bounds are IEEE-754 bit
	// patterns and the span this reads is an INTEGER literal, so the comparison would be between two
	// things that were never the same kind of number — the identical domain guard
	// `reportConstCastIfOutOfRange` makes, for the identical reason.
	function requireOtherwiseLiteralInRange(alias RangedTypeAlias, from TokenIndex, to TokenIndex, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'floatAlias'
			return
		end 'floatAlias'

		let negated = self.kindAt(from) == TokenKind.minus
		// ⚠ **THE PARENTHESES ARE LOAD-BEARING.** The bootstrap that compiles this file binds
		// `a + b if c else d` as `a + (b if c else d)` — the same trap `SplitLiveRanges.emitSpillAt`
		// documents — so without them this reads `from + (1 if negated else from)`, i.e. `2 * from` on
		// the non-negated arm. MEASURED before the fix: `otherwise -1` was refused and `otherwise 101`
		// compiled clean and returned the success value, because the index landed on an unrelated token
		// and the guard below bailed. The one arm the pinned case exercises was the one that worked.
		let literalIndex = (from + 1) if negated else from

		// The span must be the literal and NOTHING else — one token, or a `-` and one token.
		if literalIndex + 1 != to or self.kindAt(literalIndex) != TokenKind.intLiteral 'notAPlainLiteral'
			return
		end 'notAPlainLiteral'

		let value = try self.intLiteralValue(self.tokenAt(literalIndex), negated: negated)
		if literalInRange(value, low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: false) 'inRange'
			return
		end 'inRange'

		throw ParseError.otherwiseOutsideRangedReturn(value, aliasName: String.from(alias.name), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
	end 'requireOtherwiseLiteralInRange'

	// `otherwise (e) 'label' … end 'label'` — bind the caught error, then parse the labelled handler block.
	// In a match arm this block form is E2049. What `e` BINDS is `declareCaughtError`'s three-way answer, not
	// this routine's: a scalar enum's decoded ordinal, a boxed union's pointer, or — for a block whose body
	// routed two or more error types — the raw flag under a synthesized error union.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE ERROR TYPE AND THE FORM, NOT THE `TryTarget` IT USED TO DERIVE THEM FROM**, because a
	// block-form `try 'l' … end 'l' otherwise (e) 'h' … end 'h'` has NO single target: its handler is shared
	// by every routed edge in the body. For a ONE-member block the type is what those edges agreed on
	// (`TryBlockContext.throwsClause`); for a multi-member one there is no single enum at all, and `form`
	// carries the synthesized union that replaces it. The single-call form still asks `tryTargetThrows` — one
	// line up, at its own caller — so this routine keeps one shape and the two constructs reach it two ways.
	function parseOtherwiseBinding(func IrFunction, flag ValueId, errorType MaxonType, form CaughtErrorForm) returns TryHandler throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let eNameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		// ⛔ **A CAUGHT ERROR IS A DECLARATION, SO IT ASKS THE DECLARATION DOOR** — for the reason
		// `requireUnreservedName`'s header gives at length, and this is one of the two sites that proved it:
		// before this call, `try f() otherwise (__x) 'h'` declared a `__`-prefixed local and RAN (measured,
		// exit 42) where the oracle refuses it E2051. `self` was never reachable here — `consume(identifier)`
		// refuses the keyword first — which is exactly why the hole was invisible to the receiver rule.
		try self.requireUnreservedName(eNameTok, declaredName: eNameTok.value)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		return try self.parseOtherwiseBlock(func, bindingName: eNameTok.value, hasBinding: true, flag: flag, errorType: errorType, form: form)
	end 'parseOtherwiseBinding'

	// ⭐ **ENROL A CAUGHT BOXED ERROR AS OWNED.** The flag IS the box pointer (the throw ENCODE wrote it
	// verbatim), so the binding takes it with NO decode arithmetic — only its type columns are re-recorded to
	// the union (`retypeValue`) so `match e` dispatches through the box (`enumMatchFor`) and the scope-exit
	// drop routes to `__destruct_<U>` (`decrefCalleeFor`), both of which read the value TAG. `markValueOwnsHeap`
	// enrols it so the scope machinery releases it exactly once. `binding` is the NAMED `(e)` binding, or an
	// ANONYMOUS one for a discarding form (a no-binding block, `otherwise ignore`, a fallback value). ONE
	// enrolment shape for every catching form, so they cannot drift on how a transferred box becomes owned.
	//
	// ⚠ **CO-OWNED, BECAUSE THE THROW MAY HAVE RETAINED RATHER THAN MOVED.** `retainThrownField` hands the
	// caller a SECOND reference to a box the thrower's container still holds, and the flag register carries no
	// note of which transfer produced it — the catcher is a different frame and cannot ask. So a `match e` on a
	// caught box retains its payload; claiming `sole` here nulled a slot the thrower's container went on
	// reading, MEASURED as a segfault. See `OwnedHeapExclusivity`.
	function enrollCaughtBox(flag ValueId, eType MaxonType, binding VarInfo)
		self.retypeValue(flag, valueType: eType)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(flag, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
		self.ownedBindings.push(binding)
	end 'enrollCaughtBox'

	// ⭐⭐ **RECOVER THE CAUGHT ERROR INTO `currentBlock` — THE ONE DECODE, SHARED BY EVERY CONSTRUCT THAT
	// CATCHES.** `try f() otherwise (e) 'err' … end`, `if try f() 'ok' … end 'ok' else (e) 'err' … end` and a
	// block-form `try`'s handler are one operation reached by three grammars: the same flag, caught the same
	// way. THREE shapes to undo — the third is `form`'s, and it is not a decode at all:
	//   • a SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION (a block whose body routed two or more error types) — the flag is an
	//     `ordinal + bias` on some incoming edges and a box POINTER on others, so there is no ONE decode and
	//     no ONE type; `e` takes the flag untyped and the arms discriminate (`declareErrorUnionBinding`).
	// The other two are the error-flag ABI's own pair (`throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion`, the fact the throw ENCODE
	// turned on):
	//   • a BOXED union — the flag IS the box pointer, written verbatim by the throw, so the binding takes it
	//     with NO arithmetic and only its type columns move (`enrollCaughtBox`).
	//   • a SCALAR enum — `e = flag - ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, the exact inverse of the Std-tier throw ENCODE
	//     (`lowerThrowError`). Both read the one `ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, so a change to the bias cannot leave
	//     this decode silently binding `match e` to the wrong case.
	// A BOXED error is enrolled owned whether or not it is named — ANONYMOUS for a binder-less catch — so a
	// transferred box is released exactly once on the edge that leaves the branch; the caller's
	// `dropArmScopedOwned` does the releasing. A SCALAR error owns no heap and enrols nothing, so a
	// binder-less scalar catch emits no code at all.
	//
	// The caller has already opened the scope frame the binding lands in, and owns the matching
	// `ownedFloor` / `popScope` / `dropArmScopedOwned` — this routine only declares.
	function declareCaughtError(flag ValueId, errorType MaxonType, bindingName ByteArray, hasBinding bool, form CaughtErrorForm, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let caughtUnion = caughtErrorUnionMatch(form)
		if errorUnionIsSynthesized(caughtUnion) 'aSynthesizedUnion'
			try self.declareErrorUnionBinding(flag, info: requireErrorUnionInfo(caughtUnion), bindingName: bindingName, hasBinding: hasBinding, anchor: anchor)
			return
		end 'aSynthesizedUnion'

		if self.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion(errorType) 'boxedError'
			var eBinding = VarInfo.createValueBinding(EmptySourceText, varType: errorType, mutable: false, boundValue: flag)
			if hasBinding 'nameBoxedE'
				eBinding = try self.scope.declareValueBinding(bindingName, varType: errorType, mutable: false, value: flag) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(bindingName), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			end 'nameBoxedE'
			self.enrollCaughtBox(flag, eType: errorType, binding: eBinding)
			return
		end 'boxedError'

		if not hasBinding 'scalarUnnamed'
			return
		end 'scalarUnnamed'

		let bias = self.emitLiteral(ErrorFlagOrdinalBias, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		let eValue = self.mintValueOfType(errorType)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.binOp(eValue, lhs: flag, rhs: bias, op: MaxonBinOp.sub), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		_ = try self.scope.declareValueBinding(bindingName, varType: errorType, mutable: false, value: eValue) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(bindingName), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'declareCaughtError'

	// ⭐⭐ **DECLARE A SYNTHESIZED-ERROR-UNION `(e)` BINDING — the THIRD shape of caught error, and the only one
	// that owns nothing.** `e` is bound to the FLAG itself and typed `integer`, exactly as v1 binds it: the
	// flag is an `ordinal + bias` on some incoming edges and a heap box POINTER on others, so there is no one
	// type it could carry and no one drop it could owe. Claiming either would be a measured fault in one
	// direction or the other — marking it owned would decref an ordinal on a scalar member's edge, and giving
	// it a member's `named` type would tell `emitDecref` to run that member's `__destruct_` over a number.
	//
	// ⭐ **SO THE BOX IS RELEASED PER ARM INSTEAD**, where the member IS known — `parseMatchCommon`'s
	// per-arm `enrollCaughtBox`, plus `emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup` for a `default` that names none. The
	// bootstrap reaches the same split (`__union_arm_` bindings and `EmitUnionDefaultCleanupBlock`); v1 avoids
	// the default case only because its box lives in a separate slot the handler's scope drops.
	//
	// The registration is what lets `match e` find the union by the name the author wrote; `parseOtherwiseBlock`
	// pops it at the handler's `end`, so a nested handler's binding cannot be mistaken for this one.
	function declareErrorUnionBinding(flag ValueId, info ErrorUnionInfo, bindingName ByteArray, hasBinding bool, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if not hasBinding 'nothingToBind'
			return
		end 'nothingToBind'

		_ = try self.scope.declareValueBinding(bindingName, varType: MaxonType.integer, mutable: false, value: flag) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(bindingName), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		self.errorUnionBindings.push(ErrorUnionBinding.create(bindingName, flag: flag, info: info))
	end 'declareErrorUnionBinding'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION A MATCH SCRUTINEE NAMES — AND THE TEST IS THE BINDING'S IDENTITY,
	// NOT ITS NAME.** The name is how the record is FOUND; what makes it the union is that the name still
	// resolves, in the scope the `match` stands in, to the very VALUE the handler bound. shv2 permits
	// shadowing, so a name-only test handed `let e = Inner.splat` the error's dispatch and produced a silent
	// wrong answer — see `ErrorUnionInfo`'s header for the measurement. `lookupValue` IS the shadowing rule
	// (innermost declaration wins), so asking it is asking the one authority rather than re-deciding scope
	// here.
	//
	// Walked innermost-first anyway, so a nested handler's `(f)` wins over an enclosing `(e)` of the same
	// name — v1's `lastErrorUnionBindingIndex`, and its reason.
	function errorUnionForScrutinee(name ByteArray) returns ErrorUnionMatch
		if name.isEmpty() 'notABareName'
			return ErrorUnionMatch.notAUnion
		end 'notABareName'

		// An unbound name is not this handler's binding either — it is a name-resolution failure the
		// expression parse is about to report with a position of its own.
		let bound = try self.scope.lookupValue(name) otherwise return ErrorUnionMatch.notAUnion

		var i = self.errorUnionBindings.count()
		while i > 0 'innermostFirst'
			i = i - 1
			let binding = try self.errorUnionBindings.get(i) otherwise panic("errorUnionForScrutinee: errorUnionBindings.get OOB at {i} on a list walked downwards from its own count")
			if binding.name.equals(name) and binding.flag == bound 'found'
				return ErrorUnionMatch.synthesized(binding.info)
			end 'found'
		end 'innermostFirst'
		return ErrorUnionMatch.notAUnion
	end 'errorUnionForScrutinee'

	// Record that the enclosing block-form handler's binding HAS now been matched on (E3084's other half).
	// An empty `tryBindingName` — every context but an open block-form handler — can match nothing.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS KEYED ON THE NAME, WHERE `errorUnionForScrutinee` IS KEYED ON THE BINDING'S IDENTITY — and
	// the asymmetry is deliberate, because the two ask different questions.** The DISPATCH asks *"which value
	// is being discriminated?"*, which a shadow answers differently and must; this rule asks *"did the author
	// write a `match` on this name in this handler?"*, which is syntactic, and both references implement it
	// syntactically (`_tryBlockMatchedBinding` / v1's flag). Tightening it to the identity was MEASURED to
	// refuse a handler whose only `match e` names a shadow — a program the C# oracle compiles and runs — so
	// the strict reading is not the language's. `try-block-shadowed-binding.md` pins both halves.
	function noteTryBlockBindingMatched(name ByteArray)
		if self.tryBindingName.isEmpty() or not self.tryBindingName.equals(name) 'notTheHandlersBinding'
			return
		end 'notTheHandlersBinding'

		self.tryBindingMatched = true
	end 'noteTryBlockBindingMatched'

	// The scrutinee token's name when a `match` names a BARE binding and nothing else — `match e 'k'`, an
	// identifier followed immediately by the block label. Two facts about a block-form handler are asked of
	// that name and neither survives `parseExpression` (which yields the flag phi, an integer like any
	// other): whether the handler discriminated (E3084) and whether the scrutinee is a synthesized union.
	// EMPTY for every other scrutinee shape, which is exactly "this is not that binding".
	function matchScrutineeBareName(index TokenIndex) returns ByteArray
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.identifier or self.kindAt(index + 1) != TokenKind.charLiteral 'notABareBinding'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notABareBinding'

		return self.tokenAt(index).value
	end 'matchScrutineeBareName'

	// Parse an `otherwise` handler BLOCK (`otherwise ['(e)'] 'label' … end 'label'`) into the error block.
	// The caught error is recovered by the shared `declareCaughtError` — the same decode `if try … else (e)`
	// runs — in a fresh scope frame opened before the body and drained after it. Both forms are
	// E2049 in a match arm (the block label is where the arm-body single-statement rule is violated).
	function parseOtherwiseBlock(func IrFunction, bindingName ByteArray, hasBinding bool, flag ValueId, errorType MaxonType, form CaughtErrorForm) returns TryHandler throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'noLabel'
			let tok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.matchMissingBlockId(tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'noLabel'
		let labelTok = self.current()
		if self.inMatchArmBody 'inArm'
			throw ParseError.tryBlockFormInMatchArm(true, line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
		end 'inArm'
		// Consume the block-open label (`parseBlockBody` consumes only the closing `end <label>`, exactly
		// as `parseIfStatement`/`parseWhileStatement` consume their own opening labels).
		try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()

		var errBlock = self.currentBlock

		// The owned-binding height BEFORE the caught box is enrolled — a handler-local drop FLOOR, the try
		// twin of `openBlock`'s `ownedCount` (drained by `dropArmScopedOwned` below). A boxed error is an
		// owned heap value this handler received ownership of; a scalar error owns no heap and adds none.
		let ownedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()
		self.scope.pushScope()

		try self.declareCaughtError(flag, errorType: errorType, bindingName: bindingName, hasBinding: hasBinding, form: form, anchor: labelTok)

		// ⭐ **E3084's BRACKET.** A BLOCK-form handler is reached by every routed edge, so one that never
		// `match`es on its binding has silently swallowed every error the body could raise; the single-call
		// forms catch ONE call's error and are free to ignore it. The question is asked of the handler's own
		// body and nothing else, so the state is saved and restored around exactly that parse — a nested `try`
		// block inside this handler asks its own and gives this one back untouched.
		let savedBindingName = self.tryBindingName
		let savedBindingMatched = self.tryBindingMatched
		self.tryBindingName = tryBlockBindingToMatch(form, bindingName: bindingName, hasBinding: hasBinding)
		self.tryBindingMatched = false
		let unionBindingDepth = self.errorUnionBindings.count()

		let errEnd = try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: errBlock, label: labelTok.value)
		self.scope.popScope()

		let matchedTheBinding = self.tryBindingMatched
		let requiredBindingName = self.tryBindingName
		self.tryBindingName = savedBindingName
		self.tryBindingMatched = savedBindingMatched
		while self.errorUnionBindings.count() > unionBindingDepth 'closeUnionScope'
			_ = try self.errorUnionBindings.pop() otherwise panic("parseOtherwiseBlock: errorUnionBindings.pop failed above the depth this handler recorded")
		end 'closeUnionScope'

		if not requiredBindingName.isEmpty() and not matchedTheBinding 'neverDiscriminated'
			throw ParseError.tryBlockBindingNotMatched(String.from(requiredBindingName), line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
		end 'neverDiscriminated'

		// Release the caught box on whichever edge leaves the handler — a `__destruct_<U>`/decref on a live
		// fall-through (into `errEnd`), the terminating statement's own `emitScopeDrops` on a return/throw —
		// and drain it off the owned list either way, so the function-end drop never sees a handler binding.
		// A no-op when the handler enrolled nothing (a scalar error, or none).
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(ownedFloor, exitBlock: errEnd, anchor: labelTok)

		if errEnd.isTerminated() 'terminated'
			return TryHandler.terminated
		end 'terminated'
		return TryHandler.fellThrough
	end 'parseOtherwiseBlock'

	// ============================================================================
	// The BLOCK-FORM `try` — `try 'l' <statements> end 'l' otherwise (e) 'h' … end 'h'`
	// ============================================================================

	// ⭐⭐ **PARSE A BLOCK-FORM `try`: a body whose calls are BARE, and one handler every one of them
	// branches to.** The single-call form (`parseTry`) puts the `try` on ONE call and forks around it; this
	// form puts the label on a GROUP and lets every throwing call in it share a handler:
	//
	//     entry   → <body stmts>                                       ; each routed call ends its block with
	//                                                                  ;   flag != 0 ? tryblkerr : tryblkok_N
	//     tryblkok_N → <the rest of the body>                          ; the success continuation
	//     tryblkerr(flag, …carried) → <the `otherwise (e)` handler>
	//     tryblkcont ← both edges, where each fell through
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ERROR BLOCK AND ITS PHIS EXIST BEFORE THE BODY IS PARSED**, which is what makes a single-pass
	// SSA parser able to build this at all: a routed call needs the block id to branch to and the phi slots
	// to fill, and it needs them the instant it is emitted. The carried set is read off the TOKENS first —
	// `parseWhileStatement`'s device, for exactly its reason, and the reason its `blockStatementEndIndex` /
	// `assignedBindingsIn` pair is reused verbatim here rather than approximated.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BODY'S CALLS ARE ROUTED AT EMIT TIME, NOT BY A POST-PASS — and that is where shv2 departs
	// from BOTH references.** v1 emits the body normally and afterwards rescans the block range, splitting
	// blocks around each throwing call (`Parser.maxon:13195` `routeTryBlockBody`); the bootstrap routes at the
	// call site but into SLOTS (`__try_block_err_N`, mem2reg'd later). Neither shape survives here: shv2's
	// parser mints SSA values and block args as it goes, so (a) there is no slot for the shared handler to
	// read a flag out of, and (b) a post-pass cannot recover what value each mutable binding held at a call
	// it has already walked past. Routing where the call is emitted is the only point at which BOTH the flag
	// and the live carried values are in hand — see `routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock`.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND E3057 NEEDS NO CHANGE WHATEVER, WHICH IS THE POINT.** Both references suppress their
	// bare-throwing-call diagnostic with the try-block stack itself (`_tryBlockStack.Count > 0` at
	// `2-Parser.cs:23820`). shv2's E3057 is keyed on the OP KIND — `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall` fires on
	// a `call`, never on a `tryCall` — so a routed call is exempt because it IS a `tryCall`, and every call
	// this router declines stays a plain `call` and is reported exactly as before. One gate, no second
	// opinion, and a routing door this construct forgets to reach is a LOUD E3057 rather than a dropped flag.
	function parseTryBlockStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block
		let tryIndex = self.pos
		let tryTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.try)
		let labelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)

		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()
		// A match arm's body must be a single statement (E2049).
		//
		// ⚠ **ANCHORED ON THE `try`, WHERE THE BLOCK-`otherwise` FORM ANCHORS ON ITS LABEL — and the
		// asymmetry is the CANONICAL spec's, not a slip.** `specs/match-statements.md:1010` pins column 10
		// (the `try`) for this form and its sibling pins the label for the other. The arm's body BEGINS at
		// this keyword, so this is where the single-statement rule is broken; an `otherwise` is a
		// continuation of a statement that already started legally, and its label is the first token that
		// could not be there.
		if self.inMatchArmBody 'inArm'
			throw ParseError.tryBlockFormInMatchArm(false, line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'inArm'

		// Decided from the tokens, before anything is emitted — see the header. `continuesAtElse: false`:
		// what follows this construct's `end` is an `otherwise`, not an `else`, and `otherwise` opens a
		// block of its OWN (`otherwiseOpensBlock`) which the scan must not fold into the body's extent.
		let bodyEndIndex = self.blockStatementEndIndex(tryIndex, continuesAtElse: false)
		let carried = self.assignedBindingsIn(tryIndex, endIndex: bodyEndIndex)

		var errBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"tryblkerr", line: tryTok.line)
		// ⚠ **THE FLAG PHI IS MINTED FIRST AND EVERY EDGE PUSHES IT FIRST.** Block args and edge args are
		// POSITIONAL, so this order IS the pairing — the same contract `mintPhiForBinding` states for the
		// existential witness pair, one construct out.
		let flagPhi = self.mintPhi(errBlock, tag: ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		let phiVars = self.mintCarriedPhis(errBlock, carried: carried)

		self.tryBlocks.push(TryBlockContext.create(func, tryTok: tryTok, label: labelTok.value, errBlockId: errBlock.id, flagPhi: flagPhi, phiVars: phiVars, ownedFloor: self.ownedBindings.count()))
		var bodyEnd = try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: block, label: labelTok.value)
		// Guard the token scan against the parse it predicted — `parseWhileStatement`'s guard, and the
		// reason a construct added to `opensBlockAt` is safe to add: a mis-predicted extent is a loud panic
		// rather than a silently lost phi.
		self.assertScanAligned("parseTryBlockStatement", predictedEnd: bodyEndIndex)

		var ctx = try self.tryBlocks.pop() otherwise panic("parseTryBlockStatement: the try-block stack is empty after the body of '{String.from(labelTok.value)}' — it is pushed and popped in this one routine")

		try self.requireRoutableTryBlockBody(ctx, tryTok: tryTok)

		// ⭐ **THE VALUES THE BODY LEAVES, CAPTURED BEFORE THE PHIS ARE INSTALLED.** They are the OK edge's
		// half of the final merge; installing first would overwrite them with the error block's phis.
		let bodyVals = self.snapshotBindings(self.mutableVars)
		// The move state's twin of `bodyVals`, and it is captured for the same reason one statement apart:
		// the body is one of the two paths reaching the construct's merge, and the drop-gating bit has to
		// arrive there from the body rather than from whatever the handler goes on to do.
		let bodyMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()
		self.installCarriedPhis(phiVars)

		let shape = try self.requireTryBlockOtherwise(tryTok)
		// ⚠ Re-fetched by ID rather than reusing the `errBlock` alias minted before the body — NOT because an
		// `IrBlock` alias can go stale (it cannot: blocks are reference objects, and `bodyEnd` is held across
		// this whole handler parse for exactly that reason). It is fetched by id because the id is what the
		// CONTEXT carries: `errBlockId` is the only handle the router had, so reading the block back through
		// it is what proves the block the handler is written into is the one every routed edge branched to.
		self.currentBlock = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: ctx.errBlockId) otherwise panic("parseTryBlockStatement: the error block {ctx.errBlockId} of '{String.from(labelTok.value)}' is not in its own function")

		// ⭐⭐ **THE HANDLER IS PARSED AGAINST THE OWNERSHIP STATE THE THROW EDGE CARRIES, NOT THE BODY'S END
		// STATE** — `if`/`else`'s `restoreMoveMark(entryMoveMark)` before the else branch, one construct over.
		// The edge leaves the body AT a routed call, so anything the body moved AFTER that call did not happen
		// on this path and the handler must still see those bindings as live (`requireRoutableTryBlockBody`
		// has already refused a body whose routed calls disagree about this). Without the rewind, a handler
		// that reads or re-moves such a binding would be reasoning about a move that its own path never made.
		self.restoreMoveMark(ctx.edgeMoveMark)

		// ⭐ **THE SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION, BUILT ONLY NOW — the member list is complete exactly once the body
		// is, and the fused phi must be the LAST block arg, so it cannot be minted before the last routed edge
		// has recorded its own args.**
		let errorUnion = self.synthesizeErrorUnion(ctx, errBlock: self.currentBlock)

		let handler = try self.parseTryBlockHandler(func, shape: shape, flag: flagPhi, errorType: self.tryErrorEnumType(ctx.throwsClause), errorUnion: errorUnion)
		var errEnd = self.currentBlock
		let handlerMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()

		return try self.finishTryBlock(func, bodyEnd: bodyEnd, errEnd: errEnd, bodyVals: bodyVals, handler: handler, bodyMoveMark: bodyMoveMark, handlerMoveMark: handlerMoveMark, tryTok: tryTok)
	end 'parseTryBlockStatement'

	// **E3083 — a block-form `try` whose body holds no throwing call**, and the MOVED-BETWEEN-EDGES refusal
	// that could not be raised where it was found. Both are questions about the WHOLE body, so both are
	// asked once here, after it is parsed and before the handler is.
	function requireRoutableTryBlockBody(ctx TryBlockContext, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE PREMISE IS THE CLAUSE, NOT A COUNTER.** `throwsClause` is `none` exactly when nothing was
		// routed, so "did anything route?" and "what does `(e)` bind?" are ONE fact rather than two that
		// could disagree — a body that routed a call must give the handler a type, and a body that gave the
		// handler no type must have routed nothing.
		if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(ctx.throwsClause) 'noThrowingCalls'
			throw ParseError.tryBlockNoThrows(String.from(ctx.label), line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'noThrowingCalls'

		// ⚠ **THE BODY GAVE AN OUTER VALUE AWAY BETWEEN TWO ROUTED CALLS, SO THE HANDLER'S ONE BLOCK IS
		// REACHED WITH THAT VALUE BOTH LIVE AND ALREADY MOVED.** The construct's N throw edges all land on one
		// error block, and the drop that reconciles a conditional move is emitted PER EDGE
		// (`reconcileMovesAtMerge`) — so a binding live on the early edges and moved on the late ones would
		// need its release placed on some throw edges and not others, which is a per-edge landing pad this
		// block does not build (a site owing nothing gets no pad at all). Lowered anyway it is a leak on one
		// edge or a double free on another, neither of which the type system would catch, so it is refused —
		// the same call the too-wide-to-fuse arm above makes, and the same one `poisonOwningBinding` makes for
		// a move that escapes a LOOP, whose back edge poses this question in its own shape.
		// ⚠ **A MEMBER THE FUSED DISPATCH LINE CANNOT HOLD, and it only matters now.** The handler tells its
		// members apart by a single integer built from each member's own tag range; a FLOAT-backed error enum's
		// tags are IEEE-754 bit patterns, so one spanning a negative and a positive double is wider than i64
		// and no such line exists. A block routing that type ALONE is unaffected — it fuses nothing — which
		// is why the question is asked here, where the second member is known, rather than at the edge.
		if ctx.members.count() > 1 and not ctx.unfusableMember.isEmpty() 'errorTypeTooWideToFuse'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a block-form `try` whose body routes '{ctx.unfusableMember}' alongside another error type — the shared handler tells its members apart by one integer built from each member's own case tags, and this one's tags span more of the i64 range than that line can hold (a FLOAT-backed enum's tags are IEEE-754 bit patterns, so two cases can be ~1.8e19 apart). Give it its own `try` block, or route it through a single-statement `try … otherwise`", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'errorTypeTooWideToFuse'

		if ctx.conflictingMoveEdge 'movedBetweenThrowEdges'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a block-form `try` whose body MOVES a value declared outside the block (into a field, a union payload, a consuming call or a `return`) BETWEEN two throwing calls — the block's throw edges share one handler, and they would reach it with that value still owned on the earlier edges and already given away on the later ones, so its drop belongs on some throw edges and not others. Move the value before the `try` block or after it, or give the two throwing calls their own `try` blocks", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'movedBetweenThrowEdges'
	end 'requireRoutableTryBlockBody'

	// ⭐⭐ **BUILD THE SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION — the `(e)` binding's type when the body routed TWO OR MORE
	// distinct error enums, which is what most of `/specs/try-block.md` is made of.** Answers `notAUnion` for
	// a body that routed one, and that answer costs nothing: no phi is minted, no edge is touched, and the
	// block emits byte-for-byte what it emitted before this existed.
	//
	// ⭐ **THREE THINGS HAPPEN AND THEY ARE ONE THING: THE FUSED ORDINAL BECOMES A REAL SSA VALUE.**
	//   1. A phi is APPENDED to the error block's args, past the flag and the carried set. Block args and edge
	//      args are POSITIONAL, so appending on both sides in the same order IS the pairing — the contract
	//      `parseTryBlockStatement` states for the flag phi, one slot further along. It is safe to append here
	//      and nowhere earlier: every routed edge is recorded by the time the body's `end` is consumed.
	//   2. Each recorded edge PUSHES its fused ordinal onto its own arg list — the very array the `BranchEdge`
	//      holds, so the push IS the attachment.
	//   3. A BOXED member's edge finally computes its ordinal, in the landing pad forced for it (see
	//      `tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget`). It could not be computed at the edge: the load must not stand where the
	//      flag may be `0`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE LAYOUT IS A DISPATCH TABLE OVER `(member, case)` PAIRS**, in member-then-declaration order,
	// carrying the FUSED tags. `ErrorUnionInfo`'s header says why it holds no payload slots and why it is
	// registered nowhere.
	function synthesizeErrorUnion(ctx TryBlockContext, errBlock IrBlock) returns ErrorUnionMatch
		if ctx.members.count() < 2 'oneErrorTypeIsNoUnion'
			return ErrorUnionMatch.notAUnion
		end 'oneErrorTypeIsNoUnion'

		let fusedPhi = self.mintPhi(errBlock, tag: ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)

		// ⚠ **TWO WALKS, AND THE SPLIT IS THE BORROW RULE.** Computing an ordinal EMITS (a boxed member's
		// deferred tag load), so it mutates `self`; a borrow taken out of `ctx.routedEdges` — the edge's own
		// arg list — may not be held across such a call (E3019). So every ordinal is computed first, into a
		// list of this routine's own, and attached in a second walk that emits nothing.
		var ordinals = ValueIdArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto ctx.routedEdges.count() 'computeEach'
			let edge = try ctx.routedEdges.get(k) otherwise panic("synthesizeErrorUnion: routedEdges.get OOB at {k} on a list walked to its own count")
			let ordinal = self.routedEdgeOrdinalForUnion(edge.member, block: edge.block, flag: edge.flag, fusedValue: edge.fusedValue, anchor: edge.anchor)
			ordinals.push(ordinal)
		end 'computeEach'
		for k in 0 upto ctx.routedEdges.count() 'attachEach'
			let ordinal = try ordinals.get(k) otherwise panic("synthesizeErrorUnion: ordinals.get OOB at {k} — the list is filled one entry per edge in the walk above")
			var edge = try ctx.routedEdges.get(k) otherwise panic("synthesizeErrorUnion: routedEdges.get OOB at {k} on a list walked to its own count")
			edge.attachFusedOrdinal(ordinal)
		end 'attachEach'

		var layout = EnumLayout.create(SynthesizedErrorUnionName, isUnion: true, conformsTo: ByteArrayArray.create(), visibility: Visibility.global, declFilePath: CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath, backing: EnumBacking.integer)
		var caseMembers = TryBlockErrorMemberArray.create()
		for m in 0 upto ctx.members.count() 'eachMember'
			let member = try ctx.members.get(m) otherwise panic("synthesizeErrorUnion: members.get OOB at {m} on a list walked to its own count")
			for i in 0 upto member.layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
				// `declaresRawValue: false` is the literal truth and not a default: this layout exists only to
				// dispatch the fused `match` and no value is ever TYPED by it, so nothing can interpolate one.
				layout.addCase(member.layout.caseNameAt(i), tag: member.layout.tagOfIndex(i) + member.fusedTagOffset, rawValue: "", declaresRawValue: false, payloads: PayloadFieldArray.create(), backingFields: sharedEmptyEnumBackingFields)
				caseMembers.push(member)
			end 'eachCase'
		end 'eachMember'

		return ErrorUnionMatch.synthesized(ErrorUnionInfo.create(layout, caseMembers: caseMembers, fusedOrdinal: fusedPhi))
	end 'synthesizeErrorUnion'

	// The value one recorded edge contributes to the fused phi. The two SCALAR shapes settled theirs where the
	// edge was built (`routedEdgeFusedValue`); a BOXED member's is emitted HERE, into the landing pad that
	// exists for exactly this — the pad ends in an unconditional branch, which is what lets an op be appended
	// to it after the fact, and it is reached only when the flag really is a box pointer.
	function routedEdgeOrdinalForUnion(member TryBlockErrorMember, block IrBlock, flag ValueId, fusedValue ValueId, anchor Token) returns ValueId
		if not member.isBoxed 'alreadyComputed'
			return fusedValue
		end 'alreadyComputed'

		let saved = self.currentBlock
		self.currentBlock = block
		let tag = self.emitUnionTagLoad(flag, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		var fused = tag
		if member.fusedTagOffset != 0 'shifted'
			fused = self.emitAddLiteral(tag, addend: member.fusedTagOffset, anchor: anchor)
		end 'shifted'
		self.currentBlock = saved
		return fused
	end 'routedEdgeOrdinalForUnion'

	// **E2050 — a block-form `try` must be followed by its `otherwise (e) 'label' … end` handler.** The
	// construct exists to funnel several throwing calls into ONE handler; with no handler there is nothing
	// for a routed error to reach, and the error block would be an unterminated orphan. The reference
	// bootstrap refuses the same program with the same code (`2-Parser.cs:9651`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT CLASSIFIES THE HANDLER, because the three forms diverge at the token after `otherwise`
	// and nowhere else.** `/specs/try-block-otherwise-divergent.md` gives the construct three handlers:
	//   • `otherwise (e) 'h' … end 'h'` — a BLOCK handler. Its binding is REQUIRED: the handler's whole job
	//     is to tell the routed errors apart, which needs a name to `match` on.
	//   • `otherwise [(e)] panic("…")` and `otherwise [(e)] throws E.case` — TERMINAL handlers, a single
	//     expression with no `end` of their own, whose binding is OPTIONAL because nothing has to be
	//     dispatched on it.
	// The SHAPE is returned rather than re-derived at the call site: the same peek deciding admission and then
	// deciding routing would be one grammar written twice, free to admit a form it then declines to parse.
	//
	// ⚠ Whether a binder is PRESENT is deliberately not carried with it. That question belongs to
	// `parseOptionalCaughtErrorBinder`, which two constructs share and which must ask it for itself — the
	// catching `if` reaches that routine with no shape at all. Carrying it here would un-share the binder to
	// save one token test.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE HANDLER, AND BOTH ARE CANONICAL.** The handler may be ATTACHED to the body's
	// closer — `end 'work' otherwise (e) 'h'`, which `/specs/map-try-otherwise-block.md` uses and
	// `specs-shv2/map-try-otherwise-block.md` pins — or STANDALONE, opening its own line after `end 'work'`,
	// which is the only form `/specs/try-block.md` writes (30 times). The two differ by LAYOUT and nothing
	// else: the newlines between the body's `end` and the `otherwise` are the whole difference, so the
	// handler is looked for PAST them.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CURSOR MOVES ONLY WHEN AN `otherwise` IS ACTUALLY THERE.** Skipping the newlines first and
	// then testing would consume the layout ahead of an ordinary following statement, and the E2050 this
	// routine raises would be anchored past it.
	function requireTryBlockOtherwise(tryTok Token) returns TryBlockHandlerShape throws ParseError
		let handlerIndex = self.indexPastNewlines(self.pos)
		if self.kindAt(handlerIndex) != TokenKind.otherwise 'noHandler'
			throw ParseError.tryBlockMissingOtherwise(false, line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'noHandler'
		self.pos = handlerIndex

		try self.consume(TokenKind.otherwise)

		// UNBOUND: the terminal forms are the only ones that may omit the binding.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'noBinding'
			if divergentHandlerOpensAt(self.kindAt(self.pos)) 'terminalWithoutABinding'
				return TryBlockHandlerShape.divergent
			end 'terminalWithoutABinding'

			let tok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.tryBlockMissingOtherwise(true, line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'noBinding'

		// BOUND: `( <name> )` followed by the LABEL of a block handler, or by the keyword of a terminal one.
		// All FOUR positions are read, exactly as `otherwiseOpensBlock` reads them for the extent scan — so
		// this classification and that one are looking at the same shape rather than at one offset of it, and
		// the offsets carry names instead of arithmetic.
		//
		// ⚠ A MIS-SHAPEN binder (`otherwise (a, b) panic …`, `otherwise () panic …`) falls through to
		// `blockHandler` and is reported by `parseOtherwiseBinding`'s own positioned `consume` — which is the
		// right answer for it: the author wrote something that is neither form, and the parser blames the
		// token that is wrong rather than guessing which form was meant.
		let binderIsWellFormed = self.kindAt(self.pos + 1) == TokenKind.identifier and self.kindAt(self.pos + 2) == TokenKind.rightParen
		if binderIsWellFormed and divergentHandlerOpensAt(self.kindAt(self.pos + 3)) 'terminalWithABinding'
			return TryBlockHandlerShape.divergent
		end 'terminalWithABinding'

		return TryBlockHandlerShape.blockHandler
	end 'requireTryBlockOtherwise'

	// Parse an OPTIONAL `( <name> )` caught-error binder at the cursor — see `CaughtErrorBinder` for who
	// shares this and who cannot. `fallbackAnchor` is what the absent case is attributed to.
	function parseOptionalCaughtErrorBinder(fallbackAnchor Token) returns CaughtErrorBinder throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'noBinder'
			return CaughtErrorBinder.create(EmptySourceText, hasBinding: false, anchor: fallbackAnchor)
		end 'noBinder'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let eNameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		// ⛔ **A CAUGHT ERROR IS A DECLARATION, SO IT ASKS THE DECLARATION DOOR** — `requireUnreservedName`'s
		// header gives the reason at length, and BOTH of this routine's callers measured it independently:
		// `otherwise (__x) panic(…)` and `end 'c' else (__e) 'err'` each declared a `__`-prefixed local and RAN
		// where the oracle refuses it E2051.
		try self.requireUnreservedName(eNameTok, declaredName: eNameTok.value)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		return CaughtErrorBinder.create(eNameTok.value, hasBinding: true, anchor: eNameTok)
	end 'parseOptionalCaughtErrorBinder'

	// ⭐⭐ **`panic("…")` / `throws E.case` — THE TWO DIVERGING TERMINAL FORMS, PARSED ONCE.** Three positions
	// spell this pair and mean the same by it: a match arm's `default`, a match EXPRESSION arm's BODY —
	// where its `gives` would go, the keyword replacing it (`red panic("…")` —
	// `specs/match-expr-arm-divergent.md`, which is why this is a shared door and not a second fork) — and a
	// block-form `try`'s divergent `otherwise`. All admit it through
	// `divergentHandlerOpensAt`, all seat it in a block their caller chose, and all rely on it TERMINATING
	// that block. The fork was written twice before the review; the two LEAF routines were always shared,
	// which is what made the copy easy to miss.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SINGLE-CALL `try f() otherwise throw E.x` IS DELIBERATELY NOT ONE OF THEM.** It spells `throw`,
	// not `throws`, and it lives in `parseUnboundOtherwiseForm` beside `ignore`, `return`/`break`/`continue`
	// and a fallback VALUE — none of which these two admit. Same leaves, different grammar; folding it in
	// would make this the fork for a keyword set it does not have.
	function parseDivergingTerminalForm(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.throws) 'rethrow'
			self.currentBlock = block
			let throwsTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.throws)
			try self.emitThrowError(func, anchor: throwsTok)
			return
		end 'rethrow'

		if self.at(TokenKind.panic) 'abort'
			try self.parsePanicStatement(func, block: block)
			return
		end 'abort'

		panic("parseDivergingTerminalForm: the token here is neither `throws` nor `panic` — every caller admits this form through `divergentHandlerOpensAt`, which is the one roster of the two keywords")
	end 'parseDivergingTerminalForm'

	// ⭐⭐ **PARSE THE HANDLER THE SHAPE NAMED.** One fork, at the one point both handler kinds have their
	// error TYPE and their synthesized UNION in hand — so what a divergent handler's optional `(e)` binds is
	// the same thing a block handler's required one binds, by construction rather than by two agreeing copies.
	function parseTryBlockHandler(func IrFunction, shape TryBlockHandlerShape, flag ValueId, errorType MaxonType, errorUnion ErrorUnionMatch) returns TryHandler throws ParseError
		match shape 'shape'
			blockHandler then return try self.parseOtherwiseBinding(func, flag: flag, errorType: errorType, form: CaughtErrorForm.blockForm(errorUnion))
			divergent then return try self.parseDivergentOtherwise(func, flag: flag, errorType: errorType, errorUnion: errorUnion)
		end 'shape'
	end 'parseTryBlockHandler'

	// ⭐⭐ **`otherwise [(e)] panic("…")` / `otherwise [(e)] throws E.case` — THE TERMINAL HANDLERS.** Both
	// DIVERGE, so the construct has exactly one live edge past it (the body's), and both are a single
	// expression with no `end` of their own.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE OPTIONAL BINDING GOES THROUGH `declareCaughtError`, THE SAME DOOR THE BLOCK FORM USES**, so
	// what `(e)` means here is what it means there — a scalar enum's decoded ordinal or a boxed union's
	// pointer — rather than a second answer that could drift from it. Its binder is `parseOptionalCaughtErrorBinder`,
	// shared with the catching `if`'s `else (e)`, for the same reason one level down.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **E3084 DOES NOT APPLY HERE, AND THE MECHANISM IS THIS ROUTINE'S SILENCE.** A block handler owes a
	// `match` on its binding because it RUNS: one that never discriminates has swallowed every error the body
	// can raise. A divergent handler aborts or re-throws, so it swallows nothing and owes nothing. The
	// obligation is armed by `parseOtherwiseBlock` — which sets `tryBindingName`/`tryBindingMatched` around
	// its own body and checks them at its end — and this routine never calls it and never touches either
	// field, so the rule simply never arms. ⚠ It is stated HERE because that is where it happens: a
	// `CaughtErrorForm` case for it looked like documentation and was a redundant arm on a call that cannot
	// occur (review found it), since `tryBlockBindingToMatch` is reached only from `parseOtherwiseBlock`.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE TWO FIELDS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT SAVED AND RESTORED.** A divergent handler is still
	// lexically inside any ENCLOSING handler's body, so a `match e` written in its panic message or throw
	// expression legitimately discharges that enclosing handler's obligation. Only a CLOSURE crosses a
	// function boundary, and that is where the reset lives.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CAUGHT ERROR STILL OWES ITS DROP, WHICH IS WHY THE SCOPE FRAME IS HERE.** A boxed error
	// reaching a `panic` or a re-throw is a live heap box this frame received ownership of; `declareCaughtError`
	// enrols it (NAMED, or ANONYMOUS when the form binds nothing), the terminating form's own
	// `emitScopeDrops`/`emitHandoffExitDrops` releases it on the way out, and `dropArmScopedOwned` drains it
	// off the owned list either way. This is `parseOtherwiseHandler`'s bracket around the single-call
	// terminal forms, applied to the block form's — one rule, two constructs.
	function parseDivergentOtherwise(func IrFunction, flag ValueId, errorType MaxonType, errorUnion ErrorUnionMatch) returns TryHandler throws ParseError
		let binder = try self.parseOptionalCaughtErrorBinder(self.current())
		try self.rejectDivergentBindingOverErrorUnion(binder, errorUnion: errorUnion)

		let ownedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()
		let unionBindingDepth = self.errorUnionBindings.count()
		self.scope.pushScope()
		try self.declareCaughtError(flag, errorType: errorType, bindingName: binder.name, hasBinding: binder.hasBinding, form: CaughtErrorForm.blockForm(errorUnion), anchor: binder.anchor)

		// ⭐ **E3012 — A DECLARED-BUT-UNREAD `(e)` IS REFUSED, and the spec asks for it by name.** The binding
		// is optional; declaring one and never reading it says the author meant to use the error and did not.
		// It is enrolled as its own candidate KIND because a body `let` deliberately is not one
		// (`UnusedBindingKind.mutableLocal` carries the measured 82-case reason) — this is the one binding
		// `/specs/try-block-otherwise-divergent.md` states the rule for, not the general unused-`let` rung.
		if binder.hasBinding 'checkedForUse'
			self.unusedCandidates.push(UnusedBindingCandidate.create(self.scope.getInfo(binder.name), nameRange: SourceRange.ofToken(binder.anchor), kind: UnusedBindingKind.divergentHandlerBinding))
		end 'checkedForUse'

		// ⚠ **A SYNTHESIZED UNION'S BOX IS RELEASED HERE FOR THE REASON A `default` ARM'S IS.** Over a union
		// the binding owns nothing at all — the flag is an ordinal on some incoming edges and a box POINTER on
		// others, so `declareErrorUnionBinding` gives it no type and no drop, and the box is released by
		// whichever ARM names the member. A divergent handler names none, which is exactly a `default`'s
		// position, so it reuses that routine rather than inventing a second release.
		//
		// ⛔ **IT RUNS BEFORE THE FORM PARSES, AND THAT IS ONLY SOUND BECAUSE THE BINDING IS REFUSED ABOVE.**
		// The release `__destruct_`s the in-flight box; a binding readable AFTERWARDS would be naming a freed
		// one. `rejectDivergentBindingOverErrorUnion` is what makes "readable afterwards" impossible — remove
		// it and this ordering becomes a wrong VALUE, which is exactly what review measured.
		self.currentBlock = self.emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup(func, union: errorUnion, entry: self.currentBlock, flag: flag, anchor: binder.anchor)

		try self.parseDivergingTerminalForm(func, block: self.currentBlock)

		self.scope.popScope()
		while self.errorUnionBindings.count() > unionBindingDepth 'closeUnionScope'
			_ = try self.errorUnionBindings.pop() otherwise panic("parseDivergentOtherwise: errorUnionBindings.pop failed above the depth this handler recorded")
		end 'closeUnionScope'
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(ownedFloor, exitBlock: self.currentBlock, anchor: binder.anchor)
		return TryHandler.terminated
	end 'parseDivergentOtherwise'

	// ⛔⛔ **A DIVERGENT `otherwise` MAY NOT BIND THE ERROR WHEN THE BODY ROUTES TWO OR MORE ERROR TYPES —
	// and the alternative measured as a MEANINGLESS VALUE at both compilers.** `(e)` would name shv2's
	// synthesized error union, which has NO value form: it is a dispatch table over `(member, case)` pairs
	// (`ErrorUnionInfo`), the binding carries the raw flag typed `integer`, and only a `match` ARM recovers a
	// member's own type from it. Every other form that binds such a union is a `match` away from a real type;
	// this is the first that is not, because E3084 does not reach it.
	//
	// ⭐ **MEASURED BEFORE REFUSING, and the oracle is no better.** `otherwise (e) panic("failed: {e}")` over
	// a body throwing two distinct error types prints **`failed: 1`** on the C# bootstrap — the raw
	// `ordinal + bias` flag, an internal ABI value with no meaning to the author. shv2's would be worse than
	// meaningless: its fused ordinal on a BOXED member's edge is the numeric value of a pointer the union
	// cleanup one statement below has already freed. There is nothing to match, so this refuses instead — a
	// diagnostic naming what is missing beats a silent answer that is stable at neither compiler.
	//
	// It rejects nothing any spec requires: `/specs/try-block-otherwise-divergent.md` binds only over a
	// single error type, and every one of its multi-type cases is UNBOUND — which stays legal, and is exactly
	// the shape the union cleanup exists for.
	function rejectDivergentBindingOverErrorUnion(binder CaughtErrorBinder, errorUnion ErrorUnionMatch) throws ParseError
		if not binder.hasBinding or not errorUnionIsSynthesized(errorUnion) 'nothingToRefuse'
			return
		end 'nothingToRefuse'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("binding the caught error of a DIVERGENT `otherwise` whose body routes TWO OR MORE error types — `(e)` would name the synthesized error union, which has no value form: it is a dispatch table over the `(member, case)` pairs, so the binding could carry only the raw error flag and neither a message nor a payload could say which error occurred (the reference bootstrap prints that flag verbatim). Bind it in a block handler, whose `match` arms recover each member's own type (`otherwise (e) 'h' … match e … end 'h'`), or drop the binding — `otherwise panic(…)` and `otherwise throws E.case` catch every member", line: binder.anchor.line, column: binder.anchor.column)
	end 'rejectDivergentBindingOverErrorUnion'

	// ⭐ **WHERE CONTROL GOES AFTER THE CONSTRUCT — FOUR SHAPES FROM TWO INDEPENDENT FACTS**: whether the
	// BODY fell through, and whether the HANDLER did. Each is settled by its own block's terminator, so
	// there is nothing to remember and no fifth case to forget.
	//
	// ⭐ **EACH SHAPE SETTLES TWO AXES, NOT ONE — the carried VALUES and the drop-gating MOVE state** — and
	// they are settled together here for the reason `finalizeIfElse` settles them together: the surviving
	// path's state prevails, and a shape that carried the values across without the move bits would keep
	// answering correctly while silently leaking or double-freeing. MEASURED before this was written: a
	// handler that moved an outer owned binding and then returned left it marked moved on the BODY's
	// continuation, so its function-end drop was skipped (exit 101, against the bootstrap's 0).
	function finishTryBlock(func IrFunction, bodyEnd IrBlock, errEnd IrBlock, bodyVals ValueIdArray, handler TryHandler, bodyMoveMark MoveMark, handlerMoveMark MoveMark, tryTok Token) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let bodyFellThrough = not bodyEnd.isTerminated()
		// ⚠ Read off the HANDLER's own verdict rather than re-asking `errEnd.isTerminated()`. The two agree
		// today — that test is exactly how `parseOtherwiseBlock` decides what to return — and asking it again
		// here would be that one fact spelled in two places, free to disagree the day a handler form
		// terminates without terminating its final block.
		let handlerFellThrough = match handler 'h'
			terminated gives false
			fellThrough gives true
			value panic("finishTryBlock: a block-form `try`'s handler is parsed by `parseTryBlockHandler`, whose two routes are `parseOtherwiseBinding` (reaching only `parseOtherwiseBlock`, which answers `terminated`/`fellThrough`) and `parseDivergentOtherwise` (which answers `terminated` unconditionally) — a `value` handler is the single-call form's `otherwise <expr>` fallback and cannot arrive from either")
		end 'h'

		if bodyFellThrough and handlerFellThrough 'bothEdges'
			// Two live predecessors — the ordinary two-way merge, minting a phi only where the edges
			// disagree about a carried binding (`mergeAtContinuation`, the same one `if`/`else` uses).
			var cont = self.newBlock(func, label: b"tryblkcont", line: tryTok.line)
			let handlerVals = self.snapshotBindings(self.mutableVars)

			// ⭐ **A CONDITIONAL MOVE RECONCILED ACROSS THE TWO LIVE EDGES** — a binding moved on one and left
			// live on the other is dropped on the LIVE edge and marked uniformly moved past the merge, so the
			// later scope-exit drop is right on both. Emitted BEFORE the phi edges and the branches, exactly as
			// `finalizeIfElse` does it, so each decref lands in its own edge's block ahead of that block's jump.
			var moveEdges = ReachingMoveEdgeArray.create()
			moveEdges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(bodyEnd, mark: bodyMoveMark))
			moveEdges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(errEnd, mark: handlerMoveMark))
			self.reconcileMovesAtMerge(moveEdges, anchor: tryTok)

			self.mergeAtContinuation(bodyEnd, predB: errEnd, cont: cont, bindings: self.mutableVars, valsA: bodyVals, valsB: handlerVals)
			self.emitBranch(bodyEnd, targetId: cont.id)
			self.emitBranch(errEnd, targetId: cont.id)
			self.currentBlock = cont
			return cont
		end 'bothEdges'

		if bodyFellThrough 'onlyTheBody'
			// The handler terminated (`return`/`throw`/`panic`/`break`/`continue`), so neither its
			// reassignments nor its MOVES reach the continuation — restore every carried binding and the
			// drop-gating bit to what the BODY left, exactly as `finishTerminatedTry` restores the pre-try
			// state for the single-call form. The handler dropped its own live values on the way out.
			self.restoreBindings(self.mutableVars, snapshot: bodyVals)
			self.restoreMoveMark(bodyMoveMark)
			self.currentBlock = bodyEnd
			return bodyEnd
		end 'onlyTheBody'

		if handlerFellThrough 'onlyTheHandler'
			// The body terminated on every path (its last statement was a `return`/`throw`), so only the
			// handler continues and the carried bindings already read its values — nothing to merge. The move
			// restore is a no-op today for the same reason `finalizeIfElse`'s `onlyElse` arm's is (the handler
			// was parsed last and nothing has touched the state since); it is written anyway so "the surviving
			// path's state prevails" is stated once per axis rather than resting on parse order.
			self.restoreMoveMark(handlerMoveMark)
			self.currentBlock = errEnd
			return errEnd
		end 'onlyTheHandler'

		// Neither edge falls through. Control leaves through the terminators the two blocks already carry;
		// `parseStatements` reads `isTerminated()` off the block returned here and stops, so the body's is
		// what it must see.
		self.currentBlock = bodyEnd
		return bodyEnd
	end 'finishTryBlock'

	// ⭐⭐ **ROUTE A JUST-EMITTED BARE CALL INTO THE INNERMOST BLOCK-FORM `try` — the whole of the body's
	// error handling, at the one moment it can be done.** Called from the TWO doors that append a
	// `MaxonOp.call` (`emitCall` for everything the author spelled, `emitRuntimeCall` for every
	// compiler-emitted callee), with the index of the op they just appended. Declines, and leaves the op a
	// plain `call`, unless all three of these hold — and every decline is safe, because a plain throwing
	// `call` is precisely what `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall` reports E3057 on.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT CANNOT THROW, AND THAT SHAPES EVERYTHING BELOW.** `emitRuntimeCall` is not a throwing
	// function and has 88 callers; making it one — or threading an `IrFunction` to it so this could live at
	// its callers instead — would charge this construct to every runtime call the compiler emits. So the
	// error block's `IrFunction` and anchor token ride in the CONTEXT, the flag test goes through
	// `emitCompareAgainstLiteral` (which does not throw) rather than `emitCompare` (which does), and the one
	// refusal this routine can discover — a SECOND error type — is RECORDED here and raised by
	// `requireRoutableTryBlockBody` at the body's end, before the handler parses and before any of it is
	// lowered.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ROUTING DECISION AND THE `(e)` BINDING'S TYPE COME FROM ONE LOOKUP.** `throwsOf` answers both
	// "does this callee throw" and "which enum" — for a declared function from its `throws` clause, for a
	// compiler-internal callee from `runtimeThrowsClause` — so a call this admits always gives the handler a
	// type, and a callee whose family answers `none` is declined and reported by E3057 rather than routed to
	// a handler that could not bind it. That is why the gate is the CLAUSE and not `isThrowingRuntimeCallee`,
	// which is the wider of the two.
	function routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock(callee ByteArray, opIdx OpIndex, result ValueId)
		// ⚡ FIRST, and a single field read: almost no call in any program is inside a block-form `try`, and
		// this is on the path of every call the compiler emits.
		if self.tryBlocks.count() == 0 'noEnclosingTryBlock'
			return
		end 'noEnclosingTryBlock'

		// An explicit `try` is about to claim this call for its own desugar (`parseTry`); the block-form
		// handler must not catch an error the author already routed. See `tryBlockRoutingSuppressed`.
		if self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed 'explicitTryOwnsThisCall'
			return
		end 'explicitTryOwnsThisCall'

		let clause = self.signatures.throwsOf(callee)
		if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(clause) 'cannotThrow'
			return
		end 'cannotThrow'

		var ctx = try self.tryBlocks.get(self.tryBlocks.count() - 1) otherwise panic("routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock: tryBlocks.get failed on a non-empty stack")
		let member = self.recordRoutedEdge(ctx, clause: clause)

		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.rewriteRoutedCallToTryCall(opIdx, flag: flag, callee: callee)

		// The success continuation. Everything the rest of the body emits lands here, so a second throwing
		// call in the same body splits THIS block in turn — which is how N calls reach one handler with no
		// N-aware bookkeeping anywhere.
		let okBlock = self.newBlock(ctx.func, label: b"tryblkok", line: ctx.tryTok.line)
		// ⚠ **THE FUSED ORDINAL IS COMPUTED HERE, WHILE THIS BLOCK STILL HAS NO TERMINATOR.** It is the ONE
		// moment it can be: `IrModule.appendOp` refuses to append past a conditional branch (an op there would
		// run on the not-taken edge only), and the branch is two statements away. A first SCALAR member spends
		// nothing — see `routedEdgeFusedValue`.
		let fused = self.routedEdgeFusedValue(ctx, member: member, flag: flag)
		let isErr = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(flag, literal: NoErrorFlag, op: MaxonCmpOp.ne, opTok: ctx.tryTok)

		let errArgs = self.tryBlockErrorEdgeArgs(ctx, flag: flag)

		// ⛔⛔ **THIS CALL'S OWN RESULT IS OWNED ONLY ON THE OK EDGE, SO IT COMES OUT OF THE PENDING LIST
		// WHILE THE ERROR EDGE IS BUILT.** `emitCall` mints the result BEFORE it appends the op, and a managed
		// one arrives here already enrolled as an owned temp by `enrolOwnedCallTemp` → `trackOwnedTemp`. The
		// landing pad below drains that list — but the pad sits on the error edge, where the `tryCall` never
		// wrote the result register, so decrefing it there reads a register the throw zeroed: an access
		// violation, MEASURED on `let s = fetch(0)` with `fetch` returning a managed `String`. This is
		// verbatim the hazard `desugarTry` documents and guards at its own fork — one construct, two forks,
		// and now one rule.
		//
		// ⚠ **REMOVED BEFORE THE EDGE IS BUILT, NOT INSIDE IT**, so `tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget`'s "does this
		// site owe anything?" test sees the same list its drops will walk: a call whose only pending temp is
		// its own result owes NOTHING on the error edge and needs no pad at all.
		//
		// ⚠ **AND PUT STRAIGHT BACK, WHERE `desugarTry` LEAVES IT OFF UNTIL ITS FINISH PATH.** The two differ
		// because the OK path differs: `desugarTry` forks the whole statement and each finish path re-scopes
		// the result itself, while here the ok continuation is the SAME statement still being parsed, which
		// still owes the drop. It is re-pushed at the tail, which is where it was — a call's own result is the
		// last thing enrolled before the op is appended, its arguments' temporaries having been parsed first.
		let resultIsOwnedTemp = self.pendingTempsContain(result)
		if resultIsOwnedTemp 'offTheErrorEdge'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(result)
		end 'offTheErrorEdge'

		let errTargetId = self.tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget(ctx, errArgs: errArgs, flag: flag, member: member, fusedValue: fused, anchor: ctx.tryTok)

		if resultIsOwnedTemp 'backOnTheOkPath'
			self.pendingTempDrops.push(result)
		end 'backOnTheOkPath'

		self.emitCondBranch(self.currentBlock, cond: isErr, thenId: errTargetId, elseId: okBlock.id, ifTok: ctx.tryTok)
		self.currentBlock = okBlock
	end 'routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE ROUTED ERROR EDGE'S ARGUMENT LIST: THE FLAG FIRST, THEN EVERY CARRIED BINDING'S VALUE RIGHT
	// HERE.** Positional against the phis `parseTryBlockStatement` minted in that order — and the fused
	// ordinal `synthesizeErrorUnion` appends LAST lands past both, on this list and on `blockArgs` alike.
	// `edgeArgsFor(preLoop: false)` reads each binding's CURRENT value, which is exactly what this point in
	// the body left it holding.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ONE ROUTINE BECAUSE THE ORDER IS AN ABI BETWEEN TWO SITES THAT CANNOT SEE EACH OTHER.** Both
	// routing doors build this list and `errBlock.blockArgs` is matched against it BY POSITION, so a leading
	// argument added to one copy and not the other is a phi reading another binding's value — a wrong answer,
	// with nothing to report it. `parseWhileStatement` keeps its own edge list in one place
	// (`loopHeaderArgs`) for the same reason.
	function tryBlockErrorEdgeArgs(ctx TryBlockContext, flag ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
		var errArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		errArgs.push(flag)
		for arg in self.edgeArgsFor(ctx.phiVars, preLoop: false) 'carried'
			errArgs.push(arg)
		end 'carried'
		return errArgs
	end 'tryBlockErrorEdgeArgs'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHERE THE THROW EDGE ACTUALLY GOES — the shared error block, or a LANDING PAD in front of it
	// that releases what the body owns at THIS call.** A routed throw jumps over the body's `end`, so none
	// of the drops that fall-through would have run do: the temporaries the half-finished statement built
	// (`f(Item.create())` where `f` throws) and every owned binding the body declared before this point
	// would both leak. They cannot be released in the shared error block — it is reached from every routed
	// call, each owing a DIFFERENT set — so each site that owes anything gets its own one-way pad. This is
	// the bootstrap's `PendingLandingPads` (`2-Parser.cs:167`, "body-local-temps-live-at-throw") reached from
	// the same reasoning, and it is the SAME RULE the expression-level `try` reaches by another route: release
	// what the half-finished statement owes on each path that ABANDONS it, never once per construct. That fork
	// suspends its temporaries as owned bindings instead (`Parser.suspendForkTempsAsOwned`), because its
	// abandoning paths are the handler's own exits and those already carry scope drops; here the abandoning
	// paths are routed CALL SITES, which do not, so each gets a pad.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PENDING TEMP LIST IS READ, NOT CLEARED.** The OK path continues and still owes those same
	// temporaries at the end of its statement, so clearing here would leak them on the path that did not
	// throw. `desugarTry` keeps the same property by a different mechanism — it hands its list back
	// (`resumeForkTemps`) once both edges are wired.
	//
	// A site owing nothing branches straight to the error block: no pad, no block, no drops.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BOXED MEMBER'S EDGE ALWAYS TAKES A PAD, WHETHER OR NOT IT OWES A DROP.** Its fused ordinal is the
	// tag LOADED out of the box, and on the success edge the flag is `0` — so the load has exactly one place
	// it can stand: a block only the error edge reaches. That is what the pad is, and v1 builds the same block
	// for the same reason under the name `boxland`.
	function tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget(ctx TryBlockContext, errArgs ValueIdArray, flag ValueId, member TryBlockErrorMember, fusedValue ValueId, anchor Token) returns BlockId
		let owesTemps = self.pendingTempDrops.count() > WholeStatementTempFloor
		let owesBindings = self.ownedBindings.count() > ctx.ownedFloor
		if not owesTemps and not owesBindings and not member.isBoxed 'nothingToRelease'
			self.recordBranchEdge(self.currentBlock, targetId: ctx.errBlockId, argIds: errArgs)
			ctx.routedEdges.push(RoutedErrorEdge.create(errArgs, flag: flag, block: self.currentBlock, member: member, fusedValue: fusedValue, anchor: anchor))
			return ctx.errBlockId
		end 'nothingToRelease'

		var pad = self.newBlock(ctx.func, label: b"tryblkthrow", line: ctx.tryTok.line)

		// ⚠ **THE PARSER'S POSITION MOVES INTO THE PAD AND BACK, AND THE RESTORE IS LOAD-BEARING.** The two
		// drop routines take their destination differently — `emitScopeDrops` by parameter, `emitTempDrops`
		// through `emitDecref` → `currentBlock` — and the CALLER emits its `condBranch` into the block this
		// was entered in. Left pointing at the pad, that branch would terminate the pad instead.
		let saved = self.currentBlock
		self.currentBlock = pad
		self.emitTempDrops(self.pendingTempDrops, floor: WholeStatementTempFloor, anchor: ctx.tryTok)
		self.emitScopeDrops(pad, floor: ctx.ownedFloor, anchor: ctx.tryTok)
		self.currentBlock = saved

		self.recordBranchEdge(pad, targetId: ctx.errBlockId, argIds: errArgs)
		ctx.routedEdges.push(RoutedErrorEdge.create(errArgs, flag: flag, block: pad, member: member, fusedValue: fusedValue, anchor: anchor))
		self.emitBranch(pad, targetId: ctx.errBlockId)
		return pad.id
	end 'tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE FUSED ORDINAL THIS EDGE WILL CARRY, computed where the edge leaves from — or NOT computed at
	// all, which is the case that matters most.** Three shapes, and `RoutedErrorEdge`'s header argues each:
	//
	//   • **the FIRST member, SCALAR** — its `fusedTagOffset` IS `ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, so `tag + bias` is
	//     already the fused ordinal and the flag serves unchanged. **Nothing is emitted.** Every block-form
	//     `try` that turns out to route ONE error type is this shape, which is how the whole error-union
	//     mechanism costs a single-member block exactly nothing.
	//   • **a LATER member, SCALAR** — `flag + (offset - bias)`, emitted here. A second member exists only in a
	//     block that IS a union, so the op is never dead.
	//   • **a BOXED member** — the tag is a LOAD, and it cannot stand on a path where the flag may be `0`.
	//     Deferred to the landing pad `tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget` forces for exactly this reason, and filled by
	//     `routedEdgeOrdinalForUnion` only if the block turns out to be a union.
	function routedEdgeFusedValue(ctx TryBlockContext, member TryBlockErrorMember, flag ValueId) returns ValueId
		if member.isBoxed 'deferredToThePad'
			return flag
		end 'deferredToThePad'

		let shift = member.fusedTagOffset - ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
		if shift == 0 'theFlagIsAlreadyTheOrdinal'
			return flag
		end 'theFlagIsAlreadyTheOrdinal'

		return self.emitAddLiteral(flag, addend: shift, anchor: ctx.tryTok)
	end 'routedEdgeFusedValue'

	// `subject + addend` as an integer value, through the two ops the fused-ordinal shift needs and nothing
	// else. Its two callers are the two halves of that shift (the routing-time scalar form and the pad's
	// deferred boxed form), so the addition is written once.
	function emitAddLiteral(subject ValueId, addend ParsedInt, anchor Token) returns ValueId
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(anchor)
		let rhs = self.emitLiteral(addend, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let sum = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.binOp(sum, lhs: subject, rhs: rhs, op: MaxonBinOp.add), range: range)
		return sum
	end 'emitAddLiteral'

	// Convert the routed call at `opIdx` into the `tryCall` / `witnessTryDispatch` that DEFINES `flag`,
	// through the same two commit routines an explicit `try` uses — so a routed call and a `try`ed one carry
	// byte-identical ops and nothing downstream can tell them apart.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WHICH QUESTION IS BEING ASKED.** That
	// routine SEARCHES for the op an author's `try` was applied to and REJECTS a non-call (`try (a)` is a
	// user error with its own sentence). Here the index is the op this parser appended one line ago, so any
	// other kind is a broken invariant in the compiler — a panic, not a diagnostic. Sharing the search would
	// have meant answering a user's mistake with a compiler's, or the reverse.
	function rewriteRoutedCallToTryCall(opIdx OpIndex, flag ValueId, callee ByteArray)
		match try self.artifact.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteRoutedCallToTryCall: op {opIdx} is not in module.ops") 'routed'
			call(result, opCallee, args, argLabels, argRanges, argImmutableNames, argDefaultSlots, mint) then _ = self.commitTryCallRewrite(opIdx, result: result, errorFlag: flag, callee: opCallee, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, argDefaultSlots: argDefaultSlots, mint: mint)
			default panic("rewriteRoutedCallToTryCall: the op at {opIdx} for '{String.from(callee)}' is not a `call` — the two doors that route (`emitCall`, `emitRuntimeCall`) hand this the index of a `call` they appended immediately before")
		end 'routed'
	end 'rewriteRoutedCallToTryCall'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A ROUTED EDGE TELLS THE SHARED HANDLER — which UNION MEMBER it raises, and the OWNERSHIP state
	// the handler will be entered with.** Returns the member; the caller needs it to compute the edge's fused
	// ordinal. Both routing doors (a bare throwing CALL, a bare `throw`) come through here, so the member set
	// and the entry ownership are settled from one place whichever door produced the edge.
	//
	// The ownership half is settled by the FIRST routed edge and every later one must AGREE with it; a
	// disagreement is RECORDED for `requireRoutableTryBlockBody` to refuse at the body's end, because this
	// path cannot throw (see `routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock`).
	function recordRoutedEdge(ctx TryBlockContext, clause ThrowsClause) returns TryBlockErrorMember
		// The outer bindings' move state ON THIS EDGE. Truncated to the block's owned floor: the body's own
		// bindings vary in number between routed calls and the landing pad releases them anyway, so only the
		// outer ones are comparable — and only they can still be owed a drop past the handler.
		let edgeMark = moveMarkPrefix(self.captureMoveMark(), height: ctx.ownedFloor)

		if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(ctx.throwsClause) 'firstRoutedEdge'
			ctx.throwsClause = clause
			ctx.edgeMoveMark = edgeMark
		end 'firstRoutedEdge' else 'laterRoutedEdge'
			if not moveMarksAgree(ctx.edgeMoveMark, b: edgeMark) 'movedBetweenEdges'
				ctx.conflictingMoveEdge = true
			end 'movedBetweenEdges'
		end 'laterRoutedEdge'

		return self.recordRoutedErrorType(ctx, clause: clause)
	end 'recordRoutedEdge'

	// ⭐⭐ **FIND OR ADD THIS ERROR TYPE AS A MEMBER OF THE BLOCK'S SYNTHESIZED UNION, AND FIX ITS SLICE OF THE
	// FUSED DISPATCH LINE.** Deduped by NAME, in first-routed order — both references do exactly this and call
	// the position a discriminant. shv2 spends the position differently: instead of carrying it on a second
	// phi and testing a PAIR at every handler arm, each member is given `fusedTagOffset`, and
	// `<member's own case tag> + fusedTagOffset` is the ONE number an arm tests.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **WHY THAT NUMBER IS INJECTIVE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT.** Each member is handed
	// the CLOSED interval `[floor, floor + width]`, where `width` is its OWN tag range (`maxTag - minTag`)
	// and `floor` is one past the previous member's top — so the intervals are disjoint and strictly
	// ascending, and a member's own cases are distinct inside its interval because its tags are distinct
	// (E3031 refuses two cases with one raw value). Hence distinct `(member, case)` pairs have distinct fused
	// tags, for ANY tag numbering including explicit `= N` and negative raw values. **Nothing is joined and
	// no name is a key** — which is the property `/specs/try-block.md`'s
	// `union-member-names-join-injectively` was written to protect in a compiler that spells the union's
	// identity as a string (see `ErrorUnionInfo` for that comparison).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PLACEMENT CAN FAIL, AND FAILING IS NOT A PANIC.** A FLOAT-backed error enum is legal and
	// throwable (MEASURED: `enum FErr implements Error  lo = -1.5  hi = 2.5 end` compiles and throws), and
	// `tagOfIndex` hands back the IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN — so such a member can be wider than the whole i64
	// line, and a second one placed above it would overflow `ParsedInt`, which is a ranged type and so a
	// COMPILER PANIC rather than a wrapped number. `fusedPlacementAt` answers `tooWide` instead, and the
	// block is refused when a second member proves it really had to fuse.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FIRST MEMBER, WHEN SCALAR, IS GIVEN EXACTLY `ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` — and that is not an
	// optimization, it is what keeps a ONE-member block byte-identical.** Such a member's flag already IS
	// `tag + bias`, so its fused ordinal is the flag and the edge emits no op at all; a block that turns out
	// to have one member therefore spends nothing on machinery it does not use.
	function recordRoutedErrorType(ctx TryBlockContext, clause ThrowsClause) returns TryBlockErrorMember
		let name = (try throwsClauseName(clause) otherwise panic("recordRoutedErrorType: both routing doors gate on `throwsClauseIsThrowing` before reaching here")).toByteArray()

		for i in 0 upto ctx.members.count() 'seen'
			let existing = try ctx.members.get(i) otherwise panic("recordRoutedErrorType: members.get OOB at {i} on a list walked to its own count")
			if existing.name.equals(name) 'sameMember'
				return existing
			end 'sameMember'
		end 'seen'

		let layout = self.requireRoutedErrorLayout(name)
		let isBoxed = layout.isBoxed()
		let span = enumTagSpan(layout)

		// A SCALAR member's runtime value is `tag + bias`; a BOXED member's is the tag loaded out of the box.
		// Every member but the first takes the next free slice above the member before it; the first, when it
		// is SCALAR, is pinned at exactly the bias so its fused ordinal IS the flag and its edges emit nothing.
		var placement = FusedPlacement.tooWide()
		if ctx.members.count() == 0 and not isBoxed 'firstScalarMemberIsTheFlagItself'
			placement = fusedPlacementAtTheBias(span)
		end 'firstScalarMemberIsTheFlagItself' else 'aboveWhatIsAlreadyPlaced'
			placement = fusedPlacementAt(self.nextFusedFloor(ctx), span: span)
		end 'aboveWhatIsAlreadyPlaced'

		if not placement.fusable and ctx.unfusableMember.isEmpty() 'firstMemberTheLineCannotHold'
			ctx.unfusableMember = String.from(name)
		end 'firstMemberTheLineCannotHold'

		let member = TryBlockErrorMember.create(name, clause: clause, isBoxed: isBoxed, layout: layout, placement: placement)
		ctx.members.push(member)
		return member
	end 'recordRoutedErrorType'

	// The first free position on the fused line — `0` for the first member, one past the previous member's
	// top for every later one. The `+ 1` is safe without a check because a placed top is at most
	// `FusedTagCeiling`, which is held short of `i64.max` by exactly this margin.
	function nextFusedFloor(ctx TryBlockContext) returns ParsedInt
		if ctx.members.count() == 0 'nothingPlacedYet'
			return 0
		end 'nothingPlacedYet'

		let previous = try ctx.members.get(ctx.members.count() - 1) otherwise panic("nextFusedFloor: members.get failed on a list the line above tested is non-empty")
		if not previous.fusable 'thePreviousOneDidNotFitEither'
			return 0
		end 'thePreviousOneDidNotFitEither'

		return previous.fusedTop + 1
	end 'nextFusedFloor'

	// The declared layout of an error type a routed edge names. It is DECLARED by construction: a call routes
	// only on a `throws E` clause the signature table produced, and a `throw` only on a value whose type the
	// parser already resolved — so an undeclared name here is a compiler invariant break, not a user error
	// (E3004 reports an unknown type long before a throw can name it).
	function requireRoutedErrorLayout(name ByteArray) returns EnumLayout
		match self.signatures.enumOf(name) 'declared'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("requireRoutedErrorLayout: '{String.from(name)}' is routed as an error type but declares no enum or union — every routing door reads the name off a resolved `throws` clause or a resolved thrown value")
		end 'declared'
	end 'requireRoutedErrorLayout'

	// The MaxonType of the enum a throwing `callee` throws — the type an `(e)` binding recovers. A callee
	// with no throws clause is rejected by SemanticCheck (E3055), so the fallback `integer` here is never
	// lowered; it only keeps the parser from panicking while building IR the error gate will discard.
	// ⭐ **E3059 — PROPAGATE TYPE MISMATCH.** A bare `try …` (no `otherwise`) re-publishes the caught flag
	// VERBATIM into the ENCLOSING function's error register, so if the propagated callee throws a DIFFERENT
	// error type than the enclosing function declares, the caller decodes one enum's ordinals as another's
	// tags — a silent miscompile a boxed union turns into a wild free. Refused when the two named types differ,
	// AND when the enclosing function names none at all (see `rejectPropagateAgainstEnclosing`). Shared by
	// every try shape — a direct call, `try await p` (the awaited callee's throws type IS the promise's) and a
	// witness dispatch — because each answers `tryTargetThrows` and only the CLAUSE reaches here.
	function checkPropagateType(func IrFunction, throwsClause ThrowsClause, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		match throwsClause 'calleeThrows'
			none then break 'calleeThrows'
			throwing(propagated) then try self.requireErrorTypeFitsEnclosing(func, errorType: propagated, door: ErrorExitDoor.propagate, anchor: tryTok)
		end 'calleeThrows'
	end 'checkPropagateType'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE THROW DOOR'S HALF OF THE RULE ABOVE (A1s-throwsbox review), and the reason it is the SAME
	// function underneath.** An error leaves a function by exactly two doors — a bare `try` re-publishes a
	// callee's flag, a `throw` publishes a fresh one — and BOTH hand the caller a flag the caller decodes
	// against the enclosing function's DECLARED clause. So both owe the clause the same debt, and the debt was
	// paid at one door only. MEASURED at the unguarded one, in shv2 AND in the C# oracle:
	//
	//   • `throw <payload-carrying union>` under a declared SCALAR enum clause — the throw writes a heap box
	//     pointer, the catch reads an ordinal and never releases it: **exit 101, `MM leak: 1 allocation(s)
	//     remain`.** The identical signature A1s-throwsbox was opened to close, one door over.
	//   • `throw ErrB.bOne` under `throws ErrA` — no leak, a SILENT WRONG ANSWER: the caller matched the
	//     ordinal against `ErrA`'s cases and ran the `aOne` arm for a `bOne` throw.
	//   • `throw` at all in a function declaring no `throws` — the flag has nowhere to be published, so the
	//     error was silently discarded and the throw path's primary register `0` came back as a real answer.
	//     (`rejectPropagateAgainstEnclosing`'s `none` arm had already measured and refused exactly this at the
	//     `try` door; the prose describing it is kept below.)
	//
	// A value whose type is not `named` at all (`throw 1`) is refused here too: the error flag encodes an enum
	// ORDINAL or a union BOX POINTER and has no third shape, so there is nothing for the catch to decode.
	function requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing(func IrFunction, errorType MaxonType, routed bool, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if maxonTypeTag(errorType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notAnErrorType'
			throw ParseError.thrownValueNotAnErrorEnum(anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'notAnErrorType'

		// ⭐ **A ROUTED THROW OWES THE ENCLOSING CLAUSE NOTHING, AND THE PRECEDING CHECK IT STILL OWES.** The
		// rule below is about the flag a function PUBLISHES to its CALLER; a throw routed to a block-form
		// `try`'s handler publishes none — it branches inside this frame, and `main` may hold such a block
		// while declaring no `throws` at all (`try-block.bare-throw-in-nested-if` is exactly that program).
		// What it still owes is the line above: the error-flag ABI encodes an enum ORDINAL or a union BOX
		// POINTER and has no third shape, so a `throw 1` has nothing for ANY catch to decode, handler or
		// caller. The routed edge's own type agreement is `recordRoutedEdge`'s, one door over.
		if routed 'toABlockHandler'
			return
		end 'toABlockHandler'

		let thrownName = try throwsClauseName(self.thrownTypeClause(errorType)) otherwise panic("requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing: `thrownTypeClause` answered `none` for a type the check above proved is `named`")
		try self.requireErrorTypeFitsEnclosing(func, errorType: thrownName, door: ErrorExitDoor.throwStatement, anchor: anchor)
	end 'requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE AN OUTGOING ERROR TYPE IS MEASURED AGAINST THE ENCLOSING `throws` CLAUSE.** Refuses
	// when the enclosing function declares a DIFFERENT named type than `errorType` — or NO type at all. Split
	// so the nested `match`/comparison is not an inline labelled-if in a match arm (which the parser rejects).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `none` ARM IS A REJECT, NOT A FALLTHROUGH.** A bare `try f()` inside a function declaring no
	// `throws` has nowhere to re-publish the flag: `propagateError` writes the enclosing function's error
	// register, and a non-throwing function's caller never reads it — so the error is SILENTLY DISCARDED and
	// the callee's throw-path primary (`0`) comes back as a real answer. MEASURED both ways: `try inner(3)` in
	// a non-throwing `outer` exited 0 where the answer is the error path, and so did the witness spelling
	// (`try self.item.digest()` in a non-throwing generic method). The runnable oracle refuses both — E2001,
	// "try without otherwise requires the enclosing function to have 'throws'" — so this closes a divergence,
	// not a design question. `error-handling.md`'s own Error Propagation section already stated the rule
	// ("only valid in functions with `throws`"); the prose and the compiler had simply disagreed. It rides
	// E3059 through the existing free-form `errorTypeMismatch` arm rather than a new `ParseError` variant,
	// because it is the SAME fact that arm's four other messages carry: the error type does not fit where it
	// is going. A `none` enclosing clause is that mismatch at its limit — there is no there to fit into.
	// The `throw` door measured the same discard independently (see `requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing`).
	//
	// ⚠ **`door` PICKS THE SENTENCE AND NOTHING ELSE.** The two doors are one rule and must never answer
	// differently about whether a type fits; they differ only in what the author has to be told to do about
	// it, which is why the wording — and not the comparison — is what branches.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AN ENCLOSING CLAUSE THAT NAMES NO DECLARED ENUM IS NOT THIS CHECK'S TO REPORT, AND SAYING SO IS
	// WHAT KEEPS THE ROOT CAUSE ON TOP.** This check compares two error TYPES; a clause naming an interface,
	// a struct or a typo denotes no type, so there is nothing here to compare against and blaming the `throw`
	// would be blaming the symptom. Worse, its remedy would be actively wrong — `throws Error` with a
	// `throw DigestError.x` inside would be told to *declare* `throws DigestError` when the clause is the
	// thing at fault. Two checks already own that clause and both outrank this one because both can name WHY
	// the clause is wrong: A1s-throwsbox's **E3113** (`SemanticCheck.checkThrowsClausesNameDeclaredErrors`) for
	// a function's own clause, and **E3016** (`ConformanceCheck.validateThrowsConformance`) for an impl method
	// under a requirement. Nothing escapes by deferring — both run on every program this arm declines, which is
	// exactly why the condition is "the DECODE's own registry does not declare it" (`signatures.containsEnum`,
	// the registry `throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion` resolves through) and not a hand-listed set of bad shapes.
	function requireErrorTypeFitsEnclosing(func IrFunction, errorType String, door ErrorExitDoor, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		match func.throwsTypeName 'enclosing'
			none then throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch(noEnclosingThrowsDetail(errorType, door: door), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			throwing(enclosing) then try self.rejectIfDifferentThrows(errorType, enclosing: enclosing, door: door, anchor: anchor)
		end 'enclosing'
	end 'requireErrorTypeFitsEnclosing'

	// The comparison itself — ONE `equals`, for both doors. The PROPAGATE mismatch keeps its dedicated
	// `propagateTypeMismatch` arm (whose sentence the corpus pins byte-for-byte); the THROW mismatch rides the
	// free-form `errorTypeMismatch` arm, exactly as the `none` sentences above do, because it is the same fact
	// on the same code (E3059): the error type does not fit where it is going.
	function rejectIfDifferentThrows(errorType String, enclosing String, door ErrorExitDoor, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if errorType.equals(enclosing) 'fits'
			return
		end 'fits'

		// The enclosing clause denotes no error type — E3113 or E3016 owns this program (see the header above).
		if not self.signatures.containsEnum(enclosing.toByteArray()) 'clauseIsNotAnErrorType'
			return
		end 'clauseIsNotAnErrorType'

		match door 'which'
			propagate then throw ParseError.propagateTypeMismatch(errorType, enclosing: enclosing, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			throwStatement then throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("throw of '{errorType}' but the enclosing function throws '{enclosing}' — the caller decodes the error flag against '{enclosing}', so one enum's ordinals would be read as another's tags, and a payload-carrying union arriving where a scalar is expected leaks its box; throw a '{enclosing}' case, or declare 'throws {errorType}'", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'which'
	end 'rejectIfDifferentThrows'

	// The ERROR TYPE a `try` target throws — the second of the two questions the desugar asks its target, and
	// the one the two shapes answer differently: a NAMED callee is looked up in the whole-program signature
	// index; a WITNESS method carries its own clause, because no registry holds an interface method.
	function tryTargetThrows(target TryTarget) returns ThrowsClause
		return match target 'kind'
			named(callee) gives self.signatures.throwsOf(callee)
			witness(_, throwsClause) gives throwsClause
			promise(_, throwsClause) gives throwsClause
		end 'kind'
	end 'tryTargetThrows'

	function tryErrorEnumType(throwsClause ThrowsClause) returns MaxonType
		return match throwsClause 'k'
			none gives MaxonType.integer
			throwing(name) gives MaxonType.named(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(name.toByteArray()))
		end 'k'
	end 'tryErrorEnumType'

	// ⭐ **IS THE THROWN/CAUGHT ERROR A BOXED (payload-bearing) UNION?** — the ONE fact the error-flag ABI
	// turns on, and the derive-once point for it. Read at BOTH ends so the flag round-trips: the throw
	// ENCODE (`emitThrowError`, which stamps it on the `throwError` op for `lowerThrowError` to write the box
	// pointer verbatim) and the catch DECODE (`declareCaughtError`, which binds `e` to that pointer verbatim
	// for BOTH catching constructs). Both funnel through the one `enumOf(name).isBoxed()` here, so a boxed union cannot be
	// encoded as a pointer at one end and decoded as an `ordinal + bias` at the other. A SCALAR enum and a
	// non-`named` type are `false` — their flag is `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`.
	function throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion(errorType MaxonType) returns bool
		if maxonTypeTag(errorType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'
		let name = try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(errorType)) otherwise return false
		return match self.signatures.enumOf(name) 'e'
			declared(layout) gives layout.isBoxed()
			undeclared gives false
		end 'e'
	end 'throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion'

	// Emit a `__mm_decref` for every owned binding above `floor`, in REVERSE declaration order.
	//
	// ONE routine serves the two kinds of exit that release owned bindings, differing only in how far
	// down the stack they release: a FUNCTION exit (`return`, an implicit void end) drops the WHOLE
	// in-scope list (`WholeFunctionScopeFloor`), while a nested BLOCK's fall-through and a
	// `break`/`continue` drop only what the block or loop itself declared (their recorded height),
	// leaving the outer bindings for their own scope to release. The floor is the single fact that
	// tells the two apart, so the reverse-order decref loop lives here and cannot drift between them.
	//
	// Reverse, because that is the order ownership is conventionally released in — the last thing
	// built is the first torn down — and because a later binding may (once struct fields land) hold
	// a reference INTO an earlier one, so releasing the outer first would be releasing the thing
	// still being pointed at. Nothing at this rung can tell the two orders apart; the convention
	// costs nothing and is the one that stays right.
	//
	// `anchor` is the token of the statement that made control leave the scope (the `return`, the
	// block's `end`, the `break`/`continue`): no source token spawns a drop, and that statement is
	// where a reader looks for what happened to the values in it.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **`into` IS THE BLOCK THE DECREFS LAND IN, AND IT IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE IT USED TO BE
	// `self.currentBlock` — WHICH IS NOT THE SAME FACT.** Control leaves a scope at a block a caller
	// KNOWS; `currentBlock` is where the parser last emitted, and after a nested `if`/`while` those are
	// different blocks. `closeFunctionBody` believed they agreed — it said so in a comment — and put its
	// `retVoid` in the threaded `bodyEnd` while these decrefs went to a stale `currentBlock`. MEASURED:
	// a function ending in an `if` with no `else` released its locals ONLY on the taken path, so the
	// untaken path LEAKED and a loop body decref'd once PER ITERATION — a double free that corrupted
	// the heap and segfaulted (`specs-shv2/character-ownership.md`'s `local-dropped-once-after-a-loop`).
	// Naming the block is what makes that impossible to write: there is no implicit destination left to
	// go stale, and every caller states the block it means.
	function emitScopeDrops(into IrBlock, floor BindingMark, anchor Token)
		let saved = self.currentBlock
		self.currentBlock = into
		var i = self.ownedBindings.count()
		while i > floor 'eachOwned'
			i = i - 1
			let binding = try self.ownedBindings.get(i) otherwise panic("emitScopeDrops: ownedBindings.get OOB at {i} — the list is only appended to and drained at a block's end")
			// ⚠ **THE FOUR FACTS ARE READ OUT AND THE RECORD LET GO BEFORE ANYTHING IS EMITTED.** A binding
			// read out of `ownedBindings` is a BORROW of that array, and every emit below writes the parser's
			// own storage — so holding the record across them is refused (E3070), and the refusal is about a
			// real shape: this loop caches `count()` and walks downward, so an emit that enrolled a binding
			// would grow the array under a pointer into it. None of the four can dangle.
			let bindingMovedFrom = binding.movedFrom
			let bindingIsCell = binding.isCellBinding
			let bindingVarType = binding.varType
			let bindingValue = binding.boundValue
			// ⭐ **DROP-SKIP FOR A MOVED-FROM BINDING.** Its owned value was transferred to another
			// binding by a `let u = t` / `s = t`, which now owns and drops it — decref-ing it here too
			// would double-free (the exact bug #41 filed for `s = t`). The moved-from binding stays in
			// the list (removal would desync the block/loop drop marks), so the SKIP is what makes a
			// moved-out value drop exactly once. This one guard serves every exit that funnels through
			// here — `return`, a block fall-through, a `break`/`continue`.
			if not bindingMovedFrom 'liveOwner'
				// ⭐⭐ **A CELL IS DROPPED IN TWO STEPS, BECAUSE IT OWNS TWO THINGS (PBR-2)** — the record it holds
				// and the one-word box holding it. The occupant is LOADED rather than remembered, for
				// `reassignCellBinding`'s reason: a callee may have replaced it through the very pointer this
				// binding hands out, so the value the parser last saw is not what the cell contains. Releasing that
				// would free a record the cell no longer holds and leak the one it does — which is exactly the leak
				// `refcount-byref-rvalue-scratch-slot` was written to pin.
				//
				// ⚠ Only a cell this frame OWNS reaches here: a by-reference PARAMETER's cell is the caller's and is
				// never enrolled (`bindParameters`), so `ownedBindings` holds only locals and r-value scratch cells.
				if bindingIsCell 'aCellOwnsTwoThings'
					if self.signatures.typeIsManaged(bindingVarType, from: self.artifact.typeNames) 'cellHoldsAManagedRecord'
						self.emitDecref(self.emitCellLoad(bindingVarType, box: bindingValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor)), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
					end 'cellHoldsAManagedRecord'

					// The BOX, freed explicitly rather than through `emitDecref` — see `allocateCell` for why the
					// tag-routed drop would run the CONTENT's destructor over an 8-byte cell.
					self.emitOwnershipRuntimeCall(MmDecrefName, args: singletonArgs(bindingValue), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
					continue
				end 'aCellOwnsTwoThings'

				self.emitDecref(bindingValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
			end 'liveOwner'
		end 'eachOwned'
		self.currentBlock = saved
	end 'emitScopeDrops'

	// Emit a single drop — the one shape a dropped owned heap value releases through, shared by every drop
	// site: `emitScopeDrops` (a binding at scope exit), `drainPendingTemps` (an unconsumed temporary at
	// statement end), and `emitStringInterp` (its consumed scratch buffers). One door, so the drop's op
	// shape lives in ONE place and cannot drift across the callers. The result is `void` because a decref
	// returns nothing — the tag `ValueTypeTag.void` exists for exactly this, a ValueId the op shape demands
	// and no rule may ever be asked about.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE DROP DISPATCH, DONE STATICALLY** — the parser knows the type at every drop, so there is no
	// stored destructor pointer and no `callIndirect` (`StringRuntime.buildStrDecref` has the full why).
	// ⭐ **AND THE ONE DROP THAT IS *NOT* STATIC (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).** An owned existential's
	// conformer is a run-time fact, so its release takes the witness as a SECOND argument and reads
	// `destroyFunc@8` out of the table — the only drop in the parser whose callee cannot be chosen from the
	// value's type alone. It is a separate emit rather than a widened `decrefCalleeFor` because that router
	// answers a NAME and this needs an argument list; `decrefCalleeFor`'s own `interfaceRef` guard says so
	// from the other side.
	function emitDecref(value ValueId, range SourceRange)
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			self.emitOwnershipRuntimeCall(DropExistentialName, args: pairArgs(self.witnessOfValue(value), second: value), range: range)
			return
		end 'existential'

		let callee = self.decrefCalleeFor(value)
		// ⭐ **A RECORD THIS SHARED BODY GAVE A DESCRIPTOR-MEDIATED REFERENCE RELEASES IT BEFORE ITS BOX GOES**
		// (W41-opaque). Its own cascade cannot: the layout's `T` field is not managed in the DECLARATION view,
		// which is right for every OTHER reader of that layout and is exactly why the obligation is carried on
		// the value. See `recordsOwningOpaqueSlots` for the leak this closes and why the mark is per-value.
		//
		// ⛔ **UNLESS THE CASCADE ABOUT TO RUN *IS* THE DICTIONARY ONE, WHICH RELEASES THE SAME SLOTS
		// (BATCH41)** — and then this arm would release them a second time. The two are read off ONE callee
		// rather than each being argued unreachable from the other: a mark is a fact about the FRAME, a
		// dictionary destructor is a fact about the TYPE, and nothing structurally keeps a value from carrying
		// both. Where it does, the destructor is the correct releaser, because it fires at refcount ZERO and
		// this arm fires unconditionally.
		if self.recordsOwningOpaqueSlots.contains(value) and not isDictionaryDestructorCallee(callee) 'ownsOpaqueSlots'
			self.emitOpaqueSlotReleases(value, range: range)
		end 'ownsOpaqueSlots'

		// ⭐ An OPAQUE drop reads the enclosing instance's layout descriptor at run time, so it needs the
		// parameter that carries it — reserved before this body was parsed. Record a disagreement rather than
		// emitting a call the lowering can only abort on; `parseFunction` refuses it with a position (W10).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE DICTIONARY DESTRUCTOR READS ONE TOO AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT TESTED HERE**, because it could
		// never answer: `instanceBoxDropCallee` picks that callee only where the descriptor already exists, so
		// an arm for it would be a gate that cannot fail. The guarantee is held there, where the choice is
		// made, and `LowerMaxonToStd.appendWholeLayoutDescriptor` panics if the two ever come apart.
		if callee.equals(DropTypeParamName) and not self.functionCarriesLayoutParam 'noDescriptorToDropThrough'
			self.functionDroppedOpaqueWithoutDescriptor = true
		end 'noDescriptorToDropThrough'
		self.emitOwnershipRuntimeCall(callee, args: singletonArgs(value), range: range)
	end 'emitDecref'

	// ⭐⭐ **RELEASE EVERY OPAQUE `T` SLOT OF A RECORD THIS BODY BUILT AND IS ABOUT TO FREE (W41-opaque)** —
	// the drop dual of `moveOpaqueValueInto`'s store, emitted only for a record the store actually referenced
	// (`recordsOwningOpaqueSlots`).
	//
	// The slot is LOADED rather than the retained SSA value being remembered, because a tuple slot is
	// WRITABLE (`t.0 = x`) — a remembered value would be the one the write replaced, and releasing that is a
	// double free at the far end. The load is what the record actually holds at this moment, which is the only
	// thing the drop may act on.
	//
	// The descriptor is guaranteed: the store that set the mark went through `__retain_type_param`, which owes
	// the same descriptor and records its absence itself, so this cannot be the first site to need one.
	function emitOpaqueSlotReleases(box ValueId, range SourceRange)
		let layout = self.declaredLayoutOfRecordValue(box)
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let declared = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: i, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
			if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notOpaque'
				continue
			end 'notOpaque'

			self.emitOwnershipRuntimeCall(DropTypeParamName, args: singletonArgs(self.emitFieldLoad(box, layout: layout, index: i, range: range)), range: range)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'emitOpaqueSlotReleases'

	// The declared layout of a record VALUE that took a descriptor-mediated reference — a `Self{…}` or a tuple
	// literal, both `structRef`-tagged by `emitOwnedStructBox`.
	//
	// ⚠ **A UNION BOX IS THE ONE OTHER THING `moveInPayload` FILLS, AND IT CANNOT REACH HERE TODAY** — not by
	// arrangement but because the construct is refused: a generic union instantiated over a type parameter is
	// unspellable in a return position (`E2001` on `returns Holder with E`) and `E3005` through an inner
	// alias, so no shared body ever builds one and no `T` payload is ever stored. The panic states that rather
	// than a layout being silently skipped, because the day the front end admits the construct this cascade
	// owes it a release and a quiet miss would be a leak.
	function declaredLayoutOfRecordValue(box ValueId) returns StructLayout
		let name = self.valueTypeName(box)
		match self.signatures.structOf(name) 'declared'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("declaredLayoutOfRecordValue: v{box} took a descriptor-mediated reference into a slot of '{String.from(name)}', which declares no struct — a UNION box is the only other record `moveInPayload` fills, and a generic union over a type parameter is refused at the front end, so admitting one means this cascade now owes its payload a release")
		end 'declared'
	end 'declaredLayoutOfRecordValue'

	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY SYNTHESIZED RETAIN OR RELEASE GOES THROUGH HERE, AND THE ONE THING THIS DOOR ADDS IS THAT
	// IT IS NEVER ROUTED TO A BLOCK-FORM `try`'s HANDLER.** These calls are `void`, they are the compiler's
	// and not the author's, and — the reason it must be stated rather than assumed — they are emitted INTO
	// the block-form router's own output: `tryBlockThrowEdgeTarget` fills a landing pad with them while the
	// stack is still non-empty and `currentBlock` points at that pad, and `reconcileMovesAtMerge` appends
	// them to already-live edge blocks. Routed, one would split the very block the router is building.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS INERT TODAY AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY IT IS WRITTEN DOWN.** No drop or retain callee
	// (`__mm_incref`, `__mm_decref`, `__str_decref`, `__managed_decref`, `__destruct_*`, `__drop_existential`)
	// carries a throws clause, so the router already declines on the clause — an accident of which runtime
	// families are typed, not a property of this code. The day one of them becomes throwing, a silent
	// re-entry is not the failure anyone would want to debug.
	function emitOwnershipRuntimeCall(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
		let savedRoutingSuppressed = self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = true
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = savedRoutingSuppressed
	end 'emitOwnershipRuntimeCall'

	// ⭐ **PATH-SENSITIVE MOVE STATE — CAPTURE.** Snapshot the `movedFrom` bit (and current value) of every
	// owned binding in scope, so a branch's moves can be UNDONE for a sibling path (`restoreMoveMark`) or
	// reconciled at a join (`reconcileMovesAtMerge`). The twin of `snapshotBindings` for the drop-gating
	// bit. Positional over `ownedBindings`; see `MoveMark` for why only `movedFrom` rides and why the
	// height is stable at every capture point.
	function captureMoveMark() returns MoveMark
		var moved = BoolArray.create()
		var values = ValueIdArray.create()
		for binding in self.ownedBindings 'each'
			moved.push(binding.movedFrom)
			values.push(binding.boundValue)
		end 'each'
		return MoveMark.create(moved, values: values)
	end 'captureMoveMark'

	// ⭐ **PATH-SENSITIVE MOVE STATE — RESTORE.** Rewind every owned binding's `movedFrom` bit to a
	// captured mark, undoing the moves a sibling branch made so the next path is parsed against the
	// pre-branch ownership state (the twin of `restoreBindings`). Written THROUGH the shared `VarInfo`
	// reference, so every holder observes it. The owned height must match the capture — a branch's own
	// owned bindings were popped before this runs (see `MoveMark`), so a mismatch is a lockstep break.
	function restoreMoveMark(mark MoveMark)
		if self.ownedBindings.count() != mark.moved.count() 'drifted'
			panic("restoreMoveMark: owned-binding height {self.ownedBindings.count()} differs from the captured mark's {mark.moved.count()} — a branch's owned bindings must be popped before its move state is restored")
		end 'drifted'
		var i = 0
		while i < self.ownedBindings.count() 'each'
			var binding = try self.ownedBindings.get(i) otherwise panic("restoreMoveMark: ownedBindings.get OOB at {i}")
			binding.movedFrom = try mark.moved.get(i) otherwise panic("restoreMoveMark: mark.moved.get OOB at {i}")
			i = i + 1
		end 'each'
	end 'restoreMoveMark'

	// ⭐⭐ **PATH-SENSITIVE MOVE STATE — RECONCILE AT A JOIN.** Given the edges that REACH a merge (each an
	// already-live predecessor block paired with the move state it carries), settle every owned binding to
	// a UNIFORM post-merge state so the later scope-exit drop is correct on every path:
	//   • moved on SOME edges, live on OTHERS ⇒ the value was given away on the moved edges but is still
	//     owned on the live ones. Emit its drop on each LIVE edge (using that edge's own value), and mark
	//     the binding moved after the merge — settled on every path, so the scope-exit drop skips it.
	//   • moved on ALL edges ⇒ already given away everywhere; mark moved, drop nothing.
	//   • live on ALL edges ⇒ still owned everywhere; mark live, drop nothing — the scope exit drops it.
	// A binding moved before the whole construct is moved on every reaching edge (the flag is monotonic
	// within a path), so it stays moved; a binding no branch touched is live on every edge and stays live.
	// The reaching edges are UNTERMINATED blocks (a terminated branch dropped its own values and
	// contributes no edge), so a decref appended to one lands before the merge branch the caller emits
	// next. Called only with at least one reaching edge — an all-terminating construct has a dead merge and
	// leaves the move state alone.
	function reconcileMovesAtMerge(edges ReachingMoveEdgeArray, anchor Token)
		// The caller must pass at least one reaching edge — an all-terminating construct has a dead merge and
		// must NOT call this at all. With no edges every binding would read `anyMoved == false` and be marked
		// live, silently clobbering an already-moved binding's state (a mass-leak footgun on the most delicate
		// path). Enforced loudly rather than trusted, since a wrong move state here is a leak or a double-free.
		if edges.count() == 0 'noEdges'
			panic("reconcileMovesAtMerge: called with no reaching edges — an all-terminating construct has a dead merge and must leave the move state alone rather than reconcile it")
		end 'noEdges'

		let n = self.ownedBindings.count()
		for edge in edges 'checkAligned'
			if edge.mark.moved.count() != n 'drifted'
				panic("reconcileMovesAtMerge: a reaching edge's mark holds {edge.mark.moved.count()} entries but {n} owned bindings are in scope — the marks drifted out of lockstep with the owned-binding stack")
			end 'drifted'
		end 'checkAligned'

		var i = 0
		while i < n 'eachBinding'
			var anyMoved = false
			var anyLive = false
			for edge in edges 'scan'
				let movedHere = try edge.mark.moved.get(i) otherwise panic("reconcileMovesAtMerge: edge mark moved.get OOB at {i}")
				if movedHere 'movedHere'
					anyMoved = true
				end 'movedHere' else 'liveHere'
					anyLive = true
				end 'liveHere'
			end 'scan'

			if anyMoved and anyLive 'mixed'
				let saved = self.currentBlock
				for edge in edges 'dropLive'
					let movedOnEdge = try edge.mark.moved.get(i) otherwise panic("reconcileMovesAtMerge: edge mark moved.get OOB at {i}")
					if not movedOnEdge 'liveEdge'
						self.currentBlock = edge.block
						let liveValue = try edge.mark.values.get(i) otherwise panic("reconcileMovesAtMerge: edge mark values.get OOB at {i}")
						self.emitDecref(liveValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
					end 'liveEdge'
				end 'dropLive'
				self.currentBlock = saved
			end 'mixed'

			var binding = try self.ownedBindings.get(i) otherwise panic("reconcileMovesAtMerge: ownedBindings.get OOB at {i}")
			binding.movedFrom = anyMoved
			i = i + 1
		end 'eachBinding'
	end 'reconcileMovesAtMerge'

	// ⭐ **WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY DROPS `value`** — one tag-and-type router, extended per managed kind (Wave
	// D's ruling generalised):
	//   • a STRING (`__str_decref`) frees its record AND the external buffer a grown String leaves behind
	//     (`parent@32`/`buffer@0`), which the generic decref structurally cannot reach;
	//   • a STRUCT box that owns a managed field (`__destruct_<Struct>`) drops each managed field through ITS
	//     own destructor, then frees the box — because the generic decref would leak them (P1.4a wave 2);
	//   • a MANAGED-PAYLOAD union box (`__destruct_<U>`) runs its own tag-conditional cascade — drop each
	//     live managed field through ITS own destructor, then free the box — because a union box carries
	//     owned payloads the generic decref would leak;
	//   • everything else (a SCALAR union box, a struct box whose fields are all scalars, a raw interpolation
	//     buffer) is the generic refcounted `__mm_decref` — its `TrivialDestructor` frees exactly the box.
	// A struct/union routed to a cascade is decided off `managedFieldDropCallee` / `unionBoxDropCallee`,
	// which wrap the SAME predicates the destructor synthesis (and `noteDestructorUsage`) emit from, so a
	// box routed to a cascade here always has one built for it.
	function decrefCalleeFor(value ValueId) returns ByteArray
		// ⭐ **A PROMISE IS A GREEN-THREAD HANDLE, NOT A REFCOUNTED HEAP BOX (P1.5-B2 #88).** An un-awaited
		// `async` spawn dropped at scope exit / a re-arm routes to `__gt_promise_drop`, which reclaims AND
		// cancels the thread — NOT `__mm_decref` (a promise owns no `__mm_alloc` box; the handle is the GT
		// struct pointer). Checked FIRST, off the promise mark column, because a promise's TAG is `integer`
		// (its awaited scalar type), so the tag-based routing below cannot tell it from a bare int.
		if self.valueOwnsGreenThread(value) 'promise'
			return GtPromiseDropName
		end 'promise'
		let tag = self.tagOf(value)
		// ⚠ `tagIsByteRecord`, NOT `tag == ValueTypeTag.string`: WHICH DROP a value takes is decided by its
		// RECORD, and a Character's record is a String's. Left narrow, a dropped Character falls past every
		// arm below to the generic `__mm_decref`, which frees the box without releasing the external buffer a
		// detached record owns — a leak on exactly the values this rung mints.
		if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecord'
			return StrDecrefName
		end 'byteRecord'
		// ⭐ AN OPAQUE TYPE-PARAMETER binding (`push(item Element)`'s `item`, enrolled owned in a shared generic
		// body, P1.7 slice 3b-v). Its element destructor is not known statically — the body compiles ONCE for
		// every instantiation — so it drops through `__drop_type_param(descriptor, value)`, which reads the
		// enclosing instance's layout descriptor `destroyFunc@40` at run time (0 for a trivial instantiation →
		// inert). `lowerRuntimeCall` prepends the descriptor argument, the drop twin of the opaque array create.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaque'
			return DropTypeParamName
		end 'opaque'
		// A struct box: `managedFieldDropCallee` returns `__destruct_<Struct>` for one that owns a managed
		// field and the trivial `__mm_decref` otherwise, reading only allocation-free `Map.contains` /
		// per-field checks (no throwaway box), so it needs no hot-path guard of its own.
		if tagIsStructRef(tag) 'struct'
			return self.structBoxDropCallee(value)
		end 'struct'
		// ⭐ A GENERIC-INSTANCE box (`Box with String`, P1.6-B2) drops through its own `__destruct_<mangled>`
		// cascade when the instance owns managed heap, else the trivial `__mm_decref`. The gid is on the value's
		// name column (the same access `materializeLayoutArg` reads at lowering); the drop callee is decided
		// INSIDE `ProgramSignatures` over the instance's own registry/interner — a trivial instance (`IntBox`)
		// answers `__mm_decref`, byte-identical to the pre-B2 fall-through, so existing goldens do not move.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return self.instanceBoxDropCallee(value)
		end 'instance'
		// ⚠ **THE `named` PRE-CHECK IS A HOT-PATH ALLOCATION GUARD, NOT A TIDY EARLY RETURN** — the exact
		// one `valueIsUnion` documents. This runs for EVERY dropped owned value (an interpolation buffer),
		// and `enumMatchFor` returns a `DeclaredEnum` — a heap-boxed Maxon union VALUE even for its
		// `undeclared` case. Only a `named`-tagged value can be a union box; an interp buffer is `integer`,
		// so gating here is what keeps a String/interp drop from boxing a throwaway `DeclaredEnum.undeclared`
		// (measured on scale-test: the whole `allocsDelta` this rung added collapses to 0 with this guard).
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notUnion'
			return MmDecrefName
		end 'notUnion'
		let decl = self.enumMatchFor(value)
		if enumMatchIsDeclared(decl) 'union'
			// The drop callee is decided INSIDE `ProgramSignatures`, over the layout's own interner —
			// NOT here with `self.artifact.typeNames`. A signatures layout's payload ids live in the
			// signatures interner, and resolving them against this file's interner misread a managed
			// payload whenever a ranged alias shifted the interning order (a leak / wild-free, OPEN #52).
			return self.signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(requireEnumLayout(decl).name)
		end 'union'
		// A `named` value that is not a declared union is a declared STRUCT read from a FIELD: a struct
		// field's value carries `named`, not `structRef` (`fieldTypeOf` re-tags only function/generic-alias
		// fields), so the OLD value dropped on `h.n = …` arrives here. Route it through the SAME
		// `managedFieldDropCallee` the `structRef` arm and the destructor synthesis use, so a MANAGED-owning
		// struct drops through its `__destruct_<T>` cascade — the trivial `MmDecrefName` frees only the box
		// and LEAKS its managed fields (exit 101). A trivial struct answers `MmDecrefName` there too
		// (byte-identical), so only the managed case changes. The `containsStruct` gate keeps a `named` that
		// names no declared struct (a ranged typealias) on the trivial path, where `managedFieldDropCallee`
		// would otherwise panic (it classifies only struct/union/generic-alias fields).
		if self.signatures.containsStruct(self.valueTypeName(value)) 'struct'
			return self.structBoxDropCallee(value)
		end 'struct'
		return MmDecrefName
	end 'decrefCalleeFor'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHICH CASCADE FREES A RECORD BOX — the ordinary one, or the DICTIONARY one (W183).** The
	// declared-record twin of `instanceBoxDropCallee`, and it carries that function's whole argument at the one
	// record shape the instance family cannot see: a TUPLE whose elements stand at the enclosing generic's own
	// parameters. `typeIsManaged` answers `false` for a bare parameter, so `managedFieldDropCallee` hands back
	// the trivial `__mm_decref` — which frees the box and STRANDS the reference `Parser.moveOpaqueValueInto`
	// took into the slot through `retainFunc@64`.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS GATED ON THE BODY HAVING A DESCRIPTOR, exactly as `instanceBoxDropCallee` is**: a frame with
	// no descriptor has nothing to release THROUGH, and it is also a frame no real protocol ever reached — a
	// caller that carries none hands such a chain the parameterised blob whose ownership words are all zero,
	// so nothing was retained there either (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitInstanceDescriptorAddr`'s mint arm).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND ON THE RECORD'S SLOTS BEING *THIS* BODY'S OWN PARAMETERS**, which is `recordOpaqueSlotDeclaration`
	// carrying a declaration rather than a `bool`: the descriptor a method holds lays out its ENCLOSING type's
	// `uses` list, and releasing another declaration's parameter positions into it is the position mismatch
	// `BATCH41`'s review measured at exit 139 inside that rung's own cure.
	function structBoxDropCallee(value ValueId) returns ByteArray
		let name = self.valueTypeName(value)
		if self.functionCarriesLayoutParam and self.recordRestsThisBodysOpaqueSlot(name) 'aRecordOverThisBodysParameters'
			return dictionaryDestructorSymbol(name)
		end 'aRecordOverThisBodysParameters'

		return managedFieldDropCallee(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: self.typeOf(value))
	end 'structBoxDropCallee'

	// Does `name` denote a record resting a slot at a parameter of the type THIS body is a method of? The two
	// halves of the gate above that are questions about the record rather than about the frame, asked together
	// because either alone admits a record this body cannot release correctly.
	function recordRestsThisBodysOpaqueSlot(name ByteArray) returns bool
		match self.recordOpaqueSlotOwnerOf(name) 'restsASlot'
			none then return false
			declaredBy(owner) then return owner.equals(self.enclosingType)
		end 'restsASlot'
	end 'recordRestsThisBodysOpaqueSlot'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHICH CASCADE FREES AN INSTANCE BOX — the MONOMORPHIZED one, or the DICTIONARY one (BATCH41).**
	// A concrete `Box with String` has a `__destruct_Box_String` that resolves every field's release
	// statically. The instance a SHARED generic body holds — `BagIter with Element` inside
	// `type Bag uses Element` — does not: its `slot` stands at a bare parameter, `typeIsManaged` answers
	// `false` for it, and `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` therefore hands back the trivial `__mm_decref`, which
	// frees the box and STRANDS whatever reference the slot holds.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **MEASURED, and it is the whole reason this family exists: with the constructor-feed retain in place
	// and no dictionary destructor, three `generic-opaque-cursor-element` cases exit 101.** `Bag.contains`
	// (`stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable`, re-parsed per conformer) decrefs the iterator record on
	// both loop exits, and that decref was the trivial one.
	//
	// ⚠ The gate is `instanceRestsARetainedSlot` and NOT `instanceRestsAnUnnameableSlot`: the retain that puts
	// a reference in the slot reads every interned row (`typeParamFeedCanOwnAReference`), so the release must
	// too — see that predicate's header for the two kinds and why a refusal's narrower one would leak here.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND IT IS ALSO GATED ON THE BODY HAVING A DESCRIPTOR, WHICH IS A MEASURED BOUNDARY AND NOT A
	// CONVENIENCE.** Routed unconditionally, the drop of a co-owned parameter — `Holder.create(cell Cell)`
	// increfing `cell` into `Self{cell: cell}` and releasing its own reference — becomes a call needing a
	// descriptor the method reserves none of, and `requireDescriptorForOpaqueDrops` refuses it: MEASURED,
	// EIGHT committed programs (six `generic-type-substitution`, `generic-opaque-call-result`,
	// `generic-type-nested-array-typealias`), every one of which is CORRECT as it stands, because the record
	// is co-owned by the `Self{…}` it was stored into and this decref provably cannot be the last.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT THE GATE LEAVES IS EXACTLY TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR, AND IT IS NARROWER THAN IT SOUNDS.** A body
	// carrying NO descriptor that is nevertheless the LAST owner of such a record falls through to
	// `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee`, which frees the box and leaves the slot — a LEAK the gate reports, not a
	// use-after-free. ⚠ **The one spelling of it that could be written down does not COMPILE, on this tree or
	// on the merge base**: `static discard(cell Cell) returns Integer` — a consumed declaration-view parameter
	// never stored — is refused by `E3005: expected 'Cell_T<digest>', got 'StrCell'`, because a static that
	// does not return `Self` never has its parameters substituted through the receiver instance. So the
	// residue is stated as a BOUND on this routing rather than as a measured defect; the shapes that DO reach
	// a descriptor-less body all store what they take.
	//
	// Closing it properly is a descriptor-RESERVATION question (which method shapes get one) and not a
	// drop-routing one — and widening the reservation was MEASURED to refuse programs of its own against
	// `staticLayoutNeedsSelfReturn`, so it is a rung and is reported as one.
	//
	// ⭐ It is ALSO the guarantee `emitDecref` does not restate: because the dictionary callee is chosen only
	// where the descriptor exists, no drop site can emit one without a descriptor to prepend — which is why
	// that function's reservation check has no arm for this family and `LowerMaxonToStd`'s prepend panics
	// rather than testing.
	function instanceBoxDropCallee(value ValueId) returns ByteArray
		let gid = self.valueNameId(value)
		if self.functionCarriesLayoutParam and self.signatures.instanceRestsARetainedSlot(gid) 'aDeclarationViewRestingARetainedSlot'
			return dictionaryDestructorSymbol(self.signatures.mangleGenericInstance(gid))
		end 'aDeclarationViewRestingARetainedSlot'

		return self.signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(gid)
	end 'instanceBoxDropCallee'

	// ⭐ **CALL AN EMITTED RUNTIME ENTRY POINT** (`__mm_alloc`, `__mm_decref`) — the door the parser
	// reaches the heap through, and the only one.
	//
	// It is NOT `emitCall`, and the difference is what a runtime call IS: `emitCall` types its
	// result from the whole-program signature index and slots its arguments against a declaration.
	// The runtime has neither — it is built at the STD tier, after lowering (`installMmRuntime`),
	// so no file declares it, `mintCallResult` would type it `unresolved` (a tag that PANICS at
	// lowering), and `SemanticCheck` would report E3004 against a name the user never wrote.
	// `isRuntimeCallee` is what tells the two apart, and it is one predicate read by the three
	// places that care (here, `validateCall`, `lowerCall`).
	//
	// So the caller states the result's type, because the caller is the compiler and it KNOWS: an
	// `__mm_alloc` returns the struct it just built, a `__mm_decref` returns nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY ARGUMENT IS POSITIONAL, IN DECLARATION ORDER — FOR A `__`-PREFIXED RUNTIME CALLEE.** There
	// is no label to reorder by: the runtime has no parameter names for a label to match, so `lowerCall`
	// passes them straight through. `emptyArgLabels` is what says so on the op, and `lowerCall`'s guard is
	// what makes a labelled runtime call fail loudly rather than mis-slot.
	//
	// ⭐ **`argLabels` IS THE ONE EXCEPTION, AND IT EXISTS FOR THE CORPUS SEGMENTER (see
	// `CorpusFindGraphemeEnd`).** A callee that IS declared — a stdlib free function this compiler routes a
	// builtin method to — is slotted against its declaration by `slotCallArgs`, so a labelled parameter
	// needs its label present or the argument lands in the wrong slot. Supplying it is therefore the whole
	// difference between the two kinds of callee, and it is a PARAMETER rather than a second emitter
	// because everything else about the two is identical: same op, same routing hook, same result mint.
	//
	// ⚠ An EMPTY column means "all positional", never "zero arguments" — `slotCallArgs` INDEXES this
	// column, so a positional call must still hand it a real per-argument one (`emptyArgLabels`'s own
	// header). The two cases cannot be confused: a call with no arguments has nothing to slot either way.
	function emitRuntimeCall(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultNameId TypeNameId, range SourceRange, argLabels ByteArrayArray = NoRuntimeCallLabels) returns ValueId
		return self.emitRuntimeCallInto(self.mintValue(resultTag, nameId: resultNameId), callee: callee, args: args, range: range, argLabels: argLabels)
	end 'emitRuntimeCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME EMISSION, ONTO A RESULT THE CALLER ALREADY MINTED — for the one construct whose
	// ARGUMENTS have to name the record the call will hand back.** `<Union>.fromName("case", payload…)` moves
	// its payload arguments in at the parser (`parseEnumFromNameCase`), and a move-in into a slot declared at
	// an opaque type parameter MARKS the record that owes the slot's release (`moveOpaqueValueInto` →
	// `markRecordOwningOpaqueSlots`). That record is this call's result, and the call cannot be emitted first:
	// `try` binds to the LAST op the target emitted (`rewriteLastCallToTryCall`), so the move-in ops must
	// precede it. Minting the result up front is what lets both facts hold at once.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPLIT IS THE MINT, WHICH IS WHY THE FRONT DOOR IS NOT A THIN WRAPPER.** Every other runtime call
	// wants its result minted from the tag and the type name, and must not be free to mint it twice or from a
	// different tag than the one the op is emitted under.
	function emitRuntimeCallInto(result ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, range SourceRange, argLabels ByteArrayArray) returns ValueId
		var labels = argLabels
		if labels.count() == 0 'allPositional'
			labels = emptyArgLabels(args)
		end 'allPositional'

		// The index the op below will occupy — read before the append, so the ROUTING hook after it names an
		// op rather than re-deriving "the last one" from a block whose contents it does not own.
		let opIdx = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		// `notMinted`: a runtime entry's callee is not the author's bytes either, but its noun is a question
		// about the NAME — `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod` answers it, keyed by name, and may be. Only the
		// primitive-static mint needs the per-SITE bit. See `CalleeMint`.
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.call(result, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: labels, argRanges: uniformArgRanges(args, range: range), argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, argDefaultSlots: NoDefaultArgSlots, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted), range: range)
		// ⭐ **ONE OF THE TWO ROUTING DOORS FOR A BLOCK-FORM `try` BODY** — this one carries every
		// compiler-emitted callee, which is how `arr.get(i)`, `map.get(k)` and a checked divide inside such a
		// body reach the shared handler. It declines instantly (one field read) when there is no enclosing
		// block-form `try`, which is every call in almost every program.
		//
		// ⚠ **`result` IS HANDED OVER EVEN THOUGH NOTHING HAS ENROLLED IT YET, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** A
		// runtime call's managed result is tracked by its CALLER after this returns
		// (`emitContainerElementAccessor`), so the router's pending-list test answers false here today — by an
		// ordering nobody had written down. Passing it makes the rule "the routed call's own result never
		// rides the error edge" hold at both doors by statement rather than by accident, so a future door
		// that enrols before emitting is already correct.
		self.routeBareThrowingCallToTryBlock(callee, opIdx: opIdx, result: result)
		return result
	end 'emitRuntimeCallInto'

	// ⭐ **EMIT A VOID `__mm_incref` OF `value`** — the one spelling of "take a second owner of this box,
	// discarding the returned nothing". Single-sources every site that increfs a managed box in place:
	// `retainThrownField` (#64 — a thrown borrowed-container field), `retainBorrowedPayload` (D1b — a managed
	// payload bound out of a borrowed union), the two `try … otherwise` phi reconciliations (the ok-edge
	// borrow that merges with an owned fallback, and `transferFallbackToPhi`'s aliased-binding error edge),
	// and `coOwnConcreteAggregateFeed`, which both of `handleEscapingBorrowFeed`'s sinks take a co-owned
	// aggregate's reference through. `__mm_retain` is the RETURNING twin
	// (`retainBorrowedAggregate`) for when a FRESH SSA name is needed; this void form is for when the box is
	// increfed in place and the caller already holds the ValueId it means.
	function emitIncref(value ValueId, range SourceRange)
		self.emitOwnershipRuntimeCall(MmIncrefName, args: singletonArgs(value), range: range)
		// ⭐ **AND THE OP IS THE FACT: after this line `value`'s allocation has two owners.** The mark rides
		// here rather than at the named doors above because that is what makes it unforgettable — see
		// `markValueCoOwnsHeap`. It matters even for a value that was FRESH: `writeBackPayload` increfs a
		// payload the arm moved out of a box it solely owned, and the box is now the second owner of it, so a
		// nested `match` on that same binding must retain rather than steal the slot back.
		self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(value)
	end 'emitIncref'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE `__mm_alloc` CALL.** Every heap box the compiler builds — a struct record, a fused
	// String record, an interpolation scratch buffer, a boxed union — is allocated through here, so the
	// `__mm_alloc(size, destructor)` ABI lives in exactly ONE place: the destructor is the SECOND
	// argument, and a box whose contents cascade to nothing passes `TrivialDestructor`. An added or
	// reordered alloc argument that reached three of those call sites and missed the fourth would
	// corrupt that box silently — nothing but this consolidation forces the four to agree. `size` is an
	// already-emitted value (a size literal, or the interpolation length sum), so the caller decides how
	// it is computed; and enrolling the result as an owned temp is the caller's decision too (a struct
	// box is not tracked, a union and an interpolation String are), so `trackOwnedTemp` stays at the
	// call site rather than hiding in here.
	// ⭐ `tag` is the mm-trace label for a box that is NOT any declared type's — `MmTag.Untagged` for every
	// box whose type the LOWERING can name from `resultTag`/`resultNameId`, which is the common case. It is
	// recorded against the `__mm_alloc` call op immediately below, and read by nothing but
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.allocTagIndexOf`. See `Project.allocTagSites` for why it travels beside the type
	// rather than in it.
	function emitBoxAlloc(size ValueId, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultNameId TypeNameId, range SourceRange, tag MmTag = MmTag.Untagged) returns ValueId
		let destructor = self.emitLiteral(TrivialDestructor, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		var allocArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		allocArgs.push(size)
		allocArgs.push(destructor)
		// ⚠ IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the call is emitted, and it must be: the recorder keys on "the op the very
		// next append creates", which is exactly the index `emitRuntimeCall` reads for the same op. An
		// intervening emit would tag a literal instead of the allocation.
		self.recordAllocTag(tag)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(MmAllocName, args: allocArgs, resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: resultNameId, range: range)
	end 'emitBoxAlloc'

	// Record a box's mm-trace tag on the artifact, unless it has none to record. `Untagged` is the ordinary
	// answer and writing it would fill the sparse table with the entry its absence already means.
	function recordAllocTag(tag MmTag)
		match tag 'tag'
			Untagged then return
			StringRecord to ParameterCell then break 'tag'
		end 'tag'

		self.artifact.recordAllocTagSite(tag)
	end 'recordAllocTag'

	// ⭐ **TRACK AN OWNED HEAP TEMPORARY.** The one door `emitStringInterp` reaches ownership through:
	// it records the value's provenance (so `parseBinding` knows the binding owns a drop) AND enrolls it in
	// the statement's pending-drop list (so an UNBOUND result is dropped at statement end). Both from one
	// call, so the two facts — "this value owns heap" and "someone must drop it" — cannot be set apart.
	//
	// ⚠ **`exclusivity` IS THREADED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, AND THIS DOOR IS EXACTLY WHY IT HAD TO BE.** Its
	// header read "a FRESH owned heap temporary" for as long as most of its callers were allocations, and three
	// of them are not: a `try … otherwise` result phi, a `match` result phi, and `coOwnAggregateAsTemp`, which
	// increfs somebody else's box. A door that had baked `sole` in here would have handed a stolen slot to each
	// of those. See `OwnedHeapExclusivity`.
	function trackOwnedTemp(value ValueId, exclusivity OwnedHeapExclusivity)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(value, exclusivity: exclusivity)
		self.pendingTempDrops.push(value)
	end 'trackOwnedTemp'

	// ⭐ **`trackOwnedTemp`'s EXACT INVERSE — the value is no longer a managed temporary at all (W35).** Its
	// one caller is `foldByteViewIntoStreamWrite`, which rewrites the call that PRODUCED the byte-view record
	// INTO the write that consumed it: there is no record any more, so both halves of the enrolment have to
	// go. Dropping only the pending entry would leave `valueOwnsHeap` set on what is now an integer byte
	// count, and a `let n = __Builtins.writeStdout(…)` would then bind a value the scope exit tries to
	// DECREF — which is why this is the inverse of the whole two-step and not of its second half.
	//
	// ⚠ It is NOT a general "cancel an ownership" door and must not become one: releasing a temporary whose
	// record still EXISTS is a leak. The caller's obligation is that the producing op is gone.
	function releaseOwnedTemp(value ValueId)
		try self.valueOwnsHeap.set(value, value: false) otherwise panic("releaseOwnedTemp: v{value} has no owned-heap slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
		// The exclusivity claim goes with the ownership it qualified — see `markValueCoOwnsHeap`.
		self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(value)
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(value)
	end 'releaseOwnedTemp'

	// Set `value`'s owned-heap provenance bit WITHOUT enrolling it as a statement-scoped pending temporary
	// — the half of `trackOwnedTemp` a CONSUMED PARAMETER needs (P1.4a wave 2). The param already drops at
	// FUNCTION scope exit through `ownedBindings` (or is skipped once moved into a field), so adding it to
	// `pendingTempDrops` too would drop it a SECOND time at the enclosing statement's end — the double-free
	// that made `Outer.create(inner)` free its own field. A temporary, by contrast, IS statement-scoped and
	// wants both.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF THE OWNED BIT, WHICH IS WHY THE EXCLUSIVITY ARGUMENT IS REQUIRED HERE.** Every
	// door that makes a value owned passes through this line, so a required `exclusivity` is a question no
	// producer — present or future — can leave unanswered: the compiler refuses the call. See
	// `OwnedHeapExclusivity` for what the two answers mean and for the six segfaults that came of having only
	// one bit to say them with.
	function markValueOwnsHeap(value ValueId, exclusivity OwnedHeapExclusivity)
		try self.valueOwnsHeap.set(value, value: true) otherwise panic("markValueOwnsHeap: v{value} has no owned-heap slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")

		match exclusivity 'howMany'
			sole then try self.valueOwnsHeapAlone.set(value, value: true) otherwise panic("markValueOwnsHeap: v{value}{SoleOwnershipSlotPanicTail}")
			coOwned then self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(value)
		end 'howMany'
	end 'markValueOwnsHeap'

	// ⭐⭐ **SOMETHING ELSE OWNS THIS ALLOCATION TOO — the one write of `false` into the exclusivity column**,
	// shared by the three statements that mean it: an ownership RELEASED (`releaseOwnedTemp` — a value that owns
	// nothing is the sole owner of nothing, and a stale `true` would be a destructive-move licence on an
	// integer), an ownership DECLARED co-owned by its producer (`markValueOwnsHeap`), and an ownership
	// DOWNGRADED by an incref.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE DOWNGRADE IS AUTOMATIC WHERE THE OTHER TWO ARE DECLARED, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** A value marked
	// `sole` can STOP being sole later, so the downgrade is a property of the OP that mints the second owner
	// rather than of the doors that reach it — the only form of it that cannot be forgotten. **EVERY such op
	// calls this on its SOURCE, and there are FIVE of them, not one:**
	//
	//   • `emitIncref` — `__mm_incref` in place (a write-back taking the box's own reference, a consuming
	//     argument co-owning a borrow, a `try … otherwise` phi edge). Its operand may already carry a fresh
	//     allocation's `sole` claim: `writeBackPayload` increfs a payload the arm moved out of a box it solely
	//     owned, and the box becomes that payload's second owner.
	//   • `emitRetainCall` — `__mm_retain`, the RETURNING twin, which mints a fresh name for the same box.
	//   • `retainBorrowedByteRecord` — `__str_retain`, an incref on its heap branch.
	//   • `emitRetainExistentialCall` — `__retain_existential`, an incref through the witness.
	//   • `coOwnBorrowedOpaque` — `__retain_typeparam`, an incref through the instance descriptor.
	//
	// ⛔ **THIS LIST USED TO SAY "co-ownership is not expressible without that op" AND NAME ONLY THE FIRST —
	// false, and it was the invitation to the edit that would break it.** The other four were latent rather than
	// live (their sources are gated borrowed, or are records with no nullable slot), which is exactly why fixing
	// it was free and why leaving it would have been the trap: a sentence claiming completeness is load-bearing
	// only while it is true. This rung's whole thesis, applied to its own new code.
	//
	// The reverse direction has no door at all: nothing may promote a co-owner to sole, because a decref this
	// frame emits cannot be known to be the allocation's last.
	function markValueCoOwnsHeap(value ValueId)
		try self.valueOwnsHeapAlone.set(value, value: false) otherwise panic("markValueCoOwnsHeap: v{value}{SoleOwnershipSlotPanicTail}")
	end 'markValueCoOwnsHeap'

	// Mark `value` as an `async` spawn's promise handle (P1.5-B1a).
	function markValuePromise(value ValueId)
		try self.valuePromiseMarks.set(value, value: true) otherwise panic("markValuePromise: v{value} has no promise slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
	end 'markValuePromise'

	// ⭐⭐ **"IS THIS A PROMISE?" AND "DOES THIS FRAME OWN A GREEN THREAD?" ARE TWO QUESTIONS SINCE B2c, AND
	// COLLAPSING THEM BACK IS A DOUBLE FREE.** Both were `valueIsPromise` while a promise could only ever be
	// a spawn's own handle; storage split them, and the split is exactly what `specs/async-await.md`'s
	// *"Known boundary"* paragraph describes:
	//
	//   • `valueIsPromise` — **may this be `await`ed?** True for a spawn's handle AND for a value whose TYPE
	//     is a `Promise with (T[, E])`, because the storage type carries everything the await needs.
	//   • `valueOwnsGreenThread` — **does THIS frame owe a `__gt_promise_drop`?** The MARK alone. A promise
	//     read back out of a container is untracked by construction: the container holds the handle, this
	//     frame merely read it, and enrolling it here would drop a thread the container still names.
	//
	// ⇒ every OWNERSHIP door (the binding enrolment, the re-arm, `decrefCalleeFor`, the merge phi) asks the
	// second; the two AWAIT doors — `emitAwait`'s throwing-promise gate and `emitAwaitOp`'s E2015 refusal —
	// ask the first.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND SO DOES A THIRD, WHICH IS A RECLAIM: `methodCallCancelsAPromise`, the `p.cancel()` gate.** It
	// asks `valueIsPromise` DESPITE reclaiming, and that is the right question rather than an oversight — the
	// ownership question would answer "no" for a promise read back out of a container, and `p.cancel()` on a
	// stored promise is precisely a thing the language allows. What `cancel` needs to know is "is this a
	// promise at all", exactly as `await` does; what it does about the ownership half it does through
	// `consumePromiseThread`, at the site (see `emitPromiseCancel`).
	function valueOwnsGreenThread(value ValueId) returns bool
		return try self.valuePromiseMarks.get(value) otherwise panic("valueOwnsGreenThread: v{value} has no promise slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
	end 'valueOwnsGreenThread'

	function valueIsPromise(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.valueOwnsGreenThread(value) or typeIsPromiseStorage(self.signatures, t: self.valueMaxonType(value))
	end 'valueIsPromise'

	// The value's own `(tag, nameId)` pair as a `MaxonType` — the two dense columns read back as the one
	// thing they encode. `maxonTypeOfTag` is the shared decoder, so a value's type is reconstructed the same
	// way here as everywhere else it is asked for.
	function valueMaxonType(value ValueId) returns MaxonType
		return maxonTypeOfTag(self.tagOf(value), nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'valueMaxonType'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE DEFINITION OF "OWNED": does `value` own a droppable heap allocation it must transfer or
	// drop?** The single predicate the DECLARATION (`parseBinding`), the REASSIGNMENT
	// (`reassignBindingValue`), the RETURN move-out, and the match `gives` transfer all ask, so none of
	// them can drift on what enrols a binding in `ownedBindings`. It is PROVENANCE, never the type tag —
	// see `valueOwnsHeap`'s header for why the tag cannot answer:
	//
	//   • a String's tag conflates an owned heap copy with a borrowed immortal rdata literal;
	//   • a struct/boxed-union tag (`structRef`/`named`) conflates a fresh OWNED box with a BORROWED
	//     PARAMETER — a struct param is `structRef` and a union param `named`, and neither owns anything.
	//     A borrowed aggregate treated as owned would make `let q = a` (re-borrow of a struct param) move
	//     ownership out of a borrow and free it twice — the cross-call double-free this rung exists to
	//     avoid. The bit says NO for a param (`bindParameters` never sets it) and YES for every owned box.
	//
	// So the bit is the whole answer, uniformly across String / struct / union: it is set at every owned
	// heap PRODUCER and nowhere else, through one of the two doors that set it —
	//   • `trackOwnedTemp` (bit + a STATEMENT-scoped drop obligation) — an interpolation, a promotion, a
	//     struct literal, a boxed-union construct, an owned call result;
	//   • `markValueOwnsHeap` (the bit ALONE) — a value whose drop is owed by a BINDING or by the caller
	//     instead, so a pending-temp entry would drop it twice: a consumed parameter, a `for` loop value, a
	//     managed payload bound out of a union (`ownPayloadBinding` — moved out of an owned one or retained
	//     out of a borrowed one), a thrown field, a closure env.
	// An O(1) column read, seeded `false` by `mintValue`/`recordValueType`. The day a third owned heap kind
	// (an owned array) lands, its producer calls one of those two and this answers for it too — no arm to
	// add, because the bit already means exactly "owns droppable heap".
	function valueIsOwnedHeap(value ValueId) returns bool
		return try self.valueOwnsHeap.get(value) otherwise panic("valueIsOwnedHeap: v{value} has no owned-heap slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
	end 'valueIsOwnedHeap'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE STRICTER QUESTION — is `value`'s allocation owned by THIS FRAME AND NOBODY ELSE?** The
	// predicate the two DESTRUCTIVE transfers ask, and the only two: `declarePayloadBindings` before nulling a
	// payload slot, `emitThrowError` before nulling a thrown field's slot. Both write through a box they will
	// never read again, so both need a promise about every OTHER reader of that box — which is precisely what
	// `valueIsOwnedHeap` does not make.
	//
	// ⛔ **DO NOT REACH FOR THIS ANYWHERE ELSE WITHOUT THE SAME ARGUMENT.** A drop, a move poison, a
	// reassignment's move-vs-copy and a hand-off are all correct for a CO-OWNER — each releases exactly the one
	// reference this frame holds — so asking soleness there would refuse or leak on programs that are fine.
	// The distinguishing property is not "does this touch ownership" but "does this write into a slot whose
	// other readers this frame cannot enumerate".
	function valueIsSoleHeapOwner(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value) and try self.valueOwnsHeapAlone.get(value) otherwise panic("valueIsSoleHeapOwner: v{value}{SoleOwnershipSlotPanicTail}")
	end 'valueIsSoleHeapOwner'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A RESULT PHI INHERITS FROM ITS EDGES — sole ONLY if every edge is.** A phi is one name for a
	// value several paths produced, so a destructive move-out through it is sound only where it is sound on ALL
	// of them; one co-owning edge makes the whole merge a co-owner. That is the same conservative direction
	// `propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi` takes at the same two merges and for the same structural reason — the
	// alternative licenses the theft on precisely the path that shares the box.
	//
	// Its two callers are the two constructs that merge an OWNED value: a `match`/ternary result
	// (`finalizeMatchMerge`) and a `try … otherwise <expr>` result (`finishValueTry`). An EMPTY edge list is a
	// caller error rather than a vacuous `sole`: "every edge is sole" is trivially true of no edges, and this is
	// the file where an unearned `sole` costs a segfault.
	function mergedHeapExclusivity(edges ValueIdArray) returns OwnedHeapExclusivity
		if edges.isEmpty() 'nothingToJoin'
			panic("mergedHeapExclusivity: asked to join an EMPTY edge list — a phi has at least one incoming value, and answering `sole` for none would be an ownership claim nothing established")
		end 'nothingToJoin'

		for edge in edges 'each'
			if not self.valueIsSoleHeapOwner(edge) 'thisPathSharesIt'
				return OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned
			end 'thisPathSharesIt'
		end 'each'

		return OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole
	end 'mergedHeapExclusivity'

	// ⭐ **IS `value` AN ENROLLED OPAQUE OWNED type-parameter binding (P1.7 slice 3b-v)?** A consumed opaque
	// ARRAY-feed parameter, enrolled owned in a shared generic body (`bindParameters`): its `typeParameter`
	// tag is opaque (the shared body has no static type) yet it owns heap for a managed instantiation. THE one
	// predicate the forward-consume GATE (`isOpaqueOwnedFeedArg`) and the forward TRANSFER
	// (`transferConsumedArg`'s opaque arm) both read, so the "what is an opaque owned value" question is
	// answered once and the two cannot drift into moving different things (a wrong-consume double-free).
	function valueIsOpaqueOwned(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter and self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value)
	end 'valueIsOpaqueOwned'

	// ⭐ **RECORD THAT `closureValue` IS A CAPTURING CLOSURE carrying `env` (P1.5-A2b).** The two facts move
	// together — a value is a capturing closure IFF it has an env — so one door sets both columns and they
	// cannot drift. Read back by `isCapturingClosure` (the escape checks) and `closureEnvOf` (the call
	// threading).
	function markCapturingClosure(closureValue ValueId, env ValueId)
		try self.valueIsCapturingClosure.set(closureValue, value: true) otherwise panic("markCapturingClosure: v{closureValue} has no closure-mark slot — every SSA value is minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
		try self.valueClosureEnv.set(closureValue, value: env) otherwise panic("markCapturingClosure: v{closureValue} has no closure-env slot — the two closure columns are filled in lockstep")
	end 'markCapturingClosure'

	// Is `value` a closure that CAPTURES its enclosing frame? Every escape check (through
	// `rejectEscapingClosure`, which is where the routes are enumerated) and the in-frame call threading both
	// ask this ONE predicate, keyed on the VALUE — so an unrelated binding of the same NAME in another
	// function is never flagged (`-name-not-leaked`).
	function isCapturingClosure(value ValueId) returns bool
		return try self.valueIsCapturingClosure.get(value) otherwise panic("isCapturingClosure: v{value} has no closure-mark slot — every SSA value is minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
	end 'isCapturingClosure'

	// The environment a function value carries, or 0 when it carries none. A real environment is ALWAYS a
	// minted value id (`>= 1` — a heap env block, a materialized companion-env parameter, or a const-0 op is
	// never id 0, which is the first parameter), so a stored 0 unambiguously means "no environment". Set for a
	// capturing closure literal (its heap block, `markCapturingClosure`) AND for a function-typed parameter
	// (its companion env parameter, `bindClosureEnvParams`) — the two producers of a value that travels with a
	// live environment under the uniform ABI.
	function closureEnvOf(value ValueId) returns ValueId
		return try self.valueClosureEnv.get(value) otherwise panic("closureEnvOf: v{value} has no closure-env slot — every SSA value is minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
	end 'closureEnvOf'

	// Does function value `value` travel with a live environment (a capturing closure's heap block or a
	// function-typed parameter's companion env)? A plain function reference and a non-capturing closure carry
	// none — the uniform ABI passes a null in their place. Keyed on the VALUE through `valueClosureEnv`.
	function valueCarriesEnv(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.closureEnvOf(value) != 0
	end 'valueCarriesEnv'

	// ⭐ **THE ENVIRONMENT ARGUMENT THREADED AT A CALL THROUGH A FUNCTION VALUE (P1.5-A2b-2).** Under the
	// uniform `(userargs, env)` ABI EVERY indirect call passes an environment: the value's OWN environment
	// when it carries one, else a materialized null. This is the ONE place that answers "what environment
	// travels with this value?" for an indirect call, keyed on the callee VALUE — the mirror of the direct
	// call's `closureEnvOf`-driven threading (`LowerMaxonToStd.appendCalleeEnvArgs`). A non-capturing value
	// gets a fresh `0` literal so the wasm `call_indirect` functype (arg-count-derived) still has its env slot.
	function emitClosureEnvArg(callee ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if self.valueCarriesEnv(callee) 'carriesEnv'
			return self.closureEnvOf(callee)
		end 'carriesEnv'
		return self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'emitClosureEnvArg'

	// ⭐ **REFUSE A CAPTURING CLOSURE THAT ESCAPES ITS FRAME (E3099).** The ONE gate every escape route funnels
	// through, so the "is this a capturing closure?" question is answered once (keyed on the VALUE), and only
	// the route-specific message and position differ at each site. A non-capturing closure and a plain function
	// reference pass freely, so this never fires for the values A2a already handles. In-frame USES (a call, a
	// pass DOWN to a callee that only calls it) are NOT stores and never reach here — over-rejecting them
	// would be the worse failure.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROUTES ARE ITS CALL SITES AND ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TALLIED HERE.** This header carried a census
	// ("a return, a struct-field store, a union payload, a payload-binding store") that was already wrong
	// twice over — the payload-binding store did not exist yet, and the container literal was missing — so the
	// list is now the caller set, which a grep answers and a comment cannot get stale about. What the census
	// was really claiming is the part worth keeping: **this is the only door, and a value sink that does not
	// call it is refused only by `SemanticCheck.checkCapturingClosureUses`, in generic words.**
	function rejectEscapingClosure(value ValueId, message String, tok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.isCapturingClosure(value) 'escapes'
			throw ParseError.capturingClosureEscapes(message, line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'escapes'
	end 'rejectEscapingClosure'

	// ⭐ **REFUSE A CAPTURING CLOSURE PASSED AS AN INDIRECT-CALL ARGUMENT (P1.5-A2b-2).** A capturing closure
	// handed DOWN to a DIRECT callee now works — the lowering threads its environment as the companion
	// argument at the function-typed parameter position (`LowerMaxonToStd.appendCalleeEnvArgs`). But an
	// INDIRECT call `f(g)` — where `f` is itself a function VALUE whose type takes a function parameter `g` —
	// has no resolved parameter types to drive that threading (a function TYPE carries none), so the closure's
	// environment would not reach `f`'s eventual callee: the higher-order function-VALUE case, which no case at
	// this rung reaches. Refused as UNSUPPORTED rather than compiled into a segfault, keyed on the VALUE
	// exactly as the escape gate is — a non-capturing closure or a plain function reference passes freely.
	function rejectCapturingClosureArg(value ValueId, tok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.isCapturingClosure(value) 'capturingArg'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("passing a closure that captures as an argument to an INDIRECT call — a function VALUE's type carries no parameter types, so the closure's environment cannot be threaded to its callee (a direct call to a named function threads it; a higher-order function value does not yet)", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'capturingArg'
	end 'rejectCapturingClosureArg'

	// Ownership of `value` has been TRANSFERRED to a binding, so the statement no longer owes its drop:
	// remove it from the pending list. A filter-rebuild rather than a pop because, while the transferred
	// value is normally the last one pushed (a binding's initializer is the statement's final expression),
	// keying on identity rather than position is what stays correct if that ever stops holding.
	function removeFromPendingTemps(value ValueId)
		var kept = ValueIdArray.create()
		for pending in self.pendingTempDrops 'each'
			if pending != value 'notTransferred'
				kept.push(pending)
			end 'notTransferred'
		end 'each'
		self.pendingTempDrops = kept
	end 'removeFromPendingTemps'

	// Is `value` still an UNCONSUMED owned temporary — a value `trackOwnedTemp` enrolled and nothing has
	// transferred to a binding yet? The two owned-value homes are disjoint by construction (a transfer to a
	// binding `removeFromPendingTemps` first — `parseBinding`/`moveInPayload`), so a value in this list is
	// exactly one owned by no binding. `parseMatchCommon` reads it to tell a temporary scrutinee (which
	// needs match-scoped enrollment) from an already-bound one (which does not).
	function pendingTempsContain(value ValueId) returns bool
		for pending in self.pendingTempDrops 'each'
			if pending == value 'found'
				return true
			end 'found'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'pendingTempsContain'

	// ⭐⭐ **MOVE A TEMPORARY'S DROP FROM THE STATEMENT TO THE ENCLOSING SCOPE — the ONE promotion, and the
	// answer to every "this value is still being pointed into after the statement ends" question the parser
	// asks (A3h).** An UNCONSUMED owned temporary is freed by `drainPendingTemps` at the statement's tail; a
	// value some longer-lived thing still reaches must instead be freed at the scope frame's exit, and the
	// mechanism for that is already the ordinary one — a nameless entry in `ownedBindings`, which every exit
	// drops through (`emitScopeDrops` for a `return`/`throw` and for the frame's own end, the loop floor for a
	// `break`/`continue`). Exactly once on every path, which is what a hand-placed decref could not promise.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE GATE IS `pendingTempsContain`, NOT `valueIsOwnedHeap`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A DOUBLE FREE.** A
	// value a BINDING already owns is owned-heap too, and enrolling it a second time emits two decrefs of one
	// record (measured at the `for … in` site: `for x in arr` over a `var arr` segfaulted). The pending list is
	// precisely the owned values NO binding owns (`pendingTempsContain`), which is the question being asked —
	// so a non-temporary source is a silent, correct no-op and every caller may ask unconditionally.
	//
	// ⚠ **SIX CALLERS.** They are: the `for … in` SOURCE and, separately, the per-trip ELEMENT an `Iterable`
	// cursor's `current()` mints; the temporary `match` scrutinee; the box a member BORROW was taken off
	// (`dispatchMethodOnBase`); the container an ARRAY element borrow was taken out of; and the
	// callee-storage escaping feed argument. All one fact — *"something outlives this statement and points
	// into this record"* — and a seventh copy of the two-line remove-and-enrol is how one of them would come
	// to do only half of it. ⚠ A COUNT in a header is a claim like any other: keep it true or do not write
	// it, because a reader who trusts a stale one stops looking for the sites it omits. It said FIVE while
	// there were seven (recounted at review), and the seventh — the container a `__ManagedList` node HANDLE
	// pointed into — is gone at `W138`: a handle RETAINS its list now, which outlives the scope this
	// promotion could only reach the end of.
	// `name` is inert (the binding is never installed in a `Scope`, so no source line can name it) and each
	// caller states its own, so a dump of `ownedBindings` still says WHY each nameless entry is there.
	function giveTemporaryScopeLifetime(value ValueId, name ByteArray)
		if not self.pendingTempsContain(value) 'notATemporary'
			return
		end 'notATemporary'

		self.removeFromPendingTemps(value)
		self.ownedBindings.push(VarInfo.createValueBinding(name, varType: self.typeOf(value), mutable: false, boundValue: value))
	end 'giveTemporaryScopeLifetime'

	// The value a binding holds has been MOVED OUT — a `return` hands its box to the caller — so the
	// binding must leave `ownedBindings`, else `emitScopeDrops` would decref a box the callee no longer
	// owns (a double-free with the caller's own drop). Keyed on the bound VALUE rather than on position:
	// a returned binding is rarely the last declared, so identity is what stays correct. A filter-rebuild,
	// the `removeFromPendingTemps` shape for the owned-BINDING list.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* `liveOwnerIndexOf`, AND IT MUST NOT BE "CONSOLIDATED" ONTO IT.** That scan
	// finds the ONE LIVE owner (`isLiveOwnerOf` skips `movedFrom` ghosts) and stops; this asks a different
	// question — which entries HOLD this value, ghosts included, ALL of them. A moved-from ghost left behind
	// here would be dropped at scope exit against a box the caller now owns, which is the double-free the
	// removal exists to prevent. Every OTHER reader of `ownedBindings` does go through the one scan.
	function removeFromOwnedBindings(value ValueId)
		var kept = VarInfoArray.create()
		for binding in self.ownedBindings 'each'
			if binding.boundValue != value 'notMovedOut'
				kept.push(binding)
			end 'notMovedOut'
		end 'each'
		self.ownedBindings = kept
	end 'removeFromOwnedBindings'

	// ⭐ **THE OWNED-VALUE CLEANUP A `return <value>` AND A `throw <value>` SHARE** — the two exits that
	// HAND a heap value to the caller. Both must, in this exact order, drop every owned value this function
	// still holds EXCEPT the one in flight, then let the caller emit its own terminator (`ret` / `throwError`):
	//
	//   • MOVE the handed-off value out of the local drop sets — it leaves BOTH the pending-temp list (a
	//     freshly built `return U.case(x)` / `throw U.case(x)` temporary) and the owned-binding list (a
	//     `return t` / re-thrown `throw e` caught binding), one call each, both no-ops on the other's case —
	//     so the caller becomes its sole owner and neither drop below frees it. A no-op for a scalar.
	//   • DROP the exit expression's leftover TEMPORARIES: `return wrap("v{x}")` / `throw E.p(len("v{x}"))`
	//     build an interpolation this statement owns but does NOT hand back. The `count() > 0` guard is
	//     `drainPendingTemps`' own — almost no exit leaves a temporary, so the common exit pays nothing.
	//   • DROP every owned BINDING in scope (`emitScopeDrops`).
	//   • RESTORE the owned-binding list past those drops: it OUTLIVES this exit, and a SIBLING exit edge of
	//     an enclosing block still owns the handed-off binding on ITS runtime path (this exit terminated only
	//     its own), so `removeFromOwnedBindings`' NEW array leaves the original for those edges.
	//
	// The drops land AFTER the caller computed the handed-off value (already a register read, no longer a
	// heap access) and BEFORE the caller's terminator (a `ret`/`throwError` ENDS the block — anything after
	// it sits on a path control has left, and the value leaks: exit 101). Factoring the WHOLE sequence, not
	// just the move-out, is what keeps the two exits from drifting on it: a `throw` that drained its bindings
	// but not its temporaries leaked exactly one interpolation buffer until this became one path.
	function emitHandoffExitDrops(handedOff ValueId, anchor Token)
		let savedOwnedBindings = self.ownedBindings
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(handedOff) 'movedOut'
			self.removeFromOwnedBindings(handedOff)
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(handedOff)
		end 'movedOut'

		if self.pendingTempDrops.count() > 0 'dropExitTemps'
			self.drainPendingTemps(WholeStatementTempFloor, anchor: anchor)
		end 'dropExitTemps'

		self.emitScopeDrops(self.currentBlock, floor: WholeFunctionScopeFloor, anchor: anchor)
		self.ownedBindings = savedOwnedBindings
	end 'emitHandoffExitDrops'

	// Does `binding` currently owe a scope-exit drop — i.e. is it a LIVE owner in `ownedBindings`? The
	// reassignment path's test for whether the value being OVERWRITTEN is owned. It reads MEMBERSHIP,
	// not `valueIsOwnedHeap(binding.boundValue)`, and that distinction is the whole point: a
	// loop-carried `var`'s current value is a header phi with no owned-heap provenance, so the value
	// column would answer `false` for a binding the loop owns (see `reassignBindingValue`). Membership
	// survives the phi; the provenance bit does not.
	//
	// Keyed on `boundValue`, the `removeFromOwnedBindings` idiom — an owned heap value has a single LIVE
	// owner, so at most one non-moved owned binding holds any given value. `movedFrom` GHOSTS are
	// skipped: a moved-from binding stays in the list but shares its stale value with the binding it
	// moved to, so counting it would report two owners of one value. An O(ownedBindings) scan; a
	// reassignment is not a hot path.
	function bindingIsOwned(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return not self.noLiveOwner(self.liveOwnerIndexOf(binding.boundValue))
	end 'bindingIsOwned'

	// ⭐ **WHAT MAKES AN OWNED-SET ENTRY THE ONE LIVE OWNER OF `value`**: it holds that value AND has not
	// itself been moved from. Single-homed on purpose — the `valueIsOwnedHeap` pattern. `bindingIsOwned`
	// (does this binding still owe a drop?) and `poisonMoveSource` (which binding does a move poison?)
	// both need exactly this predicate, and if it drifted between the two — a new ownership flag taught
	// to one scan and not the other — one would count a `movedFrom` ghost as a live owner: a double-free
	// or a wrongly-skipped drop, and invisible at either call site read alone. The GHOST skip is the
	// load-bearing half: a moved-from binding shares its stale `boundValue` with the binding it moved to,
	// so a value has exactly one owner only once the flag is checked.
	function isLiveOwnerOf(owned VarInfo, value ValueId) returns bool
		return not owned.movedFrom and owned.boundValue == value
	end 'isLiveOwnerOf'

	// True iff the RHS just parsed — the tokens in `[startPos, self.pos)` — RESOLVES TO a bare reference
	// to a LOCAL binding (`let u = t` / `s = t`, and equally `let u = (t)`). That is the ONLY RHS shape
	// that can MOVE an owned value out of an existing binding: it resolves to that binding's own SSA
	// value (`parseVariableReference` returns `binding.boundValue`), so `poisonMoveSource`'s value-keyed
	// scan can match it. Every other RHS — a call, an interpolation, a field read, any arithmetic —
	// mints a FRESH temp that is the value of no binding (a CONSUME), so the scan is guaranteed to find
	// nothing.
	//
	// A BARE read is a rooted span with no field steps, which is what `rhsBareLocalSourceName` answers with
	// — the chain grammar itself is written down once, at `rhsRootLocalName`. This is that answer reduced to
	// its emptiness, for the callers that need only the yes/no.
	function rhsWasBareLocalReference(startPos TokenIndex) returns bool
		return not self.rhsBareLocalSourceName(startPos).isEmpty()
	end 'rhsWasBareLocalReference'

	// ⭐ **WHICH LOCAL A BARE-READ RHS NAMES — the same answer as `rhsWasBareLocalReference` with the NAME
	// KEPT**, empty when the span is not one (a call, a field read, any expression). The two rules a rebind
	// asks need different halves of it: the MOVE model wants the bool (*can this poison anything?*) and the
	// immutable-alias rule wants the name (*was that local declared `let`?*). Answered once so the two
	// cannot disagree about which local the RHS was — a bool that said "bare local" while the name walk
	// said "no root" would move a value out of a binding nothing had named.
	function rhsBareLocalSourceName(startPos TokenIndex) returns ByteArray
		if self.rhsSpanReadsAField(startPos) 'readsAField'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'readsAField'

		return self.rhsRootLocalName(startPos)
	end 'rhsBareLocalSourceName'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN IMMUTABLE REBIND IS A SECOND REFERENCE, NOT A MOVE (the ownership ruling, 2026-08-04) — the
	// ONE predicate BOTH rebind doors ask.** Maxon is single-ownership and everything is a reference; what
	// makes a second name for one value SAFE is that neither can be written through, which is MUTABILITY and
	// not linearity. So a bare read of a `let` never leaves its source moved-from, at a declaration
	// (`declareInitializedBinding`) or at an assignment (`reassignBindingValue`) alike.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO DOORS AGREE ON THE RULE AND NOT ON WHAT IT COSTS, WHICH IS WHY THIS ANSWERS THE PREDICATE
	// AND NEITHER DOOR'S ACTION.** A DECLARATION's alias is free: the new name is declared inside the
	// source's scope, so the source structurally outlives it and keeps the ONE drop, and no refcount is
	// involved. An ASSIGNMENT's destination already exists at a scope depth this door cannot bound, and it
	// already owes a drop of its own — so it takes a real reference (`coOwnImmutableRebindSource`). Written
	// as one predicate at two doors the rule cannot drift; written as one ACTION it would have double-freed.
	//
	// A source with no name is no bare local read at all — the emptiness is `rhsRootLocalName`'s
	// discriminant, not a sentinel — so it is asked here rather than at each caller.
	function rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias(sourceName ByteArray) returns bool
		return not sourceName.isEmpty() and not self.scope.getInfo(sourceName).mutable
	end 'rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH LOCAL THE RHS SPAN IS ROOTED AT — the `t` of `t`, `(t)`, `t.x`, `t.0`, `(t).x`, `(t.x.y)` —
	// or the EMPTY name when the span is not a chain of members rooted at a readable local.**
	//
	// Emptiness IS the discriminant and not a sentinel answer: an identifier token can never be empty, so
	// "no root" has no other spelling to be confused with. It is the same idiom `bareArgImmutableName` and
	// `borrowSubjectNameAt` already answer in (`EmptySourceText`), for the same reason.
	//
	// ⭐ **THREE RULES ASK IT, AND THEY ASK DIFFERENT HALVES OF ONE QUESTION.** The MOVE model asks *"was
	// this a bare read of a local?"* (`rhsWasBareLocalReference` — only that shape can move an owned value
	// out of an existing binding, because only that shape resolves to the binding's own SSA value). E3078
	// asks *"whose storage is this initializer reaching into?"*, which a field chain answers too — `var i =
	// o.inner` aliases `o`'s box through a writable name exactly as `var i = o` would. And the IMMUTABLE-
	// ALIAS rule asks *"was that local declared `let`?"* (`rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias`), which needs the
	// NAME the first of the three reduces away. One walk answers all three, so the grammar of "a name, then
	// field steps" is written down once.
	//
	// ⚠ **PARENS ARE PART OF THE CHAIN GRAMMAR, and that is a soundness requirement, not tidiness.**
	// `parseParenthesizedExpression` returns its inner value UNCHANGED, so `(t)` / `((t))` alias the
	// same owned box a bare `t` does. A token-count-only gate (`self.pos == startPos + 1`) called them
	// non-bare-local, so the poison never ran, both bindings dropped the one box, and the #41
	// double-free returned.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND STRIPPING ONLY THE PAIR THAT WRAPS THE WHOLE SPAN WAS NOT ENOUGH — MEASURED.** `(t).x` opens
	// with a `(` that no trailing `)` balances at the end of the span, so the strip did not fire, the root
	// token was the `(`, and the answer was "no root": `var b = t.x` from an immutable `t` was refused while
	// `var b = (a).x` COMPILED and its `b.x = 99` wrote through `t` (the oracle refuses both). So the walk
	// recognizes the whole postfix grammar instead — a run of opening parens, the root name, then hops and
	// closers in any order — which is the shape `parsePrimary` + `parseMemberAccess` actually accept. It
	// stays a SOUND over-approximation for the move half: every span it newly admits contains a `.`, so
	// `rhsWasBareLocalReference` (which demands no field step) cannot see one of them.
	//
	// ⚠ **A HOP'S NAME IS `namesMemberAt`'s QUESTION, NOT `tokenCanBeAName`'s.** This walk used to spell
	// "what may follow a `.`" itself, and the copy had already drifted: it did not admit the INT LITERAL of a
	// tuple's positional access, so `var a = t.0` escaped the same refusal `var a = t._0` receives — the same
	// wrong answer, one spelling over. The door that owns that question is asked instead.
	//
	// (`shiftCountAnchor` still carries the plain whole-span strip; see the note there for why the two are
	// deliberately not shared. They were never the same question — that one anchors a diagnostic on the token
	// the author wrote, and it wants the outermost paren gone and nothing else.)
	//
	// ⭐ **A GATE, NOT THE DECISION.** `poisonMoveSource` keeps the authority — given a bare-local RHS it
	// still matches the value against the owned set, so a bare local that is NOT owned (a scalar) still
	// poisons nothing. This only decides whether calling the scan can possibly do anything, so the
	// common case — every consume `let u = build()` — skips the O(ownedBindings) scan the poison used to
	// run on EVERY owned bind and reassignment. Skipping it when this is false is a proven no-op, not a
	// behaviour change: the scan could not have matched a non-bare-local RHS anyway.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROOT TOKEN MAY BE A KEYWORD (D8), because a BINDING's name may be** — asked through
	// `tokenCanBeAName`, exactly as `consumedParamAfterColon` (the SWEEP's copy of this same question, and
	// its header names this routine) asks it. The two MUST agree, and the failure when they do not is a
	// wrong ANSWER: `Self{value: from}` inside `static function create(from String)` had the sweep saying
	// "the callee consumes it" while this said "the RHS is not a bare local", so the parameter was never
	// poisoned and the callee dropped at scope exit what it had just stored into the field — a SEGFAULT
	// on `Box.create(msg)` where the identical program spelling the parameter `path` ran. It stays a
	// sound over-approximation: `scope.contains` below, and `poisonMoveSource`'s own owned-set match,
	// keep the authority.
	//
	// The root is excluded when it names a top-level binding (`scope.contains` false) or a self-field
	// alias (`isSelfField`, whose read mints a fresh load rather than aliasing a binding's value) —
	// neither is a live owned local the move scan would match, and neither is a local whose mutability
	// E3078 could read. Those are the same two conditions `parseVariableReference` treats as a bare LOCAL
	// read, so the gate cannot diverge from what actually resolves to a binding's `boundValue` — parens
	// included, since a parenthesized read resolves through the very same `parseVariableReference`.
	function rhsRootLocalName(startPos TokenIndex) returns ByteArray
		let hi = self.pos
		var lo = startPos
		var openParens = 0
		while lo < hi and self.kindAt(lo) == TokenKind.leftParen 'stripOpeningParens'
			lo = lo + 1
			openParens = openParens + 1
		end 'stripOpeningParens'

		if lo >= hi 'emptySpan'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'emptySpan'

		let tok = self.tokenAt(lo)
		if not tokenCanBeAName(tok) 'notAName'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notAName'

		// Everything past the root must CONTINUE the chain — a `. member` hop, or a `)` closing one of the
		// parens the root was opened behind. A call's `(`, an operator, an index or a trailing method call
		// all mint a FRESH value that is the storage of no binding, which is precisely the case both askers
		// must exclude.
		var closeParens = 0
		var i = lo + 1
		while i < hi 'eachStep'
			if self.kindAt(i) == TokenKind.rightParen 'closesAWrap'
				closeParens = closeParens + 1
				i = i + 1
				continue
			end 'closesAWrap'

			if self.kindAt(i) != TokenKind.dot or i + 1 >= hi or not self.namesMemberAt(i + 1) 'notAMemberHop'
				return EmptySourceText
			end 'notAMemberHop'
			i = i + 2
		end 'eachStep'

		// Every paren the root sits behind must be closed by the end of the span. Fewer closers means the
		// `(` opened something this walk did not read — a call's argument list, a parenthesized operand —
		// and the span is then not rooted at the name it happens to start with.
		if closeParens != openParens 'parensDoNotWrapTheChain'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'parensDoNotWrapTheChain'

		if not self.scope.contains(tok.value) or self.scope.isSelfField(tok.value) 'notAReadableLocal'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'notAReadableLocal'

		return tok.value
	end 'rhsRootLocalName'

	// Did the RHS span reach into a FIELD of its root rather than reading the root itself? A field step is
	// a `.`, and a bare name span cannot contain one — so this separates `t` from `t.x` without re-walking
	// the chain grammar `rhsRootLocalName` owns. Spans here are a handful of tokens, so the scan is
	// cheaper than carrying a second return value out of that walk.
	function rhsSpanReadsAField(startPos TokenIndex) returns bool
		var i = startPos
		while i < self.pos 'eachToken'
			if self.kindAt(i) == TokenKind.dot 'fieldStep'
				return true
			end 'fieldStep'
			i = i + 1
		end 'eachToken'

		return false
	end 'rhsSpanReadsAField'

	// ⭐ **MOVE THE VALUE OUT OF ITS CURRENT OWNER.** If `value` is the current value of a LIVE owned
	// binding — i.e. the RHS of a bind/assign was a bare reference to that binding (`let u = t`) — mark
	// that source `movedFrom`, so a later read of it is use-after-move (E3102) and `emitScopeDrops`
	// skips its drop (only the new owner drops the value). A fresh owned temp (`build()`, `"{x}"`) is
	// the value of no binding, so nothing is poisoned — that is a CONSUME (move-in), not a
	// source-poisoning move.
	//
	// Finds the ONE live owner via `isLiveOwnerOf` (the same predicate `bindingIsOwned` reads), so a
	// value already moved once — a `movedFrom` ghost — can never be picked as a live source. An
	// O(ownedBindings) scan. **The callers GATE it on `rhsWasBareLocalReference`**, so it runs only when
	// the RHS actually was a bare local reference — never on a consume, which is what kept it off the hot
	// path of every owned bind (the scan-and-find-nothing that made accumulating N owned bindings O(N²)).
	//
	// ⚠ **A MOVE OF A BINDING DECLARED OUTSIDE THE INNERMOST LOOP IS REJECTED HERE** (`anchor` positions the
	// diagnostic). The path-sensitive drop reconciliation (`reconcileMovesAtMerge`) settles an `if`/`match`
	// join, but a LOOP has two exits the join model does not reach: the back edge (which would re-move the
	// binding on the next iteration — a double-free) and a `break` (which leaves the binding live on the
	// normal exit while giving it away on the break edge — a leak or, once the join restores the sibling
	// path, a double-free). Placing the compensating drop on those edges needs elaboration across the loop
	// boundary, a later wave; until then a move that escapes the innermost loop is refused rather than
	// miscompiled. A binding declared INSIDE the loop body (index at or above the loop's owned floor) is
	// reconciled within the body, once per iteration, and is unaffected.
	// ⭐ **`await`/`try await` CONSUMES a promise's green thread (P1.5-B2 #88) — the DUAL of the E3100 linear-
	// await check.** The runtime driver (`__gt_await`/`__gt_try_await`) reclaims the GT struct AT the await, so
	// the thread must NOT also be dropped at scope exit — mark its owning binding `movedFrom` (the scope-exit
	// drop then skips it, exactly as an owned box moved by `let u = t` is skipped), and clear it from the
	// pending-temp list (an UNBOUND awaited spawn, `await async f()`). Keyed on the thread's ValueId — its
	// identity — so awaiting through ANY alias consumes the one thread (`let q = p; await q` consumes `p`'s
	// thread). An unbound-and-unowned promise has no owner to mark, so the scan quietly finds nothing, which is
	// correct — the pending-temp clear is all it needs. Unlike `poisonMoveSource`, it does NOT reject a move
	// that escapes a loop: a promise spawned outside a loop and awaited inside is already E3100 (double-await
	// across the back-edge), so it never reaches lowering, and marking its (outer) owner here is harmless.
	function consumePromiseThread(promise ValueId)
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(promise)

		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(promise)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'unownedSpawn'
			return
		end 'unownedSpawn'

		// A `var` bound from `.get(index)`: the flag is written THROUGH the shared `VarInfo` reference (the
		// `Scope.setValue` idiom `poisonOwningBinding` uses), so every holder observes it.
		var owned = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("consumePromiseThread: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		owned.movedFrom = true
	end 'consumePromiseThread'

	// ⭐ **IS A GREEN THREAD `p` OWNS STILL NAMED BY A LIVE ALIAS? (P1.5-B2 #88, Finding 2)** `let q = p` binds
	// `q` to `p`'s thread ValueId without enrolling a second owner (a promise aliases, it does not move), so
	// re-arming `p` must first confirm no such live alias exists — else dropping the thread dangles the alias
	// (a UAF at `await q`). `thread` is the value being re-armed away. Two shapes:
	//   • DIRECT — `thread` is the spawn's ValueId (a non-loop re-arm): scan the symbol table for a DIFFERENT
	//     live name bound to it.
	//   • MERGE PHI — a re-armed `var` across a loop / branch holds a phi as `thread`; the phi itself is named
	//     by no alias, but the thread it MERGES (on the first reaching edge) may be. So also scan the phi's
	//     incoming edge values (`phiIncomingValues`) — the pre-loop value an outer `let q = p` aliases.
	// Purely a re-arm-time scan (no per-binding/per-value state), so the async-free scale corpus pays nothing.
	function promiseThreadHasLiveAlias(func IrFunction, reArmedName ByteArray, thread ValueId) returns bool
		if self.threadNameAliasedBy(reArmedName, thread: thread) 'direct'
			return true
		end 'direct'
		for incoming in self.phiIncomingValues(func, phi: thread) 'eachIncoming'
			if self.threadNameAliasedBy(reArmedName, thread: incoming) 'phiAlias'
				return true
			end 'phiAlias'
		end 'eachIncoming'
		return false
	end 'promiseThreadHasLiveAlias'

	// A live name of `thread` OTHER than `reArmedName` — the raw symbol-table scan `promiseThreadHasLiveAlias`
	// runs on the re-armed value and on each of its phi's incoming values. What counts as a live name is
	// `VarInfo.isLiveNameOf`, shared with `poisonValueAliases`; the name exclusion is this scan's alone.
	function threadNameAliasedBy(reArmedName ByteArray, thread ValueId) returns bool
		for (name, info) in self.scope.allVars() 'each'
			if name != reArmedName and info.isLiveNameOf(thread) 'alias'
				return true
			end 'alias'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'threadNameAliasedBy'

	function poisonMoveSource(value ValueId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		// The OWNER first, and the aliases only once it has been found: the owned-set scan reads
		// `isLiveOwnerOf`, which a poisoned owner fails — so marking the names first would hide the owner
		// from its own scan, and with it the loop-escape refusal below.
		try self.poisonOwningBinding(value, anchor: anchor)
		self.poisonValueAliases(value)
	end 'poisonMoveSource'

	// ⭐ **WHERE IN `ownedBindings` THE ONE LIVE OWNER OF `value` SITS — or `ownedBindings.count()` when this
	// frame owns it through no BINDING at all** (a statement-scoped temporary, or a value it does not own).
	// Past-the-end is the same answer `typeParamPositionOf` gives for a name that is not a parameter, and it is
	// read the same way: `bindingIndexEscapesLoop` compares it against a loop's `ownedFloor`, which is a mark
	// taken at loop entry and so never exceeds the count — a past-the-end index is therefore below no floor,
	// which is exactly right for "no binding owns this, so no move of it can escape anything".
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE SCAN, BECAUSE ITS TWO CALLERS MUST AGREE ON *WHICH* BINDING THE MOVE IS ABOUT.** The move itself
	// (`poisonOwningBinding`, which marks that binding `movedFrom`) and the question asked BEFORE moving
	// (`moveOfValueEscapesLoop`, which decides whether an opaque container store may move at all) would
	// otherwise be two walks free to settle on two owners — and then a store would take a reference against one
	// binding's position while the poison landed on another's.
	function liveOwnerIndexOf(value ValueId) returns BindingMark
		var i = 0
		while i < self.ownedBindings.count() 'each'
			let owned = try self.ownedBindings.get(i) otherwise panic("liveOwnerIndexOf: ownedBindings.get OOB at {i} — the list is only appended to and drained at a block's end")
			if self.isLiveOwnerOf(owned, value: value) 'source'
				return i as BindingMark
			end 'source'
			i = i + 1
		end 'each'
		return self.ownedBindings.count()
	end 'liveOwnerIndexOf'

	// ⭐ **DOES A `liveOwnerIndexOf` ANSWER MEAN "NO BINDING OWNS THIS"?** The past-the-end convention above,
	// given a NAME so that its six readers spell the test identically. Written out, `index >=
	// ownedBindings.count()` is a bare comparison whose MEANING lives only in the scan's header — and the
	// day the convention changes (a real sentinel, a lookup union) every copy is a site to find rather than
	// a body to edit.
	function noLiveOwner(index BindingMark) returns bool
		return index >= self.ownedBindings.count()
	end 'noLiveOwner'

	// ⭐ **WOULD MOVING `value` CROSS OUT OF THE INNERMOST ENCLOSING LOOP?** The question a door asks when it
	// has a second way to discharge its obligation and wants to know whether the cheap one is available — see
	// `moveElementIntoContainer`'s opaque arm, which takes a REFERENCE instead when the answer is yes. Asked of
	// the same binding `poisonOwningBinding` would poison, so the two doors cannot disagree about which move
	// this is.
	function moveOfValueEscapesLoop(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.bindingIndexEscapesLoop(self.liveOwnerIndexOf(value))
	end 'moveOfValueEscapesLoop'

	// The owned-set half of `poisonMoveSource`: mark the ONE live owner of `value` moved-from, refusing a
	// move that would escape the innermost loop.
	function poisonOwningBinding(value ValueId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(value)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'noBindingOwnsIt'
			return
		end 'noBindingOwnsIt'

		if self.bindingIndexEscapesLoop(index) 'escapesLoop'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("moving a value declared outside this loop from inside the loop body — its drop on the loop's other exit paths (the back edge would re-move it next iteration; a `break` leaves it live on the normal exit) needs path-sensitive elaboration across the loop boundary, which arrives with a later wave. Move the value into the loop body, or restructure so the move does not cross the loop boundary", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'escapesLoop'

		// A `var` bound from `.get(index)`, not a bare `for` element: a field write needs a mutable binding,
		// and it is written THROUGH the shared `VarInfo` reference (the `Scope.setValue` idiom), so the flag is
		// observed by every holder — `ownedBindings`, `Scope.vars`, any loop-phi list.
		var owned = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("poisonOwningBinding: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		owned.movedFrom = true

		// ⭐⭐ **E3077's shv2-ONLY DOOR: BEING MOVED OUT OF IS A USE OF MUTABILITY HERE, AND IN NEITHER
		// REFERENCE.** MEASURED on the oracle: `var a = Point.create(7)` + `let u = a` + `return u.x` is
		// E3077 there, because a copy out of a `var` is a copy out of a `let` and the keyword changes
		// nothing. In shv2 it changes everything — `let u = a` from a MUTABLE `a` MOVES (this poison), and
		// from an IMMUTABLE `a` it ALIASES, both names staying live (the ownership ruling
		// `declareInitializedBinding` states). So the keyword is load-bearing at the source, and telling the
		// author to write `let` would silently turn their move into an alias.
		//
		// It is the whole of `specs-shv2/moves.md`, `conditional-move-drops.md` and
		// `try-block-ownership.md`: every one of them binds a `var`, moves out of it once and never
		// reassigns it, and under the oracle's rule the move semantics they exist to pin would have no
		// legal spelling at all.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE DOOR IS BROADER THAN THE ARGUMENT, AND DELIBERATELY NOT NARROWED TO MATCH IT.** The
		// argument above is about a binding-to-binding rebind, where advising `let` would silently convert a
		// move into an alias. This fires on EVERY move-out — `return s`, a consuming call argument, a
		// container store — and for those `let` would have been perfectly legal, so the rule is simply lost
		// on them. That is a MISSED diagnostic and never a refused program, which is the direction that costs
		// nothing; narrowing it would mean a second classification of what kind of move this is, at a door
		// whose whole job is that it does not care.
		self.markMutabilityExercised(owned)
	end 'poisonOwningBinding'

	// ⭐⭐ **A MOVE POISONS EVERY NAME THAT READS THE VALUE, NOT ONLY THE ONE THAT OWNS IT.** An immutable
	// rebind (`let b = a`) enrols no second owner — `b` simply reads `a`'s value — so `poisonMoveSource`'s
	// owned-set scan finds `a` and stops. That is the right OWNER, and it is not the whole answer: after
	// `f(b)` or `return b` the callee has the value, and `b` would go on reading a box this frame no longer
	// owns. The names are not interchangeable to the diagnostic either — a later `print(b)` must say `b`.
	//
	// So the symbol table is walked and every live binding on the same value is marked. Only the READ
	// rejection turns on this bit for an alias: an alias is in no owned set, so no drop is skipped or added
	// by marking it, which is why marking too widely here can only ever add an E3102, never a leak.
	//
	// ⚠ **GATED ON THE FUNCTION HAVING MINTED AN ALIAS AT ALL.** Without an alias no two live bindings can
	// share a value, so the walk is provably empty — and consuming call arguments reach the poison on every
	// call in the program, which is exactly where an ungated per-move symbol-table scan would become
	// quadratic in a long function body.
	function poisonValueAliases(value ValueId)
		if not self.functionMintedValueAliases 'noAliasesInThisFunction'
			return
		end 'noAliasesInThisFunction'

		for (name, binding) in self.scope.allVars() 'eachBinding'
			if not binding.isLiveNameOf(value) 'notALiveNameOfIt'
				continue
			end 'notALiveNameOfIt'

			// Re-read through `getInfo` for a `var`: the map's `for` element is immutable, and the flag must
			// be written THROUGH the shared `VarInfo` so every holder observes it — the `Scope.setValue`
			// idiom `poisonOwningBinding` uses, for its reason.
			var alias = self.scope.getInfo(name)
			alias.movedFrom = true
		end 'eachBinding'
	end 'poisonValueAliases'

	// Does moving the owned binding at `index` cross OUT of the innermost enclosing loop — i.e. was that
	// binding declared before the loop body was entered (`LoopContext.ownedFloor`)? Only such a move is
	// unsound at this rung (see `poisonMoveSource`); a move of a body-local binding, or any move outside a
	// loop entirely, is fine. The innermost loop is the top of the loop-context stack.
	function bindingIndexEscapesLoop(index BindingMark) returns bool
		if self.loops.count() == 0 'notInLoop'
			return false
		end 'notInLoop'

		let innermost = try self.loops.get(self.loops.count() - 1) otherwise panic("bindingIndexEscapesLoop: loops.get failed on a non-empty stack")
		return index < innermost.ownedFloor
	end 'bindingIndexEscapesLoop'

	// ⭐ **PROMOTE A BORROWED String TO AN OWNED HEAP COPY** so a `returns String` function ALWAYS hands
	// back something owned. `return "hi"` yields an immortal rdata literal (capacity == -2), and a
	// `returns String` caller drops its result unconditionally — decref'ing read-only data. Copying the
	// source bytes into a fresh fused record — the SAME single-`__mm_alloc` path an interpolation builds,
	// with the source as its one part (`__str_copy` blits `buffer@0`/`length@8`) — yields an owned
	// temporary the caller can safely drop. `emitStringInterp` marks it owned (`trackOwnedTemp`); the
	// return's move-out then takes it out of the pending list. NO second record layout is hand-rolled.
	// ⚠ **THE PROMOTION KEEPS THE SOURCE'S TYPE, AND GETTING THAT WRONG WAS A REACHABLE BUG.** The copy is
	// built by the interpolation encoder, whose result is a `String` — so promoting a borrowed CHARACTER
	// used to hand back a `string`-tagged value, and `var found = 'é'` (a `var` promotes its managed
	// initializer at the declaration) typed `found` as a **String**. The next line then refused a
	// Character: *"cannot assign 'Character' to variable 'found' of type 'String'"*, on a program that is
	// plainly legal. The RECORD is identical either way — that is the whole premise of `tagIsByteRecord` —
	// so only the tag was wrong, which is exactly the class of bug a shared representation invites.
	// `resultTag` is threaded from here rather than fixed at the encoder because the encoder has a second
	// caller (`parseStringInterpolation`) whose result genuinely IS a String.
	function promoteToOwnedString(source ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		var partBufs = ValueIdArray.create()
		var partLens = ValueIdArray.create()
		var scratchIds = ValueIdArray.create()
		self.materializeStringRecordPart(source, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
		return self.emitStringInterp(partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range, resultTag: self.tagOf(source))
	end 'promoteToOwnedString'

	// ⭐ **RETAIN A BORROWED MANAGED AGGREGATE AS A FRESH OWNED VALUE (OPEN #40).** The struct/union analogue
	// of `promoteToOwnedString`: where a String promotes by COPYING into a fresh owned record, an aggregate
	// has no cheap owning copy and MUST stay the same box (the alias is observable through a field write), so
	// it is promoted by an INCREF instead — `__mm_retain` raises the box's refcount and hands back the
	// pointer. That the runtime fn RETURNS the pointer (rather than reusing the void `__mm_incref`) is the
	// point: the result is a FRESH SSA value distinct from `source`, so a later re-borrow of `source`
	// (`let r = p`) cannot `poisonMoveSource`-match this binding, and moving this binding poisons IT, not the
	// source. The result carries the SOURCE's exact type (same tag + name id), so the binding is the same
	// aggregate the source is — its fields, its `match` dispatch, and its drop callee all resolve
	// identically. `markValueOwnsHeap` (not `trackOwnedTemp`) sets the owned-heap provenance WITHOUT
	// enrolling a statement-scoped pending drop: `parseBinding` binds this to the `var`, which drops it at
	// ITS scope exit through `ownedBindings` — the same door a consumed managed parameter drops through.
	//
	// ⭐ **CO-OWNED IS THIS DOOR'S DEFINING FACT, NOT A CAVEAT ON IT** — the paragraph above says the box is
	// deliberately SHARED and the alias deliberately observable, so the result is the textbook co-owner. Claiming
	// `sole` here is what let `let borrowed = h.ty` steal `h`'s payload slot.
	function retainBorrowedAggregate(source ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let retained = self.emitRetainCall(source, range: range)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(retained, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
		self.carryInterfaceWitness(retained, from: source)
		return retained
	end 'retainBorrowedAggregate'

	// ⭐ **THE BYTE-RECORD TWIN OF `retainBorrowedAggregate` (W56)** — same ownership bookkeeping, a different
	// runtime entry, and the difference is that a byte record has TWO provenances where an aggregate has one.
	// `__str_retain` copies an immortal `.rdata` literal and increfs a heap record; either way the result is a
	// FRESH SSA value the merge phi owns outright, and `markValueOwnsHeap` rather than `trackOwnedTemp` for
	// the reason stated one function up — the phi, not this statement, owes the drop.
	//
	// The result carries the SOURCE's tag and name id, so a `Character` edge stays a `Character`.
	//
	// CO-OWNED for the reason the two provenances give it: `__str_retain` increfs a HEAP record, and which of its
	// two branches ran is exactly what this frame cannot know (that is why the entry exists). A byte record has no
	// slot a move-out can null, so the claim costs nothing today and is the true one.
	function retainBorrowedByteRecord(source ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let retained = self.emitRuntimeCall(StrRetainName, args: singletonArgs(source), resultTag: self.tagOf(source), resultNameId: self.valueNameId(source), range: range)
		// The SOURCE is downgraded too, for `emitIncref`'s reason — see `markValueCoOwnsHeap`. On the heap branch
		// this op is an incref, so afterwards two names hold one record; on the `.rdata` branch it is a copy and
		// the source was never sole anyway, so the mark is true either way and the parser need not know which ran.
		self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(source)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(retained, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
		return retained
	end 'retainBorrowedByteRecord'

	// ⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL'S FRESH NAME KEEPS THE SOURCE'S WITNESS (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).** A retain
	// takes a second owner of the conformer's record — or, for a byte record, a CLONE of it — and in neither
	// case does the conformer change, so the co-owner dispatches through the same table. But it is a different
	// ValueId, and the witness column is keyed by ValueId, so the pairing has to be carried or the first
	// dispatch on the result panics in `witnessOfValue`. A non-existential source has no witness half and
	// pairs nothing, which is what lets the two retain doors share this step.
	//
	// ⚠ Single-sourced because `retainBorrowedAggregate` and `coOwnBorrowedForConsume` are two callers of ONE
	// retain and only the first of them used to handle the result at all — the second discarded it, so this
	// step existed once and looked like a property of that door rather than of the retain.
	function carryInterfaceWitness(retained ValueId, from ValueId)
		if self.tagOf(from) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
			return
		end 'notAnExistential'

		self.pairInterfaceWitness(retained, witness: self.witnessOfValue(from))
	end 'carryInterfaceWitness'

	// ⭐⭐ **A PROMOTION'S FRESH NAME KEEPS THE SOURCE'S DECLARED SURFACE (W41)** — `carryInterfaceWitness`'s
	// twin, for the other per-value column a fresh SSA name would otherwise drop on the floor.
	// `__ManagedMemory` and the `Array` around it are ONE record told apart by PROVENANCE (see
	// `bufferSurfaceValues`), and that provenance is keyed by ValueId — so a promotion that mints a new id
	// hands back a value the buffer members no longer serve, and `m.length()` becomes
	// `E2015: Array member 'length'` on a name whose initializer plainly denoted the buffer.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, and it is what W41's own promotion woke.** `let (m, n) = pair()` over
	// `returns (__ManagedMemory, Int)` binds `m` to a field load off the hidden tuple temp; that load is now a
	// rebindable-slot read, so the binding promotes — and
	// `managed-memory-methods.tuple-destructuring-serves-the-roster` went red on exactly that E2015. The gap
	// was always there (a `var` of the same read took the same door); nothing had reached it.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT BELONGS TO THE PROMOTION AND NOT TO THE RETAIN, AND PUTTING IT ON THE RETAIN IS A MEASURED
	// WRONG ANSWER.** `retainBorrowedAggregate` is shared with the ADOPTION door (`ByteArray.init(mm)`), and
	// there the fresh unmarked name is the whole POINT — the surface deliberately FLIPS, so that `a.push(5)`
	// is served on the array roster while `mm.setByte(…)` goes on being served on the buffer roster over the
	// same record. `specs-shv2/array-init.md`'s `the-surface-flips` states it outright: *"It compiles because
	// the retain mints a fresh, unmarked name."* Carried there, NINE `array-init` cases turned red with the
	// roster exactly inverted. A promotion says "the same value under a new name"; an adoption says "a
	// different view of the same record" — two operations that share an incref and must not share this.
	//
	// It carries EVERY carrier, because they describe one value space and a promotion preserves all of them:
	// what the value DENOTES, whose record it IS (W192), what its tuple SLOTS denote and what its array
	// ELEMENTS denote are unchanged by taking a second owner (or, for a String, a copy).
	// `markDeclaredSurface` cannot serve here — it reads a DECLARATION, and a promotion has none to read; the
	// source value is the only thing that knows.
	function carryDeclaredSurface(promoted ValueId, from ValueId)
		if self.valueIsBufferSurface(from) 'sourceDenotesTheBuffer'
			self.markBufferSurface(promoted)
		end 'sourceDenotesTheBuffer'

		// ⚠ ONE ARM PER CARRIER, not an `else if` off the arm above, even though the sized answer implies the
		// buffer one: chaining them would make one column's carry conditional on another's, which is the
		// coupling the "every site handles ALL of them" obligation exists to keep out. The redundant insert
		// costs a hash on a set the promotion has already detached.
		if self.valueIsASizedContainersBuffer(from) 'sourceIsASizedContainersOwnRecord'
			self.markSizedContainerBuffer(promoted)
		end 'sourceIsASizedContainersOwnRecord'

		if self.valueElementIsBufferSurface(from) 'sourceHoldsBuffers'
			self.markBufferSurfaceElements(promoted)
		end 'sourceHoldsBuffers'

		self.markBufferSurfaceSlots(promoted, surface: try self.bufferSurfaceSlots.get(from) otherwise sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)
	end 'carryDeclaredSurface'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH RETAIN A BORROWED AGGREGATE TAKES — and an EXISTENTIAL takes a DIFFERENT one, because a
	// CONCRETE type's protocol is a compile-time fact and a conformer's is not (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).**
	// `__mm_retain` is the answer for exactly one shape: a heap aggregate whose alias is observable. A
	// conformer may be neither — `int`/`bool` conform intrinsically and are held at an interface as their RAW
	// VALUE (which is what makes them representable there where a `float` is not, E3121), and a `String`
	// literal is an IMMORTAL `.rdata` record that must be COPIED rather than increfed. So the existential arm
	// dispatches through the witness's `retainFunc@16` — `__retain_existential(witness, value)` — which is the
	// same three-way choice `promoteBorrowedToOwned` makes statically, deferred to run time.
	//
	// **BOTH SHORTCUTS WERE MEASURED AS 0xC0000005**: a plain `__mm_retain` on `use(h Comparable)` called with
	// `7`, writing through `<the integer> - 24 + 16` where the program answers 11; and a hardcoded incref
	// under the gate on `widen("abc")`, writing into read-only `.rdata`.
	//
	// ⚠ Both entries return a value the caller must USE — but only `__mm_retain` is guaranteed to hand back
	// what it was given, so a caller may never substitute the source for the result.
	function emitRetainCall(source ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if self.tagOf(source) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			return self.emitRetainExistentialCall(source, range: range)
		end 'existential'

		// `__mm_retain` gives `source`'s box a second owner exactly as `__mm_incref` does, so the source is
		// downgraded here for the identical reason — see `markValueCoOwnsHeap`.
		self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(source)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(MmRetainName, args: singletonArgs(source), resultTag: self.tagOf(source), resultNameId: self.valueNameId(source), range: range)
	end 'emitRetainCall'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE `__retain_existential` CALL**, so its argument ORDER — the witness first, matching
	// `__drop_existential` — is spelled once. BOTH callers want the same half of it, the returned id: the
	// entry dispatches through the witness's `retainFunc@16`, which for a byte-record conformer is a
	// `__str_clone` handing back a DIFFERENT record, so the co-owner is what came out and never what went in.
	// The result carries the SOURCE's exact type so a caller that binds it holds the same existential.
	function emitRetainExistentialCall(source ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		// Downgraded for `markValueCoOwnsHeap`'s reason. Which of the witness's three protocols runs is a RUN-TIME
		// choice (incref a record, copy a byte record, hand back a raw value), and the co-owned answer is the
		// sound one under every branch — the incref one makes it literally true, and the others make it harmless.
		self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(source)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(RetainExistentialName, args: pairArgs(self.witnessOfValue(source), second: source), resultTag: self.tagOf(source), resultNameId: self.valueNameId(source), range: range)
	end 'emitRetainExistentialCall'

	// ⭐ **BECOME A SECOND OWNER OF `source`'s BOX, AS AN UNBOUND STATEMENT-SCOPED TEMPORARY** — the door for
	// an expression that hands back a record SOMEBODY ELSE already owes a drop on, and whose own result may be
	// bound to nothing.
	//
	// It is `retainBorrowedAggregate` PLUS `trackOwnedTemp`, and the second half is the part that is easy to
	// leave off: the door above sets the owned-heap bit ALONE, because it is written for a value whose drop a
	// BINDING will owe (`promoteBorrowedToOwned`). A value that may never reach a binding —
	// `ByteArray.init(mm).count()`, `f(bytes.managed)` — needs the STATEMENT-scoped obligation as well, or the
	// retain is never released (measured as exit 101 with `trackOwnedTemp` removed, review of BATCH2 slice 6).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TWO CALLERS ARE EXACT INVERSES OF EACH OTHER AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS ONE FUNCTION**:
	// `parseArrayInitStatic` adopts a buffer AS the array around it, `bufferSurfaceOf` hands the array's buffer
	// BACK. They differ in exactly ONE line — whether the fresh id is marked `markBufferSurface` — and until
	// this door existed they agreed on the ownership pair only by each spelling it out. A third ownership fact
	// added to one of the two copies would have been a leak or a double free in the other, with nothing to say
	// so: the surfaces are inverses, but the refcount question they answer is the same question.
	// ⭐⭐ **TRANSITIVE CONSUME: A BORROWED AGGREGATE REACHING A CONSUMING POSITION TAKES ITS OWN REFERENCE**
	// (the user ruling, 2026-08-04; P1.7a-existentials slice 2). Both doors that hand a value to durable
	// storage — a CONSUMED ARGUMENT (`transferConsumedArg`, the callee will store it) and a DIRECT move-in
	// (`moveManagedValueInto`, this frame stores it) — used to REFUSE a borrowed struct/union/existential with
	// an `E2015` naming the call-graph fixpoint that would decide it. This is that decision, and it is a
	// refcount rather than a fixpoint: the storage becomes a second owner, the borrow's own owner is untouched,
	// and the container's destructor releases exactly the reference taken here. It is the SAME `__mm_incref`
	// co-ownership shv2 already sanctions for a slice view, a `try … otherwise` phi reconciliation and a
	// borrowed aggregate bound into a `var` — generalized to the one position that was still refused.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `retainBorrowedAggregate`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WHAT OWNS THE RESULT.** That door marks
	// its result owned because a BINDING will drop it at scope exit; here the new owner is a SLOT in another
	// record, so this frame marks nothing and drops nothing — the container's destructor releases exactly the
	// reference taken here.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **BUT THE RESULT IS STILL WHAT THE CALLER MUST STORE, AND DISCARDING IT WAS A MEASURED ACCESS
	// VIOLATION.** The struct/union arm below hands back the same box (`__mm_incref` is void and an aggregate
	// must stay one record), and the existential arm used to do the same on the reasoning that a fresh id
	// "would buy nothing". It buys the whole correctness of a byte-record conformer: `__retain_existential`
	// dispatches through the witness's `retainFunc@16`, which for a `String`/`Character` is a `__str_clone`
	// handing back a DIFFERENT, independently-droppable record. Returning the source instead put an immortal
	// `.rdata` literal into durable storage AND leaked the clone — MEASURED as `widen("abc")` printing `A` and
	// dying with 0xC0000005.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS NOT A MOVE.** The source is deliberately NOT poisoned: co-ownership is what makes
	// `Holder.create(p)` twice legal in one body, where a move would make the second an E3102 and a single
	// transferred `+1` a double free at the second destructor.
	function coOwnBorrowedForConsume(value ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			let retained = self.emitRetainExistentialCall(value, range: range)
			self.carryInterfaceWitness(retained, from: value)
			return retained
		end 'existential'

		self.emitIncref(value, range: range)
		return value
	end 'coOwnBorrowedForConsume'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE REFERENCE A REBIND FROM AN IMMUTABLE SOURCE OWES — the assignment door's half of the
	// 2026-08-04 alias ruling** (`rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias` carries the rule; this is what it costs
	// here). The source is NOT poisoned, so it keeps the drop it has always owed; this binding is enrolled
	// in `ownedBindings` and owes one too, so it must hold a reference of its own or one box is released
	// twice. That is the same co-ownership the 2026-08-12 durable-store ruling gives a field, an element and
	// a by-reference parameter's cell (`storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink`) — the difference is only WHO owns
	// the result, which is why that door cannot be reused verbatim: there the new owner is a SLOT and the
	// result is marked nothing, here it is a BINDING and the result must carry the owned-heap bit.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FRESH SSA NAME IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT A BY-PRODUCT OF WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY WAS HANDY.** Both
	// entries RETURN the reference they took (`__mm_retain`'s whole reason for existing beside the void
	// `__mm_incref` — see `retainBorrowedAggregate`), so this binding holds an id the source does not: a
	// later move out of either name resolves to exactly one owner, where a shared id would let
	// `liveOwnerIndexOf` pick the wrong binding and poison — or drop — the other one's reference.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT MUST CARRY THE PER-VALUE COLUMNS ACROSS THAT FRESH NAME.** A promotion already owes this
	// (`promoteBorrowedToOwned` wraps `carryDeclaredSurface` for exactly the W41 measurement); a retain that
	// re-mints a name owes the identical debt, and the W117 immutable-global taint is the one whose loss is
	// not an `E2015` but a WRITE that reaches a record the language calls immutable. The interface witness
	// is carried inside `retainBorrowedAggregate`, where the existential arm that needs it lives.
	//
	// ⚠ **`promoteBorrowedToOwned` IS THE NEAREST-LOOKING SIBLING AND IS NOT THIS.** Its precondition is a
	// BORROWED value (its own `panic` says so), and both of its answers are wrong here: at the `binding`
	// door a byte record COPIES, and at `merged` a trivial tuple COPIES — where this door's whole subject is
	// a value the source still owns, whose IDENTITY the two names must go on sharing. What the two have in
	// common is the column carry, which is why that is called and not re-spelled.
	function coOwnImmutableRebindSource(value ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		// **THE SPLIT IS "IS THERE A SECOND OWNER TO KEEP ALIVE?"** — `storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink`'s
		// split, asked here for its reason and answered the same way. An owned value NO BINDING holds has no
		// other owner at all, so this binding ADOPTS the `+1` it was handed and the caller's
		// `removeFromPendingTemps` cancels the statement-end drop; an incref/decref pair around a value
		// nothing else releases is pure churn. The shape that reaches it is a `for` element — declared
		// IMMUTABLE (`declareLoopVariable`) and enrolled in no owned set, so it satisfies the alias rule
		// while owning its value through the statement rather than through a name.
		if self.noLiveOwner(self.liveOwnerIndexOf(value)) 'ownedTemporaryIsAdopted'
			return value
		end 'ownedTemporaryIsAdopted'

		// The two retain protocols, and they are the SAME two `storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink` routes
		// between: a byte record through `__str_retain` (an incref on the heap branch, a copy of an immortal
		// `.rdata` literal on the other, and the parser cannot know which), everything else through
		// `__mm_retain`, which is also where an existential's witness-dispatched retain lives.
		let retained = self.retainBorrowedByteRecord(value, range: range) if tagIsByteRecord(self.tagOf(value)) else self.retainBorrowedAggregate(value, range: range)

		self.carryDeclaredSurface(retained, from: value)
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: value, to: retained)
		return retained
	end 'coOwnImmutableRebindSource'

	function coOwnAggregateAsTemp(source ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let owned = self.retainBorrowedAggregate(source, range: range)
		// `coOwned` restates the retain above rather than contradicting it — this call adds the statement-scoped
		// DROP obligation and must not launder the ownership claim while it is there.
		self.trackOwnedTemp(owned, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
		return owned
	end 'coOwnAggregateAsTemp'

	// ⭐ **MAKE A BORROWED MANAGED VALUE OWNED — the ONE `String`-copy-vs-aggregate-retain decision every site
	// that needs an owned value out of a borrowed one must make.** A borrowed value cannot be enrolled as
	// owned as-is: its owner is elsewhere, so a scope-exit drop would free the caller's box a second time. So
	// it is promoted — a String COPIES into a fresh owned record (value semantics), a struct/union INCREFS the
	// shared box (reference semantics: an aggregate has no cheap owning copy and its alias is observable
	// through a field write). The caller has already established that `value` is a BORROWED managed heap value
	// (`valueIsManagedHeap and not valueIsOwnedHeap`), so the two arms are exhaustive and a scalar — which owns
	// no heap and never reaches an owned binding — is a parser invariant break, not an unhandled case.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THREE DOORS, NOT TWO (S5).** A DECLARATION (`var q = p`) and a REASSIGNMENT (`q = p2`) were the
	// first two; a HAND-OFF is the third — a `return` (`emitOwnedValueReturn`) and a match/ternary arm merged
	// into an owned result phi (`promoteBorrowedGive`), which both used to REFUSE a borrowed aggregate outright
	// on the reasoning that co-owning across a call boundary needed the cross-call consume. It does not: the
	// callee increfs, the caller adopts and decrefs once, and the borrow's own owner is untouched — the
	// refcount is balanced on every path, which is exactly what this incref already buys inside one frame. The
	// value oracle settles the SEMANTICS the incref implies (measured: a `return self` whose result is written
	// through shows the write on the receiver — `p.x=5 q.x=5`), and v1 emits the same `__mm_incref` before the
	// `ret` for the same programs (`InsertRefcounts.planReturnIncrefs`).
	//
	// ⚠ Single-sourced BECAUSE the two callers straddle a boundary that let them drift: before this, the
	// reassignment path spelled only the String half (`promoteToOwnedString`) and, once the declaration
	// began making struct/union `var`s owned (OPEN #40), string-promoted a borrowed STRUCT on `q = p2` — a
	// blit of the struct's fields as a fused String record, a SILENT miscompile the type checker waves
	// through (both sides are the same aggregate type) and the suite was green over. Routing both sites
	// here makes a future divergence a compile error rather than a wrong answer.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AN AGGREGATE READ OUT OF AN IMMUTABLE GLOBAL IS REFUSED HERE (P1.7 slice 2b), and this is the ONE
	// place that can refuse it.** The aggregate arm promotes by INCREF — the owner becomes a second owner of
	// the SAME box, deliberately, because the alias is observable — so a mutable binding of a value read from
	// a `let`-declared global would be a writable alias of a record the language says is immutable:
	// `var b = A` then `b.push(9)` grows A, with E2013 and E3019 both intact and nothing to say so (MEASURED
	// at 3 where 2 is correct, through BOTH callers: the declaration `var b = A` and the reassignment
	// `b = A`). A String source never reaches it — the text arm COPIES, which is why `var b = <a String let>`
	// stays legal and correct.
	//
	// The refusal is E2015 and names the missing mechanism, because that is exactly what this is: a real COPY
	// promotion for a borrowed array would make the program work, and shv2 has none (an `Array` has no clone;
	// `retainBorrowedAggregate` is all there is). Widening the incref into a copy is not an option here — a
	// borrowed array bound to a `var` SHARES in both reference compilers when the source is mutable
	// (measured: the oracle returns 3 for a `var` global and for a parameter, exactly as shv2 does), so
	// copying would break an agreement rather than fix a disagreement. Immutability of the SOURCE is the
	// whole discriminator.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE PER-VALUE SURFACE COLUMN RIDES ACROSS, HERE AND NOT IN ANY ARM (W41).** All three arms mint a
	// ValueId distinct from `value` — that is what makes them promotions — and the buffer-surface provenance
	// is keyed by ValueId, so it must be carried or a promoted `__ManagedMemory` stops answering on its own
	// roster. One line at the door rather than one per arm, and deliberately NOT one level down at
	// `retainBorrowedAggregate`, which the ADOPTION also uses and which needs the opposite: see
	// `carryDeclaredSurface`, where both halves are recorded with the measurement that separates them.
	function promoteBorrowedToOwned(value ValueId, door BorrowLaunderDoor, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let promoted = try self.ownedFormOfBorrowedValue(value, door: door, range: range)
		self.carryDeclaredSurface(promoted, from: value)
		return promoted
	end 'promoteBorrowedToOwned'

	// The three-way protocol choice `promoteBorrowedToOwned` is written around — copy a String, copy a
	// trivial tuple at a hand-off, incref anything else — split out so the surface carry above can wrap all
	// three without being written into each. Every word of that door's header describes this body; it is
	// called from nowhere else.
	function ownedFormOfBorrowedValue(value ValueId, door BorrowLaunderDoor, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if tagIsByteRecord(self.tagOf(value)) 'byteRecord'
			// ⭐⭐ **A MERGE EDGE CO-OWNS WHERE THE OTHER TWO DOORS COPY, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A WRITE THAT MUST
			// REACH THE CALLER (W56).** `grow(make() if c else s)` hands the phi to a callee that WRITES it, so a
			// copy of the `s` edge is a write nobody sees: MEASURED `v=ab` against the oracle's `v=abXY`. The
			// split is by DOOR rather than by widening the copy away because the other two doors have their own
			// settled answers and no measured defect: a BINDING becomes a new owner in this frame — which is
			// what keeps `var b = <a String let>` legal (see the immutable-global refusal below, whose soundness
			// rests on the text arm copying) — and a `return` a new owner in the CALLER's, which is what makes a
			// `try f() otherwise <owned>` merge's ok edge provably a heap record rather than its caller's
			// literal.
			//
			// ⚠ **AND IT CANNOT BE `emitIncref`, BECAUSE THE PARSER CANNOT KNOW WHICH KIND OF RECORD THIS IS.**
			// A borrowed `String` parameter is heap when its caller owned one and immortal `.rdata` when its
			// caller wrote a literal, and `pick(v, …)` / `pick("ab", …)` over one `pick(s String, …)` differ in
			// nothing this frame can read. `__str_retain` is that decision, made where it can be — off the
			// record's own `capacity@16`. See its header for the two measurements that bound it on both sides.
			// ⭐⭐ **A HAND-OFF RETAINS, IT DOES NOT COPY — `__str_retain`, WHICH IS THE ONLY PARTY THAT CAN
			// TELL AN IMMORTAL LITERAL FROM A HEAP RECORD.** Both hand-off doors take this arm: a MERGE edge
			// always did (a copy of one edge is a write nobody sees — `grow(make() if c else s)` printed
			// `v=ab` against the oracle's `v=abXY`), and a `return` now does too. The property the return
			// door was copying FOR is unchanged: `__str_retain` clones an immortal `.rdata` literal and
			// increfs a heap record, so what the caller adopts is an owned, independently-droppable record
			// either way — which is exactly what makes a `try f() otherwise <owned>` merge's ok edge
			// provably a heap record rather than its caller's literal.
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **COPYING A RETURNED BORROWED STRING IS AGAINST THE LANGUAGE.** Maxon is single-ownership
			// with reference semantics — *"everything is a reference; if you want a copy you do it explicitly
			// with `clone()`"* (user ruling) — so a `return name` handing back a DIFFERENT record than the `name`
			// it was given is a copy the author did not write, and the caller's value stops being the callee's.
			// The rule is the reason; the cost is not.
			//
			// ⚠ **AND THE COST IS *NOT* THE REASON — MEASURED, because it was the suspicion that found this
			// door.** A self-compile made the shv2-built compiler allocate **1,089,279** times against the
			// bootstrap-built one's **618,817** on the same input (1.76x), and this arm was the first
			// hypothesis. Removing the copy here moved it to **1,078,206** — about 11,000 of a ~470,000 gap,
			// ~2%. So the blowup is somewhere else and is STILL OPEN; do not read this comment as having
			// explained it.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE `binding` DOOR STILL COPIES AND IS NOT THIS CHANGE.** Its copy is load-bearing for a
			// different rule — it is what keeps `var b = <a String let>` legal — and the ownership ruling
			// says that program should be **E3078** (`a var may not be declared from an immutable
			// reference`) rather than a silent copy. That is a spec-visible decision about a refusal, not a
			// cost fix, so it is left where it is and named here rather than folded in.
			if borrowLaunderDoorIsHandOff(door) 'handOff'
				return self.retainBorrowedByteRecord(value, range: range)
			end 'handOff'

			return self.promoteToOwnedString(value, range: range)
		end 'byteRecord'

		// ⭐ **A TRIVIAL TUPLE HANDED OFF GETS ITS OWN RECORD (P1.10), AND IT IS ASKED BEFORE THE AGGREGATE ARM
		// BECAUSE A TUPLE IS A `structRef` THAT ARM WOULD INCREF INSTEAD.** The caller adopts and frees whatever
		// a `returns (…)` hands back, and a TRIVIAL tuple — unlike a struct — may simply be COPIED: the only way
		// to obtain one is the expression that produced it, so within the set `valueIsCopyableTuple` admits there
		// is no prior identity to break (see `moveManagedValueInto` for the full argument). Without it,
		// `function echo(t (Num, Num)) returns (Num, Num) return t end` would share one record where a tuple's
		// whole point is that it does not.
		//
		// ⚠ **A MANAGED-ELEMENT tuple is NOT admitted and therefore falls through to the INCREF, which is the
		// oracle's answer too** — measured on both compilers: writing through a returned `(String, Num)` shows on
		// the source (`a.1=99 b.1=99`), while the trivial spelling one line over does not (`a.1=3 b.1=99`). So the
		// split is not "tuples have no identity" — a managed one demonstrably does — it is that a shallow copy of
		// a managed element would free one String twice, and the incref is the sound answer available today.
		//
		// ⚠ It is gated on the door because a BINDING is not a hand-off: `var q = t` aliases on both compilers
		// (see `borrowLaunderDoorIsHandOff`), so copying there would break an agreement rather than fix one.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT SITS AHEAD OF THE IMMUTABLE-GLOBAL ARM BELOW, AND THAT ORDER IS SOUND RATHER THAN LUCKY.**
		// That arm carries the mark across an INCREF because an incref hands back the SAME record; a COPY
		// carries nothing — the fresh record is a different record, and writing it cannot reach the global —
		// which is the same reason the `byteRecord` arm above is already ahead of it. So a marked TUPLE is
		// copied and comes out UNMARKED, which is the right answer and not a hole. It cannot arise today (a
		// top-level global cannot hold a tuple: `let A = (1, 2)` is E2010 in shv2 AND in the bootstrap oracle),
		// and the day one can, copying is what keeps the global immutable.
		if borrowLaunderDoorIsHandOff(door) and self.valueIsCopyableTuple(value) 'copyableTuple'
			return self.copyTupleValue(value, range: range)
		end 'copyableTuple'

		if self.valueIsNonTextAggregate(value) 'aggregate'
			// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE MARK IS CARRIED ACROSS THE PROMOTION, NOT REFUSED AT IT (W117, ⚖ user 2026-08-14).**
			// Reading a `let`-declared top-level global and naming the result is legal; MUTATING through what
			// comes back is the error. So an incref of a marked record mints a value that is marked too, and
			// the refusal moved to the doors that write: `requireWritableInstance` (`b.n = 5`),
			// `immutableReceiverNameOf` (`b.push(9)`) and the call-argument check that reads what those two
			// publish. Before this, the promotion itself was refused — which made a read-only accessor
			// unwritable and blocked `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`'s eleven presets outright.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE `returned` DOOR IS THE ONE THAT CAN STILL REFUSE, AND ONLY WHEN THE CALLER HAS NOT BEEN
			// TOLD.** A promotion at `binding` or `merged` stays in THIS function's SSA space, where the mark
			// travels with the value; a `return` hands the record to a frame whose marks are its own, so it is
			// safe exactly when the whole-program sweep published this function as one whose result must be
			// marked (`enclosingFunctionMayReturnAGlobalAlias`). When it did not — a `return` shape the sweep
			// deliberately does not recognise, or a CLOSURE body, which has no static callee for any fact to
			// be published against — the value would escape into a caller with no idea what it holds, and the
			// original refusal is what stops it. That fallback is what lets the sweep be narrow: a shape it
			// misses costs this refusal, never a silent acceptance.
			if self.immutableGlobalReads.contains(value) 'immutableSource'
				if door == BorrowLaunderDoor.returned and not self.enclosingFunctionMayReturnAGlobalAlias 'escapesUntracked'
					throw ParseError.unsupported(borrowLaunderRefusal(door), line: range.line, column: range.column)
				end 'escapesUntracked'

				let owned = self.retainBorrowedAggregate(value, range: range)
				self.markImmutableGlobalRead(owned)
				return owned
			end 'immutableSource'

			return self.retainBorrowedAggregate(value, range: range)
		end 'aggregate'

		panic("ownedFormOfBorrowedValue: v{value} is neither text nor a managed aggregate — the caller has already gated on `valueIsManagedHeap`, so a borrowed value promoted to owned is always a String (copy) or a struct/union box (retain); a scalar owns no heap and never reaches an owned binding's promotion")
	end 'ownedFormOfBorrowedValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **MINT THE ONE OWNED REFERENCE A RESULT PHI'S BORROWED EDGE OWES IT, INTO THAT EDGE'S OWN BLOCK.**
	// The `merged` door has TWO callers — a `match`/ternary arm (`promoteBorrowedGive`) and a
	// `try … otherwise <expr>` fallback (`finishValueTry`) — and they owe the phi the identical THREE steps, of
	// which only the block differs. Spelled at both sites they were held together by a comment reading *"exactly
	// as `promoteBorrowedGive` does"*, which is a test nobody wrote: a third obligation added to one edge kind
	// would not fail to compile at the other, it would silently leak or double-free on the arm that missed it.
	//
	// The three steps, and why each:
	//   • EMIT INTO THE EDGE, not into `currentBlock`. A match arm's promotion belongs in its still-unterminated
	//     exit block, or the incref runs on the path that did not take the arm. `finishValueTry` passes the
	//     block it is already in (`errEnd`), so the save/restore is inert there and the rule stays stated once.
	//   • PROMOTE through the one door — a String copies, a trivial tuple gets its own record, an aggregate
	//     increfs.
	//   • DE-LIST it from the statement's pending temps. The phi owns it now; a statement-end drain would free a
	//     record the merged result still holds. ⚠ And this is why the promotion must NOT also be handed to
	//     `transferFallbackToPhi`: a retain mints a fresh SSA name that is no pending temp, so that helper would
	//     read it as an aliased BINDING and incref a second time — a leak. `transferFallbackToPhi` settles an
	//     ALREADY-OWNED edge, which is the other branch of the question, not a further step of this one.
	function promoteBorrowedMergeEdge(value ValueId, edgeBlock IrBlock, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let saved = self.currentBlock
		self.currentBlock = edgeBlock

		let owned = try self.promoteBorrowedToOwned(value, door: BorrowLaunderDoor.merged, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(owned)

		self.currentBlock = saved
		return owned
	end 'promoteBorrowedMergeEdge'

	// ⭐ **DROP EVERY OWNED TEMPORARY THE STATEMENT LEFT UNCONSUMED**, in REVERSE creation order (the
	// `emitScopeDrops` convention — the last built is the first torn down), into `currentBlock`. Called at
	// the end of a statement, so the drop lands exactly where control is when the statement finishes: a loop
	// body, an `if` branch, a short-circuit merge. Clears the list — a temporary belongs to ONE statement.
	//
	// `anchor` is the statement's own token; no source token spawns a drop, so it rides the statement that
	// made control leave the temporary's lifetime, exactly as `emitScopeDrops` rides the `return`.
	// ⭐ **RELEASE EVERY PENDING TEMPORARY ABOVE `floor`, AND FORGET THEM.** `WholeStatementTempFloor` ends
	// the whole list (a statement's tail, a hand-off exit); a REGION passes the height it recorded before
	// parsing itself, so it releases what IT built and leaves the enclosing statement's temporaries alone.
	// That distinction is the whole point: an `if` inside `let v = f("{x}", …)` must not release the
	// interpolation `f` has not been handed yet.
	//
	// A floor ABOVE the current count is not an error and does nothing — the list is *shorter* than the
	// region's start only when something already released past it (a `return` inside a match arm drains the
	// whole list on its own edge), and there is then nothing left for the region to release.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REGIONS THAT MUST CALL THIS, WRITTEN DOWN ONCE — TEACH A NEW ONE HERE.** Every construct that
	// FORKS owes its own temporaries a release in a block their definitions dominate; miss one and the shape
	// is a leak (a release on too few paths) or a release of a value the taken path never built (which the
	// register allocator reports, several passes later, as "a use dominates its def"):
	//   • `parseIfStatement` / `parseWhileStatement` — the condition, in its exit block before the branch,
	//     EXCEPT a CATCHING `if`, whose condition is a fork and releases them at the head of EACH EDGE
	//     (`takeCatchingForkTemps`). ⚠ That is NOT `desugarTry`'s rule and this bullet said it was: an `if`'s
	//     condition is FINISHED when it branches, so its temporaries are dead on both edges, while an
	//     expression-level `try` forks mid-expression and its operands are not;
	//   • `parseMatchCommon` — the scrutinee before the first test, and each ARM at its own exit
	//     (`finishArmExit`, which releases the arm's SCOPE-lifetime records in the same block and by the same
	//     rule);
	//   • `parseTernaryExpression` — the condition, and each of the two arms (`finishArmExit` again);
	//   • `emitShortCircuit` — the right-hand side, which runs on one path only;
	//   • `desugarTry` — the odd one out, and deliberately: its fork temporaries belong to the ENCLOSING
	//     EXPRESSION, which is only half-built at the fork, so they are LIVE past it on every PATH that goes
	//     on to finish that expression. For the handler's parse they leave this list entirely
	//     (`takeForkTemps`) and are enrolled as anonymous OWNED BINDINGS
	//     (`suspendForkTempsAsOwned`), so that each abandoning path releases them through its own scope drop;
	//     `resumeForkTemps` hands them back for the paths that merge. Never swept here.
	// There is no automatic check behind this list, and that is a known gap rather than an oversight: the
	// flat list is always drained by *something* downstream, so an end-of-parse "nothing left over" assertion
	// cannot see a release that landed in the wrong BLOCK — the defect this discipline exists to prevent. A
	// real guard needs a def-block per temporary plus a dominance query. `specs-shv2/block-condition-temporaries.md`
	// pins one case per region in the meantime, and the leak gate (`!= 0`, so it catches an over-release too)
	// is what makes those cases fail loudly.
	function drainPendingTemps(floor TempDropMark, anchor Token)
		self.emitTempDrops(self.pendingTempDrops, floor: floor, anchor: anchor)
		while self.pendingTempDrops.count() > floor 'forget'
			_ = try self.pendingTempDrops.pop() otherwise panic("drainPendingTemps: pop above floor {floor} failed on a list of {self.pendingTempDrops.count()} — the loop pops strictly while the count exceeds the floor")
		end 'forget'
	end 'drainPendingTemps'

	// ⭐⭐ **SUSPEND THE ENCLOSING EXPRESSION'S PENDING TEMPORARIES FOR THE DURATION OF A `try` HANDLER — the
	// pair `desugarTry` brackets its handler parse with, and the whole of what makes an abandoning path
	// release them without this file knowing which paths abandon.**
	//
	// `takeForkTemps` moves them OFF `pendingTempDrops`, because a handler's own drains
	// (`emitHandoffExitDrops` behind an `otherwise return`, a handler block's statement tails) read the WHOLE
	// list with a whole-statement floor: left in place they would release values the ok edge still owns AND
	// FORGET them, leaking on that path. It COPIES rather than moving the field's array — a handle taken out
	// of a field that is then reassigned is the exact shape that once crashed this parser (`emitTempDrops`
	// reading a pointer-shaped word where a `ValueId` belonged) — and the list is empty for almost every
	// `try`, so the copy is a `create()` and nothing else.
	function takeForkTemps() returns ValueIdArray
		var taken = ValueIdArray.create()
		for temp in self.pendingTempDrops 'eachPending'
			taken.push(temp)
		end 'eachPending'

		self.pendingTempDrops = ValueIdArray.create()
		return taken
	end 'takeForkTemps'

	// ⭐⭐ **AND ENROL THEM AS ANONYMOUS OWNED BINDINGS, WHICH IS WHERE THE RELEASE ACTUALLY COMES FROM.**
	// `parseOtherwiseHandler` makes the identical enrolment one level in for the caught error box, and states
	// the reason this one exists too: *"so a terminating form's own `emitScopeDrops` releases it"*. Every
	// path that leaves the enclosing expression already funnels through a scope drop with a FLOOR — a
	// hand-off exit through the whole-function one, a `break`/`continue` through its target's — so putting
	// the temporaries in that registry answers, for free and by construction, both "which paths owe a
	// release" and "does THIS `break` leave the `try` at all". See `desugarTry`'s fork for the enumeration.
	//
	// They are enrolled ANONYMOUSLY (`EmptySourceText`): an operand of a half-built expression has no name
	// and must acquire none — every name-keyed reader (`requireBindingLive`, the move poisoner, the
	// reassignment scan) reaches a binding through `Scope`, which this never enters.
	//
	// ⚠ `movedFrom` is false and stays false — nothing inside a handler can name these to move out of one —
	// so `emitScopeDrops` never skips them and `captureMoveMark`'s positional snapshot is unaffected beyond
	// its height, which `desugarTry` takes AFTER this call for exactly that reason.
	//
	// Returns the height to rewind to, so the un-enrolment cannot drift from the enrolment's count.
	function suspendForkTempsAsOwned(forkTemps ValueIdArray) returns BindingMark
		let mark = self.ownedBindings.count()
		for temp in forkTemps 'eachSuspended'
			self.ownedBindings.push(VarInfo.createValueBinding(EmptySourceText, varType: self.typeOf(temp), mutable: false, boundValue: temp))
		end 'eachSuspended'
		return mark
	end 'suspendForkTempsAsOwned'

	// ⭐ **RESUME: un-enrol, and hand the temporaries back to the statement that owns them.** The paths that
	// reach here are the ones that go on to finish the enclosing expression — the ok edge, and a merged error
	// edge — so from here the ordinary statement-end drain is the correct releaser again.
	//
	// They go back UNDER everything enrolled since (the value merge's phi, a re-enrolled managed try-result),
	// because the drop order is creation order reversed and these were built first.
	//
	// ⚠ The owned-binding pop is a TRUNCATION to the recorded mark rather than a `removeFromOwnedBindings`
	// per temp: mid-stack removal desyncs every block and loop drop mark taken since, which is the reason
	// `reassignBindingValue` gives for leaving a moved-from ghost in the list rather than deleting it.
	function resumeForkTemps(forkTemps ValueIdArray, suspendedMark BindingMark)
		while self.ownedBindings.count() > suspendedMark 'unenrol'
			_ = try self.ownedBindings.pop() otherwise panic("resumeForkTemps: ownedBindings.pop above the suspension mark {suspendedMark} failed on a list of {self.ownedBindings.count()} — the loop pops strictly while the count exceeds the mark")
		end 'unenrol'

		if forkTemps.count() == 0 'nothingToHandBack'
			return
		end 'nothingToHandBack'

		var merged = ValueIdArray.create()
		for temp in forkTemps 'builtBeforeTheFork'
			merged.push(temp)
		end 'builtBeforeTheFork'
		for temp in self.pendingTempDrops 'enrolledSinceTheFork'
			merged.push(temp)
		end 'enrolledSinceTheFork'

		self.pendingTempDrops = merged
	end 'resumeForkTemps'

	// Emit the `__mm_decref` for each of `temps` above `floor`, in REVERSE creation order, into
	// `currentBlock`, WITHOUT touching the list. The drop LOOP, split out so a fork that releases the SAME
	// temporaries into more than one block — the catching `if`, through `emitTempDropsInto`, and the block
	// form's landing pads — reuses it instead of spelling the reverse walk a second time. A second copy is how
	// they would come to disagree on the order, and the order is a rule (`emitScopeDrops`' convention: the
	// last built is the first torn down).
	function emitTempDrops(temps ValueIdArray, floor TempDropMark, anchor Token)
		var i = temps.count()
		while i > floor 'eachTemp'
			i = i - 1
			let temp = try temps.get(i) otherwise panic("emitTempDrops: temps.get OOB at {i} — the list is walked strictly downwards from its own count")
			self.emitDecref(temp, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'eachTemp'
	end 'emitTempDrops'

	// ⭐⭐ **RELEASE A CATCHING `if`'s CONDITION TEMPORARIES AT THE HEAD OF ONE EDGE — in that edge's OWN
	// block, whatever block the parse is standing in.** The condition is fully evaluated before the split, so
	// nothing downstream can still consume what it built: the temporaries are dead on BOTH edges and each
	// releases them exactly once, in a block their definitions dominate. `currentBlock` is saved and restored
	// because the caller is not done with the block it was standing in.
	//
	// ⛔ **`desugarTry` USED TO SHARE THIS AND MUST NOT.** An expression-level `try` forks in the MIDDLE of an
	// expression, so its pending temporaries are operands the ok edge has yet to consume rather than garbage —
	// releasing them at the head of `tryok` freed a value stored into a struct three ops later. That fork
	// suspends them as owned bindings instead (`suspendForkTempsAsOwned`), so each ABANDONING PATH releases
	// them through its own scope drop; the difference between the two forks is exactly whether an unfinished
	// expression survives the split.
	function emitTempDropsInto(block IrBlock, temps ValueIdArray, anchor Token)
		if temps.count() == 0 'nothingToRelease'
			return
		end 'nothingToRelease'

		let saved = self.currentBlock
		self.currentBlock = block
		self.emitTempDrops(temps, floor: WholeStatementTempFloor, anchor: anchor)
		self.currentBlock = saved
	end 'emitTempDropsInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **A CATCHING `if` IS A `try` FORK, SO ITS CONDITION'S TEMPORARIES MAY NOT DIE IN THE ENTRY BLOCK.**
	// A PLAIN condition's do, and that is `drainPendingTemps`' own rule: `entry` is the single path through
	// the condition and dominates then, else and continuation alike, so one release there is one release on
	// every path.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE PLACEMENT IS PER EDGE, because the two edges merge in the continuation and one release there is
	// one release too few on the other path.** The then and else blocks are the fork's two edges, and each
	// gets the drops at its head, through `emitTempDropsInto`.
	//
	// ⚠ **`desugarTry` LOOKED LIKE THE SAME FORK AND IS NOT, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY THEY SHARED THE
	// RULE.** An `if`'s CONDITION is a finished expression when it branches, so nothing downstream can consume
	// what it built and the temporaries are dead on both edges. An expression-level `try` forks in the MIDDLE
	// of an expression, so its pending temporaries are operands still to be consumed — releasing them at the
	// head of `tryok` freed a value stored into a struct three ops later. That fork therefore releases only on
	// a PATH that abandons the expression (`suspendForkTempsAsOwned`), and this one is unchanged.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE HEAD OF AN EDGE IS WHERE A COMPILER EMITS THINGS THAT READ A TEMPORARY.** MEASURED when the
	// caller still took a substituted `T` result's `+1` itself: that retain landed at the head of the ok edge
	// and read out of one of these very temporaries — `if let x = try make().fetch()` released the receiver in
	// `entry`, ahead of the branch, and the then branch read `0x3F3F…` poison (`ok=4557430888798830399`). The
	// `+1` has since moved into the callee (`emitOwnedValueReturn`), so that particular reader is gone; the
	// placement stays because it is the one an edge-head emission cannot invalidate.
	function takeCatchingForkTemps(cond IfCondition, floor TempDropMark, anchor Token) returns ValueIdArray
		if not ifConditionCatches(cond) 'plainCondition'
			self.drainPendingTemps(floor, anchor: anchor)
			return self.noForkTemps
		end 'plainCondition'

		var carried = ValueIdArray.create()
		var i = floor
		while i < self.pendingTempDrops.count() 'collect'
			carried.push(try self.pendingTempDrops.get(i) otherwise panic("takeCatchingForkTemps: pendingTempDrops.get OOB at {i} on a list of {self.pendingTempDrops.count()}"))
			i = i + 1
		end 'collect'
		while self.pendingTempDrops.count() > floor 'forget'
			_ = try self.pendingTempDrops.pop() otherwise panic("takeCatchingForkTemps: pendingTempDrops.pop above floor {floor} failed on a list of {self.pendingTempDrops.count()} — the loop pops strictly while the count exceeds the floor")
		end 'forget'

		return carried
	end 'takeCatchingForkTemps'

	// ⭐ **OPEN A CATCHING `if`'s OK EDGE — the fork's condition temporaries are released at its head.** They
	// can be, because the condition is a FINISHED expression when it branches: nothing downstream consumes
	// them. ⛔ `desugarTry` may NOT do this and this header used to say it did — an expression-level `try`
	// forks mid-expression, and releasing its operands at the head of `tryok` was a use-after-free. A plain
	// `if` carries none (`takeCatchingForkTemps` drained them into the entry block, where a single path makes
	// that right) and this is inert for it.
	function openCatchingOkEdge(cond IfCondition, okBlock IrBlock, temps ValueIdArray, anchor Token)
		if not ifConditionCatches(cond) 'plainCondition'
			return
		end 'plainCondition'

		self.emitTempDropsInto(okBlock, temps: temps, anchor: anchor)
	end 'openCatchingOkEdge'

	// Parse an `if` statement (M4):
	//   if <cond> 'thenLabel'
	//       <then-stmts>
	//   end 'thenLabel' [ else 'elseLabel' <else-stmts> end 'elseLabel'
	//                   | else if <cond> …  (the else branch is itself a nested `if`) ]
	//
	// Lowering shape: the entry block (`block`) evaluates `cond` (a comparison, whose
	// value the Std→x64 pass fuses with the branch) and gets a `condBranch` terminator
	// selecting the then block vs the false target (the else block, or the continuation
	// when there is no else). A branch that returns terminates itself; one that falls
	// through gets a `branch` to the continuation. Blocks are created then→else→
	// continuation so `.text` lays them out in source order.
	//
	// Returns the CONTINUATION — where control resumes after the `if`. It is either
	// reachable (Terminator.unset, so subsequent statements emit into it — e.g. the
	// `return 0` after a simple `if`) or, when both branches return, unreachable
	// (Terminator.dead — emits no code, but is still returned so the caller always has
	// a block to continue in).
	//
	// M4b VALUE MERGE: because a `var` may be reassigned inside a branch, the value of
	// a variable at the continuation depends on which path ran. The parser snapshots
	// each carried var's value entering the branches, RESTORES it before the else/false
	// path (so that path resumes from the pre-`if` state), and at the continuation
	// merges the two incoming snapshots — a variable that differs across the paths gets
	// a continuation PHI (the same block-arg mechanism loops use), everything else keeps
	// its common value. Without this, a `var` written only on the taken path would leak
	// its then-value onto the not-taken path (a real miscompile).
	//
	// THE CARRIED SET IS THE VARS THE STATEMENT ASSIGNS, decided from the tokens before
	// anything is emitted (`assignedBindingsIn` over the whole if's extent) — a SUBSET of
	// `mutableVars`, in that list's declaration order. A var no branch assigns holds one
	// value on entry, then-exit AND else-exit, so it can never differ at the merge: it
	// needs neither a phi nor a snapshot slot, and is left out of all three. This is the
	// exact analogue of `parseWhileStatement`'s carried set, and it replaces snapshotting
	// the WHOLE mutable-var set at every branch — which was O(vars in scope) per `if`, and
	// with the scope-filler count doubling on the scale corpus made `phase:parse` BYTES
	// bend quadratically while its ALLOCATIONS stayed linear (bigger snapshots, not more).
	//
	// Each branch body is a lexical BLOCK (parseBlockBody), so the three snapshots are
	// all taken at the SAME scope depth — a `var` declared inside a branch has left
	// `mutableVars` by the time that branch's exit values are read, and is skipped by
	// `assignedBindingsIn` (not bound at entry), which keeps `entryVals` / `thenVals` /
	// `elseVals` positionally aligned with `assignedBindings`.
	function parseIfStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let ifIndex = self.pos
		let ifTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.if)

		// The carried bindings — read off the tokens covering the WHOLE statement (then plus
		// every `else`/`else if` branch, through the final `end`), before a single op is
		// emitted. Every snapshot below is positional over THIS list, so the per-branch cost
		// is O(assigned), not O(vars in scope). `ifEnd` also feeds the drift guard.
		let ifEnd = self.blockStatementEndIndex(ifIndex, continuesAtElse: true)
		let assignedBindings = self.assignedBindingsIn(ifIndex, endIndex: ifEnd)

		// `if let x = try …` / `if var x = try …` — the success binder, read off the tokens BEFORE the
		// condition because it is what makes the condition's `try` mandatory rather than optional.
		let binder = try self.parseIfBinder()

		let condTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		let cond = try self.parseIfCondition(func, block: block, ifTok: ifTok, binder: binder)
		// The condition's own blocks: a short-circuit `and`/`or` in it (`if a() and b()`)
		// has already branched, and control now sits in ITS merge block. That — not the
		// block the `if` started in — is where the two-way branch belongs, and it is the
		// `if`'s true entry for every purpose below.
		var entry = self.currentBlock

		// ⭐ **THE CONDITION'S TEMPORARIES DIE HERE, IN `entry`, BEFORE THE BRANCH.** `if [10,20,30].count()
		// == 99` builds an array that nothing owns once `count()` has read it, and `entry` is the last block
		// on the single path through the condition — it dominates the then branch, the else branch and the
		// continuation alike, so ONE release here is one release on every path. Releasing it anywhere later
		// cannot be: the branches are the two halves of a fork (a drop in the body runs only when the body
		// does — the leak), and the continuation is reached from both (a drop there runs twice, or on a path
		// that returned early, never at all).
		//
		// ⛔ **A CATCHING `if` IS THE ONE CONDITION THAT CANNOT TAKE THAT DEAL**, because its ok edge still owes
		// a co-own that READS one of these temporaries — so it gets them back here and releases them per edge
		// (see `takeCatchingForkTemps`). The list is empty for every other condition, and each of the two edges
		// below drops it exactly once.
		let forkTemps = self.takeCatchingForkTemps(cond, floor: condTempFloor, anchor: ifTok)

		// E3087 — `if x.contains(k)` guarding a `try x.get(k)` on the same key. Asked HERE, with the cursor
		// still on the then-label, because this is the first point that holds both halves the check needs: the
		// condition has been parsed (so the receiver's TYPE is resolved and recorded) and the then-block's
		// tokens are still ahead, unconsumed and readable.
		try self.requireNoRedundantContainsGet(ifIndex)

		let thenLabel = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()

		// Values entering the branches (the condition only reads, so `entry`'s values
		// are unchanged by it). Positional over `assignedBindings`.
		let entryVals = self.snapshotBindings(assignedBindings)
		// The move state entering the branches — the twin of `entryVals` for the drop-gating bit, over the
		// owned bindings the branches might move (a `let` String, a consumed param), which are NOT the
		// assigned `var`s `entryVals` covers. Reconciled at the join so a conditional move drops correctly.
		let entryMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()

		// `var` because these blocks are passed to functions that mutate them (the
		// statement parser fills the block; the finalizers wire its terminator) — a
		// `let` binding cannot be forwarded to a mutating parameter (E3019).
		var thenBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: thenLabel.value, line: thenLabel.line)

		// ⭐ **THE OK EDGE'S HEAD, IN ORDER: the lifted co-own FIRST, then the fork's temporary releases.**
		// `parseCatchingCondition` tests `flag == 0`, so the THEN block is the edge the `tryCall` wrote the
		// result on — and the `+1` the caller owes a substituted `T` result has to precede the release of the
		// temporary it takes its reference out of. Both are no-ops for a plain `if`.
		self.openCatchingOkEdge(cond, okBlock: thenBlock, temps: forkTemps, anchor: ifTok)

		var thenEnd = try self.parseIfThenBody(func, thenBlock: thenBlock, label: thenLabel.value, cond: cond, binder: binder, anchor: thenLabel)

		// Snapshot the then-branch's exit values and move state, then rewind BOTH to the entry state so the
		// else/false path is parsed against the pre-`if` values and pre-`if` ownership, not the then-branch's.
		let thenVals = self.snapshotBindings(assignedBindings)
		let thenMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()
		self.restoreBindings(assignedBindings, snapshot: entryVals)
		self.restoreMoveMark(entryMoveMark)

		// ⭐ **A CATCHING `if` WITH NO `else` STILL HAS AN ELSE BLOCK.** Its false edge is the error edge of a
		// `try`, which carries an obligation an `entry → continuation` edge structurally cannot: a caught
		// payload-bearing error is a heap box this construct owns, and it must be released on the path that
		// caught it. Giving that edge a real (possibly empty) block hands the release, the `else (e)` binding
		// and the written-`else` body to ONE routine, instead of splitting the ownership rule across the
		// two-block and three-block merge shapes. A PLAIN `if` with no `else` is untouched and keeps the
		// direct false edge.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.else) and not ifConditionCatches(cond) 'noElse'
			let cont = self.finalizeIfNoElse(func, entry: entry, cond: ifConditionTest(cond), ifTok: ifTok, thenBlock: thenBlock, thenEnd: thenEnd, bindings: assignedBindings, entryVals: entryVals, thenVals: thenVals, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, thenMoveMark: thenMoveMark)
			self.assertScanAligned("parseIfStatement", predictedEnd: ifEnd)
			return cont
		end 'noElse'

		var elseBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"ifelse", line: ifTok.line)

		// The catching fork's OTHER edge. Nothing on it reads the condition's temporaries, but it reaches the
		// continuation just as the then edge does, so it owes them the same single release — and it owes it
		// here rather than in the handler body, which may `return` before ever reaching a drain.
		self.emitTempDropsInto(elseBlock, temps: forkTemps, anchor: ifTok)

		var elseEnd = try self.parseElseBody(func, elseBlock: elseBlock, cond: cond, ifTok: ifTok)
		let elseVals = self.snapshotBindings(assignedBindings)
		let elseMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()
		let cont = self.finalizeIfElse(func, entry: entry, cond: ifConditionTest(cond), ifTok: ifTok, thenBlock: thenBlock, thenEnd: thenEnd, elseBlock: elseBlock, elseEnd: elseEnd, bindings: assignedBindings, thenVals: thenVals, elseVals: elseVals, thenMoveMark: thenMoveMark, elseMoveMark: elseMoveMark)
		self.assertScanAligned("parseIfStatement", predictedEnd: ifEnd)
		return cont
	end 'parseIfStatement'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3087 — `if x.contains(k)` FOLLOWED BY `try x.get(k)` IS TWO LOOKUPS WHERE ONE WILL DO.** The
	// membership test and the value read probe the same table for the same key, so the pattern is refused and
	// the author is pointed at `if let v = try x.get(k)`, which does one.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS A PARSER CHECK, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE DEPARTURE FROM BOTH REFERENCE COMPILERS.** The
	// bootstrap (`SemanticCheckPass.CheckRedundantContainsGet`) and v1 (`SemanticCheck.maxon`'s
	// `maybeEmitRedundantContainsGet`) both run it over the lowered IR and RECONSTRUCT the receiver's access
	// path by walking SSA def-use chains backwards — `BuildAccessPath` / `buildAccessPath` — because by then
	// the source text is gone. shv2 does not have to: its parser resolves types AND still holds the tokens, so
	// `holder.cache` and the key's spelling are READ rather than rebuilt, and the anchor the diagnostic wants
	// (the `contains` member-name token) is a token this function has in its hand. Nothing here re-derives a
	// path, so nothing here can disagree with the receiver the call actually took.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT IS NOT DEPARTED FROM IS THE RULE**, which is the references' to the letter: the receiver and the
	// key must be the SAME access path on both sides (matched structurally, so a field chain counts exactly as
	// a bare local does); the `get` must be under a `try`; and the scan of the then-block STOPS at the first
	// thing that could have invalidated the membership answer — a write to any name in either path, or any
	// other call on the same receiver. Suppressing on those is what keeps the lint from being a false accusation
	// against code that legitimately probes twice.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NON-PATH RECEIVER OR KEY IS SIMPLY NOT LINTED**, again as in both references (`BuildAccessPath`
	// answers null and the walk `continue`s). `t.get(0).contains(k)` and `m.contains(a + b)` are outside what a
	// token-level path comparison can prove equal, and an unprovable pair must not be reported.
	function requireNoRedundantContainsGet(ifIndex TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		// The condition spans `(if, then-label)`; the cursor sits on the label, which `parseIfStatement` has
		// not consumed yet. A `contains` this `if` did not dispatch — an `and`/`or` arm, a nested call, an
		// earlier statement's — fails the containment test below and stops the check.
		let condStart = ifIndex + 1
		let memberIndex = self.lastDispatchedMemberIndex
		if memberIndex <= condStart or memberIndex >= self.pos 'containsIsNotThisCondition'
			return
		end 'containsIsNotThisCondition'
		if not self.tokenAt(memberIndex).value.equals(ContainsMethod) 'notAContainsCondition'
			return
		end 'notAContainsCondition'

		// The condition must be the `contains` call and NOTHING ELSE: `if not m.contains(k)` and
		// `if m.contains(k) and f()` guard something different from what the `if` tests, so neither is this
		// pattern. `chain . contains ( args )` — the chain runs `[condStart, memberIndex - 1)` and the `(`
		// immediately follows the member name.
		if not self.isDottedChain(condStart, pastEnd: memberIndex - 1) or self.kindAt(memberIndex - 1) != TokenKind.dot 'conditionIsNotALoneContainsCall'
			return
		end 'conditionIsNotALoneContainsCall'
		if self.kindAt(memberIndex + 1) != TokenKind.leftParen or self.kindAt(self.pos - 1) != TokenKind.rightParen 'notACallSpanningTheCondition'
			return
		end 'notACallSpanningTheCondition'
		let keyStart = memberIndex + 2
		let keyPastEnd = self.pos - 1
		if not self.isDottedChain(keyStart, pastEnd: keyPastEnd) 'keyIsNotAPath'
			return
		end 'keyIsNotAPath'

		// ⛔⛔ **A PATH ROOTED IN A WRITABLE GLOBAL IS NOT LINTED, AND THAT IS SOUNDNESS RATHER THAN TASTE**
		// (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). The suppression scan below rests on a premise that holds for a
		// binding this function owns and FAILS for a top-level `var`: that the only code which can invalidate
		// the membership answer is code the scan can SEE touching this path. A `var` global's storage is
		// reachable from every function in the program, so any call in the then-block — `print(…)`, a helper,
		// anything — may `upsert` into the very table the `contains` probed, under a receiver spelling this
		// token walk has no way to connect to it. Reporting one would be a false accusation.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TEST IS `namesWritableModuleStorage`, WHICH IS THE ARGUMENT ABOVE AND NOT A PROXY FOR IT.**
		// It was written as "the base is not a local or a capture", whose complement is much larger than
		// "a mutable global": a `let` global's container refuses mutation at every door — MEASURED, both
		// `m.upsert(…)` on it directly and `fill(m)` into a mutating parameter are E3019 — so nothing in the
		// then-block can invalidate a membership answer probed through one, and a key that is a top-level
		// `let` cannot be rewritten either. Both were skipped, and both are exactly what the lint is for.
		// LOCAL FIRST, which is what keeps a shadowing local a different subject — `borrowSubjectNameAt`'s
		// rule, for its reason.
		//
		// ⚠ **BOTH REFERENCES SKIP THE WHOLE GLOBAL FAMILY, and this is where the token-level design is
		// SHARPER than what they express structurally**: their `BuildAccessPath` / `buildAccessPath` cannot
		// canonicalize a global load at all, so the path is `null` and the lint is skipped for every global —
		// while this walk holds the tokens AND the declaration, so it can tell the two apart. MEASURED against
		// the runnable oracle, which compiles the global-rooted program and refuses the byte-identical one
		// whose base is a PARAMETER; and pinned by the canonical corpus, whose
		// `map-struct-bytearray/struct-field-access-in-untaken-if-branch` is exactly the `var`-rooted program
		// and expects exit 0.
		if self.baseNamesWritableModuleStorage(condStart) or self.baseNamesWritableModuleStorage(keyStart) 'pathIsRootedInWritableModuleStorage'
			return
		end 'pathIsRootedInWritableModuleStorage'

		// The THEN block's own extent — `continuesAtElse: false`, so the scan stops at the first `end` and
		// never walks an `else` branch, which the membership test did not guard.
		if not self.thenBlockRepeatsTheLookup(self.pos, ifEnd: self.blockStatementEndIndex(ifIndex, continuesAtElse: false), chainStart: condStart, chainPastEnd: memberIndex - 1, keyStart: keyStart, keyPastEnd: keyPastEnd) 'noRedundantGet'
			return
		end 'noRedundantGet'

		let receiverText = self.dottedChainText(condStart, pastEnd: memberIndex - 1)
		let keyText = self.dottedChainText(keyStart, pastEnd: keyPastEnd)
		let owner = self.receiverTypeDisplayName(self.lastDispatchedReceiver)
		let anchor = self.tokenAt(memberIndex)
		throw ParseError.redundantContainsGet("redundant '{owner}.{String.from(ContainsMethod)}' followed by '{owner}.{String.from(GetMethod)}' on '{receiverText}': use 'if let v = try {receiverText}.{String.from(GetMethod)}({keyText})' (or 'if var') instead — performs one lookup instead of two", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireNoRedundantContainsGet'

	// Does the then-block `(labelIndex, ifEnd)` read `try <chain>.get(<key>)` on the very path the condition
	// already probed? The walk STOPS — never `continue`s past — at anything that could have
	// invalidated the membership answer, which is the references' suppression rule:
	//
	//   • a WRITE to any name the receiver or key path mentions (`m = other`, `holder.cache = fresh`), and the
	//     guard set is every segment of both paths, so a write to `cache` stops a `holder.cache` receiver;
	//   • ANY other member access on the same receiver — `m.upsert(…)` may have inserted, `m.remove(…)` may
	//     have deleted — including a `get` whose key differs or one not under a `try`.
	//
	// A nested block inside the then-block is walked like the rest: a `get` reached only conditionally is still
	// a second lookup on a path the `contains` already answered for, and the writes that would suppress it are
	// seen on the way in.
	function thenBlockRepeatsTheLookup(labelIndex TokenIndex, ifEnd TokenIndex, chainStart TokenIndex, chainPastEnd TokenIndex, keyStart TokenIndex, keyPastEnd TokenIndex) returns bool
		var i = labelIndex + 1
		while i < ifEnd 'scan'
			// A write to a guarded name — `<name> =` where `<name>` is a segment of either path. `==` is a
			// distinct token kind, so a comparison is not mistaken for an assignment.
			if self.kindAt(i + 1) == TokenKind.equals and self.tokenNamesAPathSegment(i, chainStart: chainStart, chainPastEnd: chainPastEnd, keyStart: keyStart, keyPastEnd: keyPastEnd) 'guardedNameWritten'
				return false
			end 'guardedNameWritten'

			if not self.tokensEqualAt(i, otherStart: chainStart, otherPastEnd: chainPastEnd) 'notTheReceiver'
				i = i + 1
				continue
			end 'notTheReceiver'

			// The receiver path occurs here. Every use of it decides the answer: the matching `try …get(key)`
			// fires, and anything else ends the scan.
			let memberIndex = i + (chainPastEnd - chainStart) + 1
			if self.kindAt(memberIndex - 1) != TokenKind.dot 'notAMemberAccess'
				return false
			end 'notAMemberAccess'
			if not self.tokenAt(memberIndex).value.equals(GetMethod) or self.kindAt(i - 1) != TokenKind.try 'notATryGet'
				return false
			end 'notATryGet'
			if self.kindAt(memberIndex + 1) != TokenKind.leftParen 'notACall'
				return false
			end 'notACall'
			let argStart = memberIndex + 2
			let argPastEnd = argStart + (keyPastEnd - keyStart)
			if self.kindAt(argPastEnd) != TokenKind.rightParen or not self.tokensEqualAt(argStart, otherStart: keyStart, otherPastEnd: keyPastEnd) 'differentKey'
				return false
			end 'differentKey'
			return true
		end 'scan'
		return false
	end 'thenBlockRepeatsTheLookup'

	// Is the token at `index` an identifier spelled like some segment of either guarded path? The guard set is
	// the path SEGMENTS rather than the whole path, deliberately: `holder.cache = fresh` rebinds the field a
	// `holder.cache` receiver reads, and `holder = other` rebinds its base, and both must suppress.
	function tokenNamesAPathSegment(index TokenIndex, chainStart TokenIndex, chainPastEnd TokenIndex, keyStart TokenIndex, keyPastEnd TokenIndex) returns bool
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.identifier 'notAName'
			return false
		end 'notAName'
		let name = self.tokenAt(index).value
		return self.chainMentionsName(name, chainStart: chainStart, chainPastEnd: chainPastEnd) or self.chainMentionsName(name, chainStart: keyStart, chainPastEnd: keyPastEnd)
	end 'tokenNamesAPathSegment'

	function chainMentionsName(name ByteArray, chainStart TokenIndex, chainPastEnd TokenIndex) returns bool
		var i = chainStart
		while i < chainPastEnd 'eachSegment'
			if self.tokenAt(i).value.equals(name) 'mentions'
				return true
			end 'mentions'
			// Segments alternate `ident . ident`, so stepping two lands on the next name.
			i = i + 2
		end 'eachSegment'
		return false
	end 'chainMentionsName'

	// Is `[start, pastEnd)` exactly `ident ( . ident )*` — the token shape of an access PATH, which is the
	// whole of what this lint can compare structurally. An empty span is not one.
	function isDottedChain(start TokenIndex, pastEnd TokenIndex) returns bool
		if pastEnd <= start 'empty'
			return false
		end 'empty'
		var i = start
		while i < pastEnd 'eachSegment'
			if self.kindAt(i) != TokenKind.identifier 'notAName'
				return false
			end 'notAName'
			if i + 1 >= pastEnd 'lastSegment'
				return true
			end 'lastSegment'
			if self.kindAt(i + 1) != TokenKind.dot 'notADot'
				return false
			end 'notADot'
			i = i + 2
		end 'eachSegment'
		return false
	end 'isDottedChain'

	// Do the tokens at `[start, …)` spell the same thing as `[otherStart, otherPastEnd)`? Compared by the
	// tokens' own source bytes, which is what makes `holder.cache` on one side and `holder.cache` on the other
	// the same receiver without a name table.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE KIND IS COMPARED TOO, and it is not belt-and-braces**: a PUNCTUATION token carries no source
	// bytes of its own, so a value-only test reads every `.`, `,` and `+` as the same empty string and
	// `holder + cache` would answer equal to the chain `holder . cache`.
	function tokensEqualAt(start TokenIndex, otherStart TokenIndex, otherPastEnd TokenIndex) returns bool
		var offset = 0
		while otherStart + offset < otherPastEnd 'eachToken'
			let here = self.tokenAt(start + offset)
			let there = self.tokenAt(otherStart + offset)
			if here.kind != there.kind or not here.value.equals(there.value) 'differs'
				return false
			end 'differs'
			offset = offset + 1
		end 'eachToken'
		return true
	end 'tokensEqualAt'

	// The source spelling of the dotted chain `[start, pastEnd)` — `m`, `holder.cache`. Rebuilt from the
	// tokens' own bytes rather than sliced out of the source buffer, because the chain is exactly
	// `ident ( . ident )*` and so has no interior trivia to preserve.
	function dottedChainText(start TokenIndex, pastEnd TokenIndex) returns String
		var out = ""
		var i = start
		while i < pastEnd 'eachSegment'
			if i > start 'separator'
				out.append(".")
			end 'separator'
			out.append(String.from(self.tokenAt(i).value))
			i = i + 2
		end 'eachSegment'
		return out
	end 'dottedChainText'

	// The name the diagnostic calls a receiver's TYPE — `Map` for a `Map with (String, String)`, `Set` for a
	// set, the declared name for a struct. It is the BASE name of a generic instance and not the mangled
	// instance name, because the sentence names a method (`Map.contains`) and a method belongs to the base.
	function receiverTypeDisplayName(receiver ValueId) returns String
		let tag = self.tagOf(receiver)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return String.from(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(self.valueNameId(receiver)))
		end 'instance'
		return typeTagName(tag) if self.valueNameId(receiver) == UnnamedTypeId else String.from(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(receiver)))
	end 'receiverTypeDisplayName'

	// Record the member dispatch about to happen — the token index of the member NAME and the receiver whose
	// type it is being dispatched on. TWO SCALAR FIELDS written in ONE place, exactly as `lastFieldRead*` is:
	// the redundant-`contains`/`get` lint recognizes its own condition by IDENTITY (the index must fall inside
	// the condition it is checking), so a stale record from an earlier dispatch cannot be mistaken for this
	// one, and a miss simply skips the lint rather than reporting the wrong receiver.
	function noteMemberDispatch(recv MethodReceiver)
		self.lastDispatchedMemberIndex = self.pos
		self.lastDispatchedReceiver = recv.value
	end 'noteMemberDispatch'

	// Parse an `else` branch into `elseBlock`. A PLAIN condition's else is exactly what it always was — the
	// `else` keyword and then a body. A CATCHING condition's else is the try's ERROR EDGE, which owns the
	// caught error whether or not an `else` was written, so it routes to its own opener.
	function parseElseBody(func IrFunction, elseBlock IrBlock, cond IfCondition, ifTok Token) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		match cond 'shape'
			plain then break 'shape'
			tryCatch(_, _, flag, errorType) then return try self.parseCatchingElseBody(func, elseBlock: elseBlock, flag: flag, errorType: errorType, ifTok: ifTok)
		end 'shape'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.else)
		return try self.parseElseBranchBody(func, elseBlock: elseBlock)
	end 'parseElseBody'

	// The else branch's BODY, once its `else` (and, for a catching `if`, any `(e)` binder) has been
	// consumed: either a nested `if` (the `else if …` chain — the else branch IS a nested if statement, so
	// `elseBlock` becomes that if's entry) or a plain labelled `else` block. Returns the block the else
	// branch left control in (its own continuation for a nested if; the block its last statement filled for
	// a plain else).
	//
	// The `else if` form opens NO scope frame of its own: its condition is evaluated in the ENCLOSING scope
	// (it is not inside any block), and each of the nested if's own branches opens a frame when it is
	// parsed. Only the plain `else 'label'` form is a block body.
	function parseElseBranchBody(func IrFunction, elseBlock IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.if) 'elseIf'
			return try self.parseIfStatement(func, block: elseBlock)
		end 'elseIf'

		let elseLabelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()
		return try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: elseBlock, label: elseLabelTok.value)
	end 'parseElseBranchBody'

	// ⭐ **THE ERROR EDGE OF `if try …` / `if let x = try …`, WHICH IS ALSO ITS `else` BRANCH.** It owns the
	// caught error end to end, exactly as `parseOtherwiseBlock` owns an `otherwise` handler's: the error is
	// enrolled as an owned binding — NAMED by an `else (e)` binder, ANONYMOUS otherwise — and released on
	// whichever edge leaves the branch (`dropArmScopedOwned`: a decref on a live fall-through, the
	// terminating statement's own scope drops on a `return`/`throw`).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT RUNS EVEN WHEN NO `else` IS WRITTEN**, because the error edge exists either way and a caught box
	// has to die on it. With nothing written the branch is empty: the release is all it holds and it falls
	// straight through to the merge, carrying the entry values the caller already restored.
	function parseCatchingElseBody(func IrFunction, elseBlock IrBlock, flag ValueId, errorType MaxonType, ifTok Token) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let elseWritten = self.at(TokenKind.else)
		let ownedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()
		self.scope.pushScope()

		// With no `else` written there is no token on this branch to anchor its release to, so the `if` that
		// caught the error is what the drop is attributed to — the construct a reader would look at to ask
		// where the error went. With an `else` but no binder it is the token after `else`.
		var binder = CaughtErrorBinder.create(EmptySourceText, hasBinding: false, anchor: ifTok)
		if elseWritten 'openElse'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.else)
			// `else (e) 'err'` — the caught error, named. The same OPTIONAL binder a divergent `otherwise`
			// spells, parsed by the same routine, because it is the same grammar over the same caught flag.
			binder = try self.parseOptionalCaughtErrorBinder(self.current())
		end 'openElse'
		let bindingName = binder.name
		let hasBinding = binder.hasBinding
		let anchor = binder.anchor

		// The decode (and, for a boxed error, the enrolment) belongs at the TOP of the error edge — before a
		// nested `else if`'s own condition, and before the written body's first statement.
		self.currentBlock = elseBlock
		try self.declareCaughtError(flag, errorType: errorType, bindingName: bindingName, hasBinding: hasBinding, form: CaughtErrorForm.singleCall, anchor: anchor)

		var elseEnd = elseBlock
		if elseWritten 'writtenBody'
			elseEnd = try self.parseElseBranchBody(func, elseBlock: elseBlock)
		end 'writtenBody'

		self.scope.popScope()
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(ownedFloor, exitBlock: elseEnd, anchor: anchor)
		return elseEnd
	end 'parseCatchingElseBody'

	// `if let x = try …` / `if var x = try …` — the optional success binder in front of the condition.
	// `let`/`var` here declares exactly what it declares at statement level, and goes through the same
	// `declareInitializedBinding`; only the initializer differs, and it is not optional. The binding names
	// the value a `try` PRODUCED, so `parseIfCondition` requires `try` after the `=` — and a
	// `if let x = 5 'l'` is refused there by the ordinary "expected `try`" mismatch rather than by a rule of
	// its own.
	function parseIfBinder() returns IfBinder throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.let) and not self.at(TokenKind.var) 'noBinder'
			return IfBinder.none
		end 'noBinder'

		let keywordTok = self.current()
		try self.consume(keywordTok.kind)
		let nameIndex = self.pos
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)
		return IfBinder.binding(nameIndex, mutable: keywordTok.kind == TokenKind.var)
	end 'parseIfBinder'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `if`'s CONDITION SLOT HAS TWO SHAPES, AND THE TOKEN AFTER `if` DECIDES WHICH:**
	//   • `if <expr>`                  — an ordinary bool condition; the then branch runs when it is true.
	//   • `if try <call>`              — a CATCHING try; the then branch runs when the call did NOT throw.
	//   • `if let/var x = try <call>`  — the same, with the success value bound in the then branch.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CATCHING FORM IS RECOGNIZED LEXICALLY, HERE — NOT BY A PARSER MODE THREADED THROUGH THE WHOLE
	// EXPRESSION GRAMMAR, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE THIS CONSTRUCT DEPARTS FROM v1.** v1 sets an `inIfCondition`
	// field around the condition parse (`Parser.maxon:7632`) that makes `parseTryExpression` hand back a
	// `tryCondition` from wherever inside the expression the `try` happened to sit, and the field must be
	// saved and restored so a nested `if` in the body cannot leak it back out. shv2 does not need the mode,
	// because the language admits the catching `try` in exactly ONE position — first in the condition slot —
	// and a mode that can fire anywhere is answerable for every position it can reach: `if a and try f()`
	// would have silently become "a, and f did not throw", a meaning no spec grants it. Asked at the one
	// position it is legal, the grammar states its own boundary, and the propagating `try` keeps every other
	// position it had.
	function parseIfCondition(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, ifTok Token, binder IfBinder) returns IfCondition throws ParseError
		match binder 'b'
			none then break 'b'
			binding then return try self.parseCatchingCondition(func, block: block, binder: binder)
		end 'b'

		if self.at(TokenKind.try) 'bareCatch'
			return try self.parseCatchingCondition(func, block: block, binder: binder)
		end 'bareCatch'

		return IfCondition.plain(try self.parseBoolCondition(func, block: block, kwTok: ifTok))
	end 'parseIfCondition'

	// **E3124 — THE BARE `if try <call>` MAY ONLY TEST A CALL THAT RETURNS NOTHING.** The form is a THROW
	// test: the then branch runs whenever the call did not throw, whatever it produced. So a callee with a
	// result has that result dropped here, while the source reads as though the result were the thing being
	// tested — `if try json.getBool(node, key: "enabled")` reads as *"if enabled"* and means *"if the key
	// exists and is a bool"*, which is TRUE when the stored value is `false`. MEASURED before this refusal:
	// `answer(succeed bool) returns bool throws MyError` returning `false` on its SUCCESS path still took
	// the branch, in both compilers.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE LAW, NOT A TYPE-KEYED ONE.** The refusal is on "the call produces a value", not on "the value
	// is a bool": a `bool` is only where the misreading is loudest, and a rule keyed to it would leave every
	// other type silently dropping its result. What survives is the form's honest use — a void-returning
	// throwing call, "run this, branch on whether it worked".
	//
	// The `_` binding needs no arm on THAT side: `if let _ = try f()` has SAID what happens to the result,
	// which is all this rule asks. Whether a PURE callee may be discarded that way is E3064's question, and
	// THIS DOOR does not ask it. ⚠ The reason used to read "shv2 has no purity classification to answer it
	// with", which stopped being true when `SemanticCheck.buildEffectFreeSummary` landed with the tuple-assign
	// port: the classification exists, and exactly one door consults it (the all-discard tuple assignment).
	// Wiring this one is a scope decision, not a missing mechanism (see specs-shv2/discarded-results.md).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE DUAL, WHICH IS THE SAME QUESTION FROM THE OTHER SIDE: `if let x = try voidF()` BINDS A
	// VALUE THE CALL DOES NOT PRODUCE (E3059).** A binding is a VALUE POSITION, so this is precisely the
	// fault `parseTry`'s `voidInValue` guard already refuses — same code, same sentence, same anchor. It is
	// asked here because it was asked NOWHERE: `parseCatchingCondition` shares the try-call REWRITE with the
	// expression form but never shared the question about the rewrite's RESULT, and the two compilers failed
	// two different ways on it. MEASURED before this arm existed: shv2 PANICKED in `maxonTypeOfTag` (*"a
	// `void` tag names no value"*) by way of `parseBoundThenBody` → `declareInitializedBinding` → `typeOf`,
	// while the bootstrap silently declared nothing and blamed the USE (`E2004: Undefined variable 'x'`,
	// about a name written on the line above) — or compiled clean when the binding went unread.
	// `_` is not exempt from this half: it discards a result, and here there is no result to discard.
	//
	// Both arms live in ONE function because they are ONE question — does the binder agree with what the
	// call produces — and asking them apart is exactly how the second one came to be unasked.
	// ⚠ The noun is built INSIDE each throw and not once above them, because this routine runs on every
	// `if try` parsed and `tryTargetNoun` + `String.from` allocate: the two refusals are cold, and a
	// diagnostic's words must not be paid for by the programs that have nothing wrong with them.
	function requireCaughtResultIsSpokenFor(result ValueId, target TryTarget, binder IfBinder, tryTok Token) throws ParseError
		let producesNothing = self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.void
		let bound = match binder 'b'
			none gives false
			binding gives true
		end 'b'

		if bound and producesNothing 'boundNothing'
			throw ParseError.errorTypeMismatch("'{String.from(tryTargetNoun(target, signatures: self.signatures))}' does not return a value", line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'boundNothing'

		if not bound and not producesNothing 'droppedSomething'
			throw ParseError.ifTryDiscardsResult(String.from(tryTargetNoun(target, signatures: self.signatures)), line: tryTok.line, column: tryTok.column)
		end 'droppedSomething'
	end 'requireCaughtResultIsSpokenFor'

	// ⭐ **`try <call>` IN A CONDITION: THE CALL IS REWRITTEN EXACTLY AS AN EXPRESSION `try` REWRITES IT, AND
	// THEN THE `if` — NOT `desugarTry` — OWNS THE FORK.** `parseTryCallTarget` + `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` +
	// `requireThrowingTryTarget` are the SAME three steps `parseTry` runs, so every try target shape (a free
	// call, a method, a field chain, a witness dispatch, `try await p`, a literal receiver) and every refusal
	// (E3055 on a non-throwing callee, E2015 on a non-call) reaches a condition unchanged and cannot drift
	// from the expression form. What is NOT run is `desugarTry`: its `flag != 0` split, error block, handler
	// and merge are precisely what the `if` already is, and building both would fork twice.
	//
	// What IS this form's own is `requireCaughtResultIsSpokenFor` above — the binder/result agreement, in
	// both directions. Only a CONDITION can drop the call's result without saying so (E3124), and only a
	// condition reaches a binding through a path the expression form's void guard never ran on (E3059).
	//
	// The test is `flag == 0` — TRUE on the success path — so the then branch is the ok edge and the else
	// branch the error edge, with no swapping anywhere downstream.
	function parseCatchingCondition(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, binder IfBinder) returns IfCondition throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block
		let tryTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.try)

		let targetOpFloor = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		let result = try self.parseTryCallTarget(func)
		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		let target = try self.rewriteLastCallToTryCall(func, flag: flag, tryTok: tryTok, targetOpFloor: targetOpFloor)
		try self.requireThrowingTryTarget(target, tryTok: tryTok)
		try self.requireCaughtResultIsSpokenFor(result, target: target, binder: binder, tryTok: tryTok)

		// ⭐ **A MANAGED SUCCESS VALUE IS OWNED ON THE THEN EDGE ALONE, so it leaves the pending-temp list
		// here** — the same removal `desugarTry` makes at its fork, and for the same reason: on the error edge
		// the result register is NULL (a throw zeroes it), while `parseIfStatement` drains the condition's
		// temporaries into the ENTRY block, which both edges flow from. `parseIfThenBody` re-homes it onto the
		// `if let`/`if var` binding, or onto a drop of its own when the boolean form discards it. A scalar
		// result is in no drop set and this is inert for it.
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) 'managedResultOffEntry'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(result)
		end 'managedResultOffEntry'

		let zero = self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tryTok))
		let test = try self.emitCompare(flag, rhs: zero, op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, opTok: tryTok)
		return IfCondition.tryCatch(test, result: result, flag: flag, errorType: self.tryErrorEnumType(self.tryTargetThrows(target)))
	end 'parseCatchingCondition'

	// Parse the then branch's body, giving a catching `if`'s success value the home its condition could not.
	function parseIfThenBody(func IrFunction, thenBlock IrBlock, label ByteArray, cond IfCondition, binder IfBinder, anchor Token) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		match cond 'shape'
			plain then break 'shape'
			tryCatch(_, result, _, _) then return try self.parseCaughtThenBody(func, thenBlock: thenBlock, label: label, result: result, binder: binder, anchor: anchor)
		end 'shape'

		return try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: thenBlock, label: label)
	end 'parseIfThenBody'

	// ⭐ **THE OK EDGE OF A CATCHING `if`, WHERE ITS SUCCESS VALUE FINDS AN OWNER.** Two shapes, and they are
	// the two ways the value can be owned:
	//   • BOUND (`if let x = …` / `if var x = …`) — the ordinary binding machinery takes it, in a scope frame
	//     one level outside the body's own.
	//   • DISCARDED (`if try …`) — nothing names it, and this edge is the only one it is live on, so a MANAGED
	//     result is dropped at the TOP of the then block. Never in the continuation: the error edge reaches
	//     that too, with a NULL register a decref would fault on.
	function parseCaughtThenBody(func IrFunction, thenBlock IrBlock, label ByteArray, result ValueId, binder IfBinder, anchor Token) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		// ⚠ **THE SUBSTITUTED `T` CO-OWN IS ALREADY AT THE HEAD OF `thenBlock`, PUT THERE BY
		// `openCatchingOkEdge` BEFORE THIS RUNS** — and it has to be, because the fork's temporary releases go
		// in behind it and this routine is entered after them. What is left here is the binding or the discard
		// drop, both of which name the promoted value and both of which must follow it.
		match binder 'b'
			none then break 'b'
			binding(nameIndex, mutable) then return try self.parseBoundThenBody(func, thenBlock: thenBlock, label: label, result: result, nameIndex: nameIndex, mutable: mutable)
		end 'b'

		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(result) 'dropDiscardedResult'
			self.currentBlock = thenBlock
			self.emitDecref(result, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'dropDiscardedResult'

		return try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: thenBlock, label: label)
	end 'parseCaughtThenBody'

	// The bound half of the above. The binding lives in a frame ONE LEVEL OUTSIDE the body's own, which is
	// what makes it visible to every statement of the body and — just as importantly — makes it leave
	// `mutableVars` and `ownedBindings` before `parseIfStatement` snapshots this branch's exit values (those
	// snapshots are positional over the carried set computed at the `if`, which cannot contain a `var` the
	// branch itself declared).
	function parseBoundThenBody(func IrFunction, thenBlock IrBlock, label ByteArray, result ValueId, nameIndex TokenIndex, mutable bool) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let nameTok = self.tokenAt(nameIndex)
		let mark = self.openBlock()
		self.currentBlock = thenBlock

		// ⭐ **THE SAME DECLARATION A STATEMENT-LEVEL `let`/`var` MAKES, THROUGH THE SAME DOOR.** That is what
		// carries the success value's FULL type across — a function-typed result keeps its signature, so
		// `if let g = try pick(true) 'ok' … g(4)` calls through a properly typed value — and it is the whole
		// reason this is not a second, shorter binding path. No source name: the success value is a fresh
		// call result held by no binding, so there is nothing to poison and no immutable source E3078 could
		// blame; it is a CONSUME, exactly as `let x = f()` is.
		try self.declareInitializedBinding(func, nameTok: nameTok, value: result, sourceName: EmptySourceText, sourceReadsAField: false, mutable: mutable)

		let thenEnd = try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: thenBlock, label: label)
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(mark.ownedCount, exitBlock: thenEnd, anchor: nameTok)
		self.closeBlock(mark)
		return thenEnd
	end 'parseBoundThenBody'

	// Finalize an `if` with no `else`: the false path flows straight to the
	// continuation, so the continuation is always reachable. Wire the entry's
	// condBranch (then vs continuation). When the then branch falls through, the
	// continuation merges the false-edge values (`entryVals`) with the then-branch's
	// exit values (`thenVals`) — a differing var gets a continuation phi. When the then
	// branch returns, the continuation is reached only from the false edge, whose values
	// are already the restored `entryVals` (and whose move state is the restored `entryMoveMark`).
	//
	// ⭐ **A CONDITIONAL MOVE IS RECONCILED HERE.** An owned binding the then branch MOVED but the false
	// path left LIVE must be dropped on the false path so it does not leak. When the then branch FALLS
	// THROUGH, both edges reach the continuation: `reconcileMovesAtMerge` drops the binding on the false
	// edge (routed through a fresh block, since the direct entry->cont edge has none) and marks it moved.
	// When the then branch RETURNS, the continuation is reached only from the false edge — the move state
	// is already the caller's restored `entryMoveMark`, so the binding stays live and its scope exit drops it.
	function finalizeIfNoElse(func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, cond ValueId, ifTok Token, thenBlock IrBlock, thenEnd IrBlock, bindings VarInfoArray, entryVals ValueIdArray, thenVals ValueIdArray, entryMoveMark MoveMark, thenMoveMark MoveMark) returns IrBlock
		var cont = self.newBlock(func, label: b"ifcont", line: ifTok.line)
		if thenEnd.isTerminated() 'thenTerminated'
			self.emitCondBranch(entry, cond: cond, thenId: thenBlock.id, elseId: cont.id, ifTok: ifTok)
			return cont
		end 'thenTerminated'

		// Both the false edge (entry state) and the then edge (then state) reach the continuation. A binding
		// the then branch MOVED but the entry left LIVE must be dropped on the false path — but the direct
		// entry->cont false edge is `entry`, which already ends in the two-way condBranch and cannot take an
		// appended decref. Only then is a fresh `iffalse` block minted on that edge to hold the drop; an `if`
		// with no such conditional move takes the direct edge and its codegen is byte-identical to before.
		if self.thenMovesLiveBinding(entryMoveMark, thenMoveMark: thenMoveMark) 'falseEdgeDrop'
			var falseBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"iffalse", line: ifTok.line)
			self.emitCondBranch(entry, cond: cond, thenId: thenBlock.id, elseId: falseBlock.id, ifTok: ifTok)

			var edges = ReachingMoveEdgeArray.create()
			edges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(falseBlock, mark: entryMoveMark))
			edges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(thenEnd, mark: thenMoveMark))
			self.reconcileMovesAtMerge(edges, anchor: ifTok)

			self.mergeAtContinuation(falseBlock, predB: thenEnd, cont: cont, bindings: bindings, valsA: entryVals, valsB: thenVals)
			self.emitBranch(falseBlock, targetId: cont.id)
			self.emitBranch(thenEnd, targetId: cont.id)
		end 'falseEdgeDrop' else 'directFalseEdge'
			self.emitCondBranch(entry, cond: cond, thenId: thenBlock.id, elseId: cont.id, ifTok: ifTok)
			// The false edge is the direct entry->cont edge; the post-merge move state is the then state (a
			// binding moved before the `if` stays moved on both edges, and nothing new was moved to drop).
			self.restoreMoveMark(thenMoveMark)
			self.mergeAtContinuation(entry, predB: thenEnd, cont: cont, bindings: bindings, valsA: entryVals, valsB: thenVals)
			self.emitBranch(thenEnd, targetId: cont.id)
		end 'directFalseEdge'
		return cont
	end 'finalizeIfNoElse'

	// Does the then branch move an owned binding the ENTRY left live — the one shape an `if` with no `else`
	// must place a drop on its false edge for (see `finalizeIfNoElse`)? The move flag is monotonic within a
	// path, so a binding live at entry and moved after the then branch was moved BY that branch. A binding
	// already moved before the `if` is moved on both edges (nothing to reconcile); a binding no branch
	// touched is live on both.
	function thenMovesLiveBinding(entryMoveMark MoveMark, thenMoveMark MoveMark) returns bool
		var i = 0
		while i < entryMoveMark.moved.count() 'scan'
			let entryLive = not (try entryMoveMark.moved.get(i) otherwise panic("thenMovesLiveBinding: entryMoveMark.moved.get OOB at {i}"))
			let thenMoved = try thenMoveMark.moved.get(i) otherwise panic("thenMovesLiveBinding: thenMoveMark.moved.get OOB at {i}")
			if entryLive and thenMoved 'movedInThenOnly'
				return true
			end 'movedInThenOnly'
			i = i + 1
		end 'scan'
		return false
	end 'thenMovesLiveBinding'

	// Finalize an `if`/`else`: the entry's condBranch targets the then and else blocks.
	// The continuation is reachable only when a branch falls through; when BOTH return
	// it is unreachable and marked `dead`. When BOTH fall through, the continuation
	// merges their exit values (`thenVals`/`elseVals`) with phis for the vars that
	// differ. When only ONE falls through, the continuation simply carries that branch's
	// values (no phi — a single predecessor).
	function finalizeIfElse(func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, cond ValueId, ifTok Token, thenBlock IrBlock, thenEnd IrBlock, elseBlock IrBlock, elseEnd IrBlock, bindings VarInfoArray, thenVals ValueIdArray, elseVals ValueIdArray, thenMoveMark MoveMark, elseMoveMark MoveMark) returns IrBlock
		self.emitCondBranch(entry, cond: cond, thenId: thenBlock.id, elseId: elseBlock.id, ifTok: ifTok)
		let thenTerminated = thenEnd.isTerminated()
		let elseTerminated = elseEnd.isTerminated()

		if thenTerminated and elseTerminated 'bothReturn'
			// Nothing reaches the continuation — see `newDeadBlock` for why it is marked rather than left
			// unterminated, and why the unreachable tail may still be parsed into it.
			return self.newDeadBlock(func, label: b"ifcont", line: ifTok.line)
		end 'bothReturn'

		var cont = self.newBlock(func, label: b"ifcont", line: ifTok.line)
		if not thenTerminated and not elseTerminated 'bothFallThrough'
			// ⭐ **CONDITIONAL MOVE RECONCILED across the two live edges** — a binding moved in one branch and
			// left live in the other is dropped on the live edge (both are real, unterminated blocks) and
			// marked uniformly moved past the merge. Emitted BEFORE `mergeAtContinuation` records the phi
			// edges and before the branches, so the decref lands in the branch's own body.
			var edges = ReachingMoveEdgeArray.create()
			edges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(thenEnd, mark: thenMoveMark))
			edges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(elseEnd, mark: elseMoveMark))
			self.reconcileMovesAtMerge(edges, anchor: ifTok)

			self.mergeAtContinuation(thenEnd, predB: elseEnd, cont: cont, bindings: bindings, valsA: thenVals, valsB: elseVals)
			self.emitBranch(thenEnd, targetId: cont.id)
			self.emitBranch(elseEnd, targetId: cont.id)
		end 'bothFallThrough' else if not thenTerminated 'onlyThen'
			// Only the then branch reaches the continuation — its values AND its move state carry through
			// (the else branch terminated, having dropped its own live values on the way out).
			self.restoreBindings(bindings, snapshot: thenVals)
			self.restoreMoveMark(thenMoveMark)
			self.emitBranch(thenEnd, targetId: cont.id)
		end 'onlyThen' else 'onlyElse'
			// Only the else branch reaches the continuation — its values and move state ARE the current
			// state (else was parsed last and nothing has mutated either since), so both restores are
			// no-ops today. They are written explicitly anyway, symmetric with `onlyThen` above: "the
			// surviving branch's state prevails at the merge" is then stated once per axis rather than
			// left as an unstated dependence on branch-parse order — which, if ever reordered, would keep
			// values correct (explicit restore) while silently diverging the drop-gating move state into a
			// leak or double-free (a wrong answer, not a compile error).
			self.restoreBindings(bindings, snapshot: elseVals)
			self.restoreMoveMark(elseMoveMark)
			self.emitBranch(elseEnd, targetId: cont.id)
		end 'onlyElse'
		return cont
	end 'finalizeIfElse'

	// Merge two incoming value snapshots at a continuation block. A carried binding whose
	// value differs across the paths gets a continuation PHI (a block-arg) and each
	// predecessor records the value it carries on its edge; a binding with the same value
	// on both paths keeps that value with no phi. Each merged binding's current value is
	// set to the phi (or the common value) so code after the `if` reads the merged value.
	//
	// `bindings` is the statement's carried set (`assignedBindings`) in `mutableVars`
	// declaration order, and both snapshots are positional over it. Phis are therefore
	// minted in that same order — which is what makes the emitted code identical to the
	// old whole-`mutableVars` merge: a var no branch assigned had a == b there and got no
	// phi, so leaving it out of `bindings` changes no decision, only the work done to
	// reach it. Both snapshots and `bindings` are the SAME length (asserted): every branch
	// body is a lexical block, so a `var` declared inside one is off `mutableVars` — and so
	// never entered `bindings` — before its exit values are snapshotted.
	function mergeAtContinuation(predA IrBlock, predB IrBlock, cont IrBlock, bindings VarInfoArray, valsA ValueIdArray, valsB ValueIdArray)
		self.assertSnapshotAligned(bindings, snapshot: valsA, site: "mergeAtContinuation/valsA")
		self.assertSnapshotAligned(bindings, snapshot: valsB, site: "mergeAtContinuation/valsB")

		var edgeArgsA = ValueIdArray.create()
		var edgeArgsB = ValueIdArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto bindings.count() 'eachVar'
			var binding = try bindings.get(k) otherwise panic("mergeAtContinuation: bindings.get OOB at {k}")
			let a = try valsA.get(k) otherwise panic("mergeAtContinuation: valsA.get OOB at {k}")
			let b = try valsB.get(k) otherwise panic("mergeAtContinuation: valsB.get OOB at {k}")
			if a == b 'same'
				binding.boundValue = a
			end 'same' else 'differ'
				let phiId = self.mintPhiForBinding(cont, binding: binding)
				binding.boundValue = phiId
				self.pushMergedEdgeValue(edgeArgsA, binding: binding, value: a)
				self.pushMergedEdgeValue(edgeArgsB, binding: binding, value: b)

				// ⭐⭐ **AN `if` CONTINUATION IS A MERGE WHOSE INCOMING SET IS COMPLETE AT THE MINT, SO IT
				// ASKS FOR ITS ALIAS CLAIM BACK (G14 review).** `mintPhi` withholds the claim from EVERY
				// merge because a LOOP HEADER cannot answer — its back edge is not parsed yet — and that is
				// the right default; but this continuation is not a loop header. `predA` and `predB` are the
				// only two blocks that will ever branch here (`finalizeIfElse`/`finalizeIfNoElse` mint
				// `cont` one statement earlier, hand it exactly this pair, and emit both branches on the
				// spot), and `a`/`b` are final — `reconcileMovesAtMerge` has already run and the same
				// statement records them as the edge args.
				//
				// ⚠ **WITHOUT IT THE COMMONEST RANGED-`var` SHAPE IN THE LANGUAGE PAID TWICE, AND ONE OF THE
				// TWO WAS A DIVERGENCE FROM THE ORACLE.** MEASURED on `var t = a` / `if c: t = b` / `return t`
				// over `Num = int(0 to 1000)` with both parameters declared `Num`: the `return` grew a THIRD
				// cascade over a value the two entry guards had already proved, and `t as Num` — which the
				// bootstrap refuses with E3010 *"'Num' already fits in 'Num'"* — compiled clean and ran.
				// Both are the withheld claim, and both edges prove it, so it is lifted rather than lost.
				self.recordMergeAliasProof(phiId, incoming: pairArgs(a, second: b))
			end 'differ'
		end 'eachVar'
		self.recordBranchEdge(predA, targetId: cont.id, argIds: edgeArgsA)
		self.recordBranchEdge(predB, targetId: cont.id, argIds: edgeArgsB)
	end 'mergeAtContinuation'

	// Rebind each carried binding to a previously-captured snapshot value (from
	// `snapshotBindings` over the SAME `bindings` list). Used to rewind to the pre-branch
	// state before parsing an `if`'s false path, and to select a single surviving branch's
	// values at a continuation. Written THROUGH the binding, not by name, so a value is
	// never routed to a shadow (see LoopPhiVar). A var not in `bindings` is assigned by no
	// branch, so its value is already the one every path agrees on — nothing to restore.
	function restoreBindings(bindings VarInfoArray, snapshot ValueIdArray)
		self.assertSnapshotAligned(bindings, snapshot: snapshot, site: "restoreBindings")
		for k in 0 upto bindings.count() 'each'
			var binding = try bindings.get(k) otherwise panic("restoreBindings: bindings.get OOB at {k}")
			binding.boundValue = try snapshot.get(k) otherwise panic("restoreBindings: snapshot.get OOB at {k}")
		end 'each'
	end 'restoreBindings'

	// Every value snapshot is positional over the carried `bindings` list and must
	// therefore have exactly its length — block scoping is what guarantees that (a
	// branch-local `var` is drained at the block's `end`, so it never entered `bindings`
	// and can never lengthen a snapshot taken outside the block). A mismatch means the two
	// fell out of step, which would misattribute every later binding's value by one, so it
	// fails loudly here rather than silently.
	function assertSnapshotAligned(bindings VarInfoArray, snapshot ValueIdArray, site String)
		let expected = bindings.count()
		if snapshot.count() != expected 'drifted'
			panic("{site}: value snapshot holds {snapshot.count()} entries but the if carries {expected} assigned bindings — the snapshot drifted out of lockstep with the carried-bindings list")
		end 'drifted'
	end 'assertSnapshotAligned'

	// Parse a `while` loop (M4b):
	//   while <cond> 'label'
	//       <body>
	//   end 'label'
	//
	// Lowering shape (the first BACKWARD-branching CFG): the current block becomes the
	// PREHEADER, which branches unconditionally into a fresh HEADER. The header re-
	// evaluates the condition each iteration (a comparison, fused into `cmp`+`jcc` by
	// the Std→x64 pass, exactly as an `if`) and takes a two-way branch to the BODY or
	// the EXIT. The body ends with a BACK-EDGE branch to the header; `break` branches
	// to the exit, `continue` to the header. `resolveBlockJumps` already patches
	// backward rel32, so no new encoder work is needed.
	//
	// On-the-fly SSA: the header carries one PHI (block-arg) per mutable var the loop
	// ASSIGNS, minted eagerly BEFORE the body is parsed. Each phi becomes the var's current
	// value, so the condition and body read the loop-carried value; the preheader and every
	// back-edge feed the phis via the predecessor's `branchEdges`. Returns the EXIT block.
	//
	// **THE CARRIED SET IS THE VARS THE LOOP ASSIGNS — AND EAGER MINTING IS WHAT MAKES THAT
	// THE RIGHT QUESTION.** A var the loop never assigns holds the same value on every
	// iteration, so its pre-loop definition DOMINATES the header and a read inside the body
	// binds it correctly with no phi at all. A var the loop DOES assign needs one, and it
	// must exist before the body is parsed — because every read in the body, and every value
	// SNAPSHOT taken inside it (`parseIfStatement`'s entry/then/else snapshots, a nested
	// loop's preheader), captures whatever the binding holds AT THAT MOMENT. Mint the phi
	// later — on the var's first mention, say — and every snapshot already taken inside the
	// loop is holding the stale PRE-LOOP value, and an `if` merge in the body would carry it
	// onto the not-taken path. That is a silent miscompile on iteration 2, and it is why the
	// carried set is decided UP FRONT rather than discovered as the body is parsed:
	//
	//     var x = 1
	//     while c 'l'
	//         if d 'b'      // entry snapshot of x taken HERE
	//             x = 2     // a phi minted only now is invisible to that snapshot
	//         end 'b'       // ⇒ the false edge would carry 1, resetting x every iteration
	//     end 'l'
	//
	// So the set is read off the TOKENS, before a single op is emitted: `assignedBindingsIn`
	// over the loop's token extent. It is an over-approximation on purpose (a shadowed inner
	// declaration of the same name counts, and so does a `let`), and over-approximating is
	// FREE while under-approximating is a miscompile — a surplus phi costs nothing:
	//   • a var the loop never READS gets a phi nothing reads — but the back edge passes
	//     that phi to ITSELF, so it is self-sustaining and liveness holds it live around
	//     the whole loop. `pruneDeadBlockArgs` (a Std pass) deletes those; without it, two
	//     sequential loops inflate maxlive by one per dead var and can FALSE-E5001.
	//   • a var the loop reads but never WRITES gets a phi that copies a value which never
	//     changes. Biased coloring coalesces it with its incoming value into one register
	//     and the edge copy self-elides (see RegisterAllocator.copyGroupBlocked).
	//
	// What this REPLACES is a phi for every mutable var IN SCOPE, which was quadratic in the
	// program: a function's `var`s accumulate as it goes, so the Nth loop minted phis for all
	// ~N of them and assigned a ValueId to each — including every one `pruneDeadBlockArgs`
	// then deleted. `blockArgIdBound`, and every dense column sized by it, is O(ValueIds), so
	// the phis nobody kept were still being paid for in bytes at every later pass.
	function parseWhileStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let whileIndex = self.pos
		let whileTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.while)

		// Decided from the tokens, before anything is emitted — see the header.
		let bodyEndIndex = self.blockStatementEndIndex(whileIndex, continuesAtElse: false)
		let carried = self.assignedBindingsIn(whileIndex, endIndex: bodyEndIndex)

		var header = self.newBlock(func, label: b"whilehdr", line: whileTok.line)

		let phiVars = self.mintLoopHeaderPhis(header, carried: carried)

		self.recordBranchEdge(block, targetId: header.id, argIds: self.edgeArgsFor(phiVars, preLoop: true))
		self.emitBranch(block, targetId: header.id)

		// Condition, evaluated in the header — so `continue` and the back edge, which both
		// target the header, re-evaluate it. A short-circuit `and`/`or` in the condition
		// (`while check() and more()`) spreads that evaluation over blocks BETWEEN the
		// header and the two-way branch; `condExit` is the block it ends in, and that is
		// where the branch — and the exit's incoming edge — belong. The header stays the
		// loop's entry, so nothing about `continue` or the back edge changes.
		let condTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		let condOwnedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()
		let cond = try self.parseBoolCondition(func, block: header, kwTok: whileTok)
		var condExit = self.currentBlock

		// ⭐ **PER ITERATION, NOT PER LOOP** — and that is why the release goes HERE rather than in the exit
		// block. The condition is re-evaluated on every trip (the back edge and `continue` both target the
		// header), so it builds a FRESH temporary each time; `condExit` is inside the loop, so the release
		// rides round with it and the live count stays flat. Hoisted to `exit` it would run once for N
		// allocations — a leak that grows with the trip count. The `if` reasoning applies unchanged for the
		// two edges: `condExit` dominates both the body and the exit.
		self.drainPendingTemps(condTempFloor, anchor: whileTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **AND A SCOPE-LIFETIME TEMPORARY THE CONDITION HELD COMES BACK PER ITERATION TOO (A3h) — the one
		// place the `if` reasoning does NOT carry over.** An `if` condition runs ONCE and dominates everything
		// after it, so a container `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` held for a borrow may legitimately live to the
		// frame's exit. A `while` condition runs on every trip and rebuilds that container each time, while the
		// enrolment records ONE `ValueId` and drops it ONCE — so the frame's exit would free the LAST trip's
		// record and leak all the others. MEASURED as exit **101** on
		// `while n < (try make().get(0) otherwise …).count()`, which is the leak gate catching exactly the
		// direction this rung's risk inverts to. The borrow cannot escape the condition (it is a `bool`), so
		// releasing here is sound as well as necessary — the short-circuit rhs's argument, one construct out.
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(condOwnedFloor, exitBlock: condExit, anchor: whileTok)

		let loopLabelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()

		var body = self.newBlock(func, label: loopLabelTok.value, line: loopLabelTok.line)
		var exit = self.newBlock(func, label: b"whileexit", line: whileTok.line)
		self.emitCondBranch(condExit, cond: cond, thenId: body.id, elseId: exit.id, ifTok: whileTok)

		// `ownedFloor` is the owned-binding height NOW, before the body opens its block — so a
		// `break`/`continue` releases exactly what the body declares (see LoopContext.ownedFloor).
		// A `while`'s continue target IS its header: the condition is re-evaluated on the back edge,
		// so there is no increment to run first and no step block to run it in.
		self.loops.push(LoopContext.create(loopLabelTok.value, continueTargetId: header.id, exitId: exit.id, phiVars: phiVars, ownedFloor: self.ownedBindings.count()))

		// The body is a lexical BLOCK: its declarations are gone — and its `var`s off
		// `mutableVars` — before the back-edge args below are read, so those args name the
		// loop's own phi bindings and never a body-local shadow.
		var bodyEnd = try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: body, label: loopLabelTok.value)

		// Guard the token scan against the parse it predicted — see `assertScanAligned`.
		self.assertScanAligned("parseWhileStatement", predictedEnd: bodyEndIndex)

		let ctx = try self.loops.pop() otherwise panic("parseWhileStatement: loop stack empty after body of '{String.from(loopLabelTok.value)}'")

		// Normal back-edge: a body that falls through goes wherever a COMPLETED iteration goes, with
		// its (possibly reassigned) current values. A body that always returns/breaks/continues on
		// every path leaves `bodyEnd` terminated — no fall-through edge.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE TARGET IS `ctx.continueTargetId`, DERIVED, NOT THE HEADER SPELLED AGAIN.** "Where a
		// normally-completing iteration lands" is ONE fact, and `continue` already reads it off the
		// context (`parseContinueStatement`); writing `header.id` here too made it a fact stated twice
		// per construct, agreeing only by coincidence. For a `while` the two ARE the header — which is
		// exactly why the duplicate stayed invisible — and for a `for` the fall-through must reach the
		// STEP block or the counter never advances.
		if not bodyEnd.isTerminated() 'fallsBack'
			self.recordBranchEdge(bodyEnd, targetId: ctx.continueTargetId, argIds: self.edgeArgsFor(phiVars, preLoop: false))
			self.emitBranch(bodyEnd, targetId: ctx.continueTargetId)
		end 'fallsBack'

		self.wireLoopExit(condExit, exit: exit, ctx: ctx)
		return exit
	end 'parseWhileStatement'

	// ⭐ **MINT ONE HEADER PHI PER CARRIED BINDING** — the front half BOTH loop statements run, and the
	// only place a loop-carried phi is created. `carried` is the construct's carried set, read off the
	// tokens by its caller (see `parseWhileStatement`'s header for why it must be known up front) and
	// already in `mutableVars` DECLARATION ORDER, which is what makes a merge inside the body mint its
	// own phis in the same order — both come from `assignedBindingsIn`, so it is one ordering rather
	// than two that agree.
	//
	// Each binding's PRE-LOOP value is captured into the `LoopPhiVar` as it goes, because installing the
	// phi overwrites `boundValue` — that snapshot is what `edgeArgsFor(preLoop: true)` reads to build the preheader
	// edge, and reading it here, inside the single forward pass, is what keeps the two positional.
	//
	// Index-based so each binding is a MUTABLE reference — a `for x in …` loop variable is immutable,
	// and installing the phi writes through the binding.
	function mintLoopHeaderPhis(header IrBlock, carried VarInfoArray) returns LoopPhiVarArray
		let phiVars = self.mintCarriedPhis(header, carried: carried)
		self.installCarriedPhis(phiVars)
		return phiVars
	end 'mintLoopHeaderPhis'

	// The MINT half, without the install — one phi per carried binding, in `carried`'s order, each
	// remembering the value the binding held BEFORE the phi existed.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS SPLIT FROM THE INSTALL BECAUSE THE TWO CONSTRUCTS THAT MINT THESE PHIS INSTALL THEM AT
	// DIFFERENT MOMENTS.** A loop header DOMINATES its body, so its phis are the values the body itself must
	// read and the install belongs here. A block-form `try`'s error block does NOT dominate the try body —
	// it is a SIBLING the body branches to — so its phis must not be visible while the body parses (the body
	// reads and reassigns the ordinary values, and each routed edge carries whatever they hold at that point);
	// they are installed only for the HANDLER, which is the block those phis dominate. One mint, two install
	// points, and the positional contract `edgeArgsFor` depends on is written down once.
	function mintCarriedPhis(block IrBlock, carried VarInfoArray) returns LoopPhiVarArray
		var phiVars = LoopPhiVarArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto carried.count() 'mkPhi'
			var binding = try carried.get(k) otherwise panic("mintCarriedPhis: carried.get OOB at {k}")
			// Read BEFORE the mint, and in its own statement: the value a binding held before its phi
			// existed is what every pre-merge edge carries, and folding it into the `create` call below
			// would make that ordering an argument-evaluation accident.
			let entryValue = binding.boundValue
			phiVars.push(LoopPhiVar.create(binding, phiId: self.mintPhiForBinding(block, binding: binding), entryValue: entryValue))
		end 'mkPhi'
		return phiVars
	end 'mintCarriedPhis'

	// Make each carried binding READ its phi from here on — the install half above. Index-based for
	// `mintCarriedPhis`' reason: a `for x in …` loop variable is immutable, and this writes through the
	// binding.
	function installCarriedPhis(phiVars LoopPhiVarArray)
		for k in 0 upto phiVars.count() 'install'
			var phiVar = try phiVars.get(k) otherwise panic("installCarriedPhis: phiVars.get OOB at {k}")
			phiVar.binding.boundValue = phiVar.phiId
		end 'install'
	end 'installCarriedPhis'

	// ⭐⭐ **PARSE A `for` LOOP (P1.8 slice A).** Three source spellings, ONE lowering:
	//
	//     for <name> in <lo> to   <hi> 'label'    — a counted range, INCLUSIVE of `hi`
	//     for <name> in <lo> upto <hi> 'label'    — the same, EXCLUSIVE of `hi`
	//     for <name> in <collection>   'label'    — walked by whichever of the four collection forks fits it
	//
	// FOUR blocks, and the fourth is the one `while` does not have:
	//
	//     preheader → evaluate the source ONCE → br header(idx0, …carried)
	//     header    → idx <= hi ? body : exit          (`<` for `upto`; for a counted record, `idx < count`)
	//     body      → bind the loop variable; <user statements>; br step
	//     step      → br header(idx + 1, …carried)     ← `continue` targets THIS, not the header
	//     exit
	//
	// ⭐ **THE STEP BLOCK IS MANDATORY.** Every path that continues the loop — the body's tail and every
	// `continue` — lands there, so the counter advances exactly once per iteration however the body was
	// left. Without it a `continue` re-enters the header with the counter unchanged and the loop spins
	// forever; both reference compilers hit that and both wrote a comment about it (v1
	// `Parser.maxon:10137-10139`, bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:13150-13153`), and so did shv2's own
	// `LoopContext` before this rung existed.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE COUNTER FORM IS WHAT A SOURCE WITH NO `createIterator` GETS — AND NEITHER CORPUS CONTAINER
	// IS ONE ANY MORE.** `stdlib/Array.maxon` is listed
	// (`StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules`), so `materializeIterableIterationSource` rewrites an
	// array source to the `ArrayIterator` its `createIterator()` returns BEFORE the fork below is
	// reached, and `for x in [10, 20, 30]` emits `Array.createIterator` / `ArrayIterator.current` /
	// `ArrayIterator.advance` — MEASURED in the emitted IR, not inferred.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND `stdlib/Vector.maxon` JOINED IT AT `W189`, WHICH THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO DENY IN SO MANY
	// WORDS.** It named *"a `Vector`, whose record IS an array's and which is compiler-served with no
	// `createIterator`"* as the standing example of a counter source — a present-tense fact that `W86`
	// predicted would flip **on VISIBILITY alone**, and it did: the corpus declares `createIterator`, so a
	// vector source takes the rewrite exactly as an array does. **MEASURED, both directions: a
	// `for x in v` summing a `Vector with 3 Int` answers 9 either way, and with the corpus
	// `createIterator` sabotaged to `throw IterationError.exhausted` it answers 0** — i.e. the loop runs
	// the declaration's cursor. **The trade was taken deliberately and it is not free: that program grows
	// 13,552 -> 14,974 bytes (+10.5%)**, trading a counted read against a per-loop iterator struct and a
	// call per step. It is taken for UNIFORMITY — one rule for both corpus containers, the one `Array`
	// already follows — and because the alternative is a second loop form kept alive by a name.
	//
	// What still arrives here as a counter is every source that supplies no cursor of its own: a program's
	// OWN `type Array … implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` that declares none (see `requireIterableSource`'s
	// array arm, which is the door that admits exactly those). The counted ranges share the shape.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT THE COUNTER IS STILL THE RIGHT SHAPE FOR, AND WHY THE COMPILER NEVER SYNTHESIZES A
	// CURSOR.** The bootstrap's fast path (`2-Parser.cs:13143-13244`) was taken over v1's
	// compiler-BUILT heap cursor (`Parser.maxon:10205-10373`) for three measured reasons: v1's cursor
	// snapshots `source.buffer` and dangles when the source is freed at its last use
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:9750-9760`), it double-retains and leaked the backing of every
	// scope-local collection (`:9789-9800`), and it costs two heap allocations per loop. None of that
	// is an argument against the cursor a source DECLARES: that one is an ordinary struct whose
	// lifetime and refcounting are its own declaration's business and the ordinary call machinery's,
	// which is precisely why the rewrite above is a rewrite and not a sixth loop form.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE LENGTH IS RE-READ LIVE IN THE HEADER**, never snapshotted into the preheader. The header
	// comparison against the real length IS the bounds check, which is what lets the element read below
	// be the UNCHECKED one; a stale snapshot would let that read run off the end of the buffer.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE DESTRUCTURING LOOP LIVES IN A FRAME OF ITS OWN, AND THAT IS E5001's CURE APPLIED AT THE
	// FUNCTION BOUNDARY.** `for (k, v) in m` binds one name per tuple element, which is a LOOP — and it sat
	// at the far end of `parseForStatement`, a function that by then holds two dozen locals it still needs
	// afterwards. Every one of them was live ACROSS this loop, so the allocator owed each a register for its
	// whole body: shv2 refused its own source with *"the loop at Parser.maxon:36919 needs 11 more
	// register(s) than are available — 25 values"*, of which **sixteen were read 0 times in the loop**. They
	// are not the loop's working set at all; they are the CALLER's. Behind a call they spill around it at no
	// cost, which is exactly what the diagnostic's *"the values idle across the loop were already spilled
	// around it"* sentence describes for everything outside a loop.
	//
	// ⚠ It takes six arguments and that is the honest count: this is a self-contained step of the parse
	// (*"bind one loop variable per tuple element"*) and each argument is a fact only the caller holds.
	function declareDestructuredLoopVariables(func IrFunction, nameToks TokenArray, loopValue ValueId, patternTok Token, forTok Token, bodyEndIndex TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		let layout = try self.requireDestructurableTuple(loopValue, nameToks: nameToks, keywordTok: forTok, patternSubject: ForPatternSubject, sourceNoun: ForPatternSourceNoun, needsClause: ForPatternNeedsClause)
		for i in 0 upto nameToks.count() 'eachName'
			let nameTok = try nameToks.get(i) otherwise panic("declareDestructuredLoopVariables: nameToks.get OOB at {i} below count {nameToks.count()}")
			try self.declareLoopVariable(func, nameTok: nameTok, value: self.emitFieldLoad(loopValue, layout: layout, index: i, range: SourceRange.between(patternTok, last: nameTok)), bodyEndIndex: bodyEndIndex)
		end 'eachName'
	end 'declareDestructuredLoopVariables'

	// ⭐ **THE ELEMENT IS A BORROW** (`emitContainerElementAccessor`, `borrowed`) — the array keeps
	// ownership and its own `__managed_decref` walk destroys it, exactly as `get`/`first`/`last` already
	// yield (`dispatchArrayMethod`). The loop variable is therefore enrolled in NEITHER `ownedBindings`
	// NOR `pendingTempDrops`, and the loop costs zero refcount traffic per iteration. The reference
	// bootstrap RETAINS instead (`MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedMemory.cs:309-358`); shv2 has exactly
	// ONE door for reading an array element and it borrows, so retaining here would be a second
	// ownership rule for the same read. v1 borrows too (`Parser.maxon:10337`, `markBorrowSlot`).
	function parseForStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let forIndex = self.pos
		let forTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.for)

		// Decided from the tokens, before anything is emitted — see `parseWhileStatement`'s header.
		let bodyEndIndex = self.blockStatementEndIndex(forIndex, continuesAtElse: false)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE LOOP VARIABLE IS A PATTERN, NOT A NAME — `for (key, value) in m` (TUPLES + `Map`).** A
		// `Map`'s element IS a `(Key, Value)` tuple (`MapIterator.current()`), so the destructuring spelling is
		// how `/specs/map.md` and `/specs/tuples.md` both write a map loop; the single-name form is the same
		// pattern with one position and no parens. Read as a LIST of names either way, so every question below
		// — the anchor, the discard, the E3012 candidate, the borrow — is asked once per position rather than
		// once per spelling.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ANCHOR IS THE PATTERN'S FIRST TOKEN AND NOT A NAME**, because a destructuring header has no
		// single name to blame and `_` may occupy the position that would otherwise be it.
		let patternTok = self.current()
		let nameToks = try self.parseLoopVariablePattern(forTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.in)

		// ⭐ **THE `for` STATEMENT'S OWN LEXICAL FRAME.** Two things live in it and must be gone at the
		// loop's `end`: the loop VARIABLE (`block-scoping.md`'s `for-iterator-not-accessible-after` pins
		// that reading it after the loop is E2004) and, when the source is a fresh OWNED array, the
		// internal binding that owns it. It is opened BEFORE the source is parsed so the owned mark sits
		// below anything the source enrols.
		let forMark = self.openBlock()

		// ⭐ **THE SOURCE IS EVALUATED ONCE, IN THE PREHEADER** — both range bounds, or the array record.
		// Measured against the runnable oracle, which stores the end bound in a `__range_end_N` local
		// ahead of the header (`2-Parser.cs:13490`) rather than re-evaluating it per trip: a bound that
		// calls a side-effecting function is called exactly once.
		let sourceTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		let sourceStart = self.pos
		// The op index the SOURCE EXPRESSION's own ops start at — `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`'s `targetOpFloor`,
		// so a source whose throw the loop absorbs (`absorbThrowingCursorSource`) can never rewrite a call the
		// previous statement emitted.
		let sourceOpFloor = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		// `var` for the ONE rewrite a collection source can undergo: a `Map` is replaced by its materialized
		// entries below, which is what makes every question after this point an ARRAY's question.
		var source = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)

		// `to` / `upto` after the first expression is what makes this a RANGE. The operator's token INDEX
		// is carried (not the `Token` itself — a `var` may not alias an immutable aggregate binding,
		// E3078) so the loop test below can anchor its type diagnostic on the operator the user wrote;
		// an array form has no such operator and anchors on the `for`.
		// ⭐⭐ **THE TEN FACTS THE SOURCE EXPRESSION SETTLES, IN ONE RECORD — E5001's OTHER CURE, AND THE ONE
		// THIS FUNCTION NEEDED.** Each was a `var` of its own, and all ten were live from the header analysis
		// down to the merge at the end — so the allocator owed ten registers across a function that also
		// needs its five blocks, its phis and its carried-var columns. shv2 refused its own source here:
		// *"the code at Parser.maxon:36458 needs 11 more register(s) than are available — 25 values must be
		// held in registers at once at this point"*, with the note that everything spillable already had
		// been. A RECORD's fields live in memory and are never promoted, which is exactly what the
		// diagnostic asks for — and the bundle is not arbitrary: these ten ARE one answer, *what kind of
		// thing is being iterated*, settled once by the header and read by every phase after it.
		var src = ForSourceFacts.create(source, testAnchorIndex: forIndex)
		// ⭐⭐ **A RANGE WHOSE BOUNDS ARE BOTH CHARACTER LITERALS IS A RANGE OF `Character`S** (A5m-ab), and
		// this is the one place that is decided. Every character literal is a `Character`
		// (`parseCharLiteral`), so `for c in 'a' to 'e'` arrives here with two RECORDS where the loop needs two
		// COUNTERS — and the body needs a Character per trip, which no record in memory holds.
		//
		// Both halves fall out of coercing the bounds to their CODEPOINTS through the ordinary
		// `integerizedOperand` door: the loop below is then the counted integer range it always was, stepping
		// codepoint by codepoint, and the ELEMENT is minted from the counter by `__char_from_cp`. Nothing about
		// the range machinery changes, and no `.rdata` record is registered for a bound that became a number.
		//
		// ⚠ **BOTH, OR NEITHER.** `for i in 'a' to n` is an INTEGER range from 97 — one bound is a number, so
		// the range is over numbers, exactly as `cp == '-'` is a comparison of numbers. The element type
		// follows the PAIR and not either half, which is why the two tags are read BEFORE the coercion.
		if self.at(TokenKind.to) or self.at(TokenKind.upto) 'rangeHeader'
			src.isRange = true
			src.rangeInclusive = self.at(TokenKind.to)
			src.testAnchorIndex = self.pos
			self.advance()
			let limitStart = self.pos
			src.limit = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			let bothWereCharacters = self.tagOf(source) == ValueTypeTag.character and self.tagOf(src.limit) == ValueTypeTag.character
			source = try self.integerizedOperand(source, expected: ValueTypeTag.integer, start: sourceStart, pastEnd: src.testAnchorIndex)
			src.limit = try self.integerizedOperand(src.limit, expected: ValueTypeTag.integer, start: limitStart, pastEnd: self.pos)
			// A pair of Characters that did NOT both coerce is a pair of bounds the counter cannot step — a
			// binding rather than a literal, or a multi-codepoint cluster — and `requireCountableRangeBound`
			// below refuses it in its own words rather than this flag promising an element it cannot mint.
			src.isCharacterRange = bothWereCharacters and tagIsIntegral(self.tagOf(source)) and tagIsIntegral(self.tagOf(src.limit))
		end 'rangeHeader'

		// ⭐ **A COUNTED RANGE'S BOUNDS MUST BE INTEGERS**, asked here — the moment both are in hand and
		// before a single block of the loop exists, so a refused program emits no op. See
		// `requireCountableRangeBound`: without it a `float` range PANICKED in the x64 emitter and a
		// `bool` range was silently accepted.
		if src.isRange 'countedRange'
			try self.requireCountableRangeBound(source, anchor: self.tokenAt(sourceStart))
			try self.requireCountableRangeBound(src.limit, anchor: self.tokenAt(src.testAnchorIndex))
		end 'countedRange'

		// ⭐⭐ **ONLY THE ARRAY FORM LOCKS ITS SOURCE, AND THE FORK IS WHERE THAT IS DECIDED.** The lock
		// exists for ONE reason — the array form hands the body a BORROWED element the array still owns,
		// so a mutation that reallocates or clears it leaves that borrow dangling. A RANGE has no borrow
		// to protect: both bounds are snapshotted into the preheader above and the loop variable is the
		// header phi, so a write to a bound's variable inside the body cannot reach the loop at all.
		//
		// ⚠ It was taken BEFORE this fork and that was a FALSE REJECT of a legal program: `for i in n
		// upto 3 'l' n = n + 1 … end` locked `n` and reported **E2013**, where the runnable oracle
		// compiles it and returns 3. The asymmetry is what named it a bug rather than a policy — the END
		// bound's variable stayed writable, because only the FIRST expression starts at `sourceStart`, so
		// one range had two rules for its two halves. Both directions are pinned by
		// `specs-shv2/for-range-bound-mutation.md`, together with the array half, in ONE file: they are
		// two outcomes of one decision and a spec that pinned only one would let the other drift back.
		//
		// Taken here, it is still well before the loop VARIABLE is declared, which is what keeps
		// `for arr in arr` locking the SOURCE binding rather than the loop variable that shadows its name.
		// ⭐ **A STRING SOURCE IS ITS OWN THIRD FORM** (P1.8 slice B) — neither a counted range nor an array.
		// It shares the range form's answer to every question the ARRAY form asks specially: it takes no
		// iteration LOCK and records no borrow SUBJECT, because it hands the body a value it MINTS rather
		// than one the source stores (see the ownership note on the element read below), so there is no
		// borrow for a mutation to invalidate. What it does share with the array form is that its source is
		// a heap record evaluated once into the preheader.
		// ⭐⭐ **A LIST SOURCE WAS A FOURTH FORM HERE — A NODE CURSOR THE LOOP BUILT — AND IT WENT WITH THE
		// SYNTHESIZED `List` (W153).** The header's block-arg carried a NODE POINTER seeded from `__list_head`,
		// tested against 0, read through `__list_node_value` and advanced by `__list_node_next`. A corpus
		// `List` declares `Iterable with (Element, ListIter)`, so it is rewritten to its own `ListIterator` by
		// `materializeIterableIterationSource` before this fork is reached and takes the CURSOR form below,
		// exactly as a corpus `Array` and a corpus `Map` already do.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE COMPLEXITY THE OLD FORM EXISTED TO GUARANTEE IS UNCHANGED, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT A
		// REGRESSION DRESSED AS A SIMPLIFICATION.** `/specs/list.md`'s *"a cached cursor … O(1) per element"* is
		// a statement about the traversal holding a NODE between trips, and `ListIterator` holds exactly that
		// (`var node as ENode`, advanced through `next()`). The shape this must never take — a counter over
		// `get(i)`, O(n²) — is not what a declared cursor does either.
		// ⭐⭐ **A CURSOR-PROTOCOL USER TYPE IS THE FIFTH FORM, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE THE SOURCE WALKS ITSELF**
		// (conditional extensions). The other four are compiler-owned records whose traversal this function
		// BUILDS — an ordinal, a byte position, a node pointer. Here the source supplies it: the body reads
		// `current()` and the step calls `advance()`, and the header's block-arg carries `advance`'s ERROR FLAG
		// rather than a position at all. `none` when the source is not one; the SURFACE is carried (not a bool)
		// because the two calls need it — a struct source emits direct calls and an EXISTENTIAL dispatches
		// through the table it carries. See `cursorIterationSurface`.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A DO-WHILE AND THE OTHER FOUR ARE WHILE LOOPS, AND THAT IS THE PROTOCOL'S OWN CONTRACT.**
		// `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:130-142`: a live cursor is ALREADY POSITIONED on an element — its constructor
		// throws `exhausted` for an empty collection rather than producing an empty cursor — and `advance()`
		// throws when called while positioned on the LAST element, leaving the position unchanged. So the first
		// element must be yielded BEFORE the first advance, which falls out of seeding the flag to 0 in the
		// preheader and computing the next flag in the STEP: a while-shaped loop would advance first and drop
		// element 0. `partial-availability` counting exactly 2 over a 2-element sequence is the arity check on it.
		// What the header's error-flag block-arg starts at for a CURSOR source (W3). `atTheFirstElement` is the
		// bare cursor form's literal 0 — "no throw yet", which is what makes the first trip unconditional — and
		// `fromFactory` is a throwing `createIterator()`'s own flag, so an empty collection reports itself
		// through the loop's ordinary exit test. See `materializeIterableIterationSource`.
		// ⭐ **THE E3070 SUBJECT OF THE ELEMENT THIS LOOP WILL BORROW** — resolved HERE, beside the lock and
		// off the same `sourceStart`, because both are answers about the SOURCE and both must be taken before
		// the loop VARIABLE is declared: `for arr in arr` must key on the source binding, not on the loop
		// variable that shadows its name. Empty for a range (no borrow) and for a source that names no
		// binding, exactly as the lock is.

		// ⭐⭐ **THE CURSOR GETS A SCOPE OF ITS OWN, AND THE SOURCE EXPRESSION'S TEMPORARIES STAY OUTSIDE IT.**
		// `forMark` above holds everything the SOURCE EXPRESSION enrolled — a record `keepFieldSourceAliveForItsRead`
		// held alive so a field could be read out of it, which is what `for pair in makeBox().entries` produces — and
		// those exist on EVERY path out of this statement. The cursor, when there is one, exists only on the path
		// that got one: a throwing `createIterator()` reports an empty collection by throwing, and that edge
		// (`emitEmptyIterableEntry`) must not reach a `__destruct_<Cursor>`.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **THE TWO USED TO SHARE ONE FRAME, AND THE EMPTY EDGE THEREFORE SKIPPED BOTH — A LEAK.** MEASURED on
		// thirty lines of user code with no stdlib container type in it: `for (k, v) in h.boxed().m` over an EMPTY
		// `Map` exits **101**, where the same loop over a one-entry map exits 0 and the same empty map through a
		// `let b = h.boxed()` binding exits 0. The record `h.boxed()` returned was released in `forexit` and nowhere
		// else, so a zero-trip loop never released it. `stdlib/helpers/http/httpHelpers.maxon`'s
		// `for (hdrName, hdrValue) in request.headers().headers` is exactly that shape over a request with no
		// user headers, so every `HttpClient.get` leaked one `HttpHeaders`.
		//
		// ⚠ It is a nested BLOCK rather than a recorded height because the two batches must be emitted into two
		// DIFFERENT blocks off one list: `closeBlock` is what takes the cursor off `ownedBindings` between them, so
		// the second emission cannot name it. A range opens an empty one and pays nothing — it enrols no cursor and
		// has no empty edge — and the exit's drops come out in the order they always did (cursor first, then the
		// source's temporaries in reverse), because that is the order one call over the shared list produced.
		let cursorMark = self.openBlock()
		if not src.isRange 'collectionSource'
			// ⚠ **A `Map` REWRITE STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH `__map_entries` (W105).** It replaced a SYNTHESIZED
			// map source with an `Array with (Key, Value)` the runtime walk materialized, because that record
			// stored no element a loop body could be handed. A corpus `Map` declares `Iterable` and its
			// `MapIterator` hands out the pair itself, so it takes the cursor rewrite below like every other
			// declared iterable.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **AN `Iterable` SOURCE BECOMES ITS CURSOR, AND THEN IT IS A CURSOR LIKE ANY OTHER (W3).** It
			// runs before `lockIterationSource`/`borrowSubjectNameAt`, and both of those key on `sourceStart` —
			// the token the USER wrote — so `for pair in m` locks `m` and blames `m`, not a temporary that has
			// no name in the source at all. See `materializeIterableIterationSource` for why the ENTRY SEED
			// travels with the cursor.
			let iterable = try self.materializeIterableIterationSource(func, source: source, sourceOpFloor: sourceOpFloor, forTok: forTok)
			source = match iterable 'walkedValue'
				notIterable gives source
				cursor(iterator, _) gives iterator
			end 'walkedValue'
			src.cursorEntrySeed = match iterable 'walkedSeed'
				notIterable gives src.cursorEntrySeed
				cursor(_, seed) gives CursorEntrySeed.fromFactory(seed)
			end 'walkedSeed'

			// ⭐ **RESOLVED HERE AND HANDED TO THE ADMISSION TEST, so the door that REFUSES and the fork that
			// EMITS read one answer.** They used to resolve it separately, which is two chances to admit on one
			// reading and emit on another.
			//
			// ⚠ It is NOT the only resolution in this statement, and saying so would be false:
			// `materializeIterableIterationSource` above resolves a surface of its own, necessarily — it runs on
			// the source BEFORE the rewrite and this one describes the cursor AFTER it, so they are surfaces of
			// two different values. What is shared is the FUNCTION, so they cannot disagree; what is not shared
			// is the cost, and for a witness-dispatched source each ask walks the slot list per protocol name.
			src.cursorSurface = try self.cursorIterationSurface(source, forTok: forTok)
			try self.requireIterableSource(source, cursorSurface: src.cursorSurface, forTok: forTok)
			src.isString = tagIsText(self.tagOf(source))

			// ⭐⭐ **THE LOCK IS EVERY COLLECTION FORM'S; ONLY THE BORROW SUBJECT IS THE ARRAY'S.** The
			// String form shipped without the lock, on the reasoning that a String hands the body a MINTED
			// Character rather than a slot the source keeps, so no mutation can dangle a borrow.
			//
			// ⚠ **THAT REASONING ANSWERS THE WRONG QUESTION, AND THE GAP WAS A REACHABLE SEGFAULT.** The
			// lock does not only stop a mutation that destroys a borrowed ELEMENT; it stops a REBIND that
			// destroys the RECORD THE LOOP ITSELF IS WALKING. `source` is the record evaluated once into
			// the preheader, and every trip re-reads `length@8` and `buffer@0` off it — so `var s = "…"`
			// with `s = "z"` in the body decrefs that record while the header still reads it. MEASURED:
			// **0xC0000005** on `for c in s 'each' … s = "z" … end`, where the array twin is E2013.
			//
			// ⚠ **AND THE PROGRAM THE MISSING LOCK WAS MEANT TO KEEP LEGAL IS NOT LEGAL IN THE ORACLE
			// EITHER.** `for c in s 'each' s.append(t) end` — the case the old comment named — is
			// **E3019** on the runnable bootstrap, and the rebind is **E2013** there. Both halves of the
			// full lock therefore MATCH the oracle; the exemption diverged from it on the one program it
			// was protecting and segfaulted on the one it was not.
			src.sourceLock = self.lockIterationSource(sourceStart)

			// The BORROW SUBJECT is the SLOT-BACKED forms' — the array's and the vector's — and the
			// String form's alone is exempt, for the reason the lock is not: a String's element is
			// MINTED, so no live reference into the source outlives the read and E3070 has nothing to
			// track (`attachPendingBorrows` is a no-op on a value that filed no pending borrow).
			// Recording a subject here would enrol a borrow that does not exist.
			// A CURSOR is exempt for the String's reason and not the array's, even though its source is a
			// record: the element the body sees is whatever `current()` RETURNS, so its ownership is that
			// call's answer (`mintCallResult`) and not a borrow this loop took out of a slot it can name.
			if not src.isString and not methodSurfaceExists(src.cursorSurface) 'slotBorrowConcerns'
				src.elementBorrowSubject = self.borrowSubjectNameAt(sourceStart)
			end 'slotBorrowConcerns'

			// ⭐ **AN OWNED ARRAY TEMPORARY BECOMES AN OWNED BINDING OF THIS FRAME.** `for x in [1,2,3]`
			// / `for x in build()` produces a record the statement owns; left in `pendingTempDrops` it
			// would be dropped by the FIRST `return` inside the body (which drains the WHOLE list,
			// `emitHandoffExitDrops`) and then LEAK on the fall-through path, because the list is empty
			// by the time the exit block drains it. As a binding it is dropped exactly once on every
			// exit: the loop's own `end` (`emitScopeDrops` below), a `return`/`throw` out of the body
			// (the whole binding stack, with the list restored afterwards so this path still drops it),
			// and never by a `break` (whose floor is the BODY's height, above this one).
			//
			// ⚠ The gate is `pendingTempsContain`, NOT `valueIsOwnedHeap`, and the difference is a
			// DOUBLE FREE: `var arr = IntArray.create()` is an owned VALUE whose drop a binding already
			// owes, so adopting it here too emitted two `__managed_decref`s into the exit block (measured —
			// `for-in-iteration` segfaulted). The pending list is exactly the owned values NO binding
			// owns (see `pendingTempsContain`), which is the question being asked — and it is asked
			// inside `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime`, the ONE door this promotion has.
			self.giveTemporaryScopeLifetime(source, name: IterationSourceBindingName)
		end 'collectionSource'

		// Every OTHER temporary the source expression built — an interpolation handed to a factory call
		// — belongs to this statement and dies here, in the preheader, exactly as a `while` condition's
		// does. It is released BEFORE the loop rather than at the exit for the reason above: a `return`
		// inside the body would otherwise take the whole list with it.
		self.drainPendingTemps(sourceTempFloor, anchor: forTok)

		var header = self.newBlock(func, label: b"forhdr", line: forTok.line)

		// ⭐ **THE INDEX PHI IS A HEADER BLOCK-ARG, NOT A CARRIED BINDING**, and it is minted FIRST so
		// `header.blockArgs` reads [index, …carried]. It is DEFINED in the header, which dominates the
		// body, the step and the exit, so no other edge has to carry it: only the preheader's entry edge
		// and the step's back edge name it, and both are built right here. That is what lets
		// `break`/`continue` and `wireLoopExit` go on using `edgeArgsFor` unchanged.
		//
		// A RANGE's index IS the user's loop variable, so it takes the START bound's type (a ranged
		// alias stays one). An ARRAY's index is a compiler-owned counter and is a plain integer.
		let indexTag = self.tagOf(source) if src.isRange else ValueTypeTag.integer
		let indexNameId = self.valueNameId(source) if src.isRange else UnnamedTypeId
		let indexPhi = self.mintPhi(header, tag: indexTag, nameId: indexNameId)

		// The carried set is selected against `mutableVars` HERE, not at the extent scan above, because
		// that is where the walk it replaces used to make the selection. Nothing between the two pushes a
		// `var` (the source expression declares no binding, and the loop variable is bound in the body),
		// so the two points agree today — reading it here is what keeps them agreeing tomorrow.
		let phiVars = self.mintLoopHeaderPhis(header, carried: self.assignedBindingsIn(forIndex, endIndex: bodyEndIndex))

		// The preheader is `currentBlock`, not `block`: a short-circuit `and`/`or` inside a bound leaves
		// control in a fresh merge block, and the entry edge belongs on the block control is actually in.
		// ⭐ **THE HEADER'S CARRIED POSITION, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME KIND OF THING IN ALL FOUR FORMS.** A RANGE
		// carries the user's own loop variable, an ARRAY and a STRING a compiler-owned index starting at 0, and
		// a USER CURSOR the last `advance()`'s ERROR FLAG, seeded 0 (no throw yet) so its first test always
		// enters the body. The cursor takes the zero-literal arm the counted forms take, and that coincidence of
		// value is not a coincidence of meaning: 0 is "index 0" for one and "no error" for the other, and both
		// are what the preheader owes its header.
		var firstIndex = source
		if not src.isRange 'counterStartsAtZero'
			firstIndex = self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok))
		end 'counterStartsAtZero'
		// The carried bindings' PRE-loop values, captured here because the empty-`Iterable` edge below needs
		// them again at the join and the bindings themselves are rebound as the body is parsed.
		let entryCarried = self.edgeArgsFor(phiVars, preLoop: true)
		self.recordBranchEdge(self.currentBlock, targetId: header.id, argIds: self.loopHeaderArgs(firstIndex, carried: entryCarried))

		// ⭐⭐ **AN EMPTY `Iterable` SKIPS THE LOOP *AND ITS CURSOR'S DROP* (W3)**, which is why this is a BRANCH
		// and not a seeded flag. A throwing `createIterator()` reports emptiness by throwing
		// (`stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:132-134`), and on that path it returned NO cursor — so the exit block's
		// drop of the cursor must not run.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT SKIPS THE CURSOR'S DROP AND NOT "THE EXIT DROPS", AND THIS SENTENCE SAID THE SECOND UNTIL A
		// LEAK MEASURED THE DIFFERENCE.** The exit releases TWO batches and only one of them is conditional on
		// having got a cursor; the other is the SOURCE EXPRESSION's temporaries, built in this preheader above
		// the branch and live on both edges. `cursorMark` is what separates them and
		// `joinAfterEmptyIterable` is where this edge pays its half.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED, on the first cut, which seeded the header's error flag with the factory's instead:**
		// the loop correctly ran zero trips and then `__destruct_<Cursor>` was called on the throw path's
		// leftover return register — **SIGSEGV**, on `for v in <an empty Iterable>`. Seeding gets the CONTROL
		// FLOW right and says nothing about OWNERSHIP, and shv2's ownership is static: one owner, dropped at
		// one point, with no conditional-drop form to express "only if it was created". So the path that owns
		// nothing must not reach the point that drops.
		var emptyExit = EmptyIterableExit.none
		match src.cursorEntrySeed 'entry'
			atTheFirstElement then self.emitBranch(self.currentBlock, targetId: header.id)
			fromFactory(flag) then emptyExit = EmptyIterableExit.entered(self.emitEmptyIterableEntry(func, flag: flag, header: header, forTok: forTok))
		end 'entry'

		// The loop test, in the header. For a range it is the user's own comparison against the upper
		// bound and goes through `emitCompare`, so `for i in 1 to "x"` is the ordinary comparison type
		// error; for an array it is `idx < __managed_count(arr)`, both operands compiler-minted integers.
		//
		self.currentBlock = header
		var bound = src.limit
		var testOp = MaxonCmpOp.le if src.rangeInclusive else MaxonCmpOp.lt
		// ⭐ **A USER CURSOR'S TEST IS `flag == 0`** — "the last `advance()` did not throw" — and it is the one
		// form that needs no length read, because the value the header carries already IS the end condition.
		// The preheader seeds it 0 (see `firstIndex` above), which is what makes the FIRST trip unconditional —
		// the do-while this form's header argues for.
		if methodSurfaceExists(src.cursorSurface) 'cursorStillPositioned'
			bound = self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok))
			testOp = MaxonCmpOp.eq
		end 'cursorStillPositioned'
		if not src.isRange and not methodSurfaceExists(src.cursorSurface) 'lengthReadLive'
			// ⭐ **THE LENGTH IS RE-READ LIVE IN THE HEADER for a String too** — `length@8` off the record,
			// the same discipline the array form's live `__managed_count` follows and for the same reason: the
			// header comparison IS the bounds check that licenses the unchecked read below it.
			//
			// ⚠ The String's index is a BYTE position, not a cluster ordinal, so the header asks "is there
			// a byte left?" — which is exactly the reference's `countGraphemes` loop condition
			// (`while pos < len`). A cluster ordinal would need the count, which costs a whole extra scan.
			bound = self.emitLoadStringField(source, offset: StringLengthOffset, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok)) if src.isString else self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCountName, args: singletonArgs(source), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok))
		end 'lengthReadLive'
		let cond = try self.emitCompare(indexPhi, rhs: bound, op: testOp, opTok: self.tokenAt(src.testAnchorIndex))

		let loopLabelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()

		var body = self.newBlock(func, label: loopLabelTok.value, line: loopLabelTok.line)
		var step = self.newBlock(func, label: b"forstep", line: loopLabelTok.line)
		var exit = self.newBlock(func, label: b"forexit", line: forTok.line)
		self.emitCondBranch(header, cond: cond, thenId: body.id, elseId: exit.id, ifTok: forTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-TRIP FRAME, AND IT EXISTS FOR THE STRING FORM ALONE.** A range's loop variable is the
		// header phi and an array's is a BORROW the array still owns — neither owns anything, so for those
		// two this frame opens and closes empty and emits not one op. A STRING's element is different in
		// kind: it is MINTED fresh every trip (`__char_at` allocates a record), so exactly one owner exists
		// and this loop is it, once per iteration.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS A FRAME AND NOT A LIST OF DROP SITES, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.** Four paths leave a
		// trip — the body's fall-through, `continue`, `break`, and a `return`/`throw` out of the body — and
		// every one of them already drains owned bindings down to a MARK. Enrolling the element as an
		// ordinary owned binding of its own frame therefore makes all four correct by construction, where a
		// per-site drop would be a hand-maintained door list of exactly the kind that has already been
		// wrong twice in this rung's own machinery (see the write-door debt in PLAN.md).
		//
		// The ORDER of the three lines below is load-bearing and each is a different exit path:
		//   • the frame opens BEFORE the element is minted, so `closeBlock` below pops it and the loop's
		//     own `end` (which drains `forMark`) does NOT drop a value that is dead by then — and could
		//     not name it anyway, since the element is defined in the body and does not dominate the exit;
		//   • `ownedFloor` is captured from THIS mark rather than after the enrolment, so `break`/`continue`
		//     (`dropLoopScopedOwned`) drain THROUGH the element rather than stopping above it;
		//   • the fall-through drain below uses the same mark, which is the one path with no drop of its own.
		let tripMark = self.openBlock()

		// `ownedFloor` is the per-trip frame's height — so a `break`/`continue` releases the body's
		// declarations AND the trip's minted element, and leaves the iteration source (enrolled below this
		// mark) for the exit.
		self.loops.push(LoopContext.create(loopLabelTok.value, continueTargetId: step.id, exitId: exit.id, phiVars: phiVars, ownedFloor: tripMark.ownedCount))

		// The loop variable: the index itself for a range, a BORROWED element read for an array, a MINTED
		// grapheme cluster for a String. `_` is the discard (`DiscardBindingName`) and binds nothing at all
		// — declaring it would make an ordinary `_ = f()` discard statement inside the body a write to a
		// `let`.
		self.currentBlock = body
		var loopValue = indexPhi
		var graphemeEnd = indexPhi
		// ⭐ **A CHARACTER RANGE MINTS ITS ELEMENT FROM THE COUNTER**, where a String mints it from a byte
		// range — two producers of the SAME owned per-trip Character, which is why the ownership below is
		// asked once for both rather than beside each. See `GraphemeRuntime.buildCharFromCodepoint`.
		if src.isCharacterRange 'mintFromCodepoint'
			loopValue = self.emitRuntimeCall(CharFromCodepointName, args: singletonArgs(indexPhi), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.character, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(patternTok))
		end 'mintFromCodepoint'
		if src.isString 'readCluster'
			graphemeEnd = self.emitRuntimeCall(CorpusFindGraphemeEnd, args: pairArgs(source, second: indexPhi), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(patternTok), argLabels: corpusFindGraphemeEndLabels())
			loopValue = self.emitRuntimeCall(CharAtName, args: tripleArgs(source, second: indexPhi, third: graphemeEnd), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.character, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(patternTok))
		end 'readCluster'
		if src.isString or src.isCharacterRange 'ownsTheTripsCharacter'
			// ⭐⭐ **THE OWNERSHIP RULING: A MINTED CHARACTER IS OWNED, WHERE AN ARRAY'S ELEMENT IS BORROWED — and
			// the difference is not a preference, it is a fact about where the value lives.** Slice A could
			// borrow because the ARRAY KEEPS the element and its own `__managed_decref` walk destroys it, so
			// there was an owner to borrow from. A String's grapheme clusters are stored NOWHERE (the record
			// holds bytes, and a Character is a record) and a character RANGE stores nothing at all — it has
			// only a counter — so in both the value the body sees did not exist until `__char_at` /
			// `__char_from_cp` built it. A minted value has exactly one owner and it is this trip.
			//
			// Enrolled even when the name is `_`, and that is not an oversight: the discard binds no NAME
			// but the record was still allocated, so the drop is owed regardless of whether anything could
			// read it. (`for _ in s` is exactly `grapheme-clusters/grapheme-boundary-combining-iteration`,
			// and `for _ in 'a' to 'e'` is `ranges/ranges.character-range` — both count without looking at one,
			// and both would leak every character they counted.)
			//
			// ⚠ **THE PROVENANCE BIT AND THE BINDING ARE BOTH REQUIRED, AND THE BINDING ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH.**
			// `ownedBindings` is what DROPS the value; `markValueOwnsHeap` is what makes every other owner-
			// aware rule recognize it as owned — above all `reassignBindingValue`, which asks
			// `valueIsOwnedHeap` to decide MOVE-vs-COPY. Without the bit, `found = c` promoted a COPY of the
			// cluster (a second `__mm_alloc` per trip) and left the original with no owner, which the leak
			// gate caught as exit 101. `markValueOwnsHeap` rather than `trackOwnedTemp` for the reason a
			// consumed parameter uses it: this value already drops through `ownedBindings`, and enrolling it
			// in `pendingTempDrops` as well would drop it a SECOND time at the statement's end.
			//
			// A DESTRUCTURING header owns the cluster under the loop's own element name, because no name the
			// source wrote holds the whole element. That program is refused a few lines below anyway (a
			// Character is not a tuple), and the enrolment runs first — so the label has to be a name, and
			// the honest one is the one the element already has where nothing spells it.
			// `sole` — the record did not exist until `__char_at`/`__char_from_cp` built it one line up, which is
			// the paragraph above's whole argument ("a minted value has exactly one owner and it is this trip").
			self.markValueOwnsHeap(loopValue, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
			self.ownedBindings.push(VarInfo.createValueBinding(self.loopVariableOwnerName(nameToks), varType: self.typeOf(loopValue), mutable: false, boundValue: loopValue))
		end 'ownsTheTripsCharacter'
		// ⭐ **THE INDEXED-CONTAINER FORM READS ITS ELEMENT THROUGH THE SHARED ACCESSOR**: the carried header
		// value is an ordinal into the record's buffer, the read hands back a BORROW the container still owns,
		// it is the UNCHECKED read the header's own test licenses, and it mints the E3070 borrow against
		// `elementBorrowSubject` — ownership, type and borrow are `emitContainerElementAccessor`'s one answer.
		//
		// ⚠ **TWO FORMS SHARED THIS DOOR UNTIL W153**, the second being a `List`'s node cursor, which read
		// `__list_node_value` off the carried NODE where an array reads `__managed_get_unchecked` off an
		// ordinal. A corpus `List` is a declared `Iterable` now and reads its element through `current()` like
		// every other cursor.
		if not src.isRange and not src.isString and not methodSurfaceExists(src.cursorSurface) 'readElement'
			loopValue = self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ManagedGetUncheckedName, container: source, args: pairArgs(source, second: indexPhi), giid: self.valueNameId(source), reference: ContainerElementReference.borrowed, borrowSubjectName: src.elementBorrowSubject, range: SourceRange.ofToken(patternTok))
		end 'readElement'
		// ⭐⭐ **A CURSOR'S ELEMENT IS WHATEVER `current()` RETURNS, AND ITS OWNERSHIP IS THAT CALL'S ANSWER —
		// NOT A THIRD RULE HERE.** The other four forms each needed one: an array/list element is a BORROW the
		// container keeps, a String's cluster is MINTED and owned by the trip. This one is an ordinary user
		// call, so `emitCall` has already decided — a scalar owns nothing, a managed return is tracked as
		// an owned pending temporary. All this arm adds is the LIFETIME: a pending temp is dropped at the
		// STATEMENT's tail, which for a value the body may hold across the whole trip is too early on one path
		// and (once a `return` inside the body drains the list) a leak on another. `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime`
		// re-scopes it to the per-trip frame, where all four trip exits already drop to a mark — and it is a
		// silent no-op on the scalar case, which is why the call is unconditional rather than gated on a
		// managed-return test that would be a second opinion about what the call minted.
		if methodSurfaceExists(src.cursorSurface) 'readCursorElement'
			loopValue = try self.emitReceiverProtocolCall(src.cursorSurface, methodName: CurrentMethodName, anchor: patternTok, resultUsed: true)
			self.giveTemporaryScopeLifetime(loopValue, name: IterationElementBindingName)
		end 'readCursorElement'

		// ⭐⭐ **ONE PATTERN POSITION IS BOUND TO ONE VALUE, AND A DESTRUCTURING HEADER SIMPLY HAS MORE OF
		// THEM.** A single name takes the element WHOLE; `for (key, value) in m` takes one tuple FIELD each,
		// which is the desugaring `parseDestructuringBinding` already writes for `let (x, y) = t` (v1
		// `parseTupleDestructureVarDecl`, bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:10165-10305`) — a field load per name, and
		// nothing else.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND IT NEEDS NO HIDDEN BINDING, WHERE `let (x, y) = t` DOES.** That one binds the box to an
		// unspellable name because the RHS is a freshly built tuple SOME binding has to drop. A loop element is
		// a BORROW the container still owns (`emitContainerElementAccessor`, `borrowed`) — its `__managed_decref`
		// walk destroys every pair — so there is no ownership obligation here to give a name to.
		//
		// ⚠ E3070 records no borrow for the destructured names, and that is the LOCK's doing rather than an
		// omission: what could dangle a field load is a write that frees the pair box, and `lockIterationSource`
		// has already made every write to the source binding E2013 for the loop's whole extent. The element's
		// own pending borrow is left unclaimed and cut at the next statement boundary
		// (`dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows`), exactly as the `for _ in …` discard leaves it.
		if self.patternNamesOneWholeElement(nameToks) 'bindTheWholeElement'
			try self.declareLoopVariable(func, nameTok: self.soleLoopVariableName(nameToks), value: loopValue, bodyEndIndex: bodyEndIndex)
		end 'bindTheWholeElement' else 'bindEachDestructuredName'
			try self.declareDestructuredLoopVariables(func, nameToks: nameToks, loopValue: loopValue, patternTok: patternTok, forTok: forTok, bodyEndIndex: bodyEndIndex)
		end 'bindEachDestructuredName'

		var bodyEnd = try self.parseBlockBody(func, block: body, label: loopLabelTok.value)

		// Guard the token scan against the parse it predicted — see `assertScanAligned`.
		self.assertScanAligned("parseForStatement", predictedEnd: bodyEndIndex)

		let ctx = try self.loops.pop() otherwise panic("parseForStatement: loop stack empty after body of '{String.from(loopLabelTok.value)}'")
		self.releaseIterationSource(src.sourceLock)

		// The step block, read off the context rather than spelled again — see `parseWhileStatement`'s
		// fall-through for why the two loop parsers share that derivation.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE FALL-THROUGH IS THE ONE TRIP EXIT WITH NO DROP OF ITS OWN**, so it takes the per-trip
		// frame's drain here — `break`/`continue` already emitted theirs down to `ctx.ownedFloor` (which IS
		// this mark), and a `return`/`throw` drained the whole binding stack. It is skipped on a TERMINATED
		// `bodyEnd` for `parseBlockBody`'s reason: one of those three has already run, and dropping again
		// would double-free.
		if not bodyEnd.isTerminated() 'fallsToStep'
			self.currentBlock = bodyEnd
			self.emitScopeDrops(bodyEnd, floor: tripMark.ownedCount, anchor: forTok)
			self.recordBranchEdge(bodyEnd, targetId: ctx.continueTargetId, argIds: self.edgeArgsFor(phiVars, preLoop: false))
			self.emitBranch(bodyEnd, targetId: ctx.continueTargetId)
		end 'fallsToStep'

		// The per-trip frame closes BEFORE the step/exit wiring: its element is dead past the body, and
		// leaving it enrolled would let the exit block's own drain (`forMark`) name a value that the exit
		// does not dominate.
		self.closeBlock(tripMark)

		// ⭐ **THE STEP BLOCK IS A MERGE, so it carries phis of its own.** Its predecessors are the
		// body's fall-through AND every `continue`, and a carried `var` assigned before a `continue`
		// holds a different value on each — the same shape the exit has for `break` (`wireLoopExit`).
		// The INDEX needs none: it is defined in the header, which dominates the step.
		self.currentBlock = step
		for k in 0 upto ctx.phiVars.count() 'mkStepPhi'
			var pv = try ctx.phiVars.get(k) otherwise panic("parseForStatement: phiVars.get OOB at {k}")
			pv.binding.boundValue = self.mintPhiForBinding(step, binding: pv.binding)
		end 'mkStepPhi'
		// ⭐ **WHAT ONE STEP IS.** A range and an array advance by ONE (an ordinal); a String advances to the
		// END OF THE CLUSTER the body just saw, because its index is a BYTE position and a cluster is
		// 1..18 bytes wide. `graphemeEnd` is computed in the body's first block, which dominates the step
		// (every predecessor of the step — the fall-through and every `continue` — is inside the body), so
		// it is read straight rather than carried on a phi. Computing it again here would run the whole
		// UAX #29 scan a second time per trip.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **A USER CURSOR ADVANCES BY CALLING `advance()`, AND ITS ERROR FLAG *IS* THE NEXT CARRIED VALUE.**
		// The step is exactly where that call belongs: every path that continues the loop — the body's
		// fall-through and every `continue` — lands here, so the cursor moves exactly once per trip however the
		// body was left, which is the same argument the step block itself rests on. A throw therefore ends the
		// loop NORMALLY (the header's next test fails) rather than escaping it, so `has()` need not declare
		// `throws` to iterate — the contract `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon` states and both references implement.
		var nextIndex = graphemeEnd
		if methodSurfaceExists(src.cursorSurface) 'cursorAdvanceStep'
			nextIndex = try self.emitCursorAdvance(func, cursorSurface: src.cursorSurface, anchor: forTok)
		end 'cursorAdvanceStep' else 'builtinSourceStep'
			if not src.isString 'ordinalStep'
				let one = self.emitLiteral(1, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok))
				nextIndex = self.emitMachineWordAdd(indexPhi, rhs: one, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok))
			end 'ordinalStep'
		end 'builtinSourceStep'
		self.recordBranchEdge(step, targetId: header.id, argIds: self.loopHeaderArgs(nextIndex, carried: self.edgeArgsFor(phiVars, preLoop: false)))
		self.emitBranch(step, targetId: header.id)

		// The header carries the two-way branch, so it is the exit's false-edge source. Run AFTER the
		// step wiring: `wireLoopExit` settles each carried binding's POST-loop value, and the step loop
		// above sets those same fields to its own phis on the way through.
		self.wireLoopExit(header, exit: exit, ctx: ctx)

		// The loop variable and the iteration source's binding both leave scope here — the drops for the
		// latter land in the exit block, which every non-terminating path through the loop reaches.
		//
		// ⭐ TWO EMISSIONS, AND THEY GO TO TWO DIFFERENT BLOCKS. The CURSOR is the loop's own and dies in the
		// exit, which only a path that got one can reach; the SOURCE EXPRESSION's temporaries die at the
		// statement's single CONTINUATION, which is that same exit when the loop is the only way out and the
		// JOIN when an empty `Iterable` supplies a second entry edge. Splitting them is the whole point —
		// see `cursorMark` for the leak one shared frame cost.
		//
		// The cursor's frame is closed BETWEEN the two so the second emission cannot name it again, and the
		// decrefs come out in the order one call over the shared list produced (that call walked in reverse
		// and the cursor was its top entry). A loop whose source enrolled nothing emits nothing here, which
		// is every loop in the corpus that does not read a field out of a call result.
		self.currentBlock = exit
		self.emitScopeDrops(exit, floor: cursorMark.ownedCount, anchor: forTok)
		self.closeBlock(cursorMark)

		// The empty-`Iterable` path rejoins at a JOIN, PAST the cursor's drop — see `emitEmptyIterableEntry`.
		// EACH ARM LEAVES THE PARSER IN THE STATEMENT'S CONTINUATION and releases the source expression's
		// temporaries INTO it, which is why the two are one decision rather than a block computed here and a
		// drop emitted after: the block that drops is the block control is in.
		match emptyExit 'emptied'
			none then self.emitScopeDrops(exit, floor: forMark.ownedCount, anchor: forTok)
			entered(empty) then self.joinAfterEmptyIterable(func, empty: empty, exit: exit, entryCarried: entryCarried, phiVars: phiVars, sourceFloor: forMark.ownedCount, forTok: forTok)
		end 'emptied'

		self.closeBlock(forMark)
		return self.currentBlock
	end 'parseForStatement'

	// ⭐ **THE PREHEADER'S "DID THE FACTORY GIVE US A CURSOR?" BRANCH (W3)** — the taken edge is the ordinary
	// loop entry recorded by the caller, the other lands in a fresh block that carries NOTHING and rejoins past
	// the loop's exit drops (`joinAfterEmptyIterable`). The test is `flag == 0`, the SAME sense the header's own
	// exit test uses on `advance()`'s flag, and it is emitted through the same `emitCompareAgainstLiteral` an
	// enum `match` arm uses — so "did it throw?" is one comparison in this file, not two.
	function emitEmptyIterableEntry(func IrFunction, flag ValueId, header IrBlock, forTok Token) returns IrBlock
		let empty = self.newBlock(func, label: b"forempty", line: forTok.line)
		let gotCursor = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(flag, literal: 0, op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, opTok: forTok)
		// The empty block carries no binding: every carried value it needs is supplied on ITS edge into the
		// join, straight from the pre-loop set, because nothing between here and there can have assigned one.
		self.recordBranchEdge(self.currentBlock, targetId: empty.id, argIds: ValueIdArray.create())
		self.emitCondBranch(self.currentBlock, cond: gotCursor, thenId: header.id, elseId: empty.id, ifTok: forTok)
		return empty
	end 'emitEmptyIterableEntry'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE MERGE THE SECOND ENTRY EDGE FORCES (W3).** With an empty-`Iterable` path the loop statement has
	// TWO successors to reconcile — the exit, where the carried bindings hold whatever the body last assigned,
	// and the empty block, where they still hold their pre-loop values — so the statement's continuation is a
	// JOIN with a phi per carried binding. It is `wireLoopExit`'s shape (`mintPhiForBinding` +
	// `pushMergedEdgeValue`, in that pairing order) applied one block later, and it exists for the same reason:
	// a `var` assigned inside the loop must read correctly after it on BOTH paths.
	//
	// The pre-loop args are the caller's own entry-edge args, reused rather than re-derived: `edgeArgsFor`
	// answers off `pv.entryValue`, and asking it twice would be one fact computed twice.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE THE SOURCE EXPRESSION'S TEMPORARIES COME TO DIE, because the join is the ONE block
	// both entry edges reach.** The empty edge got no cursor, so it must not reach a `__destruct_<Cursor>` —
	// but everything the source expression itself enrolled was built in the preheader, ABOVE the branch, and
	// is live on this edge exactly as it is on the loop's. `sourceFloor` is the `for` statement's own owned
	// mark, and the caller has already closed the cursor's frame, so this releases precisely the outer batch.
	//
	// It hands nothing back: the join IS `currentBlock` when it returns, which is what the caller's other arm
	// leaves too, so the statement's continuation is read from one place.
	function joinAfterEmptyIterable(func IrFunction, empty IrBlock, exit IrBlock, entryCarried ValueIdArray, phiVars LoopPhiVarArray, sourceFloor BindingMark, forTok Token)
		var join = self.newBlock(func, label: b"forjoin", line: forTok.line)

		// Collected BEFORE the phis are minted, because minting rebinds each binding to its phi and the exit
		// edge must carry what the binding held on the way OUT of the loop.
		var loopVals = ValueIdArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto phiVars.count() 'exitEdge'
			var pv = try phiVars.get(k) otherwise panic("joinAfterEmptyIterable: phiVars.get OOB at {k}")
			self.pushMergedEdgeValue(loopVals, binding: pv.binding, value: pv.binding.boundValue)
		end 'exitEdge'

		for k in 0 upto phiVars.count() 'mkJoinPhi'
			var pv = try phiVars.get(k) otherwise panic("joinAfterEmptyIterable: phiVars.get OOB at {k}")
			pv.binding.boundValue = self.mintPhiForBinding(join, binding: pv.binding)
		end 'mkJoinPhi'

		self.recordBranchEdge(exit, targetId: join.id, argIds: loopVals)
		self.emitBranch(exit, targetId: join.id)
		self.recordBranchEdge(empty, targetId: join.id, argIds: entryCarried)
		self.emitBranch(empty, targetId: join.id)

		self.emitScopeDrops(join, floor: sourceFloor, anchor: forTok)
		self.currentBlock = join
	end 'joinAfterEmptyIterable'

	// ⭐ **THE `for` HEADER'S LOOP-VARIABLE PATTERN, as the LIST OF NAMES it always was.** `for (key, value) in`
	// is `parseDestructuringNames`' pattern — the same one `let (x, y) = …` reads, so `_` discards a position
	// and a one-name pattern is refused by one sentence rather than two — and a bare `for item in` is that
	// pattern with a single position and no parens, which is why it comes back as a list too. Everything
	// downstream then asks its questions per POSITION and needs to know nothing about the spelling.
	function parseLoopVariablePattern(forTok Token) returns TokenArray throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'destructuringPattern'
			return try self.parseDestructuringNames(forTok)
		end 'destructuringPattern'

		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
		var names = TokenArray.create()
		names.push(nameTok)
		return names
	end 'parseLoopVariablePattern'

	// ⭐ **DOES THE HEADER'S PATTERN NAME THE ELEMENT WHOLE?** — one position, which is what a bare
	// `for item in` comes back as. Its two readers FORK on it and used to ask it in two spellings (`== 1` at
	// the binding, `!= 1` at the owner name below), which is one fact written down twice: a pattern rule that
	// changed at one would have gone on binding at the other. It is also the PROOF the `get(0)` beneath each
	// answer rests on, which is why the accessor sits beside it rather than at either site.
	function patternNamesOneWholeElement(nameToks TokenArray) returns bool
		return nameToks.count() == 1
	end 'patternNamesOneWholeElement'

	// The sole name of such a pattern. Both readers guard it with the predicate above, so the panic names ONE
	// impossible state rather than two sites each describing it in their own words.
	function soleLoopVariableName(nameToks TokenArray) returns Token
		return try nameToks.get(0) otherwise panic("soleLoopVariableName: `patternNamesOneWholeElement` answered true for a pattern holding no name at 0")
	end 'soleLoopVariableName'

	// The name the OWNED loop element is enrolled under when the loop mints one (a String's grapheme cluster).
	// A single-name header owns it under the name the author wrote; a destructuring header has no name for the
	// whole element, so it takes the one the compiler already uses where nothing spells it.
	function loopVariableOwnerName(nameToks TokenArray) returns ByteArray
		if not self.patternNamesOneWholeElement(nameToks) 'destructuredElementHasNoName'
			return IterationElementBindingName
		end 'destructuredElementHasNoName'

		return self.soleLoopVariableName(nameToks).value
	end 'loopVariableOwnerName'

	// ⭐ **DECLARE ONE POSITION OF A `for` HEADER'S PATTERN.** `_` is the discard (`DiscardBindingName`) and
	// binds nothing at all — declaring it would make an ordinary `_ = f()` discard statement inside the body a
	// write to a `let`. Everything else is declared in the per-trip frame, so it is in scope for the whole body
	// and gone at the loop's `end`; immutable, which `for-iterator-immutable` pins as E2013 for a write.
	//
	// ⭐ **E3012 CANDIDATE: A DECLARED LOOP VARIABLE MUST BE MENTIONED** (`reportUnusedBindings`). Recorded
	// past the discard guard, so `for _ in` — and a destructured `for (_, v) in` — is exempt by the same fact
	// that makes the refusal fair: `_` binds no name, and it is already the legal spelling in BOTH compilers
	// for a loop that wants the iteration and not that part of the element.
	//
	// ⭐ E3070: what this name binds may be a BORROW, and it is live for the BODY'S EXTENT rather than to the
	// variable's last use — see `attachPendingBorrows`'s `liveUntil`. A STRING's element is OWNED rather than
	// borrowed, so there is no borrow to attach and the call is a no-op on it (`attachPendingBorrows` reads the
	// pending-borrow list the array accessor fills, and a minted value files none).
	function declareLoopVariable(func IrFunction, nameTok Token, value ValueId, bodyEndIndex TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		if nameTok.value.equals(DiscardBindingName) 'discardsThisPosition'
			return
		end 'discardsThisPosition'

		let loopBinding = try self.scope.declareValueBinding(nameTok.value, varType: self.typeOf(value), mutable: false, value: value) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(nameTok.value), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		self.unusedCandidates.push(UnusedBindingCandidate.create(loopBinding, nameRange: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok), kind: UnusedBindingKind.loopVariable))
		self.attachPendingBorrows(func, borrower: loopBinding, boundValue: value, liveUntil: bodyEndIndex)
	end 'declareLoopVariable'

	// ⭐ **ONE ZERO-ARGUMENT METHOD CALL ON A RECEIVER VALUE — the whole of the `for … in` protocol surface.**
	// `<T>.createIterator(source)`, `<T>.current(source)` and `<T>.advance(source)` are the same call three
	// times over: an ordinary direct call on the source as receiver, differing only in the method name and in
	// whether the result is used. They share the argument construction rather than each spelling it, because a
	// second copy is how they would come to disagree about the receiver's position — the one thing a direct
	// method call gets from its caller and cannot re-derive.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FACTORY USED TO BE A SECOND FUNCTION, ON THE ARGUMENT THAT IT "TAKES ITS RECEIVER FROM THE
	// ITERABLE"** — a difference in what the CALLER does with the result, never in what is emitted. Generic substitution made
	// that visible by giving both bodies two more obligations they must discharge identically (below), which is
	// exactly the moment two copies of one call become two places for one rule to rot.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE RECEIVER'S CONCRETE INSTANCE IS THREADED, AND THE RESULT IS READ THROUGH IT** — the same
	// two things `dispatchMethodOnBase` does for a written `b.next()`, and for the same reasons. Without the
	// first, a protocol method whose parameter FEEDS an opaque field cannot tell whether its argument owns
	// managed heap for this instance; without the second, `Box.createIterator() returns BoxIter` hands back the
	// SHARED body's `BoxIter with Box.T` and every later call on that cursor is typed against an opaque `T`
	// the call site had already fixed. A plain-struct receiver supplies `none` and substitutes nothing, so
	// every non-generic iterable emits exactly what it emitted before.
	//
	// `CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier` because the HEAD is the surface's declaring type — resolved from the
	// receiver, not read from this call site — while the member is the protocol's. The method half is joined
	// the way `declaredMethodName` already joined it when the signature was published; what the mint records
	// is that no token here spells the type, so a moved stdlib declaration's protocol call is the compiler's
	// own name and not an author reaching into the reserved space (F31).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL SOURCE TAKES THE OTHER FORK, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT OP RATHER THAN A DIFFERENT
	// NAME.** There is no callee to name: the conformer is unknown in this body, so the protocol call is a
	// `witnessDispatch` through the table the value carries — the same op, through the same door, that a
	// written `s.current()` on that value emits. Everything the loop then does to the result is shared:
	// `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` already routes a `witnessDispatch` to `commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite`, so
	// `emitCursorAdvance` needs no arm of its own for the throwing half.
	function emitReceiverProtocolCall(surface MethodSurface, methodName ByteArray, anchor Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		match surface 'how'
			none then panic("emitReceiverProtocolCall: a `{String.from(methodName)}` protocol call on a value with no method surface — every caller emits only after `surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol` or `surfaceIterableFactory` answered off this same surface")
			witnessDispatched(receiver) then return try self.emitWitnessProtocolCall(receiver, methodName: methodName, anchor: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
			declared(declaringType, receiver) then return try self.emitDeclaredProtocolCall(declaringType, methodName: methodName, source: receiver, anchor: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'how'
	end 'emitReceiverProtocolCall'

	// The DIRECT-CALL fork of the router above — the body this function had before the second receiver kind
	// arrived, unchanged, and a fork of its own so the `declared` arm binds both of its payloads in the one
	// statement a `match` arm is.
	function emitDeclaredProtocolCall(typeName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray, source ValueId, anchor Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `var` on all three: `emitCall` supplies omitted defaults by PUSHING onto the argument columns
		// (`fillOmittedArgDefaults`), so they are its output as well as its input. No protocol method declares
		// a parameter, so nothing is ever appended — but the mutability is the signature's, not the callee's,
		// and E3019 asks about the signature.
		var args = singletonArgs(source)
		var argLabels = emptyArgLabels(args)
		var argRanges = uniformArgRanges(args, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		let result = try self.emitCall(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: methodName), anchor: anchor, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: self.receiverConstructorInstance(source), mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier)
		self.retypeInstanceMethodResult(result, receiver: source)
		return result
	end 'emitDeclaredProtocolCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE EXISTENTIAL FORK OF THE CALL ABOVE: one zero-argument requirement, dispatched through the
	// table the receiver carries.** Every step is the one `dispatchWitnessMethod` takes for a written
	// `s.current()` — the same target resolution, the same result mint, the same single emit door — with the
	// argument list built rather than parsed, because the loop has no source text to read one from.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO `retypeInstanceMethodResult`, and that is not an omission.** That retype exists to reopen a
	// SHARED generic body's opaque return against the receiver's concrete instance; an interface's declared
	// return type is already concrete, and the one that is not — `Self` — is answered by the receiver inside
	// `mintWitnessDispatchResult` rather than substituted afterwards. `mintWitnessDispatchResult`'s own header
	// states the same thing for the written form.
	function emitWitnessProtocolCall(receiver ValueId, methodName ByteArray, anchor Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		match try self.witnessProtocolTarget(receiver, methodName: methodName, anchor: anchor) 'resolved'
			notRequired then panic("emitWitnessProtocolCall: what this receiver dispatches through requires no zero-argument `{String.from(methodName)}` — the admission test and this emit both go through `witnessProtocolTarget`, so a miss here means one of them was not asked")
			required(requirement) then return try self.emitProtocolWitnessDispatch(requirement, receiver: receiver, anchor: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'resolved'
	end 'emitWitnessProtocolCall'

	// The emit half of the fork above, taking the already-resolved requirement. `singletonArgs` is
	// `withWitnessReceiver`'s answer for a call with no user arguments — the receiver alone at `args[0]`,
	// which is the convention `emitWitnessDispatch` and every reader below it index by.
	function emitProtocolWitnessDispatch(requirement WitnessDispatchTarget, receiver ValueId, anchor Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let result = try self.mintWitnessDispatchResult(requirement, receiver: receiver, methodTok: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		self.emitWitnessDispatch(result, args: singletonArgs(receiver), target: requirement, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor), siteTok: anchor)
		return result
	end 'emitProtocolWitnessDispatch'

	// ⭐⭐ **CALL `advance()` AND HAND BACK ITS ERROR FLAG** — the value a cursor loop's header carries, and
	// the whole of its termination condition.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT GOES THROUGH `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`, THE SAME REWRITE A SOURCE-WRITTEN `try` USES**, rather
	// than emitting a `tryCall` op directly: that function is where "a call becomes one that also defines an
	// error flag" is decided, and a second site building the op itself would be a second answer to which
	// results a `tryCall` defines and in what order. What this does NOT reuse is `desugarTry`, and that is the
	// whole point of the form — the desugar builds a two-way split plus a handler around ONE statement, where
	// this flag's split is the LOOP HEADER, one block and a full trip away.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE THROWN VALUE IS DISCARDED, AND THAT IS SOUND ONLY BECAUSE OF WHAT MAY BE THROWN.** The loop
	// binds no error, so nothing releases one; `surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol` is what makes the thrown type
	// `IterationError` — compiler-owned and payload-free, hence a scalar tag with nothing to release. That
	// admission test and this discard are one decision, and the reason it is stated in both places, and it
	// covers BOTH receiver kinds because both read the clause through `throwsClauseIsIterationError`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REWRITE SERVES AN EXISTENTIAL UNCHANGED.** `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` already routes a
	// `witnessDispatch` in the last opRef slot to `commitWitnessTryDispatchRewrite`, so the existential's
	// `advance()` becomes a dual-register `witnessTryDispatch` by exactly the route a struct's becomes a
	// `tryCall` — the reason this function needed no arm of its own for the second receiver kind.
	function emitCursorAdvance(func IrFunction, cursorSurface MethodSurface, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The floor `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` measures "the target's own op" against — see `parseTry`, which
		// captures it at the same point and for the same reason.
		let targetOpFloor = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		// ⚠ **THE SECOND SITE THAT CLAIMS A CALL FOR ITS OWN REWRITE, SO THE SECOND THAT MUST SUPPRESS THE
		// BLOCK-FORM ROUTER** — `parseTryCallTarget` is the other, and the rule is one sentence for both:
		// whoever is going to turn this `call` into a `tryCall` itself must stop the router turning it into a
		// DIFFERENT one first. A `for x in <cursor>` loop inside a `try 'l' … end` body is the reaching
		// program. It does not bite TODAY only because `runtimeThrowsClause` has no cursor arm, so the router
		// declines on the clause — an accident of which families are typed, not a property of this code, and
		// the day that arm is added the advance would branch to the block's handler and this rewrite would
		// then meet a `tryCall` it must reject. Suppressed here, that cannot happen.
		let savedRoutingSuppressed = self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = true
		_ = try self.emitReceiverProtocolCall(cursorSurface, methodName: AdvanceMethodName, anchor: anchor, resultUsed: false)
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = savedRoutingSuppressed
		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		_ = try self.rewriteLastCallToTryCall(func, flag: flag, tryTok: anchor, targetOpFloor: targetOpFloor)
		return flag
	end 'emitCursorAdvance'

	// The block-arg list an edge into a `for` HEADER carries: the index value FIRST (the header's own
	// block-arg 0 — see `parseForStatement`), then one slot per carried binding. Both edges that name
	// the header go through here, so the index can never end up in a different slot on one of them.
	function loopHeaderArgs(indexValue ValueId, carried ValueIdArray) returns ValueIdArray
		var vals = ValueIdArray.create()
		vals.push(indexValue)
		for v in carried 'each'
			vals.push(v)
		end 'each'
		return vals
	end 'loopHeaderArgs'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN `Iterable` SOURCE IS REPLACED BY THE CURSOR ITS `createIterator()` RETURNS (W3)** — the SECOND
	// rewrite `parseForStatement`'s collection fork performs (the `Map`-entries rewrite beside it went with the
	// synthesized hash table at W105): what comes back is a cursor, and the cursor form already knows
	// everything about walking one.
	// It is what makes `for item in self` inside `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable` mean what that
	// file's own documentation says it means (*"types that can be iterated over (used by for-in loops)"*), and
	// it is how `stdlib/Range.maxon` and `stdlib/helpers/string/views.maxon` become iterable.
	//
	// ⭐ **A SOURCE THAT IS ALREADY A CURSOR IS LEFT ALONE, AND THAT PRECEDENCE IS THE PROTOCOL'S.** An iterator
	// may itself declare `createIterator` (the identity passthrough both references implement), and
	// `for x in iter` over a HALF-CONSUMED iterator must resume from where it stands rather than restarting —
	// so the cursor protocol is tested first and wins.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ENTRY SEED IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE ANSWER, AND WITHOUT IT AN EMPTY COLLECTION RUNS ONE TRIP.**
	// The cursor form is a DO-WHILE (see `parseForStatement`'s header): its first test is licensed by the
	// protocol's invariant that a live cursor is already positioned on an element. A throwing factory is
	// exactly the case where that invariant does not hold — `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:132-134` has the
	// constructor throw `exhausted` for an empty collection — so the factory's own error flag becomes the
	// value the header starts from, and the loop's ordinary exit test does the rest. An INFALLIBLE factory
	// cannot report emptiness at all, so its seed is the literal 0 the bare cursor form uses.
	function materializeIterableIterationSource(func IrFunction, source ValueId, sourceOpFloor OpIndex, forTok Token) returns IterableIterationSource throws ParseError
		// The surface gate is `methodSurfaceOf`'s and is there for its reason: a source with no
		// method surface has no `createIterator` to find, and no store to ask for one.
		let surface = self.methodSurfaceOf(source)
		let alreadyACursor = try self.surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol(surface, anchor: forTok)
		if alreadyACursor 'walksItself'
			return try self.absorbThrowingCursorSource(func, source: source, sourceOpFloor: sourceOpFloor, forTok: forTok)
		end 'walksItself'

		match try self.surfaceIterableFactory(surface, anchor: forTok) 'factory'
			notDeclared then return IterableIterationSource.notIterable
			infallible then return IterableIterationSource.cursor(try self.emitReceiverProtocolCall(surface, methodName: CreateIteratorMethodName, anchor: forTok, resultUsed: true), entrySeed: self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(forTok)))
			throwsIterationError then break 'factory'
			throwsSomethingElse(errorName) then throw ParseError.unsupported("`for … in` over a `{String.from(self.methodSurfaceTypeName(surface))}`, whose `{String.from(CreateIteratorMethodName)}()` throws `{errorName}` — the loop absorbs what the traversal protocol throws without binding it, which is sound only for the payload-free `{String.from(IterationErrorTypeName)}`; a factory throwing anything else must be called with an explicit `try` and its result iterated", line: forTok.line, column: forTok.column)
		end 'factory'

		// The THROWING factory. `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` is the same rewrite `emitCursorAdvance` uses on
		// `advance()`, taken here for the same reason: it is the ONE place a `call` becomes one that also
		// defines an error flag, and a second site building the `tryCall` op itself would be a second answer to
		// which results that op defines and in what order.
		let targetOpFloor = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		// ⚠ **THE BLOCK-FORM ROUTER IS SUPPRESSED FOR `emitCursorAdvance`'s STATED REASON** — whoever is going
		// to turn this `call` into a `tryCall` must stop the router turning it into a different one first. A
		// `for x in someIterable` inside a `try 'l' … end` body is the reaching program.
		let savedRoutingSuppressed = self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = true
		let cursor = try self.emitReceiverProtocolCall(surface, methodName: CreateIteratorMethodName, anchor: forTok, resultUsed: true)
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = savedRoutingSuppressed

		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		_ = try self.rewriteLastCallToTryCall(func, flag: flag, tryTok: forTok, targetOpFloor: targetOpFloor)
		return IterableIterationSource.cursor(cursor, entrySeed: flag)
	end 'materializeIterableIterationSource'

	// ⭐⭐ **A SOURCE THAT IS ALREADY A CURSOR MAY HAVE BEEN PRODUCED BY A THROWING CALL, AND THE LOOP ABSORBS
	// THAT THROW EXACTLY AS IT ABSORBS A FACTORY'S (G19).** `for (iter, item) in arr.withIterator()` is
	// `/specs/with-iterator.md`'s own spelling and the bootstrap runs it; the call hands back a
	// `WithIterIterator`, which declares the cursor protocol, so the factory rewrite above never sees it and
	// the user-written call was left a bare `call` — **E3057, `throwing function requires try`**, on the
	// canonical program. `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon` declares `withIterator() … throws IterationError` for the
	// same reason `createIterator()` does: an EMPTY collection has no first element to position on. So the
	// answer is the same answer — the call's own error flag becomes the header's entry seed, and the loop's
	// ordinary exit test runs it zero times.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ABSORBS ONLY `IterationError`, and that bound is `surfaceIterableFactory`'s own** (see its
	// `throwsSomethingElse` refusal): the loop takes the error WITHOUT binding it, which is sound only for a
	// payload-free error the protocol itself defines. A source call throwing anything else keeps its E3057 and
	// must be written with an explicit `try`.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CALL AN ENCLOSING `try` BLOCK ALREADY ROUTED IS LEFT ALONE**, because it is no longer a `call`:
	// the router rewrote it, the author asked for that handler by writing the block, and a second rewrite of
	// one op is the shape `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`'s own guards exist to prevent.
	function absorbThrowingCursorSource(func IrFunction, source ValueId, sourceOpFloor OpIndex, forTok Token) returns IterableIterationSource throws ParseError
		if not self.sourceCameFromAnIterationThrowingCall(source, sourceOpFloor: sourceOpFloor) 'nothingToAbsorb'
			return IterableIterationSource.notIterable
		end 'nothingToAbsorb'

		let flag = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		_ = try self.rewriteLastCallToTryCall(func, flag: flag, tryTok: forTok, targetOpFloor: sourceOpFloor)
		return IterableIterationSource.cursor(source, entrySeed: flag)
	end 'absorbThrowingCursorSource'

	// Was this cursor produced, as the LAST thing the source expression emitted, by a direct call declaring
	// `throws IterationError`? Three conditions and every one of them is load-bearing: the op must be one the
	// SOURCE emitted (`sourceOpFloor` — the guard `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`'s own header records a measured
	// wrong-statement rewrite for), it must DEFINE the value being iterated (so `for x in f().cursor` does not
	// rewrite `f`), and its callee's clause must be the one the loop can absorb.
	function sourceCameFromAnIterationThrowingCall(source ValueId, sourceOpFloor OpIndex) returns bool
		let refCount = self.currentBlock.opRefs.count()
		if refCount == 0 'theSourceEmittedNoOp'
			return false
		end 'theSourceEmittedNoOp'

		let opIdx = try self.currentBlock.opRefs.get(refCount - 1) otherwise panic("sourceCameFromAnIterationThrowingCall: opRefs.get OOB on a non-empty block")
		if opIdx < sourceOpFloor 'notTheSourcesOwnOp'
			return false
		end 'notTheSourcesOwnOp'

		match try self.artifact.module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("sourceCameFromAnIterationThrowingCall: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops") 'op'
			call(result, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then return result == source and throwsClauseIsIterationError(self.signatures.throwsOf(callee))
			literal to switch then return false
			witnessDispatch to errorFlagEncode then return false
		end 'op'
	end 'sourceCameFromAnIterationThrowingCall'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A `for … in` MAY ITERATE.** An `Array` instance whose element read is a random-access index,
	// which is what the counter lowering requires — plus the `Vector` whose record IS an array's, a
	// `String`'s grapheme clusters, a `List`'s node chain, and any source supplying the cursor protocol,
	// whether by DECLARING it or by REQUIRING it of what the value dispatches through. A corpus `Array` and
	// a corpus `Map` both reach here as the CURSOR their own `createIterator()` returned, so they are
	// admitted by the cursor arm and not by a container arm at all. Everything else is a clean E2015 naming
	// the gap rather than a silent acceptance.
	//
	// ⚠ **`cursorSurface` ARRIVES RESOLVED, and is the same one `parseForStatement` will emit through.** This
	// door and that fork must not resolve it twice: an existential's resolution is a walk of the interface's
	// slot list per protocol name, and two walks are two chances to admit on one answer and emit on another.
	//
	// TWO sentences come out of here, and the split is which one is TRUE of the source. The shared one
	// enumerates what shv2 iterates and ends "any type declaring the cursor protocol", which misdescribes a
	// witness-dispatched receiver; that one refuses in its own words.
	function requireIterableSource(source ValueId, cursorSurface MethodSurface, forTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⚠ **THIS ARM IS NOT THE CORPUS `Array`'S, AND SAYING SO WOULD BE THE STALEST STATEMENT IN THIS
		// FILE.** `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares `createIterator()`, so a corpus array was already rewritten
		// to its `ArrayIterator` by `materializeIterableIterationSource` and is admitted below by the cursor
		// arm. What still arrives here as an `Array` INSTANCE is a program's own
		// `type Array … implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` that declares NO cursor and no factory: the name is
		// still served by the compiler (`isArrayBaseName` — an `Array`'s record IS its buffer), so the
		// counter lowering can read `count`/`get` off it, and this is the only door that admits it.
		// MEASURED: such a program emits `__managed_count` / `__managed_get_unchecked`, where the corpus array emits
		// `Array.createIterator` / `ArrayIterator.current`.
		if self.signatures.isArrayInstanceAt(self.tagOf(source), nameId: self.valueNameId(source)) 'array'
			return
		end 'array'
		// A STRING iterates its GRAPHEME CLUSTERS (P1.8 slice B) — see `parseForStatement`'s String arm.
		if tagIsText(self.tagOf(source)) 'string'
			return
		end 'string'
		// ⚠ A LIST arm stood here until W153, admitting the synthesized chain so `parseForStatement` could
		// build it a node cursor. A corpus `List` declares `Iterable with (Element, ListIter)`, so it arrives
		// as the `ListIterator` its own `createIterator()` returned and is admitted by the cursor arm below —
		// the same route the corpus `Array` and the corpus `Map` take.
		// A VECTOR iterates exactly as an `Array` does — its record IS an array's, its length is its fixed
		// size, and `parseForStatement`'s counted form reads both off the record and the element through the
		// shared accessor, so the loop needed no arm of its own: only this admission.
		if self.signatures.isVectorInstanceAt(self.tagOf(source), nameId: self.valueNameId(source)) 'vector'
			return
		end 'vector'
		// A USER TYPE walks its own CURSOR (conditional extensions) — the one source here that supplies its
		// own traversal instead of having one built for it, and the reason this function no longer answers
		// only for compiler-owned containers. See `parseForStatement`'s cursor arm and
		// `surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol`, which is where the two method names live. The surface arrives
		// RESOLVED from `parseForStatement`, which is the party that also emits through it.
		if methodSurfaceExists(cursorSurface) 'cursor'
			return
		end 'cursor'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE SURFACE THE SHARED SENTENCE IS WRONG ABOUT REFUSES IN ITS OWN WORDS**, read off the
		// source's OWN surface rather than off a tag — "how are this value's methods reached?" is a question
		// `methodSurfaceOf` answers, and asking it a second way here is exactly the duplication the
		// `MethodSurface` union exists to remove. (It was TWO until a parameterized interface became an
		// existential: an instance over an interface base is now the SAME `witnessDispatched` receiver a bare
		// one is, and it reaches this refusal by the same route and in the same words.)
		//
		// ⚠ **IT MAY NOT READ `cursorSurface`, and the first cut did — measured, both refusals silently reverted
		// to the shared sentence.** That parameter is the CURSOR surface: `cursorIterationSurface` collapses it
		// to `none` for any source that does not supply the protocol, which is precisely every source that
		// reaches this refusal. The two are different questions — "how are this value's methods reached" against
		// "does it walk its own cursor" — and only the first can say what is wrong.
		//
		// Re-deriving it here is one tag test plus, for a generic instance, one registry lookup, on the THROW
		// path only. It cannot disagree with the admission test's reading because it is the same function on the
		// same value; the expensive half — walking the slot list per protocol name — is not repeated.
		match self.methodSurfaceOf(source) 'sharedSentenceIsWrong'
			declared then break 'sharedSentenceIsWrong'
			none then break 'sharedSentenceIsWrong'
			// ⭐ **A DISPATCHED RECEIVER: the shared sentence ends *"any type DECLARING the cursor protocol"*,
			// which is true of a struct source and false here.** A conformer may declare both methods and still
			// be unwalkable through this value, because the table the dispatch jumps through has a slot only for
			// what the INTERFACE requires. Naming the provider is what turns "shv2 cannot iterate this" into "add
			// the requirement", and `witnessDispatchProviderPhrase` words it for an existential and for a
			// constrained type parameter alike.
			//
			// ⚠ It says the requirements are not the protocol rather than that they are ABSENT, because the two
			// near misses reach here too: an `{String.from(AdvanceMethodName)}()` naming the wrong error type, and
			// one half of the pair supplied without the other.
			witnessDispatched(receiver) then throw ParseError.unsupported("`for … in` over a value dispatched through {self.witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver)}, which supplies neither the cursor protocol `{String.from(CurrentMethodName)}()` + `{String.from(AdvanceMethodName)}() throws {String.from(IterationErrorTypeName)}` nor a `{String.from(CreateIteratorMethodName)}()` factory as a zero-argument REQUIREMENT — the traversal is a jump through the witness table the value carries, so it has to be something the interface requires; a conformer declaring it supplies no slot to dispatch through", line: forTok.line, column: forTok.column)
		end 'sharedSentenceIsWrong'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`for … in` over a `{typeTagName(self.tagOf(source))}` value — shv2 iterates an `Array`, a `{String.from(VectorBuiltinBaseName)}`, a `List`, a `{String.from(MapBuiltinBaseName)}`'s `(key, value)` entries, a `String`'s grapheme clusters, the counted ranges `<lo> to <hi>` / `<lo> upto <hi>`, and any type declaring the cursor protocol `{String.from(CurrentMethodName)}()` + `{String.from(AdvanceMethodName)}() throws {String.from(IterationErrorTypeName)}`", line: forTok.line, column: forTok.column)
	end 'requireIterableSource'

	// ⭐ **THE SURFACE THIS SOURCE WALKS ITS OWN CURSOR THROUGH, or `none` when it is not a cursor**
	// (conditional extensions). A surface and not a `bool`, because every caller that needs the answer also
	// needs the surface: it is what decides whether `current`/`advance` are emitted as direct calls or as
	// witness dispatches, so a separate lookup would be a second chance to resolve the receiver differently
	// from the admission test.
	function cursorIterationSurface(source ValueId, forTok Token) returns MethodSurface throws ParseError
		let surface = self.methodSurfaceOf(source)
		let suppliesTheProtocol = try self.surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol(surface, anchor: forTok)
		if not suppliesTheProtocol 'noProtocol'
			return MethodSurface.none
		end 'noProtocol'

		return surface
	end 'cursorIterationSurface'

	// ⭐⭐ **HOW A VALUE'S TRAVERSAL-PROTOCOL METHODS ARE REACHED** — the ONE answer both `for … in` doors
	// above ask, and the reason neither says `structRef` any more. See `MethodSurface`.
	//
	// A plain struct answers its own name. A GENERIC INSTANCE answers its BASE, because shv2 compiles ONE body
	// per generic type (P1.6-A): `Box with Integer`'s `createIterator` IS `Box.createIterator`, the same symbol
	// a `Box with String` reaches, and it is the name every other method door already mangles onto
	// (`denotedGenericBaseName`, `dispatchMethodOnBase`). An EXISTENTIAL answers `existential`: it has a name,
	// but its name is an INTERFACE, whose requirements no callee registry holds and whose conformers' bodies
	// are not what a dispatch binds against. Everything else — an int, a `String`, a closure — answers `none`.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE TWO DOORS ASKED `tagOf(source) != structRef` AND THAT GATE WAS THE BUG, NOT THE DESIGN.** A
	// `genericInstance` carries its base name AND its concrete type arguments, which is strictly MORE than a
	// `structRef` has — so `for x in b` over a `Box with Integer` that declares the whole cursor protocol was
	// **`E2015: for … in over a 'struct' value`**, a refusal whose own sentence names the protocol the value
	// supplies. (The word "struct" in it came from `typeTagName` of a tag that was not `structRef` at all.)
	//
	// ⛔ **AND ANSWERING A BARE NAME KEPT THE EXISTENTIAL OUT FOR THE SAME REASON ONE STEP LATER.** An
	// interface value's methods are not `declaresCallee` facts at all, so the only honest answer under a
	// `ByteArray` return was the empty one — and `for item in source` over an `Iterator`-shaped interface was
	// **`E2015: for … in over a 'interface' value`**, again enumerating a protocol the value's own interface
	// requires. The kind had to become part of the answer before the answer could be right.
	function methodSurfaceOf(value ValueId) returns MethodSurface
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'plainStruct'
			return MethodSurface.declared(self.valueTypeName(value), receiver: value)
		end 'plainStruct'
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			let base = self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(self.valueNameId(value))
			// ⭐⭐ **AN INSTANCE OVER AN `interface` BASE IS NOT A VALUE SHAPE AT ALL — IT IS AN EXISTENTIAL THAT
			// SOMETHING FAILED TO RESOLVE.** `ProgramSignatures.instanceDenotedType` makes `Seq with Integer` the
			// fat pointer `Seq` at every door that turns a written type into a `MaxonType`, so a VALUE carrying
			// this shape means a type reached the parser from a store none of those doors repaired. It used to be
			// its own `MethodSurface` arm carrying a refusal — five arms and a sentence claiming shv2 "has nowhere
			// to record the `with` arguments", which stopped being true the moment the arguments turned out not to
			// need recording (they are the conformances', folded by `associatedAbiBindingOf`, and E3125 verifies
			// what a use site claims they are).
			//
			// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE SAME PREDICATE `instanceDenotedType` ASKS, ON THE SAME MAP**, which is exactly what
			// makes this a compiler invariant rather than a program error: if that door saw this type it answered
			// `interfaceRef`, so the only way here is a type that never crossed it.
			if isInterfaceName(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: base) 'interfaceBase'
				panic("methodSurfaceOf: a value is typed `{String.from(base)} with …`, an instance over an INTERFACE base — `ProgramSignatures.instanceDenotedType` resolves every such instance to the existential `interfaceRef({String.from(base)})`, so this value's type reached the parser without crossing a type-resolution door. Whichever store handed it over needs the same repair `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` gives a swept field")
			end 'interfaceBase'

			return MethodSurface.declared(base, receiver: value)
		end 'instance'
		// Both witness receiver kinds, one arm — see `MethodSurface.witnessDispatched`.
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef or self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'dispatched'
			return MethodSurface.witnessDispatched(value)
		end 'dispatched'

		return MethodSurface.none
	end 'methodSurfaceOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS SURFACE SUPPLY THE CURSOR PROTOCOL?** — `ProgramSignatures.declaresCursorProtocol`'s
	// question, asked of whichever store holds the declarations. The two stores are genuinely different (a
	// whole-program callee index against an interface's transitive requirement list) and the RULE is one: both
	// want `current()` beside an `advance()` whose clause names `IterationError`, which is why the clause test
	// is `throwsClauseIsIterationError` on both sides rather than a callee lookup on one and a slot walk on
	// the other.
	function surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol(surface MethodSurface, anchor Token) returns bool throws ParseError
		var receiver = 0 as ValueId
		match surface 'k'
			none then return false
			declared(typeName, _) then return self.signatures.declaresCursorProtocol(typeName)
			witnessDispatched(dispatchedOn) then receiver = dispatchedOn
		end 'k'

		match try self.witnessProtocolTarget(receiver, methodName: CurrentMethodName, anchor: anchor) 'current'
			notRequired then return false
			required then break 'current'
		end 'current'

		return match try self.witnessProtocolTarget(receiver, methodName: AdvanceMethodName, anchor: anchor) 'advance'
			notRequired gives false
			required(advance) gives throwsClauseIsIterationError(advance.throwsClause)
		end 'advance'
	end 'surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol'

	// `ProgramSignatures.iterableFactory`'s question, asked of whichever store holds the declaration —
	// `surfaceDeclaresCursorProtocol`'s twin, split the same way and for the same reason. The EXISTENCE half
	// differs per store; `iterableFactoryFromClause` classifies what was found, once, for both.
	function surfaceIterableFactory(surface MethodSurface, anchor Token) returns IterableFactory throws ParseError
		var receiver = 0 as ValueId
		match surface 'k'
			none then return IterableFactory.notDeclared
			declared(typeName, _) then return self.signatures.iterableFactory(typeName)
			witnessDispatched(dispatchedOn) then receiver = dispatchedOn
		end 'k'

		return match try self.witnessProtocolTarget(receiver, methodName: CreateIteratorMethodName, anchor: anchor) 'factory'
			notRequired gives IterableFactory.notDeclared
			required(create) gives iterableFactoryFromClause(create.throwsClause)
		end 'factory'
	end 'surfaceIterableFactory'

	// What a diagnostic calls the traversal surface: the DECLARING TYPE for a direct-call source, and for an
	// existential the INTERFACE the value is held at — which is the name that answers "why can this not be
	// walked?", because the requirement list is what the loop was refused by.
	function methodSurfaceTypeName(surface MethodSurface) returns ByteArray
		match surface 'k'
			declared(typeName, _) then return typeName
			witnessDispatched(receiver) then return self.internedName(self.valueNameId(receiver))
			none then panic("methodSurfaceTypeName: a value with no method surface has no declaration to name — every caller asks after the surface answered something else")
		end 'k'
	end 'methodSurfaceTypeName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE REQUIREMENT AN EXISTENTIAL'S INTERFACE SUPPLIES UNDER A PROTOCOL NAME, resolved through the
	// SAME door a written `s.current()` resolves through.** `findWitnessDispatchCandidates` reads the
	// interface off the receiver's name id and the table off its witness pairing, walks the TRANSITIVE slot
	// list (so a protocol inherited through `extends` is found), and hands back targets already carrying the
	// slot index, the declaring interface and the declared `throws`. Re-deriving any of that here would be a
	// second answer to which slot a dispatch jumps through — the one thing that must not have two.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FILTER IS "TAKES A RECEIVER, AND TAKES NOTHING ELSE", and both halves matter.** A `static`
	// requirement of the name declares no receiver slot, so prepending the source to it puts an argument in
	// front of a callee with no parameter for it — `requireWitnessDispatchCandidates` documents the wasm
	// `indirect call type mismatch` that produced. And the loop supplies no arguments at all, so a
	// `current(at Index)` requirement is not the protocol however plausibly it is named; taking it would slot
	// an actual the loop never had. Those two ARE the filter `dispatchWitnessMethod` applies — the receiver
	// test verbatim, and `pickWitnessDispatchByArity`'s at the arity the loop always calls with.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE SURVIVORS GO THROUGH `pickSoleWitnessCandidate`, BECAUSE THE FIRST CUT RETURNED ON THE
	// FIRST HIT AND THAT IS THE EXACT BUG `WitnessCandidatePick` EXISTS FOR.** `interface Derived extends Base`
	// with a zero-argument `current()` on EACH supplies two DISTINCT slots of that name — the shape
	// `pickWitnessDispatchByArity`'s header calls the ordinary spelling — and `witnessCandidateAlreadyCollected`
	// does not merge them, because they are two requirements and not two routes to one. MEASURED on this
	// rung's own tree before the fix: `source.current()` was **E3114** while `for item in source` COMPILED and
	// printed 10, silently binding whichever slot the transitive walk emitted first. That is a wrong function
	// pointer rather than a diagnostic, and it broke the rule `dispatchMethodOnBase` states in full — the door
	// a receiver came through may not change what the answer IS.
	function witnessProtocolTarget(receiver ValueId, methodName ByteArray, anchor Token) returns WitnessProtocolTarget throws ParseError
		let candidates = try self.findWitnessDispatchCandidates(receiver, methodName: methodName, errorTok: anchor)
		var protocolShaped = WitnessDispatchTargetArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto candidates.count() 'eachCandidate'
			let candidate = try candidates.get(i) otherwise panic("witnessProtocolTarget: candidates.get OOB at {i} below count {candidates.count()}")
			if witnessRequirementTakesReceiver(candidate) and candidate.paramTypeNames.count() == ProtocolMethodArity 'theProtocolShape'
				protocolShaped.push(candidate)
			end 'theProtocolShape'
		end 'eachCandidate'

		match pickSoleWitnessCandidate(protocolShaped) 'pick'
			none then return WitnessProtocolTarget.notRequired
			one(target) then return WitnessProtocolTarget.required(target)
			// The SAME sentence the written call gets, from the same door, at the one arity this filter selected
			// on — which is also the arity the loop calls with, so the two are `ProtocolMethodArity` and not two
			// literals that could drift apart.
			ambiguous(first, second) then throw ParseError.ambiguousWitnessDispatch(String.from(methodName), provider: self.witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver), argCount: ProtocolMethodArity, first: renderWitnessRequirement(first), second: renderWitnessRequirement(second), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'pick'
	end 'witnessProtocolTarget'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A COUNTED RANGE'S BOUNDS MAY BE — the RANGE form's twin of `requireIterableSource`, and
	// the rung shipped without it.** A counted range is an INDEX PHI stepped by `emitMachineWordAdd`, a
	// raw i64 add; a bound of any other domain hands that add something it cannot step, and the failure
	// was never a diagnostic:
	//   • `for x in 1.0 to 3.0` reached the x64 emitter and PANICKED — *"rax is in the gpr register file
	//     where the xmm file is required"* (`X64Backend.maxon:1387`), an internal error against a program
	//     the runnable oracle refuses cleanly (`E3005: Range expressions require int or Character, got
	//     'Float'`);
	//   • `for b in false to true` was silently ACCEPTED and ran two trips, incrementing a BOOL.
	// Everything else happened to land on a comparison type error (`"a" to "c"`, `1 to 3.5`) — which is
	// luck, not a rule: it is `emitCompare` refusing two operands, and it says nothing about the STEP.
	//
	// ⚠ **The test is `tagIsIntegral`, NOT `== ValueTypeTag.integer`** — see `columnValueTagMatches`'s
	// header for the measured false refusal the narrow spelling caused. A ranged alias (`typealias Row =
	// int(0 to 63)`) carries `named` until TypeResolution collapses it, and `for r in 0 to lastRow` where
	// `lastRow` is such a parameter must stay legal.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CHARACTER BOUND HAS ALREADY BEEN CONVERTED BY THE TIME THIS IS ASKED (A5m-ab).** `'a' to 'e'` is a
	// pair of `Character` RECORDS as written, and `parseForStatement` runs BOTH bounds through
	// `integerizedOperand` before this rule sees them — so what arrives is two codepoints and this passes for
	// the reason a counted range does. It is NOT that a char literal "is an integer codepoint here": the
	// ELEMENT is a `Character` minted per trip from the counter (`ranges.character-range` prints `abcde`), and
	// a bound this rule still refuses is one the conversion could not express (a Character BINDING, a
	// multi-codepoint cluster) — which is exactly why the refusal is left to say so in its own words.
	//
	// Both bounds are asked, each anchored on its own half — the START on the source's first token, the
	// END on the `to`/`upto`, so a mixed range names the side that is wrong.
	function requireCountableRangeBound(bound ValueId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if tagIsIntegral(self.tagOf(bound)) 'countable'
			return
		end 'countable'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("a counted `for … in <lo> to|upto <hi>` range needs INTEGER bounds — got a '{typeTagName(self.tagOf(bound))}'. The loop variable is a counter stepped by 1, which no other domain has a meaning for; iterate a `float`/`String` by indexing an `Array` over it", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireCountableRangeBound'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAKE THE ITERATED SOURCE UNWRITABLE FOR THE BODY** — the borrow's safety obligation, and the
	// reason `for n in arr 'l' … arr.push(9) … end` is **E3019** rather than a use-after-free. The loop
	// variable holds an element the ARRAY still owns, so a body that makes the array destroy it
	// (`clear`/`set`/`resize`/`remove`), or that rebinds the source out from under the loop, would leave
	// the borrow pointing at freed memory. The runnable oracle refuses the same program with the same
	// code, and the reference bootstrap locks the same thing across the body (`2-Parser.cs:13377-13390`).
	//
	// It records the binding a write would have to go THROUGH, which is the source itself for a bare name
	// and the chain's BASE for `for it in b.items` — the array's own value cannot identify it, because a
	// re-read of `b.items` inside the body mints a fresh ValueId for the same record.
	//
	// ⚠ **A UAF WAS MEASURED THROUGH EXACTLY THAT SECOND SHAPE**, which is why the lock is a list here and
	// not the two writability bits flipped on the binding: `for it in b.items 'scan' b.items.clear();
	// t = t + it.value end` returned **63 (0x3F, the free-poison byte)** where the answer is 5, because a
	// field-chain receiver never asks `immutableReceiverNameOf` at all (shv2 deliberately permits
	// `self.items.push(v)` through a `let`). The reference bootstrap's lock has the same reach and the same
	// hole — it keys on a VARIABLE name — and gets away with it only because it RETAINS the element.
	//
	// A source with no binding at all (a literal, a call result) locks nothing, and needs to: nothing else
	// can name that record, so no statement in the body can reach it.
	function lockIterationSource(sourceStart TokenIndex) returns bool
		if not self.sourceNamesABase(sourceStart) 'notAName'
			return false
		end 'notAName'

		// A SELF-FIELD ALIAS is locked like any other binding, and it has to be: `for it in items 'l'
		// items.clear() … end` inside a method reached the free-poison byte (0x3F) in a probe before it
		// was. The alias's `VarInfo` is installed once at method entry, so `scope.getInfo` hands back the
		// same object the write door resolves — see `selfFieldIsWritable`, which is where that door asks.
		// The name comes through `iterationSubjectNameAt` and NOT `baseBindingName`, because the subject
		// of `for it in self.items` is the FIELD, not the receiver — see that function.
		let baseName = self.iterationSubjectNameAt(sourceStart)
		if not self.scope.contains(baseName) 'notALocalBinding'
			return false
		end 'notALocalBinding'

		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty before the first push — see `sharedEmptyIterationLocks`.
		if self.iterationLockedBindings is sharedEmptyIterationLocks 'firstLockInThisFunction'
			self.iterationLockedBindings = VarInfoArray.create()
		end 'firstLockInThisFunction'

		self.iterationLockedBindings.push(self.scope.getInfo(baseName))
		return true
	end 'lockIterationSource'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SCOPE NAME OF THE STORAGE A CHAIN AT `baseIndex` IS WRITTEN THROUGH — THE ONE
	// DERIVATION**, shared by the site that TAKES the iteration lock (`lockIterationSource`) and the
	// site that CONSULTS it for a field-chain write (`iterationLockNameAt`). A divergence between the
	// two is a use-after-free rather than a compile error, and it WAS one.
	//
	// ⚠ **`self.items` RESOLVES TO THE FIELD, NOT TO THE RECEIVER**, and that is the whole reason this
	// is not `baseBindingName`. A self-field's storage has TWO source spellings (`items`, `self.items`)
	// and FOUR write spellings, and `baseBindingName` answers `SelfParamName` for half of them — so the
	// lock and the doors keyed on two different objects for one storage and half the combinations
	// silently missed. MEASURED, before this: `for it in self.items 'l' items = IntArray.create() end`
	// SEGFAULTED (0xC0000005) and `for it in self.items 'l' items.clear() end` was silently ACCEPTED,
	// while the very same programs spelled `for it in items` were correctly E2013/E3019.
	//
	// Nothing is lost by not naming the receiver: `self` is a PARAMETER and no write can go through it
	// (`requireMutableBinding`), so it was never the subject. And the field's name resolves to the one
	// alias `VarInfo` every spelling reaches, because a local may NOT shadow a self-field name inside a
	// method (`Scope.declareBinding`, E3006) — the name is unambiguous by construction, not by luck.
	function iterationSubjectNameAt(baseIndex TokenIndex) returns ByteArray
		let baseTok = self.tokenAt(baseIndex)
		if baseTok.kind != TokenKind.self 'namesItself'
			return baseTok.value
		end 'namesItself'

		if self.kindAt(baseIndex + 1) == TokenKind.dot and self.kindAt(baseIndex + 2) == TokenKind.identifier 'selfRootedChain'
			return self.tokenAt(baseIndex + 2).value
		end 'selfRootedChain'

		// A bare `self` with no hop denotes the receiver itself, which is what `baseBindingName` says.
		return SelfParamName
	end 'iterationSubjectNameAt'

	// Is the iteration source at `sourceStart` a plain NAME or a FIELD CHAIN rooted at one — the two
	// shapes whose base binding is what a write has to go through? It is exactly such a chain when the
	// shared walk ends where the source's own tokens did (the cursor); a call, a literal or an operator
	// expression ends elsewhere and locks nothing. `AnyFieldHops`, because a BARE name is the common
	// case and is every bit as lockable as `b.items`.
	function sourceNamesABase(sourceStart TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.fieldChainEndAfter(sourceStart, minHops: AnyFieldHops) == self.pos
	end 'sourceNamesABase'

	// Undo `lockIterationSource` once the body is parsed — the source is writable again on the next line.
	// The list is a stack (a nested `for` pushes above this one and pops first), so the release is a pop.
	function releaseIterationSource(locked bool)
		if not locked 'nothingLocked'
			return
		end 'nothingLocked'
		_ = try self.iterationLockedBindings.pop() otherwise panic("releaseIterationSource: the iteration lock stack is empty — every lock is pushed by `lockIterationSource` and popped exactly once here")
	end 'releaseIterationSource'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `binding` BEING ITERATED BY AN ENCLOSING `for … in`?** THE one fact, and it is read at
	// exactly the FIVE doors a write can NAME the iterated array through. Each one alone leaves a
	// use-after-free open, which is why they share this rather than each carrying its own flag:
	//   • `immutableReceiverNameOf` — `arr.clear()` on the source binding itself, and the same binding
	//     handed to a callee that mutates it (`grow(arr)`, whose blame name this also supplies);
	//   • `parseFieldChainCall` — `b.items.clear()`, whose receiver is a VALUE and so never asks the
	//     binding question at all (the shape the first 0x3F probe found);
	//   • `selfFieldIsWritable` — `items.clear()` on a bare self-field alias, whose receiver binding is
	//     MINTED FRESH per access and so cannot be found by identity (the second 0x3F probe);
	//   • `requireMutableBinding` — `arr = other`, which drops the record the loop is still walking;
	//   • `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` — `items = other` / `self.items = other`, the SAME rebinding one
	//     indirection out. It was the door this rung shipped without and it SEGFAULTED (0xC0000005 on
	//     every one of the four source×write spellings); see that function.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO KEYS, ONE LIST — and which key is used is DERIVED from the binding, never chosen by a
	// caller.** A LOCAL is matched by OBJECT IDENTITY, so a body that shadows the source's name declares
	// a different `VarInfo` and is correctly writable. A FIELD OF THE ENCLOSING `self` is matched by its
	// LAYOUT INDEX (`selfFieldIsIterationLocked`), because `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` holds an index and
	// no alias at all, and because the alias is the only object identity available to the other doors.
	// Every live entry belongs to the function being parsed (`iterationLockedBindings` is reset per
	// function), and a function belongs to one type, so an index cannot mean a field of another layout.
	// A linear scan over a list whose length is the enclosing `for` nesting depth.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, AND WHY THAT IS NOT THIS CONSTRUCT'S BUG.** The lock stops a write
	// the loop's own source NAMES. It cannot stop one that reaches the array without naming it — a field
	// chain handed to a mutating callee (`wipe(b.items)`), or a method of the enclosing struct that
	// clears its own field (`b.wipe()`, which shv2 rules does not implicate the caller — see
	// `receiverOwnerMask`). Both destroy the element the loop borrowed, and both read 0x3F.
	//
	// **They are the PRE-EXISTING borrow-on-get hazard, not a for-in one, and the proof is that neither
	// needs a loop:** `let it = try arr.get(0) otherwise …` then `arr.clear()` then `it.value` reads the
	// same 0x3F with no `for` in the program. `for … in` reads its element through the SAME door
	// (`emitContainerElementAccessor`, `borrowed`) under the SAME rule, so it inherits that hazard and
	// adds none. Its cure is the thesis decision PLAN.md's P1.7 Slice 3a residual (a) already names —
	// incref-on-get, copy-on-get, or E3070 borrow-liveness — and it is not a loop's to make. The
	// runnable oracle ACCEPTS both uncovered programs (measured: `wipe(b.items)` returns 5), because it
	// RETAINS the element; refusing them here would diverge from it on a legal program while the other
	// half of the same class stayed open.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE EMPTY CHECK IS NOT DEFENSIVE, IT IS THE COST.** Two of the three readers run on the hot
	// statement path (every assignment, every container method call), and the list is empty in every
	// function that contains no `for … in` at all — which, on the scale corpus, is all of them. Entering
	// the walk regardless billed **+270 allocations at rung 0 rising to +2,564 at rung 5** (linear, A/B
	// against the parent built in the same worktree): a `for x in <Array>` over an EMPTY array still
	// materializes the backing record the loop reads its length from. Answering `false` off a `count()`
	// makes the common path free and leaves the walk for the loops that actually have a lock.
	function bindingIsIterationLocked(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		if binding.isSelfField 'fieldOfTheEnclosingSelf'
			return self.selfFieldIsIterationLocked(binding.fieldIndex)
		end 'fieldOfTheEnclosingSelf'

		if self.iterationLockedBindings.count() == 0 'noEnclosingForIn'
			return false
		end 'noEnclosingForIn'

		for locked in self.iterationLockedBindings 'each'
			if locked is binding 'match'
				return true
			end 'match'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'bindingIsIterationLocked'

	// ⭐ **IS FIELD `index` OF THE ENCLOSING `self` BEING ITERATED?** The INDEX-keyed half of the one
	// lock above, split out for the door that has an index and no binding: the self-field STORE
	// (`emitCheckedSelfFieldStore`), which both `items = x` and `self.items = x` converge on. Reading the
	// same list under the key that door actually holds is what keeps the two spellings from needing two
	// rules — the shape that let four spellings of one write disagree in the first place.
	function selfFieldIsIterationLocked(index FieldIndex) returns bool
		if self.iterationLockedBindings.count() == 0 'noEnclosingForIn'
			return false
		end 'noEnclosingForIn'

		for locked in self.iterationLockedBindings 'each'
			if locked.isSelfField and locked.fieldIndex == index 'match'
				return true
			end 'match'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'selfFieldIsIterationLocked'

	// The E3019 blame name for a write reached through a FIELD CHAIN (`b.items.push(x)`) — non-empty only
	// while an enclosing `for` is iterating through that base. It asks the iteration lock and NOT
	// `immutableReceiverNameOf`, deliberately: shv2 rules that a container held in a FIELD may be written
	// through a `let` base (`receiverOwnerMask`'s header records the measurement and the ruling), so
	// routing the chain through the general rule would newly reject programs the corpus pins.
	// It takes the SUBJECT'S NAME already resolved rather than the base token's index, and that is two
	// reasons which are one reason: the subject of `self.items.clear()` is `items`, and blaming `self` sent
	// the reader to a parameter nothing could be done about (`iterationSubjectNameAt`); and E3070's door at
	// the same call site needs the identical answer — so it is derived ONCE, by `borrowSubjectNameAt`, and
	// handed to both. Empty in, empty out: a chain over no nameable storage locks nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ It resolves through `borrowSubjectBindingOf` and NOT `scope.getInfo`, because the subject space is
	// wider than `Scope`: a MODULE-storage subject has a stand-in `VarInfo` that no scope holds, and
	// `getInfo` panics on a name it does not have. A stand-in is never pushed by `lockIterationSource`
	// (which locks only what `Scope` holds), so it correctly answers "not locked" by identity.
	function iterationLockNameOf(subjectName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if subjectName.isEmpty() 'namesNoStorage'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'namesNoStorage'

		if not self.bindingIsIterationLocked(self.borrowSubjectBindingOf(subjectName)) 'writable'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'writable'

		return subjectName
	end 'iterationLockNameOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE STORAGE A CHAIN AT `baseIndex` NAMES, or empty when it names none** — `iterationSubjectNameAt`
	// (THE one derivation of *which binding a write goes through*) plus the one test that says the name denotes
	// storage this function body can reason about at all.
	//
	// **Two predicates share it, and that sharing IS the E3070 design.** The iteration lock asks of this name
	// *"is this binding being iterated?"* (`iterationLockNameAt`, immediately above); the borrow set asks
	// *"is an element of it still referenced?"* (`borrowSubjectIdOf`). Written twice, a chain shape taught to
	// one and not the other would leave a write locked under one key and checked under another — which is
	// precisely the divergence `iterationSubjectNameAt`'s ⚠ block records as a measured segfault.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL `var` IS STORAGE THIS BODY CAN NAME TOO, and leaving it out was a REACHABLE
	// USE-AFTER-FREE.** `var g = [...]` at module scope with `let s = try g.get(0) otherwise ""` then
	// `g.clear()` compiled clean and faulted with **0xC0000005** — the oracle reports E3070 on that very
	// program. The same array reached through `grow(g)`, a rebind `g = <fresh>` (whose store decrefs the
	// record the slot held — `emitCheckedGlobalStore`) and `for it in g` each faulted the same way. A
	// global differs from a local in WHERE the record is anchored and in nothing a borrow can see.
	//
	// ⚠ **LOCAL FIRST, which is what keeps a shadowing local a DIFFERENT subject** — the same order
	// `parseAssignment` resolves an assignment target in, and the reason `shadowed-source-is-writable`
	// compiles. A name that reaches the second test is one no binding of this frame holds.
	function borrowSubjectNameAt(baseIndex TokenIndex) returns ByteArray
		let baseName = self.iterationSubjectNameAt(baseIndex)
		if self.scope.contains(baseName) 'bindingOfThisFrame'
			return baseName
		end 'bindingOfThisFrame'

		return self.moduleStorageSubjectName(baseName)
	end 'borrowSubjectNameAt'

	// ⭐ **THE BORROW SUBJECT A MODULE-STORAGE NAME IS — itself, once it denotes storage a write can reach.**
	// Split out of the token-index derivation above so the STATIC MEMBER write door can ask it of a name no
	// single token spells (W65, `storeToGlobal`); a second spelling of "is this global a subject" would be
	// free to admit an immutable binding, and the header below says what admitting one costs.
	function moduleStorageSubjectName(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return name if self.namesWritableModuleStorage(name) else EmptySourceText
	end 'moduleStorageSubjectName'

	// Does `name` denote a top-level `var` — module storage a body can BORROW an element out of and can
	// WRITE? An immutable top-level binding is excluded on purpose rather than for tidiness: every write
	// door refuses it already (E2013 at `storeToGlobal`, E3019 at `noteReceiverWrite`), so it can never be
	// half of a conflict, and admitting it would mint borrows and subjects no diagnostic could ever read.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BOOL GATE IN FRONT OF THE RESOLUTION IS THE COST**, and it is `managedTopLevelBaseAt`'s for
	// its reason: `globalDeclFor` BOXES a union per call, while this runs on every array-method and
	// struct-method receiver in the program. `namesManagedTopLevel` is a set membership test and a `false`
	// from it is final, so a program with no top-level managed binding — the whole scale corpus — never
	// reaches the box.
	// Does the chain base at `index` name a top-level `var` — module storage any function in the program can
	// write? `namesWritableModuleStorage` with the SCOPE test in front of it, which is what keeps a local
	// that shadows a global's name reading as the local (`borrowSubjectNameAt`'s order, for its reason).
	function baseNamesWritableModuleStorage(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		let baseTok = self.tokenAt(index)
		return not self.baseNamesAValueInScope(baseTok) and self.namesWritableModuleStorage(baseTok.value)
	end 'baseNamesWritableModuleStorage'

	function namesWritableModuleStorage(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.namesManagedTopLevel(name) 'notAManagedTopLevelName'
			return false
		end 'notAManagedTopLevelName'

		return match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
			found(_, _, mutable) gives mutable
			notFound gives false
		end 'global'
	end 'namesWritableModuleStorage'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BINDING A SUBJECT NAME DENOTES — THE ONE RESOLUTION, and the only place the two kinds of
	// storage are told apart.** A name `Scope` holds is that binding; anything else reaching here is a
	// top-level `var` (`borrowSubjectNameAt` admits nothing else) and gets its interned stand-in.
	//
	// Every asker of "which storage is this?" comes through here — the subject id, the storage mask, and
	// the iteration lock — so a third kind of storage taught to one of them is taught to all three.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE STORAGE A RECEIVER'S ELEMENT BORROW AND ITS WRITE BOTH KEY ON (W61).** A receiver that names a
	// binding answers with that name. One that names NOTHING and IS the enclosing `self` answers `self`,
	// because a record-collapsed `Array` keeps its elements in its OWN storage: a bare `get(0)` borrows out of
	// the very thing a bare `set(0, …)` writes, and with no name between them neither door could say so.
	// MEASURED `0xC0000005` on `get`/`set` spelled bare inside `type Array`'s own body, with every prefixed
	// spelling of that same program already refused — and `stdlib/Array.maxon` writes the bare spelling
	// throughout.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE DERIVATION, TWO ASKERS, ON PURPOSE** — the mint (`emitContainerElementAccessor`) and the write
	// (`noteReceiverBorrowSubjects`). Answered separately they would be two opinions about which storage this
	// is, and a borrow filed under one key against a write filed under another is a use-after-free with
	// nothing anywhere left to report it. The empty name WAS that disagreement, spelled as an absence.
	//
	// ⚠ Inside a CLOSURE `self` is a capture rather than a `Scope` binding, and `receiverIsEnclosingSelf`
	// answers false there — so this yields no subject and the closure body is left exactly as it was.
	function receiverBorrowSubjectName(subjectName ByteArray, receiver ValueId) returns ByteArray
		if not subjectName.isEmpty() 'namesABinding'
			return subjectName
		end 'namesABinding'

		if not self.receiverIsEnclosingSelf(receiver) 'namesNoStorageThisBodyCanKey'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'namesNoStorageThisBodyCanKey'

		return SelfParamName
	end 'receiverBorrowSubjectName'

	function borrowSubjectBindingOf(subjectName ByteArray) returns VarInfo
		if self.scope.contains(subjectName) 'bindingOfThisFrame'
			return self.scope.getInfo(subjectName)
		end 'bindingOfThisFrame'

		return self.topLevelBorrowSubjectOf(subjectName)
	end 'borrowSubjectBindingOf'

	// ⭐ **ONE STAND-IN PER TOP-LEVEL NAME, INTERNED FOR THE FILE** — see
	// `VarInfo.createModuleStorageSubject` for what it stands in for and why identity on the parser's
	// per-access receiver bindings would not do. Interning is what makes `g.get(0)` on line 4 and
	// `g.clear()` on line 5 the SAME subject.
	//
	// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty (`sharedEmptyTopLevelBorrowSubjects`), the
	// `sharedEmptyIterationLocks` pattern with its invariant: a `Parser` is built per top-level constant by
	// `SignatureIndex.walkConstInitializer`, so an unconditional `create()` in the constructor would be one
	// map per constant in the program, and the overwhelming majority of files name no writable global at a
	// borrow subject at all.
	function topLevelBorrowSubjectOf(subjectName ByteArray) returns VarInfo
		let existing = try self.topLevelBorrowSubjects.get(subjectName) otherwise 'mint'
			if self.topLevelBorrowSubjects is sharedEmptyTopLevelBorrowSubjects 'firstSubjectInThisFile'
				self.topLevelBorrowSubjects = VarInfoMap.create()
			end 'firstSubjectInThisFile'

			let minted = VarInfo.createModuleStorageSubject(subjectName, varType: self.moduleStorageTypeOf(subjectName))
			self.topLevelBorrowSubjects.upsert(subjectName, value: minted)
			return minted
		end 'mint'
		return existing
	end 'topLevelBorrowSubjectOf'

	// The declared type of the top-level `var` `subjectName` names, read through the ONE home of that
	// question. A `notFound` is impossible rather than merely unexpected — `borrowSubjectNameAt` admits a
	// name only past `namesWritableModuleStorage`, which resolves through this very lookup — so it panics
	// rather than inventing a type nothing declared.
	function moduleStorageTypeOf(subjectName ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(subjectName, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
			found(_, valueType, _) then return valueType
			notFound then panic("moduleStorageTypeOf: '{String.from(subjectName)}' is not a top-level binding — a subject name reaches here only past `namesWritableModuleStorage`, which asks this same lookup")
		end 'global'
	end 'moduleStorageTypeOf'

	// ⭐ **THE FUNCTION'S BORROW FACTS, COPY-ON-WRITTEN off the shared empty before the first write.** A body
	// that borrows nothing keeps the shared instance for its whole life and allocates not one array — the
	// `sharedEmptyIterationLocks` pattern, with the same invariant: the shared instance is NEVER mutated, so
	// it is safe across the per-file parse fan-out. Every writer below comes through here first.
	// ⚠ **ASKED BEFORE A SUBJECT NAME IS DERIVED, AND THAT ORDER IS THE COST.** Deriving one costs a
	// `Scope` hash lookup, and two of the write doors — the local rebind and the self-field store — sit on
	// the hot statement path. A write ABOVE every borrow's activation can never conflict (the resolver
	// rejects it on `activationToken > site.token`), so a function that has not yet completed a borrow —
	// nearly every function in any program — answers `false` off one read of a permanently empty array and
	// pays nothing else. The same reading `bindingIsIterationLocked`'s own empty check states, for the same
	// reason: it is not defensive, it IS the cost.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO SITES DERIVE THE SUBJECT FIRST ANYWAY, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN OVERLOOKED.** A method
	// call on an array or a struct resolves its subject unconditionally (`dispatchMethodOnBinding`,
	// `dispatchMethodOnBinding`), because the name is needed to MINT a borrow as well as to record a write —
	// and minting happens precisely when no borrow exists yet, so the gate cannot be hoisted in front of it.
	// The optimizer measured the one variant that could be — gating the array-method derivation on
	// `functionHoldsABorrow() or containerElementIsManaged()` — and it read -19k +120k -228k -618k ticks,
	// SIGN-FLIPPING across the ladder, i.e. inside the noise band. It does not ship.
	function functionHoldsABorrow(func IrFunction) returns bool
		return func.borrowFacts.borrows.count() > 0
	end 'functionHoldsABorrow'

	function borrowFactsOf(func IrFunction) returns BorrowFacts
		if func.borrowFacts is sharedEmptyBorrowFacts 'firstFactInThisFunction'
			func.borrowFacts = BorrowFacts.create()
		end 'firstFactInThisFunction'
		return func.borrowFacts
	end 'borrowFactsOf'

	// ⭐ **THE SUBJECT ID FOR `subjectName` — resolved through `Scope`, so the KEY is the binding and not the
	// spelling.** A body that SHADOWS a borrowed array's name declares a different `VarInfo` and gets a
	// different subject (correctly writable); a self-field's two spellings (`items`, `self.items`) both resolve
	// to the ONE alias installed at method entry and share one subject. That is the same two-property key
	// `bindingIsIterationLocked` matches by object identity, reached the same way.
	//
	// A linear scan over a list whose length is the number of DISTINCT storages this body borrows from or
	// writes — a handful — and it is only ever entered once a borrow exists (`noteBorrowSubjectWrite`'s gate).
	// Callers must have established that the name denotes storage, which `borrowSubjectNameAt` is.
	function borrowSubjectIdOf(func IrFunction, subjectName ByteArray) returns BorrowSubjectId
		let binding = self.borrowSubjectBindingOf(subjectName)
		var facts = self.borrowFactsOf(func)
		for k in 0 upto facts.subjects.count() 'seen'
			if (try facts.subjects.get(k) otherwise panic("borrowSubjectIdOf: subjects.get OOB at {k} inside count()")) is binding 'match'
				return k
			end 'match'
		end 'seen'

		facts.subjects.push(binding)
		return facts.subjects.count() - 1
	end 'borrowSubjectIdOf'

	// Record that the element accessor at `range` handed out a BORROW of `subjectName`. Pending until a
	// binding takes it (`attachPendingBorrows`) — an accessor whose result no name holds is unreachable
	// afterwards and borrows nothing that could dangle.
	function mintPendingBorrow(func IrFunction, subjectName ByteArray, borrowValue ValueId, range SourceRange)
		// The id is minted FIRST because `borrowSubjectIdOf` is what copy-on-writes the facts off the shared
		// empty — so the push below is always into this function's own.
		let subject = self.borrowSubjectIdOf(func, subjectName: subjectName)
		let firstPending = func.borrowFacts.pending.count()
		func.borrowFacts.pending.push(PendingBorrow.create(subject, borrowValue: borrowValue, borrowLine: range.line))

		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE STORAGE IT CAME OUT OF MAY ITSELF BE BORROWED, in which case this is a borrow of that
		// base too** — the composition rule, stated once in `BorrowCheck.composePendingBorrowOntoBases` and
		// applied at the ONE mint every borrow in the language leaves through, so a source of borrows added
		// later composes without being told. It carries the measured 0xC0000005 this closes.
		composePendingBorrowOntoBases(func.borrowFacts, firstPending: firstPending, borrowValue: borrowValue, range: range)
	end 'mintPendingBorrow'

	// ⭐⭐ **MOVE A PENDING BORROW ONTO THE VALUE A `try` MERGE HANDS BACK.** `let s = try arr.get(0)
	// otherwise ""` does not bind the accessor's result — it binds the CONTINUATION PHI the two edges meet
	// at — so without this the binding would claim nothing and the borrow would silently vanish.
	//
	// It is called from the ONE site that mints that phi (`finishValueTry`), so the key and the value the
	// source actually binds move together and cannot drift apart. The DIVERGING forms need no retarget and
	// correctly get none: `otherwise return`/`throw`/`panic` leave the accessor's own result as the try's
	// value, and so does a propagating `try`.
	function retargetPendingBorrow(func IrFunction, from ValueId, to ValueId)
		// Scanned from the END, because the entry a merge is retargeting was almost always minted by the very
		// expression the merge closes. The walk is over a list one statement long
		// (`dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows`), so it cannot grow with the body.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **IT RUNS ON PAST THE FIRST MATCH, AND THIS SAID THE OPPOSITE UNTIL ARR-borrow.** The old reason
		// was *"a value id is DEFINED ONCE, so at most one entry can carry it"* — true of the id, and never
		// the claim that mattered. SEVERAL entries carry ONE id whenever a borrow COMPOSES
		// (`BorrowCheck.composePendingBorrowOntoBases`): a read out of borrowed storage files one pending per
		// link of the chain, all keyed on the same accessor result. Stopping at the first left every other
		// link pointing at the pre-merge id, which no binding then claims — so `try p.items.get(0)
		// otherwise ""` silently dropped one end of its own chain and the write to it was waved through.
		// Which end survived depended only on push order, which is not a rule.
		// It is the same correction, for the same reason, that `attachPendingBorrows`'s ⚠ block already
		// carries about ITS walk.
		var k = func.borrowFacts.pending.count()
		while k > 0 'each'
			k = k - 1
			let pending = pendingAt(func.borrowFacts, index: k)
			if pending.borrowValue == from 'thisOne'
				// The entry is REBUILT rather than written through: a `for … in` element is an immutable binding
				// and a field store through one is E2013, which is the language's rule and not a limitation to
				// route around.
				try func.borrowFacts.pending.set(k, value: PendingBorrow.create(pending.subject, borrowValue: to, borrowLine: pending.borrowLine)) otherwise panic("retargetPendingBorrow: pending.set OOB at {k}, the index just read")
			end 'thisOne'
		end 'each'
	end 'retargetPendingBorrow'

	// ⭐⭐ **CUT THE PENDING CLAIM LIST BACK TO `mark` — the ONE thing that keeps it from growing with the
	// function body.** A `PendingBorrow` is minted by an element ACCESSOR and claimed by the BINDING or the
	// MERGE that takes its value, and every one of those claimants is inside the same STATEMENT as the
	// accessor. So whatever is still pending when a statement ends can never be claimed, and both walks over
	// this list (`retargetPendingBorrow`, `attachPendingBorrows`) would otherwise re-read it for the rest of
	// the body.
	//
	// It is a WATERMARK and not a reset, and that distinction is the whole reason this is safe where a reset
	// was not: `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise return 1` parses its handler by RE-ENTERING the statement
	// parser mid-initializer, and a reset there wiped the OUTER statement's mint — no borrow recorded, the
	// following `arr.clear()` waved through, measured **0xC0000005** (see `PendingBorrow`'s ⭐⭐ block). The
	// nested parse takes its own mark ABOVE the outer's entry and can only ever cut back to it.
	//
	// ⚠ **`parseStatements` IS THE ONLY CALLER, AND MUST STAY SO.** The two direct `parseStatement` call
	// sites — a `try … otherwise return/break/continue` handler and a `match` arm body — are both re-entrant
	// parses INSIDE a statement whose accessors are still claimable; cutting there is exactly the reset that
	// segfaulted. Their leftovers are cut by the enclosing statement's own mark instead.
	//
	// MEASURED, on `Testing/ladders/genborrow.sh <K> pending` — K statements of `s = try arr.get(0)
	// otherwise ""`, an ASSIGNMENT, which takes no borrow a binding can complete and so left one entry behind
	// each time. Before: +0.44M / +0.48M / +2.02M / +7.74M / +24.2M CPU ticks over the parent binary at
	// K = 64 … 1024 — ×4.2 ×3.8 ×3.1 per doubling, and 19% of `phase:parse` at K=1024.
	function dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows(func IrFunction, mark PendingBorrowCount)
		while func.borrowFacts.pending.count() > mark 'dropOne'
			_ = try func.borrowFacts.pending.remove(func.borrowFacts.pending.count() - 1) otherwise panic("dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows: pending.remove OOB at the last index of a non-empty list")
		end 'dropOne'
	end 'dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows'

	// ⭐ **COMPLETE, AT THE BINDING, EVERY BORROW THE INITIALIZER JUST TOOK.** This is where the borrowing NAME
	// and the ACTIVATION point exist, and activating here rather than at the accessor is what makes a mutation
	// inside a `try … otherwise` HANDLER legal: the handler is parsed BEFORE this runs, so its writes sit below
	// the activation and the ordering says "not borrowed yet" by construction rather than by a special case.
	// Both references are emphatic about the same choice (`MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon:425-428`;
	// `BorrowCheckPass.cs:72-80`).
	//
	// Everything pending belongs to THIS statement: `parseStatements` cuts the list back to its entry
	// watermark after every one (`dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows`), so nothing an earlier statement minted and
	// no name took can still be here to be claimed by the wrong binding.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **`liveUntil` IS WHERE THE TWO BORROW KINDS DIFFER, AND IT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE.**
	//
	//   • A `let`/`var` binding passes **`self.pos`** — the activation itself, so a name never read again is
	//     dead the instant it is bound and only a real later use extends it. That is NLL, and it is what
	//     keeps `borrow-nll-allowed` compiling.
	//   • A `for … in` ELEMENT passes the loop's **`end`** — its liveness is LEXICAL, the body's extent, and
	//     ordinary NLL there is a use-after-free:
	//
	//         for it in arr 'l'
	//             wipe(arr)        // the borrow must be live HERE …
	//         end 'l'              // … even though `it` is never read again
	//
	//     The last use of `it` is at or before the write, so last-use liveness would call the borrow dead
	//     and wave the program through — but the ITERATION keeps reading the record on every later trip
	//     (`__managed_count` in the header, `__managed_get_unchecked` for the element), so the freed buffer is read
	//     again. The body's extent IS that liveness, and it is exactly what P1.8 Slice A's lexical lock
	//     already models, so the two halves of one `for` agree instead of one expiring under the other.
	//
	// `requireBindingLive` only ever moves a last use FORWARD, so an ordinary read of the loop variable
	// inside the body cannot shorten the lexical extent back to itself.
	function attachPendingBorrows(func IrFunction, borrower VarInfo, boundValue ValueId, liveUntil TokenIndex)
		var facts = func.borrowFacts
		if facts.pending.count() == 0 'borrowedNothing'
			return
		end 'borrowedNothing'

		// Scanned from the END, because the borrow a name claims is almost always the one its own initializer
		// just minted; and the claimed entry is REMOVED, which keeps the list down to the accessors no name
		// took.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT RUNS ON PAST THE FIRST MATCH, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN BELT-AND-BRACES.** A
		// MERGE retargets EVERY arm's pending borrow onto the one phi (`finalizeMatchGives` calls
		// `retargetPendingBorrow` per give), so `let s = match m 'p' first gives try a.get(0) otherwise ""
		// second gives try b.get(0) otherwise "" end 'p'` leaves TWO entries carrying the phi's id — one per
		// SUBJECT — and `s` genuinely borrows out of both. MEASURED: both `a.clear()` and `b.clear()` are
		// refused, and a `return` here would silently drop whichever arm was minted first, waving one of them
		// through as a use-after-free. `borrow-liveness.md:match-gives-merges-every-arms-borrow` pins it.
		var k = facts.pending.count()
		while k > 0 'findTheValueThisNameBinds'
			k = k - 1
			let pending = pendingAt(facts, index: k)
			if pending.borrowValue != boundValue 'someOtherValue'
				continue
			end 'someOtherValue'

			facts.borrows.push(BorrowRecord.create(pending.subject, borrower: borrower, activationToken: self.pos, borrowLine: pending.borrowLine))
			_ = try facts.pending.remove(k) otherwise panic("attachPendingBorrows: pending.remove OOB at {k}, the index this loop just read")

			// FORWARD-ONLY, the same rule `requireBindingLive` writes this field under. Both writers obey it,
			// so a seed can never pull a live borrow's last use BACKWARDS — which would expire it early, and
			// that is a use-after-free with no diagnostic anywhere.
			if liveUntil > borrower.lastUseToken 'extendsTheBorrowersLife'
				borrower.lastUseToken = liveUntil
			end 'extendsTheBorrowersLife'
		end 'findTheValueThisNameBinds'
	end 'attachPendingBorrows'

	// ⭐⭐ **RECORD A WRITE THAT GOES THROUGH A BORROW SUBJECT — the SECOND predicate over the one derivation.**
	// Called at every door `bindingIsIterationLocked` is, for exactly the same reason: a write can NAME the
	// array through a binding receiver, a field chain, a self-field alias, a rebind of the binding, or a
	// self-field store, and each one alone leaves a use-after-free open.
	//
	// ⚠ It is called at ONE door the lock is not — the MODULE-STORAGE rebind (`storeToGlobal`) — and that is
	// a difference in SUBJECT SPACE, not in the rule: the lock only ever holds bindings `Scope` gave it, so
	// a top-level `var` cannot be iteration-locked, while a borrow out of one is an ordinary borrow (see
	// `borrowSubjectNameAt`, which is where the two subject spaces meet).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `borrows.count()` GATE IS NOT DEFENSIVE, IT IS THE COST.** Two of these doors sit on the hot
	// statement path (every assignment, every container method call). A write ABOVE every borrow's activation
	// can never conflict — the resolver would reject it on `activationToken > site.token` — so a function that
	// has not yet completed a borrow records nothing, allocates nothing, and leaves its facts at the shared
	// empty. That is the same reading `bindingIsIterationLocked`'s own empty check states, and the same reason.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE RECEIVER IS PASSED, BECAUSE THE SUBJECT NAME ALONE CANNOT SPEAK FOR THE BARE SPELLING (W61).**
	// A container method on the enclosing `self` — `set(0, value: b)` inside `type Array`'s own body — names
	// no storage at all, so this door used to return on the empty name and the write went unrecorded. The
	// `managed.`-prefixed spelling of the identical program was refused while the bare one **SEGFAULTED**
	// (measured `0xC0000005`, and `stdlib/Array.maxon` writes the bare spelling throughout). The receiver
	// answers it: `self` stands for every self-field storage of the enclosing receiver, which is the rule
	// `noteReceiverBorrowSubjects` already held for the CALL-ARGUMENT door and now holds for both.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **E3070 HAS TWO HALVES AND THEY ARE RECORDED HERE, OFF ONE FLAG — WHICH IS THE FIX FOR A WHOLE
	// FAMILY OF USE-AFTER-FREES.** The SAME-BODY half is the site pushed below; the CROSS-FUNCTION half is
	// `storageWrittenParamMask`, which `BorrowCheck.mutationSiteWrites` reads at every call site to ask
	// *"does handing this borrowed container to that callee reach a write of it?"*. The storage column used
	// to be set by `noteReceiverWrite` instead, off **E3019's** `mutatesReceiver` answer — so the two halves
	// of one question read two different flags, and any surface whose two answers differ got one half and
	// not the other. `__ManagedList` was exactly that surface (`managedListMethodFreesANode` was narrower than
	// `managedListMethodMutatesReceiver`), and the gap was a MEASURED 0xC0000005: `let node =
	// chain.insertLast(s)` + `wipe(chain)` whose body is `chain.clear()` + `node.value()` compiled clean and
	// faulted. Recorded here, a surface cannot set the cross-function half without the same-body half,
	// because there is only one call and only one answer.
	//
	// ⚖ **THAT MOTIVATING SURFACE IS NOW THE STARKEST CASE OF THE SAME POINT, NOT A RETRACTION OF IT.** USER
	// RULING 2026-08-17 made a `__ManagedList` node handle a SECOND OWNER of a refcounted node, so nothing on
	// that surface can dangle a handle: its E3070 answer went from *narrower* to **EMPTY**, while its E3019
	// answer is unchanged and non-empty. `dispatchManagedListMethod` therefore does not call this function at
	// all — which is only expressible BECAUSE the two halves come off one flag at one door. Under the old
	// arrangement the storage column would still be set from E3019's answer and the surface would over-refuse
	// `a-returned-handle-survives-a-clear`. The program above runs correctly there now; that case is kept and
	// running (`borrow-liveness:a-callee-clearing-a-managed-list-frees-a-node-handle`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COLUMN IS RECORDED BEFORE THE `borrows.count()` GATE, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING.** The
	// gate above asks whether THIS body holds a borrow; the storage column is about the body's CALLERS,
	// which hold theirs somewhere this function cannot see. Below the gate it would be set only for a
	// function that happened to borrow something itself.
	//
	// The mask is passed rather than re-derived: it is `subjectStorageMask(subjectName)`, which every caller
	// already computed for `noteReceiverWrite`'s W117 union, and computing it twice per mutating call would
	// pay a scope lookup on the hot statement path for an answer already in hand.
	function noteBorrowSubjectWrite(func IrFunction, receiver ValueId, subjectName ByteArray, receiverStorage ParamPositionMask, display ByteArray, anchor Token, mutatesSubject bool)
		if not mutatesSubject 'notAWrite'
			return
		end 'notAWrite'

		func.storageWrittenParamMask = func.storageWrittenParamMask or receiverStorage

		if not self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'nothingBorrowedYet'
			return
		end 'nothingBorrowedYet'

		self.noteReceiverBorrowSubjects(func, callee: EmptySourceText, display: display, anchor: anchor, argColumn: 0, id: receiver, subjectName: subjectName)
	end 'noteBorrowSubjectWrite'

	// ⭐ **THE REBIND DOOR — `arr = other`, `self.items = other`, and a top-level `var`'s store.** Separate
	// from the container door above because a rebind has no RECEIVER to stand for a storage: its target
	// names one or there is nothing to record, so there is no fan-out to ask for. The write's token is the
	// same derivation either way (`containerWriteToken`, reached through the shared recorder), and its
	// answer for a rebind is the cursor itself unless the RHS opens with a group — which is the RHS the
	// write still happens after, so the later token is right there too.
	function noteBorrowSubjectRebind(func IrFunction, subjectName ByteArray, anchor Token)
		if subjectName.isEmpty() 'notAWriteThroughANamedBinding'
			return
		end 'notAWriteThroughANamedBinding'

		self.recordContainerMutationSite(func, subjectName: subjectName, display: ReassignMutationDisplay, anchor: anchor)
	end 'noteBorrowSubjectRebind'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TOKEN A CONTAINER WRITE HAPPENS AT IS THE CLOSING `)` OF ITS ARGUMENT LIST, NOT THE METHOD
	// NAME (W61)** — and the difference is a whole class of correct programs. `xs.set(w, value: v)` evaluates
	// `v` and only THEN writes, and every borrowed value a container write ACCEPTS has its reference taken
	// before the call: `moveManagedValueInto` promotes a borrowed String to an owned copy,
	// `referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement` retains a borrowed opaque `T` through `retainFunc@64`, and a borrowed
	// struct is refused outright. So a borrow whose LAST USE is the write's own argument cannot be read after
	// the write, and the record the write displaces is not the one it stores.
	//
	// Recorded at the method name instead, such a borrow read as LIVE and `let v = try managed.get(i) …` +
	// `try managed.set(w, value: v) …` — the move-an-element-down step every one of
	// `stdlib/helpers/sort/`'s partition and merge bodies is built from — was refused with no sound program
	// to replace it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CALL-ARGUMENT DOOR ALREADY RECORDED THE LATER TOKEN** (`recordCallArgMutationSite` runs from
	// `parseCallArgs`, past the argument), so this is the two doors agreeing on when a write happens rather
	// than a new rule — and their disagreement was the odd one out.
	function containerWriteToken() returns TokenIndex
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'noArgumentList'
			return self.pos
		end 'noArgumentList'

		return self.matchingRightParenOf(self.pos)
	end 'containerWriteToken'

	// ⭐ **THE CALL-ARGUMENT DOOR: a borrow subject handed to a CALLEE.** `grow(arr)` destroys the element the caller
	// borrowed exactly as `arr.clear()` does (measured: the oracle reports E3070 and shv2 segfaulted), and it
	// is the one write the parser cannot settle — whether `grow` mutates what it was handed depends on its
	// BODY, possibly in another file. So the site is recorded with the callee and the argument's column and
	// SemanticCheck settles it against the whole-program `ParamMutationSummary` (`mutationSiteWrites`), the
	// same summary E3019's call-site half reads. Never a second copy of "which parameters does this callee
	// write" — that question already has one home.
	//
	// The diagnostic anchors on the CALL, not the argument, which is the oracle's anchor (measured `10:2` for
	// `\tgrow(arr)` — the callee token) and the anchor `checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam` already uses.
	function noteBorrowSubjectCallArg(func IrFunction, callee ByteArray, anchor Token, argColumn ParamCount, argLabel ByteArray, valueStart TokenIndex)
		if not self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'nothingBorrowedYet'
			return
		end 'nothingBorrowedYet'

		// ⚠ **AN UNNAMED CALLEE IS A WITNESS DISPATCH, AND THE QUESTION IS UNASKABLE — NOT MERELY UNASKED.**
		// The conflict this door records is "does the CALLEE write the storage this argument names?", answered
		// from the by-callee mutation summary. Under dictionary-passing a witness dispatch resolves to no one
		// impl, so there is no callee to key on (`dispatchWitnessMethod` passes empty, for the same reason
		// it discards `argImmutableNames` — E3019 cannot ask there either). Refused HERE, explicitly, because
		// recording a site whose summary lookup is guaranteed to miss would be a silent no-op wearing the shape
		// of a check.
		if callee.isEmpty() 'noSingleCallee'
			return
		end 'noSingleCallee'

		// ⭐ **A FIELD CHAIN COUNTS, NOT ONLY A BARE NAME**, and it has to: `wipe(b.items)` destroys the
		// element a `for it in b.items` — or a `let s = try b.items.get(0)` — is holding, and it is the one
		// route P1.8 Slice A's lock structurally cannot reach (its five doors all NAME the array; this one
		// hands it to a callee). The predicate is `sourceNamesABase`, THE shared walk `for … in` decides
		// lockability with, so an argument shape the lock understands is an argument shape this door
		// understands. E3019's own bare-name rule is untouched — it asks `argWasBareName`, a different
		// question about a different blame.
		if not self.sourceNamesABase(valueStart) 'notANameableStorage'
			return
		end 'notANameableStorage'

		self.recordCallArgMutationSite(func, callee: callee, anchor: anchor, argColumn: argColumn, argLabel: argLabel, subjectName: self.borrowSubjectNameAt(valueStart))
	end 'noteBorrowSubjectCallArg'

	// ⭐ **THE CONTAINER-WRITE RECORDER — the site shape whose write the PARSER already settled**, so it
	// carries no callee and `mutationSiteWrites` waves it straight through. The `functionHoldsABorrow` gate
	// is repeated here for `recordCallArgMutationSite`'s reason: this is the authority, and each caller's own
	// test is the cost gate that keeps a `Scope` walk off the hot statement path.
	function recordContainerMutationSite(func IrFunction, subjectName ByteArray, display ByteArray, anchor Token)
		if subjectName.isEmpty() 'namesNoStorage'
			return
		end 'namesNoStorage'

		if not self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'nothingBorrowedYet'
			return
		end 'nothingBorrowedYet'

		self.pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder(func, site: MutationSite.createContainerWrite(self.borrowSubjectIdOf(func, subjectName: subjectName), subjectName: borrowSubjectBlameName(subjectName), token: self.containerWriteToken(), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column, display: display))
	end 'recordContainerMutationSite'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SITES ARE KEPT IN TOKEN ORDER, AND SINCE W61 THAT IS NO LONGER THE ORDER THEY ARRIVE IN.** A
	// container write's token is the closing `)` of its argument list (`containerWriteToken`), so an OUTER
	// write is recorded first and happens LAST: `xs.set(0, value: try ys.pop() otherwise "")` files `xs` at
	// the outer `)` and then `ys` at the inner one, which is a DECREASE. The resolver's walk is linear only
	// because the sites are non-decreasing, and it panics on a decrease rather than silently stopping to
	// report conflicts (`checkFunctionBorrowConflicts`) — so the order is restored at the ONE push instead
	// of being sorted out later or asserted away.
	//
	// The scan is backwards from the end, so a site that already belongs last — every site of a body with no
	// nested container write, which is nearly all of them — costs one comparison. Equal tokens keep arrival
	// order, which is what a receiver fan-out's several sites want.
	function pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder(func IrFunction, site MutationSite)
		var facts = func.borrowFacts
		var at = facts.mutations.count()
		while at > 0 'findTheInsertionPoint'
			let previousToken = (try facts.mutations.get(at - 1) otherwise panic("pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder: mutations.get OOB at {at - 1} inside count()")).token
			if previousToken <= site.token 'belongsHere'
				break
			end 'belongsHere'

			at = at - 1
		end 'findTheInsertionPoint'

		if at == facts.mutations.count() 'appends'
			facts.mutations.push(site)
			return
		end 'appends'

		facts.mutations.insert(at, value: site)
	end 'pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder'

	// The recorder every call-ARGUMENT door shares, taking the subject ALREADY RESOLVED — which is the whole
	// difference from `noteBorrowSubjectCallArg` above, and why it is a separate function rather than an
	// overload: the SOURCE's arguments have to be shape-tested to find a subject, while the prepended
	// RECEIVER was handed one by the base that produced it, and `self` supplies several. One record, one
	// column convention, one settlement against the storage mask.
	//
	// The `functionHoldsABorrow` test is paid TWICE on the source-argument path (its caller asks first, to
	// keep a `Scope` walk off the hot statement path — see `functionHoldsABorrow`). That is deliberate: this
	// is the authority, and the caller's is the cost gate.
	function recordCallArgMutationSite(func IrFunction, callee ByteArray, anchor Token, argColumn ParamCount, argLabel ByteArray, subjectName ByteArray)
		if subjectName.isEmpty() 'namesNoStorage'
			return
		end 'namesNoStorage'

		if not self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'nothingBorrowedYet'
			return
		end 'nothingBorrowedYet'

		self.pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder(func, site: MutationSite.createCallArgWrite(self.borrowSubjectIdOf(func, subjectName: subjectName), subjectName: borrowSubjectBlameName(subjectName), token: self.pos, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column, callee: callee, argColumn: argColumn, argLabel: argLabel))
	end 'recordCallArgMutationSite'

	// The index of the `end` token that closes the block statement opening at `startIndex`,
	// found by counting block openers against `end`s — the same depth-counted token walk
	// `skipBalancedParens` does for `(`/`)`, and run for the same reason: the answer is needed
	// BEFORE the real parse reaches it. The cursor is not moved.
	//
	// `continuesAtElse` is the ONE difference between the two constructs that call this, and it
	// is exactly the fact that a loop's extent ends at its first depth-0 `end` while an `if`
	// chain's does not:
	//   • `while` passes FALSE — the first depth-0 `end` closes the loop.
	//   • `if` passes TRUE — at each depth-0 `end` the scan PEEKS past the optional block label
	//     (`elseFollowsEnd`); a following `else` opens the next branch (a plain `else`, or the
	//     `if` of an `else if`), re-opens the depth, and the scan carries on, so the extent
	//     spans the then body AND every `else`/`else if` branch through the final `end`.
	//
	// Deliberately TOLERANT of a malformed tail, like `scanFunctionSignatures`: an unterminated
	// construct yields the end of the token stream rather than a diagnostic. That
	// over-approximates the extent, which is the SAFE direction (a carried set is never too
	// SMALL — see `parseWhileStatement` / `assignedBindingsIn`), and the real parse runs next
	// and reports the unexpected EOF with a position.
	//
	// ⭐ **A NESTED CONSTRUCT IS STEPPED OVER, NOT WALKED INTO, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THIS LINEAR.** The
	// walk itself is unchanged — the depth counting, the `elseFollowsEnd` continuation and the tolerant
	// tail all still decide the answer — but a construct opening at depth ≥ 1 has its whole extent skipped
	// in ONE array read (`matchingEndOf`). That cannot change the answer: inside a nested pair the depth
	// never returns to 0, so neither the `depth == 0` test nor `elseFollowsEnd` could have fired there, and
	// the pair contributes net zero to the depth on the way through.
	//
	// Walking IN is what made this scan cost `O(depth × tokens)`: a construct nested D deep had its tokens
	// re-walked by all D scans enclosing it. MEASURED on `Testing/ladders/gennest.sh`, which holds
	// `depth × stmtsPerLevel` fixed so the program barely changes size while the nesting doubles: at a
	// constant 1024 statements `phase:parse` CPU climbed ×1.20 ×1.33 ×1.50 ×1.74 per depth doubling, and
	// with this skip it climbs ×1.11 ×1.20 ×1.39 ×1.40 — HALF the depth term, to the tick. The other half
	// is `assignedBindingsIn`, whose own scan still walks token by token and so still re-reads a nested
	// construct's tokens once per enclosing construct. ⚠ **THAT HALF IS STILL THERE.** Inverting it — a
	// per-file `name → ascending assignment sites` index with a binary-searched `isAssignedIn` predicate —
	// was measured at `phase:parse` ×3.5 on the corpus back when its per-test constant was multiplied by a
	// Θ(bindings × constructs) walk (rung 5: 10.0G → 34.5G CPU ticks). That walk is GONE as of the
	// 2026-07-28 entry in `docs/optimization-log.md`, so the measurement no longer stands either way and
	// the question is open again; it is bounded by nesting DEPTH, which `LoopNestBase` doubles and
	// `gennest.sh` isolates.
	function blockStatementEndIndex(startIndex TokenIndex, continuesAtElse bool) returns TokenIndex
		self.buildBlockExtentIndex()

		let count = self.tokens.count()
		var depth = 0
		var i = startIndex
		while i < count 'scan'
			if self.opensBlockAt(i) 'open'
				if depth > 0 'nested'
					// `i` lands on the nested construct's own `end`; the step below moves past it, so this
					// scan never sees the pair at all. An UNTERMINATED nested construct answers `count`,
					// which ends the walk exactly where walking into it would have.
					i = self.matchingEndOf(i)
				end 'nested' else 'ownOpener'
					depth = depth + 1
				end 'ownOpener'
			end 'open' else if self.closesBlockAt(i) 'close'
				depth = depth - 1
				if depth == 0 and (not continuesAtElse or not self.elseFollowsEnd(i)) 'matched'
					return i
				end 'matched'
			end 'close'

			i = i + 1
		end 'scan'
		return count
	end 'blockStatementEndIndex'

	// Does an `else` follow the `end` at `endIndex` — i.e. does an `if` chain continue past
	// it? Skips the ONE optional block label (`end 'x' else …`) that may sit between them; a
	// bare `end else …` has none. Peeking exactly one charLiteral is enough because an `end`
	// carries at most a single label, and the token after it is either `else` or the next
	// statement. This is the one place `blockStatementEndIndex` decides an `if` chain
	// continues, so a missed `else` under-approximates the extent — which the drift guard
	// converts to a loud panic rather than a silent lost phi.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE `else` IT FINDS MUST NOT BE A NAME**, the same care `elseFollowsBlockEnd` — its BACKWARD
	// twin — takes by asking `closesBlockAt` rather than testing `TokenKind.end`. `else` is a legal case name
	// and a legal declared name (D8), so `end 'x' else gives …` spells a labelled closer followed by a match
	// ARM named `else`. Left raw, one of the two twins knew the exclusion and the other did not — and the
	// failure is a MIS-PREDICTED EXTENT, which is a panic rather than a compile error. I could not construct a
	// reaching program (an arm is newline-separated from the `end` above it, and a newline breaks the
	// adjacency this needs), so this is the asymmetry closed, not a repro fixed.
	function elseFollowsEnd(endIndex TokenIndex) returns bool
		var next = endIndex + 1
		if self.kindAt(next) == TokenKind.charLiteral 'skipLabel'
			next = next + 1
		end 'skipLabel'

		return self.kindAt(next) == TokenKind.else and not self.keywordIsAName(next)
	end 'elseFollowsEnd'

	// Does the token at `index` open a block that a matching `end` closes?
	//
	// THIS IS MAXON'S BLOCK STRUCTURE, WRITTEN DOWN A SECOND TIME — `parseStatement` and
	// `parseBlockBody` own the first copy. It is kept honest by the drift panic in
	// `parseWhileStatement` rather than by hope: get this wrong and that panic fires, loudly,
	// on the first program that nests the missing construct in a loop.
	//
	// ⭐ `if`/`else` ARE THE SUBTLE PAIR, because the inline conditional `<t> if <c> else <f>`
	// (P1.5-A2c) spells BOTH keywords in EXPRESSION position with NO closing `end`. Counting a
	// ternary `if`/`else` as a block opener over-counts the depth and runs the extent past the real
	// `end`, so the drift guard fires (`let x = a if c else b` inside a loop body is enough). A block
	// `if`/`else` is told from a ternary one by POSITION — the same split the reference bootstrap
	// draws (2-Parser.cs `SkipToMatchingEnd`):
	//   • a block `if` is at STATEMENT START — the previous token is a `newline` (a fresh statement)
	//     or an `else` (an `else if` chain); a ternary `if` follows an operand (`ifBeginsStatement`).
	//   • a block `else` closes a then-branch — it follows that branch's `end` and its one optional
	//     label (`elseFollowsBlockEnd`); a ternary `else` follows the condition operand.
	// `while` and `for` (statement-only) and `match` (statement OR expression) always close with their
	// own `end`, so all three unconditionally open a block.
	//
	// `else`'s pre-ternary subtlety is still live: a plain `else 'label'` opens a block body of its
	// own, but an `else if` does NOT — the else branch simply IS a nested `if` statement
	// (parseElseBody), and that `if` is the opener whose `end` closes it.
	//
	// `otherwise` is the other subtle one: only the BLOCK forms of a `try … otherwise` open a
	// block (`otherwiseOpensBlock`). The rest (`ignore`, a fallback value, a single-statement
	// return/break/continue/throw/panic) open nothing, so counting a bare `otherwise` would
	// leave an `end` unaccounted for — the very miscount OPEN #62 was.
	//
	// ⚠ A construct added here almost certainly has a CONDITION or SCRUTINEE, and an expression can build an
	// owned temporary — so it owes that temporary a release too. See `drainPendingTemps`, which keeps the
	// list of regions that settle their own.
	function opensBlockAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		let kind = self.kindAt(index)
		// Asked through the SHARED set rather than left to the chain below to answer by exhaustion, so that
		// set is a real dependency of this routine and not a comment about it — see
		// `kindSpellsBlockStructure`.
		if not kindSpellsBlockStructure(kind) 'notBlockStructure'
			return false
		end 'notBlockStructure'

		// `end` only ever CLOSES — `closesBlockAt` owns it — so it opens nothing, whatever it names. Answered
		// HERE rather than by falling off the end of the chain below, which is what lets that tail be a loud
		// drift panic instead of a silent `false`.
		if kind == TokenKind.end 'closerOnly'
			return false
		end 'closerOnly'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE KEYWORD-AS-A-NAME EXCLUSION, ONCE, AHEAD OF EVERY ARM — and it is one check rather than
		// one per arm because the arm that FORGOT it was a reachable compiler PANIC.** Three arms spelled
		// `not self.keywordIsAName(index)` for themselves and the fourth, `otherwise`, answered purely from
		// its position test. But `otherwiseOpensBlock`'s caught-error binding form is `otherwise ( <ident> )
		// <label>` — which a keyword-named METHOD CALL at the end of a block header spells character for
		// character. `while Ops.otherwise(i) 'loop'` (D8 made `.otherwise` a legal member name) and
		// `while k == Kw.otherwise 'loop'` (a keyword-named enum CASE member, legal since
		// `keyword-named-case-members.md`) both opened a block nothing closed, and `assertScanAligned` took
		// the compiler down on a correct program. It is the same shape that spec already pins for `end`
		// (`match Kw.end 'm'` — a member plus a block label spelling a labelled closer); `otherwise` has a
		// labelled form too, and the exclusion had not reached it.
		//
		// ⚠ So the exclusion is NOT an arm's business. An arm decides whether this POSITION opens a block;
		// whether the token is block structure AT ALL is one question, asked once, that a new arm cannot
		// fail to inherit. `closesBlockAt` is the same single check for the closing half.
		if self.keywordIsAName(index) 'aName'
			return false
		end 'aName'

		if kind == TokenKind.while or kind == TokenKind.match or kind == TokenKind.for 'alwaysOpens'
			return true
		end 'alwaysOpens'

		if kind == TokenKind.if 'ifKw'
			return self.ifBeginsStatement(index)
		end 'ifKw'

		if kind == TokenKind.otherwise 'tryBlock'
			return self.otherwiseOpensBlock(index)
		end 'tryBlock'

		// `try` is the third position-sensitive one: only the BLOCK form spells a LABEL after the keyword, and
		// a label is told from a character-literal RECEIVER (`try 'A'.asciiValue()`) by the `.` behind it — see
		// `tryOpensBlockAt`, which owns that rule and which this comment used to contradict by asserting no call
		// could begin with a charLiteral. It is the same discriminator `parseStatement` routes on, asked once
		// there rather than restated here.
		if kind == TokenKind.try 'tryKw'
			return self.tryOpensBlockAt(index)
		end 'tryKw'

		if kind == TokenKind.else 'elseKw'
			// An `else if` opens NOTHING of its own: the else branch simply IS a nested `if` statement, and
			// that `if` is the opener whose `end` closes it.
			if self.kindAt(index + 1) == TokenKind.if 'elseIf'
				return false
			end 'elseIf'

			return self.elseFollowsBlockEnd(index)
		end 'elseKw'

		// ⚠ **THE DRIFT GUARD `kindSpellsBlockStructure`'s HEADER PROMISES.** Every kind that set admits has
		// an arm above, so this is unreachable — and a kind ADDED to that set without an arm here lands
		// loudly, on the first program that spells it, rather than silently opening no block at all.
		panic("opensBlockAt: '{kind.rawValue}' spells block structure but no arm here answers for it — a kind added to `kindSpellsBlockStructure` needs an arm here too")
	end 'opensBlockAt'

	// Does the token at `index` CLOSE a block — `opensBlockAt`'s counterpart, and its own predicate for the
	// same reason `assignsAt` is one. Every token scan in this file that counts block depth has to make the
	// keyword-as-a-NAME exclusion (`end` is a legal enum case name, so `end then …` is an arm and `Kw.end`
	// is a member read — neither is a closer), and eight of them had spelled it out independently. Spelled
	// once, a scan cannot be taught the opener rule and miss the closer rule — which would leave a depth
	// counter that never comes back down.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SCAN THAT TESTS `TokenKind.end` ITSELF IS THIS BUG WAITING**, which is why the two that did
	// (the sweep's `blockDepthAfter` and the match arm loop's end-of-arms test) now ask here instead.
	function closesBlockAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.end and not self.keywordIsAName(index)
	end 'closesBlockAt'

	// Is the `if` at `index` a block-opening STATEMENT `if` rather than the `if` of an inline
	// conditional `<t> if <c> else <f>`? A block `if` sits at statement start: the preceding token is
	// a `newline` (a new statement) or an `else` (the `if` of an `else if` chain). A ternary `if`
	// follows an operand, so neither holds. Index 0 has nothing before it and cannot be a ternary, so
	// it counts as statement start. Mirrors the reference bootstrap (2-Parser.cs `SkipToMatchingEnd`:
	// a block `if` iff `prevWasNewline || prev == else`).
	function ifBeginsStatement(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		if index == 0 'atStart'
			return true
		end 'atStart'

		let prev = self.kindAt(index - 1)
		return prev == TokenKind.newline or prev == TokenKind.else
	end 'ifBeginsStatement'

	// Does the `else` at `index` close an if/else then-branch (a block `else`) rather than tail an
	// inline conditional? A block `else` follows the then-branch's `end` and its ONE optional block
	// label — the backward twin of `elseFollowsEnd` (`end 'x' else` or bare `end else`). A ternary
	// `else` follows the condition operand, so the token before it (past an optional label) is not a
	// block-closing `end`. Index 0 cannot be an `else`, so it is never a block `else`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS `closesBlockAt`, NOT "is the previous token an `end`"**, and the difference is a
	// reachable PANIC: a ternary whose CONDITION ends in a keyword-named case member puts a literal
	// `end` immediately before the `else` (`let x = 1 if i == 0 or Kw.end == Kw.end else 2`). Read as
	// a block `else`, that ternary opens a block nothing closes and the enclosing loop's extent scan
	// runs past its `end` — `assertScanAligned` fires. `keyword-named-case-members.md` pins it.
	// (`elseFollowsEnd`, the forward twin, needs no such care: it is only ever asked at a token
	// `closesBlockAt` has already accepted.)
	function elseFollowsBlockEnd(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		if index == 0 'atStart'
			return false
		end 'atStart'

		var prev = index - 1
		if self.kindAt(prev) == TokenKind.charLiteral 'skipLabel'
			if prev == 0 'noRoom'
				return false
			end 'noRoom'
			prev = prev - 1
		end 'skipLabel'

		return self.closesBlockAt(prev)
	end 'elseFollowsBlockEnd'

	// Does the `otherwise` at `index` begin a BLOCK-form handler — one closed by its own `end`?
	// Two block forms exist: `otherwise 'label' … end` (a bare charLiteral label) and
	// `otherwise (e) 'label' … end` (a caught-error binding). The NON-block forms open nothing:
	// `otherwise ignore`, a fallback VALUE, and the single-statement `return`/`break`/`continue`/
	// `throw`/`panic`. This is `parseOtherwiseHandler`'s split written down a second time (the drift
	// guard keeps them honest), but with ONE extra care it does not need: `otherwise` IS a legal
	// enum case name (`tokenCanBeAName`), so a keyword-spelled match arm `otherwise(x) then` also
	// leads with `otherwise (`. The binding form is told apart by its full SHAPE — `( identifier )`
	// followed by the label charLiteral — which a payload case (`( bindings ) then/gives`, or
	// multi-binding `( a , b )`) never matches. `parseOtherwiseHandler` needs no such check because
	// by the time it runs the leading `try` has already disambiguated the context.
	function otherwiseOpensBlock(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		let next = self.kindAt(index + 1)
		if next == TokenKind.charLiteral 'labelForm'
			return true
		end 'labelForm'

		return next == TokenKind.leftParen and self.kindAt(index + 2) == TokenKind.identifier and self.kindAt(index + 3) == TokenKind.rightParen and self.kindAt(index + 4) == TokenKind.charLiteral
	end 'otherwiseOpensBlock'

	// ⭐ **THE BLOCK-FORM `try` — A LABEL AFTER THE KEYWORD, AND A `.` IS WHAT TELLS IT FROM A CHARACTER.**
	// `try 'work' … end 'work'` opens a block. It is the SAME discriminator `parseStatement` routes the two
	// forms on and `parseTry` refuses the block form in expression position by — asked here so the extent scan
	// and the grammar cannot disagree about which `try` owns an `end`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THIS ROUTINE ONCE CLAIMED THE TWO FORMS WERE DISJOINT ON THE TOKEN ALONE — *"no call can begin
	// with a charLiteral"* — AND THAT WAS FALSE THE MOMENT A CHARACTER LITERAL GREW METHODS.**
	// `try 'A'.asciiValue() otherwise 0` is a call that begins with one, and MEASURED it was **E3059**,
	// *"a block-form `try 'label' … end` groups statements and yields no value"* — a diagnostic about a
	// construct the program does not contain, over a program the oracle runs and prints 65 for.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE DISAMBIGUATOR IS THE `.` AND NOTHING ELSE NEEDS TO BE.** A block LABEL is always followed by a
	// STATEMENT, so the lexer has emitted a `newline` behind it; it can never be followed by `.`. A character
	// literal used as a `try` TARGET must be a call, so it is always followed by `.`. One token of lookahead
	// past the label separates them, and a character literal that is NOT a receiver (`try 'A' otherwise 0`)
	// is not a call either — it stays the block form, and `parseTry`'s own refusal names it.
	function tryOpensBlockAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.charLiteralOpensTryBlockAt(index + 1)
	end 'tryOpensBlockAt'

	// The label-vs-receiver test itself, over the token AFTER `try`. Split out because the two askers stand in
	// different places: the extent scans hold the `try`'s index, and `parseTry` has already consumed it.
	function charLiteralOpensTryBlockAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.charLiteral and self.kindAt(index + 1) != TokenKind.dot
	end 'charLiteralOpensTryBlockAt'

	// Is the keyword at `index` being used as a NAME rather than as block structure? Read off the memo
	// `buildBlockExtentIndex`'s walk recorded — see `blockKeywordNames` and `keywordIsANameAt`, which is
	// where the question is actually decided and why it cannot be decided here.
	//
	// ⚡ A BISECT of the ascending memo, and the `count()` read is the whole cost for very nearly every
	// file: only a file that spells a keyword where block structure could stand has any entry at all.
	function keywordIsAName(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		self.buildBlockExtentIndex()
		return ascendingTokenIndexHolds(self.blockKeywordNames, index: index)
	end 'keywordIsAName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DERIVATION BEHIND `keywordIsAName`, ASKED ONCE PER TOKEN BY THE BLOCK-EXTENT WALK.** It is
	// separate from the reader above for one reason: **two of the four shapes below are rules about a token
	// standing at the top level of a `match` BODY, and no bounded lookaround can see that.** The walk can —
	// its opener stack holds exactly the blocks still open BEHIND the cursor — so it supplies `inMatchBody`
	// and records the verdict, and every later scan reads that one answer instead of guessing again.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE SCOPING IS NOT TIDINESS — AN UNSCOPED ARM LOOKAHEAD MISREAD A CONDITION AS A MATCH ARM AND
	// TOOK THE COMPILER DOWN.** The rule used to say a control keyword is *never* followed by an arm
	// separator, *"`if` takes a condition, `while` a condition"* — and D8 falsified it in a file this one
	// never touches: a condition may BEGIN with a keyword-named binding, so
	// `function twosComplementDistance(to ByteOffset, from ByteOffset)` makes `if to >= from 'forward'`
	// (`Targets/Shared/GlobalDataTable.maxon`) an `if` whose next token is `to`. Read as an arm separator,
	// that `if` opened no block, its `end` closed one that never opened, and `assertScanAligned` fired on a
	// program the bootstrap compiles. `while`, `match` and `for` take the same shape, and `then`/`gives`/
	// `upto`/`or` are equally declarable. Scoped to a match body the premise is TRUE again, because a case
	// name can stand nowhere else.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN ENUM CASE MAY BE SPELLED WITH A KEYWORD** (`enum TokenType { function if else end … }`,
	// keyword-as-case-name.md), so `if`/`else`/`end`/`while`/`match`/`for` can appear in a program as
	// an ordinary NAME. The block-extent token scans (`opensBlockAt`/`closesBlockAt`, and every scan
	// that counts depth through them) read those keywords as block STRUCTURE, so each place a keyword
	// is a name has to be excluded here or a scan miscounts — the construct's `end` is mispredicted
	// and `assertScanAligned` fires (or the arm loop stops early, or the declaration sweep records a
	// local as a top-level binding).
	//
	// **FOUR SHAPES, ONE FACT, so ONE predicate** — a divergence would leave the opener rule taught and
	// the closer rule not, or one shape taught and the next not. The first two are ARM shapes and run only
	// under `inMatchBody`; the last two hold wherever the keyword stands:
	//
	//   • **A MATCH-ARM CASE NAME — the LOOKAHEAD.** `if gives …`, `end then …`, `else to critical then
	//     …`. Inside a match body a keyword immediately followed by `gives`/`then`/`to`/`upto`/`or` is a
	//     case name: an arm is the only thing that stands at that level, and a `match` header's own
	//     scrutinee sits BEFORE the body rather than in it.
	//     ⛔ **OUTSIDE a match body this lookahead is WRONG, and the header above records the panic it
	//     cost.** Its old premise — *"a control keyword is never followed by an arm separator in real
	//     control flow"* — reads `if`/`while`/`match`/`for` as taking a condition or a scrutinee, and a
	//     condition may BEGIN with a keyword-named binding (D8). `if to >= from 'forward'` is the shape.
	//   • **A MATCH-ARM CASE NAME CARRYING A PAYLOAD BINDING LIST — the SAME LOOKAHEAD, PAST THE LIST.**
	//     `end(m) then …`, `while(a, b) gives …`. The separator sits after the list, not next to the
	//     keyword, so a lookahead of ONE token misses it entirely. ⚠ Both directions were REACHABLE and
	//     both refused a program the oracle compiles and runs: `end(m) then …` ended the arm loop, so the
	//     match reported `E2026 … not exhaustive, missing: end, omega`; `while(m) then …` opened a block
	//     nothing closed and took the compiler down in `assertScanAligned`.
	//     ⚠⚠ **THE SEPARATOR SET HERE IS DELIBERATELY NARROWER — `then`/`gives` ONLY — AND WIDENING IT
	//     BREAKS THE STDLIB.** A PARENTHESIZED condition is legal Maxon and is followed by whatever the
	//     expression around it needs: `if (driveByte >= 65 and driveByte <= 90) or (driveByte >= 97 …)
	//     'isDrive'` (`stdlib/FilePath.maxon`) puts `or` right past the group, and `while (try …
	//     otherwise 0) > 0 'loop'` puts an operator there. `then` and `gives` are the only two
	//     separators a payload-carrying arm can take AND the only two no real `if`/`while`/`match`/`for`
	//     header can be followed by. `to`/`upto` belong to scalar RANGE patterns, which carry no
	//     payload. ⚠ **`or` REMAINS THE ONE GENUINE GAP, and it is now a gap rather than a moot point**:
	//     `end(m) or omega then …` reads as a closer, and since payload bindings on an or-arm became
	//     legal (`requireOrArmPayloadSlotsAgree`) nothing else refuses that program. Widening the set to
	//     admit it is what the stdlib line above forbids, so a KEYWORD-named case binding a payload as
	//     the first alternative of an `or`-chain is still not spellable — the narrow shape a later rung
	//     owes, and one no spec reaches today. ⚠ **AND IT FAILS AS A MISCOUNT, NOT AS A DIAGNOSTIC**: the
	//     scan misreads the arm before any binding check runs, so it surfaces as the two faults this list
	//     already records — a bogus `E2026 … not exhaustive` when the arm loop ends early, or the
	//     `assertScanAligned` compiler PANIC when a block is opened that nothing closes.
	//     `keyword-named-case-members.md` pins the payload arms AND the parenthesized-condition shapes
	//     that forbid the wider set.
	//   • **A LABEL — the `:` LOOKAHEAD.** `weigh(i, for: 3)`. See the arm below.
	//   • **A MEMBER NAME — the LOOKBEHIND.** `Kw.end`, `Kw.while`, `obj.end()`. A `.` is followed by a
	//     member name and by nothing else in this grammar (Maxon has no `..` range and a float literal
	//     is one token), so a keyword whose PREVIOUS token is a `.` is a name, whatever follows it.
	//     ⚠ This half is what a reachable compiler PANIC was made of, in BOTH directions:
	//     `keyword-named-case-members.md` pins them. `Kw.end` read as a CLOSER predicted a construct's
	//     `end` too early; `Kw.while`/`Kw.match`/`Kw.for` read as OPENERS predicted it too late. Worst
	//     of all is `match Kw.end 'm'`, whose scrutinee plus the match's own block label spell `end 'm'`
	//     — character for character a labelled closing `end`, and only the preceding `.` says otherwise.
	//     (`Kw.if`/`Kw.else`/`Kw.otherwise` never panicked, because those three are not unconditional
	//     block markers and their POSITION tests already refused a member — an unrelated reason, which
	//     is why they are pinned too.)
	//   • **A DECLARED NAME — an INDEX, not a lookaround (D8).** `function while(n Integer)` names a function
	//     `while`, and `function f(end Integer)` names a parameter `end`; both are legal
	//     (`keyword-as-a-declared-name.md`), and both are exactly as dangerous here as a case member was.
	//     Unexcluded, the first opens a block nothing closes and the second closes one that never opened —
	//     so the file's whole extent index shifts and the declaration sweep's depth never returns to zero.
	//     ⚠ **THIS ONE CANNOT BE A LOOKAROUND, WHICH IS WHY IT COSTS AN INDEX.** The function NAME could be
	//     (`functionDeclarationAt(index - 1)`), but the PARAMETER cannot: `( match x` is a parenthesized
	//     match EXPRESSION and `( match X` is a parameter named `match`, and those are the same token shape
	//     — only the enclosing list's OWNER separates them, which no bounded lookaround can reach. See
	//     `buildDeclaredNameIndex`, whose one linear pass reads paren matching and `functionDeclarationAt`
	//     and NOTHING about block structure, so it cannot be circular with the extent index that reads it.
	function keywordIsANameAt(index TokenIndex, inMatchBody bool) returns bool
		let next = self.kindAt(index + 1)

		// The two MATCH-ARM shapes, and they are asked only where an arm can stand. Everything below is
		// position-independent and stays unscoped.
		if inMatchBody 'armShapes'
			if next == TokenKind.gives or next == TokenKind.then or next == TokenKind.to or next == TokenKind.upto or next == TokenKind.or 'matchCaseName'
				return true
			end 'matchCaseName'

			// ⚠ **A POSITIVE ANSWER ONLY — it must FALL THROUGH to the lookbehind, never answer `false` for
			// it.** A keyword followed by `(` can equally be a member READ of a keyword-named case that
			// carries a payload (`Kw.end(20, b: 22)`), which has the `.` before it AND the `(` after it.
			// Returning the separator test's verdict outright hid the lookbehind from exactly those reads —
			// measured, mid-review: `if tagOf(Kw.end(20, b: 22)) == 42 'ok'` took `assertScanAligned` down.
			if next == TokenKind.leftParen 'payloadBindingList'
				// Asked of the SHARED paren walk, so a nested group inside the list (or inside a parenthesized
				// condition this must NOT claim) is matched by the one rule `skipBalancedParens` uses.
				let separator = self.kindAt(self.matchingRightParenOf(index + 1) + 1)
				if separator == TokenKind.then or separator == TokenKind.gives 'armSeparator'
					return true
				end 'armSeparator'
			end 'payloadBindingList'
		end 'armShapes'

		// ⭐ **A LABEL — the OTHER lookahead, and it is a `:` (D8).** `weigh(i, for: 3, match: 4)` labels a
		// keyword-named PARAMETER. (An ARGUMENT label is the only reader: a struct literal's `Self{x: 3}` keys
		// on a FIELD name, and a field DECLARATION admits no keyword — `var end as Integer` is
		// `E2010 Expected identifier but got 'end'` — so there is no keyword-named field to label.) A `:`
		// follows a NAME and nothing else in this grammar — no `if`/`while`/`match`/`for` header may be followed by one,
		// and Maxon has no `?:` and no label syntax that puts a colon after a keyword — so a keyword whose next
		// token is `:` is a name, whatever precedes it. Unexcluded, `for: 3` opened a block nothing closes and
		// `assertScanAligned` took the compiler down on the program `keyword-as-a-declared-name.md` pins.
		if next == TokenKind.colon 'argumentOrFieldLabel'
			return true
		end 'argumentOrFieldLabel'

		// Index 0 has no previous token, so it cannot be a member name — and no declaration spells a name
		// there either, since every one of them is introduced by a keyword that sits before it.
		if index == 0 'atFileStart'
			return false
		end 'atFileStart'

		if self.kindAt(index - 1) == TokenKind.dot 'memberName'
			return true
		end 'memberName'

		// LAST, because it is the only shape that can trigger a build: the three above are O(1) lookarounds,
		// and a real closing `end` — the overwhelmingly common token this is asked about — reaches here.
		return self.namesADeclaration(index)
	end 'keywordIsANameAt'

	// Does a DECLARATION spell its NAME at `index` — a function's own name, or one of its parameters' (D8)?
	//
	// ⚡ A BISECT of the ascending `declaredNameKeywords`, and the `count()` read is the whole cost for very
	// nearly every file: only a file that actually declares a keyword-named function or parameter has any
	// entry at all (see the field).
	function namesADeclaration(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		self.buildDeclaredNameIndex()
		return ascendingTokenIndexHolds(self.declaredNameKeywords, index: index)
	end 'namesADeclaration'

	// ⭐ **BUILD `declaredNameKeywords`: the KEYWORD tokens at which a declaration spells a name.** One linear
	// pass — at every `function` keyword, record the declared NAME (if the spelling has one) and every name
	// position in the parameter list that follows, keeping only the tokens that are not plain identifiers.
	//
	// **BOTH SPELLINGS OF `function` ARE COVERED, and deliberately.** A DECLARATION (`function <name> (`)
	// records its name and its parameters; a CLOSURE LITERAL (`function (`) has no name but records its
	// parameters just the same — a closure's parameters go through the very same `parseFunctionParameters`,
	// so they may be keyword-named too, and a closure body sits INSIDE a statement whose extent scan reads
	// those tokens.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CURSOR RESUMES PAST THE PARAMETER LIST, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE LIST ASCENDING** — which the
	// bisect in `namesADeclaration` rests on. A `function(` inside a parameter TYPE (`cb function(Integer)
	// returns Integer`) would otherwise be reached by the outer walk AFTER its enclosing list had already
	// contributed, pushing lower indices behind higher ones and duplicating them. Skipping the list also costs
	// nothing to correctness: a function TYPE's parenthesized part names types, never parameters.
	//
	// Idempotent, and the shared-empty `is`-identity of the list is the "have I run?" gate (see the field): the
	// fresh list installed below is what says it has run, whether or not the file declares anything.
	//
	// ⚡ LINEAR, and provably: every token is visited at most twice — once by the outer walk, once by
	// `recordParamNameKeywords` — because the walk resumes past each list it descends into. Nothing is
	// allocated per function.
	function buildDeclaredNameIndex()
		if self.declaredNameKeywords is sharedEmptyDeclaredNameKeywords 'notBuiltYet'
			let tokenCount = self.tokens.count()
			var found = TokenIndexColumn.create()

			var i = 0 as TokenIndex
			while i < tokenCount 'scan'
				if self.kindAt(i) == TokenKind.function 'functionKeyword'
					// A named declaration's list starts one token later than a closure literal's, because the name
					// sits between the keyword and the `(`.
					var listAt = i + 1
					if self.functionDeclarationAt(i) 'named'
						self.recordNameKeyword(found, index: i + 1)
						listAt = i + 2
					end 'named'
					i = self.recordParamNameKeywords(found, parenPos: listAt)
					continue
				end 'functionKeyword'

				i = i + 1
			end 'scan'

			self.declaredNameKeywords = found
		end 'notBuiltYet'
	end 'buildDeclaredNameIndex'

	// Append `index` to the ascending index if the token there is a KEYWORD rather than a plain identifier.
	// Identifiers are dropped because no scan ever asks about one: `keywordIsAName` is consulted only for the
	// seven block-structure keywords, so an identifier entry would be a list entry nothing can ever match.
	function recordNameKeyword(found TokenIndexColumn, index TokenIndex)
		if self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.identifier 'plainIdentifier'
			return
		end 'plainIdentifier'
		found.push(index)
	end 'recordNameKeyword'

	// ⭐ Record the parameter NAMES of the list at `parenPos` — by offering EVERY word-shaped token inside its
	// parentheses, which is deliberately COARSER than the parameter grammar and is what makes this walk both
	// exact enough and free. Returns the index the outer walk resumes at: past the list, or `parenPos` itself
	// when there is no list to descend into.
	//
	// **THE ARGUMENT: NO BLOCK STRUCTURE MAY APPEAR INSIDE A PARAMETER LIST.** A list holds names and TYPES —
	// `int(0 to 100)`, `function(X) returns Y`, `Array with T` — and not one statement, so there is no real
	// `while`/`match`/`for`/`end`/`if`/`else`/`otherwise` in there for an over-record to HIDE. The only reader
	// asks solely about those seven (`opensBlockAt`/`closesBlockAt`), so recording a type's `to` or `with`
	// costs nothing: no scan ever asks about them. UNDER-recording is the failure mode that matters, and
	// recording everything cannot under-record.
	//
	// ⚡ **AND IT IS WHY THIS DOES NOT SHARE `paramNamesIn`'s WALK.** That walk cannot answer without producing
	// per-parameter INDEXES, and the shared-index-columns version of this measured **+7,794 allocations at
	// rung 0 of the scale ladder, doubling per rung** — linear, but three heap objects on a path taken once
	// per `function` keyword in every file. This loop allocates nothing and duplicates no grammar: it does not
	// use the parameter grammar at all.
	//
	// TOLERANT at both ends, like every sweep helper: a bare `function` keyword (a legal enum CASE name)
	// reaches here with whatever token follows it, and an UNTERMINATED list records nothing rather than
	// swallowing the rest of the file. Both are the real parse's to report, with a position.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE "NO BLOCK STRUCTURE IN HERE" PREMISE IS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED** — see
	// `parenGroupCanBeAParameterList`. It has to be, because the caller reaches this off a pure TOKEN SHAPE.
	function recordParamNameKeywords(found TokenIndexColumn, parenPos TokenIndex) returns TokenIndex
		if self.kindAt(parenPos) != TokenKind.leftParen 'noParamList'
			return parenPos
		end 'noParamList'

		let listEnd = self.matchingRightParenOf(parenPos)
		if self.kindAt(listEnd) != TokenKind.rightParen 'unterminated'
			return parenPos
		end 'unterminated'

		if not self.parenGroupCanBeAParameterList(parenPos, listEnd: listEnd) 'notAParameterList'
			return parenPos
		end 'notAParameterList'

		var q = parenPos + 1
		while q < listEnd 'eachToken'
			if tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(q)) 'wordShaped'
				self.recordNameKeyword(found, index: q)
			end 'wordShaped'
			q = q + 1
		end 'eachToken'

		return listEnd
	end 'recordParamNameKeywords'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THE `( … )` SPANNING `[parenPos, listEnd]` REALLY A PARAMETER LIST?** The CHECK that
	// `recordParamNameKeywords`' safety argument used to be a COMMENT about, and it needs to be one: that
	// routine offers EVERY word-shaped token inside the parentheses, which hides nothing only while nothing
	// in there is REAL block structure — true of a parameter list, which holds names and TYPES and not one
	// statement, and NOT true of an arbitrary expression.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND AN ARBITRARY EXPRESSION DOES REACH IT, because `functionDeclarationAt` is a pure TOKEN SHAPE.**
	// `function` is itself a legal enum CASE name (`keyword-as-case-name.md`), so a `gives` arm spelled
	// `function gives (…)` matches `function <name> (` exactly as a declaration does — and its parentheses
	// hold whatever the arm's value is. MEASURED before this check existed: a `function gives (match y 'n' one
	// gives 1 two gives 2 end 'n')` arm recorded the inner match's `match` AND its `end` as declared names,
	// so `closesBlockAt` stopped seeing that `end`, the inner match's arm loop read it as one more case name,
	// and the program was refused `E3034 unknown enum case: 'end'` — while the reference bootstrap compiles it.
	//
	// **THE TEST IS WHERE A BLOCK KEYWORD STANDS, because that is the entire difference.** In a parameter list
	// one may stand only as a NAME — immediately after the `(`, or after a top-level `,` — and a parameter's
	// TYPE (`Integer`, `int(0 to 100)`, `function(X) returns Y`, `Array with T`) can never contain one. So a
	// block keyword anywhere else says this group is an expression: nothing in it is recorded, and the caller
	// resumes at the `(` rather than past it, so the outer walk still descends and still finds a closure
	// literal nested inside.
	function parenGroupCanBeAParameterList(parenPos TokenIndex, listEnd TokenIndex) returns bool
		var depth = 0
		var namePosition = true
		var q = parenPos + 1
		while q < listEnd 'eachToken'
			let kind = self.kindAt(q)
			if not namePosition and kindSpellsBlockStructure(kind) 'blockStructureOutsideANamePosition'
				return false
			end 'blockStructureOutsideANamePosition'

			if kind == TokenKind.leftParen 'nestedOpen'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'nestedOpen'
			if kind == TokenKind.rightParen 'nestedClose'
				depth = depth - 1
			end 'nestedClose'

			// The token AFTER a top-level `,` is the next parameter's name; every other token is inside a type.
			namePosition = depth == 0 and kind == TokenKind.comma

			q = q + 1
		end 'eachToken'

		return true
	end 'parenGroupCanBeAParameterList'

	// ⭐ **THE FILE'S BLOCK-EXTENT INDEX: per token, the `end` that closes the block opened there** — one
	// linear bracket-matching pass over the token array, run on the first construct that asks and shared
	// by every `if`/`while`/`match` after it. It is what lets `blockStatementEndIndex` step OVER a nested
	// construct in one array read instead of walking through its tokens.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT DECIDES `blockKeywordNames` IN THE SAME PASS, AND THAT IS NOT A CONVENIENCE.** Whether a
	// keyword is block structure or a match-arm case NAME depends on whether a `match` BODY is open around
	// it (`keywordIsANameAt`), and the only thing that knows is this walk's own opener stack. So the walk
	// asks the question at each token, from the left context it already holds, and RECORDS the verdict —
	// which is what lets `keywordIsAName`'s other readers share one answer instead of re-deriving it from a
	// context none of them has. The two outputs are one derivation and share this routine's gate.
	//
	// Idempotent, and the shared-empty `is`-identity of the extent column is the "have I run?" gate (see the
	// field): the fresh column installed below is what says it has run, whether or not the file opens a
	// single block.
	function buildBlockExtentIndex()
		if self.blockExtentEnds is sharedEmptyBlockExtentEnds 'notBuiltYet'
			let tokenCount = self.tokens.count()
			// One entry per token, and ZERO wherever no block opens — a value no real answer can take, since
			// a matched opener's `end` sits after it and an unmatched one is seeded below with the end of the
			// token stream. `matchingEndOf` panics on a zero rather than inventing an extent.
			var extentEnds = TokenIndexColumn.create()
			extentEnds.resize(tokenCount)

			// ⚠ **BOTH COLUMNS ARE INSTALLED BEFORE THE WALK, AND THE WALK WRITES THROUGH `self`.** The walk
			// asks `opensBlockAt`, which asks `keywordIsAName`, which asks for this index — so the gate above
			// has to be SHUT before the first of those calls or the builder re-enters itself forever. Shutting
			// it early is sound because nothing the walk reaches READS an extent (`matchingEndOf` has no
			// caller here), and every name-ness read it makes is of a token BEHIND the cursor, already
			// recorded.
			self.blockExtentEnds = extentEnds
			self.blockKeywordNames = TokenIndexColumn.create()

			// The openers not yet closed, innermost last — the bracket-matching stack.
			var openers = TokenIndexColumn.create()

			var i = 0 as TokenIndex
			while i < tokenCount 'scan'
				// BEFORE the two predicates below, because both of them read it back through
				// `keywordIsAName` for this very token.
				self.recordBlockKeywordName(i, openers: openers)

				if self.opensBlockAt(i) 'opensBlock'
					openers.push(i)
					// UNTERMINATED until a closer says otherwise — the end of the token stream, which is the
					// tolerant answer `blockStatementEndIndex`'s header states for a construct with no `end`.
					// Seeded at the PUSH so there is no post-scan drain to keep in step with this loop.
					try self.blockExtentEnds.set(i, value: tokenCount) otherwise panic("buildBlockExtentIndex: opener {i} is outside this file's token space")
				end 'opensBlock' else if self.closesBlockAt(i) 'closesBlock'
					// A closer with nothing open closes nothing — the same malformed input the walk in
					// `blockStatementEndIndex` carries straight past on a negative depth.
					if openers.count() > 0 'hasOpener'
						let opener = try openers.pop() otherwise panic("buildBlockExtentIndex: pop from an opener stack just checked non-empty")
						try self.blockExtentEnds.set(opener, value: i) otherwise panic("buildBlockExtentIndex: opener {opener} is outside this file's token space")
					end 'hasOpener'
				end 'closesBlock'

				i = i + 1
			end 'scan'
		end 'notBuiltYet'
	end 'buildBlockExtentIndex'

	// Is the innermost block still open a `match` — i.e. does the cursor stand at the top level of a match
	// BODY, which is where an arm spells its case name and the only place one can? Read off the walk's
	// opener stack, which holds only openers BEHIND the cursor, so the answer is a fact about the left
	// context and cannot be circular with the extent this same walk is computing.
	//
	// A construct nested inside an arm's body pushes its own opener and becomes the innermost, so "the
	// innermost is a match" is exactly "at the match's own level": an arm body is a statement on the arm's
	// line, and it cannot re-enter this level without opening a block of its own first.
	function innermostOpenBlockIsAMatch(openers TokenIndexColumn) returns bool
		if openers.isEmpty() 'noneOpen'
			return false
		end 'noneOpen'

		let innermost = try openers.last() otherwise panic("innermostOpenBlockIsAMatch: the opener stack is non-empty by the guard above and has no last")
		return self.kindAt(innermost) == TokenKind.match
	end 'innermostOpenBlockIsAMatch'

	// Decide whether the token at `index` is a keyword standing where block structure could stand but
	// spelling a NAME, and append it to the ascending memo if it is — the one caller of `keywordIsANameAt`.
	//
	// Only the kinds `kindSpellsBlockStructure` admits are offered, because those are the only ones any
	// reader asks about (`opensBlockAt` early-returns on that set and `closesBlockAt` tests `end` first):
	// an entry for anything else would be one the bisect can never be asked to match. That gate stands
	// FIRST, so the stack read below runs at the few tokens that can answer anything and not at every one.
	function recordBlockKeywordName(index TokenIndex, openers TokenIndexColumn)
		if not kindSpellsBlockStructure(self.kindAt(index)) 'notBlockStructure'
			return
		end 'notBlockStructure'

		if not self.keywordIsANameAt(index, inMatchBody: self.innermostOpenBlockIsAMatch(openers)) 'isBlockStructure'
			return
		end 'isBlockStructure'

		self.blockKeywordNames.push(index)
	end 'recordBlockKeywordName'

	// The index of the `end` that closes the block opened at `index`, or the end of the token stream when
	// nothing does — O(1) off `buildBlockExtentIndex`'s column.
	//
	// ⚠ ONLY MEANINGFUL AT AN OPENER, which is why a zero entry PANICS rather than answering: no block
	// opens at that token, and any extent returned for it would be invented. Every caller asks under
	// `opensBlockAt`, so the panic is a tripwire and not a path.
	function matchingEndOf(index TokenIndex) returns TokenIndex
		let closer = try self.blockExtentEnds.get(index) otherwise panic("matchingEndOf: token {index} is outside this file's token space")
		if closer == 0 'notAnOpener'
			panic("matchingEndOf: no block opens at token {index} — the extent column holds a matching `end` only for a token `opensBlockAt` accepts")
		end 'notAnOpener'

		return closer
	end 'matchingEndOf'

	// THE GUARD ON A TOKEN-EXTENT SCAN, shared by `parseWhileStatement` and `parseIfStatement`.
	// `blockStatementEndIndex` re-derives Maxon's block structure at the token
	// level to fix a construct's carried-variable set BEFORE its body is parsed — a SECOND copy
	// of a fact the recursive-descent parser owns. If the two disagree the scan's extent is too
	// SHORT, the carried set is a SUBSET, and a carried var silently loses its merge phi: a wrong
	// answer with no diagnostic. This converts it into a loud one — the `end` the parser actually
	// consumed to close the last body (`lastBlockBodyEnd`) must be the one the scan predicted.
	//
	// It is what makes the duplication safe to keep. A future block statement (`for`, `match`)
	// added to `parseStatement` without teaching `opensBlockAt` about it trips this panic on the
	// first spec that nests it in a loop or an `if`, instead of miscompiling it. (Over-shooting
	// the extent cannot hide here: it never under-counts the carried set, and it would make the
	// prediction miss the actual `end` and panic too — so the scan is pinned from both sides.)
	function assertScanAligned(construct String, predictedEnd TokenIndex)
		if self.lastBlockBodyEnd != predictedEnd 'scanDrifted'
			panic("{construct}: the token scan predicted the closing `end` at token {predictedEnd} but the parser closed the last body at token {self.lastBlockBodyEnd} — `opensBlockAt` does not know every statement that opens a block, so the carried-variable set it computed may be missing a carried var")
		end 'scanDrifted'
	end 'assertScanAligned'

	// Set up the exit block's post-loop variable values. With no `break`, the exit is
	// reached only from the condition's false edge, so each var's value there IS its
	// header phi (no exit phi needed). With ≥1 `break`, the exit MERGES the condition-
	// false value with each break's value, so it gets its own phis and the false edge
	// passes the header phi values into them (the break edges were recorded as the breaks
	// were parsed).
	//
	// `condExit` — the block the condition ENDED in, which carries the two-way branch — is
	// the false edge's source, and it is only the header when the condition short-circuited
	// nowhere. Recording the edge on the header instead would attach the exit's phi operands
	// to a block that does not branch to the exit at all.
	//
	// Index-based in both loops so each phi var is a MUTABLE binding — a `for x in …`
	// loop variable is immutable, and installing the post-loop value writes through the
	// binding it carries.
	function wireLoopExit(condExit IrBlock, exit IrBlock, ctx LoopContext)
		if not ctx.hasBreak 'noBreak'
			for k in 0 upto ctx.phiVars.count() 'inherit'
				var pv = try ctx.phiVars.get(k) otherwise panic("wireLoopExit: phiVars.get OOB at {k}")
				pv.binding.boundValue = pv.phiId
			end 'inherit'
			return
		end 'noBreak'

		var headerVals = ValueIdArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto ctx.phiVars.count() 'mkExitPhi'
			var pv = try ctx.phiVars.get(k) otherwise panic("wireLoopExit: phiVars.get OOB at {k}")
			let exitPhiId = self.mintPhiForBinding(exit, binding: pv.binding)
			pv.binding.boundValue = exitPhiId
			self.pushMergedEdgeValue(headerVals, binding: pv.binding, value: pv.phiId)
		end 'mkExitPhi'
		self.recordBranchEdge(condExit, targetId: exit.id, argIds: headerVals)
	end 'wireLoopExit'

	// Drop the owned bindings a `break`/`continue` skips past — everything declared since the TARGET's
	// body was entered (a `LoopContext.ownedFloor`, or a `MatchContext.ownedFloor`), in reverse order,
	// into `block` BEFORE the branch that terminates it. A `break`/`continue` leaves its block ALIVE (it
	// jumps forward/back rather than falling through the `end`), so `parseBlockBody`'s fall-through drop
	// and `dropArmScopedOwned`'s live-exit drop never run on this path — this is the only place that
	// iteration's, or that arm's, body-local Strings/structs are released. For a labelled control to an
	// OUTER target, `floor` is that outer target's, so it releases every binding down to it. The decrefs
	// land in `block` because `emitScopeDrops` emits into `currentBlock` and a `break`/`continue` moves
	// control nowhere until its branch, so `currentBlock` is `block`.
	//
	// It takes the FLOOR rather than a context, because the two context types record the same fact and a
	// second copy of these two lines is exactly how their drop rules would drift apart.
	function dropTargetBodyOwned(block IrBlock, floor BindingMark, anchor Token)
		self.currentBlock = block
		self.emitScopeDrops(block, floor: floor, anchor: anchor)
	end 'dropTargetBodyOwned'

	// Parse `break ['label']` (M4b): leave the innermost enclosing break TARGET — the match statement if
	// one is open, else the loop (`resolveControlTarget`). The two destinations are wired differently
	// enough to be their own functions: a loop's exit block exists and takes its phi args now, while a
	// match's merge does not exist yet.
	function parseBreakStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let breakTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.break)
		let target = try self.resolveControlTarget(breakTok)
		match target 'target'
			loopExit(ctx) then self.jumpToLoopBlock(block, ctx: ctx, targetId: ctx.exitId, anchor: breakTok)
			matchMerge(ctx) then self.breakToMatchMerge(func, block: block, ctx: ctx, breakTok: breakTok)
		end 'target'
	end 'parseBreakStatement'

	// Parse `continue ['label']` (M4b): branch to the target loop's CONTINUE TARGET, carrying the
	// current phi-var values so that block's phis see this path. For a `while` the target is the
	// header (the condition is re-evaluated on the back edge); for a `for` it is the STEP block, whose
	// increment must run before the header or the counter never advances — see `LoopContext`.
	function parseContinueStatement(block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let continueTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.continue)
		let target = try self.resolveControlTarget(continueTok)
		match target 'target'
			loopExit(ctx) then self.jumpToLoopBlock(block, ctx: ctx, targetId: ctx.continueTargetId, anchor: continueTok)
			matchMerge then panic("parseContinueStatement: `continue` resolved to a match — only `break` searches the match stack, and `resolveControlTarget` keys that on the keyword's own token kind")
		end 'target'
	end 'parseContinueStatement'

	// Wire a `break`/`continue` that leaves a LOOP: release what the loop body declared, then branch,
	// carrying the loop's phi values so the destination's phis see this path. `targetId` is the ONLY
	// thing that differs between the two keywords here — the exit for a `break`, the continue target for
	// a `continue` — which is why they share this rather than each spelling the same three lines.
	function jumpToLoopBlock(block IrBlock, ctx LoopContext, targetId BlockId, anchor Token)
		self.dropTargetBodyOwned(block, floor: ctx.ownedFloor, anchor: anchor)
		self.recordBranchEdge(block, targetId: targetId, argIds: self.edgeArgsFor(ctx.phiVars, preLoop: false))
		self.emitBranch(block, targetId: targetId)
	end 'jumpToLoopBlock'

	// ⭐⭐ **A `break` THAT LEAVES A MATCH STATEMENT ENROLS ITS BLOCK AS A REACHING ARM EXIT.**
	//
	// There is nothing to branch to: `finalizeMatchMerge` mints the merge block LAST, deliberately, so it
	// lays out after every arm body. So instead of a branch this contributes the very record a
	// fall-through arm contributes — exit block, carried-var snapshot, no `gives` value, move state — and
	// the merge then emits the branch and the phi args through the ONE join every arm already uses. No
	// new block in the merge's path, no early block, and no existing match's layout moves.
	//
	// ⚠ **NEITHER REFERENCE'S LOWERING WAS AVAILABLE HERE, and copying either would have been a
	// regression.** The bootstrap emits a branch to a merge that does not exist yet because it branches by
	// LABEL STRING (`2-Parser.cs:13626`); shv2 branches by `BlockId` — the rewrite's thesis — so there is
	// no name to forward-reference. v1 creates the merge EAGERLY in `parseMatchHeader`
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:7909-7910`), which would reorder every existing match's
	// blocks and churn every committed golden for a program that has no `break` in it at all.
	function breakToMatchMerge(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, ctx MatchContext, breakTok Token)
		// Release what the arm declared, exactly as a live arm exit's `dropArmScopedOwned` would — the
		// break skips the arm's end, so this is the only drop its payload/`let` bindings get.
		self.dropTargetBodyOwned(block, floor: ctx.ownedFloor, anchor: breakTok)
		// A local `var` alias, not `ctx.breakExits.push(…)`: the mutating-parameter analysis reads a write
		// through a parameter's FIELD as a write to the parameter itself, and a `match` arm's payload
		// binding is immutable, so `parseBreakStatement` could not then pass `ctx` at all (E3019). The
		// alias is the same array the live stack entry holds, so the push lands where it must.
		var breakExits = ctx.breakExits
		breakExits.push(MatchArmExit.create(block, exitVals: self.snapshotBindings(ctx.carried), give: MatchGiveValue.none, moveMark: moveMarkPrefix(self.captureMoveMark(), height: ctx.ownedFloor), blame: EmptySourceText))

		// ⭐ **CONTROL LEAVES, YET `block` MUST STAY UNTERMINATED** — terminating a reaching arm's exit is
		// `finalizeMatchMerge`'s job, and it has not run. So the statement continues in a fresh DEAD block,
		// the same shape `parseIfStatement` gives the continuation of a fully-terminated `if`. THAT is what
		// keeps every `isTerminated()` reader downstream right by construction rather than by a flag each
		// would have to remember to consult: `parseArmBody` must not enrol this arm a SECOND time,
		// `dropArmScopedOwned` must not re-drop what was just dropped, and `parseOtherwiseHandler`'s
		// caught-box drop must not double-free after an `otherwise break`.
		self.currentBlock = self.newDeadBlock(func, label: b"afterbreak", line: breakTok.line)
	end 'breakToMatchMerge'

	// ⭐⭐ **BREAK TARGETS ARE MATCHES ∪ LOOPS; CONTINUE TARGETS ARE LOOPS ONLY.**
	//
	// No label → the innermost break target (the match, if one is open; else the innermost loop). A label
	// → the enclosing match, else the enclosing loop, with that label. Naming the innermost LOOP is
	// redundant (E2048) — but only when no match is open, see below. A label matching no enclosing target,
	// or a keyword with no target at all, is E2047. Returns the live stack entry (a reference), so a
	// `break` reaches the same `LoopContext`/`MatchContext` the stack still holds.
	function resolveControlTarget(kwTok Token) returns ControlTarget throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE ONE BIT THE TWO KEYWORDS DIFFER BY.** `break` leaves an enclosing construct, and a match
		// statement is one; `continue` starts the next ITERATION, which only a loop has. All THREE places
		// they diverge read this — which stacks are searched, whether the innermost loop's label is
		// redundant, and the noun the E2047 texts use — so they cannot come to disagree.
		let isBreak = kwTok.kind == TokenKind.break
		let matchDepth = self.matches.count() if isBreak else 0
		let loopDepth = self.loops.count()

		if loopDepth == 0 and matchDepth == 0 'noTarget'
			throw ParseError.controlOutsideLoop(String.from(kwTok.value), targets: controlTargetNoun(isBreak), line: kwTok.line, column: kwTok.column)
		end 'noTarget'

		if not self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'unlabelled'
			// ⚠ **THE MATCH WINS UNCONDITIONALLY, AND THAT IS CORRECT ONLY BECAUSE OF E2049.** "Innermost
			// break target" is what the rule says, and preferring the top of `matches` over the top of
			// `loops` computes it only because a match arm body may not OPEN a loop
			// (`parseArmBody` → `isBlockOpeningStatement` → E2049) and a closure resets both stacks — so no
			// loop can ever be nested INSIDE an open match, and a match on the stack always IS the innermost
			// target. Both references depend on this and neither records it. A push-depth counter would
			// remove the dependency and was deliberately not built: it is speculative generality for a
			// nesting the language forbids. **If E2049 is ever relaxed, this preference must become an
			// interleaved depth comparison.**
			if matchDepth > 0 'innermostMatch'
				return ControlTarget.matchMerge(try self.matches.get(matchDepth - 1) otherwise panic("resolveControlTarget: matches.get({matchDepth - 1}) failed after the count check"))
			end 'innermostMatch'
			return self.loopTargetAt(loopDepth - 1, isBreak: isBreak)
		end 'unlabelled'

		let labelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)

		// Matches first, innermost out — a labelled break names its target directly, and a match's label is
		// NEVER redundant: `break 'check'` inside `match … 'check'` says exactly what the bare `break` says,
		// but the redundancy diagnostic exists for LOOP labels only (E2048's text names a loop), and both
		// references accept the match spelling silently. `match-arm-field-assignment` and
		// `match-statements.break-exhaustive-enum` are both written that way.
		for i in 0 upto matchDepth 'matchSearch'
			let ctx = try self.matches.get(matchDepth - 1 - i) otherwise panic("resolveControlTarget: matches.get OOB during the label search")
			if ctx.label.equals(labelTok.value) 'foundMatch'
				return ControlTarget.matchMerge(ctx)
			end 'foundMatch'
		end 'matchSearch'

		for i in 0 upto loopDepth 'loopSearch'
			let ctx = try self.loops.get(loopDepth - 1 - i) otherwise panic("resolveControlTarget: loops.get OOB during the label search")
			if ctx.label.equals(labelTok.value) 'foundLoop'
				// E2048 — the label says nothing the bare keyword would not. ⚠ The `matchDepth == 0` conjunct
				// is load-bearing and not a refinement: with a match open, a bare `break` targets the MATCH,
				// so naming the enclosing loop is the ONLY way to say "the loop" and is required rather than
				// redundant. For `continue` the conjunct is `0 == 0` by construction, so its rule is
				// unchanged — `specs-shv2/break.md:49` pins that text byte for byte.
				if i == 0 and matchDepth == 0 'ownLabel'
					throw ParseError.labelTargetsOwnLoop(String.from(kwTok.value), label: String.from(labelTok.value), line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
				end 'ownLabel'
				return self.loopTargetAt(loopDepth - 1 - i, isBreak: isBreak)
			end 'foundLoop'
		end 'loopSearch'
		throw ParseError.unknownLoopLabel(String.from(kwTok.value), targets: controlTargetNoun(isBreak), label: String.from(labelTok.value), line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
	end 'resolveControlTarget'

	// The `loopExit` target for the loop at `index`, flagging its exit as a `break` destination when the
	// keyword is one — the exit then merges the condition's false edge with each break's values, so
	// `wireLoopExit` gives it phis. The flag is written HERE, at the one place a loop becomes a target,
	// rather than by the caller: it must land on the live stack entry, and a caller that unpacked
	// `ControlTarget` could not write it — a `match` arm's payload binding is immutable.
	function loopTargetAt(index ElementIndex, isBreak bool) returns ControlTarget
		var ctx = try self.loops.get(index) otherwise panic("loopTargetAt: loops.get({index}) OOB — the index was found by searching the live stack")
		if isBreak 'exitIsAMergePoint'
			ctx.hasBreak = true
		end 'exitIsAMergePoint'
		return ControlTarget.loopExit(ctx)
	end 'loopTargetAt'

	// The current SSA value of each binding in `bindings`, in the list's order — one of an
	// `if`'s three merge snapshots (entry / then-exit / else-exit), positional over the
	// statement's carried set (`assignedBindings`).
	function snapshotBindings(bindings VarInfoArray) returns ValueIdArray
		var vals = ValueIdArray.create()
		for binding in bindings 'each'
			vals.push(binding.boundValue)
		end 'each'
		return vals
	end 'snapshotBindings'

	// The mutable bindings an `if` statement's token extent ASSIGNS — its carried set, the
	// SUBSET of `mutableVars` a continuation phi can be minted for, returned in `mutableVars`
	// DECLARATION ORDER. That order is load-bearing: the phis a merge mints follow it, and it
	// is what makes the sparse merge emit byte-identical code to the old one that iterated the
	// whole `mutableVars` list (see mergeAtContinuation). Token/assignment order would permute
	// two phis of one `if` and move codegen — the scale corpus's rotating write set proves it
	// (`w5 = …; w0 = …` assigns in the order 5, 0 but must merge in the order 0, 5).
	//
	// THE ONE TOKEN SCAN, shared by `if`, `match`, `while` and `for`. A reassignment is the ONLY way
	// a `var` already in scope changes value, and its syntax is exactly `identifier =`
	// (parseStatement's two-token lookahead). `==` is a single `equalsEquals` token, so a comparison
	// cannot be mistaken for one.
	//
	// It OVER-approximates, and every case where it does is harmless because a surplus carried
	// binding is harmless while a missing one is a miscompile:
	//   • `let r = …` / `var t = …` inside the extent match the shape too. Those names are not
	//     mutable bindings in scope at the construct, so they select nothing — unless an outer var
	//     of the same name is being SHADOWED, in which case the outer one is carried when it need
	//     not be. Costs one snapshot slot (an `if`: a == b, so no phi) or one coalesced copy (a
	//     loop); the alternative is tracking shadowing at token level, which is the block structure
	//     written down a THIRD time.
	//   • a name assigned only PAST the extent, when `blockStatementEndIndex` runs wide.
	// UNDER-approximating would drop a real phi — a silent miscompile — so the extent errs wide and
	// the drift guard (`assertScanAligned`) refuses a too-short one. A name in scope but NOT
	// mutable, or not bound at entry (a branch-local `var`), is in no `mutableVars` entry and is
	// correctly absent.
	//
	// ⚠ THIS SCAN IS EXHAUSTIVE ONLY BECAUSE PARAMETERS ARE IMMUTABLE. Everything below reads
	// TOKENS, so it can only ever find a write that IS tokens — `identifier =`. A `var` parameter is
	// written by `mutate(p)` at the call site, leaving no `=` in this loop at all: the scan would
	// return without `p`, a loop header would mint no phi, and `p` would reset to its entry value
	// every iteration. Silent, and invisible to the drift panic that guards this scan's OTHER
	// assumption (the construct's extent), because the extent would be right.
	//
	// The tripwire for it is in `bindParameters`, on the mutability flag itself at the moment it is
	// passed — not here. This scan cannot enumerate parameters to check them, and a guard here
	// reading the `ParametersAreMutable` constant would be defeated by the very change it guards
	// against: a per-parameter feature stops reading that constant, and the guard goes quiet
	// exactly when it was needed.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT COSTS O(tokens in the extent), NOT O(vars in scope), AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.** It
	// used to build a `Set` of the assigned NAMES and then test that set once per mutable binding in
	// scope — Θ(bindings × constructs) probes, which is quadratic in a program that grows both, and
	// invisible to the memory columns because a probe allocates nothing. MEASURED on the scale
	// corpus at rung 5: 39,400,068 probes across 18,571 constructs, ×3.99 per doubling, against a
	// carried set that was never bigger than TWO. Asking the inverse index per assigned NAME instead
	// makes the whole thing linear (see `MutableVarPositionByName`).
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE ANSWER MUST STILL COME BACK IN `mutableVars` DECLARATION ORDER.** That order is
	// load-bearing: the phis a merge mints follow it, so token/assignment order would permute two
	// phis of one `if` and MOVE CODEGEN — the scale corpus's rotating write set proves it
	// (`w5 = …; w0 = …` assigns in the order 5, 0 but must merge in the order 0, 5). Ascending
	// POSITION is that order, which is why the positions are collected ascending rather than sorted
	// afterwards (see `collectCarriedPositions`).
	function assignedBindingsIn(startIndex TokenIndex, endIndex TokenIndex) returns VarInfoArray
		var positions = MutableVarPositionArray.create()
		var i = startIndex
		while i < endIndex 'scan'
			if self.assignsAt(i) 'assignment'
				self.collectCarriedPositions(positions, name: self.tokenAt(i).value)
			end 'assignment'

			// ⭐⭐ **A TUPLE ASSIGNMENT'S BARE TARGETS ARE WRITES THIS SCAN CANNOT SEE AS `identifier =`.**
			// `(a, b) = step(a)` writes both names and neither is followed by an `=` — `a` is followed by a
			// comma and `b` by a `)`. Left out, a loop carrying either would mint no header phi and the name
			// would reset to its entry value every trip: silent, and exactly the miscompile this function's
			// header calls out as the one direction it may not err in.
			//
			// ⚠ **BOTH HALVES GO THROUGH THE SHARED READERS — the SPAN through `tupleAssignEqualsAt` and WHICH
			// TOKENS IN IT ARE TARGETS through `tupleAssignTargetIndices`.** This used to re-derive the second
			// half inline ("every identifier in the span"), under a comment claiming it did not, which is the
			// copy that gets taught a new target shape last: teach the grammar a target that is not a bare
			// identifier and the parse learns it while this scan does not, dropping a header phi with no
			// diagnostic anywhere. Both reviewers found it independently.
			//
			// Only REBINDING targets are collected — a `let`/`var` target declares a name the loop header
			// cannot be carrying, and `_` writes nothing. A DECLARING destructure (`let (x, y) = t`) never
			// reaches here at all: `tupleAssignEqualsAt` refuses a `(` that follows the keyword, at the
			// grammar rather than here.
			// ⚠ The `!= i` guard is not a readability nicety: `tupleAssignTargetIndices` ALLOCATES its answer,
			// and this loop visits every token of every construct extent in the program. Called unguarded it
			// would mint an empty array per token — see this header's own note on the Θ(bindings × constructs)
			// probe that was removed from here for exactly that kind of cost.
			let equalsIndex = self.tupleAssignEqualsAt(i)
			if equalsIndex != i 'aTupleAssignmentOpensHere'
				for nameIndex in self.tupleAssignTargetIndices(i, equalsIndex: equalsIndex) 'eachTarget'
					if tupleAssignTargetRebinds(self.tupleAssignTargetAt(nameIndex)) 'rebindingTarget'
						self.collectCarriedPositions(positions, name: self.tokenAt(nameIndex).value)
					end 'rebindingTarget'
				end 'eachTarget'
			end 'aTupleAssignmentOpensHere'

			i = i + 1
		end 'scan'

		var bindings = VarInfoArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto positions.count() 'each'
			let position = try positions.get(k) otherwise panic("assignedBindingsIn: positions.get OOB at {k}")
			bindings.push(try self.mutableVars.get(position) otherwise panic("assignedBindingsIn: carried position {position} is off the end of a {self.mutableVars.count()}-deep mutable-binding stack"))
		end 'each'
		return bindings
	end 'assignedBindingsIn'

	// Add every mutable binding named `name` to `positions` — the innermost one and each entry it
	// SHADOWS, which is exactly the set a walk of `mutableVars` testing `name` would have found.
	//
	// The walk stops at the first position already present, and that is sound rather than an
	// optimization: a position only ever enters `positions` through this routine, which inserts the
	// whole chain below it in the same call, so a chain whose head is present is present entire.
	function collectCarriedPositions(positions MutableVarPositionArray, name ByteArray)
		var position = self.innermostMutableVarPosition(name)
		while position != NoMutableVar 'chain'
			if not insertCarriedPosition(positions, position: position) 'chainAlreadyCollected'
				return
			end 'chainAlreadyCollected'

			position = try self.mutableVarShadowed.get(position) otherwise panic("collectCarriedPositions: position {position} is off the end of a {self.mutableVarShadowed.count()}-deep shadow column")
		end 'chain'
	end 'collectCarriedPositions'

	// One slot per carried binding, in phi order — the block-arg list of EVERY edge in a loop's phi
	// model. `preLoop` picks which value each slot carries, and that is the only thing the two kinds of
	// edge disagree about:
	//   • the PREHEADER edge (`preLoop: true`) carries the value captured OUTSIDE the loop, which
	//     `LoopPhiVar.entryValue` holds because installing the phi overwrote `boundValue`;
	//   • every edge from INSIDE (a back-edge, a `continue` to the step block, a `break` to the exit)
	//     carries the binding's value right now — read off the BINDING, so a `break` out of a block that
	//     SHADOWS a loop-carried `var` still carries the loop's own binding, not the shadow's value.
	//
	// ONE walk, because what must never drift is the POSITIONAL contract (in `phiVars` order, exactly one
	// entry per element, no filtering): a second copy that grew a filter would misalign one edge against
	// the header's block-args, which is a silent miscompile rather than a compile error.
	function edgeArgsFor(phiVars LoopPhiVarArray, preLoop bool) returns ValueIdArray
		var vals = ValueIdArray.create()
		for pv in phiVars 'each'
			let carried = pv.entryValue if preLoop else pv.binding.boundValue
			self.pushMergedEdgeValue(vals, binding: pv.binding, value: carried)
			// ⭐⭐ **A LOOP NAMES ITS PHI SEPARATELY FROM THE BINDING, WHICH IS WHY W117's MARK IS CARRIED HERE
			// AS WELL (review).** On a BACK edge the binding's own value IS what is being pushed, so the carry
			// `pushMergedEdgeValue` makes is a self-carry and the HEADER phi — the value every later read of the
			// name sees — inherits nothing. `pv.phiId` is that phi, and it is the loop's own to name.
			//
			// ⛔ MEASURED without this line: `var b = ["q"]` + `for _ in 0 upto 2  b = expose()  end` +
			// `grow(b)` compiled and grew a `let`-declared global (exit 6), while the same rebind under an `if`
			// was already refused. The `if` half and this one are the same defect at two merge shapes.
			self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: carried, to: pv.phiId)
		end 'each'
		return vals
	end 'edgeArgsFor'

	// ============================================================================================
	// `match` on SCALAR scrutinees (int/bool/float) — P1.1b wave A. No enums.
	//
	// A match lowers to a CHAIN of two-way `condBranch` test blocks and a block-arg join — the
	// exact primitives `if`/`else`/`while` already use. There is NO new IR op:
	//
	//   test0:  <pattern0 → bool cond0> ; condBranch(cond0, then: body0, else: test1)
	//   test1:  <pattern1 → bool cond1> ; condBranch(cond1, then: body1, else: test2)
	//   ...
	//   testN:  <the default body — the last real arm's `else` target>
	//   body0:  <arm 0's body> ; branch merge   (or → next body on `and fallthrough`)
	//   ...
	//   merge:  phi(s) ; <the result, for a `gives` match>
	//
	// A pattern is OR-folded into ONE boolean, exactly as both reference compilers do: a single
	// value is `scrut == v`, a range `lo to hi` is `(scrut >= lo) and (scrut <= hi)` (a bitwise
	// `and` over the two `bool`s — eager, since a pattern has no side effects), an `or` is a
	// bitwise `or` of the alternatives, `upto` uses `<` for the upper bound, and `min`/`max` drop
	// the corresponding compare.
	//
	// ⭐ The `gives` result and every cross-arm `var` mutation join through BLOCK-ARGS, not v1's /
	// the bootstrap's mutable result SLOT — see MatchArmExit's header. shv2 has no slots and no
	// mem2reg, so the parser threads each reaching arm's value to the merge as a block-arg, the
	// same on-the-fly SSA `if`/`while` use.
	// ============================================================================================

	// The STATEMENT form: `match <expr> 'l' <arms> end 'l'`. Returns the continuation control
	// resumes in — reachable when an arm falls through to it, `dead` when every arm terminates.
	function parseMatchStatement(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns IrBlock throws ParseError
		let outcome = try self.parseMatchCommon(func, block: block, isExpression: false)
		return outcome.cont
	end 'parseMatchStatement'

	// The EXPRESSION form: `let r = match …`, `match … * 2`. Yields the merge's result phi and
	// leaves control in the merge, exactly as `emitShortCircuit` does — so the climber continues
	// from `currentBlock` after it.
	function parseMatchExpression(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let outcome = try self.parseMatchCommon(func, block: self.currentBlock, isExpression: true)
		match outcome.result 'r'
			value(id) then return id
			// ⚠ **THE GUARANTEE IS `finalizeMatchMerge`'s REFUSAL, NOT "every arm gives".** An arm may now
			// diverge (`match-expr-arm-divergent`), so `reachingArms` can legitimately be short — and when it
			// is EMPTY, that routine throws E2015 rather than returning a valueless outcome, which is what
			// makes this arm unreachable. Any non-empty reaching set yields a result phi.
			none then panic("parseMatchExpression: an expression match produced no value — an empty reaching set is refused E2015 by `finalizeMatchMerge`, so a `none` here means a reaching arm carried no give")
		end 'r'
	end 'parseMatchExpression'

	// The shared core, driving both forms off `isExpression` (which decides `then` vs `gives` and
	// whether the merge carries a result phi). The single owner of the scrutinee, the block label,
	// the arm loop, exhaustiveness, and the merge join.
	function parseMatchCommon(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, isExpression bool) returns MatchOutcome throws ParseError
		let matchIndex = self.pos
		let matchTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.match)

		// The carried-variable set — the mutable bindings the WHOLE match assigns, read off the
		// tokens before anything is emitted, exactly as `if`/`while` read theirs. `matchEnd` feeds
		// the drift guard below.
		let matchEnd = self.blockStatementEndIndex(matchIndex, continuesAtElse: false)
		let carried = self.assignedBindingsIn(matchIndex, endIndex: matchEnd)

		// The scrutinee. It may itself short-circuit and move `currentBlock`, so the first arm's
		// test goes into wherever the scrutinee left control — its exit block, not `block`.
		let scrutTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		let scrutStart = self.pos
		// ⭐ **READ OFF THE TOKEN, BEFORE THE EXPRESSION IS PARSED AWAY.** A block-form `try`'s `(e)` binding
		// evaluates to the error block's flag phi — an integer indistinguishable from any other — so the two
		// questions its handler poses are asked of the NAME: has the handler discriminated at all (E3084), and
		// is this scrutinee the synthesized error union (in which case the whole dispatch below reads a fused
		// ordinal and a synthesized layout rather than the scrutinee's own type).
		let scrutName = self.matchScrutineeBareName(scrutStart)
		self.noteTryBlockBindingMatched(scrutName)
		let errorUnion = self.errorUnionForScrutinee(scrutName)
		let isErrorUnionMatch = errorUnionIsSynthesized(errorUnion)
		let scrut = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)
		let scrutTag = self.tagOf(scrut)

		// ⭐ **E3077's SCRUTINEE DOOR.** A `match` arm may WRITE a payload back into the scrutinee's box
		// (`full(value) then value = 42` — `VarInfo.isPayloadBinding`), which is legal only for a `var`
		// scrutinee, so matching on one is a use of its mutability. Both references say the same
		// (`_reassignedVars.Add(scrutineeVarName)` when the scrutinee is a mutable var), and
		// `specs-shv2/mutable-enums.md`'s six cases are exactly this shape and nothing else. Marked for a
		// BARE-NAME scrutinee through the shared cursor test, which is the only shape that names a binding.
		if self.argWasBareName(self.tokenAt(scrutStart), valueStart: scrutStart) 'bareNameScrutinee'
			self.markNameMutabilityExercised(self.tokenAt(scrutStart).value)
		end 'bareNameScrutinee'

		if not self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'noLabel'
			let missing = self.current()
			throw ParseError.matchMissingBlockId(missing.line, column: missing.column)
		end 'noLabel'
		let labelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		let matchLabel = labelTok.value
		try self.requireNewlineAfterBlockLabel()

		let entryVals = self.snapshotBindings(carried)

		// Arm-loop state. The first arm's test goes into the block the scrutinee left control in
		// (`self.currentBlock`); `prevTestBlockId`/`prevExitBlock` are seeded with it too and read only
		// under the flag that makes them meaningful (`hasRealArm` / `prevFallthrough`) — a block has
		// no empty value, and a sentinel would be a lie a later read could believe.
		//
		// ⭐ `prevTestBlockId` is an ID, not a second NAME for `currentTestBlock`: the advance below is
		// `prevTestBlockId = currentTestBlock.id`, and copying the BINDING there would move the block out
		// of `currentTestBlock` (single ownership — E3102 on the next arm's read of it). The one reader
		// re-fetches, which `IrModule.getBlockByIdIn` asks for anyway across block-minting calls.
		var currentTestBlock = self.currentBlock
		var prevTestBlockId = self.currentBlock.id
		var hasRealArm = false
		var sawDefault = false
		var prevFallthrough = false
		var prevExitBlock = self.currentBlock
		var prevExitVals = ValueIdArray.create()
		var seenValues = SeenPatternValues.create()
		var reachingArms = MatchArmExitArray.create()

		// ⭐ ENUM vs SCALAR is decided ONCE, from the scrutinee's type. A `named(X)` scrutinee whose
		// name is a declared enum/union takes the enum path — case-name patterns, the OPEN #21 range
		// rule, coverage over the case set; every other scrutinee is a scalar match (Wave A). The two
		// share this whole dispatch engine (the `condBranch` chain, the arm bodies, the merge); only
		// the PATTERN emission, the `default` rule, and the exhaustiveness check branch on it.
		// ⭐ **A SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION TAKES THE ENUM PATH WITH A LAYOUT THAT IS NOT THE SCRUTINEE'S TYPE.**
		// The value `e` holds is a bare flag; what an arm dispatches on is the `(member, case)` pair the routed
		// edges fused into one ordinal, and the layout below is the table over those pairs. Everything after
		// this point — coverage, the interval plan, the dispatch, the arm bodies, the merge — is the ordinary
		// enum machinery, which is the whole reason shv2 needs no second match engine where both references
		// wrote one (`parseErrorUnionMatch` / `ParseUnionMatch`).
		var enumMatch = self.enumMatchFor(scrut)
		if isErrorUnionMatch 'synthesizedLayout'
			enumMatch = DeclaredEnum.declared(requireErrorUnionInfo(errorUnion).layout)
		end 'synthesizedLayout'
		let isEnumMatch = enumMatchIsDeclared(enumMatch)
		// One coverage bit per case, in declaration order — the enum twin of `seenValues`. Populated
		// only for an enum match; a bare case marks its bit, a range marks the slice it covers, and a
		// second mark on one bit is E2027. Its all-true state IS exhaustiveness.
		var enumCovered = BoolArray.create()
		if isEnumMatch 'initEnumCoverage'
			let layout = requireEnumLayout(enumMatch)
			for _ in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
				enumCovered.push(false)
			end 'eachCase'
		end 'initEnumCoverage'

		// The DISPATCH SUBJECT — what each arm's tag compare reads — through `emitEnumTagOf`, the one place
		// that answers where an enum value's tag lives (`.ordinal` reads the same door; see there for the
		// wrong answer their separate copies of this decision produced). For a boxed union that is a load
		// emitted ONCE here, into the first test block, which dominates every arm; for a scalar enum it is
		// the scrutinee itself and nothing is emitted. The scrutinee `scrut` stays the BOX either way, which
		// is what a payload binding reads its slots out of. Mirrors v1's `matchDispatchSubject`.
		//
		// The `isEnumMatch` guard stays HERE and is not the helper's: it is a question about the SCRUTINEE
		// (does this value belong to a declared enum at all?), where the helper's is about the LAYOUT. A
		// scalar match has no layout to ask.
		var dispatchSubject = scrut
		if isErrorUnionMatch 'fusedOrdinalIsTheSubject'
			// Already merged through the error block's last phi — every routed edge computed it where the
			// member was statically known, which is the only place a boxed member's tag COULD be read.
			dispatchSubject = requireErrorUnionInfo(errorUnion).fusedOrdinal
		end 'fusedOrdinalIsTheSubject' else if isEnumMatch 'enumDispatchSubject'
			dispatchSubject = self.emitEnumTagOf(scrut, layout: requireEnumLayout(enumMatch), range: SourceRange.ofToken(matchTok))
		end 'enumDispatchSubject'

		// ⭐ **MATCH-CONSUME BOOKKEEPING (P1.3 slice 2).** A managed payload BINDING takes its field out of
		// the box, and `scrutSolelyOwned` decides HOW: a box this frame is the ONLY owner of is MOVED out of;
		// anything else — a BORROWED scrutinee (a param/receiver/field read, whose owner is the caller's local)
		// and equally a CO-OWNED one (a box this frame holds a SECOND reference to) — is RETAINED from instead
		// (D1b — `moveOutManagedPayload` vs `retainBorrowedPayload`). If ANY arm MOVES a managed payload out, the
		// whole scrutinee is consumed: its binding is marked `partiallyMoved` — a re-read is E3102, but the
		// box is still dropped, its cascade null-guarding the moved slots. A match that retains, or binds
		// nothing managed, BORROWS — the union survives.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **TWO QUESTIONS, TWO PREDICATES, AND MERGING THEM WAS A SEGFAULT.** `scrutOwned` below is
		// *"does this value owe a drop?"* and is what the temporary-scrutinee enrolment needs — a co-owned
		// temporary owes exactly the same drop a fresh box does. `scrutSolelyOwned` is *"will anything else ever
		// read this box's slots?"* and is what the destructive move-out needs. One predicate served both for
		// three rungs, and six programs faulted through the gap; see `OwnedHeapExclusivity`.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE MARK IS PATH-SENSITIVE, AND IT LANDS AT THE ARM THAT MOVED — not after the loop.** The
		// scrutinee is dead for the REST OF THAT ARM the instant its payload leaves the box: the slot is
		// nulled, so a re-read inside the same arm (`text(s) gives grab(m)`, or a nested `match m`) hands
		// a null payload to whoever reads it next. Marked only after the loop, that read parsed against a
		// LIVE scrutinee and compiled to a SIGSEGV — measured both ways, and D1b widened it from "a nested
		// match in the same arm" to "any callee that binds the payload", because the borrowed bind a callee
		// needs was E2015 until this rung. A FRESH sibling arm rewinds it (`beginFreshArm` — the arms are
		// mutually exclusive, so an arm that did not run moved nothing), while an `and fallthrough`
		// successor INHERITS it, exactly as it inherits `movedFrom`.
		let scrutOwned = self.valueIsOwnedHeap(scrut)
		let scrutSolelyOwned = self.valueIsSoleHeapOwner(scrut)
		// ⭐ **AND WHETHER THE ARMS MAY WRITE THROUGH THEIR BINDINGS (`specs/mutable-enums.md`)** — read
		// HERE, beside the ownership question it is the second half of, and BEFORE the temporary-scrutinee
		// enrolment below so the question is asked of the author's own bindings. See
		// `scrutineeBindingIsMutable`.
		let scrutMutable = self.scrutineeBindingIsMutable(scrut)
		// "Some reaching path left the scrutinee consumed" — the merge-level union of the per-arm bit, which
		// is NOT the same as "some arm of THIS match moved a payload out": a nested `match m` in an arm body
		// consumes the same binding, and `beginFreshArm` carries that out of the arm it happened in.
		var scrutConsumed = false

		// ⭐ **A TEMPORARY OWNED SCRUTINEE IS ENROLLED AS A MATCH-SCOPED OWNED BINDING (P1.3).** A
		// constructed union box used directly — `match U.case(x) …` — is an owned heap TEMPORARY: it sits
		// in the pending-drop list, which is drained per STATEMENT, so whichever arm parses first and
		// terminates (`return`/`break`/`continue`) would drain it into that arm's block ALONE, leaking it
		// on every other arm's runtime path (exit 101, arm-order-dependent). Enrolling it as an owned
		// binding at the enclosing scope BEFORE the arm loop makes it drop EXACTLY ONCE on all four exits —
		// a `return` arm and an implicit end through `emitScopeDrops`, a `break`/`continue` arm through the
		// loop-floor drop, a fall-through arm through the enclosing scope's own exit drop — the same
		// machinery a `let m = U.case(x)` scrutinee already rides. A managed box still drops through its
		// `__destruct_<U>` cascade, null-guarding any slot a binding arm moved out. An ALREADY-BOUND
		// scrutinee (`let m = …; match m`) is already in `ownedBindings` (never in the pending list), so it
		// is left alone — a second enrolment would double-drop the one box.
		// The `scrutOwned` half stays HERE rather than moving into the shared door: the pending list also
		// carries an `async` spawn's PROMISE (`emitAsyncCall` enrols one directly), which owns a green thread
		// rather than heap and must not be re-homed as an owned heap binding by a `match` on it.
		if scrutOwned 'enrollTempScrutinee'
			self.giveTemporaryScopeLifetime(scrut, name: ScrutineeSourceBindingName)
		end 'enrollTempScrutinee'

		// ⭐ **AND THE SCRUTINEE EXPRESSION'S OTHER TEMPORARIES DIE HERE, BEFORE THE FIRST CASE TEST.** The
		// enrolment above rescues the scrutinee VALUE when it is itself an owned box; it says nothing about
		// what the expression built on the way to producing it — `match [1,2,3].count()` yields a plain
		// integer and abandons an array. Left in the list, that array is released by whichever arm's body
		// drains first, on that arm's path alone (the same arm-order-dependent leak the enrolment comment
		// above describes, one level down). `currentBlock` is the scrutinee's exit block, which dominates
		// every arm test, every arm body and the merge — so one release here covers all of them.
		self.drainPendingTemps(scrutTempFloor, anchor: matchTok)

		// The move state entering the arms — captured after the temp-scrutinee enrolment so the scrutinee
		// binding is included. Each FRESH arm is parsed from it (`restoreMoveMark` at the arm-restore sites,
		// the twin of `restoreBindings`), and each reaching arm captures its own exit move state for the
		// merge reconciliation. A `and fallthrough` arm does NOT restore — it inherits the prior arm's state.
		let entryMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()

		// ⭐ **THE MATCH BECOMES A `break` TARGET FOR ITS ARMS** (see `MatchContext`). A match STATEMENT
		// only: every arm of an EXPRESSION feeds the one result phi, and a `break` that skipped an arm would
		// leave that phi an operand short. Pushed AFTER the temporary-scrutinee enrolment so `ownedFloor`
		// sits ABOVE that box — the box outlives the match, and a break must not drop it.
		if not isExpression 'pushBreakTarget'
			self.matches.push(MatchContext.create(matchLabel, carried: carried, ownedFloor: self.ownedBindings.count()))
		end 'pushBreakTarget'

		// ⭐ **DISPATCH STRATEGY (match-dispatch Slice 1).** An integer-like scrutinee builds a normalized
		// interval PLAN and dispatches by a balanced binary search (or a chain when small); a `String`/
		// `float` scrutinee keeps the per-arm compare chain (`emitPatternCondition`). Under the plan the arm
		// loop emits NO test blocks or branches — it only collects `DispatchInterval`s and parses bodies —
		// and the whole dispatch is emitted after the loop, into `dispatchEntry` (the block holding
		// `dispatchSubject`, which every compare then reads). `planMissBlock`/`planMissArgs` name where a
		// value in no interval goes: the `default` body (set when the default arm is parsed) or a `dead`
		// block for an exhaustive enum (created after the loop).
		let usePlan = self.matchUsesIntervalPlan(isEnumMatch, scrut: scrut)
		// ⭐ **THE ENTRY BLOCK IS HELD BY ITS `BlockId`, NOT AS A SECOND NAME FOR `currentTestBlock`** —
		// `IrModule.getBlockByIdIn`'s own rule (*"use this instead of holding an IrBlock struct alias across
		// other block-creating / block-mutating calls"*), and the arm loop below is exactly such a span: it
		// mints a block per arm. Aliasing it here would also be a second mutable name for one owned value,
		// which single ownership refuses (E3102 on the next read of `currentTestBlock`) — the block is
		// re-fetched at the one place the dispatch is emitted.
		let dispatchEntryId = currentTestBlock.id
		var dispatchIntervals = DispatchIntervalArray.create()
		// The block a value in no interval branches to. Seeded to the entry only as a placeholder — it is
		// always overwritten before the dispatch is emitted (a `default` body, or an exhaustive enum's dead
		// block), and a scalar match with no default throws E2026 before then.
		var planMissBlockId = dispatchEntryId
		var planMissArgs = ValueIdArray.create()

		while true 'arms'
			self.skipNewlines()
			// The closing `end` — unless the keyword is a NAME here (an `end then …` arm on an enum whose
			// case is spelled `end`, or a member read `Kw.end`). Asked of `closesBlockAt` rather than
			// spelled out, so this loop and the block-extent scans cannot drift on what an `end` is.
			if self.closesBlockAt(self.pos) 'endOfArms'
				break
			end 'endOfArms'

			if self.at(TokenKind.default) 'defaultArm'
				// A second `default`, or any `default` that is not last, is E2029 — reported when a
				// pattern arm FOLLOWS a default (below); a default following a default is the same rule.
				if sawDefault 'defaultAfterDefault'
					let dupTok = self.current()
					throw ParseError.matchDefaultNotLast(dupTok.line, column: dupTok.column)
				end 'defaultAfterDefault'

				let defTok = self.current()
				try self.consume(TokenKind.default)
				// The default's body: for a PLANNED match its own block (the dispatch's miss edges branch
				// here); for the linear chain the last real arm's `else` target (`currentTestBlock`), or the
				// entry block when the match is default-only. `var` because `parseArmBody` mutates it.
				//
				// ⚠ **SEATED FROM AN ID, BECAUSE `currentTestBlock` IS CARRIED BY THE ARM LOOP.** Binding it
				// to a second name here would MOVE it out of the loop (a bare-local read is a move), which is
				// E2015. `.id` is a field read off a borrowed receiver, and the block is re-fetched — the same
				// id-threading the arm loop already uses for `prevTestBlockId`.
				var defaultBodyId = currentTestBlock.id
				if usePlan 'planDefaultBlock'
					defaultBodyId = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matchdefault", line: defTok.line).id
				end 'planDefaultBlock'
				var defaultBody = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: defaultBodyId) otherwise panic("parseMatchCommon: the default body block {defaultBodyId} is not in its own function")
				if prevFallthrough 'defaultIsFtTarget'
					let matchEdgeArgs = self.setupFallthroughTargetPhis(defaultBody, carried: carried, entryVals: entryVals, prevExitBlock: prevExitBlock, prevExitVals: prevExitVals)
					if usePlan 'planDefaultArgs'
						// The dispatch's miss edges into the default body carry these phi args — recorded when
						// the dispatch is emitted after the loop, not here.
						planMissArgs = matchEdgeArgs
					end 'planDefaultArgs' else if hasRealArm 'defaultFailEdge'
						// The fail edge into the default body — the last real arm's `condBranch` else, already
						// emitted; only its phi args are recorded now.
						var prevTestBlock = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: prevTestBlockId) otherwise panic("parseMatchCommon: the previous test block {prevTestBlockId} is not in its own function")
						self.recordBranchEdge(prevTestBlock, targetId: defaultBody.id, argIds: matchEdgeArgs)
					end 'defaultFailEdge'
				end 'defaultIsFtTarget' else 'defaultFresh'
					if self.beginFreshArm(carried, entryVals: entryVals, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, scrut: scrut) 'priorArmConsumed'
						scrutConsumed = true
					end 'priorArmConsumed'
				end 'defaultFresh'

				if usePlan 'planMissTarget'
					planMissBlockId = defaultBody.id
				end 'planMissTarget'

				// ⭐ **A `default` ON AN ERROR UNION MAY BE HOLDING A BOX NOTHING ELSE WILL RELEASE.** Every
				// PATTERN arm names its member, so a boxed one enrols the box and drops it (the arm's `enrollCaughtBox`);
				// a `default` names none, and on the edges where a boxed member IS the error in flight the box
				// would simply be abandoned. The cleanup runs first and the body is parsed into the block it
				// leaves control in — so `defaultBody` is re-seated AFTER the miss target and the fallthrough
				// phis, which both belong to the block the edges actually branch to.
				defaultBody = self.emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup(func, union: errorUnion, entry: defaultBody, flag: scrut, anchor: defTok)

				// ⛔⛔ **THE FLOORS ARE TAKEN ONCE, ABOVE THE FORK, BECAUSE BOTH DEFAULT SHAPES OWE THE SAME
				// EXIT — and the DIVERGING one did not pay it (measured: a compiler panic).** `default throws
				// E.p(Box.make().name)` takes a member borrow off a temporary, so `dispatchMethodOnBase` calls
				// `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime`, which moves the box from `pendingTempDrops` into
				// `ownedBindings`; `emitHandoffExitDrops` then RESTORES `ownedBindings`, so the record survives
				// the arm with nothing draining it. With no floor to drain to, the mark and the owned-binding
				// stack fell out of lockstep and `reconcileMovesAtMerge` panicked *"a reaching edge's mark holds
				// 0 entries but 1 owned bindings are in scope"* — on a program the bootstrap compiles and runs
				// (measured: `gives=5`, `throws=9`, exit 0).
				//
				// ⚠ **A3h FIXED THE PLAIN DEFAULT AND LEFT THIS ONE**, which is why the floors moved UP here
				// rather than a second pair being taken inside the diverging branch: one arm, one exit
				// obligation, taken in one place. Of the three positions sharing `parseDivergingTerminalForm`,
				// the other two already bracketed it — a block-form `try`'s divergent `otherwise` through
				// `dropArmScopedOwned`, a diverging EXPRESSION arm through `finishArmExit` — so this was the
				// odd one out rather than a new rule.
				let defaultTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
				// ⚠ The catch-all arm had NO owned floor at all until A3h, so a scope-lifetime record it
				// enrolled — today a `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` temporary, and a managed payload binding
				// the day a `default` can bind one — accumulated into the enclosing scope and was decref'd
				// in the continuation, on every OTHER arm's path too. It settles exactly as a pattern arm
				// does now, through the one door.
				let defaultOwnedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()

				// ⭐ **`default throws E.case` / `default panic("…")`** (P1.4b) — a DIVERGING catch-all that
				// covers every unmatched case (so the match is exhaustive). It is the ONLY legal enum
				// default; a scalar match may use it too. A PLAIN `default` (then/gives) stays E2046 on an
				// enum match (a catch-all would silently mask a case added later) and a real body on a
				// scalar match. A diverging default terminates its body and so reaches no merge.
				if divergentHandlerOpensAt(self.kindAt(self.pos)) 'divergingDefault'
					try self.parseDivergingTerminalForm(func, block: defaultBody)
					// The exit is `currentBlock`, not `defaultBody`: the diverging form's own expression may
					// have moved control (a `try` inside a thrown value's arguments), exactly as
					// `parseDivergentOtherwise` reads it. It carries no give — the discard says so, where a
					// `MatchGiveValue` variable would only be read back as the `none` it went in as.
					_ = try self.finishArmExit(MatchGiveValue.none, tempFloor: defaultTempFloor, ownedFloor: defaultOwnedFloor, exitBlock: self.currentBlock, anchor: defTok)
				end 'divergingDefault' else 'plainDefault'
					if isErrorUnionMatch 'errorUnionPlainDefault'
						throw ParseError.errorUnionMatchDefaultMustThrow(defTok.line, column: defTok.column)
					end 'errorUnionPlainDefault'
					if isEnumMatch 'enumPlainDefault'
						let layout = requireEnumLayout(enumMatch)
						throw ParseError.matchDefaultEnumMustThrow(layout.kindWord(), typeName: String.from(layout.name), line: defTok.line, column: defTok.column)
					end 'enumPlainDefault'
					var defaultArmBody = try self.parseArmBody(func, bodyBlock: defaultBody, isExpression: isExpression, carried: carried)
					defaultArmBody.give = try self.finishArmExit(defaultArmBody.give, tempFloor: defaultTempFloor, ownedFloor: defaultOwnedFloor, exitBlock: defaultArmBody.exitBlock, anchor: matchTok)
					// `default` is the last arm, so a fall-through has no next arm to reach — it simply
					// continues to the merge. So the test is "did control leave?", not `reachesMerge`.
					if not defaultArmBody.exitBlock.isTerminated() 'defaultReaches'
						reachingArms.push(MatchArmExit.create(defaultArmBody.exitBlock, exitVals: defaultArmBody.exitVals, give: defaultArmBody.give, moveMark: self.captureMoveMark(), blame: defaultArmBody.blame))
					end 'defaultReaches'
				end 'plainDefault'
				sawDefault = true
				prevFallthrough = false
			end 'defaultArm' else 'patternArm'
				if sawDefault 'armAfterDefault'
					let strayTok = self.current()
					throw ParseError.matchDefaultNotLast(strayTok.line, column: strayTok.column)
				end 'armAfterDefault'

				let patternTok = self.current()
				// `var` because the body block is forwarded to functions that fill it (payload phis, the arm
				// body) — a `let` cannot back a mutating parameter.
				var bodyBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matcharm", line: patternTok.line)
				// The linear chain's next test block, held by ID rather than as a second NAME for
				// `bodyBlock` — one block, one owner, so aliasing it would poison `bodyBlock` for the
				// pattern collectors below (E3102). A planned match emits its dispatch after the loop and
				// mints its own test blocks there, so it creates none here (an unused block would be dead).
				var nextTestId = bodyBlock.id
				if not usePlan 'chainNextTest'
					nextTestId = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matchnext", line: patternTok.line).id
				end 'chainNextTest'

				// The pattern's facts: an interval PLAN (enum tags, or ordered-scalar values — collected, no
				// compare emitted) or an emitted per-arm CONDITION (a `String`/`float` chain). A pattern never
				// short-circuits, so `currentBlock` does not move; it matters only for the emitted-cond path.
				self.currentBlock = currentTestBlock
				var cond = 0 as ValueId
				var payloadBindings = PayloadBindingArray.create()
				// ⭐ **WHOSE LAYOUT THIS ARM'S PAYLOAD PLAYS BY.** For an ordinary enum match it is the
				// scrutinee's own, as it always was. For an ERROR UNION it is the arm's MEMBER: the synthesized
				// table carries no payload slots (it is a dispatch table — see `ErrorUnionInfo`), and a payload
				// diagnostic must name the enum the author actually wrote rather than `__ErrorUnion`.
				//
				// ⚠ **IT STARTS `undeclared` RATHER THAN AS A SECOND NAME FOR `enumMatch`** — that binding would
				// MOVE the match's scrutinee enum out of the arm loop (E2015). Every branch that produces payload
				// bindings records the layout it produced them from, so `undeclared` here can only survive to the
				// use below on an arm with NO bindings, where nothing reads it; `requireEnumLayout` is what says
				// so, and it panics rather than guessing if that ever stops holding.
				var armPayloadEnum = DeclaredEnum.undeclared
				// Does this arm's member deliver a heap BOX the ARM must release? Only an error union can, and
				// `armMemberBoxType` is read only under this flag — the `hasBinding`/`bindingName` pairing this
				// file uses wherever one fact governs the meaning of another.
				var armOwnsMemberBox = false
				var armMemberBoxType = MaxonType.integer
				let intervalMark = dispatchIntervals.count()
				if isErrorUnionMatch 'errorUnionPattern'
					let arm = try self.collectErrorUnionPatternInterval(requireErrorUnionInfo(errorUnion), covered: enumCovered, bodyBlockId: bodyBlock.id, into: dispatchIntervals)
					payloadBindings = arm.bindings
					armPayloadEnum = DeclaredEnum.declared(arm.member.layout)
					armOwnsMemberBox = arm.member.isBoxed
					armMemberBoxType = self.tryErrorEnumType(arm.member.clause)
					if prevFallthrough 'ftTargetBindsUnion'
						try self.rejectFallthroughIntoBindingArm(payloadBindings, layout: arm.member.layout, patternTok: patternTok)
					end 'ftTargetBindsUnion'
				end 'errorUnionPattern' else if isEnumMatch 'enumPattern'
					let armLayout = requireEnumLayout(enumMatch)
					payloadBindings = try self.collectEnumPatternIntervals(armLayout, covered: enumCovered, bodyBlockId: bodyBlock.id, into: dispatchIntervals, intervalMark: intervalMark)
					// An `and fallthrough` edge reaches this arm's BODY from a different case, and this arm's
					// bindings sit at the head of that body — so they would destructure a case the value does
					// not hold. Checked here, where the pattern's bindings are first in hand and before any
					// phi or edge is wired for the fallthrough. Only an enum pattern produces bindings, so a
					// scalar match cannot reach it.
					if prevFallthrough 'ftTargetBinds'
						try self.rejectFallthroughIntoBindingArm(payloadBindings, layout: armLayout, patternTok: patternTok)
					end 'ftTargetBinds'
					armPayloadEnum = DeclaredEnum.declared(armLayout)
				end 'enumPattern' else if usePlan 'scalarPlanPattern'
					try self.collectScalarPatternIntervals(scrutTag, seenValues: seenValues, bodyBlockId: bodyBlock.id, into: dispatchIntervals)
				end 'scalarPlanPattern' else 'scalarChainPattern'
					cond = try self.emitPatternCondition(scrut, scrutTag: scrutTag, seenValues: seenValues)
				end 'scalarChainPattern'

				var matchEdgeArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
				if prevFallthrough 'armIsFtTarget'
					matchEdgeArgs = self.setupFallthroughTargetPhis(bodyBlock, carried: carried, entryVals: entryVals, prevExitBlock: prevExitBlock, prevExitVals: prevExitVals)
				end 'armIsFtTarget' else 'armFresh'
					if self.beginFreshArm(carried, entryVals: entryVals, entryMoveMark: entryMoveMark, scrut: scrut) 'priorArmConsumed'
						scrutConsumed = true
					end 'priorArmConsumed'
				end 'armFresh'

				if usePlan 'planEdgeArgs'
					// Stamp the carried-var phi args across every interval this arm contributed (an or-arm or a
					// split range reaches one body through several intervals); the dispatch records the
					// test→body edge with them. No `condBranch` here — the dispatch emits all of them later.
					for k in intervalMark upto dispatchIntervals.count() 'stampArgs'
						var interval = try dispatchIntervals.get(k) otherwise panic("parseMatchCommon: dispatchIntervals.get OOB at {k}")
						interval.edgeArgs = matchEdgeArgs
					end 'stampArgs'
				end 'planEdgeArgs' else 'chainEdge'
					self.recordBranchEdge(currentTestBlock, targetId: bodyBlock.id, argIds: matchEdgeArgs)
					self.emitCondBranch(currentTestBlock, cond: cond, thenId: bodyBlock.id, elseId: nextTestId, ifTok: patternTok)
				end 'chainEdge'

				// A `walk(dir)` arm's payload bindings are declared at the HEAD of the arm body, in a
				// scope frame that closes at the arm's `end` so they cannot leak into the next arm. Each
				// reads a slot out of the scrutinee BOX (`scrut`), not the loaded tag `dispatchSubject`.
				// Only an enum pattern produces bindings, so `requireEnumLayout` is guarded.
				// The owned-binding height BEFORE the arm's payload bindings — an arm-local drop FLOOR, the
				// enum twin of `openBlock`'s `ownedCount`. A managed payload binding is enrolled in
				// `ownedBindings` above this floor, and must be dropped on the edge that leaves the arm (see
				// `dropArmScopedOwned`) rather than accumulating there to be decref'd in the continuation,
				// where a value defined in only one arm's block is garbage on every other arm's path.
				let armOwnedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()

				// ⭐⭐ **AN ERROR-UNION ARM ON A BOXED MEMBER OWNS THE BOX FOR THE LENGTH OF THE ARM.** The `(e)`
				// binding owns nothing (see `declareErrorUnionBinding`): the flag is a pointer on some incoming
				// edges and an ordinal on others, so only here — where the arm has NAMED its member — is the
				// answer known. Enrolled BELOW the payload bindings, so the reverse-order arm drop releases the
				// payload's own reference first and the box last.
				if armOwnsMemberBox 'boxedMemberArm'
					self.currentBlock = bodyBlock
					// The SAME enrolment a single-type boxed catch makes at the handler's head — co-owned for
					// its stated reason (the throw may have RETAINED rather than moved). Only the SCOPE differs,
					// and it differs because only an arm knows which member is in flight.
					self.enrollCaughtBox(scrut, eType: armMemberBoxType, binding: VarInfo.createValueBinding(EmptySourceText, varType: armMemberBoxType, mutable: false, boundValue: scrut))
				end 'boxedMemberArm'

				let hasPayloadBindings = payloadBindings.count() > 0
				if hasPayloadBindings 'openArmBindings'
					self.currentBlock = bodyBlock
					self.scope.pushScope()
					let armMovedManaged = try self.declarePayloadBindings(payloadBindings, scrutBox: scrut, scrutSolelyOwned: scrutSolelyOwned, scrutMutable: scrutMutable, layout: requireEnumLayout(armPayloadEnum))
					if armMovedManaged 'consumed'
						// The scrutinee is dead for the REST OF THIS ARM from here — its slot is nulled, so the
						// arm body's own re-read must be E3102 rather than a null deref. See the match-consume
						// header for the measured SIGSEGV this ordering is the cure for.
						//
						// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT LATCH `scrutConsumed`, AND THAT IS `mutable-enums`.** "This arm took a
						// payload out" and "this arm LEFT the scrutinee consumed" stopped being the same fact the
						// moment a write-back could put the payload back (`writeBackPayload`), so the merge-level
						// bit is read off the arm's EXIT state instead — by the next arm's `beginFreshArm`, and
						// for the last arm by the single read after the loop.
						self.setBindingPartiallyMoved(scrut, value: true)
					end 'consumed'
				end 'openArmBindings'

				let armTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
				var armBody = try self.parseArmBody(func, bodyBlock: bodyBlock, isExpression: isExpression, carried: carried)

				if hasPayloadBindings 'closeArmBindings'
					self.scope.popScope()
				end 'closeArmBindings'

				// An expression arm's OWNED give value escapes to the result phi, so move it out of its owner
				// BEFORE the arm's records are released — else a `text(s) gives s` arm would drop the payload
				// it is handing back (a use-after-free), and an owned-interpolation give would be double-freed
				// by the post-match drain. A BORROWED give out of one of those records is laundered instead,
				// and a give may therefore come back as a different SSA name — see `finishArmExit`, which is
				// the one door all three of an arm's exit obligations go through.
				armBody.give = try self.finishArmExit(armBody.give, tempFloor: armTempFloor, ownedFloor: armOwnedFloor, exitBlock: armBody.exitBlock, anchor: patternTok)

				// ⚠ **AND THE FLAG GOES BACK TO BEING THE UNION BINDING.** One SSA value serves every arm of an
				// error-union match, and a boxed arm re-typed it to ITS member so the drop `finishArmExit` has
				// just emitted routes to that member's `__destruct_`. Left standing, the next arm — which may
				// name a different member, or a scalar one — would see `e` typed as the previous arm's enum.
				// The arms are mutually exclusive, so the claim is only ever true inside the arm that made it.
				if armOwnsMemberBox 'flagIsTheUnionBindingAgain'
					self.retypeValue(scrut, valueType: MaxonType.integer)
				end 'flagIsTheUnionBindingAgain'

				if armBody.fellThrough 'fellThrough'
					prevFallthrough = true
					prevExitBlock = armBody.exitBlock
					prevExitVals = armBody.exitVals
				end 'fellThrough' else 'notFallThrough'
					prevFallthrough = false
					if armBody.reachesMerge 'armReaches'
						reachingArms.push(MatchArmExit.create(armBody.exitBlock, exitVals: armBody.exitVals, give: armBody.give, moveMark: self.captureMoveMark(), blame: armBody.blame))
					end 'armReaches'
				end 'notFallThrough'

				prevTestBlockId = currentTestBlock.id
				hasRealArm = true
				if not usePlan 'chainAdvance'
					currentTestBlock = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: nextTestId) otherwise panic("parseMatchCommon: the chain's next test block {nextTestId} is not in its own function")
				end 'chainAdvance'
			end 'patternArm'
		end 'arms'

		// ⚠ An exhaustive enum match's FINAL `else` target — the last arm's fail edge — is UNREACHABLE
		// (every value matches some arm), but the last arm's `condBranch` named it, so the CFG edge
		// exists and the block needs a terminator. `dead` marks it unreachable, the same terminator the
		// parser gives code after a fully-terminated `if`/`else`. A scalar match cannot reach here with
		// a dangling block: it is exhaustive only through a `default`, whose BODY consumes this block.
		// ⚠ Skipped when `sawDefault` (a `default throws`/`panic`): the final-else block IS that diverging
		// default's body, already given a real terminator above — overwriting it with `dead` would orphan
		// the throw/panic and silently drop it.
		if isEnumMatch and not sawDefault 'enumFallOffUnreachable'
			// The dispatch's miss target. A planned match routes misses to a fresh `dead` block; the linear
			// chain's final `else` target IS that block (`currentTestBlock`). Either way the miss is
			// unreachable (every value matches some arm).
			if usePlan 'planDeadMiss'
			planMissBlockId = self.newDeadBlock(func, label: b"matchdead", line: matchTok.line).id
			end 'planDeadMiss' else 'chainDeadMiss'
				currentTestBlock.terminator = Terminator.dead
			end 'chainDeadMiss'

			// A trailing `and fallthrough` on the LAST arm has no following arm or `default` to fall
			// INTO (a pattern arm cannot follow a default, and an enum match has no legal default at
			// this rung), so it falls OUT of the match to the continuation — exactly as an arm that
			// reaches the merge does. Without this the arm body is left unterminated and
			// `assertAllBlocksTerminated` panics; the reference compiler accepts it as a no-op tail.
			// Statement form only: `and fallthrough` after a `gives` arm is already rejected, so the
			// give is `none`. A SCALAR match cannot reach this with `prevFallthrough`: a trailing
			// fallthrough with no `default` is rejected by the `notExhaustive` throw below.
			if prevFallthrough 'trailingFallthrough'
				reachingArms.push(MatchArmExit.create(prevExitBlock, exitVals: prevExitVals, give: MatchGiveValue.none, moveMark: self.captureMoveMark(), blame: EmptySourceText))
			end 'trailingFallthrough'
		end 'enumFallOffUnreachable'

		// EXHAUSTIVENESS. An enum match must cover every case (E2026 listing the missing ones); a
		// scalar match is exhaustive only through a `default` (without one it would silently do nothing
		// for an unmatched value). Both are anchored at the closing `end`, as both references anchor it.
		if isEnumMatch and not sawDefault 'enumExhaustive'
			// A `default throws`/`panic` covers every unmatched case, so the case-set need not be complete.
			try self.checkEnumExhaustive(requireEnumLayout(enumMatch), covered: enumCovered, union: errorUnion)
		end 'enumExhaustive' else if not isEnumMatch and not sawDefault 'notExhaustive'
			var subject = "match"
			if isExpression 'exprSubject'
				subject = "match expression"
			end 'exprSubject'
			let endMissing = self.current()
			throw ParseError.matchNotExhaustive(subject, line: endMissing.line, column: endMissing.column)
		end 'notExhaustive'

		// Drift guard, mirroring the `if`/`while` token-extent scans: the `end` the parser reached
		// must be the one `blockStatementEndIndex` predicted, or `opensBlockAt` misclassified a
		// construct nested in the arms and the carried set may be short.
		self.lastBlockBodyEnd = self.pos
		let endTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.end)
		self.assertScanAligned("parseMatch", predictedEnd: matchEnd)

		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'endLabel'
			let closeTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
			if not closeTok.value.equals(matchLabel) 'labelMismatch'
				// ⛔ **`got` IS HAND-SPELLED HERE ON PURPOSE — DO NOT ROUTE IT THROUGH `tokenDisplayText`.**
				// The rule stated on `ParseError.unexpectedToken` ("`got` is `tokenDisplayText`'s at every
				// raiser") is about TOKENS THE AUTHOR MIGHT NOT HAVE SPELLED, and its fallback rests on
				// "an empty value means the KIND implies the text" — true of a punctuator (`equals` is
				// literally `=`), FALSE of a block label, which is a `charLiteral` whose value IS the
				// label and may legitimately be EMPTY. MEASURED: `end ''` reports `expected 'pick', got ''`
				// today; through the helper it would report `got 'character literal'` — the reader hunting
				// for a string that is not in the file, which is the exact D11c defect the diagnostic
				// sweep removed everywhere else. A label is always spelled, so there is nothing to fall
				// back to. (Found by the S2f review, which read the new rule as broad enough to invite a
				// later tidy-up here.)
				throw ParseError.matchMismatchedBlockId(String.from(matchLabel), got: String.from(closeTok.value), line: endTok.line, column: endTok.column)
			end 'labelMismatch'
		end 'endLabel'

		// A binding-match that moved a managed payload out CONSUMES the scrutinee: mark its binding
		// `partiallyMoved` so a later read is E3102 (a re-match would deref a nulled slot). The box is
		// still dropped at scope exit — its cascade null-guards the moved slots. A TEMPORARY scrutinee's
		// SYNTHETIC binding (enrolled above) is marked too, which is harmless — it has no name to re-read,
		// and `partiallyMoved` skips no drop, so the box still drops through the cascade.
		//
		// ⚠ Re-asserted here rather than left to the in-arm mark, because the LAST arm parsed may have been
		// a fresh non-consuming one, which rewound it (`beginFreshArm`). The consuming arm's mark governs
		// its OWN body; this one governs the code after the whole match, where any arm may have run.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE LAST ARM'S OWN EXIT STATE IS READ HERE, and it is the same read every EARLIER arm gets
		// from the `beginFreshArm` that follows it.** No arm follows the last one, so without this line its
		// consume would be lost. Reading the STATE rather than latching a bit at the bind is what lets a
		// write-back un-consume the scrutinee (`writeBackPayload` — `mutable-enums`): the question is what
		// the arm LEFT BEHIND, not what it did on the way.
		scrutConsumed = scrutConsumed or self.bindingIsPartiallyMoved(scrut)

		if scrutConsumed 'consumeScrut'
			self.setBindingPartiallyMoved(scrut, value: true)
		end 'consumeScrut'

		// ⭐ **EMIT THE PLANNED DISPATCH** — after every arm body and the default/dead miss target are in
		// hand, and after exhaustiveness has run (so a non-exhaustive scalar match threw before this).
		// `planMissBlock` is always set for a planned match by here: a `default` set it, an exhaustive enum's
		// dead block set it, and a scalar match with no default cannot reach here. The chain path already
		// wired its dispatch inline, so this runs only for a planned match.
		if usePlan 'emitPlan'
			let normalized = self.normalizeDispatchIntervals(dispatchIntervals)
			try self.emitMatchDispatch(func, entryId: dispatchEntryId, subject: dispatchSubject, intervals: normalized, missTarget: planMissBlockId, missArgs: planMissArgs, opTok: matchTok)
		end 'emitPlan'

		// ⭐⭐ **DRAIN THE BREAK EXITS INTO THE REACHING SET — BEFORE `finalizeMatchMerge` COUNTS IT.** That
		// ordering IS the cure for v1's all-break merge-dead bug (`MatchContext.breakExits`): the merge is
		// sealed `dead` on `reachingArms.count() == 0`, and a match whose every arm breaks has an empty
		// reaching set until this line runs. Appended at the end rather than interleaved in arm order — the
		// edges are recorded on each arm's OWN block, so the merge's predecessor order is not a property of
		// this list, and `finalizeMatchMerge`'s only order-sensitive read is "arm 0 is the type reference",
		// which a statement match does not use.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **PUSHED ONE AT A TIME, AND `append` WOULD BE A WRONG ANSWER — THE DIVERGENCE THAT STOPPED SHV2
		// SELF-HOSTING.** `Array.append` DEEP-CLONES a managed element in shv2, deliberately and by its own
		// runtime's design: shv2 is move-only, so two arrays holding one element record would double-destroy
		// it (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedCloneManagedName` states the rule and why both references, which
		// refcount, may incref instead). A `MatchArmExit` cloned that way carries a CLONE of its
		// `exitBlock` — so `finalizeMatchMerge` terminated a copy and the module's real block was left
		// `Terminator.unset`. `push` co-owns the record, which is what this drain needs and what the
		// reference compilers happened to give `append` for free.
		//
		// ⛔ MEASURED end to end: the bootstrap-built compiler compiled this file correctly (its `append`
		// shares), so the defect was invisible until shv2 compiled ITSELF — and the shv2-built compiler then
		// died on every `break` out of a `match`: *"IrBlock 'matcharm' (id=2) has no terminator — parser left
		// it in Terminator.unset"*, on 87 specs at once. The one-line source repro is
		// `b.append(a)` over an `Array with <a struct holding a managed field>`: **oracle 5, shv2 0**.
		if not isExpression 'drainBreakExits'
			let matchCtx = try self.matches.pop() otherwise panic("parseMatchCommon: the match stack is empty at the end of a statement match — one is pushed before the arm loop")
			for i in 0 upto matchCtx.breakExits.count() 'eachBreakExit'
				reachingArms.push(try matchCtx.breakExits.get(i) otherwise panic("parseMatchCommon: breakExits.get OOB at {i} inside its own count()"))
			end 'eachBreakExit'
		end 'drainBreakExits'

		let outcome = try self.finalizeMatchMerge(func, matchTok: matchTok, carried: carried, reachingArms: reachingArms, construct: MergeConstruct.matchExpression if isExpression else MergeConstruct.matchStatement)
		self.currentBlock = outcome.cont
		return outcome
	end 'parseMatchCommon'

	// ⭐ **DROP A MATCH ARM'S OWNED (managed) PAYLOAD BINDINGS**, the enum twin of `closeBlock` +
	// `parseBlockBody`'s fall-through drop. The arm's managed bindings own heap that must be released when
	// the arm exits, and the release is keyed on HOW it exits (the block rule, exactly):
	//   • a LIVE exit — a fall-through to the merge, or `and fallthrough` to the next arm — still owns the
	//     bindings here, so drop them into the exit block before it merges (a decref left for the
	//     continuation would run in a block where a one-arm value is garbage on every other arm's path — a
	//     use-after-free, exit 0xC0000005);
	//   • a TERMINATED exit — a `return`/`break`/`continue` — already dropped them via `emitScopeDrops`
	//     down to its own floor, so drop nothing.
	// The tracking is drained to `floor` EITHER way, so the next arm starts clean and the function-end
	// drop never sees an arm binding. `patternTok` anchors the drop, as the arm's pattern is where a
	// reader looks for what became of its bindings.
	function dropArmScopedOwned(floor BindingMark, exitBlock IrBlock, anchor Token)
		if not exitBlock.isTerminated() 'leavesAlive'
			// `emitScopeDrops` saves and restores `currentBlock` itself now, so the arm no longer does.
			self.emitScopeDrops(exitBlock, floor: floor, anchor: anchor)
		end 'leavesAlive'
		while self.ownedBindings.count() > floor 'drain'
			_ = try self.ownedBindings.pop() otherwise panic("dropArmScopedOwned: ownedBindings.pop failed above the floor {floor}")
		end 'drain'
	end 'dropArmScopedOwned'

	// ⭐ **BEGIN A FRESH (non-fallthrough) MATCH ARM** — rewind every piece of per-path state the preceding
	// arm advanced, back to what the match was entered with. THE one door, so the "what does a fresh arm
	// inherit?" question is answered in a single place for the `default` arm and the pattern arm alike; a
	// fact rewound at one site and not the other is exactly the drift that leaks one arm's moves onto its
	// sibling. An `and fallthrough` target deliberately calls NONE of this — control really did arrive from
	// the previous arm, so it inherits that arm's bindings, its moves, and its consumed scrutinee.
	//
	// The scrutinee's `partiallyMoved` is rewound because the arms are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE: an arm that did
	// not run moved nothing out of the box, so a sibling arm reads a whole union. It cannot have been set
	// BEFORE the match — `match m` reads `m`, and `requireBindingLive` refuses a partially-moved read — so
	// `false` is the entry state rather than a captured one.
	//
	// ⚠ **RETURNS WHETHER THE PRECEDING PATH LEFT THE SCRUTINEE CONSUMED, and the caller MUST carry that to
	// the merge** — this rewind is per-PATH, but the code AFTER the match runs on every path, so a consume
	// on any one of them must survive. Returned rather than left to the caller's own bookkeeping because
	// the arm need not have consumed the scrutinee ITSELF: a NESTED `match m` inside the arm body sets the
	// same bit, and only the value read here can see it. (Without the carry, `text then match m …` followed
	// by a payload-free arm would leave `m` reading LIVE after the outer match, and a later bind would load
	// the nulled slot — the same SIGSEGV this rewind exists to close, one construct out.)
	function beginFreshArm(carried VarInfoArray, entryVals ValueIdArray, entryMoveMark MoveMark, scrut ValueId) returns bool
		let consumedOnThePrecedingPath = self.bindingIsPartiallyMoved(scrut)

		self.restoreBindings(carried, snapshot: entryVals)
		self.restoreMoveMark(entryMoveMark)
		self.setBindingPartiallyMoved(scrut, value: false)

		return consumedOnThePrecedingPath
	end 'beginFreshArm'

	// Set the `partiallyMoved` bit of the binding a match scrutinee denotes — `true` where a binding arm
	// moved a managed payload out (a re-read is then E3102, but the box is still dropped: see
	// `VarInfo.partiallyMoved`), `false` where a fresh sibling arm rewinds that.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE THREE QUESTIONS BELOW ARE ALL "WHAT DOES THE BINDING BEHIND THIS SCRUTINEE SAY?", AND THEY ALL
	// GO THROUGH `liveOwnerIndexOf`** — THE one owner scan (see its header). They each used to walk
	// `ownedBindings` themselves, which was three more spellings of a predicate whose whole point is being
	// single-homed: a refinement of what "the live owner" means, taught to the scan and not to a copy, would
	// have one of these answering about a `movedFrom` ghost while the move machinery answered about the real
	// owner — a disagreement invisible at either site read alone. A temporary scrutinee is owned by no
	// binding, so all three are correctly inert on it: it has no name to re-read, write through or ask.

	// Set the `partiallyMoved` bit of the binding `owner` denotes — `true` where something moved a managed
	// payload OUT of a box this binding still owns (a re-read is then E3102, but the box is still dropped:
	// see `VarInfo.partiallyMoved`), `false` where a fresh sibling `match` arm rewinds that, or where a
	// write-back has refilled the last vacated slot (`writeBackPayload`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **TWO PRODUCERS, ONE FACT — AND THE SECOND IS WHY THIS IS NO LONGER NAMED FOR SCRUTINEES.** A
	// binding-match nulls a union's payload slot; `__ManagedList.remove` nulls a NODE's `value@16` when it
	// hands the element to the caller (`ListRuntime.buildListUnlinkNode`). Both leave a binding owning a box
	// whose payload is gone, and both want the identical answer: refuse the re-read, keep the drop.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS INERT ON A VALUE NO BINDING OWNS**, which is exactly right for both: a temporary scrutinee
	// has no name to re-read, and so does `ml.remove(ml.insertLast(x))`.
	function setBindingPartiallyMoved(owner ValueId, value bool)
		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(owner)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'noBindingToMark'
			return
		end 'noBindingToMark'

		// A `var` bound from `.get(index)`: the flag is written THROUGH the shared `VarInfo` reference (the
		// `Scope.setValue` idiom `poisonOwningBinding` uses), so every holder observes it.
		var owned = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("setBindingPartiallyMoved: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		owned.partiallyMoved = value
	end 'setBindingPartiallyMoved'

	// Has the binding `owner` denotes had a managed payload moved out of it on the path parsed so far? The
	// reader half of the pair above. Only the `match` machinery asks — `remove` sets and never re-reads.
	function bindingIsPartiallyMoved(owner ValueId) returns bool
		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(owner)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'noBindingToAsk'
			return false
		end 'noBindingToAsk'

		let owned = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("bindingIsPartiallyMoved: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		return owned.partiallyMoved
	end 'bindingIsPartiallyMoved'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY A `match` ARM WRITE THROUGH THIS SCRUTINEE'S PAYLOAD BINDINGS (`specs/mutable-enums.md`)?**
	// True iff a MUTABLE binding is the live owner of the box — `var b = Box.full(10)` yes, `let b` no.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ASKS OWNERSHIP, WHERE v1 ASKS THE TOKENS.** v1 probes for `identifier` + the match label BEFORE
	// parsing the scrutinee, so only a bare local can ever be written through. Ownership answers the same
	// question from the fact that actually licenses the store, and answers it in shv2's own currency (a
	// `ValueId`): a parameter, a receiver, a field read and a computed expression all own nothing here, so
	// none of them is writable, and a TEMPORARY (`match makeBox() …`) is enrolled by
	// `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` as `mutable: false` precisely so a write into a box that is about to be
	// dropped is refused. Those are v1's answers, reached without a second grammar for "what shapes may
	// appear before a match label".
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS MEMBERSHIP, AND THE ACQUISITION DECISION (`scrutSolelyOwned`) IS PROVENANCE — THEY DISAGREE ON
	// THE LOOP-CARRIED PHI.** This one is right about mutability there and the provenance columns are wrong about
	// ownership, so a `var` union matched inside the loop that reassigns it comes out mutable-but-retained.
	// `declarePayloadBindings` demotes that combination to read-only at the branch that chose the retain —
	// see the `borrowedRetain` arm, and the exit-101 leak that measured it.
	function scrutineeBindingIsMutable(scrut ValueId) returns bool
		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(scrut)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'ownedByNoBinding'
			return false
		end 'ownedByNoBinding'

		let owner = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("scrutineeBindingIsMutable: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		return owner.mutable
	end 'scrutineeBindingIsMutable'

	// Parse ONE arm's body, in one of THREE shapes. An EXPRESSION arm is either a `gives` — a single
	// expression, whose value feeds the merge phi — or a DIVERGING terminal form (`panic("…")` /
	// `throws E.case`), which yields no value and reaches no merge; which one is decided by
	// `divergentHandlerOpensAt`, the roster the whole file shares, at the `'divergingArm'` block below.
	// A `then` arm is a single statement, and `and fallthrough` may follow it. Reports E2049 for a
	// block-opening statement body, E2025 for `return … and fallthrough`, and E2001 for a stray
	// `and` after either shape of expression arm.
	function parseArmBody(func IrFunction, bodyBlock IrBlock, isExpression bool, carried VarInfoArray) returns MatchArmBody throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = bodyBlock

		if isExpression 'givesArm'
			// ⭐⭐ **`<pattern> panic("…")` / `<pattern> throws E.case` — A DIVERGING ARM OF A MATCH EXPRESSION**
			// (`specs/match-expr-arm-divergent.md`), admitted through `divergentHandlerOpensAt` and read by
			// `parseDivergingTerminalForm`, exactly as the `default` arm one line of grammar away already is.
			// The keyword REPLACES `gives`: the arm yields no value, so it feeds no merge phi and the result
			// type is inferred from the `gives` arms alone. Its pattern still marks its coverage bit — that is
			// stamped by `collectEnumPatternIntervals` from the PATTERN, before this routine is entered — so a
			// diverging arm counts toward exhaustiveness just as the spec says, with nothing to add here.
			//
			// ⚠ **EXPRESSION FORM ONLY, WHICH IS THE SPEC'S OWN LINE.** A match STATEMENT arm already spells
			// both through its ordinary single-statement body (`red then panic("…")`, `red then throw E.case`),
			// so admitting the bare form there would be a second grammar for a thing the language already has.
			// A statement arm reaching a `panic` here therefore still gets `Expected 'then'`.
			if divergentHandlerOpensAt(self.kindAt(self.pos)) 'divergingArm'
				try self.parseDivergingTerminalForm(func, block: self.currentBlock)
				let divergedBlock = self.currentBlock
				try self.rejectStrayAndOnExpressionArm()

				// ⛔ **THE TERMINATOR IS CHECKED HERE, WHERE THE FAILURE CAN STILL BE NAMED.** Both routes
				// guarantee one — `parsePanicStatement` ends in `emitDeadReturn`, `emitThrowError` sets one —
				// and everything below depends on it: `reachesMerge` is stated `false` on the strength of it,
				// and `exitVals` is EMPTY because a block control never leaves has no values to carry out.
				// Left to be discovered downstream, a break here surfaces as `giveValueOf`'s generic "an
				// expression-match arm carries no `gives` value" twenty frames away, or as a short read inside
				// the merge's carried-var phi loop — neither of which names this arm or this form.
				if not divergedBlock.isTerminated() 'divergentArmLeftControlLive'
					panic("parseArmBody: a `panic`/`throws` arm of a match expression left its block UNTERMINATED — `parseDivergingTerminalForm` terminates on both of its routes, and the merge below relies on it: an arm that reached the result phi carrying no `gives` value would leave the phi an operand short")
				end 'divergentArmLeftControlLive'

				return MatchArmBody.create(false, reachesMerge: false, exitBlock: divergedBlock, exitVals: ValueIdArray.create(), give: MatchGiveValue.none, blame: EmptySourceText)
			end 'divergingArm'

			try self.consume(TokenKind.gives)
			let valueTok = self.current()
			let valueStart = self.pos
			let value = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)

			// ⭐ **MERGE (P1.5-A2b): a capturing closure may not be a `gives` arm of a match EXPRESSION** —
			// every arm feeds ONE result phi (one slot, the code pointer), and the env rides a side column a
			// phi cannot carry, so the merged closure would be called with env=0 and nil-deref, whether or not
			// the result escapes. This is the REACHABLE form of the ternary-arm refusal (match is parsed;
			// `a if c else b` is not), so it must be caught here or a closure launders through the merge into a
			// segfault. Refused via the same E3099 gate the escape routes use, with the merge message.
			try self.rejectEscapingClosure(value, message: "cannot use a closure that captures as a `gives` arm of a match expression: {CapturingClosureMergeSuffix}", tok: valueTok)

			let exitBlock = self.currentBlock
			try self.rejectStrayAndOnExpressionArm()
			return MatchArmBody.create(false, reachesMerge: not exitBlock.isTerminated(), exitBlock: exitBlock, exitVals: self.snapshotBindings(carried), give: MatchGiveValue.value(value), blame: self.expressionImmutableBlame(valueTok, valueStart: valueStart, value: value))
		end 'givesArm'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.then)

		// Each arm body is a single statement — a block-opening one (`if`/`while`/`for`/`match`)
		// has no place for its nested scope on a single-line arm.
		let firstTok = self.current()
		if self.isBlockOpeningStatement(firstTok.kind) 'blockOpener'
			throw ParseError.matchBlockStatement(firstTok.kind.rawValue, line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'blockOpener'

		// Whether the body is a `return` is read BEFORE it is parsed — `and fallthrough` after a
		// `return` cannot combine (the `return` has already left the arm).
		let isReturn = self.at(TokenKind.return)
		// ⭐ A match arm body is a single statement, so a `try` used here may take only a single-statement
		// form — the block forms are E2049 (P1.4b). `parseTry` reads this flag; saved/restored so a nested
		// construct unwinds it. On a throw the parse aborts, so restoring on the error path is moot.
		let savedInMatchArm = self.inMatchArmBody
		self.inMatchArmBody = true
		let exitBlock = try self.parseStatement(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		self.inMatchArmBody = savedInMatchArm

		var fellThrough = false
		if self.at(TokenKind.and) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.fallthrough 'fallthroughSuffix'
			let andTok = self.current()
			if isReturn 'ftReturn'
				throw ParseError.matchFallthroughWithReturn(andTok.line, column: andTok.column)
			end 'ftReturn'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.and)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.fallthrough)
			fellThrough = true
		end 'fallthroughSuffix'

		let reachesMerge = not exitBlock.isTerminated() and not fellThrough
		return MatchArmBody.create(fellThrough, reachesMerge: reachesMerge, exitBlock: exitBlock, exitVals: self.snapshotBindings(carried), give: MatchGiveValue.none, blame: EmptySourceText)
	end 'parseArmBody'

	// ⭐ **`and fallthrough` IS MEANINGLESS ON EITHER SHAPE OF MATCH-EXPRESSION ARM, so the refusal is one
	// thing.** A `gives` arm yields a value and a DIVERGING arm leaves the function; neither has a successor
	// to fall into, and an `and` after either is a leftover rather than an operator — the Pratt climber has
	// already stopped at it (see `parseBinary`), so it sits at the cursor and would otherwise be read by the
	// arm loop as the next arm's PATTERN, blaming a token two lines further on. Shared because the two arm
	// shapes are positioned to see the identical token and must not come to disagree about whether it is
	// legal: the divergent shape had no refusal at all when it was added, which is exactly that drift.
	function rejectStrayAndOnExpressionArm() throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.and) 'noStrayAnd'
			return
		end 'noStrayAnd'

		let andTok = self.current()
		throw ParseError.strayToken(tokenDisplayText(andTok), line: andTok.line, column: andTok.column)
	end 'rejectStrayAndOnExpressionArm'

	// The block-opening statement keywords — an arm body may not start with one (E2049). `try`'s
	// block form is also one, but `try` is a later rung and cannot appear here yet.
	function isBlockOpeningStatement(kind TokenKind) returns bool
		return kind == TokenKind.if or kind == TokenKind.while or kind == TokenKind.for or kind == TokenKind.match
	end 'isBlockOpeningStatement'

	// Set up the phis of a FALLTHROUGH-TARGET body block — one reached both by its match edge(s)
	// (carrying the pre-match `entryVals`) and by the previous arm's fallthrough edge (carrying
	// that arm's exit values). A carried var gets a merge phi exactly where the two disagree; the
	// fallthrough edge is recorded and emitted here, and the differing `entryVals` are RETURNED for
	// the caller to record on the match edge (a regular arm's `condBranch` then-edge, or the
	// default's fail edge).
	function setupFallthroughTargetPhis(bodyBlock IrBlock, carried VarInfoArray, entryVals ValueIdArray, prevExitBlock IrBlock, prevExitVals ValueIdArray) returns ValueIdArray
		var matchEdgeArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		var fallthroughEdgeArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto carried.count() 'each'
			var binding = try carried.get(k) otherwise panic("setupFallthroughTargetPhis: carried.get OOB at {k}")
			let entryV = try entryVals.get(k) otherwise panic("setupFallthroughTargetPhis: entryVals.get OOB at {k}")
			let prevV = try prevExitVals.get(k) otherwise panic("setupFallthroughTargetPhis: prevExitVals.get OOB at {k}")
			if entryV == prevV 'same'
				binding.boundValue = entryV
			end 'same' else 'differ'
				let phiId = self.mintPhiForBinding(bodyBlock, binding: binding)
				binding.boundValue = phiId
				self.pushMergedEdgeValue(matchEdgeArgs, binding: binding, value: entryV)
				self.pushMergedEdgeValue(fallthroughEdgeArgs, binding: binding, value: prevV)
			end 'differ'
		end 'each'
		self.recordBranchEdge(prevExitBlock, targetId: bodyBlock.id, argIds: fallthroughEdgeArgs)
		self.emitBranch(prevExitBlock, targetId: bodyBlock.id)
		return matchEdgeArgs
	end 'setupFallthroughTargetPhis'

	// Create the merge block LAST (so it lays out after every arm body) and join the reaching arms
	// into it. An empty set means every arm terminated — the merge is `dead`. Otherwise mint the
	// block-args in order — the result phi (expression form) first, then one phi per carried var
	// that differs across the reaching arms — and record each arm's edge in the SAME order.
	function finalizeMatchMerge(func IrFunction, matchTok Token, carried VarInfoArray, reachingArms MatchArmExitArray, construct MergeConstruct) returns MatchOutcome throws ParseError
		// Every value-producing construct (a match EXPRESSION and a ternary) runs the give-agreement,
		// promotion and ownership rules below; a match STATEMENT gives no value and skips them. So the one
		// value/statement bit is DERIVED from the construct rather than passed beside it — the two cannot
		// then disagree — and `construct` itself only ever matters where the DIAGNOSTIC NOUN differs (#80).
		let isExpression = construct != MergeConstruct.matchStatement

		var merge = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matchcont", line: matchTok.line)

		if reachingArms.count() == 0 'allTerminate'
			merge.terminator = Terminator.dead
			// ⛔⛔ **A MATCH EXPRESSION EVERY ARM OF WHICH DIVERGES — REFUSED E2015, AND IT USED TO BE A COMPILER
			// PANIC.** Two doors reach it: a `default panic(…)`/`default throws` as the only arm (reachable since
			// P1.4b — measured before `match-expr-arm-divergent` landed), and every PATTERN arm diverging
			// (`match-expr-arm-divergent`, which added the second). Nothing feeds the result phi, so there is no
			// value to hand the enclosing expression, and shv2 has no `never`-typed expression result to stand in
			// for one — `parseExpression` returns a `ValueId` the whole Pratt climber then reads and types.
			//
			// ⭐ **E2015 IS THE HONEST CODE, and the registry's own words are the test**: "the source uses a
			// language construct this compiler does not implement YET". That is exactly this. The bootstrap
			// COMPILES these programs and lets the unreachable continuation stand (measured), so the refusal is a
			// BOUNDED GAP, not a language rule — which is why it is not `strayToken`/E2001 and not a semantic
			// E3xxx, both of which would file a pending feature as a permanent illegality (the mistake
			// `expectedDeclaration` and `selfOutsideInstanceMethod` each record having made in the other
			// direction). Pinned by `specs-shv2/match-expr-all-arms-diverge.md`, whose prose carries the
			// divergence so the case documents the gap rather than freezing it as intended behaviour.
			//
			// ⚠ **A TERNARY CANNOT REACH THIS**, so the message names the match form without asking `construct`
			// for a noun: `a if c else b` has two arms that are both EXPRESSIONS, and there is no divergent
			// spelling for either — every ternary arm produces a value or fails to parse.
			if isExpression 'exprNoValue'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("a match EXPRESSION whose every arm diverges — each arm ends in `panic(…)` or `throws`, so no arm reaches the merge and there is no value to give the binding or expression around it. Write it as a match STATEMENT (`then panic(…)` arms need no value), or give at least one arm a `gives`", line: matchTok.line, column: matchTok.column)
			end 'exprNoValue'
			return MatchOutcome.create(merge, result: MatchGiveValue.none)
		end 'allTerminate'

		// The first reaching arm is the reference every "do the arms differ / what is the common
		// value" question is asked against, so it is fetched once here (the count check above proves
		// it exists).
		let firstArm = try reachingArms.get(0) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get(0) failed after count check")

		// ⭐ **CROSS-REGISTER-CLASS ARMS ARE UNIFIED TO FLOAT (P1.5 #31), not refused.** Every arm's `gives`
		// value feeds ONE result phi, and a value's register file (GP vs XMM) is fixed by its type at birth —
		// so an integer give and a float give hand the phi two values from different files, which the
		// allocator cannot color (`X64Backend.emitRegRegMove` panics "crosses register files"). The reference
		// oracle promotes the INTEGER arms to float (`cvtsi2sd`) so the result is uniformly float, and now
		// that floats are a nameable, working type in shv2 (the stale blocker this once cited is gone) shv2
		// does the same with the instruction it already has (`promoteToFloat`). The promote lands in the
		// ARM'S OWN exit block — its value is computed there, and the branch to the merge is not emitted until
		// below — so the pure `cvtsi2sd` is on the conditional path, never hoisted before the branch. A float
		// arm meeting a NON-numeric arm (a bool/String/struct/union/type-parameter give) is not promotable and
		// is a genuine type mismatch, reported HERE before any phi is built: `checkGiveTypes` below cannot be
		// its backstop, because a `typeParameter`/`unresolved` give AGREES with the float and would slip
		// through to the mixed-class phi. Statement matches give no value and skip this, as they skip every
		// give rule below.
		if isExpression 'unifyRegisterClass'
			var anyFloat = false
			for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'scanFloat'
				let a = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
				if tagIsFloat(self.tagOf(self.giveValueOf(a.give))) 'floatArm'
					anyFloat = true
				end 'floatArm'
			end 'scanFloat'

			if anyFloat 'promoteIntegerArms'
				for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'eachArm'
					var arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
					let give = self.giveValueOf(arm.give)
					if not tagIsFloat(self.tagOf(give)) 'notYetFloat'
						if not self.giveIsPromotableToFloat(give) 'nonNumericArm'
							throw self.valueMergeGiveMismatch(construct, reachingArms: reachingArms, firstArm: firstArm, gotArm: arm, matchTok: matchTok)
						end 'nonNumericArm'
						let saved = self.currentBlock
						self.currentBlock = arm.exitBlock
						let promoted = self.promoteToFloat(give, opTok: matchTok)
						self.currentBlock = saved
						arm.give = MatchGiveValue.value(promoted)
					end 'notYetFloat'
				end 'eachArm'
			end 'promoteIntegerArms'
		end 'unifyRegisterClass'

		// ⭐ **OPEN #54 SLICE C — EVERY REACHING `gives` ARM MUST YIELD THE FIRST ARM'S TYPE.** The result
		// phi minted below takes the FIRST arm's type (`mintPhi` off `firstGive`), and a value's type is
		// fixed at birth — so an arm giving a value of another type hands the phi a value its own type is a
		// lie about, with no diagnostic. The float/non-float split threw `unsupported` above, so a surviving
		// mismatch is same-register-class and takes one of TWO shapes, each a memory-SAFETY defect:
		//   • a SCALAR-CLASS disagreement — a `String` give and an `int` give, a `bool` give and an `int`
		//     give — whose phi derefs the wrong-domain value as the first arm's type (the int read as a
		//     String pointer: a segfault). Caught by `typesAgree`, the SYMMETRIC form of the rule the
		//     coercion-site twins (`return`, reassignment, `otherwise`, the call argument) ask one-sided as
		//     `checkDeclaredType`. It is `typesAgree`, NOT a raw tag compare, so a ranged-int alias give and
		//     a plain `int` give (both numeric) — or an `ExitCode` give and an `int` give — STAY valid: the
		//     numeric subtypes share a register file and a phi holds either.
		//   • an AGGREGATE-IDENTITY disagreement — two DIFFERENT structs/unions (both aggregates, naming
		//     distinct types), OR a boxed struct/union give and a SCALAR give (`match c { 0 gives
		//     Holder.holds(box); default gives 7 }`). Both are caught by `namedAggregatesConflict` (the ONE
		//     name-agreement rule the coercion sites share, extended by #59 to fire on aggregate-vs-scalar
		//     too). `ValueTypeTag.named` is OVERLOADED — a boxed union VALUE carries it, and so does a
		//     ranged-int alias — so `typesAgree(named, integer)` wrongly AGREES and the phi would deref the
		//     int `7` as a box pointer; but `aggregateNameOf` NAMES a declared struct/union and returns EMPTY
		//     for every scalar/String/ranged-int alias, so the two gives disagree exactly when their
		//     aggregate names differ (one empty, or both non-empty and unequal).
		// The two compose: both-scalar → `typesAgree`; anything with an aggregate → `namedAggregatesConflict`.
		// Read off the scalar tag/name columns, so a scalar match materializes no `MaxonType` and allocates
		// nothing. Placed BEFORE `resolveGivesOwnership` and the phi so an ill-typed match is refused before
		// any phi is built. Expression-only: a statement match gives no value, exactly as the float check above.
		if isExpression 'checkGiveTypes'
			let firstGive = self.giveValueOf(firstArm.give)
			let firstTag = self.tagOf(firstGive)
			let firstNameId = self.valueNameId(firstGive)
			let firstAggregate = self.aggregateNameOf(firstTag, nameId: firstNameId)
			for i in 1 upto reachingArms.count() 'scanGiveType'
				let arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
				let giveVal = self.giveValueOf(arm.give)
				let giveTag = self.tagOf(giveVal)
				let giveNameId = self.valueNameId(giveVal)
				let giveAggregate = self.aggregateNameOf(giveTag, nameId: giveNameId)

				let classDisagrees = not typesAgree(giveTag, right: firstTag)
				let nameDisagrees = self.signatures.aggregatesConflict(firstAggregate, expectedTag: firstTag, expectedNameId: firstNameId, got: giveAggregate, gotTag: giveTag, gotNameId: giveNameId)
				if classDisagrees or nameDisagrees 'giveTypeMismatch'
					throw self.valueMergeGiveMismatch(construct, reachingArms: reachingArms, firstArm: firstArm, gotArm: arm, matchTok: matchTok)
				end 'giveTypeMismatch'
			end 'scanGiveType'
		end 'checkGiveTypes'

		// ⭐ **FUNCTION-VALUE ARMS MUST AGREE IN SIGNATURE — RECORDED HERE, CHECKED WHOLE-PROGRAM (P1.5 #81).**
		// `checkGiveTypes` proves every arm shares the FIRST arm's class, so if the first give is a `function`
		// every arm is — but a `function` tag carries no arity or parameter types, so `unary if c else binary`
		// slips it: both arms are the `function` class, no aggregate, and the merge silently adopts the FIRST
		// arm's signature for the phi. A caller then checks its indirect call against `unary`'s one parameter
		// while the value at runtime is `binary`, which reads a garbage second argument — a stack corruption
		// the class check cannot see. The signatures CANNOT be compared here: a forward-referenced or cross-file
		// arm's parameter types are not in any registry the parser has at parse time (`self.artifact` holds only
		// THIS file's functions parsed so far; the whole-program `project.funcSignatures` is not assembled until
		// merge). So the arms' governing NAMES + the anchor + the noun are RECORDED, and the agreement is drained
		// whole-program in `checkValueMergeSignatures` — before `resolveTypes` erases the alias names the
		// diagnostic prints. This is v1's model (`PendingTernaryArmCheck` drained in TypeResolution) and the
		// reference oracle's whole-program `MergeTernaryArmFnTypes` (2-Parser.cs:16149).
		if isExpression 'recordFnMergeCheck'
			if self.tagOf(self.giveValueOf(firstArm.give)) == ValueTypeTag.function 'firstArmIsFunction'
				var armNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
				for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'collectArmNames'
					let arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
					armNames.push(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(self.giveValueOf(arm.give))))
				end 'collectArmNames'
				self.artifact.pendingFnMergeChecks.push(PendingFnMergeCheck.create(armNames, line: matchTok.line, column: matchTok.column, filePath: self.filePath, isTernary: construct == MergeConstruct.ternary))
			end 'firstArmIsFunction'
		end 'recordFnMergeCheck'

		// ⭐ **OWNED-GIVE OWNERSHIP TRANSFER (P1.3).** If any reaching arm gives an OWNED value the result
		// phi takes over ownership (`resultOwned` → the phi is marked owned below): each arm's give value has
		// already left its own owner (`transferGiveToPhi` in the arm loop), so neither `dropArmScopedOwned`
		// nor the post-match statement drain frees it, and a borrowed give (a String literal) is promoted to
		// an owned copy so the phi is uniformly owned and its consumer drops it unconditionally. When NO arm
		// gives an owned value the phi stays borrowed, exactly as before. `finalGives` carries each reaching
		// arm's post-promotion give value (empty for a statement match), used below for the phi and the edges.
		var finalGives = ValueIdArray.create()
		let resultOwned = try self.resolveGivesOwnership(reachingArms, isExpression: isExpression, matchTok: matchTok, finalGives: finalGives)

		// ⭐ **CONDITIONAL MOVE RECONCILED across the reaching arms** — an owned binding moved in one arm and
		// left live in another is dropped on the live arms' exit blocks (all reaching arms are unterminated)
		// and marked uniformly moved past the merge. Emitted BEFORE the branches so the decref lands in the
		// arm's own body. A returning/breaking arm contributed no reaching entry, so it is not reconciled —
		// it dropped its own live values on the way out.
		var moveEdges = ReachingMoveEdgeArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'collectMoveEdges'
			let arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
			moveEdges.push(ReachingMoveEdge.create(arm.exitBlock, mark: arm.moveMark))
		end 'collectMoveEdges'
		self.reconcileMovesAtMerge(moveEdges, anchor: matchTok)

		// Every reaching arm branches to the merge (its exit block is, by definition, unterminated).
		for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'branchToMerge'
			let arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
			self.emitBranch(arm.exitBlock, targetId: merge.id)
		end 'branchToMerge'

		// A carried var needs a merge phi iff its value is not identical across every reaching arm.
		var differs = BoolArray.create()
		for k in 0 upto carried.count() 'eachVar'
			let v0 = try firstArm.exitVals.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: exitVals.get OOB at {k}")
			var anyDiff = false
			for i in 1 upto reachingArms.count() 'eachArm'
				let arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
				let vi = try arm.exitVals.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: exitVals.get OOB at {k}")
				if vi != v0 'diff'
					anyDiff = true
				end 'diff'
			end 'eachArm'
			differs.push(anyDiff)
		end 'eachVar'

		// Mint the merge block-args. The result phi (if any) leads, so it is block-arg 0 and every
		// edge lists its `gives` value first.
		var result = MatchGiveValue.none
		if isExpression 'resultPhi'
			let firstGive = try finalGives.get(0) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: finalGives is empty for an expression match — resolveGivesOwnership fills one per reaching arm")
			let giveType = self.typeOf(firstGive)
			try self.refuseExistentialValueMerge(maxonTypeTag(giveType), what: "the value a `match` expression / ternary gives", tok: matchTok)

			let phiId = self.mintPhi(merge, tag: maxonTypeTag(giveType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(giveType))
			// The phi owns the transferred give value (see the ownership-transfer block above), so it is an
			// owned temp: `parseBinding`/the statement drain drops it once.
			//
			// ⚠ **"THE NEW SOLE OWNER" IS WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY, AND IT IS TRUE OF THE DROP AND FALSE OF
			// THE ALLOCATION.** The phi is the only thing that will DROP the value, which is what the transfer
			// block establishes; whether anything ELSE still owns the allocation is a question about the ARMS —
			// `promoteBorrowedGive` promotes a borrowed give by `__mm_incref`, so `pick if c else h.ty` reaches
			// here holding a box the struct field also holds. So the claim is asked of the edges
			// (`mergedHeapExclusivity`), which is also the direction `propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi` takes
			// four lines down.
			if resultOwned 'ownedResultPhi'
				self.trackOwnedTemp(phiId, exclusivity: self.mergedHeapExclusivity(finalGives))
			end 'ownedResultPhi'

			// ⭐⭐ **AN IMMUTABLE-GLOBAL READ SURVIVES THE MERGE (P1.7 slice 2b).** The mark that refuses
			// `var b = <a `let` array>` rides the VALUE — deliberately, so it needs no second encoding of the
			// name-resolution order — and a merge MINTS A NEW VALUE, so without this the mark simply stops at
			// the join and the refusal is bypassed by writing the same thing one keyword longer:
			// `var pick = A if c else M`, or a `match … gives A` arm, then `pick.push(9)` grew a `let` array
			// with E2013, E3019 and the direct E2015 all intact (MEASURED at 3 where 2 is correct, through the
			// ternary AND the match-expression door, including the degenerate `A if c else A`).
			//
			// ANY marked arm marks the phi: the phi is one value standing for every arm's, so it is laundered
			// if even one path can reach it holding an immutable global's record. That is the conservative
			// direction, and it is the only sound one here — the alternative refuses nothing on the path that
			// actually aliases.
			self.propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi(phiId, gives: finalGives)

			// ⭐ **AND THE BUFFER-SURFACE FAMILY, at this merge and at `finishValueTry`'s** — the same rule for
			// the same reason, with its own two measurements in its header.
			self.propagateBufferSurfaceToPhi(phiId, gives: finalGives)

			// ⭐ **AN E3070 ELEMENT BORROW SURVIVES THE MERGE TOO, and for the very same reason** — a borrow is
			// keyed on the VALUE the accessor produced, and a merge MINTS A NEW VALUE, so without this a
			// `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise "" if c else "…"` (and the `match … gives` spelling one
			// construct over) bound a phi no borrow was attached to and the following `arr.clear()` was waved
			// through: MEASURED 0xC0000005 on both. This is the second of the THREE merges the parser has; the
			// third is `finishValueTry`'s, hooked at its own `mintPhi`. A short-circuit's merge is excluded by
			// its TYPE — its phi is a bool, and no bool is an element borrow.
			for give in finalGives 'eachGive'
				self.retargetPendingBorrow(func, from: give, to: phiId)
			end 'eachGive'

			// ⭐ **THE RESULT PHI'S ALIAS CLAIM, ASKED OF EVERY ARM RATHER THAN INHERITED FROM ARM 0 (G14).**
			// The phi is named off `giveType` — the FIRST arm's type — and `checkGiveTypes` above deliberately
			// lets a ranged-alias give and a plain `int` give merge (*"the numeric subtypes share a register
			// file and a phi holds either"*), so arm 0's ALIAS is not a fact about arm 1's VALUE. MEASURED:
			// `alpha(n) gives n` / `end(a, b) gives a + b` over `Num = int(0 to 1000)` denoted `Num`, returned
			// **1800** through a `returns Num`, and reported `<join> as Num` as an unneeded cast. `finalGives`
			// is the complete reaching set — a returning/breaking arm contributes no entry and no edge — so
			// this merge is one of the two that may ask at all.
			self.recordMergeAliasProof(phiId, incoming: finalGives)

			// ⭐⭐ **AN E3019 BLAME SURVIVES THE MERGE, on the same reasoning `propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi`
			// above rests on and for the same construct pair.** `grow(g)` on a `let` is refused, so
			// `grow(g if flag else g)` must be too — the merge copies nothing, so the record a callee writes
			// through the phi IS the record the binding names. ANY blamed arm blames the phi, exactly as any
			// marked arm marks it: the phi stands for every arm's value, so one path reaching it holding an
			// immutable binding's record is enough.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS NOT GATED ON THE PHI BEING BORROWED, AND THE GATE WAS WRITTEN AND THEN MEASURED AWAY.**
			// The argument for one was that an OWNED phi copies a borrowed byte-record arm
			// (`promoteBorrowedToOwned`), so a write through it could not reach the binding — which is true of
			// the RECORD and false of the RULE. MEASURED on the oracle, both shapes it would have exempted:
			// `grow(makeStr() if c else <a `let` String>)` and `grow(make() if c else <a `let` Array>)` are
			// **E3019 on the bootstrap**, and the second aliases outright (a `let` array binding is OWNED, so
			// nothing promotes or copies it and the push lands in the binding). A gate here would have kept
			// that second one accepted, which is the hole this rule exists to close.
			self.recordMergeBlame(phiId, blamed: firstArmBlame(reachingArms))

			result = MatchGiveValue.value(phiId)
		end 'resultPhi'

		for k in 0 upto carried.count() 'setBindings'
			var binding = try carried.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: carried.get OOB at {k}")
			let isDifferent = try differs.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: differs.get OOB at {k}")
			if isDifferent 'phiVar'
				let phiId = self.mintPhiForBinding(merge, binding: binding)
				binding.boundValue = phiId

				// ⭐ **A CARRIED BINDING'S MERGE IS THE `if` CONTINUATION'S TWIN, ONE CONSTRUCT OVER (G14
				// review)** — same withheld claim, same complete incoming set, same two costs when it is
				// never lifted (a redundant cascade at the next ranged `return`, and the E3010 the bootstrap
				// still reports for `t as Num`; MEASURED on a `match` whose one arm assigns a `Num` var and
				// whose other `break`s). `reachingArms` is the complete reaching set — a returning arm
				// contributes no entry and no edge — and this column is exactly the one `recordEdges` below
				// pushes onto each arm's edge, so the values judged here are the values the edges carry.
				var incoming = ValueIdArray.create()
				for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'eachArm'
					let arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
					incoming.push(try arm.exitVals.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: exitVals.get OOB at {k}"))
				end 'eachArm'
				self.recordMergeAliasProof(phiId, incoming: incoming)
			end 'phiVar' else 'common'
				binding.boundValue = try firstArm.exitVals.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: exitVals.get OOB at {k}")
			end 'common'
		end 'setBindings'

		// Record each reaching arm's edge args, in the block-arg order minted above.
		for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'recordEdges'
			var arm = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
			var argIds = ValueIdArray.create()
			if isExpression 'giveArg'
				argIds.push(try finalGives.get(i) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: finalGives.get OOB at {i} — one entry per reaching arm"))
			end 'giveArg'
			for k in 0 upto carried.count() 'eachVarArg'
				let isDifferent = try differs.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: differs.get OOB at {k}")
				if isDifferent 'diffArg'
					self.pushMergedEdgeValue(argIds, binding: try carried.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: carried.get OOB at {k}"), value: try arm.exitVals.get(k) otherwise panic("finalizeMatchMerge: exitVals.get OOB at {k}"))
				end 'diffArg'
			end 'eachVarArg'

			// The edge is pushed onto the arm's exit block DIRECTLY here, not through `recordBranchEdge`,
			// and that is a deliberate bootstrap-fixpoint workaround, not a style slip: the whole-program
			// mutating-parameter analysis that decides whether `recordBranchEdge`'s `block` is mutated does
			// not converge once the give-ownership helpers enter this function's call graph, so it
			// alternately demands `let` (E3019) and `var` (E3077) for the loop's arm. Mutating `arm`'s own
			// block here is analysed locally and is stable. `argIds.count() == 0` means no phi rides this
			// edge — the same guard `recordBranchEdge` applies — so nothing is recorded for a zero-arg edge.
			if argIds.count() > 0 'hasEdgeArgs'
				arm.exitBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argIds))
			end 'hasEdgeArgs'
		end 'recordEdges'

		return MatchOutcome.create(merge, result: result)
	end 'finalizeMatchMerge'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS MERGE ARM'S GIVE VALUE AN INTEGER-DOMAIN NUMBER a float arm promotes to float (P1.5 #31)?**
	// True for a plain `int`, an `ExitCode`, or a ranged-INT alias — everything `cvtsi2sd` reads soundly.
	// False for a `float` or ranged-float alias (ALREADY in the float domain — no promotion), for a
	// bool/String/function/type-parameter (NON-numeric — a genuine type mismatch), and for a `named`
	// AGGREGATE, which is numeric-CLASSED (`typeClassOf(named) == numeric`) but is a POINTER to a box that
	// `cvtsi2sd` would corrupt — told apart from a numeric alias by `aggregateNameOf`, exactly as
	// `checkGiveTypes` tells them apart. Reads the tag/name columns only, materialising no `MaxonType`.
	function giveIsPromotableToFloat(give ValueId) returns bool
		let tag = self.tagOf(give)
		let nameId = self.valueNameId(give)

		// A plain `int` or an `ExitCode` is an integer-domain number — always promotable.
		if tagIsPlainInteger(tag) 'plainInteger'
			return true
		end 'plainInteger'

		// A `named` value is a ranged alias OR a declared aggregate. A ranged-FLOAT alias is already
		// float-domain (no promotion); a declared aggregate (union/enum/per-instance alias) is a POINTER,
		// not a number, that `cvtsi2sd` would corrupt (`aggregateNameOf` names it); only a plain ranged-INT
		// alias — numeric, no aggregate name — promotes.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.named 'namedType'
			if self.namedIsFloatAlias(nameId) 'floatAlias'
				return false
			end 'floatAlias'
			return self.aggregateNameOf(tag, nameId: nameId).isEmpty()
		end 'namedType'

		// A `float` (already float-domain), bool, String, function, struct, type parameter, generic
		// instance, unresolved or void give is not an integer-domain number and does not promote.
		return false
	end 'giveIsPromotableToFloat'

	// A give-type mismatch between the FIRST arm and `gotArm`, phrased for `construct` through the shared
	// `valueMergeMismatchError` mapping. A ternary always names its FALSE (second) branch whichever arm
	// actually mismatched — it has exactly two arms and the message reads them in source order — so the
	// displayed "got" arm is arm 1 for a ternary and the mismatching arm itself for a match.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH ARMS PRINT THROUGH `valueDisplayTypeName`, THE ONE VALUE-DISPLAY DOOR** (user ruling,
	// 2026-08-04). It had a private renderer that read `aggregateNameOf` — the IDENTITY `checkGiveTypes`
	// compares on — and for a generic instance that identity is a canonical mint: MEASURED,
	// `ternary expression type mismatch: true branch is 'Array_Integer' but false branch is 'Array_String'`
	// where the author wrote `Ints` and `Strs`.
	function valueMergeGiveMismatch(construct MergeConstruct, reachingArms MatchArmExitArray, firstArm MatchArmExit, gotArm MatchArmExit, matchTok Token) returns ParseError
		var displayArm = gotArm
		if construct == MergeConstruct.ternary 'ternaryFalseArm'
			displayArm = try reachingArms.get(1) otherwise panic("valueMergeGiveMismatch: a ternary merge has no second (false) arm")
		end 'ternaryFalseArm'
		return valueMergeMismatchError(construct == MergeConstruct.ternary, expectedDisplay: self.valueDisplayTypeName(self.giveValueOf(firstArm.give)), gotDisplay: self.valueDisplayTypeName(self.giveValueOf(displayArm.give)), line: matchTok.line, column: matchTok.column)
	end 'valueMergeGiveMismatch'

	// The `gives` value an expression arm carries. Only ever asked of an expression-match arm that REACHES
	// THE MERGE, so a `none` is a parser invariant break, not a program error. Takes the `MatchGiveValue`
	// itself so both `MatchArmExit` and the arm loop's `MatchArmBody` reach it through the one extractor.
	//
	// ⚠ **A VALUELESS ARM IS NOT ITSELF THE BREAK — REACHING THE MERGE WITH ONE IS.** Two arm shapes carry
	// no give: a STATEMENT arm (which never meets this routine, because a statement match runs no give
	// rule), and a DIVERGING expression arm (`panic`/`throws`), which is legal and simply terminates —
	// `parseArmBody` checks its terminator on the spot and keeps it out of `reachingArms`. So the message
	// names the collection this value came out of rather than the arm's spelling: whichever produced it,
	// the defect is that a non-giving arm was admitted to the merge.
	function giveValueOf(give MatchGiveValue) returns ValueId
		match give 'g'
			value(id) then return id
			none then panic("giveValueOf: an arm in a match expression's REACHING set carries no `gives` value — a statement arm never reaches this rule and a diverging `panic`/`throws` arm is kept out of `reachingArms` by its terminator check, so an arm reached the merge that should not have")
		end 'g'
	end 'giveValueOf'

	// The union an arm hands back: the one it ARRIVED with, unless the settlement really produced a different
	// SSA name.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOT TIDINESS — A MEASURED ALLOCATION.** `MatchGiveValue` carries a payload and is therefore a heap
	// box, so rebuilding one per arm unconditionally cost **+1,060 allocations at rung 5 of the scale ladder**
	// against a like-for-like base build — one box per `match`/ternary arm in the program, and the whole of this
	// rung's movement in that column. Reusing brings it back to Δ0. Almost no arm launders, so almost every arm
	// hands back the union it arrived with.
	function armGiveUnion(arrived MatchGiveValue, settled ValueId) returns MatchGiveValue
		return arrived if settled == self.giveValueOf(arrived) else MatchGiveValue.value(settled)
	end 'armGiveUnion'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERYTHING A FORK ARM OWES AS CONTROL LEAVES IT, IN THE ONE ORDER THAT IS SOUND** — shared by a
	// `match` arm and a ternary arm, which used to spell the first two steps separately (in `finishArmValues`,
	// which this replaces) and the third not at all. Three steps, and the order is the whole content:
	//
	//   1. SETTLE THE GIVE — the value escaping to the result phi leaves its owner, or, when it is a BORROW
	//      into something this arm is about to release, is given a reference of its own (`settleArmGive`).
	//   2. RELEASE THE ARM'S ABANDONED TEMPORARIES, in the arm's OWN exit block — the only block their
	//      definitions dominate. An arm runs on one edge, so a release after the merge would free a value the
	//      other arms never built.
	//   3. RELEASE THE ARM'S SCOPE-LIFETIME RECORDS — its managed payload bindings, and (A3h) any temporary
	//      `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` held for a borrow taken out of it — by the same rule and into the same
	//      block.
	//
	// Step 1 MUST precede steps 2 and 3: an escaping value released by either sweep is a use-after-free
	// (`text(s) gives s` would drop the payload it is handing back), and a borrow laundered AFTER its source
	// is released increfs freed memory.
	//
	// Step 2 is inert for a `then` arm: its body is a STATEMENT, and the statement tail already released the
	// whole pending list on its own edge, leaving nothing above the floor. It is the `gives` arm of a match
	// EXPRESSION — which parses an expression and so has no statement tail — that step 2 exists for. Step 1 is
	// inert there too, and structurally so: `parseArmBody` mints `value(…)` only on the `isExpression` path,
	// so a `then` arm's give is `none` and the match below skips it.
	//
	// It takes and returns a `MatchGiveValue` rather than a `ValueId` plus a flag, because a STATEMENT arm
	// genuinely carries no value and the union is what says so — and the caller must take the answer BACK: a
	// laundered give is a FRESH SSA name, and the merge (`resolveGivesOwnership`) has to join the value the
	// arm really hands over rather than the borrow it started from.
	function finishArmExit(give MatchGiveValue, tempFloor TempDropMark, ownedFloor BindingMark, exitBlock IrBlock, anchor Token) returns MatchGiveValue throws ParseError
		var settled = give
		match give 'g'
			// A STATEMENT arm carries no value, so there is nothing to settle — not "settle it to itself".
			none then break 'g'
			value(v) then settled = self.armGiveUnion(give, settled: try self.settleArmGive(v, ownedFloor: ownedFloor, exitBlock: exitBlock, anchor: anchor))
		end 'g'

		let resumeBlock = self.currentBlock
		self.currentBlock = exitBlock
		self.drainPendingTemps(tempFloor, anchor: anchor)
		self.currentBlock = resumeBlock

		self.dropArmScopedOwned(ownedFloor, exitBlock: exitBlock, anchor: anchor)
		return settled
	end 'finishArmExit'

	// ⭐⭐ **A BORROW MAY NOT LEAVE AN ARM THAT IS ABOUT TO FREE WHAT IT POINTS INTO (A3h).** Everything in
	// `ownedBindings` above this arm's floor dies at the arm's exit — its managed payload bindings, and the
	// temporaries `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` held for borrows taken inside the arm — so a give that is
	// still a BORROW when the arm holds any of them may point into one of them, and the merge would read
	// freed memory. It is given a reference of its own instead, through the SAME `promoteBorrowedMergeEdge`
	// the merge's own uniform-ownership promotion uses (`promoteBorrowedGive`); the phi then owns on this
	// edge, `resolveGivesOwnership` sees an owned arm and promotes the others to match, and the count
	// balances exactly as it does when an arm gives a fresh box.
	//
	// **MEASURED, both constructs, both reading `0x3F3F3F3F3F3F3F3F`:** `match p 'k' first gives
	// makeA().name second gives makeB().name end 'k'` and its ternary twin `makeA().name if t else
	// makeB().name` — two BORROW arms, so nothing else made the phi owned and nothing laundered them. The
	// oracle answers 5 and 2.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HEIGHT TEST IS THE SET ITSELF, WHICH IS WHY THIS NEEDS NO PROVENANCE COLUMN.** The question is
	// *"can this borrow point into anything this arm is about to release?"*, and the arm's own owned-binding
	// tail IS that set. A borrow out of an OUTER binding is laundered too, and that is balanced rather than
	// wasteful: the incref is released by the phi's single drop.
	//
	// ⛔ **EXCEPT FOR A READ OF AN IMMUTABLE GLOBAL, AND THE CLAUSE IS THE PREDICATE STATED CORRECTLY RATHER
	// THAN AN EXEMPTION**: a give read out of MODULE storage borrows from a record that outlives every scope,
	// so it demonstrably cannot point into anything an arm releases and there is nothing here to launder.
	// ⚠ **THAT — AND ONLY THAT — IS WHY IT SURVIVES W117.** The clause arrived with a second reason attached:
	// omitting it NARROWED THE LANGUAGE, because `match w 'k' held(h) gives A nothing gives B` over a `let`
	// array put the height above the floor, sent the give to `promoteBorrowedMergeEdge`, and was REFUSED by an
	// aggregate arm that then refused every incref of an immutable global's record. That arm no longer refuses
	// (the rule moved to the write), so the second reason is spent and is recorded here as spent — the first
	// one is what the clause now rests on, alone. Skipping the promotion does not lose the mark either: the
	// give reaches the phi unchanged, and `propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi` reads the marks off the gives.
	//
	// An arm holding NOTHING (the overwhelming majority) reaches the two doors below: an outliving
	// IMMUTABLE binding's value is co-owned (`giveAliasesAnOutlivingImmutableBinding`), and everything
	// else settles through `transferGiveToPhi` — a no-op for a borrowed or scalar give.
	function settleArmGive(give ValueId, ownedFloor BindingMark, exitBlock IrBlock, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.giveBorrowsIntoDyingRecords(give, ownedFloor: ownedFloor) 'borrowIntoDyingRecords'
			return try self.promoteBorrowedMergeEdge(give, edgeBlock: exitBlock, anchor: anchor)
		end 'borrowIntoDyingRecords'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN ARM READING AN IMMUTABLE BINDING CO-OWNS IT — IT DOES NOT MOVE IT (⚖ 2026-08-04).**
		// The arm's own edge takes a SECOND reference and the binding keeps the one it already owed, so both
		// stay live and the box is released once by each. `emitIncref` in place rather than `__mm_retain` for
		// the reason its header gives: the phi mints its own id, so no two BINDINGS come to share one — which
		// is the whole of `coOwnImmutableRebindSource`'s fresh-name argument and does not apply here.
		if self.giveAliasesAnOutlivingImmutableBinding(give, ownedFloor: ownedFloor) 'coOwnedImmutableSource'
			// The incref belongs on the ARM's edge, not on `currentBlock`: the arm runs on one path, and a
			// reference taken on the unconditional path would be owed by the phi on an edge that never took it.
			// The same placement `promoteBorrowedMergeEdge` makes, for the same reason.
			let saved = self.currentBlock
			self.currentBlock = exitBlock
			self.emitIncref(give, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
			self.currentBlock = saved
			return give
		end 'coOwnedImmutableSource'

		try self.transferGiveToPhi(give, anchor: anchor)
		return give
	end 'settleArmGive'

	// The four clauses of `settleArmGive`'s question, named so the sentence above is the code: the arm holds
	// records that die at its exit, the give is a MANAGED pointer (a scalar borrows nothing), it does not
	// already own a reference of its own, and it is not a read of module storage that outlives every scope.
	function giveBorrowsIntoDyingRecords(give ValueId, ownedFloor BindingMark) returns bool
		if self.ownedBindings.count() == ownedFloor 'armHoldsNothing'
			return false
		end 'armHoldsNothing'

		return self.valueIsManagedHeap(give) and not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(give) and not self.immutableGlobalReads.contains(give)
	end 'giveBorrowsIntoDyingRecords'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS GIVE NAME A VALUE AN IMMUTABLE BINDING GOES ON OWNING PAST THE MERGE?** The three
	// clauses, and each excludes a give the merge already answers correctly:
	//
	//   • a BORROWED give owns nothing to co-own — `giveBorrowsIntoDyingRecords` above and
	//     `resolveGivesOwnership`'s promotion decide it, and they are the doors that hold the borrow rules;
	//   • an owned TEMPORARY (`gives build(1)`) has no other owner at all, so the phi ADOPTS the one
	//     reference it was handed — increfing it would leak, which is `transferFallbackToPhi`'s measurement;
	//   • a binding this ARM declared (`text(s) gives s`) dies at the arm's exit, so a move is both correct
	//     and free: `dropArmScopedOwned` skips a poisoned binding, where co-ownership would incref and
	//     immediately decref. `ownedFloor` is the arm's own height, so the index test IS that question.
	//
	// What is left is a binding declared OUTSIDE the arm and declared `let`, which is exactly the shape the
	// alias ruling blesses (`rebindSourceIsAnImmutableAlias` — this is the same question keyed by VALUE, since
	// a give arrives as an SSA id and not as a name).
	//
	// ⚠ **A MUTABLE SOURCE STILL MOVES, and that is the ruling's rationale read the other way**: a `var`
	// can be written through after the merge, so a second name for its value could watch it change. That is
	// `specs-shv2/moves.md`'s boundary, and `error.gives-mutable-binding-still-moves` is its pin here.
	function giveAliasesAnOutlivingImmutableBinding(give ValueId, ownedFloor BindingMark) returns bool
		if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(give) 'borrowed'
			return false
		end 'borrowed'

		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(give)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'ownedTemporary'
			return false
		end 'ownedTemporary'

		if index >= ownedFloor 'declaredByThisArm'
			return false
		end 'declaredByThisArm'

		let owner = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("giveAliasesAnOutlivingImmutableBinding: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		return not owner.mutable
	end 'giveAliasesAnOutlivingImmutableBinding'

	// ⭐ **TRANSFER AN OWNED `gives` VALUE OUT OF ITS OWNER (P1.3).** An expression arm whose yielded value
	// is OWNED — an owned interpolation/heap `String`, a struct box, or a managed payload a binding arm
	// moved out (`text(s) gives s`) — hands that value to the merge's result phi, which becomes its sole
	// owner (`finalizeMatchMerge` marks the phi owned so its consumer drops it once). So the value must
	// leave its current owner HERE, before `dropArmScopedOwned` releases the arm's bindings and before the
	// post-match statement drain runs: an owned temporary leaves the pending list, and a bare-local owner
	// (the payload binding of `text(s) gives s`, or an outer MUTABLE binding) is poisoned so `emitScopeDrops`
	// skips its drop and a later read of it is use-after-move. A borrowed/scalar give owns nothing, so both
	// calls are no-ops — a borrowed String is promoted at the merge instead, where phi ownership is decided.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN OUTER binding declared `let` NEVER REACHES HERE (⚖ 2026-08-04).** `settleArmGive` co-owns it
	// instead, so the phi takes a second reference and the source stays live — the same answer
	// `transferFallbackToPhi` gives the `try … otherwise <binding>` edge. This door is left with exactly the
	// two owners a move is right for: an arm-scoped binding, and a mutable one.
	function transferGiveToPhi(give ValueId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(give) 'borrowed'
			return
		end 'borrowed'
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(give)
		try self.poisonMoveSource(give, anchor: anchor)
	end 'transferGiveToPhi'

	// Decide whether an expression match's result phi OWNS its value, and make every arm's give owned so it
	// does. The phi is owned iff any reaching arm gives an owned value (`finalizeMatchMerge` then marks it an
	// owned temp); when it is, each arm that gives a BORROWED value is made owned by `promoteBorrowedGive`
	// (a borrowed String is copied; a borrowed non-text aggregate has no cheap copy and is E2015-refused)
	// so the phi is uniformly owned across all edges. A statement match, or an expression match whose arms
	// all give scalars / immortal rdata, keeps a borrowed phi (returns false).
	//
	// The per-arm give value the merge should use — after any promotion — is collected into `finalGives`
	// (one entry per reaching arm, in order) rather than written back onto the `MatchArmExit`, so nothing
	// here mutates the arm records the caller still reads. `finalGives` is empty for a statement match.
	// Carry the immutable-global-read mark from a merge's arms onto its result phi (P1.7 slice 2b) — see the
	// call site in `finalizeMatchMerge` for why the phi must inherit it at all.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT READS THE FINAL GIVES (post-`resolveGivesOwnership`) — the values the phi genuinely joins — AND
	// SINCE S5 THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN MERELY TIDY.** A marked value is a borrowed non-text AGGREGATE
	// (only an ARRAY binding is both stored and immutable — a `let` String has no slot to read). The previous
	// argument was that such an arm could never reach an owned phi at all, because `promoteBorrowedGive`
	// REFUSED a borrowed aggregate; it now PROMOTES one, so that argument is gone and this one replaces it:
	// a marked arm that would be INCREF'd into an owned phi is refused inside the promotion, at the `merged`
	// door (`promoteBorrowedToOwned`), before a final give exists.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LOAD-BEARING PROPERTY IS "NO OWNED PHI ALIASES A MARKED RECORD", NOT "NO OWNED PHI HAS A MARKED
	// ARM"**, and the two came apart in review. A promotion that COPIES (a String, a trivial tuple) yields a
	// FRESH record, so it is correctly unmarked and is allowed through rather than refused — it launders
	// nothing, because writing the copy cannot reach the global. Only the INCREF arm hands back the SAME box,
	// which is why only it is refused. So an owned phi may hold an unmarked COPY of a marked arm's record,
	// which is right, and can never hold the record itself, which is the property this rests on.
	function propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi(phi ValueId, gives ValueIdArray)
		for give in gives 'eachGive'
			if self.immutableGlobalReads.contains(give) 'markedArm'
				self.markImmutableGlobalRead(phi)
				return
			end 'markedArm'
		end 'eachGive'
	end 'propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BUFFER-SURFACE FAMILY SURVIVES A MERGE TOO — AND UNTIL W192's SECOND REVIEW IT DID NOT.**
	// The sibling above's twin, at the same two merges and for the identical structural reason: these facts
	// ride the VALUE, a merge MINTS A NEW VALUE, and a fact that stops at the join is bypassed by writing the
	// same program one keyword longer.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS TWO WRONG ANSWERS AT ONCE, BOTH MEASURED.** With the mark stopping here:
	//   • a `__ManagedMemory` value through a merge came out on the **`Array` ROSTER, EXACTLY INVERTED** —
	//     `var m = a.managed if flag else a.managed` then `m.length()` was refused as an unknown `Array`
	//     member while `m.count()` compiled, on a growable array, with no `Vector` in sight. That is A2j's
	//     defect at the one position A2j did not reach, and it is what the first two arms below repair.
	//   • a SIZED container's record laundered its identity through the join: inside an `extension Vector`,
	//     `var m = managed if flag else managed` and `var m = try managed.slice(0, 99) otherwise managed`
	//     each cleared the vector's own record and printed `len=0 count=3` — the rung's own reproducer,
	//     reached by a ternary and by a `try` fallback with every other door already shut.
	//
	// ⚠ **ANY MARKED EDGE MARKS THE PHI, which is the conservative direction and the only sound one** —
	// `propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi`'s argument word for word: the phi is one value standing for every
	// edge's, so it must answer for the strongest claim any path can reach it holding. The arms do not chain,
	// for `carryDeclaredSurface`'s reason: one arm per carrier, so no column's carry is conditional on
	// another's. (`markSizedContainerBuffer` takes the buffer mark as well — that is its door invariant, not
	// this loop's business.)
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TUPLE-SLOT COLUMN IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT.** It holds a `DeclaredSurface` TREE rather than a
	// bit, so "any edge" is not a rule for it — joining two edges' trees needs the COMBINE rule the
	// `bufferSurfaceValues` field header records as unwritten, and inventing one here on the parser's merge
	// path is not this review's to do. What that leaves open is a TUPLE OF BUFFERS through a merge, which is
	// the roster inversion above one container out; it is reported rather than papered over.
	function propagateBufferSurfaceToPhi(phi ValueId, gives ValueIdArray)
		for give in gives 'eachGive'
			if self.valueIsASizedContainersBuffer(give) 'aSizedContainersOwnRecord'
				self.markSizedContainerBuffer(phi)
			end 'aSizedContainersOwnRecord'

			if self.valueIsBufferSurface(give) 'aBuffer'
				self.markBufferSurface(phi)
			end 'aBuffer'

			if self.valueElementIsBufferSurface(give) 'holdsBuffers'
				self.markBufferSurfaceElements(phi)
			end 'holdsBuffers'
		end 'eachGive'
	end 'propagateBufferSurfaceToPhi'

	// The first reaching arm that names an immutable binding, or empty — the fold `finalizeMatchMerge` puts
	// on the result phi. ANY blamed arm is enough, so the first is the answer and the walk stops there.
	function firstArmBlame(reachingArms MatchArmExitArray) returns ByteArray
		for arm in reachingArms 'eachArm'
			if not arm.blame.isEmpty() 'blamedArm'
				return arm.blame
			end 'blamedArm'
		end 'eachArm'
		return EmptySourceText
	end 'firstArmBlame'

	function resolveGivesOwnership(reachingArms MatchArmExitArray, isExpression bool, matchTok Token, finalGives ValueIdArray) returns bool throws ParseError
		if not isExpression 'statementMatch'
			return false
		end 'statementMatch'

		var resultOwned = false
		for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'anyOwned'
			let a = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("resolveGivesOwnership: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
			let give = self.giveValueOf(a.give)
			if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(give) 'owned'
				resultOwned = true
			end 'owned'
		end 'anyOwned'

		for i in 0 upto reachingArms.count() 'collectGives'
			let a = try reachingArms.get(i) otherwise panic("resolveGivesOwnership: reachingArms.get OOB at {i}")
			let give = self.giveValueOf(a.give)
			if resultOwned and not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(give) 'promoteBorrowed'
				finalGives.push(try self.promoteBorrowedGive(give, arm: a, matchTok: matchTok))
			end 'promoteBorrowed' else 'keepGive'
				finalGives.push(give)
			end 'keepGive'
		end 'collectGives'

		return resultOwned
	end 'resolveGivesOwnership'

	// Make a BORROWED `gives` value owned so a match/conditional whose OTHER arms give owned values has a
	// uniformly-owned result phi its consumer can drop unconditionally. It reaches the shared
	// `promoteBorrowedToOwned` through `promoteBorrowedMergeEdge`, as the `return`'s twin: a borrowed STRING is copied (the fused-record
	// blit), a borrowed TRIVIAL TUPLE gets its own record, a borrowed non-text AGGREGATE is INCREF'd, and either
	// way the arm hands the phi a value it really owns. Which block it lands in and what else the edge owes the
	// phi are `promoteBorrowedMergeEdge`'s, shared with the `try … otherwise` fallback that asks the same thing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE AGGREGATE ARM USED TO BE A REFUSAL WHOSE STATED REASON WAS FALSE (S5):** *"shv2 has neither
	// `__mm_incref` nor an aggregate deep-copy"*. It had both — `__mm_retain` since OPEN #40 (wired to the
	// `var q = p` door one screen up) and the `__clone_<T>` cascade since P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b. Left unpromoted
	// a borrowed give really would hand the owned phi a box it does not own (OPEN #14, a leak/UAF reachable
	// through a `match … gives` arm AND a ternary arm alike); the incref is what makes it owned, so the phi's
	// single drop balances against a real second reference instead of the borrow's owner's one.
	//
	// An UNRESOLVED give reaches here too, and is DEFERRED, not refused: a give whose type could not be
	// pinned at parse is a call NO file declares (`mintCallResult` defers rather than throwing), and
	// `checkGiveTypes` let it through because `typesAgree` lets `unresolved` agree with anything and its
	// `aggregateNameOf` is empty. The program is doomed — SemanticCheck rejects the undeclared call (E3004)
	// before any codegen, so the phi's ownership never matters — so the give is left unpromoted (the pre-#14
	// behavior) to let the real diagnostic surface instead of a parser panic. A RESOLVED scalar give cannot
	// reach here: `checkGiveTypes` rejects a resolved scalar arm mixed with an owned aggregate/String give,
	// so it is a parser invariant break.
	function promoteBorrowedGive(give ValueId, arm MatchArmExit, matchTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let giveTag = self.tagOf(give)
		if self.valueIsManagedHeap(give) 'managed'
			return try self.promoteBorrowedMergeEdge(give, edgeBlock: arm.exitBlock, anchor: matchTok)
		end 'managed'

		if tagIsUnresolved(giveTag) 'deferredCallError'
			return give
		end 'deferredCallError'

		panic("promoteBorrowedGive: a RESOLVED borrowed scalar give reached an OWNED result phi — checkGiveTypes rejects a resolved scalar mixed with an owned aggregate/String give, and an unresolved give is deferred above, so this is unreachable")
	end 'promoteBorrowedGive'

	// Emit one arm's pattern as a single boolean condition, OR-folding the alternatives (`1 or 2 or
	// 3`). Records single-value patterns for the duplicate check and rejects a pattern whose type
	// cannot be compared to the scrutinee (E2028). Emits into `currentBlock`.
	function emitPatternCondition(scrut ValueId, scrutTag ValueTypeTag, seenValues SeenPatternValues) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		var cond = try self.emitPatternAtom(scrut, scrutTag: scrutTag, seenValues: seenValues)
		while self.at(TokenKind.or) 'orChain'
			let orTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.or)
			let rhs = try self.emitPatternAtom(scrut, scrutTag: scrutTag, seenValues: seenValues)
			cond = try self.emitBinOp(cond, rhs: rhs, op: MaxonBinOp.bitOr, opTok: orTok)
		end 'orChain'
		return cond
	end 'emitPatternCondition'

	// One alternative of a pattern: an open-lower range (`min to/upto hi`), a closed/open-upper
	// range (`lo to/upto hi`, `lo to max`), or a single value.
	function emitPatternAtom(scrut ValueId, scrutTag ValueTypeTag, seenValues SeenPatternValues) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let startTok = self.current()
		let loStart = self.pos

		if self.isOpenBoundIdentifier(startTok, marker: MinBoundField) 'openLower'
			self.advance()
			return try self.emitOpenLowerRange(scrut, scrutTag: scrutTag)
		end 'openLower'

		let lo = try self.emitPatternValue(scrutTag)

		if self.at(TokenKind.to) or self.at(TokenKind.upto) 'rangeArm'
			return try self.emitClosedRange(scrut, scrutTag: scrutTag, loValue: lo, loStart: loStart)
		end 'rangeArm'

		// A single value: `scrut == lo`.
		try self.checkAndRecordDuplicate(lo, seenValues: seenValues, start: loStart, pastEnd: self.pos)
		return try self.emitCompare(scrut, rhs: lo, op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, opTok: startTok)
	end 'emitPatternAtom'

	// `min to/upto <hi>` — the lower bound is open, so the condition is just the upper compare. A
	// `min to max` covers every value: a constant `true`.
	function emitOpenLowerRange(scrut ValueId, scrutTag ValueTypeTag) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if not (self.at(TokenKind.to) or self.at(TokenKind.upto)) 'notRange'
			let notRangeTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unexpectedToken(TokenKind.to.rawValue, got: tokenDisplayText(notRangeTok), line: notRangeTok.line, column: notRangeTok.column)
		end 'notRange'
		let isUpto = self.at(TokenKind.upto)
		let rangeTok = self.current()
		self.advance()

		if self.isOpenBoundIdentifier(self.current(), marker: MaxBoundField) 'openBoth'
			self.advance()
			return self.emitLiteral(BoolTrueValue, valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: SourceRange.ofToken(rangeTok))
		end 'openBoth'

		let hi = try self.emitPatternValue(scrutTag)
		return try self.emitCompare(scrut, rhs: hi, op: self.rangeUpperCmpOp(isUpto), opTok: rangeTok)
	end 'emitOpenLowerRange'

	// `lo to/upto <hi>` where `lo` is already emitted — a two-compare AND, or (`lo to max`) just the
	// lower compare. Entered with the cursor ON the range keyword, so `loStart` plus the cursor bracket
	// the lower bound and the arm's whole source span is available to the single-value diagnostic.
	function emitClosedRange(scrut ValueId, scrutTag ValueTypeTag, loValue ValueId, loStart TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let rangeTok = self.current()
		let isUpto = rangeTok.kind == TokenKind.upto
		self.advance()

		if self.isOpenBoundIdentifier(self.current(), marker: MaxBoundField) 'openUpper'
			self.advance()
			return try self.emitCompare(scrut, rhs: loValue, op: MaxonCmpOp.ge, opTok: rangeTok)
		end 'openUpper'

		let hiStart = self.pos
		let hi = try self.emitPatternValue(scrutTag)
		try self.checkChainRangeIsMultiValued(scrutTag, isUpto: isUpto, loStart: loStart, hiStart: hiStart)

		let loCmp = try self.emitCompare(scrut, rhs: loValue, op: MaxonCmpOp.ge, opTok: rangeTok)
		let hiCmp = try self.emitCompare(scrut, rhs: hi, op: self.rangeUpperCmpOp(isUpto), opTok: rangeTok)
		return try self.emitBinOp(loCmp, rhs: hiCmp, op: MaxonBinOp.bitAnd, opTok: rangeTok)
	end 'emitClosedRange'

	// The upper-bound comparison: `<` for `upto` (exclusive), `<=` for `to` (inclusive).
	function rangeUpperCmpOp(isUpto bool) returns MaxonCmpOp
		if isUpto 'exclusive'
			return MaxonCmpOp.lt
		end 'exclusive'
		return MaxonCmpOp.le
	end 'rangeUpperCmpOp'

	// Parse a pattern LITERAL (a value or a range bound): an optional `-`, then an int/float/bool/
	// character literal. Emits it and checks it is comparable to the scrutinee (E2028). A string
	// literal is E2028 (`"one"` on an `int` scrutinee); `min`/`max` are the caller's, not a value.
	function emitPatternValue(scrutTag ValueTypeTag) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let firstTok = self.current()

		// A STRING LITERAL pattern — legal only against a `string` scrutinee (P1.2 wave A). Its
		// condition is `__str_eq(scrut, "literal")`: `emitPatternAtom`'s single-value path emits the
		// literal here and then `emitCompare(scrut, lo, eq)`, which — both operands `string` — routes to
		// the `__str_eq` runtime call, feeding the SAME bool the scalar `or`/`default`/expression-merge
		// machinery consumes for an integer pattern. So `match name { "alice" then … }` reuses the whole
		// scalar dispatch unchanged; only "what a pattern atom IS" differs. Against a NON-string
		// scrutinee it stays the E2028 mismatch (`"one"` on an `int`).
		if self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'stringPattern'
			if tagIsText(scrutTag) 'stringScrutinee'
				// A pattern is a COMPILE-TIME literal — an interpolation there has no meaning (there is
				// nothing to match a runtime-built string against), so it is rejected rather than built.
				if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.stringInterpStart 'interpolatedPattern'
					throw ParseError.unsupported("an interpolated string as a match pattern — a `match` pattern must be a compile-time string literal", line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
				end 'interpolatedPattern'
				return try self.consumeStringLiteralValue()
			end 'stringScrutinee'
			throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch("String", scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'stringPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.minus) 'negated'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.minus)
			let negValue = try self.emitNegatedPatternValue(firstTok)
			return try self.checkPatternType(negValue, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'negated'

		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'intPattern'
			let intValue = try self.parseIntLiteral()
			return try self.checkPatternType(intValue, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'intPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'floatPattern'
			let floatValue = try self.parseFloatLiteral()
			return try self.checkPatternType(floatValue, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'floatPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.true) or self.at(TokenKind.false) 'boolPattern'
			let boolValue = try self.parseBoolLiteral()
			return try self.checkPatternType(boolValue, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'boolPattern'

		// ⭐ A PATTERN IS AN INTEGER-EXPECTING POSITION when the scrutinee is integral, so `match cp 'm' '-'
		// then …` compares codepoints while `match c 'm' 'a' then …` over a `Character` compares records —
		// through the same `integerizedOperand` every operand and declared slot goes through (A5m-ab).
		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'charPattern'
			let charStart = self.pos
			let charValue = try self.integerizedOperand(try self.parseCharLiteral(), expected: scrutTag, start: charStart, pastEnd: self.pos)
			return try self.checkPatternType(charValue, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'charPattern'

		// A bare identifier (that is not `min`/`max`, handled by the caller) is an enum-case pattern.
		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'enumCasePattern'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a match pattern naming '{String.from(firstTok.value)}' (only literal, range, and 'or' patterns over a scalar are supported; enum-case patterns arrive in a later wave)", line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'enumCasePattern'

		throw ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(firstTok), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
	end 'emitPatternValue'

	// The value after a pattern's `-`: a negated int, or `0.0 - x` for a float. Any other token
	// after `-` has no numeric meaning to negate.
	function emitNegatedPatternValue(minusTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'negInt'
			let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.intLiteral)
			let v = try self.intLiteralValue(tok, negated: true)
			return self.emitLiteral(v, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(minusTok))
		end 'negInt'

		if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'negFloat'
			let base = try self.parseFloatLiteral()
			return try self.emitFloatNegation(base, opTok: minusTok)
		end 'negFloat'

		let operandTok = self.current()
		throw ParseError.nonNumericNegation(operandTok.kind.rawValue, line: minusTok.line, column: minusTok.column)
	end 'emitNegatedPatternValue'

	// A pattern value must be comparable to the scrutinee — the same DOMAIN rule `emitCompare`
	// applies, asked HERE so the failure is the match-specific E2028 rather than the comparison's
	// E3005. `emitCompare` therefore never rejects a pattern it is handed.
	function checkPatternType(value ValueId, scrutTag ValueTypeTag, patternTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let valTag = self.tagOf(value)
		if not comparableOperands(scrutTag, right: valTag) 'mismatch'
			throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch(typeTagName(valTag), scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: patternTok.line, column: patternTok.column)
		end 'mismatch'
		return value
	end 'checkPatternType'

	// Record a single-value pattern for duplicate detection, or raise E2027 if it repeats one. Only
	// INTEGER-domain constants participate (`constIsKnown`). A float literal's bits ARE tracked since A1, but
	// in the float domain, which this deliberately does not read: two patterns that differ can still be the
	// same VALUE (`+0.0` and `-0.0` compare equal), so a bit-set membership test would report the arms
	// distinct while the second is dead. Deciding a duplicate float needs `floatOrderKey`, and no corpus case
	// tests one — so the reach is unchanged and the reason it is unchanged is now a rule rather than a gap.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ENUM-CASE ARMS JOINED THIS DOOR WITH `enum-narrow-storage`, AND IT IS NEW BEHAVIOUR RATHER THAN
	// A NO-OP.** `emitEnumTagLiteral` now records a case's tag as a known integer constant (it needed a
	// constant enum literal to be recognisable as constant, for the `.rdata` blob), so a repeated arm is
	// caught here where it used to compile: `match c` with `red` twice is now
	// **`E2027: overlapping pattern in match: 'red' is already covered`** and used to be a silently dead
	// second arm. MEASURED on exactly that program.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS SOUND AND IT WIDENS NOTHING, and both halves are worth stating.** Two DISTINCT cases can
	// never collide, because `Project.rawValueCollidesAt` refuses two cases sharing a raw value at the
	// DECLARATION (E3031) — so the only pattern pair this can now reject is literally the same case written
	// twice, which has no reading under which the second arm is live. There is no false-reject surface to
	// look for. What was here before was an arm that could not fire.
	function checkAndRecordDuplicate(value ValueId, seenValues SeenPatternValues, start TokenIndex, pastEnd TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		if not self.constIsKnown(value) 'notConst'
			return
		end 'notConst'

		try self.recordDuplicateInt(self.constOf(value), seenValues: seenValues, start: start, pastEnd: pastEnd)
	end 'checkAndRecordDuplicate'

	// The integer core of duplicate detection, shared by the compare-emitting scalar path (`value` read
	// off a known constant) and the interval-plan path (`value` decoded straight from the pattern token).
	// The set membership test throws on the FIRST repeat in arm order — the same value, the same arm, the
	// linear scan reported — in O(1) rather than O(seen).
	function recordDuplicateInt(value ParsedInt, seenValues SeenPatternValues, start TokenIndex, pastEnd TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		if seenValues.contains(value) 'duplicate'
			let patternTok = self.tokenAt(start)
			throw ParseError.matchDuplicatePattern(self.tokenTextFrom(start, endIndex: pastEnd), line: patternTok.line, column: patternTok.column)
		end 'duplicate'
		seenValues.insert(value)
	end 'recordDuplicateInt'

	// ⭐ **IS A RANGE PATTERN EMPTY?** — `upto` whose upper is at or below its lower, or an inclusive `to`
	// whose upper is below it. Written ONCE for both range paths: the scalar one lets an empty arm
	// contribute no interval, the enum one refuses it outright (E2027), and they disagreed about nothing
	// while spelling the same predicate with their operands mirrored.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE OTHER HALF OF "IS THIS RANGE DEGENERATE", AND `requireRangeIsMultiValued` LEANS ON
	// IT.** The two verdicts are disjoint — a range covering NO value cannot also cover exactly one — and
	// that is what lets each caller ask them in either order. Disjointness is a property of these two
	// expressions together, so they belong together: moving a boundary in one while the other keeps its
	// own copy would silently make one of the two rules unreachable, with no compile error anywhere.
	function rangeIsEmpty(lo ParsedInt, hi ParsedInt, isUpto bool) returns bool
		return (hi <= lo) if isUpto else (hi < lo)
	end 'rangeIsEmpty'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SINGLE-VALUE RANGE RULE — ONE PREDICATE, EVERY OPERAND SHAPE.** A range pattern covering
	// exactly one value is always a mistake: the author meant the bare value or got the bounds wrong. So
	// `5 to 5`, `red to red`, `'a' to 'a'` and their exclusive twins `5 upto 6`, `red upto green`,
	// `'a' upto 'b'` are refused (`specs-shv2/match-range-single-value.md`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OPERAND SHAPES DIFFER IN THEIR DOMAIN, NOT IN THE RULE.** An int compares values, a char
	// compares CODEPOINTS, an enum compares DECLARATION INDICES (what a range pattern means — never the
	// tags, which an explicit `= N` may reorder), a float compares `floatOrderKey`s. Each caller projects
	// its bounds into its own ordered domain and asks HERE. Both references grew a separate emit site per
	// shape — three in v1, four in the bootstrap — and copies of a refusal are chances for them to
	// disagree about what is legal.
	//
	// ⚠ **`hasAdjacentValues` IS WHY FLOAT IS NOT ANOTHER COPY.** `upto` excludes its upper bound, so it
	// covers exactly one value only where the domain has an immediate SUCCESSOR. Between two f64s there is
	// always another f64, so no float `upto` is ever single-valued and only the equal-bound `to` form is —
	// the same exclusion v1's `parseFloatRangeTail` states.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN OPEN BOUND NEVER ARRIVES HERE.** `min to X` and `X to max` span more than one value by
	// construction and the spec exempts them outright, so each caller returns before asking.
	//
	// ⚠ **CALLED WITH THE CURSOR JUST PAST THE UPPER BOUND**, which is what makes the message ONE span —
	// `[loStart, self.pos)` covers both bounds AND the keyword between them, so the range is quoted back
	// exactly as the file lays it out and nothing here re-spells `to`/`upto`. Every caller asks the moment
	// it has decoded its upper bound. The span is rendered only on the failing path: the callers hand over
	// a token INDEX, not a string, so a legal range arm allocates nothing for a diagnostic it will not raise.
	function requireRangeIsMultiValued(lo ParsedInt, hi ParsedInt, isUpto bool, hasAdjacentValues bool, loStart TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		let coversOneValue = (hasAdjacentValues and hi == lo + 1) if isUpto else hi == lo
		if not coversOneValue 'multiValued'
			return
		end 'multiValued'

		let loTok = self.tokenAt(loStart)
		throw ParseError.matchSingleValueRange(self.tokenTextFrom(loStart, endIndex: self.pos), line: loTok.line, column: loTok.column)
	end 'requireRangeIsMultiValued'

	// The single-value rule for the COMPARE-CHAIN match — the scrutinee kinds `matchUsesIntervalPlan`
	// leaves out. The interval paths project their bounds where they DECODE them (an integer already, an
	// enum case index already); these two must project theirs from their BOUND TOKENS, because neither a
	// `Character` nor a `float` bound is an integer-domain constant a pattern value can be read as.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REMAINING CHAIN SCRUTINEES HAVE NO ORDERED DOMAIN, AND THE LIST IS EXHAUSTIVE.** A `String`
	// (`"a" to "b"` has no notion of how many values it covers) and an `unresolved` scrutinee (a call to a
	// function no file declares, which `comparableOperands` DEFERS rather than rejecting) fall through
	// deliberately. Every other tag `matchUsesIntervalPlan` excludes — a struct, an interface, a generic
	// instance — cannot reach here at all: `checkPatternType` asks `comparableOperands`, so a literal bound
	// against any of them is E2028 at the LOWER bound, one call before this. A ranged-FLOAT typealias is
	// not a fourth case either: MEASURED, its scrutinee carries the `float` tag (both as `x as Ratio` and
	// as a declared `r Ratio` parameter), so it lands in the float arm below and is refused there.
	function checkChainRangeIsMultiValued(scrutTag ValueTypeTag, isUpto bool, loStart TokenIndex, hiStart TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		// A `Character` scrutinee compares grapheme-cluster RECORDS, but its bounds are char literals
		// whose CODEPOINTS are the ordered domain the rule needs (`'a' upto 'b'` is adjacent). A
		// multi-codepoint cluster has no codepoint and therefore no successor, so no single-value question
		// can be asked of it — the rule does not apply rather than guessing an answer.
		if scrutTag == ValueTypeTag.character 'characterDomain'
			let hiDecoded = try self.charLiteralCodepoint(self.tokenAt(hiStart))
			match try self.charLiteralCodepoint(self.tokenAt(loStart)) 'lowerBound'
				cluster then return
				codepoint(low) then try self.requireCharRangeIsMultiValued(low, hiDecoded: hiDecoded, isUpto: isUpto, loStart: loStart)
			end 'lowerBound'
			return
		end 'characterDomain'

		// ⚠ **`floatOrderKey`, NOT THE RAW BITS.** `-0.0` and `+0.0` are two bit patterns for one VALUE, so
		// a bit compare would call `-0.0 to 0.0` a two-value range while the compare the arm emits matches
		// exactly one. Both references compare float VALUES for the same reason.
		if tagIsFloat(scrutTag) 'floatDomain'
			try self.requireRangeIsMultiValued(floatOrderKey(try self.floatBoundBits(loStart)), hi: floatOrderKey(try self.floatBoundBits(hiStart)), isUpto: isUpto, hasAdjacentValues: false, loStart: loStart)
		end 'floatDomain'
	end 'checkChainRangeIsMultiValued'

	// ⭐ **THE f64 BITS OF A FLOAT PATTERN BOUND, READ FROM ITS TOKENS** — `[-]? floatLiteral`, decoded by
	// the one float decoder every other bound uses and sign-flipped by the one `negatedFloatBits`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `floatConstIsKnown` ON THE EMITTED VALUE, AND THAT DISTINCTION WAS A LIVE HOLE.** A
	// negated float pattern is emitted as `0.0 - x` through `emitBinOp`, and `recordBinOpFold` folds only
	// INTEGER results — so no negated float bound is ever a known float constant, and a check written
	// against the value silently passed `-1.0 to -1.0` while refusing `1.0 to 1.0`. The tokens say what
	// the author wrote whether or not the emitter folded it.
	//
	// ⚠ **ANY OTHER SHAPE IS AN INVARIANT BREAK, NOT A CASE TO SKIP.** `comparableOperands` ends
	// `tagIsFloat(left) == tagIsFloat(right)` (`IR/Maxon/TypeRules.maxon`), so an int, char, bool or string
	// bound against a float scrutinee is E2028 at `checkPatternType` before this is asked, and `min`/`max`
	// are consumed by the open-bound paths that never reach a closed range's check.
	function floatBoundBits(start TokenIndex) returns ParsedInt throws ParseError
		let isNegated = self.kindAt(start) == TokenKind.minus
		let literalIndex = (start + 1) if isNegated else start
		let literalTok = self.tokenAt(literalIndex)
		if literalTok.kind != TokenKind.floatLiteral 'notAFloatLiteral'
			panic("Parser.floatBoundBits: a float scrutinee's range bound at token {literalIndex} is '{tokenDisplayText(literalTok)}', not a float literal — every other spelling is E2028 at `checkPatternType` before this is asked")
		end 'notAFloatLiteral'

		let bits = try self.floatLiteralBits(literalTok)
		return negatedFloatBits(bits) if isNegated else bits
	end 'floatBoundBits'

	// The upper half of the character domain's projection. Split out only because a `match` arm may not
	// open another block (E2049): with the lower bound's codepoint in hand, the upper bound decides by
	// exactly the same rule, and a cluster upper bound withdraws the question for the same reason.
	function requireCharRangeIsMultiValued(low ParsedInt, hiDecoded CharLiteralCodepoint, isUpto bool, loStart TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		match hiDecoded 'upperBound'
			cluster then return
			codepoint(high) then try self.requireRangeIsMultiValued(low, hi: high, isUpto: isUpto, hasAdjacentValues: true, loStart: loStart)
		end 'upperBound'
	end 'requireCharRangeIsMultiValued'

	// Does `tok` name an open range bound (`min` / `max`) — an identifier whose text is the marker?
	// `min`/`max` lex as identifiers (they are not keywords), and appear only in a range, so their
	// text alone decides it. The bound-field constants spell the same two words the ranged-typealias
	// `.min` / `.max` do, deliberately: it is one lexical fact, not two.
	function isOpenBoundIdentifier(tok Token, marker ByteArray) returns bool
		return tok.kind == TokenKind.identifier and tok.value.equals(marker)
	end 'isOpenBoundIdentifier'

	// ============================================================================================
	// The match DISPATCH PLAN — normalized `[lo, hi]` intervals + strategy (chain vs binary search).
	//
	// A `match` over an integer-like scrutinee reduces every arm to `DispatchInterval`s here rather than
	// emitting a per-arm compare chain inline: the arm loop collects the intervals, `normalizeDispatchIntervals`
	// sorts and clips them, and `emitMatchDispatch` chooses the strategy and emits ALL the compares in one
	// place. A `String`/`float` match keeps `emitPatternCondition` (the linear chain), which is why that
	// path is untouched. NO new IR op — every compare is the same `compare`/`condBranch` the chain used.
	// ============================================================================================

	// Does this `match` build a binary-search DISPATCH PLAN, or keep the linear compare chain? An
	// enum/union tag is always an i64, so every enum match plans. A scalar match plans only when the
	// scrutinee is orderable as one signed i64 — `int`, `char` (a byte value), `bool`, `ExitCode`, or a
	// ranged-INT alias (`named`, told from a ranged-FLOAT alias by its declared backing, exactly as
	// `valueIsUnsigned` reads the alias registry). A `String` (no interval — its only comparison is a
	// runtime byte `==`) and a `float` (whose IEEE bits are not monotonic in value) keep the chain.
	function matchUsesIntervalPlan(isEnumMatch bool, scrut ValueId) returns bool
		if isEnumMatch 'enum'
			return true
		end 'enum'

		let scrutTag = self.tagOf(scrut)
		if tagIsInteger(scrutTag) or scrutTag == ValueTypeTag.boolean or scrutTag == ValueTypeTag.exitCode 'plainInteger'
			return true
		end 'plainInteger'

		if scrutTag == ValueTypeTag.named 'aliasScrutinee'
			let name = self.internedName(self.valueNameId(scrut))
			return match self.signatures.aliasOf(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'a'
				declared(alias) gives not aliasIsFloat(alias)
				undeclared gives false
			end 'a'
		end 'aliasScrutinee'

		return false
	end 'matchUsesIntervalPlan'

	// One arm's SCALAR pattern (an integer-like scrutinee), reduced to DISPATCH INTERVALS. Mirrors
	// `emitPatternCondition`'s grammar — `or`-separated alternatives, each a value, a range, or an
	// open-ended range — but yields intervals over the pattern CONSTANTS rather than an emitted `or`-fold
	// of compares. The float/String path keeps `emitPatternCondition`, so this handles only what
	// `matchUsesIntervalPlan` gates in.
	function collectScalarPatternIntervals(scrutTag ValueTypeTag, seenValues SeenPatternValues, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray) throws ParseError
		try self.collectScalarPatternAtom(scrutTag, seenValues: seenValues, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into)
		while self.at(TokenKind.or) 'orChain'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.or)
			try self.collectScalarPatternAtom(scrutTag, seenValues: seenValues, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into)
		end 'orChain'
	end 'collectScalarPatternIntervals'

	// One alternative: an open-lower range (`min to/upto hi`), a closed/open-upper range (`lo to/upto hi`,
	// `lo to max`), or a single value. Appends exactly one interval.
	function collectScalarPatternAtom(scrutTag ValueTypeTag, seenValues SeenPatternValues, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray) throws ParseError
		let startTok = self.current()
		let loStart = self.pos

		if self.isOpenBoundIdentifier(startTok, marker: MinBoundField) 'openLower'
			self.advance()
			try self.collectScalarRangeInterval(I64MinBound, loStart: loStart, scrutTag: scrutTag, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into)
			return
		end 'openLower'

		let lo = try self.decodeScalarPatternBound(scrutTag)

		if self.at(TokenKind.to) or self.at(TokenKind.upto) 'rangeArm'
			try self.collectScalarRangeInterval(lo, loStart: loStart, scrutTag: scrutTag, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into)
			return
		end 'rangeArm'

		try self.recordDuplicateInt(lo, seenValues: seenValues, start: loStart, pastEnd: self.pos)
		into.push(DispatchInterval.create(lo, hi: lo, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
	end 'collectScalarPatternAtom'

	// Consume a range upper (`to`/`upto <literal|max>`) with the cursor at the keyword, and push the arm's
	// interval `[lo, hi]` — UNLESS the range is empty, in which case the arm matches nothing and NO interval
	// is pushed (its twin `collectEnumRangeIntervals` throws `matchEmptyRange`; a scalar range has always
	// been silently empty, matching the always-false compare the old chain emitted). `upto` is exclusive, so
	// its inclusive upper is `hi - 1` — computed ONLY once the range is known non-empty, because `upto
	// i64.min` is ALWAYS empty (nothing is below `i64.min`) and `i64.min - 1` would wrap to `i64.max`,
	// turning the empty range into a near-universal one. That underflow was a real miscompile: `0 upto
	// i64.min` matched every value ≥ 0 instead of nothing.
	function collectScalarRangeInterval(lo ParsedInt, loStart TokenIndex, scrutTag ValueTypeTag, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray) throws ParseError
		let isUpto = self.at(TokenKind.upto)
		self.advance()

		// `max` reads as `i64.max` inclusive under EITHER keyword (as `emitClosedRange`'s open-upper arm drops
		// the keyword), so `lo to/upto max` covers `[lo, i64.max]` and is never empty.
		if self.isOpenBoundIdentifier(self.current(), marker: MaxBoundField) 'openUpper'
			self.advance()
			into.push(DispatchInterval.create(lo, hi: I64MaxBound, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
			return
		end 'openUpper'

		let rawHi = try self.decodeScalarPatternBound(scrutTag)

		// An empty range (as before this rung) contributes no interval — the arm body is left unreachable.
		if self.rangeIsEmpty(lo, hi: rawHi, isUpto: isUpto) 'emptyRange'
			return
		end 'emptyRange'

		// An OPEN lower bound (`min to X`) spans more than one value by construction, and the spec exempts
		// the open forms outright — asked through the same predicate the caller used to recognise it, so
		// "is this bound open?" is one question with one answer. (`X to max` already returned above.)
		// Reached only past the empty test, so `lo < rawHi` under `upto` and the successor cannot wrap.
		if not self.isOpenBoundIdentifier(self.tokenAt(loStart), marker: MinBoundField) 'closedLowerBound'
			try self.requireRangeIsMultiValued(lo, hi: rawHi, isUpto: isUpto, hasAdjacentValues: true, loStart: loStart)
		end 'closedLowerBound'

		let hi = (rawHi - 1) if isUpto else rawHi
		into.push(DispatchInterval.create(lo, hi: hi, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
	end 'collectScalarRangeInterval'

	// Decode a single scalar pattern LITERAL — an int (optionally negated), a character, or a bool — to
	// its integer value, validating it is comparable to the scrutinee (E2028), WITHOUT emitting a
	// `literal` op: the dispatch materialises the constant later, once, as an immediate compare. The
	// float/String literal cases stay E2028 exactly as `emitPatternValue`'s do (only an integer-like
	// scrutinee reaches here); a `min`/`max` bound is the caller's, and any other identifier is the
	// enum-case-pattern error `emitPatternValue` raises.
	function decodeScalarPatternBound(scrutTag ValueTypeTag) returns ParsedInt throws ParseError
		let firstTok = self.current()

		if self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'stringPattern'
			throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch("String", scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'stringPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'floatPattern'
			throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch(typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.float), scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'floatPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.minus) 'negated'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.minus)
			if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'negFloat'
				throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch(typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.float), scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
			end 'negFloat'
			if not self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'nonNumeric'
				let operandTok = self.current()
				throw ParseError.nonNumericNegation(operandTok.kind.rawValue, line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
			end 'nonNumeric'
			let intTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.intLiteral)
			let value = try self.intLiteralValue(intTok, negated: true)
			return try self.checkScalarPatternBound(value, valTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'negated'

		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'intPattern'
			let intTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.intLiteral)
			let value = try self.intLiteralValue(intTok, negated: false)
			return try self.checkScalarPatternBound(value, valTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'intPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.true) or self.at(TokenKind.false) 'boolPattern'
			let value = 1 as ParsedInt if self.at(TokenKind.true) else 0 as ParsedInt
			self.advance()
			return try self.checkScalarPatternBound(value, valTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'boolPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'charPattern'
			let charTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
			let value = try self.decodeCharPatternValue(charTok, scrutTag: scrutTag)
			return try self.checkScalarPatternBound(value, valTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, scrutTag: scrutTag, patternTok: firstTok)
		end 'charPattern'

		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'enumCasePattern'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a match pattern naming '{String.from(firstTok.value)}' (only literal, range, and 'or' patterns over a scalar are supported; enum-case patterns arrive in a later wave)", line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'enumCasePattern'

		throw ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(firstTok), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
	end 'decodeScalarPatternBound'

	// A pattern bound must be comparable to the scrutinee — the interval twin of `checkPatternType`,
	// asked over a decoded value + its tag rather than an emitted ValueId, so the failure is the same
	// match-specific E2028.
	function checkScalarPatternBound(value ParsedInt, valTag ValueTypeTag, scrutTag ValueTypeTag, patternTok Token) returns ParsedInt throws ParseError
		if not comparableOperands(scrutTag, right: valTag) 'mismatch'
			throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch(typeTagName(valTag), scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: patternTok.line, column: patternTok.column)
		end 'mismatch'
		return value
	end 'checkScalarPatternBound'

	// ============================================================================================
	// ENUM `match` — case-name patterns + the OPEN #21 range rule (P1.1b wave B).
	//
	// Everything here feeds the SAME dispatch engine the scalar match uses (`parseMatchCommon`'s arm
	// loop, `condBranch` chain and merge): the only difference is that a pattern is a case NAME
	// resolved to a tag rather than a literal, and that coverage is a bitmap over the case set rather
	// than a default arm. NO new IR op — a case compare is the same `compare`/`condBranch` a scalar
	// arm emits.
	// ============================================================================================

	// The enum/union a match scrutinee belongs to, or `undeclared` for a scalar scrutinee. Read from
	// the scrutinee's TYPE — a `named(X)` value whose name is in the enum registry — so it works
	// uniformly for a parameter (`c Code`), a local (`let c = Color.red`) and a returned enum. The
	// enum's own name is on the value even after TypeResolution collapses the SIGNATURE type to
	// `integer`, because a value's tag is set by the parser and never rewritten.
	//
	// ⚠ The value→NAME projection is `valueNameId` + `internedName`, the two named helpers, and NOT a
	// second hand-inlined copy of the same two column reads — which is what stood here. `valueIsUnion`
	// below and `valueIsFloatBackedEnum` (A4o) ask the SAME projection of the same value; a fallback
	// added to one spelling and not the other would answer "not an enum" at one door and "enum" at the
	// next, which is a wrong answer at a use site rather than an error anywhere.
	function enumMatchFor(scrut ValueId) returns DeclaredEnum
		if self.tagOf(scrut) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return DeclaredEnum.undeclared
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.signatures.enumOf(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(scrut)))
	end 'enumMatchFor'

	// Is `value` a `union` value — a `named(X)` whose type is a declared `union`? The E3066 gate
	// (`emitCompare`), keyed on the SAME `enumMatchFor` a `match` reads its scrutinee's enum through,
	// so "is this a union?" is answered in one place for both. A payload-free ENUM is `false` (its
	// value is a plain tag that `==` may compare); only `isUnion` is a union.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TAG PRE-CHECK IS A HOT-PATH ALLOCATION GUARD, NOT A TIDY EARLY RETURN.** This runs for
	// BOTH operands of EVERY comparison in the program, and `enumMatchFor` returns a `DeclaredEnum` —
	// a Maxon union VALUE, which is a heap object for its `undeclared` case as much as any other. So
	// calling it for an `int`/`bool` operand (the overwhelming majority) would allocate two throwaway
	// `DeclaredEnum.undeclared` boxes per compare, scaling with the program: scale-test charged
	// exactly that (+1,257 allocs on rung 0, doubling per rung). A value can only be a union if its
	// tag is `named`, and that test is a scalar column read that allocates nothing.
	function valueIsUnion(value ValueId) returns bool
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'
		let decl = self.enumMatchFor(value)
		if not enumMatchIsDeclared(decl) 'notEnum'
			return false
		end 'notEnum'
		return requireEnumLayout(decl).isUnion
	end 'valueIsUnion'

	// Is `value` a payload-bearing (HEAP-BOXED) enum/union — a `named(X)` whose layout `isBoxed`?
	// `valueIsUnion`'s twin, differing only in the layout bit it reads: `isBoxed` (a heap box exists),
	// not `isUnion` (a union vs a bare enum). It is what tells a call RESULT that owns a heap box (a
	// boxed-union `make()`, adopted by the caller) from a payload-free enum value (a bare i64 tag,
	// dropped by no one) — the OPEN #44 distinction the tag alone cannot make, since both are `named`.
	// The same `named` tag pre-check is the hot-path allocation guard `valueIsUnion` documents.
	function valueIsBoxedUnion(value ValueId) returns bool
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'
		let decl = self.enumMatchFor(value)
		if not enumMatchIsDeclared(decl) 'notEnum'
			return false
		end 'notEnum'
		return requireEnumLayout(decl).isBoxed()
	end 'valueIsBoxedUnion'

	// One arm's enum pattern, reduced to DISPATCH INTERVALS over the case tags (P1.1b + match-dispatch).
	// Alternatives (`red or blue`) each contribute their interval(s), and EVERY alternative may carry a
	// payload binding — the arm's bindings are the concatenation, whichever alternative wrote them
	// (`stay or walk(dir) gives dir`, `specs/match-enum-or-pattern.md`). Replaces the old compare-emitting
	// `emitEnumPatternCondition`: an enum/union tag is an i64, so an enum arm ALWAYS feeds the interval
	// plan, and the per-case `== tag` compare is emitted later — once — by the dispatch itself.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BINDINGS ARE ONE LIST FOR THE WHOLE ARM BECAUSE THEY LOAD AT ONE PLACE** — the head of the
	// single body block every alternative branches to (`parseMatchCommon` → `declarePayloadBindings`) —
	// so they are read on EVERY covered case's path, not just the one whose alternative declared them.
	// That is what makes the spec's shape well defined and what makes four other shapes garbage;
	// `requireArmBindingsAreDistinct` and `requireOrArmPayloadSlotsAgree` are where the difference is decided.
	//
	// `intervalMark` is the arm's own slice of the interval plan — `into` accumulates across ALL arms, so it
	// is what says which intervals, i.e. which cases, belong to THIS arm. It is PASSED IN rather than
	// recomputed here: the caller already holds the identical value for its own edge-arg stamp, and two
	// derivations agreeing only because nothing pushes between them is not an invariant. If they diverged,
	// E3129 would check cases the arm does not cover (or miss ones it does) while phi args landed on the
	// wrong edges.
	function collectEnumPatternIntervals(layout EnumLayout, covered BoolArray, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray, intervalMark ElementCount) returns PayloadBindingArray throws ParseError
		var bindings = PayloadBindingArray.create()
		try self.collectEnumPatternAtomIntervals(layout, covered: covered, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into, bindings: bindings)
		while self.at(TokenKind.or) 'orChain'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.or)
			try self.collectEnumPatternAtomIntervals(layout, covered: covered, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into, bindings: bindings)
		end 'orChain'

		// Both halves of E3129, in the order their answers depend on: whether the arm's bindings are a
		// bijection is a fact about the arm ALONE, and settling it first means the agreement walk below never
		// has to reason about which of two bindings of one slot it is talking about.
		try self.requireArmBindingsAreDistinct(bindings)
		try self.requireOrArmPayloadSlotsAgree(layout, bindings: bindings, into: into, intervalMark: intervalMark)
		return bindings
	end 'collectEnumPatternIntervals'

	// ⛔⛔ **AN ARM'S PAYLOAD BINDINGS MUST BE A BIJECTION — ONE NAME PER SLOT, ONE SLOT PER NAME.** The two
	// halves are one rule because they are one mechanism read from its two ends: `declarePayloadBindings`
	// walks this list emitting a LOAD, a scope DECLARATION and an ACQUISITION per entry. The list is indexed
	// by SLOT and the scope is keyed by NAME (`Scope.install` overwrites), so a repeat on EITHER axis makes
	// the arm body see something no alternative asked for — and each axis was MEASURED wrong:
	//
	//   • **TWO BINDINGS, ONE SLOT** (`shout(msg) or speak(msg)`) — two loads and two acquisitions against
	//     one slot. On a sole-owned scrutinee the acquisition is `moveOutManagedPayload`: the first entry
	//     takes the String and NULLS slot 0, the second reads the slot it just emptied and takes ownership
	//     of null, and the second declaration is the one in scope. **0xC0000005, no output**, against a
	//     CONTROL (same union, same sole-owned scrutinee, same payload, one case per arm) that exits 0.
	//   • **ONE NAME, TWO SLOTS** (`walk(dir) or run(_, dir)`) — passes every SLOT test (the slots differ,
	//     and the discard makes `run(_, dir)` spellable), so only the name axis can catch it. The second
	//     `install` silently wins: MEASURED `walk=0` where 7 was stored, and its managed twin
	//     (`shout(_, msg) or speak(msg, _)`) yielded the wrong FIELD with refcounts balanced — a wrong
	//     answer with no crash and no leak, the worst shape here.
	//
	// ⚠ **NEITHER IS ONLY A CRASH, WHICH IS WHY BOTH ARE REFUSED FOR EVERY PAYLOAD TYPE.** The scalar
	// spelling `walk(a) or run(b) gives a + b` does not fault — it quietly answers `10` for `walk(5)`,
	// because both names read the one slot — and a rule that admitted what merely misleads while refusing
	// what faults would be two rules for one mistake.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS CHECKED OVER THE ARM'S FINISHED LIST, WHICH IS WHY ONE CHECK SERVES TWO DOORS.** The name
	// axis is not an or-arm property at all: a SINGLE case's own list had the identical hole
	// (`walk(x, x)` — `parseCasePayloadBindings` never compared its names either), and an arm's list is the
	// only place both are visible. So the error-union arm asks through here too, and neither door needs its
	// own copy. Discards are skipped on both axes: `_` emits no load and installs no name, so it can
	// contest nothing.
	function requireArmBindingsAreDistinct(bindings PayloadBindingArray) throws ParseError
		for i in 0 upto bindings.count() 'eachBinding'
			let binding = try bindings.get(i) otherwise panic("requireArmBindingsAreDistinct: bindings.get OOB at {i} on a range walked to the array's own count")
			if binding.isDiscard 'readsNothing'
				continue
			end 'readsNothing'

			// Positioned at the SECOND binding throughout — the token the author must rename or delete.
			for j in 0 upto i 'eachEarlier'
				let earlier = try bindings.get(j) otherwise panic("requireArmBindingsAreDistinct: bindings.get OOB at {j}, below the outer index")
				if earlier.isDiscard 'installsNothing'
					continue
				end 'installsNothing'

				if earlier.slot == binding.slot 'oneSlotBoundTwice'
					throw ParseError.matchArmPayloadBindingConflict(String.from(binding.nameTok.value), reason: "payload slot {binding.slot} is already bound by '{String.from(earlier.nameTok.value)}' in this arm. An arm's alternatives share one slot region and one arm body, so binding a slot twice loads the same value twice — and for a managed payload out of a scrutinee this frame owns, moves it out of the box twice and leaves the arm holding null. Bind the slot on ONE alternative and write this one's case name bare: a bare name on a payload-carrying case is legal, and the single binding already serves every alternative", line: binding.nameTok.line, column: binding.nameTok.column)
				end 'oneSlotBoundTwice'

				if earlier.nameTok.value.equals(binding.nameTok.value) 'oneNameBoundTwice'
					throw ParseError.matchArmPayloadBindingConflict(String.from(binding.nameTok.value), reason: "this arm already binds the name '{String.from(earlier.nameTok.value)}', at payload slot {earlier.slot} rather than slot {binding.slot}. The arm body has ONE binding of that name — the later one silently wins — so on the path of the case that fills slot {earlier.slot} the body would read slot {binding.slot} instead. Give the two payloads different names", line: binding.nameTok.line, column: binding.nameTok.column)
				end 'oneNameBoundTwice'
			end 'eachEarlier'
		end 'eachBinding'
	end 'requireArmBindingsAreDistinct'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY CASE AN ARM COVERS MUST HOLD WHAT THE ARM'S BINDINGS READ (E3129).** The bindings are
	// loaded once, unconditionally, at the head of the arm BODY, and every alternative of an `or`-chain
	// branches to that one body — so the load runs on the path of every covered case, and each of them has
	// to present the bound slot as the binding's own type.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SPEC'S SHAPE IS ADMITTED BY A CONSTRUCTION GUARANTEE, NOT BY LUCK — AND THE LOAD-BEARING HALF
	// IS THE ZERO-FILL, NOT THE ALLOCATOR.** `stay or walk(dir) gives dir` reads 0 on the `stay` path because
	// `emitEnumBox` sizes every box for the WIDEST case (`boxSizeBytes` over `maxPayloadArity`) and then
	// EXPLICITLY STORES 0 into the slots a narrow case does not fill (its `zeroFill` loop, whose own header
	// states the fill is MANDATORY); the union cloner preserves it by blitting the whole box. The slab's
	// zeroing says the same thing today, but it is the WEAKER guarantee — it carries an explicit "a future
	// free-list must re-zero" caveat (ARCHITECTURE.md → "Allocator: the zeroing contract"), which the store
	// does not — so this rule cites the store. That is why `specs/match-enum-or-pattern.md` can pin `stay=0`,
	// and `emitEnumBox` now carries the back-reference saying a diagnostic depends on it.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHETHER THAT 0 IS A VALUE AT ALL IS A SEPARATE QUESTION**, asked of the binding's own type by
	// `zeroInhabitsPayloadType` — see it for the two measured faults that come of asking a drop-ownership
	// predicate instead.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE SHAPES REFUSED HERE WERE MEASURED AS WRONG ANSWERS, on programs BOTH reference compilers
	// compile and run** — the refusal this replaced (*"a binding shared across the alternatives of an
	// or-arm arrives at a later rung"*) was hiding them rather than deferring a feature:
	//   • a bound slot another covered case declares with a DIFFERENT type — `speak(msg String) or
	//     walk(dir) gives dir` — printed `speak=7078117`, the String record's POINTER read as an integer.
	//     No crash and no diagnostic, which is the worst of the three.
	//   • a binding whose type does not admit 0, on a slot a covered case leaves empty — `stay or speak(msg)
	//     gives msg.count()` dereferenced the zero-filled slot (**0xC0000005**), and so did a FUNCTION-typed
	//     payload called through address 0. The zero that makes an `int` binding well defined is a null
	//     pointer for those, so one construction fact decides the two oppositely — see
	//     `zeroInhabitsPayloadType`, which is the half that says which.
	// Neither is fixable by emitting better code: the arm body is compiled ONCE and its binding has ONE
	// type, so no per-alternative edge (block args included) can make one name mean a String on one
	// incoming edge and an integer on another — and no value exists to give a `String` binding on a case
	// that carries no String. So the honest answer is a refusal, as it is for the related hazard one
	// construct over (`rejectFallthroughIntoBindingArm`).
	//
	// Vacuous for a single-case arm — `walk(dir)` covers only `walk`, which declares the slot it binds — so
	// the rule is stated once for every enum arm rather than behind an "is this an or-chain" test.
	function requireOrArmPayloadSlotsAgree(layout EnumLayout, bindings PayloadBindingArray, into DispatchIntervalArray, intervalMark ElementCount) throws ParseError
		if bindings.count() == 0 'bindsNothing'
			return
		end 'bindsNothing'

		for ci in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			if not self.armCoversCase(layout, index: ci, into: into, intervalMark: intervalMark) 'anotherArmsCase'
				continue
			end 'anotherArmsCase'
			let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(ci)

			for binding in bindings 'eachBinding'
				// A `_` reads nothing, so no case has to hold anything for it.
				if binding.isDiscard 'discarded'
					continue
				end 'discarded'
				try self.requireCaseHoldsBoundSlot(layout, caseIndex: ci, payloads: payloads, binding: binding)
			end 'eachBinding'
		end 'eachCase'
	end 'requireOrArmPayloadSlotsAgree'

	// Does case `index` route to THIS arm — i.e. does its tag fall in one of the intervals the arm just
	// contributed? Read off the interval plan rather than collected a second time as the atoms are parsed,
	// because the plan IS the arm's coverage (a range alternative expands into it exactly as a bare case
	// does), and a parallel case list would be that fact recorded twice. Tags are unique per case, so tag
	// membership and case membership are the same question.
	function armCoversCase(layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex, into DispatchIntervalArray, intervalMark ElementCount) returns bool
		let tag = layout.tagOfIndex(index)
		for k in intervalMark upto into.count() 'eachInterval'
			let interval = try into.get(k) otherwise panic("armCoversCase: intervals.get OOB at {k} on a range walked to the array's own count")
			if tag >= interval.lo and tag <= interval.hi 'covered'
				return true
			end 'covered'
		end 'eachInterval'
		return false
	end 'armCoversCase'

	// One (covered case, binding) pair — the whole of E3129's rule, and both of its arms turn on the same
	// question: what does THIS case put in the slot the binding reads?
	function requireCaseHoldsBoundSlot(layout EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, payloads PayloadFieldArray, binding PayloadBinding) throws ParseError
		let bound = self.signatures.payloadTypeOf(binding.field, layout: layout, into: self.artifact.typeNames)

		if binding.slot >= payloads.count() 'caseLeavesTheSlotEmpty'
			// Nothing is ever stored there on this case's path — `emitEnumBox` ZERO-FILLS a narrow case's
			// unused slots — so the binding reads 0. Legal exactly where 0 is a value of the binding's own
			// type, which is a question about the SLOT and not about who owes a drop.
			if self.zeroInhabitsPayloadType(bound) 'zeroIsAValueOfThisType'
				return
			end 'zeroIsAValueOfThisType'

			throw ParseError.matchArmPayloadBindingConflict(String.from(binding.nameTok.value), reason: "case '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` carries no payload in slot {binding.slot}, so on that case's path the binding reads the zero the box was filled with — and zero is not a value of the type '{String.from(binding.field.name)}' is declared at. Give '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}' an arm of its own", line: binding.nameTok.line, column: binding.nameTok.column)
		end 'caseLeavesTheSlotEmpty'

		// Both types come from `payloadTypeOf` into THIS file's interner, which is the precondition
		// `maxonTypesDenoteSame` states for its `named` arm — two ids from different interners would be
		// incomparable.
		let here = try payloads.get(binding.slot) otherwise panic("requireCaseHoldsBoundSlot: payloads.get OOB at slot {binding.slot} after the count guard")
		if maxonTypesDenoteSame(self.signatures.payloadTypeOf(here, layout: layout, into: self.artifact.typeNames), b: bound) 'sameTypeInTheSlot'
			return
		end 'sameTypeInTheSlot'

		throw ParseError.matchArmPayloadBindingConflict(String.from(binding.nameTok.value), reason: "case '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` declares its own payload '{String.from(here.name)}' in slot {binding.slot}, of a different type from the '{String.from(binding.field.name)}' being bound — so on that case's path the binding would read one type's bytes as another's. Give '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}' an arm of its own", line: binding.nameTok.line, column: binding.nameTok.column)
	end 'requireCaseHoldsBoundSlot'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS 0 PROVABLY A VALUE OF THIS PAYLOAD TYPE? — a question about the SLOT, and deliberately NOT
	// `payloadClassIsManaged`.** That predicate is documented as the single reader of *"does this class own a
	// heap drop?"*, a DECLARATION-view question, and asking it this one instead FAILS OPEN on every type that
	// owes no drop yet has no zero: a `function`-typed payload classifies `scalar` (a code pointer is an
	// inline i64), so `idle or run(op) gives op(3)` called through address 0 — **MEASURED 0xC0000005**, on
	// `specs-shv2/first-class-functions.md`'s own `Action` union; a `typeParam` answers false for the reason
	// its own header gives (*"the DECLARATION view's answer rather than the slot's"*) while a managed
	// instantiation makes 0 a null reference; and a ranged alias excluding zero (`int(1 to 10)`) bound 0,
	// **MEASURED `off=0`**, outside its own declared domain. It is the same one-predicate-two-facts shape
	// `moveOutManagedPayload` records six segfaults for.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT FAILS CLOSED: it admits only what it can PROVE, and every unlisted type is refused.** A new
	// `ValueTypeTag` therefore lands as a false reject — a diagnostic — rather than as the null dereference
	// the open default was. That is also why it is written as an explicit admit-list and not as an exhaustive
	// `match`: the default here must be a REFUSAL, and a `match` arm added by a future tag would have to
	// choose an answer where "not yet reasoned about" is the honest one.
	//
	// A RANGED ALIAS answers from its own bounds through `literalInRange` — the one door that knows a range's
	// upper bound may be an unsigned pattern (`int(0 to u64.max)` stores `high` as -1), so a naive
	// `high >= 0` would refuse it. A FLOAT alias is skipped first: its bounds are IEEE-754 patterns and this
	// asks an integer question, the identical domain guard `requireOtherwiseLiteralInRange` makes (and
	// `requirePayloadLowerable` refuses a float payload binding upstream regardless).
	//
	// A `named` type that is NOT a ranged alias is refused, which is the arm that covers an ENUM payload:
	// an enum's tags are its values and need not include 0 at all (`red = 1`), so zero would bind a
	// non-existent case. A struct, a union, a tuple and a generic instance are refused by the same arm, each
	// because 0 there is a null pointer.
	function zeroInhabitsPayloadType(slotType MaxonType) returns bool
		if maxonTypeTag(slotType) == ValueTypeTag.named 'aNamedType'
			return match self.declaredAliasOfNameId(typeNameIdOf(slotType)) 'a'
				declared(alias) gives not aliasIsFloat(alias) and literalInRange(0 as ParsedInt, low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: false)
				undeclared gives false
			end 'a'
		end 'aNamedType'

		// The three primitive domains that contain zero: `int` spans it, `bool` reads it as `false`, and
		// `ExitCode`'s range starts at 0 (`Project.exitCodeAliasHigh` moves only the upper bound).
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(slotType)
		return tag == ValueTypeTag.integer or tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean or tag == ValueTypeTag.exitCode
	end 'zeroInhabitsPayloadType'

	// One alternative: a single case (`red`, or `walk(dir)` binding its payload) or a case-name range
	// (`low to high`, `low upto high`). Only the single-case form may carry payload bindings — a range
	// names no one case whose payload fields a binding list could resolve against. Appends the covered case
	// tag(s) as point/range intervals into `into`, and any bindings onto the ARM's list (see the caller:
	// the alternatives share one list because they share one body block).
	function collectEnumPatternAtomIntervals(layout EnumLayout, covered BoolArray, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray, bindings PayloadBindingArray) throws ParseError
		let patternTok = self.current()
		let loStart = self.pos
		let lowIndex = try self.resolveEnumArmCase(layout)

		if self.at(TokenKind.to) or self.at(TokenKind.upto) 'rangeArm'
			let isUpto = self.at(TokenKind.upto)
			self.advance()
			let highIndex = try self.resolveEnumArmCase(layout)

			// A case-name range MEANS the DECLARATION-INDEX slice, so the indices are its ordered domain —
			// never the tags, which an explicit `= N` may reorder (`collectEnumRangeIntervals` argues that
			// distinction at length). Asked before the interval collection because the two degenerate-range
			// verdicts are disjoint by construction — `rangeIsEmpty` and `requireRangeIsMultiValued` are
			// one pair of expressions kept in one place for exactly that reason — so `red upto red` (no
			// case) and `red upto green` (one case) cannot mask each other whichever runs first.
			try self.requireRangeIsMultiValued(lowIndex, hi: highIndex, isUpto: isUpto, hasAdjacentValues: true, loStart: loStart)

			try self.collectEnumRangeIntervals(layout, lowIndex: lowIndex, highIndex: highIndex, isUpto: isUpto, covered: covered, patternTok: patternTok, bodyBlockId: bodyBlockId, into: into)
			return
		end 'rangeArm'

		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'payloadBind'
			let atomBindings = try self.parseCasePayloadBindings(layout, caseIndex: lowIndex, caseTok: patternTok)
			for binding in atomBindings 'appendEach'
				bindings.push(binding)
			end 'appendEach'
		end 'payloadBind'

		try self.markEnumCovered(covered, index: lowIndex, layout: layout, patternTok: patternTok)
		let tag = layout.tagOfIndex(lowIndex)
		into.push(DispatchInterval.create(tag, hi: tag, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
	end 'collectEnumPatternAtomIntervals'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE ARM OF A MATCH ON A SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION.** The union path's twin of
	// `collectEnumPatternAtomIntervals`, and it is a SEPARATE routine for one reason: an error-union arm names
	// EXACTLY ONE `(member, case)` pair. It has no `or`-chain and no case RANGE — neither reference offers
	// them either, and both would be meaningless here (a range over the fused line would span two enums'
	// cases, which is not a thing an author can mean). What it does share is everything that matters: the
	// coverage bitmap, the dispatch interval, the payload-binding parse and the arm machinery that follows.
	//
	// ⭐ **BOTH SPELLINGS OF THE PATTERN, and the qualified one is why `resolveEnumArmCase` could not serve.**
	// That routine REFUSES `Enum.case` (E3075: the scrutinee's enum is already known, so the bare name
	// suffices) — which is exactly backwards here, where the qualifier is the only way to say which member's
	// `notFound` is meant. So: `Member.case` resolves against that member, a bare `case` resolves across all
	// members and is E3085 when two of them share the name.
	function collectErrorUnionPatternInterval(info ErrorUnionInfo, covered BoolArray, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray) returns ErrorUnionArm throws ParseError
		let firstTok = self.current()
		if not tokenCanBeAName(firstTok) 'notACaseName'
			throw ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(firstTok), line: firstTok.line, column: firstTok.column)
		end 'notACaseName'

		// The CASE-name token — the qualified spelling's second half, and the anchor every diagnostic below
		// uses. It is resolved before anything is consumed so the two spellings differ in one place only.
		let qualified = self.kindAt(self.pos + 1) == TokenKind.dot
		let caseTok = self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2) if qualified else firstTok
		if not tokenCanBeAName(caseTok) 'notACaseNameAfterTheDot'
			throw ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(caseTok), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'notACaseNameAfterTheDot'

		var index = 0 as CaseIndex
		if qualified 'qualified'
			self.advance()
			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
			self.advance()
			index = try self.errorUnionQualifiedCaseIndex(info, memberTok: firstTok, caseTok: caseTok)
		end 'qualified' else 'bare'
			self.advance()
			index = try self.errorUnionBareCaseIndex(info, caseTok: caseTok)
		end 'bare'

		let member = try info.caseMembers.get(index) otherwise panic("collectErrorUnionPatternInterval: caseMembers.get OOB at {index} — every resolver above returns an index into this column")
		let memberCaseIndex = member.layout.indexOfCase(info.layout.caseNameAt(index))

		var bindings = PayloadBindingArray.create()
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'payloadBind'
			// ⚠ **THE ARITY IS CHECKED BEFORE THE LIST IS PARSED, because E3035's ANCHOR is the case name and
			// the generic parse has already walked past it** — and because the message names the member
			// (`'ErrA.bad' expects 2`), which only this path can spell. v1 checks it eagerly at the same point
			// for the same reason. Everything the check passes then goes through the SHARED parse, so the
			// discard rule (E3081) and the binding records are the ordinary ones.
			try self.requireErrorUnionBindingCount(member, caseIndex: memberCaseIndex, caseTok: caseTok)
			bindings = try self.parseCasePayloadBindings(member.layout, caseIndex: memberCaseIndex, caseTok: caseTok)
		end 'payloadBind'

		// The NAME half of E3129 (`requireArmBindingsAreDistinct`). An error-union arm names exactly one
		// `(member, case)` pair and so has no or-chain and no slot to contest twice — but its list is minted
		// by the same `parseCasePayloadBindings`, which never compared its own names, so `bad(x, x)` reached
		// `Scope.install` here exactly as it did on the enum path. One rule, asked at both doors.
		try self.requireArmBindingsAreDistinct(bindings)

		try self.markEnumCovered(covered, index: index, layout: info.layout, patternTok: caseTok)
		let tag = info.layout.tagOfIndex(index)
		into.push(DispatchInterval.create(tag, hi: tag, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
		return ErrorUnionArm.create(member, bindings: bindings)
	end 'collectErrorUnionPatternInterval'

	// `Member.case` — the fused index of that pair. Both halves are E3034 with their own sentences, which is
	// v1's split: a qualifier naming no member of THIS union, and a member that has no such case.
	function errorUnionQualifiedCaseIndex(info ErrorUnionInfo, memberTok Token, caseTok Token) returns CaseIndex throws ParseError
		var namesTheMember = false
		var memberKindWord = ""
		for i in 0 upto info.layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			let member = try info.caseMembers.get(i) otherwise panic("errorUnionQualifiedCaseIndex: caseMembers.get OOB at {i} — the column is built case for case beside the layout's own")
			if not member.name.equals(memberTok.value) 'anotherMember'
				continue
			end 'anotherMember'
			namesTheMember = true
			memberKindWord = member.layout.kindWord()
			if info.layout.caseNameAt(i).equals(caseTok.value) 'found'
				return i
			end 'found'
		end 'eachCase'

		if not namesTheMember 'notAMember'
			throw ParseError.errorUnionUnknownMember(String.from(memberTok.value), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'notAMember'

		// The KIND WORD is the MEMBER's own — `ErrA.nope` on a scalar `enum ErrA` must not read "unknown union
		// case". `memberKindWord` is set beside `namesTheMember` above, on the same pass, so the two cannot
		// disagree about which member was found.
		throw ParseError.enumUnknownCase(memberKindWord, caseName: "{String.from(memberTok.value)}.{String.from(caseTok.value)}", line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
	end 'errorUnionQualifiedCaseIndex'

	// A BARE `case` — the fused index of the one member that declares it, **E3085 when two do**. The
	// ambiguity is the whole reason the qualified spelling exists, and it is scanned rather than precomputed
	// into a map for `EnumLayout.indexOfCase`'s reason: a union's case count is tiny, and a map would be a
	// second index of the one column above it.
	function errorUnionBareCaseIndex(info ErrorUnionInfo, caseTok Token) returns CaseIndex throws ParseError
		var found = info.layout.caseCount()
		for i in 0 upto info.layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			if not info.layout.caseNameAt(i).equals(caseTok.value) 'anotherCase'
				continue
			end 'anotherCase'
			if found < info.layout.caseCount() 'sharedByTwoMembers'
				throw ParseError.errorUnionAmbiguousBareCase(String.from(caseTok.value), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
			end 'sharedByTwoMembers'
			found = i
		end 'eachCase'

		if found >= info.layout.caseCount() 'noSuchCase'
			throw ParseError.errorUnionUnknownCase(String.from(caseTok.value), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'noSuchCase'
		return found
	end 'errorUnionBareCaseIndex'

	// **E3035 — the binding count an error-union pattern supplies against the case's declared arity**, read
	// off the TOKENS because the anchor is the case name and the shared parse consumes past it. The count is
	// exact: a binding list is `( name [, name]* )` and every element is a single identifier (or `_`), so the
	// identifiers between the parens ARE the bindings.
	function requireErrorUnionBindingCount(member TryBlockErrorMember, caseIndex CaseIndex, caseTok Token) throws ParseError
		let declared = member.layout.payloadArityAt(caseIndex)
		var supplied = 0 as ElementCount
		var scan = self.pos + 1
		while self.kindAt(scan) != TokenKind.rightParen and self.kindAt(scan) != TokenKind.eof 'countNames'
			if self.kindAt(scan) == TokenKind.identifier 'oneBinding'
				supplied = supplied + 1
			end 'oneBinding'
			scan = scan + 1
		end 'countNames'

		if supplied == declared 'agrees'
			return
		end 'agrees'

		throw ParseError.errorUnionWrongBindingCount("{String.from(member.name)}.{String.from(member.layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}", expected: declared as ElementCount, got: supplied, line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
	end 'requireErrorUnionBindingCount'

	// ⭐⭐ **RELEASE A BOXED MEMBER'S BOX ON THE PATH INTO A `default` ARM, WHICH NAMED NO MEMBER.** Returns
	// the block the default's BODY should be parsed into — `entry` itself when there is nothing to clean, so
	// the ordinary case adds no block at all.
	//
	// Every PATTERN arm releases the box through the member it named; a `default` reaches every uncovered
	// case, boxed members' included, and would otherwise abandon it. The test is a RANGE over the fused line
	// — a member owns a contiguous slice of it by construction (`recordRoutedErrorType`) — so one member costs
	// two compares and a `__destruct_` call on the path where it is really in flight. The bootstrap emits the
	// same chain (`EmitUnionDefaultCleanupBlock`); v1 needs none only because its box lives in a slot the
	// handler's own scope drops.
	function emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup(func IrFunction, union ErrorUnionMatch, entry IrBlock, flag ValueId, anchor Token) returns IrBlock
		if not errorUnionIsSynthesized(union) 'notAnErrorUnion'
			return entry
		end 'notAnErrorUnion'

		let info = requireErrorUnionInfo(union)
		var current = entry
		for m in 0 upto info.caseMembers.count() 'eachCase'
			let member = try info.caseMembers.get(m) otherwise panic("emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup: caseMembers.get OOB at {m} on a column walked to its own count")
			// One test per BOXED MEMBER, not per case — the column repeats a member once per case it declares,
			// so the first case of each is where its range is emitted and the rest are skipped.
			if not member.isBoxed or not self.isFirstCaseOfMember(info, index: m) 'nothingToReleaseForThisCase'
				continue
			end 'nothingToReleaseForThisCase'

			match self.fusedSpanOfMember(member) 'span'
				noCases then panic("emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup: a member with no cases contributes no fused case, so this walk cannot reach one")
				spanning(low, high) then current = self.emitErrorUnionMemberRelease(func, member: member, low: low, high: high, entry: current, fused: info.fusedOrdinal, flag: flag, anchor: anchor)
			end 'span'
		end 'eachCase'
		return current
	end 'emitErrorUnionDefaultCleanup'

	// Is fused case `index` the FIRST case of its member — i.e. is this the position where that member's
	// per-member work belongs? The column is grouped by member (`synthesizeErrorUnion` walks members
	// outermost), so "the previous case has a different member" is the whole test.
	function isFirstCaseOfMember(info ErrorUnionInfo, index CaseIndex) returns bool
		if index == 0 'theVeryFirst'
			return true
		end 'theVeryFirst'

		let here = try info.caseMembers.get(index) otherwise panic("isFirstCaseOfMember: caseMembers.get OOB at {index}")
		let before = try info.caseMembers.get(index - 1) otherwise panic("isFirstCaseOfMember: caseMembers.get OOB at {index - 1}")
		return not here.name.equals(before.name)
	end 'isFirstCaseOfMember'

	// The slice of the fused dispatch line this member owns — its own tag span, shifted by its offset, which
	// is the interval `recordRoutedErrorType` placed it in. Recomputed from the same two facts rather than
	// stored a second time; the shift cannot overflow because the placement checked exactly these two sums
	// before it accepted the member.
	function fusedSpanOfMember(member TryBlockErrorMember) returns EnumTagSpan
		match enumTagSpan(member.layout) 'span'
			noCases then panic("fusedSpanOfMember: '{String.from(member.name)}' declares no cases, so no fused case column can name it and the default cleanup never asks about it")
			spanning(low, high) then return EnumTagSpan.spanning(low + member.fusedTagOffset, high: high + member.fusedTagOffset)
		end 'span'
	end 'fusedSpanOfMember'

	// `if fused in [low, high] then <release the box as `member`>` — three blocks, and the block
	// control continues in is returned so the caller can chain the next member onto it.
	function emitErrorUnionMemberRelease(func IrFunction, member TryBlockErrorMember, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, entry IrBlock, fused ValueId, flag ValueId, anchor Token) returns IrBlock
		var atOrAbove = self.newBlock(func, label: b"errunionclean", line: anchor.line)
		var release = self.newBlock(func, label: b"errunionfree", line: anchor.line)
		let after = self.newBlock(func, label: b"errunionnext", line: anchor.line)

		self.currentBlock = entry
		let notBelow = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(fused, literal: low, op: MaxonCmpOp.ge, opTok: anchor)
		self.emitCondBranch(entry, cond: notBelow, thenId: atOrAbove.id, elseId: after.id, ifTok: anchor)

		self.currentBlock = atOrAbove
		let notAbove = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(fused, literal: high, op: MaxonCmpOp.le, opTok: anchor)
		self.emitCondBranch(atOrAbove, cond: notAbove, thenId: release.id, elseId: after.id, ifTok: anchor)

		// The retype is what tells `emitDecref` which `__destruct_` to call, and it is undone immediately:
		// the value is the union's flag everywhere but inside this one release block, exactly as a pattern
		// arm's is (see `parseMatchCommon`, which restores it after the arm's own drop).
		self.currentBlock = release
		self.retypeValue(flag, valueType: self.tryErrorEnumType(member.clause))
		self.emitDecref(flag, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		self.retypeValue(flag, valueType: MaxonType.integer)
		self.emitBranch(release, targetId: after.id)

		self.currentBlock = after
		return after
	end 'emitErrorUnionMemberRelease'

	// ⭐ **A FALLTHROUGH TARGET MAY NOT BIND A PAYLOAD** — an arrival from a case the arm never named, so the
	// bindings at its head would destructure a case the value does not hold.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS NO LONGER "a binding is meaningful only where EXACTLY ONE case matched" — E3129 MADE THAT
	// FALSE**, and the replacement premise is what keeps this refusal unconditional. An `or`-arm now binds
	// across several cases precisely because `requireOrArmPayloadSlotsAgree` can CHECK them: they are the
	// arm's own alternatives, enumerated in its pattern. A fallthrough SOURCE is not in that set — the arm
	// fallen into never named it — so the agreement was never asked, and the same check cannot simply be
	// pointed at the predecessor either: it carries a hazard the or-arm shape does not, namely that the
	// predecessor's arm may already have MOVED its payload out and NULLED the slot, which is a fact about
	// EMITTED CODE on one incoming edge rather than about the layout both cases share.
	//
	// ⚠ What that costs depends only on which slot happens to be read, which is why it had to become a
	// refusal rather than a note. All three MEASURED, before this check existed: a **SIGSEGV** (an owned
	// arm MOVED its payload out and nulled the slot, and the arm fallen into bound the null and printed
	// it); a silent **TYPE CONFUSION** (a borrowed arm leaves the slot intact, so an `int` binding in the
	// arm fallen into reads the String POINTER the previous case put there); and a silently **WRONG
	// NUMBER** (two scalar payloads at the same slot, so the successor re-reads its predecessor's value —
	// no crash, no diagnostic, both reference compilers agreeing on the same wrong answer).
	//
	// NEITHER REFERENCE REFUSES IT and both produce garbage of their own — the bootstrap prints the
	// payload twice in the String case, since it does not null the slot — so nothing that ever worked is
	// taken away here. Positioned at the arm being fallen INTO, which is the arm that cannot bind.
	// Falling OUT of a binding arm stays legal: those bindings were established by the case that actually
	// matched, and they drop on the fallthrough edge like any other live exit.
	function rejectFallthroughIntoBindingArm(bindings PayloadBindingArray, layout EnumLayout, patternTok Token) throws ParseError
		if bindings.count() == 0 'bindsNothing'
			return
		end 'bindsNothing'
		throw ParseError.unsupported("`and fallthrough` into the payload-binding arm '{String.from(patternTok.value)}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` — the preceding arm matched a DIFFERENT case, so on that edge these bindings would destructure a case the value does not hold (reading another case's slots, or a slot that arm already moved out and nulled). Fall through into a payload-free arm, or give this arm its own body", line: patternTok.line, column: patternTok.column)
	end 'rejectFallthroughIntoBindingArm'

	// Parse a `(dir)` / `(_)` / `(a, b)` binding list on a single-case pattern, resolving each binding
	// against the case's declared payload fields in SLOT order. A `_` is the discard — it consumes a
	// slot but binds nothing. The binding count must equal the case's declared arity.
	//
	// `caseTok` is the ARM's case-name token, carried in for one reason: it is E3081's anchor (below).
	function parseCasePayloadBindings(layout EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, caseTok Token) returns PayloadBindingArray throws ParseError
		let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		var bindings = PayloadBindingArray.create()
		// Tracked as the list is read rather than re-walked afterwards — the loop already knows which arm
		// each binding took.
		var allDiscard = true

		while true 'bindLoop'
			let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
			if bindings.count() >= payloads.count() 'tooMany'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("case '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` binds more payloads than the {payloads.count()} it declares", line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'tooMany'
			let field = try payloads.get(bindings.count()) otherwise panic("parseCasePayloadBindings: payloads.get OOB after the count guard")
			if nameTok.value.equals(DiscardBindingName) 'discard'
				bindings.push(PayloadBinding.discard(nameTok, field: field, slot: bindings.count()))
			end 'discard' else 'realBinding'
				try self.requireUnreservedName(nameTok, declaredName: nameTok.value)
				bindings.push(PayloadBinding.bound(nameTok, field: field, slot: bindings.count()))
				allDiscard = false
			end 'realBinding'

			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endBindings'
				break
			end 'endBindings'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'bindLoop'

		let closeTok = self.current()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		if bindings.count() != payloads.count() 'tooFew'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("case '{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` binds {bindings.count()} payload(s) but declares {payloads.count()}", line: closeTok.line, column: closeTok.column)
		end 'tooFew'

		// ⭐ **AN ALL-DISCARD LIST IS EXACTLY THE BARE CASE NAME, so the language admits only the one
		// spelling** (E3081, both reference compilers). The rule is about the LIST and nothing else: a
		// PARTIAL discard (`two(_, b)`) binds something and is legal, and a bare `value` on a
		// payload-carrying case is legal — it is the spelling this message recommends.
		//
		// ⚠ The references also test `count > 0`; here that condition CANNOT fail, so it is deliberately
		// absent rather than written as a check that always passes. The loop above consumes an identifier
		// before it can add anything, so `value()` is `E2010` at the missing name and an empty list never
		// reaches this line.
		if allDiscard 'everyBindingDiscarded'
			throw ParseError.matchDiscardedBindings(String.from(layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex)), discardList: discardBindingList(bindings.count()), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'everyBindingDiscarded'

		return bindings
	end 'parseCasePayloadBindings'

	// Declare a single-case pattern's payload bindings at the head of the arm body — each a
	// `loadIndirect` from the SCRUTINEE BOX (`scrutBox`). Called inside a scope frame the arm loop opens,
	// so the bindings leave scope at the arm's `end` and cannot leak into the next arm. A discard `_`
	// binds nothing (its managed payload is left in the box and dropped by the scope-exit cascade).
	//
	// ⭐ **A SCALAR binding is a COPY** (not a borrow into the box), so dropping the box does not
	// invalidate it. A MANAGED binding (String/struct/boxed union) OWNS its payload either way — it drops at its
	// own scope exit — but HOW it acquires that ownership depends on HOW MANY OWNERS THE BOX HAS, which is a
	// THREE-state question and not the two-state one this header asserted for three rungs:
	//   • a scrutinee this frame is the SOLE owner of ⇒ a MOVE-OUT, and the box slot is nulled so the box's
	//     cascade skips it (`moveOutManagedPayload`);
	//   • a BORROWED scrutinee (a parameter, a receiver, a bare field read) ⇒ a RETAIN, and the slot is left
	//     INTACT because the box's real owner is the caller's local (`retainBorrowedPayload`, D1b);
	//   • a CO-OWNED scrutinee — one this frame owns a reference to WHILE SOMETHING ELSE HOLDS THE SAME BOX ⇒
	//     the SAME RETAIN, for the same reason read one step out. `let borrowed = h.ty` (a read out of a mutable
	//     field, promoted by `__mm_retain`), a payload retained out of a borrowed union and then matched again, a
	//     call's owned result, a caught error box and a consumed parameter are all in this state, and every one
	//     of them SEGFAULTED while the move-out's gate asked `valueIsOwnedHeap` — a predicate that answers
	//     *"this value owns a reference"* where the null store needs *"nobody else will read these slots"*. The
	//     gate is `valueIsSoleHeapOwner`; see `OwnedHeapExclusivity` for the six measured programs.
	// Returns whether ANY managed field was MOVED out — the whole match then consumes the scrutinee
	// (`setBindingPartiallyMoved`). A RETAIN consumes nothing, so it must not set it: nothing left the
	// box, and marking it moved would make the other owner's later re-read a false E3102.
	//
	// ⭐ **`scrutMutable` DECIDES WHETHER THE BINDINGS ACCEPT A WRITE (`specs/mutable-enums.md`).** It is a
	// fact about the SCRUTINEE and never about the pattern: every binding of a `var` union is writable and
	// every binding of a `let` one is E2013, whatever the arm spells. Both reference compilers say the same
	// (v1 refuses a per-binding `var` keyword from overriding an immutable scrutinee), and it is the honest
	// rule — the write is a store into the scrutinee's box, so the scrutinee's own mutability is the
	// permission being asked for.
	function declarePayloadBindings(bindings PayloadBindingArray, scrutBox ValueId, scrutSolelyOwned bool, scrutMutable bool, layout EnumLayout) returns bool throws ParseError
		var movedManaged = false
		for binding in bindings 'each'
			if binding.isDiscard 'discard'
				continue
			end 'discard'
			let range = SourceRange.ofToken(binding.nameTok)
			// Through `payloadTypeOf` — the payload's own slot door — which adopts the signatures-layout id
			// into this file's interner (OPEN #52) AND resolves what the declaration sweep could not name.
			// It was a bare `adoptType` here, at the construct check and at the payload LOAD, which is the
			// spelling A4k retired: a generic-alias payload arrived `named`, i.e. an int (A4i's defect one
			// slot over).
			let declared = self.signatures.payloadTypeOf(binding.field, layout: layout, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
			let class = try self.requirePayloadLowerable(binding.field, declared: declared, layout: layout, posTok: binding.nameTok)

			// ⭐ **THE SLOT'S ADDRESS AND ITS WIDTH, DERIVED ONCE AND HANDED TO ALL THREE HALVES** — the
			// LOAD, the binding that records them for a write-back, and the move-out's null store. They were
			// derived independently inside `emitUnionPayloadLoad` and again here, which is two derivations
			// of the pair a load and its store must agree on EXACTLY; pure functions on equal inputs, so
			// they could not disagree today, but nothing made that a property rather than a coincidence.
			let offset = layout.payloadSlotOffset(binding.slot)
			let storage = payloadStorageOf(class)
			let loaded = self.emitUnionPayloadLoad(scrutBox, field: binding.field, offset: offset, valueType: payloadValueTypeOf(class, declared: declared), storage: storage, range: range)
			var bindingInfo = try self.scope.declarePayloadValueBinding(binding.nameTok.value, varType: self.typeOf(loaded), mutable: scrutMutable and payloadBindingAcceptsWrites(class), value: loaded, scrutBox: scrutBox, slotOffset: offset, storage: storage) otherwise throw ParseError.localShadowsSelfField(String.from(binding.nameTok.value), typeName: String.from(self.enclosingType), line: binding.nameTok.line, column: binding.nameTok.column)
			if payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'managedPayload'
				if scrutSolelyOwned 'ownedMoveOut'
					self.moveOutManagedPayload(bindingInfo, loaded: loaded, scrutBox: scrutBox, offset: offset, storage: storage, range: range)
					movedManaged = true
				end 'ownedMoveOut' else 'borrowedRetain'
					self.retainBorrowedPayload(bindingInfo, loaded: loaded, range: range)

					// ⭐⭐ **A RETAINED MANAGED PAYLOAD IS READ-ONLY, WHATEVER THE SCRUTINEE'S MUTABILITY SAYS —
					// AND THE STATE THIS CLOSES IS A MEASURED LEAK.** Writability is decided from
					// `scrutMutable`, which is MEMBERSHIP in `ownedBindings`; the acquisition above is decided
					// from `scrutSolelyOwned`, which is VALUE PROVENANCE — and the tree already records that those
					// two disagree (`reassignBindingValue`'s header: a loop-carried `var`'s current value is a header
					// phi, and a phi carries no provenance bit, so the value column reads `false` for a binding
					// the loop genuinely owns). A `var` union matched INSIDE the loop that reassigns it lands
					// exactly there: mutable by membership, borrowed by provenance. It took this retain path —
					// slot still occupied, box still holding a reference — while the binding was minted writable,
					// so the write-back stored straight over an occupied slot and nothing released what the box
					// held. MEASURED as exit 101 before this line existed.
					//
					// ⭐ **AND THE CO-OWNED SCRUTINEE ARRIVES HERE BY THE SAME DOOR, WHICH IS WHY THE DEMOTION IS
					// STATED ABOUT THE ACQUISITION AND NOT ABOUT THE PHI.** A `var borrowed = <a field/element
					// read>` is mutable by membership and CO-OWNED by provenance, so it also takes this retain and
					// also has no vacancy to write into — an unrelated shape reaching one rule, which is what says
					// the rule is about the retain rather than about loop-carried phis.
					//
					// It is stated HERE, at the branch that chose the acquisition, rather than folded into the
					// `mutable:` argument above — that would re-derive the move-vs-retain decision at a second
					// site, which is the very split that produced the leak. The write-back protocol shv2 has is
					// "store into a VACATED slot"; a retained payload has no vacancy, so the honest answer is
					// that this binding does not accept a write (E2013) rather than a guessed release of a
					// reference whose owner this frame cannot name. `writeBackPayload` panics if the combination
					// ever reaches it again.
					bindingInfo.mutable = false
				end 'borrowedRetain'
			end 'managedPayload'
		end 'each'
		return movedManaged
	end 'declarePayloadBindings'

	// ⭐ **MOVE A MANAGED PAYLOAD OUT OF THE SCRUTINEE BOX** into its binding — the match half of static
	// single-owner ownership. The binding OWNS the payload (enrolled in `ownedBindings`, so it drops at
	// its own scope exit through `emitDecref`'s tag routing), and the box SLOT is NULLED so the box's own
	// `__destruct_<U>` cascade — which null-guards every field — does not decref it a second time.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **REACHED ONLY WHERE THIS FRAME IS THE BOX'S SOLE OWNER (`valueIsSoleHeapOwner`), WHICH IS STRICTLY
	// NARROWER THAN "OWNED" AND USED TO SAY "OWNED".** The null store below is destructive and this function
	// never re-reads the slot, so every OTHER owner of the box is a reader it cannot account for: a BORROWED
	// union's owner is the caller's local, and a CO-OWNED one's is a struct field, an array slot, a caller's
	// payload slot or a caller's own local that the retain gave this frame a second reference to. Both take
	// `retainBorrowedPayload` instead, which leaves the slot alone; `declarePayloadBindings` is the one place
	// that picks between them, and `OwnedHeapExclusivity` records the six programs the wider gate segfaulted.
	function moveOutManagedPayload(bindingInfo VarInfo, loaded ValueId, scrutBox ValueId, offset ByteOffset, storage MaxonType, range SourceRange)
		// ⛔⛔ **CO-OWNED, AND THE ARGUMENT FOR `sole` HERE WAS THIS RUNG'S OWN BUG ONE LEVEL DOWN.** It read
		// *"the box was this frame's alone and the store below empties the slot, so the binding now holds the
		// allocation's only reference"* — and the second clause does not follow from the first. The box's
		// soleness is a fact about the BOX; the reference its slot HOLDS is a separate fact, because
		// `moveManagedValueInto`'s borrowed arm stores a borrowed aggregate by `__mm_incref`
		// (`coOwnBorrowedForConsume`) rather than by move. **So a genuinely-sole box can hold a co-owned
		// payload**, and stamping that payload `sole` re-arms the very destructive write this column exists to
		// disarm. MEASURED two ways, both 0xC0000005: `let w = Wrap.held(<a borrowed union>)` then an INLINE
		// nested `match i` nulls the CALLER's box slot
		// (`union-managed-payload/a-co-owned-payload-moved-out-of-a-sole-box-is-still-co-owned`), and
		// `held(b) then throw b.e` nulls the caller's struct field
		// (`throw-transfers-ownership/boxed-union-field-thrown-out-of-a-co-owned-in-payload`, a shape that
		// predates this rung). Both have CONTROLS that pass: the same programs with a helper call, and with the
		// payload CONSTRUCTED at the construct site — so the discriminator is move-in versus co-own-in and
		// nothing else.
		//
		// ⚖ **THE CURE IS THE UNDERSTATEMENT, BY DECISION (coordinator, this rung).** Tracking "every managed
		// payload in this box was MOVED in" per box would keep the nested move-out, and it would add a THIRD
		// ownership state and a new invariant to a rung that has already paid six segfaults for one predicate
		// answering two facts. The cost of understating is one refcount pair per nested move-out; the cost of
		// overstating is a stolen slot. `OwnedHeapExclusivity`'s own safety argument decides it.
		self.ownPayloadBinding(bindingInfo, loaded: loaded, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)

		let zero = self.emitLiteral(0 as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.emitIndirectStore(scrutBox, offset: offset, value: zero, storageType: storage, range: range)
		// The HOLE this just made, recorded ON the binding that made it. A write-back through this binding
		// fills it again, and only when NO hole is left may the scrutinee stop being `partiallyMoved` —
		// see `VarInfo.payloadSlotVacated` and `writeBackPayload`.
		bindingInfo.payloadSlotVacated = true
	end 'moveOutManagedPayload'

	// ⭐ **RETAIN A MANAGED PAYLOAD BOUND OUT OF A BORROWED UNION (D1b).** The load borrowed the payload —
	// the box's owner is the CALLER's local (a parameter, or a method's receiver), and it survives this
	// frame — so ownership cannot be MOVED: the caller may re-read the field, or match the union again, and
	// its own cascade will release every slot it still holds. So instead of a move this RETAINS:
	// `__mm_incref` raises the payload's refcount, the binding owns that SECOND reference and drops it at
	// the arm's own exit (`dropArmScopedOwned`), and the slot is left INTACT for the owner. The refcount
	// balances at exactly one free. Nothing left the box, so the match does NOT consume the scrutinee.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INCREF MUST PRECEDE ANY RELEASE OF THE BASE**, and it does because it is emitted at the bind
	// point — the op immediately after `emitUnionPayloadLoad`, before the arm body can drop anything. The
	// hazard in full is `specs/refcount-accessor-borrow-order.md`: release the base first and its
	// destructor drives the field to zero, so the following incref resurrects freed memory.
	//
	// The RETAIN twin of `moveOutManagedPayload`, and the THIRD application of the #40 retain-promotion
	// thesis after `retainBorrowedAggregate` and `retainThrownField` — whose comment states the same
	// argument for the throw channel, one field-load out. **A bounded, unconditional refcount op is the
	// point, not a shortcut.** v1 decides the same question per value with a whole-function dataflow
	// analysis (`InsertRefcounts.computeIncomingOwnerAcquire`) because its RELEASE side is derived from
	// that same analysis — a last-use decref — so a blanket acquire there genuinely leaks. shv2's release
	// is STRUCTURAL: the binding is in `ownedBindings` and drops at its scope exit whatever the analysis
	// would have said, so an unconditional acquire is unconditionally balanced and needs no analysis at
	// all. Porting v1's would buy a saved incref with a dataflow pass shv2 does not have.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE BINDING IS A CO-OWNER, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES A NESTED `match` ON IT SAFE.** The reference this
	// took is a SECOND one; the box still holds the first. So `direct(ty) gives match ty …` — a nested match on a
	// retained payload — must retain in turn, and it does because the acquisition is recorded on the value rather
	// than re-derived from its owned bit. Claiming sole here is exactly how an inline nested match came to null a
	// caller's `Ty` box slot (MEASURED; the same program with a HELPER CALL in place of the inline match was fine,
	// because the callee's parameter is borrowed and retains).
	function retainBorrowedPayload(bindingInfo VarInfo, loaded ValueId, range SourceRange)
		self.emitIncref(loaded, range: range)
		self.ownPayloadBinding(bindingInfo, loaded: loaded, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
	end 'retainBorrowedPayload'

	// The half both managed-payload bind paths share: the binding OWNS the payload's reference, however it
	// was acquired (moved out of a solely-owned box, or retained out of a box with another owner). The
	// provenance bit is what `valueIsOwnedHeap` reads — a later `let q = <it>` re-moves it correctly, and a
	// struct payload is not mistaken for a borrow — and `ownedBindings` is what DROPS it, at the arm's own exit.
	//
	// ⚠ **`exclusivity` IS THE CALLER'S BECAUSE IT IS THE ONE THING THE TWO PATHS DO NOT SHARE, and folding it
	// in here would re-derive the move-vs-retain decision at a second site** — the split that produced the
	// write-back leak `declarePayloadBindings` records, one question over.
	//
	// `markValueOwnsHeap` rather than `trackOwnedTemp`, for the reason a consumed parameter needs the same
	// half: the BINDING owns the drop, not the statement, so enrolling a statement-scoped pending temp
	// would drop the payload a second time at the enclosing statement's end.
	function ownPayloadBinding(bindingInfo VarInfo, loaded ValueId, exclusivity OwnedHeapExclusivity)
		self.markValueOwnsHeap(loaded, exclusivity: exclusivity)
		self.ownedBindings.push(bindingInfo)
	end 'ownPayloadBinding'

	// The declaration index of the case named by `nameTok`, or E3034 (unknown case) positioned at
	// `posTok`. THE one emitter of "this name is not a case of this enum" — a `Color.red` literal
	// anchors it at the TYPE name and a match arm at the CASE name, so the POSITION is the caller's to
	// pass, but the lookup and the diagnostic are shared so the two sites cannot drift on either.
	function enumCaseIndexOrThrow(layout EnumLayout, nameTok Token, posTok Token) returns CaseIndex throws ParseError
		let index = layout.indexOfCase(nameTok.value)
		if index >= layout.caseCount() 'unknownCase'
			throw ParseError.enumUnknownCase(layout.kindWord(), caseName: String.from(nameTok.value), line: posTok.line, column: posTok.column)
		end 'unknownCase'
		return index
	end 'enumCaseIndexOrThrow'

	// Resolve a case NAME in a match arm to its declaration index. A QUALIFIED name (`Color.red`) is
	// E3075 — the scrutinee's enum is already known, so the bare name suffices — positioned at the
	// type name (the bootstrap's anchor). An unknown case is E3034, at the case name.
	function resolveEnumArmCase(layout EnumLayout) returns CaseIndex throws ParseError
		let caseTok = self.current()
		if not tokenCanBeAName(caseTok) 'notACaseName'
			throw ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(caseTok), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'notACaseName'

		if self.kindAt(self.pos + 1) == TokenKind.dot 'qualified'
			self.advance()
			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
			let realCaseTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.matchQualifiedCaseName(String.from(realCaseTok.value), qualified: "{String.from(caseTok.value)}.{String.from(realCaseTok.value)}", line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'qualified'

		self.advance()
		return try self.enumCaseIndexOrThrow(layout, nameTok: caseTok, posTok: caseTok)
	end 'resolveEnumArmCase'

	// Mark case `index` covered, or raise E2027 if a prior arm already covered it. The overlap is
	// positioned at the CURRENT arm's pattern token (`patternTok`), naming the case that collides —
	// the bootstrap's exact anchor and message.
	function markEnumCovered(covered BoolArray, index CaseIndex, layout EnumLayout, patternTok Token) throws ParseError
		let already = try covered.get(index) otherwise panic("markEnumCovered: covered.get OOB at {index} — the bitmap is sized to the enum's case count")
		if already 'overlap'
			throw ParseError.matchOverlappingCase(String.from(layout.caseNameAt(index)), line: patternTok.line, column: patternTok.column)
		end 'overlap'
		try covered.set(index, value: true) otherwise panic("markEnumCovered: covered.set OOB at {index}")
	end 'markEnumCovered'

	// ⭐ **COMPARE A SCALAR AGAINST AN INTEGER LITERAL** — `subject <op> literal`, emitting `MaxonOp.compare`
	// DIRECTLY rather than through `emitCompare`. The bypass is load-bearing at every one of its callers, and
	// for one of two reasons each: `emitCompare` REFUSES a `union` operand (E3066), and `emitCompare` THROWS.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THIS LIST IS A CENSUS AND IT HAS ROTTED TWICE.** It said "four times over" while SIX families
	// called it (the cursor-protocol test and the error-union cleanup were both absent), which is the
	// enumerated-doors shape: a list that a new caller does not have to join in order to compile. Nothing
	// checks it — so when you add a caller, add its bullet, and when you read it, read `grep` first.
	//
	//   • **The MATCH DISPATCH** — a point leaf's `==`, a range leaf's `>=`/`<=`, an internal node's `<`
	//     pivot. A PAYLOAD-FREE union match dispatches on the scrutinee itself (a `named(union)` value), so
	//     routing through `emitCompare` would reject `match u` outright.
	//   • **A BLOCK-FORM `try`'s ERROR-FLAG TEST** (`flag != NoErrorFlag`), whose router is NON-THROWING by
	//     construction — one of its two emit doors, `emitRuntimeCall`, is not a throwing function — and
	//     `emitCompare` throws.
	//   • **A `for … in <cursor>` LOOP'S ADVANCE TEST** (`emitCursorAdvance`'s `flag == 0`), the same
	//     already-encoded error flag one construct over, in a routine that likewise cannot throw a compare's
	//     diagnostic at a value the author never wrote.
	//   • **A SYNTHESIZED ERROR UNION'S DEFAULT CLEANUP** (`emitErrorUnionMemberRelease`'s `>=`/`<=` pair) —
	//     the range test that decides whether a BOXED member's box is the error in flight. Its subject is the
	//     fused ordinal, a compiler-minted i64 that no source expression names.
	//   • **AN `Ordering` VERDICT** (`emitOrderingVerdict`) — the tag test that turns a `compare` result into
	//     an ordering operator's bool. Its subject is a `named(Ordering)` value, which `emitCompare` would
	//     hand to the same `valueIsUnion` gate the match dispatch dodges.
	//   • **AN ENUM ACCESSOR'S CASE SELECT** (`emitEnumCaseConstantSelect`) — one `tag == tagᵢ` term of the
	//     branchless tag→index sum. Its subject is the same `named(<Enum>)` (or loaded i64) tag the match
	//     dispatch tests, for the same E3066 reason, which is why the two read this one door.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ACCESSOR'S CASE SELECT IS THE ONLY CALLER WHOSE 0/1 IS NOT CONSUMED BY A BRANCH, AND THAT IS A
	// REAL DIFFERENCE.** Every other one feeds the result to a `condBranch`, where a `boolean` mint is exactly
	// right and the backends need never materialize it at all (x64 fuses the `cmp` into the `jcc`). The
	// ordinal sum feeds it to a MULTIPLY, as a number — so it materializes, and its mint type becomes an
	// arithmetic operand type. That caller re-records the result `integer` at its own site rather than
	// changing this door's mint, which would move all the others (see `retypeValue`'s header, where the
	// safety of that re-record is argued).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PARAMETER IS `literal`, NOT `tag`.** It was `tag` while the match dispatch was its only caller,
	// which named that caller's USE of the operand rather than the operand — and a flag test passing
	// `NoErrorFlag` is what made the mis-naming visible.
	//
	// Every caller hands it operands whose types are settled by construction: the subject is a scalar enum's
	// `named` value, a boxed union's loaded i64 tag, a scalar int/char/bool, an `Ordering` case tag, or a
	// minted error flag, and the literal is an i64. There is no operand type left to check, which is exactly
	// what makes the bypass a narrowing of `emitCompare` rather than an evasion of it.
	function emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject ValueId, literal ParsedInt, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		let rhs = self.emitLiteral(literal, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.boolean, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.compare(result, lhs: subject, rhs: rhs, op: op), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		return result
	end 'emitCompareAgainstLiteral'

	// The OPEN #21 range rule, reduced to DISPATCH INTERVALS. A range arm `low to high` MEANS the cases in
	// the declaration-index slice `[low, lastCovered]` (`upto` drops the upper). Where the covered cases'
	// tags form a closed interval `[minTag, maxTag]` containing NO uncovered case's tag (`tagRangeCovers`),
	// the whole slice is ONE interval; otherwise each covered case is its own point interval, so an
	// uncovered case whose tag falls between them cannot slip in. Same coverage bookkeeping and same
	// `tagRangeCovers` predicate the old compare-emitting `emitEnumRange` used — only the OUTPUT is an
	// interval list, from which the dispatch emits the range/point compares once.
	function collectEnumRangeIntervals(layout EnumLayout, lowIndex CaseIndex, highIndex CaseIndex, isUpto bool, covered BoolArray, patternTok Token, bodyBlockId BlockId, into DispatchIntervalArray) throws ParseError
		// An empty range: `upto` whose upper is at or before the lower, or a reversed `to` (upper
		// declared before lower). Asked before `lastCovered` so its subtraction cannot underflow, and
		// asked of `rangeIsEmpty` so the enum and scalar paths cannot drift apart on what empty means.
		if self.rangeIsEmpty(lowIndex, hi: highIndex, isUpto: isUpto) 'empty'
			throw ParseError.matchEmptyRange(String.from(layout.caseNameAt(lowIndex)), high: String.from(layout.caseNameAt(highIndex)), line: patternTok.line, column: patternTok.column)
		end 'empty'

		let lastCovered = (highIndex - 1) if isUpto else highIndex

		// Mark the covered slice (overlap-checked) and gather the min/max TAG over it.
		var loTag = layout.tagOfIndex(lowIndex)
		var hiTag = loTag
		var i = lowIndex
		while i <= lastCovered 'coverEach'
			try self.markEnumCovered(covered, index: i, layout: layout, patternTok: patternTok)
			let t = layout.tagOfIndex(i)
			if t < loTag 'newLo'
				loTag = t
			end 'newLo'
			if t > hiTag 'newHi'
				hiTag = t
			end 'newHi'
			i = i + 1
		end 'coverEach'

		if self.tagRangeCovers(layout, lowIndex: lowIndex, lastCovered: lastCovered, loTag: loTag, hiTag: hiTag) 'expressibleAsRange'
			into.push(DispatchInterval.create(loTag, hi: hiTag, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
			return
		end 'expressibleAsRange'

		// The covered cases as individual point intervals — the interval twin of the old per-case OR.
		var j = lowIndex
		while j <= lastCovered 'pointEach'
			let tag = layout.tagOfIndex(j)
			into.push(DispatchInterval.create(tag, hi: tag, targetBlockId: bodyBlockId, edgeArgs: ValueIdArray.create()))
			j = j + 1
		end 'pointEach'
	end 'collectEnumRangeIntervals'

	// Is a two-compare tag range `[loTag, hiTag]` faithful to the covered slice — i.e. does NO
	// uncovered case's tag fall inside that closed interval? The predicate at the heart of OPEN #21:
	// where it holds, `serverError`'s 404 does not sit between `ok`'s 500 and `notFound`'s 200, so the
	// range means exactly the covered set and is safe to emit; where it fails, the OR is forced.
	function tagRangeCovers(layout EnumLayout, lowIndex CaseIndex, lastCovered CaseIndex, loTag ParsedInt, hiTag ParsedInt) returns bool
		for j in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			if j >= lowIndex and j <= lastCovered 'isCovered'
				continue
			end 'isCovered'
			let t = layout.tagOfIndex(j)
			if t >= loTag and t <= hiTag 'insideInterval'
				return false
			end 'insideInterval'
		end 'eachCase'
		return true
	end 'tagRangeCovers'

	// An enum match must cover EVERY case. The uncovered ones (in declaration order) become E2026's
	// `missing:` list, positioned at the closing `end` — the enum twin of the scalar exhaustiveness
	// gate, and the mechanism that makes adding a case force every match site to handle it.
	function checkEnumExhaustive(layout EnumLayout, covered BoolArray, union ErrorUnionMatch) throws ParseError
		var missing = ""
		for i in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			let isCovered = try covered.get(i) otherwise panic("checkEnumExhaustive: covered.get OOB at {i} — the bitmap is sized to the enum's case count")
			if not isCovered 'uncovered'
				missing = appendCommaSeparated(missing, item: self.matchCaseDisplayName(layout, index: i, union: union))
			end 'uncovered'
		end 'eachCase'

		// The list IS the flag — a case name is never empty, so an exhaustive match leaves it empty and
		// there is no parallel `hasMissing` bool to keep in step with it.
		if missing.isEmpty() 'complete'
			return
		end 'complete'

		// ⚠ ONE loop, TWO sentences: the case names and the subject both change for a synthesized error
		// union, and nothing else does. Written as one walk with a per-case display name rather than a second
		// copy of the walk — the shape both references took, and the copy each of them has to keep in step.
		let endTok = self.current()
		if errorUnionIsSynthesized(union) 'anErrorUnion'
			throw ParseError.errorUnionMatchNotExhaustive(missing, line: endTok.line, column: endTok.column)
		end 'anErrorUnion'

		throw ParseError.enumMatchNotExhaustive(layout.kindWord(), typeName: String.from(layout.name), missing: missing, line: endTok.line, column: endTok.column)
	end 'checkEnumExhaustive'

	// How case `index` is SPELLED in a diagnostic: its bare name, or `Member.case` when the layout is a
	// synthesized error union — where the bare name alone would be ambiguous and, for `missing:`, unusable
	// (an author cannot act on `bang, splat` without knowing whose they are). Both references print the
	// qualified form for exactly that reason.
	function matchCaseDisplayName(layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex, union ErrorUnionMatch) returns String
		if not errorUnionIsSynthesized(union) 'anOrdinaryEnum'
			return String.from(layout.caseNameAt(index))
		end 'anOrdinaryEnum'

		let info = requireErrorUnionInfo(union)
		let member = try info.caseMembers.get(index) otherwise panic("matchCaseDisplayName: caseMembers.get OOB at {index} — the column is built case for case beside the layout's own")
		return "{String.from(member.name)}.{String.from(layout.caseNameAt(index))}"
	end 'matchCaseDisplayName'

	// ⭐ **CLIP the raw arm intervals into a SORTED, DISJOINT set where EARLIER ARMS WIN.** Overlaps are
	// legal on a scalar match (two ranges may overlap, or a later single value fall inside an earlier
	// range) and the FIRST arm claims the overlap — exactly the order the linear chain gave by testing
	// arms top to bottom. A binary search cannot rely on arm order, so the overlap is resolved HERE. The
	// result is the partition of the value line into maximal runs each OWNED by one arm, where a point's
	// owner is the EARLIEST (lowest-index) raw interval covering it — the "earlier wins" a top-to-bottom
	// chain gave for free. It is sorted by `lo`, which is what makes the median-split search correct.
	// (Enum/union intervals are already disjoint — `markEnumCovered` rejects overlap — so for them this is
	// only the sort.)
	//
	// ⚠ THIS IS O(n log n) IN THE ARM COUNT, AND IT USED TO BE Θ(n²) — a subtract-and-insert that, per raw
	// interval, scanned the whole claimed set and inserted into the middle of a sorted array. MEASURED at
	// 256→2048 distinct scalar arms, the parse phase read x2.3→x2.7→x3.0 per doubling (linear reads x2.0):
	// a 2048-arm dispatch table — a bytecode/keyword switch, the very shape a `match` is FOR — spent ~47 ms
	// of a 70 ms parse in this one function. The scale-test corpus has no `match`, so it never saw it.
	//
	// The replacement is an offline left-to-right sweep: sort the interval indices by `lo` once, then walk
	// the value line, keeping the started-but-not-ended intervals in a min-heap KEYED BY ARM PRIORITY so
	// the current owner is the heap minimum. A segment closes and a new one opens exactly when that owner
	// changes — an earlier-arm interval activating (at its `lo`) or the current owner ending (at its `hi`),
	// the only two coordinates where ownership can change — which coalesces same-owner runs and reproduces
	// the old partition segment-for-segment.
	function normalizeDispatchIntervals(raw DispatchIntervalArray) returns DispatchIntervalArray
		var result = DispatchIntervalArray.create()
		let n = raw.count()
		if n == 0 'empty'
			return result
		end 'empty'

		let order = self.dispatchIntervalsSortedByLo(raw)
		var heap = DispatchIndexArray.create()

		// `si` is the next not-yet-activated interval in `lo` order; `sweepPos` the coordinate whose owner we
		// are currently deciding. A segment for `segOwner` is open over `[segStart, sweepPos-1]` while
		// `segOpen`. (`sweepPos`, not `pos` — that name is the Parser's token cursor.)
		var si = 0 as ElementCount
		var sweepPos = (try raw.get(try order.get(0) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: order.get(0) on n>0")) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: raw.get(order[0]) OOB")).lo
		var segOpen = false
		var segStart = sweepPos
		var segOwner = 0 as ElementCount

		while true 'sweep'
			// Activate every interval that has started by `sweepPos` (its `lo <= sweepPos`); they are
			// contiguous in `order`, so this consumes a prefix. All same-`lo` intervals activate together, so
			// the heap minimum is the earliest arm among them regardless of their order within the run.
			while si < n and self.dispatchLoOf(raw, index: try order.get(si) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: order.get OOB at {si}")) <= sweepPos 'activate'
				self.dispatchHeapPush(heap, value: try order.get(si) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: order.get OOB at {si}"))
				si = si + 1
			end 'activate'

			// Drop intervals that ended before `sweepPos` (their `hi < sweepPos`); they no longer cover it.
			// Lazy: only the heap minima are checked, so each interval is discarded once, when it surfaces.
			while heap.count() > 0 and self.dispatchHiOf(raw, index: try heap.get(0) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: heap.get(0) on non-empty")) < sweepPos 'expire'
				_ = self.dispatchHeapPopMin(heap)
			end 'expire'

			if heap.count() == 0 'gap'
				// No interval covers `sweepPos`. A segment can only have closed at an owner change (below), so
				// none is open here; the next covered region begins at the next interval's `lo`.
				if segOpen 'lostSegment'
					panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: a segment was left open crossing an uncovered gap")
				end 'lostSegment'
				if si >= n 'done'
					break
				end 'done'
				sweepPos = self.dispatchLoOf(raw, index: try order.get(si) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: order.get OOB at {si}"))
				continue
			end 'gap'

			let owner = try heap.get(0) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: heap.get(0) on non-empty")
			if not segOpen 'openSegment'
				segOpen = true
				segStart = sweepPos
				segOwner = owner
			end 'openSegment' else if owner != segOwner 'ownerChanged'
				// A new interval activated at `sweepPos` outranks the running owner, which therefore owned only
				// up to `sweepPos - 1` (no underflow: `sweepPos > segStart >= i64.min` since it opened earlier).
				self.pushNormalizedSegment(result, raw: raw, owner: segOwner, lo: segStart, hi: sweepPos - 1)
				segStart = sweepPos
				segOwner = owner
			end 'ownerChanged'

			// The owner holds `sweepPos` until either a new interval starts inside its span (re-decide there)
			// or it ends at its own `hi`.
			let ownerHi = self.dispatchHiOf(raw, index: owner)
			var ownerEnds = true
			if si < n 'hasNextStart'
				let nextLo = self.dispatchLoOf(raw, index: try order.get(si) otherwise panic("normalizeDispatchIntervals: order.get OOB at {si}"))
				if nextLo <= ownerHi 'startsInsideOwner'
					sweepPos = nextLo
					ownerEnds = false
				end 'startsInsideOwner'
			end 'hasNextStart'

			if ownerEnds 'closeSegment'
				self.pushNormalizedSegment(result, raw: raw, owner: segOwner, lo: segStart, hi: ownerHi)
				segOpen = false
				if ownerHi == I64MaxBound 'atCeiling'
					// Nothing lies past i64.max, and no interval's `lo` can either, so the sweep is done —
					// and `ownerHi + 1` would overflow, so it is never computed.
					break
				end 'atCeiling'
				sweepPos = ownerHi + 1
			end 'closeSegment'
		end 'sweep'

		return result
	end 'normalizeDispatchIntervals'

	// `raw[index].lo` / `raw[index].hi` — the sweep reads these coordinates far more often than it needs
	// the whole interval, and going through one accessor keeps the `try … otherwise panic` off the sweep.
	function dispatchLoOf(raw DispatchIntervalArray, index ElementCount) returns ParsedInt
		return (try raw.get(index) otherwise panic("dispatchLoOf: raw.get OOB at {index}")).lo
	end 'dispatchLoOf'

	function dispatchHiOf(raw DispatchIntervalArray, index ElementCount) returns ParsedInt
		return (try raw.get(index) otherwise panic("dispatchHiOf: raw.get OOB at {index}")).hi
	end 'dispatchHiOf'

	// Append the segment `[lo, hi]` owned by raw interval `owner`, carrying that arm's body and edge args.
	// A single owner can yield several segments (an interior higher-priority arm splits it), each sharing
	// the same `edgeArgs` — the same reuse the old piece-splitter made, so the retained count is unchanged.
	function pushNormalizedSegment(result DispatchIntervalArray, raw DispatchIntervalArray, owner ElementCount, lo ParsedInt, hi ParsedInt)
		let src = try raw.get(owner) otherwise panic("pushNormalizedSegment: raw.get OOB at owner {owner}")
		result.push(DispatchInterval.create(lo, hi: hi, targetBlockId: src.targetBlockId, edgeArgs: src.edgeArgs))
	end 'pushNormalizedSegment'

	// The interval positions `[0, n)` sorted ascending by `raw[i].lo`. A stable merge sort — O(n log n) in
	// the arm count, the property this whole path exists to hold — over an index permutation, so the raw
	// intervals never move. Ties (equal `lo`) keep ascending index order; the sweep does not depend on that
	// (it activates a whole equal-`lo` run before picking an owner), but it costs nothing and is tidy.
	function dispatchIntervalsSortedByLo(raw DispatchIntervalArray) returns DispatchIndexArray
		let n = raw.count()
		var order = DispatchIndexArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto n 'seed'
			order.push(i)
		end 'seed'

		var scratch = DispatchIndexArray.create()
		scratch.growFilled(n, value: 0 as ElementCount)
		self.mergeSortDispatchIndices(raw, order: order, scratch: scratch, lo: 0, hi: n)
		return order
	end 'dispatchIntervalsSortedByLo'

	// Merge-sort `order[lo, hi)` by `raw[·].lo`, using `scratch` as the merge buffer. Recursion depth is
	// log n and each level touches each slot once, so O(n log n).
	function mergeSortDispatchIndices(raw DispatchIntervalArray, order DispatchIndexArray, scratch DispatchIndexArray, lo ElementCount, hi ElementCount)
		if hi - lo <= 1 'base'
			return
		end 'base'

		let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
		self.mergeSortDispatchIndices(raw, order: order, scratch: scratch, lo: lo, hi: mid)
		self.mergeSortDispatchIndices(raw, order: order, scratch: scratch, lo: mid, hi: hi)

		var i = lo
		var j = mid
		var k = lo
		while i < mid and j < hi 'merge'
			let leftIdx = try order.get(i) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: order.get OOB at {i}")
			let rightIdx = try order.get(j) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: order.get OOB at {j}")
			// `<=` takes the left (lower-index) element on a tie, which is what makes the sort stable.
			if self.dispatchLoOf(raw, index: leftIdx) <= self.dispatchLoOf(raw, index: rightIdx) 'takeLeft'
				try scratch.set(k, value: leftIdx) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: scratch.set OOB at {k}")
				i = i + 1
			end 'takeLeft' else 'takeRight'
				try scratch.set(k, value: rightIdx) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: scratch.set OOB at {k}")
				j = j + 1
			end 'takeRight'
			k = k + 1
		end 'merge'

		while i < mid 'drainLeft'
			try scratch.set(k, value: try order.get(i) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: order.get OOB at {i}")) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: scratch.set OOB at {k}")
			i = i + 1
			k = k + 1
		end 'drainLeft'
		while j < hi 'drainRight'
			try scratch.set(k, value: try order.get(j) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: order.get OOB at {j}")) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: scratch.set OOB at {k}")
			j = j + 1
			k = k + 1
		end 'drainRight'

		for t in lo upto hi 'copyBack'
			try order.set(t, value: try scratch.get(t) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: scratch.get OOB at {t}")) otherwise panic("mergeSortDispatchIndices: order.set OOB at {t}")
		end 'copyBack'
	end 'mergeSortDispatchIndices'

	// Push `value` (an interval index, and thereby its arm priority) onto the min-heap, sifting it up until
	// its parent is no larger. O(log heap).
	function dispatchHeapPush(heap DispatchIndexArray, value ElementCount)
		heap.push(value)
		var child = heap.count() - 1
		while child > 0 'siftUp'
			let parent = (child - 1) / 2
			let parentVal = try heap.get(parent) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPush: heap.get OOB at parent {parent}")
			let childVal = try heap.get(child) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPush: heap.get OOB at child {child}")
			if parentVal <= childVal 'ordered'
				break
			end 'ordered'
			try heap.set(parent, value: childVal) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPush: heap.set OOB at parent {parent}")
			try heap.set(child, value: parentVal) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPush: heap.set OOB at child {child}")
			child = parent
		end 'siftUp'
	end 'dispatchHeapPush'

	// Remove and return the smallest interval index (earliest arm) on the heap, re-heaping the tail. O(log
	// heap). The caller guarantees the heap is non-empty.
	function dispatchHeapPopMin(heap DispatchIndexArray) returns ElementCount
		let top = try heap.get(0) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.get(0) on empty heap")
		let tail = try heap.pop() otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: pop on empty heap")
		let count = heap.count()
		if count == 0 'wasLast'
			return top
		end 'wasLast'

		try heap.set(0, value: tail) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.set(0) after refill")
		var parent = 0 as ElementCount
		while true 'siftDown'
			let left = parent * 2 + 1
			if left >= count 'leaf'
				break
			end 'leaf'
			var smaller = left
			let right = left + 1
			if right < count 'hasRight'
				let leftVal = try heap.get(left) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.get OOB at left {left}")
				let rightVal = try heap.get(right) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.get OOB at right {right}")
				if rightVal < leftVal 'rightSmaller'
					smaller = right
				end 'rightSmaller'
			end 'hasRight'
			let parentVal = try heap.get(parent) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.get OOB at parent {parent}")
			let smallerVal = try heap.get(smaller) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.get OOB at smaller {smaller}")
			if parentVal <= smallerVal 'ordered'
				break
			end 'ordered'
			try heap.set(parent, value: smallerVal) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.set OOB at parent {parent}")
			try heap.set(smaller, value: parentVal) otherwise panic("dispatchHeapPopMin: heap.set OOB at smaller {smaller}")
			parent = smaller
		end 'siftDown'
		return top
	end 'dispatchHeapPopMin'

	// ⭐ **EMIT A `match`'s DISPATCH from its normalized interval plan** — the ONE place the strategy is
	// chosen. Below `MinIntervalsForDispatch` intervals it is a linear compare chain; at or above it a
	// balanced binary search over the sorted disjoint intervals. Every compare reads `subject` (loaded
	// once, in `entry`, and left in a register across the whole tree); a value in NO interval reaches
	// `missTarget` (the `default` body, or a `dead` block for an exhaustive enum). NO new IR op — internal
	// nodes compare `subject < pivot` and leaves test their interval, all with the same `compare`/
	// `condBranch` the chain used, so the backend and every later pass are untouched (a jump table is Slice 2).
	function emitMatchDispatch(func IrFunction, entryId BlockId, subject ValueId, intervals DispatchIntervalArray, missTarget BlockId, missArgs ValueIdArray, opTok Token) throws ParseError
		var entry = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: entryId) otherwise panic("emitMatchDispatch: the dispatch entry block {entryId} is not in its own function")
		try self.emitDispatchRange(func, from: 0, to: intervals.count(), block: entry, subject: subject, intervals: intervals, missTarget: missTarget, missArgs: missArgs, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitMatchDispatch'

	// The recursion. `[from, to)` is a contiguous slice of the sorted disjoint intervals. A short slice is
	// a chain; a long one splits on the median interval's lower bound — every interval below the pivot lies
	// in the left half and every one at or above it in the right (sorted + disjoint), so both halves are
	// non-empty (the count is at least `MinIntervalsForDispatch`) and the recursion terminates.
	function emitDispatchRange(func IrFunction, from ElementCount, to ElementCount, block IrBlock, subject ValueId, intervals DispatchIntervalArray, missTarget BlockId, missArgs ValueIdArray, opTok Token) throws ParseError
		let count = to - from
		if count < MinIntervalsForDispatch 'chain'
			try self.emitDispatchChain(func, from: from, to: to, block: block, subject: subject, intervals: intervals, missTarget: missTarget, missArgs: missArgs, opTok: opTok)
			return
		end 'chain'

		// A DENSE run — the covered value span is tight enough (see the three density caps) — becomes an
		// O(1) indexed jump TABLE rather than a compare tree. Tried at EVERY level, so a dense sub-range at a
		// binary-search leaf tabulates itself too (the recursion below only reaches sparse regions).
		let table = self.planDispatchTable(from, to: to, intervals: intervals)
		if table.isTable 'dense'
			try self.emitDispatchTable(from, to: to, block: block, subject: subject, intervals: intervals, plan: table, missTarget: missTarget, missArgs: missArgs, opTok: opTok)
			return
		end 'dense'

		let mid = from + count / 2
		let pivot = (try intervals.get(mid) otherwise panic("emitDispatchRange: intervals.get OOB at pivot {mid}")).lo
		self.currentBlock = block
		let cond = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject, literal: pivot, op: MaxonCmpOp.lt, opTok: opTok)
		// `var` because each half is emitted INTO its block by the recursive call, which mutates it — the
		// same mutating-parameter sensitivity `dispatchEntry` carries.
		var lowerBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matchlo", line: opTok.line)
		var upperBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matchhi", line: opTok.line)
		// The internal branch routes between two sub-TESTS, not to an arm body — neither target carries a
		// carried-var phi (both are dominated by `entry`, where the pre-match values still hold), so the
		// branch records no edge args.
		self.emitCondBranch(block, cond: cond, thenId: lowerBlock.id, elseId: upperBlock.id, ifTok: opTok)
		try self.emitDispatchRange(func, from: from, to: mid, block: lowerBlock, subject: subject, intervals: intervals, missTarget: missTarget, missArgs: missArgs, opTok: opTok)
		try self.emitDispatchRange(func, from: mid, to: to, block: upperBlock, subject: subject, intervals: intervals, missTarget: missTarget, missArgs: missArgs, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitDispatchRange'

	// ⭐ **DECIDE WHETHER A DENSE RUN IS WORTH A JUMP TABLE**, reporting the bias (its minimum) and the slot
	// count (the span). The bootstrap's `TryTableBias` verbatim (`SwitchDispatch.cs`): reject on the covered
	// WIDTH exceeding the span cap, on the span outrunning the per-interval slot budget, then on the density
	// falling below 2/5. `intervals` is sorted disjoint, so `intervals[from].lo` is the minimum and
	// `intervals[to-1].hi` the maximum. Everything is measured MODULO 2^64 (a span crossing zero, or one
	// reaching an open bound, is a real span, not an overflow), so the width is compared without ever
	// forming a difference that would overflow `i64` — see `spanExceedsCap`.
	function planDispatchTable(from ElementCount, to ElementCount, intervals DispatchIntervalArray) returns DispatchTablePlan
		let bias = (try intervals.get(from) otherwise panic("planDispatchTable: intervals.get OOB at from {from}")).lo
		let maxHi = (try intervals.get(to - 1) otherwise panic("planDispatchTable: intervals.get OOB at to-1 {to - 1}")).hi

		// Reject on the covered WIDTH exceeding the cap WITHOUT forming `maxHi - bias`, which overflows i64
		// when the span crosses far into both halves (e.g. `i64.min`..`i64.max`). `maxHi >= bias` always
		// (sorted, disjoint), so the modular width is a mathematical value in `[0, 2^64)`; it is `< cap` iff
		// `maxHi` sits within the `cap`-wide window above `bias`, which `spanWithinCap` tests overflow-safely.
		if not spanWithinCap(bias, maxHi: maxHi) 'tooWide'
			return DispatchTablePlan.notTable()
		end 'tooWide'

		// Both operands are now within `MaxTableSpanSlots` of each other, so `maxHi - bias` fits i64.
		let span = maxHi - bias + 1

		// The table must earn its slots against the compares it removes, not merely against the values it
		// covers (`MaxTableSlotsPerInterval`). `to - from` is the interval count; the product fits i64
		// (span <= 4096, the count is bounded by the arm count).
		if span > ((to - from) as ParsedInt) * MaxTableSlotsPerInterval 'sparse'
			return DispatchTablePlan.notTable()
		end 'sparse'

		var coveredSlots = 0 as ParsedInt
		for i in from upto to 'each'
			let interval = try intervals.get(i) otherwise panic("planDispatchTable: intervals.get OOB at {i}")
			coveredSlots = coveredSlots + (interval.hi - interval.lo + 1)
		end 'each'

		// Dense iff covered/span >= 2/5, kept as a cross-multiply so no division rounds. Both products fit
		// i64 (each factor <= 4096 * 5).
		if coveredSlots * MinTableDensityDenominator < span * MinTableDensityNumerator 'sparse'
			return DispatchTablePlan.notTable()
		end 'sparse'

		return DispatchTablePlan.tableWith(bias, span: span)
	end 'planDispatchTable'

	// Is the modular width `maxHi - bias` (unsigned) strictly below `MaxTableSpanSlots`? Answered WITHOUT
	// forming the difference, which overflows i64 for a span straddling both halves. `maxHi >= bias`
	// (signed) is the caller's invariant, so the width is `maxHi - bias` as a true mathematical integer in
	// `[0, 2^64)`. It is below the cap iff `maxHi` is at most `bias + (cap - 1)`:
	//   • when `bias + (cap - 1)` does NOT overflow i64 (`bias <= i64.max - (cap - 1)`), that is a plain
	//     signed compare;
	//   • when it WOULD overflow, `bias` is within `cap` of `i64.max`, so `maxHi` (<= i64.max) is
	//     necessarily within the window — accept.
	function spanWithinCap(bias ParsedInt, maxHi ParsedInt) returns bool
		let lastSlot = MaxTableSpanSlots - 1
		if bias > I64MaxBound - lastSlot 'windowReachesTop'
			return true
		end 'windowReachesTop'
		return maxHi <= bias + lastSlot
	end 'spanWithinCap'

	// ⭐ **EMIT A DENSE RUN AS A JUMP TABLE.** Bias the subject to a zero-based index (`subject − min`, a
	// modular subtract, so an out-of-range subject wraps to a large unsigned value the target op's UNSIGNED
	// bounds check sends to the default), build the dense block-id table (each interval fills its several
	// slots; holes are the default), record the phi edges for the distinct arm bodies and the default, then
	// emit the `switch` terminator. The bias subtract is elided when the minimum is 0 (a zero-based enum or
	// scalar match), so `match Color` over `red=0…` biases nothing.
	function emitDispatchTable(from ElementCount, to ElementCount, block IrBlock, subject ValueId, intervals DispatchIntervalArray, plan DispatchTablePlan, missTarget BlockId, missArgs ValueIdArray, opTok Token) throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block

		var index = subject
		if plan.bias != 0 'bias'
			index = self.emitBiasIndex(subject, bias: plan.bias, opTok: opTok)
		end 'bias'

		// Every slot in `[min, max]` needs a target; the ones no interval covers are the default. A range
		// arm and an `or`-list simply fill several slots each — the whole reason a single range arm no longer
		// forces the match onto a compare tree. `growFilled` from an empty array fills every one of `span`
		// slots with the default.
		var targets = BlockIdArray.create()
		targets.growFilled(plan.span as ElementCount, value: missTarget)
		for i in from upto to 'eachInterval'
			let interval = try intervals.get(i) otherwise panic("emitDispatchTable: intervals.get OOB at {i}")
			// Walk SLOT indices (`lo - bias` … `hi - bias`), never VALUES: a value-driven loop over an
			// interval ending at `i64.max` would never terminate. Both offsets are in `[0, span)` (the
			// interval lies within the covered span), so the subtraction cannot overflow.
			let firstSlot = (interval.lo - plan.bias) as ElementCount
			let lastSlot = (interval.hi - plan.bias) as ElementCount
			for slot in firstSlot upto (lastSlot + 1) 'fill'
				try targets.set(slot, value: interval.targetBlockId) otherwise panic("emitDispatchTable: targets.set OOB at slot {slot} (span {plan.span})")
			end 'fill'
		end 'eachInterval'

		// The phi args each dispatch edge carries. An arm body is a phi target only when it is a fall-through
		// target (`setupFallthroughTargetPhis` stamped its intervals' `edgeArgs`); the default carries
		// `missArgs`. `recordBranchEdge` is a no-op for an empty arg list, so a phi-less table costs nothing
		// here. Recorded ONCE per distinct target — an or-arm or a split range reaches one body through
		// several intervals, all carrying the SAME snapshot, so a second edge to it would double-apply the
		// copy at SSA destruction.
		self.recordBranchEdge(block, targetId: missTarget, argIds: missArgs)
		var seen = BlockIdSet.create()
		for i in from upto to 'eachEdge'
			let interval = try intervals.get(i) otherwise panic("emitDispatchTable: intervals.get OOB at {i}")
			if not seen.contains(interval.targetBlockId) 'firstEdge'
				seen.insert(interval.targetBlockId)
				self.recordBranchEdge(block, targetId: interval.targetBlockId, argIds: interval.edgeArgs)
			end 'firstEdge'
		end 'eachEdge'

		self.artifact.emitTerminator(block, op: MaxonOp.switch(index, denseTargetBlockIds: targets, defaultBlockId: missTarget), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
	end 'emitDispatchTable'

	// The zero-based table index `subject − bias`, emitted as a RAW `binOp(sub)` that MINTS an `integer`
	// result and bypasses `emitBinOp`'s operand type check — exactly as `emitCompareAgainstLiteral` emits a raw
	// `compare`. The bypass is the point: a SCALAR enum's dispatch subject is a `named(<Enum>)` value, and
	// arithmetic on an enum is a user-level type error — but this is not user arithmetic, it is the compiler
	// REINTERPRETING the tag bits as the integer index the jump table needs. The subtraction is MODULAR
	// (i64 wraparound), so a subject outside the covered span wraps to a large unsigned value the target op's
	// UNSIGNED bounds check routes to the default. The Std tier collapses the `named` operand to i64
	// (`stdTypeOf`), so the lowered `sub` is an ordinary i64 subtract.
	function emitBiasIndex(subject ValueId, bias ParsedInt, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		let biasLit = self.emitLiteral(bias, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.binOp(result, lhs: subject, rhs: biasLit, op: MaxonBinOp.sub), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		return result
	end 'emitBiasIndex'

	// A leaf region: test each interval in turn, branching to its arm body on a hit and to the next test
	// (or `missTarget` after the last) on a miss. `recordBranchEdge` is a no-op for an empty arg list, so
	// the internal chain edges (no phis) and a phi-less body/miss cost nothing.
	function emitDispatchChain(func IrFunction, from ElementCount, to ElementCount, block IrBlock, subject ValueId, intervals DispatchIntervalArray, missTarget BlockId, missArgs ValueIdArray, opTok Token) throws ParseError
		// A default-only planned match (`match x 'c' default gives … end`) collects NO intervals — every
		// value is a miss — so the entry unconditionally branches to the default, with no compare. The
		// tree recursion never reaches here empty (it splits only at count >= 4 into two non-empty halves),
		// so this is the top-level empty plan alone, but handling it here keeps the terminator obligation in
		// one place.
		if from == to 'noIntervals'
			self.recordBranchEdge(block, targetId: missTarget, argIds: missArgs)
			self.emitBranch(block, targetId: missTarget)
			return
		end 'noIntervals'

		var current = block
		for i in from upto to 'each'
			let interval = try intervals.get(i) otherwise panic("emitDispatchChain: intervals.get OOB at {i}")
			self.currentBlock = current
			let cond = try self.emitIntervalHitTest(subject, lo: interval.lo, hi: interval.hi, opTok: opTok)

			let isLast = i == to - 1
			var elseTarget = missTarget
			var elseArgs = missArgs
			// ⚠ **THE CURSOR ADVANCES BY ID, NOT BY A SECOND NAME FOR `current`.** `var nextBlock = current`
			// would MOVE the loop-carried block out of the loop (E2015); a `BlockId` is a scalar and carries
			// no ownership, so the fetch below is what re-seats the cursor — and on the last test it re-seats
			// it to the block it already names, which is what the chain's final `else` edge wants.
			var nextId = current.id
			if not isLast 'moreTests'
				nextId = self.newBlock(func, label: b"matchnext", line: opTok.line).id
				elseTarget = nextId
				elseArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
			end 'moreTests'

			self.recordBranchEdge(current, targetId: interval.targetBlockId, argIds: interval.edgeArgs)
			self.recordBranchEdge(current, targetId: elseTarget, argIds: elseArgs)
			self.emitCondBranch(current, cond: cond, thenId: interval.targetBlockId, elseId: elseTarget, ifTok: opTok)
			current = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: nextId) otherwise panic("emitDispatchChain: the chain's next test block {nextId} is not in its own function")
		end 'each'
	end 'emitDispatchChain'

	// The boolean "does `subject` fall in `[lo, hi]`?": an `==` for a point, a two-sided `>=`/`<=` `and`
	// for a range, one open compare when a bound is `i64.min`/`i64.max` (the redundant side elided, exactly
	// as `emitOpenLowerRange`/`emitClosedRange` treat an open bound), and a constant `true` for a
	// min-to-max interval that covers everything (a sole all-covering arm — after clipping it can only be
	// the whole plan, since it would subsume any later arm).
	function emitIntervalHitTest(subject ValueId, lo ParsedInt, hi ParsedInt, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if lo == hi 'point'
			return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject, literal: lo, op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, opTok: opTok)
		end 'point'

		let needLo = lo > I64MinBound
		let needHi = hi < I64MaxBound
		if needLo and needHi 'closed'
			let loCmp = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject, literal: lo, op: MaxonCmpOp.ge, opTok: opTok)
			let hiCmp = self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject, literal: hi, op: MaxonCmpOp.le, opTok: opTok)
			return try self.emitBinOp(loCmp, rhs: hiCmp, op: MaxonBinOp.bitAnd, opTok: opTok)
		end 'closed'
		if needLo 'openUpper'
			return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject, literal: lo, op: MaxonCmpOp.ge, opTok: opTok)
		end 'openUpper'
		if needHi 'openLower'
			return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(subject, literal: hi, op: MaxonCmpOp.le, opTok: opTok)
		end 'openLower'

		return self.emitLiteral(BoolTrueValue, valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
	end 'emitIntervalHitTest'

	// Mint a phi (block-arg) on `block` and return its fresh ValueId. The ONE mint site for
	// every merge phi the parser makes — if-continuation, loop header, loop exit, and (new
	// at P1.0d.2) the short-circuit merge — so a phi's Maxon-tier type is recorded in
	// lockstep with its id at all four.
	//
	// The `argType` passed here is a PLACEHOLDER, overwritten before anything reads it:
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.retypeBlockArgs` is the authority and sets every phi's Std type from the tag
	// column (it explains why the parser is the wrong tier to decide it). A phi's Maxon-tier type is
	// the `tag` recorded below, and that is the only type this function knows.
	//
	// ⚠ NOTHING OVERWROTE IT UNTIL P1.0d.4, AND THAT WAS A COMPILER CRASH: a hardcoded `i64` was
	// harmless only while every value lived in a GPR, and the day `argType` chose a phi's REGISTER
	// FILE, a float phi was coloured into a GPR and the SSA-destruction copy panicked the x64
	// emitter. See `float-phi-*` in specs-shv2/float-type.md.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY PHI IS MINTED WITH ITS RANGED-ALIAS CLAIM WITHHELD (G14).** The `nameId` above comes from ONE
	// incoming edge, so it is the type the merge was DECLARED with and not a statement about the values that
	// will reach it — see `phiAliasUnprovenMarks` for the three wrong answers reading it as a proof produced.
	// Withholding it HERE rather than at each of the eight call sites is what makes the safe answer the
	// DEFAULT: a future merge inherits it, and only a site holding the complete incoming set can lift it
	// (`recordMergeAliasProof`).
	function mintPhi(block IrBlock, tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ValueId
		let phiId = self.mintValue(tag, nameId: nameId)
		try self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks.set(phiId, value: true) otherwise panic("mintPhi: v{phiId} has no merge-alias slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")
		block.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(phiId, argType: StdType.i64))
		return phiId
	end 'mintPhi'

	// ⭐⭐ **LIFT A MERGE'S WITHHELD ALIAS CLAIM — the ONE place `mintPhi`'s blanket withholding is undone,
	// and it is undone only against the COMPLETE incoming set.** A merge denotes alias `A` exactly when every
	// value that can reach it already denoted a range inside `A`; each such value carries its own proof by the
	// induction `rangeCoversDenotedRange`'s header states, so the meet over the edges is the honest claim and
	// the elision A4f built stays legitimate wherever it was legitimate before.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TEST IS WHETHER THE EDGES ARE ALL KNOWN AT THE MINT, AND IT IS THE ONLY TEST** — four merges
	// pass it: a `try … otherwise <expr>` continuation, a `match`/ternary RESULT, an `if` CONTINUATION's
	// carried binding (`mergeAtContinuation`) and a `match`'s carried binding (`finalizeMatchMerge`'s
	// `setBindings`). What keeps the claim withheld is a merge that CANNOT answer, never one whose edges
	// merely came from an assignment: an ASSIGNMENT is a door on no tier, which is precisely why every edge
	// is asked to prove itself rather than trusted for its shape.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT STILL CANNOT ASK IS A BACK EDGE.** A loop HEADER (`mintPhiForBinding` from the while/for
	// header, the `for` index, the step phi) is minted before its back edge is parsed, and a loop EXIT phi
	// merges that header phi — so its claim is withheld transitively, which is the same answer reached for
	// free rather than a second rule. ⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH READ "ONLY TWO MERGES MAY ASK" AND LUMPED ALL FOUR
	// `mintPhiForBinding` SITES TOGETHER, WHICH COST TWO WRONG ANSWERS** (G14 review): an `if`-merged `Num`
	// var returned through a `returns Num` grew a cascade two entry guards had already proved, and
	// `t as Num` on it lost the E3010 the bootstrap oracle still reports. A merge that CAN answer and is
	// not asked is not the safe direction — it is a second defect pointing the other way.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS IS WHY `try/otherwise` LOOKED IMMUNE AND IS NOT.** `otherwise 0` keeps its elision here — the
	// ok edge is the callee's own guarded `return` and the fallback is a literal `requireOtherwiseInRangedReturn`
	// already refused out of range — which is exactly the shape the defect was probed with. MEASURED: swap the
	// literal for a variable (`otherwise w`, `w == 5000`) and the fallback is NOT checked (that function's
	// header says so outright), so the merge claimed `Num` over `5000` and `return`ed it unguarded. One rule
	// covers both: the literal edge proves, the variable edge does not.
	//
	// ⚠ It runs AFTER the edge values are final, not at the mint, so an ownership promotion
	// (`promoteBorrowedMergeEdge`) cannot leave it judging a value the edge no longer carries.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE PHI'S OWN TYPE COLUMNS — `declaredAliasOfValue`, WHICH IS TAG-GATED, AND THE GATE IS NOT
	// OPTIONAL.** A name id is only a real interner entry when the tag is `named`; a merge of any other class
	// carries `UnnamedTypeId`, and handing that to `declaredAliasOfNameId` reads whatever name the interner
	// gave id 0 — which is the FIRST name the file mentioned, since the table starts empty. Both existing
	// readers (`declaredAliasOfValue`, `declaredAliasOfType`) gate on the tag first, so this asks through one
	// of them rather than spelling a third copy of the gate — and reading the PHI rather than the values it
	// was named from is what stops the claim being judged against a type the phi does not actually carry.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE GATE IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT, AND THAT COST A WRONG ANSWER (BATCH28).** The filler id
	// is INDISTINGUISHABLE from the first real name, so a `named`-tagged value carrying it resolves to a
	// stranger rather than to nothing — there is no guard `declaredAliasOfNameId` could add. The obligation
	// is therefore on every MINT: a value tagged `named` must carry a real name id. See `emitUnaryOp`, which
	// did not, and what that printed.
	//
	// ⚠ `declaredAliasOfValue`, NOT `provenAliasOfValue`: this is deciding whether to LICENSE the declared
	// claim, so it must read the claim. The proof-aware reader answers `undeclared` for every phi by
	// construction at this point, which would make the lift unreachable.
	function recordMergeAliasProof(phiId ValueId, incoming ValueIdArray)
		match self.declaredAliasOfValue(phiId) 'dest'
			// The merge names no ranged alias, so there is no claim to lift and nothing downstream reads one.
			undeclared then break 'dest'
			declared(alias) then self.recordMergeAliasProofAgainst(phiId, alias: alias, incoming: incoming)
		end 'dest'
	end 'recordMergeAliasProof'

	// The half above that has the destination range in hand: resolved ONCE and reused across the edges,
	// because `declaredAliasOfNameId` CONSTRUCTS its `DeclaredAlias` (a probe per edge would be a probe per arm).
	function recordMergeAliasProofAgainst(phiId ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias, incoming ValueIdArray)
		for value in incoming 'eachEdge'
			if not self.edgeProvesAlias(value, alias: alias) 'edgeProvesNothing'
				return
			end 'edgeProvesNothing'
		end 'eachEdge'

		try self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks.set(phiId, value: false) otherwise panic("recordMergeAliasProofAgainst: v{phiId} has no merge-alias slot — a phi is minted through mintPhi, which sets it")
	end 'recordMergeAliasProofAgainst'

	// ⭐ **DOES ONE INCOMING EDGE PROVABLY LIE INSIDE `alias`? — THE SAME PAIR OF TESTS A DOOR APPLIES,
	// AND THE PAIR IS SPLIT ACROSS TWO TIERS RATHER THAN HELD BY ONE FUNCTION.** A door reaches "no guard"
	// two ways and only two: the value already denotes a range the destination covers — asked HERE, at the
	// parse, by `recordRangedTargetSite`/`returnGuardIsDead` through the shared `rangeCoversDenotedRange` —
	// or it is a compile-time constant of the destination's own domain that lands inside it, which
	// `InsertRangeChecks.needsRuntimeGuard` asks of the recorded site. A merge is not a door, but *"is this
	// value in range"* is the door's question, so it is answered out of the same two rules rather than a
	// third way; the containment arithmetic itself (`rangeCoversDenotedRange`, `literalInRange`) is
	// `TypeRules`', spelled once for every asker.
	//
	// ⚠ The CONSTANT half reads the PARSER's const columns while `needsRuntimeGuard` reads the Std pass's
	// `intConsts`/`floatConsts`, and the two need not agree. Neither disagreement is a wrong answer, and the
	// direction is what says so: the parser knowing a constant the pass does not still proves the value is
	// in range (a folded literal is one at run time whatever a later tier knows), and the pass knowing one
	// the parser does not merely leaves the claim withheld — a guard the pass then elides itself.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CONSTANT ARM IS WHAT KEEPS `try f() otherwise 0` ELIDING.** A folded literal denotes NO alias —
	// `rangeCoversDenotedRange`'s header calls that out as the direction that keeps the guard — so the first
	// test alone would withhold the claim from the corpus's most common fallback and re-emit a cascade over a
	// value that cannot fail it, which is the defect A4f exists to delete.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DOMAIN IS PART OF THE TEST, not decoration** — a float `const`'s payload is an IEEE-754 bit
	// pattern and an integer's is a number, so reading one as the other would compare two things that were
	// never the same kind of number. Same split, same reason, as `needsRuntimeGuard`'s two const maps.
	function edgeProvesAlias(value ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if rangeCoversDenotedRange(alias, source: self.valueDenotedAlias(value)) 'denotesACoveredRange'
			return true
		end 'denotesACoveredRange'

		if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'floatAlias'
			return self.floatConstIsKnown(value) and literalInRange(self.floatConstBitsOf(value), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: true)
		end 'floatAlias'

		return self.constIsKnown(value) and literalInRange(self.constOf(value), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: false)
	end 'edgeProvesAlias'

	// The phi a MERGED BINDING carries: its type is the binding's declared one, which the
	// binding already holds as a `MaxonType`, so decomposing it constructs nothing.
	//
	// ⭐ **PROPAGATE THE PROMISE MARK ACROSS THE MERGE (P1.5-B2 #88, Finding 1).** `valueOwnsGreenThread` is a
	// per-SSA-value mark that a merge phi does not otherwise carry, so a re-armed `var p` (whose value after a
	// loop / branch IS this phi) would lose its promise identity — its scope-exit drop would misroute to
	// `__mm_decref` on a GT pointer, and `await p` on the phi would be rejected as "not a promise". A promise
	// var's phi merges promises on EVERY reaching edge (each value it holds is a spawn handle), so the mark is
	// read off the value flowing IN here (`binding.boundValue`) and stamped on the phi. The ONE choke point
	// for loop-header, loop-exit, if-continuation and match phis, so all four inherit it at once.
	function mintPhiForBinding(block IrBlock, binding VarInfo) returns ValueId
		let phiId = self.mintPhi(block, tag: maxonTypeTag(binding.varType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(binding.varType))
		if self.valueOwnsGreenThread(binding.boundValue) 'promisePhi'
			self.markValuePromise(phiId)
		end 'promisePhi'

		// ⭐⭐ **A MERGED BINDING STILL OWNS ITS HEAP, AND THE PHI IS WHERE THAT FACT WAS DROPPED.** A phi is
		// minted through `mintPhi`, which seeds `valueOwnsHeap` FALSE like every fresh value — but this phi is
		// not a fresh value, it is the same allocation the binding already owned, renamed at a join. Left false,
		// the merged name reads as BORROWED to every door that asks, and the next thing done with it mints a
		// reference nothing owns.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **MEASURED AS EXIT 101 IN THE COMPILER'S OWN SELF-COMPILE.** `emitDispatchChain`'s
		// `var nextBlock = current` / `if not isLast  nextBlock = self.newBlock(…)  end` / `current = nextBlock`
		// leaked one `IrBlock` — eight allocations — per `matchnext` block, so every `match` of 2 or 3 intervals
		// (below `MinIntervalsForDispatch`, hence a compare chain) leaked and every larger one did not (a jump
		// table mints no chain block). The rebind door read `valueIsOwnedHeap(phi)` as false, so it did BOTH
		// halves of two different answers: `poisonMoveSource` marked the source moved-from (a MOVE, so nothing
		// drops it) and `promoteBorrowed` then emitted an `__mm_retain` (a CO-OWN, so something must). One
		// reference, no owner. The `if` alone does not leak and the assignment alone does not leak; it takes the
		// join between them, which is why nothing smaller than a self-compile found it.
		//
		// ⚠ The bit is read off the incoming the mint can see — `binding.boundValue`, the path parsed last —
		// and `pushMergedEdgeValue` checks every OTHER incoming against it, because a phi that owns on one edge
		// and borrows on another is an ownership question with no answer, not a mark to join.
		if self.valueIsOwnedHeap(binding.boundValue) 'ownedBinding'
			self.markValueOwnsHeap(phiId, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole if self.valueIsSoleHeapOwner(binding.boundValue) else OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
		end 'ownedBinding'

		// ⭐⭐ **W117's MARK CROSSES A BINDING MERGE (review), and this half alone is not enough — see
		// `pushMergedEdgeValue`.** The value flowing IN here is only ONE of the phi's incomings (the path parsed
		// last); the others arrive as edge args, which is why the twin below carries from each of them. Both are
		// needed and neither is redundant: this one covers a phi whose edges are pushed BEFORE it exists (the
		// loop join), the other covers a phi minted before its edges are known (the `if` continuation).
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: binding.boundValue, to: phiId)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL BINDING MERGES AS TWO PHIS, BECAUSE IT IS TWO VALUES
		// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** The witness half is not derivable at the merge — two arms may carry
		// two DIFFERENT conformers, and which one arrives is exactly what the branch decided — so it has to be
		// carried on the edges like any other loop- or branch-varying value.
		//
		// ⚠ **THIS IS THE ONE THING THE CORPUS'S MERGE CASE CANNOT TELL YOU IS MISSING.**
		// `cross-block-method-receiver`'s `interface-method-statement-after-if-merge` branches on a CONSTANT
		// and hands the SAME implementor to both parameters, so an implementation that carried only the value
		// half and re-used the receiver's original witness still prints the right answer. The shv2-authored
		// `interface-dispatch/dispatch-discriminating-merge` is the case that discriminates: two implementors
		// and a runtime-varying condition, asserting the branch-NOT-taken answer.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE WITNESS PHI IS MINTED IMMEDIATELY AFTER ITS DATA PHI, and every edge pushes the pair in the
		// same order (`pushMergedEdgeValue`).** Block-args and edge args are POSITIONAL, so that ordering IS
		// the pairing; the two are written as one mint and one push so a site cannot honour half of it.
		if bindingCarriesWitness(binding) 'existentialBinding'
			self.pairInterfaceWitness(phiId, witness: self.mintPhi(block, tag: ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId))
		end 'existentialBinding'

		return phiId
	end 'mintPhiForBinding'

	// ⭐ **PUSH WHAT ONE BINDING CONTRIBUTES TO AN EDGE — the twin of `mintPhiForBinding`
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** Every merge in the parser builds its edge args by pushing one value per
	// carried binding, and an existential contributes TWO in the order its phis were minted. Routed through
	// one helper so a merge that grows later cannot push the halves in the other order, or push only one.
	//
	// A non-existential value pushes exactly what it always did, so no existing edge moves.
	// ⭐ **A VALUE-PRODUCING MERGE MAY NOT YIELD AN EXISTENTIAL AT THIS SLICE — REFUSED, NOT MISCOMPILED
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** A BINDING merged across a branch carries both halves of its fat pointer
	// (`mintPhiForBinding` + `pushMergedEdgeValue`); the two merges that produce a VALUE rather than rebinding
	// a name — a `match`/ternary's `gives`, and a `try … otherwise <expr>` — build their result phi and their
	// edge args through their own code, which pushes exactly one value per edge. Letting an existential
	// through there would mint a phi for the value half and NOTHING for the witness, and the first dispatch on
	// the result would read whatever the next block-arg slot happened to hold: a wrong function pointer, with
	// no diagnostic anywhere.
	//
	// It is a positioned refusal rather than a panic because a user can write it, and it names the mechanism
	// rather than the symptom. No case in the canonical corpus needs it — every existential merge there is a
	// `var` rebound across an `if` — so the door stays shut until a case asks for it.
	function refuseExistentialValueMerge(tag ValueTypeTag, what String, tok Token) throws ParseError
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
			return
		end 'notAnExistential'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("{what} is a value held at an interface type — {ExistentialIsFatPointerReason}, and a value-producing merge carries one word per branch. Bind the arms to a `var` of the interface type and merge THAT (a rebound binding carries both halves), or give a concrete type", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'refuseExistentialValueMerge'

	function pushMergedEdgeValue(args ValueIdArray, binding VarInfo, value ValueId)
		args.push(value)
		if bindingCarriesWitness(binding) 'existentialBinding'
			args.push(self.witnessOfValue(value))
		end 'existentialBinding'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INCOMING EDGE HANDS W117's MARK TO THE PHI (review)** — placed here because this is the ONE
		// routine every merge in the parser pushes every carried binding's incoming value through, which is the
		// same census argument its header already makes about the witness half. `binding.boundValue` is the
		// merged name's settled value at each of these call sites (the continuation phi at an `if`/`match`, the
		// exit phi at a loop), so a marked incoming makes the merged name marked.
		//
		// ⛔ **WITHOUT IT A BRANCH OR A LOOP LAUNDERED THE TAINT, MEASURED** (exit 6): `var b = ["q"]` +
		// `for _ in 0 upto 2  b = expose()  end` + `grow(b)` compiled and grew a `let`-declared global, where
		// the same rebind outside a loop is refused. The value-producing merges were already covered
		// (`propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi`); the merges that REBIND A NAME were not.
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: value, to: binding.boundValue)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE OWNERSHIP HALF OF THE SAME TWINNING** (see `mintPhiForBinding`). The mint stamped the phi
		// from ONE incoming; these are the others, and they must answer the same. They do, and the reason is
		// structural rather than lucky: only an ASSIGNED binding is ever carried into a merge, every such
		// binding is a `var`, and a `var`'s managed initializer is promoted to owned at its declaration
		// (`promoteBorrowedBinding`) — so no path can hand a carried binding a borrowed managed value. A
		// disagreement therefore means that promotion has a hole, which would leak or double-free with no
		// diagnostic anywhere; it fails loudly here instead.
		let phiOwns = self.valueIsOwnedHeap(binding.boundValue)
		if phiOwns != self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value) 'edgeDisagrees'
			panic("pushMergedEdgeValue: the merged binding's phi v{binding.boundValue} {"owns" if phiOwns else "does not own"} its heap but the incoming edge value v{value} {"does not" if phiOwns else "does"} — a carried binding is a `var` whose managed initializer is promoted to owned at declaration, so every incoming to its phi must agree; one edge minting a reference the merged name does not own is a leak, and the reverse is a double free")
		end 'edgeDisagrees'

		// A phi is the sole owner only if EVERY path made it one — one co-owning edge is enough to make the
		// merged name share the box, and a stale `sole` there is a destructive-move licence on an allocation
		// somebody else still holds.
		if phiOwns and not self.valueIsSoleHeapOwner(value) 'edgeCoOwns'
			self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(binding.boundValue)
		end 'edgeCoOwns'
	end 'pushMergedEdgeValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS CARRIED BINDING CONTRIBUTE A WITNESS TO A MERGE? — THE ONE FACT BOTH HALVES READ
	// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1 review).** Block-args and edge args are POSITIONAL, so the mint
	// (`mintPhiForBinding`) and the push (`pushMergedEdgeValue`) must agree for EVERY carried binding or
	// every later binding in that merge lands in the wrong slot — silently, with no compile error and no
	// diagnostic at run time.
	//
	// ⚠ **THEY WERE KEYED OFF DIFFERENT FACTS**: the mint read `binding.varType`'s tag and the push read
	// `tagOf(value)`. Those agree today because a carried binding's value always carries the binding's tag —
	// which is an invariant nothing states and nothing checks, and exactly the shape that stops being true
	// the first time a merge carries a value the binding did not produce. Both now read the DECLARED type,
	// which is the fact the phi is minted from and the one every merge site holds (all six pass the binding
	// they are merging).
	function bindingCarriesWitness(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return maxonTypeTag(binding.varType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef
	end 'bindingCarriesWitness'

	// Allocate a fresh ValueId AND record its source-level type, in lockstep. THE only way
	// the parser mints a value: the type columns are dense by ValueId, so an id allocated
	// without a type would shift every later value's type by one — a silent misclassification
	// of every operator that dispatches on type. Splitting the two would make that possible;
	// this makes it impossible.
	//
	// Takes a TAG (plus the name id a `named` type carries), never a `MaxonType`: constructing
	// a union ALLOCATES, and this is the per-VALUE path. `mintValueOfType` is the door for a
	// caller that already holds a `MaxonType` and so constructs nothing new.
	function mintValue(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ValueId
		let id = allocValueId(self.valueIds)
		self.valueTags.push(tag)
		self.valueNameIds.push(nameId)
		// The constant columns move in the same lockstep, seeded UNKNOWN. A value's constant is
		// discovered by the op that DEFINES it, which is emitted after its id is minted — so the
		// slot must exist first, and the emitter overwrites it (`recordConst`).
		self.valueConsts.push(0)
		self.valueConstDomains.push(ConstPayloadDomain.unknown)
		// Owned-heap provenance rides the same lockstep: seeded NOT owned, set true by `trackOwnedTemp`
		// on the one value an interpolation allocates. The slot must exist before that call, exactly as
		// the constant slot must exist before `recordConst`.
		self.valueOwnsHeap.push(false)
		// And its exclusivity, seeded CO-OWNED — the safe direction, because an unclaimed value takes the
		// retain path rather than a destructive move-out (see `OwnedHeapExclusivity`).
		self.valueOwnsHeapAlone.push(false)
		// The promise mark rides the same lockstep, seeded NOT-a-promise; set by `markValuePromise` on the
		// one value an `async` spawn produces.
		self.valuePromiseMarks.push(false)
		// The node-handle mint rides the same lockstep, seeded NOT-A-MINT — which is the value's OWN id, a
		// list it can never have been minted off (see the field header).
		self.nodeHandleMintedFrom.push(id)
		// The merge-alias mark rides the same lockstep, seeded PROVEN — an ordinary value's declared alias is
		// one it passed a door to acquire. Only `mintPhi` sets it, because only a merge can name an alias no
		// door stood in front of (see the field header).
		self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks.push(false)
		// The capturing-closure mark + its env ride the same lockstep, seeded NOT-a-capture / no-env; set by
		// `parseClosureExpression` on the one value a capturing closure produces (see the field header).
		self.valueIsCapturingClosure.push(false)
		self.valueClosureEnv.push(0)
		// ⚠ **THE WITNESS COLUMN DELIBERATELY DOES *NOT* RIDE THIS LOCKSTEP** — it is grown ON DEMAND by
		// `pairInterfaceWitness`, so a function that builds no existential never grows it at all. See the
		// field header for the measurement that put it there.
		return id
	end 'mintValue'

	// Mint a value whose type is already in hand as a `MaxonType` — a `literal` op's type (the
	// op stores one anyway), a call's declared return type. It DECOMPOSES an existing union
	// rather than constructing a new one, so it allocates nothing either.
	function mintValueOfType(valueType MaxonType) returns ValueId
		return self.mintValue(maxonTypeTag(valueType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType))
	end 'mintValueOfType'

	// Re-record an EXISTING value's type columns in place. The caught error flag is a box POINTER that the
	// try fork minted as `integer` for its zero test; a boxed `(e)` binding re-records it as
	// its UNION type so `match e` dispatches through the box (`enumMatchFor`) and its scope-exit drop routes
	// to `__destruct_<U>` (`decrefCalleeFor`), both of which read the value TAG. Only the two type columns
	// move.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE Std WIDTH MOVES WITH THEM — THIS HEADER USED TO SAY IT COULD NOT, AND THAT WAS A CLAIM
	// ABOUT THE CALLERS RATHER THAN ABOUT THE DOOR.** Every caller but one stays inside the i64 family
	// (`valueTagToStdType` collapses `integer`, `named`, `structRef`, `string` alike), so for those the
	// already-emitted ops are provably unaffected — the `flag != 0` compare and the `tryCall`'s flag
	// register above are the worked example. That is a property of WHERE it is called, not of what it does:
	// the columns it writes are exactly the columns `valueTagToStdType` reads, so a re-record ACROSS width
	// families is a width change, and a caller may not assume otherwise.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ONE CALLER THAT CROSSES A WIDTH FAMILY IS `emitEnumCaseConstantSelect`, `boolean` → `integer`
	// (i1 → i64), and it is safe for two reasons that belong here rather than only at the call site.** It
	// re-records a `compare` result so the multiply that consumes it reads an i64 operand type:
	//
	//   • **NO EMITTED OP DEPENDS ON THAT VALUE'S OWN WIDTH.** `lowerCompare` takes the Std `cmp`'s
	//     `operandType` from its `lhs` — the two things being compared — never from its result, and the
	//     result is re-recorded one line after it is minted, before anything else can read it.
	//   • **THE BACKENDS TYPE A COMPARE'S RESULT FROM THE OP, NOT FROM THIS COLUMN.** wasm's valtype table
	//     records `cmp` as i32 whatever the Std type says, and widens at the consuming op
	//     (`coerceOnStack`); x64 materializes with `setcc` + a REX.W `movzx` and arm64 with `cset`, both
	//     full-width. So the widened claim is one all three already satisfy.
	//
	// ⇒ A NEW caller crossing a width family owes those same two questions about ITS value. Crossing into or
	// out of `float` (f64) or `boolean` (i1) is the case to think hardest about; within the i64 family there
	// is nothing to ask.
	function retypeValue(value ValueId, valueType MaxonType)
		try self.valueTags.set(value, value: maxonTypeTag(valueType)) otherwise panic("retypeValue: v{value} has no type slot — every SSA value is minted through mintValue")
		try self.valueNameIds.set(value, value: typeNameIdOf(valueType)) otherwise panic("retypeValue: v{value} has no type-name slot — the two type columns are filled in lockstep")
	end 'retypeValue'

	// ⭐ **PAIR AN EXISTENTIAL WITH ITS WITNESS HALF (P1.7a-existentials slice 1)** — the one writer of
	// `valueWitnessIds`, so every producer of an interface-typed value (a parameter, a merge phi, a field
	// load, an interface-returning call's result) says so through one door and cannot half-record it.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE ONE PLACE THE COLUMN GROWS.** It is extended to reach `value` here rather than pushed a
	// slot per mint, which is what makes an interface-free function pay NOTHING for it — see the field header.
	// The zeroes it lays down on the way are the same UNPAIRED filler a per-mint push wrote, so a read below
	// `value` is unchanged; a read ABOVE the grown prefix is the identical "unpaired" answer by a second
	// route, which `witnessOfValue` states once for both.
	function pairInterfaceWitness(value ValueId, witness ValueId)
		while self.valueWitnessIds.count() <= value 'growToReachIt'
			self.valueWitnessIds.push(0)
		end 'growToReachIt'

		try self.valueWitnessIds.set(value, value: witness) otherwise panic("Parser.pairInterfaceWitness: value v{value} has no witness slot after the column was grown to reach it — `growToReachIt` above pushes up to and including it")
	end 'pairInterfaceWitness'

	// ⭐ **THE WITNESS HALF OF AN EXISTENTIAL — the one reader**, and it PANICS on an unpaired value rather
	// than handing back the 0 filler. A fat pointer whose second half is absent is not a value that can be
	// dispatched, stored or returned: 0 would be a null table address, i.e. a wild jump at the first method
	// call instead of a diagnostic. Every producer of an `interfaceRef`-tagged value pairs it at the moment it
	// mints it, so a miss here is a compiler invariant break and not user input.
	//
	// A value ABOVE the grown prefix and a 0 WITHIN it are the same fact — nobody paired it — so they raise
	// the same panic rather than two that would have to be kept saying the same thing.
	// ⚠ Its LOWERING twin is `LowerMaxonToStd.pairedWitnessOf` — the same rule, read on the other side of the
	// tier boundary over the same column, and it cannot share a home: this one reads the PARSER's live column
	// and that one the `IrFunction` it was published into, and neither type can reach the other's. What they
	// must never do is disagree about what an unpaired slot MEANS, so each states the whole rule and names
	// the other.
	function witnessOfValue(value ValueId) returns ValueId
		let witness = try self.valueWitnessIds.get(value) otherwise 0 as ValueId
		if witness == 0 'unpaired'
			panic("Parser.witnessOfValue: value v{value} is typed as an interface but no witness half is paired with it — every producer of an existential (a parameter, a merge phi, a field load, an interface-returning call result) must call `pairInterfaceWitness` when it mints the value")
		end 'unpaired'

		return witness
	end 'witnessOfValue'

	// Append a value's type to the columns WITHOUT allocating an id — for the parameters,
	// whose ids (0..paramCount-1) the value numbering RESERVED rather than minted.
	function recordValueType(valueType MaxonType)
		self.valueTags.push(maxonTypeTag(valueType))
		self.valueNameIds.push(typeNameIdOf(valueType))
		// A parameter's value is the one thing the compiler provably cannot see.
		self.valueConsts.push(0)
		self.valueConstDomains.push(ConstPayloadDomain.unknown)
		// A parameter is never a fresh interpolation result, so it starts NOT owned — but its slot must
		// exist so the column stays indexable by every ValueId, params included.
		self.valueOwnsHeap.push(false)
		// A CONSUMED parameter is enrolled owned a few lines on (`bindParameters`), and it is enrolled
		// CO-OWNED: the caller may have transferred it by `__mm_incref` rather than by a move
		// (`coOwnBorrowedForConsume`), and which of the two it chose is not a fact this frame can read. So the
		// seed here is also the final answer for every parameter.
		self.valueOwnsHeapAlone.push(false)
		// A parameter is never an async spawn's handle; the slot must exist so the column stays indexable.
		self.valuePromiseMarks.push(false)
		// A parameter is never an insertion's fresh mint either — a handle ARRIVES here, it is not made here —
		// so it takes the NOT-A-MINT seed, its own id, which is the index this push is about to land at.
		// `remove` therefore refuses a bare parameter handle only if no binding owns it, and `bindParameters`
		// gives every parameter one.
		self.nodeHandleMintedFrom.push(self.nodeHandleMintedFrom.count())
		// A parameter is never a merge, and its declared range IS proved — by the entry guard `guardRangedParams`
		// stands in front of every caller (A1f). The slot must exist so the column stays indexable.
		self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks.push(false)
		// A parameter is never a capturing closure; the slots must exist so the columns stay indexable by
		// every ValueId, params (including a lifted closure's trailing `__env`) included.
		self.valueIsCapturingClosure.push(false)
		self.valueClosureEnv.push(0)
		// ⚠ The witness column is NOT seeded here either — `bindExistentialWitnessParams` grows it to exactly
		// the interface-typed parameters it pairs, and it runs after the whole parameter block is seeded
		// because the companion it pairs each one with is itself a parameter further along.
	end 'recordValueType'

	// Remember the integer behind a value the parser has just DEFINED. Called by the emitters, on
	// the id they minted a moment ago, so the slot is always there.
	function recordConst(id ValueId, folded ParsedInt)
		self.recordConstPayload(id, payload: folded, domain: ConstPayloadDomain.integer)
	end 'recordConst'

	// The FLOAT twin: remember the f64 BIT PATTERN behind a value. Its only writer is `emitLiteralIn`'s float
	// arm (a float literal, including the `-0.0` `parseNegatedNumericLiteral` folds into ONE literal), and its
	// only reader is the division-by-zero proof — see `ConstPayloadDomain` for why the two domains share one
	// payload column and why the DOMAIN, not the value's tag, is what says how to read it.
	function recordFloatConst(id ValueId, bits ParsedInt)
		self.recordConstPayload(id, payload: bits, domain: ConstPayloadDomain.float)
	end 'recordFloatConst'

	// The one write. Both halves move together by construction — a payload cannot be stored without the domain
	// that says how to read it, which is the whole reason the domain is a column and not a convention.
	function recordConstPayload(id ValueId, payload ParsedInt, domain ConstPayloadDomain)
		try self.valueConsts.set(id, value: payload) otherwise panic("Parser.recordConstPayload: value v{id} has no constant slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue")
		try self.valueConstDomains.set(id, value: domain) otherwise panic("Parser.recordConstPayload: value v{id} has no constant slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue")
	end 'recordConstPayload'

	// The DOMAIN of the constant payload the compiler holds for `id`, or `unknown` when it holds none.
	function constPayloadDomain(id ValueId) returns ConstPayloadDomain
		return try self.valueConstDomains.get(id) otherwise panic("Parser.constPayloadDomain: value v{id} has no constant slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue")
	end 'constPayloadDomain'

	// True iff the compiler holds `id`'s integer. STILL ONE ARRAY PROBE, and still the INTEGER view: a float
	// literal's slot says `float`, so every reader of this — the shift rule's count, `emitBinOp`'s fold, the
	// duplicate-pattern check — sees exactly what it saw before the float half existed.
	function constIsKnown(id ValueId) returns bool
		return self.constPayloadDomain(id) == ConstPayloadDomain.integer
	end 'constIsKnown'

	// True iff the compiler holds `id`'s f64 BIT PATTERN.
	function floatConstIsKnown(id ValueId) returns bool
		return self.constPayloadDomain(id) == ConstPayloadDomain.float
	end 'floatConstIsKnown'

	// The integer behind `id`. Only meaningful when `constIsKnown(id)` — asking otherwise is a
	// caller bug, and handing back the unseeded 0 as if it were a real constant is exactly the
	// wrong answer a sentinel would give, so it panics instead.
	function constOf(id ValueId) returns ParsedInt
		if not self.constIsKnown(id) 'unknown'
			panic("Parser.constOf: value v{id} is not a known constant — guard the call with constIsKnown")
		end 'unknown'

		return self.constPayloadOf(id)
	end 'constOf'

	// The f64 BIT PATTERN behind `id`. Guarded on `floatConstIsKnown` for `constOf`'s exact reason, and with
	// a sharper edge: the unseeded 0 IS the pattern for `+0.0`, so a sentinel here would not merely be wrong,
	// it would read as a provably-ZERO divisor and turn a legal program into an E3103.
	function floatConstBitsOf(id ValueId) returns ParsedInt
		if not self.floatConstIsKnown(id) 'unknown'
			panic("Parser.floatConstBitsOf: value v{id} is not a known float constant — guard the call with floatConstIsKnown")
		end 'unknown'

		return self.constPayloadOf(id)
	end 'floatConstBitsOf'

	// The raw payload, whichever domain it is in. Private to the two typed readers above, which is what keeps
	// the domain check in front of every read of it.
	function constPayloadOf(id ValueId) returns ParsedInt
		return try self.valueConsts.get(id) otherwise panic("Parser.constPayloadOf: value v{id} has no constant slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue")
	end 'constPayloadOf'

	// Record a fold's result, if it produced one. `notFoldable` is a DECISION and not a gap — the
	// folder declined an operator whose answer is a diagnostic rather than a number — so the value
	// simply stays unknown, and the guard downstream handles it.
	function recordFold(id ValueId, fold FoldedInt)
		match fold 'f'
			folded(value) then self.recordConst(id, folded: value)
			notFoldable then return
		end 'f'
	end 'recordFold'

	// The TAG of an SSA value's type — the hot read, and the only thing the operator rules
	// need. Every value the parser mints has one (the parameters are seeded from their
	// declarations, `mintValue` records the rest), so a miss is a parser invariant break.
	function tagOf(value ValueId) returns ValueTypeTag
		return try self.valueTags.get(value) otherwise panic("Parser.tagOf: value v{value} has no recorded type — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue")
	end 'tagOf'

	// **IS THIS VALUE'S TYPE UNSIGNED?** The question THE SHIFT RULE asks of a shift's LEFT operand,
	// and the only thing that decides whether `shr` fills with the sign or with zeros. Exhaustive
	// over `ValueTypeTag`, so a new type cannot be added without being placed on one side of it.
	//
	//   • `named`    — a ranged typealias, and unsigned exactly when its LOW BOUND is 0 or above
	//                  (`rangeIsUnsigned`). The alias is looked up in the WHOLE-PROGRAM declaration
	//                  index, never in this file's artifact: an alias declared in another file is
	//                  still an unsigned type here, and a file-local table would answer "signed" and
	//                  emit the wrong instruction. It is order-independent within a file too, which
	//                  the artifact table — filled as declarations are parsed — would not be.
	//   • `exitCode` — the builtin `ExitCode`, whose range is PLATFORM-SHAPED: `int(0 to u32.max)` on
	//                  Windows and `int(0 to 255)` on Linux, macOS and WASI (`Project.exitCodeAliasHigh`,
	//                  which mirrors `stdlib/Process.maxon`). Unsigned on EVERY target, by the same
	//                  low-bound rule `named` above applies — and it is worth saying WHICH bound carries
	//                  it, because only the high one moves: the verdict rests on the LOW bound, which is
	//                  0 on all four targets, so the platform range cannot endanger it.
	//   • `integer`  — a bare `int` is `i64.min to i64.max`. SIGNED, and it is the default every
	//                  shift in the language sign-fills under.
	//   • the rest   — not a number, so not a shift operand at all: `requireBinOpOperands` has
	//                  already rejected the program, and `emitShift` asks it BEFORE this is reached.
	//                  Answering "signed" costs nothing, because no code is kept.
	function valueIsUnsigned(value ValueId) returns bool
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.exitCode 'exitCode'
			return true
		end 'exitCode'

		// ⚠ **`declaredAliasOfValue`, NOT A SECOND SPELLING OF IT (X10 review).** X10 landed a private
		// `rangedAliasOfValueType` here — tag-guard on `named`, then `signatures.aliasOf(internedName(
		// valueNameId(v)))` — whose header said it existed so the shift rule and the division rule
		// "cannot come to disagree about which alias a value has". It was the file's THIRD copy of that
		// walk: `declaredAliasOfValue` had already been it since P1.9, statement for statement. Two
		// spellings of one lookup are the very thing the header was written against.
		return match self.declaredAliasOfValue(value) 'a'
			declared(alias) gives rangeIsUnsigned(alias.low)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'a'
	end 'valueIsUnsigned'

	// The full `MaxonType` of an SSA value, REBUILT from the two columns. It CONSTRUCTS a
	// union, so it allocates — which is why it is called only where a `MaxonType` is genuinely
	// STORED (a binding's declared type), and never on a per-value or per-operator path.
	function typeOf(value ValueId) returns MaxonType
		return maxonTypeOfTag(self.tagOf(value), nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'typeOf'

	// Record the block-arg values `block` passes to `targetId` for the Std-tier
	// phi-elim pass. A branch op carries only the target BlockId, so the per-edge phi
	// operands live on the predecessor's `branchEdges` (see IrBlock). An empty list
	// means the target has no phis — nothing to record.
	function recordBranchEdge(block IrBlock, targetId BlockId, argIds ValueIdArray)
		if argIds.count() == 0 'noArgs'
			return
		end 'noArgs'
		block.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(targetId, argIds: argIds))
	end 'recordBranchEdge'

	// Create + register a fresh block in `func`, filling the source footprint from the
	// parser's own file/position so the four `if` block-creation sites don't each
	// respell it.
	function newBlock(func IrFunction, label ByteArray, line LineNumber) returns IrBlock
		let fp = self.filePath.toString()
		let b = self.artifact.module.addBlockAt(func, label: label, filePath: fp, firstLine: line)
		return b
	end 'newBlock'

	// ⭐⭐ **A FRESH BLOCK NOTHING REACHES.** Four constructs need one — the continuation of an `if` whose
	// branches all terminate, the miss target of an exhaustive planned `match`, the tail after a `break` that
	// left its match, and the tail after a call that never returns — and all four need it for the ONE reason
	// stated here rather than four times: `assertAllBlocksTerminated` forbids a block leaving the parse with
	// `Terminator.unset`, and a block control cannot arrive at has no terminator to give it. `dead` is what
	// says so, and it is deliberately NOT an op: `emitTerminator` refuses a second op on a block that has
	// one, so an unreachable tail's `return`/`throw` may still be parsed into this block for its syntax.
	function newDeadBlock(func IrFunction, label ByteArray, line LineNumber) returns IrBlock
		var block = self.newBlock(func, label: label, line: line)
		block.terminator = Terminator.dead
		return block
	end 'newDeadBlock'

	// Set `block`'s terminator to a two-way conditional branch, anchored on the `if`
	// keyword's span.
	function emitCondBranch(block IrBlock, cond ValueId, thenId BlockId, elseId BlockId, ifTok Token)
		self.artifact.emitTerminator(block, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(cond, thenBlockId: thenId, elseBlockId: elseId), range: SourceRange.ofToken(ifTok))
	end 'emitCondBranch'

	// Set `block`'s terminator to an unconditional branch. The branch is synthetic (no
	// source token spawned it — it is the compiler wiring a fall-through), so it carries
	// the positionless `SourceRange.none()`.
	function emitBranch(block IrBlock, targetId BlockId)
		self.artifact.emitTerminator(block, op: MaxonOp.branch(targetId), range: SourceRange.none())
	end 'emitBranch'

	// Expression entry — EVERY value context: a `let`/`var` initializer, an assignment's
	// right-hand side, a `return` value, a call argument, an `if`/`while` CONDITION, the
	// inside of a parenthesis. There is ONE GRAMMAR for all of them.
	//
	// A condition once had its own, FLATTER grammar (`operand [cmpOp operand]`, operands parsed
	// at additive precedence), for exactly one reason — a comparison could only be consumed by
	// a branch, because the backend had no way to write a boolean into a register. Two things
	// ended that. `setcc` gives it one (`let r = 5 > 3` compiles), and `and`/`or` bind LOOSER
	// than comparison, so `if a > 0 and b > 0` cannot even be expressed by a grammar whose top
	// level is a single comparison. One climber, one set of precedences, and a condition is
	// simply an expression that happens to feed a branch — plus a TYPE rule, which is
	// `parseBoolCondition` below, not a second grammar.
	//
	// Seeds `currentBlock`, which every emit below targets — see the field.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE INLINE CONDITIONAL `<t> if <c> else <f>` IS HOOKED HERE (P1.5-A2c)** — a value-position
	// ternary that binds LOOSER than every binary operator, so it wraps the WHOLE `parseBinary` result and
	// is checked only after the climber has run. `parseBinary`'s RHS recursion never reaches this point, so
	// `a + b if c else d` is `(a + b) if c else (a + b's sibling)`; the `else` arm is a full `parseExpression`
	// again, so `1 if a else 2 if b else 3` right-associates. The block-form `if` cannot collide: it is a
	// STATEMENT keyword `parseStatement` catches at statement start, whereas a ternary `if` only follows an
	// operand — the same split both reference compilers draw (2-Parser.cs:5128).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPLIT IS SHARED; THE PRECEDENCE IS NOT, AND THIS COMMENT USED TO CLAIM IT WAS.**
	// MEASURED 2026-07-29 — `return 1 + 20 if false else 40` answers **40** here and **41** under the
	// bootstrap, which binds the ternary TIGHTER than `+` (`1 + (20 if false else 40)`) where shv2 binds
	// it looser (`(1 + 20) if false else 40`). Neither is blessed by the corpus: the one case that
	// exercises the combination, `specs/ternary-expression.md`'s `complex-expressions`
	// (`let x = a + b if cond else a * b`, a=3 b=2 cond=true), evaluates to **5 under BOTH readings**, so
	// it has never distinguished them — a coverage hole, not agreement. Left UNRESOLVED and unpinned
	// pending a ruling; it is a P2 self-hosting hazard, since a self-hosted parse that disagrees with the
	// bootstrap's silently changes the compiler's own meaning. See PLAN.md.
	//
	// The true arm parses before the `if` announces the form, so two facts about the block ARE captured up
	// front, allocation-free: the IR-detach mark (the start block plus its op/block-ref counts, so
	// `parseTernaryExpression` can lift the arm's ops off the unconditional path into the true branch — only
	// the SELECTED arm may run) and the pre-arm MOVE state (rewound onto the false arm). `captureMoveMark`
	// runs only when an owned binding is in scope; scalar code shares the empty mark and pays nothing.
	function parseExpression(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		self.currentBlock = block
		return try self.parseValueExpression(func)
	end 'parseExpression'

	// The climber PLUS the trailing inline conditional, over `self.currentBlock` as it already stands —
	// the entry every value context that must NOT re-seed `currentBlock` calls in place of the bare
	// `parseBinary`. A call argument, a struct-field value, a union-payload value and a parenthesised
	// group are each parsed INSIDE an expression that already moved `currentBlock` (an earlier sibling
	// short-circuited), so seeding it afresh — which `parseExpression` does — would discard that move.
	// They still need the ternary, though (`f(a if c else b)`, `T{x: a if c else b}`, `(a if c else b)`),
	// so the hook lives HERE, below the seed, and those sites call this directly. The mark is recorded off
	// the current block so `parseTernaryExpression` detaches the true arm from wherever control actually is.
	function parseValueExpression(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let block = self.currentBlock

		// ⭐ **THE E3019 BLAME NAME IS CLEARED HERE, at the one door every value expression enters through.**
		// A one-token identifier that resolved via a primary path publishing nothing must read "writable"
		// rather than inherit a NEIGHBOURING expression's answer, and the clear used to live at the call
		// ARGUMENT site alone — which was correct for arguments and left a ternary ARM, one construct over,
		// reading whatever the last argument had published. The arm token and cursor are captured for the
		// same question, allocation-free, exactly as an argument's are.
		self.lastBareBindingImmutableName = EmptySourceText
		let armTok = self.current()
		let armStart = self.pos

		let markOpRefCount = block.opRefs.count()
		let markBlockRefCount = func.blockRefs.count()
		// The pending-temporary height at the same instant as the op/block marks — everything enrolled above
		// it while the value below parses belongs to what may turn out to be a ternary's TRUE ARM, and is
		// released inside that arm rather than on the unconditional path (see `parseTernaryExpression`).
		let markTempCount = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		// ⭐ **AND THE OWNED-BINDING HEIGHT AT THE SAME INSTANT (A3h).** An expression declares no `let`, but it
		// can now ENROL an owned binding: `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` holds a temporary a borrow was taken out
		// of, so `(try make().get(0) otherwise d) if t else d` leaves one behind. It belongs to what may turn
		// out to be the TRUE ARM and must be dropped inside that arm — the mirror of `markTempCount`, for the
		// list whose entries drop at a SCOPE exit rather than a statement's.
		let markOwnedCount = self.ownedBindings.count()
		var preMoveMark = self.emptyMoveMark
		if markOwnedCount > 0 'ownedInScope'
			preMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()
		end 'ownedInScope'

		let value = try self.parseBinary(func, minPrec: OrPrecedence)

		if self.at(TokenKind.if) 'ternary'
			// The TRUE arm's blame, taken while its own tokens are still the ones the cursor last moved over
			// — the condition and the false arm both clobber that answer, and both parse below.
			return try self.parseTernaryExpression(func, trueValue: value, trueBlame: self.expressionImmutableBlame(armTok, valueStart: armStart, value: value), startBlock: block, markOpRefCount: markOpRefCount, markBlockRefCount: markBlockRefCount, markTempCount: markTempCount, markOwnedCount: markOwnedCount, preMoveMark: preMoveMark)
		end 'ternary'

		return value
	end 'parseValueExpression'

	// ⭐ **THE INLINE CONDITIONAL `<t> if <c> else <f>` (P1.5-A2c).** Called with the TRUE arm already
	// parsed (into `startBlock` since the mark) and the current token at `if`. Two obligations shape it:
	//
	//   **ONLY THE SELECTED ARM IS EVALUATED.** `0 if d == 0 else n / d` must not divide when d is 0, so
	//   neither arm may sit on the unconditional path. The false arm is easy — parsed straight into its own
	//   block. The true arm is not: nothing announced the ternary until the `if` that follows it, so it was
	//   emitted into the fall-through path and is DETACHED here and re-attached inside the true branch. The
	//   IR moves (its `module.ops` indices are unchanged; only the block referencing them changes); nothing
	//   is re-parsed, so no value id is minted twice and no closure is lifted twice — the bootstrap makes the
	//   identical move (2-Parser.cs:ParseTernaryExpression), by necessity, for the identical reason.
	//
	//   **THE TWO ARMS MERGE THROUGH ONE PHI**, exactly as a `match` expression's `gives` arms do — so the
	//   merge is `finalizeMatchMerge` over a two-arm reaching set with no carried vars. That reuse is the
	//   whole point: the give-type agreement (E3005, OPEN #54C), the cross-register-class refusal (OPEN #31),
	//   the owned-give normalisation, and the conditional-move reconciliation are ONE implementation for both
	//   forms, so a ternary cannot drift from a match on any of them.
	//
	// A CAPTURING closure may not be an arm: the phi carries the function pointer but not the capture
	// environment (the same merge hazard the match `gives` arm and the `otherwise` fallback are refused for),
	// so it is rejected E3099 at the `if` — the shared anchor for both arms, where each reference compiler
	// anchors it.
	function parseTernaryExpression(func IrFunction, trueValue ValueId, trueBlame ByteArray, startBlock IrBlock, markOpRefCount ElementCount, markBlockRefCount ElementCount, markTempCount TempDropMark, markOwnedCount BindingMark, preMoveMark MoveMark) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let ifTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.if)

		// The E3099 refusal for a capturing closure in EITHER arm — the route-specific prefix over the
		// shared merge suffix (the same shape `parseArmBody`'s `gives` refusal builds), computed once and
		// used for both arms. Anchored at the `if`, the shared point of the two arms, where each reference
		// compiler anchors it.
		let armEscapeMessage = "cannot use a closure that captures as an arm of a conditional expression: {CapturingClosureMergeSuffix}"
		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(trueValue, message: armEscapeMessage, tok: ifTok)

		// Where the true arm's VALUE lives: `startBlock` when the arm emitted only straight-line ops, or the
		// arm's own exit block when it grew control flow (a short-circuit, a nested ternary, a match).
		let armExit = self.currentBlock

		// ⭐ **THE TRUE ARM SETTLES EVERYTHING WHILE IT IS STILL DETACHABLE, AND ITS DROPS TRAVEL WITH IT.**
		// The arm was parsed onto the unconditional path before the `if` revealed the form, so its temporaries
		// (`[1,2].count() if c else …` builds one) are enrolled as though they were the enclosing statement's —
		// and after the relocation below the arm runs on ONE edge, where a release on the unconditional path
		// would free a value the false path never built. Settling HERE, before the ops are collected, puts the
		// releases in the tail of `startBlock` that `relocatedOpRefs` sweeps into `trueBranch` (or, for an arm
		// that grew control flow, into the arm's own inner exit block, which is relocated too). So they ride
		// the SAME relocation the arm does and need no second mechanism to follow them. It is also before
		// `captureMoveMark` below, so the give transfer's own poison is captured into the true arm's move state.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE OWNED-BINDING RELEASE IS THE PART THE TERNARY NEVER HAD (A3h).** Every other fork region has
		// had `dropArmScopedOwned` all along: an `if`/`else` branch, a `try`'s `otherwise` handler, a `catch`
		// handler and every `match` arm. A ternary arm could not enrol an owned binding while only a `let`
		// could, so the gap was invisible — and `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` closes that: a temporary a BORROW
		// was taken out of inside this arm is enrolled here, and left to the enclosing scope it would be
		// decref'd on the path that never built it (MEASURED as the `restoreMoveMark` height panic one screen
		// down, which is the same fact caught by the bookkeeping instead of by the register allocator).
		let settledTrue = try self.finishArmExit(MatchGiveValue.value(trueValue), tempFloor: markTempCount, ownedFloor: markOwnedCount, exitBlock: armExit, anchor: ifTok)

		// ⭐ **THE CONDITION RUNS FIRST AT RUNTIME, THOUGH IT PARSES SECOND.** The true arm was parsed before
		// the `if` revealed the form, so any binding it MOVED (an owned value into a struct field, say) is
		// poisoned now — but the condition, which runs BEFORE the true arm, must see that binding LIVE
		// (`Box.create(k) if isSet(k) else …` reads a live `k`). So the true arm's move state is snapshotted
		// and REWOUND to the pre-ternary state; the condition then parses from there, and the true path's mark
		// is re-formed below as the union of the two.
		let afterTrueArm = self.captureMoveMark()
		self.restoreMoveMark(preMoveMark)

		// ⭐ **DETACH THE TRUE ARM off the unconditional path.** Its ops are the tail of `startBlock`'s
		// `opRefs` since the mark; its terminator and outgoing edges (present only when the arm grew control
		// flow — unset/empty for a straight-line arm, since `startBlock` was unterminated at the mark) and its
		// inner blocks (the `func.blockRefs` tail since the mark) go with them. The ops keep their
		// `module.ops` indices; only the block that references them changes.
		let relocatedOpRefs = self.detachOpRefsAbove(startBlock, mark: markOpRefCount)

		let savedTerminator = startBlock.terminator
		let savedEdges = startBlock.branchEdges
		startBlock.terminator = Terminator.unset
		startBlock.branchEdges = BranchEdgeArray.create()

		var armInnerBlockRefs = BlockRefArray.create()
		for i in markBlockRefCount upto func.blockRefs.count() 'collectBlocks'
			armInnerBlockRefs.push(try func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("parseTernaryExpression: func.blockRefs.get OOB at {i}"))
		end 'collectBlocks'
		while func.blockRefs.count() > markBlockRefCount 'truncBlocks'
			_ = try func.blockRefs.pop() otherwise panic("parseTernaryExpression: func.blockRefs.pop above the mark {markBlockRefCount} failed")
		end 'truncBlocks'

		// ⭐ **THE CONDITION IS THE ONE THING THAT IS UNCONDITIONAL** — it lands where the true arm was and
		// runs before the branch. It may short-circuit and move `currentBlock`, so the two-way branch belongs
		// at the end of whichever block it left (`condExit`), exactly as `parseIfStatement` re-reads its entry
		// after the condition.
		self.currentBlock = startBlock
		// The condition is an ordinary expression that must be a bool — NOT `parseBoolCondition`, whose
		// E3005 "'if' requires a bool condition" is shared with the `if`/`while` STATEMENTS. A ternary's
		// non-bool condition is E2028 "ternary expression requires a bool condition" (#80), anchored at the
		// `if` (where `parseBoolCondition` anchors at the condition token) — matching the reference oracle.
		let cond = try self.parseExpression(func, block: startBlock)
		let condTag = self.tagOf(cond)
		if not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.boolean, tag: condTag) 'nonBoolCondition'
			throw ParseError.ternaryNonBoolCondition(typeTagName(condTag), line: ifTok.line, column: ifTok.column)
		end 'nonBoolCondition'
		let condExit = self.currentBlock

		// The CONDITION is unconditional, so its own temporaries die on the single path, in the block the
		// condition left control in — the `if`-statement rule, applied to the ternary's condition.
		self.drainPendingTemps(markTempCount, anchor: ifTok)

		// The move state after the (unconditional) condition — the base BOTH arms build on. The TRUE path is
		// that base PLUS the true arm's own moves (rewound above); the FALSE path is that base plus the false
		// arm's, and it inherits `condBase` as the live state directly, since the false arm parses next.
		let condBase = self.captureMoveMark()
		let trueMoveMark = orMoveMarks(afterTrueArm, b: condBase)

		try self.consume(TokenKind.else)

		// Reattach the true arm inside a fresh branch, physically after the condition's blocks. `trueExit` is
		// where its value lives now: the true branch itself when the arm was straight-line, else the arm's own
		// (relocated) exit block.
		var trueBranch = self.newBlock(func, label: b"ternarytrue", line: ifTok.line)
		trueBranch.opRefs = relocatedOpRefs
		trueBranch.terminator = savedTerminator
		trueBranch.branchEdges = savedEdges
		for br in armInnerBlockRefs 'reattach'
			func.blockRefs.push(br)
		end 'reattach'
		// Held by ID: a second owning NAME for `trueBranch` would move the block out of it (single
		// ownership), and the branch is still read below for the condition's `then` target. The one
		// consumer re-fetches, which is what `IrModule.getBlockByIdIn` asks for across block-minting calls
		// anyway — and the false arm's `parseExpression` between here and there mints plenty.
		var trueExitId = trueBranch.id
		if armExit.id != startBlock.id 'multiBlockArm'
			trueExitId = armExit.id
		end 'multiBlockArm'

		// The false arm parses straight into its own block, so it never runs on the true path — from the
		// post-condition move state (`condBase`, the current live state), never the true arm's moves. A full
		// `parseExpression` — a chained `… else b if c2 else d` right-associates here.
		var falseBranch = self.newBlock(func, label: b"ternaryfalse", line: ifTok.line)
		// The FALSE arm's owned floor is the height AFTER the condition, not `markOwnedCount`: the condition
		// runs unconditionally, so a temporary IT holds for a borrow belongs to the enclosing scope and must
		// survive both arms. The true arm's floor is the pre-condition height for exactly the same reason read
		// the other way — it parsed before the condition existed.
		let falseArmOwnedCount = self.ownedBindings.count()
		let falseArmTok = self.current()
		let falseArmStart = self.pos
		let falseValue = try self.parseExpression(func, block: falseBranch)
		let falseBlame = self.expressionImmutableBlame(falseArmTok, valueStart: falseArmStart, value: falseValue)
		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(falseValue, message: armEscapeMessage, tok: ifTok)
		let falseExit = self.currentBlock

		// The false arm settles in ITS exit block — the mirror of the true arm's, through the same door and
		// for the same reason: the arm runs on one edge only.
		let settledFalse = try self.finishArmExit(MatchGiveValue.value(falseValue), tempFloor: markTempCount, ownedFloor: falseArmOwnedCount, exitBlock: falseExit, anchor: ifTok)

		let falseMoveMark = self.captureMoveMark()

		// The merge is the SAME block-arg join a `match` expression uses (see the header). Two reaching arms,
		// no carried vars; `finalizeMatchMerge` runs every give-agreement/ownership rule and mints the result
		// phi and both edges. The ternary supplies only the branch out of the condition, emitted after so
		// `condExit` is still open when the merge's per-arm reconciliation runs.
		let carried = VarInfoArray.create()
		var reachingArms = MatchArmExitArray.create()
		let trueExit = try self.artifact.module.getBlockByIdIn(func, id: trueExitId) otherwise panic("parseTernaryExpression: the true arm's exit block {trueExitId} is not in its own function")
		reachingArms.push(MatchArmExit.create(trueExit, exitVals: ValueIdArray.create(), give: settledTrue, moveMark: trueMoveMark, blame: trueBlame))
		reachingArms.push(MatchArmExit.create(falseExit, exitVals: ValueIdArray.create(), give: settledFalse, moveMark: falseMoveMark, blame: falseBlame))
		let outcome = try self.finalizeMatchMerge(func, matchTok: ifTok, carried: carried, reachingArms: reachingArms, construct: MergeConstruct.ternary)

		self.emitCondBranch(condExit, cond: cond, thenId: trueBranch.id, elseId: falseBranch.id, ifTok: ifTok)

		self.currentBlock = outcome.cont
		match outcome.result 'r'
			value(id) then return id
			none then panic("parseTernaryExpression: the merge produced no value — both arms give a value, so the result phi is always present")
		end 'r'
	end 'parseTernaryExpression'

	// An `if` / `while` condition: an ordinary expression that must have type `bool`.
	//
	// **MAXON HAS NO C-STYLE TRUTHINESS.** `if 4` is not "if 4 is nonzero" — it is a type
	// error, and the ONLY tier that can say so is this one, because it is the only one holding
	// the expression's source type. The x64 lowering tests a non-fused condition with
	// `cmp reg, 0` + `jne`, which is exactly right for a bool (a bool IS 0 or 1, and a call
	// result, a parameter or a merge phi has no flags to fuse) — and is precisely why an `int`
	// condition silently "worked", branching on `n != 0`. The instruction cannot tell the two
	// apart. The type can.
	//
	// An `unresolved` condition is ADMITTED, not rejected: a cross-file callee's return type is
	// not known here, and `if isReady()` must compile. `typeClassAdmits` defers on `unknown`.
	//
	// The diagnostic anchors on the CONDITION's first token, not on the keyword: the message is
	// about the expression's type, and that is where the reader has to look — an `if` whose
	// condition spans three lines should not point at the `if`.
	function parseBoolCondition(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, kwTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let condTok = self.current()
		let cond = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)

		let condTag = self.tagOf(cond)
		if not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.boolean, tag: condTag) 'notABool'
			throw ParseError.nonBoolCondition(kwTok.kind.rawValue, got: typeTagName(condTag), line: condTok.line, column: condTok.column)
		end 'notABool'

		return cond
	end 'parseBoolCondition'

	// The precedence-climbing core. Parse a unary/primary operand, then while the next token
	// is a binary operator whose precedence is >= `minPrec`, consume it and recurse for the
	// right operand at `precedence + 1` (which makes the operator LEFT-associative). A
	// supported operator below `minPrec`, a non-operator, or the end of the expression stops
	// the climb.
	//
	// FOUR dispatches, and the second is the whole rung:
	//
	//   1. a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`/`or` over a bool left operand ⇒ not an op at all. It becomes
	//      blocks and a merge phi, and it MOVES `currentBlock` (see emitShortCircuit), which
	//      is why `left`'s block is re-read from the field after every recursive call.
	//   2. a comparison ⇒ `MaxonOp.compare`, typed `boolean`.
	//   3. REFERENCE IDENTITY `is` / `is not` ⇒ also a `MaxonOp.compare`, but over the two operands'
	//      POINTERS and with nothing dispatched. It takes its own arm because `is not` is two tokens and
	//      because neither operand may be `integerizedOperand`ed — see there.
	//   4. everything else ⇒ `MaxonOp.binOp`. This is where a bitwise `and`/`or`/`xor` lands:
	//      the short-circuit test above did not fire, so the operands are not bools, and on
	//      integers the word operators ARE the bit operators.
	//
	// No arm rejects anything any more. The old "a comparison is not a value" throw is gone
	// with the machinery that forced it.
	function parseBinary(func IrFunction, minPrec InfixPrecedence) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The LEFT operand's first token, remembered before it is parsed — the twin of `rhsStart` below,
		// and read for the same kind of question: `integerizedOperand` needs to know whether an operand
		// was written as a lone character LITERAL, which is a fact about its tokens, not about its value.
		let leftStart = self.pos
		var left = try self.parseCast(func)

		while true 'climb'
			let opIndex = self.pos
			let opTok = self.current()

			let prec = infixPrecedence(opTok.kind)
			if prec == NotAnInfixOperator or prec < minPrec 'stop'
				break
			end 'stop'

			// `and fallthrough` ends a match arm's body — it is the fallthrough SUFFIX, not a
			// bitwise `and`. `fallthrough` is a keyword and can never be a variable, so `and
			// fallthrough` has no reading as an expression ANYWHERE in the language; stopping the
			// climb here hands the suffix intact to the match arm parser (P1.1b wave A), which either
			// consumes it or — in a match EXPRESSION arm — rejects the stray `and` (E2001). No mode
			// flag is needed precisely because the pairing is unambiguous everywhere.
			if opTok.kind == TokenKind.and and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.fallthrough 'fallthroughSuffix'
				break
			end 'fallthroughSuffix'

			// The short-circuit test reads the LEFT operand's type only, and reads it BEFORE
			// the right operand is parsed — it has to, because whether the right operand is
			// evaluated AT ALL is the thing being decided. (v1 gates identically:
			// Parser.maxon:11384.)
			if shortCircuitOperator(opTok.kind) and tagIsBool(self.tagOf(left)) 'shortCircuit'
				self.advance()
				left = try self.emitShortCircuit(func, leftValue: left, opTok: opTok, rhsMinPrec: prec + 1)
				continue
			end 'shortCircuit'

			// ⭐ **REFERENCE IDENTITY `a is b` / `a is not b`** (`specs/reference-identity.md`) — a FOURTH
			// dispatch, and it takes the operator's own arm rather than the shared tail below for two reasons,
			// both of which would be silent wrong answers if it did not:
			//
			//   • **`is not` is TWO TOKENS.** The `not` is consumed HERE, before the right operand is parsed, so
			//     it never reaches `parseUnary` as a prefix `not` over a struct. v1's climber fuses it at the
			//     same point and for the same reason (maxon-selfhosted/Parser.maxon:11421).
			//   • **NEITHER OPERAND MAY BE `integerizedOperand`ed.** That door turns a lone CHARACTER LITERAL
			//     into an `int` when the other side is integral — right for `cp - '0'`, and for `is` it would
			//     replace one of the two records whose ADDRESSES are the whole question with a codepoint.
			if identityOperator(opTok.kind) 'referenceIdentity'
				self.advance()
				let negate = self.at(TokenKind.not)
				if negate 'isNot'
					self.advance()
				end 'isNot'

				let other = try self.parseBinary(func, minPrec: prec + 1)
				left = try self.emitReferenceIdentity(left, rhs: other, negate: negate, opTok: opTok)
				continue
			end 'referenceIdentity'

			self.advance()
			// The right operand's token span, remembered BEFORE it is parsed and read back after.
			// It is not what the shift rule ASKS — that is the constant folder's job — but it is
			// where E2054 POINTS, and the climber has already decided how far the operand reaches.
			let rhsStart = self.pos
			let right = try self.parseBinary(func, minPrec: prec + 1)
			let rhsEnd = self.pos

			// ⭐⭐ **A BINARY OPERATOR IS AN INTEGER-EXPECTING POSITION FOR WHICHEVER SIDE IS A CHARACTER
			// LITERAL WHEN THE OTHER SIDE IS INTEGRAL** — `cp == '-'`, `'-' == cp` and `cp - '0'` alike. Asked
			// of both operands, each against what the OTHER is, and asked HERE rather than inside the
			// comparison arm because `specs/char-literal-to-int.md` documents the rule over "a binary
			// operation", not over a comparison: its own `char-literal-arithmetic` case subtracts.
			//
			// ⚠ The second call reads `leftValue`'s tag rather than `left`'s, because the first may have
			// turned a Character into an `int` and the right operand must be judged against what the left
			// now IS. Two character literals settle each other by staying Characters, which is what makes
			// `'A' == 'A'` a Character comparison (`char-literal-to-int.md`'s own case name says so).
			let leftValue = try self.integerizedOperand(left, expected: self.tagOf(right), start: leftStart, pastEnd: opIndex)
			let rightValue = try self.integerizedOperand(right, expected: self.tagOf(leftValue), start: rhsStart, pastEnd: rhsEnd)

			if comparisonOperator(opTok.kind) 'comparison'
				left = try self.emitCompare(leftValue, rhs: rightValue, op: infixCmpOp(opTok.kind), opTok: opTok)
				continue
			end 'comparison'

			let op = infixMaxonOp(opTok.kind)

			// A shift's right operand is not an ordinary number but a DISTANCE, and Maxon's rule
			// for it (Go's) is deliberately not the hardware's. It gets its own emitter, which
			// owns the rule, the diagnostic, AND the runtime guard. See `emitShift`.
			if shiftOperator(op) 'isShift'
				left = try self.emitShift(leftValue, count: rightValue, op: op, opTok: opTok, countStart: rhsStart, countEnd: rhsEnd)
				continue
			end 'isShift'

			// A division's right operand is not an ordinary number either: it is the one operand in the
			// language whose VALUE decides whether the operation can fail. It gets its own emitter, which owns
			// the rule, both diagnostics AND the throwing desugar — the same split, for the same reason, as the
			// shift above. See `emitDivOrMod`.
			if divisionOperator(op) 'isDivision'
				left = try self.emitDivOrMod(leftValue, divisor: rightValue, op: op, opTok: opTok)
				continue
			end 'isDivision'

			left = try self.emitBinOp(leftValue, rhs: rightValue, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'climb'

		return left
	end 'parseBinary'

	// ⭐ Emit `lhs <op> count` under TypeRules' ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE — THE one place a source-level
	// shift is built. Two jobs, separate on purpose:
	//
	//   **LEGALITY.** A count the parser can FOLD is decided here: a negative one is E2054. A count
	//   of 64 or more is LEGAL and is NOT an error — it shifts every bit out — which is the whole
	//   narrowing of this diagnostic. A count the parser CANNOT fold is Go's runtime panic, which
	//   shv2 cannot raise yet; see `emitGuardedShift`.
	//
	//   **VALUE.** A folded count is rewritten into the shift its semantics define, so no count
	//   above `MaxUnguardedShiftCount` ever reaches codegen and the hardware's masking is never
	//   consulted. A count that could NOT be folded gets the GUARD, which saturates it at run time
	//   — to the SAME answer. That the two agree is not a coincidence: both are readings of
	//   `evalShift`.
	function emitShift(lhs ValueId, count ValueId, op MaxonBinOp, opTok Token, countStart TokenIndex, countEnd TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The SHIFT's OWN operand rule, asked BEFORE anything is emitted. `emitBinOp` would ask it
		// too, but by then the guard's ops are already in the IR — and the FIRST of them is an
		// `and`, so `4 shr flag` would be rejected as a bad `and` rather than as a bad `shr`
		// (E3005 for the wrong operator instead of E2004 for the right one). A rejected program
		// must also leave no ops behind.
		//
		// ⭐ Asked through `numberTagOf` for `emitBinOp`'s reason (A4o): a float-backed enum's bits are a
		// sign/exponent/mantissa triple, so `Weight.light shl 1` has no shift reading and is refused HERE,
		// in the shift's own words, rather than shifting a mantissa.
		try requireBinOpOperands(op, left: self.numberTagOf(lhs), right: self.numberTagOf(count), opTok: opTok)

		// ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE's one question, asked ONCE, of the LEFT operand — and then CARRIED: to
		// the opcode, to the fold, and to the guard. Never re-derived, because the whole class of
		// bug this file has already paid for is two readers of one rule reaching two answers.
		let signFills = shiftFillsWithSign(op, leftOperandIsUnsigned: self.valueIsUnsigned(lhs))
		let shiftOp = shiftOpcodeFor(op, signFills: signFills)

		if not self.constIsKnown(count) 'dynamic'
			return try self.emitGuardedShift(lhs, count: count, op: shiftOp, opTok: opTok)
		end 'dynamic'

		let folded = self.constOf(count)
		if shiftCountIsNegative(folded) 'negative'
			// Constructed and thrown in ONE expression. A `throw self.someHelper(…)` — building the
			// error in a helper and throwing what it returns — LEAKS the boxed union under the
			// bootstrap's refcounting, which the suite's leak check catches as an extra stderr line.
			let anchor = self.shiftCountAnchor(countStart, countEnd: countEnd)
			throw ParseError.shiftCountNegative(folded, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'negative'

		if not shiftCountIsUnguarded(folded) 'shiftedOut'
			// A count that shifts every bit out. There is no instruction for it — the hardware
			// would mask it straight back into range — so the compiler writes down what it MEANS.
			// A shift that fills with ZEROS (`shl`, and an unsigned `shrLogical`) leaves the constant
			// 0. One that fills with the SIGN leaves the sign, and `x shr 63` already IS the sign, so
			// it becomes a shift by `MaxUnguardedShiftCount`. Neither is the masked shift the
			// hardware would have performed (`x shl 64` == `x`, `x shl 100` == `x shl 36`).
			//
			// `lhs` keeps its ops either way: they may have side effects, and Go evaluates the
			// left operand of a shift whatever the count turns out to be.
			if not signFills 'zeroFill'
				return self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
			end 'zeroFill'

			let clamped = self.emitLiteral(MaxUnguardedShiftCount, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
			return try self.emitBinOp(lhs, rhs: clamped, op: shiftOp, opTok: opTok)
		end 'shiftedOut'

		// A count the instruction takes as written; the hardware's mask is a no-op on it. The
		// operand is emitted UNCHANGED — this compiler folds no constant expression INTO the IR
		// (`1 + 2` still emits an `add`; see FoldConstOperands' header), and a shift count that
		// folds to 3 is a shift by 3 whether it arrives as a literal or as an `add`.
		let result = try self.emitBinOp(lhs, rhs: count, op: shiftOp, opTok: opTok)

		// The shift's own value, when the parser holds both sides — RECORDED, not rewritten. It is
		// what makes `let A = 1 shl 4` a count that `x shl A` can see. It folds under the OPCODE the
		// rule already chose, so the constant recorded here and the instruction that opcode emits
		// cannot answer differently — there is one fact, not two.
		if self.constIsKnown(lhs) 'bothKnown'
			self.recordConst(result, folded: evalShift(self.constOf(lhs), count: folded, op: shiftOp))
		end 'bothKnown'

		return result
	end 'emitShift'

	// A shift whose count the compiler could NOT fold — the runtime half of THE SHIFT RULE, and
	// the half that has to fight the hardware.
	//
	// x64 masks a 64-bit shift's count to its low 6 bits, so the instruction cannot be handed the
	// count as written: `x shl 64` would compute `x shl 0` (x, UNCHANGED) and `x shl 100` would
	// compute `x shl 36`. The count is SATURATED first, by the same rule `evalShift` folds by, and
	// with only the ops the Maxon tier already has (there is no `cmov` and no select in this
	// compiler — see TargetDialect's op inventory):
	//
	//   outOfRange = (count and -64) != 0        — one `and` decides BOTH ends at once: a count is
	//                                              outside 0..63 exactly when a bit above the low 6
	//                                              is set, and a NEGATIVE count has its sign bit
	//                                              set, so it lands out-of-range too.
	//   mask       = (t or -t) shr 63            — an ARITHMETIC shr turns "t is non-zero" into
	//                                              all-ones, and "t is zero" into all-zeros.
	//   ZERO-FILL: (x <shift> count) and not mask   — `shl`, and an UNSIGNED `shrLogical`. Both
	//                                                 shift every bit out, so both saturate to 0.
	//   SIGN-FILL: x shr (count or mask)            — a signed `shr` saturates to the SIGN, and a
	//                                                 count of -1 masks to 63, which IS the sign.
	//
	// Which one applies is read off the OPCODE, which is where `emitShift` already wrote the answer:
	// `shr` is the arithmetic right shift by definition of the enum, and every other shift opcode
	// zero-fills. THE SHIFT RULE was asked once, of the left operand; this is not a second reading
	// of it but a reading of its result.
	//
	// Cost, on top of the shift: 4 ops for the mask, plus 2 (zero-fill) or 1 (sign-fill). Only a
	// count the compiler cannot see pays it — every `x shr 8` and `x shl 3` is folded and untouched.
	//
	// ⚠ TODO — Go PANICS on a negative count at run time ("if the shift count is negative at run
	// time, a run-time panic occurs"), and shv2 still does not — but ⚠ **NOT FOR THE REASON THIS
	// NOTE USED TO GIVE, WHICH WAS THAT THERE IS NO PANIC RUNTIME.** There is one: `mrt_panic` prints
	// a located message and a symbolized backtrace on both x64 lanes (A1j) and both arm64 lanes (A3n),
	// and the P1.9 range guard already reaches it through `StdOp.osPanic`. What is missing is only the
	// GUARD — a compare and a branch to a panic block at this site, with a message this function bakes
	// — which is a rung of its own and not a blocked one. Until it lands, a negative count is NOT a
	// wrong answer: the mask above reads it as out-of-range, so `x shl -1` is 0 and `x shr -1` is the
	// sign. That is defined, deterministic, and NOT the masked `x shl 63` this rule exists to kill —
	// it is simply not yet the panic Go requires. `specs-shv2/bitwise-operators.md` carries the case
	// as a `disabled-test:`, to be enabled by the rung that lands the guard.
	function emitGuardedShift(lhs ValueId, count ValueId, op MaxonBinOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(opTok)

		let outOfRangeBits = self.emitLiteral(0 - ShiftCountBits, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let bitsAboveCount = try self.emitBinOp(count, rhs: outOfRangeBits, op: MaxonBinOp.bitAnd, opTok: opTok)
		let negatedBits = self.emitUnaryOp(bitsAboveCount, op: MaxonUnaryOp.neg, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		let signCarrier = try self.emitBinOp(bitsAboveCount, rhs: negatedBits, op: MaxonBinOp.bitOr, opTok: opTok)
		let signBit = self.emitLiteral(MaxUnguardedShiftCount, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		// ARITHMETIC, and unconditionally so: this `shr` is not the user's shift but the MASK's, and
		// it works precisely BECAUSE it replicates the sign — `signCarrier shr 63` is all-ones when
		// the count is out of range and all-zeros when it is not. It is a fact about `signCarrier`
		// (a plain `int`, and therefore signed) and never about `lhs`, so the user's shift opcode has
		// no say in it.
		let mask = try self.emitBinOp(signCarrier, rhs: signBit, op: MaxonBinOp.shr, opTok: opTok)

		if op == MaxonBinOp.shr 'signFilling'
			// A count of -1 masks to 63 in the hardware, and `x shr 63` IS the sign — so saturating
			// a sign-filling shift is a CLAMP OF THE COUNT, and needs no select of the result.
			let saturatedCount = try self.emitBinOp(count, rhs: mask, op: MaxonBinOp.bitOr, opTok: opTok)
			return try self.emitBinOp(lhs, rhs: saturatedCount, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'signFilling'

		// A zero-filling shift — `shl`, or an unsigned `shrLogical`. Both shift every bit out for
		// an out-of-range count, so both saturate to 0 by masking the RESULT. The shift itself is
		// `op`, which is why one branch now serves the two of them.
		let raw = try self.emitBinOp(lhs, rhs: count, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		let keep = self.emitUnaryOp(mask, op: MaxonUnaryOp.bitNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		return try self.emitBinOp(raw, rhs: keep, op: MaxonBinOp.bitAnd, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitGuardedShift'

	// WHERE E2054 POINTS: the first token of the count as WRITTEN. `x shl SHIFT` points at `SHIFT`,
	// where the programmer would look, and not at the `let` that gave it its value. Redundant
	// parentheses are not part of what was written, so `x shl (-1)` points at the `-1`.
	function shiftCountAnchor(countStart TokenIndex, countEnd TokenIndex) returns Token
		var lo = countStart
		var hi = countEnd

		// Strip redundant outer parentheses so `x shl (-1)` anchors E2054 on the `-1`, not the `(`. The
		// same 4-line idiom `rhsWasBareLocalReference` uses to see a move through `(t)` — NOT factored
		// into a shared helper on purpose: the only shv2 return shape that could hand back both narrowed
		// bounds is a tuple, and a tuple return HEAP-ALLOCATES per call, which on the per-bind/assign
		// gate path reintroduces exactly the linear compile-time allocation the move-poison optimizer
		// removed. Each copy is independently pinned by specs (shift goldens here; `paren-move` there), so
		// a divergence cannot pass unnoticed, and the two serve unrelated ends (diagnostic anchor vs move
		// soundness) — a benign shared mechanic, not a shared fact that must agree.
		while lo + 2 <= hi and self.kindAt(lo) == TokenKind.leftParen and self.kindAt(hi - 1) == TokenKind.rightParen 'stripParens'
			lo = lo + 1
			hi = hi - 1
		end 'stripParens'

		return self.tokenAt(lo)
	end 'shiftCountAnchor'

	// Lower a BOOL `and`/`or` to SHORT-CIRCUIT control flow — the point of this rung. `a and
	// b` must not evaluate `b` when `a` is false, so it is not an operator at all: it is a
	// branch, a block, and a merge phi, minted on the same on-the-fly SSA machinery `if` and
	// `while` mint theirs.
	//
	//     entry:  <lhs already evaluated>
	//             cond_br lhs ? rhs   : merge   -- `and`: a FALSE lhs skips the rhs
	//             cond_br lhs ? merge : rhs     -- `or` : a TRUE  lhs skips the rhs
	//     rhsEnd: <the rhs is evaluated HERE, and on no other path>
	//             br merge(rhsValue)
	//     merge:  phi <- [seed from entry] [rhsValue from rhsEnd]
	//
	// The SEED is the answer the skipped path already determined: `false` for `and`, `true`
	// for `or`. Ported from maxon-selfhosted's `emitShortCircuit`
	// (Parser.maxon:11718-11787), with two deliberate divergences:
	//
	//   1. v1 seeds a VAR SLOT, stores the rhs into it, and reloads it at the merge, because
	//      v1 has slots and a `mem2reg` to promote them. shv2 has neither — its parser IS
	//      the SSA constructor — so the merge value is a BLOCK ARG fed by each predecessor's
	//      branch edge. The same shape, one tier earlier and with no store/load round trip.
	//   2. v1 NEGATES the lhs for `or` (`xor lhs, 1`) so the "then" target is always the rhs,
	//      matching its jcc-to-else convention. shv2's `condBranch` names both targets and
	//      `lowerCondBranch` emits `jcc` + `jmp` for EVERY layout, so the targets are simply
	//      SWAPPED — exact, and free, where v1's negation costs a literal and an xor on every
	//      single `or`.
	//
	// The rhs block is created BEFORE the rhs is parsed and the merge block AFTER, so a
	// NESTED short-circuit inside the rhs slots its own blocks between them and `.text` still
	// lays out in evaluation order (v1 makes the same point).
	function emitShortCircuit(func IrFunction, leftValue ValueId, opTok Token, rhsMinPrec InfixPrecedence) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `var`, not `let`: the seed literal and the two-way branch are emitted INTO it, and
		// a `let` binding cannot be forwarded to a mutating parameter (E3019).
		var entry = self.currentBlock
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(opTok)
		let takesRhsWhenTrue = opTok.kind == TokenKind.and

		// The value the SKIPPED path yields, materialized in the entry block: `false and _`
		// is false, `true or _` is true. It is the entry edge's phi operand.
		let seed = self.emitLiteralIn(entry, value: shortCircuitSeed(takesRhsWhenTrue), valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: range)

		let rhsBlock = self.newBlock(func, label: shortCircuitLabel(takesRhsWhenTrue), line: opTok.line)
		self.currentBlock = rhsBlock
		let rhsTempFloor = self.pendingTempDrops.count()
		// ⭐ **AND THE OWNED-BINDING HEIGHT — the twin floor, for the list whose entries drop at a SCOPE exit
		// (A3h).** The `for`/`match`/ternary reason, one construct over: `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` holds the
		// container a borrow was taken out of, and `a or (try make().get(0) otherwise d).count() == 3` takes
		// one INSIDE the rhs. Left to the enclosing scope, that record's decref would run on the path that
		// short-circuited past its definition — MEASURED as the allocator's `seedInUse: a use dominates its
		// def`, the same complaint the pending-temp drain below was written for.
		let rhsOwnedFloor = self.ownedBindings.count()
		let rhsValue = try self.parseBinary(func, minPrec: rhsMinPrec)
		// NOT `rhsBlock`: the rhs may itself have short-circuited (`a or b or c`), leaving
		// control in a merge block of its own. That is the block the value is defined in, and
		// the one that must branch here.
		var rhsEnd = self.currentBlock

		// ⭐ **THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE RUNS ON ONE PATH, SO ITS TEMPORARIES DIE ON THAT PATH.** `a or [4,5].count()
		// == 2` builds its array only when the lhs was false; on the short-circuit path the value does not
		// exist at all. Releasing it after the merge is therefore not a leak but the OPPOSITE mistake — a
		// release of something never built, which is a use of a register the skipped path never wrote. It was
		// caught downstream, loudly, by the allocator's dominance invariant ("a use dominates its def"), which
		// is exactly the right complaint: `rhsEnd` is the only block the rhs's definitions dominate, so this
		// is the only place the release can go. A bool operand is never owned heap, so `rhsValue` itself is
		// never in this list and cannot be released out from under the merge phi.
		self.drainPendingTemps(rhsTempFloor, anchor: opTok)

		// The same argument for the rhs's own SCOPE-lifetime holds, in the same block. The merge phi is a
		// BOOL, so no borrow can leave the rhs through it — which is why the record a borrow was taken out of
		// may be released here rather than laundered on the way out, unlike a ternary arm's give.
		self.dropArmScopedOwned(rhsOwnedFloor, exitBlock: rhsEnd, anchor: opTok)

		// The SHORT-CIRCUIT half of the same rule `emitBinOp` enforces — ASKED, not restated
		// (see `requireBinOpOperands`). The left operand is already known to be a bool: that is
		// what brought control here.
		let op = infixMaxonOp(opTok.kind)
		let rhsTag = self.tagOf(rhsValue)
		try requireBinOpOperands(op, left: ValueTypeTag.boolean, right: rhsTag, opTok: opTok)

		// The merge phi IS the operator's result, so its TAG is the operator's result type — and
		// it is ASKED of the same `binOpResultTag` the `binOp` path asks, not restated as a
		// literal `boolean` here. Restating it left a word operator's result type with TWO
		// deciders, one per path, and nothing making them agree: they answer the same thing today
		// only because a bool left operand makes `wordOpResultTag`'s first arm fire for every rhs
		// that survives the check above. The day that rule changes — deferring on an `unresolved`
		// rhs, say (see TypeRules' `wordOpResultTag`, and the cross-file gap in
		// `calleeResultType`) — the `binOp` path would move and this one would go on ASSERTING
		// `boolean` about a value it cannot type. A wrong tag is not a compile error; it is a
		// wrong answer at whatever reads the tag next, which is every rule this rung added.
		let resultTag = binOpResultTag(op, left: ValueTypeTag.boolean, right: rhsTag)

		var merge = self.newBlock(func, label: b"scmerge", line: opTok.line)
		let result = self.mintPhi(merge, tag: resultTag, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)

		self.recordBranchEdge(entry, targetId: merge.id, argIds: singletonArgs(seed))
		self.recordBranchEdge(rhsEnd, targetId: merge.id, argIds: singletonArgs(rhsValue))
		self.emitShortCircuitBranch(entry, cond: leftValue, rhsId: rhsBlock.id, mergeId: merge.id, takesRhsWhenTrue: takesRhsWhenTrue, range: range)
		self.emitBranch(rhsEnd, targetId: merge.id)

		self.currentBlock = merge
		return result
	end 'emitShortCircuit'

	// The two-way branch out of a short-circuit's entry block. `and` takes the rhs when the
	// lhs is TRUE; `or` takes it when the lhs is FALSE — so the two differ by nothing but
	// which target sits in which slot.
	function emitShortCircuitBranch(entry IrBlock, cond ValueId, rhsId BlockId, mergeId BlockId, takesRhsWhenTrue bool, range SourceRange)
		var thenId = mergeId
		var elseId = rhsId
		if takesRhsWhenTrue 'isAnd'
			thenId = rhsId
			elseId = mergeId
		end 'isAnd'
		self.artifact.emitTerminator(entry, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(cond, thenBlockId: thenId, elseBlockId: elseId), range: range)
	end 'emitShortCircuitBranch'

	// A prefix-operator operand — the climber's leaf.
	//
	// The two prefix operators bind their operands DIFFERENTLY, and both readings are pinned
	// by a spec:
	//   • `-` takes a PRIMARY, so `- -x` fails at the second `-` (parsePrimary raises E2004)
	//     — specs/unary-operators.md's double-negation case.
	//   • `not` takes another UNARY, so `not not a` is legal and is 42 for a == 42
	//     — specs/bitwise-operators.md's `bitwise-not-double`.
	// v1 splits them the same way (its `-` recurses into parsePrimary, its `not` into
	// parseUnary — Parser.maxon:12034-12055).
	//
	// `not` is TYPE-DIRECTED, like `and`/`or`/`xor`: on a bool it flips bit 0 (`not true` is
	// false), on an integer it complements all 64 (`not 5` is -6). The two are different
	// instructions, and choosing the integer one for a bool would give -1/-2 — both of which
	// still read as TRUE in a condition. So the parser, which is the tier that knows the
	// type, picks the opcode; the Std tier just lowers what it is given.
	//
	// `-` is NOT type-directed: it is ARITHMETIC, so its operand must be a number. `-flag` has
	// no reading — it negated the bool's 1 payload and produced -1, which is still TRUE in a
	// condition — so it is rejected by the same numeric-domain rule the binary arithmetic
	// operators use (TypeRules' `typeClassAdmits`).
	// The `as`-cast layer, LOOSER than unary `-`/`not` but TIGHTER than every binary operator: `-5 as Age`
	// is `(-5) as Age`, matching the bootstrap (whose `as` loop sits outside `ParsePrimary`, after the
	// unary), so a negative literal narrowed into a ranged alias is checked as the NEGATIVE value — which
	// is what `error.negative-out-of-range` pins. The cast is therefore NOT a `parsePostfix` operator
	// (that band — the indirect call `f(x)` — stays TIGHTER than `-`, so `-f()` is `-(f())`); it wraps the
	// whole unary result here. `applyCast` retags / range-checks / float-converts (P1.9).
	function parseCast(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let operandStart = self.pos
		var current = try self.parseUnary(func)

		// ⚠ Asked only once an `as` is actually there, and this is a HOT path — `parseCast` wraps every
		// expression leaf in the program, while a cast is rare. `rhsWasBareLocalReference` walks tokens and
		// probes the scope; paying that per leaf would be a cost the feature does not earn.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.as) 'noCast'
			return current
		end 'noCast'

		// ⭐ **WAS THE OPERAND A BARE REFERENCE TO A LOCAL?** Asked HERE, while the cursor still sits just
		// past the operand, because `applyCast` may not stamp a declared alias onto a value that another
		// BINDING also names (see `ValueDeclaredAliasMap`). A later hop of the loop casts a CAST RESULT,
		// which is this parse's own temporary and is bound to no name, so the answer is false from then on.
		var currentIsBareLocal = self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(operandStart)
		while self.at(TokenKind.as) 'cast'
			let asTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.as)
			// Captured BEFORE the target is parsed: E2003 anchors on the TARGET NAME, and by the time
			// `parseTypeReference` returns the cursor is past it. (For a two-token `Wrapper.Idx` target
			// this is the first of the two, which is the one the reference blames.)
			let targetTok = self.current()
			let targetType = try self.parseTypeReference()
			current = try self.applyCast(current, targetType: targetType, asTok: asTok, targetTok: targetTok, sourceIsBareLocal: currentIsBareLocal)
			currentIsBareLocal = false
		end 'cast'
		return current
	end 'parseCast'

	// ⭐ **A BODY CAST WHOSE TARGET NAMES AN ALIAS THIS FILE CANNOT SEE — RECORDED, NOT THROWN.**
	//
	// Two things follow from recording rather than throwing, and both are wanted. The parse CONTINUES,
	// so the rest of the file is still checked and the cast's own value flows on as the alias's erased
	// `int` (the diagnostic gates the build, so nothing is emitted from it). And the report is DEFERRED
	// to the drain, which is what puts every E3062 in the program ahead of it — the order
	// `export-keyword/error.non-exported-typealias-cross-file` pins, and one that per-file reporting
	// cannot give (see `DeferredNameDiagnostic`).
	//
	// The TOP-LEVEL constant spelling of the same cast throws instead (`requireConstCastTargetKind`),
	// and cannot do otherwise: its `Parser` is transient and its artifact is discarded, so it has no
	// route to this list — its diagnostics are recorded on the declaration and re-raised where the
	// declaration is written. Both spellings decide by the ONE predicate below and report the ONE
	// message (`HiddenTypeNameMessage`) at the same anchor; only WHEN they surface differs.
	function recordHiddenCastTarget(targetAlias DeclaredAlias, targetTok Token)
		if not self.aliasHiddenFromThisFile(targetAlias) 'visible'
			return
		end 'visible'

		self.artifact.recordHiddenTypeName(DeferredNameDiagnostic.create(targetTok.value, filePath: self.filePath, line: targetTok.line, column: targetTok.column))
	end 'recordHiddenCastTarget'

	// Did the lookup find a ranged typealias that some OTHER file declared and did not export? The one
	// decision both cast sites make. It takes the ALREADY-RESOLVED lookup rather than a name, so the
	// body site does not probe the registry a second time for a question its own `targetAlias` already
	// answers — `aliasOf` CONSTRUCTS its `DeclaredAlias`, so a repeat ask is an allocation and not a
	// field read (`RangedAliasRegistry.underlyingInAnyFile` measured what one box per site costs).
	//
	// A name no alias registry knows is not this check's business — it is E3011's, from
	// `denotedCastTargetTag`, which is a sharper answer for a sharper mistake.
	function aliasHiddenFromThisFile(found DeclaredAlias) returns bool
		return match found 'a'
			undeclared gives false
			declared(alias) gives self.everyDeclarationHidden(alias)
		end 'a'
	end 'aliasHiddenFromThisFile'

	// ⭐ **THE CLAIM E2003 MAKES IS ABOUT EVERY DECLARATION OF THE NAME, NOT ABOUT THE ONE `lookup`
	// RETURNED** (P1.9 review) — "a declaration binds this name, elsewhere, and did not export it" is
	// false the moment ANY declaration of it did.
	//
	// `lookup` hands back exactly one, so judging the resolved declaration alone judged the file walk:
	// MEASURED, an exported `Score` in `a.maxon` beside a private one in `b.maxon` REFUSED a third
	// file's `as Score`, while the same two files named `bbb`/`aaa` compiled it. The second probe is
	// paid only on the path that was about to report an error, so a clean build never makes it.
	//
	// ⚠ **`A1f-aliasclash` FIXED THAT ONE DOOR EARLIER, AND THE SECOND PROBE STAYS ANYWAY.** `lookup`
	// now resolves a contested name to a declaration the reader MAY name, so `visibleFrom` above
	// answers `true` on exactly the shapes this probe was built to rescue, and the probe answers `true`
	// wherever it is still reached. Keeping it is what keeps E2003's claim ("no declaration of this
	// name is yours to name") a question about ALL declarations rather than about whichever one
	// `lookup` picked — the two must not be re-coupled, and an E3063 rung that narrows `lookup`'s
	// nameable tier makes this probe load-bearing again without touching this line.
	// See `RangedAliasRegistry.nameableFromAnyFile`.
	// ⭐⭐ **REFUSE A BARE TYPE NAME MORE THAN ONE VISIBLE DECLARATION COULD MEAN — E3063 (N1c).**
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE RAISE SITE COVERS ALL THREE POSITIONS v1 NEEDS TWO FOR**, because shv2 reads a cast TARGET
	// through `parseTypeReference` like any other type reference (`parseCast` calls it directly). So a
	// parameter, a field, a return clause, a body `as` and a top-level constant's `as` all arrive here, and
	// the anchor is the NAME token — the one the user has to rewrite, and v1's anchor
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:1416-1430`).
	//
	// ⚠ **GATED ON `allFilesFolded`, and that is the only correct answer rather than a defensive one**: the
	// tolerant declaration sweep reads declared types through this same routine while the very index this
	// verdict is taken from is still being built, so a refusal there would be a fact about how far the sweep
	// had got — the identical argument the alias-registry arms above it make for themselves.
	//
	// ⚠ **A QUALIFIED REFERENCE NEVER REACHES IT AND NEEDS NO TEST.** `api.Score` is consumed by the dotted
	// arm far above and, were it not, `api.Score` is a registry key ONE file declares — so it is not
	// contested and the candidate set is empty. The qualified spelling is the CURE this diagnostic
	// prescribes; it cannot also be a case of the disease.
	// The NAME is a parameter rather than read off `anchor`, because the two can differ: an extension body's
	// associated type is substituted to the conformer's binding before any arm resolves it
	// (`parseTypeReference`), and it is that BINDING whose meaning has to be unambiguous — the author's
	// `Element` names one thing by construction. `anchor` stays the token a reader is pointed at, which is
	// still the one they wrote.
	function requireUnambiguousTypeName(name ByteArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'theIndexIsStillBeingBuilt'
			return
		end 'theIndexIsStillBeingBuilt'

		let candidates = self.signatures.ambiguousAliasCandidates(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
		if candidates.count() == 0 'exactlyOneMeaning'
			return
		end 'exactlyOneMeaning'

		throw ParseError.ambiguousTypeAlias(String.from(name), candidates: renderCandidateList(candidates), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireUnambiguousTypeName'

	function everyDeclarationHidden(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if self.signatures.aliasVisibleFrom(alias, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'thisOneIsVisible'
			return false
		end 'thisOneIsVisible'

		return not self.signatures.aliasNameableFromAnyFile(alias.name)
	end 'everyDeclarationHidden'

	function parseUnary(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.minus) 'neg'
			let opTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.minus)

			// ⭐ **A LEADING `-` ON A LITERAL IS PART OF THE LITERAL**, not an operator over it. Two
			// separate wrong answers came from treating it as an operator, and both are fixed here:
			//
			//   • `-9223372036854775808` is exactly `i64.min`, but its MAGNITUDE (`i64.max + 1`) does
			//     not fit a positive i64 — so reading the magnitude first reported E2011 on a literal
			//     that is perfectly in range. `parseNegatedInt` already knew this; only the range-bound
			//     reader was asking it. Now the expression parser asks the same decoder.
			//   • `-0.0` compiled to `0.0 - 0.0`, which IEEE-754 says is `+0.0`. A sign flip is
			//     `negatedFloatBits`, and the declaration/range-bound paths always spelled it that way;
			//     only the expression path went through `emitFloatNegation` and lost the zero's sign.
			//
			// It also costs one op fewer for every negative literal, which is why the emitted code for
			// `let x = -5` changes from `mov 5; neg` to a single negative immediate.
			if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) or self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'negatedLiteral'
				return try self.parseNegatedNumericLiteral(opTok)
			end 'negatedLiteral'

			let operand = try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parsePrimary(func), resultUsed: true)

			// ⭐ **A PREFIX `-` IS A QUESTION ABOUT THE NUMBER, SO IT ASKS `numberTagOf` (A4o).** Both
			// PREFIX operators are emitted here rather than through `emitBinOp`/`emitShift`, and reading
			// `tagOf` was therefore a SECOND answer to the operand-domain question those two ask through
			// the door: a float-backed enum's `named` tag is not `tagIsFloat`, so `-Weight.light` took the
			// integer arm and printed `-4612811918334230528` — the negated IEEE-754 bits.
			let operandTag = self.numberTagOf(operand)
			if not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.numeric, tag: operandTag) 'notANumber'
				throw ParseError.nonNumericNegation(typeTagName(operandTag), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
			end 'notANumber'

			// The DECODE is paid only past that refusal, for `emitBinOp`'s stated reason: a rejected
			// program must leave no ops behind. Every non-enum value passes through untouched, so the
			// integer arm below negates exactly what it negated before.
			if tagIsFloat(operandTag) 'float'
				return try self.emitFloatNegation(self.floatBehindEnumValue(operand, anchor: opTok), opTok: opTok)
			end 'float'

			// ⭐⭐ **THE NEGATION OF AN INTEGRAL VALUE IS AN `integer`, WHATEVER TYPE IT WAS SPELLED WITH —
			// the answer `notOperatorFor` already gives the OTHER prefix operator for `named to exitCode`,
			// and it has to be this one's too (BATCH28).** The float arm has returned, so every tag still
			// here is integral, and negating leaves the declared type in every case that had one: `-x` for
			// an `int(0 to 255)` x is not an `int(0 to 255)`, and `-Level.high` is not a `Level`.
			//
			// ⛔ **CARRYING THE OPERAND'S TAG WAS A WRONG ANSWER, AND IT WAS THE NAME COLUMN THAT MADE IT
			// ONE.** `emitUnaryOp` mints with `nameId: UnnamedTypeId` — correct, the result names no type —
			// so a `named` result tag left the value claiming a declared type with the FILLER id in its name
			// column. Every reader of a declared alias gates on the tag and then reads that id, and the
			// filler is INDISTINGUISHABLE from the first real name the file interned (`UnnamedTypeId` is 0
			// and `TypeNameInterner` starts empty), so the alias registry answered about a stranger.
			// **MEASURED:** `enum Level  high = 200` then `print("{-Level.high}")` printed
			// `18446744073709551416` — the unsigned rendering, selected because the file's id 0 happened to
			// be a non-negative ranged alias. Latent while the divide was the domain's only consumer; X11's
			// formatter is what reached it. Pinned by `enums-simple/float-backed-negation`, whose int-backed
			// half is exactly this value.
			return self.emitUnaryOp(operand, op: MaxonUnaryOp.neg, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		end 'neg'

		if self.at(TokenKind.not) 'not'
			let opTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.not)
			let operand = try self.parseUnary(func)

			// `numberTagOf` for the `-` arm's reason, and it decides the same thing `emitShift` decides
			// with it: `not` is a bitwise operator, a double's bits are a sign/exponent/mantissa triple,
			// so a float-backed enum is REFUSED here in `not`'s own words rather than complementing an
			// encoding. Nothing is emitted on this path for a float, so there is no decode to place.
			let operandTag = self.numberTagOf(operand)
			match notOperatorFor(operandTag) 'which'
				op(kind, resultTag) then return self.emitUnaryOp(operand, op: kind, resultTag: resultTag, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
				unknownOperandType then throw ParseError.notOnUnknownType(opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
				nonIntegralOperand then throw ParseError.nonIntegralOperands(opTok.kind.rawValue, operand: typeTagName(operandTag), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
			end 'which'
		end 'not'

		// `async <call>` SPAWNS a green thread and yields a Promise (P1.5-B1a); `await <expr>` blocks on one.
		// Both are prefix operators, alongside `-`/`not`. `async` must be followed by a free call; `await`
		// takes a unary operand (so `await async f()` — spawn then immediately await — parses).
		if self.at(TokenKind.async) 'async'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.async)
			return try self.parseAsyncCall(func)
		end 'async'

		if self.at(TokenKind.await) 'await'
			return try self.parseAwait(func)
		end 'await'

		return try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.parsePrimary(func), resultUsed: true)
	end 'parseUnary'

	// `await <unary>` — the operand grammar and the emit, written ONCE for the TWO positions that admit it:
	// the prefix operator above, and the bare `await p` STATEMENT (`parseStatement`'s `awaitStmt` arm). The
	// cursor sits on `await`.
	//
	// Single-homing it is the point. A statement-position copy is exactly the shape that drifts — it would
	// be spelled `await <primary>` by whoever wrote it next and then `await async f()` would parse in one
	// position and not the other, for no reason anyone could state.
	function parseAwait(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let awaitTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.await)
		let promise = try self.parseUnary(func)
		return try self.emitAwait(func, promise: promise, awaitTok: awaitTok)
	end 'parseAwait'

	// A primary followed by any trailing INDIRECT CALL `(args)` through a function VALUE (P1.5-A1). This
	// is the ONE place a trailing `(` on a function value becomes an indirect call, which is what makes
	// every producer of a function value callable UNIFORMLY: a local (`f(21)`), a call result
	// (`pick()(21)`), and a field read (`h.op(21)`, `o.inner.op(21)`, `self.op(x)`) all reach here holding
	// a `function`-tagged value, and the trailing `(` is applied the same way regardless of how the value
	// was produced. It also chains — `pick()(2)` or a curried `g(1)(2)` — because an indirect call's
	// result is itself a value the loop re-examines.
	//
	// ⚠ The `as`-CAST is NOT handled here — it is a LOOSER operator than unary `-` (see `parseCast`), so
	// `-5 as Age` is `(-5) as Age`. An indirect call, by contrast, binds TIGHTER than `-` (`-f()` is
	// `-(f())`), which is why it stays in this postfix band and the cast does not.
	//
	// `resultUsed` is whether this postfix chain's FINAL value is read — `false` only for a bare-call
	// STATEMENT (`callStmt`), `true` in every expression. It gates ONLY a VOID indirect-call / container-
	// mutator result, and forwarding it uniformly to every hop is correct wherever a void result is
	// TERMINAL: the loop does continue past one — every `.member` is now a hop — but the next hop refuses
	// a `void`-TAGGED value (`structBaseOfReceiver` reports a member access on it, positioned at the
	// member).
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT ARGUMENT DOES NOT COVER THE CONTAINER VOID MUTATORS, AND THE GAP IS AN OVER-ACCEPTANCE
	// RATHER THAN A WRONG VALUE** (measured in review of D11). `emitArrayVoidCall` and
	// `Set.insert` deliberately hand back the RECEIVER — "what the statement still denotes" — so their
	// result is NOT void-tagged and the next hop is perfectly happy: `arr.push(2).count()` as a STATEMENT
	// compiles here, where the oracle refuses it (`E2001 unexpected token: '.'`). Nothing wrong is
	// computed — the chain pushes, counts and discards — and the VALUE spelling is still refused, because
	// `let n = arr.push(2).count()` forwards `resultUsed: true` to the `push` hop, which is E2004 at
	// `push`. The honest statement is that `resultUsed` describes the chain's LAST hop and the
	// receiver-returning arms make the earlier hops' own use invisible to it.
	function parsePostfix(func IrFunction, value ValueId, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		var current = value
		while true 'postfix'
			// A trailing `(` on a FUNCTION value is an indirect call; on anything else it is not a postfix
			// operator at all and the loop ends (a `(` after a non-function value is left for the caller,
			// which is a syntax error there — calling a non-function).
			if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) and self.tagOf(current) == ValueTypeTag.function 'indirectCall'
				current = try self.parseIndirectCall(func, callee: current, resultUsed: resultUsed)
				continue
			end 'indirectCall'

			// ⭐⭐ **A `.member` ON A VALUE — ONE ARM, THE SAME SWITCH A BINDING RECEIVER GOES THROUGH.** The
			// receiver is whatever the expression just built: a `"…"` / `[…]` / `b"…"` LITERAL, a CALL RESULT
			// (`Inner.make(9).get()`), a PARENTHESISED expression (`(i).get()`), a FIELD read
			// (`o.inner.get()`, whose chain stops one hop early exactly so the receiver arrives here), an
			// array element, or a previous hop of this very loop.
			//
			// It used to be THREE arms — array, type-parameter, builtin-conformer — and the set was not a
			// design: it was whichever receiver types someone had happened to need from a value. Everything
			// else `break`ed with the cursor still on the `.`, which is how the nonsensical `E2015:
			// Unsupported: . statement` was reached for `"abc".byteLength()`. Routing every tag through
			// `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` means a type that gains a method gains it in BOTH spellings at once,
			// and a receiver that genuinely has no members gets a positioned diagnostic naming it.
			//
			// A postfix receiver is bound to no name, so it carries no E3019 blame name and no E3070 subject
			// — see `valueReceiver`.
			// ⭐⭐ **AN ENUM ACCESSOR ON A VALUE — `b.c.name`, and it is asked BEFORE the method dispatch for
			// the reason `parseDottedPrimary` asks its own enum arm before the field arm: a declared enum
			// erases to `integer`, so a value of one reaching the method door has no receiver type that could
			// serve `.name`. The two arms are disjoint by the `(` — `atEnumInstanceAccessor` excludes a call,
			// so an enum's real METHOD still goes to `dispatchMethodOnReceiver`.
			//
			// This is the THIRD spelling of an enum member read and it shares the other two's answer: the
			// binding's (`s.ordinal`), the type-qualified case's (`Op.doubleOp.rawValue`) and this one differ
			// ONLY in how the tag is obtained, and all three hand that tag to `emitEnumInstanceAccessor`.
			// The anchor is the `.` itself — a postfix receiver is bound to no name, so there is no earlier
			// token the read can honestly be said to span from.
			if self.at(TokenKind.dot) 'memberAccess'
				if self.atEnumAccessorHopOn(current) 'enumAccessorHop'
					let hopLayout = requireEnumLayout(self.enumMatchFor(current))
					let hopAnchor = self.current()
					current = try self.consumeEnumAccessorHop(hopLayout, tag: self.emitEnumTagOf(current, layout: hopLayout, range: SourceRange.ofToken(hopAnchor)), anchorTok: hopAnchor)
					continue
				end 'enumAccessorHop'

				current = try self.dispatchMethodOnReceiver(func, recv: self.valueReceiver(current, immutableName: EmptySourceText, subjectName: EmptySourceText), resultUsed: resultUsed)
				continue
			end 'memberAccess'

			break
		end 'postfix'
		return current
	end 'parsePostfix'

	// Apply one `as <targetType>` cast to `value` (P1.9). Four shapes, decided by the source value's
	// domain and the target's:
	//   • int → float  — emit `promoteToFloat` (`cvtsi2sd`); the value BECOMES a float. This is the
	//     conversion the old no-op `as` skipped (`explicit-cast-int-to-float`).
	//   • float → int  — emit `trunc` (`cvttsd2si`), the truncating bridge out of the float domain.
	//   • same representation (int→int, float→float) — the value flows on UNCHANGED, keeping its own tag,
	//     UNLESS the target type says something the source's tag does not. Then the cast MINTS a distinct
	//     value carrying the target type (`emitRetaggedCastValue`) — see the two predicates below for
	//     exactly when, and the paragraph after this one for why "unchanged" was a wrong answer.
	//     The value's alias name is what a narrowing check needs, and it rides the recorded site rather
	//     than the tag: when the target is a ranged alias that site is recorded so `InsertRangeChecks`
	//     guards / E3005s it.
	//
	//     ⚠ **A RE-TAG CANNOT BE DONE IN PLACE, AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED.** `let w = x as Word`
	//     leaves `w` and `x` naming ONE SSA value under two declared types (M2 binds a name to a value
	//     and emits no load), so re-recording that value's tag would make `x` unsigned too. Against the
	//     bootstrap oracle, `(w shr 62) + (x shr 62)` of `0xC000000000000000` is `3 + -1`; an in-place
	//     re-tag answers `3 + 3` and a dropped tag `-1 + -1`. Only a DISTINCT value gives both halves
	//     their own answer, which is why this mints one exactly as the per-instance cast already did.
	//
	//     ⚠ **WHAT USED TO BE HERE, AND WHY EACH REASON FELL.** The no-op justified itself by (1) a
	//     `named` value TYPE reaching `lowerMaxonToStd` unresolved and (2) codegen stability. (1) named a
	//     closure capture as its route, which `fieldStorageType` closed for env slots; and it does not
	//     apply to a re-tag at all — a value's TAG is `named` all the time (a `Word` parameter, a
	//     `Word`-returning call result), and `valueTagToStdType` collapses `named` to i64 without help.
	//     Only an OP's stored type reaches `maxonTypeToStdType`, and this mints no op that carries one.
	//     (2) is kept, and it is what the two predicates are for: a cast whose target answers every
	//     question the tag column decides exactly as the source's own type does is still the no-op it
	//     was, byte for byte.
	//
	// `sourceIsBareLocal` is whether the OPERAND was written as a bare reference to a local — the one fact
	// `applyCast` cannot recover for itself (it holds a ValueId, and a ValueId does not say how many names
	// point at it). It gates the declared-alias record the result carries; see `finishCast`.
	function applyCast(value ValueId, targetType MaxonType, asTok Token, targetTok Token, sourceIsBareLocal bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ REJECT A FUNDAMENTALLY-INCOMPATIBLE KIND CAST FIRST (E3009), before any conversion is chosen. A
		// managed aggregate (String/struct/function) has no conversion to or from a scalar, nor to a
		// DIFFERENT aggregate; letting such a cast reach the float/no-op branches below would emit a
		// `promoteToFloat` or a range check on a POINTER, or leave a mismatched drop (a String drop run on a
		// struct box — a double free). See `castHasNoLegalConversion` and `ParseError.invalidCast`.
		// ⭐ The legality guards are NOT here — they moved INTO `convertValueToDeclaredType` with the
		// conversion they protect, so the second position that performs this conversion cannot be given
		// fewer of them than this one. See that function's ⛔⛔ block.
		let sourceTag = self.tagOf(value)

		// The target's ranged-alias declaration, resolved ONCE for EVERY question that needs it — may
		// this file NAME it (P1.9), WHICH alias a narrowing check names, and WHAT its range says about
		// the shift fill. It is hoisted above the float branches because the visibility question is
		// asked on all three paths, and hoisting also retires the float path's own second probe: each
		// ask CONSTRUCTS a `DeclaredAlias`, so a repeat is an allocation and not a field read.
		//
		// ⚠ It reads the RESOLVED target type and not the target TOKEN, which is what makes a qualified
		// `x as Wrapper.Idx` ask about the joined name rather than about `Wrapper`.
		let targetAlias = self.declaredAliasOfType(targetType)
		self.recordHiddenCastTarget(targetAlias, targetTok: targetTok)

		// ⭐ **E3010, ABOVE THE THREE CONVERSION ARMS** — because it is a question about the two DECLARED
		// types and not about the conversion any arm chooses: the int→float widening `i as JsonFloat` is as
		// unneeded as the identity `b as Byte`. RECORDED rather than thrown, so the parse walks on and every
		// unneeded cast in the file is reported by ONE compile — which is exactly what
		// `error.unneeded.multiple-in-one-function` (three of them) and `.multiple-across-functions` (two,
		// in two functions) pin, and what type-casting.md's prose calls a recoverable diagnostic.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND IT IS THE SAME CONTAINMENT THE RANGE-CHECK SITE BELOW ASKS (A4f)** — `rangeCoversRange`,
		// through the two doors' two spellings of the unwrap. That is why a cast reported here NEVER reaches
		// the site record's own elision in a program that builds: containment at an `as` IS this diagnostic,
		// and this diagnostic is an error. The door asks anyway, because the redundancy belongs to E3010
		// rather than to the emitter — and because a compiler that emitted a check it had just proved
		// impossible is the defect A4f exists to delete.
		self.reportUnneededCast(self.valueDenotedAlias(value), target: targetAlias, asTok: asTok)

		return try self.convertValueToDeclaredType(value, sourceTag: sourceTag, targetType: targetType, targetAlias: targetAlias, opTok: asTok, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal, mustCarryTargetType: false)
	end 'applyCast'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONVERSION ITSELF — the three arms and the two records that turn a value OF one declared type
	// INTO a value of another.** Split from `applyCast` above because a SECOND position performs exactly this
	// conversion and must not perform the two steps left behind it: an array literal's element under an
	// `<alias> from […]` head (`admitArrayLiteralElement`), where the target type is not written down at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT STAYS IN `applyCast` IS EVERYTHING THAT IS ABOUT THE `as` THE AUTHOR WROTE — and that is
	// exactly TWO things, both RECORDS rather than guards.** `reportUnneededCast` is E3010 against a cast the
	// source does not contain, so `ByteArray from [10 as Byte, …]` would be refused for a redundancy the
	// runnable oracle accepts (MEASURED on this tree: it compiles and runs). `recordHiddenCastTarget` asks
	// whether the READING file may name the target alias, which the `from` head never asks of the element:
	// the visibility question was already settled where `typealias ByteArray = Array with Byte` was declared.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY LEGALITY GUARD LIVES HERE, BELOW, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION — THEY USED TO SIT IN
	// `applyCast` AND THE SECOND CALLER SILENTLY GOT FEWER OF THEM.** `admitArrayLiteralElement` re-spelled
	// one of the three (`requireCastKindsConvert`) and dropped `requireCastSourceIsNotEnum` and the
	// function-type door, so the element position accepted a conversion this compiler refuses when it is
	// WRITTEN — measured: `Color.green as Byte` is E3009, while `ByteArray from [Color.red, Color.green]`
	// compiled and read back `1`, stamping `Byte` onto an enum. **There was no oracle defence either: the
	// bootstrap also compiles that program and answers `0`, so two compilers disagreed on a program neither
	// refused**, which is worse than either refusing.
	//
	// ⇒ The guards are **inside the conversion**, so a caller gets the whole set by reaching the conversion
	// at all — not by keeping a list in step with this one. The header above used to name two things while
	// `applyCast` asked four, which is this repo's signature bug (an enumeration that fails open at a new
	// door, with a comment asserting the enumeration is complete) in the smallest possible form. A guard
	// added below is a guard both positions get; there is no second list to forget.
	//
	// `sourceTag` is passed rather than re-read for `applyCast`'s stated reason — nothing between its binding
	// and here re-tags `value`, so a re-read is the same answer twice.
	function convertValueToDeclaredType(value ValueId, sourceTag ValueTypeTag, targetType MaxonType, targetAlias DeclaredAlias, opTok Token, sourceIsBareLocal bool, mustCarryTargetType bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ REJECT A FUNDAMENTALLY-INCOMPATIBLE KIND CONVERSION FIRST (E3009), before any arm is chosen. A
		// managed aggregate (String/struct/function) has no conversion to or from a scalar, nor to a
		// DIFFERENT aggregate; letting one reach the float or no-op arms below would emit a `promoteToFloat`
		// or a range check on a POINTER, or leave a mismatched drop (a String drop run on a struct box — a
		// double free). See `castHasNoLegalConversion` and `ParseError.invalidCast`.
		//
		// ⭐ **AN ENUM SOURCE IS ITS OWN REFUSAL** — `requireCastSourceIsNotEnum` carries why both backings
		// share one rule. It is the guard the element door was missing.
		let targetTag = try self.castTargetTag(targetType, asTok: opTok)
		try self.requireCastSourceIsNotEnum(value, targetTag: targetTag, asTok: opTok)
		try self.requireCastTargetIsNotAContainer(value, targetType: targetType, targetTag: targetTag, asTok: opTok)
		try self.requireCastKindsConvert(sourceTag, targetTag: targetTag, asTok: opTok)

		// ⭐ **A FUNCTION-TO-FUNCTION CONVERSION IS THE ONE `requireCastKindsConvert` LETS THROUGH
		// UNEXAMINED.** Both ends are `TypeClass.function`, so `castHasNoLegalConversion` sees the same class
		// and stands aside, and the value then falls all the way to the representational no-op at the end of
		// this function — untouched, and now claiming to be a function type it may share nothing with but its
		// tag. `intish as FloatFn` compiled clean and the integer `intish` returns was read back out of the
		// float return register. The id `castTargetTag` discards is exactly what names the target's
		// signature, which is why this reads `targetType` and not the reduced tag.
		if isFunctionTypeDoor(targetTag, got: sourceTag) 'functionTypeDoor'
			self.recordFunctionTypeDoor(typeNameIdOf(targetType), gotNameId: self.valueNameId(value), door: FunctionTypeDoor.cast, placeName: EmptySourceText, anchor: opTok)
		end 'functionTypeDoor'

		let sourceIsFloat = tagIsFloat(sourceTag)
		let targetIsFloat = self.typeIsFloatDomain(targetType)

		if sourceIsFloat and not targetIsFloat 'floatToInt'
			// ⚠ **A CONCRETE INTEGER TARGET NO LONGER REACHES HERE — it is E3009 above** (`float → int` is
			// lossy; the language's answer is `trunc`/`round`/`floor`/`ceil`). What still does is a target no
			// tag can judge: an opaque type parameter `T`, whose cast `castHasNoLegalConversion` deliberately
			// stands aside from. The truncation is kept for it rather than deleted, because it is the only
			// reading `f as T` has ever had and nothing in the corpus asks for a different one.
			//
			// The trunc RESULT is the narrowed integer, so it — not the float source — is what a ranged INT
			// target checks (`500.7 as Byte` guards `500`). The result has its own Std op, but the guard still
			// anchors at the cast's BLOCK (below) for the same reason an int->int cast does.
			//
			// ⚠ **AND THE SOURCE RANGE A4f'S ELISION READS IS THE `trunc` RESULT'S, WHICH IS NONE — NOT the
			// float operand's.** `trunc` changes the number, so the operand's alias describes a value this
			// door is no longer checking; anchoring on the result is what stops a `float(0.0 to 1.0)` operand
			// vouching for an integer, and a freshly minted result denotes no alias at all.
			let narrowed = self.emitUnaryOp(value, op: MaxonUnaryOp.trunc, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
			self.recordRangeCheckSite(narrowed, targetAlias: targetAlias, line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
			return self.finishCast(narrowed, source: value, targetAlias: targetAlias, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal)
		end 'floatToInt'

		if not sourceIsFloat and targetIsFloat 'intToFloat'
			return self.finishCast(self.promoteToFloat(value, opTok: opTok), source: value, targetAlias: targetAlias, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal)
		end 'intToFloat'

		// ⚠ **THE RANGE-CHECK SITE IS THE SOURCE, NOT THE MINTED RESULT, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS
		// REQUIREMENT** — which is why it is recorded HERE, once, above a branch whose two arms both
		// anchor on the same value. The source and the mint hold the same number by construction, so
		// either would guard correctly at RUN time; but `InsertRangeChecks` decides COMPILE-TIME E3005
		// versus a runtime guard by looking the site's value up in a map built from Std `const` ops (plus
		// `neg` of one). A constant that arrived through the mint's `add` is in neither, so anchoring on
		// the result would silently downgrade `3 as Big` from a diagnostic to a guard that fires at run
		// time — `ranged-typealias.error.unsigned-max-upper-literal-out-of-range` is the case that says
		// so. The float→int cast above anchors on its RESULT for the opposite reason, stated there:
		// `trunc` changes the number, so the source is not the value being checked.
		self.recordRangeCheckSite(value, targetAlias: targetAlias, line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)

		// ⭐ **A CAST THAT CHANGES WHAT THE VALUE'S TYPE SAYS IS A CONVERSION, NOT A RE-TAG.** It yields a
		// DISTINCT result value of the target type and leaves the SOURCE untouched, because the source
		// binding stays usable under its own identity: `let b = aTag as WB.Idx` must not corrupt `aTag`
		// (still `WA.Idx`), and `let w = x as Word` must not make `x` unsigned. FIVE things a target can
		// say, and each has its own predicate rather than one blurred test — a nominal IDENTITY (P1.6-C),
		// a SHIFT FILL, (A1) whether a DIVISION by the value can fail, (X10) at what SIGNEDNESS a division
		// of the value runs, and (`ranged-int-bit-packing`) how the value would be STORED as an array
		// element — because they are answered from different registries and none implies the others.
		// Everything else is the representational no-op below, with no new value and no new op.
		//
		// ⭐ **`mustCarryTargetType` IS THE CALLER'S DEMAND, NOT A SIXTH QUESTION.** The five above are asked
		// of a value to decide whether an `as` MEANS anything; a caller passing `true` has already decided —
		// `admitArrayLiteralElement` reaches this only when the element's storage differs from the instance's
		// slot, and its result has to be TYPED as that element or the literal's own homogeneity check refuses
		// a value it just converted. It is a parameter rather than a predicate here because the fact lives at
		// the POSITION, and no reading of the value could recover it. ⛔ It is deliberately not folded into the
		// five: `applyCast` passes `false`, which is what keeps a plain `x as Byte` a no-op — see
		// `castChangesTheElementPacking`'s ⛔⛔ block for the two programs that broke when every cast retagged.
		if mustCarryTargetType or self.castConvertsPerInstance(value, targetType: targetType) or self.castChangesTheShiftFill(value, targetAlias: targetAlias) or self.castChangesTheDivisionProof(value, targetAlias: targetAlias) or self.castChangesTheDivisionSignedness(value, targetAlias: targetAlias, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal) or self.castChangesTheElementPacking(value, targetAlias: targetAlias) 'retaggingCast'
			return self.finishCast(self.emitRetaggedCastValue(value, targetType: targetType, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok)), source: value, targetAlias: targetAlias, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal)
		end 'retaggingCast'

		// ⭐⭐ **RENAME, NOT MINT — the third answer, and the one a narrowing `as` needs.** A cast to an alias
		// that STORES the value differently has to be visible on the value, because `arrayLiteralInstanceFor`
		// types a bare `[…]` from its first element and nothing downstream can recover what the author wrote.
		// Minting says so and costs an op, a live range and an owned temporary; `retypeValue` says the same
		// thing by rewriting this value's two type columns in place, emitting nothing. Nothing else changes:
		// the bits are identical, and the value had no other reader (below).
		if self.mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage(value, targetAlias: targetAlias, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal) 'renameInPlace'
			self.retypeValue(value, valueType: targetType)
		end 'renameInPlace'

		return self.finishCast(value, source: value, targetAlias: targetAlias, sourceIsBareLocal: sourceIsBareLocal)
	end 'convertValueToDeclaredType'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY THIS VALUE BE RENAMED TO THE TARGET IN PLACE?** Three conditions, each with its own reason,
	// and all three are necessary — each was measured by a program that broke without it.
	//
	//   • **THE STORAGE MUST ACTUALLY DIFFER.** Otherwise the rename says nothing and only costs a wrong
	//     answer later: `x as Integer` must leave a plain `int` a plain `int`.
	//   • **THE SOURCE MUST NOT BE A BARE LOCAL**, because with no mint the "result" IS the source `ValueId`
	//     — renaming it renames the BINDING. `let a = 42` then `let b = a as Byte` must not make `a` a `Byte`
	//     for its later readers. This is the identical condition, for the identical reason, that
	//     `finishCast` withholds its alias record on; a non-bare-local operand is this expression's own
	//     fresh result, which in the parser's on-the-fly SSA has exactly one consumer — the cast.
	//   • **THE SOURCE MUST BE A PLAIN INTEGER.** A managed or opaque type-parameter value is not a number
	//     in a slot; relabelling one as a narrow integer alias would tell the ownership layer it has nothing
	//     to release. `generic-type-substitution/error.a-name-the-scope-does-not-bind-binds-nothing` casts an
	//     opaque `U` to `ExitCode` and is exactly this case.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT "IS THE SOURCE A CONSTANT", AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.** Gating on
	// constants restores the three-answers bug `castChangesTheElementPacking` records — `[7 as Byte, 3 as Byte]`
	// would type differently from `[v as Byte, w as Byte]` and refuse `[v as Byte, 3 as Byte]` outright.
	// Single-use and constant are different questions and only the first is sound; both literals and locals
	// reach the rename here, so all three spellings agree.
	function mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias, sourceIsBareLocal bool) returns bool
		if sourceIsBareLocal 'wouldRenameTheBinding'
			return false
		end 'wouldRenameTheBinding'

		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.integer 'notANumberInASlot'
			return false
		end 'notANumberInASlot'

		return self.storageDiffersFromAlias(value, targetAlias: targetAlias)
	end 'mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage'

	// ⭐ **THE CAST'S RESULT DENOTES THE TARGET ALIAS — recorded on the result, and read by E3010 and by
	// `arrayLiteralInstanceFor`** (the second reader arrived with `ranged-int-bit-packing`; see
	// `ValueDeclaredAliasMap` for what the withhold below costs it, which is NOT a missing diagnostic).
	// It emits no op and moves no bit: `ValueDeclaredAliasMap` exists precisely so that knowing
	// `let b = 42 as Byte` makes `b` a `Byte` costs no instruction, where minting a value to carry it would
	// have cost one at every ranged cast in the corpus.
	//
	// ⚠ **THREE OF THE FOUR ARMS MINT A FRESH VALUE**, which nothing else names, so the record is
	// unambiguous there. The fourth flows the SOURCE value on unchanged, and there the record is WITHHELD
	// when the operand was a bare local: `let a = 42` then `let b = a as Byte` leaves ONE value under two
	// names, and stamping it would make a later `a as Byte` — a narrowing the program genuinely needs —
	// report E3010 against a plain `int`. See `ValueDeclaredAliasMap` for the false negative that trades for.
	function finishCast(result ValueId, source ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias, sourceIsBareLocal bool) returns ValueId
		if result == source and sourceIsBareLocal 'sharedWithABinding'
			return result
		end 'sharedWithABinding'

		self.recordValueDeclaredAlias(result, alias: targetAlias)
		return result
	end 'finishCast'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3010 — "unneeded cast: 'A' already fits in 'B'".** It fires only when BOTH ends are DECLARED
	// ranged typealiases, and that gate is the whole discriminator rather than a convenience: `let x = 42`
	// then `x as Float` is LEGAL — the source is a bare `int`, which names no alias and so states nothing
	// the cast could be repeating — while `let i = identity(42)`, whose callee DECLARES `returns Integer`,
	// then `i as JsonFloat` is E3010. The two programs differ in nothing else, and both reference compilers
	// gate it the same way (v1's `namedAliasNameOrEmpty`, the bootstrap's resolved alias name).
	function reportUnneededCast(source DeclaredAlias, target DeclaredAlias, asTok Token)
		match source 's'
			undeclared then break 's'
			declared(sourceAlias) then self.reportUnneededCastFrom(sourceAlias, target: target, asTok: asTok)
		end 's'
	end 'reportUnneededCast'

	// The second unwrap. A `match` arm carries one expression and there are two unions to open, so the pair
	// reads as the two sentences it is rather than as a nested match.
	function reportUnneededCastFrom(source RangedTypeAlias, target DeclaredAlias, asTok Token)
		match target 't'
			undeclared then break 't'
			declared(targetAlias) then self.reportUnneededCastBetween(source, target: targetAlias, asTok: asTok)
		end 't'
	end 'reportUnneededCastFrom'

	// The verdict and the words. `rangeCoversRange` is the rule; a cast that CAN lose a value is the one
	// that earns its keep and is left alone. The message names the ALIASES the program wrote — not the
	// erased primitives `typeTagName` prints for E3009 — because "already fits in" is a statement about the
	// two ranges, and both spell `int` once erased.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH NAMES SPELL THROUGH `sourceSpelledAliasName`, for the reason `literalOutOfRangeMessage`
	// states and this site did not (X6 review).** A RANGE-CONTESTED name reaches here wearing the
	// compiler's mint, and "the alias is quoted as SOURCE spells it" is a property of the DIAGNOSTIC
	// vocabulary, not of one diagnostic — two E-codes naming one alias two ways is the same fact written
	// twice. MEASURED before the fix, from `bytearray-element-size`'s narrow `Byte`: **`unneeded cast:
	// 'Byte$0_200' already fits in 'ExitCode'`**, which names no line the author wrote.
	function reportUnneededCastBetween(source RangedTypeAlias, target RangedTypeAlias, asTok Token)
		if not rangeCoversRange(target, source: source) 'narrowing'
			return
		end 'narrowing'

		self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.semanticUnneededCast, message: "unneeded cast: '{String.from(sourceSpelledAliasName(source.name))}' already fits in '{String.from(sourceSpelledAliasName(target.name))}'", range: SourceRange.ofToken(asTok))
	end 'reportUnneededCastBetween'

	// ⭐ **THE CAST TARGET'S KIND AS THE AUTHORITY DENOTES IT — not as `maxonTypeTag` reads the
	// SPELLING.** `parseTypeReference` answers a handful of type spellings syntactically (`Self`, the
	// enclosing type's own name, `String`, a type parameter, a generic-instance alias, a function
	// alias) and hands back a bare `MaxonType.named` for everything else — a declared `type` named
	// from OUTSIDE its own body, a declared enum/union, a ranged typealias, a qualified inner alias,
	// and a name nothing declares at all. `maxonTypeTag` reads all five as one tag, so the legality
	// gate was asking about a NAME, and its answer for a name that denotes nothing was the same as
	// for a ranged int alias: convert nothing, flow the value on. `5 as CompletelyMadeUpNameXyz`
	// compiled and returned 5; so did `5 as Point`, which `applyCast`'s own header claims to reject.
	//
	// `denotedNamedType` is THE list of the sources a named type can come from — the same list whose
	// `unknown` verdict is `TypeResolution`'s E3011 — so a cast site and the resolution pass cannot
	// come to disagree about what a name means. There is deliberately no second cascade here.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RESOLVED TYPE IS FOR THIS GATE ONLY and is NOT substituted for `targetType`.** The
	// conversion below still reads the `named` type, because that is what carries the alias NAME a
	// narrowing check records (`recordRangeCheckSite`) and the nominal identity a per-instance cast stamps
	// (`castConvertsPerInstance`). Erasing it here would silently drop both — the erasure is the
	// SIGNATURE's, not the cast's.
	function castTargetTag(targetType MaxonType, asTok Token) returns ValueTypeTag throws ParseError
		let spelledTag = maxonTypeTag(targetType)
		if spelledTag != ValueTypeTag.named 'alreadyConcrete'
			return spelledTag
		end 'alreadyConcrete'

		let nameId = typeNameIdOf(targetType)
		return try self.denotedCastTargetTag(self.internedName(nameId), id: nameId, asTok: asTok)
	end 'castTargetTag'

	// The KIND a NAMED cast target denotes, or E3011 anchored on the `as` — the half of the cast-target
	// decision that ASKS THE AUTHORITY. It is a function and not four lines at each site because BOTH
	// cast sites ask it (`castTargetTag` for a body cast, `requireConstCastTargetKind` for a top-level
	// `let`'s), and a name that denotes nothing must reach the same code with the same wording at the
	// same anchor whichever site met it.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT FLOAT-RESOLVES WHAT IT ANSWERS.** A ranged FLOAT alias denotes a `float`, and the `named`
	// tag cannot say so — `tagIsIntegral` reads every `named` as an int (its own header says why it cannot
	// do otherwise). That is `castHasNoLegalConversion`'s stated precondition, and it decides two things at
	// once: `f as Float` stays the identity conversion instead of being refused as the lossy `float → int`,
	// and `true as Float` says "Cannot cast from bool to **float**" instead of naming the alias's erased
	// `int`. Resolved HERE, at the one door both cast spellings ask, so neither can skip it.
	function denotedCastTargetTag(name ByteArray, id TypeNameId, asTok Token) returns ValueTypeTag throws ParseError
		let denoted = try denotedNamedType(self.signatures, name: name, id: id) otherwise 'undeclared'
			throw ParseError.unknownTypeName(String.from(name), line: asTok.line, column: asTok.column)
		end 'undeclared'

		return self.floatResolvedTagOfName(maxonTypeTag(denoted), name: name)
	end 'denotedCastTargetTag'

	// ⛔⛔ **A CAST WHOSE SOURCE IS AN ENUM/UNION IS E3009, WHATEVER THE TARGET (A4o)** — the oracle's rule,
	// word for word (`Cannot cast from enum to float` / `… to int`, MEASURED on both backings).
	//
	// It cannot be `castHasNoLegalConversion`'s job, because that rule reads only the TAG and an enum wears
	// the same `named` tag a ranged alias does — which is exactly why every enum cast fell through to the
	// representational no-op at the end of `applyCast` and handed back the raw i64 wearing the target's
	// type. For a FLOAT-backed enum that was a silent wrong answer: `Weight.light as Real` printed
	// `4612811918334231000.0`, the bit pattern of 2.5 widened as if it were a magnitude.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH BACKINGS, ONE RULE.** An int-backed enum's no-op returned the right NUMBER, so refusing it
	// looks like a loss — but what that spelling provided was `.rawValue` under another name, an accessor
	// this compiler does not have and the oracle does not reach this way either. Two rules ("a float-backed
	// enum may not be cast, an int-backed one may") would be one fact written twice, and the day
	// `.rawValue` lands the wrong half would still be here.
	function requireCastSourceIsNotEnum(value ValueId, targetTag ValueTypeTag, asTok Token) throws ParseError
		// `valueIsUnion`'s measured allocation guard: `enumMatchFor` boxes a `DeclaredEnum` even to answer
		// `undeclared`, and only a `named` value can be an enum at all.
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return
		end 'notNamed'

		let decl = self.enumMatchFor(value)
		if not enumMatchIsDeclared(decl) 'notAnEnum'
			return
		end 'notAnEnum'

		throw ParseError.invalidCast(requireEnumLayout(decl).kindWord(), targetType: typeTagName(targetTag), line: asTok.line, column: asTok.column)
	end 'requireCastSourceIsNotEnum'

	// May a `sourceTag` value BECOME a `targetTag` one (E3009) — the other half of the cast-target
	// decision, and shared for a sharper reason than tidiness. Sharing `denotedNamedType` alone would
	// have left the two sites agreeing on what a name MEANS while each kept its own copy of what to DO
	// about the answer: the rule, the wording and the anchor, in a `named` arm and a `ConstValue` arm
	// that nothing makes agree. A clause added to one would then be a WRONG ANSWER at the other — a
	// `let X = 5 as T` accepted where `x as T` is refused — and not a compile error anywhere. Mirrors
	// `requireConstOperands`, which is this file's existing answer to the identical split.
	// ⛔⛔ **A CONTAINER IS NOT A RETAGGING, AND THIS SPELLING USED TO BE ACCEPTED AND THEN IGNORED.**
	// `col as RegNumColumn` parsed, produced a value still wearing its ORIGINAL type, and failed later at
	// whatever use site first noticed — naming neither the cast nor its target. Every other cast here is a
	// retagging or a scalar conversion of ONE value; a container is neither.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT COULD NOT BE MADE A RETAGGING, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A REFUSAL RATHER THAN A GAP.**
	// `Array with int(0 to u64.max)` strides EIGHT bytes per element and `Array with int(0 to 63)` strides
	// ONE (`rangedAliasStorageBytes`), so a retag hands every later read a stride the buffer was not written
	// at — the silent wrong answer `specs-shv2/bytearray-element-size.md` measures at length. Converting
	// would mean a second buffer and a range check per element: a real operation, and not one an `as`
	// should perform in silence.
	//
	// ⚠ It reads the TARGET's tag as `castTargetTag` resolved it, so `as SomeAlias` is judged by what the
	// alias denotes rather than by its spelling, and names the two types as the SOURCE writes them —
	// `invalidCast`'s tag words would say `Cannot cast from int to int` here.
	function requireCastTargetIsNotAContainer(value ValueId, targetType MaxonType, targetTag ValueTypeTag, asTok Token) throws ParseError
		if targetTag != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'theTargetIsNotAContainer'
			return
		end 'theTargetIsNotAContainer'

		let written = String.from(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(targetType))) if maxonTypeTag(targetType) == ValueTypeTag.named else self.displayTypeNameOf(maxonTypeTag(targetType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(targetType))
		throw ParseError.castTargetIsAContainer(self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), targetType: written, line: asTok.line, column: asTok.column)
	end 'requireCastTargetIsNotAContainer'

	function requireCastKindsConvert(sourceTag ValueTypeTag, targetTag ValueTypeTag, asTok Token) throws ParseError
		if castHasNoLegalConversion(sourceTag, target: targetTag) 'incompatibleKinds'
			throw ParseError.invalidCast(typeTagName(sourceTag), targetType: typeTagName(targetTag), line: asTok.line, column: asTok.column)
		end 'incompatibleKinds'
	end 'requireCastKindsConvert'

	// Does this int→int `as` convert per-instance identity (P1.6-C) — either STAMPING a per-instance target
	// onto the result (`x as StrWrapper.Idx`) or STRIPPING a per-instance source into a plain target
	// (`idx as ExitCode`)? Only then does the cast mint a distinct value; a plain-to-plain `as` stays a no-op.
	function castConvertsPerInstance(value ValueId, targetType MaxonType) returns bool
		if maxonTypeTag(targetType) == ValueTypeTag.named and self.signatures.isPerInstanceAlias(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(targetType)), readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'stampTarget'
			return true
		end 'stampTarget'
		return self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.named and self.signatures.isPerInstanceAlias(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)), readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'castConvertsPerInstance'

	// ⭐ **DOES THIS int→int `as` CHANGE HOW A RIGHT SHIFT OF THE VALUE FILLS?** The shift rule reads the
	// value's TAG (`valueIsUnsigned`), so a cast that drops the target alias drops the SIGNEDNESS with it —
	// MEASURED against the bootstrap oracle, `let w = x as Word` (`Word = int(0 to u64.max)`) then
	// `w shr 62` of `0xC000000000000000` answered **-1 here and 3 there**, and the other direction
	// (an unsigned parameter cast to a signed alias) answered **3 here and -1 there**. A silent wrong
	// answer, in the one place no diagnostic can catch it.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE FILL IS THE ONLY ANSWER THAT CAN MOVE INSIDE THIS GATE, AND THAT IS WHY THE GATE IS A
	// DECLARED RANGED ALIAS AND NOTHING ELSE.** A value's tag also picks its DROP routing
	// (`decrefCalleeFor`), its builtin-conformance dispatch (`builtinConformerOfType`) and its float
	// register class — so a broader "always re-tag" would be a different change with a different blast
	// radius. Inside the gate all three are provably fixed: a ranged INT alias is unmanaged (its name is
	// an alias, never an enum/union — `resolveNamedAlias`'s header states a name is one of those, never
	// two — so `valueIsBoxedUnion` cannot start answering `true`), it reduces to `integer` for the
	// conformer exactly as a bare `int` does, and it is not float (a float alias is refused below, since
	// its bounds are f64 BIT PATTERNS that the integer range predicates would read as signed integers).
	// A target OUTSIDE the gate is left alone for the same reason it always was — a `structRef` target
	// would re-point a drop at another type's destructor, and `ExitCode` would trade a fill that cannot
	// differ (see the observability rule below) for a conformer it does not have (`notAConformer`).
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT ASKS WHETHER THE FILL CAN BE OBSERVED, NOT MERELY WHETHER THE SIGNEDNESS DIFFERS.** An
	// arithmetic and a logical shift differ only on an operand whose bit 63 is set, so a target whose
	// range cannot hold such a value answers the same under either reading — `bitwise-operators.md` says
	// exactly this ("a value of `int(0 to 100)` is provably non-negative, so an arithmetic and a logical
	// shift of it agree bit for bit"), and the cast is guarded INTO that range by the site
	// `recordRangeCheckSite` records, so the premise is enforced rather than hoped for. That is what keeps
	// `x as Byte`, `x as Age` and `x as U32` the byte-for-byte no-ops they have always been while
	// `x as Word` is not.
	//
	// It is handed the ALREADY-RESOLVED alias (`declaredAliasOfType`) rather than the target type: the
	// narrowing-check site needs the identical resolution, and `applyCast` is where the two meet.
	function castChangesTheShiftFill(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias) returns bool
		return match targetAlias 'a'
			declared(alias) gives self.aliasChangesTheShiftFill(value, alias: alias)
			// Not a declared ranged alias — a bare primitive, a struct, a name nothing declares. Nothing
			// the tag column would read differently, so the cast stays the no-op. (⚠ `ExitCode` was on this
			// list until X6 gave it a range; it now arrives as a DECLARED alias and is answered above —
			// unsigned, and not the `u64.max`-upper shape, so `aliasChangesTheShiftFill` still says no.)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'a'
	end 'castChangesTheShiftFill'

	// The half of the question above that is about the ALIAS. Split out because the two arms below read
	// as the two sentences they are.
	function aliasChangesTheShiftFill(value ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'floatAlias'
			return false
		end 'floatAlias'

		if not rangeIsUnsigned(alias.low) 'targetIsSigned'
			// A signed range admits negatives, so bit 63 is reachable and the two fills genuinely differ.
			// The cast is load-bearing exactly when the source reads UNSIGNED and the target does not.
			return self.valueIsUnsigned(value)
		end 'targetIsSigned'

		// The target is unsigned. A signed source can only be told apart from it by a bit-63-set value,
		// which an unsigned range admits ONLY in the `u64.max`-upper shape — the same shape the runtime
		// cascade and the compile-time literal check both key on, asked here through the same predicate
		// so a fourth reading of it cannot appear.
		return not self.valueIsUnsigned(value) and rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(alias.low, high: alias.high)
	end 'aliasChangesTheShiftFill'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS int→int `as` CHANGE WHETHER A DIVISION BY THE VALUE CAN FAIL? (A1)** — the third thing a
	// cast target can say, and the one that makes `let d = z as NonZero` the language's escape hatch from a
	// checked divide rather than a no-op the divide cannot see.
	//
	// ⭐ **WITHOUT IT THE ESCAPE HATCH IS UNREACHABLE, AND THE CANONICAL SPELLING OF IT IS THE CORPUS'S OWN.**
	// `divisorProof` asks `declaredAliasOfValue`, which is tag-guarded on `named` — so a cast that
	// leaves the value's tag `integer` leaves the alias invisible, and `100 / d` after `let d = z as NonZero`
	// would demand a `try`. `specs-shv2/ranged-typealias.md`'s `cast-guard-fires-before-the-division` and
	// `field-default-then-guarded-casts` spell exactly that and were green before A1; `specs/arithmetic-operators.md:37`
	// documents it as THE way out. A rung that broke the spelling of its own exemption would be no exemption.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SAFETY ARGUMENT IS `aliasChangesTheShiftFill`'s, VERBATIM, BECAUSE THE GATE IS THE SAME GATE** —
	// a DECLARED RANGED INT alias and nothing else. Inside it a value's other tag-dispatched answers are
	// provably fixed: the alias is unmanaged (so `decrefCalleeFor` cannot start routing a drop), it reduces to
	// `integer` for `builtinConformerOfType` exactly as a bare `int` does, and it is not float (a float alias
	// is refused below, because its bounds are f64 BIT PATTERNS and — the sharper reason here — a
	// `named`-tagged float would be given `StdType.i64` and land in a GPR).
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT ASKS WHETHER THE PROOF CAN MOVE, NOT MERELY WHETHER THE TARGET EXCLUDES ZERO.** A source
	// already provably non-zero (a non-zero literal) gains nothing from the retag, and a target that ADMITS
	// zero can only ever weaken the answer — so neither mints. That is what keeps `x as Byte`, `x as Percent`
	// and `x as AsciiCode` the byte-for-byte no-ops they have always been while `z as NonZero` is not.
	function castChangesTheDivisionProof(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias) returns bool
		return match targetAlias 'a'
			declared(alias) gives self.aliasChangesTheDivisionProof(value, alias: alias)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'a'
	end 'castChangesTheDivisionProof'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS int→int `as` CHANGE THE SIGNEDNESS A DIVISION OF THE VALUE RUNS AT, IN THE ONE
	// SPELLING NO RECORD CAN CARRY? (X10 review)** — the FOURTH of `applyCast`'s five questions, and the
	// one X10 added a READER for (`integerValueDomain`) without adding any writer.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **MEASURED AGAINST THE BOOTSTRAP ORACLE, AND IT WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** With
	// `Word = int(0 to u64.max)` holding `u64.max` and a divisor cast to a non-negative alias that
	// ADMITS ZERO (`int(0 to 255)`, or the builtin `ExitCode`), `try (w / d)` answered **0 here and
	// 6148914691236517205 there** — the signed reading of `-1 / 8` against the unsigned reading the
	// oracle picks from the same two declared ranges.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE OTHER THREE SPELLINGS NEED NO MINT, AND THAT IS WHY THIS ONE IS NARROW.** A cast of a call
	// result, of a literal or of another cast's temporary is recorded in `ValueDeclaredAliasMap`, and
	// `integerValueDomain` reads it through `domainDeclaringAlias` — no new value, no new op, no
	// codegen movement anywhere the answer does not actually change. `finishCast` WITHHOLDS that record
	// for exactly one shape: an operand that was a BARE LOCAL REFERENCE, where the result IS the source
	// and stamping it would make `a` claim the alias `b` was given. A fresh value is the only identity
	// that shape can be given, so it is the only shape that mints.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT COSTS AN EXTRA OP ONLY WHERE A RANGE GUARD IS ALREADY BEING EMITTED.** It fires only
	// when the source's domain is `signed` — the source declares no non-negative range — against an
	// unsigned target, which is exactly the containment `rangeCoversRange` cannot prove, so A4f's
	// elision never applies and the cast emits a runtime guard (or an E3005) regardless. `n as Byte`
	// off an already-non-negative alias, and `3 as Byte`, stay the byte-for-byte no-ops they were.
	function castChangesTheDivisionSignedness(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias, sourceIsBareLocal bool) returns bool
		if not sourceIsBareLocal 'theRecordCanCarryIt'
			return false
		end 'theRecordCanCarryIt'

		return match targetAlias 'a'
			declared(alias) gives self.aliasChangesTheDivisionSignedness(value, alias: alias)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'a'
	end 'castChangesTheDivisionSignedness'

	// The half of the question above that is about the ALIAS, split out exactly as its two siblings are.
	//
	// ⚠ The float arm is `aliasChangesTheDivisionProof`'s, for its reason: a ranged FLOAT alias's bounds
	// are f64 BIT PATTERNS, which `rangeIsUnsigned` would read as a signed integer — and a `named`-tagged
	// float would be given `StdType.i64` and land in a GPR.
	function aliasChangesTheDivisionSignedness(value ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'floatAlias'
			return false
		end 'floatAlias'

		if not rangeIsUnsigned(alias.low) 'targetAdmitsNegatives'
			return false
		end 'targetAdmitsNegatives'

		return self.integerValueDomain(value) == IntegerValueDomain.signed
	end 'aliasChangesTheDivisionSignedness'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS `as` MOVE THE VALUE BETWEEN THE BYTE AND SUB-BYTE STORAGE LADDERS?** The FIFTH of
	// `applyCast`'s five questions — it was the fourth until X10's review inserted the division-SIGNEDNESS
	// one ahead of it, and the count is stated in `applyCast` alone so the two cannot drift apart again.
	// An `Array with T` literal types its element off the values it is given, so a cast that moves an
	// element between the two ladders has to be visible ON THE VALUE.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS WIDENED TO COMPARE THE WHOLE ELEMENT STRIDE AND THAT WAS WITHDRAWN — MEASURED, TWICE, AND
	// DO NOT RETRY IT WITHOUT READING THIS.** The widening looked obviously right: this predicate's headline
	// asks how a value is STORED, a stride is `elementStrideOf(packedBits, storageBytes)`, and comparing only
	// the packed half is half of its own sentence. It made `[10 as Byte, …]` mint an `Array with Byte`, which
	// `specs/codegen-internals.md`'s `rdata-byte-array-uses-i8` pins and the runnable oracle agrees with.
	//
	// **It broke two programs, because `emitRetaggedCastValue` EMITS AN OP** (`binOp add 0`) — so firing on
	// every narrowing ranged cast in the language creates a SECOND VALUE at each one:
	//   • `ranged-typealias/many-live-guarded-casts` — 24 `let w = widen(i)` then 24 `let s = w as Small`.
	//     Source and retag are both live across the same point, so the function needed **10 more registers
	//     than exist** and no longer compiled at all (E5001).
	//   • `generic-type-substitution/error.a-name-the-scope-does-not-bind-binds-nothing` — its
	//     `(try w.first() otherwise panic(…)) as ExitCode` casts an OPAQUE type-parameter value. Splitting it
	//     in two left the opaque source an owned temporary `main` must release, which `main` reserves no
	//     layout descriptor for: a spurious E2015 during the PARSE, which preempted the E3005 that case
	//     exists to pin. **A refusal appeared to fail open when what actually happened is that an earlier
	//     tier rejected the program first, for a reason invented by this predicate.**
	//
	// ⇒ **THE BYTE-WIDTH HALF IS SERVED BY A RENAME INSTEAD, AND THAT IS WHERE IT BELONGS** —
	// `mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage` + `retypeValue`, which rewrites the value's two type columns in place and
	// emits nothing. Both broken programs stay fixed because neither reaches it: the 24-cast function casts
	// BARE LOCALS (renaming one would rename the binding, so it is refused), and the opaque-`U` cast is not a
	// plain integer (relabelling it would tell the ownership layer it has nothing to release). ⇒ **the answer
	// was never "which casts should mint" but "minting is the wrong instrument for a representational
	// no-op".** This predicate stays narrow: it fires only where the two PACKING ladders differ, which is a
	// question about the value's own type and genuinely does need a distinct value.
	//
	// ⚠ And the obvious narrowing does not work: gating on a CONSTANT source restores the exact three-answers
	// inconsistency the paragraph below records — it would make `[7 as Byte, 3 as Byte]` an `Array with Byte`,
	// `[v as Byte, w as Byte]` an `Array with int`, and `[v as Byte, 3 as Byte]` a false *"mixed element
	// types"* refusal of a legal program. Single-use and constant are different questions; only the first is
	// sound, and it is the one the rename asks.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **WITHOUT IT THE SAME WRITTEN TYPE PRODUCED THREE DIFFERENT ANSWERS — MEASURED, and one of them
	// REFUSED A LEGAL PROGRAM.** The alternative was to read the alias out of `ValueDeclaredAliasMap`, and
	// that map cannot answer this: `finishCast` deliberately WITHHOLDS the entry when the result IS the
	// source and the source is a bare local (one value under two names — see that map's header). So the
	// alias survived a cast of a LITERAL and vanished on a cast of a LOCAL, for `N = int(0 to 15)`:
	//   • `[7 as N, 3 as N]`  →  `Array with N`,   element_size **-4**
	//   • `[v as N, w as N]`  →  `Array with int`, element_size **8**
	//   • `[v as N, 3 as N]`  →  **E2015 "mixed element types"** — on a literal whose two elements are
	//     written with the SAME type, so the refusal is of a legal program and its message is false.
	// Minting settles all three at once, because the result then carries the alias in its own TAG and the
	// element type is read off the VALUE rather than off a record a sharing rule may withhold. ⭐ It is also
	// what keeps `ValueDeclaredAliasMap` to the ONE reader its safety argument assumes: a missing entry there
	// is again only a missing E3010, never a silently different representation.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks the SOURCE as well as the target, so it fires only where the answer would actually move —
	// a cast between two aliases that pack identically stays the no-op it always was. That narrowness is
	// load-bearing, not incidental: every firing costs an op and a live range (see the ⛔⛔ block above).
	function castChangesTheElementPacking(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias) returns bool
		return match targetAlias 'a'
			declared(alias) gives rangedAliasPackedBits(alias.low, high: alias.high) != self.elementPackingOfValue(value)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'a'
	end 'castChangesTheElementPacking'

	// The packed width the value ALREADY carries — the source half of the question above. A value with no
	// declared ranged alias (a bare `int`, a call result, a parameter typed `int`) is byte-strided, which is
	// `NotSubBytePacked`, so a cast to a packing alias is a genuine move between the two ladders.
	function elementPackingOfValue(value ValueId) returns PackedBitWidth
		return match self.declaredAliasOfValue(value) 'a'
			declared(alias) gives rangedAliasPackedBits(alias.low, high: alias.high)
			undeclared gives NotSubBytePacked
		end 'a'
	end 'elementPackingOfValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **WOULD THIS VALUE BE STORED DIFFERENTLY FROM WHAT THIS ALIAS SAYS?** — the WHOLE element stride,
	// both ladders. Two callers: `admitArrayLiteralElement`, which asks it of a stated instance's element slot,
	// and `mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage`, which asks it of an `as` target.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS NOT ONE OF `applyCast`'s FIVE MINT QUESTIONS, AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE.** It was, briefly, and
	// the ⛔⛔ block on `castChangesTheElementPacking` records the two programs that stopped compiling: an arm
	// of that list MINTS a value, and asking this there doubled the live values at every narrowing ranged cast
	// in the language. The question itself is asked freely — `mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage` puts it to every
	// `as` — because its yes now costs a RENAME rather than a value.
	//
	// ⚠ The two predicates are not redundant and neither subsumes the other: `castChangesTheElementPacking`
	// asks whether an `as` moved a value between the byte and sub-byte LADDERS, which is a question about the
	// value's own type; this asks whether a value fits the slot an instance declares, which is a question
	// about a position. `ByteArray from [10, 20, 30]` is the case that separates them — the packing answer is
	// "no" (both sides are byte-strided) while the storage answer is "yes" (8 bytes into a 1-byte slot).
	function storageDiffersFromAlias(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias) returns bool
		return match targetAlias 'a'
			declared(alias) gives aliasElementStride(alias) != self.elementStrideOfValue(value)
			undeclared gives false
		end 'a'
	end 'storageDiffersFromAlias'

	// The stride the value ALREADY occupies — the source half of the question above. A value with no declared
	// ranged alias (a bare `int`, a call result, a parameter typed `int`) takes a full machine word.
	function elementStrideOfValue(value ValueId) returns ElementStride
		return match self.declaredAliasOfValue(value) 'a'
			declared(alias) gives aliasElementStride(alias)
			undeclared gives MachineWordBytes
		end 'a'
	end 'elementStrideOfValue'

	// The half of the question above that is about the ALIAS, split out exactly as
	// `aliasChangesTheShiftFill` is and for the same reason: the arms read as the sentences they are.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS ONLY THE ZERO HALF OF THE PROOF, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE AFTER A1x.** A cast target can now
	// move the OTHER hazard too — `z as SmallCount` for an `int(0 to 100)` would tell a `mod` its divisor is
	// never `-1` and save it the overflow guard — and this predicate answers `false` for it, because the
	// target admits zero. The consequence is a needless 4-op guard on that one shape and NOTHING else: no
	// wrong answer, no lost diagnostic, and the divide there is already paying a full checked expansion.
	// Widening it would cost far more than it saves — every zero-admitting narrowing cast in the language
	// (`x as Byte`, `x as Percent`, `x as AsciiCode`) would start MINTING a value where it is a byte-for-byte
	// no-op today, which is the property the header above exists to protect.
	function aliasChangesTheDivisionProof(value ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'floatAlias'
			return false
		end 'floatAlias'

		if not rangeExcludesZero(alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: false) 'admitsZero'
			return false
		end 'admitsZero'

		return divisorProofZeroVerdict(self.divisorProof(value)) != DivisorZeroVerdict.neverZero
	end 'aliasChangesTheDivisionProof'

	// ⭐ **EMIT A REPRESENTATIONALLY-NO-OP CAST AS A DISTINCT VALUE.** The conversion moves no bits
	// (int→int), so its RESULT is `value + 0` — one op, minted with a FRESH ValueId typed as the target —
	// which leaves the source ValueId's identity untouched (an in-place re-tag would corrupt `aTag` when a
	// later use reads it as `WA.Idx`, and would make `x` unsigned after `let w = x as Word`). ONE emitter
	// for both reasons a cast mints: a per-instance IDENTITY and a changed SHIFT FILL. Both directions of
	// the per-instance case ride it — STAMP (`x as StrWrapper.Idx`, result typed the per-instance alias,
	// sidestepping the bootstrap's E3010 "unneeded cast" quirk) and STRIP (`idx as ExitCode`, result the
	// plain target, so a per-instance Idx decays to the numeric it is). `foldConstOperands` folds the op
	// to a single `binOpImm add 0`.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND SINCE G17 IT HAS A CALLER THAT IS NOT A CAST AT ALL: `emitFieldLoad`'s PROMISE ARM.** Reading
	// `p.inner` off an unboxed promise is the same problem in different words — the value already IS the
	// answer, and what is wanted is a DISTINCT ValueId wearing the field's declared type, so the handle does
	// not arrive carrying the promise's own marks (`valueOwnsGreenThread`, `valueIsPromise`), which would
	// enrol a second owner of one green thread and double-drop it. This is the compiler's one debugged
	// spelling of "the same bits, a new identity, a new type"; a second would be a second thing to get right.
	// ⚠ Its `range` is a SourceRange rather than the cast's `asTok`, for that caller: a field read is
	// anchored `SourceRange.between(baseTok, last: memberTok)` and has no single token to point at.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RECORDS NO RANGE-CHECK SITE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** The site is the SOURCE, not this
	// mint, so `applyCast` records it above the branch — see the ⚠ there for why anchoring on the result
	// would downgrade `3 as Big` from an E3005 to a runtime guard.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PARSER'S CONSTANT VIEW CROSSES THE MINT**, and that is not an optimization either. `x + 0`
	// is `x`, so a constant source names a constant result — and the parser's const table is what turns a
	// shift COUNT into a compile-time decision (`emitShift` rejects a negative count and folds an
	// over-wide one, both guarded on `constIsKnown`). Without this, `a shl (0 - 1 as Word)` would trade
	// its diagnostic for a runtime guard purely because the cast started minting. `recordFold` is what
	// `emitBinOp` does with the same `+`, so the two agree on what a folded `add` knows.
	//
	// ⚠ The base-range compatibility check the spec names (`fooIdx as BarContainer.Idx` requires the SAME
	// low/high) is inert at this rung — every inner alias is `int(0 to u64.max)` — and its diagnostic form is
	// deferred with the first narrower inner range.
	function emitRetaggedCastValue(value ValueId, targetType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValueOfType(targetType)
		let zero = self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.binOp(result, lhs: value, rhs: zero, op: MaxonBinOp.add), range: range)

		if self.constIsKnown(value) 'constantSource'
			self.recordConst(result, folded: self.constOf(value))
		end 'constantSource'

		return result
	end 'emitRetaggedCastValue'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A RANGE-CHECK SITE IS APPENDED** for a position whose declared alias the parser
	// already holds — an `as` cast, a struct-literal field, a field store, a self-field store, a field's
	// declared default, an array element, and an argument at an OPAQUE type parameter (ARRR, whose alias is
	// the receiver instance's substituted type argument rather than anything the callee declared). Each
	// caller decides exactly ONE thing: which alias (through
	// `declaredAliasOfType`, the one asker, so every door resolves the question the same way).
	// `undeclared` — a bare primitive, a struct, a name nothing declares — records nothing. (⚠ `ExitCode`
	// was in that list until X6 gave it a range; every door below now records a site for it, which is the
	// whole of what that rung closed.)
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO DECIDE A SECOND THING — WHICH HALVES THE SITE OWES — AND SINCE `A1f-arrayelem` THERE
	// IS NOTHING LEFT TO DECIDE.** Every door that reaches here owes BOTH halves; the array element was the
	// last that did not, and its guard now goes at the store. A `guard` field carrying one value at every
	// site is a fact written twice, one copy of which can go stale against the prose — which is exactly what
	// happened to the five copies of the door rule A1f's review had to rewrite. `RangeCheckGuard` itself
	// stays: the CALL-ARGUMENT door still owes the compile-time half alone, and it asks `guardVerdict`
	// directly, holding no site (`InsertRangeChecks.checkArgAgainstParamRange`).
	//
	// ⭐ **EVERY ONE OF THOSE DOORS STILL HOLDS THE UN-ERASED TYPE, which is precisely why they can resolve
	// for themselves and the CALL-ARGUMENT door cannot.** A cast names its target outright, and a struct
	// field's declared type is never rewritten (`TypeResolution.checkStructFieldTypes` validates and
	// discards its result), so `ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf` still answers `named(Percent)` for the whole
	// compile. A parameter's does not survive `resolveTypes` — see `recordConstantArgRangeChecks`.
	//
	// `block` is the value's CHECKING block (`self.currentBlock`), so a runtime guard runs on the path the
	// value is on — a cast in an unselected ternary arm / if-branch does not fire it. `maxonOpPos` is WHERE
	// in that block: the block's op count RIGHT NOW, which is the ordinal the op that spends the value is
	// about to take, because every door here records BEFORE it emits that op (`applyCast` above its two
	// conversion arms, `requireFieldType` before its caller stores, `fillFieldDefault` before the literal
	// reaches the slot). Anchoring on the block alone put the guard at the block's END and let the whole
	// rest of the body run first — see `RangeCheckSite`.
	//
	// It takes the RESOLVED alias rather than the declared type because `applyCast` already holds one: its
	// shift-fill predicate asks the identical question of the identical type, and `signatures.aliasOf`
	// allocates per call.
	//
	// The anchor arrives as line/column rather than a `Token` because the field-DEFAULT door has no token
	// of its own: the default is a literal from a declaration that may live in another file, so its
	// diagnostic is anchored on the struct literal that took it, which that door holds as a `SourceRange`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A SITE THE DESTINATION PROVABLY ADMITS IS NOT A SITE AT ALL (A4f), AND IT IS DECIDED BELOW
	// BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE ALL FIVE ALIAS-NAMED DOORS MEET.** The rule is `rangeCoversDenotedRange`, which
	// is the containment E3010 reports, asked of the pair every door already holds — so *"unneeded cast:
	// 'Byte' already fits in 'ExitCode'"* and the runtime cascade behind it can no longer be two answers to
	// one question. (That cascade spells `0 ≤ x ≤ 4294967295` on WINDOWS only — `ExitCode`'s high bound is
	// platform-shaped, `Project.exitCodeAliasHigh` — and the bound it carries is not what makes it dead; the
	// containment is, on every target.) Removing the redundant cast used to ADD that cascade, on a value whose
	// declared type cannot fail it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT SKIPS THE COMPILE-TIME HALF TOO, AND THAT COSTS NO DIAGNOSTIC.** `InsertRangeChecks` reports
	// E3005 only for a value it can FOLD, and a folded literal denotes no alias (`valueDenotedAlias` falls
	// through to its `integer` tag) — so containment cannot hold for one, and every literal door still
	// records. Where a folded constant DOES carry an alias it got it from a cast, whose own site reported
	// the E3005 already; a range inside the destination cannot then put it outside the destination.
	function recordRangeCheckSite(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
		match targetAlias 'a'
			declared(alias) then self.recordRangedTargetSite(value, target: alias, line: line, column: column)
			undeclared then break 'a'
		end 'a'
	end 'recordRangeCheckSite'

	// ⚠ **THE SOURCE RANGE IS RESOLVED HERE AND NOT ONE FRAME UP, BECAUSE A DESTINATION THAT NAMES NO
	// RANGED ALIAS MUST NOT PAY FOR IT.** `valueDenotedAlias` CONSTRUCTS a `DeclaredAlias`, and the doors
	// above it are the ordinary field store and array-element store — almost every one of which writes an
	// `int` slot and has nothing to prove. MEASURED on the scale ladder: asking unconditionally read
	// **+3,848 `phase:parse` allocations at rung 5, doubling per rung**, for a question whose answer was
	// `undeclared` at every one of those sites.
	function recordRangedTargetSite(value ValueId, target RangedTypeAlias, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber)
		if rangeCoversDenotedRange(target, source: self.valueDenotedAlias(value)) 'provablyInRange'
			return
		end 'provablyInRange'

		self.rangeCheckSites.push(RangeCheckSite.create(value, aliasName: target.name, block: self.currentBlock.id, maxonOpPos: self.currentBlock.opRefs.count(), line: line, column: column))
	end 'recordRangedTargetSite'

	// Is `t` in the FLOAT domain — the bare `float` keyword, or a `named` type that denotes a ranged
	// float alias? Everything else (bare int, an int alias, `ExitCode`, a struct/String/function) is
	// the integer/pointer domain. Exhaustive over `ValueTypeTag`, so a new tag cannot slip through as a
	// silent `false`. `namedIsFloatAlias` is asked only on the `named` arm, where its interner
	// precondition holds.
	function typeIsFloatDomain(t MaxonType) returns bool
		return match maxonTypeTag(t) 'k'
			boolean to integer gives false
			float gives true
			named gives self.namedIsFloatAlias(typeNameIdOf(t))
			// `exitCode to genericInstance` extends the old `exitCode to function` past the P1.6-A tail:
			// a type parameter is an opaque i64 and a generic instance is a struct pointer, neither in the
			// float domain.
			exitCode to genericInstance gives false
			// A Character is a record pointer, not a number in any domain — `string`'s answer, which
			// `exitCode to genericInstance` above covers for every other aggregate.
			character gives false
			// An existential's value half is a box pointer — `character`'s answer, for `character`'s reason.
			interfaceRef gives false
		end 'k'
	end 'typeIsFloatDomain'

	// ⭐⭐ **EMIT `a / b` OR `a mod b` — THE ONE PLACE THE DIVIDE-BY-ZERO POLICY LIVES (A1).** Three ways out,
	// decided per site from what the compiler can prove about the DIVISOR:
	//
	//   • **PROVABLY ZERO** (a folded `0`, `0.0` or `-0.0`) — E3103, at compile time. A divide that can only
	//     ever fail is a bug, and a catchable error would offer the program a recovery from one.
	//   • **PROVABLY NON-ZERO** (a non-zero folded constant, or a ranged type whose range excludes 0) — a
	//     BARE `binOp`, byte for byte what this compiler emitted before `/` became fallible. No guard, no
	//     branch, no call. This is the language's escape hatch and its cost is exactly zero.
	//   • **ANYTHING ELSE** — the divide THROWS `__DivisionByZeroError`, so it must sit in a `try` (E3057,
	//     reported by `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall` when it does not).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE THROWING FORM IS EMITTED CALL-SHAPED, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN STYLISTIC.**
	// `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` converts only a `call` or an `await`, and routes the `literal to asyncCall`
	// range — which CONTAINS `binOp` — to `rejectTryTarget`. A `binOp` divide is therefore categorically
	// unreachable from `try`, whatever else were done. Riding the ordinary `call` → `tryCall` → error-flag ABI
	// also means the `try` requirement, the `otherwise (e)` binding, the propagate path and the fork's drop
	// sets are the ones every other throwing callee already has; nothing here is new plumbing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `try`-CONTEXT STACK, AND SHV2 DOES NOT NEED ONE.** The bootstrap asks
	// `_inTryContext || _tryBlockStack.Count > 0` HERE and raises E3057 itself
	// (`2-Parser.cs:23709-23716`). In shv2 a divide outside a `try` simply stays a plain `call` — nothing
	// rewrites it — and `validatePlainCall` reports E3057 off the same `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` every other
	// throwing builtin is reported through. One gate instead of two, and it cannot disagree with the parser's
	// E3055 accept.
	function emitDivOrMod(dividend ValueId, divisor ValueId, op MaxonBinOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The OPERAND rule first, for `emitShift`'s stated reason: a rejected program must leave no ops
		// behind, and the proof below would otherwise decide the fallibility of operands that are not numbers.
		// Through `numberTagOf`, and the DECODE below it, for `emitBinOp`'s reason (A4o): a `/` is a question
		// about the number, so `Weight.light / 2.0` divides 2.5 rather than an encoding.
		let leftTag = self.numberTagOf(dividend)
		let rightTag = self.numberTagOf(divisor)
		try requireBinOpOperands(op, left: leftTag, right: rightTag, opTok: opTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE DIVISION SIGNEDNESS RULE, ASKED ONCE AND THEN CARRIED (X10).** This is the last tier that
		// CAN ask it: the rule reads each operand's DECLARED ranged type, and an alias name resolves against
		// the file that is being parsed (`aliasOf(…, readerFilePath:)`) — by the Std tier one module holds
		// every file's values and there is no reader to resolve against. So the answer is written into the
		// OPCODE here, exactly as `emitShift` writes the shift's fill into `shrLogical`, and the fold, the
		// `-1` overflow gate, the expansion's callee and the Std lowering all READ it rather than re-deriving
		// it. Two readers of one rule reaching two answers is the class of bug this file has already paid for.
		//
		// It is asked BEFORE the divide-by-zero proof because the two are independent — a range's SIGN and
		// its zero are different questions about it — and because the proof's `-1` half is answered in terms
		// of the opcode this line mints.
		let divideOp = divisionOpcodeFor(op, unsigned: self.divisionRunsUnsigned(dividend, divisor: divisor, leftTag: leftTag, rightTag: rightTag))

		// ⭐⭐ **ONE PROOF, TWO HAZARDS, THREE ROUTES (A1x).** The verdict is asked ONCE and both of its facts
		// are read here, because this is the only place that knows which OPERATOR is being emitted — and the
		// two operators want different halves of it. Reading the zero half alone is precisely how
		// `i64.min mod -1` came to die on the `neverZero` route: a divisor proven non-zero is not a divisor
		// proven not `-1`, and `mod`'s answer at `-1` exists while `/`'s does not.
		let proof = self.divisorProof(divisor)
		let zeroVerdict = divisorProofZeroVerdict(proof)

		// A SIGNED REMAINDER whose divisor is proven non-zero but might still be `-1` is TOTAL and needs a
		// guard, so it is neither refused, nor thrown from, nor emitted bare — it takes the expansion route
		// below with a callee of its own. Asked before the zero verdict's `neverZero` arm can send it to the
		// bare emitter.
		//
		// ⚠ **`== MaxonBinOp.mod` NOW MEANS "A SIGNED `mod`", AND THAT IS THE OPCODE DOING ITS JOB** — an
		// unsigned remainder is `modUnsigned` and is EXCLUDED here on purpose. `idiv`'s `#DE` at `-1` is an
		// overflow of the SIGNED quotient; read unsigned, `-1` is `u64.max` and `a mod u64.max` is an ordinary
		// answer (`5`, not `0`). Guarding it would not merely be wasted work — the guard ANSWERS `0` at that
		// divisor, so it would turn every `a mod u64.max` into a wrong answer.
		let needsOverflowGuard = divideOp == MaxonBinOp.mod and divisorProofMayBeNegativeOne(proof)

		// ⚠ A provably-zero divisor is refused whatever the other hazard says: `a mod 0` has no answer at all,
		// so there is nothing for the overflow guard to guard and E3103 is still the whole verdict. Kept an
		// EXHAUSTIVE match over the verdict rather than an `== alwaysZero` test, so a fourth answer to "can it
		// be zero?" cannot reach the routing below by omission.
		match zeroVerdict 'verdict'
			alwaysZero then throw ParseError.divisionByProvableZero(opTok.kind.rawValue, line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
			neverZero to possiblyZero then break 'verdict'
		end 'verdict'

		// ⭐ **THE FLOAT-BACKED ENUM DECODE (A4o), PAID ONCE FOR BOTH ROUTES BELOW.** It sits past every
		// refusal in this function — the operand rule and E3103 — so a rejected program still leaves no ops
		// behind, and past `divisorProof`, which must read the value the SOURCE wrote. Every other value
		// passes through unchanged, so the two routes divide exactly what they divided before.
		let dividendNumber = self.floatBehindEnumValue(dividend, anchor: opTok)
		let divisorNumber = self.floatBehindEnumValue(divisor, anchor: opTok)

		// The BARE divide goes to `emitBinOpOverCheckedOperands`, not to `emitBinOp` — which now REFUSES a
		// division outright, so that a division reaching the general emitter is a loud parser bug rather
		// than an unguarded `idiv`. The operand rule this verdict sits behind is the one that door would
		// have asked, so nothing is skipped by entering past it.
		//
		// ⭐ **NEITHER HAZARD OPEN IS THE ONLY ROUTE TO A BARE `idiv`**, and that conjunction is the rung: the
		// left half alone was A1's rule, and it waved `i64.min mod -1` through.
		if zeroVerdict == DivisorZeroVerdict.neverZero and not needsOverflowGuard 'noHazardLeftOpen'
			return self.emitBinOpOverCheckedOperands(dividendNumber, rhs: divisorNumber, op: divideOp, leftTag: leftTag, rightTag: rightTag, opTok: opTok)
		end 'noHazardLeftOpen'

		// ⭐ **THE PROMOTION IS THE SAME ONE `emitBinOp` OWES, PAID HERE FOR THE SAME REASON.** The Std
		// expansion divides the two argument values directly, so a mixed `1.0 / n` must hand it an f64 on both
		// sides or the emitted `divsd` would read an i64 out of a GPR. `promotedOperand` is idempotent and is
		// asked of both sides, exactly as in `emitBinOp`, so the two paths cannot promote differently.
		//
		// It happens AFTER the verdict, which reads the operand the SOURCE wrote: the divisor's own constant
		// domain is what says whether it is zero, and a promotion would only move the same value into a
		// different domain's spelling.
		let promotedDividend = self.promotedOperand(dividendNumber, left: leftTag, right: rightTag, opTok: opTok)
		let promotedDivisor = self.promotedOperand(divisorNumber, left: leftTag, right: rightTag, opTok: opTok)

		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(promotedDividend)
		args.push(promotedDivisor)

		// ⭐ **THE ZERO VERDICT ALONE PICKS THE NAME, and that is what keeps the error channel a property of
		// the divisor's ZERO hazard only.** A guarded remainder reaching here is `neverZero` by construction —
		// the bare route above is the only other thing a `neverZero` divisor can take — so it gets the TOTAL
		// callee and, downstream, no `throws` clause and no E3057.
		let callee = divisionExpansionCalleeForOp(divideOp, fallible: zeroVerdict == DivisorZeroVerdict.possiblyZero)
		let resultTag = binOpResultTag(divideOp, left: leftTag, right: rightTag)
		let result = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))

		// ⭐ **AN EXPANSION IS STILL A FOLD SITE — see `recordBinOpFold` for the diagnostic that was lost
		// when it was not.** Asked on BOTH expansion routes and not only the guarded one: a `possiblyZero`
		// divisor is by definition not a constant, so the fallible route simply never folds, and gating it
		// would be a second copy of that reasoning rather than a saving.
		self.recordBinOpFold(result, lhs: dividend, rhs: divisor, op: divideOp, resultTag: resultTag)

		return result
	end 'emitDivOrMod'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS `/` OR `mod` RUN UNSIGNED?** — the parser's half of TypeRules' ⭐⭐ THE DIVISION
	// SIGNEDNESS RULE: it reads each operand's domain and hands the pair to the rule, which is stated once,
	// there, over `IntegerValueDomain` alone.
	//
	// The FLOAT gate is not a shortcut. `divsd` has no signedness, a ranged float alias carries tag `float`
	// rather than `named` (so `integerValueDomain` would answer `signed` for it anyway, for the wrong
	// reason), and the answer must be `false` on this path or `lowerDivisionExpansion` would meet an
	// unsigned float divide — which it panics on, and rightly. Asked through `operandsAreFloat`, the same
	// predicate `promotedOperand` uses to decide whether the pair meets in the float domain at all.
	function divisionRunsUnsigned(dividend ValueId, divisor ValueId, leftTag ValueTypeTag, rightTag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
		if operandsAreFloat(leftTag, right: rightTag) 'floatDivide'
			return false
		end 'floatDivide'

		return divisionOperandsAreUnsigned(self.integerValueDomain(dividend), divisor: self.integerValueDomain(divisor))
	end 'divisionRunsUnsigned'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE VALUE'S DECLARED TYPE SAYS ABOUT ITS SIGNEDNESS** — the value-level twin of
	// `valueIsUnsigned`, and three-valued where that one is two-valued, because both consumers have to
	// tell "this value ASKS for an unsigned reading" apart from "this value merely FITS one".
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO CONSUMERS READ IT, AND THE SECOND IS WHY IT IS NAMED FOR THE VALUE (X11).** The DIVIDE
	// asks it of both operands (`divisionRunsUnsigned` → `divisionOperandsAreUnsigned`); the DECIMAL
	// RENDERER asks it of one (`materializeInterpExpr` → `valueRendersUnsigned`), which is what makes
	// `"{w}"` for a `w` of `int(0 to u64.max)` holding `u64.max` print `18446744073709551615` rather
	// than `-1`. They read ONE walk rather than two, because a second spelling of "is this value
	// unsigned" is what this family has cost three times (`X8`, `X10`, `X11`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A DECLARED RANGE ANSWERS ALONE, BOTH WAYS, AND THE ORDER IS THE RULE.** A value of
	// `int(-10 to 10)` that happens to hold `3` is an operand whose TYPE admits negatives, so it reads
	// `signed` and the folded `3` is never consulted: letting the value speak over the declaration would
	// make `w / a` unsigned on the strength of a NUMBER where the language's answer comes from a TYPE. The
	// bootstrap reads the same two facts in the same order (`DeclaredIntRange` before `TryFoldIntConst`).
	//
	// `exitCode` is the builtin `ExitCode` — a declared non-negative range with no alias declaration to look
	// up, placed here exactly as `valueIsUnsigned` places it. Its HIGH bound is platform-shaped (`u32.max` on
	// Windows, 255 elsewhere — `Project.exitCodeAliasHigh`) and none of that reaches this decision:
	// "non-negative" is a statement about the LOW bound, which is 0 on every target.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT READS THE CAST-RECORDED ALIAS AS WELL AS THE TAG COLUMN, AND THAT DIFFERENCE WAS FOUR
	// SILENT WRONG ANSWERS (X10 review).** A cast to a non-negative alias that ADMITS ZERO —
	// `int(0 to 255)`, or the builtin `ExitCode` — is deliberately a representational NO-OP
	// (`applyCast`: it changes neither the shift fill nor the divide-by-zero proof nor the element
	// packing), so it mints no value and the tag column still says what the SOURCE was. Read from the
	// tag alone the operand answered `signed`, which dragged the whole divide signed and misread the
	// OTHER operand: with `Word = int(0 to u64.max)` holding `u64.max`, `try (w / d)` answered **0 here
	// and 6148914691236517205 in the bootstrap oracle**. `domainDeclaringAlias` reads the record the
	// cast already leaves, and the entry IS a proof — the `as` emits the range guard. The one spelling
	// no record can carry, a cast of a BARE LOCAL (one value under two names), is what
	// `castChangesTheDivisionSignedness` mints for.
	function integerValueDomain(value ValueId) returns IntegerValueDomain
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.exitCode 'exitCode'
			return IntegerValueDomain.declaredNonNegative
		end 'exitCode'

		match self.domainDeclaringAlias(value) 'declaredType'
			declared(alias) then return IntegerValueDomain.declaredNonNegative if rangeIsUnsigned(alias.low) else IntegerValueDomain.signed
			undeclared then break 'declaredType'
		end 'declaredType'

		if self.constIsKnown(value) and self.constOf(value) >= 0 'fitsWithoutAsking'
			return IntegerValueDomain.provablyNonNegative
		end 'fitsWithoutAsking'

		return IntegerValueDomain.signed
	end 'integerValueDomain'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE DIVIDE-BY-ZERO PROOF, IN ONE WALK.** Asked of the divisor's OWN constant domain, never of
	// the divide's RESULT kind — which is what lets `1.0 / n` and `n / 2` each be decided by the operand
	// actually written. (The bootstrap asks by result kind and therefore cannot prove `1.0 / 2`'s integer
	// literal non-zero at all; its own comment calls that safe over-strictness.)
	//
	// The float arm reads BOTH zeros: `floatOrderKey` maps `+0.0` and `-0.0` to 0 (IEEE-754 makes them equal),
	// and `x / ±0.0` is `±inf` either way, so both must read as zero. `-0.0` reaches here as a single literal
	// with its sign bit set — `parseNegatedNumericLiteral` folds the sign into the literal — so no float
	// arithmetic has to be folded to see it.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY GAP FALLS TO `possiblyZero`, AND THE TWO DIRECTIONS ARE NOT SYMMETRIC.** See the union's own
	// header: a wrong `neverZero` is a silent hardware trap, a wrong `possiblyZero` is a visible needless `try`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A RANGED FLOAT ALIAS IS PROVEN HERE THROUGH `valueDenotedAlias`, AND THIS WAS A GAP UNTIL SHV2
	// COMPILED ITSELF.** A value of a ranged FLOAT alias carries tag `float`, not `named` — its register class
	// depends on the tag, so the alias name cannot ride there (see `aliasChangesTheShiftFill`'s float arm) —
	// so `declaredAliasOfValue`, which reads the TAG, has nothing to look up. The side map that holds it
	// already existed: `ValueDeclaredAliasMap` is written for exactly this case (`applyCast`, and the
	// `returns <float alias>` result at the call door), and `valueDenotedAlias` is the reader that consults
	// it BEFORE falling back to the tag. This function read the fallback DIRECTLY and so could not see it.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED on this compiler's own source, where the header used to say "no corpus case spells one".**
	// `ScaleCorpus.floatDivideSource` divides by a `FloatGroupDivisor` = `float(2.25 to 5.25)` binding, and
	// the two compilers then disagreed in a way NO source spelling satisfies: shv2 demanded a `try` the
	// bootstrap refused as `E2001 try requires a function call` (it proves the range and sees no throw). The
	// canonical rule is `specs/arithmetic-operators.md`'s — *"a value whose ranged type excludes 0"* compiles
	// to a bare divide, **"for both integer and float division"** — so the bootstrap was right and this was
	// simply unable to ask.
	//
	// ⚠ **G14's WITHHOLD IS UNTOUCHED, because it lives in the FALLBACK.** `valueDenotedAlias` is
	// `recordedCastAlias` first and `provenAliasOfValue` second, so a merge whose declared alias nothing
	// proved still answers `undeclared` here and still gets `possiblyZero`. What the recorded arm adds is a
	// value whose range WAS enforced — an `as` cast and a ranged `return` both emit the guard, which is the
	// same enforcement the list below turns on.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE ALIAS ARM IS ONLY AS SOUND AS THE RANGE IS ENFORCED, AND THE ENFORCEMENT IS NOT THIS
	// FUNCTION'S — it is `InsertRangeChecks`', and this verdict is WRONG rather than merely coarse
	// wherever that enforcement has a hole.** A `NonZero` value reaching here is proof only because every
	// door it could have come through checks the range at RUNTIME: the `as` cast, the field store, the
	// field default and the ranged `return` each emit a guard where the value lands, and a narrowed
	// PARAMETER is guarded at its function's ENTRY (A1f) — one guard in front of every caller, direct,
	// indirect and witness-dispatched alike, which is what makes `neverZero` off a `d NonZero` parameter a
	// real proof rather than an assumption. The parameter case was MEASURED as a hole before A1f closed
	// it: a runtime 0 reached the bare `idiv` and died `panic: integer divide by zero`, an UNCATCHABLE
	// fault where the language promises a catchable `DivisionByZero`, and the bootstrap traps identically
	// — it was the LANGUAGE's unenforced premise, not an shv2 divergence.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY DOOR IS NOW SHUT — the ARRAY ELEMENT was the last, and `A1f-arrayelem` shut it at the
	// STORE** (`requireContainerElementType`), which is the one place that still knows the element type: A1f's
	// callee-entry cure could not reach it, because the element travels into a shared `Array` body whose
	// parameter is the opaque element type. A runtime 0 `push`/`set`/`insert`ed into an `Array with NonZero`
	// is now refused where it is written.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT REMAINS IS NOT A DOOR, and it is the one way left for this verdict to be wrong.**
	// `Array.resize` GROWS an array by EXPOSING zero-initialized slots — which `stdlib/Array.maxon`
	// documents as elements for exactly the inline types a ranged alias resolves to — so an
	// `Array with NonZero` acquires a `0` element with no value crossing any door and nothing for a store
	// guard to stand in front of. Pinned by `specs-shv2/safety.md`'s
	// `divide-by-zero-fault-through-a-resized-array-slot`, and filed for its own ruling: whether `resize`
	// should be refused for an element range that excludes its zero, as it already is (E3106) for a MANAGED
	// element it cannot invent, is a language decision and not this function's to make.
	function divisorProof(divisor ValueId) returns DivisorProof
		if self.floatConstIsKnown(divisor) 'floatConstant'
			// A FLOAT divisor cannot reach a `mod` at all (`requireBinOpOperands` refuses it), and `divsd`
			// cannot fault, so the overflow hazard has no float form to admit.
			let isZero = floatOrderKey(self.floatConstBitsOf(divisor)) == 0
			return divisorProofOf(DivisorZeroVerdict.alwaysZero if isZero else DivisorZeroVerdict.neverZero, mayBeNegativeOne: false)
		end 'floatConstant'

		if self.constIsKnown(divisor) 'intConstant'
			let value = self.constOf(divisor)
			return divisorProofOf(DivisorZeroVerdict.alwaysZero if value == 0 else DivisorZeroVerdict.neverZero, mayBeNegativeOne: value == OverflowingDivisorPattern)
		end 'intConstant'

		// ⚠ **`provenAliasOfValue`, NOT `declaredAliasOfValue` — A MERGE'S DECLARED ALIAS IS NOT A PROOF, AND
		// THIS IS THE READER WHERE THAT COSTS AN UNCATCHABLE FAULT (G14).** The list above ends *"WHAT REMAINS
		// IS NOT A DOOR"* and names only `Array.resize`; a phi is the second such producer. MEASURED: a
		// `NonZero` binding decremented to `0` around a loop kept the alias on its header phi, this answered
		// `neverZero`, `100 / d` compiled to a BARE `idiv` needing no `throws DivisionByZero` at all — and the
		// runtime `0` died `panic: integer divide by zero`, the exact uncatchable fault A1f closed the
		// PARAMETER door to prevent. The withheld claim falls to `undeclared` below, which is the conservative
		// arm this function already documents as the safe direction.
		return match self.valueDenotedAlias(divisor) 'a'
			// One alias, both bounds tests — and the float arm answers `mayBeNegativeOne: false` for the same
			// reason the float CONSTANT arm does, not because a float range was examined and cleared.
			declared(alias) gives divisorProofOf(DivisorZeroVerdict.neverZero if rangeExcludesZero(alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: aliasIsFloat(alias)) else DivisorZeroVerdict.possiblyZero, mayBeNegativeOne: not aliasIsFloat(alias) and not rangeExcludesNegativeOne(alias.low, high: alias.high))
			// Nothing known: BOTH hazards admitted. This is the answer every gap in the proof falls to, and
			// the second fact is exactly as conservative as the first for the same stated reason.
			undeclared gives divisorProofOf(DivisorZeroVerdict.possiblyZero, mayBeNegativeOne: true)
		end 'a'
	end 'divisorProof'

	// Append a `binOp` op combining two SSA values; return the ValueId it defines. The
	// result's TYPE is the operator's: arithmetic and shifts are integer; `and`/`or`/`xor`
	// follow their operands (see `wordOpResultTag`) — the ONE place a bitwise op's result
	// could be a bool, which is what `consume(f.a or f.b)` against a `bool` parameter needs.
	// The source range anchors on the operator token (the conventional binop anchor).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A `/` OR `mod` CANNOT BE EMITTED THROUGH THIS DOOR AT ALL — REFUSED, NOT DOCUMENTED.** This
	// function is the emitter for the fourteen operators whose result is just a number; `emitDivOrMod` owns
	// the divide-by-zero policy and reaches the bare emit below through `emitBinOpOverCheckedOperands`, past
	// this refusal, on its provably-non-zero verdict alone.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COMMENT THAT USED TO STAND HERE WAS THIS RUNG'S OWN EVIDENCE THAT A COMMENT IS NOT ENOUGH.**
	// It asked callers not to bring a division here — and `emitMonotonicMs` already had, silently, for as
	// long as it had existed: a second division path in a compiler whose whole A1 claim is that there is
	// one. A new intrinsic that divides is exactly as easy to write today, and the next one would be found
	// the same way (by reading) or not at all. A `panic` names the caller, on the first program that
	// compiles it, and needs nobody to be reading.
	function emitBinOp(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonBinOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE OPERAND RULE IS ASKED OF THE NUMBER, NOT OF THE ENCODING (A4o).** `numberTagOf` answers
		// `float` for a float-backed enum, which decides two things at once and both match the oracle:
		// `Weight.light + Weight.heavy` is float addition (6.5 — MEASURED, against the sum of two bit
		// patterns shv2 answered), and `Weight.light shl 1` becomes the E3005 `lacksIntegralReading` refuses
		// a float with, instead of shifting a mantissa. UNCONDITIONAL here because every operator this
		// emitter serves is a question about the number; only `emitCompare`'s equality has an identity
		// reading, and it gates.
		let leftTag = self.numberTagOf(lhs)
		let rightTag = self.numberTagOf(rhs)
		try requireBinOpOperands(op, left: leftTag, right: rightTag, opTok: opTok)

		if divisionOperator(op) 'divisionSkippedItsPolicy'
			panic("emitBinOp: '{op.rawValue}' is a DIVISION, and reaching this emitter is how one gets emitted with NO divide-by-zero verdict asked — an unguarded `idiv` where a catchable `DivisionByZero` was promised. Route it through `emitDivOrMod`, which owns the rule for every `/` and `mod` in the language; `parseBinary`'s `divisionOperator` gate is the source-level door and `emitMonotonicMs` is the intrinsic-level one.")
		end 'divisionSkippedItsPolicy'

		// The DECODE is paid only past the rule, so a refused program leaves no ops behind — the property
		// `emitShift`'s header states and the reason the tag question above is asked without emitting. Bound
		// to locals rather than written inline, because each one may EMIT and the left operand's
		// reinterpretation has to reach the block before the right one's.
		let left = self.floatBehindEnumValue(lhs, anchor: opTok)
		let right = self.floatBehindEnumValue(rhs, anchor: opTok)

		return self.emitBinOpOverCheckedOperands(left, rhs: right, op: op, leftTag: leftTag, rightTag: rightTag, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitBinOp'

	// The emit itself, entered only with the operand rule ALREADY asked — so a caller that has done the
	// checking itself reaches the emit without either re-asking `requireBinOpOperands` or carrying a
	// "trust me" flag through the door above. The result's TYPE is the operator's: arithmetic and
	// shifts are integer; `and`/`or`/`xor` follow their operands (`wordOpResultTag`).
	//
	// ⭐ **ITS TWO CALLERS TAKE IT FOR DIFFERENT REASONS, and neither is "convenience".**
	//   • `emitDivOrMod` — because `emitBinOp` REFUSES `/` and `mod` outright (it panics on them), so
	//     this is the only route to the emit, taken on its provably-non-zero verdict alone.
	//   • `emitBinaryMathIntrinsic` — because `min`/`max` are BUILTIN CALLS, not operators. `emitBinOp`
	//     would accept them, but `requireBinOpOperands` words its rejection in terms of an OPERATOR
	//     ("the operands of '<op>' …"), and there is no operator symbol anywhere in `min(3.0, true)`.
	//     The builtin path has already rejected a non-numeric operand in the callee's own name
	//     (`numericBuiltinOperandAt`), which is the sentence the user can act on.
	// So the door is not "the division door" — it is the door for a caller that owns its own operand
	// rule, and the division was merely the first such caller.
	function emitBinOpOverCheckedOperands(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonBinOp, leftTag ValueTypeTag, rightTag ValueTypeTag, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		let resultTag = binOpResultTag(op, left: leftTag, right: rightTag)

		// ⭐ **NUMERIC PROMOTION, PAID FOR.** `TypeRules.arithResultTag` answering `float` is a
		// PROMISE that both operands are already in the float domain, and this is the one place
		// it is kept: an i64 in a GPR is not an f64 in an XMM, and `addsd` cannot read one. It
		// happens HERE, in the parser, because this is the last tier that knows each operand's
		// source type — by the Std tier `operandType` is a single answer for the whole op, and
		// there is no longer a mixed pair to see.
		let left = self.promotedOperand(lhs, left: leftTag, right: rightTag, opTok: opTok)
		let right = self.promotedOperand(rhs, left: leftTag, right: rightTag, opTok: opTok)

		let result = self.mintValue(resultTag, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.binOp(result, lhs: left, rhs: right, op: op), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))

		self.recordBinOpFold(result, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, resultTag: resultTag)

		return result
	end 'emitBinOpOverCheckedOperands'

	// Fold, when the parser holds both operands — so `x shl 63 + 1` is a count of 64 and not an unknown.
	// RECORDED, not rewritten: this compiler emits the `add` either way (see FoldConstOperands' header on
	// why `foldConstants` is deferred).
	//
	// It reads the ORIGINAL ids, not the promoted ones, and it cannot confuse the two: the gate is
	// `tagIsInteger(resultTag)`, and a promotion happens only when the result is a float — so on every path
	// that folds, the promoted operands ARE the originals.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A SHARED METHOD BECAUSE A1x GAVE IT A SECOND CALLER, AND THE ONE THAT LEFT IT BEHIND LOST
	// A DIAGNOSTIC.** A `mod` by a constant `-1` no longer reaches the bare emitter above — it is expanded
	// with the overflow guard — and it is the ONLY constant divisor that does, so the guarded route quietly
	// stopped recording a value the constant domain used to hold. **MEASURED before the fix:**
	// `let z = 10 mod -1` then `try (100 / z) otherwise 7` COMPILED AND PRINTED 7, where the same program
	// was **E3103** ("the divisor of '/' is always 0") before this rung — a provable divide-by-zero
	// downgraded to a catchable runtime throw. One emit site, two routes, one fold.
	function recordBinOpFold(result ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonBinOp, resultTag ValueTypeTag)
		if not tagIsInteger(resultTag) or not self.constIsKnown(lhs) or not self.constIsKnown(rhs) 'notBothKnown'
			return
		end 'notBothKnown'

		self.recordFold(result, fold: foldIntBinOp(op, lhs: self.constOf(lhs), rhs: self.constOf(rhs)))
	end 'recordBinOpFold'

	// One operand of a binary op or comparison, moved into the float domain if the PAIR meets
	// there. Takes both tags because promotion is a property of the pair, not of the operand —
	// `5` stays an int in `5 + 3` and becomes an f64 in `5 + 2.0`, and only the other side says
	// which.
	//
	// Both operands go through it unconditionally, and `promoteToFloat` is idempotent, so the
	// already-float side costs nothing and no caller has to work out which side to convert.
	function promotedOperand(value ValueId, left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		if not operandsAreFloat(left, right: right) 'integral'
			return value
		end 'integral'

		return self.promoteToFloat(value, opTok: opTok)
	end 'promotedOperand'

	// Move a value into the float domain — the int→f64 half of the implicit-conversion lattice.
	// `TypeRules.arithResultTag` states the TYPE rule; this emits the INSTRUCTION that makes it
	// true (`cvtsi2sd`, via `MaxonUnaryOp.promoteToFloat`).
	//
	// A value already there is returned untouched, which is what makes it idempotent — and it is
	// also what keeps `siToFp` from ever naming a float→float conversion (`lowerUnaryOp`'s
	// `assertConversionCrossesDomain` is the backstop that says so).
	//
	// Ported from v1's `promoteToFloat` (LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:3459). ONE divergence, and it is
	// the reason shv2 has one type oracle instead of two: v1 promotes during LOWERING, off an
	// `isFloat` side-map it maintains there because by then the source types are gone. shv2
	// promotes in the PARSER, off the `valueTags` column it already keeps for the word operators.
	// Same op, same place in the value graph, one fewer table.
	function promoteToFloat(value ValueId, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		// ⚠⚠ **THE ENUM DECODE COMES FIRST, AND SKIPPING IT IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER BY ~300 ORDERS OF
		// MAGNITUDE.** A float-backed enum value is an f64 already — in the WRONG REGISTER FILE — so the
		// move into the float domain is a reinterpretation, never the `cvtsi2sd` below. This is the same
		// hazard `coerceToDeclared` PANICS against for an unlifted ranged float alias; an enum cannot take
		// that cure (its value must stay a GPR i64 for `match`), so it takes this one.
		let number = self.floatBehindEnumValue(value, anchor: opTok)
		if tagIsFloat(self.tagOf(number)) 'alreadyFloat'
			return number
		end 'alreadyFloat'

		return self.emitUnaryOp(number, op: MaxonUnaryOp.promoteToFloat, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.float, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
	end 'promoteToFloat'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DOOR EVERY SITE THAT READS A FLOAT-BACKED ENUM AS A NUMBER GOES THROUGH (A4o).** The
	// value is an i64 carrying the f64's IEEE-754 bits (`EnumLayout`'s header states why the representation
	// is uniform), so the read is a `movq xmm, r64` REINTERPRETATION — `MaxonUnaryOp.bitsToFloat`, the same
	// op `emitContainerElementAccessor` reads a float array element back through. Any other value passes
	// through untouched, which is what lets a caller apply it unconditionally.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A DECODE, NOT A PROMOTION, AND THE TWO ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.** `promoteToFloat`'s
	// `cvtsi2sd` reads the operand as a MAGNITUDE: applied to `light = 2.5`'s bits it answers
	// 4612811918334231000.0. MEASURED before this door existed, all four silently: `"{w}"` printed
	// `4612811918334230528`; `Weight.light * scale` and `Weight.light + Weight.heavy` multiplied and added
	// bit patterns; `takesFloat(Weight.light)` widened the encoding; and `below < mid` ordered `-2.2`
	// against `-1.1` BACKWARDS, because a negative double's bits do not order like the double.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `==`/`!=` OF ONE ENUM DELIBERATELY DOES NOT COME HERE** — an enum's equality is its IDENTITY,
	// answered by the i64 tag compare that `match` dispatch uses, and routing it through a float compare
	// would make `SignedZero.negz == SignedZero.posz` disagree with `match negz` (`enums-simple.md`'s
	// `float-backed-signed-zero` pins that single equality). See `emitCompare`'s gate.
	function floatBehindEnumValue(value ValueId, anchor Token) returns ValueId
		if not self.valueIsFloatBackedEnum(value) 'notAFloatBackedEnum'
			return value
		end 'notAFloatBackedEnum'

		return self.emitUnaryOp(value, op: MaxonUnaryOp.bitsToFloat, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.float, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'floatBehindEnumValue'

	// ⭐ **THE TAG OF THE NUMBER BEHIND A VALUE — the question every OPERAND RULE asks, and the one
	// `tagOf` cannot answer for a float-backed enum.** The value's own tag is `named` and its register file
	// is a GPR, both truthfully; what the rule needs to know is the DOMAIN the operator will work in, and
	// for a float-backed enum that is `float`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS SPLIT FROM `floatBehindEnumValue` SO THE RULE CAN BE ASKED WITHOUT EMITTING** — a refused
	// program must leave no ops behind (`emitShift`'s header), and a decode placed before the verdict would
	// leave a stray `bitsToFloat` in the block of every program rejected for its operands. Same split, same
	// reason, as `rangedAliasResolvedTag` beside `promoteToFloat`.
	function numberTagOf(value ValueId) returns ValueTypeTag
		return ValueTypeTag.float if self.valueIsFloatBackedEnum(value) else self.tagOf(value)
	end 'numberTagOf'

	// Is `value` a FLOAT-BACKED enum's value — `valueIsUnion`'s twin, differing only in the layout fact it
	// reads. The `named` pre-check is that function's measured hot-path guard, kept for the identical
	// reason: this is asked of both operands of every binary operator and of every interpolation hole, and
	// a scalar column read allocates nothing where a registry probe on an `int` would.
	function valueIsFloatBackedEnum(value ValueId) returns bool
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'

		return self.signatures.enumIsFloatBacked(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)))
	end 'valueIsFloatBackedEnum'

	// ⭐ **FLOAT NEGATION IS A SUBTRACT FROM ZERO**, and that is a PORT, not a shortcut — v1 says
	// the same of its own parser ("Float negation is normally lowered by the parser as `0.0 - x`",
	// LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:5964).
	//
	// x64 has NO `neg` for an XMM register. The hardware spelling of a sign flip is `xorpd`
	// against a sign-bit mask that has to be materialized in rdata — a new opcode, a new constant,
	// and a new relocation — whereas `subsd` is already in the band this rung landed. So `-x`
	// costs nothing new, and `MaxonUnaryOp.neg` stays what it has always been: the INTEGER negate.
	//
	// ⚠ It differs from a true negation on exactly one input, and it is worth naming rather than
	// discovering: `0.0 - (+0.0)` is `+0.0`, where `-(+0.0)` is `-0.0`. Nothing in the language
	// can yet observe a zero's sign — that needs `print`, which is P1.2's — and v1 has shipped
	// this trade for its whole life. `0.0 - nan` is `nan`, so the unordered case is unaffected.
	function emitFloatNegation(operand ValueId, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let zero = self.emitLiteral(FloatZeroBits, valueType: MaxonType.float, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		return try self.emitBinOp(zero, rhs: operand, op: MaxonBinOp.sub, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitFloatNegation'

	// Append a `unaryOp` op over one SSA value; return the ValueId it defines. The source
	// range anchors on the operator token, mirroring emitBinOp.
	function emitUnaryOp(operand ValueId, op MaxonUnaryOp, resultTag ValueTypeTag, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValue(resultTag, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.unaryOp(result, operand: operand, op: op), range: range)

		// `-1` is `neg` of the literal 1, not a negative literal — so without this arm the parser
		// could not see the count in `x shl -1` at all.
		if tagIsInteger(resultTag) and self.constIsKnown(operand) 'known'
			self.recordFold(result, fold: foldIntUnaryOp(op, operand: self.constOf(operand)))
		end 'known'

		return result
	end 'emitUnaryOp'

	// Append a `compare` op over two SSA values; return the BOOLEAN ValueId it defines.
	// Mirrors emitBinOp but emits the distinct comparison variant (a comparison is not a
	// `binOp` — see MaxonCmpOp). The source range anchors on the comparison operator.
	//
	// A comparison is STRICT IN BOTH ITS CLASS AND ITS DOMAIN — `TypeRules.comparableOperands`,
	// which is a third question and not `typesAgree`. Two bools compare fine (`a == b`); two ints
	// compare; two floats compare. A MIXTURE does not, in either of the two ways a pair can be
	// mixed:
	//
	//   • a bool against a number — the only thing there is to compare is the bool's 0/1 payload,
	//     which is not what the source wrote: `4 < flag` is `4 < 1`, i.e. always false. v1 states
	//     the identical rule at TypeResolution.maxon:2726 ("Comparisons are category-strict: both
	//     sides must land in the same CastCategory").
	//
	//   • ⭐ an int against a FLOAT — and this one is the rule that ARITHMETIC does not share.
	//     `5 + 2.0` promotes and is 7.0; `f > i` is E3005 (specs/contextual-literal-typing.md,
	//     four cases). A promotion inside a comparison would be INVISIBLE — the result is a bool
	//     either way, so nothing in the program would show that the int had been converted, and
	//     `3.5 > i` would silently answer a question about `3.5 > 2.0` nobody asked.
	//
	// ⚠ THIS FUNCTION ONCE PROMOTED, and that is exactly the bug P1.0d.4 shipped and then fixed:
	// the promotion built for `emitBinOp` was routed through here too, because a compare's
	// operands looked like a binOp's. They are not: one produces a number, the other a verdict.
	function emitCompare(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let leftTag = self.tagOf(lhs)
		let rightTag = self.tagOf(rhs)

		// ⭐ A BYTE RECORD'S `==`/`!=` IS A RUNTIME BYTE COMPARE, not a `compare` op (which would compare two
		// box POINTERS — reference equality answering a question about VALUES). Handled at the TOP, before
		// `comparableOperands`: a matching STRING pair does not `comparableOperands` at all (that rule
		// governs ORDERING, which a String has none of), so it must be intercepted here or it would be
		// rejected as a mismatch — and a matching CHARACTER pair, which since A5m-c DOES `comparableOperands`,
		// must be intercepted for the opposite reason: it would otherwise fall through to the raw
		// `MaxonOp.compare` below and order two clusters by their ADDRESSES. A byte-record-vs-anything-else
		// pair falls through to the mismatch gate below — a clean type error, not a crash.
		//
		// ⚠ **`leftTag == rightTag` IS THE HALF THAT KEEPS A STRING AND A CHARACTER APART, AND WITHOUT IT
		// THIS IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** They share a record, so `__str_eq` would happily compare a
		// one-cluster String against a Character and report `true` for two values whose TYPES disagree —
		// admitting `s == c` to the language through the back door, where every other rule refuses it
		// (`typeClassOf` gives them different classes precisely so it is E3005). The equality of the two tags
		// is what makes the interception a rule about ONE type at a time.
		//
		// ⚠ It is spelled `tagIsByteRecord(leftTag) and leftTag == rightTag` rather than testing each side,
		// because the property being asserted is "both operands are the SAME byte-record type" — one
		// sentence, not two that a later edit could widen independently.
		if tagIsByteRecord(leftTag) and leftTag == rightTag 'byteRecordCompare'
			return try self.emitByteRecordCompare(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'byteRecordCompare'

		// ⭐⭐ **A `StringIndex` COMPARES BY ITS GRAPHEME INDEX, AND ONLY BY THAT** — the reference's own
		// `equals`/`compare` (`stdlib/String.maxon:933-946`) read `charIdx` and never `bytePosition`, so this
		// is a PORT of a stated rule and not a shortcut. Handled at the TOP, beside the byte-record arm and
		// before every rule below, because those rules would answer WRONGLY rather than not at all: two
		// `structRef`s reach `comparableOperands`, which refuses them ("cannot compare struct with struct") —
		// so `idx2 == s.endIndex()`, the commonest thing there is to do with an index and the one
		// `string-type/find` pins, would be a type error against a perfectly good program.
		//
		// ⚠ **A GENERAL STRUCT `==` WOULD NOT HAVE SERVED, AND WOULD HAVE BEEN WRONG.** Whatever shape it
		// took — a box-pointer compare or a memberwise one — it could not give this answer: two indices
		// carrying the same `charIdx` are equal here even when their `bytePosition`s differ, which is
		// precisely what an index into a DIFFERENT string can do. The type declares `equals` for a reason,
		// and that reason is that its two fields do not both carry identity.
		//
		// ⭐ ALL SIX operators route here, not just `==`/`!=`: the reference declares
		// `implements Equatable, Comparable` and its `compare` orders by the same one field, so `<` on two
		// indices is a real question with the same answer shape. Restricting to equality would need a
		// bespoke rejection for four operators the type genuinely has — vocabulary WITHHELD from its
		// consumer, which is the opposite error to shipping it ahead of one.
		//
		// ⚠ Spelled as BOTH operands rather than either, exactly as the byte-record arm above is: the
		// property is "both sides are indices". An index against anything else falls through to the
		// mismatch gate below — a clean type error, not a compare of one field against a whole box.
		if self.valueIsStringIndex(lhs) and self.valueIsStringIndex(rhs) 'stringIndexCompare'
			return try self.emitStringIndexCompare(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'stringIndexCompare'

		// ⭐ A `union` VALUE HAS NO COMPARISON — of ANY kind (E3066). Refused BEFORE `comparableOperands`,
		// which would pass a `named`-vs-`named` pair (they agree) and lower to a compare of two BOX
		// ADDRESSES — the same reference-equality wrong answer that rule already refuses for two structs.
		// `==`/`!=` have no synthesized equality; `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` a fortiori have no meaning either, so
		// ALL of them reject here (the message names whichever operator the author wrote). An ENUM's value
		// IS its i64 tag, so `Color.red == c` is a real tag compare and stays legal; only a `union`
		// (`isUnion`) is rejected. The match dispatch (`emitCompareAgainstLiteral`) builds its tag compare with
		// its own `MaxonOp.compare` and never routes through here, so a `match` arm is unaffected.
		if self.valueIsUnion(lhs) or self.valueIsUnion(rhs) 'unionCompare'
			throw ParseError.unionNotComparable(comparisonOpSpelling(op), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'unionCompare'

		// ⭐ **EVERY COMPARISON ON AN ENCLOSING (opaque) TYPE PARAMETER dispatches through a witness** — `==`/`!=`
		// through `Equatable.equals` (P1.7a slice 2b), `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` through `Comparable.compare` (slice
		// 2b-ii) — the SAME tables `<T>.equals()` / `<T>.compare()` use. Inside the shared body `T`'s concrete
		// type is unknown, so a scalar `compare` would compare two opaque WORDS: right for an `int` by luck, a
		// POINTER compare for a struct `T` (a silent wrong answer). An UNCONSTRAINED enclosing `T` has no witness
		// and is E3005.
		//
		// ⚠ ALL SIX operators are gated, and the ordering half is why: until slice 2b-ii, `<` on a type parameter
		// fell straight past here to the raw `MaxonOp.compare` below, because `typeClassesAgree` lets a
		// `typeParameter` agree with anything (TypeRules) — the same pointer-compare wrong answer the equality
		// half already refused, reachable the moment anything could conform to `Comparable`.
		// ⚠ Only a value of an ENCLOSING type parameter opts in: a `typeParameter`-tagged value in a NON-generic
		// caller is a concrete generic-method result (`let v = intBox.get()`) whose real type IS known (`int`) —
		// it must stay a scalar compare (`isEnclosingTypeParam` is false there, so it falls through to
		// `comparableOperands`).
		if self.isEnclosingTypeParam(lhs) or self.isEnclosingTypeParam(rhs) 'typeParamComparison'
			return try self.emitTypeParamComparison(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, opTok: opTok, leftIsParam: self.isEnclosingTypeParam(lhs))
		end 'typeParamComparison'

		// ⭐⭐ **`a == b` ON TWO VALUES OF ONE USER STRUCT DISPATCHES ITS OWN `equals` (BATCH2 slice 3).**
		// Without this, `comparableOperands` refused the pair — *"cannot compare struct with struct"* — for a
		// program the oracle compiles and runs; `specs/filepath.md`'s `filepath-equality` /
		// `filepath-inequality` are the corpus cases that reach it, and `stdlib/FilePath.maxon:181` is the
		// `equals` they mean to run (case-insensitive on Windows, so a POINTER compare or a memberwise one
		// would both be the WRONG ANSWER, not merely a different one).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE RULE IS "HAS AN `equals` METHOD", NOT "DECLARES `implements Equatable`" — MEASURED AGAINST
		// THE ORACLE, one program each.** A struct with an `equals` and NO conformance clause compiles and
		// prints `equal`; it is exactly the rule `materializeStructToStringPart` already states for
		// interpolation (`toString` by NAME, not by `Stringable`), and stating it the other way would refuse
		// a legal program. It is also why this needs no site record: unlike `<Type> from "…"`, whose oracle
		// DOES demand the conformance (E3005, measured), the question here is answerable from the
		// whole-program signature index at the cursor, order-independently.
		//
		// ⚠ **EQUALITY ONLY.** `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` on two structs stay the E3005 below: ordering means
		// `Comparable.compare` returning an `Ordering`, which is a different method, a different verdict
		// shape and has no consumer anywhere in this cone — and admitting the four operators here would
		// silently answer them with an EQUALITY test. `!=` is the negation of the same call, built from it,
		// so the two can never come to disagree about what equality means — `emitByteRecordCompare`'s rule.
		if self.structEqualityOperands(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op) 'structEquality'
			return try self.emitStructEqualityCall(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'structEquality'

		// ⭐⭐ **`a == b` ON TWO ARRAYS IS A CONTENT COMPARE, AND BEFORE array-hashable IT WAS A SILENT WRONG
		// ANSWER.** Two `genericInstance` operands AGREE and are neither float, so they fell straight through
		// to the raw `MaxonOp.compare` below — an integer compare of two RECORD ADDRESSES. MEASURED on
		// `let a = [1, 2, 3]` beside `let b = [1, 2, 3]`: `a == b` answered **false** and `a != b` **true**,
		// for two arrays whose contents are identical. It is exactly the reference-identity hazard
		// `comparableOperands` already refuses for two structs and two existentials, except that here it
		// COMPILED, so nothing said so.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE FIX IS A DISPATCH, NOT A REFUSAL, and that is what separates this case from those two.**
		// A struct has no synthesized structural equality and an existential cannot prove its two dynamic
		// types match, so both are refused; an `Array` DECLARES `equals` (conditionally on its element), and
		// dispatching it is what both references do. `!=` is that same call negated, built from it, so the
		// two operators can never come to disagree — `emitByteRecordCompare`'s rule.
		//
		// ⚠ **ONLY `Array` INSTANCES, AND ONLY EQUALITY.** A `Set`/`Map`/`List`/user-generic pair keeps
		// today's fall-through, because none of them has an `equals` for this to reach; ordering has no
		// `equals` reading at all (`stdlib/Array.maxon` declares no `Comparable`), so `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` on
		// two arrays stay the E3005 below.
		if self.arrayEqualityOperands(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op) 'arrayEquality'
			return try self.emitArrayEqualityCall(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'arrayEquality'

		// ⭐⭐ **A COMPARISON OF TWO EXISTENTIALS IS REFUSED WITH ITS OWN REASON, NOT WITH "cannot compare
		// Comparable with Comparable".** `comparableOperands` already returns false for the pair (see there for
		// the measured wrong answers), so this changes no verdict — it changes what the author is told. The
		// generic mismatch message names the two tags, which for a pair of existentials reads as though the
		// types disagree when in fact they agree perfectly and the OPERATION is what has no meaning. Asked
		// before that message for the same reason `unionNotComparable` is.
		//
		// ⚠ The predicate is BOTH-operands, and `comparableOperands` asks the SAME one — it used to be spelled
		// `or` there and `and` here, two spellings of one fact that agreed only because `typesAgree` runs
		// first. A MIXED pair (an existential against an `int`) really is a type mismatch and keeps the
		// message below, which names the two tags.
		if operandsAreInterfaceRef(leftTag, right: rightTag) 'existentialComparison'
			throw ParseError.existentialNotComparable(comparisonOpSpelling(op), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'existentialComparison'

		// ⭐⭐ **A FLOAT-BACKED ENUM IS COMPARED AS ITS NUMBER — EXCEPT WHERE THE COMPARISON IS AN IDENTITY
		// TEST (A4o).** Everything else here is exact for an enum already, because an enum value IS its i64
		// tag; this is the one question the tag cannot answer, and it splits by operator:
		//
		//   • ORDERING (`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`) has no identity reading at all, so it is always the number.
		//     MEASURED before this arm, on `below = -2.2` and `mid = -1.1`: shv2 answered `below < mid`
		//     FALSE, because a negative double's bit pattern does not order like the double. The oracle
		//     answers true. A bit-pattern order that happens to agree for POSITIVE cases is exactly why
		//     `enum-match-exhaustive`'s float range arm passes under both readings and pins neither.
		//   • EQUALITY between two values of ONE enum stays the TAG compare, and that is not a concession:
		//     `x == Type.case` is the language's way of asking which CASE a value is, and it must agree
		//     with `match`, whose dispatch is an i64 compare (`emitCompareAgainstLiteral`). Routing it through
		//     `ucomisd` would make `SignedZero.negz == SignedZero.posz` TRUE while `match negz` selects
		//     `negz` — `enums-simple.md`'s `float-backed-signed-zero` pins that single equality.
		//   • EQUALITY against anything else (`w == 2.5`) names no case, so it is the number, which is what
		//     lets the oracle's `w == 2.5` compile here too.
		//
		// ⚠ The VERDICT is asked of the number TAGS and the decode is paid only past it, so a comparison
		// rejected for its operands still leaves no ops behind — `emitBinOp`'s split, for its reason. The
		// message names those same tags, so a refused float-backed enum reads "cannot compare float with
		// bool" rather than the `int` `typeTagName` prints for every `named`.
		let identityEquality = comparisonIsEquality(op) and leftTag == ValueTypeTag.named and self.valueNameId(lhs) == self.valueNameId(rhs)
		let leftNumberTag = leftTag if identityEquality else self.numberTagOf(lhs)
		let rightNumberTag = rightTag if identityEquality else self.numberTagOf(rhs)

		if not comparableOperands(leftNumberTag, right: rightNumberTag) 'mismatch'
			throw ParseError.comparisonTypeMismatch(typeTagName(leftNumberTag), right: typeTagName(rightNumberTag), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'mismatch'

		let left = lhs if identityEquality else self.floatBehindEnumValue(lhs, anchor: opTok)
		let right = rhs if identityEquality else self.floatBehindEnumValue(rhs, anchor: opTok)

		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.boolean, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.compare(result, lhs: left, rhs: right, op: op), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		return result
	end 'emitCompare'

	// ⭐⭐ **`a is b` / `a is not b` — DO THESE TWO NAMES DENOTE ONE OBJECT?** (`specs/reference-identity.md`.)
	// The deliberate opposite of `emitCompare`: that one answers about CONTENTS and dispatches a type's own
	// `equals`, this one compares the two POINTERS and dispatches nothing. So it is a sibling of `emitCompare`
	// rather than an arm of it — routing `is` through that function would have to bypass, in order, the
	// byte-record compare, the `StringIndex` compare, the struct-`equals` dispatch and the `Array`-`equals`
	// dispatch, i.e. every arm that function has.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT IS COMPARED IS THE VALUE'S OWN WORD, WHICH FOR EVERY ADMITTED TAG IS THE OBJECT'S ADDRESS:**
	// a struct's `__mm_alloc`'d box, a generic instance's record, and — for a `String` and a `Character` — the
	// address of the 56-byte FUSED RECORD, not of the `buffer@0` it points at. The record is the object: two
	// String records legitimately SHARE one buffer (a view carries its source in `parent@32`), so a buffer
	// compare would report a substring and its source as the same object. That the value in the register
	// already IS that address is what makes this one `MaxonOp.compare`, with no load anywhere: there is one
	// spelling of a `String`, so `s is s` cannot come out unequal through two routes to one value.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE ANSWER IS OBSERVABLE FOR LITERALS, WHICH THE SPEC PINS.** `"hello" is "hello"` is TRUE
	// because `lowerStringLiteral` registers the blob and the record through `registerRdataConstant`, which
	// DEDUPES by bytes — every occurrence of one literal shares one immortal `.rdata` record. That is a
	// property this operator only reveals; nothing here arranges it.
	//
	// The `eq`/`ne` split is the whole of `is not`: one operand pair, one compare, the predicate inverted —
	// so the two spellings cannot come to disagree the way a separate negation emit could.
	function emitReferenceIdentity(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, negate bool, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let op = referenceIdentityOpSpelling(negate)
		let leftTag = self.tagOf(lhs)
		let rightTag = self.tagOf(rhs)

		// Asked of BOTH operands and BEFORE the agreement test below, because a pair of `int`s AGREES and the
		// thing wrong with `a is b` there is not that the types differ — it is that neither value is an object.
		if not tagHasReferenceIdentity(leftTag) or not tagHasReferenceIdentity(rightTag) 'notAReference'
			throw ParseError.refIdentityOnPrimitive(op, line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'notAReference'

		if not self.valuesNameOneType(lhs, rhs: rhs) 'typeMismatch'
			throw ParseError.refIdentityTypeMismatch(op, left: self.valueDisplayTypeName(lhs), right: self.valueDisplayTypeName(rhs), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'typeMismatch'

		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.boolean, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.compare(result, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, op: MaxonCmpOp.ne if negate else MaxonCmpOp.eq), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
		return result
	end 'emitReferenceIdentity'

	// ⭐⭐ **DO THESE TWO VALUES NAME ONE TYPE? — THE ONE HOME for a question three operator gates ask**, and it
	// had three spellings before it had one. `structEqualityOperands`, `arrayEqualityOperands` and the `is`
	// rule's second half (*"both operands must be the same type"*) each wrote out
	// `tagOf(a) == X and tagOf(b) == X and valueNameId(a) == valueNameId(b)`, and each carried its own comment
	// promising that *"a later edit cannot widen one side alone"* — a promise three copies of it is precisely
	// what breaks. The two equality gates keep their own TAG clause, which is what makes each about ONE family;
	// the agreement itself is here.
	//
	// The tag decides it outright for the two builtin byte records: there is exactly one `String` type and
	// exactly one `Character` type, so a matching tag IS a matching type. The two NOMINAL tags
	// (`tagIdentityIsNominal`) name a family and additionally need the declared name — `Point` against `Box`,
	// `Array with int` against `Array with String`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A CONVENIENCE — WITHOUT THE TAG CLAUSE `s is c` IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** A `String` and
	// a `Character` are the same 56-byte record, so a raw pointer compare across them would answer a question
	// about two values whose types every other rule keeps apart. `emitCompare`'s byte-record arm spells the
	// same guard for the same reason.
	function valuesNameOneType(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId) returns bool
		let leftTag = self.tagOf(lhs)
		if leftTag != self.tagOf(rhs) 'differentTags'
			return false
		end 'differentTags'

		if not tagIdentityIsNominal(leftTag) 'theTagIsTheType'
			return true
		end 'theTagIsTheType'

		return self.valueNameId(lhs) == self.valueNameId(rhs)
	end 'valuesNameOneType'

	// Is this an `==`/`!=` between two values of ONE user struct that declares an `equals` method? The gate in
	// front of the dispatch below, and every clause is load-bearing:
	//
	//   • EQUALITY ONLY — the four ordering operators have no `equals` reading (see the call site).
	//   • BOTH operands `structRef`, and of the SAME struct — spelled as one property, exactly as the
	//     byte-record and `StringIndex` arms above spell theirs, so a later edit cannot widen one side alone
	//     into a compare of one type's box against another's.
	//   • the struct DECLARES the method, asked of the WHOLE-PROGRAM index (`declaresCallee`) rather than of
	//     this file — `equals` may be declared in any file, including the one using it, which is
	//     `materializeStructToStringPart`'s reason for asking the same question the same way. A struct with
	//     no `equals` falls through to the existing E3005, unchanged.
	function structEqualityOperands(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp) returns bool
		if not comparisonIsEquality(op) 'notEquality'
			return false
		end 'notEquality'

		// The TAG clause is this gate's own — it is what makes the rule about STRUCTS — and the agreement is
		// the shared `valuesNameOneType`, which supplies the other side's tag and the declared name together.
		if not (self.tagOf(lhs) == ValueTypeTag.structRef and self.valuesNameOneType(lhs, rhs: rhs)) 'notOneStructsPair'
			return false
		end 'notOneStructsPair'

		return self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(lhs)), methodName: EqualsMethodName))
	end 'structEqualityOperands'

	// `a == b` / `a != b` on one user struct, as the ORDINARY instance call `a.equals(b)` — same callee key,
	// same argument slotting, same `bool` result, so nothing downstream learns that an operator produced it.
	// The receiver is argument 0, which is the whole of shv2's method-dispatch convention (`parseCallNamed`).
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THAT `bool` RESULT IS CHECKED HERE, NOT ASSUMED — it was asserted by this comment and by
	// nothing else** (BATCH2 slice 3 review). See `structEqualsResultNotBool`: the WITNESS form of this same
	// dispatch already refuses a name-matched `equals` that returns anything but `bool`
	// (`witnessTargetIsProtocol`, whose header carries the measurement), because matching by METHOD NAME is
	// what both forms do and a bare name proves nothing about a result. The direct form skipped it and both
	// operators went silently wrong: `==` evaluated to whatever `equals` returned, and `!=` `logicalNot`ted
	// that value while stamping the result `boolean`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES NEITHER THE E3019 NOR THE E3070 DOOR, and that is `emitTypeParamComparison`'s answer to
	// the same question two screens up, not a shortcut.** Both doors describe a callee that WRITES through
	// an argument and name the binding to blame; an OPERATOR wrote this call, its operands are values the
	// climber already produced, and the witness form of exactly this dispatch passes empty for the identical
	// reason. So the compiler-written-call columns (`emptyArgLabels` / `uniformArgRanges` /
	// `NoImmutableArgNames`) are the honest ones here — every argument shares the operator's own span,
	// because that IS where each came from.
	function emitStructEqualityCall(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let callee = qualifiedMethodName(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(lhs)), methodName: EqualsMethodName)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(opTok)
		// `var`, because `emitCall`'s caller-side consume may rewrite an argument in place (E3019).
		var args = pairArgs(lhs, second: rhs)
		// `var` for `args`' reason one line up, plus one of its own: `emitCall` GROWS all three columns in
		// lockstep when the callee defaults an argument the call omits (BATCH2 slice 4).
		var argLabels = emptyArgLabels(args)
		var argRanges = uniformArgRanges(args, range: range)

		// `resolvedTypeQualifier`: the author wrote an OPERATOR, so not one byte of this callee's head is at
		// the call site — it is the left operand's resolved type name (F31).
		let equal = try self.emitCall(callee, anchor: opTok, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: opTok, resultUsed: true, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier)

		// The RESULT is asked of the emitted call rather than of the declaration, because `emitCall` has
		// already resolved the callee's real return type and a second lookup would be a second answer. A
		// `void` `equals` never reaches here — `resultUsed: true` refuses it inside `emitCall` — so this
		// names exactly the wrong-typed case.
		let resultTag = self.tagOf(equal)
		if resultTag != ValueTypeTag.boolean 'equalsResultNotBool'
			throw ParseError.structEqualsResultNotBool(String.from(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(lhs))), got: typeTagName(resultTag), op: comparisonOpSpelling(op), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'equalsResultNotBool'

		return match op 'op'
			eq gives equal
			ne gives self.emitUnaryOp(equal, op: MaxonUnaryOp.logicalNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
			// Unreachable: `structEqualityOperands` admits only `eq`/`ne`, and it is the one gate in front of
			// this function.
			lt to ge panic("emitStructEqualityCall: reached with the ordering operator {comparisonOpSpelling(op)}, which `structEqualityOperands` refuses")
		end 'op'
	end 'emitStructEqualityCall'

	// Is this an `==`/`!=` between two values of ONE `Array` instance? `structEqualityOperands`' twin, and
	// every clause is that one's, one tag over: EQUALITY only, BOTH operands the SAME generic instance
	// (spelled as one property so a later edit cannot widen one side into a compare of one instance's record
	// against another's), and the instance an `Array` rather than any other builtin or user generic.
	//
	// ⚠ It does NOT ask whether the element conforms. That is the availability question, and it is asked at
	// the emit below so that a non-conforming element gets `Array.equals`'s own E4006 rather than falling
	// through to a comparison type mismatch naming two identical instance names.
	function arrayEqualityOperands(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp) returns bool
		if not comparisonIsEquality(op) 'notEquality'
			return false
		end 'notEquality'

		// The TAG clause is this gate's own and the agreement is shared — `structEqualityOperands`' split, one
		// tag over.
		if not (self.tagOf(lhs) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.valuesNameOneType(lhs, rhs: rhs)) 'notOneInstancesPair'
			return false
		end 'notOneInstancesPair'

		// The bare `isArrayInstance` and not `isArrayInstanceAt`: the tag half of that two-part test is the
		// line above, already asked of BOTH operands, so routing through the combined door would ask it a
		// third time over an id it has already qualified.
		return self.signatures.isArrayInstance(self.valueNameId(lhs))
	end 'arrayEqualityOperands'

	// `a == b` / `a != b` on one `Array` instance, as the byte-compare `a.equals(b)` lowers to — the SAME
	// `__managed_eq` entry the method door emits, so the operator and the method cannot answer differently.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE AVAILABILITY RULE IS THE METHOD'S, ASKED AT THE OPERATOR'S SPAN.** `==` on an array means
	// `Array.equals`, which exists only where the element satisfies the conditional conformance — so an
	// array of a non-conforming element is E4006 here, naming `equals` and the clause, rather than the
	// pointer compare it used to be.
	//
	// ⚠ Both operands are BORROWED (`__managed_eq` reads two records and frees nothing), so each stays whatever
	// pending temporary the climber made it and drops at statement end, AFTER the compare —
	// `emitBuiltinConformanceCall`'s contract for a managed receiver.
	function emitArrayEqualityCall(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireArrayConformanceAvailable(self.valueNameId(lhs), methodName: EqualsMethodName, anchor: opTok)

		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(opTok)
		let equal = self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedEqName, args: pairArgs(lhs, second: rhs), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)

		return match op 'op'
			eq gives equal
			ne gives self.emitUnaryOp(equal, op: MaxonUnaryOp.logicalNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, range: range)
			// Unreachable: `arrayEqualityOperands` admits only `eq`/`ne`, and it is the one gate in front of
			// this function.
			lt to ge panic("emitArrayEqualityCall: reached with the ordering operator {comparisonOpSpelling(op)}, which `arrayEqualityOperands` refuses")
		end 'op'
	end 'emitArrayEqualityCall'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS COMPARISON AN EQUALITY ONE (`==`/`!=`) RATHER THAN AN ORDERING ONE?** — the ONE place the
	// operator families are split, because both readers derive the same consequence from it: a struct's own
	// `equals` dispatch has no ordering reading (`structEqualityOperands`), and the witness dispatch picks
	// `Equatable` here and `Comparable` there (`requireOperatorWitness`). That second site spelled it
	// `op == MaxonCmpOp.eq or op == MaxonCmpOp.ne` inline; two spellings of one partition of six operators is
	// the shape where a seventh operator gets taught to one and not the other.
	function comparisonIsEquality(op MaxonCmpOp) returns bool
		return match op 'op'
			eq gives true
			ne gives true
			lt to ge gives false
		end 'op'
	end 'comparisonIsEquality'

	// Two `StringIndex`es compared through their `charIdx` fields — the reference's `equals`/`compare` bodies,
	// which read that one field and never `bytePosition`. Two field loads and the ORDINARY integer `compare`
	// op the operator would have produced on two ints, so every operator keeps its own meaning and none of
	// them needs an arm here.
	function emitStringIndexCompare(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(opTok)
		return try self.emitCompare(self.emitStringIndexField(lhs, index: StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex, range: range), rhs: self.emitStringIndexField(rhs, index: StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex, range: range), op: op, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitStringIndexCompare'

	// ⭐ **EVERY COMPARISON ON A MATCHED PAIR OF BYTE RECORDS** — a `String` pair or a `Character` pair, which
	// `emitCompare`'s one interception hands here. `==` is the `__str_eq` runtime call (content equality:
	// equal lengths, then equal bytes) and `!=` is its logical negation, built from the SAME call so the two
	// can never disagree about what equality means; both answer for either type, because the two share the
	// record. ORDERING is where they part (see `emitByteRecordOrdering`).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT WAS `emitStringCompare`, AND THE NAME WAS THE BUG'S OTHER HALF.** It has served `Character`
	// since P1.8 slice B — `tagIsByteRecord` is what routes here — so a function named for one of its two
	// types wrote its diagnostic in that type's noun and reported *"cannot compare String with String"* for
	// a program that mentions no String. The message is now the operand's own (`emitByteRecordOrdering`), and
	// the name says which question this answers rather than which caller arrived first.
	function emitByteRecordCompare(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		match op 'op'
			eq then return self.emitStrEqCall(lhs, rhs: rhs, opTok: opTok)
			ne then return self.emitUnaryOp(self.emitStrEqCall(lhs, rhs: rhs, opTok: opTok), op: MaxonUnaryOp.logicalNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
			lt to ge then return try self.emitByteRecordOrdering(lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'op'
	end 'emitByteRecordCompare'

	// ⭐⭐ **`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` ON A BYTE-RECORD PAIR — WHERE `Character` AND `String` PART COMPANY (A5m-c).**
	// The two types share a 56-byte record and every other rule in this file treats them as one thing; this
	// is the single question whose answer differs, and it differs because the CORPUS says so:
	// `stdlib/Character.maxon:19` declares `implements … Comparable` and writes the order out at `:80-104`,
	// while `stdlib/String.maxon:49` declares no such conformance. The oracle answers both accordingly —
	// `'é' < 'ü'` runs, `"apple" < "banana"` is refused.
	//
	// ⭐ **A CHARACTER'S ORDERING IS THE `Comparable` CASCADE, NOT AN INLINE COMPARE.** `__char_cmp` is the
	// SAME graph installed under `Character.compare` for the witness door
	// (`BuiltinConformanceRuntime.buildByteRecordCompare`), and the verdict is read off its `Ordering` by the
	// SAME `emitOrderingVerdict` a `where T is Comparable` dispatch uses — so `c < d`, `c.compare(d)` through
	// a witness, and a `match` range arm over Characters cannot come to mean three different things.
	//
	// ⚠ **A POINTER COMPARE HERE WOULD HAVE COMPILED AND ANSWERED NONSENSE**, which is the hazard
	// `comparableOperands` already names for two structs and two existentials: a Character's value is its
	// RECORD'S ADDRESS, so `MaxonOp.compare` would order two clusters by where they happen to be allocated.
	// That is why the refusal came first and the cascade second, rather than the operator being let through.
	function emitByteRecordOrdering(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// A precondition, not a case: `emitCompare` routes here only on `tagIsByteRecord(left) and left ==
		// right`, so the pair is two Strings or two Characters and nothing else.
		if tagIsText(self.tagOf(lhs)) 'noStringOrdering'
			throw ParseError.stringNotOrderable(comparisonOpSpelling(op), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'noStringOrdering'

		// Typed through `interfaceReturnMaxonType`, the SAME typer `emitBuiltinConformanceCall` gives
		// `i.compare(j)`'s result, so the `Ordering` this hands `emitOrderingVerdict` carries the enum's
		// `named` tag exactly as a witness dispatch's does.
		let orderingType = self.interfaceReturnMaxonType(OrderingTypeName)
		let ordering = self.emitRuntimeCall(CharCmpName, args: pairArgs(lhs, second: rhs), resultTag: maxonTypeTag(orderingType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(orderingType), range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))

		return self.emitOrderingVerdict(ordering, op: op, opTok: opTok)
	end 'emitByteRecordOrdering'

	// Is `v` a value of one of the ENCLOSING type's parameters (`self.item`, `other T` inside `type Box uses T`)
	// — a genuinely opaque `T`, NOT a concrete generic-method result that the parser happens to tag
	// `typeParameter` in a non-generic caller (P1.7a slice 2b)? The gate that keeps `==` a scalar compare for
	// `let v = intBox.get(); v == 42` while making it a witness dispatch for `item == other` inside `Box`'s own
	// body.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS AN IDENTITY QUESTION AND IT IS NOW ASKED AS ONE (W14).** It used to be
	// `valueNameId(v) < enclosingTypeParams.count()` — a RANGE test standing in for an identity test, which is
	// the collision this rung removed in miniature: any other type's parameter at a position this type also
	// has would answer yes. Now the token is re-spelled against THIS declaration's own `uses` names, so the
	// answer is "does this token name one of MY parameters" and nothing weaker. Outside a generic body the
	// list is empty and the scan cannot match, exactly as the range test could not.
	function isEnclosingTypeParam(v ValueId) returns bool
		if self.tagOf(v) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notParam'
			return false
		end 'notParam'
		// The EXISTENCE door, not a discarded `typeParamPositionIn`: the POSITION is not wanted here, only
		// whether the token names one of this declaration's parameters at all — and a PURE result may not be
		// dropped in any spelling (E3064), which is what this line used to do.
		return namesTypeParamOf(self.valueNameId(v), declarationName: self.enclosingType, paramNames: self.enclosingTypeParams)
	end 'isEnclosingTypeParam'

	// ⭐ **LOWER A COMPARISON ON A CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER TO A WITNESS DISPATCH (P1.7a slice 2b / 2b-ii).**
	// ONE shape serves both operator families, because both are "dispatch one interface method through the
	// constraint's witness, then read a verdict off its result": `==`/`!=` dispatch `Equatable.equals` and take
	// (or negate) its bool; `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` dispatch `Comparable.compare` and test its `Ordering` against a
	// case tag. The receiver choice, the constraint search, the `Self`-argument check, the result typing and the
	// emit are therefore written ONCE, and only the (method, interface) pair and the verdict differ. The
	// dispatch shape is exactly what `dispatchWitnessMethod` builds for `<T>.equals(other)` /
	// `<T>.compare(other)`, so the operator form and the method form cannot come to mean different things.
	//
	// The `typeParameter` operand is the witness receiver. Equality is symmetric, so a `T`-vs-value mix may use
	// whichever side is the parameter; ORDERING IS NOT — `self.a < self.b` must dispatch on the LEFT operand —
	// and the shared `Self`-argument check is what guarantees it does: `compare`'s formal is `Self`, so an actual
	// that is not a value of the RECEIVER'S OWN parameter is rejected BEFORE anything is emitted. The emit is
	// therefore only ever reached with both operands values of the SAME enclosing parameter, where `leftIsParam`
	// holds and the receiver IS `lhs`; a `42 < self.item` never reaches it to be silently flipped into
	// `self.item > 42`, and neither does a `self.a < self.b` over two DIFFERENT parameters (see
	// `isReceiverTypeParamValue` — that second half was not true until a review probe segfaulted on it).
	function emitTypeParamComparison(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token, leftIsParam bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let receiver = lhs if leftIsParam else rhs
		let other = rhs if leftIsParam else lhs
		let receiverParam = self.valueNameId(receiver)
		let target = try self.requireOperatorWitness(receiver, op: op, opTok: opTok)

		// The `other` operand fills the method's `Self`-typed formal, so it must be a value of the receiver's own
		// `T` — a concrete literal (`item == 42`, `item < 42`) would be marshalled into the `Self` pointer and
		// faulted. Same reject the method-call form runs, one shared helper.
		let args = pairArgs(receiver, second: other)
		try self.requireGenuineSelfArgs(target, args: args, what: comparisonOpSpelling(op), receiverParam: receiverParam, errorTok: opTok)

		// Typed through the SAME witness-dispatch result typer the `.method()` form uses, so `equals`'s `bool`
		// and `compare`'s `Ordering` are minted here exactly as they are there (the `Ordering` result carries the
		// enum's `named` tag, which is what lets a `match` on a `compare()` result recognize it). Its void and
		// `Self` arms cannot fire for an operator: `Equatable`/`Comparable` each declare one value-returning
		// method, and `resultUsed` is true because a comparison is only ever parsed for its verdict.
		let dispatchResult = try self.mintWitnessDispatchResult(target, receiver: receiver, methodTok: opTok, resultUsed: true)
		self.emitWitnessDispatch(dispatchResult, args: args, target: target, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok), siteTok: opTok)

		// The verdict. `==` is the dispatch's own bool and `!=` negates that SAME dispatch, so the two can never
		// disagree about what equality means (the `emitByteRecordCompare` shape); the four ordering operators read
		// the `Ordering` (below).
		match op 'verdict'
			eq then return dispatchResult
			ne then return self.emitUnaryOp(dispatchResult, op: MaxonUnaryOp.logicalNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
			lt to ge then return self.emitOrderingVerdict(dispatchResult, op: op, opTok: opTok)
		end 'verdict'
	end 'emitTypeParamComparison'

	// The ordinal test that turns a `compare` result's `Ordering` into an ordering operator's bool (P1.7a
	// slice 2b-ii). Its two callers are the two ways a `compare` is reached — the WITNESS dispatch on a
	// constrained type parameter (`emitTypeParamComparison`) and the DIRECT `__char_cmp` call a `Character`
	// pair lowers to (`emitByteRecordOrdering`, A5m-c) — and they share this because the verdict is a fact
	// about `Ordering`, not about how the ordering was obtained.
	//
	// It reads the THREE-VALUED result: `<` is `lessThan` and `>` is `greaterThan`, while the
	// INCLUSIVE pair is the NEGATION of the strict opposite — `<=` is "not greaterThan", `>=` is "not lessThan"
	// — which is what makes `equalTo` satisfy both without a second compare, and is the bootstrap's mapping
	// (`2-Parser.cs:22293-22302`, the correct 3-valued reading).
	//
	// Every tag is read OFF the `Ordering` layout (`EnumLayout.tagOfCase`), never written as a literal; the layout is
	// fetched ONCE here rather than per arm. The compare itself is `emitCompareAgainstLiteral`, the SAME tag compare
	// an enum `match` arm emits — so `a < b` and a hand-written `match a.compare(b)` agree by construction.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE LAYOUT IS THE CORPUS'S OWN (W3)** — `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s declaration, out of the
	// whole-program index, rather than a transcription of it in `Project.builtinOrderingEnum`. The Std-tier
	// `compare` impls whose result this tests read the SAME declaration
	// (`BuiltinConformanceRuntime.installBuiltinConformanceRuntime`), so the producer and the consumer of
	// this tag cannot come to number the cases differently.
	function emitOrderingVerdict(orderingResult ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		let ordering = requireCorpusDeclaredEnum(self.signatures.enumTypes, typeName: OrderingTypeName, reader: "the `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` lowering")
		let lessThanTag = ordering.tagOfCase(OrderingLessThanCaseName)
		let greaterThanTag = ordering.tagOfCase(OrderingGreaterThanCaseName)

		match op 'ordinal'
			eq to ne then panic("emitOrderingVerdict: `==`/`!=` are the equality verdict, answered by each caller's own equality path (`emitTypeParamComparison`'s `Equatable` dispatch, `emitByteRecordCompare`'s `__str_eq`) before an ORDERING operator is routed here")
			lt then return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(orderingResult, literal: lessThanTag, op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, opTok: opTok)
			gt then return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(orderingResult, literal: greaterThanTag, op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, opTok: opTok)
			le then return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(orderingResult, literal: greaterThanTag, op: MaxonCmpOp.ne, opTok: opTok)
			ge then return self.emitCompareAgainstLiteral(orderingResult, literal: lessThanTag, op: MaxonCmpOp.ne, opTok: opTok)
		end 'ordinal'
	end 'emitOrderingVerdict'

	// The witness a comparison operator on type parameter `paramPos` dispatches through (P1.7a slice 2b/2b-ii),
	// or E3005 when the parameter carries no constraint that PROVIDES THE OPERATOR'S PROTOCOL — `Equatable` for
	// `==`/`!=`, `Comparable` for `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE PROTOCOL IS FETCHED, NOT DESCRIBED.** The method name the constraints are searched for, the
	// result type the verdict reads and the FORMALS the operator can fill are ALL taken off the interface
	// `builtinInterface` synthesizes — the whole `InterfaceMethodSig` is handed on rather than picked apart
	// into parallel `want*` arguments, so this function names ONE fact (which protocol the operator belongs
	// to) instead of several parallel spellings of it.
	function requireOperatorWitness(receiver ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, opTok Token) returns WitnessDispatchTarget throws ParseError
		let protocol = requireBuiltinInterface(EquatableInterfaceName if comparisonIsEquality(op) else ComparableInterfaceName)
		let method = try protocol.methods.get(0) otherwise panic("requireOperatorWitness: the synthesized protocol '{String.from(protocol.name)}' declares no method — a comparison operator dispatches its SOLE method (`singleMethodInterface`), so `builtinInterface` must give it exactly one")

		// The search itself runs by METHOD (the shared core the `.equals()`/`.compare()` dispatch uses), so a hit
		// still has to be PROVED to be the protocol's — see `witnessTargetIsProtocol`. It is now the FILTER
		// rather than a check applied to whichever candidate came first, which is strictly stronger: a
		// constraint declaring a non-protocol `equals` used to MASK a genuinely `Equatable` constraint written
		// after it, since the search stopped at the first name match and then rejected it.
		var protocolShaped = WitnessDispatchTargetArray.create()
		let candidates = try self.findWitnessDispatchCandidates(receiver, methodName: method.name, errorTok: opTok)
		for i in 0 upto candidates.count() 'eachCandidate'
			let candidate = try candidates.get(i) otherwise panic("requireOperatorWitness: candidates.get OOB at {i} below count {candidates.count()}")
			if witnessTargetIsProtocol(candidate, want: method) 'isTheProtocol'
				protocolShaped.push(candidate)
			end 'isTheProtocol'
		end 'eachCandidate'

		match pickSoleWitnessCandidate(protocolShaped) 'pick'
			one(target) then return target
			// A near-miss reports the SAME E3005 as no constraint at all, because the author's cure is the same
			// sentence: constrain the parameter with the protocol. (Constructed inline rather than through a
			// shared factory — `throw self.helperReturningAParseError(…)` leaks the boxed union under the
			// bootstrap's refcounting; see `floatPayloadMessage`.)
			none then throw ParseError.operatorRequiresConformance(comparisonOpSpelling(op), typeParamName: String.from(self.typeParamDisplayName(self.valueNameId(receiver))), interfaceName: String.from(protocol.name), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
			// TWO constraints genuinely shaped like the protocol. They are two DISTINCT witness slots holding
			// two DISTINCT conformers' impls, so "they are both `Equatable`" does not make them interchangeable
			// — the operator would silently take whichever was written first. Same reject as the `.method()`
			// form's, for the same reason.
			ambiguous(first, second) then throw ParseError.ambiguousWitnessDispatch(String.from(method.name), provider: self.witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver), argCount: method.paramTypeNames.count(), first: renderWitnessRequirement(first), second: renderWitnessRequirement(second), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column)
		end 'pick'
	end 'requireOperatorWitness'

	// ⭐ **A CONSTRAINT DECLARING A METHOD OF THE PROTOCOL'S NAME IS NOT THE PROTOCOL** — and the verdict below
	// reads the dispatch result as if it were. `findWitnessDispatchCandidates` matches by METHOD NAME because the
	// `.method()` dispatch it is shared with must, so a USER interface with its own `compare`/`equals` satisfies
	// it: **MEASURED — an `interface Weird` declaring `function compare(other Self) returns Integer` made
	// `a < b` mean `a.compare(b) == 0`, i.e. "less than" whenever the author's `compare` said EQUAL; the twin
	// with `equals` made `a == b` evaluate to the raw `7` that `equals` returned.** Both are silent wrong
	// answers, not crashes, so nothing downstream was ever going to catch them.
	//
	// Requiring the found method's RESULT to be the protocol's own declared result is what makes the target
	// genuinely the protocol's — `equals` must yield the `bool` the `eq`/`ne` arms take, `compare` the `Ordering`
	// whose tags `emitOrderingVerdict` tests. A near-miss reports the SAME E3005 as no constraint at all,
	// because the author's cure is the same sentence: constrain the parameter with the protocol. (Constructed
	// inline at both sites rather than through a shared factory — `throw self.helperReturningAParseError(…)`
	// leaks the boxed union under the bootstrap's refcounting; see `floatPayloadMessage`.)
	//
	// ⚠ **A THROWING METHOD IS REFUSED HERE FOR THE SAME REASON A WRONG-RESULT ONE IS (P1.7a slice 2b-v).**
	// `Equatable.equals` and `Comparable.compare` are synthesized non-throwing, so a user interface whose
	// `equals` declares `throws E` is no more the protocol than one returning `7` is — and there is no
	// spelling that would make it safe: an OPERATOR has nowhere to put a `try`, so the dispatch would be
	// emitted single-register against a callee returning through the dual-register error ABI, dropping the
	// error exactly as the missing `witnessTryCall` did. Same E3005, because the author's cure is the same
	// sentence.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND SO ARE THE FORMALS — BOTH HOW MANY AND WHAT TYPE — WHICH WERE THE THIRD AND FOURTH HOLES IN
	// THE SAME WALL (P1.7a slice 2b-vi; the type half found in its review).** An operator has exactly one
	// operand to give and that operand is a value of `T`, so the formal LIST is as much a part of "is this
	// the protocol?" as the result type is. Both halves are one question, asked once, against the
	// SYNTHESIZED interface's own `paramTypeNames` — never written as a literal `1` or a literal `Self`, for
	// the reason this function's header gives. Neither is cosmetic; both were MEASURED silent wrong answers:
	//
	// - **ARITY.** `interface Weird { function equals(a Self, b Self) returns bool }` under `where T is
	//   Weird` compiled CLEAN and the impl read its unsupplied second `Self` formal out of an uninitialised
	//   argument register, dereferencing it (a fault away from a crash).
	// - **FORMAL TYPE.** `interface WeirdEq { function equals(other int) returns bool }` under `where T is
	//   WeirdEq` compiled CLEAN and `self.item == self.other` handed the RECEIVER'S `T` POINTER to a formal
	//   the impl reads as an `int` — `equals(other int) { return other == 7 }` answered `false` for two
	//   equal values (measured on `main` as well as on this branch). `requireGenuineSelfArgs` cannot cover
	//   it: it validates the actuals at `Self` formals, and this interface declares none. The `float` twin
	//   is worse — `floatWidenedWitnessArgs` sees a `float` formal and `cvtsi2sd`s the pointer — and a
	//   `String` formal is a type confusion, an unmanaged struct pointer read through a managed header.
	//
	// The `.method()` form reaches neither: `slottedWitnessArgs` checks its arity against the interface, and
	// its `Self` formals really are `Self`. The operator form was the last door left open, and closing the
	// arity half is what makes `requireGenuineSelfArgs`' missing-actual case unrepresentable.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A PREDICATE AND NOT A REJECT, AND IT LIVES IN `IrInterface` (R10c).** It used to throw E3005
	// at the single candidate the name search had already committed to; it is now the FILTER that search
	// feeds, so a program constraining a parameter by BOTH a real protocol and a look-alike picks the real one
	// instead of being refused by whichever the author wrote first. The reject stays here, in
	// `requireOperatorWitness`'s empty arm, where "nothing here is the protocol" is the same fact as "no
	// constraint declares it"; the shape TEST went to `IrInterface.witnessTargetIsProtocol`, beside
	// `interfaceFormalsMatch`, because it reads no `self`.

	// ⭐ **REJECT ANY NON-`Self` VALUE SUPPLIED FOR A `Self`-TYPED WITNESS-METHOD ARGUMENT (P1.7a slice 2b).** A
	// witness-dispatched method whose formal is `Self` (`Equatable.equals`'s and `Comparable.compare`'s `other
	// Self`) requires an ACTUAL argument that is a value of the receiver's OWN enclosing type parameter — the
	// only thing the `where` constraint proves. Anything else marshalled into that `Self` pointer/slot is a
	// MISCOMPILE: the callee's `equals(other Point)` dereferences whatever word it was handed as its `other`
	// pointer (0xC0000005). Run UNIFORMLY for the operator (`T == 42`, `T < 42`) and method-call
	// (`T.equals(42)`, `T.compare(42)`) forms — their one shared arg-validation point. It also carries a SECOND
	// guarantee for the asymmetric ordering operators: rejecting a non-`Self` actual is what proves the ordering
	// dispatch's receiver is always the LEFT operand (see `emitTypeParamComparison`). `args[0]` is the receiver;
	// interface formal `j` maps to `args[j+1]`.
	//
	// ⭐ **EVERY FORMAL HAS AN ACTUAL BY THE TIME THIS RUNS, AND BOTH CALLERS NOW GUARANTEE IT (P1.7a slice
	// 2b-vi).** It used to skip a formal with no actual, "an arity concern left to the caller" — and no caller
	// had it: the `.method()` form performed no arity check at all, and the operator form supplies one actual
	// to whatever arity the constraint's method declares. Both holes are closed (`slottedWitnessArgs` slots
	// against the interface's `paramNames`; `witnessTargetIsProtocol` requires the protocol's own formals), so a
	// missing actual is a compiler defect rather than a program's, and it panics instead of quietly passing an
	// under-supplied dispatch through to the emit.
	function requireGenuineSelfArgs(target WitnessDispatchTarget, args ValueIdArray, what String, receiverParam TypeParamId, errorTok Token) throws ParseError
		for j in 0 upto target.paramTypeNames.count() 'eachFormal'
			let formalTypeName = try target.paramTypeNames.get(j) otherwise panic("requireGenuineSelfArgs: paramTypeNames.get OOB at {j}")
			if not formalTypeName.equals(InterfaceSelfTypeName) 'notSelfFormal'
				continue
			end 'notSelfFormal'

			let actual = try args.get(j + 1) otherwise panic("requireGenuineSelfArgs: formal {j} of '{what}' has no actual — args holds {args.count()} entries (receiver + {args.count() - 1}) for {target.paramTypeNames.count()} formals, so the caller's arity check did not run")
			if not self.isReceiverTypeParamValue(actual, receiverParam: receiverParam) 'notSelfArg'
				throw ParseError.witnessSelfArgNotTypeParam(what, typeParamName: String.from(self.typeParamDisplayName(receiverParam)), got: self.selfArgActualDescription(actual), line: errorTok.line, column: errorTok.column)
			end 'notSelfArg'
		end 'eachFormal'
	end 'requireGenuineSelfArgs'

	// ⭐ **THE `.method(…)` FORM'S `Self`-FORMAL GATE, SPLIT BY RECEIVER KIND (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).**
	// A `Self`-typed formal demands an argument of the receiver's OWN dynamic type, and only one of the two
	// receivers can supply that proof:
	//
	//   • a CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER can — every value of `T` in the body IS the receiver's type, so
	//     `isReceiverTypeParamValue` decides it exactly (see `requireGenuineSelfArgs`);
	//   • an EXISTENTIAL cannot, and no compiler can. Two values held at one interface may have DIFFERENT
	//     dynamic types, so `b.equals(c)` over two `Backend`s would marshal one conformer's box into the
	//     other's `other Self` and dereference it as the wrong type — a fault, or worse a wrong answer. Both
	//     reference compilers admit that program; shv2 refuses it, because the check that would make it sound
	//     is a runtime type test the language has no expression for.
	//
	// Refused with the "unsupported construct" code rather than an argument-type mismatch, because nothing the
	// author could write at the ARGUMENT would fix it — the requirement itself is undispatchable through an
	// existential.
	function requireWitnessSelfArgs(target WitnessDispatchTarget, args ValueIdArray, receiver ValueId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.tagOf(receiver) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'constrainedTypeParameter'
			try self.requireGenuineSelfArgs(target, args: args, what: String.from(methodTok.value), receiverParam: self.valueNameId(receiver), errorTok: methodTok)
			return
		end 'constrainedTypeParameter'

		for j in 0 upto target.paramTypeNames.count() 'eachFormal'
			let formalTypeName = try target.paramTypeNames.get(j) otherwise panic("requireWitnessSelfArgs: paramTypeNames.get OOB at {j} below count {target.paramTypeNames.count()}")
			if not formalTypeName.equals(InterfaceSelfTypeName) 'notSelfFormal'
				continue
			end 'notSelfFormal'

			throw ParseError.unsupported("a `Self`-typed parameter of '{self.witnessCalleeName(target)}' dispatched on a value held at {self.witnessDispatchReceiverPhrase(receiver)} — a `Self` formal requires an argument of the RECEIVER's own dynamic type, and {ExistentialPairUnprovableReason}. Dispatch it on a concrete value, or on a type parameter constrained to the interface", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'eachFormal'
	end 'requireWitnessSelfArgs'

	// ⭐ **IS `actual` A VALUE OF THE RECEIVER'S OWN TYPE PARAMETER — the ONLY thing a `Self`-typed formal
	// accepts?** "A value of SOME enclosing type parameter" is not enough, and the difference is a **MEASURED
	// 0xC0000005**: in `type Mix uses A, B where A is Comparable, B is Comparable` instantiated at
	// `(Point, Integer)`, `self.a < self.b` dispatched `A`'s witness — `Point.compare` — with a `B` actual, so
	// the callee dereferenced the integer `9` as its `other Point`. Reversed to `(Integer, Point)` it does not
	// fault but silently compares an int against a heap pointer, and the `.compare()` METHOD form takes the same
	// route. `Self` means the receiver's parameter, not "a type parameter", and the witness table it dispatches
	// through belongs to ONE parameter, so the position must match.
	//
	// (A genuine cross-parameter COMPARISON — `A` vs `B` where both conform — is a real feature and needs a
	// second witness plus a conversion; it is not this, and refusing it is what keeps the door shut until then.)
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COMPARISON IS BETWEEN TWO IDENTITIES AND IT ALWAYS MEANT TO BE (W14).** Before the type-parameter
	// token it compared two POSITIONS, which is the same answer only while both come from one declaration —
	// the property this door exists to check rather than to assume.
	function isReceiverTypeParamValue(actual ValueId, receiverParam TypeParamId) returns bool
		return self.isEnclosingTypeParam(actual) and self.valueNameId(actual) == receiverParam
	end 'isReceiverTypeParamValue'

	// WHAT was supplied where a `Self` was required, for `witnessSelfArgNotTypeParam`'s message — the two ways
	// an actual fails `isReceiverTypeParamValue`, described in ONE place so the diagnostic never calls a value
	// of another type parameter "a concrete value".
	function selfArgActualDescription(actual ValueId) returns String
		if not self.isEnclosingTypeParam(actual) 'concrete'
			return "a concrete value"
		end 'concrete'

		return "a value of type parameter '{String.from(self.typeParamDisplayName(self.valueNameId(actual)))}'"
	end 'selfArgActualDescription'

	// The declared NAME the type-parameter token `param` was minted from (P1.7a slice 2b) — `T` in `type Box
	// uses T`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO RE-SPELL THE TOKEN AGAINST THE *READER'S* ENCLOSING DECLARATION, AND A READER THAT
	// ENCLOSES NOTHING MADE THAT RENDERING EMPTY (W181).** It read `typeParamPositionIn(param,
	// declarationName: self.enclosingType, …)` and panicked on the miss, on the argument that "every caller
	// reaches here past `isEnclosingTypeParam`". Four callers do not — the three DIAGNOSTIC PHRASE builders
	// below and `requireDefaultableFieldType` — and at FILE SCOPE `self.enclosingType` is
	// `EmptySourceText`, so the panic read `names no parameter of '', which declares 0`. MEASURED: a value
	// whose type a substitution gap left as a bare `Bag.Element` reached `main`, a member call on it found no
	// witness, and the compiler died BUILDING THE SENTENCE THAT WOULD HAVE SAID SO — a diagnostic the program
	// had already earned, replaced by a stack trace.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A PARAMETER'S NAME IS A PROPERTY OF THE TOKEN, NOT OF WHOEVER IS LOOKING AT IT.** Since W14 a
	// token IS a digest of `(declaring type, parameter name)`, so the name is answerable wherever the token
	// is — inside the declaring body, in a sibling generic, or in a `main` that declares nothing. TWO tables
	// can invert it, and the order below is not a preference: it is the only order that is TOTAL.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE ORDER IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED — REGISTRY-FIRST PANICS DURING THE SWEEP.** The obvious
	// simplification is to read `typeParamOwnerOf` alone and call the local list redundant. It is not:
	// `requireDefaultableFieldType` runs inside `foldDeclaredSignaturesInto`, which is the pass that FILES
	// those owners, so the registry is empty for the very declaration being folded. MEASURED on this branch:
	// `field-initialization/type-parameter-field-{literal,expression}-default-errors` — two committed cases
	// expecting a positioned E2015 naming `'T'` — died with `typeParamDisplayName: type-parameter token
	// -5864020729385773348 has no owner`, inside `readStructFieldInto`. **`enclosingTypeParams` is what a
	// sweep-time reader has and the registry is what a post-fold reader has**, and neither covers the other's
	// phase.
	//
	// ⇒ The DECLARATION the cursor is inside answers first (available from the moment the `uses` clause is
	// read, and correct by construction for its own parameters); the whole-program registry answers for every
	// OTHER token, which is what a file-scope reader holding a foreign parameter needs and what the old
	// `enclosingTypeParamName` could not do — it panicked `names no parameter of ''` while building the very
	// sentence that would have reported the program's own error (W181). The two agree wherever both can
	// answer, because both spell the same half of the same W14 digest.
	//
	// Absence from BOTH is an invariant break: a token neither this declaration nor any fold has seen was
	// minted inside a declaration that never reached either.
	function typeParamDisplayName(param TypeParamId) returns ByteArray
		if let position = try typeParamPositionIn(param, declarationName: self.enclosingType, paramNames: self.enclosingTypeParams) 'oneOfTheCursorsOwn'
			return try self.enclosingTypeParams.get(position) otherwise panic("typeParamDisplayName: enclosingTypeParams.get OOB at {position}, a position `typeParamPositionIn` just read out of the same list")
		end 'oneOfTheCursorsOwn'

		return (try self.signatures.typeParamOwnerOf(param) otherwise panic("typeParamDisplayName: type-parameter token {param} names no parameter of the declaration being parsed ('{String.from(self.enclosingType)}') and has no owner in the whole-program registry either — `recordStruct` and `recordInterfaceDeclaration` file every declared parameter as the sweep folds, so a token missing from both was minted inside a declaration neither fold saw")).paramName
	end 'typeParamDisplayName'

	// ⭐ **WHAT PROVIDES THE REQUIREMENTS A WITNESS DISPATCH SEARCHED — for the diagnostics that have to name
	// it (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** One dispatch mechanism serves two receivers and only this differs:
	// a constrained type parameter's requirements come from its `where` constraints, an existential's from the
	// one interface the value is held at. Built on the FAILURE path only, exactly as
	// `IrInterface.separatorFreedomSubjectOf` is, so the success path allocates no String.
	function witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver ValueId) returns String
		if self.tagOf(receiver) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			return "interface '{String.from(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(receiver)))}'"
		end 'existential'

		return "the constraints on type parameter '{String.from(self.typeParamDisplayName(self.valueNameId(receiver)))}'"
	end 'witnessDispatchProviderPhrase'

	// The same receiver in the other grammatical slot — "a value OF this cannot reach a `static` requirement".
	// Two phrases rather than one for the reason `ParseError.staticRequirementNotDispatchable` states.
	function witnessDispatchReceiverPhrase(receiver ValueId) returns String
		if self.tagOf(receiver) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existential'
			return "interface '{String.from(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(receiver)))}'"
		end 'existential'

		return "type parameter '{String.from(self.typeParamDisplayName(self.valueNameId(receiver)))}'"
	end 'witnessDispatchReceiverPhrase'

	// Emit the `__str_eq(lhs, rhs)` runtime call, typed `boolean` (its i64 result is 0/1). The one
	// caller-typed emit site for both `==` and `!=`, and for a `string`-scrutinee `match` pattern.
	function emitStrEqCall(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opTok Token) returns ValueId
		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(lhs)
		args.push(rhs)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(StrEqName, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.ofToken(opTok))
	end 'emitStrEqCall'

	// ⭐ **IS THE IDENTIFIER AT THE CURSOR A FUNCTION-TYPED LOCAL — the callee of an INDIRECT call?** A
	// call on such a name (`let f = double; f(21)`) goes THROUGH the value (P1.5-A1), not to a function
	// NAMED by the identifier. It is the ONE diversion that turns `<name>(` from a direct call into an
	// indirect one, and it must read the SAME in both positions the grammar reaches a bare `<name>(`:
	// EXPRESSION position (`parsePrimary`'s `namedPrimary`) and STATEMENT position (`parseStatement`'s
	// `callStmt`). A non-function local of the same name keeps the direct-call path (`x(5)` with an int
	// `x` is still "call function x", E3004), and a name that is no local at all is a plain direct call —
	// so a genuinely undefined statement-position call is still E3004, not silently accepted.
	//
	// The cursor must sit on the identifier: both callers guard with `at(identifier)` and a `(` peeked
	// behind it, so `current().value` is the callee name and `getInfo` is reached only past `contains`.
	function calleeIsFunctionTypedLocal() returns bool
		return self.scope.contains(self.current().value) and maxonTypeTag(self.scope.getInfo(self.current().value).varType) == ValueTypeTag.function
	end 'calleeIsFunctionTypedLocal'

	// A primary expression: an integer literal, a `true`/`false` literal, a CHARACTER literal,
	// a parenthesized sub-expression, a type-qualified numeric bound (`u16.max`), a call, or a
	// variable reference. Anything else where an operand is required (an operator, a delimiter,
	// EOF) is E2004 — "Expected expression but got '<got>'". This is the site `- -x` hits
	// at its second `-`: unary minus's operand is a primary, and a leading `-` is not one.
	function parsePrimary(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'intLit'
			return try self.parseIntLiteral()
		end 'intLit'

		if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'floatLit'
			return try self.parseFloatLiteral()
		end 'floatLit'

		if self.at(TokenKind.true) or self.at(TokenKind.false) 'boolLit'
			return try self.parseBoolLiteral()
		end 'boolLit'

		// A charLiteral HERE — where an operand is required — is a character literal. The
		// same token kind is a BLOCK LABEL elsewhere, and the two never collide because a
		// label never sits in operand position (see the CharLiteralValue header).
		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'charLit'
			return try self.parseCharLiteral()
		end 'charLit'

		// A `String` LITERAL (P1.2 wave A). A pure literal is a SINGLE `stringLiteral` token; an
		// INTERPOLATED string (`"{x}"`) is the same token followed by `stringInterpStart`, which is
		// Wave B — `parseStringLiteral` rejects it.
		if self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'strLit'
			return try self.parseStringLiteral(func)
		end 'strLit'

		// A `b"…"` BYTE-STRING LITERAL (P1.7 slice 2) — its own token kind, so it cannot ride the string arm
		// above. It materializes as an owned `Array with Byte` over an immortal rdata blob.
		if self.at(TokenKind.byteStringLiteral) 'byteStrLit'
			return try self.parseByteStringLiteral()
		end 'byteStrLit'

		// `[a, b, c]` — an ARRAY LITERAL (P1.7 slice 1). Its own arm because `[` is its own token. It
		// desugars to `__managed_create` + a `__managed_push` per element, yielding an owned `Array with integer`.
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'arrayLiteral'
			return try self.parseArrayLiteral(func, element: ArrayLiteralElementSource.inferred)
		end 'arrayLiteral'

		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'group'
			return try self.parseParenthesizedExpression(func)
		end 'group'

		// `match <expr> 'label' … end 'label'` in value position — `let r = match …`, `return
		// match …`, `match … * 2`. A distinct parser gap from the statement form: the expression
		// parser had no `match` arm at all (E2004 `Expected expression but got 'match'`), where the
		// statement parser reached lowering before failing (E2015). It leaves control in the merge
		// block and yields the merge's result phi, exactly as `emitShortCircuit` does.
		if self.at(TokenKind.match) 'matchExpr'
			return try self.parseMatchExpression(func)
		end 'matchExpr'

		// `try <call> [otherwise <handler>]` in value position — `let r = try f() otherwise 0`, `return try
		// f() otherwise 42`. Yields the merged result value and leaves control in the merge (or the ok block
		// for a terminating/propagate handler), exactly as `parseMatchExpression` does.
		if self.at(TokenKind.try) 'tryExpr'
			return try self.parseTry(func, resultUsed: true)
		end 'tryExpr'

		// `function(params) gives <expr>` — a CLOSURE LITERAL in value position (P1.5-A2a). `function` is
		// its own keyword, so it cannot ride the identifier arm below. A NON-capturing closure lifts to a
		// top-level function and yields a plain `functionRef` value (the A1 machinery); one that captures an
		// enclosing local is refused (A2b). See `parseClosureExpression`.
		if self.at(TokenKind.function) 'closureLiteral'
			return try self.parseClosureExpression(func)
		end 'closureLiteral'

		// `Self{…}` — a struct literal naming the enclosing type — and `Self.<member>`, which is every DOTTED
		// form the enclosing type's own NAME has (D9). `Self` is its own KEYWORD token, not an identifier, so
		// it cannot ride the identifier arm below; but the `{`-vs-`.` lookahead that splits the two here is
		// the SAME split that arm makes for a name, and it routes to the SAME two routines. (`Self` in a TYPE
		// position never reaches an expression at all.)
		if self.at(TokenKind.selfType) 'selfPrimary'
			if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'selfDotted'
				return try self.parseDottedPrimary(func, resultUsed: true)
			end 'selfDotted'
			return try self.parseStructLiteral(func)
		end 'selfPrimary'

		// `self.x` / `self.m(…)` — the RECEIVER, which is a different keyword from `Self` the TYPE
		// (`self` vs `selfType`; the lexer splits them, so nothing here has to). Its own arm for the
		// same reason `Self{…}` has one: a keyword cannot ride the identifier arm below.
		if self.at(TokenKind.self) 'selfReceiver'
			return try self.parseSelfPrimary(func, resultUsed: true)
		end 'selfReceiver'

		// `sizeof(Type)` (P1.6-B1) — its own arm because `sizeof` is a KEYWORD, so it cannot ride the
		// identifier arm below (which would treat it as a value-position call to a function named `sizeof`,
		// leaving `T` an "undefined variable").
		if self.at(TokenKind.sizeof) 'sizeofExpr'
			return try self.parseSizeof()
		end 'sizeofExpr'

		// `countof(Type)` (W190) — its own arm for `sizeof`'s reason, one line up: `countof` is a KEYWORD, so
		// it cannot ride the identifier arm below, which would read `countof(Self)` as a value-position call
		// to a function nobody declares.
		if self.at(TokenKind.countof) 'countofExpr'
			return try self.parseCountof()
		end 'countofExpr'

		// ⭐ **`int.fromString(s)` — A STATIC ON A PRIMITIVE TYPE (A1s-prim).** Its own arm for `Self`'s
		// reason, one arm up: `int`/`float`/`bool` are KEYWORDS, so they cannot ride the identifier arm
		// below — and `keywordIsANameInOperandPosition` cannot admit them either, because it requires the
		// next token to be `(` or the name to be bound, and `int` followed by `.` is neither. It routes to
		// `parseDottedPrimary`, which is where every OTHER type-named dotted form is split, so the scope
		// shadowing rule and the arm order are read from one place rather than restated. `byte.fromString(…)`
		// needs no arm at all — `byte` is an identifier here, so it is already below.
		if self.primitiveStaticCallAt(self.pos) 'primitiveStaticCall'
			return try self.parseDottedPrimary(func, resultUsed: true)
		end 'primitiveStaticCall'

		// ⭐ **A KEYWORD JOINS THE NAMED ARM WHEN A NAME IS ALL IT CAN BE (D8)** — `return type` reading the
		// parameter of `function identity(type Integer)`, `return from(41)` calling a function named `from`.
		// It is the SAME arm and not a parallel one, so a keyword-named value gets the four forms an
		// identifier gets (call, struct literal, dotted, bare read) rather than a subset that would diverge.
		// See `keywordIsANameInOperandPosition` for why the ORDER of the arms above is the whole safety
		// argument.
		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) or try self.keywordIsANameInOperandPosition() 'namedPrimary'
			// `Set from [1, 2, 3]` — a builtin `Set` CONSTRUCTED from an array literal (P1.7b slice B). The bare
			// `Set` identifier followed by `from` is a construction, NOT a variable reference (`Set` names no
			// binding, so the plain path below reports E2004 `Undefined variable 'Set'`). Its element type is
			// inferred from the literal.
			if self.atSetFromConstruction() 'setFromConstruction'
				return try self.parseSetFromConstruction(func)
			end 'setFromConstruction'

			// `List from [a, b, c]` — the same shape one container over, and it is here rather than folded into
			// the arm above because the two build DIFFERENT things from the same tokens; sharing an arm would
			// mean a runtime callee chosen by re-testing the base name that already picked the arm.
			if self.atListFromConstruction() 'listFromConstruction'
				return try self.parseListFromConstruction(func)
			end 'listFromConstruction'

			// `Vector from [10, 20, 30]` — the same shape a third container over, and its own arm for the
			// `List` arm's reason: the three build different things from the same tokens.
			if self.atVectorFromConstruction() 'vectorFromConstruction'
				return try self.parseVectorFromConstruction(func)
			end 'vectorFromConstruction'

			// `ByteArray from [10, 20, 30]` — the ALIAS-headed form, whose head names an `Array` instance
			// rather than a builtin base name. Its own arm for the reason the three above are three arms, and
			// it cannot collide with any of them: the bases are disjoint, and a name that aliases an `Array`
			// is not `Set`, `List` or `Vector`.
			if self.atArrayFromConstruction() 'arrayFromConstruction'
				return try self.parseArrayFromConstruction(func)
			end 'arrayFromConstruction'

			// `FilePath from "C:\\x.txt"` — the `InitableFromStringLiteral` construction, which is the SECOND
			// `<Type> from …` form (see `atStringLiteralInitConstruction`, and the paragraph above it that
			// used to say `Set from` was the only one). It is asked AFTER the `Set` arm because that arm
			// claims a name no `type` declares, so the order is a reading order and not a precedence.
			if self.atStringLiteralInitConstruction() 'literalInitConstruction'
				return try self.parseStringLiteralInitConstruction(func)
			end 'literalInitConstruction'

			// One-token lookaheads split the four forms an identifier can open. `(` makes it a CALL
			// (the same shape the statement parser uses to tell a call apart from a bare name); `{`
			// makes it a STRUCT LITERAL; `.` makes it one of the three DOTTED forms
			// (`parseDottedPrimary` splits those); anything else is a variable reference.
			if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftParen 'callExpr'
				// A LOCAL binding of FUNCTION type shadowed by the same name is called THROUGH its value
				// — an INDIRECT call (`f(21)` where `let f = double`), not a direct call to a function
				// NAMED `f` (P1.5-A1). Resolve it as a variable reference and let the postfix loop
				// (`parsePostfix`) apply the `(args)`. The same diversion — reading the SAME predicate —
				// runs at statement position (`parseStatement`'s `callStmt`); see `calleeIsFunctionTypedLocal`.
				if self.calleeIsFunctionTypedLocal() 'functionLocalCall'
					return try self.parseVariableReference()
				end 'functionLocalCall'

				// A call in an EXPRESSION — its result IS the expression, so a void callee has nothing
				// to give and `parseCall` rejects it (E2004).
				return try self.parseCall(func, resultUsed: true)
			end 'callExpr'
			if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftBrace 'structLiteral'
				return try self.parseStructLiteral(func)
			end 'structLiteral'
			if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'dotted'
				return try self.parseDottedPrimary(func, resultUsed: true)
			end 'dotted'
			return try self.parseVariableReference()
		end 'namedPrimary'

		let tok = self.current()
		throw ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'parsePrimary'

	// ⭐ **IS THE KEYWORD AT THE CURSOR A NAME HERE, in OPERAND position (D8)?** A keyword-named PARAMETER
	// has to be readable or the declaration is decoration (`return type`), and a keyword-named FUNCTION has
	// to be callable or it is dead code the author cannot reach (`return from(41)`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAFETY ARGUMENT IS THE ARM ORDER ABOVE, AND IT IS THE SAME POSITION-SENSITIVITY ARGUMENT
	// `tokenCanBeAName` rests on.** Every keyword with an expression meaning of its own has already been
	// claimed by the time this is asked: `match`, `try`, `function`, `Self`, `self`, `sizeof`, `true` and
	// `false` by the arms directly above; `not`, `async`, `await` and a prefix `-` by `parseUnary`, which
	// runs before `parsePrimary` is ever reached. So a keyword still standing at an operand's first token
	// opens NOTHING, and a name is the only reading left. Those eight therefore may be DECLARED as names but
	// not READ bare — which is exactly what the reference bootstrap does (measured: a parameter named `match`
	// declares and compiles, and `return match` there is `Expected expression but got '+'`).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS GATED ON THE NAME ACTUALLY RESOLVING, so no diagnostic moves.** A keyword that names nothing
	// falls through to the same `expectedExpression` it has always reported, on the same token. Two shapes
	// resolve, and they are the two the grammar can distinguish without a signature lookup:
	//   • a `(` after it makes it a CALL — the callee's existence is `SemanticCheck`'s to answer (E3004,
	//     which names the callee and is the better message anyway), exactly as it is for an identifier;
	//   • a BINDING of that name, local or captured, makes it a value READ.
	//
	// ⚠ A keyword-named free function called as a STATEMENT (`from(3)` on a line of its own) is NOT covered,
	// and cannot be by this argument: the statement dispatcher's own arms mean `while (…)` and `if (…)` are
	// a loop and a conditional with a parenthesized condition, so `<keyword> (` at statement start is
	// genuinely ambiguous rather than merely unclaimed. No corpus file calls a keyword-named function for
	// its effect, and both reference compilers reject the whole shape (see the file header).
	//
	// ⛔ **AND ONE SHAPE IS REFUSED HERE RATHER THAN ACCEPTED, BECAUSE THE TOKEN SCANS CANNOT SEE IT.** A BARE
	// read of a binding named `while`/`match`/`for`/`end` — `acc + while` — is a NAME to this arm and BLOCK
	// STRUCTURE to `opensBlockAt`/`closesBlockAt`, whose verdict for those four is position-INDEPENDENT
	// (`alwaysOpens`, and `end` closes wherever it stands). The scans run over raw tokens with no scope, so
	// they cannot know the name is bound; accepted here, the program panics in `assertScanAligned` instead of
	// compiling. `if` and `else` need no such care and are never refused — their POSITION tests already answer
	// no for an operand (`ifBeginsStatement`, `elseFollowsBlockEnd`).
	//
	// ⚠ **`otherwise` IS REFUSED IN EXACTLY ONE POSITION, AND THE DERIVATION IS WHY THAT COMES OUT RIGHT
	// WITHOUT ANYONE KNOWING IT.** This comment used to group it with `if`/`else` as never-refused, which was
	// FALSE: `otherwiseOpensBlock`'s labelled-handler form is `otherwise 'label'`, and a bare `otherwise` read
	// as a WHOLE block condition is followed by that block's own label — so `while otherwise 'loop'` is
	// refused while `acc + otherwise` compiles (both measured). Nothing had to be corrected in the CODE for
	// that, because the code asks the scans rather than consulting a list; only the prose was wrong. **That is
	// the argument for deriving a set: a list would have carried the same error into the behaviour.**
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SET IS DERIVED, NOT LISTED**: the question is exactly "would a scan read THIS token, HERE, as
	// block structure?", so it is asked of the scans themselves. A rung that teaches them the operand-position
	// exclusion turns this refusal off by construction, with nothing to remember.
	function keywordIsANameInOperandPosition() returns bool throws ParseError
		let tok = self.current()
		if tok.kind == TokenKind.identifier or not tokenCanBeAName(tok) 'notAnUnclaimedKeyword'
			return false
		end 'notAnUnclaimedKeyword'

		if not (self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftParen or self.scope.contains(tok.value) or self.captureBinds(tok.value)) 'namesNothingHere'
			return false
		end 'namesNothingHere'

		if self.opensBlockAt(self.pos) or self.closesBlockAt(self.pos) 'readAsBlockStructure'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("reading '{String.from(tok.value)}' as a value here — it is a legal DECLARED name, but the token scans that re-derive Maxon's block structure read a bare `{String.from(tok.value)}` in this position as block structure and have no scope to tell them otherwise. Pass it under a different name, or read a differently-named binding", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'readAsBlockStructure'

		return true
	end 'keywordIsANameInOperandPosition'

	// ⭐ **`sizeof(Type)`** (P1.6-B1) — the byte size of a type. Two outcomes, decided by the operand:
	//
	//   • A CONCRETE type (a primitive, a ranged alias, a declared struct) is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT,
	//     folded to a `literal` right HERE — which is where the struct registry lives. It must fold here
	//     and cannot ride the `sizeofType` op as a `structRef(id)`: `mergeArtifact` remaps signatures and
	//     field types but NOT op operands, so a file-local struct id would survive into a multi-file
	//     lowering as garbage. A concrete `sizeof` is a constant anyway, exactly like `let n = 42`.
	//   • A TYPE PARAMETER (`sizeof(T)` inside `type Sizer uses T`) has NO single compile-time answer — the
	//     body compiles ONCE against an opaque `T` — so it emits a `sizeofType(typeParameter)` op that
	//     lowers to a runtime read of the instance's layout descriptor. `typeParameter` carries a POSITION,
	//     not an interned id, so it survives the merge unremapped.
	function parseSizeof() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let sizeofTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.sizeof)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let operand = self.resolveNamedStruct(try self.parseTypeReference())
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		if maxonTypeTag(operand) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'runtimeSize'
			// ⚠ **A RECEIVER MUST BE IN SCOPE (B1).** The layout descriptor reaches a body by being THREADED
			// from the receiver instance a method is called on (dictionary-passing). A `static function` (called
			// on the TYPE) and a lifted closure (whose ValueId 0 is its first param, not the enclosing `self`)
			// are both RECEIVERLESS, so a caller has no instance whose descriptor to pass. The parser would
			// still reserve the hidden slot for such a body, but a call site would then reach
			// `finalizeCallArgsWithLayout` with no receiver argument (static) — or the closure's own
			// `sizeofType` op would reach lowering with no descriptor param — and PANIC. So reject `sizeof(T)`
			// here, cleanly (E2015), exactly like the multi-parameter case below. `__self` is bound in scope
			// ONLY inside an instance method's own body (the receiver param, the same binding
			// `requireReceiverValue` reads), so its absence IS the receiverless test. Read the size through an
			// instance method on `self` (or a concrete instance); threading a descriptor into a static call
			// site's alias, or capturing it into a closure, is a later slice.
			if not self.scope.contains(SelfParamName) 'noReceiver'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("sizeof of a type parameter with no instance receiver in scope — a `static function` and a closure are both receiverless, and B1 threads the layout descriptor from the instance a method is called on; read the size through an instance method on `self`, or a concrete instance, instead", line: sizeofTok.line, column: sizeofTok.column)
			end 'noReceiver'

			// ⚠ **SINGLE-TYPE-PARAMETER ONLY (B1).** The layout descriptor carries ONE `elementLogicalSize`
			// slot, so `sizeof(K)` in a `uses K, V` generic — which would need a per-parameter size — is a
			// clean E2015 here, not a lowering panic. A multi-parameter descriptor is a later slice.
			if self.enclosingTypeParams.count() > 1 'multiParamGeneric'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("sizeof of a type parameter of a MULTI-parameter generic type (its layout descriptor carries a single element size, so per-parameter sizes need a wider descriptor — a later slice); sizeof(T) on a single-type-parameter generic is supported", line: sizeofTok.line, column: sizeofTok.column)
			end 'multiParamGeneric'

			let result = self.mintValueOfType(MaxonType.integer)
			self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.sizeofType(result, targetType: operand), range: SourceRange.ofToken(sizeofTok))
			return result
		end 'runtimeSize'

		return self.emitLiteral(try self.concreteSizeofBytes(operand, sizeofTok: sizeofTok), valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(sizeofTok))
	end 'parseSizeof'

	// The byte size of a CONCRETE `sizeof` operand, computed at parse time. A struct is its full laid-out
	// box (`StructLayout.sizeBytes`); a generic INSTANCE is the base struct's box; every non-aggregate type
	// is its `primitiveTypeByteSize`. `typeParameter` never reaches here (its caller routes it to the
	// descriptor path); `unresolved` is a TypeResolution bug and `void` is not a value type.
	function concreteSizeofBytes(t MaxonType, sizeofTok Token) returns ParsedInt throws ParseError
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'struct'
			return self.namedStructSizeBytes(typeNameIdOf(t))
		end 'struct'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			// ⚠ **AN INSTANCE IS INTERNED FOR WHATEVER A `with` NAMED, SO THE BASE MAY BE NOTHING AT ALL**, and
			// this fold runs at PARSE time while the E2055 `checkGenericInstance` recorded against that `with` is
			// drained whole-program — so the user would never see it. `sizeof(IntMap)` for `typealias IntMap =
			// Map with (Int, Int)` therefore died inside `genericInstanceBoxSize` with a stack trace and no
			// `error E….` at all. Positioned here instead, carrying the cause itself, exactly as the field-access
			// door does (`Parser.missingBaseLayout`). A BUILTIN base is NOT refused: its box is the fixed record
			// and `sizeof(Array with Int)` is 48 — which is why the gate is `genericInstanceBaseIsKnown` and not
			// the field walks' `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`.
			if not self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseIsKnown(typeNameIdOf(t)) 'unknownBase'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("sizeof of a type that instantiates '{String.from(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(typeNameIdOf(t)))}', which no file declares as a generic `type`, so it has no box to size — the `with` that names it is the error", line: sizeofTok.line, column: sizeofTok.column)
			end 'unknownBase'

			// The instance's box size is THE one authority on ProgramSignatures — the same
			// `genericInstanceBoxSize` the descriptor's `size@0` slot is emitted through — so a concrete
			// `sizeof(Box with Integer)` folded here and a `sizeof(T)` descriptor read for the same instance
			// cannot drift apart. The gid is whole-program (resolved in the real parse), so it needs no
			// file-local interner, unlike the `structRef` arm above.
			return self.signatures.genericInstanceBoxSize(typeNameIdOf(t)) as ParsedInt
		end 'instance'
		return primitiveTypeByteSize(tag) as ParsedInt
	end 'concreteSizeofBytes'

	// The full box byteSize of a declared struct, by its interned name id (a `structRef` operand's id is
	// against THIS file's interner, so it resolves through the file table, not the merged one).
	function namedStructSizeBytes(id TypeNameId) returns ParsedInt
		let name = try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("namedStructSizeBytes: a `structRef` id is not in this file's interner — `resolveNamedStruct` interned it from a name in the same table")
		match self.signatures.structOf(name) 'lookup'
			declared(layout) then return layout.sizeBytes() as ParsedInt
			undeclared then panic("namedStructSizeBytes: `structRef` names '{String.from(name)}', which `resolveNamedStruct` only tags for a declared struct")
		end 'lookup'
	end 'namedStructSizeBytes'

	// ⭐⭐ **`countof(Type)`** (W190) — how many ELEMENTS a fixed-size container type holds, `sizeof`'s twin
	// one axis over: that one answers in BYTES per value, this one in ELEMENTS per instance. Two outcomes,
	// decided by whether the operand STATES a count:
	//
	//   • An instance that states one (`Vector with 3 Int`, and every alias for it) is a COMPILE-TIME
	//     CONSTANT folded to a `literal` right here, which is where the instance registry lives. It cannot
	//     ride the op for `parseSizeof`'s reason one door over.
	//   • The sized container's OWN `Self` states none — `ProgramSignatures.ownRecordSelfInstance` interns at
	//     `NoFixedSize`, deliberately, so ONE shared body serves every size (`substitutedFixedSize`) — so
	//     inside `type Vector uses Element` there is no compile-time answer, and the expression IS the hidden
	//     FIXED-ELEMENT-COUNT parameter every call site fills in — so it emits NO OP AT ALL, the way a `let`
	//     bound to a literal emits none: the value is already defined, by the entry block's `param`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE COUNT TRAVELS IN ITS OWN HIDDEN PARAMETER AND NOT AS A TENTH LAYOUT-DESCRIPTOR WORD, AND THAT
	// IS A MEASUREMENT.** See `ProgramSignatures.fixedElementCountNeeds`: a descriptor word describes the type
	// ARGUMENT, is FORWARDED wholesale to a callee whose instance is written over the caller's parameters, and
	// therefore cannot carry a fact about the INSTANCE — a `Vector with 4 Element` reached from inside
	// `type Holder uses Element` read Holder's count, 0, instead of 4.
	//
	// ⭐ **ITS GATE IS THE COUNT SLOT, NOT `sizeof`'s `__self`-IN-SCOPE, AND THAT DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT.**
	// `parseSizeof` refuses a receiverless body because B1 threads the descriptor from the instance a method is
	// called on; a STATIC has a second source — its own RESULT, when it returns `Self`
	// (`staticLayoutNeedsSelfReturn` is what makes that true) — and `Vector.create()` is exactly that shape and
	// is the whole reason this expression exists. So the question asked here is the one that actually decides
	// whether the read can be served: does THIS function carry the count parameter? A body the pre-scan did not
	// see gets a positioned refusal rather than the compiler abort `lowerSizeofType`'s divergence produces.
	function parseCountof() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let countofTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.countof)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let operand = self.resolveNamedStruct(try self.parseTypeReference())
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// ⚠ **A COUNT IS A COORDINATE OF THE INSTANCE, SO ONLY AN INSTANCE CAN HAVE ONE.** A primitive, a
		// declared struct, a type PARAMETER and an unsized container all answer nothing here — and answering 0
		// would be a sentinel the caller has no way to tell from a real count.
		//
		// ⛔ Asked through `isVectorInstanceAt`, the (tag, id) PAIR door, and not through `isVectorInstance`
		// plus a hand-written tag test: a `TypeNameId` and a `GenericInstanceId` share one numeric space, so
		// the id half alone can answer TRUE about an instance this operand is not. That door exists for
		// exactly this shape and stating the pairing here again would be the same rule written twice.
		if not self.signatures.isVectorInstanceAt(maxonTypeTag(operand), nameId: typeNameIdOf(operand)) 'noElementCount'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("countof of a type that states no element count — only a fixed-size container type has one (`Vector with 3 Int`, or `Self` inside the sized container's own body). A growable `Array`'s length is a runtime field of the record, not part of its type, so ask it for its `count()`", line: countofTok.line, column: countofTok.column)
		end 'noElementCount'

		return try self.fixedElementCountValue(typeNameIdOf(operand), anchor: countofTok)
	end 'parseCountof'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE COUNT OF ONE SIZED INSTANCE, AT THE PARSE — a literal when the instance STATES one, this
	// function's own hidden slot when it does not.** Two askers and one answer: the `countof(T)` expression a
	// program writes, and the `Self{}` construction `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `create()` is (W190). They must
	// agree exactly — a record built with one number and read back through another is a `count()` that
	// disagrees with `get()`, which is the silent wrong answer `requireVectorElementType`'s header measures
	// from the other direction.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A LIFTED CLOSURE IS A DIFFERENT FUNCTION, AND `functionCarriesFixedElementCountParam` DESCRIBES
	// THE ENCLOSING ONE.** A closure body is parsed in place but emitted as its own top-level function with
	// its own parameter space, so the slot the enclosing method reserved is not a value the closure has —
	// returning its ValueId there names a value the lifted function never defines. **MEASURED:
	// `panic at Parser.tagOf: value v1 has no recorded type`**, on a `function() gives countof(Self)`
	// inside a `Vector` method. `parseSizeof` reaches the same conclusion through its own gate (a closure
	// is receiverless, so `__self` is not in scope); a count has no `__self` to test, so the closure is
	// named directly. Reading the count OUTSIDE the closure and capturing the integer is the cure, and it
	// is the one the message gives.
	function fixedElementCountValue(giid GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let stated = self.signatures.genericInstanceFixedSize(giid)
		if stated != NoFixedSize 'aStatedCount'
			return self.emitLiteral(stated, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
		end 'aStatedCount'

		if not self.closureCaptureScopes.isEmpty() 'insideALiftedClosure'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("countof of the sized container's own `Self` inside a closure — a closure is lifted to its own function, which carries none of the enclosing method's hidden parameters, so the count is not in scope there. Read `countof(Self)` into a binding outside the closure and capture that", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'insideALiftedClosure'

		if not self.functionCarriesFixedElementCountParam 'noCountSlotToReadItFrom'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("countof of the sized container's own `Self`, in a function that carries no fixed-element-count parameter — the count of a countless `Self` is the RECEIVER's, and it reaches a shared body through a hidden argument every call site fills in from the instance it holds. The pre-scan that reserves that slot reads raw tokens, so spell the operand `Self` (or the container's own name) directly rather than through an alias", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'noCountSlotToReadItFrom'

		return self.fixedElementCountParamValueId
	end 'fixedElementCountValue'

	// `( <expr> )` — grouping, which is what makes `(2 + 3) * 4` mean 20 rather than 14.
	// The parentheses produce NO op: the group's value IS the inner expression's, so the
	// only thing they change is the shape of the tree the climber builds.
	//
	// It re-enters the climber at the LOOSEST level (via `parseValueExpression`, so `(a if c else b)`
	// groups a ternary), restoring every operator — `not (a or b)` is the `or` of two bools, `(x > 0)
	// and (y > 0)` groups explicitly. It does NOT go through `parseExpression`, which would re-seed
	// `currentBlock` and undo any short-circuit that moved it.
	//
	// ⭐ **A COMMA AFTER THE FIRST INNER EXPRESSION IS WHAT MAKES IT A TUPLE INSTEAD (P1.10)**, and that is the
	// whole disambiguator — both reference compilers use exactly this rule (`2-Parser.cs:17119-17153`;
	// v1 `Parser.maxon:17083-17089`). It is decided AFTER the first element is parsed rather than by a
	// lookahead, because no bounded lookahead can find the comma: `(f(a, b), c)` has one at depth 1 first.
	function parseParenthesizedExpression(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let openTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		// Captured before the element is parsed so `rhsWasBareLocalReference` can decide, afterwards, whether
		// it was a bare local — which is what a MANAGED element's move-in poisons. The struct literal's
		// discipline (`parseStructLiteralFields`), for the identical reason.
		let firstStart = self.pos
		let value = try self.parseValueExpression(func)
		if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'grouping'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
			return value
		end 'grouping'

		return try self.parseTupleLiteral(func, first: value, firstStart: firstStart, openTok: openTok)
	end 'parseParenthesizedExpression'

	// ⭐ **`(e0, e1, …)` — A TUPLE LITERAL, WHICH IS A STRUCT LITERAL OF A SYNTHESIZED TYPE.** The cursor sits
	// on the comma after the first element, which the caller has already parsed.
	//
	// It ends at `emitStructLiteral` — the very routine `Self{…}` uses — so the box, its `__mm_alloc`, the
	// per-element `storeIndirect` at `offsetOfField(i)` and the STORE of a managed element (`moveInPayload`,
	// which promotes a borrowed String literal, co-owns a value a live binding keeps and adopts an owned
	// temporary) are all the machinery that already exists. A tuple gets nothing of its own, which is the point of `TupleTypeNamePrefix`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT TYPES ARE THE VALUES' OWN, so there is no declared type to coerce against** — the one
	// step a struct literal takes that this cannot (`requireFieldType`). A tuple declares no field types; it
	// ADOPTS them, which is what makes `(40, 2.5)` a `(int, float)` and not an ill-typed `(int, int)`.
	function parseTupleLiteral(func IrFunction, first ValueId, firstStart TokenIndex, openTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		var elementValues = ValueIdArray.create()
		var elementBareLocal = BoolArray.create()
		var elementTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		// ⭐ The values already stored into an owning slot of this literal — `parseStructLiteralFields`'
		// `movedOwningValues`, for its reason and with its gate: a slot that takes its OWN reference may be
		// repeated freely (`(s, s)` over a String now co-owns twice and is released twice), and only a slot
		// that INHERITS this frame's single reference makes a second occurrence E3102
		// (`repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree`).
		var movedOwningValues = ValueIdArray.create()

		try self.collectTupleElement(elementValues, elementBareLocal: elementBareLocal, elementTypes: elementTypes, movedOwningValues: movedOwningValues, value: first, valueStart: firstStart)

		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'eachElement'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			let valueStart = self.pos
			let value = try self.parseValueExpression(func)
			try self.collectTupleElement(elementValues, elementBareLocal: elementBareLocal, elementTypes: elementTypes, movedOwningValues: movedOwningValues, value: value, valueStart: valueStart)
		end 'eachElement'

		let closeTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		let layout = try self.internTupleLayout(elementTypes, anchor: openTok)

		return try self.emitStructLiteral(layout, fieldValues: elementValues, fieldBareLocal: elementBareLocal, anchor: openTok, range: SourceRange.between(openTok, last: closeTok))
	end 'parseTupleLiteral'

	// Append one parsed element to the three lockstep columns a tuple literal builds, having first refused a
	// repeated owning move. One writer, so a caller that pushed a value and forgot its type would be
	// unrepresentable — `StructLayout.addField`'s discipline applied to the literal side — and one gate, so
	// the first element (parsed by the caller before it knew this was a tuple) is checked exactly as the rest.
	//
	// The element's own type is what is passed as `fieldType`: a tuple ADOPTS its element types, so the slot
	// a value moves into is owning iff the value itself owns heap.
	function collectTupleElement(elementValues ValueIdArray, elementBareLocal BoolArray, elementTypes MaxonTypeArray, movedOwningValues ValueIdArray, value ValueId, valueStart TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		try self.rejectDoubleOwningStore(movedOwningValues, value: value, valueStart: valueStart)
		elementValues.push(value)
		elementBareLocal.push(self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart))
		elementTypes.push(self.typeOf(value))
	end 'collectTupleElement'

	// ⭐ **`(T0, T1, …)` IN A TYPE POSITION (P1.10)** — a parameter, a return type, a `typealias` RHS. It
	// resolves to the SAME `structRef` a literal of those element types mints, which is what makes
	// `sum(t (Integer, Integer))` accept the `(10, 32)` a caller built.
	// One element of a tuple TYPE, refused through the family door if it is an existential — the eighth
	// interface-typed position, and the one that reached the backend. `parseTupleTypeReference` read its
	// elements straight from `parseTypeReference` and asked nothing, so an `interfaceRef` travelled into
	// `internTupleType` → `mangleTypeArg` and PANICKED in `LayoutDescriptor.primitiveTypeTagName` — on an
	// assertion that a container of existentials is refused at the front end, which was true of every OTHER
	// container and false of this one. MEASURED: the merge base answered `E3011 Unknown type` (existentials
	// did not exist), and all three spellings — a `typealias`, a parameter, a return — panicked here.
	//
	// Both element reads come through this one function so the first element and the rest cannot come to be
	// checked differently, which is the shape the loop above already had for `parseTypeReference`.
	function readTupleElementType() returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		let anchor = self.tokenAt(self.pos)
		let element = try self.parseTypeReference()
		try self.requireStorableDeclaredType(element, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.tupleElement, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		return element
	end 'readTupleElementType'

	function parseTupleTypeReference() returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		let openTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		// ⚠ `()` is not the empty tuple — shv2 has no unit type, and `void` is not a `MaxonType`
		// (`MaxonReturnType.void` carries it). Reported before the element loop so the message names the
		// construct rather than a missing type.
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'emptyTuple'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("an empty tuple type '()' (a tuple has at least {MinTupleArity} elements; a function that returns nothing simply omits its `returns` clause)", line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column)
		end 'emptyTuple'

		var elements = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		elements.push(try self.readTupleElementType())
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'eachElement'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			elements.push(try self.readTupleElementType())
		end 'eachElement'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// ⚠ **A ONE-ELEMENT TUPLE TYPE IS REFUSED, AND `(a)` STAYS GROUPING FOREVER** — see `MinTupleArity`.
		// The EXPRESSION side needs no such check: a literal is only a tuple once a comma has been seen.
		if elements.count() < MinTupleArity 'singleElement'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a one-element tuple type (a tuple has at least {MinTupleArity} elements, and parentheses around a single type would be indistinguishable from grouping)", line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column)
		end 'singleElement'

		return MaxonType.structRef(self.artifact.typeNames.intern((try self.internTupleLayout(elements, anchor: openTok)).name))
	end 'parseTupleTypeReference'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE DOOR A TUPLE TYPE IS MINTED THROUGH** — a literal, a type annotation, a `typealias` RHS.
	// It interns the type whole-program (`ProgramSignatures.internTupleType`, which canonicalizes and keys the
	// layout) and files THIS FILE's own copy of the layout as an artifact contribution, so `mergeArtifact`
	// commits it to `project.structTypes` and `installStructDestructors` synthesizes the drop cascade for a
	// tuple that owns a String. Both registrations happen here, together, because a layout in one and not the
	// other is what makes the destructor installer ask a question the index cannot answer.
	function internTupleLayout(elements MaxonTypeArray, anchor Token) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		let name = try self.signatures.internTupleType(elements, from: self.artifact.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: self.filePath) otherwise (rejection) 'rejected'
			throw self.tupleElementError(rejection, anchor: anchor)
		end 'rejected'

		if let known = try self.tupleLayouts.get(name) 'alreadyAdopted'
			return known
		end 'alreadyAdopted'

		// Copy-on-write off the shared empty, keyed on its `is`-identity: a file that mints no tuple must
		// never allocate a table (`sharedEmptyTupleLayouts`), and the shared one must never be written.
		if self.tupleLayouts is sharedEmptyTupleLayouts 'firstTupleInThisFile'
			self.tupleLayouts = StructLayoutMap.create()
		end 'firstTupleInThisFile'

		let layout = self.signatures.adoptTupleLayout(name, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
		self.tupleLayouts.upsert(name, value: layout)
		self.artifact.recordStructType(layout)
		return layout
	end 'internTupleLayout'

	// Turn a whole-program tuple-element rejection into a POSITIONED diagnostic. The index raises it (only it
	// knows what a canonical element is); the parser is the only party holding a token to anchor it on, which
	// is why the two halves are split at all.
	function tupleElementError(rejection TupleElementRejection, anchor Token) returns ParseError
		return match rejection 'why'
			unsupportedTag(position, tagName) gives ParseError.unsupported("a tuple whose element {position} is '{tagName}': a tuple's identity IS its element types, and neither a generic type parameter (whose concrete type the shared body does not know) nor an unresolved type can key one", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			separatorInElementName(position, elementName) gives ParseError.unsupported("a tuple whose element {position} is named '{elementName}': a tuple type's name joins its elements with '{TupleElementSeparator}', so an element whose own name holds that character would make two different tuples spell one name", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'why'
	end 'tupleElementError'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY CHARACTER LITERAL IS A `Character`, AND AN INTEGER-EXPECTING POSITION COERCES IT TO ITS
	// CODEPOINT AT COMPILE TIME** (A5m-ab, user ruling 2026-08-05). `'a'`, `'\n'`, `'0'`, `'é'`, `'🎉'` and a
	// whole EGC all materialize the same way: an immortal `.rdata` byte record, typed `character`.
	//
	// It is the model BOTH reference compilers use (`BuiltinCharLiteral`), and it is what
	// `specs/char-literal-to-int.md`'s own Documentation describes — *"the compiler automatically converts
	// the character literal to its Unicode codepoint value at compile time"*, which is a statement about a
	// CONVERSION and therefore about a literal that was something else first.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT REPLACES A WIDTH RULE — one byte was an `int`, wider was a `Character` — AND THE WIDTH RULE'S
	// COST WAS A TYPE HOLE, not a divergence in wording.** `'A'.asciiValue()` was *"'int' has no method named
	// 'asciiValue'"* where the oracle prints 65; `let c = 'G'` bound an `int` so a Character `match` arm could
	// not see it; `for c in 'a' to 'e'` printed `97 98 99 100 101`; and `Self{ch: 'e'}` / `f(target: 'a')`
	// were refused at a `Character` field and a `Character` parameter. All five are one fact — the literal was
	// typed by its bytes rather than by what it IS — and all five close here.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INTEGER DIRECTION IS `integerizedOperand`, AND IT REWRITES RATHER THAN ORPHANS.** See there: a
	// discarded `stringLiteral` op still registers `.rdata`, so `cp == '-'` must never MATERIALIZE a record it
	// does not use. `cp == '-'`, `cp - '0'` and `if c >= '0'` therefore emit exactly the op they always did.
	function parseCharLiteral() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.charLiteral)
		return self.emitCharacterLiteral(try self.charLiteralBytes(tok), range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok))
	end 'parseCharLiteral'

	// A `charLiteral` token's payload decoded to the UTF-8 bytes of the one character it holds, or the
	// positioned diagnostic the literal earned. THE one door onto `decodeCharLiteral` for every site that
	// wants BYTES — `parseCharLiteral`, `charLiteralCodepoint` and the top-level constant evaluator — so a
	// literal one of them accepts and another rejects cannot differ (`decodedStringLiteralBytes` is the String
	// twin, and exists for the same reason).
	//
	// The `byte`/`multiByte` split survives only INSIDE here, where it is what it always was: a fact about
	// how many bytes the character occupies, and no longer a fact about its type.
	function charLiteralBytes(tok Token) returns ByteArray throws ParseError
		match decodeCharLiteral(tok.value) 'decoded'
			byte(value) then return byteSlice(value as Byte)
			multiByte(bytes) then return bytes
			empty then throw ParseError.invalidCharacterLiteral("a character literal must contain exactly one character", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			badEscape(text) then throw ParseError.invalidCharacterLiteral("unknown escape sequence '{text}'", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			badDigitEscape(text, offset, marker) then throw invalidDigitEscapeError(text, kind: LiteralEscapeKind.characterLiteral, offset: offset, marker: marker, tok: tok)
		end 'decoded'
	end 'charLiteralBytes'

	// ⭐⭐ **A CHARACTER LITERAL IN AN INTEGER-EXPECTING POSITION IS ITS CODEPOINT** — the whole of the
	// ruling's second half, at the one kind of site that can unambiguously ask for it (A5m-ab).
	//
	// Every character literal materializes as a `Character` (`parseCharLiteral`). What `cp == '-'`,
	// `cp - '0'` and `if c >= '0'` need is the other direction, and it is the direction
	// `specs/char-literal-to-int.md` names outright: *"the compiler automatically converts the character
	// literal to its Unicode codepoint value at compile time"*. Both reference compilers coerce here and
	// nowhere else — MEASURED on the oracle: `cp == 'é'` compares against **233**, `'A' + 1` is **66**,
	// while `f(x Small)` called as `f('A')` is refused (*"expected 'Small', got 'Character'"*). So this is a
	// rule about OPERANDS and DECLARED SLOTS, never a general int↔Character conversion.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT KEYS ON THE OPERAND BEING A LITERAL, NEVER ON THE TWO TAGS.** `c == 45` where `c` is a
	// Character binding stays a type error, so nothing here makes two different tags comparable, and
	// `character-ownership.md`'s `string-and-character-do-not-compare` still pins its half.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `.rdata` RECORD IS REWRITTEN, NOT ORPHANED, AND THAT IS NOT A TIDINESS CHOICE.**
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerStringLiteral` walks `block.opRefs` and registers a blob + a 56-byte record +
	// a fixup for EVERY `stringLiteral` op it finds, read or not; `foldConstOperands`'s const DCE
	// deliberately excludes `rdataAddr`, and no general DCE pass is scheduled. One orphan in a whitelisted
	// stdlib module would therefore renumber every later `__str_blob_`/`__fconst_`/`__jumptable_` label in
	// EVERY program off the single `GlobalDataTable.nextStringId` counter — the measured 317-golden move
	// that listing `Builtins.maxon` cost. Rewriting the op in place leaves the op stream, the op INDEX, the
	// ValueId and the source range exactly as an integer literal's always were, so `cp == '-'` emits the
	// byte-identical code it emitted under the old width rule.
	function integerizedOperand(value ValueId, expected ValueTypeTag, start TokenIndex, pastEnd TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if not tagIsIntegral(expected) or self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.character 'notAnIntegerPosition'
			return value
		end 'notAnIntegerPosition'

		// The operand must be the character literal ITSELF and nothing else: exactly one token, and that
		// token a `charLiteral`. A compound expression that merely CONTAINS one spans more than one token
		// and is left alone, which is what keeps this a rule about literals — and, with the tag test above,
		// what stops a Character BINDING (`let c = 'a'` then `c == 45`) from being silently re-typed.
		if pastEnd != start + 1 or self.kindAt(start) != TokenKind.charLiteral 'notALoneLiteral'
			return value
		end 'notALoneLiteral'

		let tok = self.tokenAt(start)
		match try self.charLiteralCodepoint(tok) 'converted'
			codepoint(cp) then return self.retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint(value, codepoint: cp, tok: tok)
			// A cluster the coercion cannot express stays a `Character`, and the operator raises its own
			// type error over it — see `CharLiteralCodepoint`.
			cluster then return value
		end 'converted'
	end 'integerizedOperand'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CODEPOINT A CHARACTER LITERAL DENOTES — THE one door, because two readers ask.**
	// `integerizedOperand` asks it for an operand or a declared slot; `decodeCharPatternValue` asks it for a
	// `match` INTERVAL bound, which emits no op and so cannot go through the operand path at all. Written
	// twice, one of them would have coerced `'é'` and the other refused it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A MULTI-CODEPOINT CLUSTER HAS NONE.** `'é'` and `'中'` are one codepoint each and convert (MEASURED
	// on the oracle: `cp == 'é'` compares against **233**); a ZWJ family emoji or a regional-indicator flag is
	// a SEQUENCE of codepoints, and answering with the FIRST would be a wrong number wearing a conversion's
	// name. Each caller decides what to do with that answer — stay a `Character`, or raise E2028.
	function charLiteralCodepoint(tok Token) returns CharLiteralCodepoint throws ParseError
		let bytes = try self.charLiteralBytes(tok)
		let decoded = decodeUtf8Sequence(bytes, start: 0)
		if decoded.byteLength != bytes.count() 'notOneCodepoint'
			return CharLiteralCodepoint.cluster
		end 'notOneCodepoint'

		return CharLiteralCodepoint.codepoint(decoded.codepoint)
	end 'charLiteralCodepoint'

	// ⭐⭐ **REPLACE A CHARACTER LITERAL'S `stringLiteral` OP WITH THE INTEGER `literal` OP IT WOULD HAVE
	// BEEN, IN PLACE** — the write `integerizedOperand`'s header argues for. Everything an emitted integer
	// literal carries is restored on the SAME ValueId: the op, the two type columns, and the parser's
	// constant view (without which `53 - '0'` would stop folding and every fold-sensitive golden would move).
	//
	// The op INDEX comes from `charLiteralOps`, filed by `emitCharacterLiteral` — an O(1) read where
	// `ValueOriginTable.lookup` is a linear scan its own header calls THE COLD PATH.
	function retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint(value ValueId, codepoint ParsedInt, tok Token) returns ValueId
		let opIndex = try self.charLiteralOps.get(value) otherwise panic("retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint: v{value} is tagged `character` at a lone charLiteral token '{String.from(tok.value)}' but `emitCharacterLiteral` filed no op for it — every character-tagged literal is minted there")
		try self.artifact.module.ops.set(opIndex, value: MaxonOp.literal(value, value: codepoint, valueType: MaxonType.integer)) otherwise panic("retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint: ops.set({opIndex}) OOB — the index was filed by `emitCharacterLiteral` as it appended that very op")
		self.retypeValue(value, valueType: MaxonType.integer)
		self.recordConst(value, folded: codepoint)

		return value
	end 'retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint'

	// ⭐ Append the `stringLiteral` op a character literal materializes as, and mint a `character`-tagged
	// value for it. It is the SAME op a String literal emits — one immortal `.rdata` byte record,
	// `capacity@16 = ImmortalRecordCapacity`, never allocated or dropped — because a Character IS that record
	// (see `GraphemeRuntime`'s header). Only the value's TAG differs, and the tag is what every rule reads,
	// so `'é' == "é"` is a type error and `print(c)` is refused while `"{c}"` works.
	function emitCharacterLiteral(bytes ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.character, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		// Filed BEFORE the emit, off the count that IS the index the append will land at — the same
		// `module.ops.count()` floor `parseTry` reads for the same reason.
		self.recordCharLiteralOp(result, opIndex: self.artifact.module.opCount())
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.stringLiteral(result, bytes: bytes), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitCharacterLiteral'

	// File the op a byte-view value was produced by, so the stream-write intrinsic can rewrite it in place.
	// `recordCharLiteralOp`'s twin — same COW-off-the-shared-empty discipline, same reason.
	function recordStringBytesViewOp(value ValueId, opIndex OpIndex)
		if self.stringBytesViewOps is sharedEmptyStringBytesViewOps 'stillShared'
			self.stringBytesViewOps = StringBytesViewOpMap.create()
		end 'stillShared'
		self.stringBytesViewOps.upsert(value, value: opIndex)
	end 'recordStringBytesViewOp'

	function recordCharLiteralOp(value ValueId, opIndex OpIndex)
		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty map, exactly as `recordValueInstanceAlias` does and for its
		// reason: a function that writes no character literal never allocates one.
		if self.charLiteralOps is sharedEmptyCharLiteralOps 'stillShared'
			self.charLiteralOps = CharLiteralOpMap.create()
		end 'stillShared'
		self.charLiteralOps.upsert(value, value: opIndex)
	end 'recordCharLiteralOp'

	// Decode a character literal to the CODEPOINT its pattern bound denotes, or the same positioned
	// diagnostic `parseCharLiteral` gives for an empty/bad-escape literal. Its one caller is the match
	// INTERVAL plan, which needs the VALUE (a pattern bound) rather than an emitted `literal` op — which is
	// why it reaches `charLiteralCodepoint` directly instead of through `integerizedOperand`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INTERVAL PLAN IS ONLY EVER BUILT FOR AN INTEGER-LIKE SCRUTINEE** (`matchUsesIntervalPlan`), so
	// `scrutTag` here is an `int`/`bool`/`ExitCode`/ranged-alias and never a `character`. A `match` over a
	// CHARACTER scrutinee keeps the linear compare chain, whose bounds come from `emitPatternValue` and whose
	// compares are `emitCompare`'s — which is what makes a Character range arm work at all (A5m-c).
	function decodeCharPatternValue(tok Token, scrutTag ValueTypeTag) returns ParsedInt throws ParseError
		match try self.charLiteralCodepoint(tok) 'converted'
			codepoint(value) then return value
			// ⚠ **A MULTI-CODEPOINT CLUSTER HERE IS A `Character` PATTERN AGAINST A NON-CHARACTER SCRUTINEE,
			// AND THAT IS AN ORDINARY TYPE MISMATCH.** It used to be an `unsupported` naming a missing feature —
			// *"comparing Characters in a `match` needs pattern dispatch over a runtime equality"* — which was
			// true when a Character `match` did not exist and became a wrong noun the moment it did: the
			// capability is built, and what is wrong with a cluster pattern over an `int` scrutinee is that an
			// `int` and a `Character` do not compare, exactly as a `String` pattern against an `int` does not
			// (`decodeScalarPatternBound`'s own string arm, one screen up, says it in the same words).
			cluster then throw ParseError.matchPatternTypeMismatch(typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.character), scrutineeType: typeTagName(scrutTag), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'converted'
	end 'decodeCharPatternValue'

	// `"hello"`, `""`, `"Use \{expr\} for interpolation"` — a `String` LITERAL in operand position (P1.2
	// wave A). It materializes as a `stringLiteral` op carrying the DECODED bytes, typed `string`;
	// lowering turns it into an immortal `.rdata` record + a `lea` of its address (see
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerStringLiteral`). No heap, no ownership — a literal is never allocated or
	// dropped.
	//
	// ⚠ AN INTERPOLATED STRING IS DEFERRED. The lexer splits `"a{x}b"` into `stringLiteral("a")`,
	// `stringInterpStart`, the `x` tokens, `stringInterpEnd`, `stringLiteral("b")` — so a `stringLiteral`
	// token FOLLOWED BY `stringInterpStart` is an interpolation, which builds an owned HEAP string and is
	// Wave B. `escaped-braces` (`\{`/`\}`) is NOT interpolation: the lexer's escape handling means the
	// braces never open one, so it is a single `stringLiteral` fragment and `decodeEscapes` turns `\{`
	// into `{` here.
	function parseStringLiteral(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// A `stringLiteral` FOLLOWED BY `stringInterpStart` is an interpolation (`"a{x}b"`), which builds
		// the first OWNED heap String (P1.2 wave B). A lone `stringLiteral` is an immortal rdata literal.
		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.stringInterpStart 'interpolated'
			return try self.parseStringInterpolation(func, prefix: EmptySourceText, suffix: EmptySourceText)
		end 'interpolated'

		return try self.consumeStringLiteralValue()
	end 'parseStringLiteral'

	// Consume the current NON-interpolated `stringLiteral` token and emit its decoded bytes as an immortal
	// rdata String. The shared body of the two callers that reach a plain literal — value position
	// (`parseStringLiteral`) and match-pattern position (`emitPatternValue`) — so the decode-and-emit lives
	// in one place.
	function consumeStringLiteralValue() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.stringLiteral)
		return self.emitStringLiteral(try self.decodedStringLiteralBytes(tok), range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok))
	end 'consumeStringLiteralValue'

	// A `stringLiteral` token's payload with its escapes resolved, or the positioned diagnostic the escape
	// earned. THE one door onto `decodeEscapes` for string literals, because there are two callers with
	// nothing else in common: the value path above, which emits a record, and `panic("…")`'s message, which
	// bakes bytes and emits no value at all. Written twice, the panic path would have been free to accept an
	// escape the value path rejects.
	function decodedStringLiteralBytes(tok Token) returns ByteArray throws ParseError
		match decodeEscapes(tok.value) 'decoded'
			bytes(buf) then return buf
			unknownEscape(text) then throw ParseError.unsupported("the escape sequence '{text}' in a string literal is not a recognized escape", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			badDigitEscape(text, offset, marker) then throw invalidDigitEscapeError(text, kind: LiteralEscapeKind.stringInterpolation, offset: offset, marker: marker, tok: tok)
		end 'decoded'
	end 'decodedStringLiteralBytes'

	// Append a `stringLiteral` op carrying the DECODED bytes; return the `string`-typed ValueId it
	// defines. The one emit site, so a caller that already holds decoded bytes (none today, but the
	// interpolation path at Wave B will hand a materialized buffer here) reaches the type + op in one
	// place.
	function emitStringLiteral(bytes ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.string, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.stringLiteral(result, bytes: bytes), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitStringLiteral'

	// ⭐ **`b"hello"`, `b""`, `b"\xc0"`, `b"À"` — a BYTE-STRING LITERAL in operand position (P1.7 slice 2).**
	// It decodes to a raw byte sequence (escapes resolved, one Latin-1 byte per source codepoint) and
	// materializes as an OWNED `Array with Byte` value: a `constArrayLiteral` op that lowers to a heap 48-byte
	// Array record over an IMMORTAL rdata blob (see `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerConstArrayLiteral`). Its value type
	// is pinned to the concrete `Array with Byte` gid so `.count()`/`.get()` dispatch through a real generic
	// instance — a bare `named(Array)` would trip the generics machinery. Enrolled as an owned temp so a
	// scope-exit / statement-end drop (`__managed_decref`) frees the record (the rdata buffer is left alone).
	//
	// A codepoint above 0xFF (`b"Ā"`, a `\uNNNN` naming one, an emoji) has no single-byte encoding and is
	// REJECTED with E1004 — the self-hosted oracle's behaviour, deliberately UNLIKE the C# bootstrap, which
	// silently Latin-1 best-fit-transliterates the codepoint away. Use `\xNN` to embed an exact raw byte.
	function parseByteStringLiteral() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.byteStringLiteral)
		let bytes = try decodeByteStringLiteral(tok.value, tok: tok)
		return try self.emitByteStringLiteralValue(bytes, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok))
	end 'parseByteStringLiteral'

	// Append a `constArrayLiteral` op carrying the DECODED bytes at a one-byte stride; return the owned `Array with Byte`
	// ValueId it defines. `emitStringLiteral`'s twin, and split out for its reason: a second caller
	// already holds decoded bytes — a use of a top-level managed constant (`emitManagedConstant`), whose
	// bytes were decoded once by the declaration sweep. One emit site, so the value's TYPE (the concrete
	// `Array with Byte` gid) and its OWNERSHIP (an owned temp the statement drops) cannot drift between
	// the two spellings of the same literal.
	function emitByteStringLiteralValue(bytes ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement(bytes, line: range.line, column: range.column)

		let giid = self.signatures.internArrayByteInstance(self.filePath)
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, nameId: giid)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.constArrayLiteral(result, elementSize: ByteStringElementSize, elementCount: bytes.count() as ParsedInt, bytes: bytes), range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return result
	end 'emitByteStringLiteralValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERYTHING A `b"…"` BLOB MUST SATISFY TO BE A VALUE OF `Array with Byte`, ASKED IN ONE PLACE.**
	// Two rules, and they are two halves of one question — *"is this run of raw bytes a legal value of the
	// element THIS file's `Byte` denotes?"* — so they are asked together or the literal has been let through
	// on half an answer:
	//
	//   1. **THE STRIDE.** The blob is byte-PACKED by construction, so the record it produces strides 1 while
	//      every other `Array with Byte` record strides whatever `Byte`'s declared range needs. The rule, the
	//      silent wrong answer it costs, and why it is a refusal rather than a widened blob are all on
	//      `byteStringBlobStrideMessage`, beside the words it emits.
	//   2. **THE BOUNDS (A3r).** ⛔ **A ONE-BYTE SLOT IS NOT A BYTE, AND THE STRIDE CANNOT SEE THE
	//      DIFFERENCE.** `rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives EVERY non-negative range that fits `u8.max` a
	//      one-byte slot, so `typealias Byte = int(0 to 100)` passes rule 1 and cannot hold `223`. MEASURED
	//      on `origin/main`: `takes(b"\xdf")` into `takes(b Array with Byte)` returned **223** out of an
	//      element declared `int(0 to 100)` — exit 223, no diagnostic. It is the same reading
	//      `SignatureIndex.isByteElementName`'s header records twice for the boundary door, reached this time
	//      with no compiler-synthesized buffer at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **RULE 2 IS PER-VALUE, NOT PER-TYPE, AND THAT ASYMMETRY WITH RULE 1 IS THE POINT.** A wide `Byte`
	// makes EVERY literal unemittable (the blob's layout is wrong whatever bytes are in it); a narrow one
	// makes only the literals that overflow it wrong — `b"abc"` under `int(0 to 100)` is three bytes that all
	// fit and must keep compiling. So this is the ordinary compile-time narrowing every other literal gets
	// (`literalInRange` against the element's DECLARED bounds, E3005), applied once per byte, and NOT a
	// second `unsupported`: E2015 would say *"shv2 cannot emit this"*, which is true of a wide `Byte` and
	// false here — the emission is fine and the program is wrong.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STRIDE IS ASKED FIRST, AND THE BOUNDS TEST DEPENDS ON IT HAVING PASSED.** Bounds ride as RAW
	// 64-bit patterns (`RangedTypeAlias`), so `int(0 to u64.max)` carries `high == -1` and a negative lower
	// bound is indistinguishable from one above `i64.max`. `rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives every such range
	// the machine word, so a stride of 1 is exactly the proof that `low` and `high` are honest small
	// non-negative numbers — `literalInRange` is handed no ambiguous pattern to mis-order.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE WHOLE RULE HAS A SECOND DOORWAY**, `requireConstByteStringBlobFitsItsElement`, because a
	// top-level `let G = b"…"` / `var G = b"…"` never reaches the expression emitter: the initializer sweep
	// folds it to bytes and `ModuleInit` builds the record, so a STORED byte-string global would otherwise
	// slip the gate (measured for the stride rule: it did, and the narrow rule inherits the same doorway).
	// The two differ ONLY in the error union their tier speaks — `ParseError` here, `ConstEvalError.defective`
	// there — so the RULE is this one function and only the `throw` is written twice. Wiring rule 2 beside
	// rule 1 rather than into this function's callers is what makes "one of the two doors was taught" a shape
	// the code cannot express.
	function requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement(bytes ByteArray, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		// ONE read of the instance, so the stride and the bounds are provably the same element's — the
		// coupling `bytearray-element-size.md` names as the thing that would go quiet if the literal ever
		// stopped resolving through `ProgramSignatures.internArrayByteInstance(readerFilePath)`.
		let giid = self.signatures.internArrayByteInstance(self.filePath)

		// Rule 1 is the shared byte-packed producers' rule (`bytePackedRecordStrideMessage`), asked of the
		// instance already in hand rather than of a second mint of it — which is the coupling above.
		try self.requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte(giid, producer: BytePackedProducer.byteStringLiteral, line: line, column: column)

		match self.signatures.arrayElementAliasOf(giid, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'element'
			// No declared range constrains the element, so no byte can be outside one. It is the program
			// that declares no `Byte` at all, whose only `Array with Byte` record is the literal's own
			// (`SignatureIndex.undeclaredNamedElementSlot`).
			undeclared then return
			declared(alias) then try self.requireEveryByteInRange(bytes, alias: alias, line: line, column: column)
		end 'element'
	end 'requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE RULE BOTH BYTE-PACKED PRODUCERS ASK (A4c)** — the `b"…"` literal above, whose blob is packed
	// by CONSTRUCTION, and `__ManagedMemory.create(count, elementSize: 1)`, whose buffer is packed by its own
	// ARGUMENT. One rule, one sentence (`bytePackedRecordStrideMessage`), because for a row's length the
	// literal was refused for this incoherence and the buffer was accepted with it — and a record striding 1
	// under a type striding N is what makes every question further down, including E3118's slot question,
	// unanswerable.
	//
	// ⚠ It takes the INSTANCE rather than minting one, so the literal's caller can hand over the very giid its
	// bounds clause reads and the two provably speak about one element. `create`'s caller hands over the
	// instance its own `elementSize` picked, which is the same requirement seen from the other side: the
	// refusal must be about the type the result will WEAR, not about a fresh derivation of it.
	function requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte(giid GenericInstanceId, producer BytePackedProducer, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		let declaredStride = self.signatures.arrayElementSize(giid)
		if declaredStride == ByteStringElementSize 'thisFilesByteIsOneByte'
			return
		end 'thisFilesByteIsOneByte'

		throw ParseError.unsupported(bytePackedRecordStrideMessage(producer, declaredStride: declaredStride), line: line, column: column)
	end 'requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte'

	// Rule 2's walk, split out so the rule above reads as its two clauses. EVERY byte is tested and the FIRST
	// offending one is reported: a blob is a run the source never spelled out, so a diagnostic naming one byte
	// of it has to name WHICH (`byteStringByteOutOfRangeMessage`).
	function requireEveryByteInRange(bytes ByteArray, alias RangedTypeAlias, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		let isFloat = aliasIsFloat(alias)
		let len = bytes.count()
		var i = 0 as ByteOffset
		while i < len 'eachByte'
			let value = try bytes.get(i) otherwise panic("requireEveryByteInRange: i < len just held but get({i}) failed")
			if not literalInRange(value, low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat) 'outsideTheElementsRange'
				throw ParseError.byteStringByteOutOfRange(value, offset: i as ParsedInt, aliasName: String.from(alias.name), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat, line: line, column: column)
			end 'outsideTheElementsRange'
			i = i + 1
		end 'eachByte'
	end 'requireEveryByteInRange'

	// The doorway the top-level initializer sweep speaks through — the rule above in `ConstEvalError`'s
	// union, positioned at the `b"…"` token of the declaration rather than at a use.
	function requireConstByteStringBlobFitsItsElement(bytes ByteArray, tok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		try self.requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement(bytes, line: tok.line, column: tok.column) otherwise (err) 'rejected'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'rejected'
	end 'requireConstByteStringBlobFitsItsElement'

	// ⭐⭐ **`"prefix {x} suffix"` — WHERE THE FIRST OWNED HEAP STRING IS BUILT.** An interpolation lowers to
	// ONE `__mm_alloc`'d fused record: the 56-byte String record with its UTF-8 bytes INLINE right behind it
	// (`buffer@0 = record + StringRecordBytes`), the concatenation of every part's bytes. It is an owned
	// TEMPORARY — `emitStringInterp` enrolls it for the statement-scoped drop — so `print("{x}")` frees it
	// at statement end and `let s = "{x}"` transfers it to a scope-exit drop (`parseBinding`).
	//
	// The lexer splits `"a{x}b"` into `stringLiteral("a") stringInterpStart <expr> stringInterpEnd
	// stringLiteral("b")` — a leading fragment, then any number of `(expr, fragment)` pairs. Each part is
	// materialized to a `(buffer, length)` pair as it is parsed (a literal fragment and a `{s}` String part
	// alike load `buffer@0`/`length@8` from a String record; a primitive renders into a freed scratch
	// buffer), and `emitStringInterp` sums the lengths, allocates once, and copies the parts in.
	//
	// Each `{expr}` is parsed in the block control CURRENTLY occupies, not the entry block — an expression
	// that opens blocks (a short-circuit `{a and b}`) leaves control in its merge, and the next part and the
	// assembly must chain off that merge (the `multiple-try-fragments` regression, deferred here for `try`).
	// ⭐ `prefix`/`suffix` BRACKET the interpolation's own parts with literal bytes the SOURCE does not
	// contain. Exactly one caller passes them: an interpolated `panic(…)`, whose runtime line must carry the
	// `panic at <file>:<line>: ` location and the trailing newline — two parse-time facts no later tier
	// holds (`renderedPanicMessagePointer`). An EMPTY one materializes NO part at all, because
	// `materializeFragmentBytes` skips a zero-length run, so every other caller emits byte-identical IR.
	function parseStringInterpolation(func IrFunction, prefix ByteArray, suffix ByteArray) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let startTok = self.current()
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(startTok)

		var partBufs = ValueIdArray.create()
		var partLens = ValueIdArray.create()
		// The OWNED scratch buffers a primitive part rendered into. Freed after the copy in
		// `emitStringInterp` — they are consumed by the blit and otherwise leak (the interp-scratch-buffer
		// leak the leak gate would catch). A literal or String part collects none (its bytes are borrowed).
		var scratchIds = ValueIdArray.create()

		self.materializeFragmentBytes(prefix, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
		try self.materializeLiteralFragment(partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
		while self.at(TokenKind.stringInterpStart) 'parts'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.stringInterpStart)
			let exprTok = self.current()
			let exprVal = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			let format = try self.acceptInterpFormatSpec()
			try self.consume(TokenKind.stringInterpEnd)
			try self.materializeInterpExpr(exprVal, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
			try self.materializeLiteralFragment(partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
		end 'parts'
		self.materializeFragmentBytes(suffix, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)

		return self.emitStringInterp(partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
	end 'parseStringInterpolation'

	// The `:08x` of `"{n:08x}"`, if the author wrote one. The lexer has already decided WHERE the
	// expression stopped and the specifier began (`Lexer.scanInterp`), so this is a token test and not a
	// second reading of the same boundary question.
	function acceptInterpFormatSpec() returns InterpFormatSpec throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.stringFormatSpec) 'noSpecifier'
			return InterpFormatSpec.absent()
		end 'noSpecifier'

		return try InterpFormatSpec.parse(try self.consume(TokenKind.stringFormatSpec))
	end 'acceptInterpFormatSpec'

	// Consume the current literal fragment token and, if it decodes to a non-empty byte run, materialize it
	// as a part. An EMPTY fragment (the `""` the lexer emits around adjacent interpolations, `"{a}{b}"`)
	// contributes nothing and is skipped — the bootstrap's `if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(...)) continue`.
	function materializeLiteralFragment(partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		let tok = try self.consume(TokenKind.stringLiteral)
		match decodeEscapes(tok.value) 'decoded'
			bytes(buf) then self.materializeFragmentBytes(buf, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			unknownEscape(text) then throw ParseError.unsupported("the escape sequence '{text}' in a string literal is not a recognized escape", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			badDigitEscape(text, offset, marker) then throw invalidDigitEscapeError(text, kind: LiteralEscapeKind.stringInterpolation, offset: offset, marker: marker, tok: tok)
		end 'decoded'
	end 'materializeLiteralFragment'

	function materializeFragmentBytes(buf ByteArray, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
		if buf.count() == 0 'empty'
			return
		end 'empty'
		self.materializeStringRecordPart(self.emitStringLiteral(buf, range: range), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
	end 'materializeFragmentBytes'

	// A `{expr}` part. A String value (a literal or an interpolation result) is copied by its bytes exactly
	// as a literal fragment is — `materializeStringRecordPart` reads `buffer@0`/`length@8` from either. An
	// integer renders to decimal text via `__int_to_string` into a scratch buffer, a bool via
	// `__bool_to_string`, a float via `__float_toString` and a user struct via its own `toString`. An enum
	// renders its VALUE — the integer for an int backing, the decoded double for a float one; its case NAME
	// is a later rung (`string-interpolation.md`'s two `disabled-test:` cases).
	function materializeInterpExpr(exprVal ValueId, exprTok Token, format InterpFormatSpec, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, scratchIds ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		// ⚠ **THE RAW TAG IS CORRECT HERE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** `tagIsIntegral`
		// answers `true` for a `named` tag deliberately (its own header records the trade), so a float
		// carrying one would be claimed by the INTEGRAL arm below and rendered through `__int_to_string`
		// — the IEEE bit pattern printed as a decimal integer. No shape reaches that: a ranged FLOAT
		// alias is resolved to a `float` tag before it can arrive here — a call result by
		// `resolveFloatAliasType`, a parameter and a field by the same erasure — and interpolating a
		// `Percent` parameter, a `Percent` field and a `Percent` return all print correctly with the raw
		// tag (`specs-shv2/string-interpolation.md`'s `float-ranged-alias-positions`, which is the pin).
		// Resolving again here would be a second answer to a question already answered upstream.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **AN ENUM IS THE ONE SHAPE THAT DOES ARRIVE `named` CARRYING AN f64, AND THAT REASONING NEVER
		// COVERED IT (A4o).** A float-backed enum's value cannot be lifted at its producer the way an alias
		// is — it must stay a GPR i64 for `match` — so it is DECODED here instead, and the `float` arm below
		// then renders it through the one `__float_toString` every float in the language uses. MEASURED
		// before this line: `enum Weight  light = 2.5` then `"{w}"` printed `4612811918334230528`. An
		// int-backed enum passes through untouched and keeps the integral arm, which is what
		// `constants/string-interpolation` and `string-interpolation/int-enum-interpolation` pin.
		let value = self.floatBehindEnumValue(exprVal, anchor: exprTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN ENUM RENDERS BY ITS BACKING, AND UNTIL W81 EVERY ONE OF THEM FELL THROUGH TO THE INTEGRAL
		// ARM BELOW.** The rule is `/specs/string-interpolation.md:89`: *"For int-backed enums, the numeric
		// value is shown. For simple enums, the case name is shown. For string-backed enums, the raw string
		// value is displayed"*. shv2 rendered the raw i64 for all of them, which is the right answer for
		// exactly one. MEASURED against the bootstrap oracle on one program, before this arm existed:
		//
		//   | declaration                         | oracle   | shv2      |
		//   | `enum Plain  red  green`            | `green`  | `1`       |
		//   | `enum IntB  low = 5  high = 9`      | `9`      | `9`  ✓    |
		//   | `enum StrB  active = "Active"`      | `Active` | `0`       |
		//   | `enum ChrB  comma = ','  dot = '.'` | `.`      | `46`      |
		//   | `union Payloadless  alpha  beta`    | `beta`   | `1`       |
		//   | `union Shape  circle(r Small)  blank` | `circle` | `5111832` |
		//
		// ⛔ **THE LAST ROW IS A HEAP ADDRESS**, and it MOVES between runs — the same defect
		// `emitEnumInstanceAccessor`'s header records for `.ordinal` ("every boxed union's `.ordinal` was a
		// box pointer"), reached again through the interpolation door because this site read `tagOf` where
		// the accessor asks `emitEnumTagOf`. That is why the boxed question is asked FIRST here, exactly as
		// the reference's ladder asks it first (`MaxonToStandardConversion.Strings.cs:873`).
		// ⚠ The `named` pre-check is `valueIsUnion`'s measured allocation guard, for its reason: `enumMatchFor`
		// returns a union VALUE, so asking it of the `int` and `String` holes that are almost every hole in
		// the corpus would box a `DeclaredEnum.undeclared` per hole.
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.named 'possiblyAnEnum'
			let decl = self.enumMatchFor(value)
			if enumMatchIsDeclared(decl) 'anEnumPart'
				try self.materializeEnumPart(requireEnumLayout(decl), value: value, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
				return
			end 'anEnumPart'
		end 'possiblyAnEnum'

		let tag = self.tagOf(value)
		// ⚠ `tagIsByteRecord`, NOT `tagIsText`: this asks whether `buffer@0`/`length@8` can be read off the
		// value and blitted, which is a question about the RECORD. A Character carries the same record, so
		// `"{c}"` is the identical two loads — and interpolation is how a Character reaches stdout at all,
		// `print` itself taking a String and refusing one.
		//
		// ⚠ **A SPECIFIER ON TEXT IS IGNORED, WHICH IS THE ORACLE'S ANSWER AND WAS MEASURED, NOT
		// ASSUMED.** `print("[{s:8}]")` on a `String` prints `[hi]` under the bootstrap, as do `"{b:8}"`
		// on a bool and `"{c:8}"` on a Character — a field width is a NUMERIC field in both references,
		// and no spec in either corpus pins any other reading. Refusing one here would refuse programs
		// the language accepts today; padding one would be this compiler inventing a rule. It is left
		// exactly where the references leave it, named rather than fallen through.
		if tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'byteRecordPart'
			self.materializeStringRecordPart(value, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			return
		end 'byteRecordPart'

		// ⭐⭐ **WHICH DECIMAL RENDERER — DECIDED HERE, ONCE, FROM THE VALUE'S DECLARED TYPE (X11).**
		// `__int_to_string` is SIGNED by construction (see `buildIntegerToString`: it derives a sign and
		// multiplies each digit back by it), so a value of `int(0 to u64.max)` holding `u64.max` printed
		// `-1` while the bootstrap oracle printed `18446744073709551615`. The front end never asked whose
		// question that was, and it is `integerValueDomain`'s — the very walk the DIVIDE's signedness
		// already comes from, so this reuses the rule rather than respelling it. Only a DECLARED
		// non-negative range selects the unsigned entry point; a folded constant that merely FITS one does
		// not (`valueRendersUnsigned`), which is X10's rule verbatim and what keeps the corpus movement to
		// the values that actually carry a declared type.
		if tagIsIntegral(tag) 'intPart'
			// ⭐⭐ **ONLY THE FORMATTED SPELLING MOVES TO stdlib, AND THAT IS THE REFERENCES' TRADE TOO.**
			// `"{n}"` keeps `__int_to_string` into a scratch buffer, whose budget is bounded BY
			// CONSTRUCTION (an i64 has at most 20 digits and a sign) — it is the hot path the fused
			// single-allocation record rests on, and the defect a field width introduces cannot exist
			// there. A FIELD has no such bound (`"{n:200}"`), so it is built over a String that grows.
			//
			// ⚠ The two cannot drift the way a second hand-written converter would: decimal conversion of
			// a magnitude has exactly one answer, and the one thing that DID differ in the references —
			// signedness — is decided HERE, once, off the value's declared type, and handed over as an
			// argument rather than re-derived from bit 63 at the far end.
			// ⚠ **BOTH BRANCHES LIVE IN `materializeIntegerPart`, BECAUSE THE ENUM ARM NEEDS THE SAME
			// ANSWER (W81).** An int-backed enum renders EXACTLY as the integer it is — specifier included —
			// so if the choice between the formatted and the scratch path were spelled here, the enum arm
			// would have to spell it a second time. It did, and the second spelling silently DROPPED the
			// specifier: `"{code:08}"` on an int-backed enum printed `404` where the oracle prints
			// `00000404` (`string-interpolation/int-format-enum-backing`).
			try self.materializeIntegerPart(value, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
			return
		end 'intPart'

		// A specifier on a bool is ignored — see the byte-record arm above for the measurement.
		if tagIsBool(tag) 'boolPart'
			self.materializeScratchPart(value, converter: BoolToStringName, scratchBytes: BoolToStringScratchBytes, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
			return
		end 'boolPart'

		// ⭐⭐ **A FLOAT — `"{x}"` → a DIRECT `__float_toString(x)` CALL, NOT a `materializeScratchPart`
		// CONVERTER.** Its two neighbours above render into a fixed-size scratch buffer because their
		// worst cases are 21 and 5 bytes and their algorithms are a loop of divides. A float's is
		// neither: the shortest decimal that reads back as the same double is found with EXACT
		// arbitrary-precision arithmetic (`stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s float-text band), it allocates as it
		// goes, and its fixed-notation spelling runs to ~325 characters for the least subnormal. A
		// hand-built Std-tier graph would be a second implementation of a routine the COMPILER already
		// needs and has — so the routine lives in `stdlib/` and both callers reach the one copy.
		//
		// The call is the same door `materializeStructToStringPart` takes one arm down: an ordinary
		// `emitCall` whose String result is classified +1 OWNED (`enrolOwnedCallTemp` →
		// `trackOwnedTemp`) and freed at statement end, its bytes spliced by
		// `materializeStringRecordPart` exactly as any other String part's are.
		if tagIsFloat(tag) 'floatPart'
			try self.materializeFloatToStringPart(value, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			return
		end 'floatPart'

		// ⭐ **A USER STRUCT — `"{p}"` → a DIRECT `<Type>.toString()` CALL (P1.7a slice 1).** The concrete
		// type is statically known here, so dispatch is a plain method call, NOT a witness (exactly what the
		// monomorphizing bootstrap emits). Witnesses are needed only for an UNKNOWN concrete type — a
		// constrained type parameter — which is slice 2.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'structPart'
			try self.materializeStructToStringPart(value, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			return
		end 'structPart'

		// ⚠ NAMES WHAT IS UNSUPPORTED, NOT WHAT IS SUPPORTED. A message enumerating the handled types goes
		// stale every time an arm is added above — which is exactly how its predecessor came to promise
		// float and struct interpolation as future work while both arms sat directly above it.
		throw ParseError.unsupported("interpolating a value of type '{typeTagName(tag)}' — no conversion to text exists for it", line: exprTok.line, column: exprTok.column)
	end 'materializeInterpExpr'

	// ⭐⭐ **RENDER AN INTEGER — FORMATTED OR PLAIN — AND IT IS THE ENUM ARM'S `rawNumber` ANSWER TOO.**
	// The integral arm and an int-backed enum are the SAME question: an enum with a raw number renders
	// exactly as that number, specifier and all. Keeping the formatted/plain choice here rather than at
	// the two call sites is not tidiness — when the enum arm spelled it separately it simply had no
	// formatted branch, so `"{code:08}"` printed `404` against the oracle's `00000404`.
	//
	// ⚠ The NAME and the SCRATCH BUDGET are two halves of ONE signedness choice: the signed budget
	// carries a sign byte the unsigned rendering never writes, and the signed renderer taken with the
	// unsigned budget would write one byte past `i64.min`'s scratch. The FORMATTED path cannot select an
	// entry point that way — it needs the signedness as a VALUE to hand on — which is why the two
	// branches ask `valueRendersUnsigned` separately rather than sharing a local.
	function materializeIntegerPart(value ValueId, exprTok Token, format InterpFormatSpec, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, scratchIds ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		if format.present 'formatted'
			try self.materializeFormattedIntPart(value, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, rendersUnsigned: valueRendersUnsigned(self.integerValueDomain(value)), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			return
		end 'formatted'

		let signedness = DivideSignedness.unsigned if valueRendersUnsigned(self.integerValueDomain(value)) else DivideSignedness.signed
		self.materializeScratchPart(value, converter: integerToStringName(signedness), scratchBytes: integerToStringScratchBytes(signedness), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
	end 'materializeIntegerPart'

	// ⭐⭐ **INTERPOLATE AN ENUM / UNION VALUE (W81)** — see `materializeInterpExpr`'s arm for the measured
	// table this dispatch was written from. The rendering is decided by `enumInterpRendering` below and the
	// CONSTANT is selected by the machinery `.name` and `.rawValue` already use
	// (`emitEnumCaseTypedSelect`), so the three spellings of "what does case `i` render as" stay one answer.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TAG COMES FROM `emitEnumTagOf`, WHICH IS THE WHOLE FIX FOR THE BOXED ROW.** A payload-bearing
	// union's VALUE is a heap box and its tag is the i64 at offset 0; reading the value as a number printed
	// the address. That door is the same one `emitEnumInstanceAccessor` asks question 1 through, so the
	// interpolation and the accessor now agree about where a union keeps its tag by construction.
	//
	// A selected case NAME or raw TEXT is an immortal `.rdata` String record, so its bytes are spliced by
	// `materializeStringRecordPart` exactly as a `{s}` part's are and nothing is owned or dropped.
	// ⚠ **THE SPECIFIER REACHES THE `rawNumber` ARM AND NOTHING ELSE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN
	// ASSUMED.** A format specifier is a NUMERIC one, so the two TEXT arms have nothing to do with it.
	// The oracle, on one program carrying all three backings: `"{code:08}"` on an int-backed enum gives
	// `00000404`, while `"{p:6}"` on a plain enum gives `green` and `"{t:6}"` on a string-backed one
	// gives `Active` — both IGNORING the specifier, exactly as the bool and byte-record arms above do.
	function materializeEnumPart(layout EnumLayout, value ValueId, exprTok Token, format InterpFormatSpec, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, scratchIds ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		let tag = self.emitEnumTagOf(value, layout: layout, range: range)

		match enumInterpRendering(layout) 'rendering'
			caseName then self.materializeStringRecordPart(try self.emitEnumCaseTypedSelect(layout, tag: tag, column: EnumCaseConstant.caseName, valueType: MaxonType.string, anchor: exprTok, range: range), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			declaredText then self.materializeStringRecordPart(try self.emitEnumCaseTypedSelect(layout, tag: tag, column: EnumCaseConstant.declaredRawValue, valueType: MaxonType.string, anchor: exprTok, range: range), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			rawNumber then try self.materializeIntegerPart(tag, exprTok: exprTok, format: format, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
		end 'rendering'
	end 'materializeEnumPart'

	// ⭐ **INTERPOLATE A FLOAT VIA `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s `__float_toString`** — see the arm in
	// `materializeInterpExpr` for why this is a call rather than a scratch-buffer converter.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS MINTED `CalleeMint.primitiveStatic`, WHICH IS WHAT LETS A RESERVED CALLEE THROUGH** — and
	// the classification is a claim, not a trick: `"{x}"` on a float MEANS `float.toString(x)`, the
	// primitive static whose body `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declares in the reserved space, exactly as
	// `int.fromString(s)` means `__int_fromString(s)`. A user cannot write `__float_toString(x)` (E3004
	// refuses it, unminted); the compiler spells it out of a source `"{x}"` carrying no `__`, which is the
	// case that mint exists for. A diagnostic about the call therefore quotes `float.toString(…)`
	// (`SemanticCheck` → `primitiveStaticSourceSpelling`) — the noun for what the interpolation does,
	// rather than a reserved symbol nobody wrote. The struct arm one function down takes the same view of
	// its own call for the same reason.
	function materializeFloatToStringPart(exprVal ValueId, exprTok Token, format InterpFormatSpec, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(exprVal)

		// The callee is spelled by `primitiveStaticCallee`, the ONE speller of a primitive's reserved-space
		// static, out of the same two components a source `float.toString(x)` would join — so this lowering
		// cannot come to name a function the mint would spell differently.
		//
		// ⭐ **A PRECISION IS ABSENT RATHER THAN DEFAULTED, AND THAT IS WHY THERE ARE TWO CALLEES.**
		// `"{f:8}"` is the language's own spelling of the value — the shortest decimal that reads back —
		// right-aligned in a field; `"{f:.2}"` is two decimals. Giving the padded form a default number of
		// decimals is what would let a WIDTH silently change the DIGITS, so the two are different
		// functions rather than one with a sentinel precision.
		var method = ToStringMethodName
		if format.present 'inAField'
			method = FloatToStringPaddedMethodName
			if format.hasPrecision 'fixedDecimals'
				method = FloatToStringFixedMethodName
				args.push(self.emitLiteral(format.precision, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
			end 'fixedDecimals'
			args.push(self.emitLiteral(format.width, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
			args.push(self.emitLiteral(boolPayload(format.zeroFill), valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: range))
		end 'inAField'

		try self.materializeCallPart(primitiveStaticCallee(FloatConformerName, methodName: method), args: args, mint: CalleeMint.primitiveStatic, exprTok: exprTok, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
	end 'materializeFloatToStringPart'

	// ⭐⭐ **`"{n:08x}"` → `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s `__int_toStringFormatted`, WHICH TAKES CONSTANTS AND
	// READS NO TEXT.** Every one of its five trailing arguments is a compile-time answer this parse
	// already has: the width and the fill from the specifier, the base and its letter case from the
	// specifier, and the SIGNEDNESS from the value's declared type.
	//
	// ⚠ **`signed` IS THE COMPILER'S ANSWER, NOT A RE-READING OF BIT 63, AND IT IS AN `and` OF TWO
	// FACTS.** A base that spells the bit pattern (`x`, `X`, `o`, `b`) never writes a sign whatever the
	// value's type — `"{allBits:x}"` on `0 - 1` is `ffffffffffffffff`. And a DECIMAL rendering of a value
	// whose declared range is non-negative is unsigned — `"{u:d}"` on `u64.max` is
	// `18446744073709551615`, the same number `"{u}"` prints, because a format specifier never changes
	// WHICH number is being printed. The renderer this replaces in both references derived signedness
	// from the sign bit and answered `-1`.
	function materializeFormattedIntPart(exprVal ValueId, exprTok Token, format InterpFormatSpec, rendersUnsigned bool, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(exprVal)
		args.push(self.emitLiteral(boolPayload(format.spellsASign and not rendersUnsigned), valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: range))
		args.push(self.emitLiteral(format.halfRadix, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
		args.push(self.emitLiteral(boolPayload(format.uppercase), valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: range))
		args.push(self.emitLiteral(format.width, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
		args.push(self.emitLiteral(boolPayload(format.zeroFill), valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: range))

		try self.materializeCallPart(primitiveStaticCallee(IntConformerName, methodName: IntToStringFormattedMethodName), args: args, mint: CalleeMint.primitiveStatic, exprTok: exprTok, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
	end 'materializeFormattedIntPart'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE PART, RENDERED BY A CALL — the shared spelling of every `toString`-shaped arm.** The
	// float arms, the formatted-integer arm and the user-struct arm choose a different callee and hand
	// over different arguments; every step after that is the SAME protocol, and the steps are not
	// optional: the call's String result is classified +1 OWNED by `emitCall` (`enrolOwnedCallTemp` →
	// `trackOwnedTemp`) so it is freed at STATEMENT END, after `emitStringInterp` has copied its bytes,
	// and its bytes are spliced by `materializeStringRecordPart` exactly as any other String part's are.
	// Written once because a step added to one copy and not the others is a leak or a use-after-free at
	// RUNTIME rather than a compile error — the bootstrap's `EmitStdlibTextCall` records the same reason.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LABELS ARE EMPTY AND THE ARGUMENTS ARE THEREFORE POSITIONAL** (`argSlotPosition`), which is
	// what a compiler-minted call wants: these arguments have no author-written spelling to agree with,
	// and binding them by NAME would couple this lowering to the parameter names `stdlib` happens to use.
	function materializeCallPart(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, mint CalleeMint, exprTok Token, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		var argLabels = ByteArrayArray.create()
		var argRanges = SourceRangeArray.create()
		for _ in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArg'
			argLabels.push(EmptySourceText)
			argRanges.push(range)
		end 'eachArg'

		let result = try self.emitCall(callee, anchor: exprTok, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: exprTok, resultUsed: true, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: mint)
		self.materializeStringRecordPart(result, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
	end 'materializeCallPart'

	// ⭐ **INTERPOLATE A USER STRUCT VIA A DIRECT `<Type>.toString()` CALL (P1.7a slice 1).** A struct is
	// interpolable iff it HAS a `toString` method (by NAME, not by declaring `Stringable` — the oracle's
	// rule); a struct WITHOUT one is E3016, positioned at the interpolated expression. The call's result is
	// classified +1 OWNED by `emitCall` (`enrolOwnedCallTemp` → `trackOwnedTemp`), so the fresh String is
	// freed at STATEMENT END — after `emitStringInterp` has copied its bytes into the interp result. Its
	// bytes are spliced exactly as a `{s}` String part's are (`materializeStringRecordPart`), the same
	// machinery a String-returning call already interpolates through. Without the owned classify, one
	// String would leak per interpolated struct (a v1 leak trap).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A FORMAT SPECIFIER MAKES IT THE `FormattedStringable` OVERLOAD — `"{c:verbose}"` IS
	// `c.toString("verbose")`.** The specifier's text is handed over as an ordinary `String` argument,
	// so the SELECTION is the language's own overload resolution rather than a rule this lowering
	// invents: the call is emitted under the bare `<Type>.toString` name with two arguments, and
	// `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` retargets it to the member whose arity and parameter type
	// fit — the same machinery that sends the ZERO-argument `"{c}"` to `toString()` on a type declaring
	// both (`string-interpolation/stringable-and-formatted-interp-selects-the-zero-arg-overload`).
	//
	// ⚠ A type that declares only `toString()` and is interpolated WITH a specifier is therefore
	// reported by the ordinary argument-count check against the member it does declare, not by a rule
	// here. That is deliberate: the parser has no per-callee arity to ask (`declaresCallee` is a name
	// lookup, see its header), and inventing a second answer to "which overload did this mean?" is the
	// thing overload resolution exists to prevent.
	function materializeStructToStringPart(exprVal ValueId, exprTok Token, format InterpFormatSpec, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		let structName = self.internedName(self.valueNameId(exprVal))
		let callee = qualifiedMethodName(structName, methodName: ToStringMethodName)

		// Asked of the WHOLE-PROGRAM index, not of this file: a struct's `toString` may be declared anywhere,
		// including in the file that uses it in an interpolation. `declaresCallee` answers exactly that with a
		// `Map.contains`; the return type it does not fetch is fetched a few lines down by `emitCall`'s
		// `mintCallResult`, which is where it is actually needed.
		if not self.signatures.declaresCallee(callee) 'noToString'
			self.recordArtifactDiagnostic(ErrorCode.semanticPartialInterfaceImpl, message: "Type '{String.from(structName)}' used in string interpolation must have a toString method", range: SourceRange.ofToken(exprTok))
			// The recorded diagnostic fails the build; materialize an EMPTY placeholder part so the interp
			// assembly (which needs at least one part) does not panic on a bare `"{s}"`.
			self.materializeStringRecordPart(self.emitStringLiteral(EmptySourceText, range: range), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
			return
		end 'noToString'

		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(exprVal)
		if format.present 'formattedOverload'
			// ⚠ **THESE BYTES ARE RAW, AND `emitStringLiteral`'s header asks for DECODED ones.** Every
			// other caller hands it the output of `decodeEscapes`, because every other caller is
			// emitting a string LITERAL the author quoted and `\n` in one means a newline. A specifier
			// is not quoted: `Lexer.scanInterpFormatSpec` takes its bytes verbatim and stops at the
			// first `}` whatever precedes it, so there is no escape grammar over them to decode and
			// `"{c:a\nb}"` names a three-character format `a\nb` — which is the string the author's
			// `toString(format String)` compares against. Decoding here would hand it a different one.
			args.push(self.emitStringLiteral(format.text, range: range))
		end 'formattedOverload'

		// `resolvedTypeQualifier`: the reserved-space door must judge this exactly as it judges the same call
		// spelled out (`p.toString()`), and that is the same mint the written form takes — the head is the
		// interpolated value's resolved type, and here the author wrote no member name either (F31).
		try self.materializeCallPart(callee, args: args, mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier, exprTok: exprTok, partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, range: range)
	end 'materializeStructToStringPart'

	// ⭐ **ONE VALUE'S TEXT, WITH NOTHING AROUND IT — `"{x}"` MINUS THE LITERAL FRAGMENTS.** The whole of
	// `x.toString()` on a builtin-typed receiver (`dispatchBuiltinConformanceMethod`), and it is the
	// interpolation path rather than a path LIKE it: the same `materializeInterpExpr` chooses the same arm
	// per tag (`__int_to_string` for an integer and for a ranged `Byte` alias, `__bool_to_string` for a
	// bool, the `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` `__float_toString` call for a float), and the same
	// `emitStringInterp` assembles the same fused owned record and frees the same scratch. So there is no
	// second formatter to keep in step, and `x.toString()` cannot come to disagree with `"{x}"` — the
	// property this project has already paid to learn the cost of.
	//
	// The result is an owned temporary exactly as an interpolation's is (`emitStringInterp` →
	// `trackOwnedTemp`): `let s = x.toString()` transfers it to a scope-exit drop through `parseBinding`,
	// and a bare `x.toString()` statement drops at statement end.
	//
	// ⚠ A format specifier is a property of an INTERPOLATION HOLE, and `x.toString()` is not one — the
	// spelling that takes one is `x.toString(spec)`, a written call this lowering is not on the path of.
	function emitValueToString(exprVal ValueId, exprTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		var partBufs = ValueIdArray.create()
		var partLens = ValueIdArray.create()
		var scratchIds = ValueIdArray.create()
		try self.materializeInterpExpr(exprVal, exprTok: exprTok, format: InterpFormatSpec.absent(), partBufs: partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
		return self.emitStringInterp(partBufs, partLens: partLens, scratchIds: scratchIds, range: range)
	end 'emitValueToString'

	// A String-record part: load `buffer@0` and `length@8` off the record. Shared by literal fragments and
	// `{s}` String parts — both are pointers to the fused 56-byte record, so both read the same two fields.
	function materializeStringRecordPart(rec ValueId, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
		partBufs.push(self.emitLoadStringField(rec, offset: StringBufferOffset, range: range))
		partLens.push(self.emitLoadStringField(rec, offset: StringLengthOffset, range: range))
	end 'materializeStringRecordPart'

	// Render a primitive part into a freshly `__mm_alloc`'d scratch buffer via a `(value, dest) -> length`
	// converter runtime function, enroll the scratch for the post-copy free (it is an owned buffer the blit
	// consumes — otherwise the interp-scratch-buffer leak), and record `(scratch, length)`. The shared shape
	// of every primitive part — int, bool, float — differing only in the converter and its worst-case byte
	// budget. A `named` integer alias is already i64, so the value needs no widening at this rung.
	function materializeScratchPart(exprVal ValueId, converter ByteArray, scratchBytes ParsedInt, partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, scratchIds ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
		let scratchSize = self.emitLiteral(scratchBytes, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let scratch = self.emitBoxAlloc(scratchSize, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range, tag: MmTag.InterpolationScratch)

		var convArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		convArgs.push(exprVal)
		convArgs.push(scratch)
		let length = self.emitRuntimeCall(converter, args: convArgs, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)

		partBufs.push(scratch)
		partLens.push(length)
		scratchIds.push(scratch)
	end 'materializeScratchPart'

	// ⭐ **ASSEMBLE THE FUSED OWNED STRING.** Sum the part lengths, allocate ONE `__mm_alloc` record big
	// enough for the 56-byte record + the bytes + a NUL, copy every part into the inline region at a running
	// cursor, write the record fields, and free the scratch. The result is an owned temporary
	// (`trackOwnedTemp`) — an INLINE String (`capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`) whose bytes die with the
	// record's slot, so its destructor is `TrivialDestructor` (0), exactly a struct's: `__mm_decref` →
	// `__mm_free` reclaims record and bytes together, with no external buffer and no destructor cascade.
	function emitStringInterp(partBufs ValueIdArray, partLens ValueIdArray, scratchIds ValueIdArray, range SourceRange, resultTag ValueTypeTag = ValueTypeTag.string) returns ValueId
		let totalLen = self.sumPartLengths(partLens, range: range)

		let overhead = self.emitLiteral(StringInterpOverheadBytes, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let allocSize = self.emitMachineWordAdd(totalLen, rhs: overhead, range: range)
		let result = self.emitBoxAlloc(allocSize, resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)

		// The inline byte region begins right after the record. Copy each part in at a cursor that advances
		// by the part's length; the NUL at `buffer[totalLen]` is already zero (the slab zeroes the payload).
		let recordBytes = self.emitLiteral(StringRecordBytes as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let inlineBuf = self.emitMachineWordAdd(result, rhs: recordBytes, range: range)
		var cursor = inlineBuf
		for i in 0 upto partBufs.count() 'copyParts'
			let buf = try partBufs.get(i) otherwise panic("emitStringInterp: partBufs.get OOB at {i} — partBufs and partLens grow in lockstep")
			let length = try partLens.get(i) otherwise panic("emitStringInterp: partLens.get OOB at {i} — partBufs and partLens grow in lockstep")
			var copyArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
			copyArgs.push(cursor)
			copyArgs.push(buf)
			copyArgs.push(length)
			_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(StrCopyName, args: copyArgs, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
			cursor = self.emitMachineWordAdd(cursor, rhs: length, range: range)
		end 'copyParts'

		self.emitStoreStringField(result, offset: StringBufferOffset, value: inlineBuf, range: range)
		self.emitStoreStringField(result, offset: StringLengthOffset, value: totalLen, range: range)
		// ⭐ **`capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity` — the buffer-ownership encoding's INLINE provenance**
		// (`BufferOwnership`), and the one fact the whole record has to publish about its bytes: they are
		// part of this box, so they are neither writable in place beyond their exact length nor separately
		// releasable. `__managed_grow` reads it — the growth every String append now reaches through
		// `String.append` -> `managed.append` -> `__managed_append` -> `__managed_reserve` — and any negative capacity
		// is below every required length, so an inline String always detaches into a private buffer before it
		// grows. (`__str_append` read it directly until W49 wave 8 retired it; the encoding is unchanged, and
		// that it survived the change of reader is the point of putting it in `BufferOwnership`.) `__str_decref` reads it
		// (a negative capacity sends it to `parent@32`, which is `NoOwedAllocation` here, so the record's
		// own free reclaims record and bytes together). It used to publish `length` — an ordinary
		// NON-NEGATIVE capacity, which the shared encoding reads as "I own a separate buffer" — and marked
		// inline-ness in `parent@32` instead, which nothing read: the drop path was correct only because
		// `__str_decref` carried a String-only branch that re-derived inline-ness from the layout.
		// `parent@32` needs no store at all now: `NoOwedAllocation` is 0, which `__mm_alloc`'s zeroing
		// contract already supplies (the same reason `__managed_create` leaves four of its six fields alone).
		let capacity = self.emitLiteral(InlineBufferCapacity, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.emitStoreStringField(result, offset: StringCapacityOffset, value: capacity, range: range)
		let elementSize = self.emitLiteral(ByteStringElementSize, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.emitStoreStringField(result, offset: StringElementSizeOffset, value: elementSize, range: range)
		// `element_destroy@40 = TrivialElementDestroy` and `singleByteGraphemes@48 = 0` (conservative) both come
		// from the slab's zeroing — a String's elements are `Byte`s, so the first is the TRUTH about this
		// record and not a placeholder (W157).

		// Free the owned scratch now its bytes are copied. A `__mm_decref` (rc 0 → `__mm_free`), never a raw
		// free, so the leak counter stays balanced — the same door `emitScopeDrops` drops through.
		for i in 0 upto scratchIds.count() 'freeScratch'
			let scratch = try scratchIds.get(i) otherwise panic("emitStringInterp: scratchIds.get OOB at {i}")
			self.emitDecref(scratch, range: range)
		end 'freeScratch'

		return result
	end 'emitStringInterp'

	// Sum the parts' lengths into a single value. At least one part exists — a real interpolation carries a
	// `{expr}`, so `parseStringInterpolation` always materialized one before calling here.
	function sumPartLengths(partLens ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		var total = try partLens.get(0) otherwise panic("sumPartLengths: an interpolation materialized no parts — parseStringInterpolation only reaches emitStringInterp after materializing at least one interpolated part")
		for i in 1 upto partLens.count() 'sum'
			let length = try partLens.get(i) otherwise panic("sumPartLengths: partLens.get OOB at {i}")
			total = self.emitMachineWordAdd(total, rhs: length, range: range)
		end 'sum'
		return total
	end 'sumPartLengths'

	// `lhs + rhs` as raw i64 MACHINE-WORD arithmetic — the interpolation's cursor advance, buffer offset
	// and size math, and a `for` loop's index step. It emits `MaxonOp.binOp(add)` DIRECTLY, bypassing
	// `emitBinOp`'s operand type rules, because the operands are compiler-owned words (a String pointer
	// `result + StringRecordBytes`, a byte length, a loop counter) rather than a source-level `a + b`
	// whose two sides must be shown to agree. The result is integer-tagged; the Std tier reads a String
	// lhs as its i64 pointer (`valueTagToStdType`), so the `add` is a clean i64 add either way.
	function emitMachineWordAdd(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.binOp(result, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, op: MaxonBinOp.add), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitMachineWordAdd'

	// Load an 8-byte field of a String record — `buffer@0`, `length@8`. Integer-tagged: the values are a raw
	// pointer and a byte count. The `rec` addr is a String pointer, read by the Std tier as an i64.
	//
	// It routes through `emitIndirectLoad` rather than emitting the op itself, exactly as `emitFieldLoad`
	// does: a String record is a BOX like any other, and its declared type and its storage type coincide
	// at `integer` only because a raw pointer and a byte count are both machine words. That is a fact
	// about this box, and it belongs here — not a reason to own a second copy of the emit.
	function emitLoadStringField(rec ValueId, offset ByteOffset, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		return self.emitIndirectLoad(MaxonType.integer, box: rec, offset: offset, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'emitLoadStringField'

	// Store an 8-byte field of a String record — the mirror of `emitLoadStringField`, the one place
	// `emitStringInterp` writes the fused record's SEVEN fields. It writes FOUR of them
	// (`buffer@0`, `length@8`, `capacity@16`, `element_size@24`); `parent@32`, `element_destroy@40` and
	// `singleByteGraphemes@48` all ride the slab's zeroing, which is the right value for each.
	function emitStoreStringField(rec ValueId, offset ByteOffset, value ValueId, range SourceRange)
		self.emitIndirectStore(rec, offset: offset, value: value, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'emitStoreStringField'

	// `true` / `false`. A bool is an i1 carrying 1 or 0 — the same `literal` op an
	// integer uses, typed `boolean` so the Maxon→Std collapse gives it i1 rather than
	// i64. The `bool` TYPE already existed (a comparison produces one, an `if` consumes
	// one); these are the literals that let a program NAME one.
	// ONE emit site, not one per arm: the two literals differ only in the token they
	// consume and the payload they carry, so a change to how a bool is materialized (its
	// MaxonType, its op) lands in a single place and cannot apply to `true` but not `false`.
	function parseBoolLiteral() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let tok = self.current()
		var value = BoolTrueValue
		if self.at(TokenKind.true) 'isTrue'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.true)
		end 'isTrue' else 'isFalse'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.false)
			value = BoolFalseValue
		end 'isFalse'
		return self.emitLiteral(value, valueType: MaxonType.boolean, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok))
	end 'parseBoolLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Self{x: x, y: y}` — WHERE THE HEAP ENTERS THE LANGUAGE.** The first, and at this rung
	// the only, expression that allocates.
	//
	// It lowers to exactly four kinds of op, none of them new:
	//
	//     literal   16                 -- the box's payload size (StructLayout.sizeBytes)
	//     literal   0                  -- the destructor (TrivialDestructor; see there)
	//     call      __mm_alloc         -- returns the PAYLOAD pointer; field i is at [ptr + i*8]
	//     storeIndirect <ptr> 0 <x>    -- one per field, at StructLayout.offsetOfField(i)
	//     storeIndirect <ptr> 8 <y>
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FIELDS ARE STORED IN DECLARATION ORDER, NOT IN THE ORDER THEY WERE WRITTEN**, and the
	// values are parsed in the order they were written. Both are true at once because the values are
	// evaluated into SSA ids as the literal is read, and the STORES are emitted afterwards by walking
	// the layout — so `Point{y: 4, x: 3}` evaluates `4` first and still puts `3` in slot 0. Emitting
	// each store as its field was parsed would have written the box in source order, which is the same
	// thing for these two cases and the wrong thing for one field in a slot the layout disagrees about.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY FIELD MUST END UP INITIALIZED, and the allocator's zeroing guarantee is what makes
	// that a RULE rather than a nicety.** An omitted slot reads back as a clean 0 — a plausible
	// number — so `Point{x: 1}` would silently have `y == 0`. E3086 rejects it.
	//
	// A slot gets its value from one of TWO sources, asked in this order: the literal
	// (`parseStructLiteralFields`), then the field's declared DEFAULT (`applyFieldDefaults`). The
	// literal always wins — the default is consulted only for a slot `filled` says nothing wrote.
	// `requireAllFieldsInitialized` then rejects whatever neither supplied.
	//
	// ⭐ **A MANAGED FIELD IS MOVED IN** (`emitStructLiteral` → `moveInPayload`), not stored raw — the
	// CONSUME-into-field half of cross-call ownership. Every managed field kind is now constructible: a
	// `String` (promoted/moved in, dropped through `__str_decref`), a `struct` (moved in, dropped through
	// its own `__destruct_<Struct>`), and a payload-bearing (boxed) UNION field (moved in, dropped through
	// the union's `__destruct_<U>` or the trivial `__mm_decref` for a scalar-only box — P1.4b wave 2c). The
	// box holds exactly ONE reference to each and its `__destruct_<Struct>` cascade releases exactly those. A
	// borrowed struct/union value has no `clone`, so it is CO-OWNED rather than copied (`moveInPayload` — the
	// transitive-consume ruling of 2026-08-04).
	function parseStructLiteral(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `Self{…}` and `Point{…}` arrive as ONE token here — `typeBaseTokenAt` has already turned the keyword
		// into the name it denotes — so the literal's own reader knows nothing about `Self`.
		let typeTok = try self.consumeTypeBaseToken()

		// ⭐⭐ **`<InnerAlias>{}` — A TYPEALIAS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE IN THE BODY THAT DECLARES IT (S2i).** E3076's
		// rule is about WHERE the literal is written, and a `typealias` a type body declares is written inside
		// that body: `stdlib/Set.maxon:43` opens `var elems = ElementArray{}` two lines below its own
		// `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element`. **The rule is about the alias's DECLARATION SITE and
		// NOT about genericity** — measured against the oracle, which compiles and runs the identical program
		// with no type parameters anywhere (`inner-alias-construction.nongeneric-inner-array-alias`). A
		// TOP-LEVEL alias is declared in no body, so no body may construct it and it stays E3076 below, where
		// both reference compilers already agree.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS ROUTED BEFORE `requireConstructible` BECAUSE THAT ROUTINE RETURNS A `StructLayout` AND AN
		// ALIAS TO `Array with X` HAS NONE.** Widening its location test alone would carry the literal into the
		// struct registry asking for a name only the generic-instance index holds. What the construct MEANS is
		// the alias's EMPTY CONTAINER — measured: the oracle's value is then `resize`d and `count()`s back —
		// so it is served by the producer `<InnerAlias>.create()` already goes through, never a second one.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TYPE'S OWN NAME — AND ITS TYPE PARAMETERS — WIN OVER AN ALIAS SHADOWING THEM, and that
		// precedence is READ OFF `parseTypeReference` RATHER THAN CHOSEN HERE**: `namesInnerAliasHere` is the
		// one home of it, so this door, the static-call door and `parseTypeReference` itself cannot come to
		// disagree about what one written name denotes. MEASURED without it: `Self{}` in a body declaring
		// `typealias Holder = Array with ExitCode` (which arrives here as `Holder`) built an ARRAY, and the
		// type's own `create` was refused for returning `Holder.Holder` where it declared `Holder`. Neither
		// reference compiler refuses the shadowing declaration — the bootstrap compiles the program and
		// recurses until the stack overflows — so the guard is what keeps the two spellings agreeing.
		if self.namesInnerAliasHere(typeTok.value) 'innerAliasLiteral'
			return try self.parseInnerAliasLiteral(typeTok)
		end 'innerAliasLiteral'

		let layout = try self.requireConstructible(typeTok)

		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftBrace)
		// Parallel to the LAYOUT, not to the source: `fieldValues[i]` is the value for the field the
		// type declares at `i`, whatever position the literal wrote it in. `filled` is what turns an
		// unwritten slot into E3086 rather than into a zero nobody chose. `fieldBareLocal[i]` records
		// whether the literal's value for field `i` was a bare reference to a local binding — what a
		// managed field's move-in poisons (`moveInPayload`), captured here because only the parse can see it.
		var fieldValues = ValueIdArray.create()
		var filled = BoolArray.create()
		var fieldBareLocal = BoolArray.create()
		for _ in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'initSlots'
			fieldValues.push(0 as ValueId)
			filled.push(false)
			fieldBareLocal.push(false)
		end 'initSlots'

		try self.parseStructLiteralFields(func, layout: layout, fieldValues: fieldValues, filled: filled, fieldBareLocal: fieldBareLocal)
		let rbraceTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBrace)

		// Defaults are applied BEFORE the check, which is what makes an omitted-but-defaulted field
		// legal without teaching `requireAllFieldsInitialized` a second rule. That one goes on
		// asking exactly what it always asked — "does every slot have a value by now?" — and a
		// default is simply one of the two ways a slot comes to have one.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **A DEFAULT CAN NOW BE A MANAGED VALUE (S2f), AND ITS `fieldBareLocal` STAYING FALSE IS WHAT
		// MAKES THAT SAFE RATHER THAN A COINCIDENCE.** This line used to say a default was always a scalar
		// literal. It is not: `as IntArray = IntArray.create()` emits a CALL, whose result the mint already
		// owns and lists as this statement's temporary (`mintOwnedCallResult` → `trackOwnedTemp`). The
		// store loop below cannot tell that slot from a written one, and must not: it moves the value in
		// exactly as it moves a written `Self{items: makeItems()}`, and `fieldBareLocal` false is the
		// correct answer for BOTH — a call result names no binding, so there is nothing to poison and the
		// temp is simply consumed out of the pending drops (`transferOwnedInto`).
		//
		// ⭐ **AND THE DEFAULTS RUN AFTER EVERY WRITTEN FIELD'S EXPRESSION, DELIBERATELY.** The literal's
		// own values are evaluated as it is read (`parseStructLiteralFields`), so a side-effecting default
		// runs after every side-effecting value the author actually wrote — source order for what is
		// written, then the defaults, in declaration order. v1 splices its default-init call in immediately
		// after the allocation instead, i.e. AHEAD of the written fields; no case in either corpus can tell
		// the two apart, and this order is the one that keeps the two fills independent passes over the same
		// two columns rather than one interleaved walk.
		try self.applyFieldDefaults(layout, fieldValues: fieldValues, filled: filled, anchor: typeTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **`Self{}` INSIDE `type Array` IS THE EMPTY CONTAINER (W57)** — the very answer `<InnerAlias>{}`
		// gets one door down (S2i, `parseInnerAliasLiteral`), and for the same reason: `Array`'s sole field IS
		// its record, so an empty literal leaves no slot unwritten — it names the empty buffer, which is what
		// `Array.create()` builds. `stdlib/Array.maxon` opens both `create()` and `from(source)` with it.
		//
		// ⚠ It is an arm here rather than a field DEFAULT in the corpus, and the difference is not style: a
		// default is source the OTHER two compilers read as well, and it would cost a `coOwnAggregateAsTemp`
		// retain on a record the default had just built. The NON-empty spelling (`Self{managed: m}`) keeps the
		// ordinary path and reaches `emitFusedWrapperLiteral`'s identity arm, so the two forms stay the two
		// statics they mirror — `create()` and `init(managed)`.
		// ⭐⭐ **AND `Self{}` INSIDE `stdlib/Vector.maxon` IS THE SAME FORM ON THE SIZED CONTAINER, AND SINCE
		// W190 IT IS THE WHOLE OF `Vec3.create()`.** This paragraph read *"what it BUILDS is a countless
		// container, i.e. ZERO slots, and that is why `create` is the one member of that surface which cannot
		// retire"* — true of the emit as it then stood, and it stopped being true when the literal learned to
		// publish. `emitOwnedContainerCreate`'s sized-container arm sizes the record from
		// `fixedElementCountValue`: a folded literal at a concrete `Vec3{}`, and the hidden count parameter
		// inside `Vector.create`, whose `Self` states none (`ownRecordSelfInstance`) so ONE body serves every
		// size. The declaration's `return Self{}` is unchanged, which is what lets the BOOTSTRAP compile the
		// same corpus line — it has no `countof` and needs none, because the count is the literal's.
		if self.signatures.declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord(layout.name) and not (try filled.get(0) otherwise panic("parseStructLiteral: filled.get OOB at 0 — `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` established a sole field and the column is sized to the layout's field count")) 'theEmptyContainer'
			return try self.emitOwnedContainerCreate(self.signatures.ownRecordSelfInstance(layout.name), aliasTok: typeTok, aliasName: layout.name, range: SourceRange.between(typeTok, last: rbraceTok))
		end 'theEmptyContainer'

		try self.requireAllFieldsInitialized(layout, filled: filled, anchor: typeTok)

		return try self.emitStructLiteral(layout, fieldValues: fieldValues, fieldBareLocal: fieldBareLocal, anchor: typeTok, range: SourceRange.between(typeTok, last: rbraceTok))
	end 'parseStructLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **`<InnerAlias>{}` IS THE ALIAS'S EMPTY CONTAINER — the very value `<InnerAlias>.create()` builds**
	// (S2i). A TOTAL router, so a form nobody thought about meets a sentence naming what it is rather than
	// E3076's "use a static factory", a cure that exists for exactly one of the four arms below.
	//
	// `enclosingInnerAliases` holds two FORMS (`readNestedTypeAlias`: a GENERIC-INSTANCE alias and a RANGED
	// numeric one — a nested TUPLE alias is refused at its declaration and a nested FUNCTION alias is a
	// file-scope name that never enters the set). They split four ways here, because a generic instance is
	// either a container this compiler OWNS or a user type's, and because an alias declared in an `extension`
	// body reaches this set without reaching either whole-program registry.
	function parseInnerAliasLiteral(aliasTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The whole-program key an inner alias is registered under — the SAME name `parseTypeReference`'s
		// inner-alias arm stamps a field type with and `parseQualifiedCall`'s `<InnerAlias>.create()` looks the
		// instance up by, because a bare member collides with a same-named inner alias of another type.
		let qualified = joinQualifiedName(self.enclosingType, member: aliasTok.value)

		// ⛔ A RANGED NUMERIC ALIAS DECLARES NO FIELDS, so there is nothing for a literal to write and no
		// container for `{}` to mean. Both reference compilers CRASH on this program rather than diagnose it
		// (the bootstrap with an unhandled `IrRangedPrimitiveType`→`IrStructType` cast inside
		// `ParseStructLiteral`, reported as an internal `E9001`), so there is no oracle spelling to match and
		// the message names the cure instead — the enum/union refusal in `requireConstructible` one door over
		// is the same shape for the same reason.
		if self.signatures.isInnerAlias(qualified) 'rangedNumeric'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a struct literal naming `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}`, a ranged numeric typealias declared by `{String.from(self.enclosingType)}` — a number declares no fields to write, so write the number itself. `\{\}` on an inner alias builds the EMPTY CONTAINER a container alias denotes, and a numeric alias denotes no container", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column)
		end 'rangedNumeric'

		// ⛔ THE ALIAS IS IN NEITHER WHOLE-PROGRAM REGISTRY, so there is no interned instance to create.
		//
		// ⭐ **W3 REMOVED THE CASE THIS ARM WAS WRITTEN FOR AND LEFT THE ARM.** It said an `extension` body's
		// nested alias is *never* keyed whole-program — true until `foldExtensionDeclarationInto` began keying
		// one per CONFORMER, which is what makes `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable` compile
		// (`map`/`filter` open with `var result = ElementArray\{\}`). What can still arrive here is the
		// declaration sweep having failed to publish an alias the REAL parse went on to accept locally: both
		// walks are tolerant by design (`recordScannedType`, `foldExtensionDeclarationInto`), and a malformed
		// member earlier in the body ends the sweep's walk while `enclosingInnerAliases` is filled by the parse
		// that reaches this literal. That program is being refused for its real fault a moment later; this arm
		// exists so the refusal it gets HERE is not E3076's: that one prescribes a static factory, and the
		// factory an unkeyed alias would need (`{alias}.create()`) has nothing to resolve to either.
		if not self.signatures.isGenericAlias(qualified) 'notKeyedWholeProgram'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a struct literal naming `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}`, an inner typealias of `{String.from(self.enclosingType)}` that the whole-program declaration sweep did not key — so there is no container instance for the literal to build. A sweep stops early at a member it cannot read, so the declaration this one shares a body with is the fault to look for", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column)
		end 'notKeyedWholeProgram'

		let giid = self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(qualified, readerFilePath: self.filePath)

		// ⛔ A USER TYPE'S GENERIC INSTANCE KEEPS E3076, and that is the ONE inner-alias form the widening
		// deliberately does not reach. `typealias IntPair = Pair with Count` denotes `Pair`, whose invariants
		// are `Pair`'s own to establish — writing its literal here is exactly what the restriction is for, and
		// the static factory it prescribes IS WRITABLE HERE (`IntPair.create(…)`): it resolves through
		// `genericAliasKeyFor` and is pinned by `user-generic-inner-alias-create-is-the-cure`, the half W7
		// built after this
		// sentence had claimed it for a while without a case. (The oracle ACCEPTS it, which voids its own E3076 for
		// any type willing to declare an alias; that divergence is deliberate — and it is why no oracle pin
		// covers this anchor.) Anchored inside the brace, which is where `requireConstructible` anchors the
		// same refusal, so ONE rule places E3076 wherever it is raised from rather than two arms each
		// matching their own pins.
		if not self.signatures.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(giid)) 'userType'
			let insideBrace = self.structLiteralBraceInteriorToken()
			throw ParseError.constructorRestricted(String.from(aliasTok.value), line: insideBrace.line, column: insideBrace.column)
		end 'userType'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftBrace)
		self.skipNewlines()

		// ⛔ The literal is the container's EMPTY value and nothing else — a builtin container's record is the
		// runtime's, with no user-writable field for a `name: value` pair to name. Anchored at the field the
		// author wrote, which is the token that is inadmissible.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.rightBrace) 'writtenField'
			let fieldTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a field written inside `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}\{…\}` — the literal builds the EMPTY container `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` builds, and a builtin container has no user-writable fields; fill it after creating it", line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column)
		end 'writtenField'

		let rbraceTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBrace)
		return try self.emitOwnedContainerCreate(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok, aliasName: qualified, range: SourceRange.between(aliasTok, last: rbraceTok))
	end 'parseInnerAliasLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHERE AN INADMISSIBLE STRUCT LITERAL IS BLAMED: THE TOKEN FOLLOWING THE `{`, NOT THE TYPE NAME.**
	// Read off the oracle's emitter rather than chosen here — `2-Parser.cs:21017-21048` captures the line at
	// the `{`, then `Advance()`s past it and takes `Current().Column`, for E3072 and E3076 alike. MEASURED on
	// three programs: `let b = Box{v: 5}` blames column 14 (`v`) where `Box` is 10, `let bb = Box{v: 5, w: 6}`
	// blames 15, and `let e = Empty{}` blames the `}` — which is what says the rule is *the token after the
	// brace* rather than *the first field label*. `specs/struct-construction-restriction.md` and
	// `specs/managed-file.md` pin those columns, so it is the acceptance criterion and not a preference;
	// anchoring at the type name would read better to me, and that is not an argument.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE RAW NEXT TOKEN, AND FOR A MULTI-LINE LITERAL THAT IS THE END-OF-LINE NEWLINE — WHICH IS
	// THE TOKEN THE ORACLE BLAMES TOO, SO THERE IS NO DIVERGENCE HERE TO REPAIR.** The oracle reads the LINE
	// off the `{` and the COLUMN off the token after it, where this reads both off that token; the two are
	// nonetheless the same position on every input, because a newline is a TOKEN in both lexers and carries
	// the PRECEDING line's number with the newline character's own column (`Lexer.newline`, and the
	// bootstrap's `1-Lexer.cs:301-307` — identical shape), and neither parser's advance skips it.
	//
	// ⛔ **SO DO NOT "ALIGN WITH THE ORACLE" BY SKIPPING TO THE FIRST FIELD ON THE NEXT LINE.**
	// `indexPastNewlines(self.pos + 1)` is the shape that suggests itself, and it would CREATE the divergence
	// this paragraph used to claim already existed — on `Point{` at end of line, the commonest spelling of a
	// multi-line literal, which the corpus writes everywhere.
	//
	// ⚠ One unaudited edge, PRE-EXISTING and deliberately not chased here: on CRLF shv2's newline token
	// starts at the `\r` (`Action.skipCr`), which is the one column at which these two lexers could disagree.
	// No pinned case reaches it.
	function structLiteralBraceInteriorToken() returns Token
		// ⛔ **THE PREMISE IS THE ROUTE, NOT THE PAIR OF CALL SITES.** Every caller reached its literal
		// through `parsePrimary`'s `{`-LOOKAHEAD arm (`peekKind() == leftBrace`), which is what puts the
		// cursor on the brace. Being "a refusal raised between `consumeTypeBaseToken` and the literal's own
		// `consume(leftBrace)`" does NOT imply it and must not be read as implying it: the `enumBody` arm
		// below is exactly such a refusal and is reachable with the cursor on ANY token, through the
		// braceless `Self` route — see the reason recorded there for why it keeps its own anchor.
		//
		// Asserted rather than asserted-in-prose: a future arm moved onto this reader from the braceless
		// route would otherwise silently blame a token from the middle of an unrelated construct.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftBrace) 'cursorNotOnBrace'
			panic("Parser.structLiteralBraceInteriorToken: the cursor is on '{tokenDisplayText(self.current())}', not the '\{' of a struct literal — this reader is only meaningful between `consumeTypeBaseToken` and the brace's own `consume`")
		end 'cursorNotOnBrace'

		return self.tokenAt(self.pos + 1)
	end 'structLiteralBraceInteriorToken'

	// ⭐ **E3076 — CONSTRUCTION IS RESTRICTED TO THE TYPE'S OWN BODY.** `Self{…}` and `Point{…}`
	// inside `type Point`; nothing anywhere else. An external caller goes through
	// `Point.create(…)`.
	//
	// The point is that a type gets ONE place its invariants are established. Both reference
	// compilers impose exactly this restriction under exactly this code, and it is what makes
	// `static function create` the universal shape of the corpus rather than a convention.
	//
	// The order of the two rejections is the decision: a name that is not a struct AT ALL is not a
	// constructor-restriction violation, so it reports what it actually is.
	// ⚠ It takes the TOKEN and reads the name off it, rather than taking both: the name and the position are
	// one fact about one token, and a second parameter is a licence for a later caller to make a diagnostic
	// NAME one token while POINTING AT another.
	function requireConstructible(typeTok Token) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		let typeName = typeTok.value

		// The RESTRICTION is checked before the LOOKUP, and the order is the decision: `Foo{…}`
		// outside `type Foo` is E3076 whether or not `Foo` exists, because the restriction is about
		// WHERE the literal is written, not about what it names. Asking the registry first would
		// report "no such type" for a `Point{…}` written in `main` — naming a type that is right
		// there in the file, which is a confusing answer to a question the user did not ask.
		// ⭐ **A COMPILER BUILTIN MANAGED TYPE IS REFUSED BEFORE THE LOCATION CHECK (E3072, R4.1).** The
		// order is the decision, exactly as the paragraph above makes one: E3076 says "not HERE" and
		// prescribes a static factory THE USER COULD WRITE, but `__ManagedFile` has NO source body for a
		// literal to be legal in and no user-writable static at all — its handle comes from the OS. So the
		// location question never applies to it, and asking it first would answer a program's
		// `__ManagedFile{_handle: 0}` with a cure that cannot be written for that type at all.
		// ⚠ **BOTH REFUSALS ARE ANCHORED INSIDE THE BRACE, NOT AT THE TYPE NAME** — the oracle's choice, read
		// off its emitter once and written down once in `structLiteralBraceInteriorToken`. The reader is asked
		// INSIDE each arm and not once above them, because this routine is ALSO reached with no brace in
		// sight: `parsePrimary`'s `selfPrimary` arm routes a bare `Self` here on the token alone, and the
		// missing brace is the E2010 from the `consume` below. Hoisting the read would turn that program's
		// diagnostic into this file's panic. Which arms are safe on that route is a question per arm:
		//
		//   • **E3076 (`outsideItsOwnBody`) — STRUCTURALLY unreachable there.** `typeBaseTokenAt` mints a
		//     bare `Self`'s token from `requireEnclosingTypeName`, so the name it carries IS the enclosing
		//     declaration and `namesEnclosingType` cannot be false.
		//   • **E3072 (`builtinManagedType`) — unreachable only CONTINGENTLY, and the premise is scheduled to
		//     weaken.** It rests on no `__`-prefixed type being declarable outside `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`
		//     (`requireUnreservedName`) and on Builtins declaring none of these names — while
		//     `isBuiltinManagedTypeName`'s own header (`SignatureIndex.maxon`) says the list grows as those
		//     types arrive in the corpus. ⇒ **The day one is declared as a `type`, a bare `Self` in its body
		//     panics here.** The durable fix is `selfPrimary` gating on `peekKind() == leftBrace`, which is
		//     NOT a ride-along: it moves a bare `Self` in an enum body off the `enumBody` sentence below and
		//     onto E2010, and that needs a pin sweep first.
		//   • **The `enumBody` arm keeps its own anchor for this reason** — see it below.
		if isBuiltinManagedTypeName(typeName) 'builtinManagedType'
			let insideBrace = self.structLiteralBraceInteriorToken()
			throw ParseError.builtinTypeConstruction(String.from(typeName), line: insideBrace.line, column: insideBrace.column)
		end 'builtinManagedType'

		if not self.namesEnclosingType(typeName) 'outsideItsOwnBody'
			let insideBrace = self.structLiteralBraceInteriorToken()
			throw ParseError.constructorRestricted(String.from(typeName), line: insideBrace.line, column: insideBrace.column)
		end 'outsideItsOwnBody'

		// ⛔ **AN `enum`/`union` HAS NO FIELDS TO WRITE, SO A STRUCT LITERAL CANNOT CONSTRUCT ONE (D9)** — the
		// exact twin of `enclosingLayout`'s enum guard, one door over, and reached the same way: the check
		// above has just established the name IS the enclosing declaration, which for an enum body means the
		// lookup below asks the STRUCT registry for a name only the ENUM registry holds. It took the compiler
		// down at the panic below while blaming the declaration sweep for a disagreement that never happened —
		// `Self{}` and `Toggle{}` inside `enum Toggle`'s own body, both of them.
		//
		// A positioned refusal, because the source can perfectly well write it (⚠ **and BOTH reference
		// compilers CRASH on it** — the bootstrap with an unhandled `IrEnumType`→`IrStructType` cast inside
		// `ParseStructLiteral`, reported as an internal `E9001`; so there is no oracle spelling to match here
		// and the message names the cure instead).
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **THIS ARM ANCHORS AT THE TYPE TOKEN AND MUST NOT BE MOVED ONTO `structLiteralBraceInteriorToken`
		// — IT IS THE ONE ARM DEMONSTRABLY REACHED WITH NO BRACE AT ALL.** A bare `Self` inside an enum body
		// arrives through `parsePrimary`'s `selfPrimary` route, which routes on the token alone with no `{`
		// lookahead, and lands here: MEASURED, `let x = Self` in `enum Color`'s own method answers
		// `E2015 …:6:11: Unsupported: a struct literal naming `enum`/`union` `Color` …`. Routing it through
		// the brace reader would turn that program into a compiler panic. The anchor differing from E3076's
		// two arms above is therefore a consequence of the ROUTE, not a spec pin this arm happens to match.
		if self.enclosingTypeIsEnum 'enumBody'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a struct literal naming `enum`/`union` `{String.from(typeName)}` — an enum/union declares no fields to write; a value of it is a CASE (`{String.from(typeName)}.<case>`, or `Self.<case>` from inside its own body)", line: typeTok.line, column: typeTok.column)
		end 'enumBody'

		match self.signatures.structOf(typeName) 'declared'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			// Unreachable: `namesEnclosingType` held and the enclosing declaration is a `type`, so this IS
			// the struct currently being declared — and the declaration sweep recorded every `type` in the
			// program before this file's parse began. A miss means the sweep and the real parse disagree
			// about what a type declaration is, which is the drift `ProgramSignatures.declaredOutcome`
			// panics on for a constant, stated here for a type.
			undeclared then panic("requireConstructible: `type {String.from(typeName)}` is being parsed right now, but the declaration sweep never recorded it — `recordScannedType` and `parseTypeDeclaration` disagree about what opens a type declaration")
		end 'declared'
	end 'requireConstructible'

	// The `name: value` pairs inside the braces, into the LAYOUT-parallel columns. The caller has
	// consumed `{`; this leaves the cursor on `}`.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY FIELD IS LABELLED, INCLUDING THE FIRST — which is the opposite of a CALL's rule**
	// (`consumeArgLabel`: the first argument is positional, E2052 if labelled). They are two
	// syntaxes, not one: a call's arguments are ordered by the DECLARATION and the first needs no
	// name to find its place, while a struct literal names slots outright. So this reads its own
	// labels rather than reusing the call's, and `slotCallArgs` is not involved — there is nothing
	// to reorder, because the label IS the destination.
	function parseStructLiteralFields(func IrFunction, layout StructLayout, fieldValues ValueIdArray, filled BoolArray, fieldBareLocal BoolArray) throws ParseError
		// A struct literal may span lines — `Self{` then one `field: value` per line (the spec writes big
		// constructors this way). Newlines are skipped at each field boundary: after the `{` (here), after a
		// field's value (before the `,`/`}` test), and after the `,` (before the next field name). A single-line
		// literal has no newline to skip, so its token stream — and its codegen — is unchanged.
		self.skipNewlines()
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightBrace) 'noFields'
			return
		end 'noFields'

		// ⭐ **THE VALUES ALREADY STORED INTO AN OWNING FIELD OF THIS LITERAL (P1.6-B2 double-free fix).** A
		// field that takes its OWN reference may hold a value a second field holds too — `Self{a: v, b: v}` over
		// a concrete managed `v` increfs per slot and is released per slot (the 2026-08-12 ruling). What is
		// still refused is a repeat at a field that INHERITS this frame's single reference, which would drop it
		// twice; `rejectDoubleOwningStore` asks that through the gate the ARGUMENT guard asks too.
		var movedOwningValues = ValueIdArray.create()

		while true 'fieldLoop'
			let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.colon)

			let index = try self.requireFieldIndex(layout, fieldTok: nameTok)

			let alreadyFilled = try filled.get(index) otherwise panic("parseStructLiteralFields: filled.get OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
			if alreadyFilled 'duplicate'
				throw ParseError.duplicateFieldInitializer(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
			end 'duplicate'

			// `parseValueExpression` (the climber plus a trailing ternary), not `parseExpression`, for
			// `parseCallArgs`' reason: a field's value is parsed INSIDE an expression that has already
			// seeded `currentBlock`, and re-seeding it would discard a short-circuit an earlier field
			// performed — while `T{f: a if c else b}` still needs the ternary. The value's start position is
			// captured first so `rhsWasBareLocalReference` can decide, after the value is parsed, whether
			// it was a bare local — a managed field's move-in poisons that source.
			let valueStart = self.pos
			let parsed = try self.parseValueExpression(func)
			let fieldType = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
			// The COERCED value is what fills the slot — `Self{v: 3}` into a `float` field stores the
			// widened f64, not the integer's bytes. A widening only ever produces a fresh scalar, so the
			// ownership questions below still read the value the source actually named.
			let value = try self.requireFieldType(layout, index: index, fieldType: fieldType, value: parsed, anchor: nameTok)
			try self.rejectDoubleOwningStore(movedOwningValues, value: parsed, valueStart: valueStart)

			// ⭐ **ESCAPE (P1.5-A2b): a capturing closure may not fill a struct field at CONSTRUCTION either** —
			// a `Self{op: closure}` box is the same heap slot `x.op = closure` writes (one carries only the
			// code pointer, not the env), and construction bypasses `emitFieldWrite`, so the escape gate must be
			// asked HERE too or the closure reaches lowering and panics on its unresolved env. Same message and
			// route as the field-WRITE site, positioned at the field name.
			try self.rejectEscapingClosure(value, message: "cannot store a closure that captures in field '{String.from(nameTok.value)}' of '{String.from(layout.name)}': {CapturingClosureEscapeSuffix}", tok: nameTok)

			try fieldValues.set(index, value: value) otherwise panic("parseStructLiteralFields: fieldValues.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
			try filled.set(index, value: true) otherwise panic("parseStructLiteralFields: filled.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
			try fieldBareLocal.set(index, value: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart)) otherwise panic("parseStructLiteralFields: fieldBareLocal.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")

			// AFTER `rhsWasBareLocalReference` reads `[valueStart, self.pos)` — skipping here first would widen
			// that window past the single-identifier value and silently mis-decide a managed field's move-in.
			self.skipNewlines()
			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endFields'
				break
			end 'endFields'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'fieldLoop'
	end 'parseStructLiteralFields'

	// A field initializer / assignment must agree with the field's DECLARED type. A SCALAR field takes the
	// `checkDeclaredType` coercion-site rule (the same one a call argument, a `return` and an assignment
	// ask). A MANAGED field (a `String` or a `struct`) takes the tag-AND-name check `requirePayloadArgType`
	// uses for a union payload — a struct field's declared type is `named(<Struct>)` (unresolved), which the
	// tag path would read as `int` (`typeTagName(named)`), so it MUST classify first (`classifyUnionPayload`
	// resolves it to `managed(structRef)`) rather than compare the raw tag.
	//
	// It is the parser's to check and not SemanticCheck's, unlike a call ARGUMENT: a call's callee
	// may be declared in another file, but a struct literal's layout is already in hand here — the
	// parser had to have it to know the field exists at all.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT RETURNS THE VALUE THE FIELD WANTS, AND EVERY CALLER REBINDS.** A scalar field is a coercion
	// site like any other, so an int meeting a declared `float` widens (`assignableValue`) — and the
	// promoted id is a DIFFERENT SSA value from the one that was parsed. All three callers (the struct
	// literal, the field write, the self-field write) once stored the value they already held, so
	// `Self{v: 3}` into a float field wrote the integer's raw bytes into an f64 slot and read back
	// 1.5e-323. Answering with the coerced value is what makes that unwriteable rather than merely fixed
	// at three sites: the check and the conversion are ONE call, in ONE place, for all three doors.
	//
	// A MANAGED field returns its value untouched — there is no scalar widening on a pointer, and the
	// move-in the callers perform is a separate decision made downstream.
	function requireFieldType(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, fieldType MaxonType, value ValueId, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: fieldType, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		// ⭐⭐ **AN INTERFACE-TYPED FIELD ASKS ITS OWN QUESTION (P1.7a-existentials slice 2)** — it is the one
		// field whose slot has TWO words, so what it admits is decided by which values carry both
		// (`widenValueIntoExistentialField`), not by the storage-type comparison below. Routed out FIRST
		// because that comparison would otherwise be asked with a one-word storage type it has no answer for.
		if payloadClassIsExistential(class) 'existentialField'
			return try self.widenValueIntoExistentialField(layout, index: index, fieldType: fieldType, value: value, anchor: anchor)
		end 'existentialField'
		if payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'managed'
			try self.requireManagedSlotValueMatches(class, declared: fieldType, value: value, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.field, placeName: self.fieldNameAt(layout, index: index), placeOwner: layout.name, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.assignedPlace, anchor: anchor)
			return value
		end 'managed'

		// The SAME verdict-plus-coercion the assignment doors ask, through the `field` door — which is what it always
		// was, spelled out a second time: the four `DeclaredTypeMatch` arms that stood here were a
		// character-for-character copy of `requireAssignable`'s, and a rule with two homes drifts.
		let stored = try self.assignableValue(anchor, value: value, declared: maxonTypeTag(fieldType), declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(fieldType), door: FunctionTypeDoor.field, placeOwner: layout.name, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.assignedPlace)
		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE IDENTITY THAT VERDICT CANNOT SEE — the door `parseAssignment` has had since OPEN #54
		// and this one did not.** `assignableValue`'s verdict is tag-based, and a payload-free ENUM field is
		// `named` + SCALAR, so it takes the arm above rather than the managed one: `checkDeclaredType(named,
		// named)` AGREES for any two enums, and `h.c = Shade.light` on a `Color` field compiled and stored
		// (MEASURED 2026-08-06, BATCH32 — `requireSlotAggregateIdentity` carries the full note). All three
		// field doors funnel through here, so the literal and the two writes are closed by the one check.
		try self.requireSlotAggregateIdentity(fieldType, value: value, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.field, placeName: self.fieldNameAt(layout, index: index), placeOwner: layout.name, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.assignedPlace, anchor: anchor)
		// ⭐ **THE FIELD'S DECLARED RANGE BINDS WHAT IS STORED IN IT (P1.9).** The site is recorded on the
		// COERCED value, not the parsed one: that is the value the slot receives, and for a widening door
		// (an int meeting a declared float) it is a different SSA id. All three field doors — the struct
		// literal, the field write, the self-field write — funnel through here, so one record covers them.
		//
		// ⚠ **A ranged FLOAT alias field is NOT reached**: `declaredSlotType` re-tags a float alias to bare
		// `float` before `fieldTypeOf` answers, so `declaredAliasOfType` sees no alias and the slot is
		// unchecked. That is a named boundary of this rung — the erasure is `declaredSlotType`'s, whose own
		// comment records it — and not something this door can decide.
		self.recordRangeCheckSite(stored, targetAlias: self.declaredAliasOfType(fieldType), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		return stored
	end 'requireFieldType'

	// ⭐ **REJECT A REPEATED OWNING STORE OF ONE VALUE INTO TWO FIELDS OF ONE LITERAL (P1.6-B2)** — a clean E3102
	// on `Self{a: v, b: v}` instead of a double-free, for the ONE class of value whose store still INHERITS this
	// frame's reference rather than taking its own. Which class that is, is not this door's to decide:
	// `repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree` is the shared gate, and the ARGUMENT guard asks it too. It runs
	// at PARSE, before any move-in, so the value is still live at both references; `valueStart`'s token names
	// and positions the second use.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO REFUSE AN OWNED CONCRETE MANAGED VALUE, AND THAT ARM IS RETRACTED (⚖ user ruling,
	// 2026-08-12).** Its whole justification was *"shv2 is move-only (no `__mm_incref`), so a single owned value
	// cannot be OWNED by two destinations"* — which stopped being true the day a durable store started taking
	// its own reference (`moveManagedValueInto`). `Self{a: v, b: v}` over a concrete managed `v` now increfs
	// twice, so the record ends at refcount 3 against two slots plus the source binding and is released exactly
	// three times. Keeping the refusal would have meant a diagnostic whose stated reason the file next door
	// contradicts, which is this project's signature defect rather than a conservative choice.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND IT USED TO ASK ABOUT THE FIELD'S DECLARED TYPE, WHERE ITS SIBLING ASKED ABOUT THE VALUE — TWO
	// ANSWERS TO ONE QUESTION (found at review).** An opaque field holding a value the descriptor CAN retain
	// (`opaqueSlotTakesItsOwnReference`) takes one reference per slot and was refused here while the identical
	// repeat at two consuming ARGUMENTS was accepted; a substituted-concrete value in an opaque field was
	// refused for the same non-reason. Both doors now read the value, through one predicate.
	function rejectDoubleOwningStore(movedOwningValues ValueIdArray, value ValueId, valueStart TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		if not self.repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree(value) 'everyDestinationTakesItsOwnReference'
			return
		end 'everyDestinationTakesItsOwnReference'
		if self.isRepeatedOwningMove(movedOwningValues, value: value) 'doubleMove'
			let valueTok = self.tokenAt(valueStart)
			throw ParseError.useAfterMove(String.from(valueTok.value), line: valueTok.line, column: valueTok.column)
		end 'doubleMove'
	end 'rejectDoubleOwningStore'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE "this value has ALREADY reached an owning destination in this construct" LEDGER (P1.7 slice
	// 3a).** Returns true when `value` was ALREADY recorded in `movedOwningValues` (the caller throws its own
	// positioned E3102), else records it and returns false. Shared by the STRUCT-LITERAL guard
	// (`rejectDoubleOwningStore` — `Self{a: v, b: v}`) and the CALL-ARGUMENT double-consume guard
	// (`transferConsumedArgs` — `Pair.create(box, b: box)`), so the two sites cannot drift on what a REPEAT is —
	// and, since review, they no longer drift on WHICH VALUES it is asked of either: both gate on the one shared
	// `repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree`. What each caller still keeps is its own diagnostic name and
	// position — the name is resolved only on a repeat, where the value is always a bound local (a temporary is
	// minted by one expression and cannot reach two destinations), never an anonymous temp.
	//
	// ⛔ **BOTH GATES USED TO ADMIT A CONCRETE OWNED MANAGED VALUE, ON THE PREMISE THAT shv2 HAS NO
	// `__mm_incref` — RETRACTED 2026-08-12.** A concrete durable store now takes its own reference, so two of
	// them are two references and nothing is dropped twice. What is left is the opaque case alone, whose reason
	// is a MISSING DESCRIPTOR rather than a missing instruction.
	function isRepeatedOwningMove(movedOwningValues ValueIdArray, value ValueId) returns bool
		for prior in movedOwningValues 'eachPrior'
			if prior == value 'doubleMove'
				return true
			end 'doubleMove'
		end 'eachPrior'
		movedOwningValues.push(value)
		return false
	end 'isRepeatedOwningMove'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT MAY STAND IN AN INTERFACE-TYPED FIELD (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).** `emitFieldStore` writes
	// BOTH halves of the fat pointer and reads the witness off the value's own column (`witnessOfValue`), so
	// every value reaching that store must already carry one. Exactly one kind does: an EXISTENTIAL, which
	// forwards the witness it was given.
	//
	// Same-interface is EXACT, and deliberately not the `extends`-transitive rule: a `Derived` witness
	// addresses `Derived`'s table, whose slot ORDER is not `Base`'s, so forwarding one into a `Base` slot is a
	// wrong function pointer rather than a widening (`aggregateNameOf`'s existential arm carries the
	// measurement). `requireManagedValueMatches` decides that, over the DECLARED interface type — which is why
	// this door does not restate the comparison.
	//
	// ⛔ **A CONCRETE CONFORMER IS REFUSED HERE, AND THE REASON IS A TIER BOUNDARY RATHER THAN A MISSING
	// WIDTH.** Widening one needs its witness table NAMED at the Maxon tier — the call ARGUMENT never has to,
	// because lowering reads the CALLEE's declared parameter types and materializes it there
	// (`appendExistentialWitnessArgs`), and a field store has no callee to read a signature off. Naming it
	// here would need two things this tier does not have: a Maxon op carrying `(conformer, interface)`, and
	// the conformance VERDICT — `ConformanceCheck.valueWidensToInterface` takes the whole `Project`, and the
	// parser writes only into a `FileParseArtifact` (it holds no `Project` at all). Without that verdict a
	// non-conformer would reach `ensureWitnessTable` and PANIC in `witnessSlotImpl`, which is the one
	// unacceptable outcome. So it is refused with the position of the value and the workaround the ABI
	// already supports — pass it through a named function's interface PARAMETER, which is what every widening
	// in the corpus does.
	function widenValueIntoExistentialField(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, fieldType MaxonType, value ValueId, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'aConcreteConformer'
			throw self.existentialFieldStoreUnsupported(layout, index: index, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'aConcreteConformer'

		try self.requireManagedValueMatches(fieldType, isString: false, value: value, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.field, placeName: self.fieldNameAt(layout, index: index), placeOwner: layout.name, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.assignedPlace, anchor: anchor)
		return value
	end 'widenValueIntoExistentialField'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE SPELLING OF "NOTHING THIS TIER CAN PRODUCE MAY FILL AN INTERFACE-TYPED FIELD" (W116
	// review)** — raised by the BODY's literal, where the value is a concrete conformer, and by a TOP-LEVEL
	// struct construction, where there is no value at all because a constant evaluator mints none. The two
	// doors have different reasons for arriving and the identical reason for being refused, which is the
	// whole of what the sentence says; written twice it would be one wall described two ways, free to drift
	// into two.
	//
	// It takes a POSITION rather than a token because its second caller has no token to offer: a top-level
	// binding is refused at its declared NAME, which the decl carries as a line and a column.
	function existentialFieldStoreUnsupported(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) returns ParseError
		return ParseError.unsupported("storing a value of a CONCRETE type into the interface-typed field '{self.fieldSiteName(layout, index: index)}' — {ExistentialIsFatPointerReason}, and the second word is the address of the conformer's witness table, which only a widening SITE can name. A field store has no callee whose declared parameter types could name it, unlike a call argument. Pass the value to a named function taking the interface as a PARAMETER and store THAT parameter (which arrives already widened), or declare the field at a concrete type", line: line, column: column)
	end 'existentialFieldStoreUnsupported'

	// The `<Struct>.<field>` label a managed-field type mismatch names.
	// The declared name of one field — the single `get` that both the dotted prose name below and the
	// assignment sentence's place are built from (`assignedPlacePhrase`, which the field doors reach through
	// `placeName`). It is the NAME and not the phrase, because the sentence is worded only where the voice
	// quotes a place at all.
	function fieldNameAt(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns ByteArray
		return try layout.fieldNames.get(index) otherwise panic("fieldNameAt: fieldNames.get OOB at {index}")
	end 'fieldNameAt'

	function fieldSiteName(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns String
		return "{String.from(layout.name)}.{String.from(self.fieldNameAt(layout, index: index))}"
	end 'fieldSiteName'

	// ⭐⭐ **A CLASSIFIED MANAGED SLOT'S THREE-PART IDENTITY CHECK, IN ONE PLACE — the value type, the String
	// flag and the check itself.** A struct FIELD (`requireFieldType`) and a CONTAINER ELEMENT
	// (`requireContainerElementType`) ask the identical question of a `PayloadClass`: may this value fill this
	// slot? They differ only in the three things a diagnostic needs — the label, the voice and the anchor — so
	// those are threaded and the derivation is not.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT WAS SPELLED AT BOTH SITES, AND THE PAIR DEMONSTRABLY DRIFTS: W55 HAD TO CORRECT BOTH COPIES FROM
	// `payloadStorageOf` TO `payloadValueTypeOf` IN ONE DIFF.** That is the failure mode this door removes —
	// two independently-correct-looking lines that must agree about which of the two answers
	// `SignatureIndex`'s width-vs-identity split gives, where getting it wrong is a nonsense tag mismatch on a
	// slot the language admits (`struct-managed-field.md`) rather than a compile error.
	//
	// ⚠ **`requirePayloadArgType`'s ARMS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT ROUTED THROUGH HERE.** It is itself a `match`
	// over `PayloadClass`, so each arm reads the case's OWN fields — which is what lets the `nestedUnion` arm
	// state `isString: false` as a fact about union boxes rather than deriving it — and folding it in would
	// trade a legible exhaustive match for a call that re-derives what the arm already destructured.
	function requireManagedSlotValueMatches(class PayloadClass, declared MaxonType, value ValueId, placeDoor FunctionTypeDoor, placeName ByteArray, placeOwner ByteArray, voice SlotMismatchVoice, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		// `payloadValueTypeOf`, NEVER `payloadStorageOf` (W55): the question is what a value filling the slot must
		// BE, and for a boxed-union slot those two parted company when the payload slot's WIDTH became the machine
		// word a box is. Asked of the width, a `Ty` box meets `integer`; asked of the identity, it meets `Ty` —
		// and the identity is also what `containerSlotValueType` types the value a read hands BACK, so a slot
		// admits exactly what it can return. `SignatureIndex.payloadStorageOf` is the one home of that split.
		try self.requireManagedValueMatches(payloadValueTypeOf(class, declared: declared), isString: payloadClassIsString(class), value: value, placeDoor: placeDoor, placeName: placeName, placeOwner: placeOwner, voice: voice, anchor: anchor)
	end 'requireManagedSlotValueMatches'

	// ⭐ **A MANAGED VALUE MUST BE THE MATCHING HEAP KIND** — the ONE check a struct FIELD and a union
	// PAYLOAD share: a `String` slot takes a `string`; a `struct` slot takes a `structRef` of the SAME struct
	// name (a pointer of the wrong struct would pass a tag-only check). The names are compared through this
	// file's interner, the one the classifier's `structRef` id and the value's own id were both minted from.
	// `assignedPlace` is the `<Type>.<field>` / `<Union>.<case>(<field>)` label the ASSIGNMENT voice quotes;
	// the argument voice names the mutator's parameter instead, which `slotMismatchError` decides.
	function requireManagedValueMatches(storage MaxonType, isString bool, value ValueId, placeDoor FunctionTypeDoor, placeName ByteArray, placeOwner ByteArray, voice SlotMismatchVoice, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let got = self.tagOf(value)
		let wantTag = maxonTypeTag(storage)
		if got != wantTag 'tagMismatch'
			// ⚠ **`got` NAMES THE TYPE, NOT THE TAG'S CLASS WORD** (review, 2026-08-04). It read
			// `typeTagName(got)`, so this arm answered `got 'struct'` for the very struct the arm below
			// answers `got 'Other'` for — one door, two sentences, sixty lines apart in `specs-shv2/arrays.md`.
			// The bootstrap names it (`expected 'String', got 'Item'`), and naming the type IS the ruling.
			throw self.slotMismatchError(voice, placeDoor: placeDoor, placeName: placeName, placeOwner: placeOwner, got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), gotTag: got, resolvedExpected: typeTagName(wantTag), declared: wantTag, declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(storage), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'tagMismatch'
		if isString 'stringOk'
			return
		end 'stringOk'

		// The tag check above (`got != wantTag`) is EXACT, so a value reaching the identity question from
		// HERE shares its tag with the slot — and the only tag a struct/union storage and a value can share
		// while disagreeing on aggregate identity is the overloaded `named` (a boxed union slot met by a
		// ranged-int alias value, or the reverse). `namedAggregatesConflict` (extended by #59) catches that
		// scalar-vs-aggregate case as well as two distinct aggregates.
		try self.requireSlotAggregateIdentity(storage, value: value, placeDoor: placeDoor, placeName: placeName, placeOwner: placeOwner, voice: voice, anchor: anchor)
	end 'requireManagedValueMatches'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE AGGREGATE-IDENTITY HALF OF A SLOT STORE, ASKED BY THE MANAGED FIELD DOOR AND BY THE SCALAR
	// ONE — because a `named` ENUM FIELD IS SCALAR, AND IT WAS THE COERCION SITE THAT NEVER ASKED.**
	// `aggregatesConflict`'s header names the parser's five symmetric doors, and the fifth is the
	// "union-payload / struct-literal store" — which reached only the MANAGED path. A payload-free enum's
	// value is its i64 tag, so an enum-typed field is not managed and fell through `requireFieldType`'s
	// scalar arm, where `assignableValue`'s verdict is `checkDeclaredType(named, named)` — and that AGREES
	// for any two enums.
	//
	// **MEASURED 2026-08-06 (BATCH32), on `type Holder` with `export var c as Color`:** `h.c = Shade.light`
	// and `Self{c: Shade.light}` both COMPILED and stored, where the bootstrap oracle refuses both
	// (`error E3005 … to field 'c' of 'Holder', which holds 'Color'`). The stored tag then reads back under
	// the other enum's name — `specs/assignment.md`'s own `error.retype-enum-field-errors` and
	// `error.wrong-enum-in-struct-literal-field-errors` are the pair that names it, and it is the identical
	// defect `parseAssignment`'s door was given this check for one slot up (OPEN #54/#59).
	//
	// It is EXTRACTED rather than copied for the reason `requireFieldType`'s own header gives about the four
	// `DeclaredTypeMatch` arms that once stood there: a rule with two homes drifts, and this one already had
	// two readers before it had a name.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO NAMES DECIDE THE VERDICT AND DO NOT SPELL IT** — `parseAssignment`'s aggregate-retype
	// check carries the identical note for the identical reason (user ruling, 2026-08-04): an aggregate
	// name is the IDENTITY two values are compared on, and a generic instance's identity is a canonical
	// mint no source line holds. Both sides render through `displayTypeNameOf` so this door and the
	// ARGUMENT door spell one type one way — and where the DECLARED side is the scalar half of the
	// mismatch, that renderer prints its primitive rather than the empty name the verdict was decided on.
	//
	// ⚠ It reads the PARSED value at both call sites and never a coerced one, exactly as
	// `parseAssignment`'s check does: a widening only ever produces a float scalar, whose aggregate name is
	// empty either way, so the question is the same one before and after the coercion.
	function requireSlotAggregateIdentity(storage MaxonType, value ValueId, placeDoor FunctionTypeDoor, placeName ByteArray, placeOwner ByteArray, voice SlotMismatchVoice, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let got = self.tagOf(value)
		let wantTag = maxonTypeTag(storage)
		// ⛔⛔ **AN OPAQUE `T` SLOT IS NOT EXEMPT FROM THIS QUESTION, AND EXEMPTING IT WAS A WILD WRITE
		// (BATCH32 review).** This door briefly opened `if wantTag == typeParameter then return`, because the
		// unsubstituted `T` answers an EMPTY aggregate name and so conflicted with the very `Label` a
		// `Box with Label` exists to accept. The argument written beside it — *"the concrete identity is pinned
		// at the CALL SITE by `SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt`"* — is FALSE, and falsifiable in one
		// line: **a field WRITE has no call site**. `checkTypeParameterArgsAt` is reached only from
		// `checkTypeParameterArgs` on a CALL's arguments, so `b.value = Other.create(99)` is pinned by nothing.
		// **MEASURED 2026-08-06:** it compiled and exited **0xC0000005**, where the bootstrap refuses it.
		//
		// ⇒ the cure is to SUBSTITUTE rather than to skip: `storedFieldType` binds `T` from the box before the
		// check is asked, so a concrete instance arrives here already spelling `Label` and the two legal
		// `generic-types` cases pass on the identity they actually have. What still reaches this line opaque is
		// the SHARED BODY, where `self.value = v` meets a `T`-typed value — and there BOTH names are empty, so
		// `namedAggregatesConflict` defers to the caller's tag check with no special case needed. A CONCRETE
		// value in that body (`self.value = Label.create(77)` inside `Box uses T`) is a genuine mismatch the
		// shared body cannot justify for every instantiation, and it is refused here: unguarded it had compiled
		// and exited **101, a leak**, printing a box pointer as an integer.
		let wantName = self.aggregateNameOf(wantTag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(storage))
		let gotName = self.aggregateNameOf(got, nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
		if self.signatures.aggregatesConflict(wantName, expectedTag: wantTag, expectedNameId: typeNameIdOf(storage), got: gotName, gotTag: got, gotNameId: self.valueNameId(value)) 'nameMismatch'
			throw self.slotMismatchError(voice, placeDoor: placeDoor, placeName: placeName, placeOwner: placeOwner, got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), gotTag: got, resolvedExpected: self.displayTypeNameOf(wantTag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(storage)), declared: wantTag, declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(storage), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'nameMismatch'
	end 'requireSlotAggregateIdentity'

	// ⭐ **FILL EVERY OMITTED SLOT THAT DECLARES A DEFAULT** — `export var value = 0` + `Self{}`.
	//
	// **A LITERAL-PROVIDED VALUE ALWAYS BEATS THE DEFAULT**, and `filled` is what says so: this only
	// ever looks at slots the literal did not write, so an override is not a race between two stores
	// — the default's `literal` op is never emitted at all. That is the rule
	// `field-initialization.md` states ("A literal-provided value always overrides a declared
	// default") and `literal-overrides-default` pins.
	//
	// The literal is minted at the field's STORAGE type, not its declared one: `emitLiteral` uses
	// one `MaxonType` for both the value's tag and the op's, and `maxonTypeToStdType` PANICS on a
	// `named` — so a field declared `as Integer = 7` would otherwise carry an alias into an op that
	// must be concrete by lowering. Nothing reads this value's tag (it flows straight into the
	// store), and `emitFieldStore` independently derives the same storage type for the slot, so the
	// two agree by construction.
	//
	// The span is the LITERAL's anchor — the `Self` token — and not the field declaration's, which
	// may be in another file entirely: a `SourceRange` is an offset into THIS file's bytes.
	function applyFieldDefaults(layout StructLayout, fieldValues ValueIdArray, filled BoolArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(anchor)
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let wasFilled = try filled.get(i) otherwise panic("applyFieldDefaults: filled.get OOB at {i} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
			if wasFilled 'literalWins'
				continue
			end 'literalWins'

			match layout.fieldDefault(i) 'declared'
				// The literal did not fill this slot and the declaration offers nothing. That is
				// E3086, and `requireAllFieldsInitialized` is what says so — not this function.
				none then continue
				literal(payload, tag) then self.fillFieldDefault(layout, index: i, given: ConstValue.create(payload, tag: tag), fieldValues: fieldValues, filled: filled, range: range)
				helper(helperName) then try self.fillFieldDefaultFromHelper(helperName, index: i, fieldValues: fieldValues, filled: filled, anchor: anchor)
			end 'declared'
		end 'eachField'
	end 'applyFieldDefaults'

	// ⭐⭐ **EMIT THE CALL THAT PRODUCES ONE FIELD'S ARBITRARY DEFAULT (S2f)** — an ORDINARY `MaxonOp.call`
	// to the nullary helper the declaration synthesized, exactly as an omitted ARGUMENT emits one
	// (`fillArgsFromParamDefaults`), and by the same door for the same reasons: `emitCall` is what OWNS the
	// result (`mintCallResult` → `mintOwnedCallResult` → `trackOwnedTemp`), so a managed default arrives
	// enrolled as this statement's owned temporary and is then CONSUMED into the box by the store loop.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT MUST NOT BE `emitRuntimeCall`.** That door mints its result type by hand and enrols nothing,
	// so the box would end up holding a value nobody owns — a leak or a double free, decided by whatever
	// the field's type turns out to be.
	//
	// ⭐ **EACH CONSTRUCTION GETS ITS OWN VALUE**, which is `field-defaults.fresh-per-construction`'s whole
	// point, and it is a property of emitting a CALL rather than a rule stated anywhere: two `Bag.create()`
	// calls parse two `Self{}` literals, emit two calls, and the helper allocates on each.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ANCHOR IS THE `Self` TOKEN AND NOT THE FIELD'S DECLARATION**, for `applyFieldDefaults`'
	// stated reason: a `SourceRange` is an offset into THIS file's bytes, and it also serves as the call's
	// `rparenTok` because the call has no parentheses in the source at all — nobody wrote it.
	function fillFieldDefaultFromHelper(helperName ByteArray, index FieldIndex, fieldValues ValueIdArray, filled BoolArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let value = try self.emitCall(helperName, anchor: anchor, args: ValueIdArray.create(), argLabels: ByteArrayArray.create(), argRanges: SourceRangeArray.create(), argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: anchor, resultUsed: true, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.fieldDefaultHelper)
		self.recordFieldDefaultValue(index, value: value, fieldValues: fieldValues, filled: filled)
	end 'fillFieldDefaultFromHelper'

	// Record `value` as slot `index`'s value and mark the slot filled — the last two lines of BOTH ways a
	// declared default becomes one (a folded literal, a call to the synthesized helper), so the two columns
	// are written from one place and the out-of-bounds sentence is stated once.
	function recordFieldDefaultValue(index FieldIndex, value ValueId, fieldValues ValueIdArray, filled BoolArray)
		try fieldValues.set(index, value: value) otherwise panic("recordFieldDefaultValue: fieldValues.set OOB at {index} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
		try filled.set(index, value: true) otherwise panic("recordFieldDefaultValue: filled.set OOB at {index} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
	end 'recordFieldDefaultValue'

	// Emit one default's `literal` and record it as slot `index`'s value. Split out so the payload
	// is bound by the match arm that proved there IS one, rather than read back out of the union
	// through an accessor with a `none` case to mishandle.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A FIELD DEFAULT IS COERCED**, and the only place it can be: `readStructFieldInto`
	// records the literal exactly as the source wrote it, because the declaration sweep it shares cannot
	// resolve a ranged-alias field type. Here the whole program IS known — `fieldTypeOf` answers `float`
	// for a `float(low to high)` alias — so a `= 3` against it widens to the f64 bit pattern. Left unpaid
	// it wrote the integer's raw bytes into an f64 slot and read back 1.5e-323.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO QUESTIONS, AND EACH IS ASKED EXACTLY ONCE.** The RESOLVED type says whether the slot is an
	// f64 at all; the RECORDED TAG — inside `constFloatBits`, the shared primitive — says whether putting
	// `given` in it is a conversion or a no-op. That second read is the whole reason the tag is stored:
	// `= 3` and `= 3.0` are two `ParsedInt`s and nothing else once the tag is gone, so no rule anywhere
	// else in the compiler could tell them apart, and none is consulted.
	//
	// ⚠ It does NOT re-ask `widensIntToFloat` on the way in, and that is deliberate rather than an
	// oversight: `constFloatBits` already turns away a float, so a guard naming the literal's tag as well
	// would be a second, REDUNDANT read that merely LOOKS load-bearing — measured, replacing it with a
	// constant changed no test. A condition that cannot fail is worse than no condition, because the next
	// reader trusts it.
	function fillFieldDefault(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, given ConstValue, fieldValues ValueIdArray, filled BoolArray, range SourceRange)
		let declared = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
		let bits = self.constFloatBits(given) if tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(declared)) else given.payload
		let value = self.emitLiteral(bits, valueType: self.fieldStorageType(declared), range: range)
		// A declared default is a value coming to rest in the slot exactly as a written one is, so the
		// field's range binds it (P1.9) — `export var v as Percent = 500` is the same E3005 `Self{v: 500}`
		// gets. It is always a LITERAL, so `InsertRangeChecks` settles it entirely at compile time and the
		// runtime half it is nominally owed is never emitted; it is recorded exactly as the WRITTEN field
		// store is (`requireFieldType`), so the two doors say one thing rather than two.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND THAT SENTENCE IS WHY A LITERAL DEFAULT KEEPS ITS OWN ARM (S2f).** Everything else a
		// field can default to is now a synthesized nullary helper, which is one mechanism where the
		// parameter twin has one too (`SignatureIndex.ParamDefaultInfo` argues at length that a constant
		// fast path is not worth having, and for a PARAMETER it is right). A field's literal is the case
		// that argument does not cover: it is the arm that makes the range check DISAPPEAR. A call result
		// cannot be constant-folded, so routing `= 500` through a helper would emit — in 158 spec files —
		// a runtime range check that costs nothing today. The fast path is not redundant; it is the only
		// path on which `InsertRangeChecks` can see a constant.
		//
		// ⚠ **A4f'S CONTAINMENT ELISION CANNOT FIRE HERE**: `emitLiteral` mints the value two lines up, and a
		// freshly minted literal denotes no ranged alias — so the compile-time half this door exists for is
		// untouched.
		self.recordRangeCheckSite(value, targetAlias: self.declaredAliasOfType(declared), line: range.line, column: range.column)

		self.recordFieldDefaultValue(index, value: value, fieldValues: fieldValues, filled: filled)
	end 'fillFieldDefault'

	// Every declared field must have been given a value — see `parseStructLiteral` for why the
	// allocator's zeroing guarantee makes an omission a WRONG ANSWER rather than a safe default,
	// and `applyFieldDefaults` for the other way a slot comes to have one.
	// Reports the FIRST omitted field, in declaration order, so the message is a function of the
	// type rather than of a hash walk.
	function requireAllFieldsInitialized(layout StructLayout, filled BoolArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let wasFilled = try filled.get(i) otherwise panic("requireAllFieldsInitialized: filled.get OOB at {i} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
			if wasFilled 'given'
				continue
			end 'given'
			let fieldName = try layout.fieldNames.get(i) otherwise panic("requireAllFieldsInitialized: fieldNames.get OOB at {i}")
			throw ParseError.fieldNotInitialized(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(fieldName), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'requireAllFieldsInitialized'

	// Emit the box: allocate, then fill every slot. `fieldValues` is parallel to the LAYOUT.
	//
	// The result is typed `structRef(layout.name)` — which is what makes `let p = Point.create(…)`
	// bind a Point rather than an integer, since the factory returns this value and
	// `parseTypeReference` typed its `returns Self` the same way. The two meet at
	// `parseReturnStatement`'s `checkDeclaredType`, and they agree because both came from the
	// enclosing type's name.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BOX IS ENROLLED AS AN OWNED HEAP TEMPORARY** (`trackOwnedTemp`), exactly as a boxed union
	// construct and an interpolation String are: it sets the provenance bit `valueIsOwnedHeap` reads, so a
	// binding of it drops at scope exit and an UNBOUND `Self{…}` (a struct literal passed as a borrowed
	// call argument) is freed at statement end. A `return Self{…}` moves it back OUT of the pending list
	// (`parseReturnStatement`), so the factory does not drop what it hands to its caller. It is the bit —
	// not the `structRef` tag — that owns the drop, because a borrowed struct PARAMETER also carries the
	// tag and owns nothing.
	// ⭐ **A MANAGED FIELD IS MOVED IN** (`moveInPayload`) — the struct half of static single-owner
	// ownership, the exact mirror of `emitEnumBox`'s payload move. A scalar field is stored as-is; a managed
	// field (a `String` or a nested `struct`) stores its value into the box: a borrowed String literal is
	// promoted to an owned copy, an owned TEMPORARY is adopted out of the pending drops, an owned value a LIVE
	// BINDING keeps is CO-OWNED (the binding stays readable and releases its own reference at scope exit — the
	// 2026-08-12 ruling), and a borrowed struct is CO-OWNED as well (`moveInPayload` — the cross-call consume;
	// it used to be an E2015 refusal until the transitive-consume ruling of 2026-08-04). Whichever arm ran, the
	// box holds exactly ONE reference per field and its `__destruct_<Struct>` cascade releases exactly that.
	function emitStructLiteral(layout StructLayout, fieldValues ValueIdArray, fieldBareLocal BoolArray, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐⭐ **A FUSED WRAPPER'S LITERAL IS NOT A STRUCT LITERAL, AND THE LOOP BELOW WOULD CORRUPT IT.**
		// `type String`'s `managed` is embedded whole at offset 0 (`StructLayout.isInlineManagedField`), so
		// offset 0 is the RECORD'S OWN `buffer@0` — the ordinary field store would put a `__ManagedMemory`
		// POINTER there and every later read would take that pointer for a byte buffer. The wrapper's record
		// is built by its own producer instead.
		if layout.conformsToBuiltinManagedWrapper() 'fusedWrapper'
			return try self.emitFusedWrapperLiteral(layout, fieldValues: fieldValues, anchor: anchor, range: range)
		end 'fusedWrapper'

		let box = self.emitOwnedStructBox(layout, range: range)

		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let value = try fieldValues.get(i) otherwise panic("emitStructLiteral: fieldValues.get OOB at {i} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
			let bareLocal = try fieldBareLocal.get(i) otherwise panic("emitStructLiteral: fieldBareLocal.get OOB at {i} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
			let fieldType = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: i, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
			let class = classifyUnionPayload(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: fieldType, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
			let stored = try self.moveInPayload(class, arg: value, argBareLocal: bareLocal, owner: box, anchor: anchor, range: range)
			self.emitFieldStore(box, layout: layout, index: i, value: stored, range: range)
		end 'eachField'

		return box
	end 'emitStructLiteral'

	// The ONE writer of `recordsOwningOpaqueSlots` (W41-opaque) — `markRebindableSlotRead`'s twin, for the
	// identical copy-on-write reason and through the same one home for it (`privateValueMarkSet`).
	function markRecordOwningOpaqueSlots(box ValueId)
		self.recordsOwningOpaqueSlots = privateValueMarkSet(self.recordsOwningOpaqueSlots, anchor: sharedEmptyRecordsOwningOpaqueSlots)
		self.recordsOwningOpaqueSlots.insert(box)
	end 'markRecordOwningOpaqueSlots'

	// The ONE writer of `recordsHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlots` — its twin above, through the same copy-on-write
	// home and for the identical reason.
	function markRecordHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlot(box ValueId)
		self.recordsHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlots = privateValueMarkSet(self.recordsHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlots, anchor: sharedEmptyReferencedOpaqueRecords)
		self.recordsHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlots.insert(box)
	end 'markRecordHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlot'

	// ⭐ **ALLOCATE THE HEAP RECORD A STRUCT-SHAPED LITERAL LIVES IN, AND ENROL IT AS THIS STATEMENT'S OWNED
	// TEMPORARY** — the three steps that MUST happen together (the size literal read off the layout, the
	// `__mm_alloc`, and `trackOwnedTemp`, which sets the provenance bit AND the statement's drop obligation
	// in one call). Shared by `emitStructLiteral` (a `Self{…}` and a tuple literal) and `copyTupleValue` (the
	// value-semantics copy, P1.10), because a second copy of the sequence is how a change to what a record
	// costs — a header, an alignment, a different allocator door — reaches one producer and not the other,
	// and a box allocated without the enrolment simply leaks (exit 101).
	// ⭐⭐ **BUILD A FUSED WRAPPER'S RECORD — `String{managed: X, singleByteGraphemesFlag: F}` and
	// `Character{managed: X}` (THE ENVELOPE COLLAPSE, W17).** The result IS the 56-byte byte record, so its
	// value tag is `string`/`character` and NOT `structRef`: that is the whole of what closed
	// `E3005 Cannot return 'struct' from function declared to return 'String'` at `stdlib/String.maxon:115`,
	// and it is not a cast — a corpus `String` and shv2's builtin `String` are one type with one layout,
	// which is what convergence MEANS.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FLAG IS OPTIONAL AND ITS ABSENCE IS `Character`'s CASE, NOT AN ERROR.** `type Character`
	// declares `managed` alone, so there is no flag value to pass and the bytes must be classified instead —
	// which is exactly `__str_from_bytes`. Two producers, chosen by whether the declaration HAS the field,
	// rather than one producer with a sentinel meaning "scan it yourself".
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **`BuiltinArrayLiteral` NEEDS NO PRODUCER AT ALL, WHICH IS WHY IT IS AN ARM AND NOT A THIRD
	// `__str_*` ENTRY (W57).** The two `String` producers exist because a `String` record is BUILT from its
	// buffer: `__str_from_bytes` classifies the bytes and writes its own answer at `@48`, `__str_of_buffer`
	// writes the flag it was handed there. An `Array` record has nothing of the sort to write — since the
	// envelope collapse `Array with T` and `__ManagedMemory with T` are ONE `GenericInstanceId`
	// (`SignatureIndex.canonicalGenericBaseName`), so `Array{managed: m}` is not a construction but an
	// IDENTITY on the record, and the only work left is the pair `parseArrayInitStatic` already does for the
	// same identity written as a static call: a FRESH `ValueId` (which is what flips the surface, since the
	// ARRAY surface is the ABSENCE of `markBufferSurface`) and a refcount that balances.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE REFUSAL BELOW STILL COVERS EVERY OTHER `BuiltinArrayLiteral` CONFORMER, BECAUSE THE HAZARD
	// IT NAMES IS REAL FOR THEM AND VACUOUS HERE.** That hazard is that construction DISCARDS every declared
	// field the fused record has no slot for. `stdlib/Array.maxon:104-106` declares exactly ONE field and it
	// IS the record, so there is nothing to discard — which is precisely what
	// `ProgramSignatures.firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer` tests, structurally, rather than trusting the name alone.
	// A conformer that declares a second field meets the unchanged sentence, because for it the sentence is
	// still true.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE GATE IS THE FIELD, NOT THE NAME, AND THAT IS ARR5's HALF OF THIS ARM.** It read
	// `isArrayBaseName(layout.name)` and then admitted on a sole `managed` field — a question about the
	// field's NAME and the declaration's MARKER, never about its TYPE. So `type Array uses Element
	// implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` with `var managed as Num` took this arm and `coOwnAggregateAsTemp`
	// handed the raw `Num` back AS the array. See `ProgramSignatures.declarationIsTheManagedRecord` for
	// the two measurements. Keyed on the field, such a declaration never reaches the identity at all.
	function emitFusedWrapperLiteral(layout StructLayout, fieldValues ValueIdArray, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer(layout) 'theBufferRecord'
			try self.requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord(layout, anchor: anchor)
			let buffer = try fieldValues.get(0) otherwise panic("emitFusedWrapperLiteral: fieldValues.get OOB at 0 — `requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord` established a sole field and the column is sized to the layout's field count")
			return self.coOwnAggregateAsTemp(buffer, range: range)
		end 'theBufferRecord'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S OWN RECORD IS THE SAME IDENTITY, RETYPED (W189).** `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s
		// `init(managed ElementMemory)` writes `return Self{managed: managed}`, and a `Vector`'s runtime
		// representation IS an `Array`'s — the same 48-byte fused record, dropped by the same
		// `__managed_decref` — so there is nothing to construct here either. What the array arm above cannot do
		// is hand the value back with the RIGHT identity: the buffer is an `Array with Element` and `Self` is a
		// `Vector`, whose gid carries the SIZE as a second coordinate. So the identity is taken and the value is
		// retyped to the declaration's own instance, exactly as `parseVectorFromConstruction` retypes the array
		// literal it is handed.
		//
		// ⚠ **STDLIB SOURCE ONLY, AND THE GATE IS PROVENANCE RATHER THAN SHAPE.** A USER `type Vector uses
		// Element implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` is shaped identically and must go on meeting
		// `requireNameIsNotABuiltinContainerBase` below — `W112` measured what admitting it does: a fixed-size
		// three-element vector grown to nine through `push`/`resize`, compiling clean and printing the wrong
		// answer. The corpus declaration is not a second conformer competing with the compiler's container; it
		// is the DECLARATION OF that container, which is what listing the module means.
		if self.isStdlibSource and self.signatures.declarationIsASizedContainerRecord(layout) 'theSizedContainersOwnRecord'
			try self.requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord(layout, anchor: anchor)
			let sizedBuffer = try fieldValues.get(0) otherwise panic("emitFusedWrapperLiteral: fieldValues.get OOB at 0 — `requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord` established a sole field and the column is sized to the layout's field count")
			let record = self.coOwnAggregateAsTemp(sizedBuffer, range: range)
			self.retypeValue(record, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(self.signatures.ownRecordSelfInstance(layout.name)))
			return record
		end 'theSizedContainersOwnRecord'

		try self.requireNameIsNotABuiltinContainerBase(layout, anchor: anchor)
		try self.requireArrayMarkerDeclaresTheBuffer(layout, anchor: anchor)
		let tag = try self.requireFusedWrapperTag(layout, anchor: anchor)

		let managedIndex = layout.inlineManagedFieldIndex()
		if managedIndex >= layout.fieldCount() 'noManagedField'
			// A wrapper conformer with no `managed` field declares itself the compiler's byte record and then
			// does not carry one. Nothing in the corpus does this; a stdlib edit that did would otherwise
			// reach `fieldValues.get` past its end.
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(layout.name)}` names a builtin literal marker, so its record is the fused byte record, but it declares no `managed` field for that record's buffer to come from", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'noManagedField'

		let source = try fieldValues.get(managedIndex) otherwise panic("emitFusedWrapperLiteral: fieldValues.get OOB at {managedIndex} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")

		let flagIndex = layout.indexOfField(SingleByteGraphemesFieldName)
		if flagIndex >= layout.fieldCount() 'classifiesItsOwnBytes'
			return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(StrFromBytesName, args: singletonArgs(source), resultTag: tag, resultNameId: self.fusedWrapperNameId(tag, layout: layout), range: range)
		end 'classifiesItsOwnBytes'

		let flag = try fieldValues.get(flagIndex) otherwise panic("emitFusedWrapperLiteral: fieldValues.get OOB at {flagIndex} — the column is sized to the layout's field count")
		return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(StrOfBufferName, args: pairArgs(source, second: flag), resultTag: tag, resultNameId: self.fusedWrapperNameId(tag, layout: layout), range: range)
	end 'emitFusedWrapperLiteral'

	// A byte-record tag carries no name (`String` and `Character` are their own `MaxonType` arms); a
	// `structRef` is nothing BUT its name, and `p.flag` on the result needs it to find the layout. One place
	// decides, so the two producers above cannot disagree about which values carry one.
	function fusedWrapperNameId(tag ValueTypeTag, layout StructLayout) returns TypeNameId
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef 'nominal'
			return self.artifact.typeNames.intern(layout.name)
		end 'nominal'
		return UnnamedTypeId
	end 'fusedWrapperNameId'

	// ⭐⭐ **A LITERAL MARKER ON A NAME SHV2 ALREADY SERVES A CONTAINER UNDER (W112)** — asked FIRST of this
	// family's sentences, because the NAME disqualifies the declaration whatever its fields say.
	//
	// The others each presume the compiler could own the record if the declaration were shaped right:
	// `requireArrayMarkerDeclaresTheBuffer` tells a conformer to make its first field the buffer,
	// `requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord` tells it to drop its second field, and `requireFusedWrapperTag` says the
	// compiler owns no record under that name. **All three are the wrong advice here.** A declaration named
	// `Vector` or `List` may be perfectly shaped — `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s is, to the field — and it is still
	// not the array record, because shv2 serves a container of its own under that name and a `Vector with 3 T`
	// carries a SIZE the record has no coordinate for.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS A NOUN, NOT A GATE, AND THE MEASUREMENT SAYS SO — READ IT BEFORE ASSUMING WHAT IT BUYS.**
	// The rule that actually keeps a container off the array surface is
	// `ProgramSignatures.noteArrayLiteralConformer`'s skip, and it is also what makes this door REACHABLE:
	// without the skip, `isArrayBaseName` answers TRUE for the name, `firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer` follows, and
	// the caller returns down its IDENTITY arm before any of these sentences is asked. **Sabotage-measured on
	// the three `array-declared-record.error.a-conformer-named-for-…` cases**: with the skip reverted and this
	// refusal in place, the two constructing cases COMPILE CLEAN; with the skip in place and this refusal
	// reverted, they are refused with `requireFusedWrapperTag`'s `String`/`Character` sentence — accurate
	// about a `Holder` and misleading about a `Vector`, which is the whole of what this door adds. The
	// conformance REQUIRES `static init(value __ManagedMemory) returns Self`, so a conformer that constructs
	// always reaches here; one that DELEGATES never does, and rests on the skip alone (that case's table).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT IS GATED ON THE ARRAY MARKER, AND RENDERING THE MARKER INTO THE SENTENCE WAS NOT ENOUGH —
	// MEASURED (review, W112).** This header used to claim that because the marker is RENDERED rather than
	// spelled, "a `BuiltinStringLiteral` conformer named for a container meets its own marker in the sentence
	// rather than the array one". **That is false, and rendering is why it reads as true**: the marker's NAME
	// interpolates correctly while the REASON below it stays hardcoded to the array surface. Measured on
	// `type Vector implements BuiltinStringLiteral` with a `managed` + `flag` pair — the
	// `interface-conformance.error.literal-marker-conformer-…` shape with only the name changed — this door
	// answered *"…not the `__ManagedMemory` record a `BuiltinStringLiteral` conformer's literal is an identity
	// on. Honouring the marker would put every `Vector` on the growable array's surface — `push`, `resize`"*.
	// Every clause of that is wrong for it: the declaration is NOT generic, so there is no `Vector with …`;
	// `noteArrayLiteralConformer` is never called for a string marker, so no array surface is at stake; and it
	// PREEMPTED `requireFusedWrapperTag`'s sentence, which is the right one and is what the identical
	// declaration named `Wrapped` gets. The wrong NOUN is the one failure this door exists to prevent, so it
	// arrived carrying it.
	//
	// ⇒ **The gate is the array marker, the same opening clause `requireArrayMarkerDeclaresTheBuffer` has**,
	// because the hazard the sentence names — the growable surface reached through
	// `isArrayBaseName`'s widening — is reachable through no other marker. A container-named conformer of any
	// OTHER literal marker falls through to `requireFusedWrapperTag`, whose subject is the byte record and
	// whose sentence is true of it.
	// (`array-declared-record.error.a-string-marker-conformer-named-for-a-container-is-not-this-doors-subject`)
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OPENING CLAUSE READ *"serves from its own runtime rather than from a declaration"* AND W190
	// FALSIFIED IT.** `stdlib/Vector.maxon` serves `create`, `count`, `get` and `set` now — there is no
	// runtime surface left to name. What is still true, and is the whole of what this refusal rests on, is
	// that shv2 SYNTHESIZES the RECORD: a `Vector with …` is this compiler's instance whatever a declaration
	// of that name says, which is exactly why the marker's identity cannot be honoured on it.
	function requireNameIsNotABuiltinContainerBase(layout StructLayout, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if not layout.requireBuiltinManagedWrapperInterfaceName().equals(BuiltinArrayLiteralInterfaceName) 'notTheArrayMarker'
			return
		end 'notTheArrayMarker'

		if not self.signatures.isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord(layout.name) 'aNameNoContainerHolds'
			return
		end 'aNameNoContainerHolds'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(layout.name)}` is a generic container shv2 SYNTHESIZES the record for, so a `{String.from(layout.name)} with …` is already this compiler's own instance and not the `{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}` record a `{String.from(BuiltinArrayLiteralInterfaceName)}` conformer's literal is an identity on. Honouring the marker would put every `{String.from(layout.name)}` on the growable array's surface — `push`, `resize` — under the container's own name, which is a wrong answer rather than a refusal. Rename the declaration, or drop the marker", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireNameIsNotABuiltinContainerBase'

	// ⭐⭐ **A GENERIC `BuiltinArrayLiteral` CONFORMER WHOSE FIRST FIELD IS NOT THE BUFFER (ARR5)** — it exists
	// because the family's other sentences are each about something this declaration does not get wrong. (The
	// ordinal that used to open this line was a count kept in prose with nothing checking it, and adding a
	// fourth sentence at `W112` was the edit that would have made it silently false.)
	//
	// `requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord` is about a SECOND field, and there is only one here.
	// `requireFusedWrapperTag` is about the two NAMES the compiler owns a byte record for, and it would say
	// "a conformer of any other name" to a declaration whose name is beside the point — what is wrong is
	// that its one field is a `Num`, or a `String`, where the record it claims requires a `__ManagedMemory`
	// over the very parameter it declares. Under the old NAME-keyed admission this shape was ADMITTED and
	// the identity handed the raw field value back as the container; the only thing that refused it was a
	// later type compare, whose sentence is about a return
	// (`ProgramSignatures.declarationIsTheManagedRecord` carries the measurement).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TYPE-PARAMETER GATE IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS DOES NOT SWALLOW `Holder`.** A NON-generic
	// conformer cannot be the buffer record at all — the record is generic over its element — so nothing
	// about its field could be repaired and the name sentence is the right one for it. That is the boundary
	// `array-declared-record.error.a-conformer-of-another-name-is-still-refused` pins.
	function requireArrayMarkerDeclaresTheBuffer(layout StructLayout, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if not layout.requireBuiltinManagedWrapperInterfaceName().equals(BuiltinArrayLiteralInterfaceName) 'notTheArrayMarker'
			return
		end 'notTheArrayMarker'

		if self.signatures.declaredFieldsBeginWithABuffer(layout) 'theFirstFieldIsABuffer'
			return
		end 'theFirstFieldIsABuffer'

		// A declaration with no type parameter is `Holder`'s case, above: it is not this refusal's subject,
		// and its element name — which the sentence has to spell — does not exist to be spelled.
		let element = try self.signatures.structTypeParamsOf(layout.name).get(0) otherwise return

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(layout.name)}` implements `{String.from(BuiltinArrayLiteralInterfaceName)}` over the parameter `{String.from(element)}` it declares, so its record IS a `{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)} with {String.from(element)}` and its literal is an identity on that buffer — but its first field is not one. A value whose bytes are the buffer's and whose declared fields say otherwise has those fields discarded at construction and read back past the record's end when it is cloned or destroyed. Declare exactly one field, `{String.from(InlineManagedFieldName)} as {String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)} with {String.from(element)}`, or drop the marker so the declaration keeps the box its fields describe", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireArrayMarkerDeclaresTheBuffer'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS DECLARATION THE BUFFER RECORD IT ALREADY IS? (W57, made structural by ARR5)** — the
	// admission test for `emitFusedWrapperLiteral`'s identity arm, and the SECOND half of a shape the arm
	// has already asked the first half of. The arm's gate is `ProgramSignatures.firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer`;
	// what is left to demand is that the buffer is the declaration's ONLY field.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS IS ITS POSITIONED HALF — A REFUSAL WHOSE NOUN IS THE SHAPE, NOT THE NAME.**
	// `requireFusedWrapperTag`'s sentence is about which NAMES the compiler owns a record for, and it would
	// be actively misleading here: this declaration's first field IS the buffer, so the compiler owns its
	// record and the extra FIELDS are what disagree. Such a declaration would have every field past the
	// buffer discarded by the identity — the very hazard the sibling sentence names — so it is refused with
	// the field it is about.
	function requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord(layout StructLayout, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if layout.fieldCount() == 1 'itsOwnRecord'
			return
		end 'itsOwnRecord'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(layout.name)}` implements `{String.from(layout.requireBuiltinManagedWrapperInterfaceName())}`, and a record whose first field IS its `{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}` is that buffer — one record under two names — so its literal is an identity on the buffer that field names. This declaration has {layout.fieldCount()} field(s), and every one past the buffer would be silently dropped at construction and then read back past the record's end when the value is cloned or destroyed. Declare the buffer alone, or drop the literal marker so the declaration keeps the box its fields describe", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH TAG A FUSED LITERAL PRODUCES — DECIDED BY THE TYPE'S NAME, AND A NAME THE COMPILER DOES NOT
	// OWN THE RECORD FOR IS REFUSED OUTRIGHT.** The marker cannot decide it: keying on the marker alone gave
	// EVERY `BuiltinStringLiteral` conformer the `string` tag, so a type of any other name got a `string`-tagged
	// value out of its own `Self{…}` while `returns Self` still resolved to its struct —
	// `E3005 Cannot return 'String' from function declared to return 'struct'`, measured on a corpus-provenanced
	// probe.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND KEYING ON THE NAME WHILE STILL BUILDING THE RECORD IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME DEFECT, WHICH
	// IS WHY THIS REFUSES RATHER THAN FALLING BACK TO `structRef`.** A conformer of any other name was handed a
	// value whose BYTES are the fused byte record's and whose IDENTITY is a struct's, and the three consumers of
	// that value each read it as the thing it is not — measured, all three, on ordinary user programs:
	//   * **Construction DISCARDS every field the fused record has no slot for.** `emitFusedWrapperLiteral`
	//     takes `managed` as its source and `singleByteGraphemesFlag` as its flag and passes on nothing else, so
	//     a conformer whose second field is named anything else reaches `__str_from_bytes`, which CLASSIFIES the
	//     bytes and writes its OWN answer at @40 — the offset that field occupies. `Self{flag: false}` read
	//     back **TRUE**: exit 7 on a program whose only literal wrote `false`, no fault, no leak, no diagnostic.
	//   * **The DROP is `__destruct_<T>`**, built from the DECLARED field list rather than from the record that
	//     was actually built, so a third field is loaded from past the fused record's end and handed to
	//     `__str_decref`: **0xC0000005**.
	//   * **The CLONE is `__clone_<T>`**, built from the identical list, and faults identically.
	// There is no tag that fixes this: the value is one record and two descriptions of it. The DECLARATION
	// stays legal (its layout is a real answer, which is what `sizeof` reports) and the CONSTRUCTION is refused.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS NOW REACHED ONLY BY A CONFORMER THE IDENTITY ARM DID NOT TAKE (W57).** The corpus's own
	// `type Array` no longer arrives here at all: its record needs no producer, so it is served one door up by
	// `ProgramSignatures.firstFieldIsItsOwnBuffer`. What still arrives is every OTHER `BuiltinArrayLiteral`
	// conformer — a type whose first field is not the buffer over its own type parameter, whatever it is
	// named — and for each of those the three consequences above hold unchanged, so the sentence they meet is
	// unchanged too. (A conformer whose first field IS that buffer but which declares a SECOND one is served
	// by `requireBufferIsItsOwnRecord`, whose noun is the extra field rather than the name.)
	//
	// ⇒ Only the two names the compiler already owns denote a builtin byte record: `String` and `Character`
	// are on `TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`, which is what guarantees no USER file can mint one, so a
	// layout carrying one of those names IS the corpus's own declaration — and its values are tagged
	// `string`/`character` and dropped through `__str_decref`, never through a struct cascade.
	//
	// The two byte-record names get DISTINCT tags for `IrInterface.CharacterConformerName`'s stated reason: a
	// `Character` and a `String` share one representation and are told apart only by their type — and the
	// name→tag decision is `byteRecordTagOfTypeName`'s, shared with the two type-reference doors so a literal's
	// tag and its declared type's tag are one answer. That same call is the ADMISSION test here, so the set of
	// names that may be constructed and the set that may be REFERENCED cannot drift apart.
	function requireFusedWrapperTag(layout StructLayout, anchor Token) returns ValueTypeTag throws ParseError
		let byteRecord = byteRecordTagOfTypeName(layout.name)
		if byteRecord != ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'theCompilersOwnByteRecord'
			return byteRecord
		end 'theCompilersOwnByteRecord'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(layout.name)}` implements `{String.from(layout.requireBuiltinManagedWrapperInterfaceName())}`, one of `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s literal markers, so its record would be the compiler's own fused byte record rather than the fields it declares. shv2 mints that record only for the two names it owns the record FOR — `{String.from(StringConformerName)}` and `{String.from(CharacterConformerName)}` — because a conformer of any other name gets a VALUE whose bytes are a byte record's and whose IDENTITY is a struct's: every declared field the fused record has no slot for is discarded at construction, and the struct cascade that later drops or clones it reads past the record's end", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireFusedWrapperTag'

	function emitOwnedStructBox(layout StructLayout, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let size = self.emitLiteral(layout.sizeBytes(), valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let box = self.emitBoxAlloc(size, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.structRef, resultNameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(layout.name), range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(box, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return box
	end 'emitOwnedStructBox'

	// ⭐ **THE THREE DOTTED EXPRESSIONS**, split here and nowhere else. The cursor is on an
	// identifier followed by `.`, and the language now spells three unrelated things that way:
	//
	//   1. `Point.create(3, y: 4)` — a QUALIFIED STATIC CALL. Recognized by the `(` at +3, which is
	//      the token neither of the others can have: `u16.max(` is not legal Maxon (a bound is a
	//      value, not a callee) and `p.x(` would be a call THROUGH a field, which needs the
	//      function-typed values P1.2 brings.
	//   2. `p.x` — a FIELD ACCESS, when the base name is bound in this function's scope. Asked
	//      SECOND and by SCOPE, which is the same "LOCAL FIRST" rule `parseVariableReference`
	//      already resolves a bare name by: a local named `u16` would shadow the sized type, exactly
	//      as a local `OFFSET` shadows a top-level constant of that name.
	//   3. `u16.max` — a TYPE-QUALIFIED NUMERIC BOUND, the fallback. It was the only dotted form the
	//      language had, and it stays the answer for a base name that is neither callable nor bound.
	//
	// The order is the decision. Testing the bound table FIRST would have to guess whether `p` is a
	// sized type before knowing `p` is a local, and `sizedTypeBound` answers `notASizedType` for
	// every struct — so `p.x` would have reported "expected a sized numeric type (u8/u16/…)" for a
	// perfectly good field read.
	//
	// `resultUsed` is passed through to the two CALL forms (the method call and the qualified static call)
	// and means what it means everywhere else: is this call's value read? It is `true` from expression
	// position and `false` from a `try` TARGET, where the try's own position decides value usability
	// (`parseTryCallTarget`). Threaded rather than pre-dispatched by the caller: an arm order lives in ONE
	// place, so a form added ahead of the method arm here cannot be silently skipped by a second copy of
	// the split. Hard-coding it `true` is what rejected a VOID throwing static under `try` — `try
	// Gate.check(1)` reported E2004 "does not return a value" for a statement whose value nobody wanted,
	// while its instance twin `try g.bump(1)` was accepted; the reference compiler takes both.
	function parseDottedPrimary(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **A BOUND BASE IS A VALUE, AND A VALUE OUTRANKS A TYPE NAME.** `p.x` and `p.add(10)` are
		// an instance's field and an instance's METHOD; `Point.create(10)` is a static method, and
		// `u16.max` is a bound. The base being IN SCOPE is what tells the first two from the last
		// two, and it is asked FIRST for `parseVariableReference`'s stated reason: a local binding
		// shadows a name from any wider scope, which is what every language with both does.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE `(` AT +3 IS NOT ENOUGH TO FIND A STATIC CALL**, and that is the trap this order
		// avoids: `p.add(10)` and `Point.create(10)` have IDENTICAL token shapes. Testing `(` first
		// sends `p.add(10)` to `parseQualifiedCall`, which would mangle the callee out of the
		// VARIABLE's name — `p.add`, which no file declares — and report E3004 against a method that
		// is right there in the type. `methodCallsAt` is where that scope test lives, shared with the
		// statement dispatcher.
		// `arr.managed` joins the method arm rather than the field arm below — see `arrayManagedFieldAt`. It is
		// asked WITH `methodCallsAt` because both route to `parseMethodCall` and the two shapes are disjoint.
		// This is the ONE position that reads the UNCHAINED predicate: an expression is where a bare
		// `arr.managed` is a value somebody wants (`f(content.managed)`), which the two call-only positions
		// (`methodCallStmt`, the `try` target) are not.
		if self.methodCallsAt(self.pos) or self.arrayManagedFieldAt(self.pos) 'methodCall'
			return try self.parseMethodCall(func, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'methodCall'

		// A base that names a VALUE — a local binding, or a CAPTURED enclosing one (`level.rawValue` inside a
		// closure, P1.5-A2b-2, which `parseFieldAccess` reads out of the env via `resolveFieldChain`'s captured
		// arm). ONE arm and ONE predicate for the two, because they had one BODY between them: two `if`s
		// returning the same `parseFieldAccess()` call, whose tests were also the two `methodCallsAt` spells out
		// for the statement door. See `baseNamesAValueInScope` — it is where the shadowing rule now lives, and
		// where `Self` is excluded from it.
		// `s.ordinal` on an enum-typed binding — asked BEFORE the field arm and never instead of it. The two
		// are disjoint by the member name, and the order is what matters: an enum binding reaching
		// `parseFieldAccess` is refused by `requireStructBase` (a declared enum erases to `integer`, so it
		// has no struct layout to find), which is the E2015 this arm exists to route past. See
		// `enumAccessorFieldAt`.
		if self.enumAccessorFieldAt(self.pos) 'enumAccessor'
			return try self.parseEnumAccessor()
		end 'enumAccessor'

		if self.baseNamesAValueInScope(self.current()) 'valueBase'
			return try self.parseFieldAccess()
		end 'valueBase'

		// ⭐ **EVERY FORM BELOW NAMES A TYPE, AND ITS BASE IS READ THROUGH `typeBaseTokenAt` — WHICH IS WHERE
		// `Self` BECOMES THE TYPE IT DENOTES (D9).** So `Self.off` inside `enum Toggle` takes the enum-case arm
		// `Toggle.off` takes, and `Self.make(v)` inside `type Box` takes the static-call arm `Box.make(v)`
		// takes. There is deliberately no `Self` arm anywhere: the two spellings converge at the ONE token a
		// type-named form reads its base from, so an arm added here serves both without knowing that.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE VALUE-BASED ARM ABOVE IS ASKED FIRST, AND `Self` IS THE ONE BASE IT MAY NOT CLAIM.** A local
		// named after the enclosing type (`let Box = 1` inside `type Box`) shadows `Box.` — that is the "a value
		// outranks a type name" rule — while `Self.` cannot mean a local under any spelling, and a parameter
		// named `Self` is admitted by D8's keyword-as-a-declared-name rule, so the exclusion has to be stated
		// rather than assumed. It is stated once, in `baseNamesAValueInScope`, with the measurements.
		let baseTok = try self.typeBaseTokenAt(self.pos)

		// ⭐ **A DOTTED PRIMARY WHOSE HEAD NAMES A TYPE IS A MENTION OF THAT TYPE** — `Color.blue`,
		// `Point.create(…)`, `Config.MAX_SIZE`. Recorded HERE, above the arms that decide WHICH of those
		// it is, for exactly the reason `recordTypeNameUse` states about its other site: a recording site
		// does not know what a written name denotes and must not have to ask, and hooking the arms instead
		// would need one hook per arm and silently miss the next one added.
		//
		// ⚠ **AN EXPRESSION-POSITION MENTION COUNTS, AND IT DID NOT BEFORE.** Only type POSITIONS were
		// recorded, which is all E3062 needs — an alias is used when something is typed by it. But
		// `checkUnusedExports` asks the wider question, and an enum used cross-file is used almost entirely
		// from expression position: MEASURED, `export enum Color` read as `Color.blue` in another file was
		// reported E3092 by `export-keyword/exported-enum-cross-file`, whose whole subject is that the
		// cross-file reference WORKS. E3062 only ever filters this set against a file's own non-exported
		// typealiases, so a widened set can make it report LESS, never wrongly — and a name mentioned in
		// any position is a name the file uses, which is the question E3062 asks too.
		if self.signatures.containsStruct(baseTok.value) or self.signatures.containsEnum(baseTok.value) 'headNamesAType'
			self.recordTypeNameUse(baseTok.value)
		end 'headNamesAType'

		// `Color.red` and `Move.walk(Direction.east)` — an ENUM CASE reference or a UNION CONSTRUCT,
		// the base being a declared enum/union. Asked AFTER the scope-based forms above (a bound base
		// outranks a type name, exactly as `u16.max` is the last resort) but BEFORE the qualified STATIC
		// CALL below, because a payload construct `Move.walk(…)` has the SAME `<name>.<name>(` shape a
		// static call does: testing `(` at +3 first would mangle it to a callee `Move.walk` no file
		// declares (E3004) rather than building the box. The two are disjoint by name — a name is one
		// type, so a struct's `Point.create(…)` never resolves as an enum here.
		// Gated on the non-allocating `containsEnum` rather than `enumOf` directly: this runs for EVERY
		// dotted primary, and `enumOf` would box a throwaway `DeclaredEnum.undeclared` for every
		// `Point.create(...)` (scale-test charged exactly that — see `ProgramSignatures.containsEnum`).
		// The `enumOf` inside the guard runs only for a real enum reference, which is rare.
		// ⚠ **A DECLARED ENUM THIS FILE MAY NOT NAME IS NOT AN ENUM REFERENCE HERE (N1a)** — it falls through
		// to the hidden-base guard below, which reports the one mistake the reader can act on. The visibility
		// probe is inside the `containsEnum` hit, so it costs nothing on the path this arm is guarded for.
		if self.signatures.containsEnum(baseTok.value) and self.typeNameVisibleHere(baseTok.value) 'enumRef'
			match self.signatures.enumOf(baseTok.value) 'declared'
				declared(layout) then return try self.parseEnumLiteral(func, declaredLayout: layout)
				undeclared then panic("parseDottedPrimary: containsEnum answered yes but enumOf answered undeclared — the two read the same enum registry, under the same key")
			end 'declared'
		end 'enumRef'

		// ⭐⭐ **`lib.inner.deepHelper()` — A MULTI-SEGMENT DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED FREE CALL (N1b), AND THE FOURTH
		// CLAIMANT ON THIS `.` IS WHAT MADE IT NEED AN ARM AT ALL.** The `Type.method` arm below is a fixed
		// THREE-TOKEN lookahead, so a longer chain fell past it to the LAST-RESORT bound arm and was refused as
		// a malformed `u64.max` (`E2010: Expected ''min' or 'max'' but got 'inner'`) — the SIZED-BOUND grammar,
		// which is the claimant this rung's plan had not counted.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ONE-DOT SHAPE IS DELIBERATELY NOT ASKED FOR HERE** (`MethodQualifierDots + 1`): it belongs to
		// `parseQualifiedCall` below, which asks the SAME door about the SAME rule with a name it has already
		// built. See `namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt` for what asking twice measured.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE CLAIMANTS ARE SEPARATED BY ORDER AND BY A REGISTRY, AND NEVER BY "IS THE HEAD A
		// NAMESPACE?"** A value base and an enum reference are claimed above; this then demands a trailing `(`
		// (which a bound has not) AND a registered directory-qualified free function (which a bound's `u64` is
		// not). Anything it declines falls through untouched to the two arms that have always followed.
		if self.namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt(self.pos, minimumDots: MethodQualifierDots + 1) > 0 'multiSegmentNamespaceCall'
			return try self.parseNamespaceQualifiedCall(func, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'multiSegmentNamespaceCall'

		if self.kindAt(self.pos + 3) == TokenKind.leftParen 'qualifiedCall'
			return try self.parseQualifiedCall(func, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'qualifiedCall'

		// ⭐ **`Counter.count` — A READ OF A STATIC MEMBER (W65), which is a read of a top-level binding whose
		// NAME happens to carry a dot.** So it routes to `topLevelBindingReference`, the very door a bare
		// global name uses: a `static let` INLINES its folded value and a `static var` LOADS from its `.data`
		// slot, through the same two probes and the same emitted ops. Nothing about this arm is static-member
		// machinery — it only spells the name.
		//
		// Placed with the type-based arms and BELOW the call shapes, which is the precedence
		// `top-level-let-name-shadowed-by-type` already pins: a form that names a TYPE is tried before the
		// managed-global fallback beneath it, and a base bound to a VALUE has already claimed the whole
		// expression above.
		if self.namesStaticMemberAt(baseTok, memberIndex: self.pos + TokensPerQualifierSegment) 'staticMemberRead'
			return try self.parseStaticMemberAccess(func, baseTok: baseTok, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'staticMemberRead'

		// ⭐ **A BASE NAMING A TYPE THIS FILE MAY NOT SEE BINDS NOTHING HERE (N1a)** — not a value, not a type
		// — so it is the same mistake as a misspelled name and takes the same diagnostic, which is the
		// reference's answer for it (`export-keyword/error.non-exported-enum-cross-file`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE CALL SHAPE NEVER REACHES THIS LINE — IT WENT TO `parseQualifiedCall`, WHICH GATES NOTHING**,
		// and that is the rule rather than an omission: `Hidden.create(…)` is refused (or not) by the
		// STATIC's own tier at `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom`, so an `export`ed static on a hidden type
		// stays callable and the reader meets the type's own visibility at the first FIELD access
		// (`requireFieldAccessible` ⇒ E4006). Both references decide it exactly there and exactly that way —
		// v1 at `TypeResolution.maxon:10200-10220`, which returns without a diagnostic for a visible method
		// and defers to the field gate. Asked LAST so a visible type still takes whichever arm above claims it.
		// ⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL MANAGED GLOBAL'S FIELD — `db.sourceFiles`** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`), the
		// one value base that is neither in scope nor captured. Asked HERE, below every type-based arm, and
		// that placement is the rule rather than an accident: see `chainBaseNamesAValue`.
		if self.managedTopLevelBaseAt(self.pos) 'managedTopLevelField'
			return try self.parseFieldAccess()
		end 'managedTopLevelField'

		if not self.typeNameVisibleHere(baseTok.value) 'hiddenTypeBase'
			throw ParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(baseTok.value), line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column)
		end 'hiddenTypeBase'

		return try self.parseTypeQualifiedBoundExpression()
	end 'parseDottedPrimary'

	// ⭐ **HOW MANY `.<name>` PAIRS AT `index` FORM A DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED CALLEE (N1b)** — `0` when the tokens
	// are anything else, which is every dotted expression in every program that does not use the form.
	//
	// The walk is v1's, character for character in its shape (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:14570-14603`):
	// assemble the whole chain, then ask the registry about the ASSEMBLED name. What shv2 does not port is
	// v1's LONGEST-PREFIX memory — v1 needs it because its type-position walk must stop where a registered
	// name ends and leave the rest to a per-instance member; a CALL's chain is terminated by the `(` instead,
	// so the whole chain either names a function or does not.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE RULE, ASKED THROUGH ONE DOOR** — `ProgramSignatures.resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction`, which
	// `parseQualifiedCall` also asks. This routine's whole job is turning TOKENS into the name that door
	// answers about; it decides nothing itself.
	//
	// ⚡⚡ **`minimumDots` IS NOT A TUNING KNOB — IT IS THE CALLER SAYING WHICH SHAPE ANOTHER ARM AT ITS OWN
	// POSITION ALREADY OWNS, AND IT WAS PUT THERE BY A MEASUREMENT.** In EXPRESSION position the one-dot shape
	// belongs to `parseQualifiedCall`, which mangles `Type.method` for its own reasons and can therefore ask
	// the door with a name it ALREADY HOLDS; assembling that same name here first cost a join at every
	// `Point.create(…)` in the program. **MEASURED, two binaries swapped into one path at `--repeat=1`:
	// +297 / +461 / +693 / +1,221 / +2,277 / +4,421 allocations across the doubling ladder — ratios 1.55,
	// 1.50, 1.76, 1.86, 1.94, converging on 2.00 from below, which is a LINEAR term and not the flat install
	// cost it was meant to be.** In STATEMENT position no arm owns any dotted call (a static call on a line of
	// its own stays refused), so nothing there has a name in hand and one dot is admitted.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN EMPTY-SET SHORT-CIRCUIT WOULD NOT HAVE FIXED IT, WHICH IS WHY THERE ISN'T ONE.** It was written
	// first — `namespaceQualifiedFunctions` is empty for a program whose files all sit at the compile anchor —
	// and the numbers above are what it MEASURED: `stdlib/` and its seven subdirectories are namespaced in
	// every compile there is, so the set is never empty and the guard never fired.
	//
	// PEEK-ONLY: the cursor is untouched, so a `0` leaves every arm after this one looking at the tokens it
	// expects. The token walk allocates nothing; only a chain that has already passed the shape test is
	// assembled.
	function namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt(index TokenIndex, minimumDots ElementCount) returns ElementCount
		let dots = self.dottedChainLengthAt(index)
		if dots < minimumDots or self.kindAt(index + dots * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 1) != TokenKind.leftParen 'notADottedCall'
			return 0
		end 'notADottedCall'

		return dots if self.signatures.resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction(self.assembleDottedChainAt(index, dots: dots)) else 0
	end 'namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt'

	// How many `.<name>` pairs follow the identifier at `index` — the peek-only, allocation-free half of the
	// walk above. `0` for a bare name and for anything that is not an identifier at all.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **TWO POSITIONS ASK IT, AND THEY ARE ONE QUESTION (N1b review)**: the CALL door above, and
	// `parseTypeReference`'s `Instance.Member` / `dir.Name` arm. It was written twice — once here and once as
	// an inline loop in the type arm that demanded `TokenKind.identifier` per segment — and the copies had
	// already come apart: `lib.from.helper()` compiled while `21 as lib.from.Score` did not. Two spellings of
	// "where does a dotted name end" can only ever agree by accident, and neither site reads as wrong on its
	// own. See `parseTypeReference` for the measurement.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HEAD MUST BE A PLAIN IDENTIFIER AND THE SEGMENTS AFTER IT NEED ONLY BE NAMEABLE (D8)**, which
	// is the asymmetry v1 also draws (`tokenIsIdentifierLike`, `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:14577`).
	// A trailing segment is the FUNCTION'S OWN NAME, and D8 lets a declaration take a keyword — `export
	// function from()` is legal and `declaredMethodName` files its qualified spelling under `lib.from`, so a
	// walk that demanded `identifier` there would register a name no call could ever reach. The HEAD is held
	// to `identifier` because it is the position every other arm of `parseDottedPrimary` reads its base from,
	// and widening it would offer this arm keyword bases (`int.`, `Self.`) that those arms own.
	function dottedChainLengthAt(index TokenIndex) returns ElementCount
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.identifier 'notADottedName'
			return 0
		end 'notADottedName'

		var dots = 0 as ElementCount
		while self.kindAt(index + dots * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 1) == TokenKind.dot and tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(index + dots * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 2)) 'walk'
			dots = dots + 1
		end 'walk'
		return dots
	end 'dottedChainLengthAt'

	// The `a.b.c` NAME a chain of `dots` pairs at `index` spells, built through `joinQualifiedName` so the
	// registry is asked about the same bytes `namespaceQualifiedName` filed it under. Called only once
	// `dottedChainLengthAt` has accepted the shape, so the joins are paid by a real dotted call or a real
	// dotted TYPE reference and by nothing else — the two callers of the walk above, sharing its assembly for
	// the same reason they share its predicate.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE SYNTHETIC NAME TOKEN A `Type.member` STATIC ACCESS DENOTES (W65)** — `value` is the storage
	// key `Counter.count`, built by the one join every member name in this file is built by, and the SPAN is
	// the whole chain the author wrote (`baseTok`'s first byte through `memberTok`'s last), so a diagnostic
	// or a `SourceRange` taken off it points at the source rather than at half of it.
	//
	// ⭐ **SYNTHESIZING A TOKEN IS WHAT MAKES THE TWO STATIC-MEMBER DOORS ONE LINE EACH.** The whole
	// top-level binding machinery — the constant probe, the slot probe, the load, the checked store, E2013,
	// E2004, E3070's rebind subject, the blame name a bare argument carries — is keyed by a token's `value`
	// and anchored on its position. A static member differs from a file-scope global in NOTHING but how its
	// name is spelled, so spelling it is the entire adaptation; the alternative was threading a second
	// "which name did you mean" parameter through six shared functions, where the token and the name would
	// have been free to disagree.
	//
	// ⚠ It is `identifier`-kinded for `typeBaseTokenAt`'s reason, one door along: the token IS a name now,
	// and every reader downstream asks what it spells rather than which keyword produced it.
	function staticMemberNameToken(baseTok Token, memberTok Token) returns Token
		return Token.create(TokenKind.identifier, line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column, value: qualifiedMethodName(baseTok.value, methodName: memberTok.value), startByte: baseTok.startByte, endByte: memberTok.endByte)
	end 'staticMemberNameToken'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Counter.count` AND `Names.items.push(9)` — THE ONE READER OF A STATIC-MEMBER ACCESS (W65).**
	// The cursor sits on the base; `baseTok` is what `typeBaseTokenAt` made of it, so `Self.count` inside the
	// declaring type arrives here as `Counter.count` and needs no arm of its own.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SHAPES ARE SPLIT ON WHETHER A MEMBER CALL FOLLOWS, AND THAT SPLIT IS A SOUNDNESS FIX
	// RATHER THAN A CONVENIENCE.** A plain read hands back a value; a read that RECEIVES a method must arrive
	// at the dispatcher wearing the four facts a NAME carries, and the E3019 blame name is the one that
	// matters. **MEASURED before this split existed**: `type Cache { static let xs = [1, 2, 3] }` plus
	// `try Cache.xs.pop()` COMPILED CLEAN and returned 5 — a mutation of a binding declared `let` — because
	// the value fell out to `parsePostfix`, whose receiver is deliberately nameless ("A postfix receiver is
	// bound to no name"), which is true of a literal and a call result and false of this. The identical
	// program spelled with a file-scope `let xs = [1, 2, 3]` is E3019, and now both are.
	//
	// ⭐ So the call form goes to `dispatchOnManagedReceiver` — the SAME door a bare top-level managed
	// receiver uses (`parseMethodCall`'s `managedTopLevelBase` arm) — with the qualified name as the
	// binding's. That also makes `Names.items.push(9)` legal as a STATEMENT, which the runnable oracle
	// refuses outright (`E3003: 'Names' is a type and cannot be used directly as a value`, measured): a
	// managed static you may declare and may not call a method on is a slot nothing can use, and shv2 has
	// the receiver door to hand.
	//
	// ⚠ **A SCALAR `static let` REACHES THE `none` ARM AND FALLS BACK TO THE PLAIN READ**, which is the right
	// answer rather than a gap: an inlined scalar constant has no slot and no record, so there is nothing a
	// method could mutate and no ownership for a blame name to protect. Its member (if any) is then served by
	// `parsePostfix`'s value receiver exactly as an integer literal's is.
	function parseStaticMemberAccess(func IrFunction, baseTok Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let memberTok = self.tokenAt(self.pos + TokensPerQualifierSegment)
		let nameTok = self.staticMemberNameToken(baseTok, memberTok: memberTok)

		// ON the member token, not past it: `dispatchMethodOnBinding` advances once off the receiver's LAST
		// token and lets `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` consume the `.` — the arrangement every receiver door
		// shares, and the whole reason the cursor is moved here rather than past the pair.
		self.pos = self.pos + TokensPerQualifierSegment

		if self.memberCallFollows(self.pos + 1) 'aMethodOnTheMember'
			match self.managedReceiverBindingOf(nameTok) 'binding'
				constant(bytes, kind) then return try self.dispatchOnManagedReceiver(func, baseTok: nameTok, value: try self.emitManagedConstant(bytes, kind: kind, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)), varType: self.signatures.managedConstValueType(kind, elementFilePath: self.filePath), mutable: false, resultUsed: resultUsed)
				global(label, valueType, mutable) then return try self.dispatchOnManagedReceiver(func, baseTok: nameTok, value: self.emitGlobalLoad(label, valueType: valueType, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)), varType: valueType, mutable: mutable, resultUsed: resultUsed)
				none then break 'binding'
			end 'binding'
		end 'aMethodOnTheMember'

		self.advance()
		return try self.topLevelBindingReference(nameTok)
	end 'parseStaticMemberAccess'

	// ⭐ **DOES `<baseTok>.<member at memberIndex>` NAME A DECLARED STATIC MEMBER (W65)?** — the ONE
	// recognizer both the read arm (`parseDottedPrimary`) and the write arm (`staticMemberAssignsAt`) ask,
	// so an expression `Counter.count` and a statement `Counter.count = 1` cannot come to disagree about
	// which spellings are static members. They would: only one of them would then take the static route, and
	// the other would be refused as a malformed sized-numeric bound or as a field chain through a binding
	// that does not exist.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `containsStruct` GATE IN FRONT IS THE COST, and it is `containsEnum`'s gate for exactly its
	// reason.** This runs on every dotted expression that reaches it — `v == u64.max` among them — and the
	// probe below ALLOCATES the joined name plus the union box each lookup door returns. `containsStruct` is
	// a set membership on bytes already in hand, allocating nothing, and a `false` from it is FINAL: a static
	// member is declared in a `type` body, so its base is a declared struct by construction and `u16` never
	// pays the join.
	function namesStaticMemberAt(baseTok Token, memberIndex TokenIndex) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.containsStruct(baseTok.value) 'notADeclaredTypeBase'
			return false
		end 'notADeclaredTypeBase'

		// D8 lets a declaration take a keyword as its name, so the member half is `tokenCanBeAName` rather
		// than `identifier` — the same asymmetry `dottedChainLengthAt` draws between a chain's head and its
		// trailing segments, and for the same reason.
		if not tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(memberIndex)) 'notAMemberName'
			return false
		end 'notAMemberName'

		return self.namesTopLevelBinding(qualifiedMethodName(baseTok.value, methodName: self.tokenAt(memberIndex).value))
	end 'namesStaticMemberAt'

	// Does `name` denote a top-level binding this file may read — a `let` it would INLINE, or a slot it
	// would LOAD? Asked as ONE question because the two doors are disjoint by construction (one storage key
	// holds one decl) and a caller routing on it does not care which of them answered:
	// `topLevelBindingReference` asks them again in that same order and materializes whichever hit.
	function namesTopLevelBinding(name ByteArray) returns bool
		let isConstant = match self.signatures.constantValueOf(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'constant'
			found to foundManaged gives true
			notFound gives false
		end 'constant'

		if isConstant 'inlined'
			return true
		end 'inlined'

		return match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
			found gives true
			notFound gives false
		end 'global'
	end 'namesTopLevelBinding'

	function assembleDottedChainAt(index TokenIndex, dots ElementCount) returns ByteArray
		// The first pair is joined outside the loop so the accumulator starts OWNED (a `var` may not adopt a
		// borrowed token field — E3078); the caller's `dots > 0` guarantees there is one, and a one-dot chain
		// simply runs the loop zero times.
		var assembled = joinQualifiedName(self.tokenAt(index).value, member: self.tokenAt(index + 2).value)
		for i in 1 upto dots 'eachFurtherSegment'
			assembled = joinQualifiedName(assembled, member: self.tokenAt(index + i * TokensPerQualifierSegment + 2).value)
		end 'eachFurtherSegment'
		return assembled
	end 'assembleDottedChainAt'

	// `utils.helper(…)` / `lib.inner.deepHelper(…)` — consume the qualifier chain and emit the ORDINARY direct
	// call it names. Reached only through `namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt`: from the STATEMENT door at any
	// segment count, and from the EXPRESSION door at TWO OR MORE (a one-dot expression is `parseQualifiedCall`'s,
	// which reaches the same rule through the same door with a name it had already built — see there).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CALLEE IS THE FINAL SEGMENT, AND THAT IS WHY THE EMITTED CODE IS UNTOUCHED.** shv2's function
	// registry is bare-name and whole-program (a second declaration of one name is E3006), so `utils.helper`
	// and `helper` are necessarily the same function: the qualified spelling is a ROUTE to it, not a second
	// symbol, and the call op, the linked name and the golden are byte-identical to a bare call's. It also
	// puts the tier decision back on the ordinary path — `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom` refuses
	// `utils.hidden()` with E3008/E3088, naming the mistake the reader can act on. v1 goes the other way,
	// making `{ns}.{name}` the SYMBOL and the bare form the fallback (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:2071`),
	// which changes what a function is CALLED throughout the backend and is not what this slice is for.
	//
	// The chain length is re-read rather than passed in: `dottedChainLengthAt` is a pure token walk that
	// allocates nothing and cannot disagree with the recognizer, and an `else if` predicate has nowhere to
	// bind a value for its own body.
	function parseNamespaceQualifiedCall(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let headTok = self.current()
		// Bound BEFORE the loop, which moves the cursor: re-reading it from `self.pos` on each iteration
		// would count a chain that is one segment shorter every time round.
		let qualifierDots = self.dottedChainLengthAt(self.pos)

		// ⚠ **ASSEMBLED BEFORE THE CURSOR MOVES, for the same reason `qualifierDots` is** — the loop below
		// walks the chain away, and this is the last position from which the whole of it is readable. WHETHER
		// the written chain or the bare member ends up the callee is `namespaceQualifiedCallee`'s one rule,
		// asked by both directory-qualified call doors; see there.
		let writtenName = self.assembleDottedChainAt(self.pos, dots: qualifierDots)

		// Stops ON the final segment rather than past it: `parseCallNamed` consumes that token itself (it may
		// be a keyword standing as a name, D8) and then the `(`.
		for _ in 0 upto qualifierDots 'eachQualifierSegment'
			self.advance()
			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		end 'eachQualifierSegment'

		return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: self.namespaceQualifiedCallee(writtenName, bare: self.current().value), anchor: headTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.none, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)
	end 'parseNamespaceQualifiedCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **ROUTE OR NAME? — THE ONE PLACE THAT QUESTION IS ANSWERED, FOR BOTH DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED CALL
	// DOORS (N1c review).** A qualifier is ordinarily a ROUTE and the call resolves onto the BARE declared
	// name (N1b's thesis, and what keeps `utils.helper()` and `helper()` byte-identical). That holds
	// precisely while a bare name names ONE function. Once two directories declare it, each is registered
	// under its own qualified spelling (`declaredMethodName`) and the qualifier IS the name.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT WAS WRITTEN IN ONE DOOR AND NOT THE OTHER, WHICH IS THIS PROJECT'S SIGNATURE BUG.** The
	// multi-segment arm (`parseNamespaceQualifiedCall`) carried the rule; the one-dot arm
	// (`parseQualifiedCall`) went on passing `memberTok.value` — the bare member — and reached the right
	// function only because `resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction` was declining it, so it fell through to
	// the `Type.method` static path which happened to have built the identical string. Two doors, two
	// answers, agreeing by accident; the accident ended the moment that veto was corrected.
	function namespaceQualifiedCallee(written ByteArray, bare ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		return written if self.signatures.isContestedFreeFunction(bare) else bare
	end 'namespaceQualifiedCallee'

	// `Color.red` — the value of an enum/union case: its TAG, as an i64 `literal` (the raw value for
	// an int backing, the IEEE-754 bit pattern for a float). Named `Color.green` for a case the enum
	// does not have is E3034, positioned at the TYPE name (the bootstrap's anchor, measured).
	//
	// ⭐ The value is TAGGED `named(Color)` while its literal op is typed `integer`, and the two are
	// deliberately different: the TAG carries the enum's name so a `match` can recognize the scrutinee
	// as a `Color`, and the OP's `integer` type is what the Std lowering reads to give it an i64
	// (a `named` op-type would hit `maxonTypeToStdType`'s panic). Both collapse to i64, so the value's
	// register class and its lowering agree — see `emitEnumTagLiteral`.
	function parseEnumLiteral(func IrFunction, declaredLayout EnumLayout) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `Self.off` reaches here as `Toggle.off` (D9) — see `typeBaseTokenAt`.
		let typeTok = try self.consumeTypeBaseToken()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)

		// ⭐⭐ **`Shape.unionCases.<anything>` IS THE COMPANION'S `<anything>` — ONE HOP, THEN THIS FUNCTION
		// CONTINUES AS IF THE COMPANION HAD BEEN NAMED DIRECTLY.** Every member below — a bare CASE,
		// `allCases`, `allCaseNames`, `fromRawValue` — means for the companion exactly what it means for any
		// other payload-free enum, so the whole of `.unionCases` support past the sweep is this substitution.
		// Writing it as a re-dispatch instead would need each member's arm duplicated under a second head.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ANCHOR STAYS `typeTok`**, i.e. `Shape` and not `unionCases`: a diagnostic about a case the
		// companion does not have names the type the reader wrote, which is what E3034's position means for
		// every other enum reference.
		let layout = self.unionCasesCompanionOf(declaredLayout, typeTok: typeTok)

		// ⭐ **`<Enum>.allCaseNames` IS A STATIC MEMBER, NOT A CASE** — it is claimed here, ahead of the case
		// lookup, because that lookup's answer for it is E3034 and there is nothing further along this path
		// that could reconsider. The member exists on EVERY enum and union (see `EnumAllCaseNamesMemberName`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TWO REFERENCES DISAGREE ABOUT AN ENUM THAT DECLARES A CASE *NAMED* `allCaseNames`, AND NO
		// SPEC COVERS THE COLLISION.** v1 decides it in this order — member name first
		// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:14856`), so the member wins — while the BOOTSTRAP does the
		// opposite: `GetCase(memberName)` runs first and returns the case when it finds one
		// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:19051`), with its `allCaseNames` arm only at `:19099`. shv2
		// follows v1. Nothing in `/specs` states which is right, no corpus or spec enum declares such a case,
		// and reserving the name outright would be a refusal with corpus-wide blast radius that nothing is
		// asking for — so this is a recorded divergence, not a settled rule.
		//
		// The cursor test is `atStaticEnumMember`, the SAME predicate the sweep interns the member's
		// `Array with String` off. The two must agree exactly: the sweep decides whether the instance exists
		// and this decides whether it is read, so a shape one accepts and the other declines is a parse-time
		// lookup for an instance nobody interned.
		if self.atStaticEnumMember(EnumAllCaseNamesMemberName) 'allCaseNames'
			let memberTok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			return try self.emitEnumAllCaseNames(layout, range: SourceRange.ofToken(typeTok))
		end 'allCaseNames'

		// ⭐⭐ **`allCases` AND `fromRawValue` ARE BOTH *ENUM*-ONLY (WHERE `allCaseNames` IS ON BOTH), AND A
		// UNION IS REFUSED THEM IN TWO DIFFERENT SENTENCES — THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE REFERENCE'S, MEASURED, AND
		// EACH HALF HAS ITS OWN PIN.** `allCases` earns a refusal BY NAME: `allCases is not available on
		// unions`, E4006, at the TYPE token (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:19089-19091` and
		// `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:14850-14851`, which agree to the character; pinned by
		// `specs-shv2/enum-allcases.md`'s `enum-allcases.error-enum`). `fromRawValue` instead FALLS THROUGH to
		// the case lookup and comes out `unknown union case: 'fromRawValue'`, E3034
		// (`2-Parser.cs:19113-19116`; pinned by `specs-shv2/enum-full.md`'s
		// `error.fromRawValue-associated-values`). ⇒ **A change that moves either pin has merged two rules the
		// language keeps apart.**
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **THIS COMMENT USED TO RECORD THE E4006 MEASUREMENT AND THEN CONCLUDE THAT *BOTH* MEMBERS FALL
		// THROUGH — the two halves of one paragraph saying opposite things — and the code obeyed the wrong
		// half.** `allCases` sat inside a single `not layout.isUnion` gate with `fromRawValue`, so a union got
		// E3034 for it and `enum-allcases.error-enum` was red on a spec whose expectation the same paragraph
		// quotes correctly. The measurement was right; the disposition it drew was the OTHER member's.
		//
		// `allCaseNames` (above) needs no gate at all: it returns only NAMES, which every declaration has
		// (see `EnumAllCaseNamesMemberName`).
		//
		// The SYNTHESIZED `<Union>.unionCases` companion is an ENUM, which is precisely how a payload-bearing
		// union reaches this surface at all — through the hop above, never through the refusal here.
		//
		// `<Enum>.allCases` is interned by the same sweep walk `allCaseNames` is; the two are siblings in
		// every respect but their element type. A UNION never reads that instance, so the throw below cannot
		// desynchronize the sweep from this read (see `atStaticEnumMember`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE REFUSAL IS ASKED BEFORE THE CASE LOOKUP, so it now reaches a union that DECLARES a case
		// named `allCases` as well** — which is the member-name-first ordering `allCaseNames` above records as
		// a divergence from the bootstrap (whose `GetCase` runs first), extended to the one construct where
		// the gate previously hid it. Nothing in `/specs` or the corpus declares such a case, so the
		// divergence stays exactly as wide as the one already recorded; it is not a second decision.
		if self.atStaticEnumMember(EnumAllCasesMemberName) 'allCases'
			if layout.isUnion 'allCasesOnUnion'
				throw ParseError.allCasesOnUnion(typeTok.line, column: typeTok.column)
			end 'allCasesOnUnion'

			self.advance()
			return try self.emitEnumAllCases(layout, range: SourceRange.ofToken(typeTok))
		end 'allCases'

		// `<Enum>.fromRawValue(raw)` — the one member of this surface that is a CALL, and the one that
		// throws. Claimed here so the case lookup never sees it, EXCEPT on the two constructs where the
		// member does not exist and that lookup is the right answer: a UNION (per the pin above) and —
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **A STRUCT-BACKED ENUM.** `.rawValue` on such an enum denotes no VALUE in shv2 — only
		// `.rawValue.<field>` does, a select per field — so there is nothing `fromRawValue` could take as an
		// argument and nothing for it to hand back. Declining the claim here lets `enumCaseIndexOrThrow` word
		// it, which is `E3034 unknown enum case: 'fromRawValue'` at the TYPE name — the verdict BOTH
		// references reach (v1 by never synthesizing the helper and rejecting the call in
		// `classifyEnumLookupForFromRawValue`; the bootstrap the same way), and the one
		// `specs-shv2/enum-struct-backing.md`'s `error.struct-backing-fromRawValue` pins.
		//
		// ⚠ It is deliberately NOT the E2015 the other non-integer backings get
		// (`requireFromRawValueBacking`): those declare a raw value shv2 declines to SEARCH, which is a
		// different sentence from a member that is not there.
		if self.atStaticEnumMember(EnumFromRawValueMemberName) and not layout.isUnion and layout.backing != EnumBacking.structRecord 'fromRawValue'
			let memberTok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			return try self.parseEnumFromRawValue(func, layout: layout, memberTok: memberTok, range: SourceRange.ofToken(typeTok))
		end 'fromRawValue'

		// ⭐⭐ **`<Enum>.fromName(name)` — THE SIBLING LOOKUP, AND THE ONE MEMBER OF THIS SURFACE CLAIMED
		// UNCONDITIONALLY.** Every arm above it carries a gate — `allCases` is refused on a union, `fromRawValue`
		// declines the claim on a union and on a struct backing — and this one carries none, which is a fact
		// about the QUESTION and not an oversight. Those two read the case TAG column, whose meaning depends on
		// the declaration's keyword and backing; this reads the case NAME column, which every declaration has in
		// full. See `EnumFromNameMemberName` for the derivation and for both references agreeing.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED BEFORE THE CASE LOOKUP, so a declaration that names a case `fromName` loses it to the
		// member** — the member-name-first ordering `allCaseNames` records above as a divergence from the
		// bootstrap (whose `GetCase` runs first), and this is the same divergence rather than a second decision.
		// MEASURED on `enum Weird  fromName  other end` + `let w = Weird.fromName`: the bootstrap compiles it as
		// the CASE and exits 0; shv2 reads the member and refuses with **E2010** *Expected '(' but got
		// 'newline'*. shv2 therefore errs toward a REFUSAL where the reference errs toward silently picking one
		// of two readings, which is the safe direction for a collision no `/specs` case and no corpus
		// declaration contains — but the diagnostic is the parser's generic one and does not name the
		// collision, so this is a recorded divergence and not a settled rule, exactly as its sibling's is.
		if self.atStaticEnumMember(EnumFromNameMemberName) 'fromName'
			let memberTok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			return try self.parseEnumFromName(func, layout: layout, memberTok: memberTok, range: SourceRange.ofToken(typeTok))
		end 'fromName'

		let caseTok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.enumCase)

		let index = try self.enumCaseIndexOrThrow(layout, nameTok: caseTok, posTok: typeTok)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(typeTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **`Op.doubleOp.rawValue`, `Preset.large.width`, `TestOp.mul.latency` — A MEMBER READ ON A CASE
		// NAMED THROUGH ITS TYPE.** A type-qualified case is the ONE enum reference that reaches an accessor
		// without ever being bound to a name, so nothing upstream can route it: `parseDottedPrimary`'s accessor
		// arm tests a base that is a BINDING, and the postfix loop reads `. <name>` as a method call and
		// demanded a `(` (*"Expected '(' but got 'newline'"* — MEASURED on `Mime.json.rawValue` and on
		// `Preset.large.width`, both of which the oracle compiles). So the hop is claimed here and answered by
		// the SAME emitter a binding's accessor uses — one dispatch, not a second one free to disagree.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **IT IS ASKED *BEFORE* THE REPRESENTATION SPLIT BELOW, AND THAT IS WHAT LETS A BOXED UNION REACH
		// IT.** The case is already resolved, so its tag is a compile-time constant and no value need exist
		// (`emitEnumCaseTagConstant`) — which is the whole difference between reading `TestOp.mul`'s metadata
		// and CONSTRUCTING `TestOp.mul`, an act that would demand the `dest`/`src` arguments the read has none
		// of. `/specs/union-struct-backing.md` writes exactly that read.
		//
		// ⚠ **NOTHING IS ALLOCATED ON THIS PATH, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT THE DECLINED LIFETIME QUESTION.**
		// `Shape.circle(1).rawValue` — a member read on a CONSTRUCTED rvalue box — keeps its existing
		// *"Expected '('"* routing, because there the box IS built and enrolled as an owned temporary, and
		// reading one field out of it asks who drops it and when. That question is still nobody's; this path
		// never puts it.
		//
		// ⚠ **A TRAILING `(` LEAVES IT ALONE, which is how a binding's accessor is separated from a same-named
		// METHOD too** — there by `parseDottedPrimary` asking `methodCallsAt` first, here by
		// `atEnumInstanceAccessor` stating it, because no earlier arm claims the type-qualified call shape.
		if self.atEnumInstanceAccessor(layout, index: self.pos) 'accessorHop'
			return try self.consumeEnumAccessorHop(layout, tag: self.emitEnumCaseTagConstant(layout, index: index, range: range), anchorTok: typeTok)
		end 'accessorHop'

		// ⭐ A PAYLOAD-BEARING union is HEAP-BOXED, and EVERY case of it takes the boxed form — a
		// payload-free case (`Container.empty`) is a bare tag in a box, so the box lifetime is uniform
		// (`isBoxed` decides once, per the union, not per the case). A payload-free enum/union stays
		// the bare-i64-tag representation P1.1b emits.
		if layout.isBoxed() 'boxedUnion'
			return try self.parseEnumBoxConstruct(func, layout: layout, caseIndex: index, caseTok: caseTok, range: range)
		end 'boxedUnion'

		// A payload-free enum/union case carries no value list. `Color.red(5)` is a payload on a
		// construct that declares none — refused rather than silently dropped.
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'unexpectedPayload'
			let parenTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a payload on case '{String.from(caseTok.value)}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}`, which declares no payload on any case", line: parenTok.line, column: parenTok.column)
		end 'unexpectedPayload'

		// An unboxed case's VALUE is its tag, which is the unboxed arm of the very function the hop above
		// takes its tag from — so the two readings are one line and cannot come apart.
		return self.emitEnumCaseTagConstant(layout, index: index, range: range)
	end 'parseEnumLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **`. <member>` — THE TWO-TOKEN SHAPE ITSELF, WRITTEN ONCE FOR BOTH SPELLINGS OF AN ENUM ACCESSOR.**
	// A binding's (`op.rawValue`, `enumAccessorFieldAt`) and a type-qualified case's (`Op.doubleOp.rawValue`,
	// `atEnumInstanceAccessor`) read the identical dot-then-member test at two different offsets, and the
	// OFFSET ARITHMETIC is what must be written once: `index` is the dot and `index + 1` is the member, and a
	// door that spells that itself is free to classify the wrong token.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE TWO DOORS NOW ADMIT THE SAME MEMBER SET AGAIN — ONE RULE, `enumMemberIsReadable`.** For
	// one rung they did not: the BINDING door admitted a struct backing's FIELD (`p.width`) while the
	// type-qualified one admitted only the three accessors, and the header here argued that each door should
	// supply its own admission rule. **`/specs/union-struct-backing.md` writes `TestOp.mul.latency`, which is
	// that missing half**, and the oracle compiles the enum spelling of it (`Preset.large.width` ⇒ 9,
	// MEASURED) — so the asymmetry was a gap wearing a design's clothes. What is shared is now the shape AND
	// the rule; what stays each door's own is the `(` exclusion, which genuinely differs (see
	// `atEnumInstanceAccessor`).
	function enumMemberTokenAt(index TokenIndex) returns Token
		return self.tokenAt(index + 1)
	end 'enumMemberTokenAt'

	// Is there a `. <member>` at `index` at all — the shape both doors open with, before either asks whether
	// the member is one IT admits.
	function enumMemberReadFollowsAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.dot
	end 'enumMemberReadFollowsAt'

	// Is there a `. <member>` at `index` naming something readable off a value of `layout`, and is it NOT a
	// call — the tail of `Op.doubleOp.rawValue`, of `Preset.large.width`, of `TestOp.mul.latency`? The
	// admission rule is `enumMemberIsReadable`, shared with the binding door; the `(` exclusion is genuinely
	// this door's own, because a BINDING's accessor gets it from `parseDottedPrimary` asking `methodCallsAt`
	// first, while nothing upstream claims the type-qualified `Color.red.name()` shape.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE MEMBER SET IT NOW ADMITS, ENUMERATED — because the last two rungs each widened a predicate and
	// each admitted one member its premise was false for.** `enumMemberIsReadable` says yes to exactly two
	// things: one of the three accessors the language defines on every enum VALUE, and — on a `structRecord`
	// backing whose struct some file DECLARES — a field of that struct. The second is the new half. Both are
	// answered from the case's TAG and nothing else (`emitEnumInstanceAccessor`), and the tag of a
	// type-qualified case is a compile-time constant in BOTH representations
	// (`emitEnumCaseTagConstant`) — so unlike `.ordinal`'s measured defect there is no value here whose
	// representation the answer could be wrong about, boxed or not.
	function atEnumInstanceAccessor(layout EnumLayout, index TokenIndex) returns bool
		if not self.enumMemberReadFollowsAt(index) 'notAMemberRead'
			return false
		end 'notAMemberRead'

		if self.enumMemberTrailerAt(index) == TokenKind.leftParen 'aCall'
			return false
		end 'aCall'

		return self.enumMemberIsReadable(layout, memberTok: self.enumMemberTokenAt(index))
	end 'atEnumInstanceAccessor'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THE CURSOR ON AN ACCESSOR HOP READABLE OFF `value`?** ONE door for the two callers that hold a
	// VALUE rather than a binding or a type name: `parsePostfix`, where the receiver is whatever the
	// expression just built, and `emitEnumBackingFieldSelect`, whose selected leaf may itself be enum-typed.
	// Both spelled these gates out, and the field-chain fix was about to spell them a third time.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ANSWERS `bool` AND NEVER TOUCHES `enumMatchFor`, AND THE LADDER IS WHY.** A `DeclaredEnum` is a
	// union VALUE that heap-allocates even to say `undeclared` — the cost `valueIsUnion` and
	// `enumAccessorFieldAt` both arrange their gates around — and this is asked at EVERY `.` in every postfix
	// chain in the program. Written to RETURN one, it charged **+288 allocations at rung 0 of the scale
	// ladder, doubling at every rung**; the same reading survived putting `containsEnum` in front of the
	// lookup, because the box was the RETURN and not the query. So the three cheap tests — token shape, the
	// value's scalar tag column, and a set membership over bytes already in hand — stand in front, and the
	// only allocating call left is `enumOf` on a value that really is an enum. The caller re-derives the
	// LAYOUT on the taken path, which is `enumAccessorFieldAt`'s own arrangement with `parseEnumAccessor`.
	function atEnumAccessorHopOn(value ValueId) returns bool
		if not self.enumMemberReadFollowsAt(self.pos) 'noMemberFollows'
			return false
		end 'noMemberFollows'

		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedValue'
			return false
		end 'notANamedValue'

		return self.atEnumAccessorHopOfType(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)))
	end 'atEnumAccessorHopOn'

	// ⭐ **THE TAIL BOTH DOORS SHARE — is the cursor on an accessor hop readable off a value whose TYPE is
	// spelled `typeName`?** `atEnumAccessorHopOn` reaches it from a VALUE's tag column and
	// `enumAccessorFollowsFieldTyped` from a FIELD's declared type; past the name they are the same two
	// questions, so they are asked here once. Written twice, one door could come to admit a member the other
	// refuses — and the two decide the SAME hop from opposite sides of one field load.
	//
	// ⚠ **`containsEnum` BEFORE `enumOf`, and it is the allocation guard both callers' headers record**: a
	// `DeclaredEnum` heap-allocates even to say `undeclared`, and this runs at every `.` in every postfix
	// chain and at every intermediate field hop. The membership test is over bytes already in hand.
	function atEnumAccessorHopOfType(typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.containsEnum(typeName) 'notAnEnumName'
			return false
		end 'notAnEnumName'

		return self.atEnumInstanceAccessor(requireEnumLayout(self.signatures.enumOf(typeName)), index: self.pos)
	end 'atEnumAccessorHopOfType'

	// The kind of the token FOLLOWING the member — `index + 2`, the third of the three this shape spans, and
	// the one the `(` exclusion tests. It sits beside `enumMemberTokenAt` on that helper's own argument: the
	// OFFSET ARITHMETIC is what must be written once, and a door spelling `+ 2` itself is free to classify the
	// wrong token. `+ 1` lived here and `+ 2` did not, which is that argument one offset short.
	function enumMemberTrailerAt(index TokenIndex) returns TokenKind
		return self.kindAt(index + 2)
	end 'enumMemberTrailerAt'

	// ⭐⭐ **CONSUME THE `. <member>` HOP AND ANSWER — THE MIRROR OF `enumMemberTokenAt`'S OFFSET ARITHMETIC,
	// AND WRITTEN ONCE FOR THE IDENTICAL REASON.** Both callers past `atEnumInstanceAccessor` had these three
	// lines verbatim, differing only in the tag expression and the anchor token: `parseEnumLiteral`'s
	// type-qualified case and `emitEnumBackingFieldSelect`'s hop past a selected enum-typed field.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COPIES COULD NOT BE MADE TO DISAGREE LOUDLY.** `consumeMemberNameToken` already admits more than
	// an identifier (a keyword, and a NUMBER rewritten into a name for a tuple member); widen the member to
	// TWO tokens and one site consumes one token too few — a parse error at a token nobody wrote, not a
	// compile error anywhere. `anchorTok` is the caller's because the RANGE is the caller's fact: the read
	// spans from the type name in one spelling and from the first field name in the other.
	function consumeEnumAccessorHop(layout EnumLayout, tag ValueId, anchorTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let memberTok = try self.consumeMemberNameToken()

		return try self.emitEnumInstanceAccessor(layout, tag: tag, memberTok: memberTok, range: SourceRange.between(anchorTok, last: memberTok))
	end 'consumeEnumAccessorHop'

	// ⭐ **CONSUME A `unionCases .` HOP AND ANSWER WITH THE COMPANION'S LAYOUT** — or hand back the layout that
	// was named, unchanged, when the cursor is on anything else.
	//
	// ⚠ **A COMPANION THE SWEEP DID NOT SYNTHESIZE IS AN ORDINARY UNKNOWN CASE, NOT A PANIC.** `unionCases` is
	// synthesized only for a union with associated values (`appendUnionCasesCompanion`), so writing
	// `Color.unionCases.red` on a payload-free enum finds no registered companion — and the honest answer is
	// the one the reader can act on: E3034 at the type name, exactly as any other member that names no case
	// gets. So the hop is NOT consumed when the companion is absent, and the case lookup below reports it.
	function unionCasesCompanionOf(layout EnumLayout, typeTok Token) returns EnumLayout
		if not self.atUnionCasesAccess() 'notTheCompanion'
			return layout
		end 'notTheCompanion'

		let found = self.signatures.enumOf(unionCasesCompanionName(layout.name))
		if not enumMatchIsDeclared(found) 'noCompanionRegistered'
			return layout
		end 'noCompanionRegistered'

		self.advance()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot) otherwise panic("unionCasesCompanionOf: `atUnionCasesAccess` required a `.` after `{String.from(UnionCasesMemberName)}` at `{String.from(typeTok.value)}` and it is not there — the predicate and this consumption read the same two tokens")

		return requireEnumLayout(found)
	end 'unionCasesCompanionOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **MATERIALIZE `<Enum>.allCases` — an OWNED `Array with <Enum>` of every case value, in declaration
	// order.** `emitEnumAllCaseNames`' twin one column over, and deliberately built the same way: what the
	// member denotes is a VALUE of a type the language already has, so `.count()`, `.get(0)` and
	// `for c in …` reach it through the ordinary machinery with no accessor protocol of its own.
	//
	// ⭐ **ITS ELEMENTS NEED NO MOVE-IN, WHERE `allCaseNames`' DO.** A case value is a bare i64 tag — a scalar
	// the array copies by value — so there is no ownership to transfer; the names member has to promote each
	// borrowed literal because the array's element destructor releases every live slot.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE IS READ BACK, NEVER FIRST INTERNED HERE** — `atSetFromConstruction`'s discipline, and
	// `emitEnumAllCaseNames`' note in full: a parse-time intern would be the first write to a shared registry
	// mid-parse and the gid would depend on parse order. `ProgramSignatures.internEnumAllCasesInstances`
	// interns one per declared enum for any program that names the member.
	function emitEnumAllCases(layout EnumLayout, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let giid = self.signatures.builtinInstanceForNamedElement(self.signatures.arrayRecordBaseName(), element: layout.name)
		let arr = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)

		for i in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			self.emitArrayPush(arr, element: self.emitEnumTagLiteral(layout.name, tag: layout.tagOfIndex(i), range: range), range: range)
		end 'eachCase'

		self.trackOwnedTemp(arr, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return arr
	end 'emitEnumAllCases'

	// ⭐⭐ **`<Enum>.fromRawValue(raw)` — THE REVERSE LOOKUP, EMITTED AS A CALL TO THE PER-ENUM Std HELPER.**
	// The parser's whole job here is the argument and the two refusals; the search itself is
	// `Runtime/EnumLookupRuntime.maxon`'s, which is where the reason it must be a call is written down.
	//
	// ⚠ **ENUM-ONLY, WHICH IS THE REFERENCE'S RULE AND `union-scalar-backing.md`'s COMMITTED PROSE.** A union
	// answers `unknown union case: 'fromRawValue'` (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:19113`, and that spec's
	// own documentation section says so outright), so the member is claimed only for an enum and a union
	// falls through to the case lookup that already words it that way. The SYNTHESIZED companion is an enum,
	// which is exactly how `Shape.unionCases.fromRawValue(0)` is reached.
	function parseEnumFromRawValue(func IrFunction, layout EnumLayout, memberTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireFromRawValueBacking(layout, memberTok: memberTok)

		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let argTok = self.current()
		let raw = try self.parseValueExpression(func)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// The argument's domain is the backing's, and past the guard above that domain is `int`. The sentence
		// and the anchor are the reference's (`constants.md`'s `error.fromRawValue-type-mismatch` pins
		// `type mismatch: 'expected int, got String'`), reached through the shared renderer so this position
		// and the payload one cannot come to word one rejection two ways.
		if not tagIsIntegral(self.tagOf(raw)) 'notANumber'
			throw ParseError.typeMismatchExpectedGot(typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.integer), got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(raw), line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
		end 'notANumber'

		return self.emitRuntimeCall(enumFromRawValueSymbol(layout.name), args: singletonArgs(raw), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.named, resultNameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(layout.name), range: range)
	end 'parseEnumFromRawValue'

	// ⭐ **WHICH BACKINGS `fromRawValue` IS BUILT FOR, AND WHY THE OTHER THREE ARE A REFUSAL RATHER THAN A
	// SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** The helper searches the layout's TAG column, which is the raw-value column only
	// for an `integer` backing and for an unnumbered enum (whose raw value IS its declaration index). A
	// STRING backing's tags are declaration indices and its raw values are text; a FLOAT or CHARACTER raw
	// value is not an i64 argument at all; and a FUNCTION raw value has no literal spelling to look up by at
	// all — a code address is not something a program can write down, which is why the reference refuses that
	// backing here too (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:19889`). Each of those needs a
	// differently-typed parameter and a different comparison, and no committed case exercises one — so they
	// are named and refused here, which is strictly better than the `unknown enum case: 'fromRawValue'` they
	// got before this rung.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE STRUCT BACKING IS NOT ONE OF THEM, AND ITS ANSWER IS AN ABSENCE RATHER THAN A REFUSAL** — see
	// the claim site in `parseEnumLiteral`, which never lets one reach here. The distinction is real: every
	// backing named below HAS a raw value a program can write down and shv2 declines to search FOR it, while a
	// struct-backed enum has no `.rawValue` VALUE at all in shv2 (only `.rawValue.<field>`, a select per
	// field), so there is no argument `fromRawValue` could take and no member for it to be.
	function requireFromRawValueBacking(layout EnumLayout, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		match layout.backing 'backing'
			integer then break 'backing'
			unresolved then break 'backing'
			float to functionRef then throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(EnumFromRawValueMemberName)}` on `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}`, which is {enumBackingNoun(layout.backing, structName: layout.backingStructName)}-backed — shv2's lookup searches the case TAGS, which carry the declared raw values only for an int-backed or unnumbered enum", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
			structRecord then panic("requireFromRawValueBacking: `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}` is struct-backed and reached the `{String.from(EnumFromRawValueMemberName)}` guard — `parseEnumLiteral` does not CLAIM the member for that backing, so such a call is an unknown case (E3034) and never arrives here")
		end 'backing'
	end 'requireFromRawValueBacking'

	// ⭐⭐ **`<Enum>.fromName(name)` / `<Union>.fromName("case", payload…)` — THE LOOKUP BY CASE NAME,
	// EMITTED AS A CALL TO A PER-DECLARATION Std HELPER.** Its sibling `fromRawValue`'s shape exactly — the
	// search is `Runtime/EnumLookupRuntime.maxon`'s and the reason it must be a call is written down there —
	// with the whole of the difference in one place: the argument is a String, so no backing is refused and no
	// keyword is excluded (see `EnumFromNameMemberName`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A LITERAL NAME IS RESOLVED AT COMPILE TIME, AND STILL EMITS THE CALL.** Both references validate
	// a literal against the case list and report an unknown one as E3034 rather than leaving it to a run-time
	// throw (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:7746-7748`,
	// `maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:19026-19028`), and `/specs/constants.md`'s
	// `error.fromName-invalid-case` pins that verdict. The CALL stays because `try` binds to one: a resolved
	// literal simply makes the helper's throw unreachable, which costs the compare chain and nothing else.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ANCHOR FOR EVERY REFUSAL HERE IS THE MEMBER TOKEN, AND THE TWO REFERENCES DISAGREE ABOUT IT —
	// the SPEC decides, and it decides for the bootstrap.** The bootstrap anchors all three of this member's
	// refusals at `methodToken` (`2-Parser.cs:7726`, `:7747`, `:7755`); v1 anchors them at the ARGUMENT's range
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:19022-19035`, `argRange0`). `/specs/constants.md` pins column 25
	// of `let _d = try Direction.fromName("…")` and `/specs/enum-full.md` column 25 of
	// `let _c = try Container.fromName("value")` — both the `fromName` token, so v1 is simply wrong here, which
	// is consistent with its own note that the `fromName-associated-*` fragments were never whitelisted.
	//
	// ⚠ It is deliberately NOT the TYPE token a bare `Color.green` case refusal uses: what the author got
	// wrong is an argument to this member, not the enum they named.
	function parseEnumFromName(func IrFunction, layout EnumLayout, memberTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)

		if self.atLiteralCaseNameArgument() 'compileTimeName'
			return try self.parseEnumFromNameLiteral(func, layout: layout, memberTok: memberTok, range: range)
		end 'compileTimeName'

		let name = try self.parseValueExpression(func)
		// The argument's domain is `String`, whatever the declaration is — read through the shared renderer so
		// this position and the literal one cannot come to word one rejection two ways. The sentence is the
		// bootstrap's (`2-Parser.cs:7724-7726`).
		if not tagIsText(self.tagOf(name)) 'notAString'
			throw ParseError.typeMismatchExpectedGot(typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.string), got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(name), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'notAString'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		return self.emitEnumFromNameCall(layout, args: singletonArgs(name), range: range)
	end 'parseEnumFromName'

	// ⭐ **IS THE ARGUMENT A BARE STRING LITERAL — the shape whose case is a COMPILE-TIME fact?** A
	// `stringLiteral` token standing immediately before the `)` or the `,` IS the whole argument: there is no
	// operator it could be the left side of (a following `stringInterpStart` would make it an interpolation,
	// and an interpolation's first token cannot be followed by either of these). Asked at the CURSOR rather
	// than off the emitted op, because the literal path must decide BEFORE it emits — an unknown name and a
	// wrong arity are refusals that leave no code behind.
	function atLiteralCaseNameArgument() returns bool
		if not self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'notALiteral'
			return false
		end 'notALiteral'

		let after = self.peekKind()
		return after == TokenKind.rightParen or after == TokenKind.comma
	end 'atLiteralCaseNameArgument'

	// The compile-time half: resolve the literal to a case, check the payload arity the call supplies against
	// the one that case declares, and emit the call the resolution earned — the general helper for a
	// payload-free case, the per-case one for a payload-bearing case (see `synthesizeEnumFromNameCase`).
	function parseEnumFromNameLiteral(func IrFunction, layout EnumLayout, memberTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let nameTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.stringLiteral)
		let wanted = try self.decodedStringLiteralBytes(nameTok)

		let index = layout.indexOfCase(wanted)
		if index >= layout.caseCount() 'noSuchCase'
			throw ParseError.enumNoCaseNamed(layout.kindWord(), caseName: String.from(wanted), typeName: String.from(layout.name), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'noSuchCase'

		if layout.payloadArityAt(index) == 0 'payloadFree'
			// A payload-free case takes the name and nothing else; anything further is the arity refusal
			// below's business, stated once for both directions.
			try self.requireNoFurtherPayloadArgs(wanted, expected: 0 as CaseIndex, memberTok: memberTok)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
			return self.emitEnumFromNameCall(layout, args: singletonArgs(self.emitStringLiteral(wanted, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))), range: range)
		end 'payloadFree'

		return try self.parseEnumFromNameCase(func, layout: layout, caseIndex: index, nameTok: nameTok, memberTok: memberTok, range: range)
	end 'parseEnumFromNameLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **`<Union>.fromName("value", 42)` — A LITERAL NAME NAMING A PAYLOAD-BEARING CASE.** The payload
	// arguments are read, TYPE-CHECKED and MOVED IN here, at the parser, through the very doors
	// `Container.value(42)` goes through (`requirePayloadLowerable`, `requirePayloadArgType`,
	// `moveInPayload`), and the synthesized helper receives values that are already owned and stores them
	// verbatim. That split is deliberate: ownership is a fact about the CALLER's bindings — which local was
	// poisoned, which temporary stopped owing a drop — and a Std-tier body can see none of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENTS ARE POSITIONAL, WHERE `Container.value(n: 42)`'s ARE NOT.** A construct's payload list
	// follows the call-arg rule (first positional, the rest named, slotted by payload FIELD name); this list is
	// `fromName`'s own, and the bootstrap reads it as a plain positional tail — one `Expect(Comma)` plus one
	// `ParseExpression` per declared slot (`2-Parser.cs:7758-7760`). v1 states no rule of its own: it rewrites
	// the CALLEE of an already-parsed call and inherits whatever that call's argument syntax allowed
	// (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Parser.maxon:19005`, whose `args` arrive from the caller). No spec exercises
	// a labelled one, so shv2 takes the reference that HAS a rule; inventing a second slotting rule for a
	// spelling nothing writes would be a divergence with no measurement behind it.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RESULT IS MINTED BEFORE THE ARGUMENTS ARE MOVED IN**, because an opaque slot's move-in has to
	// name the record that will own it and that record IS this call's result — see `emitRuntimeCallInto`.
	function parseEnumFromNameCase(func IrFunction, layout EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, nameTok Token, memberTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
		let arity = layout.payloadArityAt(caseIndex)
		let caseName = layout.caseNameAt(caseIndex)

		var slotValues = ValueIdArray.create()
		var slotBareLocal = BoolArray.create()
		var slotAnchors = TokenIndexArray.create()
		for _ in 0 upto arity 'eachSlot'
			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'tooFew'
				throw ParseError.enumFromNamePayloadArity(String.from(caseName), expected: arity, line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
			end 'tooFew'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)

			// The value's start is captured before it is parsed so `rhsWasBareLocalReference` can decide
			// afterwards whether it was a bare local — what a managed payload's move-in poisons. The token AFTER
			// the argument anchors that argument's own TYPE refusal, exactly as `parsePayloadArgs` does.
			let valueStart = self.pos
			slotValues.push(try self.parseValueExpression(func))
			slotBareLocal.push(self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart))
			slotAnchors.push(self.pos)
		end 'eachSlot'

		try self.requireNoFurtherPayloadArgs(caseName, expected: arity, memberTok: memberTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// Validate + classify every slot before emitting anything permanent — `emitEnumBox`'s discipline, for
		// its reason: a refused payload leaves no code behind.
		var classes = PayloadClassArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto arity 'validate'
			let field = try payloads.get(i) otherwise panic("parseEnumFromNameCase: payloadsAt returned fewer fields than its own count at {i}")
			let declared = self.signatures.payloadTypeOf(field, layout: layout, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
			let class = try self.requirePayloadLowerable(field, declared: declared, layout: layout, posTok: memberTok)
			let arg = try slotValues.get(i) otherwise panic("parseEnumFromNameCase: slotValues.get OOB at {i} — the columns are filled one per declared slot")
			let anchorIndex = try slotAnchors.get(i) otherwise panic("parseEnumFromNameCase: slotAnchors.get OOB at {i} — captured in lockstep with the values")
			try self.requirePayloadArgType(field, declared: declared, class: class, value: arg, caseTok: memberTok, argTok: self.tokenAt(anchorIndex))
			classes.push(class)
		end 'validate'

		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.named, nameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(layout.name))

		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(self.emitStringLiteral(caseName, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok)))
		for i in 0 upto arity 'moveIn'
			let class = try classes.get(i) otherwise panic("parseEnumFromNameCase: classes.get OOB at {i} — filled in lockstep with the fields")
			let arg = try slotValues.get(i) otherwise panic("parseEnumFromNameCase: slotValues.get OOB at {i} after the validation pass")
			let bareLocal = try slotBareLocal.get(i) otherwise panic("parseEnumFromNameCase: slotBareLocal.get OOB at {i} — captured in lockstep with the values")
			args.push(try self.moveInPayload(class, arg: arg, argBareLocal: bareLocal, owner: result, anchor: memberTok, range: range))
		end 'moveIn'

		return self.trackEnumFromNameResult(layout, result: self.emitRuntimeCallInto(result, callee: enumFromNameCaseSymbol(layout.name, caseName: caseName), args: args, range: range, argLabels: NoRuntimeCallLabels))
	end 'parseEnumFromNameCase'

	// A literal name has consumed its argument; anything but the closing `)` is a payload list the case does
	// not declare that many slots for. ONE refusal for both directions — see `enumFromNamePayloadArity` for
	// why over-supply is E3036 here where the bootstrap lets it fall out as a parse error.
	function requireNoFurtherPayloadArgs(caseName ByteArray, expected CaseIndex, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'nothingFurther'
			return
		end 'nothingFurther'

		throw ParseError.enumFromNamePayloadArity(String.from(caseName), expected: expected, line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
	end 'requireNoFurtherPayloadArgs'

	// Emit the GENERAL lookup call and give its result the type and the ownership the declaration says it
	// has. Its per-case sibling reaches the same two facts through `emitRuntimeCallInto` and
	// `trackEnumFromNameResult`, because it must mint the result before its arguments — which is exactly why
	// the OWNERSHIP half is a function of its own rather than a line in this one.
	function emitEnumFromNameCall(layout EnumLayout, args ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		return self.trackEnumFromNameResult(layout, result: self.emitRuntimeCall(enumFromNameSymbol(layout.name), args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.named, resultNameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(layout.name), range: range))
	end 'emitEnumFromNameCall'

	// ⭐ **A BOXED UNION'S LOOKUP HANDS BACK AN OWNED HEAP RECORD; A PAYLOAD-FREE DECLARATION'S HANDS BACK A
	// BARE TAG.** The helper allocates the box (`EnumLookupRuntime.emitBoxWithTag`), so the result owes a drop
	// exactly as a written `Container.empty` does — enrolled through the same door that construct uses, which
	// is what makes the statement-end drop and the binding's ownership one rule rather than two.
	function trackEnumFromNameResult(layout EnumLayout, result ValueId) returns ValueId
		if layout.isBoxed() 'ownsARecord'
			self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		end 'ownsARecord'
		return result
	end 'trackEnumFromNameResult'

	// ⭐⭐ **MATERIALIZE `<Enum>.allCaseNames` — an OWNED `Array with String` of the case names, in
	// declaration order.** It builds exactly what the source literal `["red", "green", "blue"]` builds, op
	// for op: one `__managed_create` over the `Array with String` instance, then one promoted String per case
	// pushed into it. That is the whole design decision — the member is a VALUE of a type the language
	// already has, so it needs no representation, no accessor protocol and no runtime helper of its own, and
	// `.count()` / `.get(0)` / `for name in …` reach it through the postfix loop like any other array.
	//
	// ⭐ **A UNION IS THE SAME QUESTION.** `caseNames` is one column whatever the declaration's keyword or a
	// case's payload arity, so `Container.value(n Integer)` contributes `"value"` and nothing else — which is
	// exactly what `/specs/enum-allcasenames.md` requires of a union with associated values.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE IS READ BACK, NEVER FIRST INTERNED HERE.** The sweep interns `Array with String` for
	// any file naming the member (`AllCaseNamesProducerBit`), so a parse-time intern would be the first write
	// to a shared registry mid-parse and the gid would depend on parse order — `atSetFromConstruction`'s
	// stated discipline, and the reason the probe walks whole files rather than the declaration loop.
	//
	// ⚠ Each name is emitted as an IMMORTAL rdata String and then PROMOTED to an owned record by the shared
	// move-in door, because the array's `element_destroy@40` stamp releases every live slot: a borrowed
	// literal handed straight in would be released by an owner it never gave a reference to. `argBareLocal`
	// is `false` and cannot be otherwise — the value was minted two lines up and names no binding to poison.
	function emitEnumAllCaseNames(layout EnumLayout, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let giid = self.signatures.builtinInstanceForElement(self.signatures.arrayRecordBaseName(), element: MaxonType.string)
		let arr = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)

		for i in 0 upto layout.caseCount() 'eachCase'
			let literal = self.emitStringLiteral(layout.caseNameAt(i), range: range)
			let owned = try self.moveManagedValueInto(literal, argBareLocal: false, isString: true, range: range)
			self.emitArrayPush(arr, element: owned, range: range)
		end 'eachCase'

		self.trackOwnedTemp(arr, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return arr
	end 'emitEnumAllCaseNames'

	// ⭐ **CONSTRUCT A BOXED UNION VALUE** — `Move.walk(Direction.east)`, `LexErr.pair(7, column: 13)`,
	// or a payload-free case of a boxed union (`Container.empty`). Parses the payload argument list into
	// LAYOUT-parallel slot columns (`parsePayloadArgs`), checks every declared slot was filled
	// (`requireAllPayloadsProvided`), and hands off to `emitEnumBox`, which validates each field's TYPE
	// (rejecting the managed/float ones slice 1 cannot lower) before it emits a single op.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE PAYLOAD ARGUMENT LIST FOLLOWS THE CALL-ARG RULE** — the first argument is positional, the
	// second and later are named (`consumeArgLabel`) — crossed with the struct literal's slot-by-name: a
	// named argument lands in the slot whose payload FIELD its label names, whatever position it was written
	// in. A case's payload is ordered by its declaration, so the first argument needs no name to find its
	// place; the rest do, exactly as a call's arguments do. This is where the two syntaxes meet, and it is
	// why `slotBareLocal` is captured per SLOT and not per source position — `emitEnumBox`/`moveInPayload`
	// reads `argsBareLocal[i]` for the field at slot `i`, so a reordering named argument must poison the
	// right source (get it wrong and a managed payload's move-in poisons a use-after-move or leaks).
	function parseEnumBoxConstruct(func IrFunction, layout EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, caseTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
		let arity = layout.payloadArityAt(caseIndex)

		// LAYOUT-parallel columns, the union twin of `parseStructLiteral`'s field columns: `slotValues[i]`
		// is the value for the payload field the case declares at slot `i`, whatever position/label the
		// construct wrote it in; `filled[i]` turns an unwritten slot into a missing-argument error rather
		// than a zero nobody chose; `slotBareLocal[i]` records whether slot `i`'s value was a bare reference
		// to a local binding — what a managed payload's move-in poisons (`moveInPayload`); `slotAnchors[i]`
		// is the token the slot's TYPE refusal is anchored at, which the type check (in `emitEnumBox`, after
		// the whole list is read) can no longer see for itself — see `parsePayloadArgs`.
		var slotValues = ValueIdArray.create()
		var filled = BoolArray.create()
		var slotBareLocal = BoolArray.create()
		var slotAnchors = TokenIndexArray.create()
		for _ in 0 upto arity 'initSlots'
			slotValues.push(0 as ValueId)
			filled.push(false)
			slotBareLocal.push(false)
			slotAnchors.push(self.pos)
		end 'initSlots'

		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'withArgs'
			try self.parsePayloadArgs(func, layout: layout, payloads: payloads, slotValues: slotValues, filled: filled, slotBareLocal: slotBareLocal, slotAnchors: slotAnchors, caseTok: caseTok)
		end 'withArgs'

		try self.requireAllPayloadsProvided(layout, payloads: payloads, filled: filled, caseTok: caseTok)

		return try self.emitEnumBox(layout, caseIndex: caseIndex, args: slotValues, argsBareLocal: slotBareLocal, argAnchors: slotAnchors, caseTok: caseTok, range: range)
	end 'parseEnumBoxConstruct'

	// Parse the `(pos, label: value, …)` payload argument list into the layout-parallel columns. The caller
	// has confirmed the `(`; this consumes it through the closing `)`. The FIRST argument is positional and
	// fills slot 0; every later argument is named and slots by the payload FIELD name it labels
	// (`payloadSlotFor`). A slot written twice is a duplicate, mirroring `parseStructLiteralFields` — a
	// payload field IS a field, so the same struct-literal diagnostics fit.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ALSO RECORDS EACH SLOT'S DIAGNOSTIC ANCHOR, AND ONLY THIS FUNCTION CAN.** A payload argument's
	// TYPE is judged in `emitEnumBox`, once the whole list is read, and by then the cursor is past the
	// construct — so the position the two canonical specs pin (`,` or `)`, the token FOLLOWING the argument)
	// is not recoverable there. It is captured here, at the one moment it is `self.pos`, and by SLOT rather
	// than by source position for `slotBareLocal`'s stated reason: a named argument may fill a slot the
	// author wrote out of order, and an anchor paired with the wrong slot points at another argument.
	// It is the anchor `rejectEscapingClosure` already uses one line further down, which is why the two
	// refusals about one argument point at the same place.
	function parsePayloadArgs(func IrFunction, layout EnumLayout, payloads PayloadFieldArray, slotValues ValueIdArray, filled BoolArray, slotBareLocal BoolArray, slotAnchors TokenIndexArray, caseTok Token) throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)

		// A case that declares NO payload takes no argument LIST — it is written `U.empty`, never `U.empty()`
		// or `U.empty(x)` (both reference compilers reject the parens). Rejected before any argument is
		// parsed, because the columns are empty and every slot lookup below would be out of bounds. A
		// payload-free case with no parens skips this function entirely and constructs a bare-tag box.
		if payloads.count() == 0 'noPayload'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a payload argument list on case '{String.from(caseTok.value)}' of `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}`, which declares no payload for that case", line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'noPayload'

		var argIndex = 0 as ParamCount
		while true 'argLoop'
			// The label token anchors an unknown/duplicate-label diagnostic; captured before `consumeArgLabel`
			// consumes it, exactly as `parseCallArgs` captures `argFirstTok`.
			let labelTok = self.current()
			let label = try self.consumeArgLabel(argIndex, rule: ArgLabelRule.ordinary)
			let slot = try self.payloadSlotFor(layout, payloads: payloads, argIndex: argIndex, label: label, labelTok: labelTok)

			let alreadyFilled = try filled.get(slot) otherwise panic("parsePayloadArgs: filled.get OOB at slot {slot} — the columns are sized to the case's arity and payloadSlotFor returns an in-range slot")
			if alreadyFilled 'duplicate'
				throw ParseError.duplicateFieldInitializer(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(label), line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
			end 'duplicate'

			// `parseValueExpression` (the climber plus a trailing ternary), not `parseExpression`, for
			// `parseStructLiteralFields`' reason: an argument is parsed INSIDE an expression that already
			// seeded `currentBlock`. The value's start position is
			// captured first so `rhsWasBareLocalReference` can decide, after the value is parsed, whether it
			// was a bare local — a managed payload's move-in poisons that source.
			let valueStart = self.pos
			let value = try self.parseValueExpression(func)

			// ⭐ **THE TOKEN AFTER THE ARGUMENT — READ ONCE, AND BOTH REFUSALS ABOUT THIS ARGUMENT TAKE IT.** The
			// escaping-closure reject is positioned there (matching its golden) and so is the TYPE refusal
			// (`emitEnumBox`, through `slotAnchors`). Taking `self.current()` for one and `self.pos` for the
			// other five lines apart made that agreement a coincidence of two reads of a cursor nothing pins
			// between them; one binding makes it structural.
			let afterArg = self.pos

			// ⭐ **ESCAPE (P1.5-A2b): a capturing closure may not fill a union PAYLOAD** — the payload is a heap
			// box slot that outlives the frame, so it would carry a closure whose env is gone (exactly a struct
			// field, one indirection out).
			let payloadField = try payloads.get(slot) otherwise panic("parsePayloadArgs: payloads.get OOB at slot {slot} — the columns are sized to the case's arity")
			try self.rejectEscapingClosure(value, message: "cannot store a closure that captures in payload '{String.from(payloadField.name)}' of case '{String.from(caseTok.value)}': {CapturingClosureEscapeSuffix}", tok: self.tokenAt(afterArg))

			try slotValues.set(slot, value: value) otherwise panic("parsePayloadArgs: slotValues.set OOB at slot {slot} — the columns are sized to the case's arity")
			try filled.set(slot, value: true) otherwise panic("parsePayloadArgs: filled.set OOB at slot {slot} — the columns are sized to the case's arity")
			try slotBareLocal.set(slot, value: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart)) otherwise panic("parsePayloadArgs: slotBareLocal.set OOB at slot {slot} — the columns are sized to the case's arity")
			try slotAnchors.set(slot, value: afterArg) otherwise panic("parsePayloadArgs: slotAnchors.set OOB at slot {slot} — the columns are sized to the case's arity")

			argIndex = argIndex + 1

			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endArgs'
				break
			end 'endArgs'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		end 'argLoop'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
	end 'parsePayloadArgs'

	// The layout SLOT a payload argument fills. Argument 0 is positional (`consumeArgLabel` guaranteed it
	// carries no label) and fills slot 0 in declaration order — the payload twin of a call's first argument
	// filling parameter 0. A later argument is named and slots by the payload FIELD name it labels; a label
	// naming no field of this case is an unknown-field error, mirroring `requireFieldIndex`. Slot 0 is in
	// range because a payload-free case never reaches the argument loop (`parsePayloadArgs` rejects its
	// parens before any lookup).
	function payloadSlotFor(layout EnumLayout, payloads PayloadFieldArray, argIndex ParamCount, label ByteArray, labelTok Token) returns CaseIndex throws ParseError
		if argIndex == 0 'positional'
			return 0 as CaseIndex
		end 'positional'

		for i in 0 upto payloads.count() 'each'
			let field = try payloads.get(i) otherwise panic("payloadSlotFor: payloadsAt returned fewer fields than its own count at {i}")
			if field.name.equals(label) 'match'
				return i
			end 'match'
		end 'each'

		throw ParseError.unknownField(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(label), line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
	end 'payloadSlotFor'

	// Every declared payload slot must have been given a value. A union payload has NO defaults (unlike a
	// struct field, so there is no `applyFieldDefaults` step here), which makes an omitted slot always an
	// error — reported at the FIRST unfilled slot in declaration order, mirroring `requireAllFieldsInitialized`,
	// so the message is a function of the case rather than of a hash walk. Anchored at the case name.
	function requireAllPayloadsProvided(layout EnumLayout, payloads PayloadFieldArray, filled BoolArray, caseTok Token) throws ParseError
		for i in 0 upto payloads.count() 'each'
			let wasFilled = try filled.get(i) otherwise panic("requireAllPayloadsProvided: filled.get OOB at {i} — the column is sized to the case's arity")
			if wasFilled 'given'
				continue
			end 'given'
			let field = try payloads.get(i) otherwise panic("requireAllPayloadsProvided: payloadsAt returned fewer fields than its own count at {i}")
			throw ParseError.fieldNotInitialized(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(field.name), line: caseTok.line, column: caseTok.column)
		end 'each'
	end 'requireAllPayloadsProvided'

	// Emit the box: validate + classify every payload field (reject a float payload, type-check each
	// argument) BEFORE any op, then `__mm_alloc` the `8 + maxArity*8` bytes, store the case TAG at
	// offset 0, store each payload at `8 + slot*8`, and ZERO-FILL the unused slots `[arity, maxArity)`.
	//
	// ⭐ The box is TAGGED `named(<union>)` — the same tag a scalar enum value carries, so `match`
	// recognizes it through the one `enumMatchFor` door — and enrolled as an OWNED HEAP TEMPORARY
	// (`trackOwnedTemp`), so `parseBinding` binds its drop to scope exit and an unbound `Move.walk(5)`
	// statement is freed at statement end.
	//
	// ⭐ **A MANAGED PAYLOAD IS MOVED IN** (`moveInPayload`) — no incref, no copy of an owned value. The
	// box owns the payload after the store; the source binding is poisoned (E3102) and an owned temporary
	// is removed from the statement's pending drops. A wholly-scalar union keeps slice 1's trivial
	// destructor; a managed one drops through `__destruct_<U>` (see `emitDecref`/`synthesizeUnionDestructor`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ZERO-FILL IS MANDATORY** even though the slab returns zeroed memory: it pins a
	// deterministic 0 in a narrow case's unused slots, and a NULL slot is exactly what the cascade's
	// null-guard skips — so a zeroed managed slot is never decref'd as a bogus pointer.
	function emitEnumBox(layout EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, args ValueIdArray, argsBareLocal BoolArray, argAnchors TokenIndexArray, caseTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let payloads = layout.payloadsAt(caseIndex)

		// Validate + classify every field before emitting a single op — a refused payload emits no code.
		var classes = PayloadClassArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto payloads.count() 'validate'
			let field = try payloads.get(i) otherwise panic("emitEnumBox: payloadsAt returned fewer fields than its own count at {i}")
			let declared = self.signatures.payloadTypeOf(field, layout: layout, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
			let class = try self.requirePayloadLowerable(field, declared: declared, layout: layout, posTok: caseTok)
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("emitEnumBox: args.count() was checked to equal the arity but get OOB at {i}")
			let anchorIndex = try argAnchors.get(i) otherwise panic("emitEnumBox: argAnchors.get OOB at {i} — captured in lockstep with the args")
			try self.requirePayloadArgType(field, declared: declared, class: class, value: arg, caseTok: caseTok, argTok: self.tokenAt(anchorIndex))
			classes.push(class)
		end 'validate'

		let size = self.emitLiteral(layout.boxSizeBytes(), valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let box = self.emitBoxAlloc(size, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.named, resultNameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(layout.name), range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(box, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)

		self.emitUnionTagStore(box, tag: layout.tagOfIndex(caseIndex), range: range)

		for i in 0 upto payloads.count() 'storeEach'
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("emitEnumBox: args.get OOB at {i} after the arity check")
			let class = try classes.get(i) otherwise panic("emitEnumBox: classes.get OOB at {i} — it is filled in lockstep with the fields")
			let bareLocal = try argsBareLocal.get(i) otherwise panic("emitEnumBox: argsBareLocal.get OOB at {i} — captured in lockstep with the args")
			let stored = try self.moveInPayload(class, arg: arg, argBareLocal: bareLocal, owner: box, anchor: caseTok, range: range)
			self.emitIndirectStore(box, offset: layout.payloadSlotOffset(i), value: stored, storageType: payloadStorageOf(class), range: range)
		end 'storeEach'

		// ⚠ **A DIAGNOSTIC NOW RESTS ON THIS LOOP.** E3129 admits `stay or walk(dir) gives dir` — a binding
		// read on the path of a case that declares no such slot — because this fill pins a deterministic 0
		// there, and `Parser.zeroInhabitsPayloadType` decides whether that 0 is a value of the binding's
		// type. It is THIS store the guarantee rests on, not the slab's zeroing (which carries a
		// "a future free-list must re-zero" caveat this does not); the union cloner preserves it by blitting
		// the whole box (`MmRuntime.synthesizeUnionCloner`). Removing the fill silently un-defines that spec.
		for j in payloads.count() upto layout.maxPayloadArity() 'zeroFill'
			let zero = self.emitLiteral(0 as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
			self.emitIndirectStore(box, offset: layout.payloadSlotOffset(j), value: zero, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		end 'zeroFill'

		return box
	end 'emitEnumBox'

	// ⭐ **MOVE A MANAGED VALUE INTO DURABLE STORAGE** — a union payload slot or a struct field, the
	// construct half of static single-owner ownership and the mirror of `parseBinding`'s move-on-bind. A
	// scalar is stored as-is. A managed value is stored so the box holds exactly ONE reference to it:
	//   • a BORROWED String literal/param is PROMOTED to an owned heap copy first (`promoteToOwnedString`),
	//     so the box owns a droppable payload — the copy is a fresh temp, removed from the pending list;
	//   • an already-OWNED value (an owned String, an interpolation temp, an owned struct/union box, a
	//     CONSUMED param — one the analysis enrolled as owned) is transferred: a bare-local reference is
	//     POISONED (E3102 on later use), an owned temporary is removed from the pending drops. No incref, no
	//     copy.
	//   • a BORROWED struct/union/existential value (a borrowed param, a borrowed field read, a non-consumed
	//     argument) has NO STATIC clone, so it is CO-OWNED instead — the box takes its own reference and the
	//     borrow's owner is untouched (`coOwnBorrowedForConsume`, the transitive-consume ruling of
	//     2026-08-04). (A String is the one managed kind with a promote, so a borrowed String takes a real
	//     copy rather than a second reference — and an EXISTENTIAL whose conformer IS a String takes that same
	//     copy at run time, through its witness, which is why `coOwnBorrowedForConsume` answers with a value
	//     rather than with the one it was handed.)
	//   • a slot declared at the enclosing generic's own TYPE PARAMETER takes its reference at RUN TIME
	//     (`moveOpaqueValueInto`, W41-opaque) — the shared body cannot name the protocol, but the record it
	//     fills is concrete at the caller and its destructor drops the slot regardless.
	// `owner` is the RECORD the slot belongs to — passed so the one door that takes a descriptor-mediated
	// reference can record the release obligation ON THE RECORD, rather than a caller inferring "a reference
	// was taken" from the returned value differing (which the substituted-concrete arm also does).
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS SIBLING IS `writeBackPayload`, AND THE TWO PROTOCOLS DIFFER WHILE THE INVARIANT DOES NOT.** This
	// is the MOVE: the box becomes the value's SOLE owner, the source is poisoned or de-listed, no incref.
	// A write-back is the RETAIN: the binding keeps its own reference and lives on for the rest of the arm, so
	// the box takes a SECOND one. Both leave the box holding exactly one reference it will release through
	// `__destruct_<U>`, which is why they agree — but a caller reaching for the wrong one either strands a
	// reference or frees the binding's value out from under it.
	function moveInPayload(class PayloadClass, arg ValueId, argBareLocal bool, owner ValueId, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if payloadClassIsTypeParam(class) 'opaqueSlot'
			return try self.moveOpaqueValueInto(arg, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, owner: owner, anchor: anchor, range: range)
		end 'opaqueSlot'
		if not payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'scalar'
			return arg
		end 'scalar'
		return try self.moveManagedValueInto(arg, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, isString: payloadClassIsString(class), range: range)
	end 'moveInPayload'

	// ⭐⭐ **MOVE A VALUE INTO A SLOT DECLARED AT THE ENCLOSING GENERIC'S OWN TYPE PARAMETER (W41-opaque)** —
	// `moveManagedValueInto`'s twin for the one slot kind whose protocol is not a compile-time fact. The split
	// it makes is the SAME split: an already-OWNED value is TRANSFERRED (a consumed feed parameter moved into
	// `Self{value: v}` or into an opaque array — the caller already handed over its `+1`, so a reference taken
	// here would leak it), and a BORROWED one is CO-OWNED. Only the second arm is new, and only it could ever
	// have been right: a body reading `items.get(i)` holds a borrow the array still owns.
	//
	// ⚠ **A VALUE WHOSE TAG IS NO LONGER THE OPAQUE PARAMETER HAS BEEN SUBSTITUTED**, and then the static
	// protocol is both available and strictly better — a String COPIES rather than dispatching to `__str_clone`
	// through a load, and the two answers are the same answer. It is routed through the ordinary doors rather
	// than through the descriptor so that a body which knows the type never pays a run-time dispatch for it.
	function moveOpaqueValueInto(arg ValueId, argBareLocal bool, owner ValueId, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.tagOf(arg) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'substitutedToAConcreteType'
			if not self.valueIsManagedHeap(arg) 'trivialConcrete'
				return arg
			end 'trivialConcrete'

			return try self.moveManagedValueInto(arg, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, isString: tagIsByteRecord(self.tagOf(arg)), range: range)
		end 'substitutedToAConcreteType'

		if self.opaqueSlotTakesItsOwnReference(arg) 'referenceThroughTheDescriptor'
			// The record becomes the reference's owner, so it owes the release if IT is dropped here — recorded at
			// the one line that takes the reference, which is what makes the store half and the drop half one act
			// rather than two that agree.
			self.markRecordOwningOpaqueSlots(owner)
			return self.coOwnBorrowedOpaque(arg, range: range)
		end 'referenceThroughTheDescriptor'

		// The slot INHERITS the reference this frame holds (`opaqueSlotTakesItsOwnReference` names the three
		// shapes and why each takes nothing). Only the OWNED one has bookkeeping left: this frame stops owing the
		// drop. The other two owe nothing here, because the reference the slot ends up holding was never this
		// frame's to give away.
		if self.valueIsOpaqueOwned(arg) 'thisFrameStopsOwningIt'
			try self.transferOwnedInto(arg, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, anchor: anchor)
		end 'thisFrameStopsOwningIt'

		return arg
	end 'moveOpaqueValueInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES AN OPAQUE `T` DESTINATION TAKE ITS OWN REFERENCE TO `value`, OR INHERIT THE ONE THIS FRAME
	// HOLDS? — THE one home of that routing question, because THREE doors ask it and a disagreement between them
	// is a double free rather than a diagnostic.** The STORE acts on the answer (`moveOpaqueValueInto`); the two
	// REPEAT guards read it to decide whether ONE value may fill TWO owning destinations in a single construct
	// (`rejectDoubleOwningStore` for `Self{a: v, b: v}`, `transferConsumedArgs` for `Pair.create(v, b: v)`, both
	// through `repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree`). A destination that takes its OWN reference may be
	// repeated — N destinations take N references and each is released by its own owner; one that INHERITS may
	// not, because the single reference this frame holds cannot answer for two owners.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THREE SHAPES INHERIT, AND "IS IT OWNED" NAMES ONLY THE FIRST.** A constructor FEED parameter and a
	// generic no `with` ever names both read as BORROWED to the ownership bit while taking no reference at all,
	// so a guard asking `valueIsOpaqueOwned` alone would admit a repeat of either — two owners of one `+1`. Each
	// arm below says why it takes nothing; the consequence for a repeat is identical across all three.
	//
	// Precondition: `value` carries the opaque `typeParameter` tag. A SUBSTITUTED value is concrete and is
	// routed through the ordinary doors, which CO-OWN and may therefore always be repeated.
	function opaqueSlotTakesItsOwnReference(value ValueId) returns bool
		// An already-OWNED value is TRANSFERRED — a consumed feed parameter moved into `Self{value: v}` or into
		// an opaque array. The caller already handed over its `+1`, so a reference taken here would leak it.
		if self.valueIsOpaqueOwned(value) 'ownedIsTransferred'
			return false
		end 'ownedIsTransferred'

		// ⭐⭐ **A `constructedValue` FEED PARAMETER IS ALREADY PAID FOR AT THE CALL SITE (W41-opaque).**
		// `Box.create(v T)`'s `Self{value: v}` is the whole of P1.6-B2's constructor feed: the CONCRETE caller
		// consumed its argument precisely because this store takes ownership of it, so a reference here would be
		// a second one and the caller's would leak. It reads as a BORROW to the ownership bit — deliberately, so
		// the shared body drops nothing (`bindParameters`) — which is exactly why it needs its own mark.
		if self.constructorFeedParams.contains(value) 'settledByTheCallSite'
			return false
		end 'settledByTheCallSite'

		// ⭐ **A GENERIC NO `with` EVER NAMES HAS NO `T` TO REFERENCE, AND THIS IS THE SAME PREDICATE THE
		// RESERVATION READ** (`genericTypeIsInstantiated`, asked here of the value's own token). One question,
		// two askers, so the descriptor the store wants and the descriptor the seed reserved are one decision —
		// where two predicates would meet as `requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores` refusing a legal program.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* "IS SOME INSTANTIATION MANAGED".** That would be free here and wrong at
		// the seed, which is computed before any body is parsed and so cannot see a CONCRETE instantiation at
		// all — see `genericTypeIsInstantiated`, which carries the measurement. Asking the weaker question at
		// both ends is what keeps them the same question.
		//
		// The zero word is what makes the retain free where it is not needed: `retainFunc@64` is 0 for an
		// instantiation whose argument owns NO RECORD — an `int`/`bool`/`float`, a ranged alias, a payload-free
		// enum — so `__retain_type_param` loads it, skips the `callIndirect` and hands back exactly the value
		// it was given. WHICH protocol applies stays the DESCRIPTOR's to say — minted per instance after every
		// file is parsed — and is never re-decided here.
		//
		// ⚠ **"TRIVIAL" IS NOT THE LINE, AND THIS SAID IT WAS (W41-trivial).** An all-scalar STRUCT is trivial
		// in the CONSUME sense (`typeArgIsOwned` false — the call site borrows it and the caller keeps it) and
		// is a refcounted heap box all the same, so its word is `__mm_retain` and always was. The two senses
		// share a word and do not share a boundary; `typeOwnershipProtocol` is the one that decides this slot.
		return self.signatures.typeParamOwnerIsInstantiated(self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'opaqueSlotTakesItsOwnReference'

	// ⭐ **THE REPEAT GUARDS' SHARED GATE: would a SECOND owning destination for `value` in this construct free
	// one reference twice?** Only an opaque `T` value whose destination INHERITS this frame's reference can —
	// everything else takes an independent reference per destination and may repeat freely: a concrete managed
	// value co-owns (the 2026-08-12 ruling), a substituted opaque is concrete, and a borrowed opaque the
	// descriptor can retain takes one per slot. Spelled once because its two askers must ask the SAME question:
	// the field guard used to test the DESTINATION's declared type and the argument guard the VALUE's ownership,
	// which made `Self{a: v, b: v}` and `Pair.create(v, b: v)` two answers to one question about one value.
	function repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter and not self.opaqueSlotTakesItsOwnReference(value)
	end 'repeatedOwningDestinationWouldDoubleFree'

	// ⭐⭐ **BECOME A SECOND OWNER OF A BORROWED OPAQUE `T`, THROUGH THE ENCLOSING INSTANCE'S DESCRIPTOR
	// (W41-opaque)** — `coOwnBorrowedForConsume`'s arm for the type the shared body cannot name.
	// `__retain_type_param(descriptor, value)` reads `retainFunc@64` and `callIndirect`s it: `__str_clone` for a
	// byte-record argument, `__mm_retain` for an aggregate, and nothing at all for a trivial one, whose word is
	// 0. `lowerRuntimeCall` prepends the descriptor, exactly as it does for the drop twin.
	//
	// **TWO DOORS OWE THIS REFERENCE, AND THEY OWE THE SAME ONE.** A STORE into a durable slot
	// (`moveOpaqueValueInto`) and a RETURN out of the body (`emitOwnedValueReturn`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-a) each
	// hand a borrowed `T` to an owner that will release it, and neither can name the protocol. Written twice
	// they would be two answers to one question about a reference count — a leak on one side and a double free
	// on the other.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RESULT IS WHAT THE CALLER USES AND NEVER `value`** — the byte-record arm hands back a DIFFERENT
	// record, which is the identical trap `coOwnBorrowedForConsume`'s existential arm measured as an access
	// violation plus a leaked clone. This frame marks the result neither owned nor pending, and that is right
	// for both doors: the new owner is a SLOT in another record whose destructor releases exactly this
	// reference, or the CALLER, which adopts it (`enrolOwnedCallTemp`).
	//
	// ⚠ It records a missing descriptor rather than emitting a call the lowering can only abort on — the W10
	// arrangement `emitDecref` already has for the drop, so `parseFunction` refuses it with a position.
	function coOwnBorrowedOpaque(value ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if not self.functionCarriesLayoutParam 'noDescriptorToRetainThrough'
			self.functionStoredOpaqueWithoutDescriptor = true
		end 'noDescriptorToRetainThrough'

		// Downgraded for `markValueCoOwnsHeap`'s reason: the instance descriptor's retain is an incref for every
		// managed instantiation, and inert for a trivial one — so a second owner either exists or there is
		// nothing to own, and neither case licenses a destructive write through `value`.
		self.markValueCoOwnsHeap(value)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(RetainTypeParamName, args: singletonArgs(value), resultTag: self.tagOf(value), resultNameId: self.valueNameId(value), range: range)
	end 'coOwnBorrowedOpaque'

	// ⭐ **STORE AN ALREADY-CLASSIFIED-MANAGED VALUE INTO DURABLE STORAGE** — the core of `moveInPayload`,
	// factored out so a struct field / union payload (`moveInPayload`) and an ARRAY element (P1.7 slice 3,
	// `moveElementIntoContainer`) share the ONE transfer rule and cannot drift on it. A borrowed String is promoted
	// to an owned copy (a fresh temp de-listed); a borrowed struct/union/existential is CO-OWNED
	// (`coOwnBorrowedForConsume`, which for an existential answers with the reference it TOOK — that may be a
	// fresh record); an owned value held by a LIVE BINDING is CO-OWNED too (`storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink`,
	// the ruling below); an owned TEMPORARY is adopted outright, its statement-end drop cancelled. Precondition:
	// the value is managed.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A DURABLE STORE OF AN OWNED MANAGED VALUE INCREFS — IT DOES NOT MOVE (⚖ user ruling, 2026-08-12).**
	// It used to POISON the source binding, on the premise that shv2 is move-only and one `+1` cannot be owned
	// twice. That premise made the two-sink program the language's own witness-return case is built from — persist
	// into a durable field AND push into a transient local array — an E3102 that no rewriting of the source can
	// avoid, because BOTH sinks genuinely need a reference. The ruling is that each durable store ADDS one: the
	// sinks co-own, the source binding stays LIVE, and its own scope-exit drop releases the reference it has
	// always held. Net refcount for the ordinary single-sink store is unchanged (+1 here, −1 at scope exit).
	//
	// ⚠ **A REBIND OF A PLAIN LOCAL IS NOT A DURABLE STORE AND IS UNTOUCHED.** `let u = t` / `s = t` over an
	// ordinary local still MOVE (`reassignBindingValue`, `declareInitializedBinding` → `poisonMoveSource`), so
	// `specs-shv2/moves.md`'s use-after-move family is exactly as it was. A second NAME for a value is not a
	// sink: it owes no drop of its own, it inherits the one the old name owed.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE EXCEPTION IS A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING, AND IT IS A REBIND THAT *IS* A DURABLE STORE.** `s = t`
	// where `s` is a by-reference parameter (PBR-2) never reaches `reassignBindingValue` at all —
	// `parseAssignment` routes it to `reassignCellBinding`, which discharges the write through THIS door and so
	// co-owns. That is right, and it is right for the ruling's own reason: a cell is the CALLER's storage and
	// outlives this frame, exactly as a field or a global's slot does (`reassignCellBinding` says so in its own
	// words). Routing it back to a move would rebuild the inconsistency in the other direction — `self.f = t`
	// co-owning while `byRefParam = t` moved.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OPAQUE `T` DOORS DELIBERATELY STILL MOVE** (`moveOpaqueValueInto`, `moveElementIntoContainer`'s
	// opaque arm, `transferConsumedArg`'s opaque arm). A shared generic body cannot name an incref: its only
	// retain is `retainFunc@64` out of the enclosing instance's layout descriptor, which a body carrying no
	// descriptor (a `static` over a trivial instantiation) does not have — so co-owning there converts a working
	// program into a refusal, where here it converts a refusal into a working program. Those doors already take a
	// reference at the one place a move is provably wrong (W58/W60: across a loop back edge), which is the part of
	// this ruling they can express. Widening the rest of the way needs the descriptor-need seed
	// `handleEscapingBorrowFeed` names, and that is a rung.
	function moveManagedValueInto(arg ValueId, argBareLocal bool, isString bool, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⚠⚠ **THE BATCH41 ARRIVAL GUARD STOOD HERE AND IS GONE, BECAUSE THE THING IT REFUSED NOW WORKS.**
		// It refused every managed value entering durable storage whose type rests a slot at the enclosing
		// parameter. A FIELD of the enclosing type is the arrival that reached it — `Self\{one: EBox.create(x,
		// …)\}` — and the enclosing instance is CONCRETE at every site that frees it, so `__destruct_Bag_String`
		// already drops that field through `__destruct_Box_String` and releases the slot. What was missing was
		// the reference, not the release, and the constructor feed now takes it
		// (`referenceOrMarkOpaqueFeed`). MEASURED: `a-record-over-the-enclosing-parameter-in-a-plain-field`
		// prints its payload and exits 0.
		//
		// ⇒ what still owes a refusal is the arrival whose release is a STAMPED ONE-ARGUMENT function pointer
		// — a container's element, a `List` node, a `Map`/`Set` column — because a declaration view's release
		// is a two-argument dictionary destructor and no such stamp can hold one. That is asked at the two
		// container CREATE sites (`requireColumnDropIsNameable`) and at the callee-storage FEED
		// (`transferConsumedArgs`), which are the doors those arrivals pass and this one is not.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **AND ONE FIELD ARRIVAL STILL OWES IT TOO, WHICH THE FIRST CUT OF THIS DELETION MISSED (BATCH41
		// review).** The argument above — *"the enclosing instance is CONCRETE at every site that frees it"* — is
		// a claim about the ENCLOSING type, and it is false exactly when that type is ITSELF held as a
		// declaration view. Asked first, before any copy or reference is taken, so no half-performed store
		// precedes the throw.
		try self.requireStoredNestedViewDropIsNameable(arg, range: range)
		// ⭐⭐ **W5′ — AND A SIZED CONTAINER'S OWN RECORD MAY NOT BE STORED AT ALL (W192 second review).** This
		// is the DURABLE-STORE half of *"the record may not leave the declaration"*, and it is asked here
		// because this one door is every sink that will outlive the statement: a struct or TUPLE literal's
		// field (`emitStructLiteral` → `moveInPayload`), a container ELEMENT (`moveElementIntoContainer`), a
		// `Set`/`Map` column, a cell, a field ASSIGNMENT. A per-sink refusal would be the enumeration that
		// misses the next sink, and two of these were MEASURED live before this line existed —
		// `b.push(managed)` out of an `Array with ElementMemory` and `let t = (managed, 1)` — each reading
		// back `len=0 count=3`, the rung's own reproducer.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE STORE IS WHAT IS REFUSED, NOT THE SINK'S TYPE.** A `slice` carries no mark and is stored
		// freely, which is the advice the sentence itself gives; and `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s own
		// `Self{managed: managed}` stores a PARAMETER, which no mark is written on (see `bindParameters`).
		// Asked before any copy or reference is taken, exactly as the line above is, so no half-performed
		// store precedes the throw.
		try self.refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping(arg, escape: SizedContainerRecordEscape.storedInDurableStorage, line: range.line, column: range.column)
		// ⭐⭐ **A TUPLE IS COPIED HERE, NOT MOVED — BECAUSE A TUPLE IS A VALUE (P1.10).** A struct is CO-OWNED
		// below when it is borrowed and MOVED when it is owned, and both are right for a struct: it has
		// reference identity a program can observe (an alias, a mutation through one), so a second record
		// would be a second thing. A tuple has none — the only way to obtain one is the expression that just
		// produced it — so the durable slot takes its OWN record and the source keeps its own. That is what
		// makes `xs.push(p)` followed by `return p` mean what `specs-shv2/tuples.md` says it means: the array
		// owns one record and the return owns another, where a move would poison `p` and a share would free
		// one record twice. The bootstrap states the same rule from the other end (`IrStructType.
		// IsTwoRegisterValueTuple`: *"a tuple returned by value has no prior identity to break"*).
		//
		// ⚠ **ONLY A TRIVIAL-ELEMENT TUPLE, AND THE RESTRICTION IS NOW A DELIBERATE NON-GOAL RATHER THAN A
		// SOUNDNESS ONE.** It read *"a shallow copy would leave two records pointing at one String and free it
		// twice"* — an argument from move-only that the durable-store ruling retired: a per-element retain is
		// available today and would make a shallow copy of a managed-element tuple perfectly droppable. What is
		// NOT available is the VALUE semantics a copy exists for. A retain hands back a second record whose
		// elements ALIAS the first's, so a write through one tuple would show on the other — precisely what a
		// trivial tuple's copy exists to prevent, and what `returned-tuple-copies-only-when-trivial` pins from
		// the other side. A managed-element tuple therefore keeps the struct rules below (share one record,
		// co-own it) until the rung that gives it a DEEP clone, the only copy that would mean what a tuple
		// means.
		// The copy is de-listed from the statement's pending drops for the promoted String's reason one branch
		// down: the durable slot owns it now, and a statement-end `__mm_decref` on it would free a record the
		// box still holds.
		if self.valueIsCopyableTuple(arg) 'copyTuple'
			let copied = self.copyTupleValue(arg, range: range)
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(copied)
			return copied
		end 'copyTuple'

		if isString 'string'
			if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(arg) 'promoteBorrowed'
				// ⭐⭐ **A BORROWED BYTE RECORD IS CO-OWNED, NOT COPIED — THE SAME ANSWER THE STRUCT/UNION ARM
				// DIRECTLY BELOW GIVES, AND FOR THE SAME REASON.** Maxon is single-ownership with reference
				// semantics: *"everything is a reference; if you want a copy you do it explicitly with
				// `clone()`"*. A `Self{name: n}` that stores a DIFFERENT record than the `n` it was handed is
				// a copy the author did not write, and it makes the field's identity diverge from the
				// source's — which is precisely the divergence the aggregate arm refuses to introduce.
				// `__str_retain` is what lets this be said at all: it increfs a HEAP record and copies only
				// an immortal `.rdata` literal, which cannot be increfed because its header is in a
				// read-only image section. The copy that remains is therefore about the LITERAL's storage
				// class, not about borrowing.
				//
				// ⚠ **THE ALLOCATION SAVING IS REAL AND SMALL, AND THE RULE IS WHY THIS CHANGED — NOT THE
				// COST.** Every registry this compiler builds is keyed and valued by NAMES, so this door was
				// the obvious suspect for the self-compile's allocation gap (a shv2-built compiler allocates
				// **1,089,279** times against the bootstrap-built one's **618,817** on the same input, 1.76x,
				// concentrated in `phase:signatures`). MEASURED: removing the copy here took the total to
				// **1,077,766** — a few hundred allocations, against a ~470,000 gap. **The gap is still
				// unexplained and this comment does not explain it.**
				//
				// ⚠ The result is a FRESH SSA value that this frame owns, exactly as the copy was, so the
				// pending-temp bookkeeping is unchanged: the sink adopts the reference and the statement
				// drain must not also drop it.
				let owned = self.retainBorrowedByteRecord(arg, range: range)
				self.removeFromPendingTemps(owned)
				return owned
			end 'promoteBorrowed'
		end 'string' else 'structOrUnion'
			if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(arg) 'borrowedManaged'
				// ⭐ **CO-OWNED, NOT MOVED (the transitive-consume ruling, 2026-08-04).** An aggregate has no
				// clone, so the durable slot becomes a SECOND OWNER of the same record; the borrow's own owner
				// is untouched and this frame poisons nothing, so the `transferOwnedInto` below is skipped
				// deliberately rather than fallen past. `coOwnBorrowedForConsume` states the rule in full, and
				// its ARGUMENT twin (`transferConsumedArg`) answers the same question one call boundary out.
				// ⚠ Its RESULT is what is stored, never `arg`: an EXISTENTIAL's reference is taken through the
				// witness and a byte-record conformer's protocol there is a COPY, so the two can differ.
				return self.coOwnBorrowedForConsume(arg, range: range)
			end 'borrowedManaged'
		end 'structOrUnion'

		// The owned-set scan inside runs only under the cheap syntactic `argBareLocal` gate, which is the same
		// arrangement (and the same reason) `poisonMoveSource`'s callers use: an unconditional O(ownedBindings)
		// scan on every store is quadratic in a long function body.
		if argBareLocal 'sourceMayBeALiveBinding'
			return self.storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink(arg, isString: isString, range: range)
		end 'sourceMayBeALiveBinding'

		// With the gate false the value came from an EXPRESSION rather than a name, so no binding owns it and
		// the slot simply ADOPTS the `+1` it was just handed — the statement-end drop is cancelled and nothing
		// is poisoned, because there is no source to poison. Spelled here rather than routed through
		// `transferOwnedInto`: that door's other arm is the move-poison, which is now unreachable from this
		// function, and calling it with a statically-false flag would read as though a move could still happen.
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(arg)
		return arg
	end 'moveManagedValueInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **HAND AN OWNED CONCRETE MANAGED VALUE TO A DURABLE SINK — THE ONE ACT, SHARED BY THE DIRECT STORE AND
	// THE CONSUMING ARGUMENT** (`moveManagedValueInto`, which carries the 2026-08-12 ruling, and
	// `transferConsumedArg`, which is the same sink one call boundary out). Written twice, the two would be free
	// to settle differently on what a durable sink owes a value — a leak on one side and a double free on the
	// other, from a distinction the language does not draw.
	//
	// **THE SPLIT IS "IS THERE A SECOND OWNER TO KEEP ALIVE?"** A value a LIVE BINDING holds keeps that binding
	// and its scope-exit drop, so the sink must take its OWN reference. An owned TEMPORARY has no other owner at
	// all, so the sink ADOPTS the `+1` the expression just minted and the statement-end drop is cancelled — an
	// incref/decref pair around a value nothing else reads would be pure churn.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE SPLIT IS AN `if`/`else`, WHICH MAKES A PRECONDITION LOAD-BEARING THAT USED TO BE BELT-AND-BRACES:
	// A VALUE IS NEVER IN `ownedBindings` AND `pendingTempDrops` AT THE SAME TIME.** The door this replaced at
	// `transferConsumedArg` ran the poison AND `removeFromPendingTemps` unconditionally, on the argument that
	// each is a no-op on the other's case — so it stayed correct even if a value were somehow in both. This one
	// takes the co-own arm for a bound value and never de-lists, so a value in both lists would keep a
	// statement-end `__mm_decref` against a reference the SINK now owns: a use-after-free on the next read, not
	// a leak. The invariant is `markValueOwnsHeap`'s and `trackOwnedTemp`'s to keep — the two owned-bit doors
	// exist precisely because a binding's drop and a statement's drop are alternatives, never both (see
	// `markValueOwnsHeap`, whose header carries the measured double-free from adding a pending entry for a
	// consumed parameter that already dropped through `ownedBindings`).
	//
	// Two retain protocols, and they are the SAME two the BORROWED arms already route between: a byte record
	// through `__str_retain` (an incref on the heap branch, a copy of an immortal `.rdata` literal on the other),
	// an aggregate or existential through `coOwnBorrowedForConsume`. The RESULT is what the sink receives and
	// never `arg` — both of those may hand back a DIFFERENT record, which is the measured access violation
	// `coOwnBorrowedForConsume` records.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SOURCE IS STILL MARKED "MUTABILITY EXERCISED", THOUGH IT IS NO LONGER MOVED OUT OF.** E3077 asks
	// whether a `var` had to be a `var`, and `poisonOwningBinding` answered YES for every move-out — a door its own
	// header calls "broader than the argument", since `let` would have been legal for a store. Narrowing it here
	// would make `var b = …` + `xs.push(b)` a fresh E3077 on programs this ruling exists to ACCEPT, which is a
	// second behaviour change riding a refcount fix. The answer is deliberately held at exactly what it was.
	function storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink(arg ValueId, isString bool, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let index = self.liveOwnerIndexOf(arg)
		if self.noLiveOwner(index) 'ownedTemporaryIsAdopted'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(arg)
			return arg
		end 'ownedTemporaryIsAdopted'

		// A `var` bound from `.get(index)`: the mark is written THROUGH the shared `VarInfo` reference (the
		// `Scope.setValue` idiom `poisonOwningBinding` uses), so every holder observes it.
		var owner = try self.ownedBindings.get(index) otherwise panic("storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink: ownedBindings.get OOB at {index} — the index came from `liveOwnerIndexOf` and was just bounds-tested")
		self.markMutabilityExercised(owner)

		if isString 'byteRecord'
			return self.retainBorrowedByteRecord(arg, range: range)
		end 'byteRecord'

		return self.coOwnBorrowedForConsume(arg, range: range)
	end 'storeOwnedValueIntoDurableSink'

	// Transfer an already-OWNED managed value into durable storage: a bare-local reference to a binding is
	// POISONED (`movedFrom` — E3102 on later use); an owned temporary is removed from the statement's pending
	// drops (the box owns it now). THE one place the "who no longer owns this value" bookkeeping lives, so a
	// union payload move and a struct-field move cannot drift on it.
	function transferOwnedInto(arg ValueId, argBareLocal bool, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if argBareLocal 'moveOnBind'
			try self.poisonMoveSource(arg, anchor: anchor)
		end 'moveOnBind' else 'consumeTemp'
			self.removeFromPendingTemps(arg)
		end 'consumeTemp'
	end 'transferOwnedInto'

	// Store the case TAG at the box's offset 0 as an i64 — the same i64 tag `emitEnumTagLiteral` mints
	// for a bare enum and the same `match` loads back to dispatch, so construct and match cannot
	// disagree about what a case's tag is.
	function emitUnionTagStore(box ValueId, tag ParsedInt, range SourceRange)
		let tagLit = self.emitLiteral(tag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		self.emitIndirectStore(box, offset: UnionTagOffset, value: tagLit, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'emitUnionTagStore'

	// Classify a payload field's storage, rejecting the one kind the UNION-payload store still cannot
	// lower — a FLOAT.
	//
	// ⚠ THE FLOAT REJECT IS NO LONGER WAITING ON AN OPCODE. Both bitcast directions now exist
	// (`StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastF64ToI64`/`bitcastI64ToF64`, reachable as `__Builtins.floatToBits`/
	// `bitsToFloat`), so the INSTRUCTIONS a float payload needs are here. What is still missing is the
	// LOWERING that uses them: an int-tagged box stores a payload as an i64, so a float field has to be
	// re-encoded on the way in and decoded on the way out at BOTH the construct and the extract site,
	// and neither site does that today. Until it does, this refuses cleanly rather than storing an f64
	// register through an i64 slot. A SCALAR, a String, a struct and a nested boxed UNION are
	// lowerable; the returned class carries the storage type and, for a managed
	// payload, whether it is a String. The reject is at the construct/extract USE site (not the
	// declaration), so the tolerant enum sweep never truncates a layout over it.
	//
	// ⚠ **v1 PANICS ON A FLOAT PAYLOAD** (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:12529-12536`, `:12574-12578`) where this
	// refuses positioned; the divergence is deliberate and shv2's answer is the better one.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE nestedUnion REJECT THAT USED TO STAND HERE WAS A MISSING WIDTH, NOT A MISSING CASCADE (W55).**
	// Its message said a nested payload-bearing union "needs its own destructor cascade"; it never did — the
	// cascade is generic (`managedFieldDropCallee` → `unionBoxDropCallee`) and a boxed-union STRUCT FIELD had
	// been dropping through it since P1.4b wave 2c. What the payload path actually lacked was the width-vs-
	// identity split `payloadStorageOf` now states in full — this door's store width was being asked of the
	// slot's IDENTITY, and a boxed union's identity is a `named` that `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on. Only
	// `emitEnumBox`/`declarePayloadBindings` call this; the struct-field path classifies through
	// `classifyUnionPayload` directly and never reaches here.
	// `declared` is the payload's type out of `payloadTypeOf` — a signatures-layout id adopted into this
	// file's interner (or a ranged alias that shifts the interning order makes `classifyUnionPayload` read
	// the wrong name off the id, the wrong E3005 reject — OPEN #52) AND resolved. It is threaded in rather
	// than derived here because both callers need it for themselves as well (the arg check's declared tag,
	// the load's value type), and asking one door twice per payload is what a shared answer replaces.
	function requirePayloadLowerable(field PayloadField, declared MaxonType, layout EnumLayout, posTok Token) returns PayloadClass throws ParseError
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: declared, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		match class 'c'
			scalar then break 'c'
			managed then break 'c'
			// A nested boxed union is a managed heap pointer whose slot is the same one machine word a struct
			// FIELD holding one occupies — see the ⚠ above for the derivation that was missing.
			nestedUnion then break 'c'
			undeclaredName then break 'c'
			// An opaque `T` payload lowers exactly as a scalar does — one machine word out of
			// `payloadStorageOf` — which is what it did while it WAS classified `scalar`. What the split bought
			// is the store-side reference (`moveInPayload`), not a different slot.
			typeParam then break 'c'
			floatPayload then throw ParseError.unsupported(self.floatPayloadMessage(field, layout: layout), line: posTok.line, column: posTok.column)
			// An interface-typed PAYLOAD is refused at the DECLARATION (`requireStorableDeclaredType`,
			// `DeclaredStoragePosition.unionPayload`) — a struct field carves out two slots for a fat pointer
			// and a payload slot cannot, because every case shares one slot region. So this arm is the
			// backstop for that refusal, not a second verdict on it (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).
			existential then panic("requirePayloadLowerable: a union payload is declared at an interface type — `Parser.requireStorableDeclaredType` refuses that at the declaration, so a payload reaching here came through a route that refusal does not cover")
		end 'c'
		return class
	end 'requirePayloadLowerable'

	// A payload argument must agree with its field's classified type. A SCALAR uses `checkDeclaredType`,
	// the same coercion-site rule a struct literal field, a call argument and a `return` ask, PLUS the
	// nominal identity `checkDeclaredType` structurally cannot ask (`requireSlotAggregateIdentity`); a MANAGED
	// payload requires the matching heap kind (a String, or a struct/boxed union of the same name).
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO ANCHORS, AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.** `caseTok` names the CONSTRUCT and is where a
	// refusal about the case as a whole belongs (a payload the tier cannot lower, a slot left unfilled);
	// `argTok` is the token following THIS argument and is where a refusal about ONE argument's type
	// belongs, which is the position the canonical specs pin. Passing the construct's anchor for both is
	// what made `union-payload.error.wrong-struct` point at `tint` rather than at the argument.
	function requirePayloadArgType(field PayloadField, declared MaxonType, class PayloadClass, value ValueId, caseTok Token, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		match class 'c'
			scalar then try self.requireScalarPayloadArg(field, declared: declared, value: value, caseTok: caseTok, argTok: argTok)
			// ⚠ The scalar rule, spelled as its OWN arm and deliberately not folded into a `scalar to typeParam`
			// RANGE: `typeParam` is the last variant of `PayloadClass` and `scalar` the first, so that range
			// would swallow every case between them — the band-append trap, one union down.
			// `checkDeclaredType`'s type-parameter arm is where an argument meets an opaque formal, which is
			// what checked this payload while it was classified `scalar`; the store-side split changed nothing
			// an argument must satisfy.
			typeParam then try self.requireScalarPayloadArg(field, declared: declared, value: value, caseTok: caseTok, argTok: argTok)
			// The shared managed door directly — `requireManagedPayloadArg` stood here and did nothing but
			// forward, once the payload voice stopped needing a `<Union>.<case>(<field>)` label built for it.
			managed(storage, isString) then try self.requireManagedValueMatches(storage, isString: isString, value: value, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.payload, placeName: field.name, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.payloadArgument, anchor: argTok)
			// ⭐ **A NESTED BOXED UNION TAKES THE MANAGED DOOR, over its `named(<union>)` VALUE type and not over
			// the machine word its slot is (W55).** A boxed union's value carries `named`, which every ranged-int
			// alias and every payload-free enum also carries — so the tag comparison alone AGREES for any two of
			// them, and it is `requireManagedValueMatches`' second half (`requireSlotAggregateIdentity`) that
			// makes `Wrap.wrap(Other.a(5))` a positioned refusal rather than an `Other` box read back under
			// `Inner`'s destructor. `isString: false` — a union box is an aggregate, released by
			// `__destruct_<U>`/`__mm_decref` and co-owned by `__mm_retain`, never by the fused-record COPY
			// protocol a String takes.
			nestedUnion(storage) then try self.requireManagedValueMatches(storage, isString: false, value: value, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.payload, placeName: field.name, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.payloadArgument, anchor: argTok)
			// An undeclared payload name is a user error TypeResolution reports (E3011); the op is kept
			// well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's error gate throws — nothing to check here.
			undeclaredName then break 'c'
			floatPayload then panic("requirePayloadArgType: a float payload reached the arg check — requirePayloadLowerable rejects it first")
			existential then panic("requirePayloadArgType: an interface-typed payload reached the arg check — an interface-typed union payload is refused at its DECLARATION (`requireStorableDeclaredType`), and a struct FIELD at one is checked by `requireStructFieldArgType`, never here")
		end 'c'
	end 'requirePayloadArgType'

	// ⚠ **THE TAG COMES OFF THE RESOLVED `declared`, NEVER OFF `field.declaredType` (A4k).** Read raw, a
	// FUNCTION-alias payload is a `named` — an int — so `Payload.op(twice)` met the
	// `functionIntoNonFunction` arm below and was refused E3005 on a program the payload cascade had, until
	// this rung, never even classified as a scalar. Resolved, it is the function type the value is.
	function requireScalarPayloadArg(field PayloadField, declared MaxonType, value ValueId, caseTok Token, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		let declaredTag = maxonTypeTag(declared)
		let got = self.tagOf(value)

		// ⭐⭐ **A FUNCTION VALUE INTO A FUNCTION-TYPED PAYLOAD IS THE FOURTH DOOR, AND IT REACHED NONE OF THEM
		// (BATCH18).** `checkDeclaredType` answers `ok` for `function` meeting `function` on the TAG alone —
		// which is right, because two function TYPES share that tag exactly as two structs share `structRef`
		// and no tag comparison can settle it. Every other position that holds a declared function type
		// records a door for `SemanticCheck.checkFunctionTypeDoors`; this one fell through the `ok` arm and
		// compared nothing at all. MEASURED on a two-parameter function stored in a one-parameter payload and
		// called through it: arm64-macOS prints `v=7` — the second argument is the leftover slot — and
		// `wasm32-wasi` traps *"indirect call type mismatch"*, `call_indirect` being the one target that
		// checks. Recorded rather than decided here for `recordFunctionTypeDoor`'s reason: the shapes live in
		// the whole-program registries and are compared RESOLVED.
		if isFunctionTypeDoor(declaredTag, got: got) 'functionIntoFunctionPayload'
			self.recordFunctionTypeDoor(typeNameIdOf(declared), gotNameId: self.valueNameId(value), door: FunctionTypeDoor.payload, placeName: field.name, anchor: caseTok)
		end 'functionIntoFunctionPayload'

		match checkDeclaredType(got, declared: declaredTag) 'declared'
			ok then break 'declared'
			mismatch then throw self.slotMismatchError(SlotMismatchVoice.payloadArgument, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.payload, placeName: field.name, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), gotTag: got, resolvedExpected: typeTagName(declaredTag), declared: declaredTag, declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(declared), line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
			narrowsFloatToInt then throw ParseError.implicitNarrowing(typeTagName(got), targetType: typeTagName(declaredTag), line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
			// A FUNCTION VALUE into a scalar union PAYLOAD whose declared type is NOT a function — the same
			// E3005 a function value meeting any other non-function slot earns. A function-ALIAS payload no
			// longer reaches it: `declaredTag` is `function` there and `checkDeclaredType` answers `ok`.
			functionIntoNonFunction then throw ParseError.functionValueIntoNonFunction("payload", name: String.from(field.name), heldType: typeTagName(declaredTag), line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
		end 'declared'

		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE NOMINAL IDENTITY, WHICH `checkDeclaredType` STRUCTURALLY CANNOT ASK — THE SIXTH DOOR
		// ON `requireSlotAggregateIdentity`, AND IT WAS THE ONE STILL MISSING.** A payload-free enum's value
		// IS its i64 tag, so an ENUM-typed payload is `PayloadClass.scalar` and its verdict was
		// `checkDeclaredType(named, named)` — which AGREES for any two enums, exactly as it did for an
		// enum-typed struct FIELD until BATCH32 wired that door (see the header there). MEASURED on
		// `union Paint tint(c Color) end` handed a `Shade`: it COMPILED, and the binding `c` came back out as
		// a `Shade` ordinal wearing `Color`'s name — `specs/union-cases.md`'s `union-payload.error.wrong-enum`
		// is that program. The MANAGED half of the payload door was already asking (through
		// `requireManagedValueMatches`), which is why the STRUCT twin of the same hole was refused all along
		// and only the enum one was open.
		//
		// Asked past the verdict rather than instead of it: a tag disagreement is the coarser fact and reads
		// better said first, and a value that failed it has no identity worth comparing.
		try self.requireSlotAggregateIdentity(declared, value: value, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.payload, placeName: field.name, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.payloadArgument, anchor: argTok)
	end 'requireScalarPayloadArg'

	// Load a boxed union's i64 TAG from offset 0 — the DISPATCH SUBJECT a `match` compares against
	// each case's tag. Its result is `integer`-tagged (a plain i64), not `named`, so the dispatch
	// compare is an i64 equality the E3066 union guard never sees. Loaded ONCE per match (it dominates
	// every arm's test block). Mirrors v1's `matchDispatchSubject`.
	function emitUnionTagLoad(box ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		return self.emitIndirectLoad(MaxonType.integer, box: box, offset: UnionTagOffset, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'emitUnionTagLoad'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHERE AN ENUM VALUE'S TAG LIVES — THE ONE ANSWER, read by the `match` DISPATCH and by `.ordinal`.**
	// A SCALAR enum's value IS its tag (`parseEnumLiteral` emits the tag as the value), so the tag is the
	// value and this emits nothing. A BOXED union's value is a heap POINTER, so its i64 tag is LOADED from
	// offset 0 through `emitUnionTagLoad` above.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS ONE FUNCTION BECAUSE THE DECISION DRIFTS, NOT ONLY THE OFFSET — AND THE DRIFT IS MEASURED.**
	// Both readers already shared `emitUnionTagLoad`, so they could never disagree about WHERE in the box a
	// tag sits; they each carried their own copy of WHETHER to look there, and that is the copy that came
	// apart. `.ordinal` did not have one at all: it read `tagsAreDeclarationIndices` (true for a boxed
	// union, whose tags are `0,1,2,…`) as permission to treat the value as the tag, so
	// `Container.value(7).ordinal` printed 7077912 and `Container.empty.ordinal` printed 7078078 against the
	// oracle's 1 and 0 — box addresses, which moved between runs. Reunited here, a reader that forgets the
	// question cannot be written: there is nowhere to ask it.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY CASE OF A BOXED UNION IS BOXED, PAYLOAD-FREE ONES INCLUDED** — `isBoxed` decides per UNION
	// (`maxPayloadArity() > 0`), not per case, so `empty` beside a `value(n)` is a bare tag in a box and its
	// value is a pointer too. That is why the predicate here is the layout's and never the case's.
	//
	// A caller that has not already established the value IS an enum keeps that guard at its own site — the
	// `match` dispatch's `isEnumMatch`, which is a question about the SCRUTINEE and not about the layout.
	function emitEnumTagOf(value ValueId, layout EnumLayout, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		// ⭐⭐ **THIS FILE REACHED INTO A VALUE OF `layout.name`, AND THIS IS THE ONE LINE THAT KNOWS IT** —
		// `requireFieldAccessible`'s twin for enums and unions, recorded through the same one-writer channel and
		// for the same reason. A `match` arm spells CASE names (`small`, `big(n)`), `.ordinal` spells a member,
		// and an interpolation hole spells nothing at all, so a file that reaches another file's union this way
		// leaves NO written name for `usedTypeNames` to hold — and E3092 told the author to drop the `export`
		// that made the program compile. Third instance of one class; see `UnusedExportCheck`'s header for the
		// first two.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **RECORDED HERE AND NOT AT `enumMatchFor`, WHICH IS THE WIDER DOOR — AND THE DIFFERENCE IS
		// MEASURED, NOT ARGUED.** That one also answers for a drop cascade (`decrefCalleeFor`), a compare
		// operand (`valueIsUnion`) and a refused cast (`requireCastSourceIsNotEnum`) — none of them a
		// source-level reach. Crediting the drop is exactly the callee-RETURN-type credit `noteCalleeSignature`
		// refuses by name. MEASURED (BATCH43) by putting the credit there and rebuilding:
		// `unused-export/error.unused-exported-union-nothing-outside-reaches` went RED — "expected a compile
		// error but compilation succeeded" — while all three `match`/accessor cases stayed green. That case's
		// whole subject is a file that HOLDS such a value and never opens it, so the wider door deletes it.
		//
		// ⚠ Written BEFORE the `isBoxed` split: an UNBOXED enum's value IS its tag and emits nothing, but the
		// file reached the layout either way. A cure behind the load would pass the union cases and still report
		// every plain `enum`.
		self.recordMemberAccessedTypeName(layout.name)

		if not layout.isBoxed() 'theValueIsTheTag'
			return value
		end 'theValueIsTheTag'

		return self.emitUnionTagLoad(value, range: range)
	end 'emitEnumTagOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION WHERE THERE IS NO VALUE TO ASK IT OF — a case named through its TYPE
	// (`Preset.large`, `TestOp.mul`), whose tag is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT.** It sits beside `emitEnumTagOf`
	// because it is the third answer to that function's one question, and the split between its two arms is
	// exactly the split there: for an UNBOXED declaration a case's tag IS its value, so the enum-typed literal
	// this mints serves both readers — the construct `Color.red` evaluates to and the tag an accessor selects
	// from. For a BOXED union the two part company (the value is a heap box, the tag a bare i64 at offset 0),
	// and only the tag is knowable without arguments, so that is all this mints.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE WHOLE OF WHY `TestOp.mul.latency` CAN WORK ON A PAYLOAD-BEARING UNION.**
	// Constructing `mul` would demand its `dest`/`src` arguments; reading its compile-time metadata needs no
	// value at all, so the read is answered from the tag alone and no box is ever allocated — which also means
	// there is no owned heap record minted mid-expression, the lifetime question `emitEnumBackingRawValue`
	// declines for a materialized backing.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BOXED ARM MUST MINT `integer` AND NOT `named(<Union>)`.** A `named` claim over a bare tag is
	// this rung's own measured defect class — a box pointer stored as a tag, `0xC0000005`
	// (`enumBackingNamedFieldDomain`) — read in the other direction: `enumTagAsANumber` asks precisely
	// "is this already a bare number", and answering "no, it is a TestOp" about an i64 that IS one would be a
	// false claim kept honest only by luck.
	function emitEnumCaseTagConstant(layout EnumLayout, index CaseIndex, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let tag = layout.tagOfIndex(index)
		if layout.isBoxed() 'theValueIsNotTheTag'
			return self.emitLiteral(tag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		end 'theValueIsNotTheTag'

		return self.emitEnumTagLiteral(layout.name, tag: tag, range: range)
	end 'emitEnumCaseTagConstant'

	// Load a payload field out of the box at `8 + slot*8` — the `emitFieldLoad` split, one op further.
	// A SCALAR is minted at the field's DECLARED type (so a nested `match dir` sees a `Direction`, and the
	// shift rule reads a ranged alias's low bound); a MANAGED payload is minted at its concrete storage
	// type (`structRef`/`string`) so an extracted struct's field access (`b.mass`) resolves and its
	// ownership is read off the tag; a nested BOXED UNION is minted at its `named(<union>)`, so a `match` on
	// the extracted payload recognizes it and its drop routes to `__destruct_<U>`. The op itself always
	// carries a concrete storage WIDTH, which for the boxed-union case is not the value type
	// (`maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named`) — see `payloadStorageOf`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ADDRESS, THE WIDTH AND THE VALUE TYPE ARE ALL THE CALLER'S, exactly as they are on the STORE
	// side (`emitEnumBox`), and that is not a convenience.** `declarePayloadBindings` needs the slot's
	// `PayloadClass` anyway — to decide managed-vs-scalar ownership, and to record the slot's ADDRESS and
	// WIDTH on a binding a write-back may later store through — and it used to classify the slot a SECOND
	// time to get it, after which each of the three numbers was derived twice. A load and the store that
	// mirrors it must agree on the address and the width EXACTLY or the store corrupts the box, so they are
	// derived ONCE at the caller and passed to everything that emits with them.
	//
	// A scalar payload's `valueType` is its DECLARED type (an enum payload stays `named(<Enum>)` so a
	// nested `match` recognizes it) and never the storage: a function-alias payload bound out as a bare
	// `named` is an int, which is what made `print("{f(3)}")` on such a binding `E2015 … a value of type
	// 'unknown'` (A4k). The caller reaches it through `payloadValueTypeOf`, off the same `payloadTypeOf`
	// call that adopts the id into this file's interner — without which the minted value carries a
	// signatures id `enumMatchFor` would misresolve (OPEN #52).
	function emitUnionPayloadLoad(box ValueId, field PayloadField, offset ByteOffset, valueType MaxonType, storage MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.emitIndirectLoad(valueType, box: box, offset: offset, storageType: storage, range: range)

		// ⭐ **A PAYLOAD DECLARED `__ManagedMemory` BINDS AS THE BUFFER SURFACE (BATCH22)** — the declared
		// SPELLING, which `valueType` cannot carry (the alias resolves to the same `genericInstance` a
		// `ByteArray` payload does), taken off the payload record's own column. It sits at THIS mint for
		// `emitFieldLoad`'s reason: every `match` arm, every nesting depth and every scrutinee ownership
		// mode funnels through this one line, and a per-door mark would be the enumeration that misses one.
		self.markDeclaredSurface(result, declared: self.signatures.payloadSurfaceOf(field))

		return result
	end 'emitUnionPayloadLoad'

	// Emit a case's TAG as an i64 `literal` whose VALUE tag is `named(<enum>)`. The one site that
	// mints an enum value, so the tag/op-type split (see `parseEnumLiteral`) lives in exactly one
	// place. See the ⭐ block on the `recordConst` below for why the tag IS written down, and what that
	// retired: this header said the const was "deliberately NOT recorded (an enum value is not folded into
	// arithmetic — it is only ever a `match` scrutinee)" until `enum-narrow-storage` needed a constant enum
	// literal to be RECOGNISABLE as constant.
	function emitEnumTagLiteral(enumName ByteArray, tag ParsedInt, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let nameId = self.artifact.typeNames.intern(enumName)
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.named, nameId: nameId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.literal(result, value: tag, valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)

		// ⭐ **THE TAG IS WRITTEN DOWN AS A KNOWN CONSTANT, AND IT IS THE SAME FACT `bool` ALREADY WRITES DOWN
		// RATHER THAN A WIDENING OF ONE.** `emitLiteralIn` records an INTEGER-tagged literal's value; this mint
		// is deliberately separate (the value is `named(E)` while the op carries `integer`), so the recording
		// has to be spelled here or the compiler declines to write down a value it has in hand.
		//
		// ⚠ **WHAT THAT COST WAS MEASURABLE, and it is the identical measurement `emitLiteralIn`'s float/bool
		// split carries**: a CONSTANT `[Color.red, Color.green, Color.blue]` could not be recognised as constant
		// at all, so `enum-narrow-storage`'s six literals could not become the `.rdata` blobs their spec pins.
		// The domain is INTEGER because a payload-free enum's value IS an i64 tag — the same reading
		// `emitEnumTagOf` and `TypeResolution` give it — so no float pattern can reach `foldIntBinOp` through
		// this door. A BOXED union never arrives: `emitEnumCaseTagConstant` sends it to `emitLiteral` at
		// `integer`, because its value is a box pointer and its tag is not it.
		self.recordConst(result, folded: tag)

		return result
	end 'emitEnumTagLiteral'

	// ⭐ **IS THE NOMINAL TYPE `name` — IF THE PROGRAM DECLARES ONE AT ALL — NAMEABLE FROM THIS FILE (N1a)?**
	//
	// A name NO declaration binds answers `true`: "there is no such type" is a different mistake with a
	// different diagnostic (E3004 at the mangled callee, E2004 at a bare base), and answering `false` here
	// would collapse both into this one's wording. That is `ProgramSignatures.aliasVisibleFrom`'s rule, one
	// registry over, and it is why this predicate is spelled as a question about a DECLARATION rather than as
	// a membership test.
	//
	// ⚠ Both probes are gated on the NON-ALLOCATING `contains` twin, for `parseDottedPrimary`'s measured
	// reason: `structOf`/`enumOf` box a `DeclaredStruct`/`DeclaredEnum` even to say `undeclared`, and this
	// runs on paths where nearly every name is neither.
	function typeNameVisibleHere(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if self.signatures.containsStruct(name) 'declaredStruct'
			match self.signatures.structOf(name) 'layout'
				declared(layout) then return self.signatures.isVisibleFrom(layout.visibility, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
				undeclared then panic("typeNameVisibleHere: containsStruct answered yes for '{String.from(name)}' but structOf answered undeclared — the two read one registry under one key")
			end 'layout'
		end 'declaredStruct'

		if not self.signatures.containsEnum(name) 'notANominalDeclaration'
			return true
		end 'notANominalDeclaration'

		match self.signatures.enumOf(name) 'layout'
			declared(layout) then return self.signatures.isVisibleFrom(layout.visibility, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
			undeclared then panic("typeNameVisibleHere: containsEnum answered yes for '{String.from(name)}' but enumOf answered undeclared — the two read one registry under one key")
		end 'layout'
	end 'typeNameVisibleHere'

	// `Point.create(3, y: 4)` — a call to a type's static method.
	//
	// It is an ORDINARY call with a mangled name, and that is the whole implementation: the callee
	// is `Point.create`, which is exactly the key `parseFunction` declared the method under
	// (`declaredMethodName`), so `emitCall` types the result from the signature index and
	// `slotCallArgs` reorders `y: 4` against the declaration — the SAME slotting every free call
	// gets, not a second one. A static method has no receiver, so there is nothing else to pass.
	//
	// ⚠ The base is NOT checked to be a declared type here, and that is deliberate rather than
	// missing: `Foo.bar()` where `Foo` is nothing at all resolves to a callee `Foo.bar` that no file
	// declares, which `SemanticCheck.validateCall` reports as E3004 — the authority for "no such
	// function", reading the registry the real parse built. A check here would be a second opinion
	// from the tolerant sweep, and the sweep must never own a veto (see `CalleeReturn.undeclared`).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **`Adder.bump(a)` — AN INSTANCE METHOD NAMED STATICALLY — REACHES HERE AND IS *NOT* REFUSED,
	// AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CLAIM IT WAS "REJECTED BY ARITY". THAT CLAIM WAS FALSE AND IT WAS
	// COSTING AN ACCESS VIOLATION.** The call carries no receiver, so the argument list is one short of
	// the signature (whose parameter 0 is `SelfParamName`) — but that is a refusal only WHEN THE COUNTS
	// DIFFER, and they COINCIDE for every zero-parameter instance method called with one argument. The
	// author's first argument then simply lands in slot 0, because that is where an unlabelled argument
	// goes; `Adder.bump(a)` on a real `Adder` IS `a.bump()`, and the runnable oracle compiles and runs
	// it (exit 42, measured), so the FORM is legal and refusing it here would refuse a working program.
	//
	// ⇒ What was missing was never a diagnostic about spelling: it was the ARGUMENT TYPE CHECK on that
	// slot, which `SemanticCheck.checkArgTypes` exempted outright. `Adder.bump(7)` COMPILED CLEAN and
	// dereferenced an `int` as a record pointer. The exemption is gone (see its header for the three
	// measured faults), the receiver is judged like any other parameter, and E3005 now says
	// `argument type mismatch for 'self': expected 'Adder', got 'int'` — the oracle's own sentence,
	// which shv2 already spells for every other parameter. Nothing about it belongs in this routine.
	//
	// (The COUNTS that E3036 carries when the arities genuinely differ ARE inflated by the receiver,
	// which is not this routine's doing and is not restated here — see the receiver prepend in
	// `parseCallNamed`, the one place that causes it, for both callers.)
	function parseQualifiedCall(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `Self.create(…)` reaches here as `Point.create(…)` — `consumeTypeBaseToken` resolved the keyword, so
		// the callee mangling, the alias interceptions and the E3062 use hook below all see one spelling (D9).
		let typeTok = try self.consumeTypeBaseToken()
		// A `Type.method()` base NAMES a type, so it is a USE for the E3062 check — and it is the only
		// place many generic-instance aliases are ever named (`IntArray.create()`). It never reaches
		// `parseTypeReference`, which is why this second hook exists at all (`recordTypeNameUse`).
		self.recordTypeNameUse(typeTok.value)

		// ⭐ **`CharacterSet.letters()` WAS INTERCEPTED HERE UNTIL W115, AND IS NOW AN ORDINARY MANGLED
		// STATIC.** The interception's stated premise — *"`CharacterSet` has no base struct: its statics BUILD
		// a box out of the set runtime, they do not name a user method"* — was true only while no file
		// declared the type. `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` is listed, so all twelve (the eleven presets and
		// `from`) are declared statics on a declared struct and mangle exactly as `Point.create` does.
		// `__ManagedFile.openRead(path)` — the compiler-owned handle type's statics (R4.1). Intercepted here
		// because the type has a registered LAYOUT but no `type` declaration and no user methods, so the
		// mangled `__ManagedFile.openRead` below would name a callee no file declares.
		if typeTok.value.equals(ManagedFileTypeName) 'managedFileStatic'
			return try self.parseManagedFileStaticCall(func, typeTok: typeTok)
		end 'managedFileStatic'

		// `__ManagedDirectory.openSearch(path)` — the search type's statics (R4.3), intercepted here for the
		// identical reason.
		if typeTok.value.equals(ManagedDirectoryTypeName) 'managedDirectoryStatic'
			return try self.parseManagedDirectoryStaticCall(func, typeTok: typeTok)
		end 'managedDirectoryStatic'

		// `__ManagedSocket.tcpConnect(host, port: p)` — the socket type's one static, intercepted here for the
		// identical reason.
		if typeTok.value.equals(ManagedSocketTypeName) 'managedSocketStatic'
			return try self.parseManagedSocketStaticCall(func, typeTok: typeTok)
		end 'managedSocketStatic'

		// `__ManagedMemory.create(count, elementSize)` (R4.2) — intercepted BEFORE the generic-alias arm
		// below, which would route it to `parseArrayStaticCall` (the alias IS bound to an `Array` instance)
		// and refuse its two arguments against `Array.create()`'s none. That is the measured red:
		// `E2010 Expected ')' but got 'integer literal'`.
		// ⚠ **AN ALIAS TO THE BUFFER REACHES THIS DOOR TOO, AND LEAVING IT OUT REPRODUCED THE VERY RED THE
		// PARAGRAPH ABOVE DESCRIBES.** `stdlib/Character.maxon:172` writes `ByteMemory.create(1, 1)` over its
		// own `typealias ByteMemory = __ManagedMemory with Byte`, and a qualifier test that matched only the
		// literal spelling sent it to `parseArrayStaticCall` — `E2010 Expected ')' but got '1'`, the same
		// measured shape one line down, arrived at through the alias instead of through the arm order.
		// `aliasDenotesBufferSurface` is the existing answer to "does this spelling denote the buffer?" and is
		// already what `declaredSurfaceOfSpelling` asks in the other position, so this is that one question
		// asked here rather than a second test of it.
		if self.spellingDenotesBufferSurface(typeTok.value) 'managedMemoryStatic'
			return try self.parseManagedMemoryStaticCall(func, typeTok: typeTok)
		end 'managedMemoryStatic'

		// ⚠ **`String` HAS NO ARM HERE, AND USED TO (W55).** `String.from(bytes)` / `String.init(managed)` were
		// intercepted for `CharacterSet`'s reason — *"`String` is a BUILTIN with no `type` declaration behind
		// it, so the mangled `String.from` below would name a callee no file declares"*. W17 listed
		// `stdlib/String.maxon`, which declares both, so the mangling below now names exactly the corpus
		// functions that serve them and the interception had become a longer spelling of the fall-through.
		// `CharacterSet`, `__ManagedFile`, `__ManagedDirectory`, `__ManagedSocket` and `__ManagedMemory` keep
		// theirs because that premise is still true of all of them: none has a `type` declaration anywhere.

		// ⭐⭐ **THE GENERIC ALIAS THIS CALL'S BASE NAMES, RESOLVED ONCE — file-scope (`IntBox`) or INNER
		// (`Plain.IntPair`) — and all FOUR of this routine's alias-driven decisions read THIS name rather than
		// the written token (W7):** the builtin-container interception below, the base a static mangles onto
		// (`denotedGenericBaseName`), the instance a constructor builds (`constructorInstanceOf`), and the type
		// its result adopts (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`). Which registration a written name denotes
		// is `genericAliasKeyFor`'s alone — the inner spelling used to be recognized here and then dropped for
		// the other three, which is exactly how one call site came to hold two readings of one alias.
		let aliasName = self.genericAliasKeyFor(typeTok.value)

		// `IntArray.create()` / `IntSet.create()` / `StrMap.create()` / `PointList.create()`, and
		// `ElementArray.create()` for the inner spelling of the same thing (P1.7 slice 1, P1.7b slice A, `Map`,
		// `List`, slice 3b-i) — a static call on a BUILTIN-CONTAINER instance alias. Every one is intercepted
		// before the generic-alias → base rewrite below, which would mangle them to `Array.create` /
		// `Set.create` / `Map.create` / `List.create` — callees no file declares, because no builtin container
		// has a base struct. Their statics lower to their own runtime, not to a user method.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ALIAS QUESTION IS ASKED ONCE AND THE INSTANCE RESOLVED ONCE**, then the resolved id is tested.
		// Written as self-contained arms it was two index resolves per container to answer one question, and
		// each new container added two more — the shape that grows fastest is the one to collapse first.
		//
		// ⛔ **ONE ROSTER, AND A NARROWER SECOND ONE WAS A WRONG ANSWER TWICE.** The inner spelling first
		// claimed `Array` and `List` only, on the argument that a `Set`/`Map` inner alias "has never reached the
		// shared parse" — but the fallthrough it left them on was not a diagnostic. MEASURED (S2i):
		// `typealias Nums = Set with ExitCode` inside a type body, then `Nums.create()`, produced a value of
		// type `unknown`, and the program was refused for an `insert` on an `unknown` value. S2i widened the
		// roster to `isBuiltinGenericBaseName` and left the NON-builtin fall-through still reading a bare
		// member, which is the identical failure one category over (W7): `typealias IntPair = Pair with
		// ExitCode` then `IntPair.create(9)`, refused for a field access on `unknown` while the bootstrap runs
		// it. Two rosters of "which containers does this compiler own", and two keys for one alias, are the
		// same duplication in two columns.
		if self.signatures.isGenericAlias(aliasName) 'containerAliasStatic'
			let aliasGiid = self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(aliasName, readerFilePath: self.filePath)

			// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS A BUILTIN CONTAINER WHOSE `create` THE COMPILER STILL EMITS (W190).** The
			// interception exists because a mangled `Base.create` would name a callee no file declares; the
			// day a listed module declares one, the mangle below IS the right answer and the interception
			// becomes the wrong one. `instanceCreateIsDeclared` is that question's ONE home — the same
			// derivation `ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall` emits off and
			// `requireGlobalContainerIsCreatable` routes on — so a body's `Vec3.create()` and a global's
			// cannot come to disagree about whose code builds the record. `Map`(W41), `Set`(W105) and
			// `List`(W153) left this door by leaving `isBuiltinGenericBaseName`; `Vector` cannot, because
			// that predicate also decides what `Vector with 3 Int` MEANS, so it leaves through the
			// derivation instead.
			if self.signatures.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(aliasGiid)) and not self.signatures.instanceCreateIsDeclared(aliasGiid) 'containerStatic'
				return try self.parseContainerAliasStaticCall(func, aliasTok: typeTok, aliasName: aliasName, giid: aliasGiid)
			end 'containerStatic'
		end 'containerAliasStatic'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)

		// ⭐⭐ **`int.fromString(s)` / `byte.fromString(s)` — A PRIMITIVE'S STATIC, WHICH IS A FREE FUNCTION IN
		// THE RESERVED SPACE (A1s-prim).** THE join site, and the only one: door 1 (`int`/`float`/`bool`, a
		// keyword resolved by `typeBaseTokenAt`) and door 2 (`byte`, a plain identifier that has always flowed
		// through here) converge on the same base token, so one arm serves both. It is intercepted for the
		// reason `CharacterSet` and `String` are one arm up — a primitive has no `type` declaration, so the
		// mangled `int.fromString` below would name a callee no file declares.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE MINT IS GATED ON THE TARGET BEING DECLARED, AND THAT GATE *IS* THE FAILURE PATH.** An
		// unknown member simply does not mint, so the callee stays what the author wrote and every diagnostic
		// downstream names `int.frobnicate` — never `__int_frobnicate`. Both references leak the mangled symbol
		// or worse: the bootstrap rewrites unconditionally and reports **E2004 `Undefined function
		// '__int_frobnicate'`** (`2-Parser.cs:24399-24411`), naming a symbol the author never wrote; v1 reports
		// NOTHING and PANICS in the backend (`Targets/Shared/StdOpHelpers.maxon:362`, `lookupFuncParamTypes`),
		// which its own `TypeResolution.maxon:10597-10616` admits. shv2 ruled on this class at D11c, so the
		// check that decides whether to rewrite is the same check that keeps the user's own spelling in the
		// message — one question, not a rewrite plus an apology. Where that spelling then goes is
		// `requirePrimitiveStaticIsResolvable`'s, which is where the two qualifiers part company.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **A USER DECLARATION OUTRANKS THE PRIMITIVE READING, AND THAT CLAUSE IS `byte`'s ALONE.** `type
		// int` is refused at its own name (`E2010: Expected identifier but got 'int'`, measured), so
		// `int.`/`float.`/`bool.` can never be contested — but `byte` is an ordinary identifier here, and a
		// `type byte` declaring `static function fromString` COMPILES AND RUNS on this tree today (measured:
		// exit 7). Minting ahead of it would silently re-point a call that already worked at the stdlib body,
		// which is the shape a green suite cannot see. Asked as `declaresCallee` of the SAME mangled name the
		// ordinary path below builds, so the two readings of one call site cannot disagree about what "the
		// user declared it" means. ⭐ **AND ASKING IT OF THAT NAME MAKES A GENERIC ALIAS FALL OUT FOR FREE** —
		// `typealias byte = Box with Num` resolves `byte.fromString` to `Box.fromString` before this test, so a
		// user's own generic instance keeps its static (measured: exit 9) with no arm of its own here.
		//
		// ⛔ **AND THE CLAUSE STOPPED BEING `byte`'s ALONE AT W133, WHICH IS WHY THIS PARAGRAPH IS AMENDED
		// RATHER THAN LEFT TO READ TRUE-BY-ACCIDENT.** It said the test *"is the identity for the three KEYWORD
		// qualifiers, which can name no declaration at all"* — true only while no declaration could be written
		// against `int`. `extension int` can, and a `static function make()` in one publishes `int.make`: this
		// clause is what makes that call LINK, by declining to mint `__int_make` for a name the program
		// actually has. The rule needed no change — it was always *"a declaration outranks the primitive
		// reading"* — but the reason under it had expired, and a premise nobody re-checks is how a guard gets
		// deleted as dead. What `requirePrimitiveStaticIsResolvable` then tests is a qualifier the author
		// wrote for which NO declaration exists, which is still exactly the case it words its sentence for.
		//
		// The order of the two probes is cost: `namesAPrimitiveType` is four `equals` on a short slice and
		// allocates nothing (the discipline the math-intrinsic table measured), while each `declaresCallee`
		// needs a joined name. Every qualified call in the program pays the first; only a primitive-based one
		// pays the rest.
		let memberTok = self.current()
		let declaredCallee = qualifiedMethodName(self.signatures.denotedGenericBaseName(aliasName, readerFilePath: self.filePath), methodName: memberTok.value)
		if namesAPrimitiveType(typeTok.value) and not self.signatures.declaresCallee(declaredCallee) 'primitiveStatic'
			let minted = primitiveStaticCallee(typeTok.value, methodName: memberTok.value)
			if self.signatures.declaresCallee(minted) 'declaredPrimitiveStatic'
				return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: minted, anchor: memberTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.none, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.primitiveStatic)
			end 'declaredPrimitiveStatic'

			try requirePrimitiveStaticIsResolvable(typeTok, memberTok: memberTok, declaredCallee: declaredCallee)
		end 'primitiveStatic'

		// ⭐⭐ **`utils.helper()` — A DIRECTORY-QUALIFIED FREE CALL (N1b), DECIDED HERE BECAUSE THE NAME IS
		// ALREADY BUILT.** `declaredCallee` is the mangling this routine links a static by, and it is exactly
		// the name the namespace index is keyed on — so the one-dot shape costs this arm a probe and not a
		// join. `parseDottedPrimary`'s multi-segment arm therefore starts at TWO dots on purpose; assembling
		// this name a second time up there measured a linear term (see `namespaceQualifiedCallDotsAt`).
		//
		// The rule itself is `resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction`'s — the TYPE reading wins wherever there is
		// one — asked here and by the multi-segment arm and nowhere else. Which NAME it resolves to is
		// `namespaceQualifiedCallee`'s, asked by both doors for the same reason: ordinarily the bare member,
		// which leaves the emitted code untouched and the visibility decision where it already was; the
		// written chain when the bare name is contested and no longer names one function.
		if self.signatures.resolvesAsNamespaceQualifiedFunction(declaredCallee) 'namespaceQualifiedFreeCall'
			return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: self.namespaceQualifiedCallee(declaredCallee, bare: memberTok.value), anchor: memberTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.none, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)
		end 'namespaceQualifiedFreeCall'

		// ⭐ `String.create(…)` — an unknown static on a type name the COMPILER owns (W55). LAST of the doors,
		// so every interception above still wins: it speaks only for a base no file may declare and a member
		// no file does. See `requireCompilerOwnedStaticIsResolvable` for why this one class is the parser's to
		// refuse while a user type's stays SemanticCheck's.
		try requireCompilerOwnedStaticIsResolvable(typeTok, memberTok: memberTok, declaredCallee: declaredCallee, signatures: self.signatures)

		// ⭐ **ASKED ONCE AND READ TWICE.** The consume side (which argument OWNS the box being built) and the
		// result side (which instance the value IS) are two halves of one fact, and each used to re-derive it
		// from `aliasName`: a second derivation is a second chance to answer the same call site differently,
		// which is what `staticCallClaimsBase`'s own header says about the callee name one door up.
		// ⭐⭐ **THIS DOOR IS THE `static` SIDE OF A `static`/INSTANCE PAIR, AND THE SYNTAX IS THE WHOLE
		// DISCRIMINATOR** (`same-name-methods.md`). `Type.m(…)` means the static and `receiver.m(…)` the
		// instance — with identical user parameter lists there is nothing else to decide it — so the pair is two
		// registration names (`staticMemberRegistrationName`) and this is the only door that asks for the
		// static one. Every INSTANCE call door is untouched, which is exactly why the instance keeps the source
		// spelling.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS ONE RECORDED FACT AND NOT A PROBE-THEN-FALL-BACK.** Both references try `<name>~static`
		// first and take the bare name when nothing answers, which makes the resolution a property of what the
		// registry happens to hold; here the CONTEST is recorded by the one sweep that sees both declarations
		// and the name is DERIVED from it — by `memberRegistrationKey`, the same reader that chose the key this
		// declaration's facts were filed under, so the call and the declaration cannot come to spell one symbol
		// two ways. That also keeps `Adder.bump(a)` — an INSTANCE method named statically, which this routine's
		// header settles as legal — on the source spelling without a second rule: no static of that name
		// exists, so there is no contest.
		//
		// `takesReceiver: false` is the reason and not a convention: a `Type.m(…)` call passes no receiver, so
		// it is asking for the receiverless member of that name.
		let staticCallee = self.signatures.memberRegistrationKey(declaredCallee, takesReceiver: false)

		let constructed = self.constructorInstanceOf(aliasName)
		try self.requireConstructedVectorElementType(constructed, aliasTok: typeTok)
		let result = try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: staticCallee, anchor: memberTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.none, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: constructed, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)
		match constructed 'constructed'
			none then break 'constructed'
			instance(gid) then self.retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult(result, gid: gid, aliasName: aliasName)
		end 'constructed'
		return result
	end 'parseQualifiedCall'

	// The concrete instance a `Type.method(…)` call constructs (P1.6-B2) — an `instance(gid)` when `Type` is
	// a generic-instance alias (`StrBox`), so the caller-side consume can move its managed arguments into the
	// owning box; `none` for a plain type's static method. It reads the whole-program index, complete in the
	// real parse (during the tolerant sweep `isGenericAlias` answers no, so the consume simply does not fire —
	// which is sound: the sweep's artifact is discarded).
	//
	// ⭐ **A BARE GENERIC TYPE NAME INSIDE A TYPE-PARAMETER SCOPE IS THE SECOND WAY A BASE NAMES AN INSTANCE
	//** — `Inner.make(items)` written in `type Outer uses T` builds an `Inner with Outer.T`. It is the
	// same question as the alias arm's and gets the same answer shape, so the two live at one door: what
	// differs is only where the ARGUMENTS come from, a `typealias`'s written list or the enclosing scope's
	// parameters. See `bareGenericInstanceInScope` for the binding rule and for why the caller-side gate is
	// spelled here.
	function constructorInstanceOf(baseName ByteArray) returns ConstructorInstance
		if self.signatures.isGenericAlias(baseName) 'genericAlias'
			return ConstructorInstance.instance(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(baseName, readerFilePath: self.filePath))
		end 'genericAlias'
		if self.enclosingTypeParams.count() == 0 'noTypeParameterScope'
			return ConstructorInstance.none
		end 'noTypeParameterScope'

		return self.bareGenericInstanceInScope(baseName)
	end 'constructorInstanceOf'

	// ⭐ **A GENERIC CONSTRUCTOR'S RESULT IS THE INSTANCE, not the base struct (P1.6-B1).**
	// `BoolSizer.create(…)` links to `Sizer.create`, which returns `Self` — the BASE struct `Sizer`
	// (`structRef`) — because the shared body is compiled once against the opaque `T`. But the VALUE is a
	// `Sizer with bool`, and dictionary-passing needs that instance identity ON the value so a later
	// `s.typeSize()` can pass the right layout descriptor. So when the result adopted the base struct's
	// type, retype it to `genericInstance(gid)`, carrying the gid the method call site reads out of the
	// value's name column. A method returning a SCALAR (`typeSize() returns Integer`) or a DIFFERENT struct
	// keeps its type — only a `Self`/base-struct return is the instance, which the base-id guard enforces.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE ARRIVES AS A PARAMETER RATHER THAN BEING RE-DERIVED FROM `aliasName`**, and that is not
	// tidying: the CONSUME side already asked `constructorInstanceOf` for exactly this value, so re-deriving it
	// was a second reading of one call site — and with generic substitution the two readings would genuinely differ, because a
	// base name may denote an instance the alias registry knows nothing about (`Inner` inside `type Outer uses
	// T`). `aliasName` is still taken, for the one thing only the WRITTEN spelling can answer.
	//
	// ⚠ The `GenericAlias` in the name is now the NARROWER of the two bases it serves; it is kept because three
	// cross-file comments cite it (`SemanticCheck.maxon:2813, 2960`, `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1560`) and this
	// rung owns neither file. A rename is a two-file comment edit and belongs to whoever holds them.
	function retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult(result ValueId, gid GenericInstanceId, aliasName ByteArray)
		self.retypeConstructorResultToInstance(result, gid: gid)

		// ⛔⛔ **PROVENANCE IS ABOUT WHAT THE SITE WROTE, NOT ABOUT WHETHER A RETYPE WAS NEEDED — and it used
		// to sit BEHIND the retype's two guards, so a factory whose result was ALREADY the instance recorded
		// NOTHING.** That is the common case, not the rare one: only a DECLARED generic's `create() returns
		// Self` comes back as the shared body's base `structRef` and needs retyping, while every builtin
		// container's factory — `OtherCol.create()` over `Array` — is already typed and fell straight out of
		// the first guard. **MEASURED with a probe at the display door: `named=''` for exactly that value,
		// while its own function's provenance map existed** — which is why a diagnostic could not say which of
		// two R-1-merged aliases the blamed statement had written, and named the first-declared instead.
		//
		// ⚠ The guards below are the ones this fact needs, and they are NOT the retype's: the value must BE an
		// instance and it must be THIS one. A result that is some other base's struct is not described by
		// this alias and must not be stamped with its name.
		if self.tagOf(result) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAnInstanceValue'
			return
		end 'notAnInstanceValue'

		if self.valueNameId(result) != gid 'aDifferentInstance'
			return
		end 'aDifferentInstance'

		// ⭐ Carry the FACTORY's instance-alias provenance onto the result (P1.6-C), so a later
		// `w.getTag()` / `w.setTag(…)` on this value can re-qualify an inner-alias return/parameter to
		// `WrapperA.Idx`. The shared gid cannot recover `WrapperA`; only the syntactic `aliasName` here can.
		//
		// ⚠ **A BARE GENERIC BASE HAS NO SUCH SPELLING TO CARRY** — `Inner` names the BASE, and the
		// per-instance registries are keyed on alias names (`perInstanceAliasFromParts` resolves the prefix
		// through the generic-alias registry, which has no entry for a base). Stamping the base name here
		// would file a per-instance question under a name that answers no per-instance registry; the
		// substitution the value actually needs is `retypeInstanceMethodResult`'s instance arm, which reads
		// the gid and never the spelling.
		if self.signatures.isGenericAlias(aliasName) 'writtenAlias'
			self.recordValueInstanceAlias(result, aliasName: aliasName)
		end 'writtenAlias'

		// ⭐ **AND THE ALIAS'S OWN ARGUMENT SPELLING** — `emitOwnedContainerCreate`'s line for the builtin
		// `Array`, at the door a DECLARED generic's factory leaves through. `BufCell.make(…)` mints the only
		// kind of value that has no declared type to read its surface from, and `gid` cannot answer for it:
		// `Cell with __ManagedMemory` and `Cell with ByteArray` are one instance. Only the written spelling can,
		// and it is the same spelling `recordValueInstanceAlias` is here for.
		self.markDeclaredSurface(result, declared: surfaceOfRootBits(self.signatures.aliasSpellingSurfaceBits(aliasName, readerFilePath: self.filePath)))
	end 'retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult'

	// ⭐ **THE RETYPE HALF.** A DECLARED generic's `create() returns Self` hands back the shared body's base
	// `structRef`, and only the call site knows which instance was asked for. A result that is already an
	// instance — every builtin container's factory — needs nothing done to it, which is precisely why the
	// provenance above may not be written behind these guards.
	function retypeConstructorResultToInstance(result ValueId, gid GenericInstanceId)
		if self.tagOf(result) != ValueTypeTag.structRef 'notBaseStruct'
			return
		end 'notBaseStruct'

		let baseId = self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(gid))
		let resultNameId = try self.valueNameIds.get(result) otherwise panic("retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult: result v{result} has no name-id — the two type columns are dense and in lockstep")
		if resultNameId != baseId 'notThisBase'
			return
		end 'notThisBase'
		self.retypeValue(result, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(gid))
	end 'retypeConstructorResultToInstance'

	// ⭐ **`p.add(10)` / `self.add(10)` — AN INSTANCE METHOD CALL, WHICH IS AN ORDINARY DIRECT CALL
	// WITH ONE EXTRA ARGUMENT.**
	//
	// The callee is `Adder.add` — mangled from the RECEIVER'S DECLARED TYPE and the method name,
	// which is exactly the key `parseFunction` declared it under (`declaredMethodName`), the same
	// key `Point.create` is linked by. So the result is typed from the signature index, arguments
	// are slotted by `slotCallArgs`, arity and labels are validated by `SemanticCheck`, and the
	// backend passes the receiver in argument register 0 — none of which learns that methods exist.
	//
	// **THE RECEIVER IS PREPENDED AS ARGUMENT 0, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE DISPATCH MECHANISM.** It
	// pairs with the `SelfParamName` parameter `parseFunction` pushed at position 0: the call's
	// first argument is POSITIONAL, so `slotCallArgs` puts it at position 0 without being told
	// anything, and the user's `10` — also positional, at index 1 — lands on the first user
	// parameter. The two halves meet through the ordinary label rules and nothing else.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER IS BORROWED — the callee neither owns it nor drops it.** It is passed as a
	// parameter, and a parameter is not owned by the callee: the CALLER owns the +1, so no incref
	// is emitted here and no drop is emitted there. The receiver's box is dropped by the binding
	// that owns it, at ITS scope exit (`ownedBindings`), which is why `c.increment()` twice over
	// followed by `c.getCount()` is well-defined and returns 42 rather than reading freed memory.
	// Both references agree for the receiver specifically — the bootstrap gives `self`
	// `OwnershipFlags.IsParam`, and v1's `__self` is param 0, which its refcounter treats as a
	// borrow — so this is not the borrow-vs-consume ruling struct-typed PARAMETERS are deferred
	// for. A consume would have to be a SINK: a store of the RECEIVER into heap storage, or its
	// passing to a callee that consumes it.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE SINK IS UNREACHABLE BECAUSE A BORROWED AGGREGATE CANNOT BE MOVED INTO ONE — NOT BECAUSE
	// `self` IS NOT A VALUE, AND NOT BECAUSE FIELDS ARE SCALAR.** Both of those earlier answers were
	// written here and both have since gone false, so what is left is the one that was always doing the
	// work. `var next as Node` inside `type Node` is a legal struct-typed field, and `self` IS a value
	// (S5) — so `next = self`, `bag.push(self)`, `Self{f: self}` and `Cell.create(self)` all PARSE now.
	// Every one of them is refused where it belongs, by the rules keyed on BORROWED-ness rather than on
	// the `self` keyword: a store into durable storage is `moveManagedValueInto`'s refusal, a consuming
	// argument is `requireConsumableArg`'s. **`self` reaches them as parameter 0, exactly as `other Node`
	// does** — which is why the same probe refuses `next = other`, and why an earlier note claiming that
	// spelling *"compiles TODAY, to a heap store with no incref"* is no longer true either.
	//
	// ⭐ **A HAND-OFF IS NOT A SINK, and that distinction is the whole of what S5 changed.** `return self`
	// and a `gives self` arm hand the receiver to the CALLER, which adopts one reference and drops it; they
	// are co-owned by an `__mm_retain` (`promoteBorrowedToOwned`) and the receiver's own owner is untouched.
	// A SINK is durable storage that OUTLIVES the statement, where co-ownership is not enough because the
	// language's answer there is a MOVE — one owner, source poisoned — and moving out of a borrow needs the
	// transitive-consume fixpoint. **A rung that weakens either durable-storage refusal re-opens this**,
	// which is why they are named here rather than counted once; the probes live in
	// `specs-shv2/enum-union-method-receiver.md` and `specs-shv2/structs.md`.
	//
	// v1 does not assert "the receiver is borrowed" anywhere: it FALLS OUT of `__self` being param 0 plus a
	// structural per-param consume analysis (`ParamConsumeAnalysis.maxon:5-13`), and it pays for the
	// hand-off with the same incref shv2 now emits (`InsertRefcounts.planReturnIncrefs`, whose own comment
	// names `FilePath.normalize` and `FilePath.resolve` as its drivers). shv2 will need that analysis at the
	// moment a borrowed aggregate may be MOVED into a sink, and not before.
	function parseMethodCall(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let baseTok = self.current()
		if self.scope.contains(self.baseBindingName(baseTok)) 'localReceiver'
			// The subject is read at the cursor, which still sits on the base — the derivation this door made
			// internally until a receiver arrived whose storage no single token spells (see there).
			return try self.dispatchMethodOnBinding(func, binding: self.scope.getInfo(self.baseBindingName(baseTok)), baseTok: baseTok, subjectName: self.borrowSubjectNameAt(self.pos), resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'localReceiver'

		// A CAPTURED enclosing receiver (P1.5-A2b-2): `b.doubled()` inside a closure that captures the
		// struct `b`. The base is not in the closure's OWN scope (checked above) but resolves into an
		// enclosing one.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **IT IS ASKED BEFORE THE TOP-LEVEL FALL-THROUGH, AND THAT ORDER *IS* THE SHADOWING RULE —
		// ONE RULE, ENCODED IN THREE PLACES, WHICH IS WHY EACH OF THEM SAYS SO.** `parseVariableReference`
		// states it for a bare read ("an enclosing local that SHADOWS a same-named global is caught as the
		// capture it is, not silently resolved to the global — a wrong answer"), and `methodCallsAt` asks
		// its two membership tests in the same order. A method RECEIVER is the same name in the same
		// position, so it must resolve to the same binding.
		//
		// This arm sat BELOW the managed one until the P1.7-slice-2a review, and the inversion was a LIVE
		// wrong answer rather than a latent one. With a top-level `var msg = "GLOBAL-TWELVE"` and a
		// `let msg = "ab"` in `main`, a closure body read the CAPTURE for `{msg}` (printing `ab`) and the
		// GLOBAL for `msg.byteLength()` (yielding 13) — two resolutions of one name, in one expression,
		// neither of them a compile error. The `var` half is the worse one: a `var` receiver is WRITABLE,
		// so a mutating method would have written the global while the program named a local.
		match try self.captureScopeOf(self.baseBindingName(baseTok), anchor: baseTok) 'captured'
			found(enclosing) then return try self.capturedMethodCall(func, baseTok: baseTok, enclosing: enclosing, resultUsed: resultUsed)
			notCaptured then break 'captured'
		end 'captured'

		// ⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL MANAGED BINDING IS A RECEIVER** — a `let` (`Live.count()`, `name.byteLength()`,
		// P1.7a) or a `var` (`msg.append("!")`, `Buffer.count()`, P1.7 slice 2a). Neither is a binding in
		// scope and neither is a capture, so without this arm the base reads as a TYPE NAME and the call is
		// mangled to a callee `Live.count` no file declares (E3004, measured).
		//
		// It materializes the receiver and then hands it to the SAME `dispatchMethodOnBinding` a local
		// receiver goes through, wearing a SYNTHETIC binding. That is not a shortcut: the dispatch already
		// routes by TAG (String / Array / Set / conformer / struct) and already enforces the one
		// immutable-receiver rule (`noteReceiverWrite`, E3019). Re-deciding either here would be a
		// second copy of both.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE TWO KINDS ARE TOLD APART BY MATCHING THE RESOLUTION, NOT BY RE-ASKING FOR IT.** Past the
		// gate this resolves the base ONCE and dispatches on the arms; it used to test a predicate that
		// resolved it and then resolve it again to build the receiver. The `let` and the `var` differ in
		// exactly two places — where the value comes from and whether the binding is mutable — and
		// `mutable` is what makes `Live.push(1)` an E3019 while `Buffer.push(1)` is legal.
		//
		// A `none` here is a base spelled like a managed binding but not VISIBLE from this file. It cannot
		// arrive through `methodCallsAt` (which resolved the same answer and declined the shape), and with
		// the capture already ruled out above there is nothing left for it to be — so it joins the gate's
		// own `false` at the one impossible-state panic below rather than getting an arm of its own.
		//
		// ⭐ **A `var` RECEIVER IS THE SLOT'S CURRENT OCCUPANT, LOADED — AND IT IS NOT STORED BACK.** Every
		// receiver-writing method rewrites the RECORD in place and never moves it: `__managed_append` grows a
		// String's fused record through `buffer@0`/`length@8`, and an `Array`'s `push`/`set`/`clear` detach or rewrite the
		// element buffer while the 48-byte record stays put (`dispatchArrayMethod` states the same
		// invariant for a local receiver). So the pointer in the `.data` slot is still the right pointer
		// after the call, and a store-back would be a second write of a value that did not change. The
		// loaded pointer is BORROWED — the slot owns it (see `emitGlobalLoad`) — so nothing drops it here
		// and `__maxon_global_cleanup` releases it at exit.
		//
		// A `let`'s literal is re-materialized at the use, exactly as a bare reference to it would be.
		if self.managedTopLevelBaseAt(self.pos) 'managedTopLevelBase'
			match self.managedReceiverBindingOf(baseTok) 'binding'
				constant(bytes, kind) then return try self.dispatchOnManagedReceiver(func, baseTok: baseTok, value: try self.emitManagedConstant(bytes, kind: kind, range: SourceRange.ofToken(baseTok)), varType: self.signatures.managedConstValueType(kind, elementFilePath: self.filePath), mutable: false, resultUsed: resultUsed)
				global(label, valueType, mutable) then return try self.dispatchOnManagedReceiver(func, baseTok: baseTok, value: self.emitGlobalLoad(label, valueType: valueType, range: SourceRange.ofToken(baseTok)), varType: valueType, mutable: mutable, resultUsed: resultUsed)
				none then break 'binding'
			end 'binding'
		end 'managedTopLevelBase'

		panic("parseMethodCall: '{String.from(baseTok.value)}' is neither a local binding, a capture, nor a top-level managed binding, but `methodCallsAt` gated this call — the two must agree on what a method receiver is, and on the ORDER they resolve one in")
	end 'parseMethodCall'

	// ⭐ **THE SHAPE OF A SYNTHETIC RECEIVER BINDING, DECIDED ONCE.** A top-level managed receiver is not a
	// declaration the parser read, so its `VarInfo` is invented here — and every field of it is a claim the
	// dispatcher acts on: the NAME an E3019 would blame, the TYPE the tag-routing reads, `mutable`, and
	// (by omission) `isParameter: false`, which is what keeps `immutableReceiverNameOf`'s parameter
	// exemption from silently admitting a write to a `let`-bound constant. Two arms building their own
	// would be two chances for one of those to drift.

	// ⚠ **THE E3070 SUBJECT COMES OFF THE BINDING'S NAME, NOT OFF THE CURSOR'S TOKENS (W65).** Every receiver
	// this door serves is module storage the base is not a local for, so the token-index derivation's scope
	// arm is dead here and only its tail applies. Deriving it from the name is also what lets a STATIC
	// MEMBER through: its storage is `Names.items`, which no single token spells, so a token read would
	// answer `Names` — a name that denotes no storage — and silently drop the note.
	function dispatchOnManagedReceiver(func IrFunction, baseTok Token, value ValueId, varType MaxonType, mutable bool, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		return try self.dispatchMethodOnBinding(func, binding: VarInfo.createValueBinding(baseTok.value, varType: varType, mutable: mutable, boundValue: value), baseTok: baseTok, subjectName: self.moduleStorageSubjectName(baseTok.value), resultUsed: resultUsed)
	end 'dispatchOnManagedReceiver'

	// ⭐ **BUILD THE RECEIVER A BINDING DENOTES AND HAND IT TO THE ONE DISPATCHER.** The cursor sits ON the
	// base token; this consumes it and `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` consumes the `.`. Shared by a LOCAL
	// receiver and a top-level MANAGED CONSTANT one, which differ only in where the binding came from — so
	// the two spellings cannot come to disagree about which dispatch a `String`, an `Array`, a `Set` or a
	// conformer receiver gets.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERYTHING BINDING-SPECIFIC HAPPENS HERE, AND NOTHING ELSE DOES.** This door owns exactly the
	// four facts a NAME carries and a temporary cannot — the live-binding guard, the self-field
	// materialization, the E3019 blame name and E3070 subject, and the owning-parameter mask — and then the
	// receiver is just a value. That split is what lets `parsePostfix` serve a literal, a call result or a
	// field read through the SAME switch instead of growing a parallel arm per type (the shape
	// `IrInterface.maxon:570-573` already prescribes for the builtin-conformer door: "to keep the two
	// dispatch spellings from disagreeing").
	// ⚠ **`subjectName` IS THE CALLER'S, and it is E3070's: a method call on a container hands the receiver
	// to the conflict check as the implicit ARGUMENT 0 it is.** It used to be derived here from `self.pos` —
	// which is exact for a receiver that IS one token and wrong for one that is not. A static member's
	// receiver is `Names.items` (W65), three tokens, and the cursor-based read answered `Names`: a name
	// denoting no storage, so the borrow was recorded under nothing and could never meet the rebind. Each
	// caller holds the fact directly — a local's from the cursor, module storage's from its own name — so
	// the derivation is stated where it is known rather than guessed from a position.
	function dispatchMethodOnBinding(func IrFunction, binding VarInfo, baseTok Token, subjectName ByteArray, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The receiver must be LIVE (a moved-from String / Array / Set / struct touched through a method is
		// use-after-move, E3102), checked before the method name is even resolved. It was written once per
		// dispatcher and is asked ONCE here — the question is about the BINDING, and every arm past this
		// point holds only a value.
		try self.requireBindingLive(binding, nameTok: baseTok)

		// ⭐ **E3077's RECEIVER DOOR — the one place a method call holds the BINDING it dispatches on.** A
		// method may write its receiver (`arr.push(9)`, and any user method whose `self` is written), so a
		// `var` that receives one has exercised its mutability. Marked before the dispatch splits into its
		// arms, because every arm past here holds only a value.
		//
		// ⚠ **UNCONDITIONALLY, WITHOUT ASKING WHETHER THIS METHOD WRITES — which makes shv2 weaker than the
		// bootstrap on exactly one shape, deliberately.** For a USER method the answer is whole-program and
		// arrives a phase after this diagnostic is decided, and the bootstrap marks every user-method
		// receiver unconditionally for that reason. For a CONTAINER method the parser DOES know
		// (`noteReceiverWrite`'s `mutatesReceiver`), and the bootstrap marks only the mutating ones — so
		// `var a = …` + `a.count()` is E3077 there and is accepted here. That is a MISSED diagnostic, never
		// a refused program, and buying it back means threading the receiver's name through six
		// `noteReceiverWrite` callers to make a corpus-wide refusal fire on MORE programs. Not worth it: the
		// direction that costs something is the other one.
		self.markMutabilityExercised(binding)

		// ⚠ The OWNER mask is read off the binding as it ARRIVED, before `baseBindingOf` materializes
		// a self-field alias into an ordinary value binding — materializing is exactly what erases the fact
		// that the record belongs to `self`. See `receiverOwnerMask`.
		let owner = try self.receiverOwnerMask(func, binding: binding, baseTok: baseTok)
		// Read BEFORE the materialization too: `baseBindingOf` hands back an ordinary value binding,
		// which is exactly the fact the struct fallthrough still has to refuse on.
		let selfFieldName = baseTok.value if binding.isSelfField else EmptySourceText

		// ⭐⭐⭐ **`managed.length()` INSIDE `stdlib/String.maxon` HANDS OVER THE STRING'S OWN RECORD AND MINTS
		// NOTHING (W49 wave 7 cure).** Reading a fused wrapper's inline `managed` normally goes through
		// `emitFieldLoad`, which goes through `__str_bytes_view` so the value can be TYPED as an
		// `Array with Byte` — and when the only thing that value does is receive ONE member call, that whole
		// round trip exists for a callee that reads two words the String record already carries at the same
		// offsets. ⚠ **THE ROUND TRIP WAS A 48-BYTE MINTED RECORD UNTIL W157 AND IS A REFCOUNT PAIR NOW**
		// (`__mm_incref` in, `__managed_decref` out), so what this door saves is smaller than the measurement
		// below — which was taken against the allocation — and is still the whole of it on a hot path.
		//
		// ⛔ **MEASURED, AND IT IS WHY THIS EXISTS**: `s.byteLength()` — whose corpus body is exactly
		// `return managed.length()` — ran **86.7x** slower than the synthesized arm it replaced, against the
		// 2-4x a pure call frame costs. `String.byteAt` is the same shape once PER BYTE, which is what turned
		// `hashString` into one allocation per byte (35.9x). Neither was a leak or a wrong answer; both were
		// an allocation nobody needed.
		//
		// ⭐ **IT IS THE ARRAY PATH'S OWN SHAPE, NOT A NEW ONE.** `arr.managed.setLength(2)` already dispatches
		// with the receiver's record and mints nothing — `dispatchArrayMethod`'s `.managed` arm splits on
		// `memberCallFollows` and re-enters with `viaManagedField: true`. That split is exactly this one: a
		// `managed` that is CALLED needs no record of its own, and a `managed` used as a VALUE (bound,
		// returned, passed, or stored in a `String{…}` literal) still mints, because the caller may drop it
		// and dropping the receiver's own record is the double free `bufferSurfaceOf`'s header measures.
		//
		// ⚠ The member is asked through `fusedManagedMemberTakesTheRecord`, which carries the `@40` census:
		// `slice` is NOT on it, so `clone()`/`toByteArray()` keep the view they genuinely need.
		//
		// ⛔⛔⛔ **IT PRESENTED `internSynthesizedByteInstance()` TO EVERY RECEIVER, AND THAT LIE IS THE WHOLE
		// OF W194.** The instance is what `emitFieldLoad` stamps on the VIEW it mints, and for a `String` or a
		// `Character` it is exactly right — their record IS a byte buffer. For an `Array` or a `Vector` it is
		// a stride lie the very next header down the file already spelled out (`emitFieldLoad`'s
		// `inlineManagedIsTheReceiver` arm: *"a stride lie for an `Array with Element`, whose slots are 8
		// bytes wide"*) — and the members that CONSULT the instance rather than merely reading the record
		// answered about a `Byte` whatever the receiver's element really was. **Three of the six did, and each
		// failed differently**, all measured on this rung's own RED baseline:
		//   • `append` → `ProgramSignatures.arrayAppendArgAdmits` asked `containerElementIsOpaque` of the BYTE
		//     instance (false), fell through to `sameTrivialArrayShape`, asked `arrayElementSize` of the
		//     ARGUMENT, and `primitiveTypeByteSize` **PANICKED** on the type parameter.
		//   • `setByte` → E3110 (`requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer`) and E3118
		//     (`requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot`) both read the instance, so on an `Array with String` the
		//     write was admitted as a `Byte`'s: it **COMPILED CLEAN AND EXITED 101**, a byte landing in a
		//     String POINTER slot and the leak that follows.
		//   • `makeCharFromBytes` → `__char_at` reads `singleByteGraphemes@48`, off the end of a 48-byte
		//     buffer record. Closed separately and structurally, by the tag test in `dispatchArrayMethod`.
		// In every one of the three the `self.managed.…` spelling — the VALUE path, which carries the
		// receiver's real instance — already answered correctly, which is what says the ROUTE was the fault
		// and never the operation.
		//
		// ⇒ **THE DOOR HANDS OVER THE RECEIVER'S OWN BUFFER-SURFACE INSTANCE** (`bufferSurfaceInstanceOf`),
		// which is the same answer `bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord` retypes its minted surface to — one fact,
		// one home, so the record path and the value path cannot come to disagree about the element. That is
		// what the paragraph above was reaching for when it said *"the two spellings of one field answer to
		// one element rule"*: the rule was right and the constant was wrong.
		//
		// ⛔ **AND THE FAST PATH IS KEPT FOR ALL FOUR CONFORMERS, BECAUSE THE ALTERNATIVE WAS MEASURED AND IT
		// COSTS 1.65–1.9x.** W194's first cure ROUTED `Array` and `Vector` to the value door instead. That is
		// correct and it is dearer than it looks: `Array.hash` and `Array.equals` are NOT on
		// `arraySurfaceMemberNames`, so unlike `count`/`get` they are corpus bodies a program actually calls,
		// and each loops `try managed.byteAt(i)` **once per byte** (`stdlib/Array.maxon:670-688`) — which the
		// re-route turned into a `coOwnAggregateAsTemp` per byte. Hashing a 64-element `Array with Int`
		// 200,000 times, interleaved against the same source built by the same bootstrap: **413 ms → 684 ms**
		// (coordinator, min of four) and **327 ms → 624 ms** (review, min of three, with `Character.hash`
		// unchanged one type away as the control). `Array.hash` is what `Map` and `Set` key on. Fixing the
		// instance costs nothing at run time and fixes all three members at once.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S LENGTH-WRITER REFUSAL IS THIS DOOR'S OWN AND MUST STAY HERE** — see
		// `refuseLengthChangeOnASizedContainersBuffer`. This door mints no value, so there is nothing to
		// carry a mark; and the instance it now hands over is spelled `Array with Element` for a vector too
		// (that retype is load-bearing, W189), so no `giid` test downstream can tell a vector's record from a
		// growable array's either. Only the ENCLOSING DECLARATION answers here, asked BY NAME because that is
		// the form `enclosingType` holds.
		//
		// The SYNTACTIC half is asked first because it is total and cheap; only then is the layout resolved,
		// which needs a `try` and so cannot join a condition chain.
		if binding.isSelfField and self.memberCallFollows(self.pos + 1) and fusedManagedMemberTakesTheRecord(self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2).value) 'aCalledManagedFieldMember'
			// ⚠ The layout is bound here rather than asked through `selfFieldIsInlineManaged` because this
			// door needs it TWICE — once to admit the field, once to say which instance the record's buffer
			// surface wears. `StructLayout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord` is that admission's one home, so
			// asking it directly is the SAME question and not a second reading of it.
			let layout = try self.enclosingLayout(baseTok)
			if layout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord(binding.fieldIndex) 'inlineManagedServesTheRecord'
				try self.refuseLengthChangeOnASizedContainersBuffer(self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2), onASizedContainersRecord: self.signatures.declarationIsASizedContainerRecordNamed(self.enclosingType))

				let receiver = try self.requireReceiverValue(baseTok)
				let record = try self.selfFieldValueBinding(binding, baseTok: baseTok, value: receiver)
				let surface = self.bufferSurfaceInstanceOf(receiver, layout: layout)

				self.advance()
				try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
				return try self.dispatchArrayMethod(func, arr: self.bindingValue(record, range: SourceRange.ofToken(baseTok)), giid: surface, resultUsed: resultUsed, immutableReceiverName: self.immutableReceiverNameOf(record, baseTok: baseTok), borrowSubjectName: subjectName, receiverOwner: owner, receiverStorage: self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: subjectName), viaManagedField: true)
			end 'inlineManagedServesTheRecord'
		end 'aCalledManagedFieldMember'

		let box = try self.baseBoxOf(binding, baseTok: baseTok)
		let held = try self.baseBindingOf(binding, baseTok: baseTok, box: box)

		self.advance()
		return try self.dispatchMethodOnReceiver(func, recv: MethodReceiver.create(box, tag: maxonTypeTag(held.varType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(held.varType), anchor: baseTok, immutableName: self.immutableReceiverNameOf(held, baseTok: baseTok), subjectName: subjectName, owner: owner, selfFieldName: selfFieldName), resultUsed: resultUsed)
	end 'dispatchMethodOnBinding'

	// Is this self-field alias the INLINE `managed` of a fused wrapper — the one field whose address IS the
	// receiver's? The same two questions `emitFieldLoad`'s own arm asks, and since the W194 review that is a
	// MECHANISM rather than a claim: both reach `StructLayout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord`, whose header
	// carries the wrong answer a divergence between them would produce. What is left here is the only part
	// that is this door's own — resolving the ENCLOSING layout, which the field-load door is handed.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ADMITS ALL FOUR CONFORMERS — `String`, `Character`, `Array` and `Vector` — AND EVERY ASKER WANTS
	// THAT.** `receiverOwnerMask`'s E3019 column is about a write reaching the CALLER's record, which is true
	// of all four; and the fused record door admits all four too, because W194's cure is to hand each of them
	// its OWN buffer instance rather than to turn any of them away (`bufferSurfaceInstanceOf`). ⛔ W194's
	// FIRST cure did narrow this to the two byte records, in a `selfFieldIsTheEnclosingByteRecord` that is
	// gone: correct, and measured at 1.65–1.9x on `Array.hash` — see the fused door's header for the numbers
	// and for why the instance, not the routing, was the thing that was wrong.
	function selfFieldIsInlineManaged(binding VarInfo, baseTok Token) returns bool throws ParseError
		let layout = try self.enclosingLayout(baseTok)
		return layout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord(binding.fieldIndex)
	end 'selfFieldIsInlineManaged'

	// ⭐⭐ **E3077's METHOD-CALL DOOR: THE STORAGE THE RECEIVER DENOTES, AND EVERY NAME IN THE ARGUMENT
	// LIST.** Called at the `.`, before anything is consumed, so the whole call is still ahead of the cursor.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER HALF IS `subjectName`, WHICH IS THE CHAIN'S ROOT AND NOT THE IMMEDIATE VALUE** — the
	// E3070 column, already derived through the one `borrowSubjectNameAt`. That is exactly the name E3077
	// needs: `b.items.push(42)` writes storage rooted at `b`, and MEASURED on the oracle it is `b`'s `var`
	// that the write excuses. A field READ does not excuse it (`var q` + `return q.x` is E3077 there), which
	// is why this hangs off the METHOD dispatch and not off `requireStructBase`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ARGUMENT HALF IS A TOKEN SWEEP, AND THAT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A SHORTCUT.** A call
	// argument may be written by the callee, so both references count every mutable-var argument
	// (`ParseCallArgValue`'s three `_reassignedVars.Add` sites) — MEASURED: `var b = IntArray.create()` +
	// `a.append(b)` is accepted by the oracle, and it is `specs/arrays.md:append-empty-to-nonempty`, a
	// CANONICAL program shv2 may not edit. `parseCallArgs` covers a user call, but a BUILTIN container
	// method parses its own arguments with a bare `parseExpression` at dozens of arms across six
	// dispatchers, and a marker added to each would be a list that fails OPEN: the arm somebody forgets is a
	// program REFUSED, which is the expensive direction. A sweep of the parenthesised span cannot miss an
	// arm, and what it over-marks — a name inside `a.push(b.count())` — costs a missed diagnostic and
	// nothing else.
	//
	// It is gated on the function having a `var` IN SCOPE at all, so a body that declares none pays one
	// `count()` per method call and sweeps nothing; past that, every name it finds is settled by one
	// `innermostMutableVarPosition` lookup (see `markNameMutabilityExercised`).
	function noteMethodCallMutabilityUses(recv MethodReceiver)
		if self.mutableVars.count() == 0 'noVarCouldBeTheSubject'
			return
		end 'noVarCouldBeTheSubject'

		self.markNameMutabilityExercised(recv.subjectName)

		// The cursor is ON the `.`, so the method name is the next token and its argument list — if it has
		// one — opens at the one after that.
		var index = (self.pos + MethodArgListOffset) as TokenIndex
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.leftParen 'memberTakesNoArguments'
			return
		end 'memberTakesNoArguments'

		var depth = 0 as BlockDepth
		while self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.eof 'sweep'
			let kind = self.kindAt(index)
			if kind == TokenKind.leftParen 'opens'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'opens' else if kind == TokenKind.rightParen 'closes'
				depth = depth - 1
				if depth == 0 'argumentListClosed'
					return
				end 'argumentListClosed'
			end 'closes' else if tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(index)) 'aName'
				self.markNameMutabilityExercised(self.tokenAt(index).value)
			end 'aName'

			index = (index + 1) as TokenIndex
		end 'sweep'
	end 'noteMethodCallMutabilityUses'

	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER A VALUE IS.** A temporary — a string literal, a call result, a parenthesised
	// expression, a field read — is bound to no name, and the three provenance fields are the empty answer
	// for a REASON rather than by omission:
	//   • `immutableName` — nothing may be blamed for a write through it. No `let` promises anything about a
	//     value nobody named, so a mutating method on one is legal exactly as it is on a `var`.
	//   • `subjectName` — it denotes no STORAGE a borrow could have been taken out of, so E3070 has nothing
	//     to conflict with.
	//   • `owner` — it is nobody's parameter, so a write through it mutates none.
	// The FIELD-CHAIN door is the one caller that can still name something and passes its own two
	// (`parseFieldChainCall`): the chain's base IS a name, and `for it in b.items` locks exactly it.
	//
	// The anchor is the `.` — a temporary has no token of its own, and the dot is where the member access
	// the diagnostic is about begins.
	function valueReceiver(value ValueId, immutableName ByteArray, subjectName ByteArray) returns MethodReceiver
		return MethodReceiver.create(value, tag: self.tagOf(value), nameId: self.valueNameId(value), anchor: self.current(), immutableName: immutableName, subjectName: subjectName, owner: NoParamPositions, selfFieldName: EmptySourceText)
	end 'valueReceiver'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE TAG SWITCH A `.member` ROUTES THROUGH — cursor ON the `.`.** Both spellings of a
	// receiver reach it: a BINDING (`dispatchMethodOnBinding`) and a VALUE (`parsePostfix`,
	// `parseFieldChainCall`). Before this existed the two were separate chains — eight arms keyed on a
	// `VarInfo` and four keyed on a value — and the difference between them was not a design, it was
	// whichever types someone had needed from a value so far: `[1,2].get(0)` worked and `"ab".byteLength()`
	// was `E2015: Unsupported: . statement`, `a.get().compare(b)` worked and `Inner.make(9).get()` did not.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARM ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING IN ONE PLACE**: `String` is a builtin conformer too, so its arm must
	// precede the conformer arm or `s.byteLength()` would be resolved against `String`'s intrinsic
	// conformances instead of the String method table.
	function dispatchMethodOnReceiver(func IrFunction, recv MethodReceiver, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		self.noteMethodCallMutabilityUses(recv)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		self.noteMemberDispatch(recv)

		// ⭐⭐⭐ **A MEMBER THE CORPUS DECLARES AND THE ROSTER DOES NOT GOES TO THE CORPUS (W17).**
		// Asked AHEAD of the builtin dispatchers, and gated so it can only ever catch what they would
		// REFUSE: `memberBelongsToTheCorpus` is consulted only for a name the roster does not carry, so
		// every arm below keeps every member it already served and the order rule in this header is untouched.
		// What changes is the fall-through — `Character.toString()` was *"shv2 provides
		// bytes/byteLength/asciiValue; that list IS the surface"* (76 failures the moment the module was
		// listed) and is now an ordinary call to an ordinary declared function.
		//
		// ⇒ **THIS IS HOW THE SYNTHESIZED SURFACE RETIRES, ONE MEMBER AT A TIME AND MEASURABLY**: strike a
		// name from `stringSurfaceMemberNames`, and the corpus body behind it takes over here with no other
		// edit. See `memberBelongsToTheCorpus`, which serves the `StringIndex` surface as well as the two
		// byte records.
		if self.memberBelongsToTheCorpus(recv) 'corpusMethod'
			return try self.dispatchMethodOnBase(func, base: try self.structBaseOfReceiver(recv), baseTok: recv.anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'corpusMethod'

		// ⭐⭐ **`p.cancel()` — CANCEL A GREEN THREAD (P1.5-B2c).** A promise has exactly one member, so this is
		// gated on the NAME as well as on the receiver: every other spelling keeps whatever the arms below
		// already gave it, which matters because an UNBOXED promise's tag is `integer` (`p.toString()` is a
		// perfectly good `int` conformance call) and a STORED one's is `genericInstance`.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED HERE, AHEAD OF EVERY TAG ARM, BECAUSE A PROMISE WEARS TWO TAGS AND NEITHER IS ITS
		// OWN.** Placed after them, the unboxed spelling would reach the builtin-conformer roster (which is
		// the *"'int' has no method named 'cancel'"* this closes) and the stored one would reach the struct
		// fallthrough looking for a `Promise.cancel` no file declares.
		if self.methodCallCancelsAPromise(recv) 'promiseCancel'
			return try self.emitPromiseCancel(recv, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'promiseCancel'

		// A STRING RECEIVER takes the builtin String methods rather than the struct path, which would reject
		// it as not a struct. String is the one non-struct type carrying a method table of its own.
		if recv.tag == ValueTypeTag.string 'stringMethod'
			return try self.dispatchStringMethod(func, recv: recv)
		end 'stringMethod'

		// A CHARACTER RECEIVER (P1.8 slice B) — its own dispatcher, for the reason it is its own type.
		if recv.tag == ValueTypeTag.character 'characterMethod'
			return try self.dispatchCharacterMethod(recv)
		end 'characterMethod'

		// An ARRAY RECEIVER (`arr.push(x)`, `[10,20].get(0)`) goes to the managed-memory runtime rather than through
		// the struct path (which would reject a generic instance as not a plain struct). The split is on the
		// `genericInstance` tag being an `Array` instance — the parser's type oracle.
		if self.signatures.isArrayInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'arrayMethod'
			// `viaManagedField: false` — a receiver that arrived here was named or produced, not reached
			// through `.managed`; whether it nonetheless denotes a BUFFER is a question about the VALUE
			// (`valueIsBufferSurface`), which the dispatcher asks for itself.
			return try self.dispatchArrayMethod(func, arr: recv.value, giid: recv.nameId, resultUsed: resultUsed, immutableReceiverName: recv.immutableName, borrowSubjectName: recv.subjectName, receiverOwner: recv.owner, receiverStorage: self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: recv.subjectName), viaManagedField: false)
		end 'arrayMethod'

		// ⚠ **A SET ARM AND A MAP ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THEIR RUNTIME (W105).** Each routed a receiver
		// whose base was the SYNTHESIZED record into a hand-written dispatcher over `__set_*` / `__map_*`. Both
		// containers are declared generics now, so `s.insert(e)` and `m.upsert(k, value: v)` fall through to
		// the ordinary declared-method path — the same one every other `stdlib/` type's members take.

		// ⚠ **A LIST ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH ITS RUNTIME SURFACE (W153)**, exactly as the `Set` and `Map`
		// arms above did at W105. `stdlib/List.maxon` declares the type, so `l.append(x)`, `try l.first()` and
		// `l.count()` fall through to the ordinary declared-method path.

		// A `__ManagedList` RECEIVER (`ml.insertFirst(x)`, `ml.remove(node)`, `ml.clear()`) is the SAME chain
		// under the surface that hands out node handles. ⭐ **IT CARRIES THE STORAGE MASK, BUT NOT FOR E3070's
		// SAKE ANY MORE.** W-BORROW threaded one here because an insertion's NODE was *"a reference into the
		// list"* that a `remove`/`clear` could free — a measured 0xC0000005 across a call. ⚖ `W138` withdrew
		// that premise: a handle is a SECOND OWNER of a refcounted node, so nothing on this surface can dangle
		// one and `dispatchManagedListMethod` records no borrow-subject write at all. The mask that still
		// arrives is E3019's and W117's, which the two insertions genuinely owe.
		if self.signatures.isManagedListInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'managedListMethod'
			return try self.dispatchManagedListMethod(func, list: recv.value, resultUsed: resultUsed, immutableReceiverName: recv.immutableName, borrowSubjectName: recv.subjectName, receiverOwner: recv.owner, receiverStorage: self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: recv.subjectName))
		end 'managedListMethod'

		// A NODE-HANDLE RECEIVER (`node.value()`, `node.next()`) — its own dispatcher because a node is not a
		// container: what it answers is the element it holds and the nodes beside it.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE IMMUTABLE NAME AND THE STORAGE MASK SINCE `BATCH37`, BUT NOT A BORROW SUBJECT.**
		// `setValue` WRITES through the handle, so E3019 and W117 apply to it exactly as they do to a chain's
		// insertions; E3070 still does not, because a node handle is an OWNER (`W138`) and nothing on either
		// surface can dangle one.
		if self.signatures.isManagedListNodeInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'managedListNodeMethod'
			return try self.dispatchManagedListNodeMethod(func, node: recv.value, giid: recv.nameId, resultUsed: resultUsed, immutableReceiverName: recv.immutableName, receiverOwner: recv.owner, receiverStorage: self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: recv.subjectName))
		end 'managedListNodeMethod'

		// A CURSOR RECEIVER (`c.current()`, `c.advance()`) — its own dispatcher for the node arm's reason: a
		// cursor is not a container either. It carries the borrow subject, which the node arm does not,
		// because `current()`/`peek()` hand back a BORROW of an element the SOURCE owns and E3070 is what
		// keeps that borrow from outliving a write to it.
		if self.signatures.isManagedMemoryCursorInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'cursorMethod'
			return try self.dispatchCursorMethod(func, cursor: recv.value, giid: recv.nameId, resultUsed: resultUsed, borrowSubjectName: recv.subjectName)
		end 'cursorMethod'

		// ⚠ **A VECTOR ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH ITS DISPATCHER (W190)**, exactly as the `Set`/`Map` arms
		// did at W105 and the `List` arm at W153. `stdlib/Vector.maxon` declares `create`, `count`, `get` and
		// `set`, so `v.get(0)` and `v.set(0, value: 42)` are taken by `memberBelongsToTheCorpus` at the top of
		// this switch and reach the ordinary declared-method path. **A vector's WRITE still has to reach a
		// caller's borrow**, which was this arm's storage mask and is `noteStorageReachingCall`'s edge now —
		// see the gate there, whose own header demanded this widening in advance.

		// A `StringIndex` RECEIVER (`idx.charIndex()`, `idx.bytePos()`, P1.8 slice B2) takes its own two
		// accessors rather than the struct path, under the SAME roster-wins rule that keeps `s.byteLength()`
		// a field load: the corpus declares both accessors and the struct path would resolve them, but each
		// would then be a real CALL on the byte-position read every `slice` and every corpus grapheme walk
		// makes. Two field loads instead — see `dispatchStringIndexMethod`.
		if self.isStringIndexType(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'stringIndexMethod'
			return try self.dispatchStringIndexMethod(recv)
		end 'stringIndexMethod'

		// ⭐ **`ws.contains(c)` HAD AN ARM HERE UNTIL W115.** It is deliberately NOT the `StringIndex` arm's
		// case above: that one keeps a declared accessor off the struct path because the call it would emit
		// sits on a hot byte-position read. `CharacterSet.contains` has no such argument — it is a real call
		// either way — so with the corpus declaring it there is nothing left for an arm here to buy.
		// A CONSTRAINED TYPE-PARAMETER RECEIVER (P1.7a slice 2a) — `self.item.digest()` where `item` is a `T`
		// and `T` is constrained `where T is Digest`. The concrete type is unknown in the shared body, so it
		// dispatches through the runtime WITNESS, not a direct call.
		//
		// ⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL RECEIVER TAKES THE SAME ARM (P1.7a-existentials slice 1)** — `b.run()` on a
		// `b Backend`. Its concrete type is unknown for the OTHER reason (it is chosen at run time, not
		// erased by a shared body), and the answer is the same runtime witness; what differs is only WHERE
		// the table comes from, and `dispatchWitnessMethod` reads that off the receiver's tag.
		//
		// ⚠ It must precede the builtin-conformer arm below for the reason the `String` arm precedes it: a
		// `Stringable`/`Hashable` value is held at a SYNTHESIZED protocol interface, and the conformer
		// classifier would otherwise claim the receiver and dispatch a concrete `int.hash` on a fat pointer.
		if recv.tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter or recv.tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'witnessMethod'
			return try self.dispatchWitnessMethod(func, receiver: recv.value, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'witnessMethod'

		// A BUILTIN-CONFORMER RECEIVER (`i.compare(j)`, `i.hash()`) is dispatched DIRECTLY to the synthesized
		// conformance impl rather than through the struct path, which would reject an `int` as not a struct.
		// The split is on the receiver's TYPE reduced to its conformer (`builtinConformerOfType` — the
		// parser's type oracle again). A receiver that is NOT a builtin conformer — a struct, a generic
		// instance, or a type with no conformance at all — falls through to the struct path below, which
		// resolves the first two and reports the third.
		let receiverConformer = self.builtinConformerOfType(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId)
		if isBuiltinConformer(receiverConformer) 'builtinConformer'
			return try self.dispatchBuiltinConformanceMethod(func, recv: recv, conformer: receiverConformer, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'builtinConformer'

		// A `__ManagedFile` RECEIVER (R4.1) — `f.close()`. Its own dispatcher for the reason the
		// `StringIndex` arm above has one: the layout IS registered, so the struct path would accept the box
		// and then resolve `close` against `funcSignatures`, where no file declares `__ManagedFile.close`.
		if self.isManagedFileType(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'managedFileMethod'
			return try self.dispatchManagedFileMethod(func, recv: recv)
		end 'managedFileMethod'

		// A `__ManagedDirectory` RECEIVER (R4.3) — `dir.next()`, and (the corpus's own spelling)
		// `wrapper.dir.next()`. Its own dispatcher for the reason the arm above has one, and it accepts the
		// same two tags for the same measured reason: a struct FIELD read carries `named`, not `structRef`,
		// and `specs/managed-directory.md`'s documented usage pattern reaches every instance method through a
		// field.
		if self.isManagedDirectoryType(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'managedDirectoryMethod'
			return try self.dispatchManagedDirectoryMethod(recv)
		end 'managedDirectoryMethod'

		// A `__ManagedSocket` RECEIVER — `s.close()`, and (the corpus's own spelling, `stdlib/TcpClient.maxon:43`)
		// `self.socket.sendFrom(…)`. Its own dispatcher for the reason the two arms above have one, and it
		// accepts the same two tags for the same measured reason: a struct FIELD read carries `named`, not
		// `structRef`, and a `__ManagedSocket` is documented as a FIELD of a wrapper type.
		if self.isManagedSocketType(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'managedSocketMethod'
			return try self.dispatchManagedSocketMethod(func, recv: recv)
		end 'managedSocketMethod'

		// AN ENUM/UNION RECEIVER (D1) — `d.isNorth()`, `s.action()`, `self.isStop()`, and now also
		// `pick().score()` and `Direction.south.score()`, which the oracle accepts (measured: 10) and which
		// arrive here as VALUES because D1's arm was reached only from the binding door.
		//
		// Gated on the `named` TAG first, which allocates nothing and is false for every struct, String,
		// Character, Array and Set receiver — so the interner read and the registry probe behind it are paid
		// only by a receiver that is an enum or a ranged alias, and a ranged alias reaching here was already
		// on its way to the struct fallthrough's refusal.
		if recv.tag == ValueTypeTag.named and self.signatures.containsEnum(self.internedName(recv.nameId)) 'enumMethod'
			return try self.dispatchEnumMethod(func, recv: recv, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'enumMethod'

		return try self.dispatchMethodOnBase(func, base: try self.structBaseOfReceiver(recv), baseTok: recv.anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
	end 'dispatchMethodOnReceiver'

	// Is the member about to be dispatched `cancel` on a promise? The cursor sits on the method NAME (the
	// `.` is consumed), so this peeks rather than consuming — `emitPromiseCancel` takes the token.
	function methodCallCancelsAPromise(recv MethodReceiver) returns bool
		return self.current().value.equals(PromiseCancelMethodName) and self.valueIsPromise(recv.value)
	end 'methodCallCancelsAPromise'

	// ⭐⭐ **`p.cancel()` — RECLAIM AND CANCEL THE GREEN THREAD, THROUGH THE DROP ENTRY THAT ALREADY DOES BOTH.**
	// `__gt_promise_drop` is not a reclaim with a cancel bolted on: its header calls it *"RECLAIM + CANCEL AN
	// UN-AWAITED GREEN THREAD"*, and since A2 it also runs `__gt_release_<callee>` as `ownedByThisDrop`
	// so the thread's managed arguments and its unread result-or-error are released. `cancel` wants exactly
	// that and wants it exactly once, so it CALLS that entry rather than growing a second one — which is what
	// `Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon`'s header already anticipated: *"Cancel routes through `promise_drop`, so
	// it inherits this rather than growing a third copy of the decision."*
	//
	// ⭐ **AND `consumePromiseThread` IS WHAT MAKES IT RUN ONCE.** It is the same door `await` uses: the
	// promise leaves this frame's `pendingTempDrops` and its owning binding is marked moved, so the
	// scope-exit gate (`not binding.movedFrom and bindingIsOwned(binding)`) emits NO second
	// `__gt_promise_drop` — which would be a double reclaim of a struct already back on the free list, and a
	// second releaser run over slots the first one freed. The runtime's `gt.releaser = 0` store is the
	// backstop behind that, in that order and for that reason.
	function emitPromiseCancel(recv MethodReceiver, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
		try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
		self.consumePromiseThread(recv.value)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(GtPromiseDropName, args: singletonArgs(recv.value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: SourceRange.between(recv.anchor, last: methodTok))
	end 'emitPromiseCancel'

	// The `StructBase` a receiver that reached the struct fallthrough denotes — the value form of
	// `requireStructBase`, sharing its layout resolution and differing only in that a VALUE has no binding
	// to prove live and no self-field alias to materialize (its producer already did both).
	//
	// A tag with no methods at all lands here, and this is where it is reported: a `float`, a `void` call
	// result, an enum value, a function value. The message names the member rather than the type's fields,
	// because reaching this point means a `.` followed a value the language gives no member access to.
	function structBaseOfReceiver(recv MethodReceiver) returns StructBase throws ParseError
		if not self.tagCarriesStructLayoutHere(recv.tag) 'notAStruct'
			let memberTok = self.current()

			// ⚠ The third of `requireDeclaredBaseTypeName`'s doors — a receiver with no binding to name, whose
			// refusal called it a `'int'` value: the same invented type in a third wording, which is why the
			// three doors now share the ONE verdict and none of them words this fact for itself.
			try self.requireDeclaredBaseTypeName(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId, tok: memberTok)

			// ⭐⭐ **THE SENTENCE NAMES THE GATE'S THREE ARMS, NOT A ROSTER OF TYPES — AND THAT IS THE CURE
			// FOR HOW IT WENT STALE (W105 review).** It used to end *"the builtin types (`String`,
			// `Character`, `Array`, `Set`, `StringIndex`, `CharacterSet`)"*, and `Set` stopped being a
			// builtin the day W105 deleted the synthesized hash table — a hand-spelled roster one file away
			// from the code that decides, which is this project's signature bug in a USER-FACING string.
			//
			// ⚠ **DERIVING IT FROM `isBuiltinGenericBaseName` WAS CONSIDERED AND IS THE WRONG ROSTER**, not
			// merely a costly one. That predicate answers `Array`, `List`, `Vector`, `__ManagedList`,
			// `__ManagedListNode`, `__ManagedMemoryCursor` — three reserved spellings no user program may
			// write, and it names none of `String`/`Character`/`StringIndex`/`CharacterSet`. Generating from
			// it would have replaced a stale list with a WRONG one.
			//
			// ⇒ The gate is `tagCarriesStructLayoutHere` on the line above, and it admits exactly three
			// things: a `structRef`, a `genericInstance`, and a BYTE RECORD whose corpus declaration is
			// loaded (`tagIsByteRecord` — `String` and `Character`, and only those two). Every name the old
			// roster spelled is already one of the first two: an `Array`/`Set`/`Map` value is a
			// `genericInstance`, a `StringIndex` and a `CharacterSet` are `structRef`s. A sentence that
			// enumerates nothing cannot go stale when a container is retired or added.
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a member access '{String.from(memberTok.value)}' on a '{typeTagName(recv.tag)}' value — only a struct, a generic instance and the byte records (`String`, `Character`) carry members here", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'notAStruct'

		// ⚠ **AN AGGREGATE-TYPED SELF-FIELD ALIAS IS REFUSED HERE ONLY WHEN NO VALUE OF ITS TYPE COULD EXIST**
		// — `specs-shv2/self-field-struct-typed.md` pins the refusal and its own sabotage note says the method
		// case does not merely go red without it, it goes GREEN WITH THE WRONG CODE. It sits at the FALLTHROUGH
		// and not in front of the switch because every builtin arm above serves such an alias correctly and by
		// design (`items.push(v)`, `name.append("!")` — `baseBoxOf`'s header measures both).
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE PREDICATE IS CONSTRUCTIBILITY, AND THAT IS WHAT THE ORIGINAL REFUSAL WAS ARGUING FOR ALL
		// ALONG.** Its case was never "a struct-typed field is unlowerable" — the box IS loaded correctly, by
		// `baseBoxOf`, before this receiver is built — but the `mintPhi` trap: `type Node`'s
		// `var next as Node` needs a `Node` to put there and there is no `null` and no base case, so NO PROGRAM
		// COULD OBSERVE the load being right. That argument holds for a type on a cycle in the struct-field
		// graph and for nothing else. `type Outer`'s `var inner as Inner` is built by
		// `Outer.create(Inner.create(42))`, `inner.get()` is the program `specs-shv2/method-calls.md` writes,
		// and the explicit `self.inner.get()` spelling of the very same call already compiled and answered 42
		// through this same fallthrough — the two spellings of one access must not disagree.
		// `ProgramSignatures.structTypeIsConstructible` is the one home of that question, cycles and all.
		//
		// ⭐ **`requireStructBase` NOW ASKS THE SAME NARROWED QUESTION (W66)**, through the same function. It
		// used to refuse EVERY such alias, constructible or not, because its box was `binding.boundValue` — 0,
		// the RECEIVER, the measured silent `return self.a` its own header records — and it now materializes
		// the field through `baseBoxOf` exactly as this door's producer does. One predicate, one sentence.
		//
		// ⚠ The gate is NESTED INSIDE the alias arm, not hoisted above it. `structLayoutOfType` below resolves
		// the same name for itself, but this door is on the path of EVERY method dispatch in the program while
		// a self-field alias base is rare — so lifting the resolution out to share it would pay it on every
		// call to save one dense-column index on almost none. It stays where only the alias path reaches it,
		// exactly as the enum arm above gates on its TAG before touching the interner.
		if not recv.selfFieldName.isEmpty() 'selfFieldAlias'
			try self.requireConstructibleSelfFieldBase(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId, fieldName: recv.selfFieldName, anchor: recv.anchor)
		end 'selfFieldAlias'

		return StructBase.create(try self.structLayoutOfType(BaseAccessKind.member, tag: recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId, baseTok: recv.anchor), box: recv.value, immutableName: recv.immutableName, subjectName: recv.subjectName)
	end 'structBaseOfReceiver'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY A SELF-FIELD ALIAS OF THIS AGGREGATE TYPE BE A BASE AT ALL? — ONE PREDICATE FOR THE TWO
	// DOORS THAT CAN HOLD ONE**, the METHOD receiver (`structBaseOfReceiver`) and the field-chain BASE
	// (`requireStructBase`). Both materialize the box before they get here; what is left to decide is whether
	// a value of the field's type could exist at all, and that is `ProgramSignatures.structTypeIsConstructible`'s
	// one home — a walk over the struct-field cycle, not a name comparison.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PREDICATE AND THE SENTENCE TRAVEL TOGETHER, AND THAT IS THE POINT.**
	// `specs-shv2/self-field-struct-typed.md` pins three cases against the identical text at two doors; while
	// the two doors asked the same question in two places, one of them could be narrowed and the other left
	// behind — which is exactly what happened, and W66 is the rung that paid for it. Asking through here is
	// what makes a future narrowing arrive at both doors or at neither.
	function requireConstructibleSelfFieldBase(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, fieldName ByteArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.structTypeIsConstructible(self.baseStructNameOfType(tag, nameId: nameId, baseTok: anchor)) 'inconstructible'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(structTypedSelfFieldBaseMessage(fieldName), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'inconstructible'
	end 'requireConstructibleSelfFieldBase'

	// ⭐ **DISPATCH A METHOD ON AN ENUM/UNION RECEIVER (D1).** The cursor sits on the member NAME. The
	// callee is mangled from the receiver's declared ENUM NAME and the member name — the very key
	// `parseFunction` declared the method under (`declaredMethodName`) — and the receiver is prepended as
	// argument 0 by the shared `parseCallNamed`. Nothing below the parser learns that enums have methods.
	//
	// ⚠ **THERE ARE TWO ARMS SINCE enum-hashable, and the mangled callee is only the FIRST.** A member the
	// enum does not declare may still be one of the `Hashable`/`Equatable` requirements the compiler GRANTS
	// every payload-free enum, which is a conformance dispatch and not a method call at all — see the arm
	// below. Both arms are exhaustive over one lookup, so a member reaches exactly one of them.
	//
	// ⭐ **D1 GAVE IT A BINDING RECEIVER ONLY; ROUTING IT THROUGH THE ONE SWITCH GIVES IT A VALUE ONE FOR
	// FREE (D2).** `pick().score()` and `Direction.south.score()` are programs the oracle runs (measured:
	// 10 for their sum) and shv2 answered `E2015: Unsupported: . statement` for both, because the arm was
	// reachable only from `dispatchMethodOnBinding`. It needed nothing added — an enum value carries its
	// name in the same column a binding's declared type does.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER IS BORROWED, exactly as a struct's is** (`parseMethodCall`'s header argues it in
	// full). A payload-free enum's receiver is a bare i64 tag with no ownership at all; a BOXED union's is
	// a pointer the caller's binding owns, so no incref is emitted HERE and no drop there. The callee cannot
	// leak it either — and since S5 that is because `return self` from a boxed union takes the SAME
	// hand-off PROMOTION a `returns Container` on a `c Container` parameter does, minting its own `__mm_retain`
	// inside the callee (it used to be because both hit the same borrowed-return REFUSAL). Either way `self`
	// IS parameter 0, so it is not a special case; it is the ordinary parameter rule reaching it, and the
	// call site's own accounting is unchanged because the callee pays for whatever it hands back.
	//
	// The three facts it shares with the struct path — liveness, the E3019 blame name and the E3070 borrow
	// subject — are asked through the same three functions, so an enum receiver and a struct receiver
	// cannot come to disagree about a use-after-move, an immutable receiver or a borrow conflict.
	function dispatchEnumMethod(func IrFunction, recv MethodReceiver, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The enum's name comes from the receiver's TYPE, never from the anchor token: the anchor is a
		// variable's name (`d`), a `.` for a temporary, and `self` is not even an identifier.
		let memberTok = self.current()
		let enumName = self.internedName(recv.nameId)
		let callee = qualifiedMethodName(enumName, methodName: memberTok.value)

		// ⭐⭐ **`d.hash()` AND `a.equals(b)` ON AN ENUM ARE THE GRANTED CONFORMANCE, NOT A MISSING METHOD
		// (enum-hashable).** Both were `E3004: call to undefined function 'Direction.hash'`, because the
		// conformance every enum has by declaration alone had no door on the value side. This is that door,
		// and it resolves the member against the SAME intrinsic-conformance row the E3017 answer and the
		// witness slot read (`isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`'s `payloadFreeEnum` arm) — so the set of members
		// it serves cannot come to differ from the set of protocols the enum is held to conform to.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED ONLY WHERE THE ENUM DECLARES NO METHOD OF THAT NAME**, which is the precedence v1
		// states — *"a user method wins, matching 'user override beats synthesis'"* — and here it costs
		// nothing to honour: the declared method is the ordinary D1 call below, and
		// `enumConformanceImplName` answers with that same key, so the witness slot follows the door rather
		// than contradicting it.
		match self.enumGrantedConformanceMethod(enumName, memberName: memberTok.value) 'granted'
			supplied(method) then return try self.dispatchEnumConformanceMethod(func, recv: recv, enumName: enumName, method: method, memberTok: memberTok)
			notSupplied then return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: callee, anchor: memberTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.value(recv.value, immutableName: recv.immutableName, subjectName: recv.subjectName, anchor: recv.anchor), resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier)
		end 'granted'
	end 'dispatchEnumMethod'

	// Which member of `enumName` this call reaches through the GRANTED conformance rather than through a
	// declared method — `notSupplied` for every member an enum body declares itself, and for every name the
	// granted row does not carry (a `union`'s, since the row is withheld from one, and anything that is not
	// `hash`/`equals`). Its two answers are the two arms of the dispatch above, so a member cannot be claimed
	// here and then looked for as a declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DECLARED-METHOD PROBE COMES FIRST AND IT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS OFF THE HOT PATH.** The row walk
	// below builds a protocol-name array, and EVERY enum method call in every program arrives here — so
	// `d.isNorth()` must not pay for it. A declared member is one map lookup and returns; the walk is reached
	// only by a member no declaration answers, which is a call already bound for the granted conformance or
	// for a diagnostic.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE PROBE IS `ProgramSignatures.enumDeclaresOwnConformanceMethod` AND MAY NOT BE INLINED BACK TO
	// A `declaresCallee` OF ITS OWN.** Three doors turn on this one question — this dispatch, the witness
	// slot's impl name, and E3130's check — and they were two copies of the same join until the review
	// caught it: refine the rule at one and `e.hash()` calls one body while the map stamps another.
	function enumGrantedConformanceMethod(enumName ByteArray, memberName ByteArray) returns BuiltinConformerMethodLookup
		if self.signatures.enumDeclaresOwnConformanceMethod(enumName, methodName: memberName) 'theEnumDeclaresIt'
			return BuiltinConformerMethodLookup.notSupplied
		end 'theEnumDeclaresIt'

		return builtinConformerMethod(enumName, methodName: memberName, discovered: self.signatures.discoveredConformerOf(enumName))
	end 'enumGrantedConformanceMethod'

	// ⭐ **EMIT ONE GRANTED-CONFORMANCE CALL ON AN ENUM RECEIVER** — the cursor sits on the member NAME, which
	// the shared `emitBuiltinConformanceCall` consumes along with the argument list, so the arity check, the
	// `Self`-argument rule and the result typing are the ones every direct conformance dispatch already uses.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER AND ITS `Self` ARGUMENT TRAVEL AS THEIR ENUM-TYPED VALUES AND ARE NOT CONVERTED, WHICH
	// IS THE `int` IMPL'S ABI ALREADY.** An enum value IS its i64 tag in a GPR and resolves to
	// `MaxonType.integer` below the parser, so `int.hash`/`int.equals` receive exactly the i64 they expect —
	// the same handoff `emitEnumRawValue` makes when it passes a tag straight to `__char_from_cp`. Emitting a
	// `+ 0` retag (`enumTagAsANumber`) would buy nothing here: that exists to give an ORDINAL a type of its
	// own, and no result of this call carries the enum's type.
	function dispatchEnumConformanceMethod(func IrFunction, recv MethodReceiver, enumName ByteArray, method InterfaceMethodSig, memberTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		return try self.emitBuiltinConformanceCall(func, receiver: recv.value, target: DirectConformanceTarget.enumTagValue(enumName), method: method, baseTok: recv.anchor, methodTok: memberTok)
	end 'dispatchEnumConformanceMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BOX A BASE BINDING DENOTES — and a SELF-FIELD ALIAS is the one that has to be MATERIALIZED
	// first.** Every other binding already holds its value; a self-field alias holds NONE.
	// `VarInfo.createSelfField` leaves `boundValue` 0 because a read of the alias is a `loadIndirect`
	// through the receiver — **and ValueId 0 IS the receiver** — so a door that took `boundValue` on its own
	// addressed the enclosing struct's BOX as if it were the field.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH HALVES OF THAT WERE MEASURED WRONG, and one of them silently.** With `var items as IntArray`
	// inside `type Bag`:
	//   • `items.count()` inside a method compiled clean and answered **0** for an array holding one
	//     element — `__managed_count` was handed the Bag's box and read its second word. A wrong answer with no
	//     diagnostic anywhere, and the runnable oracle answers 1.
	//   • `items.push(v)` was **E3019** — a false rejection of an ordinary struct-with-a-container method,
	//     because the alias is neither `mutable` nor `isParameter`, so `immutableReceiverNameOf` blamed it.
	//     The oracle accepts it. That half is `baseBindingOf`'s, below; this one is the load.
	//
	// `builtinConformanceReceiverValue` used to be this rule's third copy, for the conformer arm alone
	// (`count.hash()` hashing the receiver's box pointer). It is gone: one load, every door.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE FIELD-CHAIN BASE IS THE DOOR THAT USED TO REFUSE INSTEAD OF ASKING (W66).**
	// `requireStructBase` had no materialization at all, so it refused EVERY struct-typed self-field alias —
	// `inner.x` and `inner.x = 7` were E2015 one function away from an `inner.get()` that compiled and ran
	// (measured: exit 41), and the `self.inner.x = 7` spelling of that same write answered 7. Three spellings
	// of one access, two of them served and one refused, is the asymmetry this being a shared function ends.
	function baseBoxOf(binding VarInfo, baseTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if binding.isSelfField 'materializeTheField'
			return try self.parseSelfFieldRead(baseTok, binding: binding)
		end 'materializeTheField'

		return self.bindingValue(binding, range: SourceRange.ofToken(baseTok))
	end 'baseBoxOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BINDING A BASE'S WRITABILITY IS ASKED OF — the alias MATERIALIZED, anything else itself.**
	// A self field's writability is the FIELD's own `var`/`let` (`selfFieldIsWritable`), not the alias
	// binding's: the alias is built `mutable: false` because a REBIND of it is never legal, and the receiver
	// it hangs off is a parameter and so is never `mutable` either. Both of the doors that ask — E3019's
	// receiver door (`immutableReceiverNameOf`) and E2013's instance door (`requireWritableInstance`), which
	// that second function's header calls *"one write of one record under two spellings"* — read
	// `mutable`/`isParameter` off a binding, so the materialized one is what carries them the answer.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE BOX RATHER THAN LOADING ONE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS SPLIT FROM `baseBoxOf`.** The
	// field-chain door asks the two questions at two different moments — `requireStructBase` resolves the box
	// as part of proving the base is a struct at all, and the WRITE check happens later, after the chain has
	// been walked and the field resolved (`parseFieldAssignment`'s rank order). Re-deriving the binding from
	// the box it already holds emits nothing; loading again would emit a second `loadIndirect` nobody reads.
	function baseBindingOf(binding VarInfo, baseTok Token, box ValueId) returns VarInfo throws ParseError
		if not binding.isSelfField 'holdsItsOwnValue'
			return binding
		end 'holdsItsOwnValue'

		return try self.selfFieldValueBinding(binding, baseTok: baseTok, value: box)
	end 'baseBindingOf'

	// ⭐ **THE MATERIALIZED FORM OF A SELF-FIELD ALIAS, GIVEN THE VALUE THAT STANDS FOR IT.** Two doors reach
	// it and they differ ONLY in where that value comes from: the ordinary one loads the field
	// (`baseBoxOf`), the field-chain base re-derives it from the box that door already loaded
	// (`baseBindingOf`), and the fused-wrapper record door hands over the RECEIVER
	// (`dispatchMethodOnBinding`'s `inlineManagedServesTheRecord`, which mints nothing).
	//
	// ⚠ It is a function rather than two constructions because everything ELSE about the binding — the blame
	// NAME, the declared TYPE, and the writability rule that must ask the FIELD's `var`/`let` rather than the
	// alias's (`selfFieldIsWritable`, whose header records the false E3019 the other question produced) — is
	// the same in both, and a second construction is where those three come to disagree.
	function selfFieldValueBinding(binding VarInfo, baseTok Token, value ValueId) returns VarInfo throws ParseError
		return VarInfo.createValueBinding(baseTok.value, varType: binding.varType, mutable: try self.selfFieldIsWritable(binding, nameTok: baseTok), boundValue: value)
	end 'selfFieldValueBinding'

	// ⭐ **MAY A SELF-FIELD ALIAS BE WRITTEN THROUGH? — THE FIELD'S OWN `var`/`let`, NEVER THE BINDING'S.**
	// `createSelfField` binds every alias `mutable: false` and says why (a rebind of the alias is never legal),
	// so asking the BINDING calls a `var` field immutable — the false E3019 `baseBoxOf`'s header
	// measures. `layout.fieldIsMutable` is the SAME column `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` asks of `n = 1`.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH ASKERS COME THROUGH HERE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A FUNCTION.** A method RECEIVER (`items.push(v)`)
	// and a bare-name ARGUMENT (`grow(items)`, whose blame name `parseVariableReference` publishes) are the same
	// question about the same field. They were derived twice — once as `mutable:`, once as `not …` — and an
	// INVERTED restatement is the shape that lets a `var` field become one a method may push onto but no callee
	// may be handed, with nothing in either site to say the other exists.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND AN ITERATED FIELD IS NOT WRITABLE, WHATEVER THE FIELD DECLARES.** This is one of the five
	// readers of the iteration lock, and it needs its own arm rather than sharing
	// `immutableReceiverNameOf`'s, because `baseBindingOf` MINTS A FRESH `VarInfo` for the alias:
	// the lock is keyed on object identity (so a shadowing declaration is correctly not locked), and that
	// fresh object is not the one the lock holds. Asking HERE — where the alias's own binding is still in
	// hand — covers both of this function's askers at once, which is the reason it is a function.
	function selfFieldIsWritable(binding VarInfo, nameTok Token) returns bool throws ParseError
		if self.bindingIsIterationLocked(binding) 'iterated'
			return false
		end 'iterated'
		return (try self.enclosingLayout(nameTok)).fieldIsMutable(binding.fieldIndex)
	end 'selfFieldIsWritable'

	// ⭐ **COULD THE `<base> . <member> (` AT `index` BE A METHOD CALL ON A TOP-LEVEL MANAGED BINDING?** — a
	// `let` (P1.7a) or a `var` (P1.7 slice 2a). The ONE gate in front of both askers — `methodCallsAt` (is
	// this token shape a method call at all?) and `parseMethodCall` (build the receiver) — for
	// `methodCallsAt`'s own stated reason: a receiver test written twice is free to drift, and the drift
	// is silent.
	//
	// **COULD**, not IS: it answers over the whole program's managed NAMES and the competing type-based
	// readings, and deliberately not over what this FILE can see. Whether the name resolves to a binding
	// visible here is `managedReceiverBindingOf`'s answer, and both askers go on to ask it — so a `true`
	// here is permission to resolve, never a claim on the shape.
	//
	// ⭐ **BOTH KINDS ANSWER HERE, THROUGH ONE FILTER AND ONE `staticCallClaimsBase`.** They are the same
	// question — "does this base name a managed record rather than a type?" — and asking it twice would
	// pay the type-reading probe twice for every managed base and, worse, allow a future rule taught to
	// one door and not the other. Which KIND it is matters only to `parseMethodCall`, where it decides
	// `mutable`, and that comes out of the ONE `managedReceiverBindingOf` below.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT RETURNS A BOOL, AND THAT IS A COST DECISION WITH A MEASUREMENT BEHIND IT.** The obvious
	// tidier shape is to fold this into `managedReceiverBindingOf` and hand every caller the binding — but
	// a Maxon union VALUE IS A HEAP OBJECT, including the payload-free `none`, so a union-returning gate
	// allocates once per DOTTED CALL IN THE PROGRAM rather than once per managed-receiver call site.
	// MEASURED, by building exactly that and running the scale ladder: +441/+873/+1,737/+3,465 allocations
	// on rungs 2-5 of a corpus that declares no managed binding at all — doubling with the program,
	// forever. So the gate that decides "may this allocate?" must not itself allocate to say no.
	//
	// It takes the base's INDEX rather than its token because the answer needs the MEMBER name too
	// (`staticCallClaimsBase`), and deriving that from a token would re-assume the cursor is parked on
	// the base — true at both askers today, and exactly the premise that quietly stops holding.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `namesManagedTopLevel` FILTER IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION, IT IS THE COST MODEL.**
	// `methodCallsAt` runs at every dotted-call shape in the program, and the file-scoped probes behind
	// this gate BUILD A STRING KEY (~4 allocations per call — see `fileScopedDeclKey`). In front of it a
	// base that is not spelled like any managed binding in the program — every static call,
	// `Widget.create(…)` and `String.from(…)` alike, which is nearly every base that reaches here — costs
	// ONE hash of bytes it already holds and allocates nothing. `staticCallClaimsBase` sits BEHIND the
	// same filter and inherits the same model: it runs only for a base actually spelled like a managed
	// binding, so its mangled-callee probe is off the hot path entirely.
	//
	// It is a NAME set rather than a whole-program bool for a measured reason: a bool goes permanently
	// true at the first managed binding, and every static dotted call in the program then pays the full
	// probe to be told no. See `ProgramSignatures.managedTopLevelNames`.
	function managedTopLevelBaseAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.namesManagedTopLevel(self.tokenAt(index).value) 'notAManagedName'
			return false
		end 'notAManagedName'

		return not self.staticCallClaimsBase(index)
	end 'managedTopLevelBaseAt'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A MANAGED TOP-LEVEL NAME DENOTES AT A RECEIVER POSITION — the ONE resolution**, asked by
	// both readers past `managedTopLevelBaseAt`: `methodCallsAt` to decide whether the token shape is a
	// method call at all, `parseMethodCall` to build the receiver. Two probes with two arm orders would be
	// two encodings of "which door claims this name", and the failure mode is silent — one door claiming
	// the shape and the other resolving it differently.
	//
	// One storage key holds ONE declaration (`recordDecl` is first-wins on it), so the two doors are
	// disjoint by construction and the order below is a cost preference, not a precedence: the constant is
	// asked first because a managed `let` is the commoner declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ It BOXES a union per call — `none` included, which is why the GATE in front of it is a bool. That
	// is affordable only because of the gate: it runs solely for a base actually spelled like a managed
	// binding, i.e. once per managed-receiver call SITE at parse time, never per dotted call in the program.
	// A program with no managed binding — the whole scale corpus — never reaches it. MEASURED: the box is 1
	// allocation, and `parseMethodCall` used to pay it twice more, testing this as a predicate and then
	// re-resolving the identical name to build the receiver. A 16,384-site same-lineage ladder read that
	// recompute as exactly 12.000 allocations per managed-`var` site and 6.000 per managed-`let` site; it
	// showed up in the code as an impossible-state `none` panic whose whole justification was "the two read
	// the same resolution and cannot disagree". Passing the answer along makes that true by construction.
	function managedReceiverBindingOf(tok Token) returns ManagedReceiverBinding
		match self.signatures.constantValueOf(tok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'constant'
			found then break 'constant'
			foundManaged(bytes, kind) then return ManagedReceiverBinding.constant(bytes, kind: kind)
			notFound then break 'constant'
		end 'constant'

		return match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(tok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
			found(label, valueType, mutable) gives ManagedReceiverBinding.global(label, valueType: valueType, mutable: mutable)
			notFound gives ManagedReceiverBinding.none
		end 'global'
	end 'managedReceiverBindingOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES A TYPE-BASED READING ALREADY CLAIM THIS `<base> . <member> (`?** A managed constant is the
	// WEAKEST claim on that shape, and this is what says so.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE MIRROR OF `parseDottedPrimary`'S ARM ORDER, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE MANAGED-CONSTANT
	// ARM BROKE THAT ORDER'S PREMISE.** `methodCallsAt`'s header states it: the token shape alone cannot
	// tell `c.increment()` from `Point.create()`, and "only the scope knows which". That held while every
	// receiver was a LOCAL or a CAPTURE — a name was a value or a type, never both. A top-level managed
	// constant is the first receiver that can share its spelling with a declared type, so the shape has to
	// be decided against the type-based readings instead of ahead of them. `methodCallsAt` is asked FIRST
	// of the three dispatchers' arm chains, so without this the constant silently outranks all of them.
	//
	// **MEASURED AGAINST THE RUNNABLE ORACLE, one program at a time.** With `let Widget = "abcdefghij"`
	// beside a `type Widget`, the oracle answers 42 for `Widget.byteLength()` when the type declares that
	// static and 10 — the CONSTANT's byte length — when it declares only `create`. So the rule is not
	// "a type name always wins": the type-based reading is TRIED FIRST and the constant is its FALLBACK,
	// which is exactly what asking the three claims below reproduces. Without it shv2 answered 10 in both
	// cases, silently, with no diagnostic and a green suite.
	//
	// Each clause delegates to the authority that arm already uses — none of them re-derives what a type
	// is:
	//
	//   • `containsEnum` — the enum/union arm, which claims a payload CONSTRUCT `Move.walk(5)`; that has
	//     the same token shape a static call does (`parseDottedPrimary` says so where it orders the two).
	//   • `isGenericAlias` — the `IntArray.create()` / `IntSet.create()` statics `parseQualifiedCall`
	//     intercepts AHEAD of its own mangle. They are the one type-based claim the callee probe below
	//     structurally cannot see: `Array`/`Set` have no base struct, so their statics lower to the array
	//     and set runtimes and NO file declares an `Array.create` to be found.
	//   • a DECLARED callee under the mangled static name — every plain struct's `Widget.byteLength`, and
	//     a user generic alias's `IntBox.create` through its base. The name is built by the SAME
	//     expression `parseQualifiedCall` builds it with, `denotedGenericBaseName` included, rather than
	//     re-spelled here: two derivations of one callee name is the drift `voidStaticCallsAt`
	//     documents at length, and its failure is a call accepted at one door and refused at the other.
	function staticCallClaimsBase(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		let written = self.tokenAt(index).value
		// ⚠ **THE SAME KEY `parseQualifiedCall` RESOLVES (W7)**, for the reason the third bullet already gives
		// about the callee name: the two doors must not read one written spelling as two different aliases, or a
		// call is claimed at one and refused at the other. An INNER alias is keyed base-qualified, so asking the
		// bare member here would hand the base to the managed-constant fallback for exactly the aliases
		// `parseQualifiedCall` does resolve.
		let baseName = self.genericAliasKeyFor(written)

		if self.signatures.containsEnum(baseName) or self.signatures.isGenericAlias(baseName) 'enumOrGenericAlias'
			return true
		end 'enumOrGenericAlias'

		return self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(self.signatures.denotedGenericBaseName(baseName, readerFilePath: self.filePath), methodName: self.tokenAt(index + 2).value))
	end 'staticCallClaimsBase'

	// Dispatch a method (or a function-typed FIELD load) on an already-resolved struct base — the shared tail
	// of a LOCAL receiver and a CAPTURED one (P1.5-A2b-2). The cursor sits on the member name.
	//
	// A member that is a FUNCTION-typed FIELD, not a method — `h.op(21)` / `self.op(x)`, where `op` is a
	// function-typed field (P1.5-A1) — is an INDIRECT call, not a method dispatch: load the field (a `function`
	// value) and let the postfix loop (`parsePostfix`) apply the `(args)`. Only a FUNCTION-typed field diverts
	// here — a non-function field followed by `(` (`p.count(…)` on an int field) is not a callable member and
	// falls through to the method path, which reports it as an unknown method. `resultUsed` does not apply to
	// the field load: the value/statement position is decided at the indirect call the postfix loop emits.
	function dispatchMethodOnBase(func IrFunction, base StructBase, baseTok Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// PEEKED, not consumed: the method path below hands the cursor to `parseCallNamed`, which consumes
		// the name itself. Only the field-read branch advances.
		let memberTok = try self.memberNameTokenAt(self.pos)
		// Read ONCE and threaded to both decisions below, which are the two halves of one question: a member
		// followed by `(` is a CALL, and a member not followed by one is a READ — so "is it a field?" and
		// "may it be reported as a missing field?" cannot come to disagree about which shape they saw.
		let callFollows = self.kindAt(self.pos + 1) == TokenKind.leftParen
		let fieldIndex = base.layout.indexOfField(memberTok.value)
		if fieldIndex < base.layout.fieldCount() 'fieldMember'
			// A member that is a FIELD is READ, in two cases: a FUNCTION-typed one however it is spelled (the
			// `(args)` after it is an INDIRECT call the postfix loop applies, not a method dispatch), and ANY
			// field with no `(` after it at all — `Inner.make(9).x`, `t.branch().tally`, a plain member access
			// on a value the chain walk could not reach because its base was not a name.
			//
			// A NON-function field WITH a `(` falls through to the method path deliberately: `p.count(…)` on
			// an int field `count` is not a call through the field, and it must report the unknown method
			// (E3004) rather than silently reading the field and applying parens to an integer.
			if maxonTypeTag(self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(base.layout, index: fieldIndex, into: self.artifact.typeNames)) == ValueTypeTag.function or not callFollows 'fieldRead'
				self.advance()
				try self.requireFieldAccessible(base.layout, index: fieldIndex, fieldTok: memberTok)
				self.keepFieldSourceAliveForItsRead(base, index: fieldIndex)
				return self.emitFieldLoad(base.box, layout: base.layout, index: fieldIndex, range: SourceRange.between(baseTok, last: memberTok))
			end 'fieldRead'
		end 'fieldMember'

		// ⭐ **A MEMBER WITH NO `(` AFTER IT CANNOT BE A METHOD CALL, so it is an UNKNOWN FIELD and is
		// reported as one.** Left to fall through, the method path hands the cursor to `parseCallNamed`,
		// which consumes the name and then reports the PUNCTUATION it wanted — `E2010: Expected '(' but got
		// 'newline'` for `Leaf.make(4).nope` — a complaint about the syntax when what is wrong is the NAME.
		// The BINDING spelling of the identical access already answers E3018 through `requireFieldIndex`
		// (`let l = Leaf.make(4)` then `l.nope`), and this rung's whole thesis is that the two spellings are
		// one question: the door a receiver came through may not change what the answer IS.
		if not callFollows 'unknownFieldRead'
			throw ParseError.unknownField(String.from(base.layout.name), fieldName: String.from(memberTok.value), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'unknownFieldRead'

		// ⭐ **E3077's THIRD DISPATCH ENTRY POINT.** `dispatchMethodOnBinding` and `dispatchMethodOnReceiver`
		// mark their receiver's storage; this is the door a receiver that is NOT a bare name comes through
		// (`o.inner.bump()`), and both references mark the chain root at it. `subjectName` is the same E3070
		// derivation the other two read, so the three doors ask one question.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS BENIGN TODAY ONLY BECAUSE A SECOND DIVERGENCE CANCELS IT, WHICH IS NOT AN INVARIANT.** A
		// method writing its own receiver's fields records no parameter mutation in shv2 (`receiverOwnerMask`'s
		// ruling), so `let o` still compiles and the missing mark refused nothing. Two independent divergences
		// that happen to cancel is not a rule — and `emitFieldWrite`'s note already says the rung that makes a
		// struct PARAMETER's field writable inherits the question. Marked here so it inherits an answer too.
		self.markNameMutabilityExercised(base.subjectName)

		try self.requireSelfForDescriptorForwarding(base, memberTok: memberTok)
		try self.requireFixedElementCountSourceForForwarding(base, memberTok: memberTok)

		// Built ONCE and threaded into both readers — the withholding guard keys its store by it, and the call
		// below dispatches on it. Derived twice, it was two string joins and an allocation on EVERY struct
		// method call in every program, for one name that cannot differ.
		let callee = qualifiedMethodName(base.layout.name, methodName: memberTok.value)

		// ⭐⭐ **`p.clone()` ON A DECLARED STRUCT — AN INDEPENDENT COPY, THROUGH THE `__clone_<T>` CLONER THIS
		// COMPILER ALREADY SYNTHESIZES** (`specs/reference-identity.md`). Both reference compilers give every
		// struct whose fields are all cloneable an auto-`Cloneable` conformance and a synthesized `<T>.clone`
		// body (2-Parser.cs:3726 + 7236, maxon-selfhosted/Parser.maxon:19629); shv2 had the BODY —
		// `MmRuntime.synthesizeStructCloner`, a fresh `__mm_alloc` box, a whole-struct blit, then a deep clone
		// of each managed field — and no door onto it. It was reachable only as an array element-cloner stamp,
		// so a source `a.clone()` resolved to a `Point.clone` no file declares and typed as `unresolved`.
		//
		// ⭐ **IT IS WIRED HERE, PAST THE `declaresCallee` TEST, SO A USER-WRITTEN `clone` STILL WINS** — which
		// is both references' rule, stated the same way (v1 skips any struct that already has a
		// `"{structName}.clone"`, Parser.maxon:19652). What shv2 departs from is the SHAPE: neither reference's
		// per-type synthesized Maxon-level function is ported, because the Std-tier cloner it would lower to
		// already exists and is already correct, and `installStructCloners` already emits exactly the ones a
		// program references. So this is a call site, not a synthesis pass.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE `__clone_` CALLEE IS WHAT ROOTS THE SYNTHESIS**: `recordCallUsage` reads the prefix into
		// `usage.neededCloners`, `noteClonerUsage` closes that set over the nested fields, and
		// `installStructCloners` builds the bodies. Nothing else has to be told.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS PLACED BELOW THE TWO GUARDS, NOT ABOVE THEM, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** Each of them calls
		// itself *"the ONE door every struct method call passes through"*, and a return above them would have
		// made both claims false — quietly, since neither has anything to say about a synthesized clone today
		// (both are about a generic receiver, which `requireStructCloneSupported` now refuses outright). An
		// invariant that holds only because the exception happens to be inert is the shape this file keeps
		// paying for, so the arm sits where the claims stay literally true.
		try self.requireWitnessSourceForForwarding(base, callee: callee, memberTok: memberTok)
		try self.requireMethodNotWithheldByConstraint(base.layout.name, callee: callee, receiver: base.box, memberTok: memberTok)

		if self.structCloneIsSynthesized(callee, memberTok: memberTok) 'synthesizedClone'
			return try self.emitSynthesizedStructClone(base, memberTok: memberTok, baseTok: baseTok)
		end 'synthesizedClone'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE FLOOR THE CORPUS CALL WILL LAND AT, READ BEFORE THE EMIT** — `emitRuntimeCall`'s own
		// discipline, for the reason the stream-write fold needs: `addressableBytes()` produces a byte-buffer
		// VALUE that `__Builtins.writeStdout` does not read, and the fold removes it by rewriting
		// THIS op's callee in place (`foldByteViewIntoStreamWrite`). Filed here because this is the one door
		// every corpus method call passes through, and `callee` is already built.
		//
		// ⚠ It is a FLOOR rather than the index, because `parseCallNamed` — not this function — appends the
		// op. The fold RE-MATCHES what it finds there and declines if it is not the call, so a
		// `parseCallNamed` that one day emits a setup op first costs an allocation rather than rewriting the
		// wrong op.
		//
		// ⚠ The guard is the RECEIVER's layout plus the member's spelling, never a join: asking
		// `conformsToBuiltinManagedWrapper` first means a user type with an `addressableBytes` of its own is
		// never filed, and it costs one bit-test on the overwhelmingly common path where the answer is no.
		let byteViewOpFloor = self.artifact.module.opCount()
		let producesAByteView = base.layout.conformsToBuiltinManagedWrapper() and memberTok.value.equals(CorpusAddressableBytes)

		// ⭐ **THE CALL IS ANCHORED AT THE METHOD NAME, THE RECEIVER ARGUMENT AT THE RECEIVER (W49 wave 8).**
		// Both were `baseTok`, which put every diagnostic about this call on the receiver — `s.append(t)`
		// reported at `s`. That is not the convention `SourceRange.between` states (*"the construct's
		// leading keyword or callee name"*) and it is not what the canonical corpus prints: `/specs`
		// positions E3019 at the method name (`immutable-method-call:5:4` over `\ts.append(" world")`) and
		// E3036 there too (`method-calls:19:4` over `\tc.increment(5)`), where shv2 read `:2` for both.
		// ⚠ It was invisible while `append` had a synthesized arm, because that arm threw at `methodTok`
		// itself; retiring the member moved the refusal to the call-site half and exposed it.
		// ⭐ **THE TRANSFORM POSITION'S PARAMETER-TYPE OFFER, for a `map` an `extension` supplies** — the corpus
		// twin of `parseElementTransformArg`'s, and it is spent by the SAME `parseFunctionParameters` rule.
		// Restored on the SUCCESS path only, exactly as the builtin offer is and for the identical reason: a
		// `ParseError` out of the arguments aborts the file's parse, so a hint left set reaches no later closure.
		let savedHint = self.closureParamHint
		self.closureParamHint = self.extensionElementTransformHint(base.box, methodName: memberTok.value, callee: callee)
		// `resolvedTypeQualifier`: the author wrote the MEMBER and a receiver; the head above came from
		// `base.layout`, so no token at this call site spells it (F31).
		let result = try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: callee, anchor: memberTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.value(base.box, immutableName: base.immutableName, subjectName: base.subjectName, anchor: baseTok), resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: self.receiverConstructorInstance(base.box), mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier)
		self.closureParamHint = savedHint
		self.retypeInstanceMethodResult(result, receiver: base.box)

		if producesAByteView 'aFoldableByteView'
			self.recordStringBytesViewOp(result, opIndex: byteViewOpFloor)
		end 'aFoldableByteView'

		return result
	end 'dispatchMethodOnBase'

	// Is this member the SYNTHESIZED struct `clone` — the name `clone`, on a struct that declares no `clone` of
	// its own? The gate in front of the emit below. `declaresCallee` is the WHOLE-PROGRAM index, not this file:
	// a user's `Point.clone` may be written in any file, which is the same reason `structEqualityOperands` asks
	// its `equals` question the same way.
	function structCloneIsSynthesized(callee ByteArray, memberTok Token) returns bool
		return memberTok.value.equals(CloneMethodName) and not self.signatures.declaresCallee(callee)
	end 'structCloneIsSynthesized'

	// `p.clone()` as the direct `__clone_<T>(box)` call it is. The result is a FRESH, SOLELY-owned box — the
	// cloner's first act is an `__mm_alloc` — so it is enrolled as an owned temporary exactly as a struct
	// literal's box is, and a binding adopts it or the statement drops it.
	//
	// The result is stamped `structRef` + the receiver's own layout name, which is the same pair
	// `emitStructLiteral` mints: a clone of a `Point` IS a `Point`, so `b.x`, `b.equals(a)` and `a is b` all
	// resolve against the one nominal identity.
	//
	// ⚠ The cloneability refusal comes BEFORE the tokens are consumed and before anything is emitted — a
	// rejected program must leave no ops behind (`emitShift`'s rule).
	function emitSynthesizedStructClone(base StructBase, memberTok Token, baseTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireStructCloneSupported(base.layout, memberTok: memberTok)
		self.advance()
		try self.consumeEmptyArgList()

		return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(clonerSymbol(base.layout.name), args: singletonArgs(base.box), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.structRef, resultNameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(base.layout.name), range: SourceRange.between(baseTok, last: memberTok))
	end 'emitSynthesizedStructClone'

	// ⛔ **THE TWO REASONS A SYNTHESIZED CLONE CANNOT BE EMITTED, AND IN BOTH THE ALTERNATIVE IS A WRONG
	// ANSWER RATHER THAN A MISSING ONE.** One question — *may this receiver be cloned here?* — with two
	// clauses, so the emit below has one gate to satisfy.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **CLAUSE 1: A GENERIC BASE. MEASURED, AND IT IS THE DEFECT THIS FUNCTION EXISTS FOR.** A user
	// generic (`type Box uses T`) is NOT intercepted by the container arms in `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` —
	// only `Array`/`Set`/`Map`/`List`/`Vector` are — so a `Box with String` receiver reaches
	// `dispatchMethodOnBase`, where `structLayoutOfType` hands back the BASE layout. Everything downstream
	// then agreed on the wrong answer, which is why nothing panicked:
	//
	//   • the field walk PASSES, because `typeIsManaged` is false for a `typeParameter`-tagged field, so the
	//     `T` slot is not examined at all;
	//   • the result was stamped `structRef` + the BARE BASE name — MEASURED: `a is b` reported
	//     *"got 'StringBox' and 'Box'"*, two names for what the author wrote as one type, and every later
	//     member dispatch would resolve against that same wrong identity;
	//   • `synthesizeStructCloner` skips the very same field, so the fresh box is blitted with the `T` word
	//     ALIASED AND NOT RETAINED — at `String`, a second owner of a record with one refcount.
	//
	// ⇒ It is refused until a cloner is minted PER INSTANCE, which is the same slice the field-side refusal
	// below already advertises for a `Box with T` FIELD. The question is asked of the LAYOUT rather than of
	// the receiver's tag on purpose: `self.clone()` inside `Box`'s own body has a `structRef` receiver and is
	// just as unclonable, because what is missing is the instance, not the pointer.
	//
	// ⛔ **CLAUSE 2: A FIELD NOTHING CAN DEEP-CLONE.** `synthesizeStructCloner` walks the same fields and
	// calls `emitCloneCall`, which PANICS on an `unsupported` strategy — so the gate and the strategy must
	// admit the same set, which is exactly what `ProgramSignatures.structSupportsDeepClone` is (it and
	// `managedFieldCloneStrategy` are read off one walk). `requireArrayElementCopyable` is this clause's twin
	// one container out, down to the E2015 it reports.
	function requireStructCloneSupported(layout StructLayout, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		if layout.typeParamCount() > 0 'genericBase'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`clone` on `{String.from(layout.name)}`, which is a GENERIC type — a clone must be minted per INSTANCE (a `{String.from(layout.name)} with String` and a `{String.from(layout.name)} with int` copy different things), and this compiler mints one per declared type only, so the copy would alias the type parameter's value instead of cloning it. Write a `clone` method on `{String.from(layout.name)}` that rebuilds it.", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'genericBase'

		if self.signatures.structSupportsDeepClone(layout.name) 'cloneable'
			return
		end 'cloneable'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`clone` on `{String.from(layout.name)}`, which owns a field this compiler cannot deep-clone: a `Vector` field, or any other generic instance with no base struct to cascade over and no runtime copy of its own (each would need a per-instance cloner, and only a DECLARED generic's instance has one) — including one held INSIDE a struct field; an OS handle (`__ManagedFile`/`__ManagedDirectory`/`__ManagedSocket`), which cannot be duplicated at all; a value held at an interface type; or a container holding containers that own managed elements (an `Array with (Array with String)`, a `List with (List with String)`). String, struct, boxed-union, enum, `Array`, `List` (managed elements included) and declared-generic instance (`Box with String`) fields ARE supported.", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
	end 'requireStructCloneSupported'

	// ⭐ **THE CONDITIONAL-EXTENSION REFUSAL, AT THE ONE DOOR EVERY STRUCT METHOD CALL PASSES THROUGH.**
	// Asked before the callee is built, because the answer is about the NAME and not about the arguments: a
	// method a `where` clause withheld does not exist for this receiver at any arity.
	//
	// A `Map.contains`-cheap probe of a store that is EMPTY in every program declaring no conditional
	// extension, which is the whole corpus but one spec — so the ordinary method call pays one miss and
	// allocates nothing: `callee` is the caller's own, which it is about to dispatch on regardless.
	function requireMethodNotWithheldByConstraint(typeName ByteArray, callee ByteArray, receiver ValueId, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.conditionalExtensionSkipOf(callee) 'skip'
			notWithheld then break 'skip'
			withheld(skip) then throw ParseError.conditionalExtensionWithheld(String.from(skip.typeName), methodName: String.from(skip.methodName), clause: skip.clause, unmetInterfaceName: String.from(skip.unmetInterfaceName), bindingName: String.from(skip.bindingName), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'skip'

		try self.requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints(typeName, callee: callee, receiver: receiver, memberTok: memberTok)
	end 'requireMethodNotWithheldByConstraint'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE OTHER HALF OF THE CONDITIONAL REFUSAL: a TYPE extension's clause is checked at the CALL, per
	// RECEIVER, because there is nowhere earlier it could be.** shv2 compiles one body per generic type
	// (P1.6-A), so `extension Box where Item is Equatable` cannot be filtered per instance the way an
	// interface extension is filtered per conformer — every `Box with X` shares the one `Box.matches`, and
	// what varies is whether a given receiver may reach it.
	//
	// ⚠ **WITHOUT THIS THE PROGRAM DOES NOT FAIL, IT PANICS.** `Box with NotEq` then `.matches(…)` reached
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable` for `__witness_NotEq.Equatable` and died in `witnessSlotImpl`
	// ("no conformance selected a member for slot 'NotEq.Equatable.equals'") — measured, in exactly the shape
	// that panic's own message predicts: a table built for a conformance nothing validated.
	//
	// Two receiver shapes, and both are the same question asked of what the call site actually knows:
	//
	//   • A CONCRETE generic instance — the type argument is known, so ask whether it conforms. This is the
	//     `materializeWitnessArg` `concrete` arm's precondition, checked where a position exists.
	//   • ANYTHING ELSE (the enclosing generic body's own `self`, a value of its type parameter) — the
	//     argument is opaque and the witness is FORWARDED from the caller's own slots, so the caller must
	//     carry the SAME constraints in the SAME order. Inside the extension's own body it does, by
	//     construction (`openExtensionBodyScope` installs that very list); inside a plain method of the same
	//     type it carries only the type's own, and the forward would read a slot that was never reserved.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO LISTS THIS FUNCTION HOLDS ARE DIFFERENT LISTS AND MUST STAY SO (W23).** The one it VALIDATES
	// is the extension's own clause — the constraints nothing else checks, and the clause a refusal quotes back
	// at the author. The one the OPAQUE arm compares against is the callee's WHOLE witness list
	// (`witnessConstraintsOfMethod`: the type's constraints then the extension's extras), because that is what
	// the forward is index-aligned to. Comparing against the extension's clause alone was right only while the
	// two coincided, i.e. only for a target type that declares no `where` of its own.
	function requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints(typeName ByteArray, callee ByteArray, receiver ValueId, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		let constraints = self.signatures.extensionMethodConstraintsOf(callee)
		if constraints.isEmpty() 'notAConditionalTypeExtensionMethod'
			return
		end 'notAConditionalTypeExtensionMethod'

		let clause = self.signatures.renderExtensionClause(typeName, constraints: constraints)
		if self.tagOf(receiver) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'opaqueReceiver'
			if whereConstraintsMatch(self.enclosingWhereConstraints, other: self.signatures.witnessConstraintsOfMethod(callee, extensionConstraints: constraints)) 'callerCarriesThem'
				return
			end 'callerCarriesThem'

			throw ParseError.unsupported("'{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(memberTok.value)}' is a conditional extension method ({clause}), and this receiver's type argument is not known here — the enclosing function carries no witness for that constraint to forward. Call it on a concrete instance, or declare the same `where` clause on the function's own extension", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'opaqueReceiver'

		// ⛔ **A NAME AN UNCONDITIONAL EXTENSION ALSO DECLARES IS NOT THIS GATE'S TO REFUSE.** The callee is a
		// bare `<Type>.<method>` and the arguments have not been read, so which of the two declarations the
		// call means is not knowable here — and refusing on the conditional one's clause refuses the other one
		// too. `SemanticCheck.checkResolvedExtensionConstraints` asks the SAME question of the member overload
		// resolution picked, which is the earliest point the answer exists. See
		// `ProgramSignatures.hasUnconstrainedExtensionDeclaration` for the measurement.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT DEFERS ONLY THE CONCRETE-INSTANCE ARM, WHICH IS WHY IT SITS BELOW THE OPAQUE ONE.** The
		// post-resolution decider judges a `genericInstance` receiver and declines an opaque one — that arm
		// is about the CALLER's witness list, not about the receiver's type argument. Deferring it too would
		// hand an unforwardable witness to the lowering, and the lowering PANICS rather than failing. So an
		// opaque receiver keeps the stricter answer: over-refused for a call that meant the unconstrained
		// overload, never miscompiled.
		if self.signatures.hasUnconstrainedExtensionDeclaration(callee) 'anotherDeclarationOfThisNameHasNoClause'
			return
		end 'anotherDeclarationOfThisNameHasNoClause'

		match receiverMeetsExtensionConstraints(self.signatures, gid: self.valueNameId(receiver), constraints: constraints) 'constraint'
			met then break 'constraint'
			unmet(interfaceName, argDisplayName) then throw ParseError.conditionalExtensionWithheld(String.from(typeName), methodName: String.from(memberTok.value), clause: clause, unmetInterfaceName: String.from(interfaceName), bindingName: String.from(argDisplayName), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'constraint'
	end 'requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints'

	// ⚠⚠ **A MANAGED FIELD READ OUT OF A TEMPORARY HOLDS THAT TEMPORARY TO THE SCOPE'S EXIT — the read is a
	// BORROW, and the box that owns what it borrows would otherwise die at the end of THIS statement.**
	// `emitFieldLoad`'s header states the invariant the borrow rests on in full: *"the read is a BORROW … so
	// the box keeps its +1 and drops the value at ITS scope exit; a `let s = b.value` re-reads a live pointer
	// and is never double-dropped."* A box bound to NO NAME had no scope exit later than the statement —
	// `trackOwnedTemp` enrolled it, the statement's pending drops freed it — so any result that outlived the
	// statement was a pointer into freed memory.
	//
	// **MEASURED, all four managed field kinds, every one of them a program the C# oracle RUNS:**
	//   • `let s = Box.make("hello").name`          — String field:  **0xC0000005**, no diagnostic anywhere
	//   • `let o = Box.make().ops` then `o.get(0)`  — Array field:   **0xC0000005**, no diagnostic anywhere
	//   • `let i = Outer.make(Inner.make(3)).inner` — struct field:  exit **0x3F3F3F3F** — the freed-memory
	//     fill byte read back as user data. A WRONG ANSWER with no crash at all, which is the one of the
	//     four that a suite full of exit codes would never have noticed.
	//   • `let s = makePair().1`                    — tuple element: **0xC0000005** (a tuple is a synthesized
	//     struct, so it needs no arm of its own — that is the point of it being one)
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT USED TO REFUSE, AND THE REFUSAL'S OWN MESSAGE NAMED THE RUNG THAT WOULD LIFT IT** — *"keeping a
	// temporary alive for a borrow taken out of it is the ownership rung's"*. ⚖ That is A3h, and the USER
	// RULING of 2026-08-01 is EXTEND THE TEMPORARY'S LIFETIME, so the refusal is gone and the box is promoted
	// to a scope-lifetime binding instead (`giveTemporaryScopeLifetime`). It is the SAME promotion, through the
	// SAME door, that a container an ELEMENT borrow came out of now gets: the two were one fact refused in one
	// place and miscompiled in the other, which is exactly why they must not have two cures.
	//
	// A SCALAR field is COPIED, not borrowed, so it is untouched — which is exactly what keeps
	// `postfix.field-read-on-a-call-result` (`Leaf.make(4).tally`) paying nothing, and what makes the gate the
	// managed classifier and not the temporary.
	function keepFieldSourceAliveForItsRead(base StructBase, index FieldIndex)
		// A box owned by a NAME — a local, a parameter, `self`, a captured env slot — or a borrow of one
		// already outlives the statement, and the read is the ordinary borrow every field chain already is.
		// Only an UNCONSUMED owned temporary needs promoting, which `giveTemporaryScopeLifetime` asks for
		// itself; this early return is the COST gate, keeping the managed classification below off the hot
		// path of every field read in a program.
		if not self.pendingTempsContain(base.box) 'boxOutlivesTheStatement'
			return
		end 'boxOutlivesTheStatement'

		// ⭐ **BOTH ARMS ARE THE DOOR THE BOX'S OWN DESTRUCTOR DECIDES THIS FIELD'S DROP THROUGH.** A generic
		// INSTANCE must SUBSTITUTE its opaque arguments before it can answer (an `Element` field is managed
		// for `Box with String` and inert for `Box with Int`); a plain struct field is `structFieldIsManaged`,
		// which is literally the per-field predicate `__destruct_<T>`'s cascade loops over. "The box frees it
		// at drop" and "the read borrows it" are then one answer and cannot come apart — a field this gate
		// let through while the destructor freed it would be exactly the crash above, wearing a guard.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE STRUCT AND THE INDEX, NOT A TYPE, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR A MEASURED CROSS-FILE
		// USE-AFTER-FREE (T1).** The former spelling routed the field type out through `fieldTypeOf(…, into:
		// self.artifact.typeNames)` — which ADOPTS it, re-interning a `named` against THIS FILE's table — and
		// then asked `typeIsManaged`, which resolved that id against the PROGRAM-WIDE one. Same number,
		// different table, so the classification followed the interning ORDER: cross-file, a scalar alias
		// field read MANAGED (a false `E2015` on a legal `Gate.twice(21).n`) and a boxed-UNION field read
		// SCALAR, admitting the very borrow-out-of-a-temporary above and hanging on the freed box. Asking by
		// STRUCT NAME and INDEX never lets a type cross an interner boundary at all, so there is no id to get
		// wrong, and the guard and the cascade read one column. `typeIsManaged` now demands its interner by
		// name (`from:`) so the two-step cannot be rebuilt by accident.
		let borrowsManagedHeap = self.signatures.genericInstanceFieldIsManaged(self.valueNameId(base.box), index: index) if self.tagOf(base.box) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance else self.signatures.structFieldIsManaged(base.layout.name, index: index)
		if not borrowsManagedHeap 'scalarFieldIsCopiedNotBorrowed'
			return
		end 'scalarFieldIsCopiedNotBorrowed'

		self.giveTemporaryScopeLifetime(base.box, name: BorrowSourceBindingName)
	end 'keepFieldSourceAliveForItsRead'

	// ⭐ **THE CONCRETE INSTANCE A METHOD'S RECEIVER SUPPLIES TO THE CALLER-SIDE CONSUME (P1.7 slice 3b-iii).**
	// A `genericInstance` receiver (`sc` of type `Container with String`) knows its instance gid at the call
	// site — the same provenance `retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult` stamped on it — so a method whose param
	// FEEDS an opaque type parameter (`push(item Element)` moving `item` into `self.items`) can decide, right
	// here, whether that argument OWNS managed heap for THIS instance and must be MOVED in. It is the method
	// twin of `constructorInstanceOf`: a generic-alias CONSTRUCTOR reads the instance off the alias name, an
	// instance method reads it off the receiver's value. A `structRef` receiver (a plain struct, or `self` in
	// the shared body, which does not know its concrete instance) supplies `none` and borrows exactly as before.
	function receiverConstructorInstance(box ValueId) returns ConstructorInstance
		if self.tagOf(box) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return ConstructorInstance.instance(self.valueNameId(box))
		end 'instance'
		return ConstructorInstance.none
	end 'receiverConstructorInstance'

	// ⭐ **RETYPE A METHOD RESULT TO CARRY PER-INSTANCE IDENTITY (P1.6-C).** Two shapes gain it from the
	// receiver, and they need DIFFERENT things off it:
	//   • a `Self` return (`withTag() returns Self`, resolved to the base `structRef`) is re-tagged to the
	//     receiver's OWN `genericInstance` — so `w = w.withTag(7)` stays the same instance and type-checks
	//     (the assignment would else reject `Wrapper` into a `genericInstance` var). It needs the receiver's
	//     GID and nothing else; the alias is propagated when the receiver has one (ARR3a — see the arm);
	//   • an INNER-ALIAS return (`getTag() returns Idx`, resolved to `named("Wrapper.Idx")`) is re-qualified
	//     to `named("{alias}.Idx")` — so `a.getTag()` is a `WrapperA.Idx` value the arg check compares by
	//     name. That one IS the alias, so a receiver with no alias leaves it plain.
	// `receiver` is BORROWED; only the result's type moves.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND A THIRD SHAPE NEEDS NO ALIAS AT ALL: A RETURN THAT IS ITSELF WRITTEN IN THE SHARED BODY'S
	// DECLARATION VIEW.** `Outer.wrap() returns Inner` resolves, inside `type Outer uses T`, to
	// `genericInstance(Inner with Outer.T)` — a type that still mentions the DECLARING type's parameter, so a
	// call on a `Outer with Integer` receiver must read it as `Inner with Integer` or nothing downstream can
	// type `w.first()`. The same is true of a bare `returns T`, which is `typeParameter(Outer.T)` and must
	// become `Integer`. Both are exactly `substituteThroughInstance` — the ONE substitution the field read, the
	// drop cascade and the argument check already share (W25) — so this arm ADDS no rule, it reaches the
	// existing one from the method-RESULT position.
	//
	// ⚠ It sits ABOVE the alias gate because it does not need a spelling, only the receiver's gid: a receiver
	// minted by a bare generic base (`Inner.make(items)` inside another generic body) carries an instance and
	// no alias, and is precisely the value this arm exists for. It cannot disturb the two arms below, which
	// answer only for a `named` or `structRef` result.
	//
	// ⚠ It goes through `slotTypeThroughReceiver` rather than calling the substitution directly, because the
	// answer has to CROSS INTERNERS: a `typeParameter` return substitutes to the instance's stored ARGUMENT,
	// which is a type in the INDEX's table, and a bare `adoptType` of it would re-intern the name while
	// leaving a `named` tag that reads as an INTEGER. That door already owns both steps and the measured
	// three-faced defect behind them; asking it here is the same field-read question at the return position,
	// and a second spelling of it is the one thing that could make the two disagree. It also returns the
	// declared type unchanged when the instance fixes nothing, so a method returning a FULLY CONCRETE instance
	// (`entries() returns IntArray`) is retyped to exactly what it already was.
	function retypeInstanceMethodResult(result ValueId, receiver ValueId)
		// FIRST, because the two are stages of one reading and not alternatives: the callee's own scope is what
		// the declared return MEANS, and the receiver's instance is what fixes it. A result promoted here to
		// `MyMapIterator with (MyMap.Key, MyMap.Value)` is then reduced by the arm below to
		// `MyMapIterator with (Integer, Integer)` when the receiver has that much; a result the promotion does
		// not touch reaches the arm exactly as it did before this rung.
		self.promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope(result, receiver: receiver)

		if self.tagOf(receiver) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.carriesDeclarationView(result) 'declarationViewReturn'
			self.retypeValue(result, valueType: self.slotTypeThroughReceiver(receiver, declared: maxonTypeOfTag(self.tagOf(result), nameId: self.valueNameId(result))))
			return
		end 'declarationViewReturn'

		let receiverAlias = self.instanceAliasNameOfValue(receiver)

		// ⭐⭐ **A `Self` RETURN IS FIXED BY THE RECEIVER'S *INSTANCE*, AND THE ALIAS IS ONLY A SPELLING IT MAY
		// ALSO CARRY — so this arm sits ABOVE the alias gate (ARR3a).** It used to sit below it, which made an
		// aliasless receiver DECAY its `Self` result to the base `structRef`, and that is not the harmless
		// widening the header's "matching the oracle" note assumed: `Array with T` and `Array` are different
		// types to every later check, so the decayed value was refused by the very declaration that produced
		// it. It only ever showed on a receiver nothing SPELLS — an array literal, a `static` field, a value
		// merged across branches — which is why a synthesized surface hid it completely.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED, on this tree, by striking `slice`/`clone` from `arraySurfaceMemberNames` so
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own `returns Self` bodies served the calls**: `stdlib/Log.maxon:50`'s
		// `return captured.clone()` was `E3005 Cannot return 'Array' from function declared to return
		// 'TraceKeyArray'` — a stdlib body refused for returning exactly what it declared — and every
		// downstream read of a `slice` result was `E3059 … 'int' does not match expected type 'type
		// parameter'`, because the base `Array` fixes no element. 78 cases under `clone` alone, 55 under
		// `slice`.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ALIAS IS STILL PROPAGATED WHEN THERE IS ONE, AND IT IS THE ALIAS THAT IS OPTIONAL RATHER
		// THAN THE INSTANCE.** `recordValueInstanceAlias` names the SPELLING a diagnostic quotes back
		// (`bytearray-element-size`'s contested-alias family lives on it); an empty one is a value no source
		// text names, not a value with no type.
		if self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.structRef and self.tagOf(receiver) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'selfReturn'
			let gid = self.valueNameId(receiver)
			let baseId = self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(gid))
			if self.valueNameId(result) == baseId 'sameBase'
				self.retypeValue(result, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(gid))
				if not receiverAlias.isEmpty() 'receiverSpellsItsInstance'
					self.recordValueInstanceAlias(result, aliasName: receiverAlias)
				end 'receiverSpellsItsInstance'
				// ⭐ **AND THE ELEMENT SURFACE RIDES WITH IT, for the reason `inheritArrayElementSurface`
				// states one door over**: a `Self` result IS a value of the receiver's own instance, and
				// `Array with __ManagedMemory` / `Array with ByteArray` are ONE `GenericInstanceId` — so the
				// element spelling cannot ride the gid and has to ride the value. The synthesized `clone` /
				// `slice` arms carry it explicitly; a corpus body returning `Self` is the same operation
				// reached from source, so it may not lose it. (MEASURED with `slice`/`clone` struck:
				// `b.get(0).length()` after `let b = a.clone()` on an `Array with __ManagedMemory` was
				// *"`Array` member 'length'"* — the exact sentence A2m added that helper to retire.)
				// A receiver holding no buffer-surface elements carries no mark, so this is a bit-test that
				// answers no for every other container and every plain struct.
				_ = self.inheritArrayElementSurface(result, source: receiver)
				return
			end 'sameBase'
		end 'selfReturn'

		// An INNER-ALIAS return is re-qualified through the receiver's alias SPELLING and has nothing to fall
		// back on without one — `named("{alias}.Idx")` cannot be built from a gid — so this arm keeps the gate
		// the arm above shed.
		if receiverAlias.isEmpty() 'noReceiverAlias'
			return
		end 'noReceiverAlias'

		if self.tagOf(result) == ValueTypeTag.named 'namedReturn'
			let member = self.signatures.innerAliasMember(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(result)))
			if not member.isEmpty() 'innerAliasReturn'
				self.retypeValue(result, valueType: MaxonType.named(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(joinQualifiedName(receiverAlias, member: member))))
			end 'innerAliasReturn'
		end 'namedReturn'
	end 'retypeInstanceMethodResult'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS VALUE'S TYPE STILL WRITTEN IN THE SHARED GENERIC BODY'S DECLARATION VIEW?** — the
	// exact set of shapes a concrete receiver can FIX, and therefore the gate on the substituting arm above.
	// Three, and each is a whole family rather than a case:
	//   • a `genericInstance` (`returns Inner`, `returns ElementArray`) — its ARGUMENTS may name the declaring
	//     type's parameters;
	//   • a bare `typeParameter` (`returns T`) — it IS one;
	//   • a TUPLE `structRef` (`returns Entry`, where `typealias Entry = (Key, Value)`) — its canonical name
	//     CITES them (`substituteTupleThroughInstance`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A NON-TUPLE `structRef` IS DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, AND THAT EXCLUSION IS LOAD-BEARING.** A `Self`
	// return is one, and it needs the OTHER arm — the one that also propagates the receiver's instance ALIAS,
	// which the substitution neither carries nor could. Answering `true` here would substitute it (a no-op, a
	// plain struct names no parameter) and then RETURN, silently dropping the alias every per-instance
	// inner-alias re-qualification downstream reads.
	function carriesDeclarationView(value ValueId) returns bool
		let tag = self.tagOf(value)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance or tag == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'nestedOrOpaque'
			return true
		end 'nestedOrOpaque'

		return tagIsStructRef(tag) and isTupleTypeName(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)))
	end 'carriesDeclarationView'

	// ⚠ **A LAYOUT DESCRIPTOR IS FORWARDED FROM THE CALLER'S OWN SLOT, SO THE CALLER MUST HOLD ONE** (P1.6-B1).
	// A method that reads `sizeof(T)` needs the descriptor threaded to it. When the receiver is a value of the
	// enclosing generic type — `self` (ValueId 0), a `let c = self.makeCopy()` bound local, a `var result =
	// Self{…}` — the descriptor must be FORWARDED, which the enclosing function can do only when its own
	// transitive need was set. That is exactly `functionCarriesLayoutParam`, so it is what this asks. A
	// CONCRETE-instance receiver (`genericInstance`, not `structRef`) supplies its OWN descriptor and never
	// reaches this guard.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE TEST USED TO BE `base.box == 0` — *is the receiver literally `self`?* — AND THAT REFUSED A LEGAL
	// PROGRAM THE ORACLE COMPILES (W43b).** `stdlib/Map.maxon:58-68` builds `var result = Self{…}` in a static
	// and calls `result.upsert(k, value: v)` on it; the receiver is a `Self` value that is not `self`, so the
	// old shape rejected it outright. The premise behind that shape — *"the token pre-scan cannot see that `c`
	// is same-typed"* — is what changed: `selfTypedLocalBoundAt` now reads the binding, so the fixpoint gives
	// such a caller a descriptor and this asks whether it actually got one instead of guessing from the
	// receiver's spelling. A caller that did NOT (a `Self` PARAMETER, `merge(source Self)`, whose enclosing
	// method the pre-scan still cannot see is same-typed) is still the CLEAN E2015 below rather than
	// `forwardCallerLayout`'s panic.
	function requireSelfForDescriptorForwarding(base StructBase, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⚠ **THE GATE WAS `enclosingTypeParams`, AND THAT FIELD IS EMPTY INSIDE AN INTERFACE EXTENSION'S
		// BODY BY DESIGN (W23c)** — so this refusal, which exists precisely to keep a non-`self` forward from
		// reaching `forwardCallerLayout`'s panic, was skipped in exactly the bodies that could now reserve a
		// descriptor. The whole-program bool is the right gate and is the cheaper one: every program with no
		// descriptor-carrying method at all — the entire generics-free corpus — leaves here before the
		// qualified name is built.
		if not self.signatures.anyFunctionNeedsLayoutDescriptor() 'noDescriptorInThisProgram'
			return
		end 'noDescriptorInThisProgram'
		if self.tagOf(base.box) != ValueTypeTag.structRef 'notSameTypeValue'
			return
		end 'notSameTypeValue'
		if self.functionCarriesLayoutParam 'hasOneToForward'
			return
		end 'hasOneToForward'
		// A `structRef` receiver whose base is the enclosing type is a `Self`-typed value — a `Self`-returning
		// result, a `var result = Self{…}` local, or a `Self` PARAMETER (`merge(source Self)`); every other
		// `structRef` (a plain non-generic struct local) names a type whose methods carry no descriptor at all.
		if not base.layout.name.equals(self.enclosingType) 'notEnclosingType'
			return
		end 'notEnclosingType'
		if self.signatures.functionNeedsLayoutDescriptor(qualifiedMethodName(self.enclosingType, methodName: memberTok.value)) 'reject'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("calling '{String.from(memberTok.value)}' (which reads sizeof of the type parameter) on a value of the enclosing type, from a function that carries no layout descriptor of its own to forward — reach it through `self`, through a local bound to a `Self\{…}`, or through a concrete instance instead", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'reject'
	end 'requireSelfForDescriptorForwarding'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE COUNT COLUMN'S TWIN OF THE GUARD ABOVE, AND IT REPLACES THREE MEASURED COMPILER ABORTS
	// (found at review of W190).** A callee that reads `countof` of the sized container's own `Self` takes the
	// answer from a hidden argument the CALL SITE fills in, and a call site has exactly TWO sources: the
	// RECEIVER's instance when it states a count, and the EMITTING function's own slot when it does not. A
	// frame with neither reached `LowerMaxonToStd.forwardCallerFixedElementCount`'s panic — an abort thousands
	// of lines from the spelling that caused it, which is what a missing edge in the reservation fixpoint has
	// always cost.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE FIXPOINT CANNOT BE MADE COMPLETE BY ADDING TOKEN SHAPES, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A DOOR AND NOT A
	// FOURTH COLUMN.** It reads RAW TOKENS: `selfTypedLocalBoundAt` admits `= Self{…}` and `= Self.<static>(`
	// because those two spellings NAME the type, and no token shape tells it that `self.dup()` returns `Self`.
	// **MEASURED, three programs the compiler accepted all the way to lowering:**
	//   • `var other = self.dup()` then `other.capacity()` — *"caller 'Vector.twice' has no
	//     fixed-element-count parameter to forward to 'Vector.cap'"*.
	//   • `var x = other` where `other` is a `Self` PARAMETER — the same, one alias further out.
	//   • `function(v Self) gives v.capacity()` — *"caller 'Vector.viaClosure$closure_0' has no
	//     fixed-element-count parameter to forward"*. A LIFTED CLOSURE carries none of the enclosing method's
	//     hidden parameters however completely the fixpoint reserved them, so no edge could have saved it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS WHAT THE FUNCTION BEING EMITTED CARRIES, NOT WHAT ITS DECLARATION RESERVED** —
	// `requireWitnessSourceForForwarding`'s W58 distinction, and the third case above is exactly it:
	// `functionCarriesFixedElementCountParam` describes the enclosing METHOD, and a closure body is parsed
	// inside that method while being emitted as its own function. `parseCountof` holds the same precondition
	// the same way.
	//
	// ⚠ The receiver test is `ProgramSignatures.valueStatesItsOwnElementCount` — the SAME predicate
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.emitFixedElementCountOf` branches on, so this refusal cannot come to disagree with the
	// emit about which receivers need a forward. Wider, it would refuse a legal program; narrower, it would
	// let the abort back through.
	//
	// The clauses are ordered by what they cost: the whole-program bool leaves every program that writes no
	// `countof` at all before anything is looked up, and the qualified-name JOIN is last, for
	// `requireSelfForDescriptorForwarding`'s stated reason.
	function requireFixedElementCountSourceForForwarding(base StructBase, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.anyFunctionReadsFixedElementCount() 'noCountInThisProgram'
			return
		end 'noCountInThisProgram'
		if self.signatures.valueStatesItsOwnElementCount(self.tagOf(base.box), nameId: self.valueNameId(base.box)) 'theReceiverStatesIt'
			return
		end 'theReceiverStatesIt'
		if self.functionCarriesFixedElementCountParam and self.closureCaptureScopes.isEmpty() 'hasOneToForward'
			return
		end 'hasOneToForward'

		if self.signatures.functionReadsFixedElementCount(qualifiedMethodName(base.layout.name, methodName: memberTok.value)) 'reject'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("calling '{String.from(memberTok.value)}' (which reads `countof` of the sized container's own `Self`) on a receiver whose type states no element count, from a function that carries no fixed-element-count parameter of its own to forward — the count reaches a shared body through a hidden argument every call site fills in from the instance it holds. Reach it through `self`, through a local bound to a `Self\{…\}` or a `Self.<static>()`, by chaining the call directly onto the expression that produced the receiver, or through a concrete sized instance; a lifted closure carries no hidden parameters at all, so read the count outside it and capture the integer", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
		end 'reject'
	end 'requireFixedElementCountSourceForForwarding'

	// ⚠ **THE WITNESS TWIN OF `requireSelfForDescriptorForwarding`, AND IT REPLACES A COMPILER ABORT (W43b).**
	// A dictionary-passing call on a value of the enclosing generic type FORWARDS the caller's own witness
	// tables (`LowerMaxonToStd.materializeWitnessArg`'s `structRef` arm), so the caller must carry them. An
	// INSTANCE method always does, and — since W43b — so does a `Self`-returning static, which sources them at
	// its call site from the concrete instance it builds. What is left with no source at all is a static that
	// returns something ELSE, and that reached `forwardCallerWitness`'s panic:
	// **MEASURED, `panic at LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1414: forwardCallerWitness: caller 'Holder.digestOf'
	// carries 0 witness parameter(s) and was asked to forward slot 0 to 'Holder.digest'`**, on
	// `static function digestOf(v T) returns HashValue` building a `Self{v: v}` and calling `.digest()` on it —
	// a program the oracle compiles and runs (exit 41).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A CLEAN REFUSAL AND NOT THE FULL ANSWER, WHICH IS THE SAME "LATER SLICE" THE DESCRIPTOR'S
	// `staticLayoutNeedsSelfReturn` NAMES:** threading the dictionary from a static CALL SITE's alias
	// (`H.digestOf(41)` knows `H = Holder with Integer`). Until that exists, both columns want the same source
	// and this refuses the same shape in the same words rather than aborting the compiler.
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS `emittingFunctionCarriesWitnessParams`, NOT the declaring function's COUNT, and the difference
	// is a LIFTED CLOSURE (W58).** Forwarding copies the caller's hidden witness PARAMETERS into the callee's,
	// by position (`LowerMaxonToStd.forwardCallerWitness`) — so the question is what the function being EMITTED
	// carries, and a lifted closure carries none however constrained the method that wrote it is. Asked of the
	// declaring count instead, a closure inside a constrained instance method would take the early return here
	// and forward parameter slots its own ABI never reserved: not a panic, a WRONG TABLE.
	function requireWitnessSourceForForwarding(base StructBase, callee ByteArray, memberTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.emittingFunctionCarriesWitnessParams() 'hasThemToForward'
			return
		end 'hasThemToForward'
		if self.tagOf(base.box) != ValueTypeTag.structRef or not base.layout.name.equals(self.enclosingType) 'notSameTypeValue'
			return
		end 'notSameTypeValue'
		if self.signatures.witnessConstraintsOfMethod(callee, extensionConstraints: self.signatures.extensionMethodConstraintsOf(callee)).isEmpty() 'calleeDispatchesNothing'
			return
		end 'calleeDispatchesNothing'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("calling '{String.from(memberTok.value)}' (which dispatches through the type's `where` constraints) on a value of the enclosing type, from a `static function` that carries no witness tables of its own to forward — a static sources them from the instance it RETURNS, so declare this one `returns Self` and call the method on the result, or make it an instance method", line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
	end 'requireWitnessSourceForForwarding'

	// ⭐ **A METHOD CALL ON A CAPTURED STRUCT RECEIVER (P1.5-A2b-2).** `b.doubled()` where `b` is an enclosing
	// struct captured by the closure. The receiver box is READ OUT OF THE ENV (`emitCaptureRead` — a borrowed
	// pointer copy), its layout resolved from the enclosing binding's struct type, then dispatched exactly as a
	// local receiver: the borrowed box is passed as `__self` (the method borrows its receiver). A captured
	// non-struct is refused (only a struct carries methods here; a captured String's builtin methods are not
	// reached by any case at this rung).
	function capturedMethodCall(func IrFunction, baseTok Token, enclosing Scope, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		self.advance()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		return try self.dispatchMethodOnBase(func, base: try self.capturedStructBase(baseTok, enclosing: enclosing), baseTok: baseTok, resultUsed: resultUsed)
	end 'capturedMethodCall'

	// ⭐ **RESOLVE A CAPTURED STRUCT BASE TO ITS (box, layout) (P1.5-A2b-2)** — the shared resolution behind a
	// captured field access (`level.rawValue`) and a captured method call (`b.doubled()`). The box is READ OUT
	// OF THE ENV (`emitCaptureRead` — a borrowed pointer copy, one `loadIndirect`, cursor-independent so a
	// caller may have already consumed the base token), and its layout is resolved from the enclosing binding's
	// struct type exactly as `requireStructBase` does for a local base. A captured value with no struct layout
	// is refused (a captured String's builtin methods are reached by no case here).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE GATE ASKS `tagCarriesStructLayoutHere`, AND IT USED TO SPELL `!= structRef` ITSELF — WHICH
	// EXCLUDED EVERY GENERIC INSTANCE.** This was the ONLY door in the file guarding a `structLayoutOfType`
	// call with a hand-rolled tag test; its two siblings (`requireStructBase` for a local base,
	// `structLayoutOfField` for a hop) both ask the predicate, and both carry the comment saying why — *"a
	// struct box OR a generic INSTANCE (`Sizer with bool`, P1.6-B1) can be a method/field base"*. Left out,
	// a closure that names a captured instance was refused, and the refusal CONTRADICTED ITSELF:
	// `typeTagName(genericInstance)` prints `"struct"`, so it read *"declared 'struct' and not a struct
	// type"*. MEASURED on shv2's OWN source — `logDebug(…, message: function() gives "… {module.funcCount()}
	// …")` where `module` is a `StdModule`, i.e. `IrModule with StdOp` — in EIGHT files, against a bootstrap
	// that compiles every one of them. It is the same A4j shape `structLayoutOfField`'s header records, one
	// door over, and the same fix: ask the predicate rather than answer for yourself.
	function capturedStructBase(baseTok Token, enclosing Scope) returns StructBase throws ParseError
		let baseName = self.baseBindingName(baseTok)
		let binding = enclosing.getInfo(baseName)
		let capturedTag = maxonTypeTag(binding.varType)
		let capturedTypeNameId = typeNameIdOf(binding.varType)
		if not self.tagCarriesStructLayoutHere(capturedTag) 'notAStruct'
			// `requireStructBase`'s undeclared-name guard, reached through a `function(…) gives` instead of a
			// bare name — identically able to assert an invented `int` without it, which is the second of
			// `requireDeclaredBaseTypeName`'s three doors.
			try self.requireDeclaredBaseTypeName(capturedTag, nameId: capturedTypeNameId, tok: baseTok)

			throw ParseError.unsupported("a field access or method call on the captured '{String.from(baseTok.value)}', which is declared '{typeTagName(capturedTag)}' and not a struct type (only a struct has fields and methods)", line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column)
		end 'notAStruct'
		// E3070 exemption, and it is one this rung can prove rather than assume: capture is BY VALUE, so a
		// method call on a CAPTURED receiver cannot reach a binding of the enclosing body, and a captured
		// managed CONTAINER — the only thing a borrow could be taken out of — is refused outright at this rung
		// (`capturedStructBase` admits only a struct). There is no storage here for a conflict to be about.
		return StructBase.create(try self.structLayoutOfType(BaseAccessKind.member, tag: capturedTag, nameId: capturedTypeNameId, baseTok: baseTok), box: try self.emitCaptureRead(baseName, enclosing: enclosing, anchor: baseTok), immutableName: self.immutableReceiverNameOf(binding, baseTok: baseTok), subjectName: EmptySourceText)
	end 'capturedStructBase'

	// ⭐ **A STRING RECEIVER'S LAST SYNTHESIZED DOOR, AND IT NOW SERVES NO METHOD OF ITS OWN (W49 wave 9).**
	// What is left is the roster gate and the builtin-CONFORMANCE lookup — `hash`/`equals`, whose impls the
	// corpus defines and which this reaches by symbol rather than by re-synthesizing. Every actual member of
	// `String` is `stdlib/String.maxon`'s and is taken by `memberBelongsToTheCorpus` before this is asked.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES NO `resultUsed`, AND THAT IS A CONSEQUENCE RATHER THAN A TIDY-UP.** The parameter existed
	// for `requireVoidMethodIsStatement`, which only a VOID member needs; `setByte` was the last one and it
	// retired. A corpus member's void-ness is checked on the corpus path, by the declaration.
	//
	// The cursor sits on the method NAME; the receiver's live-binding guard belongs to whichever door
	// resolved it (`dispatchMethodOnBinding` — a temporary has no move state to check).
	function dispatchStringMethod(func IrFunction, recv MethodReceiver) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let baseTok = recv.anchor
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE ROSTER IS THE SURFACE, AND IT IS ASKED BEFORE ANY ARM (A2q)** — `dispatchArrayMethod`'s
		// gate, one type over, for its reason: "does this member exist?" is the question every other refusal
		// about the call presupposes, and a rule about HOW a member may be used must not speak for a member
		// the receiver does not have.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL, and the derived list is what guarantees that**: every name off this
		// roster reached the hand-written refusal at the bottom of this function before and is refused here
		// now, with the same E2015 at the same token — only the sentence moved. Nothing ON the roster can be
		// refused here, because every entry matches an arm or the conformance lookup (the panic at the end of
		// this function is what says so).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ONE STDLIB-ONLY NAME PASSES THIS GATE AND IS REFUSED BY ITS OWN ARM.** That ordering is
		// the point of putting it on the roster at all: "shv2 has no such method" and "this method exists
		// and is not yours to call" are different statements, and the gate must not turn the second into the
		// first. `stdlib-only-string-methods.md` pins both directions — the real name gets the visibility
		// refusal, a near miss of it gets this roster.
		let roster = try self.requireSurfaceMember(MemberSurface.string, typeName: StringConformerName, methodTok: methodTok)

		// ⚠ **THE WHOLE P1.8 SLICE C BYTE/ASCII FAMILY USED TO SIT HERE AND NONE OF IT DOES** —
		// `startsWith`/`endsWith` (W49 wave 1), `toLower`/`toUpper`/`replace`/`replaceFirst` (wave 2),
		// `contains` (wave 4) and `split` (wave 5). Each was struck from `stringSurfaceMemberNames`, which is
		// the whole of the retirement, so not one of the seven reaches this dispatcher at all.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE THREE VIEWS WENT AT WAVE 6, WHICH IS THE ONE RETIREMENT THAT CHANGED A RETURN TYPE.**
		// `bytes()`/`codepoints()`/`utf16()` answered here with a MATERIALIZED `Array`; the corpus answers with
		// a LAZY `ByteView`/`CodepointView`/`UTF16View` holding the `String`
		// (`stdlib/helpers/string/views.maxon`). Every earlier wave was like-for-like, so the divergence this
		// dispatcher's header used to defend — *"shv2 has no `Iterable`, no associated types and no cursor
		// protocol"* — is simply no longer TRUE: measured before a line was deleted, `ByteView.create(s)`
		// already answered `count()` and drove `for b in v` to `97,98,99` through the corpus's own
		// `createIterator`/`current`/`advance`. `toByteArray()` rode `bytes()`'s emitter and is now the
		// corpus's INDEPENDENT `managed.slice` copy, which is what the reference always meant by the pair.
		//
		// ⚠ `Character.bytes()` is NOT retired and still reaches `parseByteView` from
		// `dispatchCharacterMethod` — `type Character` has no view accessor to fall through to.
		//
		// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND WAVE 7 TOOK THE SCALAR MEMBERS AND ALL FOUR READ-ONLY BYTE DOORS — `byteLength`,
		// `isEmpty`, `clone`, `addressableBytes`, `byteAt`, `byteAtOrPanic` AND `hasSingleByteGraphemes`.**
		// Six of the seven were the CHEAPEST arms in this file — a field load, a load plus a compare, one
		// runtime call — which is exactly why they were the last to go and why this wave is where the
		// accepted cost is largest per call: there is no work here for a corpus body to be *as fast as*, only
		// a call frame to add. The corpus bodies are the normative definitions these arms were transcribed
		// FROM (`stdlib/String.maxon:248-252` says so outright of the byte primitives), so the answers are
		// unchanged and only the frame is new.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE FOUR `module` MEMBERS AMONG THEM NO LONGER TAKE A LOCATION GATE, AND THAT IS A STRICT
		// IMPROVEMENT RATHER THAN A LOST CHECK.** `requireStdlibOnlyStringMethod` existed because a
		// synthesized arm answers ahead of the corpus, so the `module` keyword the corpus WROTE could never be
		// reached; retired, each is refused by its own declaration through `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom`.
		// `setByte` is the one member that cannot follow — see its arm, and the roster's header.
		//
		// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND WAVE 8 TOOK `append`, THE ONE MEMBER EVERY EARLIER WAVE HAD TO LEAVE, BECAUSE THE THING
		// HOLDING IT BACK WAS A MISSING MASK RATHER THAN A MISSING BODY.** `parseStringAppend` was the only
		// caller of `noteReceiverWrite(mutatesReceiver: true)` for a `String`, so retiring it as a plain roster
		// deletion would have deleted E3019 for `let s = "hello"; s.append(" world")` — a write through a
		// receiver the caller may only have BORROWED, which is a memory-safety fault rather than a lost
		// message. The corpus body's `writtenParamMask` was ZERO because both its writes are self-field writes.
		// `receiverOwnerMask` now derives that bit from the ENVELOPE COLLAPSE — see its header — so
		// `String.append` states its own receiver write and the call-site half
		// (`SemanticCheck.checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam`) refuses exactly what this arm used to, naming the
		// corpus's own parameter. ⚠ **VERIFIED RED**: with the roster line struck and the mask arm absent, all
		// four committed `immutable-method-call` String cases compiled clean.
		// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND WAVE 9 TOOK `setByte`, THE LAST MEMBER THIS DISPATCH SYNTHESIZED — SO THERE IS NO ARM
		// BELOW THIS LINE, ONLY THE CONFORMANCE DOOR.** It was `stdlib/String.maxon:316`, the one byte
		// primitive that WRITES and the last `module` member still refused by a LOCATION gate rather than by
		// its own declaration.
		//
		// ⛔ **ITS BLOCKER WAS NEVER OWNERSHIP AND NEVER LOWERING — IT WAS ONE POSITIONAL ARGUMENT IN THE
		// CORPUS.** `mapAsciiCase` wrote `try work.setByte(i, b + delta)` (`stdlib/String.maxon:658`), where
		// `specs/parameter-labels.md` rules that every argument after the first must be NAMED. shv2 enforces
		// that (E2053) and the bootstrap does not, so the declared member could not have served its own only
		// caller: routed to the corpus, `stdlib/` refused itself. One token — `value:` — closed it, and the
		// arm went in the same edit.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE LOWERING DID NOT CHANGE, IT MOVED ONE FRAME DOWN.** The arm handed the RECEIVER'S OWN
		// RECORD to `__managed_set_byte`, which is exactly what the corpus body's `managed.setByte(…)` means on a
		// FUSED record — a String record and an Array record agree on all five slots that entry reads
		// (`BufferOwnership`'s shared prefix), and it touches `@40` nowhere. So the corpus body reaches the
		// identical callee with the identical bound, and there was never a `__str_set_byte` to keep in step.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **IT WROTE THROUGH `__str_bytes_view(self)` UNTIL W49 WAVE 2, AND THE WRITE LANDED NOWHERE** —
		// the record-not-view fix that made this arm correct is what the corpus body now inherits, so the
		// lesson survives the arm. That view is NON-OWNING (`capacity@16 = ViewBufferCapacity`), so
		// `__managed_set_byte`'s guard sent it through `__managed_cow_detach` first: the view got a private copy, the
		// byte was written into the copy, and the copy died with the owned temporary at statement end.
		// `buildStrBytesView`'s own header called that safe on the grounds that *"nothing in `stdlib/` writes
		// through this door"* — true only because the door's one corpus caller, `mapAsciiCase`, was
		// unreachable while `toLower`/`toUpper` were synthesized. Wave 2 made it reachable and every case
		// conversion in the suite returned its receiver verbatim. ⇒ **A CLAIM THAT AN UNREACHABLE PATH IS SAFE
		// IS A CLAIM NOTHING MEASURED.**
		//
		// ⚠ **AND THE REFUSAL IT CARRIED IS NOT LOST, IT IS DELEGATED.** `stdlibOnlyRefusal("String method", …)`
		// existed because a synthesized arm answers AHEAD of the corpus, so the `module` keyword the corpus
		// WROTE could never be reached. With no arm, `String.setByte` is an ordinary declared function and
		// `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom` refuses it with E3088 exactly as it refuses the four read doors
		// wave 7 retired — one mechanism for all five, where there were two.

		// ⭐⭐ **`hash()` AND `equals(other)` ARE NOT STRING METHODS — THEY ARE THE BUILTIN CONFORMANCES, AND
		// THIS IS THE DIRECT-CALL DOOR ONTO THE IMPLS THAT ALREADY EXIST (BATCH2 slice 2).** A
		// `__witness_String.*` slot is stamped with a `funcAbs64` reloc naming `String.hash`/`String.equals`,
		// and this emits those SAME symbols — one body, three doors, and emphatically not a second djb2
		// written at a call site.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **AND THE BODY BEHIND THOSE SYMBOLS IS THE CORPUS'S, NOT A SYNTHESIZED ONE — THIS COMMENT SAID
		// THE OPPOSITE UNTIL W49 WAVE 7 MEASURED IT.** It read *"`BuiltinConformanceRuntime` synthesizes
		// `String.hash` (djb2) and `String.equals` (`__str_eq`'s content compare)"*. That installer builds an
		// impl only for a candidate the module NAMES and does not DEFINE, and `stdlib/String.maxon:351,355`
		// declare both — so the synthesized pair has not been installed since the module was listed. Measured
		// on the emitted IR: `s.hash()` is `call String.hash`, whose body is `call hashString`
		// (`stdlib/helpers/string/hash.maxon:22`).
		//
		// ⇒ **SO THERE IS NOTHING HERE FOR A RETIREMENT WAVE TO MOVE, AND THAT IS WHY WAVE 7 LEFT THIS DOOR
		// ALONE WHILE RETIRING SEVEN MEMBERS AROUND IT.** A retirement exists to make the corpus body serve
		// the member; for this pair the corpus body ALREADY serves it, and the only thing striking the two
		// names off the roster would change is which door routes the call — `memberBelongsToTheCorpus`
		// instead of the lookup below — to the identical symbol. That is churn with a return-type risk
		// (`HashValue` is `int(0 to u32.max)`; this door types the result off the `InterfaceMethodSig`) and no
		// benefit. The two hashes AGREE — same djb2, same seed, same multiplier, same low-32 mask — which is
		// what a `Set with String`'s bucket layout rides on; `BuiltinConformanceRuntime`'s header records the
		// full measurement.
		//
		// ⭐ **ASKED LAST, AND BY LOOKUP RATHER THAN BY TWO MORE `if`s.** Last, because the String method
		// table above must win — the arm-order rule `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` states, one level down. By
		// lookup, because then no method NAME is spelled here at all: the pair is DERIVED from
		// `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`'s `String` row, so this door cannot offer a method the installer
		// would decline to install, and the day `String` gains `Comparable` its `compare()` opens with no edit
		// here. A name the row does not supply falls through to the roster below, which is what keeps a typo a
		// typo rather than a permissions message.
		match builtinConformerMethod(StringConformerName, methodName: methodTok.value, discovered: DiscoveredConformer.none) 'conformanceMethod'
			supplied(method) then return try self.emitBuiltinConformanceCall(func, receiver: recv.value, target: DirectConformanceTarget.builtinValue(BuiltinConformer.stringConformer), method: method, baseTok: baseTok, methodTok: methodTok)
			notSupplied then break 'conformanceMethod'
		end 'conformanceMethod'

		// ⚠⚠ **NO `String` RECEIVER CAN REACH HERE, AND THIS IS THE CHECK THAT SAYS SO (A2q).** The gate at
		// the top admitted only the names `stringSurfaceMemberNames` lists, and each of those matches an arm
		// above or the conformance lookup on its own spelling — so arriving here means the roster gained a
		// member and the dispatch did not. That is the defect this rung exists to close, one level up, and the
		// wrong answer it would otherwise give is unusually convincing: the reader would be handed the roster
		// refusal for a member the roster PROVIDES. It is exactly what a hand-written sentence could never
		// notice, which is how `addressableBytes` and `byteAtOrPanic` went unadvertised for a whole rung.
		// ⭐ **VERIFIED RED**: pushing a bogus name onto `stringSurfaceMemberNames` and calling it reaches
		// this panic, naming the member and both remedies rather than the roster refusal.
		let drift = rosterDriftMessage("dispatchStringMethod", member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, rosterFuncs: "`stringSurfaceMemberNames` (whose tail is `IrInterface.builtinConformerMethodNameList`)")
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'dispatchStringMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BUFFER'S TWO STDLIB-ONLY MEMBERS — the `setByte` arm's twin, one surface over, and it exists
	// because the two of them shipped UNGATED and each was a memory-safety hole reachable from an
	// ordinary user program.** Both are `module function`s in the corpus (`stdlib/String.maxon:195`'s `cstr()`
	// forwards to `toCString`, `:301` IS `makeCharFromBytes`), so the visibility is the corpus's own and is
	// enforced on the file's PHYSICAL LOCATION for the reason its String twin states — shv2 has no `module`
	// keyword, and `livesUnderStdlibDirectory` is emphatically not `isStdlibSource`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS STILL A FUNCTION WHERE THE `String` SIDE BECAME AN INLINE THROW (W49 wave 7), AND THE
	// DIFFERENCE IS THE CALLER COUNT.** That side is down to ONE arm, so its sentence sits inside the arm it
	// is about and cannot go stale; this one is shared by TWO arms with two different reasons, so it keeps the
	// standing obligation its own last paragraph states — every arm that calls it must appear in its sentence.
	//
	// **What the gate is worth, measured on this tree before it existed:**
	//   * `sub.managed.toCString()` on a zero-copy view — **exit 101**, the leak gate. A `cstring` is a raw
	//     address with no length, no capacity, no refcount and no drop, so the copy `__mm_to_cstring` makes when
	//     `buffer[length]` is not already 0 belongs to nobody. A view's next byte is its PARENT's, so the copy
	//     path is what an ordinary slice takes, not an exotic one.
	//   * `mm.makeCharFromBytes(0, len: 1000000000)` — **0xC0000005**. The corpus's contract is a PRECONDITION
	//     (*"callers must guarantee `pos + len <= byteLength()`"*), so `__char_at` copies the window without
	//     checking it — correctly, since its other caller is the `for c in s` segmenter, whose window is in
	//     range by construction and which would otherwise pay a bound test per cluster. The bootstrap checks at
	//     this same door and refuses with `__ManagedMemory: byte index out of bounds`; shv2 refuses the door.
	//
	// ⇒ Neither member leaves the roster: the corpus DOES call both, and `bufferSurfaceMemberNames` records
	// what the surface HAS rather than who may reach it — the same split `dispatchStringMethod`'s roster and its
	// stdlib-only gate already keep one surface over.
	//
	// It is a second GATE rather than a `message` parameter on the first because the gate is asked at every
	// admitted call and only the throw path may pay for a message.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SENTENCE'S OPENING IS SHARED WITH `setByte`'s ARM AND IS WRITTEN ONCE** — see
	// `stdlibOnlyRefusal` below. The two refusals differ in their NOUN and their REASON and agree on
	// everything between, which is exactly the shape that becomes two sentences drifting apart: the stem
	// names `stdlib/String.maxon`, the oracle's answer and the location rule, and all three are one fact.
	function requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod(methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.livesUnderStdlibDirectory 'thestdlibsOwn'
			return
		end 'thestdlibsOwn'

		throw stdlibOnlyRefusal("`{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}` member", methodTok: methodTok, reason: "`{String.from(ManagedMemoryToCStringMethod)}` hands back a raw address with no length, no owner and no refcount, so the copy it makes when the receiver's bytes are not already NUL-terminated is reclaimed by nothing; `{String.from(ManagedMemoryMakeCharFromBytesMethod)}` trusts its caller for `pos + len <= {String.from(ManagedMemoryLengthMethod)}()` and reads off the end of the buffer otherwise. User code reaches a buffer's bytes through `{String.from(ByteAtMethod)}`, which is bounds-checked and throwing")
	end 'requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT "STDLIB-ONLY" MEANS, WRITTEN ONCE (W49 wave 7).** Both surviving location gates —
	// `dispatchStringMethod`'s `setByte` arm and `requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod` above — refuse a member the
	// corpus declares `module function` and shv2 SYNTHESIZES, so neither has a declaration whose visibility
	// could answer instead. They differ in exactly two things, which are the two parameters here: the NOUN
	// the receiver's surface is called, and the REASON that particular member is not the user's to reach.
	// Everything between was a ~150-character opening spelled out at both sites — one statement about
	// `stdlib/String.maxon`, about the oracle's not-exported answer, and about the location rule.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES A RENDERED `reason` AND THAT IS NOT AN EAGER-COST BUG**: every caller is already INSIDE
	// its own `livesUnderStdlibDirectory` refusal branch, so nothing is built on an admitted call. That is
	// the same property the two-gates-not-one-parameter arrangement above exists to preserve, kept by
	// putting the shared text below the branch rather than above it.
	//
	// ⚠ It names the oracle's ANSWER and deliberately not its CODE NUMBER — a 4-digit code written outside
	// `docs/error-codes.txt` is a copy of the number space, and this one could not even be a CHECKED copy
	// because shv2 does not claim that code and its generated `ErrorCodeRegistry` has no member for it.
	// ⇒ Written here, that argument is made once for both messages rather than at each of them.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE FACT THE STEM ASSERTS IS `livesUnderStdlibDirectory`, NOT `isStdlibSource` — THE
	// DIFFERENCE COST THE WORKSTREAM ITS MEASUREMENT COMMAND FOR ONE COMMIT.** `isStdlibSource` answers a
	// VISIBILITY question and deliberately hands a project ROOTED under `stdlib/` its own files back as the
	// USER's — so gated on it, this sentence told `maxon-shv2 build stdlib/URL.maxon` that `stdlib\URL.maxon`
	// "is not stdlib source", which is false about that path and breaks the command `StdlibLoader`'s own
	// collision rule prescribes for deciding whether a module is ready to be listed. D6 had already drawn
	// this distinction and NAMED that consequence for `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`. Being reachable by a
	// `module`-visible method is a property of the FILE, never of how a command line spelled the project.
	// ⇒ Both gates read that bit; this is the one sentence that CLAIMS it, so it is stated once here.
	function stdlibOnlyRefusal(noun String, methodTok Token, reason String) returns ParseError
		return ParseError.unsupported("{noun} '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — it is STDLIB-ONLY (`module function` in `stdlib/String.maxon`, which the reference compiler refuses to user code as a not-exported error) and this file is not under `stdlib/`. {reason}", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'stdlibOnlyRefusal'

	// ⚠ **`parseStringSplit` WAS HERE AND WENT WITH `String.split`** (W49 wave 5). It was the one builtin
	// method that CONSTRUCTED an `Array with String`, creating the array here through `emitArrayCreateOp` and
	// handing it to `__str_split` to fill. The corpus builds the same array through `StringArray.create()`
	// (`stdlib/String.maxon:604-607`), which reaches the SAME door by the ordinary generic-instance path and
	// stamps the same element size and the same `element_destroy@40` — measured on the emitted code, see
	// `ContainsMethod`'s header. `.bytes()` below is now the only member here that fills a caller-created
	// array, and it keeps this shape for its own element's sake (`requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement`).

	// ⭐ **`Character.bytes()` — hand back this record's bytes as an `Array with Byte`.** It was ONE EMITTER
	// FOR A STRING'S AND A CHARACTER'S until W49 wave 6, because they are one question about one record; the
	// `String` half now falls through to the corpus and the question is the same, asked of one receiver.
	//
	// The array is created HERE, through `emitArrayCreateOp` — the one array-creation door — and handed to
	// `__str_to_bytes` to fill, which is `split`'s split of labour and for `split`'s reason: element size
	// and the `element_destroy@40` stamp are decided by the same code a `b"…"` literal uses, so a
	// `.bytes()` result and a byte-string literal cannot disagree about what an `Array with Byte` is. They
	// are in fact the SAME interned instance (`internArrayByteInstance`).
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT MATERIALIZES, AND SINCE W49 WAVE 6 THAT IS A `Character` DECISION AND NOT A `String` ONE.**
	// `String.bytes()` reached this emitter until wave 6 and now falls through to `stdlib/String.maxon:481`,
	// whose `ByteView` is LAZY. The reason this emitter carried — *"shv2 has no `Iterable`, no associated
	// types and no cursor protocol"* — was measured FALSE before the retirement: `for b in ByteView.create(s)`
	// already walked the corpus's `createIterator`/`current`/`advance` and printed `97,98,99`.
	//
	// ⚠ **`Character` IS NOT KEPT HERE FOR WANT OF A DECLARATION — `stdlib/Character.maxon:53` DECLARES
	// `bytes()` TOO.** It is kept because retiring it is a different trade with a cost of its own: that body
	// is `ByteView.create(toString())`, and `toString()` mints a whole `String` record per call, where this
	// emitter reads the Character's own bytes in place. `Character.bytes()` is on `characterSurfaceMemberNames`
	// and off the `String` roster, so the wave that weighs that allocation is the wave that retires it.
	// The result is an OWNED temp whose `__managed_decref` frees the record and its buffer.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT IS THE READING FILE'S OWN `Byte` ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS WHY THE FILL HAS TO BE GATED
	// (A4a).** `__str_to_bytes` writes the receiver's raw UTF-8 into the record — bytes that exist only at run
	// time — so the element must admit every value a byte has. It is asked here rather than at the interning,
	// because what is wrong is the FILL and not the type: `requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement` states the whole
	// rule, and why the element may not simply be moved onto `__ManagedByte` to dodge it.
	//
	// The giid is read ONCE and threaded to both, so the instance the rule inspects is provably the instance
	// the record is typed as — `requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement`'s coupling, on the other producer of the
	// same type.
	function parseByteView(record ValueId, methodTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let giid = self.signatures.internArrayByteInstance(self.filePath)
		try self.requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
		try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
		return try self.emitMaterializedFill(giid, callee: StrToBytesName, source: record, extraArgs: ValueIdArray.create(), range: range)
	end 'parseByteView'

	// ⚠⚠ **`parseDecodedView` WAS HERE AND WENT WITH `String.codepoints()`/`String.utf16()` (W49 WAVE 6).**
	// It kept the two DECODING views off `Codepoint`/`CodeUnit16` and on the plain `integer` instance, for a
	// reason that outlived it and is restated at `SignatureIndex.SynthesizedByteElementName`: those two
	// element names are a compiler-synthesized alias and a RANGED typealias in a whitelisted stdlib module, so
	// an `Array with CodeUnit16` would take its stride from `arrayElementSize`'s registry probe — making the
	// element size of a compiler-synthesized view a function of which stdlib modules the whitelist lists that
	// day. The corpus's `CodepointView`/`UTF16View` are structs holding the `String`, so they mint no array
	// instance at all and there is nothing left for the rule to decide.

	// ⚠ **`parseMaterializedView` STOOD HERE AND IS FOLDED INTO `parseByteView` (W49 WAVE 6).** It described
	// "what a materialized view IS" for THREE methods that could otherwise have come to disagree about who
	// creates the array and who owns it. Two of the three retired, and a description with one subject is not a
	// shared description — it is a hop. The contract it carried is `emitMaterializedFill`'s and is stated
	// there, where the other callers can read it.

	// ⭐⭐ **A PUSH-ONLY RUNTIME WALK THAT FILLS A CALLER-CREATED ARRAY — the one door, for the byte view AND
	// for `map`.** Create the array HERE through `emitArrayCreateOp` (the one array-creation door),
	// hand it to the walk as the SECOND argument, and enrol the ARRAY — not the void call's result — as the
	// owned temporary.
	//
	// ⚠ **`(source, dest, …)` IS THE ARGUMENT ORDER EVERY SUCH RUNTIME ENTRY TAKES, and it is stated once
	// here.** Two callees now share it (`__str_to_bytes` and `__managed_map`) where four did until W49
	// wave 6 retired the two decoding views; a caller that assembled its own list would be free to swap the
	// two records, which reads as a valid call at every tier and writes into the source at run time.
	//
	// ⚠ The result is enrolled owned whatever the element is: `trackOwnedTemp` routes its scope-exit drop by
	// TAG through `decrefCalleeFor` (`__managed_decref`), which destroys the elements the record's own
	// `element_destroy@40` stamp names — so a managed-element fill needs nothing extra here.
	function emitMaterializedFill(giid GenericInstanceId, callee ByteArray, source ValueId, extraArgs ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let filled = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		var args = pairArgs(source, second: filled)
		args.append(extraArgs)
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(filled, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return filled
	end 'emitMaterializedFill'

	// ⭐⭐ **`container.map(transform)` — THE WHOLE OF `map`, FOR BOTH CONTAINERS THAT SERVE IT.** An `Array`
	// and a `Set` differ in ONE thing here — which runtime walk reaches the next element — so the callee is a
	// parameter and nothing else is. The result INSTANCE is the caller's to state because that is the one
	// place the two genuinely disagree: an array maps to its OWN instance, a `Set with K` to `Array with K`.
	//
	// ⭐ **`map` IS A COMPILER-OWNED CONTAINER METHOD, NOT AN `Iterable` EXTENSION, AND THAT IS A DEPARTURE
	// FROM BOTH REFERENCES.** `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:187` declares `map`/`filter`/`contains` as an
	// `export extension Iterable` written in Maxon, and v1 serves `arr.map(f)` by synthesizing that extension
	// onto the conforming type. shv2 cannot: its containers are hand-synthesized builtins with no
	// `createIterator` and no `implements` clause (`Parser`'s array dispatch header), it admits neither
	// `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon` nor `stdlib/Array.maxon` (`StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules`), and the
	// extension machinery keys off `conformersOf`, which walks DECLARED types. So `map` joins the surface the
	// same way `push`/`slice`/`count` did — a roster entry and a dispatch arm — which is what shv2's own
	// refusal has always said that surface IS. What is taken from the reference is the SEMANTICS, read off
	// that declaration: a NEW array, in iteration order, one `transform(item)` per element, and
	// element-type-PRESERVING (`function(Element) returns Element`).
	function parseContainerMap(func IrFunction, container ValueId, resultGiid GenericInstanceId, callee ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **ADOPTED INTO THIS FILE'S INTERNER, because the element type no longer only has its TAG read.**
		// `containerElementType` answers with the whole-program index's own ids, which was harmless while
		// `requireElementTransform` was the only consumer — it compares tags. Since the element type also
		// TYPES an untyped closure parameter (`parseElementTransformArg`'s hint), an id-bearing element (a
		// `Map`'s `(Key, Value)` pair `structRef`) would reach the file's value columns wearing a foreign id
		// and PANIC in `internedName` the moment anything asked for its name. MEASURED, on this spec's
		// `m.map(function(p) gives p)`: `Parser.internedName: type-name id 44 is not in this file's interner`,
		// through `valueIsCopyableTuple` on the closure's own return.
		let transform = try self.parseElementTransformArg(func, elementType: self.signatures.adoptType(self.signatures.containerElementType(resultGiid), into: self.artifact.typeNames))
		// The env travels with the VALUE, through the one door an in-frame indirect call asks
		// (`emitClosureEnvArg`): a capturing closure's heap block, or a materialized null for a plain function
		// reference or a non-capturing closure. The runtime's `callIndirect` is the uniform `(elem, __env)`
		// ABI, so a capturing transform works here for the same reason `f(x)` does.
		return try self.emitMaterializedFill(resultGiid, callee: callee, source: container, extraArgs: pairArgs(transform, second: self.emitClosureEnvArg(transform, range: range)), range: range)
	end 'parseContainerMap'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS RECEIVER'S ELEMENT SATISFY THE `where` CLAUSE OF `Array`'s OWN CONDITIONAL
	// CONFORMANCE? — the WITHHOLDING rule, and it is the SAME question, the same predicate and the same
	// diagnostic a user's own conditional extension gets.** `ConformanceCheck.typeConformsTo` over
	// `sweptConformanceIndex` is what `requireReceiverSatisfiesExtensionConstraints` asks of a declared type's
	// extension methods; the only difference here is that `Array`'s conformance is not read from a file — shv2
	// loads no `stdlib/Array.maxon` — so the clause is stated by an interface list instead of parsed. E4006 is
	// therefore the same sentence a reader gets from a `where` clause they wrote themselves.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE CLAUSE IS READ FROM `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBED** (see that
	// predicate's `Array` arm): `where Element is Hashable and Equatable` is the SAME two protocols `Array`
	// conditionally supplies, so the sentence a reader is given and the row that decides it are one fact.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS DOWN TO A SINGLE CALLER — THE `==`/`!=` OPERATOR — AND EVERY METHOD THAT USED TO SHARE IT
	// RETIRED TO THE CORPUS.** `arr.hash()` and `a.equals(b)` went to `stdlib/Array.maxon:637`'s extension at
	// X-array-retire and `arr.contains(x)` to `:534`'s at ARR3b; each states its clause in the language and
	// withholds itself. The operator has no declaration to withhold it, which is exactly why this survives
	// them — and it is why the interface list is no longer a PARAMETER: there is one clause left to ask about,
	// so a caller can no longer name a different one. (The hash-table KEY gate reads the same row but does NOT
	// come through here: it refuses a key rather than a method, so it wants the verdict's payload and not this
	// throw. See `requireArrayKeyElementConforms`.)
	//
	// ⚠ **AN OPAQUE `T` ELEMENT IS REFUSED, AND THE REASON IS A STRIDE AND NOT A CONFORMANCE.** `Array with T`
	// is opaque, so `element_size@24` — which `__managed_hash`/`__managed_eq` read to bound their byte walk — is not
	// known here. Answering E4006 instead would claim the element does not conform, which is not what is
	// known: nothing here knows what it binds to.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE METHOD NAME AND THE POSITION ARE TWO PARAMETERS, because the OPERATOR door has one and not the
	// other.** `a == b` fails this rule as `Array.equals` — that is the method whose availability was
	// withheld — but the only span to blame is the operator's, and there is no `equals` token to read a name
	// off.
	function requireArrayConformanceAvailable(giid GenericInstanceId, methodName ByteArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let interfaces = self.signatures.arrayConformanceProtocolNames()
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaqueElement'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("'{String.from(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName())}.{String.from(methodName)}' is a conditional extension method ({arrayWhereClause(interfaces)}), and this array's element type is not known here — it is the enclosing generic's own type parameter, and a compiler-owned `{String.from(methodName)}` cannot be given the element facts it needs from this body. Call it on an array of a concrete element type", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'opaqueElement'

		match self.signatures.arrayElementConstraintCheck(giid, clauseInterfaces: interfaces) 'clause'
			satisfied then return
			unmet(unmetInterfaceName, bindingName) then throw ParseError.conditionalExtensionWithheld(String.from(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName()), methodName: String.from(methodName), clause: arrayWhereClause(interfaces), unmetInterfaceName: String.from(unmetInterfaceName), bindingName: String.from(bindingName), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'clause'
	end 'requireArrayConformanceAvailable'

	// `( transform )` — `map`'s single positional argument, a FUNCTION VALUE over the container's element.
	// Parsed as an ordinary expression, so every producer of a function value reaches it: an inline closure
	// literal, a bare reference to a named function, a function-typed parameter or field.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ALSO THE ONE POSITION THAT CAN TYPE AN UNTYPED CLOSURE PARAMETER** — `m.map(function(p)
	// gives p)`, which `specs-shv2/interface-extensions.md` writes. The transform's parameter IS the
	// container's element by the declaration `map` is read from (`function(Element) returns Element`), so
	// there is nothing to infer: the type is already in hand one line above the parse that needs it, and
	// offering it is a hand-off rather than an inference pass. See `ClosureParamTypeHint`.
	//
	// ⚠ The hint is restored on the SUCCESS path only. A `ParseError` out of the argument aborts the file's
	// parse, so a hint left set can reach no later closure literal — the same reason `parseClosureExpression`
	// can leave `iterationLockedBindings` unbalanced on its own error path.
	function parseElementTransformArg(func IrFunction, elementType MaxonType) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let argTok = self.current()
		let savedHint = self.closureParamHint
		self.closureParamHint = ClosureParamTypeHint.fromContainerElement(elementType)
		let transform = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		self.closureParamHint = savedHint
		try self.requireElementTransform(transform, elementType: elementType, argTok: argTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		return transform
	end 'parseElementTransformArg'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME OFFER, FOR A CONTAINER WHOSE `map` COMES FROM `extension Iterable` RATHER THAN FROM THE
	// BUILTIN ROSTER (W41).** `stdlib/Map.maxon` is listed and the synthesized `Map` is retired, so `Map` is an
	// ordinary user generic: `m.map(f)` no longer reaches `parseContainerMap` at all, it reaches
	// `dispatchMethodOnBase` like any other struct method. The inference was keyed to the roster, so it went
	// silent on the very construct `specs-shv2/interface-extensions.md` writes — the refusal named `map` while
	// refusing the one `map` a corpus container has.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE OFFER IS THE ELEMENT OF THE ARRAY `map` IS DECLARED TO RETURN, and that is the SAME question the
	// builtin path asks** (`parseContainerMap` hands `containerElementType(resultGiid)`). It is sound because
	// `map` is element-type-PRESERVING — `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:199` declares
	// `map(transform ElementTransform) returns ElementArray` over one `Element`, which `requireElementTransform`
	// states as the rule for the builtin twin. So "what element does this `map` produce" and "what element does
	// its transform take" are one fact, and reading the DECLARATION is what keeps them one: the answer is the
	// extension's own published signature, re-parsed per conformer with `Element` already substituted, not a
	// re-derivation of the element from the `implements` clause.
	//
	// ⛔ **`StructLayout.bindingsForInterface("Iterable")` WAS THE OBVIOUS KEY AND IS THE WRONG ONE.** It answers
	// only when the type names that interface LAST (its own header says so), so `implements Iterable with (…),
	// Printable` would silently offer nothing — and it would be a SECOND derivation of the container's element,
	// disagreeing with the declaration whenever a conformance and a signature drift apart. Reading the callee's
	// own declared return cannot drift from the callee.
	//
	// ⚠ **GATED ON THE METHOD BEING THE EXTENSION'S, because a conformer that declares its OWN `map` may declare
	// anything.** `methodPublishedByExtension` is what says the signature above is the one being called; without
	// it, a `map(x Integer) returns IntArray` would have its return's element offered to a closure its own
	// parameter list never asked for.
	//
	// Every gate FAILS SAFE — an unrecognized shape returns `none`, which is the existing positioned E2015
	// naming the one construct that does infer, not a wrong type.
	function extensionElementTransformHint(receiver ValueId, methodName ByteArray, callee ByteArray) returns ClosureParamTypeHint
		if not methodName.equals(MapMethod) 'notTheTransformPosition'
			return ClosureParamTypeHint.none
		end 'notTheTransformPosition'

		if not self.signatures.methodPublishedByExtension(callee) 'theConformersOwnMap'
			return ClosureParamTypeHint.none
		end 'theConformersOwnMap'

		match self.signatures.returnTypeOf(callee, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'declaredReturn'
			declared(returnType) then return self.mappedArrayElementHint(receiver, returnType: returnType)
			undeclared then break 'declaredReturn'
		end 'declaredReturn'

		return ClosureParamTypeHint.none
	end 'extensionElementTransformHint'

	// The `value`/`void` split of the declared return, one shape over from `retypeOpaqueReturn`'s and for its
	// reason — a match arm holds one statement, so the union is unwrapped a level at a time.
	function mappedArrayElementHint(receiver ValueId, returnType MaxonReturnType) returns ClosureParamTypeHint
		match returnType 'returned'
			value(declaredArray) then return self.mappedArrayElementOffer(receiver, declaredArray: declaredArray)
			void then break 'returned'
		end 'returned'

		return ClosureParamTypeHint.none
	end 'mappedArrayElementHint'

	// ⚠ **FIXED BY THE RECEIVER'S INSTANCE FIRST.** `Iterable.map` is re-parsed once per CONFORMER, not once per
	// INSTANCE, so `Map.map`'s declared `ElementArray` is `Array with Map.Entry` in the DECLARATION view — its
	// element cites `Map`'s own type parameters. Offered unfixed, a closure would be typed against an opaque
	// tuple its body could not read a field of; `slotTypeThroughReceiver` is the door that turns it into the
	// `Array with (String, int)` this receiver actually holds, and it is the same door the field read and the
	// method return already cross.
	function mappedArrayElementOffer(receiver ValueId, declaredArray MaxonType) returns ClosureParamTypeHint
		let fixed = self.slotTypeThroughReceiver(receiver, declared: self.calleeScopedContainerReturn(declaredArray))
		if maxonTypeTag(fixed) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAContainerReturn'
			return ClosureParamTypeHint.none
		end 'notAContainerReturn'

		return ClosureParamTypeHint.fromContainerElement(self.signatures.adoptType(self.signatures.containerElementType(typeNameIdOf(fixed)), into: self.artifact.typeNames))
	end 'mappedArrayElementOffer'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SWEPT RETURN TYPE IS A BARE NAME, AND `ElementArray` IS AN INNER GENERIC ALIAS OF THE
	// CONFORMER** — `promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope`'s question asked of a TYPE instead of a minted
	// VALUE, and of the one shape that door does not reach.
	//
	// `returnTypeOf` reads the token SWEEP's table, which records every written type as the bare name the source
	// spelled (`parseTypeReference`'s `allFilesFolded` gate argues why it must). So `Iterable.map`'s
	// `returns ElementArray`, folded onto conformer `Map`, arrives here as `named("ElementArray")` — and the
	// registry that says what it MEANS keys it under the conformer, `Map.ElementArray`, because
	// `foldExtensionDeclarationInto` files a nested `typealias ElementArray = Array with Element` through
	// `qualifiedInnerGenericAlias`.
	//
	// ⚠ **`scopedGenericInstanceOf` CANNOT ANSWER FOR IT, which is why this is not simply that door reused.**
	// That one re-scopes a bare generic BASE (`Inner` ⇒ `Inner with (Map.Key, Map.Value)`) and gates on
	// `structTypeParamsOf`, which answers the shared empty for a name no `type` declares — and `ElementArray` is
	// an alias, not a type. This is `parseTypeReference`'s inner-alias GENERIC arm, reached from a call site
	// whose cursor is nowhere near the declaration that gives the name its meaning.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RECORDED NAME IS ALREADY THE REGISTRY KEY — it needs no re-qualifying, and MEASURED, re-qualifying
	// it is what made this door answer nothing.** The sweep files an extension's nested alias under the
	// CONFORMER (`qualifiedInnerGenericAlias`) and records the return type under that same qualified spelling, so
	// what arrives here is `Map.ElementArray`, not `ElementArray`. Joining the declaring type onto it a second
	// time asked the registry about `Map.Map.ElementArray`, which nothing declares, and every corpus `map` fell
	// through to the E2015 this function exists to retire.
	//
	// Anything else passes through untouched: a conformer that spells its return concretely
	// (`returns IntArray`) is already what it needs to be, and a name in no generic-alias registry answers
	// `none` two lines on rather than guessing.
	function calleeScopedContainerReturn(declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notASweptBareName'
			return declared
		end 'notASweptBareName'

		// ⚠ `allFilesFolded` for the reason `scopedGenericInstanceOf` states: the alias registry is one the
		// declaration sweep is still filling, so an ungated answer would depend on the loader's walk order.
		let name = self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(declared))
		if not self.signatures.allFilesFolded or not self.signatures.isGenericAlias(name) 'notAGenericAliasReturn'
			return declared
		end 'notAGenericAliasReturn'

		return MaxonType.genericInstance(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath))
	end 'calleeScopedContainerReturn'

	// ⭐ **`map`'s TRANSFORM MUST BE A FUNCTION, AND IT MUST ANSWER THE CONTAINER'S OWN ELEMENT TYPE.** Both
	// halves are the reference declaration read literally (`function(Element) returns Element`): shv2's `map`
	// is element-type-PRESERVING, so the result array is the SAME instance the receiver's element names and
	// there is no second element type for the parser to infer, for `emitArrayCreateOp` to stamp, or for the
	// runtime's word-shaped `callIndirect` to carry.
	//
	// The comparison is `elementTypePreserved` — nominal for an element that has an identity, and the
	// container-slot rule (already written for a `Set`'s key and a `Map`'s two columns) for one that has not —
	// and not `==`, for the reason `columnValueTagMatches`'s header measured: a ranged-int alias carries the
	// `named` tag where the element carries `integer`, and refusing that pair would reject
	// `[1,2,3].map(function(x Integer) gives x * 2)`, which is the construct itself.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND ITS PARAMETER MUST BE THE ELEMENT — ONE OF THEM (E3122) AND OF THAT TYPE (E3005) — RECORDED
	// HERE, DECIDED WHOLE-PROGRAM.** The third half of the same declaration, and the one the parser cannot
	// answer: a function value's parameter list lives in `funcSignatures`/`functionAliasShapes`, which are not
	// assembled until merge, and a lifted closure's is file-local until then (`closureReturns` records only
	// its RETURN). So this records the value's governing NAME and the ELEMENT to judge it against, and
	// `SemanticCheck.checkTransformArities` compares. See PendingTransformArity — including why it is not a
	// sixth `PendingFunctionTypeDoor` arm.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE TYPE HALF WAS A REACHABLE SEGFAULT, NOT AN ACCEPTED LOOSENESS.** `nums.map(function(a String)
	// gives a.count())` over an int array compiled clean and died SIGSEGV (exit 139): the walk hands the
	// transform the element regardless of what it declares, so the integer `1` was dereferenced as a `String`
	// record pointer. The milder `gives 1` spelling compiled and printed a plausible answer — the contract was
	// already broken there, the crash only arrives when the body USES the parameter.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS RECORDED FOR THE VALUE, NOT FOR A CLOSURE LITERAL, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.**
	// `nums.map(twoArg)` has no closure in it — the arity belongs to a function that may be declared in
	// another file — so a rule keyed on the closure parse could not see the shape at all. This header once
	// said the arity "is not checked, and nothing in shv2 checks one"; that was true and it let a program
	// through that READS AN ARGUMENT NOBODY PASSED. The walk calls the transform with one element plus the
	// uniform `__env` slot, so a second parameter is whatever that slot held: MEASURED at `sum=0` on
	// arm64-macOS, and a `wasm32-wasi` trap ("indirect call type mismatch") because `call_indirect`
	// type-checks the signature the other three targets merely assume. Refusing it DIVERGES from the
	// reference bootstrap, which accepts the shape — user ruling 2026-08-04, on the ground that agreeing
	// about an undefined program is not agreement.
	function requireElementTransform(transform ValueId, elementType MaxonType, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		let declared = maxonTypeTag(elementType)
		if self.tagOf(transform) != ValueTypeTag.function 'notAFunction'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(MapMethod)}` takes a transform FUNCTION — `function({typeTagName(declared)}) returns {typeTagName(declared)}` over the container's element — and got {articledTypeTagName(self.tagOf(transform))} value", line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
		end 'notAFunction'

		let returned = try self.elementTransformReturnType(transform, argTok: argTok)
		if not self.elementTransformAnswersElement(returned, elementType: elementType) 'wrongElementType'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(MapMethod)}`'s transform answers {articledTypeTagName(maxonTypeTag(returned))} value where the container's element is {articledTypeTagName(declared)} — shv2's `{String.from(MapMethod)}` is element-type-PRESERVING (`stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:187` declares it `function(Element) returns Element`), so a transform to another type is a later slice", line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
		end 'wrongElementType'

		self.artifact.recordTransformArity(PendingTransformArity.create(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(transform)), expectedArity: ElementTransformArity, elementTag: declared, elementIdentity: self.resolvedElementIdentity(elementType), methodName: MapMethod, line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column, filePath: self.filePath))
	end 'requireElementTransform'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE TRANSFORM ANSWER THE CONTAINER'S OWN ELEMENT TYPE — the RETURN half of the
	// element-PRESERVATION test.** The verdict is `elementTypePreserved`, shared with the PARAMETER half; what
	// is local here is only the identity EXTRACTION, and both ids are this FILE's, which is what makes the
	// names comparable (`parseContainerMap` adopts the element into the file interner, and every answer
	// `functionValueReturnType` gives is adopted there too).
	//
	// ⚠ **AN AGGREGATE ELEMENT IS SETTLED BY THE NAME, AND `columnValueTagMatches` CANNOT ANSWER FOR ONE AT
	// ALL.** It is the HASH-TABLE COLUMN rule, and its own header records that its last arm is the INT arm and
	// that `declared` is not confined to the admitted set — so a `structRef` element falls straight through it
	// to `tagIsIntegral` and reads FALSE. MEASURED on `<map>.map(function(p) gives p)`, whose element is the
	// entry pair: *"`map`'s transform answers a struct value where the container's element is a struct"*, a
	// sentence that refutes itself. Widening THAT predicate is still the wrong door — the identity belongs on
	// this side of it, which is why the hash-table column doors ask `elementTypePreserved` too now that a
	// `Map`'s VALUE column may carry one (the one-word slot gate, now `requireListElementType`).
	function elementTransformAnswersElement(returned MaxonType, elementType MaxonType) returns bool
		return elementTypePreserved(maxonTypeTag(elementType), elementIdentity: self.declaredElementIdentity(elementType), otherTag: maxonTypeTag(returned), otherIdentity: self.declaredElementIdentity(returned))
	end 'elementTransformAnswersElement'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NOMINAL IDENTITY A PARSE-TIER ELEMENT COMPARISON USES — `aggregateNameOf`'s answer, and
	// EMPTY exactly where the comparison is the structural one.**
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE GATE USED TO BE `tagMayNameAggregate` ALONE, AND THAT WAS A MEASURED FALSE REFUSAL (A5i).**
	// That predicate answers `structRef or named` — and a PLAIN RANGED ALIAS is `named` too until
	// TypeResolution collapses it (`columnValueTagMatches`'s header carries the measurement). So an element
	// declared `Array with Integer` / `Set with Integer` took the NOMINAL branch, compared the interned name
	// `Integer` against the transform's `int`, missed, and was refused *"`map`'s transform answers an int
	// value where the container's element is an int"* — a sentence that refutes itself, on a legal program.
	// The discriminator was never the CONTAINER (both are refused identically): it is how the element
	// ARRIVES, literal (`integer`, structural, accepted) versus declared (`named`, nominal, refused).
	//
	// `aggregateNameOf` already draws exactly the line that was wanted — `containsStruct` / `containsEnum` /
	// `isPerInstanceAlias` name an aggregate, a plain ranged alias does not — so the cure is to ask the
	// question the whole parser already asks, rather than to widen `tagMayNameAggregate`, which has nine
	// callers including the MAP LITERAL's key and value inference (`literalColumnTypeFor`).
	//
	// ⚠ The `tagMayNameAggregate` gate is KEPT ahead of it, and that is deliberate rather than redundant:
	// `aggregateNameOf` also names an `interfaceRef` and a `genericInstance`, which this door has always
	// refused as elements and which admitting would open a lowering path nothing in the corpus exercises. A
	// widening there is its own piece of work with its own cases, not a side effect of this one.
	function declaredElementIdentity(t MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		if not self.signatures.tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'noNominalIdentityHere'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'noNominalIdentityHere'

		return self.aggregateNameOf(tag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(t))
	end 'declaredElementIdentity'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ELEMENT IDENTITY THE WHOLE-PROGRAM PARAMETER HALF COMPARES — a STRUCT's or a boxed
	// UNION/ENUM's name, and nothing else.**
	//
	// The parameter half compares this element against a transform's RESOLVED parameter type, one tier
	// further on than `declaredElementIdentity` reads, and the two aggregate kinds get there by two routes: a
	// STRUCT keeps its `structRef(id)` through resolution and is read live off the parameter, while a boxed
	// union/enum ERASES to bare `integer` and is recovered from the carrier column
	// `FunctionShape.paramAggregateNames`. `SemanticCheck.checkTransformParamType` asks for both in that
	// order, exactly as `checkOneArgType` does with its `preErasedAggregate`, so ONE name here answers for
	// either kind.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE UNION/ENUM HALF WAS EXCLUDED HERE AND THAT WAS A REACHABLE CRASH (BATCH18 review).** The
	// argument for excluding it was that a `FunctionShape` carries no pre-erasure column — true when written,
	// and CURED rather than worked around, because the alternative it chose was to compare a union/enum
	// element STRUCTURALLY, and an erased parameter and a `named` element are both integral: EVERY enum
	// matched EVERY other. MEASURED on `<Array with Color>.map(function(c Shade) gives Color.red)` with
	// `Color` 3-case and `Shade` 2-case — compiles clean, `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW` at run, an ordinal matched
	// against a `match` that has no arm for it — and again through a named function and through a function
	// ALIAS (`ACCESS_VIOLATION`). It is the SIGSEGV this door exists to close, wearing the other aggregate.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE TEST IS "WHAT DOES THE NAME DENOTE?", NOT "IS THE TAG `structRef`?", AND THE NARROW SPELLING
	// WAS A MEASURED FALSE REFUSAL.** A `typealias PointArray = Array with Point` hands this door a
	// `named("Point")` — the SWEEP that recorded the type argument ran before anything could say what the
	// name denoted — so the tag test read it as identity-less, fell to the structural branch and refused
	// `a.map(function(p Point) gives p)` as *"the container's own element, which is 'int'"* on a program that
	// compiles. It is `declaredElementIdentity`'s trap one level down: `named` is what a declared thing
	// arrives as, and only the NAME says which kind of thing it is.
	//
	// ⚠ NARROWER than `declaredElementIdentity` still, and deliberately: an `interfaceRef`, a
	// `genericInstance` and a per-instance alias all have a nominal identity at the PARSE tier and no carrier
	// that survives to the drain, so each stays structural rather than being compared against a parameter
	// that can no longer name itself. Widening any of them is its own piece of work — a carrier first, then
	// the arm.
	function resolvedElementIdentity(elementType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		let name = self.declaredElementIdentity(elementType)
		if not self.signatures.containsStruct(name) and not self.signatures.containsEnum(name) 'erasesOrHasNoIdentity'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'erasesOrHasNoIdentity'

		return name
	end 'resolvedElementIdentity'

	// The value type `transform` answers. A VOID function value is refused HERE rather than reaching the tag
	// comparison, because "returns nothing" is a different complaint from "returns the wrong thing" and the
	// container has no element for the void case to be compared against.
	function elementTransformReturnType(transform ValueId, argTok Token) returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		match self.functionValueReturnType(transform) 'returned'
			value(returnType) then return returnType
			void then break 'returned'
		end 'returned'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(MapMethod)}`'s transform returns NOTHING — every element must be transformed INTO a value the new array can hold", line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
	end 'elementTransformReturnType'

	// ⭐ **A `Character`'S BUILTIN METHODS (P1.8 slice B)** — the `dispatchStringMethod` twin, and a
	// separate dispatcher for the reason the two types are separate: a Character has no `append`, no
	// `split` and no case conversion, and a String has no `asciiValue`. Sharing one dispatcher would make
	// each type's method set the union of both, which is precisely the type-safety the distinct tag buys.
	//
	// The cursor sits on the method NAME (`dispatchMethodOnReceiver` consumed the `.`).
	//
	// It takes no `resultUsed`, unlike its String twin: every Character method RETURNS a value, so there is
	// no void result for a value-position call to misuse (`append` is the String method that needs it).
	function dispatchCharacterMethod(recv MethodReceiver) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.between(recv.anchor, last: methodTok)

		// The roster gate, `dispatchStringMethod`'s twin — see there for why it is asked ahead of every arm.
		let roster = try self.requireSurfaceMember(MemberSurface.character, typeName: CharacterConformerName, methodTok: methodTok)

		if methodTok.value.equals(ByteViewMethod) 'bytes'
			return try self.parseByteView(recv.value, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'bytes'
		// `byteLength()` is `length@8` — the UTF-8 width of the whole cluster, which for an EGC is the sum
		// of its codepoints' widths (a family emoji is 18). The same field load a String's `byteLength()`
		// is, because it is the same field of the same record.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ByteLengthMethod) 'byteLength'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitLoadStringField(recv.value, offset: StringLengthOffset, range: range)
		end 'byteLength'
		// ⭐ **`asciiValue()` — THE ONE THROWING `Character` MEMBER**, and the one arm here whose call is a
		// `tryCall` rather than a plain one. Nothing about that is decided at this site: the arm emits an
		// ordinary call and `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` — which `GraphemeRuntime.isThrowingCharacterRuntimeCallee`
		// now joins — is what makes the parser's E3055 ACCEPT a `try` on it and SemanticCheck's E3057 REJECT a
		// bare one, off the one predicate so the accept and the reject cannot name different sets.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT WAS ABSENT UNTIL THE `if try …` FORM EXISTED, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS NEVER ABOUT THIS TYPE.**
		// The two corpus cases spell the call `if try c.asciiValue()` — the SUCCESS-TEST form of `try` in a
		// condition — and shv2 had that form for NO throwing call; the gap was in the `try` machinery, one
		// layer over. With the form built, the member is vocabulary WITH its consumer, which is the bar this
		// dispatcher's siblings are held to. A non-throwing variant was never on the table: it would have to
		// answer a non-ASCII cluster with a sentinel.
		if methodTok.value.equals(CharacterAsciiValueMethod) 'asciiValue'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(CharAsciiValueName, args: singletonArgs(recv.value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'asciiValue'
		let drift = rosterDriftMessage("dispatchCharacterMethod", member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, rosterFuncs: "`characterSurfaceMemberNames`")
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'dispatchCharacterMethod'

	// ⭐ **THE NINE `__ManagedFile` STATICS — the surface BOTH references declare, complete at R4.2.** A name
	// outside it is REFUSED BY NAME here rather than left to fall through: falling through would mangle
	// `__ManagedFile.seek` into a callee no file declares and surface as E3004 against a name the author did
	// write — the same "reject positioned, do not mangle" argument `requireCompilerOwnedStaticIsResolvable`
	// makes above (the retired `parseCharacterSetStaticCall` made it first, and its door is the corpus's now).
	// ⚠ That argument turns on `__ManagedFile` having NO `type` declaration; where one exists, E3004 off the
	// mangled callee IS the right answer and the interception is the wrong one (W55, `String`).
	//
	// ⚠ **OWNERSHIP DIFFERS PER STATIC AND IS NOT A DETAIL.** The three OPENS return a `__mm_alloc`'d box,
	// so they go through `emitOwnedRuntimeResult` and are tracked as owned temporaries — whose drop is
	// `__mf_destruct`, the RAII close (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`). `stat` returns a RAW pointer
	// the caller must hand back to `statFree`, so it is deliberately NOT tracked: nothing in the ownership
	// model can free a bare `int`, and the exit-101 leak gate is what reports a caller that forgets.
	//
	// ⚠ Throwing-ness is NOT decided here. `isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee` names the callee and the
	// composed `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` is read by the parser's E3055 accept and SemanticCheck's E3057
	// reject, so a `try` is required at exactly the places those two agree on.
	function parseManagedFileStaticCall(func IrFunction, typeTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.between(typeTok, last: methodTok)

		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileOpenReadMethod) 'openRead'
			return try self.parseManagedFileOpen(func, callee: ManagedFileOpenReadName, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'openRead'
		// ⭐ **TWO METHODS, ONE CALLEE.** `openWriteExecutable`'s only difference in either reference is a Unix
		// 0755 permission bit, and shv2's file IO is host-only Windows by the R4.1 ruling — so there is
		// nothing for a second entry point to carry. A POSIX lane splits them on the day it exists.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileOpenWriteMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileOpenWriteExecutableMethod) 'openWrite'
			return try self.parseManagedFileOpen(func, callee: ManagedFileOpenWriteName, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'openWrite'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileExistsMethod) 'exists'
			let path = try self.parseOneManagedMemoryArgList(func, paramName: ManagedFilePathParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFileExistsName, args: singletonArgs(path), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'exists'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileDeleteMethod) 'delete'
			let path = try self.parseOneManagedMemoryArgList(func, paramName: ManagedFilePathParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFileDeleteName, args: singletonArgs(path), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'delete'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileRenameMethod) 'rename'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
			let oldPath = try self.parseManagedMemoryArg(func, paramName: ManagedFileOldPathParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
