	end 'parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **`parseStringStaticCall` WAS HERE, AND THE LAST TWO SYNTHESIZED `String` MEMBERS WENT WITH IT
	// (W55).** It served `String.from(bytes)` (P1.8 Slice E) and `String.init(managed)` (R4.2), both by
	// emitting `__str_from_bytes`. `stdlib/String.maxon:114,119-121` declares both, W17 listed that module,
	// and the corpus bodies reach the SAME runtime graph through `emitFusedWrapperLiteral` — a
	// `String{managed: …, singleByteGraphemesFlag: …}` literal lowers to `__str_of_buffer`, which is
	// `__str_from_bytes` with the classification supplied rather than scanned. So the two arms were emitting
	// by hand what the declaration they were standing in for already compiles to.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE BLOCKER THIS DOOR RECORDED WAS STALE, AND IT WAS RE-MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS REMOVED.** The arm
	// said *"IT COPIES, WHERE THE REFERENCE'S `init` ADOPTS … Adopting would double-free
	// `managed-file.write-and-read`'s `var buffer`"*. That is a statement about the REFERENCE's `String{…}`,
	// not about what shv2 emits for one: `emitFusedWrapperLiteral` COPIES too, so retiring the arm changes
	// no ownership at all. MEASURED on this tree — `managed-file.write-and-read` passes, and 200 iterations
	// of `String.init(m)` + `String.from(a)` in a loop exit 0 with no leak, where a double free or a stranded
	// buffer is exit 101.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT DID CHANGE IS THE ARGUMENT RULE, AND IT CHANGED BY GAINING ONE RATHER THAN LOSING ONE.**
	// `requireByteArrayValue` asked the STRIDE (*is this a 1-byte-element array?*); a declared parameter asks
	// `ProgramSignatures.aggregatesConflict`, which is NOMINAL over the container — deliberately, because a
	// parameter hands the container over and the callee may push into it. The two disagree on exactly one
	// shape: an array whose byte element the author named something other than `Byte`. See
	// `specs-shv2/string-views.md`'s `a-user-ranged-alias-element-keeps-its-name` for the program, which
	// proves its element survives through the buffer surface now rather than through this door.

	// ⭐ **`__ManagedMemory.create(count, elementSize)` — THE ONE `__ManagedMemory` STATIC.** It yields an OWNED
	// `Array` record, so its scope-exit drop routes through `__managed_decref` (which frees the record AND its
	// element buffer) exactly as a `.create()`d array's does, and the value is MARKED as the buffer surface
	// (`markBufferSurface`) so its members are reachable through it — the one thing that makes it more than an
	// array to the code that follows.
	//
	// ⚠ **`elementSize` IS BOTH A RUNTIME ARGUMENT AND A COMPILE-TIME DECISION, AND IT HAS TO BE BOTH.** The
	// runtime stores it into `element_size@24` (the stride every accessor moves by); the FRONT END additionally
	// picks the result's `Array` instance from it (`managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize`), because `get`/`set`
	// are typed off the element and nothing later can recover a type from a register.
	function parseManagedMemoryStaticCall(func IrFunction, typeTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.between(typeTok, last: methodTok)

		// ⚠ **ONE STATIC, so the name and its two parameter labels are rendered from the very constants this
		// call is parsed with, and not a one-element roster (A2q)** — `dispatchCharacterSetMethod`'s reason,
		// with a second name's worth of drift on top: the `(count, elementSize)` in the sentence and the
		// `parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList` below must agree about what the argument labels ARE, and now do so
		// by construction.
		if not methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryCreateMethod) 'unknownStatic'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}` static '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — R4.4 provides `{String.from(ManagedMemoryCreateMethod)}({String.from(ManagedMemoryCountParamName)}, {String.from(ManagedMemoryElementSizeParamName)})`; `fromCString` and `createCursor` are not built: each needs a `cstring` / cursor type shv2 has no producer for, so no spec can reach one", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'unknownStatic'

		let elementSizeStart = self.pos
		let args = try self.parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, firstParam: ManagedMemoryCountParamName, secondParam: ManagedMemoryElementSizeParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
		let elementSize = try args.get(1) otherwise panic("parseManagedMemoryStaticCall: the two-int arg list returned fewer than two values")
		let created = self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(ManagedMemCreateName, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: try self.requireManagedMemoryElementSize(elementSize, argStart: elementSizeStart), range: range)
		self.markBufferSurface(created)
		return created
	end 'parseManagedMemoryStaticCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `Array` INSTANCE A `create`'s `elementSize` NAMES — AND WHY THE ARGUMENT MUST BE A COMPILE-TIME
	// CONSTANT.** `mm.get(0)` and `mm.set(0, value: 300)` are typed off the instance's ELEMENT, so the front end
	// has to know the element size at the call; a size that only exists in a register would leave `get` with no
	// type to give back and `set` with no range to check `300` against.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS FOLLOWS THE BOOTSTRAP AND DIVERGES FROM v1, DELIBERATELY.** The bootstrap enforces exactly this
	// (`ParseElementSizeConstant`, `2-Parser.cs:10640-10650`, called at `:11836-11859`); v1 passes `elementSize`
	// through as a real runtime SSA value (`stdlib/Internals.maxon:3907-3918`) because its `__ManagedMemory` is a
	// generic with an `Element` parameter the substitution pass binds — machinery shv2 does not have and does not
	// need, since every call site in `/specs` and in `stdlib/` writes a literal.
	// ⚠ And it REFUSES cleanly where the bootstrap's own lowering CRASHES on a non-positive size
	// (`MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedMemory.cs:695-696`). A crash is not a diagnostic.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NON-POSITIVE SIZE IS NOT REFUSED HERE, and `managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize` states why**: the
	// corpus pins `create(4, 0)` as a CAUGHT `invalidAllocation` throw from the runtime, so a parse-time refusal
	// would turn a case about error handling into a compile error.
	//
	// The constant comes from the parser's own fold (`constIsKnown`), so `let W = 8` then `create(n, W)` is
	// admitted — a `let`'s use IS its literal in shv2 — while a parameter or a computed size is refused by name.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND A CONSTANT IS NOT ENOUGH: IT MUST BE A WIDTH shv2 HAS AN ELEMENT TYPE FOR (review, R4.4).**
	// Knowing the size at the call buys nothing if the instance chosen for it then STRIDES differently from
	// the type `get`/`set` are checked against — measured on `create(4, elementSize: 4)`, which took the
	// machine-word instance, accepted `set(0, value: 5000000000)` against `int`'s range and read back
	// 705032704. `managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable` owns that set; this is the door that reports it.
	function requireManagedMemoryElementSize(elementSize ValueId, argStart TokenIndex) returns GenericInstanceId throws ParseError
		if not self.constIsKnown(elementSize) 'notAConstant'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}.{String.from(ManagedMemoryCreateMethod)}`'s `{String.from(ManagedMemoryElementSizeParamName)}` must be a compile-time constant — it picks the buffer's ELEMENT TYPE, which `get`/`set` are typed from, and a size that exists only at run time leaves them with no type. Write the literal ({ByteStringElementSize} for bytes, {primitiveTypeByteSize(ValueTypeTag.integer)} for words).", line: self.tokenAt(argStart).line, column: self.tokenAt(argStart).column)
		end 'notAConstant'

		let size = self.constOf(elementSize)
		if not self.signatures.managedMemoryElementSizeIsTypeable(size) 'noElementTypeIsThatWide'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}.{String.from(ManagedMemoryCreateMethod)}`'s `{String.from(ManagedMemoryElementSizeParamName)}` is {size}, and shv2 has no element TYPE of that width — a buffer must be byte-packed ({ByteStringElementSize}) or machine-word ({primitiveTypeByteSize(ValueTypeTag.integer)}). Typing it as a word anyway would check `get`/`set` against a range the buffer's stride cannot hold, and store the value truncated.", line: self.tokenAt(argStart).line, column: self.tokenAt(argStart).column)
		end 'noElementTypeIsThatWide'

		let instance = self.signatures.managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize(size, readerFilePath: self.filePath)

		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE INSTANCE MUST STRIDE WHAT THE RECORD WILL BE STAMPED WITH (A4c).** `__managed_mem_create`
		// stamps `element_size@24` from this very ARGUMENT, so a `create` whose instance strides something
		// else is a record and a static type disagreeing about one buffer — the `b"…"` literal's incoherence
		// through the other producer, and it was accepted for a row's length while the literal was refused
		// (`requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte` carries the measurement).
		//
		// ⚠ **ONLY THE BYTE ARM CAN DISAGREE, WHICH IS WHY THE TEST IS NOT `size == arrayElementSize`.** The
		// word arm takes the bare-`int` instance, whose stride is the machine word by construction whatever
		// `Byte` was declared to be; and a NON-POSITIVE size never reaches a live record at all — the corpus
		// pins `create(4, 0)` as a caught runtime `invalidAllocation`, so which instance a doomed `create`
		// wore is unobservable and a `size == stride` test would turn that case into a compile error.
		if size == ByteStringElementSize 'aBytePackedBuffer'
			try self.requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte(instance, producer: BytePackedProducer.managedMemoryCreate, line: self.tokenAt(argStart).line, column: self.tokenAt(argStart).column)
		end 'aBytePackedBuffer'

		return instance
	end 'requireManagedMemoryElementSize'

	// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedMemory` PARAMETER RULE OF EVERY SIGNATURE-CHECKED BUILTIN (R4.2).**
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS `requireByteArrayValue` TWIN IS GONE (W55), AND THAT LEAVES ONE STRIDE RULE WHERE THERE WERE
	// TWO.** The twin existed for `String.from`'s argument alone and asked this same predicate with a
	// different sentence; retiring that static to `stdlib/String.maxon:119` sent its argument to
	// `SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType`, which asks `ProgramSignatures.aggregatesConflict` — NOMINAL over the
	// container, because a declared parameter hands the container over and the callee may push into it. A
	// BUILTIN parameter keeps the stride rule below because it has no declaration to be nominal about.
	// `builtin-type-checking.error-managed-file-open-read-int` pins its spelling.
	function requireManagedMemoryValue(value ValueId, paramName ByteArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if self.valueIsByteArray(value) 'isByteArray'
			return
		end 'isByteArray'

		throw ParseError.builtinArgumentType(String.from(paramName), expected: String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName), got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireManagedMemoryValue'

	// Is `value` a BYTE-PACKED buffer — the instance `__ManagedMemory` is bound to, the one a `b"…"` literal
	// has, the one `.toByteArray()` produces, and the compiler-owned one a String's byte VIEW wears? The
	// shared PREDICATE behind the two rules above: they test the same thing and differ only in what they SAY.
	// Any other `Array` instance would be read at ITS element stride, so a path handed to the OS (or a String
	// built from it) would be whichever bytes fell on the stride boundaries — a silent wrong answer, refused
	// at the argument.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STRIDE QUESTION ITSELF IS `ProgramSignatures.isBytePackedArrayInstance` AND IS NOT SPELLED HERE
	// (R4.7).** The DECLARED-function argument door asks the identical question one tier up
	// (`SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType`), and a builtin parameter and a declared one disagreeing about what a
	// byte buffer is IS the defect that rung exists to close. This adapter only puts a `ValueId`'s two type
	// columns in front of it.
	function valueIsByteArray(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.signatures.isBytePackedArrayInstance(self.tagOf(value), nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'valueIsByteArray'

	// How a value's type is SPELLED in a diagnostic — its tag, resolved through the DISPLAY name behind it.
	// EVERY parser diagnostic that names a VALUE's type reads it (the builtin-argument rules, the assignment
	// and return voices, the `otherwise` and match-arm merges); written out it was the same four-call chain
	// each time, and each copy was free to reach for the identity instead.
	// The spelling THIS VALUE's own site wrote, where R-1 left the type with more than one.
	//
	// Two ranged aliases over one range are one type, so `WideCol` and `OtherCol` share a gid and no per-gid
	// answer can say which the blamed statement wrote. The provenance column (P1.6-C's `valueInstanceAlias`,
	// stamped at the container door and at every call result) is what can, and this is the parser's half of
	// reading it — SemanticCheck's is `valueTypeDisplayName`, and the two must agree or one message would
	// quote the author while its neighbour quoted the compiler.
	//
	// ⚠ **ASKED ONLY WHERE THE gid CANNOT ANSWER**, so an instance one alias spells keeps the name every
	// pinned diagnostic already agrees on, and a program with no merge probes nothing. Where the site named
	// no alias the file-scoped answer still applies, and that is never a stranger's spelling.
	function valueDisplayTypeName(value ValueId) returns String
		let tag = self.tagOf(value)
		let nameId = self.valueNameId(value)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.signatures.instanceIsMultiplyAliased(nameId) 'moreThanOneAliasSpellsThisType'
			if let aliasId = try self.valueInstanceAlias.get(value) 'theSiteNamedOneOfThem'
				return String.from(self.internedName(aliasId))
			end 'theSiteNamedOneOfThem'
		end 'moreThanOneAliasSpellsThisType'

		return self.displayTypeNameOf(tag, nameId: nameId)
	end 'valueDisplayTypeName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DISPLAY TWIN OF `aggregateNameOf`, AND THE PARSER-TIER HALF OF ONE RULE (user ruling,
	// 2026-08-04): a diagnostic names a type the way the AUTHOR WROTE IT.** `aggregateNameOf` answers with the
	// IDENTITY — for a generic instance, the canonical `Array_String` two values are compared on — and an
	// identity is not a spelling: no source line holds `Array_String`, the author wrote
	// `typealias StringArray = Array with String`. Every other tag already answers with a written name (a
	// struct, an interface, a registered union/enum) or with the primitive the author sees (`int`, `String`),
	// so the instance arm is the whole difference and the rest routes through the existing chain.
	//
	// ⚠ The SemanticCheck twin is `typeDisplayName`; the two are twins for `aggregateNameOf`'s own reason —
	// different tier, different interner — and the instance name they both quote comes from the shared
	// whole-program index, so they cannot come to spell one type two ways. **What is twinned is the LOOKUP
	// and the instance arm, which are the two tier-bound halves; the TAIL is not, and is
	// `displayNameOfAggregate` — one home, because it takes a tag and a resolved name and neither is bound to
	// an interner.**
	function displayTypeNameOf(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns String
		// THIS FILE's spelling of the type, which for one R-1 merged is not the same as anyone else's — see
		// `ProgramSignatures.instanceDisplayNameIn`. Every diagnostic the parser raises names a type through
		// here, and the parser always knows which file it is reading.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'genericInstance'
			return String.from(self.signatures.instanceDisplayNameIn(nameId, readerFilePath: self.filePath))
		end 'genericInstance'

		// Its aggregate NAME when it has one (a declared struct/union), else the scalar TYPE it actually is —
		// an `int`, a `bool`, a `String`, or a ranged-int alias whose overloaded `named` tag is NOT an
		// aggregate.
		return displayNameOfAggregate(tag, aggregate: self.aggregateNameOf(tag, nameId: nameId))
	end 'displayTypeNameOf'

	// `CharSet from ['x', 'y', 'z']` — the SOURCE form of a `Set with Character`, and since W115 the ONLY
	// form: the eleven predefined sets used to be seeded here too, from byte sequences the compiler held as
	// data, and they are now `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`'s own `static let`s. The cursor sits on the `[`.
	// Each element is a `Character` by being written as a character literal (A5m-ab; it used to have to ADOPT
	// the type here, because a one-byte literal was an int).
	//
	// The create and the insert are `emitContainerLiteralCreate`/`emitContainerLiteralElement` under `Set`'s base
	// name directly — the corpus `Set`'s own doors,
	// which is what enrols the fresh set as a pending temporary (a `let s = CharSet from ['x']` that never
	// reaches an owner is still dropped at scope exit) and what makes the insert a VOID mutator the set owns
	// its member from. Until W115 each had a one-line `…CharacterMember…` wrapper, and the pairing those
	// wrappers bought — one spelling of the member set's INSTANCE across two producers — is bought by there
	// being one producer.
	function parseCharacterMemberSetLiteral(func IrFunction, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		let chars = try self.emitContainerLiteralCreate(SetBuiltinBaseName, giid: self.signatures.setInstanceForCharacter(), anchor: anchor, range: range)
		while not self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'eachMember'
			let memberTok = self.current()
			let member = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			if self.tagOf(member) != ValueTypeTag.character 'notACharacter'
				throw ParseError.builtinOperandType(String.from(CharacterMemberSetAliasName), required: typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.character), got: typeTagName(self.tagOf(member)), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column)
			end 'notACharacter'
			try self.emitContainerLiteralElement(SetBuiltinBaseName, method: InsertMethod, container: chars, value: member, anchor: anchor, range: range)
			if not self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'moreMembers'
				try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			end 'moreMembers'
		end 'eachMember'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBracket)
		return chars
	end 'parseCharacterMemberSetLiteral'

	// ⭐ **A `StringIndex`'S BUILTIN METHODS (P1.8 slice B2)** — `charIndex()` and `bytePos()`, the corpus's
	// own two accessors (`stdlib/String.maxon:925-931`). Its own dispatcher rather than the struct path, for
	// the ROSTER-WINS reason `s.byteLength()` keeps its arm: `dispatchMethodOnBase` would resolve both names
	// against `funcSignatures` and find the corpus's real bodies, so this is not about reachability — it is
	// that each would then be a CALL, and `bytePos()` is read twice per `slice`, once per `charAt`, once per
	// `indexAfter` and once per step of every corpus loop that walks an index.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT ARGUMENT REPLACED A LOAD-BEARING ONE AT W49 WAVE 3, AND THE OLD ONE IS WORTH KEEPING
	// VISIBLE.** It read *"no file declares `__StringIndex.charIndex`, so every call would be E3004 against
	// a method that is right there in the type"* — true while shv2 registered its OWN layout under a
	// reserved name no source could spell. The corpus's declaration is the only one now, so the fall-through
	// this guarded against is gone and what is left is a cost argument. **A dispatcher kept for a reason
	// that has expired is a dispatcher nobody re-justifies**; this one still earns its place, and now says
	// on what grounds.
	//
	// Both are FIELD READS through the layout's own door (`emitFieldLoad`), not hand-written offsets: the
	// layout the corpus's declaration registered is where a `StringIndex`'s shape is stated, and reading it
	// any other way would be a second copy of the offsets.
	//
	// The cursor sits on the method NAME (`dispatchMethodOnReceiver` consumed the `.`). It takes no
	// `resultUsed`: both methods return a value, so there is no void result for a value position to misuse.
	function dispatchStringIndexMethod(recv MethodReceiver) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.between(recv.anchor, last: methodTok)

		// The roster gate, `dispatchStringMethod`'s twin — see there for why it is asked ahead of every arm.
		let roster = try self.requireSurfaceMember(MemberSurface.stringIndex, typeName: StringIndexTypeName, methodTok: methodTok)

		if methodTok.value.equals(StringIndexCharIndexMethod) 'charIndex'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitStringIndexField(recv.value, index: StringIndexCharIdxFieldIndex, range: range)
		end 'charIndex'
		if methodTok.value.equals(StringIndexBytePosMethod) 'bytePos'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitStringIndexField(recv.value, index: StringIndexBytePosFieldIndex, range: range)
		end 'bytePos'

		let drift = rosterDriftMessage("dispatchStringIndexMethod", member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, rosterFuncs: "`stringIndexSurfaceMemberNames`")
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'dispatchStringIndexMethod'

	// One field of a `StringIndex` box, read through the layout the CORPUS's declaration registered — the
	// shared body of `charIndex()`, `bytePos()`, `slice`'s two byte-position reads and the `==` comparison.
	// Five readers, one derivation of where a field lives, and since W49 wave 3 that derivation runs off the
	// same `type StringIndex` a program can name.
	function emitStringIndexField(box ValueId, index FieldIndex, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		return self.emitFieldLoad(box, layout: self.signatures.stringIndexLayoutOrPanic(), index: index, range: range)
	end 'emitStringIndexField'

	// The `__ManagedFile` type's interned name (R4.1) — adopted into THIS FILE's interner, because that is
	// the one a value's `nameId` column is read against (`mintValue`), not the whole-program one.
	//
	// ⚠ It had a `stringIndexNameId` twin until W49 wave 3, and that one went with the members that minted
	// an index: `startIndex`/`endIndex`/`findFirst`/`findLast`/`indexAfter` are `stdlib/String.maxon`'s
	// now, so every `StringIndex` value in a program is a CALL RESULT whose name comes off the callee's
	// declared return type — nothing in this file mints one, and nothing needs the id.
	function managedFileNameId() returns TypeNameId
		return self.artifact.typeNames.intern(ManagedFileTypeName)
	end 'managedFileNameId'

	// The `__ManagedDirectory` type's interned name (R4.3) — `managedFileNameId`'s twin, against the
	// ARTIFACT's interner for its reason.
	function managedDirectoryNameId() returns TypeNameId
		return self.artifact.typeNames.intern(ManagedDirectoryTypeName)
	end 'managedDirectoryNameId'

	// The `__ManagedSocket` type's interned name — `managedDirectoryNameId`'s twin, against the same interner.
	function managedSocketNameId() returns TypeNameId
		return self.artifact.typeNames.intern(ManagedSocketTypeName)
	end 'managedSocketNameId'

	// Is this receiver a `__ManagedFile`? A TAG cannot answer it — every struct box is `structRef` — so it
	// is the interned NAME behind the tag, exactly as `isStringIndexType` asks.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ACCEPTS `named` AS WELL AS `structRef`, AND WITHOUT THAT HALF THE CORPUS'S ACTUAL SPELLING
	// DOES NOT DISPATCH (R4.2).** A struct FIELD read carries `named`, not `structRef` — `fieldTypeOf`
	// re-tags only function and generic-alias fields, which `decrefCalleeFor`'s own `named` arm records — and
	// the corpus reaches every instance method through a field: `specs/managed-file.md`'s documented usage
	// pattern is a `__ManagedFile` INSIDE a wrapper type, so it is `wf.file.write(…)`, never `f.write(…)`.
	// Left narrow, that receiver falls past this arm to the struct path and reports E3004 against a method
	// the language does have. (R4.1 shipped the narrow test because its one enabled case bound the box
	// DIRECTLY — the only spelling `structRef` alone serves.)
	//
	// ⚠ **IT READS THE NAME BEHIND THE ID; IT DOES NOT INTERN ONE.** `internedName(id).equals(name)` is a
	// dense-column index plus a byte compare — the form `isStringIndexType` and `isCharacterSetType` both
	// use. The `nameId == self.managedFileNameId()` spelling it replaces asked the same question by HASHING
	// `__ManagedFile` and probing the interner on EVERY method dispatch in the program, and on a miss it
	// INSERTED — so a file that merely calls a method on some struct acquired a `__ManagedFile` entry it has
	// no use for. R4.2 widening the tag gate to `named` put that on the enum/alias receivers too, one arm
	// above the enum test whose own comment explains that it gates on the tag FIRST precisely so the interner
	// is not touched for a receiver that is not its business. The mint site (`parseManagedFileOpen`) still
	// interns, which is where the id genuinely has to exist.
	function isManagedFileType(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return self.receiverIsNamedAggregate(tag, nameId: nameId, typeName: ManagedFileTypeName)
	end 'isManagedFileType'

	// Is this receiver a `__ManagedDirectory`? `isManagedFileType`'s twin, in every respect including WHY it
	// accepts `named` beside `structRef`: `specs/managed-directory.md`'s documented usage pattern reaches
	// every instance method through a struct FIELD (`wrapper.dir.next()`), and a field read carries `named`.
	// It reads the name behind the id rather than interning one, so a receiver that is not its business
	// costs a dense-column index and a byte compare.
	function isManagedDirectoryType(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return self.receiverIsNamedAggregate(tag, nameId: nameId, typeName: ManagedDirectoryTypeName)
	end 'isManagedDirectoryType'

	// Is this receiver a `__ManagedSocket`? `isManagedDirectoryType`'s twin, in every respect including WHY it
	// admits both aggregate tags.
	function isManagedSocketType(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return self.receiverIsNamedAggregate(tag, nameId: nameId, typeName: ManagedSocketTypeName)
	end 'isManagedSocketType'

	// ⭐ **"IS THIS RECEIVER THE AGGREGATE CALLED `typeName`?" — THE SHARED BODY OF THE THREE TYPE TESTS
	// ABOVE AND BELOW** (`__ManagedFile`, `__ManagedDirectory`, `StringIndex`). Each spelled the tag gate and
	// the name compare out for itself, which is three copies of one rule — and the rule is not obvious in
	// either half: the TAG gate must admit `named` as well as `structRef` (R4.2's measured defect, recorded
	// at `isManagedFileType`), and the NAME must be read behind the id rather than interned (which would
	// insert on a miss, giving every file that calls any struct method an entry it has no use for).
	// A fourth such type gets both facts by asking, rather than by copying whichever neighbour it read.
	//
	// ⚠ The tag half is `SignatureIndex.tagMayNameAggregate`, not a fourth spelling of it: that function's
	// own header records the FOUR ways this same test had come to be written, and adding a fifth here would
	// be the thing it was written to stop.
	function receiverIsNamedAggregate(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'notAnAggregateRef'
			return false
		end 'notAnAggregateRef'
		return self.internedName(nameId).equals(typeName)
	end 'receiverIsNamedAggregate'

	// ⭐ **THE FOUR `__ManagedFile` INSTANCE METHODS — `close`, `size`, `read`, `write` (R4.2 completes
	// R4.1's one).** The cursor sits on the method NAME (`dispatchMethodOnReceiver` consumed the `.`).
	//
	// `close()` is the one that landed a rung early, and the reason was a LEAK: an open with no close leaks
	// an OS handle for the process lifetime, and that leak is invisible to the exit-101 memory gate because a
	// handle is not memory. It stays NON-throwing and void, per the spec's "Does not throw"; the other three
	// throw `__ManagedFileError`.
	//
	// An unknown method is refused by name here rather than mangled into a callee no file declares —
	// `parseManagedFileStaticCall`'s argument, on the instance side.
	function dispatchManagedFileMethod(func IrFunction, recv MethodReceiver) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.between(recv.anchor, last: methodTok)

		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileCloseMethod) 'close'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFileCloseName, args: singletonArgs(recv.value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'close'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileSizeMethod) 'size'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFileSizeName, args: singletonArgs(recv.value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'size'
		// ⚠ The buffer is BORROWED, not consumed: `__mf_read` writes into it and `__mf_write` reads out of
		// it, and neither takes ownership — which is what lets the caller keep using `buffer` afterwards (and
		// lets a `"…".toByteArray().managed` temporary drop at the end of the statement, as it does today for
		// `openRead`'s path).
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileReadMethod) 'read'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
			let managed = try self.parseManagedMemoryArg(func, paramName: ManagedFileManagedParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			// ⭐ **THE THIRD RAW-BYTE FILL (A4a)** — `__mf_read` blits a file's contents into the caller's
			// buffer, so the buffer's element has to admit every value a byte has, exactly as the byte view's
			// and `setByte`'s do. `write` is NOT gated beside it: it READS the buffer out to the file and
			// stores nothing, which is the same reason `byteAt` is untouched.
			//
			// The instance is the value's own name id: `parseManagedMemoryArg` has just proven through
			// `requireManagedMemoryValue` that this value IS a byte-packed generic instance, so the tag is
			// established before the id is read as one (`isArrayInstanceAt`'s trap, one call earlier).
			try self.requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement(self.valueNameId(managed), methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			let size = try self.parseBuiltinIntegerArg(func, paramName: ManagedFileSizeParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFileReadName, args: tripleArgs(recv.value, second: managed, third: size), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'read'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedFileWriteMethod) 'write'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
			let managed = try self.parseManagedMemoryArg(func, paramName: ManagedFileManagedParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFileWriteName, args: pairArgs(recv.value, second: managed), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'write'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedFileTypeName)}` method '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — the type has exactly the four both references declare: `size()`, `read(managed, size)`, `write(managed)` and `close()`", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'dispatchManagedFileMethod'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS THE `StringIndex` TYPE?** A TAG cannot answer it — every struct box is `structRef` — so it
	// is the interned NAME behind the tag, which is the same identity `aggregateNameOf` publishes for a
	// reassign check. The tag gate is not merely a fast path: a non-aggregate tag carries `UnnamedTypeId` in
	// the name column, which no interner is obliged to hold.
	//
	// Its askers read the two DIFFERENT records a type can live in, which is why the shared body takes
	// the pair rather than either one: `dispatchMethodOnBinding` asks a BINDING's declared type (a
	// self-field alias holds no value, so its `boundValue` is the receiver's box and would answer for the
	// wrong type), and `emitCompare`/`slice` ask a VALUE's columns (a call result is bound to no name).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ADMITS `named` BESIDE `structRef` AS OF W49 WAVE 3, AND THE RETIREMENT IS WHAT MADE THAT
	// REACHABLE.** While the type was the compiler's own `__StringIndex`, no source could NAME it, so a
	// `StringIndex` could not be a parameter, a return type or a struct field and `structRef` was the only
	// tag one could ever arrive under. The corpus's declaration can be written, so `function byteOf(idx
	// StringIndex)` and `var pos as StringIndex` are both programs now — and a struct field read carries
	// `named`, which is R4.2's measured lesson one type over (`isManagedFileType`). Left narrow, those two
	// receivers fell past this arm to the struct path, which RESOLVES both accessors against the corpus and
	// answers correctly — so the cost of the omission was a silent CALL where every other spelling gets a
	// field load, not a wrong answer. Consistency is the reason it is widened, and it is stated because the
	// absence of a wrong answer is exactly what would have let it sit.
	function isStringIndexType(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns bool
		return self.receiverIsNamedAggregate(tag, nameId: nameId, typeName: StringIndexTypeName)
	end 'isStringIndexType'

	function valueIsStringIndex(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.isStringIndexType(self.tagOf(value), nameId: self.valueNameId(value))
	end 'valueIsStringIndex'

	// `( )` — the empty argument list of a no-argument BUILTIN call, whichever family it belongs to: a String
	// accessor (`byteLength`, `isEmpty`, `clone`), an array method (`count`, `first`, `pop`, `clear`, …), a
	// set method, and the two static factories (`IntArray.create()`, `IntSet.create()`). It had four separate
	// spellings; one home means a builtin's empty argument list is parsed the same way everywhere, and any
	// future relaxation (a newline inside the parens, say) reaches all of them at once.
	function consumeEmptyArgList() throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
	end 'consumeEmptyArgList'

	// ⚠⚠ **`parseStringAppend` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH `String.append` (W49 wave 8), AND IT WAS THE LAST
	// `String` CALLER OF `noteReceiverWrite`.** Its whole job was the receiver-write refusal — the corpus body
	// (`stdlib/String.maxon:414-419`) has always carried the append itself — so retiring the member meant
	// moving that refusal rather than deleting it: `receiverOwnerMask` derives the bit from the envelope
	// collapse and `SemanticCheck.checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam` raises the same E3019 at the same token.
	// `__str_append` and its usage bit went with it; the corpus reaches `__managed_append` through
	// `managed.append(other.managed)`, whose growth is `__managed_grown_cap`'s and so stays geometric.

	// ⚠ **`parseStringByteLength` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH `String.byteLength` (W49 wave 7).** It was two
	// lines — consume `()`, load `length@8` — and had exactly ONE caller, so retiring the member took the
	// helper whole. `dispatchCharacterMethod`'s own `byteLength` arm never called it (it writes the same two
	// lines inline over `type Character`'s record) and is untouched; `StringLengthOffset` and
	// `emitLoadStringField` keep that caller and five others.

	// --- Direct builtin-conformance dispatch (P1.7a): `i.compare(j)` / `i.hash()` / `i.equals(j)`,
	// and since BATCH2 slice 2 a `String` receiver's `s.hash()` / `s.equals(t)` through the same body ---

	// ⭐ **ONE BODY SERVES BOTH DISPATCH PATHS.** A concrete receiver calls `int.compare` DIRECTLY — the
	// very symbol a `__witness_int.Comparable` slot is stamped with (`ensureWitnessTable`'s funcAbs64
	// reloc), built by the very same `BuiltinConformanceRuntime.buildIntCompare`. There is no second
	// impl for the direct path and no thunk between them: the dictionary-passing form loads the pointer
	// out of `.rdata` and the concrete form names the symbol, and they land in the same function. That is
	// v1's arrangement (`BuildWitnessTables.maxon:57-143` fills a slot with a reloc to the qualified
	// `"{typeName}.{method}"`, which is also what its concrete dispatch calls) and shv2 already minted
	// that exact name, so the only thing this rung adds is the CALL.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTALLER MUST SEE THIS REFERENCE.** `installBuiltinConformanceRuntime` builds an impl iff
	// the program REFERENCES it, and a direct call creates no `.rdata` reloc — see that file's header for
	// how the reference is found. A dispatch emitted here that the installer cannot see is a link-time
	// panic (`resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function 'int.compare'`), never a silent wrong answer.

	// Resolve the method name against the conformer's INTRINSIC conformances and emit the call. The cursor
	// sits on the method NAME. A name no intrinsic conformance supplies is a clean positioned reject — never
	// a route to a `<type>.<method>` symbol nothing installs.
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE RESIDUE OF THE `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon` RETIREMENT — THE MEMBERS WHOSE ANSWER THE
	// COMPILER DECIDES FROM A COORDINATE OF THE RECEIVER THE CORPUS BODY CANNOT STATE (W188).** `ARR4`'s
	// framing, one surface over: **a roster is the RESIDUE of what could not retire**, so this list is a
	// finding rather than a policy, and every entry owes the measurement that put it there.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **`int.toString` IS ON IT BECAUSE THE CORPUS BODY IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER FOR AN UNSIGNED
	// RECEIVER — MEASURED, NOT ARGUED.** `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:21-23` is `return "{self}"`, and
	// `self` there is declared `int`, which in this language is the WHOLE of `i64` and therefore SIGNED
	// (`specs-shv2/x8`: no ranged type is not no constraint). shv2's rule is the opposite one —
	// *"signedness is a property of the value's DECLARED type, read once by the compiler and handed to the
	// renderer"* (`specs-shv2/string-interpolation.md`) — and the declared type at the CALL is the caller's
	// `int(0 to u64.max)`, a coordinate the corpus parameter erases. With the module listed and this roster
	// empty, `string-interpolation/unsigned-decimal-tostring` printed **`[-1]` where it pins
	// `[18446744073709551615]`**, and `1 shl 63` came back `[-9223372036854775808]`.
	// ⚠ **The ORACLE prints `[-1]` too** (measured, same program), so this is not shv2 catching up — it is
	// shv2's own deliberate divergence, and it lives in the renderer the corpus cannot reach.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS THE `Vector` SIZE STORY ONE AXIS OVER, WHICH IS WHY BOTH RETIREMENTS FILE A RESIDUE.** There,
	// a declaration's own `Self` states no COUNT while every value has one; here, a declaration's own `self`
	// states no RANGE while every receiver has one. A member whose answer needs the coordinate cannot retire
	// until the coordinate travels.
	//
	// ⚠ **`hash`, `equals`, `compare` AND `clone` ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT**: each is a pure function of the
	// receiver's BITS, which is exactly what the corpus body has, and each is measurably live off the
	// declaration (the differing-declarations control moves `42.hash()` to `142`). `float.toString` and
	// `bool.toString` are absent for the same reason — neither renderer reads a range.
	function primitiveMembersTheCompilerRetains(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
		if typeName.equals(IntConformerName) 'theRangedPrimitive'
			names.push(ToStringMethodName)
		end 'theRangedPrimitive'
		return names
	end 'primitiveMembersTheCompilerRetains'

	// ⚠ **A ROSTER ENTRY BINDS THE CORPUS'S DECLARATION AND NEVER A USER'S**, which is the whole of why the
	// provenance test is here and not left implied. `W133`'s ordering rule — *a declared body beats every arm
	// below, or the author's body never runs* — is unchanged for a user program: a file that writes its own
	// `extension int implements Stringable` takes the name off the corpus entirely
	// (`ProgramSignatures.noteExtensionPublishedMethod`), and this test then answers `false` for it, so the
	// call reaches that declaration exactly as it did before the module was listed.
	// (`extension-over-primitive/a-declared-tostring-beats-the-inline-lowering` is that case, and it is RED
	// without the second clause.)
	function compilerOutranksTheCorpusOn(typeName ByteArray, member ByteArray, callee ByteArray) returns bool
		if not namesInclude(self.primitiveMembersTheCompilerRetains(typeName), name: member) 'notOnTheResidueRoster'
			return false
		end 'notOnTheResidueRoster'

		return self.signatures.extensionMethodPublishedByStdlib(callee) and not self.signatures.stdlibExtensionMethodIsBeatenByUser(callee)
	end 'compilerOutranksTheCorpusOn'

	function dispatchBuiltinConformanceMethod(func IrFunction, recv MethodReceiver, conformer BuiltinConformer, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let typeName = builtinConformerName(conformer)

		// ⭐⭐⭐ **A DECLARED BODY BEATS EVERY ARM BELOW, AND THIS TEST HAS TO COME FIRST OR TWO OF THEM ARE A
		// SILENT WRONG ANSWER (W133).** Until this rung the parser could not PARSE an `extension int`, so every
		// arm below was the only surface a primitive had and the ordering question did not exist. It does now:
		// `extension int implements Stringable` declaring a `toString()` publishes a real `int.toString`
		// function, type-checked and linked — and the `toString` arm below LOWERS INLINE AND CALLS NO SYMBOL, so
		// left ahead of this test it would emit the compiler's own conversion and the author's body would never
		// run. Not a refusal, not a link failure: a program that compiles, links, runs and prints the wrong
		// thing. `clone` is the identical shape (`emitPrimitiveClone` hands back the receiver), which is why
		// `specs-shv2/extension-over-primitive.md` spends a differing-declarations case on each.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS A PEEK, BECAUSE THE DECLARED PATH MAY NOT CONSUME THE NAME** — `parseCallNamed` consumes it
		// itself, exactly as `dispatchMethodOnBase` peeks for the same reason. The cursor is on the method name
		// (the `.` is already consumed), which is the same position `methodCallCancelsAPromise` peeks from.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE SYNTHESIZED SURFACE IS NOT RETIRED BY THIS, AND MUST NOT BE:** a program that declares
		// nothing still reaches every arm below unchanged, which is what keeps `5.hash()`, `"{x}"` and
		// `x.clone()` working with no `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon` listed. This is the `structCloneIsSynthesized`
		// rule — *"wired PAST the `declaresCallee` test, so a user-written `clone` still wins"* — asked of a
		// primitive instead of a struct, and it is both references' rule too (v1 skips any struct that already
		// has a `"{structName}.clone"`, `Parser.maxon:19652`).
		//
		// ⚠ **`declaresCallee` IS THE WHOLE-PROGRAM SWEEP, NOT THIS FILE**, for `structCloneIsSynthesized`'s
		// reason: the `extension int` may be written in any file, and an answer that depended on which file the
		// call is in would dispatch one `n.hash()` to the declaration and another to the synthesized impl.
		let methodTok = self.current()
		let declaredCallee = qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: methodTok.value)
		if self.signatures.declaresCallee(declaredCallee) and not self.compilerOutranksTheCorpusOn(typeName, member: methodTok.value, callee: declaredCallee) 'theProgramDeclaresItsOwn'
			// `resolvedTypeQualifier`: the author wrote the MEMBER and a receiver, and no token at this call
			// site spells the type — `dispatchMethodOnBase`'s mint, for its reason. `ConstructorInstance.none`
			// because a primitive has no box for a constructor to have built.
			return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: declaredCallee, anchor: methodTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.value(recv.value, immutableName: recv.immutableName, subjectName: recv.subjectName, anchor: recv.anchor), resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier)
		end 'theProgramDeclaresItsOwn'

		// The peek above only READ the name; the arms below all consume it, and this is the one place they
		// share to do it — which is also where a member that is not a name at all still gets the E2010 it
		// always got.
		try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO LOWERED PROTOCOL METHODS COME NEXT, and neither reaches an impl.** Both reference
		// compilers give `int`/`float`/`bool` these through an `extension … implements …` in
		// `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon` whose whole body is ONE expression; shv2 has that mechanism as of
		// W133 but does not yet LIST that module (`IrInterface.isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` withholds
		// `Stringable`/`Cloneable` from the primitives on purpose — `:2243-2247`), so with nothing declared it
		// still emits that one expression here and skips the frame. That is why these arms sit AHEAD of the
		// conformance lookup rather than adding protocols to it, and BEHIND the declared-body test above. They
		// are named once, beside the refusal that advertises them, in
		// `IrInterface.builtinConformerLoweredMethodNames`, whose header carries why a lowering is kept out of
		// `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`.

		// `x.toString()` is `Stringable`'s method and lowers to exactly what `"{x}"` lowers to
		// (`specs-shv2/primitive-stringable.md`, whose Notes say "uses the same conversion as string
		// interpolation" and mean it literally; the reference's body is `return "{self}"`).
		if methodTok.value.equals(ToStringMethodName) 'stringableLowering'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return try self.emitValueToString(recv.value, exprTok: methodTok, range: SourceRange.between(recv.anchor, last: methodTok))
		end 'stringableLowering'

		// ⭐ **`x.clone()` IS `Cloneable`'S METHOD AND ITS LOWERING IS THE IDENTITY** — the reference's three
		// bodies are literally `return self` (`stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:25-27`, `:70-72`, `:98-100`),
		// because a primitive IS its value: there is nothing to copy that handing back the receiver has not
		// already done. So the call frame is skipped like `toString`'s, and no `<type>.clone` symbol exists or
		// is owed.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **HANDING BACK THE RECEIVER IS SOUND HERE AND IS EMPHATICALLY WRONG FOR A MANAGED RECEIVER** —
		// see `dispatchStringMethod`'s `clone` arm, which calls `__str_clone` precisely because returning the
		// receiver would hand the caller a second owner of one box. What makes the difference is not this arm's
		// judgement but WHICH receivers can reach it: `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` routes a `String` receiver to
		// `dispatchStringMethod` and a `Character` receiver to `dispatchCharacterMethod` several arms above the
		// builtin-conformer arm, so the only conformers that arrive are the three SCALARS, which own no
		// storage, are copied by every assignment already, and can no more be aliased by this than `let y = x`
		// can. ⇒ **admitting a managed conformer to `builtinConformerOfTag` without first giving it its own
		// dispatcher would make this arm a use-after-free**, which is the same ordering fact that header
		// already calls load-bearing.
		if methodTok.value.equals(CloneMethodName) 'cloneableLowering'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return try self.emitPrimitiveClone(recv.value, methodTok: methodTok)
		end 'cloneableLowering'

		match builtinConformerMethod(typeName, methodName: methodTok.value, discovered: DiscoveredConformer.none) 'lookup'
			supplied(method) then return try self.emitBuiltinConformanceCall(func, receiver: recv.value, target: DirectConformanceTarget.builtinValue(conformer), method: method, baseTok: recv.anchor, methodTok: methodTok)
			notSupplied then throw ParseError.unsupported("'{String.from(typeName)}' has no method named '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — a builtin-typed receiver supplies {builtinConformerDispatchMethodNames(typeName)}", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'lookup'
	end 'dispatchBuiltinConformanceMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE `x.clone()` DENOTES — the receiver, EXCEPT THAT IT SHEDS THE RECEIVER'S RANGED
	// TYPEALIAS, because `Cloneable.clone() returns Self` and `Self` here is the CONFORMER, not the alias
	// the receiver was written as.**
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **MEASURED AGAINST THE ORACLE, BOTH DOMAINS, AND THE ALIAS-PRESERVING READING FAILED THIS SPEC'S
	// OWN CASE.** The reference conforms `int`/`float`/`bool` through `extension int`/`extension float`/
	// `extension bool`, so the `Self` those `clone() returns Self` bodies name is the EXTENDED PRIMITIVE —
	// a `Byte` receiver's clone is an `int`, a `Percent` receiver's is a `float`. Measured on the bootstrap:
	// `x.clone() as Byte` and `p.clone() as Percent` both COMPILE, where `x as Byte` on the same `x` is
	// E3010 "unneeded cast" in both compilers alike. `primitive-cloneable/byte.clone` is exactly that
	// program — `let y = x.clone() as Integer` — and returning the receiver unchanged refused it, on a
	// diagnostic that was true about shv2's typing and wrong about the language's.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT IS THE READING SHV2 ALREADY GIVES `Self` IN ARGUMENT POSITION.**
	// `requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs` admits an `equals`/`compare` argument by CONFORMER
	// (`valueIsConformer`, which collapses `Byte` and `Integer` alike to `int`), never by the alias the
	// argument was written as. One rule now answers `Self` in both positions.
	//
	// ⭐ **NOTHING IS EMITTED UNLESS THE ANSWER WOULD OTHERWISE MOVE** — `applyCast`'s discipline, whose five
	// mint questions each fire only where a shared value would carry a fact that is not the result's. A
	// receiver denoting no ranged alias (every `bool`, and the overwhelming majority of `int`s and `float`s:
	// a literal, a plain binding, a parameter typed `int`) IS the whole answer, so `let x = 42` then
	// `x.clone()` costs not one instruction — which is what makes this a lowering rather than a call.
	function emitPrimitiveClone(receiver ValueId, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		match self.valueDenotedAlias(receiver) 'denoted'
			undeclared then return receiver
			declared(alias) then return try self.emitAliasFreeCopy(receiver, alias: alias, methodTok: methodTok)
		end 'denoted'
	end 'emitPrimitiveClone'

	// ⭐ **THE SAME NUMBER UNDER A FRESH SSA IDENTITY THAT DENOTES NO ALIAS.** A fresh ValueId is the ONLY
	// way to say it: the alias is recorded per VALUE (`ValueDeclaredAliasMap`) and the receiver's entry is
	// the receiver's, so a result that shares its id shares its alias. `emitBinOp` mints its result
	// `UnnamedTypeId` at the operator's own domain tag, which is precisely the plain primitive wanted here.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **`v - 0` AND NOT `v + 0`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS ONLY VISIBLE ON A FLOAT.** `-0.0 + 0.0` is `+0.0`
	// — a wrong answer for `(-0.0).clone()` — while `-0.0 - 0.0` is `-0.0`. Subtracting the domain's zero is
	// an EXACT identity on every input of both domains: signed zeroes, subnormals, infinities and NaN all
	// come back bit-for-bit, and an integer subtract of 0 cannot overflow. (`applyCast`'s int-only retag
	// emitter uses `+ 0` and is correct there for the same reason it would not be here — no float reaches
	// it.) `recordBinOpFold` carries a constant receiver's value across, so the parser's const view — which
	// is what turns a shift COUNT into a compile-time decision — survives the mint.
	//
	// The alias itself says which zero, so the domain is read off the very fact that made a copy necessary
	// rather than off a second classifier: every ranged typealias is an int range or a float range
	// (`aliasIsFloat`), and there is no third.
	function emitAliasFreeCopy(receiver ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(methodTok)
		let zero = self.emitLiteral(FloatZeroBits, valueType: MaxonType.float, range: range) if aliasIsFloat(alias) else self.emitLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)

		return try self.emitBinOp(receiver, rhs: zero, op: MaxonBinOp.sub, opTok: methodTok)
	end 'emitAliasFreeCopy'

	// Parse the argument list and emit the call. The receiver is argument 0 and is BORROWED, so no incref is
	// emitted here and no drop is owed there, exactly as an instance method's receiver rides. That is a
	// property of the IMPLS rather than of the operand kind, which is why it survived `String` joining them:
	// the scalar impls read by-value operands and free nothing, and `buildStrHash`/`buildStrEq` take a String
	// POINTER but only read `buffer@0`/`length@8` and walk the bytes (see their headers). A managed argument
	// therefore stays an ordinary pending temp and drops at statement end, AFTER the compare.
	//
	// It goes through `emitRuntimeCall` rather than `emitCall` for that door's stated reason: a synthesized
	// impl is built at the Std tier AFTER the pipeline, so no file declares it, `mintCallResult` would type
	// it `unresolved`, and the argument order is the compiler's own rather than a signature's. The result is
	// typed from the interface method's declared return (`interfaceReturnMaxonType` — the SAME typing the
	// witness-dispatched form gets), so `compare`'s `Ordering` carries the enum's `named` tag a `match`
	// recognizes and `hash`'s `HashValue` carries the ranged alias's.
	function emitBuiltinConformanceCall(func IrFunction, receiver ValueId, target DirectConformanceTarget, method InterfaceMethodSig, baseTok Token, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		args.push(receiver)
		if not self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'hasArgs'
			while true 'argLoop'
				args.push(try self.parseValueExpression(func))
				if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'endArgs'
					break
				end 'endArgs'
				try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			end 'argLoop'
		end 'hasArgs'
		let rparenTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// A builtin has no signature for `SemanticCheck`'s arity check to consult, so it is raised here —
		// the same `builtinArity` every other builtin raises, counted in USER arguments (the receiver slot
		// is the compiler's, and reporting it would name an argument the author never wrote).
		let userArgs = args.count() - BuiltinConformanceReceiverArgs
		if userArgs != method.paramTypeNames.count() 'arity'
			throw ParseError.builtinArity(String.from(method.name), expected: method.paramTypeNames.count(), got: userArgs, line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'arity'

		try self.requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs(method, args: args, target: target, methodTok: methodTok)

		let resultType = self.interfaceReturnMaxonType(method.returnTypeName)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(self.conformanceImplNameOf(target, methodName: method.name), args: args, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(resultType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(resultType), range: SourceRange.between(baseTok, last: rparenTok))
	end 'emitBuiltinConformanceCall'

	// The symbol a direct conformance dispatch calls. Both arms name a body that already exists — the
	// synthesized `<conformer>.<method>`, or (for an enum) `int`'s own, which is the whole reason the enum
	// grant mints nothing.
	function conformanceImplNameOf(target DirectConformanceTarget, methodName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		match target 'target'
			builtinValue(conformer) then return qualifiedMethodName(builtinConformerName(conformer), methodName: methodName)
			enumTagValue(enumName) then return self.signatures.enumConformanceImplName(enumName, methodName: methodName)
		end 'target'
	end 'conformanceImplNameOf'

	// Is `actual` a value this target's `Self` formal accepts? The two arms are the two identities a direct
	// dispatch can have — a builtin CONFORMER, compared as conformers so `Byte` and `Integer` agree, and one
	// specific ENUM, compared by name because two enums are two types however alike their tags look.
	function valueSatisfiesConformanceSelf(actual ValueId, target DirectConformanceTarget) returns bool
		match target 'target'
			builtinValue(conformer) then return self.valueIsConformer(actual, conformer: conformer)
			enumTagValue(enumName) then return self.tagOf(actual) == ValueTypeTag.named and self.internedName(self.valueNameId(actual)).equals(enumName)
		end 'target'
	end 'valueSatisfiesConformanceSelf'

	// ⭐ **A `Self`-TYPED FORMAL TAKES A VALUE OF THE RECEIVER'S OWN CONFORMER, AND NOTHING ELSE.**
	// `Equatable.equals` and `Comparable.compare` both declare `other Self`, and each impl reads its second
	// operand the way its OWN receiver is represented: `int.equals(self, other)` compares two RAW i64s, so a
	// String actual would be compared as its heap POINTER and a struct as its box address, while
	// `String.equals` walks bytes off a pointer, so an INT actual would be dereferenced. Both directions are
	// silent wrong answers, which is precisely what `requireGenuineSelfArgs` refuses for the witness-dispatched
	// form; this is the same question asked of a CONCRETE conformer instead of a type parameter.
	//
	// ⚠ It is what makes `builtinConformerOfTag`'s `string` arm load-bearing rather than decorative: the
	// RECEIVER never reaches that classifier (`dispatchMethodOnReceiver` routes a String several arms
	// earlier), but `equals`'s ARGUMENT reaches it here, and without the arm a legal `"ab".equals("ac")`
	// would be refused for handing a String to a `Self` formal.
	//
	// ⚠ **"THE RECEIVER'S OWN CONFORMER" IS THE TARGET'S QUESTION AND NOT THIS ONE'S SINCE enum-hashable** —
	// an ENUM receiver's `Self` is that one enum, not the `int` its granted impls belong to, so admitting by
	// conformer there would compare a `Color` tag against a bare `int` or against another enum's case index.
	// `valueSatisfiesConformanceSelf` holds both readings; this walk owns only WHICH formals are asked.
	//
	// Interface formal `j` is actual `j + BuiltinConformanceReceiverArgs`; arity was checked above.
	function requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs(method InterfaceMethodSig, args ValueIdArray, target DirectConformanceTarget, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		for j in 0 upto method.paramTypeNames.count() 'eachFormal'
			let formalTypeName = try method.paramTypeNames.get(j) otherwise panic("requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs: paramTypeNames.get OOB at {j} below count {method.paramTypeNames.count()}")
			if not formalTypeName.equals(InterfaceSelfTypeName) 'notSelfFormal'
				continue
			end 'notSelfFormal'

			let actual = try args.get(j + BuiltinConformanceReceiverArgs) otherwise panic("requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs: args.get OOB at {j + BuiltinConformanceReceiverArgs} below count {args.count()} — the arity check ran first")
			if not self.valueSatisfiesConformanceSelf(actual, target: target) 'notSameConformer'
				throw ParseError.builtinOperandType(String.from(method.name), required: String.from(conformanceSelfTypeNameOf(target)), got: typeTagName(self.tagOf(actual)), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
			end 'notSameConformer'
		end 'eachFormal'
	end 'requireBuiltinConformanceSelfArgs'

	// --- Builtin `Array` parsing (P1.7 slice 1) ---

	// `[a, b, c]` — an array literal. Desugars to `__managed_create(elementSize, 0)` then one `__managed_push` per
	// element, yielding an OWNED `Array with integer`. At this slice a literal's elements are all integer
	// (a String / struct / bool / Byte element literal is a later slice, rejected E2015); an empty `[]`
	// carries no element to infer a type from and is rejected (use `Array with T` + `.create()`).
	//
	// ⭐ **`element` IS WHERE THE INSTANCE COMES FROM, AND IT IS A PARAMETER RATHER THAN A SECOND ENTRY POINT**
	// (`ArrayLiteralElementSource`). A `<alias> from […]` head states the instance the literal must build;
	// everything downstream of that one decision — the map-literal fork, the constant-blob fork, the element
	// rule, the escape gate, the owned-temp registration — is the same for both spellings, and a second
	// function to hold a different first line would have had to copy all of it.
	function parseArrayLiteral(func IrFunction, element ArrayLiteralElementSource) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let openTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(openTok)
		self.skipNewlines()

		if self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'empty'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(emptyArrayLiteralMessage(element), line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column)
		end 'empty'

		// ⚠ **THE INT-LITERAL FAST PATH IS GONE, AND ITS ONLY JOB WAS OP ORDER.** It created the record
		// BEFORE evaluating any element so that slice 1's int-array goldens would not move, and it was
		// reachable only for a BARE int literal (`arrayElementIsSingleToken`) because a token-level guard can
		// settle an element's type for no other shape. Both facts stopped mattering when the blob path
		// widened to the machine word below: an all-constant `[10, 20, 30]` now creates no record at all, so
		// there is no order to preserve, and the general path below already infers `integer` from the parsed
		// first element. What is left of it would be a second spelling of that inference.
		let firstTok = self.current()
		let firstStart = self.pos
		let first = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)

		// ⭐ **A `:` AFTER THE FIRST ELEMENT MAKES THE WHOLE THING A MAP LITERAL** — `["a": 1, "b": 2]`. Both
		// reference compilers disambiguate at exactly this point and for the same reason v1 states: no bounded
		// LOOKAHEAD can find the colon, because `[f(a, b): 1]` has a `,` at depth 1 first and `[m.get(k): 1]`
		// has an arbitrary expression in front of the `:`. So the first element is parsed unconditionally and
		// the token AFTER it is what decides which container this is.
		//
		// ⚠ It sits ahead of `arrayLiteralInstanceFor` because a map literal has NO array instance to mint: the
		// first element is its first KEY, and the instance it fixes has two columns rather than one.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS ASKED ONLY WHERE THE ELEMENT TYPE IS STILL OPEN.** A `<alias> from […]` head has already
		// named an `Array` instance, so taking this fork under one would hand the head's caller a `Map` — a
		// silently wrong TYPE rather than a diagnostic. Under a stated head a `:` is simply not part of the
		// literal, and the element rule or the closing `]` refuses it.
		if self.at(TokenKind.colon) and elementSourceIsInferred(element) 'mapLiteral'
			return try self.parseMapLiteralBody(func, firstKey: first, firstKeyTok: firstTok, range: range)
		end 'mapLiteral'

		// ⚠ **THE STATED INSTANCE IS TAKEN WITHOUT ASKING THE FIRST ELEMENT AT ALL**, which is the point: under
		// `ByteArray from […]` the first element is a bare `int` constant, and `arrayLiteralInstanceFor` would
		// answer `Array with integer` — the very inference the head exists to override.
		let giid = match element 'elementSource'
			stated(headGiid) gives headGiid
			inferred gives try self.arrayLiteralInstanceFor(first, elemTok: firstTok)
		end 'elementSource'

		// The first element is ADMITTED into that instance exactly as every `, e` after it is
		// (`admitArrayLiteralElement`) — one element rule, asked of every element including the first.
		let head = try self.admitArrayLiteralElement(first, giid: giid, elemTok: firstTok, valueStart: firstStart)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INLINE-STORED ELEMENT COLLECTS ITS ELEMENTS BEFORE IT BUILDS ANYTHING**, because if they
		// are all constants the whole literal is ONE `.rdata` blob and there is no record to push into. The
		// element type is settled by the FIRST element alone — it is what fixes `giid` — so the decision is
		// available here, one line before the create this path would otherwise emit.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE GATE IS "DOES THE SLOT OWN ITS OWN BYTES", NOT A WIDTH** (`arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob`),
		// and only a MANAGED or OPAQUE element answers no. It asked "narrower than a machine word" until
		// `codegen-internals`, which excluded `[10, 20, 30]` — the plainest constant array there is, and the
		// one the spec's own sentence and seven `RequiredRdata` cases are written about. That exclusion was
		// there to keep the int-literal goldens still; see the gate for the measurement that retired it.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ADMITTED SET IS WIDER THAN "ENUMS AND PACKED ELEMENTS", AND SAYING SO MATTERS.** A literal
		// whose first element FIXES a narrow ranged alias (`[1 as Percent, 2 as Percent]`) became a `.rdata`
		// blob where it was `__managed_create` + pushes, and every machine-word constant literal has now joined
		// them. ⭐ That drops no CHECK: `pushArrayLiteralElement` applies none a blob would skip — a ranged
		// element's runtime guard rides `parseArraySetOrInsert`, which a literal never goes through — and the
		// values were already required to be compile-time constants. ⚠ A cast to a NON-packing alias still
		// does not retag the element (`applyCast.castChangesTheElementPacking` mints a `named` result only
		// across the byte/sub-byte boundary), so which instance `[1 as Percent, …]` mints is that predicate's answer, not this
		// gate's.
		if self.signatures.arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob(giid) 'anInlineStoredElement'
			return try self.parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody(func, first: head, firstStart: firstStart, giid: giid, range: range)
		end 'anInlineStoredElement'

		let arr = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		try self.pushArrayLiteralElement(arr, elem: head, giid: giid, valueStart: firstStart, range: range)

		return try self.finishArrayLiteral(func, arr: arr, giid: giid, first: head, range: range)
	end 'parseArrayLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN INLINE-STORED LITERAL'S BODY — COLLECTED FIRST, EMITTED ONCE.** Every element is parsed and held, and
	// only at the `]` is it known whether they are ALL compile-time constants:
	//
	//   • **all constant** ⇒ ONE `constArrayLiteral` op over an immortal `.rdata` blob. This is what
	//     `ranged-int-bit-packing`'s three ```RequiredRdata cases pin BYTE-FOR-BYTE — `[0 as Q, 1 as Q,
	//     2 as Q, 3 as Q]` at 2 bits is the single byte `0b11_10_01_00` — which is a claim about the bit
	//     ORDER and the `ceil(count·bits/8)` sizing that no round-tripped value could make; and it is what
	//     `enum-narrow-storage`'s six pin at the BYTE ladder (`u8[] 0, 1, 2`, `i16[] -200, 0, 200`).
	//   • **any element non-constant** ⇒ the ordinary `__managed_create` + one `__managed_push` per element, over the
	//     values already parsed. `specs/bool-bit-packing.md` requires both spellings to reach the identical
	//     representation ("whether built from a constant literal, a NON-CONSTANT literal, or dynamically"),
	//     and they do: the stride is `arrayElementSize`'s single answer either way.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FALL-BACK COSTS NOTHING IT DID NOT ALREADY COST**, which is why collecting is safe: the values
	// it pushes are the very `ValueId`s parsing produced, in order, so nothing is parsed twice and no op is
	// wasted. What the CONSTANT arm leaves behind is the elements' own `const` materializations, now unused —
	// dead values the blob makes redundant, which the backend drops on the floor because nothing reads them.
	//
	// ⚠ Only an INLINE-STORED element reaches here — never a managed one, whose slot is a pointer to a heap
	// box — so there is no managed move-in to preserve, which is what makes holding the elements across the
	// `]` legitimate at all. `arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob` is where that exclusion is stated.
	function parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody(func IrFunction, first ValueId, firstStart TokenIndex, giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		var values = ValueIdArray.create()
		var starts = TokenIndexColumn.create()
		values.push(first)
		starts.push(firstStart)

		self.skipNewlines()
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreElements'
			let parsed = try self.parseArrayLiteralTailElement(func, giid: giid)
			values.push(parsed.value)
			starts.push(parsed.start)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'moreElements'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBracket)

		// ⚠ **THE BLOB ARM NEEDS NO ESCAPE GATE AND CANNOT.** A capturing closure is not a value the compiler
		// holds as an integer constant, so `everyElementIsAKnownConstant` has already excluded one from this
		// arm; the PUSH arm below is a real container build and asks the question in full.
		if self.everyElementIsAKnownConstant(values) 'aConstantBlob'
			return self.emitConstantArrayBlob(values, giid: giid, range: range)
		end 'aConstantBlob'

		let arr = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		for i in 0 upto values.count() 'eachElement'
			let elem = try values.get(i) otherwise panic("parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody: values.get({i}) failed inside its own upto bound")
			let elemStart = try starts.get(i) otherwise panic("parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody: starts.get({i}) failed — it is pushed in lockstep with values")
			try self.pushArrayLiteralElement(arr, elem: elem, giid: giid, valueStart: elemStart, range: range)
		end 'eachElement'

		try self.requireNoEscapingClosureElement(values)

		self.trackOwnedTemp(arr, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return arr
	end 'parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONTAINER ESCAPE ROUTE (E3099) — an array's element block is heap memory that outlives the
	// frame and stores the code pointer alone, so a capturing closure put into one loses its environment.**
	// Asked through the SAME `rejectEscapingClosure` every other route funnels through, which is why there is
	// no predicate here: that gate answers "does this value capture?" once, for all five routes.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE ELEMENT ANYWHERE IN THE LITERAL CONDEMNS THE WHOLE CONTAINER**, so the whole element list is
	// scanned and the LAST capturing one is blamed — which is what the eager tail's carried-across-the-loop
	// `offender` amounted to. A literal where nothing captures hands the gate its first element, and the gate
	// tests the value it is given, so that passes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT BLAMES THE CONTAINER, NOT THE ELEMENT, AND THAT IS THE REFERENCE'S ANCHOR — MEASURED.** The
	// sentence says *"in a container"* and the position agrees with it: the bootstrap reports at the token
	// FOLLOWING the closing `]`, wherever the offending element sits — `[double, <closure>]` and
	// `[<closure>, double]` are byte-identical diagnostics at the identical column, and a one-element literal
	// moves it only by the line's own length. So the anchor is the cursor past the literal, which is exactly
	// where both callers run it; anchoring at the element would be a different, unpinned diagnostic.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COLLECTED TAIL ASKS IT, AND THAT IS NEW.** This gate used to be the eager tail's alone, on the
	// argument that the collect path was reached only for a SUB-BYTE-PACKED element and so could hold no
	// function value and no closure. **That structural exclusion is gone**: the collect path now takes every
	// inline-stored element (`arrayElementMayBeConstantBlob`), plain `int` and function values included, so
	// the exclusion would have become an omission — a closure in `[double, <closure>]` reaching a tail with
	// no escape check. Both tails call this, which is why it is a function and not a paragraph in each.
	function requireNoEscapingClosureElement(values ValueIdArray) throws ParseError
		var offender = try values.get(0) otherwise panic("requireNoEscapingClosureElement: an array literal holds at least one element — an empty `[]` is refused at the open bracket")
		for elem in values 'each'
			if self.isCapturingClosure(elem) 'thisElementCaptures'
				offender = elem
			end 'thisElementCaptures'
		end 'each'

		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(offender, message: "cannot put a closure that captures in a container: {CapturingClosureEscapeSuffix}", tok: self.current())
	end 'requireNoEscapingClosureElement'

	// Does the compiler hold every one of these values as an integer constant — the question that decides
	// whether a collected literal becomes a blob or a run of pushes. A `bool` element's `true`/`false` folds
	// to 1/0 through the same constant table, and so does an enum case's TAG (`emitEnumTagLiteral`), which is
	// why one predicate serves every inline-stored element kind.
	function everyElementIsAKnownConstant(values ValueIdArray) returns bool
		for elem in values 'each'
			if not self.constIsKnown(elem) 'notAConstant'
				return false
			end 'notAConstant'
		end 'each'

		return true
	end 'everyElementIsAKnownConstant'

	// ⭐ **SERIALIZE THE CONSTANTS AND EMIT THE ONE OP.** Precondition: `everyElementIsAKnownConstant(values)`
	// and the instance's element stores its own bytes — both settled by the sole caller.
	//
	// The blob is composed by `constArrayBlobOf` — the ONE factory the file-scope spelling
	// (`ProgramSignatures.containerRecordOf`) also goes through, so a literal written in a body and the same
	// literal written at module scope cannot lay down different bytes. The stride it is stamped with is the
	// stride its image was serialized at, because both come from that one call.
	function emitConstantArrayBlob(values ValueIdArray, giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		var constants = ParsedIntArray.create()
		for elem in values 'each'
			constants.push(self.constOf(elem))
		end 'each'

		let blob = constArrayBlobOf(constants, stride: self.signatures.arrayElementSize(giid))
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, nameId: giid)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.constArrayLiteral(result, elementSize: blob.stride, elementCount: blob.elementCount, bytes: blob.bytes), range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return result
	end 'emitConstantArrayBlob'

	// The tail a MANAGED or OPAQUE literal takes once its record exists and its first element is pushed: the
	// `, e` elements, the closing `]`, the escape gate and the owned-temp registration. It pushes as it goes
	// rather than collecting, and that is the whole difference from `parseCollectedArrayLiteralBody`: a
	// managed element's move-in is decided from the `[valueStart, self.pos)` window it was written at, which
	// a collect-then-push walk would have already moved past.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NEWLINE IS SKIPPED ONLY AT AN ELEMENT BOUNDARY, and never before `pushArrayLiteralElement`** —
	// the same two rules `parseStructLiteralFields` states for `{…}`. A skip before the push would widen the
	// `[valueStart, self.pos)` window `moveElementIntoContainer` reads to decide whether the element was a bare
	// local, and silently mis-decide a managed element's move-in.
	function finishArrayLiteral(func IrFunction, arr ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, first ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// Held only for the escape gate below, which asks about the whole element list — see
		// `requireNoEscapingClosureElement` for why the question is the list's and not each element's.
		var values = ValueIdArray.create()
		values.push(first)

		self.skipNewlines()
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreElements'
			let parsed = try self.parseArrayLiteralTailElement(func, giid: giid)
			try self.pushArrayLiteralElement(arr, elem: parsed.value, giid: giid, valueStart: parsed.start, range: range)
			values.push(parsed.value)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'moreElements'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBracket)

		try self.requireNoEscapingClosureElement(values)

		self.trackOwnedTemp(arr, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return arr
	end 'finishArrayLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `, e` STEP ITSELF — the comma, the newline that may precede the element, the element, and the
	// type match against the literal's instance.** The ONE home of the element rule, shared by the two tails
	// above and below: `finishArrayLiteral` PUSHES what it gets, `parsePackedArrayLiteralBody` COLLECTS it.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE TRAILING `skipNewlines()` IS DELIBERATELY THE CALLER'S AND MUST STAY THERE.** It has to run
	// AFTER `pushArrayLiteralElement`, because `moveElementIntoContainer` reads `[valueStart, self.pos)` to
	// decide whether the element was a bare local — a skip before the push widens that window past a
	// single-identifier element and silently mis-decides a managed element's move-in. That is the one line of
	// this loop the two tails genuinely have to spell for themselves, and it is why this helper stops where
	// it does. (Found in review: the packed tail arrived as a second verbatim copy of the whole loop, in a
	// function whose own header says it exists so "the two entry paths cannot drift on the element rule, on
	// where a newline may fall, or on when the array becomes a tracked temp".)
	function parseArrayLiteralTailElement(func IrFunction, giid GenericInstanceId) returns ArrayLiteralElement throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		self.skipNewlines()
		let elemTok = self.current()
		let elemStart = self.pos
		let elem = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)

		return ArrayLiteralElement.create(try self.admitArrayLiteralElement(elem, giid: giid, elemTok: elemTok, valueStart: elemStart), start: elemStart)
	end 'parseArrayLiteralTailElement'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE ELEMENT ADMITTED INTO THE LITERAL'S INSTANCE** — the door the FIRST element and every `, e`
	// after it both go through, so a literal cannot come to have two element rules.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT CONVERTS WHENEVER THE ELEMENT'S STORAGE DIFFERS FROM THE INSTANCE'S SLOT — under a stated head
	// AND under an inferred one.** The gate is `storageDiffersFromAlias`: *would this value be stored
	// differently from what the instance says?* It is not a flag this function reads, and it is NOT
	// structurally false for a bare `[…]` — an earlier version of this comment claimed that and was wrong.
	//
	// ⚠ **`[10 as Byte, 20]` IS THE CASE THAT DISPROVES IT, AND IT IS ACCEPTED DELIBERATELY.** Element 1 is
	// renamed to `Byte` by its own `as` (`mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage`), so the literal mints an
	// `Array with Byte`; element 2 is a bare `20` at a machine word against a one-byte slot, so it DOES
	// convert here. shv2 used to refuse that program `MixedArrayLiteralElementsMessage` — and the refusal was
	// shv2 being stricter than the reference. **MEASURED: the runnable oracle compiles it and answers the
	// same 20.** The rule is the instance's slot, not the spelling of the head.
	//
	// ⭐ **A STATED HEAD IS THE CASE WHERE IT IS TRUE, AND WHAT IT DOES THEN IS THE `as <element>` THE SOURCE
	// DID NOT HAVE TO WRITE.** `ByteArray from [10, 20, 30]` hands over three bare `int` constants at a
	// machine word each against an instance that stores one byte each, so each is converted through
	// `convertValueToDeclaredType` — the same conversion `[10 as Byte, 20 as Byte, 30 as Byte]` performs, and
	// deliberately the same one, because the two spellings must reach the identical `i8[] 10, 20, 30` blob
	// (`specs/codegen-internals.md` pins both). Both references settle it the same way: the bootstrap resolves
	// the destination's ranged element type and hands it down to its element emitter, which range-checks and
	// re-tags each element, *"the replacement for the old `[U16{10}, U16{20}, …]` per-element construction
	// pattern"* (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:20593`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE KIND GATE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS ASKED BEFORE THE CONVERSION.** A String value against a stated
	// `Byte` element also answers the stride question TRUE (a String denotes no ranged alias, so it reads as a
	// full machine word), and the re-tag would have stamped `Byte` onto a heap POINTER — a silent wrong
	// answer. `requireCastKindsConvert` is the existing E3009 for it and needs no new sentence. (The runnable
	// oracle has no answer here to match: `ByteArray from ["a", "b"]` crashes it with an internal E9001.)
	function admitArrayLiteralElement(value ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, elemTok Token, valueStart TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let indexSpaceElement = self.signatures.containerElementType(giid)
		let targetAlias = self.declaredAliasOfIndexSpaceType(indexSpaceElement)

		// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT TYPE HAS TO BE RE-SPELLED IN THIS FILE'S INTERNER BEFORE ANY DOOR BELOW SEES IT.**
		// The instance's argument was interned by `ProgramSignatures`, and every step from here mints or reads
		// a `named` id against the FILE's table — `castTargetTag` resolves one, `emitRetaggedCastValue` stamps
		// one onto the value it mints. It is always a `named` type where the conversion runs, because
		// `storageDiffersFromAlias` answers false for a target that declares no ranged alias.
		//
		// ⛔ **NO GUARD IS SPELLED HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** This door once re-spelled
		// `requireCastKindsConvert` and thereby got ONE of the conversion's three legality guards; the enum
		// refusal it dropped let `ByteArray from [Color.red, …]` through. They live inside
		// `convertValueToDeclaredType` now, so reaching the conversion is what grants them.
		let converted = try self.convertedArrayLiteralElement(value, targetAlias: targetAlias, indexSpaceElement: indexSpaceElement, elemTok: elemTok, valueStart: valueStart)
		try self.requireArrayLiteralElementMatches(converted, giid: giid, elemTok: elemTok)

		return converted
	end 'admitArrayLiteralElement'

	// The element itself, converted to the instance's slot when its storage differs and handed straight back
	// when it does not. Split from the homogeneity check above so that check is written ONCE for both arms —
	// it used to be called in each, which is one question asked from two places.
	function convertedArrayLiteralElement(value ValueId, targetAlias DeclaredAlias, indexSpaceElement MaxonType, elemTok Token, valueStart TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if not self.storageDiffersFromAlias(value, targetAlias: targetAlias) 'storedAsTheInstanceSays'
			return value
		end 'storedAsTheInstanceSays'

		let elementType = MaxonType.named(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(self.signatures.indexSpaceTypeName(indexSpaceElement)))
		return try self.convertValueToDeclaredType(value, sourceTag: self.tagOf(value), targetType: elementType, targetAlias: targetAlias, opTok: elemTok, sourceIsBareLocal: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart), mustCarryTargetType: true)
	end 'convertedArrayLiteralElement'

	// Push one already-parsed array-literal element, moving a managed element in (the array owns it; a trivial
	// element passes through) — `moveElementIntoContainer` off the value's start position so a bare local is poisoned.
	function pushArrayLiteralElement(arr ValueId, elem ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, valueStart TokenIndex, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		self.emitArrayPush(arr, element: try self.moveElementIntoContainer(elem, giid: giid, valueStart: valueStart), range: range)
	end 'pushArrayLiteralElement'

	// THE `__managed_push` of an array being BUILT, over an element whose ownership has already been settled.
	// Its two callers settle that in different ways and so cannot share the step before it: a literal's
	// element is decided from the SOURCE TOKENS it was written at (`moveElementIntoContainer`), while a
	// synthesized case-name element (`emitEnumAllCaseNames`) was written at no tokens at all. The store
	// itself is one fact, so it is written once.
	//
	// ⚠ Distinct from the user's own `arr.push(x)`, which is `emitArrayVoidCall` — that form hands the
	// RECEIVER back as "what the statement still denotes", where a build step's result is nobody's.
	function emitArrayPush(arr ValueId, element ValueId, range SourceRange)
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedPushName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: element), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
	end 'emitArrayPush'

	// The `Array with T` instance a literal's FIRST element fixes (P1.7 slice 3) — an integer element → the
	// int instance, a String → `Array with String`, a struct/boxed-union → `Array with <name>`. All are
	// interned by the sweep (`internBuiltinIntegerInstance` / `internLiteralContainerInstances`), so this is a
	// pure lookup. A function/other element is not a supported literal element yet (E2015).
	//
	// ⭐ **`bool` AND `float` JOINED THE ADMITTED SET WITH THE `Vector` PORT, and neither was a refusal with a
	// mechanism behind it.** `/specs/vector.md` writes `Vector from [true, false, …]` and
	// `Vector from [1.5, 2.5]`, and a `Vector from […]` builds its record through THIS literal — so the
	// question was never "should a vector be special" but "why did a bare `[…]` refuse two element types the
	// managed-memory runtime already strides correctly". Measured: `bool` needed nothing but this arm (its slot is one
	// byte by `trivialElementSlot`, and `Array with bool` built through `BArr.create()` + `push` has
	// always worked); `float` needed the element-word bitcast (`containerElementWord` /
	// `containerSlotValueType`), which is the whole of what was missing and is now built.
	//
	// ⚠ **`typealias FloatArray = Array with float` IS STILL E2062, and that is now a POLICY and not a
	// limitation.** That refusal is about a type ARGUMENT in a `with` clause — `checkGenericArgType`'s rule 1,
	// pinned by `generic-types.md:error.float-type-arg-builtin-generic` — and it is deliberately untouched by
	// this rung. What it says about dictionary-passing is true of a declared generic's `T` and was never true
	// of a builtin container's element, which lives in a buffer at a known stride; retiring it is a decision
	// for whoever owns that spec file, not a side effect of this one.
	//
	// ⭐ **A CAST TO A PACKING ALIAS ARRIVES HERE AS A `named` TAG, AND THAT IS WHY THIS DOOR NEEDS NO ARM
	// OF ITS OWN FOR IT.** `[0 as Q, 1 as Q, 2 as Q, 3 as Q]` is an `Array with Q`, not an `Array with int`
	// — and it reaches the `aggregate` arm below unchanged, because `applyCast.castChangesTheElementPacking`
	// MINTS a `named`-tagged result for a cast that moves an element between the byte and sub-byte ladders.
	// ⚠ A cast that merely narrows the BYTE width does NOT retag — see that predicate's ⛔⛔ block for the two
	// programs the wider rule broke.
	// Reading the tag is therefore complete. ⚠ Asking `castSourceAlias` here instead was tried and is WRONG:
	// that record is withheld for a bare-local source, so it answered differently for `[7 as N, …]` and
	// `[v as N, …]` — the measurement is on that predicate.
	function arrayLiteralInstanceFor(elem ValueId, elemTok Token) returns GenericInstanceId throws ParseError
		return self.signatures.builtinInstanceForElement(self.signatures.arrayRecordBaseName(), element: try self.literalColumnTypeFor(elem, literalKind: ArrayLiteralSubject, columnNoun: ContainerElementColumnNoun, valueTok: elemTok))
	end 'arrayLiteralInstanceFor'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TYPE A BRACKETED LITERAL'S VALUE FIXES FOR THE COLUMN IT SITS IN.** An array literal has ONE
	// such column (its element) and a map literal has TWO (its key and its value), and the question each puts
	// is identical — *which `MaxonType` does this parsed value name?* — so it is answered here once and the
	// three callers differ only in the NOUN their refusal uses.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `named`-vs-`structRef` CHOICE IS `builtinInstanceForNamedElement`'s AND MUST STAY THERE**: a
	// synthesized TUPLE interns as `structRef` where a declared struct/union is `named`, and a second spelling
	// of that rule is a measured spurious `E3006 duplicate definition of 'Array_<T>'` (see its header). So the
	// aggregate arm asks it rather than building the `MaxonType` itself.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE REFUSAL'S ARRAY SPELLING IS PINNED BYTE-FOR-BYTE by `specs-shv2/character-ownership.md`'s
	// `an-array-literal-element-is-not-a-character`**, which is why the subject arrives as `<kind> <noun>`
	// rather than as one reworded sentence: composed from `ArrayLiteralSubject`/`ContainerElementColumnNoun` it
	// reproduces that expectation exactly, and the map literal's two columns get their own nouns without
	// touching it.
	function literalColumnTypeFor(value ValueId, literalKind String, columnNoun String, valueTok Token) returns MaxonType throws ParseError
		let tag = self.tagOf(value)
		// ⛔⛔ **`exitCode` RIDES HERE, AND WITHOUT IT THIS DOOR REFUSED A PROGRAM ITS OWN SENTENCE PERMITTED.**
		// `typeTagName(exitCode)` is `"int"`, so an `ExitCode`-tagged element fell to the throw as *"an array
		// literal element of type 'int' — a literal's elements are an integer, …"* — the identical self-refuting
		// shape this door carried for a container, one tag over. MEASURED on this tree; the bootstrap oracle
		// COMPILES the same program and prints `9`, so it was a false refusal and not a policy:
		//
		//     typealias IntThunk = function() returns ExitCode
		//     function useIt(t IntThunk) returns Integer
		//         let v = t()
		//         var arr = [v, v]          // ← E2015 before this arm
		//
		// ⚠ **A DIRECT CALL CANNOT REACH IT, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED REVIEW.** `[code(), code()]` over a
		// `function code() returns ExitCode` compiles, because a direct call's result is not `exitCode`-tagged.
		// `wordSlotStorageType`'s header names the two doors that DO mint one — `mintIndirectCallResult` (an
		// indirect call through a function alias) and `interfaceReturnMaxonType` (an interface method
		// `returns ExitCode`) — and both reach this column. That header is also this arm's ANSWER: it maps
		// `exitCode` to `MaxonType.integer` at the env-slot sink because an `exitCode` lowers to `StdType.u32`,
		// for which `accessWidthFor` has no memory-access form and panics on every backend. An
		// `Array with ExitCode` would put exactly that width on the element load, so `integer` is the only safe
		// column type here, not merely the simplest.
		if tagIsPlainInteger(tag) 'int'
			return MaxonType.integer
		end 'int'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean or tagIsFloat(tag) 'scalar'
			return MaxonType.boolean if tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean else MaxonType.float
		end 'scalar'
		if tagIsText(tag) 'string'
			return MaxonType.string
		end 'string'
		if self.signatures.tagMayNameAggregate(tag) 'aggregate'
			return self.signatures.namedElementType(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)))
		end 'aggregate'

		// ⭐⭐ **A CONTAINER VALUE — A GENERIC INSTANCE, WHOSE COLUMN TYPE IS THE INSTANCE ITSELF.** `[list1, list2]`
		// over two `List with Integer` locals is an `Array with (List with Integer)`, and that nested instance is a
		// type this door can name outright.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE ONE ARM OF THIS DOOR THAT MAY USE THE SHARED REBUILD, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS THE REASON.**
		// `typeOf` is `maxonTypeOfTag(tagOf, nameId: valueNameId)`, whose `genericInstance` arm is exactly the type
		// wanted here — so open-coding it would be a second spelling of a rule that already has a home, and
		// `valueIsUnsigned`'s header records what that costs (X10 landed "the file's THIRD copy of that walk").
		// The `named` and `function` arms genuinely CANNOT use it: their ids are the parser's FILE-LOCAL interner's
		// and must cross into the signature index's as BYTES, which is why each hands a name to
		// `signatures.namedElementType` / `functionElementType` instead. A generic instance needs no crossing
		// because **there is exactly ONE `GenericInstanceRegistry` in the compiler** (`SignatureIndex.maxon`), so
		// every giid — from whichever of the ~20 sites minted this value — is already in the index's space by
		// construction. That is a property of the registry, not a list of mint sites that could go stale.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE MECHANISM WAS ALREADY WHOLE; ONLY THIS DOOR WAS UNWIRED.** `typealias L = Array with IntList`
		// plus `.create()`/`.push()` compiles and runs today (2/2/10 on this spec's own program), because a
		// nested instance is a MANAGED element like any other: `arrayElementSize` stamps the 8-byte pointer slot,
		// `arrayElementDestroyValue` stamps the element's drop callee, and `containerElementType` reads the
		// instance back for `get`. What a bare `[…]` had no way to do was NAME that element type, exactly as the
		// `function` arm below records for a function value.
		//
		// ⚠ **NO `interfaceRef` SPLIT IS OWED HERE, AND THE REASON IS THE VALUE COLUMN — NOT `instanceDenotedType`.**
		// That door decides `interfaceRef`-vs-`genericInstance` for a TYPE REFERENCE, and two sites
		// (`parseTypeReference`'s scoped arm, `promoteBareGenericResultToCalleeScope`) mint
		// `MaxonType.genericInstance` without asking it — so it is not a claim this arm may lean on. What this arm
		// dispatches on is the VALUE's tag, where the split is already made by the tag set's own definition: an
		// existential tags the fat pointer's value half `interfaceRef` (see `ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef`), never
		// `genericInstance`. A value reaching here is a box.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'containerValue'
			return self.typeOf(value)
		end 'containerValue'

		// ⭐⭐ **A FUNCTION VALUE, WHOSE COLUMN TYPE IS ITS OWN GOVERNING NAME.** `Array with UnaryOp` has always
		// been buildable through `.create()`/`.push()` — the sibling case
		// `capturing-closure-into-container-errors` is written against one — so the runtime side of a
		// function-element container is not new; what was missing is that a bare `[…]` had no way to NAME the
		// element type, because a literal writes no alias. It takes the first element's governing name, which is
		// the same answer `enumFunctionBackingGoverningName` gives for a function-backed enum's `.rawValue` and
		// for the same reason: shv2 names a function type by something that declares it, and the first value is
		// a name that already resolves whole-program.
		//
		// ⚠ **WITHOUT THIS ARM THE ESCAPE RULE WAS UNREACHABLE ON THIS ROUTE AND THE ADVICE WAS FALSE.**
		// `[double, <capturing closure>]` was refused E2015 at `double` — before the closure was even parsed —
		// so `/specs`' pinned E3099 never fired, and its advice (*"use a function reference"*) named a program
		// this compiler also refused. The refusal has to be about the closure for the advice to be worth giving.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE NAME CROSSES INTERNERS, SO IT TRAVELS AS BYTES** — `signatures.functionElementType` does the
		// interning, for the reason its own header records with the measurement: a container instance's type
		// arguments are ids in the SIGNATURE index's interner, and the parser's `valueNameId` is an id in this
		// FILE's. Handing the raw id over compiled, parsed and then panicked at the call.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.function 'functionValue'
			return self.signatures.functionElementType(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(value)))
		end 'functionValue'

		// ⛔⛔ **THE ROSTER BELOW IS HAND-WRITTEN AND IT NO LONGER LISTS EVERY ARM ABOVE IT — MEASURED, NOT
		// SUSPECTED.** It names an integer, a float, a bool, a String, a struct and a boxed union, and the arms
		// admit two more: a FUNCTION VALUE (`[double, triple]`, admitted by the arm directly above and never
		// listed) and, since spec-port `monomorphization-recursive-type`, a CONTAINER (`[list1, list2]`). A
		// sentence that under-states what it admits is this project's most frequent single rot, and it is the
		// exact shape `requireHashTableKeyType` retired one door over by rendering its roster from the very list
		// its test walks (`reservedHashTableKeyNames` + `DiscoveredConformer`'s exhaustive match).
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS NOT RENDERED FROM THE ARMS HERE BECAUSE THE SENTENCE IS PINNED BYTE-FOR-BYTE BY A SECOND
		// SPEC.** `specs-shv2/character-ownership.md` quotes the composed message in TWO `maxoncstderr` blocks
		// (`an-array-literal-element-is-not-a-character`, `an-ascii-array-literal-is-a-character-array-too`),
		// which shv2's `--update-required` does not regenerate. Rendering the roster from the tag arms therefore
		// rewrites a committed expectation belonging to a spec this tick does not own, and that is a decision for
		// whoever owns that file — not a side effect of adding an arm here.
		throw ParseError.unsupported("{literalKind} {columnNoun} of type '{typeTagName(tag)}' — a literal's {columnNoun}s are an integer, a float, a bool, a String, a struct, or a boxed union (a bare `[…]` infers the type from the first {columnNoun})", line: valueTok.line, column: valueTok.column)
	end 'literalColumnTypeFor'

	// Every element after the first must have the SAME element type — its inferred instance must equal the
	// first's. A mismatch is an array literal with mixed element types (E2015).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **TWO FUNCTION VALUES DO NOT ANSWER THE GID QUESTION, SO THEY ARE ASKED THE SHAPE QUESTION INSTEAD.**
	// A function element's instance is keyed on its governing NAME (`literalColumnTypeFor`), so `[double,
	// triple]` is two names for what may well be one type, and a gid compare refused it *"mixed element
	// types"* with both spellings naming the same shape. What the instance genuinely asks of an element —
	// *what does this cost to store* — has one answer for every function value: one machine word, trivially
	// copied, no destructor.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **BUT RETURNING BARE HERE WAS A WRONG ANSWER, AND THIS IS THE ONLY CHECK ON THE ROUTE.** A literal
	// reaches `requireContainerElementType` from NO path — that door's own header says so ("THE ARRAY LITERAL
	// IS NOT A CALLER … every later one is compared against it by `requireArrayLiteralElementMatches`") — and
	// nothing downstream sees it either, because `Array` is parser-intrinsic: `[a, b]` builds through
	// `__managed_create`/`__managed_push`, runtime entries carrying no `FuncSignature`, so `SemanticCheck`'s argument
	// doors have nothing to check. MEASURED: `[oneArg, twoArg]` then `try ops.get(1)` compiled and CALLED
	// `twoArg` with an argument nobody passed. The shape comparison itself still cannot happen here (it is
	// over RESOLVED parameter lists, and `ProgramSignatures` holds return types and no parameter types), so it
	// is RECORDED — the same `PendingFunctionTypeDoor` every other cross-shape question uses.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE MAP LITERAL NEEDS NO SUCH DOOR, AND ONE WAS BUILT HERE AND THEN MEASURED AWAY.** `["k": v]`
	// desugars to real `Map.upsert` CALLS (`emitMapLiteralUpsert`), so its columns are already judged by
	// `SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt`'s substituted arm — a second door in `parseHashTableColumnArg`
	// reported the same mistake twice. Deleting it left the mismatch refused exactly once and every legal map
	// working, which is what identified it as redundant rather than load-bearing.
	function requireArrayLiteralElementMatches(elem ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, elemTok Token) throws ParseError
		let declaredElement = self.signatures.containerElementType(giid)
		if self.tagOf(elem) == ValueTypeTag.function and maxonTypeTag(declaredElement) == ValueTypeTag.function 'bothAreFunctionValues'
			self.recordFunctionTypeDoorByName(self.signatures.indexSpaceTypeName(declaredElement), gotName: self.internedName(self.valueNameId(elem)), door: FunctionTypeDoor.containerElement, placeName: EmptySourceText, anchor: elemTok)
			return
		end 'bothAreFunctionValues'

		if (try self.arrayLiteralInstanceFor(elem, elemTok: elemTok)) != giid 'mismatch'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(MixedArrayLiteralElementsMessage, line: elemTok.line, column: elemTok.column)
		end 'mismatch'
	end 'requireArrayLiteralElementMatches'

	// Emit `__managed_create(elementSize, elementDestroy)` for instance `giid`, yielding the array record — the
	// shared producer for a literal and an `IntArray.create()`. Element size is a compile-time constant here
	// (a concrete instance). The element destructor is the ADDRESS of the element's drop callee for a MANAGED
	// element (P1.7 slice 3), stamped into `element_destroy@40` before any element move (v1 trap #3: `__managed_create`
	// stores it inside its body, so it is set at birth); 0 for a trivial element. NOT tracked owned — the caller
	// decides that (a literal tracks after its pushes, a `create()` immediately).
	function emitArrayCreateOp(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaque'
			return try self.emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		end 'opaque'
		try self.requireColumnDropIsNameable(giid, range: range)
		let sizeVal = self.emitLiteral(self.signatures.arrayElementSize(giid), valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let destroyVal = self.arrayElementDestroyValue(giid, range: range)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCreateName, args: pairArgs(sizeVal, second: destroyVal), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
	end 'emitArrayCreateOp'

	// ⭐ **CREATE AN OPAQUE-`T` ARRAY inside a shared generic body (P1.7 slice 3b-i).** `ElementArray.create()`
	// where `ElementArray = Array with typeParameter(token)`. Two things differ from a concrete create, both
	// because the element type is opaque here (the shared body compiles ONCE and serves every concrete
	// instance through dictionary-passing):
	//   • element_size is the fixed machine-word slot every opaque `T` occupies (`LAYOUT_TYPE_PARAM_SLOT_BYTES`),
	//     NOT a source-`sizeof` — a managed element is a pointer and a trivial one an inline word, both 8 bytes.
	//   • element_destroy@40 is not known statically, so it is READ from the enclosing instance's threaded
	//     layout descriptor at RUN TIME (`destroyFunc@40`). The parser emits a ONE-argument `__managed_create`
	//     (element_size only); LowerMaxonToStd supplies the descriptor-read second argument, so the tier that
	//     owns the descriptor's ValueId (`implicitLayoutValueId`) is the tier that reads it — the same split
	//     `sizeof(T)` uses. For a TRIVIAL instance the slot reads 0 and the array's drop walk is skipped.
	//
	// ⛔ **A LIFTED CLOSURE HAS NO DESCRIPTOR TO READ (S2i), and the refusal is now the shared
	// `requireDescriptorSourceForOpaqueCreate`** — the chain's opaque create owes the identical one. Its header
	// carries the measurement and the argument.
	function emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireDescriptorSourceForOpaqueCreate(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName(), containerNoun: OpaqueArrayCreateNoun, range: range)

		let sizeVal = self.emitLiteral(LAYOUT_TYPE_PARAM_SLOT_BYTES as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCreateName, args: singletonArgs(sizeVal), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
	end 'emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp'

	// ⛔⛔ **A LIFTED CLOSURE HAS NO DESCRIPTOR TO READ — THE ONE REFUSAL BOTH DESCRIPTOR-READING CREATES OWE
	// (W134).** Its argument is `emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp`'s header in full: the reserved descriptor parameter is
	// a GENERIC TYPE'S METHOD's, and a closure literal is lifted to a top-level function whose uniform
	// `(userargs, env)` indirect ABI carries one machine word per argument and has no slot for one — so the
	// construction is emitted into a body that carries nothing, and the LOWERING panicked with no source
	// position at all. It is asked at each opaque producer, which is why it is ONE function: the chain's
	// `create` reads the identical `destroyFunc@40` off the identical parameter, so a second copy of this test
	// is a second chance to forget it, and forgetting it aborts the compiler rather than refusing a program.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SENTENCE IS PINNED BYTE-FOR-BYTE** by `specs-shv2/inner-alias-construction.md`'s two
	// `maxoncstderr` blocks, over the `Array` spelling. The container's NAME and the noun the tail uses are the
	// only parts that vary, and at `("Array", "array")` this composes exactly the committed text.
	function requireDescriptorSourceForOpaqueCreate(containerName ByteArray, containerNoun String, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		if self.closureCaptureScopes.isEmpty() 'notInsideALiftedClosure'
			return
		end 'notInsideALiftedClosure'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("constructing an opaque type-parameter `{String.from(containerName)}` inside a CLOSURE literal — the create reads its element destructor from the enclosing instance's layout descriptor at run time, and only a generic type's own METHOD reserves the parameter that carries it: a closure is lifted to a top-level function whose uniform `(userargs, env)` ABI has no slot for one. Build the {containerNoun} in the method and capture it, or move the construction out of the closure. Threading a layout descriptor into a lifted closure is a later slice", line: range.line, column: range.column)
	end 'requireDescriptorSourceForOpaqueCreate'

	// ⭐ **THE `element_destroy@40` STAMP VALUE (P1.7 slice 3).** For a MANAGED-element array it is the RAW
	// `.text` ADDRESS of the element's drop callee (`__str_decref` / `__destruct_<T>` / `__mm_decref` / nested
	// `__managed_decref`), which `__managed_decref`'s last-owner walk calls through a `callIndirect` on each live slot;
	// for a trivial element it is 0 (an inline element owns nothing, so the walk is skipped). A CONCRETE site
	// knows the element type, so the address is the existing P1.5-A1 `funcAddr` code→register path — no rdata
	// reloc, no descriptor `destroyFunc@40` (those are the opaque-`T`-in-a-generic-body case, slice 3b).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE TWO `ProgramSignatures` ACCESSORS RATHER THAN A UNION DESCRIBING THE ANSWER, and that
	// is a measured cost decision** (P1.7 slice 2b). A top-level array global's record is stamped from the
	// same pair (`ProgramSignatures.arrayRecordInitializer`), and folding the two into one union-returning
	// door was tidier — but a Maxon union value is a HEAP OBJECT, and this runs at every array creation SITE
	// in the program: MEASURED at exactly +1 allocation per site (32 sites → +32, 64 → +64, and +32 at scale
	// rung 0 doubling to +1,024 at rung 5). The DECISION is single-homed regardless — `containerElementIsManaged`
	// is the one place "does this element own anything?" is answered, and both sites ask it — so what is
	// written twice is a three-line composition, not a rule.
	function arrayElementDestroyValue(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'managed'
			return self.emitRuntimeFuncAddr(self.signatures.arrayElementDropCallee(giid), range: range)
		end 'managed'

		return self.emitLiteral(TrivialDestructor, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'arrayElementDestroyValue'

	// Emit the RAW `.text` address of a COMPILER-INTERNAL runtime function (`__str_decref`, `__destruct_<T>`,
	// `__managed_decref`, `__mm_decref`) as a value (P1.7 slice 3). It rides P1.5-A1's `functionRef` → `funcAddr`
	// path, but names a `__`-prefixed runtime callee — which `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerFunctionRef` materializes as
	// the BARE address (NOT the `__fnref_` env thunk: the destructor is invoked through a plain `callIndirect(ptr)`
	// in the drop walk, not the uniform `(userargs, env)` closure ABI) and `scanRuntimeUsage` records so the named
	// destructor is installed (its transitive `__str_decref`/`__mm_free`/nested-cascade needs then close through
	// `noteDestructorUsage`, exactly as a scope-exit `__destruct_<T>` drop's do). The `__` prefix is the ONE
	// discriminator every site reads, so a compiler-internal address and a user function value never cross.
	function emitRuntimeFuncAddr(name ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.function, nameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(name))
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.functionRef(result, funcName: name), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitRuntimeFuncAddr'

	// ⭐ **THE STATIC SURFACE OF A BUILTIN-CONTAINER INSTANCE ALIAS — ONE dispatch, both spellings of the
	// alias.** A TOP-LEVEL alias (`IntArray`) and a NESTED one (`Container.ElementArray`) differ only in the
	// NAME the instance was keyed under, so they route through here rather than each carrying its own list of
	// which containers are claimed. Two such lists is what let a `Set` inner alias's `create()` produce a value
	// of type `unknown` (see the caller's `innerAlias` arm).
	//
	// `Array` alone has a SECOND static (`init(managed)`), so it keeps its own reader; every other container
	// is `create()`-only and shares one. A container with no usable `create` for the instance in hand is
	// refused by `requireContainerColumnTypes` with a sentence naming that container, at the `.create()` the
	// author wrote, rather than falling through to the mangled `Base.create` (a callee no file declares).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE EXAMPLE THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO GIVE WAS `List with <type parameter>`, AND IT IS NO LONGER
	// REFUSED (W134)** — a chain over an opaque element reads its element destructor off the enclosing
	// instance's layout descriptor at run time, exactly as an `Array with <type parameter>` does. What the
	// door still refuses is a NODE HANDLE, which has no `create()` at all.
	function parseContainerAliasStaticCall(func IrFunction, aliasTok Token, aliasName ByteArray, giid GenericInstanceId) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.isArrayInstance(giid) 'array'
			return try self.parseArrayStaticCall(func, aliasTok: aliasTok, aliasName: aliasName, giid: giid)
		end 'array'

		return try self.parseBuiltinContainerStaticCall(aliasTok, aliasName: aliasName, giid: giid)
	end 'parseContainerAliasStaticCall'

	// The `Array` statics. `create()` (P1.7 slice 1) yields an OWNED empty array; `init(managed)` (BATCH2
	// slice 5) ADOPTS an existing buffer as the array around it. Both serve a CONCRETE instance
	// (`Array with Integer`) and an OPAQUE one (`Array with T` inside a generic body, P1.7 slice 3b) alike —
	// `emitArrayCreateOp` branches on the element, and the adoption is element-agnostic by construction.
	//
	// `create()`: a MANAGED opaque element is fully created here (`emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp` stamps its
	// `element_destroy@40` from the threaded descriptor, P1.7 slice 3b-iii); the element READ (borrow),
	// MOVE-OUT (`__drop_type_param`) and COPY (`__managed_*_opaque` via the descriptor `copyFunc@32`, P1.7 slice
	// 3b-vi-b-β) are all served through the descriptor, never creation.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE REFUSAL BELOW NAMES BOTH, AND KEEPING IT CURRENT IS NOT COSMETIC.** It read *"P1.7 slice 1
	// provides `create()` only"* until this rung, and a reader trusts a refusal's NOUN over the source — a
	// stale one sends them looking for a mechanism that is one line away.
	function parseArrayStaticCall(func IrFunction, aliasTok Token, aliasName ByteArray, giid GenericInstanceId) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.between(aliasTok, last: methodTok)

		if methodTok.value.equals(CreateMethod) 'createStatic'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return try self.emitOwnedContainerCreate(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok, aliasName: aliasName, range: range)
		end 'createStatic'

		// `StringInitMethod` is the ONE constant over the spelling `init`, not a borrowed one: this file's
		// method-name block states the rule (*"a method NAME is a token spelling, not a container's
		// property"*) and `parseStringLiteralInitConstruction` already builds a USER type's `Type.init` callee
		// from this very constant. A second `b"init"` here would be the one-fact-written-twice it exists to stop.
		// (What its `String`-prefixed NAME now understates is a naming debt, not a second fact.)
		if methodTok.value.equals(StringInitMethod) 'initStatic'
			return try self.parseArrayInitStatic(func, giid: giid, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'initStatic'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`Array` static method '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — shv2 provides two: `create()` and `init(managed)`", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'parseArrayStaticCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **`ByteArray.init(managed)` — ADOPT A BUFFER AS THE ARRAY AROUND IT (BATCH2 slice 5).**
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon:113-115` declares `init(managed ElementMemory) returns Self` with the body
	// `return Self{managed: managed}`, where `ElementMemory = __ManagedMemory with Element`. In shv2 that
	// struct literal is an IDENTITY on the record rather than a construction: `Array with T` and
	// `__ManagedMemory with T` are ONE record with TWO SURFACES (`SignatureIndex.ManagedMemoryTypeName` is
	// registered as a generic ALIAS of the `Array with Byte` instance), so there is no second record for the
	// buffer to become a field of. No allocation, no copy, no element walk.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT `String.init(managed)`'S ARM, WHICH COPIES** (`__str_from_bytes`). The two
	// statics share a name, a parameter name and an argument door, and the copy is CORRECT there for a reason
	// that does not hold here: a String record differs from an Array record at slot `@40`, so it has to build
	// one. Copying here would defeat the static's entire purpose — the caller fills a raw buffer and then
	// names it as the container.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE EMISSION IS `retainBorrowedAggregate`, AND BOTH OF ITS PROPERTIES ARE LOAD-BEARING.**
	//   • **A FRESH `ValueId` IS WHAT FLIPS THE SURFACE.** The two surfaces are told apart by PROVENANCE, and
	//     the ARRAY surface is the ABSENCE of the buffer mark (`markBufferSurface`; `bufferSurfaceValues` is
	//     only ever inserted into). The argument typically CARRIES that mark, so a pure identity handing back
	//     its own `ValueId` would return a value still dispatching on the BUFFER roster —
	//     `init(…).push(…)` refused as an unknown `Array` method, the roster exactly inverted.
	//     `array-init.the-surface-flips` is the case that says so.
	//   • **THE REFCOUNT BALANCES.** The source is still live and still drops at its own scope exit, so the
	//     adopted array must be a SECOND owner, not a stolen one. `__mm_retain` raises the same refcount word
	//     `__managed_decref` lowers — both go through `MmRuntime.emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner` — so
	//     `let a = ByteArray.init(mm)` traces rc 0 → 1 → (`a` drops) 0 → (`mm` drops) freed.
	//
	// The retain and the statement-scoped drop obligation come as ONE call (`coOwnAggregateAsTemp`), shared
	// with `bufferSurfaceOf` — the inverse direction, which owes the identical pair for the identical reason:
	// a static CALL's result may be bound to nothing (`ByteArray.init(mm).count()`), so the bare owned-heap
	// bit `retainBorrowedAggregate` sets is not enough. See that door for why the two halves travel together.
	function parseArrayInitStatic(func IrFunction, giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let managed = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.requireSameArrayInstance(managed, giid: giid, anchor: methodTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		return self.coOwnAggregateAsTemp(managed, range: range)
	end 'parseArrayInitStatic'

	// ⭐ **THE `init(managed)` ARGUMENT RULE IS SAME-INSTANCE — AND THE OBVIOUS DOOR IS THE WRONG ONE.**
	// `String.init` checks its argument with `requireManagedMemoryValue`, which is BYTE-ONLY
	// (`valueIsByteArray` requires `arrayElementSize == 1`). That is right for a String — it is reading the
	// bytes — and wrong here in the direction that costs a WRONG ANSWER rather than a refusal: it would
	// ACCEPT `IntArray.init(<a byte buffer>)`, an `Array with Integer` striding by 8 through a byte-packed
	// allocation, giving back whichever bytes fell on the boundaries. So `String.init`'s door is left alone —
	// it still needs the byte rule — and this is a second rule BESIDE it rather than a widening of it.
	//
	// The rule: the argument's generic instance must EQUAL the receiver alias's. That is element-agnostic and
	// strictly STRONGER than the byte rule, and it is what keeps the element STRIDE honest at every element
	// type rather than at one. `array-init.error.a-buffer-of-the-wrong-element` is the refusal.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY ADOPTION REACHABLE TODAY IS NONETHELESS A BYTE ONE, AND THAT IS THE `Array_int` GAP SHOWING
	// THROUGH — NOT THIS RULE.** `managedMemoryInstanceForElementSize` mints exactly two instances, and the
	// word one is `Array with int`, which NO program can name: `Array with int` is E2061 (a bare `int` is not
	// a type argument) and every ranged alias over it interns a different instance — the same `Array_int` vs
	// `Array_Integer` split `specs-shv2/default-values.md` and `string-views.md` already pin for array
	// literals. MEASURED, all three rows, in `specs-shv2/array-init.md`. Keying on element WIDTH instead
	// would reach the word row only by giving up the soundness above — a managed element and an `int` are
	// both 8 bytes wide, so an `Array with String` would adopt a buffer of raw integers as String pointers.
	//
	// ⚠ The sentence names `__ManagedMemory with <element>` because that is what the SIGNATURE declares
	// (`ElementMemory`), not the `Array` spelling of the same instance — the split `ParseError`'s two
	// builtin-argument sentences already draw, for the same reason.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS FOR THE INSTANCE, NOT FOR THE BUFFER MARK, AND THE NAME SAYS SO (review, BATCH2 s5).**
	// It was called `requireSameInstanceBuffer`, which claimed a test it does not make: an ORDINARY array of
	// the same instance is admitted too, and adopting one is an ordinary co-owning retain
	// (`array-init.an-ordinary-array-of-the-instance-adopts` pins it). Keying on `valueIsBufferSurface`
	// instead would not merely be stricter, it would be WRONG — the mark is a per-function fact about a
	// `ValueId` and it does not survive a CLOSURE CAPTURE (MEASURED: inside a closure body `mm.length()` is
	// already refused, while `ByteArray.init(mm)` must still adopt — `array-init.a-captured-buffer-adopts-
	// inside-a-closure`). A door keyed on the mark would refuse the `stdlib/File.maxon:135` shape the moment
	// it appeared inside a closure, for a reason having nothing to do with the element stride this rule
	// exists to keep honest. The instance is the fact the stride depends on, so the instance is what is asked.
	// ⚠⚠ **AND IT ADMITS THE BYTE-BUFFER BOUNDARY, WHICH IT DID NOT UNTIL W5 — THE NINTH DOOR OF THE CLASS
	// `byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`'s header ENUMERATES, found by the module the boundary exists to serve.**
	// ⚠ NINTH, and the only one that does NOT ride `aggregatesConflict`: the eight that header measured are
	// aggregate comparisons, and this rule is raw instance EQUALITY (see the paragraph above for why), so it
	// inherited nothing from them and had to be given the door by name.
	// A COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED byte buffer wears the reserved element `__ManagedByte`, deliberately a DIFFERENT
	// instance from the user-visible `Byte` (`SignatureIndex`'s two-names roster: a user may declare `Byte`,
	// and a compiler-minted buffer's stride may not follow). Raw instance EQUALITY therefore refuses it, and
	// `stdlib/Console.maxon:68` is exactly that program: `ByteArray.init(__Builtins.readStdin(n))`, refused
	// `E3005 argument type mismatch for 'managed': expected '__ManagedMemory with Byte', got
	// 'Array___ManagedByte'` — MEASURED on this rung's own readiness probe, on a module the BOOTSTRAP
	// compiles.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE ADMISSION IS THE SHARED THREE-QUESTION DOOR AND NOT A FOURTH SPELLING OF IT**, which is that
	// header's own instruction: the receiver's element must be NAMED for a byte, must hold EVERY byte value
	// in every file that declares it, and its record must stride ONE byte — and only then is the arriving
	// `__ManagedByte` admitted. All three are load-bearing here for the same reasons they are at the other
	// eight sites: a user program may contest `Byte` with `typealias Byte = int(0 to 200)` (whole-program, by
	// NAME — `export` is not read), and adopting a raw byte buffer as an array of THAT element is the
	// measured 223 the second question exists to refuse. Under all three the stride is provably 1 on both
	// sides, which is the exact property this function protects.
	//
	// ⚠ It stays ONE-WAY, like every other use of that door: only the COMPILER's own mint is admitted at a
	// declared byte position. A user's `Array with Byte` is not admitted at a `__ManagedByte` position,
	// because nothing mints a value at that spelling but the compiler.
	function requireSameArrayInstance(value ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.valueNameId(value) == giid 'sameInstance'
			return
		end 'sameInstance'

		if self.signatures.byteBufferBoundaryAdmits(ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, declaredNameId: giid, gotTag: self.tagOf(value), gotNameId: self.valueNameId(value)) 'aSynthesizedByteBuffer'
			return
		end 'aSynthesizedByteBuffer'

		throw ParseError.builtinArgumentType(String.from(StringInitManagedParamName), expected: "{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)} with {self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid)}", got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(value), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireSameArrayInstance'

	// --- The `create()` static surface the compiler-owned containers SHARE (`Set` / `Map` / `List`) ---

	// ⭐ **`IntSet.create()` / `StrMap.create()` / `PointList.create()` — ONE PARSE.** Every container whose
	// whole static surface is `create()` is born here: consume `.<name>`, refuse anything but `create`, consume
	// the empty argument list, gate the element/column types, emit the create, and TRACK THE RESULT as an owned
	// temporary so an unbound one drops at statement end and a bound one transfers to its binding.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE `trackOwnedTemp` IS THE REASON THIS IS ONE FUNCTION AND NOT THREE.** It was written three times,
	// and a fourth container whose copy omitted it would LEAK with no diagnostic anywhere — the record is
	// created, nothing tracks it, and `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` is never asked. The two things a container
	// genuinely does differently (the sentence its unknown static is refused with, and the gate/emit pair) are
	// the two routers below; the skeleton around them cannot drift because there is one of it.
	//
	// `Array` is NOT here: its static surface is several methods with arguments (`parseArrayStaticCall`), so it
	// shares the skeleton with nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **`aliasName` IS THE CALLER'S AND IS NEVER RE-DERIVED FROM `aliasTok`** (S2i review). The two doors
	// spell the key differently — a top-level alias is its own token, a NESTED one is base-qualified
	// (`Container.ElementArray`) — so `aliasTok.value` is the right key for one of them and the wrong one for
	// the other. It is only ever read by the producer's `Array` arm today, which this router cannot reach, so
	// a bare `aliasTok.value` here answered nothing and was merely a SECOND derivation of a name the caller
	// had already computed correctly: the day a non-`Array` container carries an element-spelling stamp it
	// would have started answering, wrongly, for every inner alias.
	function parseBuiltinContainerStaticCall(aliasTok Token, aliasName ByteArray, giid GenericInstanceId) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

		if not methodTok.value.equals(CreateMethod) 'unknownStatic'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(self.containerStaticSurfaceSentence(giid, method: methodTok.value), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'unknownStatic'

		try self.consumeEmptyArgList()

		return try self.emitOwnedContainerCreate(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok, aliasName: aliasName, range: SourceRange.between(aliasTok, last: methodTok))
	end 'parseBuiltinContainerStaticCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE OWNED EMPTY CONTAINER AN INSTANCE ALIAS DENOTES — the ONE producer behind BOTH spellings**,
	// `<Alias>.create()` and `<Alias>{}` (S2i). The two are the same value by measurement, not by intention:
	// the oracle's `ElementArray{}` is `resize`d and `count()`s back exactly as its `ElementArray.create()`
	// does. A second producer for the literal would be a second answer to "what does an empty `Array with T`
	// cost?", free to drift from this one the day a header, an element stamp or the buffer surface changes.
	//
	// `Array` is its own arm and is UNGATED, exactly as it has always been: its element rule is the instance's
	// own, so `requireContainerColumnTypes` returns for it, and it alone carries the buffer-surface stamp.
	//
	// ⭐ **`BufArray.create()` YIELDS AN ARRAY OF BUFFERS (A2m)** — asked of the ALIAS NAME and never of
	// `giid`, which cannot answer: `Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray` intern to the SAME
	// instance. The name is the caller's because the doors spell it differently — a top-level alias is its own
	// token, a nested one is base-qualified — and only they know which.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONTAINER, PLUS THE PROVENANCE OF THE NAME THE SITE BUILT IT UNDER (P1.6-C, extended at R-1).**
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A BUILTIN CONTAINER'S FACTORY RECORDED NO SPELLING AT ALL, AND AFTER R-1 THAT IS A WRONG ANSWER IN
	// A DIAGNOSTIC.** Two ranged aliases over one range are ONE instance, so `WideCol` and `OtherCol` share a
	// gid and only the written spelling can say which one `takesNarrow(OtherCol.create())` passed. The
	// provenance column existed and this door never wrote it: `retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult` is where a
	// DECLARED generic's factory result gets stamped, and a builtin container never reaches it — its result is
	// already an instance rather than the shared body's base `structRef`. **MEASURED: a probe here counted ZERO
	// calls to that door for `OtherCol.create()`**, and the diagnostic named the first-declared alias in the
	// file, where the bootstrap names the one at the site.
	//
	// ⚠ It is stamped in ONE place rather than in each of the three arms, because it is one fact about the
	// CALL and not about which kind of container the call built.
	function emitOwnedContainerCreate(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token, aliasName ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let container = try self.emitOwnedContainerRecord(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok, aliasName: aliasName, range: range)
		// The stamp is PAID FOR ONLY WHERE IT CAN BE READ. A diagnostic consults the provenance exactly when
		// the gid has more than one author spelling, which R-1's merge decided before this parse began
		// (`deriveInstanceDisplayNames` runs at the end of the signature sweep) — so an instance one alias
		// spells records nothing and a program with no merge at all pays one set probe per container it
		// builds. MEASURED ungated on the scale ladder: +2,069 allocations at rung 0 rising to +16,357 at
		// rung 5, for a spelling nothing would ever ask for.
		if self.signatures.instanceIsMultiplyAliased(giid) and self.signatures.isGenericAlias(aliasName) 'aWrittenAliasWorthRemembering'
			self.recordValueInstanceAlias(container, aliasName: aliasName)
		end 'aWrittenAliasWorthRemembering'

		return container
	end 'emitOwnedContainerCreate'

	// The record itself — one arm per container kind. Split from the stamping above so the provenance is written
	// once for all three rather than at each `return`.
	function emitOwnedContainerRecord(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token, aliasName ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.isArrayInstance(giid) 'array'
			let arr = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)
			self.trackOwnedTemp(arr, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
			if self.signatures.aliasElementIsBufferSurface(aliasName, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'elementsAreBuffers'
				self.markBufferSurfaceElements(arr)
			end 'elementsAreBuffers'

			return arr
		end 'array'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S RECORD IS BORN WITH ITS SLOTS PUBLISHED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT
		// STAYED THE COMPILER'S WHEN `Vector.create()` RETIRED (W190).** `Vec3.create()` is now an ordinary
		// call to `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s declared static, whose body is `return Self{}` — and this arm is
		// what that literal MEANS. It is a fact about the TYPE, exactly as the element stride is: a
		// `Vector with 3 Int` IS a three-slot record, so a literal of it publishes three slots however the
		// program spelled the construction. The bootstrap answers identically from the identical corpus line
		// — its monomorphized `Vec3.create` writes `length@8 = 3` off the same const argument.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE COUNT IS `fixedElementCountValue`'s, NOT `genericInstanceFixedSize`'s, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS
		// THE RETIREMENT.** Inside `Vector.create` the instance is the declaration's own countless `Self`
		// (`ownRecordSelfInstance`, interned at `NoFixedSize` so ONE body serves every size), so the size is a
		// fact about the CALLER and arrives in the hidden count slot; at a concrete `Vec3{}` it folds to 3.
		// Reading the registry directly here would publish ZERO slots in the one body that matters.
		if self.signatures.isVectorInstance(giid) 'sizedContainer'
			let sized = try self.emitSizedContainerRecord(giid, anchor: aliasTok, range: range)
			self.trackOwnedTemp(sized, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
			return sized
		end 'sizedContainer'

		let container = try self.gateAndEmitContainerCreate(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok, range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(container, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return container
	end 'emitOwnedContainerRecord'

	// ⭐ **THE EMPTY RECORD, THEN ITS SLOTS PUBLISHED** — `__managed_create` yields a record with no slots
	// (every index out of bounds) and `__managed_resize` grows it to the count and ZEROES the new range, which
	// together are exactly *"zero-initialized, N elements"*. `element_destroy@40` stays 0, which
	// `requireVectorElementType` is what makes correct.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOT `__managed_mem_create(N, elementSize)`, WHICH PUBLISHES `N` SLOTS IN ONE CALL AND WOULD
	// OTHERWISE BE THE OBVIOUS FIT.** That entry is THROWING (`isManagedMemoryMemberCallee` — it reports
	// `invalidAllocation` for a size the allocator cannot use), so a `create()` built on it is
	// **E3057 throwing function requires try: 'create'** on every one of `/specs/vector.md`'s cases, which
	// write a bare `create()`. Measured, on the first build of the vector port. The two-call form throws
	// nothing: `__managed_resize` rejects only a NEGATIVE length, and an element count cannot be one.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT REACHES `__managed_resize` WITHOUT PASSING `requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement`, WHOSE E3106 IS
	// THE RULE THAT NORMALLY MAKES A PUBLISHED SLOT SAFE TO READ.** `resize` grows a record by ZEROING the new
	// range, so a published slot of a MANAGED element is a null pointer every accessor will dereference. The
	// source-level rule cannot be reused here (its diagnostic is about a `resize` call the author wrote, and
	// a vector's author wrote `create()`), so the guarantee is carried by `requireVectorElementType` instead,
	// at `requireSizedContainerElementType` — the construction door, where the element is concrete. **The two
	// are one invariant with two doors — do not relax either without the other.**
	function emitSizedContainerRecord(giid GenericInstanceId, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let record = try self.emitArrayCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		let count = try self.fixedElementCountValue(giid, anchor: anchor)
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedResizeName, args: pairArgs(record, second: count), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		return record
	end 'emitSizedContainerRecord'

	// What each container says when the author names a static it does not have. A per-container ROUTER rather
	// than a name threaded into one template, because the tails are not the same sentence with a noun swapped —
	// a node handle and a cursor are constructible by NOTHING, where a chain is `create()`-only.
	//
	// ⚠ A `List` arm stood first until W153, and a `Vector` arm second until W190. Each declaring module
	// carries the type now, so a bad static on either is the ordinary
	// `E3004 call to undefined function '<Base>.<method>'` every declared type answers with, and this router
	// is never reached for one — `parseQualifiedCall`'s container interception asks `instanceCreateIsDeclared`
	// and lets both fall through to the declared-static road.
	function containerStaticSurfaceSentence(giid GenericInstanceId, method ByteArray) returns String
		if self.signatures.isManagedListInstance(giid) 'managedList'
			return "`{String.from(ManagedListBuiltinBaseName)}` static method '{String.from(method)}' — shv2 provides `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` only"
		end 'managedList'
		// ⛔ A NODE HANDLE HAS NO STATIC SURFACE AT ALL — not even `create()` — so its sentence names the
		// method the author wrote and offers nothing in its place. It lives here rather than beside the gate
		// that used to raise it because this is where "what does this container offer?" is answered, and both
		// doors that refuse a node now read it from here.
		if self.signatures.isManagedListNodeInstance(giid) 'nodeHandle'
			return "`{String.from(ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName)}` static method '{String.from(method)}' — a node handle has no static surface at all, not even `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()`: one exists only because a `{String.from(ManagedListBuiltinBaseName)}` insertion linked it, and `insertFirst`/`insertLast` hand back the handle"
		end 'nodeHandle'

		// ⛔⛔ **A CURSOR IS THE NODE HANDLE'S TWIN AND THIS ROUTER HAD NO ARM FOR IT — a user-reachable
		// COMPILER PANIC, found by the W153 review.** `ManagedMemoryCursorBuiltinBaseName`'s own header states
		// the fact (*"the SECOND type no source can construct — only `__ManagedMemory.createCursor()` mints
		// one"*), and the cursor is on `isBuiltinGenericBaseNameBesidesTheRecord`, so
		// `typealias Cur = __ManagedMemoryCursor with Int` + `Cur.frobnicate()` reached this panic and
		// `Cur.create()` reached `requireContainerColumnTypes`' — both MEASURED, both a stack trace in front of
		// an author. It is the roster-written-twice shape: the cursor joined the builtin roster and the two
		// "what does this container offer" doors were never told.
		if self.signatures.isManagedMemoryCursorInstance(giid) 'cursor'
			return "`{String.from(ManagedMemoryCursorBuiltinBaseName)}` static method '{String.from(method)}' — a cursor has no static surface at all, not even `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()`: one exists only because a `{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}` handed it out, and `{String.from(ManagedMemoryCreateCursorMethod)}()` is what mints one"
		end 'cursor'

		panic("Parser.containerStaticSurfaceSentence: generic instance {giid} is not a builtin container — every caller opens with the builtin-base test `requireContainerIsCreatable` makes, and a container admitted there owes a sentence here")
	end 'containerStaticSurfaceSentence'

	// Gate this container's element/column types and emit its `create` — ONE router, so the gate and the emit
	// cannot come to disagree about which container they are serving. The gate anchors its diagnostic on the
	// ALIAS token (the type the author wrote is what is inadmissible), the emit spans alias→method.
	function gateAndEmitContainerCreate(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireContainerIsCreatable(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok)

		// ⚠ **A `Vector` ARM STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH `emitVectorCreateOp` (W190)** — the create whose ANSWER
		// IS THE SIZE, which `countof(Self)` is what let a shared corpus body name. `stdlib/Vector.maxon`
		// declares `create()`, so `Vec3.create()` never reaches this router: `instanceCreateIsDeclared`
		// answers TRUE for it and both create doors take the declared road instead.
		if self.signatures.isManagedListInstance(giid) 'managedList'
			return try self.emitListCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		end 'managedList'
		panic("Parser.gateAndEmitContainerCreate: generic instance {giid} is not a create-only builtin container — every caller opens with the builtin-base test `requireContainerIsCreatable` makes, and a container admitted there owes a gate and an emit here")
	end 'gateAndEmitContainerCreate'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY `<Alias>.create()` BE WRITTEN FOR THIS BUILTIN CONTAINER — the WHOLE gate, asked from BOTH
	// positions.** A body's `create()` and a top-level `var g = Alias.create()` reach it through
	// `gateAndEmitContainerCreate` and `requireGlobalContainerIsCreatable` respectively, so what one refuses
	// cannot become admissible by being written at the other. It anchors every diagnostic on the ALIAS token:
	// the type the author wrote is what is inadmissible. Two steps, in the order a reader would ask them —
	// does this container have a `create`, and are its columns admissible.
	//
	// ⚠ **PRECONDITION: THE INSTANCE'S BASE IS A BUILTIN CONTAINER**, which both callers establish
	// (`isBuiltinGenericBaseName` at `parseBuiltinContainerStaticCall`'s call sites; the router's own arm at
	// the top-level door). It used to carry that test itself and refuse a DECLARED generic outright — the
	// refusal W41 removed, because a declared generic now has something better than the builtin registry's
	// description: a real `create()` static `__module_init` can CALL (`ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall`).
	// A break of the precondition is not silent: `instanceHasBuiltinCreate` admits anything that is not a node
	// handle, and `requireContainerColumnTypes` below then meets its named panic.
	function requireContainerIsCreatable(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⛔ A NODE HANDLE IS NOT CONSTRUCTIBLE — only an insertion mints one — so a `typealias N =
		// __ManagedListNode with T` + `N.create()` is refused with the sentence
		// `containerStaticSurfaceSentence` owns, rather than reaching either router's panic, which is about a
		// container they forgot rather than one that has no create.
		if not self.signatures.instanceHasBuiltinCreate(giid) 'noCreate'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(self.containerStaticSurfaceSentence(giid, method: CreateMethod), line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column)
		end 'noCreate'

		try self.requireContainerColumnTypes(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok)
	end 'requireContainerIsCreatable'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY A GLOBAL HOLD `<Alias>.create()`'s EMPTY RECORD — THE TOP-LEVEL DOOR'S WHOLE GATE, AND IT
	// ROUTES ON WHO OWNS THE `create` (W41).** `genericAliases` holds DECLARED instances
	// (`typealias StrMap = Map with (String, Integer)`, now that `stdlib/Map.maxon` is listed) beside the
	// builtin ones, and the two are admissible for different reasons: a builtin container's record is the
	// runtime's and must pass the column rules that runtime's stamps encode, while a declared generic's is
	// built by a `create()` static the program wrote and must pass the rules a CALL to that static rests on.
	// One question — *may this instance's empty record be a global?* — with the answer routed on
	// `instanceCreateIsDeclared`, the same derivation the emitted description is routed on
	// (`ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall`), so a gate can never approve a shape the emitter has no arm for.
	function requireGlobalContainerIsCreatable(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.instanceCreateIsDeclared(giid) 'declaredGeneric'
			try self.requireSizedContainerElementType(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok)
			try self.requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok)
			return
		end 'declaredGeneric'

		try self.requireContainerIsCreatable(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok)
	end 'requireGlobalContainerIsCreatable'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S ELEMENT RULE, ASKED AT A CONSTRUCTION THAT IS NOW A CALL TO A DECLARED
	// `create()` (W190).** `requireVectorElementType` used to hang off `requireContainerColumnTypes`, i.e.
	// off the compiler's own create; with `stdlib/Vector.maxon` owning that create the gate has no emit to
	// ride on, and the rule it enforces is untouched by who writes the body — **a zeroed slot is an ELEMENT
	// only for an element that lives inline in the buffer**, and the corpus `create()` publishes N of them
	// through `setLength` exactly as `__managed_resize` did. Left behind, the two symptoms
	// `requireVectorElementType`'s own header measured come straight back: `count()` answering N while every
	// `get` reports an empty slot, and a `for … in` read dereferencing the nulls (0xC0000005).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF THE INSTANCE BEING CONSTRUCTED AND NOT OF THE STATIC BEING CALLED**, because
	// `init(managed)` adopts a buffer into the same inadmissible record that `create()` would publish — the
	// element is a fact about the TYPE, and the construction is where a type with no admissible element
	// stops being an abstraction. Every other constructed generic returns immediately, so a `StrMap.create()`
	// pays one instance-registry read.
	function requireConstructedVectorElementType(constructed ConstructorInstance, aliasTok Token) throws ParseError
		match constructed 'constructed'
			none then return
			instance(gid) then try self.requireSizedContainerElementType(gid, aliasTok: aliasTok)
		end 'constructed'
	end 'requireConstructedVectorElementType'

	// The rule itself, given a resolved instance — written once because the BODY door holds a
	// `ConstructorInstance` and the TOP-LEVEL door holds a bare id, and a second copy of "is this a vector,
	// and is its element admissible" is how one position would come to admit what the other refuses.
	function requireSizedContainerElementType(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.isVectorInstance(giid) 'notTheSizedContainer'
			return
		end 'notTheSizedContainer'

		try self.requireVectorElementType(giid, tok: aliasTok)
	end 'requireSizedContainerElementType'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A DECLARED GENERIC'S `create()` MUST BE FOR `__module_init` TO CALL IT (W41).** The whole of
	// what replaced the blanket refusal. Three clauses, each a premise of the emitted call rather than a
	// defensive check:
	//
	//   * **IT MUST EXIST.** `Base.create` is the name the emitted call carries
	//     (`declaredInstanceCreateCallee`); a generic that declares no `create()` would emit a call to nothing
	//     and die at the backend's unresolved symbol, so it is refused here with the base named.
	//   * **IT MUST BE NAMEABLE FROM THIS FILE** — `requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible`'s rule, which the user
	//     factory form asks too and which carries the measurement for both.
	//   * **IT MUST RETURN ITS OWN TYPE.** The slot is typed `genericInstance(giid)` and the record's layout
	//     descriptor and constraint witnesses are sourced from the call's RESULT, which is the concrete
	//     instance only for a `Self`-returning static (`ProgramSignatures.calleeReturnsItsDeclaringType`, whose
	//     header states both sides of that ABI). A `create()` returning anything else would have its result
	//     minted as a record the callee never built — a wrong answer at run time and a decref of it at exit.
	//     Asked through the SAME predicate `LowerMaxonToStd.witnessParamCountForCallee` sizes the hidden block
	//     by, so the gate and the ABI cannot disagree about which statics are constructors.
	//
	// ⚠ **ARITY IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE — IT IS `SemanticCheck.validateCall`'s, for every top-level
	// initializer alike.** `installManagedGlobalInit` runs before the pipeline, so the emitted call is slotted
	// against the callee's signature by the same pass that slots a body's, and a `create()` with a required
	// parameter is E3036 at the global's own range. The user factory form (`evalConstFactoryCallInto`) leaves it
	// there for the same reason; re-asking it here would be a second arity rule, free to disagree with the one
	// the call is actually checked by.
	//
	// ⛔ **AN UNDECLARED BASE IS NOT THIS DOOR'S TO DESCRIBE.** `typealias B = Nonexistent with Count` interns
	// an instance like any other, and `instanceCreateIsDeclared` — the complement of the BUILTIN roster —
	// answers true for it. Saying "`Nonexistent` declares no `create()`" would describe the program wrongly:
	// its fault is that nothing declares the base at all, which `checkGenericInstance` has already recorded as
	// E2055. `genericInstanceBaseIsKnown` is the split that exists to keep those two apart, and its header
	// says they must not be collapsed.
	//
	// Every refusal is anchored on the ALIAS token: the type the author wrote is what is inadmissible, which
	// is where `requireContainerIsCreatable` anchors its own.
	function requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseIsKnown(giid) 'undeclaredBase'
			return
		end 'undeclaredBase'

		let baseName = self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(giid)
		let callee = self.signatures.declaredInstanceCreateCallee(giid)

		if not self.signatures.declaresCallee(callee) 'noCreateStatic'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a top-level `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` — `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}` instantiates the generic `{String.from(baseName)}`, which declares no `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` static, so `__module_init` has nothing to call before `main`. Give `{String.from(baseName)}` one, or build the value inside a function", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column)
		end 'noCreateStatic'

		try self.requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible(callee, anchor: aliasTok)

		if not self.signatures.calleeReturnsItsDeclaringType(callee) 'notAConstructor'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a top-level `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` — `{String.from(baseName)}.{String.from(CreateMethod)}` does not return `Self`, and a global declared this way holds the INSTANCE its alias names. A generic's layout descriptor and witnesses are read off what its `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` hands back, so only a `Self`-returning one can build `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}`. Build the value inside a function", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column)
		end 'notAConstructor'
	end 'requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY THE DECLARING FILE NAME A FUNCTION `__module_init` WILL CALL ON ITS BEHALF? — the ONE home,
	// asked by BOTH calling initializer forms.** A body's call site gets this from
	// `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom`; this position cannot, because `__module_init` is a function no file
	// wrote and that pass therefore EXEMPTS every call it makes — there is no reader path to hold them to
	// (see `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom`'s synthesized-caller arm, which names both doors). Without the
	// question asked here, an exported type's file-private `static` would be reachable from any file that
	// writes `var db = Database.create()`. MEASURED before the guard existed: a cross-file file-private
	// `Database.create` built and returned 7, where the byte-identical call from inside a function is E3008.
	//
	// It is asked HERE, where the reader file is known exactly, against the sweep's own visibility column and
	// through the very same `isVisibleFrom` the body door uses — so the two askers can disagree about no
	// declaration they both see. The refusing TIER rides back with the verdict because it picks the CODE
	// (E3008 vs E3088).
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE RULE, TWO ANCHORS.** The factory form blames the METHOD token it wrote; the container form has
	// no method token worth blaming — `create` is the only static that door admits — so it blames the ALIAS,
	// which is where every other refusal on that path is anchored. That is why the anchor is the caller's and
	// the rule is not.
	function requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible(callee ByteArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.calleeVisibilityFrom(callee, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'calleeVisibility'
			visible then return
			hidden(tier) then throw ParseError.calleeNotVisible(String.from(callee), tier: tier, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'calleeVisibility'
	end 'requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible'

	// ⭐ **THE ELEMENT/COLUMN GATE ONE CONTAINER'S `create` PASSES.** Its caller has already established that
	// the instance is a builtin container with a `create`, so this is a TOTAL router over the THREE that have
	// one: `Array` alone has no column rule (its element rule is the instance's own) and says so by returning,
	// and a FOURTH container whose rule someone forgets meets the panic rather than passing ungated.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COUNT HERE READ "five" / "a SIXTH" AND WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BY THE W153 REVIEW.** It
	// counted the whole builtin roster rather than the arms this router owes, `List` left that roster with the
	// retirement, and the cursor — counted as having a `create` — was the one whose absent arm reached the
	// panic below (see `ProgramSignatures.instanceHasBuiltinCreate`). A count written beside a router is a
	// roster written twice; this one is now the arm count, which is the only number this text can check.
	function requireContainerColumnTypes(giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.isArrayInstance(giid) 'array'
			return
		end 'array'
		if self.signatures.isManagedListInstance(giid) 'managedList'
			try self.requireListElementType(giid, tok: aliasTok)
			return
		end 'managedList'
		panic("Parser.requireContainerColumnTypes: generic instance {giid} is a builtin container with a `{String.from(CreateMethod)}` and no column rule — `requireContainerIsCreatable` admitted it, and a container admitted there owes an arm here even if that arm is `Array`'s bare return")
	end 'requireContainerColumnTypes'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE EMPTY CONTAINER EVERY MEMBER-BY-MEMBER DOOR STARTS FROM — the corpus type's own `create()`.**
	// Its callers are the `CharacterSet` member set (a preset's seed and a `CharSet from […]`), the corpus arm
	// of `Set from […]`, a `[k: v]` map literal, and `List from […]`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE RESULT IS ALREADY ENROLLED AS AN OWNED TEMPORARY, SO NO CALLER MAY TRACK IT.** `emitCall`
	// classifies its result +1 owned by the call machinery itself (`enrolOwnedCallTemp`); a caller that
	// tracked it as well would enrol ONE record twice and drop it twice.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "IN BOTH ARMS, BY DIFFERENT MEANS", AND THAT WORDING OUTLIVED ITS
	// SECOND ARM.** While a builtin `__set_create` / `__map_create` op existed, a runtime op owned nothing
	// until `trackOwnedTemp` said so, and the enrolment lived HERE precisely so the two arms could not
	// disagree. W105 deleted the runtime arm; the conditional half of the rule went with it and the
	// unconditional half is all that is left. The `Map` caller kept the now-wrong `trackOwnedTemp`
	// and double-dropped every map literal used as a temporary — see the grave at `parseMapLiteralBody`'s
	// closing brace, which is where that was found and measured.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT TAKES THE BASE NAME BECAUSE THIS WAS THREE FUNCTIONS AND THE THIRD WAS WRITTEN AT W153.**
	// `emitSetCreate`, `emitMapLiteralCreate` and `emitListCreate` were three bodies identical to the byte
	// apart from `SetBuiltinBaseName` / `MapBuiltinBaseName` / `ListBuiltinBaseName`, each header calling the
	// others its "twin" — a fact written three times, where the RETYPE below is the step a fourth copy is free
	// to omit and the omission is not a compile error but the measured
	// *"materializeStaticConstructorLayout: layout-needing static callee 'Map.create' returns a structRef"*
	// panic. Every container the retirement chain lists arrives at this door, so the door takes the name.
	function emitContainerLiteralCreate(baseName ByteArray, giid GenericInstanceId, anchor Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		var argLabels = emptyArgLabels(args)
		var argRanges = uniformArgRanges(args, range: range)
		let container = try self.emitCall(qualifiedMethodName(baseName, methodName: CreateMethod), anchor: anchor, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: anchor, resultUsed: true, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.instance(giid), mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)

		// ⭐ **THE RESULT IS THE INSTANCE, AND SAYING SO IS THE CALLER'S JOB — `emitCall` does not do it.**
		// `constructorInstance` is read by the CONSUME side alone; the type half is this separate step, exactly
		// as `parseQualifiedCall` does it after its own `parseCallNamed`. Without it `<Base>.create` hands back
		// the shared body's base `structRef` and the very next lowering step refuses it with the
		// `materializeStaticConstructorLayout` panic quoted above (measured on `Map`). A literal has no written
		// alias to carry provenance from, which is what the empty name means here.
		self.retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult(container, gid: giid, aliasName: EmptySourceText)
		return container
	end 'emitContainerLiteralCreate'

	// ⭐ **ONE MEMBER INTO A MEMBER-BY-MEMBER CONTAINER LITERAL — `emitContainerLiteralCreate`'s twin.** The
	// call is a VOID mutator; the container OWNS the value from here, and the declared parameter
	// (`Set.insert(element Element)`, `List.append(value Element)`) is what says so. Both declare it
	// POSITIONALLY, so the desugared call spells no label — the half `emitMapLiteralUpsert` has to spell and
	// this one must not, which is the one thing that keeps that function separate from this one.
	//
	// ⛔ **`emitSetInsert` AND `emitListAppend` WERE THIS FUNCTION TWICE.** They differed in the base name and
	// the method name and in nothing else — not in the ownership rule, which is the load-bearing part: a
	// SECOND, parse-time move here is the LEAK `parseSetMemberList`'s `consume: false` note measures, and a
	// copy is free to add one back.
	function emitContainerLiteralElement(baseName ByteArray, method ByteArray, container ValueId, value ValueId, anchor Token, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		var args = pairArgs(container, second: value)
		var argLabels = emptyArgLabels(args)
		var argRanges = uniformArgRanges(args, range: range)
		_ = try self.emitCall(qualifiedMethodName(baseName, methodName: method), anchor: anchor, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: anchor, resultUsed: false, constructorInstance: self.receiverConstructorInstance(container), mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)
	end 'emitContainerLiteralElement'

	// ⚠⚠ **`movedCharacterSetMember` AND `emitSetCreateOp` STOOD HERE AND ARE GONE (W105).** The first was the
	// OWNERSHIP half of the retirement switch: a SYNTHESIZED table took ownership of a member at the parse,
	// because `__set_insert` was a runtime call with no signature for `applyCallerConsume` to read. The corpus
	// `Set.insert` has one, so the ORDINARY call machinery decides — and doing BOTH was measured to be a LEAK,
	// not belt-and-braces (the parse-time move DRAINS the value from the statement's pending drops, so the call
	// then takes its own reference and the statement releases nothing). With the runtime deleted the function
	// was the identity, and its callers hand `Set.insert` the member as it was parsed.
	//
	// The second emitted `__set_create(elemDrop)` and stamped the record's `keyDrop@40`; a declared `Set`
	// builds its own fields in its own `create()`.

	// ⭐ **THE `elemDrop@40` STAMP VALUE for a `Set` (P1.7b) — the `Set` twin of `arrayElementDestroyValue`.** A
	// MANAGED key (String) → the RAW `.text` address of its drop callee (`__str_decref`), via the same P1.5-A1
	// `funcAddr` code→register path the array element destructor rides (no rdata reloc, no descriptor); a trivial
	// int element → 0. `__list_decref`'s teardown walk calls it through a `callIndirect` on each live element.
	// ⚠ It is keyed on the column's TYPE and not on a `(giid, which-column)` pair, because a `Map` has TWO of
	// these stamps and a per-column copy is exactly how the key's and the value's teardown would come to
	// disagree about what "managed" means.
	//
	// ⛔ **AN OPAQUE COLUMN HAS NO STAMP HERE AND NEVER HAD ONE** — its destructor is the enclosing instance's
	// descriptor `destroyFunc@40`, read at run time, so `emitListCreateOp` branches to `emitOpaqueChainCreateOp`
	// before asking. See `ProgramSignatures.requireStaticColumnStamp`, which is the one sentence saying so and
	// which the top-level twin of this function asks as well (W134).
	function columnDestroyValue(columnType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		self.signatures.requireStaticColumnStamp(columnType, asker: "Parser.columnDestroyValue")
		if self.signatures.slotTypeIsManaged(columnType) 'managed'
			return self.emitRuntimeFuncAddr(self.signatures.slotTypeDropCallee(columnType), range: range)
		end 'managed'
		return self.emitLiteral(TrivialDestructor, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'columnDestroyValue'

	// ⛔⛔ **A COLUMN WHOSE ELEMENT IS A RECORD OVER THE ENCLOSING TYPE PARAMETER HAS NO DESTRUCTOR TO STAMP,
	// AND STAMPING THE DECLARATION VIEW'S IS A DANGLING POINTER (W171).** The two producers above end in
	// `managed → drop callee` / `otherwise → 0`, and for `Array with (Box with Element)` inside
	// `type Bag uses Element` the managed arm answers `__mm_decref` — because `Box with Element`'s own bare
	// `T` field reads NOT MANAGED through `typeIsManaged`, which is the one question a shared body cannot
	// answer. So the record is freed and the slot it holds is not, and the store side must therefore hand the
	// slot a raw borrow: **MEASURED, `0xC0000005`**, on a `Bag with String` filled through a helper whose heap
	// `String` dies at its scope exit.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THE OTHER DIRECTION WAS MEASURED TOO, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A REFUSAL AND NOT A RETAIN.** With
	// the store side taking a reference through `retainFunc@64` the fault goes away and the same program exits
	// **101** — the reference is taken and nothing releases it. Both halves are one mechanism, and the half
	// that is missing is a descriptor slot carrying a nested instance's per-instantiation destructor.
	// `emitOpaqueFieldReassign` already refuses the REASSIGNMENT form in those words; this is the same
	// sentence at the CONSTRUCTION form, which is the only other way such a column is born.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE DOOR IS THE BUILTIN CONTAINER'S `create`, AND THE MESSAGE ONCE PROMISED MORE THAN THAT (BATCH41
	// review).** It said *"and a `List` over one"*, which was measured FALSE: `List` has been a DECLARED corpus
	// generic since W153, so `List with EBox` never reaches this door — its chain is built inside `List`'s OWN
	// shared body over `List`'s own opaque `Element`, which routes to `emitOpaqueChainCreateOp`. MEASURED at
	// this merge: `typealias BoxList = List with EBox` + `BoxList.create()` compiles and **segfaults**, while
	// the `__ManagedList with EBox` spelling — which does reach here — is refused. A message that names a shape
	// it does not refuse is worse than one that names none, so it names `__ManagedList`.
	//
	// ⛔ **TWO SPELLINGS OF THE SAME USE-AFTER-FREE ARE STILL REACHABLE AND ARE NOT THIS DOOR'S** — both
	// MEASURED `0xC0000005` at this merge, both pre-existing: `List with EBox` above, and `EBox` held as a
	// PLAIN FIELD of the shared body with no container anywhere (`var one as EBox`, fed by `Self{one:
	// EBox.create(x, …)}`). Neither passes a container `create`, so shutting them is a gate at the
	// INSTANTIATION / FIELD-DECLARATION door rather than a wider predicate here.
	//
	// ⭐ It is asked at the two CREATE sites and not inside `columnDestroyValue`, because the stamp producers
	// are also reached for a top-level global's record, where the shape is structurally impossible (a `.data`
	// global is not inside a generic body) — `requireStaticColumnStamp`'s stated arrangement, one door over.
	function requireColumnDropIsNameable(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.columnElementOwnsAnUnnameableSlot(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid)) 'nameable'
			return
		end 'nameable'

		throw self.unnameableSlotRefusal(self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid), arrival: ContainerElementArrival, range: range)
	end 'requireColumnDropIsNameable'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE ONE MESSAGE THE TWO CONTAINER ARRIVALS SHARE (BATCH41 arrival roster).** The FACT is a
	// property of the TYPE — a generic instance written over the enclosing type's own parameter, resting
	// (itself or through an instance it holds) a slot declared AT that parameter — and it is the same fact
	// whichever arrival meets it, so the sentence stating it is written once and the ARRIVAL is a noun passed
	// in. Two doors wording one refusal differently is how a reader comes to believe they are two rules; two
	// doors deriving it differently is how they come to disagree, which is why the PREDICATE is single-homed
	// too (`ProgramSignatures.instanceRestsAnUnnameableSlot`).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE ROSTER SHRANK TO TWO WHEN THE DICTIONARY DESTRUCTOR LANDED, AND THE MESSAGE HAD TO SHRINK
	// WITH IT.** It used to name FOUR arrivals — a container element, a `List` node, a `Map`/`Set` column, and
	// a FIELD of the enclosing type filled by `Self\{…\}` — because NO release existed for such a record at
	// all. One does now (`MmRuntime.dictionaryDestructorSymbol`), and it serves the field: the enclosing
	// instantiation is CONCRETE wherever it is freed, so `__destruct_Bag_String` drops that field through
	// `__destruct_Box_String`. What the dictionary destructor cannot serve is a CONTAINER's element, and the
	// reason is its signature: a column's element destructor is ONE `.text` word stamped into the record and
	// `callIndirect`ed with ONE argument, while a declaration view's release is `(descriptor, box)` — the
	// stamp has nowhere to carry the second. ⇒ the arrivals that still refuse are exactly the stamped ones.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY CURE THIS NAMES IS TESTED, and one earlier draft's was not** — it recommended nothing, but it
	// PROMISED a shape (`a List over one`) it did not refuse, which the BATCH41 review measured false. The
	// four cures below are `generic-opaque-value-store`'s
	// `a-hundred-borrowed-stores-into-the-parameters-own-container-balance` (the parameter's own container),
	// `a-record-over-the-enclosing-parameter-in-a-plain-field-round-trips` (a field rather than a column),
	// `a-trivial-instantiation-forwards-and-takes-nothing` (a trivial instantiation), and
	// `the-concrete-spelling-of-the-same-constructor-feed` (a concrete instantiation).
	function unnameableSlotRefusal(typeName String, arrival String, range SourceRange) returns ParseError
		return ParseError.unsupported("{arrival} '{typeName}' — a generic instance written over this type's OWN parameter, resting (itself, or through an instance it holds) a field declared AT that parameter — cannot be a CONTAINER ELEMENT: the shared generic body compiles once for every instantiation, so releasing such a record means releasing a field whose destructor is a fact about the enclosing instantiation, and the only entry that knows it takes the instantiation's layout descriptor as a second argument (`__destruct_dict_<instance>(descriptor, box)`). A container stamps ONE machine word as its element destructor and calls it with the element alone, so there is nowhere to carry that descriptor. It is refused at the two arrivals that stamp one: a container's element (an `Array`/`__ManagedList` `create` inside the body, and any `push`/`insert`/`upsert`/`set` of one), a `List` node, and a `Map` or `Set` column. Holding ONE such record in a FIELD of the enclosing type (`Self\{one: Inner.create(x, …)\}`) is admitted — the enclosing instantiation is concrete wherever it is freed, so its own destructor releases the field. Otherwise: hold the values in a container of the type PARAMETER itself (`Array with <type parameter>`, whose element destructor IS carried by the enclosing instance's layout descriptor), give the inner type a concrete field instead of one declared at the parameter, or build and hold the record in a method of a concrete instantiation", line: range.line, column: range.column)
	end 'unnameableSlotRefusal'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE FIELD ARRIVAL THE DICTIONARY DESTRUCTOR CANNOT SERVE — a record stored into a field of a type
	// that is ITSELF ever a nested declaration view (BATCH41 review).** The rung admits the field arrival on the
	// argument that the enclosing instantiation is CONCRETE wherever it is freed, so its own cascade drops the
	// field through a substituted symbol. That argument is exact for `Bag uses Element` holding a
	// `Box with Element`, and it FAILS for a `Mid uses Y, Z` some `Outer uses T, U` spells as `Mid with (T, U)`:
	// there the frame that frees the record is a shared body, the release would have to be the dictionary
	// destructor, and that destructor releases against a parameter one level out from the one the retain was
	// taken at.
	//
	// ⇒ MEASURED, exit **139** with the release emitted and exit **0** on the merge base, which REFUSED this
	// store. Both halves are load-bearing and either alone is a MEASURED wrong answer: without
	// `instanceRestsASlotUnderANestedView` this refuses
	// `a-record-over-the-enclosing-parameter-in-a-plain-field-round-trips`, the shape the rung exists for;
	// without the PROVENANCE half it refuses `generic-type-substitution`'s
	// `bare-generic-name-nested-three-levels`, whose stored `Cell` was handed in as a consumed parameter and
	// whose whole chain is built concretely by `main`. A record this frame referenced nothing into rests
	// nothing this frame owes, and the walk that decides the release stops one level short of it
	// (`kindWalksNestedInstances`), so the two agree.
	//
	// ⚠ It does NOT share `unnameableSlotRefusal`'s sentence, and that is not two doors wording one rule twice:
	// that message states a fact about the STAMP a container writes, and this one a fact about the ENCLOSING
	// declaration. Two different facts owe two different sentences.
	function requireStoredNestedViewDropIsNameable(arg ValueId, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		if self.tagOf(arg) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'namesNoInstance'
			return
		end 'namesNoInstance'

		if not self.recordsHoldingReferencedOpaqueSlots.contains(arg) 'thisFrameReferencedNothingInIt'
			return
		end 'thisFrameReferencedNothingInIt'

		let giid = self.valueNameId(arg)
		if not self.signatures.instanceRestsASlotUnderANestedView(giid) 'itsOwnerIsAlwaysConcrete'
			return
		end 'itsOwnerIsAlwaysConcrete'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("storing into a field a value of type '{String.from(self.signatures.instanceDisplayName(giid))}' — a generic instance written over this type's OWN parameter, resting a field declared AT that parameter, where THIS type is itself spelled over another generic's parameter — has no destructor this body can name: the enclosing record is freed by a SHARED body, so releasing the stored field means releasing a slot whose destructor is a fact about an instantiation two levels out, and the entry that knows it (`__destruct_dict_<instance>(descriptor, box)`) is handed the descriptor of the nearer one. Hold the record in a type that is instantiated concretely rather than over another generic's parameter. A descriptor carrying a NESTED declaration view's per-instantiation destructor is a later slice", line: range.line, column: range.column)
	end 'requireStoredNestedViewDropIsNameable'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE DURABLE-STORE ARRIVAL, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT DOMINATES THE ROSTER (BATCH41).** The container
	// `create` guard above stamps a column; this one refuses the VALUE coming to rest — which is where the
	// fact actually is, because a record over the enclosing parameter is perfectly sound as a LOCAL that dies
	// in the frame (MEASURED: exit 0) and unsound the moment something that OUTLIVES the borrow owns it.
	//
	// ⛔ **THREE SPELLINGS REACHED NO DOOR AT ALL BEFORE IT, ALL MEASURED `0xC0000005` AT THE BATCH41
	// REVIEW'S MERGE:** a `List with <inner alias>` — the DECLARED corpus generic, whose chain is built inside
	// `List`'s own body over `List`'s own opaque `Element` and so never passes the container guard above; a
	// PLAIN FIELD (`var one as EBox`, fed by `Self\{one: EBox.create(x, …)\}`) with no container anywhere; and a
	// `Map` VALUE column. All three pass through here, because all three are a managed value entering durable
	// storage and this is the ONE choke point for that (`moveManagedValueInto`'s own header states the
	// factoring, and the struct-field, union-payload, container-element and hash-table-column stores all
	// arrive here).
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SEE IS A RETURN**, and that is not an oversight: a record RETURNED to
	// a CONCRETE caller is released by that caller (`__destruct_Box_String`) and therefore owes a RETAIN
	// rather than a refusal, while the byte-identical declaration returned to a SHARED caller —
	// `generic-opaque-cursor-element`'s `createIterator`, reached by a `for … in self` — is released by
	// nobody and is sound exactly as it stands. The two differ only in who CALLS the method, which a body
	// compiled once cannot see. See `handleEscapingBorrowFeed` for the retain that closes the first and the
	// measurement showing it leaks on the second.
	function requireFedContainerElementDropIsNameable(arg ValueId, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		// ⚠⚠ **THE TYPE IS THE WHOLE CONDITION NOW, AND IT WAS ONE HALF OF TWO.** The other half was
		// PROVENANCE — *"this frame built the record out of a borrow it never referenced"* — which separated a
		// dangling slot from a sound one while the constructor feed took NO reference. It takes one since
		// BATCH41 (`referenceOrMarkOpaqueFeed`), so that mark could be set only where NO instantiation of the
		// parameter is managed — which is precisely where the type test below already answers `false`. The two
		// conditions became mutually exclusive and the refusal could no longer fire on anything, so the dead
		// half is deleted along with the mark that carried it rather than left standing as a gate that cannot
		// fail.
		//
		// ⚠ The TYPE half stays exactly as it was, and its narrowing is why: without it the refusal would fire
		// on a `Bag with Integer`, whose bare `T` slot owns nothing at run time and whose column's
		// `__mm_decref` IS the correct element destructor.
		if self.tagOf(arg) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'namesNoInstance'
			return
		end 'namesNoInstance'
		if not self.signatures.instanceRestsAnUnnameableSlot(self.valueNameId(arg)) 'nameable'
			return
		end 'nameable'

		throw self.unnameableSlotRefusal(String.from(self.signatures.instanceDisplayName(self.valueNameId(arg))), arrival: ContainerElementFeedArrival, range: range)
	end 'requireFedContainerElementDropIsNameable'

	// ⚠⚠ **THE HASH-TABLE KEY-ROSTER GATE STOOD HERE AND IS GONE (W105):** `requireHashTableKeyType` and its
	// five helpers (`hashTableKeyIsAdmitted`, `hashTableAdmittedKeys`, `reservedHashTableKeyNames`,
	// `grantedConformerKeyPhrase`, `requireArrayKeyElementConforms`). It admitted `int` / `String` /
	// `Character` / a conforming `Array` and refused everything else as "a later slice", because the
	// SYNTHESIZED table hashed and compared its key through witness tables the compiler itself had to name.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RULE IT ENFORCED IS THE GENERAL ONE NOW, AND
	// `specs-shv2/array-conditional-conformance-withheld.md` ALREADY SAYS SO IN ITS OWN WORDS**
	// (*"the roster is gone, and any `Hashable + Equatable` type is a key"*): `stdlib/Map.maxon` and
	// `stdlib/Set.maxon` declare `where Key is Hashable and Equatable`, so a bad key is the ordinary
	// `where`-constraint refusal at the INSTANTIATION (`checkWhereConstraints`) — which NAMES the constraint
	// that failed instead of reciting a roster, and admits any conforming user type (`map.md`'s
	// `a-user-type-can-be-a-map-key`). Its two doors — `requireContainerColumnTypes`'s hash-table arms and
	// `gateMapLiteralColumns`' synthesized arm — had both already become unreachable at W41/W90; W105 deletes
	// what they used to reach.

	// Is `name` a typealias for a `Set` instance — the alias form of a `from` construction's head? Asked of
	// the whole-program index, which is complete in the real parse; during the tolerant sweep every alias
	// answers no, which is sound because the sweep's artifact is discarded.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE *SHAPE* QUESTION, NEVER THE RUNTIME ONE (W90).** `CharSet from ['x']` is a construction
	// whoever serves the container: which runtime executes it is settled inside `parseSetFromConstruction`,
	// and it has nothing to do with whether this token sequence IS a construction. Asked through
	// the deleted `isSetInstance` — the runtime question — this answered false for every alias head the instant
	// `stdlib/Set.maxon` was listed, `atSetFromConstruction` stopped claiming the shape, and the head fell
	// through to **`E2004: Undefined variable 'CharSet'`** on six cases across `character-set`,
	// `unicode-category` and `lazy-static`. See `ProgramSignatures.isSetShapedInstance`.
	function namesSetInstanceAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.signatures.isGenericAlias(name) and self.signatures.isSetShapedInstance(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath))
	end 'namesSetInstanceAlias'

	// The same question one container over — is `name` a typealias for an `Array` instance? Its own predicate
	// rather than a base-name parameter over the pair, because the two bases are not asked the same way:
	// `isSetShapedInstance` is a name EQUALITY, while `isArrayInstance` also admits the compiler's own minted
	// `__Array_<T>` spellings (`SignatureIndex.isArrayBaseName`), and a shared base-name compare would have
	// answered `false` for every one of those.
	function namesArrayInstanceAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.signatures.isGenericAlias(name) and self.signatures.isArrayInstance(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(name, readerFilePath: self.filePath))
	end 'namesArrayInstanceAlias'

	// ⭐⭐ **`ByteArray from [10, 20, 30]` — AND IT EMITS NOT ONE RUNTIME OP OF ITS OWN**, `Vector from […]`'s
	// property and for the same reason: the array literal already builds exactly the record an `Array` is. It
	// does not even need that construction's RETYPE, because the instance was known BEFORE the literal was
	// parsed and the literal was built at it — the head's whole contribution is the `ArrayLiteralElementSource`
	// it hands down.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHICH IS THE POINT: THE ELEMENT TYPE IS THE ONE THING A `[…]` CANNOT STATE FOR ITSELF.** `[10, 20,
	// 30]` is an `Array with integer` striding 8; the same three tokens under a head naming `Array with Byte`
	// are three bytes. That is the same job `CharSet from ['x', 'y', 'z']` does for a `Set` — the alias supplies
	// what the bracket list cannot — and it is why `List`/`Vector` have no alias form: a list's and a vector's
	// element are whatever the literal's elements already are.
	//
	// The head NAMES a type, so it is a use for the E3062 unused-type check — the hook
	// `parseStringLiteralInitConstruction` takes for the same reason: this form never reaches
	// `parseTypeReference` either.
	function parseArrayFromConstruction(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let headTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		self.recordTypeNameUse(headTok.value)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.from)

		// The source must be an array literal `[…]`, exactly as it must for the other three `from` heads —
		// `<alias> from <expr>` is not a construction shv2 parses.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'notArrayLiteral'
			let srcTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(headTok.value)} from` requires an array literal `[…]` — `{String.from(headTok.value)} from <expr>` for another source is not a construction", line: srcTok.line, column: srcTok.column)
		end 'notArrayLiteral'

		return try self.parseArrayLiteral(func, element: ArrayLiteralElementSource.stated(self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(headTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath)))
	end 'parseArrayFromConstruction'

	// ⭐ **`Set from [1, 2, 3]` — CONSTRUCT A SET FROM A BRACKETED MEMBER LIST.** The cursor sits on the head
	// identifier. The two forks below split on what the members ARE: a `Set with Character` reads them as
	// character literals (`parseCharacterMemberSetLiteral`), and every other key type reads them as ordinary
	// expressions whose first fixes the instance (`parseSetMemberList`). Both desugar to `Set.create()` plus
	// one `Set.insert` per member.
	function parseSetFromConstruction(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let setTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.from)

		// The source must be an array literal `[…]`; `Set from <other-expr>` is not a construction shv2 parses.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'notArrayLiteral'
			let srcTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`Set from` requires an array literal `[…]` — `Set from <expr>` for another source is a later slice", line: srcTok.line, column: srcTok.column)
		end 'notArrayLiteral'

		// ⭐ **A CHARACTER-KEYED SET IS BUILT MEMBER BY MEMBER, NOT THROUGH AN ARRAY (P1.8 Slice D).** Every
		// other key type infers its instance from the array literal's ELEMENT type — but a single-byte
		// character literal materializes as an INT (shv2 types one by its byte width), so `['x','y','z']`
		// would infer `Array with integer` and a `Set with int` of byte values. The ALIAS states the key type
		// the elements cannot, so the bracket list is read as members directly and each element adopts
		// `Character` the way every other Character-expecting position makes a literal adopt it.
		if self.namesSetInstanceAlias(setTok.value) and self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(setTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath) == self.signatures.setInstanceForCharacter() 'characterKeyed'
			return try self.parseCharacterMemberSetLiteral(func, anchor: setTok, range: SourceRange.ofToken(setTok))
		end 'characterKeyed'

		// ⭐⭐ **A `Set` IS BUILT MEMBER BY MEMBER AND NEVER THROUGH AN ARRAY (W90).** `__set_from` was a runtime
		// entry that CONSUMED a source array one machine word at a time; the corpus type's own
		// `InitableFromArrayLiteral` `init` is not exported, so a user file naming it is
		// **`E3008: function 'Set.init' is not exported`**. `W41` met the identical wall one container over and
		// answered it the same way — `Map.create()` plus one `upsert` per pair — so this is `Map`'s route, and
		// it also spares the intermediate `Array` record the `from` spelling used to allocate. The `__set_from`
		// arm was the other half of a switch until W105 deleted that runtime.
		return try self.parseSetMemberList(func, setTok: setTok)
	end 'parseSetFromConstruction'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Set from [m0, m1, …]` ONTO A DECLARED `Set` — `Set.create()` plus one `Set.insert` per member
	// (W90).** The cursor sits on the `[`. The FIRST member fixes the element type and therefore the
	// instance, exactly as `parseMapLiteralBody`'s first pair fixes both of its columns, and every later
	// member is held to that type through the SHARED hash-table column door — so a mixed literal is refused
	// with the sentence a written `s.insert(x)` of the wrong type already gets.
	//
	// ⚠ **`consume: false` AT EVERY MEMBER, AND IT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.** The corpus `Set.insert` declares its
	// parameter, so the ORDINARY call machinery decides the move — see the W105 note above `emitContainerLiteralCreate` for the leak
	// a second, parse-time move produces. That also makes the fencing-token ordering `parseHashTableColumnArg`
	// insists on vacuous here: nothing reads `rhsWasBareLocalReference` on this path.
	function parseSetMemberList(func IrFunction, setTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(setTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		// A BRACKETED LIST MAY BE WRITTEN OVER SEVERAL LINES, and the three skips below are exactly where
		// `parseArrayLiteral` puts its own — the spelling this arm replaced, so a literal that parsed before the
		// retirement must parse after it. They are unconditional here, where the array literal's trailing one has
		// to wait for the element's move-in: nothing on this path reads `rhsWasBareLocalReference`, so there is no
		// `[valueStart, self.pos)` window a skip could widen.
		self.skipNewlines()

		// An EMPTY literal names no element, so there is no instance to create — and unlike an array literal,
		// which has `[]`'s own int default, a set's element must satisfy `Set`'s `where` clause before a
		// witness can be threaded. The alias form is the spelling that states a key type, so it is what the
		// refusal points at.
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'noMembers'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} from []` states no {HashTableKeyColumnNoun} type — an empty set is `<alias>.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` over a `{String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} with <{HashTableKeyColumnNoun}>` alias", line: setTok.line, column: setTok.column)
		end 'noMembers'

		let firstTok = self.current()
		let first = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		let keyType = try self.literalColumnTypeFor(first, literalKind: SetLiteralSubject, columnNoun: HashTableKeyColumnNoun, valueTok: firstTok)
		let giid = try self.setInstanceForLiteralKey(keyType, setTok: setTok)

		// ⭐ **THE LITERAL RECORDS ITS OWN INSTANTIATION**, because it is the one door a set is born through
		// with no `with <Key>` to anchor an E3017 on — `gateMapLiteralColumns`' arm, for its reason. The
		// verdict is `Set`'s own `where Element is Hashable and Equatable`, taken whole-program by
		// `checkWhereConstraints` where the conformance store is complete.
		self.artifact.recordInferredInstantiationSite(GenericInstantiationSite.create(giid, nameLine: setTok.line, nameColumn: setTok.column, filePath: self.filePath))

		let set = try self.emitContainerLiteralCreate(SetBuiltinBaseName, giid: giid, anchor: setTok, range: range)
		try self.emitContainerLiteralElement(SetBuiltinBaseName, method: InsertMethod, container: set, value: first, anchor: setTok, range: range)
		self.skipNewlines()

		let declaredType = self.resolvedSlotType(keyType)
		let declared = maxonTypeTag(declaredType)
		let identity = self.declaredColumnIdentity(declaredType)
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreMembers'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
			let member = try self.parseHashTableColumnArg(func, columnType: keyType, declaredType: declaredType, columnSubject: "`{String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} with {columnTypeName(declared, identity: identity)}` requires {articledColumnTypeName(declared, identity: identity)} {HashTableKeyColumnNoun}", consume: false)
			try self.emitContainerLiteralElement(SetBuiltinBaseName, method: InsertMethod, container: set, value: member, anchor: setTok, range: range)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'moreMembers'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBracket)
		return set
	end 'parseSetMemberList'

	// ⭐ **THE `Set with <key>` INSTANCE A CORPUS `Set from […]` BUILDS — bounded by what the SWEEP PRE-INTERNED
	// and by nothing else (W90).** `internWellKnownContainerInstances` over-interns exactly `Set with integer`
	// and `Set with String` for a file whose tokens hold a `Set from`, and `atSetFromConstruction`'s discipline
	// is that the parser may only READ back what the sweep wrote: a first intern here would make the gid depend
	// on parse order, which no drift guard can see.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **SO THIS IS AN INTERNING BOUND, NOT A CONFORMANCE VERDICT, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE
	// DIFFERENCE FROM THE SYNTHESIZED TWIN IT REPLACED.** `setInstanceForArrayLiteral` (deleted with the
	// runtime at W105) refused a narrow-packed element for the `__set_from` element STRIDE and everything else
	// for the builtin's key roster; neither reason survives here — create-plus-insert reads no buffer, and
	// whether the key can be hashed is `Set`'s own `where` clause, checked whole-program against the site the
	// caller records. What is left is the one thing this door can still state: the program's own
	// `typealias <X> = Set with <Key>` is how any other key type reaches a set, and it is what the refusal names.
	function setInstanceForLiteralKey(keyType MaxonType, setTok Token) returns GenericInstanceId throws ParseError
		if tagIsPlainInteger(maxonTypeTag(keyType)) 'intKey'
			return self.signatures.internBuiltinIntegerInstance(SetBuiltinBaseName)
		end 'intKey'
		if tagIsText(maxonTypeTag(keyType)) 'stringKey'
			return self.signatures.builtinInstanceForElement(SetBuiltinBaseName, element: MaxonType.string)
		end 'stringKey'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} from […]` infers its {HashTableKeyColumnNoun} type from the members, and only `{String.from(IntConformerName)}` and `{String.from(StringConformerName)}` members are inferred — declare `{String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} with <{HashTableKeyColumnNoun}>` and build it with `{String.from(CreateMethod)}` and `{String.from(InsertMethod)}` instead", line: setTok.line, column: setTok.column)
	end 'setInstanceForLiteralKey'

	// ⭐⭐ **`FilePath from "C:\\x.txt"` — THE `InitableFromStringLiteral` CONSTRUCTION, WHICH IS SUGAR AND
	// NOTHING MORE.** It is exactly `<Type>.init(<the literal as a String>)`, which is what both references
	// build (`2-Parser.cs:19795` `ParseFromStringLiteral` → `EmitFromLiteralInitCall`) and what the interface
	// itself says (`stdlib/Builtins.maxon:40` — `static function init(value String) returns Self`).
	//
	// ⭐ **SO IT REUSES `emitCall`, and that is the whole implementation.** The callee is the SAME
	// `qualifiedMethodName(<Type>, "init")` a written-out `FilePath.init("…")` builds — verified by running
	// both spellings side by side — so the result type, the arity and argument checks, the ownership of the
	// returned struct and the E3004 for a type with no `init` are the ones every other static call gets, and
	// none of them learns that this sugar exists. A bespoke emit here would be a second static-call path.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LITERAL GOES THROUGH `parseStringLiteral`, NOT A RAW TOKEN DECODE**, so an INTERPOLATED
	// `Type from "a{n}b"` materializes its String the one way every other String expression does. The
	// reference does the same (`EmitStringLiteralWithInterpolation`); decoding the token here would have been
	// a second literal decoder that silently dropped the interpolation.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CONFORMANCE IS NOT CHECKED HERE, AND IT CANNOT BE.** `implements InitableFromStringLiteral` is
	// recorded when the type's OWN file is parsed, and shv2 orders files only by source path — so the answer
	// at this cursor depends on which file came first. The site is RECORDED and the verdict taken after the
	// merge (`ConformanceCheck.checkLiteralInitConformance`), which is `checkWhereConstraints`' shape and its
	// reason. The site's anchor is the TYPE NAME token, where the oracle anchors the same E3005.
	function parseStringLiteralInitConstruction(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let typeTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		// A construction NAMES its type, so it is a use for the E3062 unused-type check — the same hook
		// `parseQualifiedCall` takes for `Type.method()`, and for its reason: this form never reaches
		// `parseTypeReference` either.
		self.recordTypeNameUse(typeTok.value)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.from)

		let literalTok = self.current()
		let literalStart = self.pos
		let value = try self.parseStringLiteral(func)

		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		var argLabels = ByteArrayArray.create()
		var argRanges = SourceRangeArray.create()
		var argImmutableNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		// The E3070 call-argument door, taken exactly as `parseCallArgs` takes it — a literal names no
		// storage, so it returns at `sourceNamesABase`, but asking through the one door is what keeps this
		// argument path from being a second, quietly different one.
		self.noteBorrowSubjectCallArg(func, callee: qualifiedMethodName(typeTok.value, methodName: StringInitMethod), anchor: typeTok, argColumn: args.count(), argLabel: EmptySourceText, valueStart: literalStart)
		// POSITIONAL and un-nameable: `init`'s single parameter takes it at position 0, and a literal is not a
		// bare binding, so it can never be the subject of an E3019 blame.
		pushCallArg(args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, value: value, label: EmptySourceText, range: SourceRange.ofToken(literalTok), immutableName: EmptySourceText)

		self.artifact.recordLiteralInitSite(LiteralInitSite.create(typeTok.value, nameLine: typeTok.line, nameColumn: typeTok.column, filePath: self.filePath))

		// `rparenTok` is the LITERAL — this form writes no parenthesis, and that token is what a diagnostic
		// about the call's tail must point at. `resultUsed: true` because a construction is an expression:
		// `parsePrimary` only reaches here in operand position.
		// `stringLiteralInit`, not `notMinted`: the author wrote `FilePath from "…"` and never named `init`, and
		// that is the only fact distinguishing this call from a written-out `FilePath.init("…")` — they build
		// the identical callee. See `CalleeMint.stringLiteralInit`.
		return try self.emitCall(qualifiedMethodName(typeTok.value, methodName: StringInitMethod), anchor: typeTok, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, rparenTok: literalTok, resultUsed: true, constructorInstance: self.constructorInstanceOf(typeTok.value), mint: CalleeMint.stringLiteralInit)
	end 'parseStringLiteralInitConstruction'

	// ⚠⚠ **`dispatchSetMethod` AND `parseSetKeyArg` STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THE `__set_*` RUNTIME (W105).**
	// They were the builtin `Set` surface — `count`/`contains`/`remove`/`insert`/`map` over the synthesized
	// record, plus the `( key )` argument list whose `consume` flag decided who owned a managed key. A `Set` is
	// a declared generic now, so every one of those members is `stdlib/Set.maxon`'s own body reached through
	// the ordinary method path, its key argument is an ordinary declared PARAMETER, and the ownership question
	// is the call machinery's. `Set.map` is `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `Iterable` extension.
	//
	// ⭐ What did NOT go with them is `parseHashTableColumnArg` below: `parseSetMemberList` and the `Map`
	// literal still parse a column argument, and that door's ordering contract is unchanged.

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE HASH-TABLE COLUMN ARGUMENT, PARSED — a `Set`'s key, a `Map`'s key and a `Map`'s VALUE.** Parse
	// the expression, characterize a lone literal against the column's declared type, refuse a mismatched tag,
	// and — when the callee KEEPS the value (`consume`) — store it so the table holds its own reference. The
	// three columns differ in the column TYPE they read and in the SUBJECT of their refusal, and in nothing
	// else, which is why those two are parameters and the sequence is not.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE MOVE-IN MUST RUN BEFORE THE ARGUMENT'S CLOSING TOKEN IS CONSUMED.** `rhsWasBareLocalReference`
	// reads `[valueStart, self.pos)` to decide whether the argument is a bare local to POISON, so a `)` — or a
	// `,` — consumed first widens that window and mis-decides. That is an OWNERSHIP wrong answer with no
	// compile error to find it by, which is exactly why the callers hand the fencing tokens in rather than this
	// function consuming them: a `Set` key is fenced by `( … )`, a `Map`'s two columns by `(`, `,` and `)`.
	// Written once per container, the ordering contract was stated twice and could be honoured once.
	//
	// ⚠ **A LONE CHARACTER LITERAL COERCES TO THE COLUMN'S TYPE WHEN THAT COLUMN IS INTEGRAL (A5m-ab).** A
	// `Set with Character` needs nothing — `chars.contains('x')` writes a Character and gets one — but a column
	// declared over a ranged integer alias does, and it is the same rule the `let`/`return`/comparison positions
	// serve (`integerizedOperand`).
	//
	// The tag rule itself is `columnValueTagMatches`, whose header carries the measured reason "is this an int?"
	// is `tagIsIntegral` and not `== ValueTypeTag.integer`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE COLUMN TYPE IS RESOLVED FIRST, AND THE COMPARISON IS `elementTypePreserved` — NOT THE BARE TAG
	// RULE — BECAUSE A `Map`'s VALUE COLUMN MAY NOW CARRY A NOMINAL IDENTITY.** Both halves were forced by the
	// same widening (the one-word slot gate, now `requireListElementType`), and each is a wrong answer without the other:
	//
	//   • **RESOLVED**: a `Map with (String, UnaryOp)` records its value argument as `named("UnaryOp")` — the
	//     declaration SWEEP ran before anything could say what the name denoted — and `tagIsIntegral(named)` is
	//     TRUE, so the raw tag said "int" about a code pointer. `resolvedSlotType` is the same first step the
	//     element READ takes (`containerSlotValueType`), so the write door and the read door agree about what
	//     the declaration names. It is exactly the omission that once made an `Array with <function alias>`
	//     element "stay `named`, i.e. an int".
	//   • **IDENTITY**: an enum column and an int column are BOTH integral to the tag rule, so `["a": Kind.X,
	//     "b": 5]` would store a bare 5 where a `match` later reads a case — the ordinal-against-a-match crash
	//     `declaredElementIdentity`'s header measures twice, one container over. `elementTypePreserved` is that
	//     rule already stated once: an identity on EITHER side settles it, and only when neither has one does
	//     the tag rule decide (which is what keeps a ranged-alias key admitted).
	//
	// ⚠ The identity is `declaredColumnIdentity`'s, which the two callers compose their SUBJECTS from, so the
	// name a refusal prints and the name this comparison read are one string by construction.
	function parseHashTableColumnArg(func IrFunction, columnType MaxonType, declaredType MaxonType, columnSubject String, consume bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let valueTok = self.current()
		let valueStart = self.pos
		let declared = maxonTypeTag(declaredType)
		let parsed = try self.integerizedOperand(try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock), expected: declared, start: valueStart, pastEnd: self.pos)

		let valueTag = self.tagOf(parsed)
		let valueIdentity = self.aggregateNameOf(valueTag, nameId: self.valueNameId(parsed))
		if not elementTypePreserved(declared, elementIdentity: self.declaredColumnIdentity(declaredType), otherTag: valueTag, otherIdentity: valueIdentity) 'mismatch'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("{columnSubject} — got a '{columnTypeName(valueTag, identity: valueIdentity)}' value", line: valueTok.line, column: valueTok.column)
		end 'mismatch'

		if not consume 'borrowed'
			return parsed
		end 'borrowed'

		return try self.moveColumnValueIntoTable(parsed, columnType: columnType, valueStart: valueStart)
	end 'parseHashTableColumnArg'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NOMINAL IDENTITY OF A COLUMN'S RESOLVED DECLARED TYPE — the ONE expression the three column
	// doors share.** `parseSetMemberList` and `parseMapColumnValue` build the refusal's SUBJECT from it and
	// `parseHashTableColumnArg` runs the `elementTypePreserved` COMPARISON with it, so "the name a refusal
	// PRINTS" and "the name the check COMPARED" are one string BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by three copies of
	// one expression agreeing. Spelled out at each site, changing one described a different program from the
	// one refused, with no compile error anywhere to say so.
	//
	// ⚠ It is the ungated `aggregateNameOf` and DELIBERATELY not `declaredElementIdentity`, whose
	// `tagMayNameAggregate` pre-gate withholds a `genericInstance`'s name — right for the `map`-transform door
	// that one serves, wrong here, where a nested-container value column is admitted and two distinct
	// instances share the tag.
	function declaredColumnIdentity(declaredType MaxonType) returns ByteArray
		return self.aggregateNameOf(maxonTypeTag(declaredType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(declaredType))
	end 'declaredColumnIdentity'

	// ⭐ **MOVE A PARSED KEY INTO A MANAGED-KEY SET (P1.7b String keys).** The `Set` twin of `moveElementIntoContainer`:
	// the table becomes the value's sole owner (its own teardown / dup-drop / remove-drop destroys it), so a
	// managed (String) key is consumed exactly as an array element is — a borrowed String literal is promoted to
	// an owned copy, an owned value is transferred (bare local poisoned / temp de-listed) — through the SHARED
	// `moveManagedValueInto`. A trivial int key owns no heap and passes through unchanged. A `Set` key is always
	// CONCRETE this slice (int or String), so there is no opaque-`T` case `moveElementIntoContainer` carries.
	// `valueStart` locates the value's first token — its bare-local source and the diagnostic anchor both.
	// ⚠ It takes the column's TYPE rather than a `giid`, so a `Set`'s key, a `Map`'s key and a `Map`'s VALUE —
	// independently managed columns of two containers — ask it without a per-column copy. Whether the column
	// owns what it holds is DERIVED from that type here, through the same `slotTypeIsManaged` the stamped
	// destructor is chosen by (`columnDestroyValue`): a caller that passed the answer in would be a second
	// place deciding which type argument is the key, and the move-in and the stamp could then disagree.
	function moveColumnValueIntoTable(value ValueId, columnType MaxonType, valueStart TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.slotTypeIsManaged(columnType) 'trivial'
			return value
		end 'trivial'
		let anchor = self.tokenAt(valueStart)
		// `tagIsByteRecord`, mirroring `moveElementIntoContainer`: the flag names the fused-record COPY
		// protocol, a question about the key's REPRESENTATION — and since P1.8 Slice D a `Set with Character`
		// reaches it, which is exactly the day this spelling was written for. A borrowed `.rdata` Character
		// literal is promoted to an owned copy here; an owned one is CO-OWNED when a live binding keeps it and
		// ADOPTED when it is a temporary with no other owner.
		let isString = tagIsByteRecord(maxonTypeTag(columnType))
		return try self.moveManagedValueInto(value, argBareLocal: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart), isString: isString, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'moveColumnValueIntoTable'

	// --- The `Map` builtin generic: `create` / `upsert` / `contains` / `get` / `count` ---

	// ⭐⭐ **A LITERAL'S COLUMNS ARE HELD TO WHOEVER SERVES THE MAP (W41-lit) — the door the retirement
	// missed.** Every other `Map` door asks its column question through `requireContainerColumnTypes`, whose
	// `map` arm was `isMapInstance`, so a corpus `Map` reached none of the builtin's rules and is governed by
	// its own declared `where` clause instead. This door called `requireMapColumnTypes` DIRECTLY and went on
	// enforcing the retired record's four-name key roster: a user `Hashable`/`Equatable` type was
	// **`E2015 … a key must be one of int, String, Character, Array`** in a `[k: v]` while the identical key
	// through `PointMap.create()` + `upsert` worked (`map.md`'s `a-user-type-can-be-a-map-key`).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CANNOT ROUTE THROUGH `requireContainerColumnTypes` ITSELF**: that function is a TOTAL router over
	// the BUILTIN containers and meets its named panic on anything else, which is exactly what a corpus `Map`
	// now is. What it shares with this is the SWITCH, not the body.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO SIBLING DOORS HAVE THIS SHAPE AND ARE NOT YET THIS DEFECT — `List from […]`
	// (`requireListElementType`) and `Vector from […]` (`requireVectorElementType`) call their column gate
	// directly too.** They are CORRECT today for a reason that is one measurement and not an assumption:
	// `isListBaseName`/`isVectorBaseName`/`isManagedListBaseName` carry no retirement switch — they are bare
	// `name.equals(…)` — so those containers have no second regime to route to, and their construction still
	// emits the runtime ops their gate describes. The day one of them is listed the way `stdlib/Map.maxon`
	// was, its `from` door needs exactly this arm and its literal-born values need
	// the no-parse-time-move rule `parseMapLiteralBody` now states, or it inherits both halves of W41-lit.
	//
	// ⛔ **THIS PARAGRAPH NAMED `isMapBaseName` AS "THE ONLY CONTAINER PREDICATE CARRYING THE SWITCH" AND
	// LISTED `isSet` AMONG THE BARE ONES — W90 FALSIFIED BOTH HALVES IN THE COMMIT THAT SHIPPED THIS FILE
	// (found at review).** The two were one shared switch, and `Set`'s own `from` door DID take this arm
	// (`parseSetMemberList`). W105 then deleted the switch entirely — both containers are declared, always —
	// so what is left is the SECOND half of this door's job, below.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **DROPPING THE ROSTER IS ONLY HALF, BECAUSE A LITERAL IS THE ONE DOOR A MAP IS BORN THROUGH WITH NO
	// `with (K, V)` TO ANCHOR AN E3017 ON.** `ProgramSignatures.instantiationSites` is built by the token
	// sweep out of `typealias` declarations, so nothing in it names an instance a literal inferred, and a key
	// implementing NEITHER interface would be admitted in silence — a witness dispatch through a table nothing
	// stamped. So the literal RECORDS its instantiation, at its first key, and the ordinary whole-program
	// check reports `Map`'s own `where Key is Hashable and Equatable` against it: the same code, sentence and
	// anchor a written alias gets, from one rule rather than two.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE VERDICT CANNOT BE TAKEN HERE, and that is structural rather than tidiness.** A type's
	// `implements` clause is recorded when its OWN file is parsed and shv2 orders files only by source path,
	// so the answer at this cursor depends on which file came first — `checkLiteralInitConformance`'s reason,
	// one door over, for the identical deferral.
	function gateMapLiteralColumns(giid GenericInstanceId, tok Token)
		self.artifact.recordInferredInstantiationSite(GenericInstantiationSite.create(giid, nameLine: tok.line, nameColumn: tok.column, filePath: self.filePath))
	end 'gateMapLiteralColumns'

	// ⚠⚠ **`moveMapLiteralColumnValue` STOOD HERE AND WAS THE OWNERSHIP HALF OF THE RETIREMENT SWITCH
	// (W41-lit), GONE WITH IT AT W105.** A SYNTHESIZED table took ownership of a `[k: v]` column at the parse,
	// because `__map_upsert` was a runtime call with no signature for `applyCallerConsume` to read. The corpus
	// `Map.upsert` has one, so the ORDINARY call machinery decides: it MOVES an owned managed argument at a
	// consuming position and CO-OWNS a borrowed one with a real `__mm_retain` (`handleEscapingBorrowFeed`).
	//
	// ⚠ **DOING BOTH WAS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES, IT WAS A LEAK, and only ONE column class ever showed it.** The
	// two protocols AGREE wherever the call CONSUMES — an `int` column owns no record, and a `String` column is
	// `typeArgIsOwned` TRUE — so the suite was green over the whole defect. An all-scalar STRUCT answers that
	// question `false` and is BORROWED, and there the drained temporary is a reference nobody releases
	// (MEASURED: `let m = [1: Pair.create(3, b: 4)]` exited **101** against the oracle's 1;
	// `specs-shv2/map.md`'s `literal.aggregate-value-column` is that program). That case is what holds the
	// no-parse-time-move rule down now that the switch it lived in has gone.

	// ⚠ **`emitMapLiteralCreate` STOOD HERE UNTIL THE W153 REVIEW** — the corpus `Map`'s own `create()` (W41),
	// which was the one place the literal's two representations parted company until W105 deleted the
	// `__map_create(keyDrop, valueDrop)` arm and the switch with it. It was `emitContainerLiteralCreate` with
	// `MapBuiltinBaseName` written in, and the `materializeStaticConstructorLayout` panic its body documented
	// is quoted at that function, which is where it was always the general rule.

	// ⭐ **ONE PAIR INTO THE MAP — `emitContainerLiteralElement`'s two-column twin, and the `value:` LABEL is
	// the half that is easy to leave off.** `Map.upsert(key Key, value Value)` declares its second parameter
	// labelled, so the synthesized call must spell it exactly as a written `m.upsert(k, value: v)` does or the
	// argument matcher answers about a parameter the author never named.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT LABEL IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS NOT THE SHARED DOOR**, and it is a real difference rather
	// than a spelling: a `Map` hands its member in as a `(key, value)` PAIR whose second half is named, so the
	// argument array is a triple and the label column is not empty. Everything else — the void result, the
	// `receiverConstructorInstance`, the no-parse-time-move rule — is that function's answer and is stated
	// there once.
	function emitMapLiteralUpsert(map ValueId, key ValueId, value ValueId, anchor Token, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		var args = tripleArgs(map, second: key, third: value)
		var argLabels = emptyArgLabels(args)
		try argLabels.set(MapUpsertValueArgSlot, value: MapUpsertValueLabel) otherwise panic("emitMapLiteralUpsert: the label column for a three-argument call has no slot {MapUpsertValueArgSlot} — `emptyArgLabels` mints one entry per argument")
		var argRanges = uniformArgRanges(args, range: range)
		_ = try self.emitCall(qualifiedMethodName(MapBuiltinBaseName, methodName: UpsertMethod), anchor: anchor, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, rparenTok: anchor, resultUsed: false, constructorInstance: self.receiverConstructorInstance(map), mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)
	end 'emitMapLiteralUpsert'

	// ⭐⭐ **`[k0: v0, k1: v1, …]` — A MAP LITERAL.** The cursor sits on the `:` after the first key, which
	// `parseArrayLiteral` has already parsed and handed over. It desugars to `Map.create()` plus one
	// `Map.upsert` per pair — the SAME two calls a `MapAlias.create()` + `upsert` program emits, so a
	// literal-built map and a hand-built one are one representation and one instance.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FIRST PAIR FIXES BOTH COLUMNS, so the record cannot be created until both halves are parsed** —
	// the create stamps the two per-column destructors, and those are read off the column TYPES. That is
	// `parseArrayLiteral`'s managed path exactly, one column wider.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO HALF OF A PAIR MOVES IN AT THE PARSE, AND THAT IS THE RULE THE DELETED `moveMapLiteralColumnValue`
	// LEAVES BEHIND (W105).** The desugared `Map.upsert` declares both parameters, so the ordinary call
	// machinery decides each column's ownership; a second, parse-time move would drain the value from the
	// statement's pending drops and leak. See the note above that function's grave for the measured case.
	function parseMapLiteralBody(func IrFunction, firstKey ValueId, firstKeyTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let keyType = try self.literalColumnTypeFor(firstKey, literalKind: MapLiteralSubject, columnNoun: HashTableKeyColumnNoun, valueTok: firstKeyTok)

		try self.consume(TokenKind.colon)
		self.skipNewlines()
		let firstValueTok = self.current()
		let firstValue = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		let valueType = try self.literalColumnTypeFor(firstValue, literalKind: MapLiteralSubject, columnNoun: HashTableValueColumnNoun, valueTok: firstValueTok)

		let giid = self.signatures.builtinInstanceForColumns(MapBuiltinBaseName, key: keyType, value: valueType)
		// The literal is the one door a map can be born through without a `with (K, V)` annotation, so it owes
		// the same column gate the annotated door owes — asked BEFORE the create, so a refused literal emits no op.
		self.gateMapLiteralColumns(giid, tok: firstKeyTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE LITERAL CALLS THE CORPUS TYPE'S OWN METHODS (W41).** The desugar is an empty map plus one
		// insert-or-replace per pair, so a literal-built map and a hand-built one are one representation and one
		// instance — the property this function's header has always claimed.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED, and it is why the W41 branch existed at all**: with the corpus type live and this door
		// still emitting the builtin ops, `let m = [1: 10]` built a fused hash-table record and then dispatched
		// `m.count()` into the corpus body that reads six declared fields — **0xC0000005 on 32 of `map.md`'s
		// cases**, a green compile every time. Both builtin emitters are deleted at W105, so the wrong half is
		// no longer spellable.
		let map = try self.emitContainerLiteralCreate(MapBuiltinBaseName, giid: giid, anchor: firstKeyTok, range: range)
		try self.emitMapLiteralUpsert(map, key: firstKey, value: firstValue, anchor: firstKeyTok, range: range)

		self.skipNewlines()
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'morePairs'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
			// Every later pair is checked AGAINST the first's columns — the map-literal twin of
			// `requireArrayLiteralElementMatches` — and rides the ordinary column-argument door, which
			// characterizes a lone literal, refuses a mismatched tag and moves the value in. A mixed literal is
			// therefore refused by the same sentence a mismatched `upsert` argument is.
			// ⚠ `consume: false` is the FIRST PAIR's answer one door along: the desugared `Map.upsert` declares
			// both parameters, so the call machinery takes each column and a parse-time move would leak.
			let key = try self.parseMapColumnValue(func, giid: giid, position: HashTableKeyArgPosition, columnNoun: HashTableKeyColumnNoun, consume: false)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.colon)
			self.skipNewlines()
			let value = try self.parseMapColumnValue(func, giid: giid, position: MapValueArgPosition, columnNoun: HashTableValueColumnNoun, consume: false)
			try self.emitMapLiteralUpsert(map, key: key, value: value, anchor: firstKeyTok, range: range)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'morePairs'
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBracket)

		// ⛔⛔ **A `trackOwnedTemp(map, exclusivity: sole)` STOOD HERE AND DOUBLE-DROPPED EVERY MAP LITERAL
		// USED AS A TEMPORARY (found at W105 review, MEASURED).** `[1: 10, 2: 20, 3: 30].count()` exited
		// **0xC0000005** against the bootstrap oracle's **3**, and `dump_ir` named the cause in `@main` — two
		// consecutive `x64.callDirect __destruct_Map_int_int` on the same record.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE LINE WAS CONDITIONALLY CORRECT UNTIL THIS RUNG, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED THE DELETION.**
		// the map create had a `__map_create` arm — a raw runtime op, and a runtime op owns nothing
		// until a caller says so. That arm is gone: the create is now an `emitCall`, and `emitCall`
		// classifies its result +1 owned by itself (`enrolOwnedCallTemp`). Tracking it again enrols one
		// record TWICE. `emitContainerLiteralCreate`'s header states exactly this rule and `parseSetMemberList` obeys it
		// by returning without tracking — the `Set` twin was correct throughout, and the fork is what let the
		// two come apart.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SUITE WAS GREEN OVER IT, AND THE REASON IS WORTH KEEPING**: `removeFromPendingTemps` strips
		// ALL occurrences, so the BOUND form (`let m = [1: 10]`) cancels both enrolments and answers
		// correctly. Only the unbound temporary — a literal consumed in value or condition position —
		// reaches scope exit still holding two. `specs-shv2/block-condition-temporaries.md` is the spec for
		// exactly that shape and every one of its cases used an ARRAY literal; the map cases below it are
		// this fix's gate.
		return map
	end 'parseMapLiteralBody'

	// ⚠⚠ **THE WHOLE BUILTIN `Map` SURFACE STOOD HERE AND WENT WITH THE `__map_*` RUNTIME (W105):**
	// `emitMapUpsert` (the one home of the `__map_upsert(map, key, value)` call), `emitMapEntriesArray` and its
	// `columnCloneValue` stamp (the `(Key, Value)` pair materialization both `for … in <map>` and `<map>.map(f)`
	// were built on), `dispatchMapMethod` (`count`/`contains`/`get`/`remove`/`upsert`/`insert`/`map` over the
	// synthesized record) and the two argument lists it read them through.
	//
	// A `Map` is a declared generic now: every one of those members is `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s own body reached
	// through the ordinary method path, `Map.get`/`Map.insert` declare `throws MapError` themselves, and the
	// entry pair is `MapIterator.current()`'s — so `for … in` and `map` both take the `Iterable` cursor route
	// (`materializeIterableIterationSource`) rather than a materialized array.
	//
	// ⭐ What did NOT go with them is `parseMapColumnValue` below: `parseMapLiteralBody` still parses a `[k: v]`
	// literal's columns, and it is the one door a map is born through with no `with (K, V)` to anchor on.

	// ONE `Map` column argument — the `Set` key's twin, and it rides the same `parseHashTableColumnArg`: this
	// end resolves WHICH column (key or value) and composes the `Map`'s own refusal subject, and nothing else.
	// The key half and the value half differ only in which type argument they read, so they are one function:
	// a per-column copy is how the two would come to disagree about the move.
	//
	// ⚠ The caller consumes the fencing tokens — `(`, the `,` before `value:` and the closing `)` — AFTER this
	// returns, because the move-in inside must run first (see `parseHashTableColumnArg`'s ordering contract).
	function parseMapColumnValue(func IrFunction, giid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition, columnNoun String, consume bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let columnType = self.mapColumnType(giid, position: position)
		// The SUBJECT names the RESOLVED type and its nominal identity for `parseHashTableColumnArg`'s reason:
		// a `named("UnaryOp")` value column printed as *"is an int"*, a sentence about a code pointer that
		// argues against itself, and a `Kind` column printed the same way about an enum.
		let declaredType = self.resolvedSlotType(columnType)
		let declared = maxonTypeTag(declaredType)
		return try self.parseHashTableColumnArg(func, columnType: columnType, declaredType: declaredType, columnSubject: "this `{String.from(MapBuiltinBaseName)}`'s {columnNoun} is {articledColumnTypeName(declared, identity: self.declaredColumnIdentity(declaredType))}", consume: consume)
	end 'parseMapColumnValue'

	// The declared type of map column `position` — a selector rather than a `position` threaded into the index,
	// because the index's own doors are named for the columns (`mapKeyType` / `mapValueType`) and a positional
	// door there would be a second way to ask. THE one place that knows which type argument is the key: whether
	// a column owns what it holds is derived from the type this hands back, never from the position again.
	function mapColumnType(giid GenericInstanceId, position TypeParamPosition) returns MaxonType
		return self.signatures.mapKeyType(giid) if position == HashTableKeyArgPosition else self.signatures.mapValueType(giid)
	end 'mapColumnType'

	// --- The `List` builtin generic: `create` / `append` / `prepend` / `get` / `first` / `removeFirst` / `count` ---

	// Emit `__list_create(elementDrop)` for instance `giid`, yielding the empty chain header. `head`/`tail`/
	// `count` are zeroed by `__mm_alloc`, so only the stamp is written; `element_drop@24` carries the element's
	// destructor (the SAME `columnDestroyValue` a `Set`'s key and a `Map`'s two columns are stamped from) so
	// the list drops what it owns at teardown.
	//
	// ⭐ **AN OPAQUE ELEMENT TAKES THE OTHER ROAD, AND THE BRANCH IS HERE FOR THE REASON `emitArrayCreateOp`'s
	// IS (W134)**: this is the ONE producer of a chain's `create`, so no door can reach the static stamp with a
	// `typeParameter` element and no door can miss the descriptor-reading form.
	function emitListCreateOp(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaque'
			return try self.emitOpaqueChainCreateOp(giid, range: range)
		end 'opaque'

		try self.requireColumnDropIsNameable(giid, range: range)
		let elementDrop = self.columnDestroyValue(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid), range: range)
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(ListCreateName, args: singletonArgs(elementDrop), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
	end 'emitListCreateOp'

	// ⭐ **CREATE AN OPAQUE-`T` CHAIN inside a shared generic body (W134)** — `EChain.create()` where
	// `EChain = __ManagedList with typeParameter(token)`, the shape `stdlib/List.maxon`'s sole field is. It is
	// `emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp` with the size argument removed, and for the same reason that one has one: a
	// chain's node holds ONE MACHINE WORD whatever the element is (`ListRuntime.maxon`'s `value@16`), so unlike
	// an array there is no stride to state and the descriptor read is the create's ONLY argument. The parser
	// therefore emits a ZERO-argument `__list_create`; `LowerMaxonToStd.appendOpaqueContainerCreate` supplies
	// the `destroyFunc@40` read, so the tier that owns the descriptor's ValueId is the tier that reads it.
	//
	// ⭐ **A TRIVIAL INSTANTIATION READS 0 OUT OF THE SAME SLOT AND ITS TEARDOWN WALK IS SKIPPED**, which is
	// what makes ONE compiled body serve a `String` bag and an `int` bag in one program — the case
	// `specs-shv2/managed-list-opaque-element.md` exists for. The alternative the refusal replaced was a
	// statically stamped 0 (`columnDestroyValue`'s trivial arm), which is the same instruction for both and
	// leaks the managed one.
	function emitOpaqueChainCreateOp(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireDescriptorSourceForOpaqueCreate(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(giid), containerNoun: OpaqueChainCreateNoun, range: range)

		return self.emitRuntimeCall(ListCreateName, args: ValueIdArray.create(), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
	end 'emitOpaqueChainCreateOp'

	// ⭐ **THE CHAIN ELEMENT-TYPE GATE.** A node's `value@16` is ONE MACHINE WORD, so the positive rule is
	// `ProgramSignatures.slotTypeFitsOneWord` (see its header for what that buys and what it cost to have two
	// of them). It serves both chain surfaces — `__ManagedList` is the same record with the same one-word value
	// slot — so the name it blames is read off the instance rather than spelled twice.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AN OPAQUE `T` IS ADMITTED, AND IT IS ASKED FIRST BECAUSE IT IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION (W134)** — a
	// `T` slot is not a type that fails to fit a word, it is a slot whose destructor is not statically known.
	// This arm used to refuse outright, on the ground that *"a container inside a generic body has no element
	// destructor to stamp, so a managed instantiation would leak the elements it holds"* — a true sentence
	// about a **0 stamp**, which is what `columnDestroyValue` would have produced, and not about the construct:
	// the destructor is in the enclosing instance's layout descriptor, whose `destroyFunc@40` the caller
	// already threads in, and `emitOpaqueContainerCreateOp` reads it there at run time exactly as an
	// `Array with <type parameter>` has since P1.7 slice 3b-i. A chain PUBLISHES no slots — it is empty at
	// birth and grows one node per insertion — so it needs none of the per-instantiation reasoning
	// `requireVectorElementType` does at this same fork; the descriptor is the whole of the answer.
	//
	// ⚠ **`float` IS NOT WHAT THE LAST REFUSAL IS FOR, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** A float
	// slot would arrive in the XMM file where every container entry is declared `i64`, but it never reaches
	// here: `typealias FList = List with F` is already **E2062 on the type ARGUMENT** (a type parameter is an
	// opaque general-purpose slot and a float has no way through it) — the same gate the deleted
	// `requireHashTableKeyType` recorded for a float key. What the last refusal is for is therefore any slot
	// type neither managed nor word-shaped that a future type kind might introduce: a clean E2015 rather than
	// a wrong stride.
	//
	// MEASURED to pass through it as a `List` ELEMENT, each printing and tearing down clean: `bool`, a ranged
	// `Byte`, a declared enum, a function alias, a nested `List`, a nested `Array`, `String`, and a struct
	// with a `String` field.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND PASSING THIS GATE IS NOT THE WHOLE ADMISSION — `TypeRules.columnValueTagMatches` DECIDES WHAT
	// MAY BE WRITTEN.** That was measured on a `Map`'s VALUE column, which shared this rule while the
	// synthesized table existed (W105 retired it, leaving the chain as the sole asker): `bool` was admitted
	// here and refused at the first `upsert`, and the arm that closes it is that predicate's.
	function requireListElementType(giid GenericInstanceId, tok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaqueElement'
			return
		end 'opaqueElement'

		let elementType = self.signatures.containerElementType(giid)
		if self.signatures.slotTypeFitsOneWord(elementType) 'fitsTheSlot'
			return
		end 'fitsTheSlot'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(giid))}` holds one machine word per {ContainerElementColumnNoun}, so its {ContainerElementColumnNoun} must be a managed type or an integral one — a '{typeTagName(maxonTypeTag(elementType))}' {ContainerElementColumnNoun} does not fit that slot", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'requireListElementType'

	// ⭐⭐ **`dispatchListMethod` STOOD HERE — THE WHOLE SYNTHESIZED `List` SURFACE — AND W153 DELETED IT.**
	// It served `count`/`isEmpty`/`append`/`prepend`/`insert`/`get`/`first`/`last`/`removeFirst`/`removeLast`/
	// `remove`/`clear` off the `__list_*` runtime, and its closing refusal ended *"clone is a later slice"*.
	// `stdlib/List.maxon` declares every one of those and `clone` besides, so a `List` receiver takes the
	// ordinary declared-method path — the same one every other `stdlib/` type's members take, and the one
	// that makes the module's bodies the thing that answers.
	//
	// ⚠ **NONE OF THE OWNERSHIP DOORS IT USED HAD A `List` ARM TO DELETE, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A DELETION AND
	// NOT A MIGRATION.** The move-in (`parseContainerElementArg`), the borrow and the move-out
	// (`emitContainerElementAccessor`) were container doors all along; the corpus declaration reaches them
	// through the ordinary call machinery, which reads the same `var self` / consuming-parameter facts off
	// the declaration that raise E3019 and E3070 for `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s members.

	// ⭐ **`clear()` ON EITHER CHAIN SURFACE** — the void-statement guard, the empty argument list, and the ONE
	// runtime entry, which is `__list_clear` for both because the two surfaces are one structure (that entry's
	// own header states it, and `__list_decref`'s last owner calls the same walk). The receiver is handed back
	// as what the statement still denotes, exactly as `emitArrayVoidCall` does.
	//
	// ⚠ The borrow-subject write is the CALLER's and not this helper's, because only ONE of the two doors owes
	// one: `List` hands out ELEMENT borrows a `clear` invalidates and records the write through
	// `listMethodMutatesReceiver` before this runs; `__ManagedList` hands out node HANDLES, which are second
	// OWNERS since `W138` and cannot be dangled, so it records nothing. Filing it here would give the surface
	// with nothing to protect the same write as the surface with something to protect.
	// ⭐ **A NO-ARGUMENT CHAIN READER — `count()` and `isEmpty()`, ON EITHER SURFACE.** Four arms asked the
	// same question of the same record and spelled the empty argument list and the call for themselves; the
	// only thing that differs between them is the entry point and the tag its answer wears, so those are the
	// two arguments and everything else is written once. `emitChainClear` below is the same consolidation for
	// the void member.
	function emitChainReader(list ValueId, callee ByteArray, resultTag ValueTypeTag, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: singletonArgs(list), resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
	end 'emitChainReader'

	// ⭐ **A VOID CHAIN MEMBER TAKING ONE NODE HANDLE — `detach`, `reinsertFirst`, `reinsertLast`.** All
	// three are the same four steps in the same order: refuse a value-position call BEFORE the argument list
	// is consumed (`requireVoidMethodIsStatement`'s ⇒ rule), parse and type-check the handle, emit, and hand
	// the RECEIVER back as what the statement still denotes. Written once because a fourth such member
	// would otherwise be a fourth chance to put the void guard in the wrong place.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RESULT TAG IS AN ARGUMENT AND NOT A CONSTANT `void`, because two of the three entries DO
	// return a value.** `__list_detach_node` shares `remove`'s graph and hands the element back for the
	// parser to discard; the reinsertions return nothing. Typing a value-returning entry `void` at the Maxon
	// tier is the mismatch `parseArraySet`'s tag note records as a wrong answer, and the SURFACE is kept void
	// by the guard rather than by the tag — `dispatchListMethod`'s `insertAtAnEnd` makes the same split.
	//
	// ⚠ **NONE OF THE THREE MARKS THE ARGUMENT'S BINDING, WHERE `remove` DOES AND MUST.** They change a
	// node's LINKS and leave `value@16` where it is, so the handle goes on answering `value()` afterwards —
	// see the `reinsertAtAnEnd` arm for the roster this makes unnecessary.
	function emitChainNodeMutator(func IrFunction, list ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, callee ByteArray, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultUsed bool, methodTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
		let node = try self.parseManagedListNodeArg(func, giid: giid, subject: methodTok.value)
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: pairArgs(list, second: node), resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)

		return list
	end 'emitChainNodeMutator'

	function emitChainClear(list ValueId, resultUsed bool, methodTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
		try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(ListClearName, args: singletonArgs(list), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		return list
	end 'emitChainClear'

	// ⭐⭐ **DISPATCH A `__ManagedList` METHOD** — the node-handle surface over the SAME chain
	// `dispatchListMethod` serves (`SignatureIndex.ManagedListBuiltinBaseName`), so every emit below is a
	// `__list_*` entry and not one new runtime call.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE HANDLE AN INSERTION HANDS BACK IS A SECOND OWNER OF THE NODE.** ⚖ USER RULING 2026-08-17
	// (PLAN `W138`, superseding the ruling taken earlier the same day): refcount the nodes, v1's model. So a
	// handle may escape into a return, a field or a parameter — which `stdlib/List.maxon` needs — and every
	// method below drops the CHAIN's reference rather than freeing, so nothing here can dangle one.
	//
	// ⛔ **NO METHOD ON THIS SURFACE INVALIDATES A HANDLE ANY MORE, AND THE PREDICATE THAT SAID OTHERWISE IS
	// GONE** (`managedListMethodFreesANode`'s grave carries the reasoning). Two designs died getting here: a
	// statement-scoped BORROW, which could not escape at all; and an owned box RETAINING THE LIST, which was a
	// measured WRONG ANSWER — `clear` frees nodes, so the retain kept nothing alive, and E3070 could not
	// follow a handle out of the source name it was minted under.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RECEIVER-WRITE IS NOTED AFTER THE ARGUMENTS ARE PARSED, WHICH IS THE ONE ORDERING DIFFERENCE
	// FROM EVERY OTHER DISPATCHER.** `ml.remove(node)` READS the handle and then WRITES the list, in that
	// order, and E3070 compares a mutation site's token against the borrow's LAST USE — so a site recorded
	// at the method token would sit before the argument's read and refuse the canonical spec's own program
	// (`/specs/ownership.md:managed-list-remove-decref`). Recorded after, the same rule does exactly the
	// right thing in both directions: a handle whose last use IS the argument is dead at the write and the
	// call is legal, and a handle read AGAIN afterwards is live at the write and the read is refused.
	function dispatchManagedListMethod(func IrFunction, list ValueId, resultUsed bool, immutableReceiverName ByteArray, borrowSubjectName ByteArray, receiverOwner ParamPositionMask, receiverStorage ParamPositionMask) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(methodTok)
		let giid = self.valueNameId(list)
		let mutates = managedListMethodMutatesReceiver(methodTok.value)

		// ⭐⭐ **W-BORROW's ARCHITECTURE, KEPT WHOLE — AND UNDER `W138` IT IS WHAT LEAVES THIS SURFACE WITH
		// NOTHING TO RECORD.** That rung moved E3070's cross-function half out of this door and onto
		// `noteBorrowSubjectWrite`, so a surface can no longer set one half of E3070 without the other, and
		// what `noteReceiverWrite` still takes a storage mask FOR is E3019 and W117 — which the two insertions
		// genuinely owe, since an insertion does write a `let` receiver's record. That half is unchanged here.
		//
		// ⚖ **THE E3070 HALF IS NOT PASSED ON, BECAUSE THERE IS NO LONGER A WRITE TO RECORD.** W-BORROW read
		// this surface's E3070 answer off `managedListMethodFreesANode`, on the premise that `remove`/`clear`
		// FREE a node an outstanding handle names — measured then as `let node = chain.insertLast("<heap>")` +
		// `wipe(chain)` at **0xC0000005**. USER RULING 2026-08-17 (option (iii)) made a handle a SECOND OWNER of
		// a refcounted node: `clear` drops the CHAIN's reference and the node lives, so that program is
		// memory-safe and RE-MEASURED here at **exit 0 with the right string**. The predicate is deleted, both
		// `noteBorrowSubjectWrite` calls with it, and `borrow-liveness:a-callee-clearing-a-managed-list-frees-a-
		// node-handle` is kept as the RUNNING case that records the boundary moving.
		//
		// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT THE `List` DOOR's ANSWER, AND THE TWO MUST NOT BE UNIFIED.** `dispatchListMethod`
		// hands out ELEMENT borrows, which a removal still hands to a new owner and a `clear` still releases;
		// its `noteBorrowSubjectWrite` stays exactly as W-BORROW landed it. An element borrow is not a node
		// handle, and no ruling about node lifetime reaches one.
		try self.noteReceiverWrite(func, immutableReceiverName: immutableReceiverName, receiverOwner: receiverOwner, receiverStorage: receiverStorage, methodTok: methodTok, mutatesReceiver: mutates)

		if methodTok.value.equals(CountMethod) 'count'
			return try self.emitChainReader(list, callee: ListCountName, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, range: range)
		end 'count'

		// The `List` surface's own `isEmpty`, on the other name for the same record — one entry, one reader.
		if methodTok.value.equals(IsEmptyMethod) 'isEmpty'
			return try self.emitChainReader(list, callee: ListIsEmptyName, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, range: range)
		end 'isEmpty'

		// The two insertions differ only in WHICH end the runtime links onto, so they share one arm — the
		// `List` dispatcher's argument, with the node kept rather than discarded.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListInsertFirstMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListInsertLastMethod) 'insertAtAnEnd'
			let value = try self.parseContainerElementArg(func, giid: giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			let callee = ListPrependName if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListInsertFirstMethod) else ListAppendName

			// ⭐⭐ **`W148` — A DISCARDED INSERTION MINTS NO HANDLE, AND THE PREDICATE IS AN ARRIVAL ROSTER
			// RATHER THAN THE `resultUsed` FLAG.** `stdlib/List.maxon`'s `append`/`prepend` discard the node
			// on every call, and the handle they were minting cost an `__mm_incref` plus a
			// `__list_node_decref` that the statement's own drain cancelled one instruction later — three
			// such pairs in `ownership/no-leak-returned-managed-list` alone, in a program with no node handle
			// in its source.
			if self.chainInsertionHandleIsRead(callee, resultUsed: resultUsed) 'handleIsRead'
				return self.emitManagedListNodeHandle(callee, args: pairArgs(list, second: value), nodeInstance: self.signatures.managedListNodeInstanceFor(giid), provenance: NodeHandleProvenance.chain(list), range: range)
			end 'handleIsRead'

			// Nobody reads the node, so the call is typed as the `List` door types it — an `integer` the
			// statement throws away — and the RECEIVER comes back as what the statement still denotes
			// (`emitArrayVoidCall`'s answer, and `dispatchListMethod`'s for the identical pair).
			_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: pairArgs(list, second: value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
			return list
		end 'insertAtAnEnd'

		// ⭐ **`head()` / `tail()` — THE TWO THROWING CHAIN NAVIGATORS.** They hand back a handle to a node
		// that ALREADY EXISTS, so the `+1` the mint door states is owed is the incref that door emits and
		// not an allocation's — see `emitManagedListNodeHandle`'s ⭐ block on `callReturnRc1`.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListHeadMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListTailMethod) 'nodeAtAnEnd'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			let callee = ListHeadCheckedName if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListHeadMethod) else ListTailCheckedName
			return self.emitManagedListNodeHandle(callee, args: singletonArgs(list), nodeInstance: self.signatures.managedListNodeInstanceFor(giid), provenance: NodeHandleProvenance.chain(list), range: range)
		end 'nodeAtAnEnd'

		// ⭐ **`insertAfter(target, value:)` / `insertBefore(target, value:)` — THE TWO INTERIOR INSERTIONS.**
		// One arm, because they differ in nothing this door can see: the same two arguments, the same
		// move-in, the same minted handle. Which side of `target` the node lands on is the runtime entry's
		// four offsets (`buildListInteriorInsert`).
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListInsertAfterMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListInsertBeforeMethod) 'insertBesideANode'
			let callee = ListInsertAfterName if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListInsertAfterMethod) else ListInsertBeforeName
			return try self.parseManagedListInsertBeside(func, list: list, giid: giid, callee: callee, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'insertBesideANode'

		// ⭐ **`reinsertFirst(node)` / `reinsertLast(node)` — MOVE AN EXISTING NODE TO AN END.** Void, so
		// statement-position only, and the receiver comes back as what the statement denotes.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE HANDLE IS NOT MARKED AND NEEDS NO ROSTER, WHERE `remove`'s DOES.** A reinsertion changes
		// the node's LINKS and leaves `value@16` exactly where it is, so the argument's handle goes on
		// answering `value()`, `next()` and `prev()` afterwards — there is nothing for a later read to be
		// refused about. `detach` is unmarked for the same reason and states it; `remove` is the ONE member
		// on this surface that empties the slot.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListReinsertFirstMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListReinsertLastMethod) 'reinsertAtAnEnd'
			let callee = ListReinsertFirstName if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListReinsertFirstMethod) else ListReinsertLastName
			return try self.emitChainNodeMutator(func, list: list, giid: giid, callee: callee, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultUsed: resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'reinsertAtAnEnd'

		if methodTok.value.equals(RemoveMethod) 'remove'
			let node = try self.parseManagedListNodeArg(func, giid: giid, subject: methodTok.value)

			// ⭐⭐ **`remove` TAKES THE ELEMENT, SO THE ARGUMENT'S OWN HANDLE MAY NOT BE READ AGAIN — AND THIS
			// IS THE NARROW FACT THAT SURVIVED `managedListMethodFreesANode`.** Deleting that predicate was
			// right: nothing on this surface FREES a node any more. But `remove` still NULLS the node's
			// `value@16` when it moves the element out, so `n.value()` after `chain.remove(n)` reads an
			// emptied slot — MEASURED as **exit 139**, a segfault, on a `String` element, where the pre-rung
			// compiler refused the same program outright. A trivial element merely printed `0`, which is how
			// it survived a whole pass unnoticed.
			//
			// ⭐ **IT IS PER-BINDING, WHICH IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE FROM THE E3070 IT REPLACES.** That refusal
			// keyed on the STORAGE, so `ml.remove(a)` also killed a handle `b` on a node the removal cannot
			// touch — the over-refusal that disabled canonical `core.remove`. This marks the ARGUMENT's
			// binding and nothing else, so siblings stay readable and that case stays green; it is the
			// regression test for the narrowing.
			//
			// ⚠ **`detach` DELIBERATELY DOES NOT REACH THIS.** It leaves the element in the node, so its
			// handle goes on answering `value()` — which is what `core.detach` asserts.
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **AND THE MARK HAS TO LAND, OR THE CALL IS REFUSED — BECAUSE A MARK THAT MISSES IS SILENT.**
			// `setBindingPartiallyMoved` is keyed to the BINDING that owns the handle, so it is INERT on a
			// handle read out of a struct FIELD, an array element, or any other expression no live binding
			// owns — and every one of those is namable a second time, which is the whole difference from an
			// unnamed temporary. MEASURED at review: `chain.remove(k.node)` then `k.read()` over a `String`
			// ran to **exit 139**, where the pre-rung compiler refused the same program at the drop router.
			// The escape is the same shape the E3070 half had — a mark filed against a name the value has
			// since left — so it is closed at the door rather than by widening the mark.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE ONE UNMARKABLE SPELLING THAT IS SAFE IS THIS CHAIN'S OWN INSERTION, HANDED IN INLINE** —
			// `ml.remove(ml.insertLast(x))`, where the handle exists only as this argument and no second
			// expression can name it. It is admitted by IDENTITY (`nodeHandleMintedFrom`, stamped by the one
			// mint door) and not by shape.
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKED `pendingTempsContain` FIRST, AND THAT ADMITTED EVERY CALL RESULT — `k.held()`
			// INCLUDED, MEASURED AT EXIT 139 BY THE COORDINATOR ON THIS BRANCH.** A method handing back a
			// field's handle is an unnamed owned temporary too, so the predicate was strictly wider than the
			// sentence it was defending — the same defect this refusal exists to close, one level up. ⚠ The
			// stamp also carries WHICH chain, so `a.remove(b.insertLast(x))` is refused here rather than left
			// to `__list_remove_node`'s cross-chain abort.
			let mintedOnThisChain = (try self.nodeHandleMintedFrom.get(node) otherwise panic("dispatchManagedListMethod: v{node} has no node-handle mint slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")) == list

			if self.noLiveOwner(self.liveOwnerIndexOf(node)) and not mintedOnThisChain 'unmarkableHandle'
				throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedListBuiltinBaseName)}.{String.from(RemoveMethod)}` takes the element OUT of the node, so its handle must be one this statement can mark as moved — a name (`{String.from(RemoveMethod)}(n)`) or an insertion on this same chain, handed in inline. A handle arriving any other way — out of a field, an element, a call result, a merge — would go on answering `{String.from(ManagedListNodeValueMethod)}()` from the emptied slot with nothing left to refuse the read; bind it to a name first, or use `{String.from(ManagedListDetachMethod)}`, which leaves the element where it is", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
			end 'unmarkableHandle'

			self.setBindingPartiallyMoved(node, value: true)
			// The element is MOVED OUT — the caller owns it, and a statement-position `remove` drops it at
			// the statement's end like every other unbound owned temporary. Emitted through the ONE element
			// accessor, so its ownership, its result type and its buffer-surface mark are the array's answers.
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ListRemoveNodeName, container: list, args: pairArgs(list, second: node), giid: giid, reference: ContainerElementReference.movedOut, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, range: range)
		end 'remove'

		// ⭐ **`detach(node)` — THE MEMBER THE SECOND RULING UNLOCKED.** Void and statement-position, like
		// `clear`; the chain lets go of the node while the caller's handle keeps it, which is only meaningful
		// because a node is refcounted. The runtime hands back the element like its `remove` twin and it is
		// discarded here — see `buildListDetachNode`.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListDetachMethod) 'detach'
			return try self.emitChainNodeMutator(func, list: list, giid: giid, callee: ListDetachNodeName, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultUsed: resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'detach'

		// ⚠ **NO BORROW-SUBJECT WRITE IS RECORDED HERE, WHERE THE `List` DOOR RECORDS ONE.** That door hands
		// out ELEMENT borrows (`first`/`last`/`get`) that a `clear` frees; this one hands out node HANDLES, which
		// are OWNERS since `W138` and cannot be dangled by anything on this surface — see
		// `dispatchManagedListMethod`'s header for the predicate that used to be asked here and why it is gone.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ClearMethod) 'clear'
			return try self.emitChainClear(list, resultUsed: resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok, range: range)
		end 'clear'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedListBuiltinBaseName)}` method '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — shv2 provides {String.from(CreateMethod)}/{String.from(CountMethod)}/{String.from(IsEmptyMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListInsertFirstMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListInsertLastMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListInsertAfterMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListInsertBeforeMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListReinsertFirstMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListReinsertLastMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListHeadMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListTailMethod)}/{String.from(RemoveMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListDetachMethod)}/{String.from(ClearMethod)}, and {String.from(ManagedListNodeValueMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListNodeSetValueMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListNodeNextMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListNodePrevMethod)} on the node handle", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'dispatchManagedListMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE PLACE A `__ManagedListNode` VALUE IS MINTED — AND IT MINTS AN OWNED SECOND OWNER OF THE
	// NODE.** ⚖ USER RULING 2026-08-17 (the second on `W138`, superseding the first): nodes are REFCOUNTED,
	// and a handle is a reference like any other. So the result is enrolled as a sole-owned temp and drops at
	// scope exit through `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` → `__list_node_decref`, which is what lets a handle
	// outlive the chain in a return, a field or a parameter — the three positions `stdlib/List.maxon` needs.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE INCREF GOES THROUGH `emitIncref` AND NOT THROUGH A SECOND SPELLING OF IT.** That door is where
	// *"after this line the allocation has two owners"* is recorded (`markValueCoOwnsHeap`) and where the
	// retain is kept off a block-form `try`'s handler edge (`emitOwnershipRuntimeCall`); a raw
	// `emitRuntimeCall(MmIncrefName, …)` here would be a sixth producer of `__mm_incref` that the door's own
	// roster does not name, which is the shape that comes to miss whatever the door gains next.
	//
	// ⭐ **EVERY HANDLE LEAVES HERE OWNING EXACTLY ONE REFERENCE, AND WHICH SIDE PAID FOR IT IS DECIDED
	// INSIDE** — see the block on the `if` below, which is where v1's `callReturnRc1` rule lands and why it
	// applies to four of the eight entries and not to the other four. A mint that forgot its `+1` would be a
	// use-after-free rather than a leak, and one that paid it twice would leak the node forever.
	//
	// ⛔ **NO BORROW IS MINTED, AND THE MISSING `mintPendingBorrow` IS THE POINT.** The first ruling's handle
	// was a borrow of the chain plus a retain of the chain, and it was measured WRONG: `clear` frees nodes,
	// the retain did not keep one alive, and E3070 could not follow a handle out of the name it was minted
	// under — so a handle in a struct field read `0x3F3F3F3F3F3F3F3F` at exit 0. A refcounted node needs no
	// refusal because there is nothing to refuse: see `managedListMethodFreesANode`'s grave.
	function emitManagedListNodeHandle(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, nodeInstance TypeNameId, provenance NodeHandleProvenance, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let node = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: nodeInstance, range: range)

		// ⭐⭐ **WHICH SIDE TAKES THE HANDLE'S `+1`, AND IT IS DERIVED FROM WHETHER THE ENTRY CAN THROW
		// RATHER THAN CARRIED AS A SECOND FLAG — because for each kind there is only ONE side that can.**
		//
		//   • A NON-THROWING entry (the four insertions) hands back a node whose only reference is the
		//     CHAIN's — `emitListClaimsNode` takes none, the fresh `__mm_alloc`'s `+1` IS the chain's — and
		//     the same entries serve the `List` surface, which DISCARDS the node. So the entry cannot incref
		//     (that arrangement was built and measured at exit **101**; `emitListClaimsNode`'s ⚠ block has
		//     the numbers) and the `+1` belongs here, at the one door that knows a handle was kept.
		//   • A THROWING entry (the four navigators) hands back a node that already has owners, and this
		//     door structurally CANNOT pay for it: the incref would be emitted before the `try` fork, so it
		//     would run on the ERROR edge over a result that is not a node — and it would displace the call
		//     as the op `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` rewrites, MEASURED as **E3055 *"try requires a throwing
		//     function: this builtin call cannot fail"*** on `try list.head()`, a program that is exactly
		//     one. Those entries incref before ok-returning, which is v1's `callReturnRc1` verbatim.
		//
		// ⚠ **`markValueCoOwnsHeap` STILL HAS TO RUN ON THE CALLEE-PAID PATH**, and it does — through
		// `trackOwnedTemp(coOwned)` below, which is the same bit `emitIncref` sets. The `+1` moving into the
		// runtime does not make the value any less co-owned: the chain holds one too.
		if not isThrowingListRuntimeCallee(callee) 'plusOneIsThisDoors'
			self.emitIncref(node, range: range)
		end 'plusOneIsThisDoors'

		self.trackOwnedTemp(node, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)

		// ⭐ **THE PROVENANCE STAMP `remove`'s ONE EXEMPTION IS KEYED TO** — see `nodeHandleMintedFrom`. It is
		// written HERE, at the only door that mints a handle, so the exemption cannot be widened by a caller.
		//
		// ⭐ **AN `unchained` MINT STAMPS THE HANDLE'S OWN VALUE, WHICH IS NOT A SENTINEL — IT IS THE SEED
		// `mintValue` ALREADY WROTE, RESTATED.** `remove`'s test compares the stamp against a CHAIN value in
		// scope, and no handle's own SSA id can be one, so *"minted from no chain"* is spelled as *"a value
		// that is not a chain"* by construction rather than by a reserved number this column would then have
		// to defend. See `NodeHandleProvenance` for which mints have no chain to name.
		let stamp = match provenance 'mintSource'
			chain(sourceList) gives sourceList
			unchained gives node
		end 'mintSource'
		try self.nodeHandleMintedFrom.set(node, value: stamp) otherwise panic("emitManagedListNodeHandle: v{node} has no node-handle mint slot — every SSA value must be minted through mintValue, which seeds the column")

		return node
	end 'emitManagedListNodeHandle'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES ANYTHING READ THE NODE AN END-INSERTION LINKED (`W148`)?** Three ways the answer is YES,
	// and `resultUsed` alone reports only the first — which is exactly the *"a check filed against a spelling
	// narrower than the fact it defends"* shape `W138` shipped five times, so the roster is written out and
	// each row is closed by something checkable rather than by prose:
	//
	//   • **THE VALUE IS READ WHERE THE CALL STANDS** — `let n = ml.insertLast(x)`, an argument, a `return`.
	//     `resultUsed`, which is what that flag actually means.
	//   • **THE POSTFIX CHAIN CONTINUES** — `ml.insertLast(x).value()` as a STATEMENT, where `parsePostfix`
	//     forwards the LAST hop's `resultUsed` to every earlier hop and its own header says so outright
	//     (*"`resultUsed` describes the chain's LAST hop and the receiver-returning arms make the earlier
	//     hops' own use invisible to it"*). The next token is the `.` that door is about to consume, which is
	//     the same test asked one call earlier.
	//   • **THE CALL IS A `try` TARGET** — `parseTryTargetExpression` parses one `resultUsed: false` while the
	//     try's result IS read, and `requireVoidMethodIsStatement`'s header records the same hole. It is
	//     closed for these two members and ONLY for them: neither throws, so `parseTry`'s
	//     `requireThrowingTryTarget` refuses the spelling (E3055) before anything is emitted. Asked of the
	//     CALLEE here rather than asserted in a comment, so a chain member that ever gains a `throws` clause
	//     takes the minting branch by construction instead of quietly falling through this hole.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COST OF A FALSE `true` IS ONE REFCOUNT PAIR; THE COST OF A FALSE `false` IS AN UNOWNED HANDLE.**
	// Every disjunct is therefore written to over-answer YES rather than risk the other direction.
	function chainInsertionHandleIsRead(callee ByteArray, resultUsed bool) returns bool
		return resultUsed or self.at(TokenKind.dot) or isThrowingListRuntimeCallee(callee)
	end 'chainInsertionHandleIsRead'

	// ⭐ **`( target , value: X )` — THE ARGUMENT PAIR THE TWO INTERIOR INSERTIONS TAKE**, and the emit, for
	// `parseArraySet`'s reason: the value is parsed and MOVED IN before the call is emitted, so the call is
	// the last op in the block and the arm has one place that knows the argument order.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING HERE DESTROYS A REJECTED ELEMENT, WHERE `parseArraySet`'s CALLEES ALL MUST.** Those entries
	// can REFUSE after the move-in has already made them the owner (an out-of-range index), which is the leak
	// `emitDestroyRejectedElement` closes twice over. These two cannot: the only thing they refuse is a target
	// in another chain, and that is an ABORT (`RuntimeAbort.managedListNodeNotInThisChain`) taken BEFORE the
	// node is allocated — the process ends, so there is no continuation for a leak to be observed in.
	//
	// ⚠ The target is parsed FIRST and the value second, which is the source order and also the order the
	// runtime entry takes them.
	function parseManagedListInsertBeside(func IrFunction, list ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, callee ByteArray, methodTok Token, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let targetTok = self.current()
		let target = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.requireManagedListNodeArg(target, giid: giid, subject: methodTok.value, argTok: targetTok)
		try self.consumeArrayArgLabel(ArrayValueLabel, required: true)
		let valueStart = self.pos
		let raw = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		let checked = try self.requireContainerElementType(func, giid: giid, value: raw, valueStart: valueStart, methodTok: methodTok)
		let value = try self.moveElementIntoContainer(checked, giid: giid, valueStart: valueStart)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		return self.emitManagedListNodeHandle(callee, args: tripleArgs(list, second: target, third: value), nodeInstance: self.signatures.managedListNodeInstanceFor(giid), provenance: NodeHandleProvenance.chain(list), range: range)
	end 'parseManagedListInsertBeside'

	// `( node )` — the single node argument `remove`, `detach` and the two reinsertions each take.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HANDLE IS NOT MOVED IN AND NOTHING IS POISONED, EVEN THOUGH IT IS OWNED.** `remove` unlinks the
	// node and drops the CHAIN's reference; the caller's own reference is untouched, which is what lets the
	// handle outlive its own removal and makes its later scope-exit drop correct — the node stays ALIVE, and a
	// second `remove` of it is a no-op (`ListRuntime.buildListUnlinkNode`'s `owner@32` gate).
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT DOES END THE HANDLE'S USEFULNESS IS THE ELEMENT LEAVING IT**, and that is the caller's
	// business rather than this parse: `remove` empties `value@16`, so `dispatchManagedListMethod`'s `remove`
	// arm marks the argument's binding moved (E3102 on a re-read) and REFUSES a handle it cannot mark. The
	// other three leave the element where it is and mark nothing.
	function parseManagedListNodeArg(func IrFunction, giid GenericInstanceId, subject ByteArray) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let argTok = self.current()
		let node = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		try self.requireManagedListNodeArg(node, giid: giid, subject: subject, argTok: argTok)

		return node
	end 'parseManagedListNodeArg'

	// ⭐ **IS THIS ARGUMENT A HANDLE ON A NODE OF *THIS* LIST'S ELEMENT TYPE?** The type test is the instance
	// IDENTITY: `managedListNodeInstanceFor` derives the one node instance this list's handles carry, so a
	// handle from a list of another element type is refused by comparing gids rather than by unpacking either
	// type.
	//
	// ⚠ **`subject` IS THE MEMBER THE AUTHOR WROTE, AND IT WAS HARD-WIRED TO `remove` UNTIL `BATCH37`.**
	// `detach` already reached this refusal and was told about `remove` — a sentence naming a member the
	// program does not contain — and this rung adds four more callers, so the noun is the caller's to supply.
	// It is the SAME defect class as the roster refusals two doors up, one argument in.
	//
	// ⚠ It does NOT decide whether the node is in this CHAIN, which is a run-time fact no parse can hold:
	// `owner@32` answers that at the entry point, and a foreign node aborts there.
	function requireManagedListNodeArg(node ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, subject ByteArray, argTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.tagOf(node) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.valueNameId(node) == self.signatures.managedListNodeInstanceFor(giid) 'aNodeOfThisList'
			return
		end 'aNodeOfThisList'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedListBuiltinBaseName)}.{String.from(subject)}` takes a node handle of this list — a `{String.from(ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName)} with {self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid)}`, which `{String.from(ManagedListInsertFirstMethod)}`/`{String.from(ManagedListInsertLastMethod)}`/`{String.from(ManagedListHeadMethod)}`/`{String.from(ManagedListTailMethod)}` hand back", line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column)
	end 'requireManagedListNodeArg'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NODE-HANDLE SURFACE — `value()`, `setValue(v)`, `next()`, `prev()`.** Four members, and the
	// split between them is exactly the split between reading a node's PAYLOAD and stepping its LINKS.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **`value()` ON A CONCRETE MANAGED ELEMENT IS AN OWNED READ, NOT A BORROW, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES
	// `setValue` REPRESENTABLE AT ALL.** The setter RELEASES the element the node held (`__list_node_set_value`),
	// so a borrow taken before it dangles the instant it runs — which is `/specs/managed-list.md`'s own
	// `node.set-value-old-survives`, a case that reads the old String AFTER the replacement and expects it
	// intact. v1 reaches the same pairing from the other end and names the same failure
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:9129-9157`, *"the set-value-old-survives use-after-free"*): its `value()` on a
	// concrete managed element is an inline load PLUS an `__mm_incref`, classified owned and released at the
	// caller's last use. The arithmetic is one line — insert ⇒ rc 1 · `let old = value()` ⇒ rc 2 ·
	// `setValue` ⇒ rc 1 (`old` alone) · print ⇒ alive · scope exit ⇒ 0, freed once.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A THREE-WAY SPLIT ON THE ELEMENT, NOT A BLANKET RULE, and v1 makes the same three.** A
	// TRIVIAL element owns nothing and never reaches the ownership arms at all
	// (`emitContainerElementAccessor` returns from its trivial arm). An OPAQUE element — a `T` inside a
	// shared generic body — stays a BORROW deliberately: `stdlib/List.maxon`'s `get`/`current` forward
	// `return node.value()`, and the reference such a body could take is `retainFunc@64`, which for a byte
	// record is a DEEP CLONE rather than an incref, so a "retain" there would hand back a different record
	// from the one the slot holds (`emitContainerElementAccessor`'s own ⚠ block on that). Only a CONCRETE
	// MANAGED element retains.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT BORROW IS ANONYMOUS (`EmptySourceText`), and that is a residual rather than a
	// decision that costs nothing.** E3070's subject is a STORAGE name, and the storage this element lives
	// in is the LIST — which this dispatcher cannot name: it holds the node, whose own binding is not
	// storage anything writes through. Closing it needs a `ValueId` → SUBJECT NAME derivation the parser does
	// not have: `borrowSubjectNameAt` reads a TOKEN INDEX, and `nodeHandleMintedFrom` — the one value→chain
	// map there is — answers with a VALUE, which is not the currency a subject is spelled in.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT IS REACHABLE, AND THE SPELLING IS A DISCARDED `remove` (`W147`). MEASURED — exit 139:**
	//
	//     let n = chain.insertLast("<heap string>") ;  let v = n.value() ;  _ = chain.remove(n)
	//     print("[{v}]")                                                    // -> 0xC0000005
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE MECHANISM IS THE MOVE-OUT, NOT THE NODE's LIFETIME — WHICH IS WHY THREE PROBES MISSED IT.**
	// `remove` hands the ELEMENT to the caller; discarded with `_ =`, that unbound temporary drops it at the
	// statement's end, and `v` — borrowed out of the node under NO subject — still names it. BIND the result
	// and the same program is correct, because the binding then owns what `v` aliases. Probes built on
	// `clear` cannot reach it at all: `clear` moves no element out, and a live handle keeps its node alive.
	// ⇒ **A NEGATIVE PROBE HERE NEEDS THIS POSITIVE CONTROL FIRST**; three `clear`-shaped ones all read
	// exit 0 and said nothing.
	// ⚠ **THE RETAINED READ NARROWS `W147` TO THE OPAQUE CASE AND DOES NOT CLOSE IT.** A concrete managed
	// element now comes back owning a reference, so that exact program is correct; the same shape inside a
	// SHARED GENERIC BODY still borrows, and the subject is still anonymous there.
	function dispatchManagedListNodeMethod(func IrFunction, node ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, resultUsed bool, immutableReceiverName ByteArray, receiverOwner ParamPositionMask, receiverStorage ParamPositionMask) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(methodTok)

		// ⚠ **`setValue` IS A WRITE THROUGH THE HANDLE, so a `let`-bound one is refused (E3019) exactly as a
		// `let`-bound chain's `insertLast` is** — which is why this dispatcher takes a receiver where it took
		// only a value before. `/specs/managed-list.md` writes `var node` for both `setValue` cases and `let`
		// for every read-only one, so the canonical spec already spells the distinction.
		try self.noteReceiverWrite(func, immutableReceiverName: immutableReceiverName, receiverOwner: receiverOwner, receiverStorage: receiverStorage, methodTok: methodTok, mutatesReceiver: managedListNodeMethodMutatesReceiver(methodTok.value))

		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListNodeValueMethod) 'value'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			let reference = ContainerElementReference.retained if self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) else ContainerElementReference.borrowed
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ListNodeValueName, container: node, args: singletonArgs(node), giid: giid, reference: reference, borrowSubjectName: EmptySourceText, range: range)
		end 'value'

		// ⭐ **`next()` / `prev()` — THE TWO THROWING NODE NAVIGATORS**, one arm because the direction is the
		// runtime entry's offset and nothing here differs.
		//
		// ⭐ **THE MINT IS `unchained`, AND THAT IS AN ANSWER RATHER THAN A GAP.** This dispatcher holds a
		// NODE, not a chain, so there is no chain value in scope to stamp — and the arrival it produces,
		// `ml.remove(n.next())`, is precisely one `remove` must refuse: an unnamed handle no statement can
		// mark as moved. `NodeHandleProvenance.unchained` is that refusal arriving by construction.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListNodeNextMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListNodePrevMethod) 'step'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			let callee = ListNodeNextCheckedName if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListNodeNextMethod) else ListNodePrevCheckedName
			return self.emitManagedListNodeHandle(callee, args: singletonArgs(node), nodeInstance: giid, provenance: NodeHandleProvenance.unchained, range: range)
		end 'step'

		// ⭐ **`setValue(v)` — VOID, so statement-position only, and the receiver comes back as what the
		// statement still denotes.** The value is MOVED IN through the SAME door an insertion uses, so a
		// borrowed literal is promoted, an owned temporary adopted and a live binding co-owned — one answer
		// to *"what does a container do with an element handed to it"*, and the runtime entry releases the
		// element it displaces so the node still holds exactly one reference afterwards.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedListNodeSetValueMethod) 'setValue'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			let value = try self.parseContainerElementArg(func, giid: giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(ListNodeSetValueName, args: pairArgs(node, second: value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
			return node
		end 'setValue'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ManagedListNodeBuiltinBaseName)}` method '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' — shv2 provides {String.from(ManagedListNodeValueMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListNodeSetValueMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListNodeNextMethod)}/{String.from(ManagedListNodePrevMethod)}", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'dispatchManagedListNodeMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **A `__ManagedMemoryCursor` RECEIVER — ITS OWN DISPATCH, because a cursor is not a container.** It
	// holds no elements: what it answers is a POSITION and the element at one, so the members that make sense
	// on it (`current`/`index`/`advance`/`retreat`/`seek`/`peek`) and the members that make sense on the
	// storage it points into (`get`/`setLength`/`grow`) are disjoint sets on two receivers.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROSTER GATE IS ASKED FIRST**, as it is on every other surface that carries one: "does this
	// member exist?" is the question every other refusal about the call presupposes — see
	// `dispatchArrayMethod` for the two measured spellings that made the ORDER matter as much as the test.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO IMMUTABLE-RECEIVER RULE, AND THAT IS THE CORPUS'S SIGNATURE RATHER THAN A RELAXATION.**
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon:514-526` declares `advance`/`retreat`/`seek` on a plain `self` and
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon:496` binds the cursor with `let`, so a `let`-bound cursor must be navigable — which
	// is the same answer a `let`-bound BUFFER already gets, for the same reason (both references declare every
	// member of both types on a plain `self`).
	//
	// ⚠ **AND NO E3070 BORROW SUBJECT EITHER, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT FACT AND A NARROWER ONE.** A navigation
	// writes the CURSOR's `position@8`; it does not write the buffer, so it cannot invalidate a borrow taken
	// out of the buffer. What COULD invalidate the cursor is a write to the SOURCE, and the cursor is
	// protected there by the retain rather than by the borrow set: the record cannot be freed under it, and a
	// position that the source has since shortened past is caught at the next navigation by the LIVE `length`
	// read (`emitCursorPositionOutOfRange`) rather than trusted from a snapshot.
	function dispatchCursorMethod(func IrFunction, cursor ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, resultUsed bool, borrowSubjectName ByteArray) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(methodTok)
		let roster = try self.requireSurfaceMember(MemberSurface.cursor, typeName: ManagedMemoryCursorBuiltinBaseName, methodTok: methodTok)

		// ⭐ **THE TWO INFALLIBLE READS.** `current()` yields the ELEMENT and goes through
		// `emitContainerElementAccessor` — the compiler-emitted element-read door, one of the two the language
		// now has (see its ⛔⛔ header for the corpus half) — as a BORROW:
		// the buffer still owns what is in the slot, so a `String` element handed back here is neither cloned
		// nor released. `index()` yields a plain integer and is emitted as one.
		if methodTok.value.equals(CursorCurrentMethod) 'current'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ManagedCursorCurrentName, container: cursor, args: singletonArgs(cursor), giid: giid, reference: ContainerElementReference.borrowed, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, range: range)
		end 'current'
		if methodTok.value.equals(CursorIndexMethod) 'index'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCursorIndexName, args: singletonArgs(cursor), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'index'

		// ⭐ **THE THREE VOID NAVIGATORS, ONE ARM.** They differ only in the callee and in whether a target is
		// written, which is the same folding `dispatchArrayMethod`'s shift arm does and for the same reason:
		// two argument protocols where there is one would be the twin these rosters exist to prevent.
		if methodTok.value.equals(CursorAdvanceMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(CursorRetreatMethod) 'step'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			let stepCallee = ManagedCursorAdvanceName if methodTok.value.equals(CursorAdvanceMethod) else ManagedCursorRetreatName
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(stepCallee, args: singletonArgs(cursor), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'step'
		if methodTok.value.equals(CursorSeekMethod) 'seek'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			let target = try self.parseOneBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, paramName: ManagedMemoryIndexParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCursorSeekName, args: pairArgs(cursor, second: target), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'seek'

		// `peek(ahead)` reads WITHOUT moving, so it is the one member that both takes an argument and yields an
		// element — the accessor door again, and a BORROW again, for `current`'s reason.
		if methodTok.value.equals(CursorPeekMethod) 'peek'
			let ahead = try self.parseOneBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, paramName: CursorPeekParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ManagedCursorPeekName, container: cursor, args: pairArgs(cursor, second: ahead), giid: giid, reference: ContainerElementReference.borrowed, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, range: range)
		end 'peek'

		// ⚠ Unreachable while the roster and these arms agree — `dispatchArrayMethod`'s drift panic, for its
		// reason: a roster member with no arm would otherwise be handed the very refusal that says it is
		// provided.
		let drift = rosterDriftMessage("dispatchCursorMethod", member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, rosterFuncs: "`cursorSurfaceMemberNames`")
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'dispatchCursorMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **`List from [a, b, c]` — `List.create()` PLUS ONE `List.append` PER MEMBER (W153).** The cursor sits
	// on the `List` identifier. It is `parseSetMemberList`'s shape one container over, and it is that shape for
	// that function's reason: `stdlib/List.maxon`'s `InitableFromArrayLiteral` `init` is not exported, so a
	// desugar naming it would be `E3008: function 'List.init' is not exported` — the identical wall `W41` met
	// on `Map` and `W90` on `Set`, answered the identical way.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO BUILD AN INTERMEDIATE `Array` AND HAND IT TO `__list_from`**, a runtime entry that
	// CONSUMED the source buffer one machine word per slot, NULLing each slot it moved an element out of. Two
	// things went with that entry: the packed-element refusal it needed (a `Byte` array strides 1, not 8) and
	// the whole intermediate record. Reading the members straight is what removes both.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE FIRST MEMBER FIXES THE ELEMENT TYPE AND THEREFORE THE INSTANCE**, exactly as the first element of
	// a `[…]` fixes an array's — through `literalColumnTypeFor`, which is the SAME door
	// `arrayLiteralInstanceFor` puts the same question to, so the element types this construction admits
	// are unchanged by the rewrite. Every later member is held to that type by the ordinary argument check on
	// the declared `List.append(value Element)`, which is also what decides the MOVE: the element is the list's
	// from there, and a second parse-time move would be the leak `emitContainerLiteralElement`'s note measures.
	function parseListFromConstruction(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let listTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.from)

		// The source must be an array literal `[…]`; `List from <other-expr>` is not a construction shv2 parses.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'notArrayLiteral'
			let srcTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`List from` requires an array literal `[…]` — `List from <expr>` for another source is a later slice", line: srcTok.line, column: srcTok.column)
		end 'notArrayLiteral'

		return try self.parseListMemberList(func, listTok: listTok)
	end 'parseListFromConstruction'

	// ⭐ **`List from [m0, m1, …]` ONTO THE DECLARED `List`** — the cursor sits on the `[`. Split out from the
	// head parse for `parseSetMemberList`'s reason: the head decides WHICH construction this is, the body
	// decides what it builds, and the newline skips a bracketed list needs belong with the body.
	function parseListMemberList(func IrFunction, listTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(listTok)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		// A BRACKETED LIST MAY BE WRITTEN OVER SEVERAL LINES — the three skips are `parseArrayLiteral`'s own,
		// placed where that parser puts them, so a literal that parsed before this rewrite parses after it.
		self.skipNewlines()

		// An EMPTY literal names no element, so there is no instance to build. `List` has no `[]`-style default
		// the way an array literal does (a bare `[]` is an `Array with integer`), because nothing here states an
		// element type — and the alias spelling that DOES state one is what the refusal points at.
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'noMembers'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(ListBuiltinBaseName)} from []` states no {ContainerElementColumnNoun} type — an empty list is `<alias>.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` over a `{String.from(ListBuiltinBaseName)} with <{ContainerElementColumnNoun}>` alias", line: listTok.line, column: listTok.column)
		end 'noMembers'

		let firstTok = self.current()
		let first = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		let giid = self.signatures.builtinInstanceForElement(ListBuiltinBaseName, element: try self.literalColumnTypeFor(first, literalKind: ListLiteralSubject, columnNoun: ContainerElementColumnNoun, valueTok: firstTok))

		// ⭐ **THE LITERAL RECORDS ITS OWN INSTANTIATION**, because it is the one door a `List` is born through
		// with no `with <Element>` to anchor a diagnostic on — `parseSetMemberList`'s arm, for its reason.
		self.artifact.recordInferredInstantiationSite(GenericInstantiationSite.create(giid, nameLine: listTok.line, nameColumn: listTok.column, filePath: self.filePath))

		let list = try self.emitContainerLiteralCreate(ListBuiltinBaseName, giid: giid, anchor: listTok, range: range)
		try self.emitContainerLiteralElement(ListBuiltinBaseName, method: AppendMethod, container: list, value: first, anchor: listTok, range: range)
		self.skipNewlines()

		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreMembers'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
			let member = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			try self.emitContainerLiteralElement(ListBuiltinBaseName, method: AppendMethod, container: list, value: member, anchor: listTok, range: range)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'moreMembers'

		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightBracket)
		return list
	end 'parseListMemberList'

	// ⚠ **`emitListCreate` AND `emitListAppend` STOOD HERE UNTIL THE W153 REVIEW.** They were
	// `emitContainerLiteralCreate` and `emitContainerLiteralElement` with `ListBuiltinBaseName` written in,
	// which is those two functions' own headers' point: a container that joins the member-by-member door joins
	// it by naming its base, not by growing a third and fourth copy of the ownership rule.

	// ==========================================================================================
	// The `Vector` builtin container — a FIXED-SIZE array (see `SignatureIndex.VectorBuiltinBaseName`).
	// ==========================================================================================

	// ⭐ **WHAT A VECTOR MAY HOLD.** Its slot is an inline word of the element's own stride, so any element the
	// managed-memory runtime already strides fits — an integer of any declared range, a `bool`, a `float`. What it may
	// NOT hold is anything MANAGED: `create()` PUBLISHES every slot (`__managed_resize`) while
	// `element_destroy@40` stays 0, so a managed element would leak at teardown and every `set` would
	// overwrite a live pointer. A `Character` is refused for that same reason and not a separate one.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AN OPAQUE `T` IS ADMITTED, AND WHAT IT IS JUDGED BY IS THE INSTANTIATION SET (S2u).** This arm
	// used to refuse outright — *"a vector inside a generic body has no statically known element stride to
	// size its storage with"* — and **that premise was false**: a vector's storage is the same fused managed
	// record an array's is, held behind ONE 8-byte field slot, and an opaque `T`'s stride is the uniform
	// machine word every type parameter occupies under dictionary-passing (`LAYOUT_TYPE_PARAM_SLOT_BYTES`,
	// which `emitOpaqueArrayCreateOp` has stamped for `Array with <type parameter>` since P1.7 slice 3b-i).
	// The SIZE is not the problem either — `Vector with 4 Element` states its 4 in the source, and the
	// substitution carries it into `Vector with 4 Int` through the intern key (`substituteInstanceArgsThrough`
	// carries `fixedSize` explicitly, for exactly this).
	//
	// ⛔ **WHAT IS GENUINELY UNSOUND IS ONE INSTANTIATION AT A TIME, AND IT IS THE `create()` THAT PUBLISHES
	// SLOTS — NOT THE STRIDE.** `emitSizedContainerRecord` grows the record to N by ZEROING it, so all N slots are
	// readable by the type's own contract; a trivial element's zero IS a value, and a managed element's is a
	// NULL. A type parameter can be instantiated at a managed type, so the question the concrete arm below
	// answers off `containerElementIsManaged` is answered here off the INSTANTIATION SET
	// (`requireEveryInstantiationPublishesAValue`) — the same boundary, asked of the thing that is knowable
	// at this position. The two are one invariant with two doors, exactly as this function and
	// `requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement` already are.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE MANAGED TEST IS `containerElementIsManaged`, AND THE TAG WHITELIST BELOW CANNOT STAND IN FOR
	// IT — THAT WAS A MEASURED SEGFAULT, NOT A THEORETICAL GAP (review finding 1).** `tagIsIntegral` answers
	// TRUE for `named`, whose comment claims a `named` type is a ranged alias; it is not, in general — a
	// boxed union, a DECLARED STRUCT and a generic-instance ALIAS NAME all carry `named` too. So this gate
	// admitted `Vector with 2 IntArray` (`typealias IntArray = Array with Int`) and the vector published two
	// NULL slots: `count()` answered **2** while `try v.get(0)` took the empty-slot branch — two elements it
	// does not have, exit 0, no diagnostic — and `for a in v` with a read of `a` dereferenced those NULLs
	// through `__managed_get_unchecked` and **ACCESS-VIOLATED (0xC0000005)**. `requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement`'s
	// E3106 is the rule that normally holds this shut, and `emitSizedContainerRecord` reaches `__managed_resize`
	// without passing it; that header predicted this exact failure in these words — *"If this refusal is ever
	// relaxed, that read becomes a null deref in a function whose own text still looks correct."*
	// ⇒ The question is asked of the ONE door that answers it (`requireListElementType` opens with the same
	// door), and the whitelist below stays as the POSITIVE rule for everything that is not managed.
	function requireVectorElementType(giid GenericInstanceId, tok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaqueElement'
			try self.requireEveryInstantiationPublishesAValue(giid, tok: tok)
			return
		end 'opaqueElement'

		if self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'managedElement'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(self.vectorElementRefusal(giid), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'managedElement'

		let tag = maxonTypeTag(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))
		if tagIsIntegral(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean or tagIsFloat(tag) 'inlineScalar'
			return
		end 'inlineScalar'

		throw ParseError.unsupported(self.vectorElementRefusal(giid), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'requireVectorElementType'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY INSTANTIATION OF THE ENCLOSING GENERIC TYPE MUST MAKE THIS VECTOR'S PUBLISHED SLOTS VALUES
	// (E2015, S2u).** A `Vector with <N> <type parameter>` is created ONCE, in a body shared by every
	// instantiation, and `create()` publishes all N slots by zeroing them — so the slots are elements for a
	// trivial instantiation and null pointers for a managed one. The shared body cannot branch on that, so
	// admitting the construct means every instantiation must be trivial, and one managed instantiation
	// refuses the body.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED, BOTH SYMPTOMS, before this gate existed** — `type Holder uses Element` with
	// `typealias Slot = Vector with 4 Element`, at `typealias StrHolder = Holder with String`: `slot.count()`
	// answered **4** while `try slot.get(0)` took `emitLoadElemAndOkReturn`'s empty-slot branch and THREW
	// (four elements it does not have, exit 0, no diagnostic), and a `for e in slot` returning `e`
	// **SEGFAULTED (139)** — `__managed_get_unchecked` drops that empty-slot arm, on the guarantee this
	// refusal supplies. They are `requireVectorElementType`'s own header's two symptoms for the CONCRETE
	// managed element, re-measured one axis over.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS `requireOpaqueArrayCopyable`'s SHAPE AND FOR ITS REASON**: the refusal is contingent on the
	// instance registry, so the construct that forces it is the INSTANTIATION rather than the body's own
	// line — and when the body is `stdlib/`'s, that line is one no user wrote.
	// `blameLibraryRefusalOnInstantiation` re-anchors it there; in a user body it already lands on a line its
	// author wrote and is left alone.
	function requireEveryInstantiationPublishesAValue(giid GenericInstanceId, tok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.genericTypeFieldManagedElementInstantiation(typeNameIdOf(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))) 'managedInstantiation'
			noInstance then return
			instance(offender) then self.blameLibraryRefusalOnInstantiation(offender)
		end 'managedInstantiation'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(VectorBuiltinBaseName)} with <N> <type parameter>` — a vector PUBLISHES all N of its slots at `{String.from(CreateMethod)}` by zeroing them, and this generic type is instantiated with a type whose slot is a heap POINTER: a zeroed slot is an element for a trivial instantiation and a NULL for a managed one, so `{String.from(CountMethod)}()` would answer N while every `{String.from(GetMethod)}` reports an empty slot and a `for … in` read dereferences the null. Instantiate this type at integer or bool elements only — a `float` TYPE ARGUMENT is refused separately today, for its own reason — or hold the elements in an `{String.from(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName())} with <type parameter>`, which publishes nothing and grows by `{String.from(ArrayPushMethod)}`", line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
	end 'requireEveryInstantiationPublishesAValue'

	// The ONE wording of "a vector cannot hold that", shared by the two gates that reach it — the managed
	// door and the tag whitelist — so a reader meets one sentence about one rule however the element was
	// refused. It names the element as the SOURCE spells it (`arrayElementDisplayName`) — a declared alias, a
	// struct, an instance, or the primitive's own keyword.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT PRINT THE TAG, and that is finding 1's own defect one layer up.** `typeTagName(named)` is
	// the word `int`, so an `IntArray` element read back as *"'IntArray' (int)"* — a sentence refusing a type
	// while calling it something it is not, in the very message explaining that the tag was the wrong thing to
	// judge by.
	function vectorElementRefusal(giid GenericInstanceId) returns String
		return "`{String.from(VectorBuiltinBaseName)}` holds its elements INLINE and publishes every slot at `{String.from(CreateMethod)}`, so it has no element destructor to stamp and a '{self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid)}' element would leak its storage and be read back as a null slot — a vector's element is an integer, a bool or a float"
	end 'vectorElementRefusal'

	// ⚠⚠ **`emitVectorCreateOp` STOOD HERE AND IS `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `create()` NOW (W190).** It was
	// `__managed_create` (an empty record, every index out of bounds) followed by `__managed_resize` to `N`,
	// which together are *"zero-initialized, N elements"*; the corpus body spells the same pair as
	// `Self{}` + `managed.grow(countof(Self))` + `managed.setLength(countof(Self))`, which is
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s own `resize` written against the buffer. `countof(Self)` is what made that
	// possible: the answer IS the size, the size is at the CALL, and a `static … returns Self` reads it out
	// of the hidden slot every call site fills in from the instance it builds.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `create()` REACHED `__managed_resize` WITHOUT PASSING `requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement`,
	// WHOSE E3106 IS THE RULE THAT NORMALLY MAKES A PUBLISHED SLOT SAFE TO READ, AND THE CORPUS BODY REACHES
	// `setLength` the same way.** A published slot of a MANAGED element is a null pointer every accessor
	// dereferences, so the guarantee is carried by `requireVectorElementType` instead — which is why that
	// gate did NOT go with the emit and is now asked at `requireConstructedVectorElementType`, the
	// construction door a declared `create()` arrives through. **The two are one invariant with two doors —
	// do not relax either without the other.**

	// ⚠ **`vectorMethodMutatesReceiver` AND `dispatchVectorMethod` STOOD HERE (W190).** The dispatcher's last
	// arm was `set`, emitted through `parseArraySet` — the same container door the `Array` uses — and its
	// mutation answer fed `noteReceiverWrite` (E3019) and `noteBorrowSubjectWrite` (E3070). Both questions are
	// the DECLARATION's now: `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `set` writes through `self` like any other declared
	// method, E3019 is a BUILTIN-surface rule a declared type is exempt from (`STDLIB-BRINGUP.md`, the ruling
	// `Set` paid three cases for), and the borrow reaches a caller through
	// `noteStorageReachingCall`'s edge instead of through a receiver mask this door supplied.

	// Is the cursor on a `Vector from` construction — the bare `Vector` identifier immediately followed by
	// `from`? `atListFromConstruction`'s twin, read by BOTH the declaration sweep (which pre-interns the
	// instances the construction can name) and `parsePrimary`'s construction arm, for that predicate's stated
	// reason: taught to one and not the other, the parser's own instance read becomes the FIRST write to the
	// shared registry mid-parse — an order-dependent gid no drift guard can see.
	//
	// ⚠ **BARE BASE NAME ONLY, where `Set` also admits an instance ALIAS.** A vector's element AND its size
	// both come from the literal, so an alias would state nothing the literal does not already carry;
	// `Vec3 from […]` keeps falling through to `E2004 Undefined variable 'Vec3'` as it does today.
	function atVectorFromConstruction() returns bool
		return self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.from and self.current().value.equals(VectorBuiltinBaseName)
	end 'atVectorFromConstruction'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Vector from [a, b, c]` — AND IT EMITS NOT ONE RUNTIME OP OF ITS OWN.** The source array literal
	// already built exactly the record a vector is (`emitArrayCreateOp` + the pushes), so the construction is
	// the array literal RETYPED: `Vector with 3 <elem>` names the same 48 bytes `Array with <elem>` does, and
	// its drop callee is the same `__managed_decref` the literal's owned temp was already registered with. A
	// `__vec_from` would have been a copy of a record into an identical record.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SIZE COMES FROM THE TOKENS, NOT FROM THE RECORD.** It is part of the TYPE, so it must be known
	// at parse time — `length@8` is a runtime fact — and the literal's element COUNT is exactly what the
	// source wrote between its brackets (`arrayLiteralElementCountAt`, the same counter the sweep pre-interns
	// with, so the instance the parser reads back is the instance the sweep minted).
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **NOTHING IS INTERNED UNTIL THE ELEMENT IS ADMITTED (review finding 6).** Minting first meant that a
	// `Vector from […]` over an element the sweep did NOT pre-intern made the parse's own read the FIRST write
	// to the shared registry mid-parse — the order-dependent gid `atVectorFromConstruction` above exists to
	// prevent — and it did so on the way to refusing the program anyway.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE GATE IS ASKED OF THE SOURCE ARRAY'S INSTANCE, WHICH IS NOT A STAND-IN — IT IS THE SAME
	// QUESTION.** `Array with T` and `Vector with N T` share their element exactly, and every door
	// `requireVectorElementType` opens (`containerElementIsOpaque`, `containerElementIsManaged`,
	// `containerElementType`, `arrayElementDisplayName`) reads only that element. So the vector's admissibility
	// is fully decided by the array the literal already built, and no vector instance need exist to decide it.
	function parseVectorFromConstruction(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let vecTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.from)

		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'notArrayLiteral'
			let srcTok = self.current()
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(VectorBuiltinBaseName)} from` requires an array literal `[…]` — a vector's SIZE is part of its type, and only a literal states it in the source", line: srcTok.line, column: srcTok.column)
		end 'notArrayLiteral'

		// The literal is parsed FIRST so its own refusals — an empty `[]`, mixed element types, an element type
		// no literal admits — are reported by the reader that owns those sentences, before this arm asks a
		// question about an element the program does not have.
		let openIndex = self.pos
		let arr = try self.parseArrayLiteral(func, element: ArrayLiteralElementSource.inferred)
		let arrGiid = self.valueNameId(arr)
		try self.requireVectorElementType(arrGiid, tok: vecTok)

		let size = try self.arrayLiteralElementCountAt(openIndex, anchor: vecTok)
		let giid = self.signatures.vectorInstanceForElement(self.signatures.containerElementType(arrGiid), size: size)

		// The retype is the WHOLE construction: same record, same bytes, same owner. `arr` is already this
		// statement's tracked owned temporary (`finishArrayLiteral`), and both instances drop through
		// `__managed_decref` (`genericInstanceBoxDropCallee`), so the value's registered ownership stays true of it.
		self.retypeValue(arr, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(giid))
		return arr
	end 'parseVectorFromConstruction'

	// ⭐ **HOW MANY ELEMENTS THE ARRAY LITERAL AT `openIndex` WRITES** — the top-level commas between its
	// brackets, plus one. Read from TOKENS because both of its callers need the answer before any expression
	// is parsed: the declaration sweep (which has no parser state at all) and the construction above (which
	// must know the instance before it can retype the value into it), and the two must agree exactly or the
	// parser's read is the first write to the shared instance registry.
	//
	// Nesting is tracked over the three bracket kinds a literal element can open, so `[f(1, 2), g(3)]` counts
	// TWO and `[[1, 2], [3, 4]]` counts two as well.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN EMPTY `[]` IS REFUSED RATHER THAN COUNTED AS ONE (review finding 6).** "Commas plus one" is only
	// true of a literal that has an element, and `[]` has none — `parseArrayLiteral` refuses it outright. Left
	// answering 1, this counter told the declaration SWEEP to intern three `Vector with 1 …` instances for a
	// literal the very next line rejects: registry rows minted on behalf of a program that cannot compile.
	function arrayLiteralElementCountAt(openIndex TokenIndex, anchor Token) returns FixedElementCount throws ParseError
		var depth = 0
		var commas = 0 as FixedElementCount
		var sawElement = false
		var i = openIndex
		while i < self.tokens.count() 'scan'
			let kind = self.kindAt(i)
			if kind == TokenKind.leftBracket or kind == TokenKind.leftParen or kind == TokenKind.leftBrace 'opens'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'opens'
			if kind == TokenKind.rightBracket or kind == TokenKind.rightParen or kind == TokenKind.rightBrace 'closes'
				depth = depth - 1
				if depth == 0 'closesTheLiteral'
					if not sawElement 'emptyLiteral'
						throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(VectorBuiltinBaseName)} from []` — an empty array literal states neither the vector's element type nor its size, and both are part of the type", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
					end 'emptyLiteral'

					return commas + 1
				end 'closesTheLiteral'
			end 'closes'
			if kind == TokenKind.comma and depth == 1 'topLevelSeparator'
				commas = commas + 1
			end 'topLevelSeparator'
			// Anything between the literal's own `[` and its `]` is an element token — except a NEWLINE, which
			// `finishArrayLiteral` skips at every element boundary, so `[\n]` is as empty as `[]`. Keyed on the
			// OPENER'S INDEX rather than on the bracket kind, so a nested `[` still counts as content.
			if i != openIndex and kind != TokenKind.newline 'elementToken'
				sawElement = true
			end 'elementToken'
			i = i + 1
		end 'scan'

		// The literal is unterminated. `parseArrayLiteral` would report it against the token it actually stops
		// at; this counter runs FIRST, so it says the same thing here rather than answering a count for a
		// literal that has no closing bracket.
		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(VectorBuiltinBaseName)} from […]` — the array literal has no closing `]`", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'arrayLiteralElementCountAt'

	// ⭐ **THE NAME AN E3019 WOULD BLAME FOR THIS BINDING-BACKED RECEIVER — EMPTY when it may be
	// written through.** The ONE place the `let` rule and the parameter exception meet, for EVERY
	// container: `String`, `Array` and `Set` all ask through here (see `noteReceiverWrite`).
	//
	// A `var` may be written through, and so may a PARAMETER — which is NOT `mutable` (a parameter
	// cannot be rebound, `ParametersAreMutable`) yet whose container very much can be written, because
	// the parameter is a borrowed reference to the caller's record. `let a = [1,2,3]` may not, however
	// it was bound — a literal, a `create()`, an alias of a field or of another local.
	//
	// **Every answer here is the runnable oracle's, measured one program at a time**, and the parameter
	// case is not a nicety: without it every container-mutating helper in the corpus is rejected.
	//
	// ⚠ **`isParameter` IS NOT `boundValue < paramCount` WEARING A FLAG, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A
	// MEASURED ANSWER.** The parameter ValueId convention (`requireReceiverValue`) tempts a reader to
	// derive this from the value instead of storing it — but a `let` that ALIASES a parameter carries
	// the parameter's own ValueId while being a `let`, and `let a = p` / `a.push(9)` is **E3019 in the
	// oracle** (measured). Deriving it from the value would silently accept that. The flag distinguishes
	// "this binding IS the parameter" from "this binding holds the parameter's value"; the ValueId
	// cannot. Do not "simplify" it away.
	//
	// ⭐ **AN ITERATED BINDING IS UNWRITABLE WHATEVER IT WAS DECLARED**, which is why the question this
	// asks is `bindingStorageIsWritableHere` and not `bindingStorageIsWritable`: a `var` and a PARAMETER
	// both answer "writable" to the declaration test and both would destroy the loop's borrow.
	function immutableReceiverNameOf(binding VarInfo, baseTok Token) returns ByteArray
		// ⭐⭐ **A VALUE THAT ALIASES A `let`-DECLARED GLOBAL IS NOT WRITABLE, WHATEVER ITS BINDING SAYS
		// (W117).** Asked FIRST, and ahead of the declaration test, because it is the stronger fact: a `var`
		// holding the record of a global the program declared immutable may no more be written through than a
		// `let` may. This function's own header already promises exactly this — "EMPTY when it may be written
		// through" — so the taint belongs here rather than at each caller.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND PUBLISHING THE NAME HERE IS ALSO WHAT REFUSES IT AS A CALL ARGUMENT**, at no extra site:
		// `parseVariableReference` publishes `lastBareBindingImmutableName` through this very function, so
		// `bump(b)` carries the blame into the op's `argImmutableNames` column and SemanticCheck's call-site
		// check finds it against the callee's write summary. One answer, three doors.
		if self.bindingAliasesAnImmutableGlobal(binding) 'aliasesAnImmutableGlobal'
			return baseTok.value
		end 'aliasesAnImmutableGlobal'

		if self.bindingStorageIsWritableHere(binding) 'writable'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'writable'

		return baseTok.value
	end 'immutableReceiverNameOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE VALUE THIS NAME DENOTES MAY-ALIAS A `let`-DECLARED TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL'S RECORD (W117)?**
	// The ONE home of the taint question the write doors ask, over the mark `markImmutableGlobalRead` maintains
	// — which by the time a binding exists covers a direct read of the global, an owned promotion of one
	// (`ownedFormOfBorrowedValue` carries it), a merge that joined one (`propagateImmutableGlobalReadToPhi`)
	// and the result of a call to a function the sweep published as returning one.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* FOLDED INTO `bindingStorageIsWritableHere`, AND THAT IS A DISTINCTION THE
	// FILE ALREADY DRAWS.** That predicate also feeds the REBIND door (`requireMutableBinding`), and a rebind
	// writes the NAME, not the record: `var b = A` followed by `b = [7]` points `b` at a fresh array and never
	// touches `A`. Refusing it would be a false reject on a legal program. See `requireMutableBinding`'s own
	// header for the name-versus-record split this rests on — "the reassignment `x = 5` gives the NAME a new
	// value; the field store `p.x = 5` leaves the name alone and writes the RECORD it denotes".
	//
	// ⚠ A self-field alias's `boundValue` is 0 and 0 is `self`'s own id (`createSelfField` leaves it so), which
	// is harmless here for a reason worth stating rather than relying on: ValueId 0 is a PARAMETER, and
	// `markImmutableGlobalRead`'s only producers are a global load, a promotion of one and a call result — all
	// of them minted above the parameter block, so 0 is never in the set and the coincidence cannot fire.
	function bindingAliasesAnImmutableGlobal(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return self.immutableGlobalReads.contains(binding.boundValue)
	end 'bindingAliasesAnImmutableGlobal'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY THE STORAGE THIS NAME DENOTES BE WRITTEN THROUGH?** — and it is NOT `binding.mutable`,
	// which answers the narrower "may this NAME be rebound?".
	//
	// A PARAMETER is the whole difference: it may not be rebound (E2013), and it IS a borrowed reference to
	// the caller's record, which the callee may legitimately mutate — `dest.append(src)` inside a helper is
	// ordinary Maxon. See `VarInfo.isParameter` for the measurement.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO RULES REST ON IT, AND THEY MUST NOT DRIFT.** E3019 refuses a mutating METHOD on an immutable
	// receiver (`immutableReceiverNameOf`); E3078 refuses a mutable BINDING made from an immutable name
	// (`requireMutableBindingSource`). Both are asking "would this write reach storage its owner declared
	// read-only?", so both must exempt a parameter — MEASURED on the runnable oracle for each: a mutating
	// call on a parameter receiver is accepted, and `var q = p` on a struct PARAMETER compiles and mutates
	// (returns 9), where the same two lines on a `let` LOCAL are E3019 and E3078 respectively. Written down
	// twice, the parameter exemption would have been added to one and not the other — and the stdlib itself
	// depends on it (`Builtins.maxon`'s `var numerator = significandDigits`, `FilePath.maxon`'s
	// `var trimmedBase = base`), so half the rule is a compiler that cannot build its own standard library.
	function bindingStorageIsWritable(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return binding.mutable or binding.isParameter
	end 'bindingStorageIsWritable'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME QUESTION, ASKED *HERE*** — the declared fact above, minus the storage an enclosing
	// `for … in` has locked. A loop keeps walking the record it captured in its preheader, so a write that
	// replaces that record frees what the header is still reading; the lock is therefore not "was this
	// declared `let`?" but "would this write destroy the loop's borrow?", and it overrules a `var` and a
	// PARAMETER alike (see `bindingIsIterationLocked`).
	//
	// ⭐ **EVERY DOOR THAT WRITES A BINDING ASKS IT, AND THEY MUST ALL GET ONE ANSWER**: the mutating-METHOD
	// receiver (E3019, `immutableReceiverNameOf`), the field STORE's instance rank (E2013,
	// `requireWritableInstance`, which is `p.x = 5`), and the REBIND (`requireMutableBinding`), which asks
	// this and then adds the one conjunct a rebind needs on top of it. They are spellings of one write — the
	// record `p` denotes is modified — so a further kind of writable binding, or a further LOCK, taught to
	// one of them and not the others is exactly the drift `bindingStorageIsWritable`'s header is about, one
	// qualifier further out. The lock therefore lives HERE, in the one conjunct they share, and nowhere else.
	//
	// ⚠ E3078's source test (`requireMutableBindingSource`) deliberately reads the UNQUALIFIED predicate:
	// it asks whether the SOURCE was declared writable, which is a fact about the declaration and not
	// about where in the body the second name is being bound.
	function bindingStorageIsWritableHere(binding VarInfo) returns bool
		return not self.bindingIsIterationLocked(binding) and self.bindingStorageIsWritable(binding)
	end 'bindingStorageIsWritableHere'

	// ⭐ **DISPATCH AN INTERFACE METHOD ON A CONSTRAINED TYPE-PARAMETER VALUE (P1.7a slice 2a).** `receiver` is a
	// `typeParameter`-tagged value (`self.item`, a `T` field read) and the cursor sits on the method name. The concrete type is UNKNOWN in the shared body, so it dispatches through the runtime WITNESS:
	// the enclosing type's `where` constraint whose interface declares this method supplies the witness, and the
	// method's decl-order slot in that interface is the table index. Emits `MaxonOp.witnessDispatch`, which
	// `lowerMaxonToStd` turns into the fused `StdOp.witnessCall`. The receiver is BORROWED (arg 0); its user
	// arguments (none for `digest()`) follow it in `args`.
	//
	// ⭐ **ITS ARGUMENT LIST IS PARSED AND SLOTTED BY THE SAME DOORS EVERY OTHER CALL USES (P1.7a slice
	// 2b-vi).** It used to be a bare comma loop of `parseValueExpression` — a third, unwritten spelling of
	// "parse an argument list" that read no `name:` label, checked no arity and performed no label→position
	// slotting. The same method therefore obeyed OPPOSITE rules depending on how it was reached: `add(1, 2)`
	// was E2053 on the concrete receiver and accepted here, while `add(1, b: 2)` was accepted there and
	// `E2004: Undefined variable 'b'` here. And the missing arity check was not cosmetic — an under-supplied
	// dispatch read an UNINITIALISED REGISTER as its missing actual and returned it (measured: exit
	// 1073754132 from a clean compile; with a ranged return type, a spurious `Range check failed` panic on a
	// correct program). `parseCallArgs` + `slotCallArgs` fix all of it by deleting the second spelling.
	function dispatchWitnessMethod(func IrFunction, receiver ValueId, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		// ⭐ **THE CANDIDATES ARE FOUND BEFORE THE ARGUMENTS AND SELECTED AFTER THEM (R10c).** "Do the
		// constraints provide this name at all?" is answerable without reading a single argument, so it is
		// still asked first and still reports before an argument expression can raise anything of its own —
		// which is what keeps this rung from moving any existing diagnostic. "Which of them did you mean?"
		// cannot be answered until the argument COUNT is known, so it waits.
		let candidates = try self.requireWitnessDispatchCandidates(receiver, methodTok: methodTok)

		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		// FRESH columns holding the USER's arguments only — the borrowed receiver is prepended after
		// slotting, not before it. `parseCallArgs` counts arguments from 0 against the SOURCE, so the
		// receiver must not be in the array or the user's first argument would be judged as its second.
		var userArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		var argLabels = ByteArrayArray.create()
		var argRanges = SourceRangeArray.create()
		// `argImmutableNames` is filled and DISCARDED: it exists to blame a binding for E3019 (an immutable
		// argument at a MUTATING parameter), and a mutation summary is keyed by callee — which a witness
		// dispatch has not got. Under dictionary-passing there is no one impl whose mutation of a parameter
		// could be the answer, so the question is not merely unasked here, it is unaskable at this call.
		var argImmutableNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		// The first argument's label is OPTIONAL at an interface / type-parameter receiver and MANDATORY
		// from the second on — see `consumeArgLabel`.
		//
		// The callee is passed EMPTY, and E3070's call-argument door is unaskable here for the SAME reason
		// `argImmutableNames` is discarded above: the borrow check asks whether the callee writes the storage
		// this argument names, and that summary is keyed by callee. Under dictionary-passing a witness dispatch
		// has no one impl to key on. `noteBorrowSubjectCallArg` refuses an unnamed callee explicitly rather
		// than letting the summary lookup miss — see there.
		try self.parseCallArgs(func, callee: EmptySourceText, anchor: methodTok, args: userArgs, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, rule: ArgLabelRule.firstMayBeNamed)
		let rparenTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		let target = try self.pickWitnessDispatchByArity(candidates, argCount: userArgs.count(), receiver: receiver, methodTok: methodTok)
		let args = try self.slottedWitnessArgs(target, receiver: receiver, userArgs: userArgs, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, methodTok: methodTok)

		// A `Self`-typed formal (`equals(other Self)`) requires an argument that is a value of `T` — a concrete
		// literal (`self.item.equals(42)`) would be marshalled into the `Self` pointer and faulted. The SAME
		// reject the `==` operator form runs, one shared helper.
		try self.requireWitnessSelfArgs(target, args: args, receiver: receiver, methodTok: methodTok)

		let range = SourceRange.between(methodTok, last: rparenTok)
		let result = try self.mintWitnessDispatchResult(target, receiver: receiver, methodTok: methodTok, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		self.emitWitnessDispatch(result, args: args, target: target, range: range, siteTok: methodTok)
		return result
	end 'dispatchWitnessMethod'

	// ⭐ **THE USER'S ARGUMENTS IN THE INTERFACE'S DECLARATION ORDER, WITH THE BORROWED RECEIVER BACK AT
	// `args[0]` (P1.7a slice 2b-vi)** — or the first slotting failure, as the SAME code a direct call's
	// would get. The slotting itself is `Project.slotCallArgs`, the one home of the label→position mapping;
	// nothing about labels is re-derived here.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE LABELS BIND AGAINST THE INTERFACE, NOT THE CONFORMER.** Under dictionary-passing the shared
	// body has no concrete callee to ask — the same reason slice 2b-v types a `try`'s binding off the
	// interface's `throws` clause — and it is forced by shv2's own conformance rule besides:
	// `ConformanceCheck.signatureMatches` compares arity and TYPES only (it reads `paramNames` to format
	// E3016 and never compares it), so two conformers may spell their parameters differently and only the
	// interface's names are common to every one of them.
	//
	// The receiver is prepended AFTER slotting, restoring the `args[0]` = receiver / interface formal `j` =
	// `args[j+1]` convention that `requireGenuineSelfArgs`, `floatWidenedWitnessArgs` and
	// `emitWitnessDispatch` all index by — none of which is label-aware, and none of which needs to be.
	function slottedWitnessArgs(target WitnessDispatchTarget, receiver ValueId, userArgs ValueIdArray, argLabels ByteArrayArray, argRanges SourceRangeArray, methodTok Token) returns ValueIdArray throws ParseError
		match slotCallArgs(target.paramNames, args: userArgs, argLabels: argLabels, argDefaultSlots: NoDefaultArgSlots) 'slot'
			ordered(ids) then return withWitnessReceiver(receiver, ordered: ids)
			// The ARITY error blames the call, the two LABEL errors blame the offending argument — the same
			// split `SemanticCheck.validateCall` makes, `methodTok` being this call's own anchor (it is what
			// `SourceRange.between(methodTok, last: rparenTok)` starts the dispatch op's range at).
			arityMismatch(expected, got) then throw ParseError.witnessArgCountMismatch(self.witnessCalleeName(target), expected: expected, got: got, line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
			unknownLabel(argIndex, label) then throw ParseError.witnessUnknownArgLabel(self.witnessCalleeName(target), label: label, line: witnessArgAnchor(argRanges, argIndex: argIndex).line, column: witnessArgAnchor(argRanges, argIndex: argIndex).column)
			duplicateArg(argIndex, paramName) then throw ParseError.witnessDuplicateArg(self.witnessCalleeName(target), paramName: paramName, line: witnessArgAnchor(argRanges, argIndex: argIndex).line, column: witnessArgAnchor(argRanges, argIndex: argIndex).column)
		end 'slot'
	end 'slottedWitnessArgs'

	// What a witness dispatch's argument diagnostics BLAME: `<Interface>.<method>`, which is what the call
	// actually binds against. A direct call names its callee; a dispatch has no callee name to name — the
	// concrete impl is not known in the shared body, and naming one conformer's would be a lie at every
	// other instantiation.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DECLARING INTERFACE, NOT THE CONSTRAINT'S (R10c).** It used to read the constraint's name out of
	// `enclosingWhereConstraints`, which is the same name for every requirement an interface supplies —
	// including the ones it merely INHERITS. Under `where T is Derived` with `interface Derived extends Base`
	// that made `Base`'s `label()` report as `Derived.label`, which is a requirement `Derived` does not
	// declare. The name now travels ON the resolved target, so the interface a message names is the interface
	// the message's arity, formals and labels were all read off.
	function witnessCalleeName(target WitnessDispatchTarget) returns String
		return "{String.from(target.declaringInterfaceName)}.{String.from(target.methodName)}"
	end 'witnessCalleeName'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE DOOR A `witnessDispatch` IS EMITTED THROUGH** — the `.method()` form and the comparison-
	// OPERATOR form (`emitTypeParamComparison`) both come here, so the op's `isThrowing` stamp and the
	// `lastWitnessDispatch*` record are written for BOTH by construction rather than remembered at each. A
	// dispatch whose record was missed would leave a `try` on it typing its `(e)` binding off a stale clause;
	// keeping the emit and the record in one place is what makes that unrepresentable.
	// ⚠ **`isVoid` IS DERIVED HERE AND WAS A PARAMETER, WHICH MADE IT A RULE WITH THREE HOMES.** It is a pure
	// function of the `target` this already holds, and the three callers each spelled their own answer — two
	// copies of `returnTypeName.equals(InterfaceVoidTypeName)` and one hard-coded `false` on the comparison
	// path (correct only because `Comparable.compare` returns `Ordering`, which the derivation also reads as
	// non-void). Nothing made the three agree, and the op's void-ness decides which register the lowering
	// reads a result from.
	function emitWitnessDispatch(result ValueId, args ValueIdArray, target WitnessDispatchTarget, range SourceRange, siteTok Token)
		let isVoid = target.returnTypeName.equals(InterfaceVoidTypeName)
		let placed = self.floatWidenedWitnessArgs(target, args: args, siteTok: siteTok)

		self.lastWitnessDispatchIndex = self.artifact.module.ops.count()
		self.lastWitnessDispatchName = target.methodName
		self.lastWitnessDispatchThrows = target.throwsClause
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.witnessDispatch(result, args: placed, witness: target.witnessValue, methodIndex: target.methodIndex, interfaceDeclIndex: target.interfaceDeclIndex, isVoid: isVoid, isThrowing: throwsClauseIsThrowing(target.throwsClause)), range: range)
	end 'emitWitnessDispatch'

	// ⭐⭐ **WIDEN AN INTEGRAL ACTUAL SUPPLIED FOR A `float` INTERFACE FORMAL — the witness twin of
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.crossFloatDomainArgs`, and it was MISSING.** A DIRECT call gets this from the
	// callee's resolved signature at the Maxon→Std boundary; a witness dispatch has no callee to look up, so
	// its arguments reached the fused `witnessCall` exactly as written, while the op's `argFloatMask` said what
	// the CALLEE reads. An `int` actual for a `float` formal therefore travelled as a GPR/i64 while the
	// conforming impl reads it from XMM/f64:
	//
	// **MEASURED (both on `interface Scaled { function scale(k float) returns float }` with
	// `self.item.scale(2)`): x64 compiled clean and returned the WRONG ANSWER** — `movRegImm32 rdx, 2` ahead of
	// `callMem [rbx + 24]`, so the callee's `mulsd xmm0, …` multiplied whatever was left in xmm0 — **and wasm
	// trapped `indirect call type mismatch`**, since `call_indirect` checks the declared functype against the
	// target's own exactly. The wasm half is the hole the `requireIndirectlyReachableParamsAreMachineWords`
	// guard could not see while it existed: it refused a narrow (i32) parameter, but an f64 one is legal and
	// only DIVERGES when the actual is in the wrong register file — which is precisely what this widening fixes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT THE ONLY WIDENING ANY MORE, AND THE SPLIT IS BY WHAT EACH END CAN SEE.**
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.witnessFormalTypes` derives the masks from the INTERFACE's declared formals, so a formal
	// that resolves to `float` declares XMM whatever the actual is — and an unwidened `int` actual is then a raw
	// integer bit pattern moved into a float register, which x64 refuses outright (MEASURED: `a
	// register-to-register move from rax to xmm0 crosses register files`). So the lowering widens too, through
	// the same `crossFloatDomainArgs` a function-value call uses.
	// What THIS one still owns is the formal spelled `float` in the interface: it makes the VALUE an f64 at
	// PARSE time, which is what types the expression around it, and nothing downstream can do that. The
	// lowering's covers the formal the interface does NOT spell — an ASSOCIATED type, whose `float` is a
	// conformer's `with float` and is invisible here. The two compose: an actual this widened is already an f64,
	// so the lowering's fast path returns the argument list verbatim.
	//
	// It runs in `emitWitnessDispatch` because that is the ONE door both dispatch forms come through, so the
	// `.method()` and comparison-OPERATOR forms cannot acquire different argument placement. `promoteToFloat`
	// is idempotent, so a genuine `float` actual passes through untouched and emits nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT WAS ARITY-TOLERANT AND IS NOT ANY MORE (P1.7a slice 2b-vi).** A witness dispatch's arity was
	// unchecked when this was written (`self.item.scale()` with no argument compiled), so it walked the
	// ACTUALS and treated a formal-less actual as non-float rather than reading past `paramTypeNames`. Both
	// dispatch forms now guarantee one actual per formal (see `requireGenuineSelfArgs`), so the two counts
	// agree and walking the actuals is simply walking the formals — the loop is unchanged, and what changed
	// is that `witnessFormalIsFloat` may now panic on an index it once absorbed.
	function floatWidenedWitnessArgs(target WitnessDispatchTarget, args ValueIdArray, siteTok Token) returns ValueIdArray
		// FAST PATH, for `crossFloatDomainArgs`'s reason: only a `float` FORMAL can force a widening and
		// almost no interface method has one, so every other dispatch returns its arguments verbatim — no array
		// built, no op emitted, and the whole conformance corpus stays byte-identical.
		if not self.witnessTargetHasFloatFormal(target) 'noFloatFormal'
			return args
		end 'noFloatFormal'

		var placed = ValueIdArray.create()

		// `args[0]` is the BORROWED receiver, which fills no formal — interface formal `j` is `args[j+1]`, the
		// same mapping `requireGenuineSelfArgs` walks. It is copied across before the loop so the loop body
		// never has to subtract from an index that could be 0.
		placed.push(try args.get(0) otherwise panic("floatWidenedWitnessArgs: a witness dispatch's args are empty — `args[0]` is the receiver, pushed by both dispatch forms before any actual"))

		for j in 0 upto args.count() - 1 'eachActual'
			let actual = try args.get(j + 1) otherwise panic("floatWidenedWitnessArgs: args.get OOB at {j + 1} below count {args.count()}")

			if not self.witnessFormalIsFloat(target, formalIndex: j) 'noWiden'
				placed.push(actual)
				continue
			end 'noWiden'

			placed.push(self.promoteToFloat(actual, opTok: siteTok))
		end 'eachActual'

		return placed
	end 'floatWidenedWitnessArgs'

	// Does ANY formal of the dispatched interface method declare `float`? The fast-path predicate above, kept
	// beside the per-formal test so the two read the same constant.
	function witnessTargetHasFloatFormal(target WitnessDispatchTarget) returns bool
		for j in 0 upto target.paramTypeNames.count() 'eachFormal'
			if self.witnessFormalIsFloat(target, formalIndex: j) 'found'
				return true
			end 'found'
		end 'eachFormal'
		return false
	end 'witnessTargetHasFloatFormal'

	// Is interface formal `formalIndex` declared `float`? Both askers stay within the declared formals — one
	// loops the formals directly, the other loops actuals a checked arity has made equinumerous with them
	// (see `floatWidenedWitnessArgs`) — so an out-of-range index is a compiler defect, not an arity mismatch
	// to absorb. The name is spelled by `FloatConformerName`, the same constant `interfaceReturnMaxonType`
	// reads it by.
	function witnessFormalIsFloat(target WitnessDispatchTarget, formalIndex ElementCount) returns bool
		return (try target.paramTypeNames.get(formalIndex) otherwise panic("witnessFormalIsFloat: paramTypeNames.get OOB at {formalIndex} below count {target.paramTypeNames.count()} — a dispatch reaching here has one actual per formal")).equals(FloatConformerName)
	end 'witnessFormalIsFloat'

	// The requirements `<receiver>.<method>(…)` could bind, or the "no constraint provides it" reject when the
	// constraints supply none. The reject is the `.method()` form's own; the OPERATOR form asks
	// `findWitnessDispatchCandidates` directly because its miss is E3005 (see `requireOperatorWitness`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A `static` REQUIREMENT IS NOT A CANDIDATE FOR A CALL THAT SUPPLIES A RECEIVER (A2w).** The search
	// matches by METHOD NAME (it must — see `findWitnessDispatchCandidates`), so a `static` requirement was
	// reached by `self.item.origin()` and its slot bound, with the receiver prepended at `args[0]` in front of
	// a callee that declares no parameter for it. **MEASURED: x64-windows compiled clean and returned the
	// right answer by ABI LUCK** — the spurious receiver landed in an argument register the zero-parameter
	// impl never reads — **while `--target=wasm32-wasi` compiled clean and TRAPPED `indirect call type
	// mismatch`**, because `call_indirect` compares the declared functype against the target's own. The same
	// wall `floatWidenedWitnessArgs` describes: an argument-placement disagreement x64's registers absorb and
	// wasm's typed calls do not.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A FILTER, NOT A VERDICT ON THE CHOSEN ONE, for `witnessTargetIsProtocol`'s reason.** Two
	// requirements may share a name and differ in receiver kind, and an instance-syntax call can only ever
	// mean the instance one — so the statics are removed BEFORE arity selects, and the arity/ambiguity
	// messages then speak only of requirements the call could have meant.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT THROWS, AND IT MAY NOT MERELY RECORD — see `ParseError.staticRequirementNotDispatchable` for
	// the three measurements.** This reject first shipped as a `recordArtifactDiagnostic` + `return
	// candidates`, on the argument that handing the statics back "keeps the parse on the path it took
	// before this reject existed". It does — and that path's next step is `pickWitnessDispatchByArity` over
	// the requirements just rejected, which then reports an argument COUNT or an ambiguity as if one of them
	// could still have been meant. Worse, a recorded diagnostic does not survive a later `ParseError` in the
	// same file at all (`abortedParseArtifact` salvages only the type names), so the refusal was contingent
	// on nothing else in the file failing.
	function requireWitnessDispatchCandidates(receiver ValueId, methodTok Token) returns WitnessDispatchTargetArray throws ParseError
		let candidates = try self.findWitnessDispatchCandidates(receiver, methodName: methodTok.value, errorTok: methodTok)
		if candidates.count() == 0 'noneProvideIt'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("no requirement named '{String.from(methodTok.value)}' is provided by {self.witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver)} — a method call on a value whose type is an interface, or on a constrained type parameter, dispatches through a witness table, so the method has to be one that interface declares", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'noneProvideIt'

		// FAST PATH, for `floatWidenedWitnessArgs`' reason: a `static` requirement is rare — a sweep of both
		// spec corpora, the compiler and the stdlib found exactly ONE outside `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s
		// literal-init protocols, and NO program anywhere that reaches one through a constraint — so almost
		// every dispatch hands its candidates straight back: no array built, and the whole conformance corpus
		// stays allocation-identical (measured: scale-test delta 0 at all six rungs).
		if not witnessCandidatesNeedReceiverFilter(candidates) 'allTakeAReceiver'
			return candidates
		end 'allTakeAReceiver'

		var dispatchable = WitnessDispatchTargetArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto candidates.count() 'eachCandidate'
			let candidate = try candidates.get(i) otherwise panic("requireWitnessDispatchCandidates: candidates.get OOB at {i} below count {candidates.count()}")
			if witnessRequirementTakesReceiver(candidate) 'takesAReceiver'
				dispatchable.push(candidate)
			end 'takesAReceiver'
		end 'eachCandidate'

		if dispatchable.count() > 0 'someTakeAReceiver'
			return dispatchable
		end 'someTakeAReceiver'

		// The filter kept NOTHING, so every requirement of this name is `static` — which makes candidate 0 one
		// of them, and the one the author wrote the call against. A second static of the same name is the same
		// fact twice, not a rival, so only the first is named.
		let refused = try candidates.get(0) otherwise panic("requireWitnessDispatchCandidates: candidates.count() is {candidates.count()} but get(0) failed")

		throw ParseError.staticRequirementNotDispatchable(self.witnessCalleeName(refused), receiver: self.witnessDispatchReceiverPhrase(receiver), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireWitnessDispatchCandidates'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY REQUIREMENT `receiver` CAN REACH UNDER `methodName` (P1.7a slice 2b; made a LIST, and made
	// TRANSITIVE, by R10c; both receiver kinds since P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** For a CONSTRAINED TYPE
	// PARAMETER that is every `where` constraint on it; for an EXISTENTIAL it is the one interface the value
	// is held at. Each candidate carries the VALUE holding the table to jump through, the requirement's SLOT
	// in that table, and the requirement's own signature.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO RETURN ON THE FIRST HIT, AND THAT WAS TWO SILENT PICKS IN ONE LOOP.** It stopped at the
	// first CONSTRAINT declaring the name (so a later constraint declaring it too was never consulted) and it
	// matched by NAME ALONE with no arity test (so the first requirement of that name took every call).
	// Neither could produce a diagnostic; both produce a bound slot. Which of the candidates is meant is
	// therefore decided by the CALLER, which is the only party that knows what it can discriminate on — an
	// argument COUNT for `.method()`, the protocol's own SHAPE for an operator — and both go through
	// `pickSoleWitnessCandidate`, so neither can silently take the first of two.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE SLOT NUMBERING IS `interfaceWitnessSlots`', asked of the DECLARATION INDEX** — the same walk
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable` lays the table out by, so `methodIndex` names the slot the emitted
	// blob actually fills. `errorTok` positions `requireInterfaceForParse`'s undeclared-interface reject.
	function findWitnessDispatchCandidates(receiver ValueId, methodName ByteArray, errorTok Token) returns WitnessDispatchTargetArray throws ParseError
		var candidates = WitnessDispatchTargetArray.create()

		// ⭐ **AN EXISTENTIAL SEARCHES THE ONE INTERFACE THE VALUE IS HELD AT, AND ITS WITNESS IS THE FAT
		// POINTER'S OWN SECOND HALF (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).** There is no constraint list to walk and no
		// hidden parameter to name: the receiver carries its table with it. Everything BELOW the search — the
		// argument slotting, the arity selection, the result typing, the emitted op — is shared verbatim,
		// which is the whole reason `WitnessDispatchTarget` names a witness VALUE rather than a constraint.
		if self.tagOf(receiver) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialReceiver'
			let iface = try self.requireInterfaceForParse(self.internedName(self.valueNameId(receiver)), methodTok: errorTok)
			self.appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates(candidates, iface: iface, witness: self.witnessOfValue(receiver), methodName: methodName)
			return candidates
		end 'existentialReceiver'

		// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER'S TOKEN IS TURNED INTO A POSITION ONCE, HERE, BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT A `where` CLAUSE
		// IS INDEXED BY (W14).** `WhereConstraint.typeParamIndex` is a POSITION in the declaring type's `uses`
		// list — the witness-slot numbering the dictionary-passing ABI threads — and the receiver carries an
		// IDENTITY. Until W14 the two were the same number and this loop compared them directly; they are now
		// different kinds of number and the conversion has to be written. A receiver that is not one of THIS
		// declaration's parameters (a concrete generic-method result the parser tags opaque in a non-generic
		// caller) matches no constraint, which is the same answer the direct compare gave and is why the miss
		// returns the empty candidate list rather than raising.
		let receiverPosition = try typeParamPositionIn(self.valueNameId(receiver), declarationName: self.enclosingType, paramNames: self.enclosingTypeParams) otherwise return candidates
		for i in 0 upto self.enclosingWhereConstraints.count() 'eachConstraint'
			let constraint = try self.enclosingWhereConstraints.get(i) otherwise panic("findWitnessDispatchCandidates: enclosingWhereConstraints.get OOB at {i}")
			if constraint.typeParamIndex != receiverPosition 'otherParam'
				continue
			end 'otherParam'

			let iface = try self.requireInterfaceForParse(constraint.interfaceName, methodTok: errorTok)
			// ⚠ The witness is resolved for EVERY constraint on this receiver, ahead of knowing which one
			// declares `methodName` — a constraint that declares none contributes no candidate, and inside a
			// closure it has already claimed an env slot and emitted a load nothing reads. That costs one
			// machine word and one dead op on the rare `where T is A and B` where only one of the two is being
			// dispatched, and it buys the property this file spends most on: ONE candidate builder, reached
			// identically by both receiver kinds (see `appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates`). Splitting the
			// witness out to resolve it lazily would give the constraint route its own copy of the slot walk.
			self.appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates(candidates, iface: iface, witness: try self.witnessValueForConstraint(i, anchor: errorTok), methodName: methodName)
		end 'eachConstraint'

		return candidates
	end 'findWitnessDispatchCandidates'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE HOLDING `where` CONSTRAINT `constraintIndex`'s TABLE, IN THE FUNCTION BEING EMITTED
	// (W58).** The ONE door every witness dispatch through a constrained type parameter goes through, and it
	// has two answers because two kinds of body can ask:
	//
	//   • the DECLARING function's own body — the hidden witness PARAMETER `parseFunction` reserved
	//     (`witnessParamValueId`). No op at all: the table is already in a register.
	//   • a LIFTED CLOSURE's body — the table is NOT a parameter of the lifted function and cannot become one:
	//     a closure is reached through the uniform `(userargs, env)` INDIRECT shape, which has exactly one
	//     hidden slot and it is the environment, and the call site (`cmp(a, b)` inside a sort helper) knows
	//     nothing of the closure's origin, let alone its constraints. So the witness travels as an ORDINARY
	//     CAPTURE — one env slot holding one machine word, stored where the closure is BUILT (inside the
	//     constrained method, which holds the table) and read back with the same `loadIndirect` every other
	//     capture is read with.
	//
	// ⭐ **SO A WITNESS IS A CAPTURED VALUE, NOT A SECOND CHANNEL — and that is forced, not preferred.** It
	// also costs nothing new downstream: `StdOp.witnessCall` takes a `witnessAddr` VALUE and resolves nothing
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.appendWitnessCall`), so a table that arrived through an env slot is indistinguishable
	// from one that arrived in a parameter — which is the property P1.7a-existentials bought when it turned the
	// op's `constraintIndex` into a ValueId, and this is the third receiver route to spend it.
	//
	// ⚠ **NESTED CLOSURES INHERIT BY CHAINING, ONE HOP PER LEVEL — which is why this works where an ordinary
	// nested capture is REFUSED** (`captureScopeOf`'s non-immediate-frame reject, whose problem is that there
	// is no env chain to walk). Each level captures from its own IMMEDIATE enclosing context, so a witness
	// reaches depth 2 as a capture of a capture, and every hop is a hop the existing env machinery already
	// makes. `fillInheritedWitnessCaptures` re-enters here, one context out, to close each one.
	//
	// The refusal is the shape `requireWitnessSourceForForwarding` refuses one door over, for its reason: a
	// `static function` (or a synthesized default helper) reserves no witness parameters, so there is no table
	// anywhere in the chain for the closure to inherit.
	function witnessValueForConstraint(constraintIndex WitnessSlotIndex, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.closureCaptureScopes.isEmpty() 'theDeclaringFunctionsOwnBody'
			return self.witnessParamValueId(constraintIndex)
		end 'theDeclaringFunctionsOwnBody'

		if constraintIndex >= self.declaringWitnessParamCount 'nothingToInherit'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a closure that dispatches through the type's `where` constraints, written inside a `static function` (or a field-default helper) that carries no witness tables of its own — the closure inherits its table through its environment from the function that built it, and a static has no receiver to source one from. Write the closure in an instance method, or take the operation as a function parameter", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'nothingToInherit'

		let slot = self.inheritedWitnessSlotFor(constraintIndex)
		// The read side of the env slot pair, derived through the SAME `wordSlotStorageType` the store side
		// (`buildClosureEnv`) asks — see `emitCaptureRead`, whose loads this deliberately mirrors rather than
		// re-deriving a width. A witness table is a plain machine word, so the declared type IS the storage
		// type; going through the shared door anyway is what keeps that true if either ever changes.
		return self.emitIndirectLoad(MaxonType.integer, box: self.closureEnvParamId, offset: envSlotOffset(slot), storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(MaxonType.integer), range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'witnessValueForConstraint'

	// The env slot carrying constraint `constraintIndex`'s inherited witness in the closure now being parsed,
	// claiming a fresh one on first sight — `captureSlotFor`'s dedup, keyed by a name no source can write, so a
	// constraint dispatched five times in one body occupies one slot and stores once.
	//
	// ⚠ The value parked in the capture column is `UnfilledInheritedWitness` and is never read as a value: this
	// is the ONLY producer of those slots and it enters every one of them in `inheritedWitnessCaptures`, which
	// `fillInheritedWitnessCaptures` drains before `buildClosureEnv` reads the column. It cannot be filled now
	// — the value belongs to the enclosing function's block, which is not current until the restore.
	function inheritedWitnessSlotFor(constraintIndex WitnessSlotIndex) returns EnvSlotIndex
		for i in 0 upto self.inheritedWitnessCaptures.count() 'existing'
			let seen = try self.inheritedWitnessCaptures.get(i) otherwise panic("inheritedWitnessSlotFor: inheritedWitnessCaptures.get OOB at {i} below count {self.inheritedWitnessCaptures.count()}")
			if seen.constraintIndex == constraintIndex 'reuse'
				return seen.slot
			end 'reuse'
		end 'existing'

		let slot = self.captureSlotFor(witnessCaptureName(constraintIndex), enclosingValue: UnfilledInheritedWitness, captureType: MaxonType.integer)
		self.inheritedWitnessCaptures.push(InheritedWitnessCapture.create(constraintIndex, slot: slot))
		return slot
	end 'inheritedWitnessSlotFor'

	// ⭐ **CLOSE EACH INHERITED WITNESS'S ONE HOP, IN THE ENCLOSING CONTEXT (W58)** — called by
	// `parseClosureExpression` after the restore, which is the first moment the enclosing function's block and
	// SSA space are current again, and before `buildClosureEnv` reads the value column it fills.
	//
	// `witnessValueForConstraint` re-entering IS the chain: for a closure written directly in the constrained
	// method it answers with that method's hidden parameter and emits nothing; for a closure nested in another
	// closure it claims a slot in the ENCLOSING closure's environment and emits that closure's own load, which
	// this same routine then fills one level further out. The recursion terminates at the declaring function,
	// which is the only context with no environment above it.
	function fillInheritedWitnessCaptures(inherited InheritedWitnessCaptureArray, capturedValues ValueIdArray, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		for entry in inherited 'eachInheritedWitness'
			let enclosingWitness = try self.witnessValueForConstraint(entry.constraintIndex, anchor: anchor)
			try capturedValues.set(entry.slot, value: enclosingWitness) otherwise panic("fillInheritedWitnessCaptures: env slot {entry.slot} is past the {capturedValues.count()} capture(s) this closure collected — every slot here came from `captureSlotFor`, which fills all three columns in lockstep")
		end 'eachInheritedWitness'
	end 'fillInheritedWitnessCaptures'

	// Does the function whose body is being EMITTED right now carry the declaring type's hidden witness
	// parameters? Not the same question as "did the declaring function reserve any" the moment a lifted closure
	// is being parsed: the closure's ABI has no room for them (see `witnessValueForConstraint`), so it carries
	// none however constrained its author was. The one door for "may I forward my own witness slots".
	function emittingFunctionCarriesWitnessParams() returns bool
		return self.declaringWitnessParamCount != 0 and self.closureCaptureScopes.isEmpty()
	end 'emittingFunctionCarriesWitnessParams'

	// ⭐ **THE HIDDEN WITNESS PARAMETER THAT CARRIES CONSTRAINT `constraintIndex`'s TABLE (P1.7a slice 2a).**
	// The parser RESERVED these slots (`parseFunction`,
	// `[user][env][existentialWitness][layout][count][witness_0..k]`),
	// so resolving one is arithmetic on the reservation this same pass made, not a whole-program re-derivation.
	// It PANICS on a function that reserved none, which is what the lowering's cross-check used to catch: a
	// dispatch through a constraint can only be emitted inside an instance method of the constrained type, and
	// that is exactly the shape `witnessParamCountFor` reserves for.
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS PRECONDITION IS "NOT INSIDE A LIFTED CLOSURE", AND `witnessValueForConstraint` IS WHAT HOLDS IT**
	// (W58) — a closure body's dispatch has no parameter to name and inherits instead, so reaching this from
	// one would return a ValueId in the DECLARING function's numbering, read inside a body with its own dense
	// SSA space. That is why the routing decision sits in one door above and not in each caller.
	function witnessParamValueId(constraintIndex WitnessSlotIndex) returns ValueId
		if self.declaringWitnessParamCount == 0 'reservesNoWitnessParameter'
			panic("Parser.witnessParamValueId: a dispatch through `where` constraint {constraintIndex} inside a function that reserved no witness parameters — `witnessParamCountFor` reserves them for an INSTANCE method of a constrained type, so a dispatch reaching here was resolved against constraints the current function does not carry")
		end 'reservesNoWitnessParameter'

		return (self.declaringWitnessParamBase + constraintIndex) as ValueId
	end 'witnessParamValueId'

	// ⭐ **EVERY REQUIREMENT OF `iface` (TRANSITIVELY) NAMED `methodName`, AS A DISPATCH TARGET THROUGH
	// `witness`** — the per-interface half of the search above, shared by both receiver kinds so a candidate is
	// built in exactly one place whichever route reached it. `witnessSlotsOf` is the memoized transitive
	// requirement list, and a requirement's INDEX in it is the table slot every reader numbers by
	// (`interfaceWitnessSlots`).
	//
	// The already-collected test is applied on BOTH routes rather than only on the constraint one: for a
	// single interface it can never fire (`interfaceWitnessSlots`' `visited` set gives each interface one run
	// of slots, and `requireUnclaimedInterfaceRequirementName` forbids one interface two requirements of a
	// name), so applying it uniformly costs an empty scan and keeps the rule stated once. See
	// `witnessCandidateAlreadyCollected` for why a requirement reached twice is one requirement, not a rival.
	function appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates(candidates WitnessDispatchTargetArray, iface IrInterface, witness ValueId, methodName ByteArray)
		let slots = self.witnessSlotsOf(iface)
		// The handle that makes `methodIndex` meaningful downstream — read ONCE per interface here rather than
		// per candidate, and off the very interface whose slot list is being numbered, so the index and the
		// numbering cannot describe two different interfaces. `iface` came out of `interfaceMap()`, which is
		// the same store `recordInterfaceDeclaration` fills, so a miss is a compiler defect.
		let interfaceDeclIndex = try self.signatures.interfaceDeclIndexOf(iface.name) otherwise panic("appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates: interface '{String.from(iface.name)}' resolved out of `interfaceMap()` but has no declaration-order index — the two are filled in one act by `recordInterfaceDeclaration`")
		for slotIndex in 0 upto slots.count() 'eachSlot'
			let slot = try slots.get(slotIndex) otherwise panic("appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates: slots.get OOB at {slotIndex} below count {slots.count()}")
			if not slot.method.name.equals(methodName) 'otherMethod'
				continue
			end 'otherMethod'

			if witnessCandidateAlreadyCollected(candidates, declaringInterfaceName: slot.sourceInterfaceName, methodName: methodName) 'reachedByAnEarlierConstraint'
				continue
			end 'reachedByAnEarlierConstraint'

			candidates.push(WitnessDispatchTarget.create(witness, methodIndex: slotIndex, interfaceDeclIndex: interfaceDeclIndex, returnTypeName: slot.method.returnTypeName, paramTypeNames: slot.method.paramTypeNames, throwsClause: slot.method.throwsClause, methodName: methodName, paramNames: slot.method.paramNames, declaringInterfaceName: slot.sourceInterfaceName, isStatic: slot.method.isStatic))
		end 'eachSlot'
	end 'appendInterfaceDispatchCandidates'

	// `iface`'s transitive witness slot list, built on this file's FIRST dispatch through that interface and
	// held after — see `sharedEmptyWitnessSlots` for the measured quadratic this removes and for why the memo
	// is per `Parser` rather than on `ProgramSignatures`.
	//
	// The list is a pure function of the interface DECLARATION INDEX, which `ProgramSignatures` fixes before
	// any parse begins and no parse can move, so one answer serves the whole file — the same property that
	// makes the numbering safe to bake into another file's emitted offsets in the first place.
	// (`foldInterfaceDeclarations` is the only writer of that index and it runs to completion inside
	// `queryProgramSignatures`, before the first body is parsed; the sweep itself parses no call, so no
	// `Parser` can memoize a slot list off a half-filled index.)
	//
	// ⚠ **THE MAP IS ASKED THROUGH `interfaceMap()`, exactly as the parser's two other interface readers ask
	// it** (`requireInterfaceForParse`, `conformanceUsesArity`). A `ProgramSignatures.interfaceWitnessSlotsOf`
	// delegate stood here and was removed at the R10c review: it added nothing to
	// `interfaceWitnessSlots(map, iface)` — which already owns the walk's accumulators — and a second door
	// onto one store is what this rung spent its `interfaceOf` → `interfaceMap` widening removing.
	function witnessSlotsOf(iface IrInterface) returns CollectedInterfaceMethodArray
		if let existing = try self.witnessSlotsByInterface.get(iface.name) 'alreadyBuilt'
			return existing
		end 'alreadyBuilt'

		// COPY-ON-WRITE off the shared empty map before the first dispatch in this file.
		if self.witnessSlotsByInterface is sharedEmptyWitnessSlots 'firstDispatchInThisFile'
			self.witnessSlotsByInterface = WitnessSlotsByInterfaceMap.create()
		end 'firstDispatchInThisFile'

		let slots = interfaceWitnessSlots(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), iface: iface)
		self.witnessSlotsByInterface.upsert(iface.name, value: slots)
		return slots
	end 'witnessSlotsOf'

	// ⭐ **WHICH CANDIDATE A `.method(…)` CALL MEANS: THE ONE TAKING THIS MANY ARGUMENTS (R10c).** An interface
	// and its `extends` parents may each declare the name — `Base.label()` beside `Derived.label(width)` is
	// the ordinary spelling — and those are two DISTINCT requirements in two DISTINCT slots, so the argument
	// count is what tells them apart. Selecting by name alone bound the call to whichever came first and then
	// reported the OTHER one's arity against it: `'Derived.label' expects 1 argument(s) but 0 were provided`,
	// a false sentence about an interface that inherits a zero-argument `label()`.
	//
	// The count is compared against the interface's FORMALS, which is the same list `slotCallArgs` then slots
	// the labelled arguments into — so a call that survives this cannot fail arity there, and one that does
	// not survive it is still reported by `slotCallArgs` whenever there is a single candidate to report
	// against (see below). The label rules stay entirely that function's.
	function pickWitnessDispatchByArity(candidates WitnessDispatchTargetArray, argCount ParamCount, receiver ValueId, methodTok Token) returns WitnessDispatchTarget throws ParseError
		var matching = WitnessDispatchTargetArray.create()
		for i in 0 upto candidates.count() 'eachCandidate'
			let candidate = try candidates.get(i) otherwise panic("pickWitnessDispatchByArity: candidates.get OOB at {i} below count {candidates.count()}")
			if candidate.paramTypeNames.count() == argCount 'takesThisMany'
				matching.push(candidate)
			end 'takesThisMany'
		end 'eachCandidate'

		match pickSoleWitnessCandidate(matching) 'pick'
			one(target) then return target
			ambiguous(first, second) then throw ParseError.ambiguousWitnessDispatch(String.from(methodTok.value), provider: self.witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver), argCount: argCount, first: renderWitnessRequirement(first), second: renderWitnessRequirement(second), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
			none then break 'pick'
		end 'pick'

		// Nothing of that name takes this many arguments. With exactly ONE candidate there is nothing to select
		// between, and the arity is `slotCallArgs`' to report — the ONE home of the label→position mapping,
		// whose E3036 already names the interface and both counts. With several, no single one of them is what
		// the author meant, so the reject lists them all rather than blaming an arbitrary one.
		if candidates.count() == 1 'soleCandidate'
			return try candidates.get(0) otherwise panic("pickWitnessDispatchByArity: candidates.count() is 1 but get(0) failed")
		end 'soleCandidate'

		throw ParseError.noWitnessRequirementOfArity(String.from(methodTok.value), provider: self.witnessDispatchProviderPhrase(receiver), argCount: argCount, available: renderWitnessRequirements(candidates), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'pickWitnessDispatchByArity'

	// The throwing form for the witness-dispatch caller, which cannot proceed without the declaration. The
	// miss is a clean positioned reject naming the constraint's interface.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE MESSAGE MAY NOT SAY "NO FILE DECLARES IT", BECAUSE THAT IS SOMETIMES FALSE AND THE COMPILER
	// CANNOT TELL WHICH (R8 review).** The index holds every interface `foldInterfaceDeclarations` could
	// READ, and that pass is TOLERANT: an `interface` whose own body has a syntax error records nothing, so a
	// declaration that is RIGHT THERE in the source lands here indistinguishably from a typo. It is not
	// enough that the real parse reports that syntax error too — MEASURED, with the broken `interface`
	// written BELOW its constrained use in the same file, THIS reject is raised at the dispatch first and the
	// file's parse stops, so the sentence below is the ONLY diagnostic the program produces
	// (`where-clauses.error.constraint-interface-unreadable` pins exactly that).
	//
	// So it states what is actually known — the name resolved to nothing READABLE — and names BOTH causes,
	// rather than asserting the one it cannot distinguish. Recording "a site claimed this name but failed to
	// read" instead would buy a sharper sentence for a SECOND store of the interface name, which is the one
	// thing this rung deleted (see `InterfaceDeclSite`); a diagnostic is not worth re-minting the duplication.
	function requireInterfaceForParse(name ByteArray, methodTok Token) returns IrInterface throws ParseError
		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: name) 'lookup'
			found(iface) then return iface
			notFound then throw ParseError.unsupported("the `where` constraint interface '{String.from(name)}' does not resolve — no file in this program declares a readable `interface` of that name, so it is either misspelled or an `interface` whose own declaration fails to parse", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'lookup'
	end 'requireInterfaceForParse'

	// Mint the SSA value a witness dispatch defines, typed from the interface method's return type (P1.7a slice
	// 2a) — the witness-dispatch twin of `mintDeclaredCallResult`. A `void` method mints a discarded `void`
	// result in statement position and reports E2004 in value position (like a void direct call); every other
	// return type is minted below and enrolled through `enrolOwnedCallTemp`, so a heap-returning interface
	// method's result is adopted owned exactly as a direct call's is. There is no retype to wait for here —
	// an interface's declared return type is already concrete, and the one that is not (`Self`) is answered
	// by the receiver rather than substituted afterwards.
	function mintWitnessDispatchResult(target WitnessDispatchTarget, receiver ValueId, methodTok Token, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if target.returnTypeName.equals(InterfaceVoidTypeName) 'voidReturn'
			if resultUsed 'noValueToUse'
				throw ParseError.voidCallResultUsed(String.from(methodTok.value), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
			end 'noValueToUse'
			return self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.void, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		end 'voidReturn'

		let result = try self.witnessDispatchValueResult(target, receiver: receiver, methodTok: methodTok)
		self.enrolOwnedCallTemp(result)
		return result
	end 'mintWitnessDispatchResult'

	// The three spellings a NON-void interface return has, split from the door above so the drop enrolment is
	// written once across all three rather than once per arm — the same one-rule-one-site reason
	// `enrolOwnedCallTemp` exists at all.
	function witnessDispatchValueResult(target WitnessDispatchTarget, receiver ValueId, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// A `Self` return is the receiver's own type (the shared body cannot know the concrete one).
		if target.returnTypeName.equals(InterfaceSelfTypeName) 'selfReturn'
			return self.mintSelfReturnedWitnessResult(receiver)
		end 'selfReturn'

		if let held = try self.heldAssociatedReturnOf(target) 'heldAtAnInterface'
			return try self.mintHeldAssociatedWitnessResult(target, held: held, receiver: receiver, methodTok: methodTok)
		end 'heldAtAnInterface'

		// ⭐⭐ **AN ASSOCIATED-TYPE RETURN THE RECEIVER'S OWN INSTANTIATION BINDS (W57)** — asked BELOW the held
		// arm, which must keep its nested witness, and ABOVE the fallback, which reads the associated type's
		// NAME as though it were a concrete type's.
		if let bound = try self.associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance(target, receiver: receiver) 'theReceiversInstanceBindsIt'
			return self.mintOwnedCallResult(bound)
		end 'theReceiversInstanceBindsIt'

		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE SAME ANSWER FOR A CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER, WHOSE `where` CLAUSE IS WHERE ITS `with`
		// ARGUMENTS ARE WRITTEN (G19).** Asked beside the instance arm rather than inside it because the two
		// receivers record the claim in two different stores — a declared existential on the VALUE, a type
		// parameter on the DECLARATION — and above the fallback for the identical reason: the fallback reads
		// the associated type's NAME as though it were a concrete type's.
		if let bound = try self.associatedReturnThroughConstraintBinding(target, receiver: receiver) 'theConstraintBindsIt'
			return self.mintOwnedCallResult(bound)
		end 'theConstraintBindsIt'

		return self.mintOwnedCallResult(self.interfaceReturnMaxonType(target.returnTypeName))
	end 'witnessDispatchValueResult'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE RECEIVER'S `with` ARGUMENTS SAY THIS ASSOCIATED-TYPE RETURN IS (W57).** `current()` on a
	// `Cursor with Element` hands back an `Element`, and the fallback below this arm hands back
	// `named("Element")` — the interface's own PLACEHOLDER read as if it were a type the program declares.
	// Where the receiver still knows its instantiation, this is the answer instead: the argument that
	// instantiation bound at the requirement's own associated POSITION.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE POSITION IS DERIVED FROM THE INTERFACE'S `uses` LIST, NOT ASSUMED TO BE ZERO** — and v1's one
	// attempt at this rule (`TypeResolution.maxon:5082-5118`, `elementTypeForIterable`, dead code the parser
	// never reaches) took `args[0]` flat, with its own comment conceding *"Array's `Iter` arg is at index 1"*.
	// For `Iterable uses Element, Iter` that is right by luck and for a requirement returning `Iter` it is the
	// wrong argument silently. `namePositionIn` is the same numbering the held arm above uses.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS A PER-VALUE ANSWER, NEVER A WHOLE-PROGRAM ONE, WHICH THE HOLDINGS RECORD ONE ARM UP IS.**
	// A holding is unique because E3125 refuses a second site claiming another; an ARGUMENT is not — a shared
	// generic body's `Iterator with Element` and a user's `Iterator with Integer` are both legal, in one
	// program, and keying either by `(interface, position)` would give one of the two loops the other's element
	// type. That is why this reads the receiver and `associatedTypeHeldAt` reads the program.
	//
	// The two guards are `heldAssociatedReturnOf`'s, for its reasons: a requirement projected from a PARENT is
	// numbered against a list that is not this interface's, and a site binding fewer arguments than the
	// interface declares is E3016's to report and claims nothing here.
	function associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance(target WitnessDispatchTarget, receiver ValueId) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let instance = try self.valueExistentialInstance.get(receiver)
		let dispatched = self.signatures.interfaceNameAtDeclIndex(target.interfaceDeclIndex)
		if not target.declaringInterfaceName.equals(dispatched) 'aProjectedParentRequirement'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'aProjectedParentRequirement'

		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: dispatched) 'base'
			notFound then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			found(iface) then return try self.instanceBoundAssociatedType(instance, iface: iface, returnTypeName: target.returnTypeName)
		end 'base'
	end 'associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance'

	// The two lookups the answer above is, over a resolved interface — split out for `heldAssociatedReturnIn`'s
	// reason: the `found` arm of a `lookupInterface` match is a call in every reader of this file, never a body.
	//
	// ⚠ The argument crosses out of the INDEX's interner through `resolvedSlotType`, exactly as
	// `slotTypeThroughReceiver` crosses a substituted field type — an `adoptType` alone would leave a
	// sweep-recorded `named` leaf that reads as an integer (see that door, and T1).
	function instanceBoundAssociatedType(instance GenericInstanceId, iface IrInterface, returnTypeName ByteArray) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let position = try namePositionIn(iface.associatedTypeNames, name: returnTypeName)
		if position >= self.signatures.genericInstanceArgCount(instance) 'boundNothingHere'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'boundNothingHere'

		return self.resolvedSlotType(self.signatures.substitutedInstanceArg(instance, position: position))
	end 'instanceBoundAssociatedType'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE RECEIVER'S OWN `where` CONSTRAINT SAYS THIS ASSOCIATED-TYPE RETURN IS (G19)** — the
	// constrained-type-parameter twin of `associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance`, and the reason
	// `where Source is Iterator with Element` exists at all. `source.current()` inside a shared generic body
	// dispatches through a receiver whose TYPE is a parameter; without the constraint's binding the fallback
	// hands back `named("Element")` — the interface's own PLACEHOLDER read as if it were a type the program
	// declares — and a tuple built from it is a different tuple type from the declared one (E3005, the first
	// of G19's three layers).
	//
	// The two guards are `associatedReturnThroughReceiverInstance`'s, for its reasons: a requirement projected
	// from a PARENT is numbered against a list that is not this interface's, and a constraint binding fewer
	// arguments than the interface declares claims nothing at the positions past them.
	function associatedReturnThroughConstraintBinding(target WitnessDispatchTarget, receiver ValueId) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		if self.tagOf(receiver) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notAConstrainedParameter'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'notAConstrainedParameter'

		let dispatched = self.signatures.interfaceNameAtDeclIndex(target.interfaceDeclIndex)
		if not target.declaringInterfaceName.equals(dispatched) 'aProjectedParentRequirement'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'aProjectedParentRequirement'

		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: dispatched) 'base'
			notFound then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			found(iface) then return try self.constraintBoundAssociatedType(receiver, iface: iface, returnTypeName: target.returnTypeName)
		end 'base'
	end 'associatedReturnThroughConstraintBinding'

	// The two lookups the answer above is — `instanceBoundAssociatedType`'s shape over the constraint list
	// rather than over an instance's arguments, split out for `heldAssociatedReturnIn`'s reason.
	function constraintBoundAssociatedType(receiver ValueId, iface IrInterface, returnTypeName ByteArray) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let position = try namePositionIn(iface.associatedTypeNames, name: returnTypeName)
		let bindings = constrainedParamAssociatedBindings(self.signatures, token: self.valueNameId(receiver), interfaceName: iface.name)
		if position >= bindings.count() 'boundNothingHere'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'boundNothingHere'

		return self.constraintBindingResultType(try bindings.get(position) otherwise panic("constraintBoundAssociatedType: bindings.get OOB at {position} below count {bindings.count()}"))
	end 'constraintBoundAssociatedType'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A CONSTRAINT'S BINDING DENOTES IN THE DECLARATION THAT WROTE IT.** A binding is a rendered type
	// name, and inside a shared generic body the interesting ones name the enclosing declaration's OWN
	// parameters — `where Source is Iterator with Element` inside `type WithIterIterator uses Source, Element`
	// binds a `typeParameter`, which is what makes the declared `returns (Source, Element)` and the built
	// tuple ONE type. Everything else is a concrete name and takes `interfaceReturnMaxonType`, the SAME typer
	// the fallback one function up uses, so the two answers differ only in what they are answering ABOUT.
	function constraintBindingResultType(binding ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		if self.typeParamPositionOf(binding) < self.enclosingTypeParams.count() 'namesAnEnclosingParameter'
			return MaxonType.typeParameter(typeParamTokenFor(self.enclosingType, paramName: binding))
		end 'namesAnEnclosingParameter'

		return self.interfaceReturnMaxonType(binding)
	end 'constraintBindingResultType'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS DISPATCH'S RESULT AN ASSOCIATED TYPE THE PROGRAM HOLDS AT AN INTERFACE?** — the position
	// within the DISPATCHED interface's own `uses` names, plus the interface it is held at, or
	// `MapError.keyNotFound` for every ordinary return (which is every return in a program that writes no
	// interface as a `with` argument, so the common path is one map probe on a name).
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE REQUIREMENT'S DECLARING INTERFACE MUST BE THE DISPATCHED ONE, AND CHECKING THAT IS NOT
	// PEDANTRY.** `methodIndex` is numbered against the DISPATCHED interface's transitive slot list, so a
	// requirement declared on a PARENT is dispatched through the CHILD's table — while the associated type it
	// names is the parent's, at a position in the PARENT's `uses` list. Associated types do not project
	// through `extends` (`checkOneInterfaceConformance` records `Sub` with an empty `associatedTypeNames`),
	// so the two lists are unrelated and a same-spelled name at the same index in both would load the child's
	// slot for the parent's position — a witness for the wrong conformance, silently. Refused rather than
	// guessed: the answer is `notHeld`, which leaves the return typed exactly as it was before this rung.
	function heldAssociatedReturnOf(target WitnessDispatchTarget) returns HeldAssociatedReturn throws MapError
		let dispatched = self.signatures.interfaceNameAtDeclIndex(target.interfaceDeclIndex)
		if not target.declaringInterfaceName.equals(dispatched) 'aProjectedParentRequirement'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'aProjectedParentRequirement'

		match lookupInterface(self.signatures.interfaceMap(), name: dispatched) 'base'
			notFound then throw MapError.keyNotFound
			found(iface) then return try self.heldAssociatedReturnIn(iface, returnTypeName: target.returnTypeName)
		end 'base'
	end 'heldAssociatedReturnOf'

	// The two lookups the answer above is, over a resolved interface — split out because the `found` arm of a
	// `lookupInterface` match is a call in every reader of this file, never a body.
	function heldAssociatedReturnIn(iface IrInterface, returnTypeName ByteArray) returns HeldAssociatedReturn throws MapError
		let position = try namePositionIn(iface.associatedTypeNames, name: returnTypeName)
		return HeldAssociatedReturn.create(try self.signatures.associatedTypeHeldAt(iface.name, position: position), slotOffset: witnessAssociatedSlotOffset(self.witnessSlotsOf(iface).count(), position: position), position: position)
	end 'heldAssociatedReturnIn'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE HALF COMES FROM THE CALL AND THE WITNESS HALF FROM THE TABLE THE CALL WAS DISPATCHED
	// THROUGH.** The impl returns its own concrete cursor in the one result register a witness call has; the
	// table to widen that cursor into an existential is a fact about the RECEIVER'S conformer, and the
	// receiver's own witness table is where its mint put it (`LowerMaxonToStd.stampAssociatedWitnessSlots`).
	// So the second word is a load plus an add, and nothing about the call's ABI changes.
	//
	// ⚠ The slot holds a distance from the table's own base, not an address — see
	// `GlobalDataTable.PendingRdataSelfRelReloc` for why `.rdata` cannot point at itself, and why the add is a
	// WRAPPING machine-word add (the distance is signed).
	//
	// ⚠ **`target.witnessValue` AND NOT `witnessOfValue(receiver)`**: the receiver of a witness dispatch is
	// either an existential or a value of a CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER, and only the first has a paired
	// witness half. The table address is what this needs and `witnessValue` is it for both, which is the whole
	// reason `WitnessDispatchTarget` names a value rather than a constraint.
	function mintHeldAssociatedWitnessResult(target WitnessDispatchTarget, held HeldAssociatedReturn, receiver ValueId, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(methodTok)
		let distance = self.emitIndirectLoad(MaxonType.integer, box: target.witnessValue, offset: held.slotOffset, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		let nested = self.emitMachineWordAdd(target.witnessValue, rhs: distance, range: range)
		let result = self.mintOwnedCallResult(MaxonType.interfaceRef(self.artifact.typeNames.intern(held.heldAt)))
		self.pairInterfaceWitness(result, witness: nested)
		// ⭐⭐ **THE HELD VALUE INHERITS THE `with` ARGUMENTS THE RECEIVER BOUND AT THIS POSITION (W57)** — the
		// second half of the chain `for item in <an Iterable>` walks, and without it the first half buys
		// nothing. `createIterator()` hands back a `Cursor` existential whose own `Element` is bound by the
		// SOURCE's instantiation (`Bag with (Element, Cursor with Element)`), not by anything the cursor's type
		// can say; carried here, `current()` on it resolves through the arm one function up. A receiver with no
		// instantiation to inherit from — a bare `Bag` parameter — passes nothing on, which is correct: it
		// bound nothing.
		if let receiverInstance = try self.valueExistentialInstance.get(receiver) 'theReceiverKnowsItsArguments'
			if let heldInstance = try self.signatures.instanceArgHeldInstance(receiverInstance, position: held.position) 'thatArgumentIsItselfInstantiated'
				self.recordValueExistentialInstance(result, instance: heldInstance)
			end 'thatArgumentIsItselfInstantiated'
		end 'theReceiverKnowsItsArguments'
		return result
	end 'mintHeldAssociatedWitnessResult'

	// ⭐ **A `Self`-RETURNING REQUIREMENT HANDS BACK THE RECEIVER'S OWN TYPE, AND EACH RECEIVER SPELLS THAT
	// DIFFERENTLY (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).**
	//
	//   • a CONSTRAINED TYPE PARAMETER: the receiver's `T`, opaque exactly as the receiver is.
	//   • an EXISTENTIAL: the SAME interface — and, crucially, the same WITNESS. `Self` means the conformer's
	//     own type, so the value coming back has the receiver's dynamic type and therefore the receiver's
	//     table. That is why this needs no second return register and no interface-returning ABI: the witness
	//     half is already in hand, so the result is paired with it and the callee returns one word, exactly as
	//     a concrete `Self`-returning impl does.
	function mintSelfReturnedWitnessResult(receiver ValueId) returns ValueId
		if self.tagOf(receiver) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'constrainedTypeParameter'
			return self.mintOwnedCallResult(MaxonType.typeParameter(self.valueNameId(receiver)))
		end 'constrainedTypeParameter'

		let result = self.mintOwnedCallResult(MaxonType.interfaceRef(self.valueNameId(receiver)))
		self.pairInterfaceWitness(result, witness: self.witnessOfValue(receiver))
		return result
	end 'mintSelfReturnedWitnessResult'

	// A non-void, non-`Self` interface return TYPE NAME (P1.7a slice 2a) as a `MaxonType`: the primitive
	// keywords map to their tags, `String` to the builtin, and every other name — a user ranged alias
	// (`Code`), a struct — to a `named` type `mintOwnedCallResult` resolves through the same alias/struct doors
	// a direct call's return does.
	//
	// ⭐ **`ExitCode` IS CLAIMED WITH THE KEYWORDS AND NOT WITH THE USER NAMES, AND THE REASON IS A WIDTH
	// (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-B).** An interface stores its return type as a rendered source STRING, so this
	// function RE-DERIVES what a declaration derived once, and the `named` fallback is only safe while the two
	// derivations agree — which they do for every name but that one. MEASURED at this door: an
	// `ExitCode`-returning interface method returned 42 on x64 and trapped under wasmtime.
	//
	// It cannot be routed through `denotedNamedType` wholesale: this function answers for the VALUE column,
	// where a ranged alias deliberately keeps its `named` identity (see `castConvertsPerInstance`), while
	// that cascade erases it for the SIGNATURE column.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **SO THE NAME→TAG TABLE IS `builtinTypeNameTag`, AND THIS DOOR IS NO LONGER THE ONLY ASKER (W1).**
	// The table used to be written out here, and here alone — which is precisely how the FUNCTION-TYPEALIAS
	// registry, a second interner-free store of the same kind, came to drop `ExitCode`'s width and trap on
	// wasm. One table, three doors; see `builtinTypeNameTag` for the measurement and for why `Character` is
	// not in it. `named` is its answer for every name it does not own, which is exactly what
	// `maxonTypeOfTag`'s `named` arm rebuilds from this file's interned id — and the intern is paid only on
	// that answer, never for a builtin whose type carries no name at all.
	function interfaceReturnMaxonType(returnTypeName ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		let tag = builtinTypeNameTag(returnTypeName)
		return maxonTypeOfTag(tag, nameId: UnnamedTypeId if tag != ValueTypeTag.named else self.artifact.typeNames.intern(returnTypeName))
	end 'interfaceReturnMaxonType'

	// The token shape of a method call on an ARRAY FIELD reached through a field chain — `<base>(.<field>)+
	// .<method>(` with AT LEAST TWO dots (a one-dot `b.m(…)` is `methodCallsAt`'s). `resolveFieldChainFrom`
	// stops the chain at the array field, so the last hop is the method. The base must be a live local (or a
	// captured enclosing struct), the same gate `methodCallsAt` uses.
	function fieldChainMethodCallAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		let chainEnd = self.fieldChainEndAfter(index, minHops: ChainMethodMinHops)
		if chainEnd == index or self.kindAt(chainEnd) != TokenKind.leftParen 'notChainMethod'
			return false
		end 'notChainMethod'
		// ASK THE SHARED DOOR. This used to re-derive `baseNamesAValueInScope`'s two-line test by hand and so
		// lost its `Self` guard (D9): `fieldChainEndAfter` bases the chain test on `tokenCanBeAName`, which
		// ADMITS `TokenKind.selfType`, and `baseBindingName` special-cases only `TokenKind.self` — so with a
		// parameter legally bound under the literal name `Self` (D8), a `Self.field.method(…)` chain read as
		// naming a VALUE base here while the other three doors read it as the TYPE.
		return self.chainBaseNamesAValue(index)
	end 'fieldChainMethodCallAt'

	// The token index just PAST a field-access chain starting at `index` — a `base` (an identifier or
	// `self`) followed by `.field` hops — or `index` itself when the tokens there are not such a chain
	// with at least `minHops` hops.
	//
	// THE ONE WALK over that shape, and it is shared because a divergence between its two askers is a
	// USE-AFTER-FREE rather than a compile error: `fieldChainMethodCallAt` decides that a WRITE goes
	// through a chain, and `sourceNamesABase` decides that a `for … in` LOCKS that chain's base against
	// exactly that write (`bindingIsIterationLocked`). Teach one a new chain shape and not the other and
	// the lock silently stops covering the door it was built to cover.
	function fieldChainEndAfter(index TokenIndex, minHops FieldHopCount) returns TokenIndex
		// The base is anything `tokenCanBeAName` admits — which subsumes the identifier and the `self` keyword
		// this used to name one at a time, and adds a KEYWORD-named binding (D8). One predicate for the base
		// exactly as `namesMemberAt` is one for the hop, and for the header's reason.
		if not tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(index)) 'notABase'
			return index
		end 'notABase'

		var q = index + 1
		var hops = 0
		while self.kindAt(q) == TokenKind.dot and self.namesMemberAt(q + 1) 'hop'
			hops = hops + 1
			q = q + 2
		end 'hop'
		return q if hops >= minHops else index
	end 'fieldChainEndAfter'

	// Resolve a field chain to the field the trailing call's RECEIVER is, and apply that call — an ARRAY
	// field's method (`b.ops.push(1)`), a nested STRUCT field's method (`o.inner.get()`), a FUNCTION-typed
	// field's indirect call (`o.inner.op()`, #97), an opaque `T` field's witness dispatch
	// (`self.item.digest()`) or a builtin conformer's (`o.count.hash()`). Shared by the STATEMENT form
	// (`resultUsed: false`) and the `try`-TARGET form (`try self.nodes.get(i) otherwise …`, P1.7 slice 3),
	// which leaves the throwing `call __managed_get` as the last op for `rewriteLastCallToTryCall`.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ADDS EXACTLY ONE THING TO THE POSTFIX LOOP: THE CHAIN'S BASE NAME.** `resolveFieldChainFrom`
	// stops before the `.member(`, so the receiver is an ordinary value and every dispatch decision is
	// `dispatchMethodOnReceiver`'s — this door exists only because the chain's base IS a name, and
	// `for it in b.items` locks exactly it against `b.items.clear()`. A postfix receiver in expression
	// position has no such name and passes the empty one (`valueReceiver`). Further hops past the first
	// (`o.inner.get().bump()`) are the postfix loop's, unnamed like every other temporary.
	function parseFieldChainCall(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The chain's BASE token, captured before the chain is resolved — it is the only thing that can
		// name the storage a write through this receiver reaches, and `for it in b.items` locks exactly it
		// (`iterationLockNameAt`). ONE derivation, read ONCE: handing `iterationLockNameOf` the answer keeps
		// a chain from resolving its base through `Scope` twice per method call.
		let baseIndex = self.pos
		let receiver = try self.parseFieldAccess()
		let subjectName = self.borrowSubjectNameAt(baseIndex)
		return try self.parsePostfix(func, value: try self.dispatchMethodOnReceiver(func, recv: self.valueReceiver(receiver, immutableName: self.iterationLockNameOf(subjectName), subjectName: subjectName), resultUsed: resultUsed), resultUsed: resultUsed)
	end 'parseFieldChainCall'

	// ⭐ **WHICH ROSTER ANSWERS FOR THIS SURFACE** — the ONE place a member list is picked, so every
	// dispatcher's gate, the refusal it throws and the drift panic at its fall-through all read the same
	// answer. `dispatchArrayMethod` decides `buffer` vs `array` once (one record, two surfaces —
	// `SignatureIndex.ManagedMemoryTypeName`); three more are the receiver's type outright; and `stringStatic`
	// is not a receiver's at all — it is the TYPE token's, which is why this is keyed on the SURFACE and not
	// on the value being dispatched.
	//
	// A roster is built on this file's FIRST call into its surface and held after — see
	// `sharedEmptySurfaceRoster` for why the memo is per Parser and why it is lazy. The eight arms differ
	// only in which field they fill, which is the whole of what Maxon cannot abstract here: there is no way
	// to hand a function the field to lazily populate, so the alternative to eight arms in one home is
	// eight homes.
	function surfaceMemberNames(surface MemberSurface) returns ByteArrayArray
		if surface == MemberSurface.buffer 'bufferSurface'
			if self.bufferSurfaceRoster is sharedEmptySurfaceRoster 'firstBufferCallInThisFile'
				self.bufferSurfaceRoster = bufferSurfaceMemberNames()
			end 'firstBufferCallInThisFile'

			return self.bufferSurfaceRoster
		end 'bufferSurface'

		if surface == MemberSurface.array 'arraySurface'
			if self.arraySurfaceRoster is sharedEmptySurfaceRoster 'firstArrayCallInThisFile'
				self.arraySurfaceRoster = arraySurfaceMemberNames()
			end 'firstArrayCallInThisFile'

			return self.arraySurfaceRoster
		end 'arraySurface'

		if surface == MemberSurface.string 'stringSurface'
			if self.stringSurfaceRoster is sharedEmptySurfaceRoster 'firstStringCallInThisFile'
				self.stringSurfaceRoster = stringSurfaceMemberNames()
			end 'firstStringCallInThisFile'

			return self.stringSurfaceRoster
		end 'stringSurface'

		if surface == MemberSurface.character 'characterSurface'
			if self.characterSurfaceRoster is sharedEmptySurfaceRoster 'firstCharacterCallInThisFile'
				self.characterSurfaceRoster = characterSurfaceMemberNames()
			end 'firstCharacterCallInThisFile'

			return self.characterSurfaceRoster
		end 'characterSurface'

		if surface == MemberSurface.stringIndex 'stringIndexSurface'
			if self.stringIndexSurfaceRoster is sharedEmptySurfaceRoster 'firstStringIndexCallInThisFile'
				self.stringIndexSurfaceRoster = stringIndexSurfaceMemberNames()
			end 'firstStringIndexCallInThisFile'

			return self.stringIndexSurfaceRoster
		end 'stringIndexSurface'

		if surface == MemberSurface.cursor 'cursorSurface'
			if self.cursorSurfaceRoster is sharedEmptySurfaceRoster 'firstCursorCallInThisFile'
				self.cursorSurfaceRoster = cursorSurfaceMemberNames()
			end 'firstCursorCallInThisFile'

			return self.cursorSurfaceRoster
		end 'cursorSurface'

		panic("surfaceMemberNames: no roster for a `MemberSurface` this function does not cover — a surface added to the enum without one, which every gate reading it would then answer for by falling through")
	end 'surfaceMemberNames'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ROSTER GATE ITSELF — ONE HOME FOR EVERY DISPATCH (found in review, A2q).** "Does this
	// surface have this member?" is asked ahead of every arm of every `dispatch*Method` that carries a roster
	// — one per `MemberSurface`, and the enum is the list rather than a sentence here naming some of them
	// (this one named five and was two short by `W86`) — because it is the question every other refusal about
	// the call presupposes. See `dispatchArrayMethod` for the two measured spellings that made the ORDER
	// matter as much as the test.
	//
	// ⚠ It was written out four times: fetch, membership test, `throw ParseError.unsupported(…)` with the
	// surface's sentence inline. Four copies of one rule, differing only in the enum value — and one of them
	// (`dispatchArrayMethod`'s) was already routing through the shared sentence while the other three were
	// not, which is exactly the asymmetry a fifth surface would copy from whichever neighbour it happened to
	// read. The roster comes back so the caller's drift panic names the same list this gate admitted from.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROSTER IS THE SURFACE'S AND THE NOUN IS THE VALUE'S, WHICH IS WHY ONLY THE NOUN IS A PARAMETER
	// (ARRO).** Picking the list and testing the name are keyed on the surface alone — that is what once let
	// the receiverless `String.from` STATIC door share this gate (A3g), and it is untouched. What the surface
	// cannot supply is the TYPE the refusal is shown about: the array surface serves every
	// `BuiltinArrayLiteral` conformer the program declares, so the constant it used to name was a type the
	// receiver need not be (see `surfaceRosterProvider`). A NAME is not a receiver — nothing here resolves,
	// and no caller has to hold a value it did not already have.
	function requireSurfaceMember(surface MemberSurface, typeName ByteArray, methodTok Token) returns ByteArrayArray throws ParseError
		let roster = self.surfaceMemberNames(surface)
		if not namesInclude(roster, name: methodTok.value) 'notASurfaceMember'
			throw ParseError.unsupported(rosterRefusalSentence(typeName, member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, noun: SurfaceMemberNoun, provider: surfaceRosterProvider(surface)), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'notASurfaceMember'

		return roster
	end 'requireSurfaceMember'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT AN ARRAY RECEIVER'S DECLARATION IS CALLED IN SOURCE (ARRO)** — the noun the roster refusal is
	// shown about. It is the instance's base name in every case but one: under a contest the corpus's
	// declaration is keyed under this compile's MINT, and a refusal quoting `__Array` would send the reader to
	// a spelling `requireUnreservedName` forbids them from writing — the same rule
	// `twoArrayDeclarationsRefusal` states one level down, applied to the one door that can meet a value of
	// either declaration.
	//
	// ⚠ The two names are EQUAL for every uncontested compile, which is the whole corpus, so the test is one
	// comparison and the answer is the base name.
	function arrayReceiverDeclaredName(giid GenericInstanceId) returns ByteArray
		let base = self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(giid)
		return self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName() if base.equals(self.signatures.arrayRecordBaseName()) else base
	end 'arrayReceiverDeclaredName'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `Array` ROSTER REFUSAL IS A WRONG ANSWER IN EXACTLY ONE PROGRAM, AND THIS IS IT (ARR2).** When a
	// user program declares its own `type Array` the compile holds TWO `Array` declarations — the user's under
	// the bare name, `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s under this compile's mint — and a member call resolves against the
	// RECEIVER's own (`memberBelongsToTheCorpus`). So `s.toByteArray().at(0)`, in a program whose own `type
	// Array` declares `at`, met *"`Array` member 'at' — P1.7 provides managed/get/…; that list IS the surface,
	// so nothing else is served here"*: every clause of it TRUE about the synthesized surface, and every clause
	// of it unhelpful to a reader whose declaration two lines up plainly has `at`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A SECOND GATE AND NOT A CLAUSE ON THE FIRST, because the two ask different questions of
	// different subjects.** `requireSurfaceMember` is keyed on a SURFACE and holds no receiver — deliberately,
	// and that is what lets every surface share it. This one is about which DECLARATION a value has, which only
	// an `Array` receiver can be asked. Threading a receiver through the shared gate to serve one surface is how
	// the shared gate stops being shared.
	//
	// It is DEAD for every program in the corpus: `hasStdlibShadowRenames` is false unless something is
	// contested, which is the one gate every cost of that mechanism sits behind.
	function refuseArrayMemberTheOtherDeclarationCarries(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token, isManagedMemory bool) throws ParseError
		// A BUFFER receiver is refused in the buffer's own words and has no second declaration to be confused
		// with — `__ManagedMemory` is the compiler's own name and no file declares a member on it.
		if isManagedMemory or not self.signatures.hasStdlibShadowRenames() 'oneArrayDeclarationInThisCompile'
			return
		end 'oneArrayDeclarationInThisCompile'

		let roster = self.surfaceMemberNames(MemberSurface.array)
		if namesInclude(roster, name: methodTok.value) 'theSynthesizedSurfaceServesIt'
			return
		end 'theSynthesizedSurfaceServesIt'

		// The receiver's own declaration has already been asked and declined (`memberBelongsToTheCorpus` runs
		// ahead of this dispatcher), so any declaration that carries the name here is by construction the other.
		let receiversOwn = self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(giid)

		// ⛔⛔ **THIS SENTENCE IS ABOUT TWO `Array`s, SO A RECEIVER THAT IS NEITHER MAY NOT BE HANDED IT** —
		// found in review, and it is the cure one door up applied everywhere EXCEPT its own twin. The gate
		// above asks only whether a CONTEST exists; since ARR5b the array surface serves every
		// `BuiltinArrayLiteral` conformer, so in a program holding `type Array` AND a `Bag`, a **`Bag`**
		// receiver reached here and was told *"this value's type is the `Array` the standard library
		// declares"*. `receiverIsTheProgramsOwn` is then `false` for the WRONG REASON, so it takes the library
		// arm and the whole sentence frame is false — not merely its noun, which is why passing
		// `arrayReceiverDeclaredName` here would fix the first word and leave the claim.
		//
		// A conformer of another name falls through to the roster refusal, which `surfaceRosterProvider`
		// already makes say `Bag`. That refusal is the right one: it states what the SURFACE serves, and the
		// surface is exactly what such a receiver has.
		if not receiversOwn.equals(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName()) and not receiversOwn.equals(self.signatures.arrayRecordBaseName()) 'receiverIsNeitherArrayDeclaration'
			return
		end 'receiverIsNeitherArrayDeclaration'

		for declaration in self.arrayDeclarationNames() 'eachArrayDeclaration'
			if not declaration.equals(receiversOwn) and self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(declaration, methodName: methodTok.value)) 'theOtherDeclarationCarriesIt'
				throw ParseError.unsupported(twoArrayDeclarationsRefusal(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName(), member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, receiverIsTheProgramsOwn: receiversOwn.equals(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName())), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
			end 'theOtherDeclarationCarriesIt'
		end 'eachArrayDeclaration'
	end 'refuseArrayMemberTheOtherDeclarationCarries'

	// ⭐⭐ **A SIZED CONTAINER'S BUFFER MAY NOT BE HANDED A MEMBER THAT MOVES ITS LENGTH (W192).** The sibling
	// above's twin, placed at the same point in `dispatchArrayMethod` and for the same stated reason: it is
	// still the question *"does this member exist ON THIS RECEIVER?"*, answered more precisely for the one
	// receiver that can tell two answers apart. Here the two answers are the two places a container's COUNT
	// comes from — an `Array`'s record, a `Vector`'s type — and `sizedContainersBufferRefusal` carries the
	// argument in full.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS CALLED FROM TWO SITES BECAUSE THE `append` ROUTE REACHES NO VALUE, AND ONE OF THEM PASSES
	// THE ANSWER OFF THE ENCLOSING DECLARATION INSTEAD OF OFF A MARK.** `fusedManagedMemberTakesTheRecord`
	// lists `append`, so a bare `managed.append(x)` inside an `extension Vector` never reaches this
	// dispatcher's value at all — `dispatchMethodOnBinding`'s `inlineManagedServesTheRecord` hands over the
	// RECEIVER's own record, minting nothing, so there is no value to mark; and the `giid` it hands over is
	// the buffer's, which W189's load-bearing retype spells `Array with Element` for a vector too, so no
	// `giid` test can tell a vector's record from a growable array's either. That site therefore asks
	// `declarationIsASizedContainerRecordNamed` of `enclosingType` — the BY-NAME form, which exists for
	// exactly that shape of caller — and calls THIS function, so the two routes are one refusal and one
	// sentence rather than two spellings of it. See `sizedContainerBufferValues` for the carrier the other
	// route reads.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THAT PARAMETER HAS BEEN REMOVED ONCE AND PUT BACK, AND THE ROUND TRIP IS THE ARGUMENT FOR IT.**
	// W194's first cure routed `Array` and `Vector` off the fused door, which left this function ONE caller —
	// at which point the parameter was a door asking its only caller for a derivation it could perform
	// itself, and the review rightly collapsed it to read the mark. The second cure hands the fused door the
	// receiver's real INSTANCE instead of turning it away (measured: routing costs 1.65–1.9x on
	// `Array.hash`), so the second caller is back and so is the reason the answer crosses the boundary rather
	// than the evidence. ⛔ **AND THE OBVIOUS COLLAPSE — READ THE MARK *OR* THE ENCLOSING DECLARATION — IS A
	// MEASURED WRONG ANSWER**, not merely a wider one: W192's own review found that inside an
	// `extension Vector` a `try managed.slice(0, 2)` bound to a local has the vector's declared buffer type
	// and is NOT the vector's record, so an `enclosingType` disjunct at the VALUE site would refuse
	// `s.clear()` on a slice. One value, one operation, two answers — the exact defect the per-value mark
	// exists to prevent.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks the length-writer list only AFTER the receiver test, so the roster it validates against is
	// built once per member call inside a sized container's body and never in any other program.
	function refuseLengthChangeOnASizedContainersBuffer(methodTok Token, onASizedContainersRecord bool) throws ParseError
		if not onASizedContainersRecord 'anOrdinaryBuffer'
			return
		end 'anOrdinaryBuffer'

		let lengthWriters = bufferLengthWritingMemberNames(self.surfaceMemberNames(MemberSurface.buffer))
		if not namesInclude(lengthWriters, name: methodTok.value) 'itLeavesTheRecordsLengthAlone'
			return
		end 'itLeavesTheRecordsLengthAlone'

		throw ParseError.unsupported(sizedContainersBufferRefusal(methodTok.value, lengthWriters: lengthWriters, growableName: self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName()), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'refuseLengthChangeOnASizedContainersBuffer'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SECOND HALF OF THE RULE: THE RECORD MAY NOT LEAVE (W192 review).** The refusal above keeps a
	// sized container's record honest WHILE it is in the declaration's hands; this keeps it in them. They are
	// two refusals and not one because they are two different injuries with two different cures — a length
	// write is *"you moved the number the type states"*, an escape is *"past here nothing can tell your
	// record from a slice of it"* — and forcing both through one noun is what would make the sentence lie.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT READS THE MARK AND NOTHING ELSE, WHICH IS THE ENTIRE CORRECTION IT EMBODIES.** The three doors it
	// serves used to INFER the fact from the declaration and were measured refusing a slice; see
	// `sizedContainersRecordEscapeRefusal` for that measurement. A value with no mark is not the record, and
	// there is no second question to ask about it.
	//
	// ⚠ The anchor is the caller's, because the three doors point at different tokens — the `return` keyword,
	// the offending ARGUMENT and the stored VALUE — and a diagnostic that pointed at the function for an
	// argument would send the reader to the wrong line of a multi-argument call.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE EXEMPTION LIVES HERE AND NOT AT A DOOR, BECAUSE IT IS ONE FACT AND THE DOORS ARE THREE
	// (W192 second review).** `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `createIterator` hands the marked record to
	// `VectorIter.create` and MUST go on working — it is what `for e in v` is built out of. That call is the
	// declaring library's own, and the library is where the record's invariant is established (`create()`
	// grows, then the literal's own element count sets the length), so the file that owns the type may hand
	// its own record around. `livesUnderStdlibDirectory` is the bit the two other stdlib-only doors already
	// use (`requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod`), and it is a property of the FILE rather than of how a command
	// line spelled it — see its field header for the measurement that settled that.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED AFTER THE MARK TEST AND NOT BEFORE**, so the common case pays the set probe it was
	// already paying and nothing more; the location question is only reached by a value that really is the
	// record, which happens in one corpus file and in an `extension Vector`.
	function refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping(value ValueId, escape SizedContainerRecordEscape, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber) throws ParseError
		if not self.valueIsASizedContainersBuffer(value) 'notTheRecordItself'
			return
		end 'notTheRecordItself'

		if self.livesUnderStdlibDirectory 'theDeclaringLibrarysOwn'
			return
		end 'theDeclaringLibrarysOwn'

		throw ParseError.unsupported(sizedContainersRecordEscapeRefusal(escape, lengthWriters: bufferLengthWritingMemberNames(self.surfaceMemberNames(MemberSurface.buffer)), growableName: self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName()), line: line, column: column)
	end 'refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CALL-ARGUMENT HALF OF THE ESCAPE RULE (W192 review)** — split out of `emitCall` so the guard
	// that makes it free stands in front of the loop rather than inside it, which is how a per-CALL cost
	// becomes a per-ARGUMENT one.
	//
	// ⚡ **THE GUARD IS AN `is` IDENTITY TEST AND IT IS WHY THIS COSTS NOTHING.** `emitCall` runs at every
	// call site in every compile; a function that has not minted a single sized-container record still holds
	// the module-level shared empty set, so the whole rule is one pointer comparison away for every program
	// that is not inside `Vector`'s declaration.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT USED TO BE SCOPED TO A CALLEE DECLARED ON THE SIZED CONTAINER, AND THAT SCOPE WAS A MEASURED
	// HOLE (W192 second review).** The argument recorded for the scope was that nothing outside the
	// declaration can name the type — which is true and beside the point: INSIDE an `extension Vector` the
	// type IS nameable, so a user's own `type Box uses T` at `Box with ElementMemory` takes the record
	// through `Box.create(managed)` and hands it straight back from `b.get()`, unmarked. MEASURED:
	// `len=0 count=3`, the rung's own reproducer, one library-free generic away. ⇒ Every callee is a door,
	// and the ONE call the corpus needs is exempted BY LOCATION at the shared refusal above rather than by a
	// name here — a name would have to be right about which of `ArrayIterator`'s members merely read.
	function refuseASizedContainersRecordEscapingIntoACall(args ValueIdArray, argRanges SourceRangeArray) throws ParseError
		if self.sizedContainerBufferValues is sharedEmptySizedContainerBufferValues 'thisFunctionHoldsNoSuchRecord'
			return
		end 'thisFunctionHoldsNoSuchRecord'

		for i in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArgument'
			let argRange = try argRanges.get(i) otherwise panic("refuseASizedContainersRecordEscapingIntoACall: argRanges.get OOB at {i} — `emitCall` builds the argument columns in lockstep with `args`")
			try self.refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping(try args.get(i) otherwise panic("refuseASizedContainersRecordEscapingIntoACall: args.get OOB at {i} — the index came from this very array's count"), escape: SizedContainerRecordEscape.passedToACall, line: argRange.line, column: argRange.column)
		end 'eachArgument'
	end 'refuseASizedContainersRecordEscapingIntoACall'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **DOES THE CORPUS DECLARE THIS MEMBER? — THE DOOR THAT MAKES A LISTED `stdlib/String.maxon` MEAN
	// SOMETHING (W17).** A roster is shv2's statement of what its SYNTHESIZED surface serves; it was the whole
	// answer while the corpus was unreadable, and it stopped being one the moment `type String` and
	// `type Character` were listed. Those declarations carry members the roster has never had —
	// `Character.toString()` is the one the suite found first, 76 times — and the refusal for them was
	// *"shv2 provides bytes/byteLength/asciiValue; that list IS the surface"*, which is now false: the
	// program HAS a `Character.toString`, written in Maxon, sitting in a module this compiler loaded.
	//
	// ⇒ **A byte-record receiver asks the CORPUS before it is refused**, and a hit becomes an ordinary
	// qualified call to an ordinary declared function. That is the retirement mechanism in one door: every
	// member the corpus declares and shv2 does not is served from source, with no arm, no runtime graph and
	// no roster entry — and the members shv2 DOES serve keep their arms, because the roster is asked first.
	//
	// ⚠ **ORDER IS THE DESIGN, NOT AN OPTIMISATION.** The roster wins, so `s.byteLength()` still lowers to a
	// field load rather than calling the corpus body that would do the same thing more slowly, and
	// `s.hash()`/`s.equals()` still reach the synthesized conformance impls a witness slot is stamped with.
	// Retiring an arm is therefore a DELETION from the roster and nothing else — the corpus catches it here,
	// which is what makes the retirement incremental and measurable one member at a time.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS ASKED FOR TWO RECEIVER KINDS, NOT ONE (W49 wave 3).** `StringIndex` joined the moment
	// its type stopped being the compiler's own: while the layout was `__StringIndex`, a name no source
	// could spell, the type had exactly the two members shv2 gave it and the roster WAS the surface. The
	// corpus's `type StringIndex` (`stdlib/String.maxon:917`) declares `equals` and `compare` as well — it
	// `implements Equatable, Comparable` — so the roster refusal became the very sentence this door exists
	// to retire: **MEASURED on this tree before the arm was added, `d.equals(e)` on two indices reported
	// `E2015 … 'equals' — shv2 provides charIndex/bytePos; that list IS the surface`** about a method the
	// program plainly has. The two accessors keep their arms (roster wins, for the field-load reason
	// `dispatchStringIndexMethod` states); everything else the corpus declares is served from source.
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND THE `Array` SURFACE IS THE THIRD ASKER (W59), WHICH TOOK A SECOND DOOR TO BECOME ONE.** The
	// instance test is `isArrayInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId)` — a question about the TYPE, which
	// is all that ADMITTING an `Array` needs, since `Array with T` and its `__ManagedMemory with T` ARE one
	// type (which surface a receiver denotes is a further question, and the ⛔ block below is what it cost to
	// leave unasked). On its own it moved the refusal rather than
	// removing it: admitting a member routes it to `structBaseOfReceiver`, and
	// `ProgramSignatures.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout` answers FALSE for every BUILTIN generic base whatever
	// any file declares — so a declared `Array.size()` got as far as `missingBaseLayout` and was refused
	// *"a field access … `Array` is a BUILTIN whose runtime record shv2 synthesizes"*, about a METHOD, which
	// is a WORSE sentence than the roster's. What admits it is `structLayoutOfType`'s own
	// `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` arm — read that header for why the FIELD WALK's gate stays where it is.
	//
	// ⚠ The SHAPE is asked as well as the instance, and the two are not the same test — see
	// `ProgramSignatures.declarationIsTheManagedRecord` for why either alone is a wrong answer. A declaration that
	// fails it is a record this compiler does NOT own, and its members must keep meeting the roster sentence
	// rather than being dispatched against a layout the value does not have.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE INSTANCE TEST ALONE PICKED THE WRONG SURFACE, BECAUSE IT IS THE TYPE THAT IS SHARED AND
	// THE SURFACE THAT IS NOT.** Being an `Array` instance is exactly as true of a receiver denoting the
	// BUFFER, so this door asked the **`Array`** roster about `__ManagedMemory`'s members — and the two
	// rosters are DELIBERATELY different lists. Every buffer member off the `Array` roster
	// (`swap`, `elementSize`, `grow`, `setLength`, `shiftLeft`, …) therefore fell through to
	// `declaresCallee("Array.<m>")` and was handed to the ENCLOSING RECORD's same-named method:
	//   • **MEASURED as a SILENT WRONG ANSWER** — a `type Array` declaring an `elementSize()` of its own got
	//     that method's value back from `managed.elementSize()`, compiled, linked and RAN, returning 99 where
	//     the buffer's answer is 8. No diagnostic anywhere.
	//   • **MEASURED as a wrong DIAGNOSTIC** — `stdlib/helpers/sort/smallSort.maxon:32`'s
	//     `try managed.swap(i, j: j)`, inside an `export extension Array` declaring its own `swap`, was
	//     `E3055 … 'Array.swap' does not throw`, about a call whose real target throws. The routing was
	//     unbounded recursion; only the throws-mismatch made it visible, which is the accident that found
	//     the whole defect.
	//
	// ⇒ **SO THE MARK IS ASKED HERE, AND IT IS THE SAME ONE `dispatchArrayMethod` DECIDES ON** — one fact,
	// read by both doors, rather than a second answer to "which of the two surfaces is this?". The buffer
	// surface is the COMPILER's own and no file declares a member on `__ManagedMemory`, so a buffer receiver
	// has nothing for the corpus to serve: it falls to `false` and reaches `dispatchArrayMethod`, where the
	// BUFFER roster gates it and a name that surface lacks is refused in the buffer's own sentence.
	//
	// ⚠ `dispatchArrayMethod` spells the same question `viaManagedField or valueIsBufferSurface(arr)`, and
	// the extra disjunct is not missing here: the two call sites that pass `viaManagedField: true` reach that
	// dispatcher DIRECTLY (`dispatchMethodOnBinding`'s fused arm and the re-entry inside the dispatcher
	// itself), so every receiver arriving at this door came by a route that passes `false`.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY THE THREE SPELLINGS WITHOUT A PRE-DOOR WERE AFFECTED, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED**: a STATEMENT
	// or `try`-TARGET `<base>.managed.<m>(…)` is claimed ahead of this by `arrayManagedMemberCallAt` and was
	// always right, so `try arr.managed.swap(…)` worked while the bare `managed.swap(…)` two lines away did
	// not. A defect reachable by three routes and masked on a fourth reads as a spelling quirk.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND THE `Array` ARM ASKS THE RECEIVER WHICH DECLARATION IT IS OF, RATHER THAN SPELLING ONE
	// (ARR2).** A user program declaring its own `type Array` moves the CORPUS declaration into the reserved
	// space (`SignatureIndex.contestStdlibTypeName`), so TWO `Array` declarations are live in one compile: the
	// user's under the bare name, `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s under this compile's mint. Both clauses below were
	// keyed on the CONSTANT, which asks about the user's declaration whatever the receiver is:
	//   • **MEASURED with `reserve` struck from the roster** — `stdlib/String.maxon:807`'s `bytes.reserve(…)`,
	//     whose receiver is the corpus's own `__Array with Byte`, looked for `Array.reserve` on the USER's
	//     container, found nothing and was refused **`E2015 … stdlib/String.maxon:807:9 … 'Array' member
	//     'reserve'`** — the compiler refusing a library line no user wrote, in a program whose only offence
	//     was declaring a container of its own. Three cases; the ⭐ block at `arraySurfaceMemberNames` records
	//     it as the reason four names could not leave the roster.
	//   • The SHAPE clause was wrong the same way, and in the more dangerous direction: it read the user
	//     declaration's field list to decide whether the CORPUS's record is the compiler's.
	// The receiver's instance carries the answer already — `Array with T` and `__ManagedMemory with T` intern
	// to one id under the name the declaring FILE spells (`canonicalGenericBaseNameInThisFile`, F31) — so this
	// is a read of a fact the type already holds and not a second scoping rule.
	function memberBelongsToTheCorpus(recv MethodReceiver) returns bool
		if tagIsByteRecord(recv.tag) 'aByteRecord'
			return self.corpusServesMemberOffRoster(byteRecordSurface(recv.tag), typeName: self.byteRecordLayoutName(recv.tag))
		end 'aByteRecord'
		if self.isStringIndexType(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'aStringIndex'
			return self.corpusServesMemberOffRoster(MemberSurface.stringIndex, typeName: StringIndexTypeName)
		end 'aStringIndex'
		if self.signatures.isArrayInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) and not self.valueIsBufferSurface(recv.value) 'anArraySurface'
			let declaration = self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(recv.nameId)
			if not self.signatures.declarationIsTheManagedRecord(declaration) 'notTheOwnRecordArray'
				return false
			end 'notTheOwnRecordArray'

			return self.corpusServesMemberOffRoster(MemberSurface.array, typeName: declaration)
		end 'anArraySurface'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S ARM, AND LISTING `stdlib/Vector.maxon` DOES NOT WORK WITHOUT IT (W189).**
		//
		// ⛔ **MEASURED, and it is the whole difference between a filtered run and the suite: `--filter=vector`
		// was 55/55 without this arm and the full suite was 5941/400.** 375 of those are one sentence —
		// `E2015 stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:229: `Vector` member 'createIterator' — shv2 provides count/get/set` —
		// because a listed `Vector` DECLARES `implements Iterable`, so `extension Iterable`'s `map`, `filter`
		// and `contains` specialize for it, and their `for item in self` asks the receiver for the very member
		// the roster did not carry. The conformance is not decoration: it is what publishes those extension
		// methods onto the type, and they are compiled.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **AND IT HAS NO ROSTER CLAUSE LEFT (W190), WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT THE WHOLE SURFACE RATHER THAN
		// ITS REMAINDER.** The three sibling arms above ask two questions — does the COMPILER serve this name
		// itself, and does the corpus declare it — because each of those surfaces still has members of its own.
		// The sized container has none: `vectorSurfaceMemberNames` and `dispatchVectorMethod` are gone, so the
		// first question is vacuous and only the declaration's answer is left.
		if self.signatures.isVectorInstanceAt(recv.tag, nameId: recv.nameId) 'aSizedContainerSurface'
			let sizedDeclaration = self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(recv.nameId)
			if not self.signatures.declarationIsASizedContainerRecordNamed(sizedDeclaration) 'notTheCorpusVector'
				return false
			end 'notTheCorpusVector'

			return self.corpusDeclaresMemberAtCursor(sizedDeclaration)
		end 'aSizedContainerSurface'

		return false
	end 'memberBelongsToTheCorpus'

	// The shared test behind the door above and its STATIC twin: "the roster does not carry this member and
	// the corpus declares it on this type". Written once because the three askers differ only in which
	// surface and which type name they mean — and getting that ORDER wrong in one of them is exactly the
	// drift this whole arrangement exists to prevent.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **"DECLARES IT" MEANS *ANY* DECLARATION, AND IT USED TO MEAN "IN THE TYPE'S OWN BODY".** It asked
	// `conformerDeclaresOwnMethod`, whose whole point is to EXCLUDE what an `extension` published — a
	// distinction the extension fold needs and this door must not inherit. A published method is the
	// conformer's at every other call site, so the exclusion made this one refuse it: **MEASURED, `"hello".
	// filter(…)` — `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s `extension Iterable` publishes `filter` onto `String`, which
	// `implements Iterable` — reported `E2015 … 'filter' — shv2 provides hash/equals; that list IS the
	// surface`, which is exactly the sentence this door exists to retire, about a method the program has.
	// `Array.sort` and `sortUnstable` live in `export extension Array` and would have been refused for the
	// identical reason.
	//
	// ⚠ It PEEKS: the cursor sits on the member name — the `.` is already consumed and no arm has read it —
	// because the caller hands that token to the dispatcher, which consumes it. `tokenCanBeAName` guards a
	// measured hazard rather than a hypothetical one: a literal carries its CONTENT in `.value`, so
	// `String."fromOwnedBytes"` would otherwise be tested against the corpus's declaration by bytes alone.
	function corpusServesMemberOffRoster(surface MemberSurface, typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		let memberTok = self.current()
		if not tokenCanBeAName(memberTok) 'notAMemberName'
			return false
		end 'notAMemberName'

		return self.corpusServesNamedMemberOffRoster(memberTok.value, surface: surface, typeName: typeName)
	end 'corpusServesMemberOffRoster'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME DOOR FOR A TYPE WITH NO SYNTHESIZED SURFACE LEFT AT ALL (W190).** The rostered form above
	// asks two questions and this one asks the second alone: the sized container has no roster to lose a name
	// to, so every member a `Vector` has is `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s. It is a SEPARATE door rather than a call
	// with an empty roster because an empty roster would need a `MemberSurface` entry, and that entry is
	// exactly what retires when a dispatcher does.
	//
	// ⚠ It PEEKS, and it carries the `tokenCanBeAName` guard for the rostered door's measured reason: a
	// literal carries its CONTENT in `.value`, so `Vector."frobnicate"` would otherwise be tested against the
	// declaration by bytes alone.
	function corpusDeclaresMemberAtCursor(typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		let memberTok = self.current()
		if not tokenCanBeAName(memberTok) 'notAMemberName'
			return false
		end 'notAMemberName'

		return self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: memberTok.value))
	end 'corpusDeclaresMemberAtCursor'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE RULE ITSELF, GIVEN A NAME RATHER THAN A CURSOR (ARRG).** The door above PEEKS, and that peek
	// is a fact about where the dispatch stands rather than about the rule — so a second asker that holds a
	// TOKEN INDEX instead of a cursor (`Parser.corpusServesArrayMember`, the descriptor fixpoint's cross-type
	// edge) can read the same two clauses rather than restate them.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND RESTATING THEM WOULD DIVERGE SILENTLY IN THE WORST DIRECTION.** The two askers are the DISPATCH
	// (which member calls reach a corpus body) and the RESERVATION (which callers must supply that body's
	// layout descriptor). A clause added to one only — a visibility test, a further roster — would leave a
	// call dispatched to a body whose descriptor nobody passes, and that is not a diagnostic:
	// `materializeLayoutArgFor` aborts the compiler. One home is what makes the two answers the same answer.
	function corpusServesNamedMemberOffRoster(member ByteArray, surface MemberSurface, typeName ByteArray) returns bool
		if namesInclude(self.surfaceMemberNames(surface), name: member) 'theSynthesizedSurfaceServesIt'
			return false
		end 'theSynthesizedSurfaceServesIt'

		return self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: member))
	end 'corpusServesNamedMemberOffRoster'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE STATIC TWIN OF THE DOOR ABOVE IS GONE (W55), AND WHAT REPLACED IT IS `parseQualifiedCall`
	// ITSELF.** `stringStaticBelongsToTheCorpus` asked this same question about a `String` STATIC, so that a
	// static the corpus declares and shv2's roster did not could reach its declaration. Once BOTH synthesized
	// statics retired, its roster test could no longer answer anything but "miss" and every call it admitted
	// went to `parseCallNamed` with a callee, receiver, mint and `constructorInstance` identical to the ones
	// the ordinary qualified-call path builds for the same tokens — including the `tokenCanBeAName` guard,
	// which lives in that path's own `consumeNameToken`. The RECEIVER door above stays because a receiver
	// dispatch has a synthesized roster that genuinely wins first (`s.byteLength()` is a field load, not a
	// call); a static has no roster left to win.

	// Which roster a byte record's tag names. The two are separate surfaces with separate rosters, exactly as
	// they are separate conformers, so the mapping is stated once here rather than at each asker.
	function byteRecordSurface(tag ValueTypeTag) returns MemberSurface
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.character 'aCharacter'
			return MemberSurface.character
		end 'aCharacter'
		return MemberSurface.string
	end 'byteRecordSurface'

	// ⭐⭐ **`arr.managed` IN VALUE POSITION — THE SAME RECORD, SEEN AS THE BUFFER (BATCH2 slice 6), AND THE
	// EXACT INVERSE OF `parseArrayInitStatic`.** `init(managed)` adopts a buffer AS the array around it; this
	// hands the array's buffer BACK. One record, two surfaces, and the two directions owe the same two things:
	// a FRESH `ValueId` to carry the surface mark, and a refcount that still balances.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE OWNERSHIP QUESTION IS "WHO ALREADY OWES THIS RECORD A DROP?", AND IT HAS TWO ANSWERS.**
	//   • **THE STATEMENT DOES** — `arr` is an UNBOUND owned temporary (`"…".toByteArray().managed`,
	//     `[1,2,3].managed`). `.managed` changes nothing about who drops it: the value carries the statement's
	//     one obligation on, to a binding that takes it over (`removeFromPendingTemps`) or to the statement-end
	//     drain. A second owner here would be a retain nobody needs, on every `path.toByteArray().managed` in
	//     the corpus. And the mark is safe on the receiver's OWN `ValueId` precisely BECAUSE it is a pending
	//     temp: `pendingTempsContain`'s contract is that such a value is owned by no binding, so there is no
	//     second name through which the `Array` surface could still be wanted — which is the whole hazard
	//     `error.managed-field-chain-does-not-mark-the-array` pins for the CHAINED spelling.
	//   • **SOMEBODY ELSE DOES** — `arr` is a BINDING's value (`var a = […]`, dropped at ITS scope exit) or a
	//     BORROW (a `ByteArray` PARAMETER, owned in the caller's frame — `stdlib/File.maxon:147`). Whatever
	//     this value is bound to or handed to drops independently, so it must become a SECOND owner through
	//     `coOwnAggregateAsTemp` — the `__mm_retain` that mints the fresh id on the same refcount word
	//     `__managed_decref` lowers, plus the statement-scoped drop an UNBOUND result needs. It is the one door
	//     `parseArrayInitStatic` takes for the other direction, so the two inverses cannot drift on ownership.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **HANDING BACK THE RECEIVER'S OWN `ValueId` IN THE SECOND CASE IS A DOUBLE FREE, NOT A COSMETIC
	// IDENTITY.** `let m = a.managed` is three tokens, so `rhsWasBareLocalReference` reads it as a CONSUME and
	// does NOT poison `a` — yet `declareInitializedBinding` enrols `m` in `ownedBindings` all the same, because
	// the value's provenance says owned-heap. `a` and `m` would be one box with two scope-exit drops.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE SURFACE FLIP, PLUS THE ONE RETYPE THE SIZED CONTAINER NEEDS (W189).** For the ARRAY record the
	// flip is the whole of it: `Array with T` and `__ManagedMemory with T` are ONE `GenericInstanceId`, so the
	// buffer surface of an array IS the array, and only the MARK changes. A `Vector with N T`'s record is the
	// same 48 bytes but a DIFFERENT id — the size is the second coordinate of its identity — so the value
	// handed to a buffer position has to say so, or the corpus's own `VectorIter.create(managed)` is refused
	// **`E3005 … expected 'Vector.ElementMemory', got 'Vector_T…'`**: one record, two ids, which is exactly the
	// disagreement `canonicalGenericBaseNameInThisFile`'s fold exists to prevent one genericity up.
	//
	// ⚠ The buffer instance is built from the RECEIVER's element rather than from the declaration's parameter,
	// so it is concrete wherever the receiver is (`Vec3`'s buffer is `Array with Int`) and opaque inside the
	// declaration's own body — which is the same instance the field's declared type resolves to.
	function bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let surface = self.bufferSurfaceOf(box, range: range)
		if not self.signatures.declarationIsASizedContainerRecordNamed(layout.name) 'theBufferIsItsOwnInstance'
			return surface
		end 'theBufferIsItsOwnInstance'

		// ⭐⭐ **W1 — THE SIZED FACT IS WRITTEN ON THE VALUE HERE BECAUSE THE VERY NEXT LINE ERASES IT FROM THE
		// TYPE.** The retype below is what makes `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `createIterator`'s `VectorIter.create(managed)`
		// type-check, and it is load-bearing (W189) — but it leaves the value spelled `Array with Element`, so
		// `genericInstanceBaseName(giid)` at the dispatch answers `Array` and no `giid` test downstream can
		// tell a vector's record from a growable array's. This `if` is the last place that knows, and it
		// already asked the question one line up.
		//
		// ⇒ It carries the answer on a per-VALUE mark instead, which reaches both spellings that mint one:
		// the bare `managed` inside an `extension Vector` (`dispatchMethodOnBinding` → `baseBoxOf` →
		// `parseSelfFieldRead`) and the explicit `self.managed` (`parseExplicitSelfFieldRead`), plus every
		// local a program binds either to — and, since the mark has to survive being re-minted, every
		// PROMOTION (`carryDeclaredSurface`) and every MERGE (`propagateBufferSurfaceToPhi`).
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ONE SPELLING THAT MINTS NO VALUE IS ANSWERED ELSEWHERE**: a fused `managed.append(…)` at
		// `dispatchMethodOnBinding`, off the ENCLOSING DECLARATION. ⛔ A `returns ElementMemory` used to be
		// listed here as a third carrier answered at `emitCall` off the callee's name — that carrier is GONE
		// and its removal is the first review's correction: a `slice` has the identical declared type, so the
		// declaration cannot tell the record from a view of it. What replaced it is the ESCAPE rule
		// (`refuseASizedContainersRecordEscaping`), which keeps the record where the mark still exists rather
		// than trying to recognise it where the mark does not.
		self.markSizedContainerBuffer(surface)

		self.retypeValue(surface, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(self.bufferSurfaceInstanceOf(box, layout: layout)))
		return surface
	end 'bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHICH GENERIC INSTANCE A FUSED WRAPPER'S BUFFER SURFACE WEARS — ONE ANSWER, TWO DOORS, AND THE
	// SECOND OF THEM IS THE WHOLE OF W194.** The value door RETYPES the surface it mints to this (above); the
	// fused record door hands it to `dispatchArrayMethod` as the receiver's `giid`, minting nothing
	// (`dispatchMethodOnBinding`'s `inlineManagedServesTheRecord`). Those are the two spellings of one field,
	// and the rule that they *"answer to one element rule"* was written in the fused door's header long before
	// anything enforced it — that door spelled a CONSTANT, `internSynthesizedByteInstance()`, which is one of
	// the three answers below and was handed to receivers wanting the other two.
	//
	// **The three cases are the three shapes of fused record, and each is somebody else's already-settled
	// answer rather than a new one:**
	//   • **A BYTE RECORD** (`String`, `Character`) — the compiler's own synthesized byte buffer, the instance
	//     `emitFieldLoad` stamps on the `__str_bytes_view` it mints for exactly these two.
	//   • **THE ARRAY RECORD** — the buffer of an `Array with T` IS that array: `Array with T` and
	//     `__ManagedMemory with T` are ONE `GenericInstanceId` (W57), so the receiver's own instance is the
	//     answer and there is nothing to build.
	//   • **THE SIZED CONTAINER'S RECORD** (`Vector`) — the same 48 bytes under a DIFFERENT id, because the
	//     size is the second coordinate of a vector's identity (W189). Its buffer must be spelled
	//     `Array with T`, built from the RECEIVER's element so it is concrete wherever the receiver is
	//     (`Vec3`'s buffer is `Array with Int`) and opaque inside the declaration's own body.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ORDER IS NOT A PREFERENCE, IT IS AN INCLUSION.** `declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord` is the
	// union of the two declared-record cases, so it is asked FIRST to send the byte records away; only then
	// is the sized half separated from the growable one, which is the same rank order
	// `bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord` above uses and for the same reason.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE FIRST ARM IS A NEGATION, SO IT ANSWERS FOR A FOURTH SHAPE THE CENSUS ABOVE DOES NOT NAME,
	// AND WHAT MAKES THAT SAFE IS A REACHABILITY ARGUMENT THAT LIVES IN ANOTHER FUNCTION (W194 second
	// review).** `declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord` is false for `String` and `Character` — and equally
	// false for ANY OTHER conformer of one of `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s three literal markers, which a user
	// file may perfectly well declare (`type Bag implements BuiltinArrayLiteral`). Such a wrapper is handed
	// the compiler's byte buffer here, which is the same stride lie W194 exists to delete — one shape further
	// out.
	//
	// ⇒ **IT CANNOT PRODUCE A WRONG ANSWER BECAUSE THAT SHAPE CANNOT PRODUCE A VALUE**, and that is
	// `Parser.requireFusedWrapperTag`'s doing, not this function's: shv2 mints a fused record only for the
	// two NAMES it owns the record for, so every other marked conformer is refused at its `Self{…}` literal
	// (`specs-shv2/interface-conformance.md` pins it, with the **exit 0xC0000005** the refusal replaced).
	// MEASURED at this review, on this tip: a `type Bag implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` over a CONCRETE
	// `__ManagedMemory with String`, whose `init` panics so no literal is ever written, COMPILES — and its
	// body's `managed.setByte(0, 65)` is admitted through this arm, exactly as the pre-cure `Array` was.
	// It leaks nothing only because no `Bag` can exist to be written into.
	//
	// ⚠ **SO THE ARM IS CORRECT TODAY AND ITS CORRECTNESS IS BORROWED.** It is left as a negation rather
	// than turned into a positive `byteRecordTagOfTypeName` test with a `panic` terminal, because that
	// panic would fire on programs the suite currently accepts — `interface-conformance.md`,
	// `array-declared-record.md` and three more declare marked conformers under five other names, and each
	// is pinned on the E2015 it gets today, not on a compiler abort. Widening `requireFusedWrapperTag`'s
	// refusal from the LITERAL to the DECLARATION would retire the borrowing entirely and is its own row.
	function bufferSurfaceInstanceOf(box ValueId, layout StructLayout) returns GenericInstanceId
		if not self.signatures.declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord(layout.name) 'aByteRecordsBufferIsTheCompilersOwn'
			return self.signatures.internSynthesizedByteInstance()
		end 'aByteRecordsBufferIsTheCompilersOwn'

		if not self.signatures.declarationIsASizedContainerRecordNamed(layout.name) 'theBufferIsItsOwnInstance'
			return self.valueNameId(box)
		end 'theBufferIsItsOwnInstance'

		return self.signatures.builtinInstanceForElement(self.signatures.arrayRecordBaseName(), element: self.signatures.containerElementType(self.valueNameId(box)))
	end 'bufferSurfaceInstanceOf'

	function bufferSurfaceOf(arr ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if self.pendingTempsContain(arr) 'statementAlreadyOwnsIt'
			self.markBufferSurface(arr)
			return arr
		end 'statementAlreadyOwnsIt'

		let buffer = self.coOwnAggregateAsTemp(arr, range: range)
		self.markBufferSurface(buffer)
		return buffer
	end 'bufferSurfaceOf'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **MAY A FUSED WRAPPER'S OWN RECORD BE HANDED STRAIGHT TO THIS BUFFER MEMBER'S RUNTIME ENTRY?
	// (W49 wave 7 cure.)** A `String` record and an `Array` record agree on all SIX buffer slots —
	// `buffer@0`, `length@8`, `capacity@16`, `element_size@24`, `parent@32`, `element_destroy@40`, every one
	// spelled from the SAME `BufferOwnership.ManagedRecord*Offset` constants — and a String record carries a
	// SEVENTH slot behind them, `singleByteGraphemes@48`, which no buffer entry knows about.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE LIST'S ORIGINAL QUESTION WAS "does this member's entry touch `@40`", AND W157 ANSWERED IT OUT
	// OF EXISTENCE.** `@40` was `element_destroy` in one record and `singleByteGraphemesFlag` (0/1) in the
	// other, so a member reading it would `callIndirect` address 0 or 1 given a String. A String record now
	// holds a REAL `element_destroy@40` (always `TrivialElementDestroy`), so no buffer entry can be wrong
	// about that slot. **The question this list still answers is the OTHER direction**: an entry that reads
	// `@48` is written for a BYTE record and must never be handed a plain 48-byte buffer record, where that
	// offset is off the end.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY LINE OF THE CENSUS BELOW IS AN ARGUMENT ABOUT A *BYTE* RECORD, AND FOR TWO WAVES NOTHING
	// SAID SO (W194).** The list is keyed on the MEMBER; the door that consults it is keyed on
	// `StructLayout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord`, which admits `Array` and `Vector` as well — whose `@24`
	// is the element's stride rather than 1, whose element may be an opaque type parameter, and which have no
	// `@48` at all. THREE of the six entries below were wrong on such a record — `append`'s `1 == 1`,
	// `setByte`'s two giid-keyed admission checks (found at this rung's REVIEW, measured as a LEAK), and
	// `makeCharFromBytes`'s seventh slot — and the whitelist could not know, because the record was somebody
	// else's question.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE CURE IS NOT TO NARROW THE DOOR BUT TO STOP IT LYING ABOUT THE RECEIVER.** Two of those
	// three read the receiver's INSTANCE, not the record, and the door was handing every receiver the same
	// constant — `internSynthesizedByteInstance()`. It now hands each wrapper its own
	// (`bufferSurfaceInstanceOf`, one home shared with the value door's retype), which makes `append`'s and
	// `setByte`'s admission checks ask about the element the receiver actually has; the third,
	// `makeCharFromBytes`, is closed independently by `dispatchArrayMethod`'s tag test, which asks what the
	// record IS rather than how it was reached. ⛔ W194's FIRST cure narrowed the door to the two byte
	// records instead. It was correct and it cost **1.65–1.9x on `Array.hash`** — a corpus body, not a
	// compiler-served member, looping `byteAt` once per byte — so all four conformers keep the fast path and
	// the entries stay member-shaped, which is the only shape a name can be tested in at the token before
	// the `(`.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS A WHITELIST AND MUST STAY ONE, BECAUSE THE DEFAULT HAS TO BE THE CONSERVATIVE ANSWER.**
	// A member absent from this list reaches its entry through `emitFieldLoad`'s `managed`-as-a-value door
	// instead, which since W157 hands over the same record with an `__mm_incref`/`__managed_decref` pair
	// around it: correct, and one refcount pair dearer. So the two directions are still not comparable, and
	// a name joins only with its entry's call graph traced.
	//
	// **The census, traced through every helper each entry calls (W49 wave 7; re-read at W157):**
	//   • `length()` -> `__managed_count` (`buildManagedCount`) — reads `@8`. Nothing else.
	//   • `byteAt(i)` -> `__managed_byte_at` — reads `@0`, and `emitBufferAccessGuard` reads `@8`/`@16` and
	//     `@24`. A String's `@24` is `ByteStringElementSize = 1` on EVERY producer, so `length·es` is the byte
	//     length, which is the bound that member means.
	//   • `setByte(i, v)` -> `__managed_set_byte` — the guard plus `__managed_cow_detach` -> `__mm_cow_detach`,
	//     which reads `@8`/`@24`/`@0` and writes `@0`/`@16`/`@32`. **W49 wave 2 already established this
	//     one and already passes the String's record for it** (`dispatchStringMethod`'s `setByte` arm); it
	//     is listed so the two doors read one list.
	//     ⛔⛔ **AND IT IS A *SECOND* ENTRY WHOSE PARSE-TIME ADMISSION CONSULTS THE RECEIVER'S INSTANCE —
	//     FOUND AT THE W194 REVIEW, WHICH IS WHY THE `append` BULLET BELOW NO LONGER SAYS "the only one".**
	//     Its arm asks `requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer(giid, …)` (E3110) and `requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot`
	//     (E3118), and BOTH read the giid rather than the record. Under the synthesized BYTE instance those
	//     answer about a `Byte` — trivial, one byte wide — whatever the receiver's real element is.
	//     **MEASURED on this rung's own RED baseline**: an `extension Array` whose body is
	//     `try managed.setByte(0, 65) otherwise ignore`, instantiated at `Array with String`, **COMPILED
	//     CLEAN and the program EXITED 101 — a memory leak**, because the byte write landed in a String
	//     POINTER slot that E3110 exists to refuse. The identical body written `self.managed.setByte(…)` —
	//     the value path, carrying the real instance — did not compile at all. One member, two spellings, a
	//     leak and a stop.
	//     ✅ **THE REAL INSTANCE CLOSES THE LEAK, AND THE TWO CHECKS THEN ANSWER ABOUT THE ELEMENT THE
	//     RECEIVER HAS.** Measured on the reworked tip, all three spellings of the same write:
	//       • CONCRETE element, `a.managed.setByte(0, 65)` on an `Array with String` — **`E3110` at 6:16**,
	//         *"a managed element is stored as a POINTER … writing them corrupts it"*. The diagnostic that
	//         was always meant to fire, firing.
	//       • OPAQUE element, bare `managed.setByte(…)` inside an `extension Array` — `panic at
	//         LayoutDescriptor.maxon:571: primitiveTypeByteSize`, through E3118's stride test.
	//       • OPAQUE element, `self.managed.setByte(…)` — the SAME panic, differing only in the frames
	//         above it. That spelling did this on the base too; the bare one compiled clean and leaked.
	//     ⚠ **A BARE FUSED `setByte` OVER A RECORD THIS COMPILER OWNS IS *ALWAYS* THE OPAQUE CASE**, because
	//     the only bodies that can spell it are that declaration's own and its extensions — and the two
	//     declarations whose record the compiler owns, `Array` and `Vector`, are both GENERIC, so the element
	//     there IS the type parameter. E3110 is therefore unreachable from this door for them, and the panic
	//     is what a generic body meets. That panic is PRE-EXISTING on the value path, present identically on
	//     the merge base, and replacing it with a positioned refusal (or a deferral of the stride question to
	//     instantiation) is a row of its own: it needs a spec to choose between them. W194 changed which
	//     ELEMENT the door on the other side is asked about, never what it asks.
	//     ⛔ **THE SENTENCE ABOVE SAID "ON A NON-BYTE RECORD" AND THAT IS WIDER THAN THE ARGUMENT SUPPORTS
	//     (W194 second review).** A user file may declare its own marked conformer over a CONCRETE element
	//     — `type Bag implements BuiltinArrayLiteral` holding a `__ManagedMemory with String` — and spell
	//     `managed.setByte(0, 65)` in its own body, which is a bare fused `setByte` on a non-byte record
	//     that is NOT the opaque case. MEASURED on this tip: it COMPILES, and the write is admitted, because
	//     `bufferSurfaceInstanceOf`'s first arm hands such a wrapper the compiler's BYTE buffer — the same
	//     lie, one shape further out. It cannot leak only because no such value can be constructed
	//     (`requireFusedWrapperTag` refuses the `Self{…}` literal); see that function's header for the
	//     borrowing, and this door's own for why the arm is left as it is.
	//   • `append(other)` -> `__managed_append` — reads both records' `@24` and ABORTS on a mismatch, which two
	//     String records pass (1 == 1); `__managed_move_elems` takes raw buffers and a stride as ARGUMENTS.
	//     ⛔ **THIS IS THE ENTRY THE MISSING RECORD TEST BIT (W194).** Its PARSE-TIME admission consults the
	//     receiver's instance (`ProgramSignatures.arrayAppendArgAdmits`), so on an `Array` record the
	//     synthesized byte instance made `containerElementIsOpaque` answer false about an opaque element and
	//     `primitiveTypeByteSize` took the compiler down.
	//     ⚠ **IT IS NOT THE ONLY SUCH ENTRY, AND THIS BULLET SAID IT WAS UNTIL THE W194 REVIEW** — see
	//     `setByte` above, whose two admission checks read the giid the same way and whose mis-service was a
	//     LEAK rather than a stop. Of the six, THREE consult something other than the record — `append` and
	//     `setByte` consult the INSTANCE, `makeCharFromBytes` consults the RECORD's seventh slot — and the
	//     remaining three (`length`, `byteAt`, `toCString`) emit a bare runtime call over slots both records
	//     share, which is why the defect surfaced as one panicking member and one leaking one rather than
	//     six wrong answers.
	//   • `toCString()` -> `__mm_to_cstring` — reads `@8` and `@0`. Not even `@24`.
	//   • `makeCharFromBytes(pos, len)` -> `__char_at` — reads `@48`, the flag, and stores it into the
	//     Character it builds (`GraphemeRuntime.buildCharAt`). ⛔⛔ **IT IS THE ONE ENTRY THAT READS THE
	//     SEVENTH SLOT, SO IT IS THE ONE THAT MUST BE GIVEN A BYTE RECORD — and this membership is what
	//     supplies one.** Handed a plain 48-byte buffer record it would read eight bytes PAST the record's
	//     end; before W157 the same mis-service merely read `TrivialDestructor` as the flag, a conservative
	//     wrong answer. Its single `stdlib/` caller is `String.makeCharFromBytes`'s
	//     `managed.makeCharFromBytes(…)` — an implicit-`self` fused read that arrives HERE. ⭐ **AND SINCE
	//     W194 THAT IS A STRUCTURAL FACT RATHER THAN A REACHABILITY ONE, RESTING ON THE TAG TEST AND NOT ON
	//     THIS LIST**: `dispatchArrayMethod`'s own guard asks the RECEIVER's tag (`tagIsByteRecord`) rather
	//     than the route it came by, so no spelling can supply it a 48-byte record — not this door, and not
	//     the `.managed` re-entry that also sets `viaManagedField`. **MEASURED at the W194 review**: a
	//     `stdlib/`-located `a.managed.makeCharFromBytes(0, 1)` over an `Array with Int` COMPILED CLEAN and
	//     RAN on the base, `__char_at` reading eight bytes past a 48-byte record. **RE-MEASURED on the
	//     reworked tip with the location gate temporarily opened, because withdrawing the routing left this
	//     guard as the ONLY thing closing that door**: BOTH `Array` spellings — the bare fused
	//     `managed.makeCharFromBytes(0, 1)` and the `.managed` one — stop at the panic below, while the
	//     positive control (`extension String`'s bare spelling, a genuine byte record) compiles, runs and
	//     prints `h`. The probe discriminates; it does not merely report a zero.
	//
	// ⭐ **`slice(start, end)` MAY NOW, AND IS DELIBERATELY STILL ABSENT.** Its exclusion was `@40`:
	// `__managed_slice` -> `emitRecordView` LOADS that slot and STORES it into the fresh view, so a String's
	// flag became the view's `element_destroy` and the first `__managed_decref` `callIndirect`ed address 1.
	// W157 removed the disagreement, so nothing is unsafe about it any more — and it removed the ALLOCATION
	// too, from the other side: `clone()`, `sliceBytes()` and `toByteArray()` reach `managed` as a VALUE, and
	// that door hands back the receiver's record rather than minting a view. What listing `slice` here would
	// still save is one `__mm_incref`/`__managed_decref` pair per call, which is a measurement, not a
	// correctness argument, and belongs to whoever takes it.
	function fusedManagedMemberTakesTheRecord(member ByteArray) returns bool
		return member.equals(ManagedMemoryLengthMethod) or member.equals(ByteAtMethod) or member.equals(ManagedMemorySetByteMethod) or member.equals(AppendMethod) or member.equals(ManagedMemoryToCStringMethod) or member.equals(ManagedMemoryMakeCharFromBytesMethod)
	end 'fusedManagedMemberTakesTheRecord'

	// Dispatch a builtin `Array` method — cursor on the method NAME, `arr` the (borrowed) receiver record.
	// Shared by the binding receiver (`arr.push(x)`) and a value receiver (`[…].get(0)`). An OPAQUE-`T` array
	// (`self.items.push(x)` in a generic body, P1.7 slice 3b) dispatches here too, and the shared body treats the
	// opaque element as an 8-byte WORD — which is correct for MOVE-IN (`push`/`set`/`insert` move the word, and
	// the call-site consume promotes a managed argument, P1.7 slice 3b-iii), for the metadata methods, for drop,
	// for READING (`get`/`first`/`last` hand back a BORROW) and for MOVING OUT (`pop`/`remove` hand back an OWNED
	// word dropped through the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param`, P1.7 slice 3b-vi-a) — the word is only
	// carried and dropped, never interpreted. A per-element COPY (`slice`/`clone`/`append`) is served too, through
	// the descriptor `copyFunc@32` (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β): `__managed_*_opaque` byte-blits a trivial element and
	// deep-clones a direct-clonable managed one, so it never duplicates a managed pointer and double-frees it —
	// unless the instantiation's element is a managed type this rung cannot single-function-clone, which
	// `requireOpaqueArrayCopyable` rejects.
	function dispatchArrayMethod(func IrFunction, arr ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, resultUsed bool, immutableReceiverName ByteArray, borrowSubjectName ByteArray, receiverOwner ParamPositionMask, receiverStorage ParamPositionMask, viaManagedField bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let methodTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(methodTok)

		// ⭐ **A `let`-BOUND ARRAY IS NOT WRITABLE** — checked HERE, once, ahead of every arm, because the
		// question is about the METHOD NAME and this is where the name is first known. It goes through
		// `noteReceiverWrite`, the ONE home this rule has for every container, and it is what makes
		// the top-level managed `let` a CONSTANT rather than a value that silently mutates a copy at each
		// read (`emitManagedConstant`).
		// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS RECEIVER THE `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER SURFACE, OR THE `Array` AROUND IT?** One record,
		// two surfaces (`SignatureIndex.ManagedMemoryTypeName`), and this is the one place the question is
		// asked — the `.managed` arm below, the four buffer-only members further down, the label rule the
		// two-argument members follow and the mutates-receiver rule on the next line all read this answer.
		//
		// ⭐ **TWO DOORS, AND NEITHER IS THE INSTANCE.** R4.2 asked `giid == internArrayByteInstance()`, which
		// was right only while the alias was byte-only: it admitted a user's own `ByteArray` and refused a
		// `__ManagedMemory` over any other element, and R4.4 makes the alias element-agnostic, so an element
		// test can no longer answer. What answers is where the receiver CAME FROM — either it was reached
		// through `.managed` (this call's `viaManagedField`), or the value itself was minted as a buffer
		// (`valueIsBufferSurface`: a `__ManagedMemory.create`, or a `slice` of one).
		let isManagedMemory = viaManagedField or self.valueIsBufferSurface(arr)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE ROSTER IS THE SURFACE, AND IT IS ASKED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE (D11b).** ⚖ USER RULING,
		// 2026-07-31. A member the buffer does not have is refused HERE, ahead of every arm and ahead of both
		// receiver rules below, because "does this member exist?" is the question every other refusal about
		// the call presupposes. Two measured spellings say why the position matters as much as the test:
		//
		//   • `let x = mm.push(7)` used to reach `push`'s own `requireVoidMethodIsStatement` and answer
		//     *"Function 'push' does not return a value"* — D11's exact false claim (the method EXISTS and is
		//     VOID) one surface over, about a name the buffer has never had.
		//   • `try mm.insert(0, value: 1)` used to answer E3055 *"try requires a throwing function"* — a
		//     second assertion that the member is real, differing only in which property it invents.
		//
		// ⇒ The same reason R4.2's review moved the buffer-only mutators out of `arrayMethodMutatesReceiver`
		// (the next line): a rule about HOW a member may be used must not speak for a member the receiver
		// does not have. That fix covered the buffer's own four; this covers the twelve the shared `Array`
		// dispatch brought with it.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **BOTH SURFACES COME THROUGH THIS ONE GATE (A2k).** D11b gated the buffer alone and left the
		// `Array` path falling through to a hand-written literal at the end of the function — which was false
		// in both directions (see `arraySurfaceMemberNames`). Two gates would have been two spellings of one
		// rule; the surface picks the roster (`surfaceMemberNames`) and the roster answers for the type it is
		// shown about (`requireSurfaceMember`, whose noun this door supplies).
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL FOR AN `Array` RECEIVER, and that is what the derived list guarantees**:
		// every name off the `Array` roster reached the fall-through refusal before and is refused here now,
		// with the same E2015 at the same token — only the sentence moved. Nothing on the roster can be
		// refused, because every entry matches an arm (the panic at the end of this function is what says so).
		// ⭐⭐ **AND AHEAD OF THAT GATE, THE ONE THING IT CANNOT SAY (ARR2).** With two `Array` declarations live
		// the roster's *"that list IS the surface"* is a wrong answer about the reader's own program — see
		// `refuseArrayMemberTheOtherDeclarationCarries`. Placed HERE for the paragraph above's own reason: it is
		// still the question *"does this member exist?"*, answered more precisely for the one compile that can
		// tell two answers apart.
		try self.refuseArrayMemberTheOtherDeclarationCarries(giid, methodTok: methodTok, isManagedMemory: isManagedMemory)
		// ⭐⭐ **AND THE SECOND THING THE ROSTER CANNOT SAY: THIS BUFFER'S COUNT IS ANSWERED BY ITS TYPE
		// (W192).** Placed beside the sibling above and for its reason — still *"does this member exist on
		// THIS receiver?"*, answered more precisely for the one receiver that can tell two answers apart. The
		// evidence is the per-value mark and NOT the `giid`, which `bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord`'s retype
		// has already spelled `Array with Element` by the time a value reaches here; the mark implies the
		// buffer answer, so `isManagedMemory` is not a second conjunct (`markSizedContainerBuffer`).
		try self.refuseLengthChangeOnASizedContainersBuffer(methodTok, onASizedContainersRecord: self.valueIsASizedContainersBuffer(arr))
		// ⭐ **THE NOUN IS THE RECEIVER'S OWN DECLARATION, AND THIS IS THE ONLY DOOR THAT KNOWS IT (ARRO).** A
		// buffer is the compiler's own `__ManagedMemory`; an array is whichever `BuiltinArrayLiteral` conformer
		// the value's instance was built from, which since ARR5b may be a `Bag` the program declared.
		let roster = try self.requireSurfaceMember(MemberSurface.buffer if isManagedMemory else MemberSurface.array, typeName: ManagedMemoryTypeName if isManagedMemory else self.arrayReceiverDeclaredName(giid), methodTok: methodTok)

		// ⚠ **THE BUFFER-ONLY MEMBERS ARE GATED ON THAT ANSWER HERE TOO, or the E3019 rule names methods the
		// receiver does not have** (found in review, R4.2). Ungated, `let a = IntArray.create()` followed by
		// `a.setLength(2)` reported `E3019 cannot pass 'a' to function that mutates parameter 'self'` — a
		// MUTABILITY complaint about a method an `Array` has no dispatch arm for; the same program with `var`
		// correctly reports the unknown-method E2015. The program is refused either way, but the two spellings
		// must be refused for the same reason, and the reason is that the method does not exist.
		let mutatesReceiver = arrayMethodMutatesReceiver(methodTok.value, isManagedMemory: isManagedMemory)

		// ⭐⭐ **A BUFFER HAS NO IMMUTABLE-BINDING RULE, AND THAT IS THE REFERENCE'S SIGNATURE RATHER THAN A
		// RELAXATION shv2 CHOSE.** Both references declare every `__ManagedMemory` member on a PLAIN `self`
		// (v1 `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:1618-1660`, bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:1470-1512`) where `stdlib/Array.maxon`
		// declares `push`/`set`/`resize` by REFERENCE — so a `let`-bound BUFFER is writable through and a
		// `let`-bound ARRAY is not. The corpus depends on it in both directions:
		// `specs/managed-memory-builtin.md` writes `let mm = try __ManagedMemory.create(…)` and then
		// `mm.setLength(3)` (E3019 would refuse ten of its eleven cases), while `specs-shv2/arrays.md`'s
		// `error.let-array-*` cases require the ARRAY refusal to stand.
		// ⇒ The exemption is spelled as the EMPTY blame name — the same answer `valueReceiver` gives for a
		// temporary, meaning "no binding may be blamed for a write through this receiver" — rather than as a
		// second `mutatesReceiver` value, because the write is REAL: E3070 and the storage-written mask below
		// must still see it, and they read `mutatesReceiver`.
		try self.noteReceiverWrite(func, immutableReceiverName: EmptySourceText if isManagedMemory else immutableReceiverName, receiverOwner: receiverOwner, receiverStorage: receiverStorage, methodTok: methodTok, mutatesReceiver: mutatesReceiver)

		// ⭐ E3070's container door — the SAME `mutatesReceiver` answer, read once, asked of the SAME receiver at
		// the same instant. The rule above refuses a write through an immutable binding; this one records the
		// write it permits, against the borrows still outstanding on that storage (`noteBorrowSubjectWrite`).
		self.noteBorrowSubjectWrite(func, receiver: arr, subjectName: borrowSubjectName, receiverStorage: receiverStorage, display: methodTok.value, anchor: methodTok, mutatesSubject: mutatesReceiver)

		// ⭐⭐ **`.managed` IS AN IDENTITY AT EVERY ELEMENT TYPE (R4.1, ungated by R4.4).**
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon:106` declares `export var managed as ElementMemory` — `__ManagedMemory with
		// Element` — and shv2 binds that to the array's OWN instance
		// (`SignatureIndex.ManagedMemoryTypeName`), so `arr.managed` yields the receiver unchanged whatever
		// the element is: no conversion, no allocation, no runtime call.
		//
		// R4.1 guarded it to the byte instance because the alias was byte-only, and that guard's own comment
		// called its refusal "a false statement about the language". It is gone: `array-slots.md`'s four cases
		// reach `.managed` on an `Array with Slot` (a STRUCT element), `managed-memory-methods.md`'s seven on
		// an `Array with Int` and its eighth on an `Array with String`.
		//
		// ⚠ It is a FIELD read, not a call: the corpus writes no parentheses. So this arm never consumes an
		// argument list; routing it through the call arms below would demand a `(` the source does not write.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ARM IS THE `Array`'s ALONE (D11b): a buffer has no `managed` member and the roster gate above
		// refuses one**, so `mm.managed.setByte(0, 65)` — a second door onto the buffer's own mutators through
		// a member the buffer does not have — is `error.buffer-has-no-managed-field` rather than an identity
		// on an identity. What re-enters here still arrives with `viaManagedField: true`, which is the ONE hop
		// the source wrote it on.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **A `.member(` AFTER IT RE-ENTERS THIS DISPATCH WITH THE BUFFER SURFACE, RATHER THAN RETURNING
		// AND LETTING `parsePostfix` DISPATCH THE HOP.** The two are not interchangeable: `.managed` is an
		// identity, so the value `parsePostfix` would carry is the ARRAY's own — indistinguishable from a bare
		// `arr` — and `arr.managed.setByte(0, 65)` would arrive as `arr.setByte(0, 65)`, which the language
		// does not give a `ByteArray`. The surface is a fact about what the SOURCE wrote here, and it is
		// handed to the one dispatch that fact reaches.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryFieldName) 'managedField'
			if not self.memberCallFollows(self.pos) 'bufferAsAValue'
				return self.bufferSurfaceOf(arr, range: range)
			end 'bufferAsAValue'

			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
			return try self.dispatchArrayMethod(func, arr: arr, giid: giid, resultUsed: resultUsed, immutableReceiverName: immutableReceiverName, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, receiverOwner: receiverOwner, receiverStorage: receiverStorage, viaManagedField: true)
		end 'managedField'

		// Throwing accessors — a plain `call` the enclosing `try` rewrites; result is the element (get/first/
		// last/pop/remove) or a discarded dummy (set). `resultUsed` is the try's concern, not checked here.
		// ⭐ get/first/last yield a BORROW of the element (the array keeps ownership — the caller must not drop
		// it); pop/remove MOVE the element OUT (the runtime nulls its slot), so their result is OWNED (P1.7 slice 3).
		if methodTok.value.equals(GetMethod) 'get'
			let index = try self.parseArrayOneArg(func)
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ManagedGetName, container: arr, args: pairArgs(arr, second: index), giid: giid, reference: ContainerElementReference.borrowed, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, range: range)
		end 'get'
		// ⭐⭐ **ONE SPELLING, TWO ENTRIES — THE BUFFER'S `set` IS BOUNDED BY CAPACITY AND THE `Array`'s BY LENGTH
		// (R4.6).** ⚖ The capacity bound is a USER RULING of 2026-07-30, taken after a measured three-way
		// contradiction the runnable oracle could not arbitrate; `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedMemSetName` carries the ruling,
		// its date and the argument that settles it (a length bound forbids the exact stage-then-publish idiom
		// canonical `setLength`'s own contract prescribes). **`specs/managed-memory-methods.md:21` says LENGTH and
		// is treated as stale — do not "fix" this back toward it.**
		//
		// ⚠ **`Array.set` MUST NOT MOVE**: `specs/arrays.md` and the array corpus are written against the length
		// bound, and the record is shared — so the surface answer that already decides this call's LABEL rule
		// decides its CALLEE too, and there is no second discriminator.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **ARR3a AND ARR1 BOTH TRIED TO RETIRE THE `Array` HALF AND BOTH PUT IT BACK — AND THE SECOND
		// ATTEMPT RETIRED THE FIRST ONE'S REASON ALONG WITH ITS OWN HOPE.** ARR3a's blocker was the RANGE CHECK
		// (the corpus declares `value Element`, so the shared callee knows no range); ARRR closed exactly that,
		// at the call site, and ARR1 verified struck that BOTH halves arrive — the E3005 on a literal and the
		// `Range check failed` panic on a computed 505 — from `recordOpaqueArgRangeChecks` with no arm involved.
		// The LENGTH bound is likewise the corpus body's own now (`stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `set`, whose missing
		// guard ARR1 measured as a LEAK and repaired there, in the body the bootstrap shares).
		//
		// ⛔ **WHAT REFUSES IS THE RECEIVER, NOT `set`.** An `Array with <a parameter of the enclosing generic>`
		// cannot reach a corpus body at all: `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s own slot columns PANIC the compiler
		// (*"primitiveTypeByteSize: a `typeParameter`'s size is a runtime layout-descriptor read"*, from
		// `MaxonToStdLowering.materializeLayoutArgFor` needing a per-instance descriptor for `Array with Key`),
		// 34 `map` cases plus four more; three further cases are REFUSED outright because
		// `memberBelongsToTheCorpus` does not admit that receiver; and five `generic-opaque-element-loop-store`
		// cases lose W58's `coOwnBorrowedOpaque`, which lives in `moveElementIntoContainer` and has no
		// counterpart on the ordinary caller-consume path. `arraySurfaceMemberNames`' `set` bullet carries the
		// full measurement. Until an opaque-element receiver can name a corpus body, this arm is how a `Map`
		// writes its own slots.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE CALLEE IS PICKED INTO A `var` BECAUSE THE BOOTSTRAP CANNOT LOWER IT AS A CONDITIONAL
		// EXPRESSION HERE.** Written `callee: ManagedMemSetName if isManagedMemory else ManagedSetName` — the shape
		// `noteReceiverWrite`'s call above already uses — the bootstrap fails the whole build with
		// `error E9001: Lowering function 'Compiler.Parser.dispatchArrayMethod' failed: assign value %97103
		// (kind=MaxonInteger) not in valueMap; assigning to '__arg_pin_27'`. ⚠ **No MECHANISM is claimed and
		// none should be inferred from this note**: the obvious hypotheses were tested against the bootstrap
		// and REFUTED — a conditional `ByteArray` argument lowers fine on its own, twice in one function, in a
		// labelled position, and under a `try`. Whatever the trigger is, it needs this function. Reverting
		// these four lines to the one-line expression reproduces it exactly.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **`set` IS A VOID MUTATOR TOO — IT SITS HERE ONLY BECAUSE IT THROWS, AND IT NEEDS BOTH HALVES OF
		// THE REFUSAL (found in review of D11).** The two halves cover DISJOINT positions and neither can do
		// the other's job: this guard answers the BARE spelling (`let x = arr.set(…)`, where `resultUsed` is
		// TRUE at the arm), and the honest `void` result tag `parseArraySet` mints answers the `try`
		// spelling (`let x = try arr.set(…) otherwise …`, where the target is parsed `resultUsed: false` and
		// only `parseTry`'s `voidInValue` can see it). The three buffer mutators below have carried both since
		// R4.4; `set` had NEITHER, which is why it compiled and ran.
		// ⇒ **MEASURED, and it is why the guard cannot be dropped in favour of the tag alone**: with the tag
		// honest and this line absent, `let x = arr.set(0, value: 7)` reaches `declareInitializedBinding` and
		// PANICS the compiler — *"maxonTypeOfTag: a `void` tag names no value"* — because SemanticCheck's
		// bare-throwing-call E3057 runs a whole pass too late to protect the parser from itself.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ArraySetMethod) 'set'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			var setCallee = ManagedSetName
			if isManagedMemory 'capacityBoundedSetter'
				try self.requireBufferSetOwnsWhatItStores(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
				setCallee = ManagedMemSetName
			end 'capacityBoundedSetter'

			return try self.parseArraySet(func, arr: arr, giid: giid, callee: setCallee, methodTok: methodTok, secondArgLabelled: not isManagedMemory, range: range)
		end 'set'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ArrayFirstMethod) 'first'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ManagedFirstName, container: arr, args: singletonArgs(arr), giid: giid, reference: ContainerElementReference.borrowed, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, range: range)
		end 'first'
		// ⭐ **`remove` IS BUFFER-SURFACE ONLY SINCE X-array-retire, AND THE GATE IS WHAT SAYS SO.** The
		// `Array` spelling is `stdlib/Array.maxon:327`'s, which is this same operation through
		// `managed.remove(at)` — one runtime, reached from source instead of from an arm. The gate is
		// unreachable-by-roster (the buffer roster carries the name and the `Array` one no longer does) and
		// is written anyway, exactly as `byteAt`/`elementSize`/`grow` write theirs: an arm that can be
		// reached on a surface the member does not belong to is D11's defect, and the gate is the only thing
		// that states the surface at the arm rather than only in a list.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(RemoveMethod) 'remove'
			let index = try self.parseArrayOneArg(func)
			return self.emitContainerElementAccessor(func, callee: ManagedRemoveName, container: arr, args: pairArgs(arr, second: index), giid: giid, reference: ContainerElementReference.movedOut, borrowSubjectName: borrowSubjectName, range: range)
		end 'remove'
		// ⭐ **`slice` IS BUFFER-SURFACE ONLY SINCE ARR3a**, for `remove`'s reason above and by its gate. The
		// `Array` spelling is `stdlib/Array.maxon:229`'s, whose body is this same `managed.slice(…)` wrapped in
		// a `Self{managed: …}` — one runtime, reached from source. Its RESULT keeps everything the arm gave it:
		// the receiver's instance and the receiver's element surface both ride the `Self` return
		// (`retypeInstanceMethodResult`), which is where a corpus body gets what `inheritArrayElementSurface`
		// hands the arm below.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(SliceMethod) 'slice'
			try self.requireArrayCopyMethodSupported(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			return try self.parseArraySlice(func, arr: arr, giid: giid, range: range)
		end 'slice'

		// ⭐ **`byteAt(index)` — THE BUFFER'S BYTE READER (R4.4)**, sitting with the throwing accessors because
		// it YIELDS a value, where its `setByte` dual is void and sits with the mutators. Its bound is
		// `length · element_size` and it throws `invalidByteRange`, both of which live in one place with the
		// setter's (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitByteRangeGuard`).
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ByteAtMethod) 'byteAt'
			try self.requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			let index = try self.parseOneBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, paramName: ManagedMemoryIndexParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedByteAtName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: index), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'byteAt'

		// Non-throwing value accessors.
		// ⭐ `__ManagedMemory.length()` IS `Array.count()` — both read `length@8` and nothing else — so the
		// name is folded onto this arm rather than given a second, byte-identical emission of its own (R4.2).
		// `setLength`/`setByte` below are deliberately NOT folded onto `resize`/`set`: those two pairs differ
		// (see the `__ManagedMemory` mutators).
		// ⚠ **WHAT IS FOLDED IS THE EMISSION, NEVER THE SURFACE (D11b).** `count` is the `Array`'s spelling and
		// `length` is the buffer's, so `mm.count()` is refused by the roster gate above and only the second
		// disjunct here is reachable on a buffer — sharing an arm must not hand a receiver a name the
		// references never gave it, or the roster's own first entry becomes decorative.
		if methodTok.value.equals(CountMethod) or (isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryLengthMethod)) 'count'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCountName, args: singletonArgs(arr), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'count'
		// ⭐ **`toCString()` — BUFFER-SURFACE ONLY**, like `length`/`byteAt`/`elementSize`: neither reference
		// gives `Array` the spelling, and the corpus reaches it through `arr.managed.toCString()`. The result is
		// a raw address, so it is `integer`-tagged exactly as `cstring` resolves in a type position — one
		// answer to "what is a NUL-terminated pointer?", given at the type door and at the value door.
		// ⭐⭐ **`makeCharFromBytes(pos, len)` — THE CORPUS'S GRAPHEME MINT, SERVED BY THE DOOR THAT ALREADY
		// EXISTS.** `__char_at(record, startPos, endPos)` is precisely this operation: *"a fresh OWNED record
		// holding a COPY of the bytes `[startPos, endPos)`"*, built through the one fused-record encoder, so a
		// Character minted here is byte-for-byte the one `for c in s` mints and drops through `__str_decref`
		// identically. Writing a second minting graph for the corpus's spelling is exactly the twin this
		// convergence exists to remove.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE CORPUS PASSES A LENGTH AND `__char_at` TAKES AN END, so the arm ADDS.** End-EXCLUSIVE, which
		// is what `pos + len` is — the corpus's own contract is *"callers must guarantee `pos + len <=
		// byteLength()`"*. Getting this backwards would read one byte too few from every multi-byte cluster,
		// silently.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **BOTH ARE GATED ON STDLIB PROVENANCE, AND THAT IS THE VISIBILITY THEIR CORPUS DECLARATIONS
		// ALREADY CARRY** — see `requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod` for the two measured failures that made the
		// omission a memory-safety hole rather than a widened surface.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryMakeCharFromBytesMethod) 'makeCharFromBytes'
			try self.requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod(methodTok)
			// ⛔⛔ **`__char_at` READS `singleByteGraphemes@48`, WHICH ONLY A BYTE RECORD HAS (W157).** A plain
			// 48-byte buffer record ends at `@48`, so serving this member one is an eight-byte read PAST the
			// record — into the next allocation's header, or off the end of the last one. Before W157 the same
			// mis-service read `TrivialDestructor` as the flag: a conservative wrong answer rather than a wild
			// read, which is why the whitelist's census could merely NOTE it.
			//
			// ⚠ **A PANIC, NOT A DIAGNOSTIC, AND `ProgramSignatures.checkFusedWrapperRecordLayouts` IS THE
			// PRECEDENT.** Nothing a user program can express reaches it — the line above refuses this member
			// outside `stdlib/`, and the corpus's ONE call site is `String.makeCharFromBytes`'s
			// `managed.makeCharFromBytes(…)`, an implicit-`self` fused read that `dispatchMethodOnBinding`
			// routes here with the String's own record. So what could fire it is an edit to a LISTED corpus
			// module, and there is no degraded answer available: the alternative to stopping is minting a
			// Character out of whatever byte followed the record.
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKS THE RECEIVER'S TAG, NOT THE ROUTE THE SOURCE WROTE — AND IT READ `viaManagedField`
			// UNTIL W194, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION.** That flag means *"the source spelled `managed`"*,
			// and BOTH doors that set it could set it over a 48-byte buffer record: the fused door admitted
			// `Array` and `Vector` before W194's gate, and the `.managed` re-entry below still turns any array
			// receiver into one. A `stdlib/` line reading `a.managed.makeCharFromBytes(0, 1)` therefore passed
			// a guard whose whole subject is the seventh slot. `tagIsByteRecord` is the representation
			// question itself — the one `TypeRules` keeps apart from `tagIsText` for exactly this class of
			// mistake — so the guard is now true of what the record IS rather than of how it was reached.
			//
			// ⛔ **MEASURED DIRECTLY AT THE W194 REVIEW, ON THE SPELLING ITSELF RATHER THAN ON A PROXY.** A
			// file placed under `stdlib/` (so `livesUnderStdlibDirectory` holds and
			// `requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod` admits the member) whose whole body is `var a = Ints.create()`
			// + `a.push(1)` + `let c = a.managed.makeCharFromBytes(0, 1)` **COMPILED CLEAN on the base, exit
			// 0, and RAN** — `__char_at` handed an `Array with Int`'s 48-byte record and reading eight bytes
			// past its end. The same file panics here. That is the hole, and it was never a hypothetical.
			//
			// ⚠ **AND THE TIGHTENING COSTS NOTHING ON THE BYTE-RECORD SIDE, WHICH WAS THE HALF WORTH
			// DOUBTING.** Five stdlib-located spellings that DO carry a genuine byte record were compiled on
			// both binaries and answer IDENTICALLY: bare `managed.makeCharFromBytes(…)` inside an
			// `extension String` (served, both), `self.managed.…`, `let b = managed` then `b.…`,
			// `s.addressableBytes().…`, and `b.managed.…` — the last four already refused or panicking on the
			// base for their own reasons. The probe DISCRIMINATES rather than merely reporting zero: the
			// `Array` spelling above is the positive control, and it is the one that moved.
			if not tagIsByteRecord(self.tagOf(arr)) 'notAByteRecord'
				panic("dispatchArrayMethod: `{String.from(ManagedMemoryMakeCharFromBytesMethod)}` reached a receiver that is NOT a fused byte-record wrapper's own `{String.from(InlineManagedFieldName)}` — `__char_at` loads `singleByteGraphemes` at {StringSingleByteGraphemesOffset}, which is one slot PAST a {ManagedRecordBytes}-byte buffer record. Reach it through a `{String.from(StringConformerName)}`/`{String.from(CharacterConformerName)}` receiver, or give `__char_at` the flag another way")
			end 'notAByteRecord'

			let window = try self.parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, firstParam: ManagedMemoryCharPosParamName, secondParam: ManagedMemoryCharLenParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			let startPos = try window.get(0) otherwise panic("makeCharFromBytes: parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList returned fewer than the two arguments it consumes")
			let byteLen = try window.get(1) otherwise panic("makeCharFromBytes: parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList returned fewer than the two arguments it consumes")
			return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(CharAtName, args: tripleArgs(arr, second: startPos, third: self.emitMachineWordAdd(startPos, rhs: byteLen, range: range)), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.character, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'makeCharFromBytes'
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryToCStringMethod) 'toCString'
			try self.requireStdlibOnlyBufferMethod(methodTok)
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(MmToCStringName, args: singletonArgs(arr), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'toCString'
		// Buffer-surface only since X-array-retire, for `remove`'s reason: `Array.capacity()` is
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon:157`'s `managed.capacity()`, which is this arm reached from source.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ArrayCapacityMethod) 'capacity'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedCapacityName, args: singletonArgs(arr), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'capacity'
		// ⭐ **`elementSize()` — THE BUFFER'S STRIDE, AND THE ONE MEMBER THAT REPORTS A REPRESENTATION**
		// (`specs/ranged-int-bit-packing.md`, `specs/bool-bit-packing.md`). It is on the BUFFER surface only,
		// like `length`/`byteAt`: `Array` has no such spelling in either reference, and a program reaches it
		// through `arr.managed.elementSize()`.
		//
		// ⚠ The result is a plain `int` and NOT the element's own type: it is a negative bit width for a
		// packed element, which no element's declared range admits, and the specs compare it against `-1`/
		// `-2`/`-4` as ordinary integers.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryElementSizeMethod) 'elementSize'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedElementSizeName, args: singletonArgs(arr), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'elementSize'
		// ⭐ **THE VOID MUTATORS — AND EACH REFUSES VALUE POSITION FROM INSIDE ITS OWN ARM
		// (`requireVoidMethodIsStatement`, whose header carries the D11 measurement).** The refusal used to
		// be one call HERE, ahead of them all, which an UNKNOWN name reached too — so a typo was told the
		// method exists and is void instead of being handed the roster below. It is the first statement of
		// each arm rather than a shared line, so it still beats every argument diagnostic, and it is
		// reachable only once a real void method NAME has matched.
		if methodTok.value.equals(ArrayPushMethod) 'push'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			let value = try self.parseContainerElementArg(func, giid: giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitArrayVoidCall(ManagedPushName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: value), receiver: arr, range: range)
		end 'push'
		if methodTok.value.equals(ArrayResizeMethod) 'resize'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			let n = try self.parseArrayOneArg(func)
			return self.emitArrayVoidCall(ManagedResizeName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: n), receiver: arr, range: range)
		end 'resize'
		// ⭐ **`clear` IS BUFFER-SURFACE ONLY SINCE ARR4**, for `remove`'s reason above and by its gate. The
		// `Array` spelling is `stdlib/Array.maxon:363`'s, whose whole body is this same `managed.clear()` — one
		// runtime, reached from source instead of from an arm. What the strike had to wait for was NOT this
		// arm: it was that a `Bag.wipe()` clearing its own field must still refuse a caller's live borrow, and
		// the fact only reached the caller through `noteReceiverWrite`, which a corpus-served member does not
		// pass. `BorrowCheck.StorageReachEdge` carries it now, and carries the measurement.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ClearMethod) 'clear'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			return self.emitArrayVoidCall(ManagedClearName, args: singletonArgs(arr), receiver: arr, range: range)
		end 'clear'
		// ⭐⭐ **ONE ARM FOR ALL THREE SPELLINGS OF ONE OPERATION — `Array.append`, `Array.appendMemory` AND THE
		// BUFFER'S `append` — BECAUSE THE CORPUS BUILDS EACH OUT OF THE LAST.** `stdlib/Array.maxon:262` is
		// `append(other Self)` whose entire body is `appendMemory(other.managed)` — the array half of `other` is
		// never read — and `:272` is `appendMemory(source ElementMemory)`, whose own body is the buffer's
		// `append`. Post-collapse `ElementMemory` and `Self` are one record, so there is no second operation to
		// emit at any of the three: `parseArrayAppend` copies the source's elements into the receiver's storage
		// at whatever elementhood the receiver has.
		//
		// ⚖ **THE BUFFER USED TO HAVE ITS OWN CALLEE (`__managed_mem_append`), ON THE CLAIM THAT ITS `append`
		// THROWS. USER RULING, 2026-08-07: IT DOES NOT** — both references make it non-throwing, and
		// `stdlib/String.maxon:414` declares a non-throwing `append` over a bare `managed.append(other.managed)`
		// that cannot compile otherwise. The whole argument is at `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedAppendName`.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND THE SPLIT WAS NOT MERELY REDUNDANT, IT WAS A WRONG ANSWER**: the buffer callee byte-blitted
		// whatever the receiver held, so `a.managed.append(b.managed)` on an `Array with String` duplicated each
		// heap pointer and double-freed it (MEASURED: SIGSEGV at teardown). Reaching the shared door instead
		// picks the MANAGED and OPAQUE emissions by element kind, exactly as the `Array` spelling always did —
		// which is what identifies this as one operation the surfaces had come to disagree about.
		//
		// ⚠ **WHAT DOES NOT FOLD IS THE SURFACE**, in either direction:
		//   • The ARGUMENT SENTENCE stays per-surface (`parseArrayAppend`'s `bufferSurface`): the buffer's
		//     parameter is declared `__ManagedMemory`, so a mismatch names that type, where `Array.append`'s
		//     names the receiver's own instance. `builtin-type-checking.error-managed-memory-append-int` pins
		//     the first and `arrays.error.append-narrower-element-array` the second. One rule
		//     (`ProgramSignatures.arrayAppendArgAdmits`), two sentences.
		//   • `appendMemory` remains an `Array` NAME and only an `Array` name — the `length`/`count`
		//     arrangement. The corpus declares it on `Array` and on nothing else, so a buffer receiver asking
		//     for it is refused by the roster gate above and never reaches here.
		if methodTok.value.equals(AppendMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(AppendMemoryMethod) 'append'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.requireArrayCopyMethodSupported(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			return try self.parseArrayAppend(func, arr: arr, giid: giid, methodTok: methodTok, bufferSurface: isManagedMemory, range: range)
		end 'append'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO `__ManagedMemory` MUTATORS (R4.2) — `setLength` and `setByte`.** VOID and THROWING,
		// which is why they sit with the void mutators (each opening with `requireVoidMethodIsStatement`,
		// so a value-position use is the void-result refusal) rather than with the throwing ACCESSORS
		// above, which all yield a value.
		//
		// ⚠ **NEITHER IS AN ALIAS OF THE `Array` METHOD IT RESEMBLES, and folding either would be a silent
		// wrong answer.** `resize` GROWS the capacity to fit where `setLength` must REFUSE a length above it —
		// `stdlib/Array.maxon:378-382` builds `resize` out of `reserve` THEN `setLength` for exactly that
		// reason — and `set` addresses ELEMENTS where `setByte` addresses BYTES, which coincide at element
		// size 1 and at no other size. See `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedSetLengthName`.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THEY GO THROUGH `emitRuntimeCall`, NOT `emitArrayVoidCall`, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE.**
		// `emitArrayVoidCall` returns the RECEIVER as the expression's value, which is right for the
		// NON-throwing mutators around it (`arr.push(x)` yields something for a statement to be). Under a
		// `try` it makes the TRY's value the array — an owned managed record — and the try's ok/error
		// reconciliation then acts on it: MEASURED as an access violation on the first `setByte` of a freshly
		// created buffer. `parseArraySet` reaches `emitRuntimeCall` for the identical reason — it is throwing on
		// every surface that still has it, which is what let ARR1 delete the flag that used to choose.
		//
		// ⭐ **R4.4 CLOSED THE HOLE THIS BLOCK USED TO NAME.** R4.2's note here read "the cost of the R4.1
		// binding is that all three are visible on a user's `ByteArray` too, which the language does not give
		// it" — true while the gate was an ELEMENT test, because a `b"…"` literal and a
		// `__ManagedMemory.create(n, 1)` were the same instance. The gate is now a PROVENANCE test
		// (`isManagedMemory` above), so `"ab".toByteArray().setLength(0)` is the ordinary unknown-`Array`-method
		// E2015 and only a real buffer reaches these arms.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemorySetLengthMethod) 'setLength'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			let newLength = try self.parseOneBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, paramName: ManagedMemoryNewLengthParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedSetLengthName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: newLength), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'setLength'
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemorySetByteMethod) 'setByte'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			// ⭐⭐ **THE RAW-BYTE WRITER ANSWERS TO ITS SLOT, AT EVERY STRIDE (E3118, A4c).** `setByte`
			// addresses a byte OFFSET, so on a record striding more than a byte it writes a FRAGMENT of an
			// element — and what the ARRAY surface reads back afterwards is an arbitrary unsigned value of
			// that element's whole SLOT. One question, one rule, whatever the width.
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **A4a ASKED "DOES THE ELEMENT HOLD EVERY BYTE" HERE BEHIND A STRIDE PRECONDITION, AND ITS
			// OWN HEADER CALLED THAT A BOUNDARY RATHER THAN A PROOF.** It was: the skip let through four
			// silent wrong answers measured on that tree — `typealias Wide = int(0 to 1000)` reading back
			// **57089**, `int(-5 to 5)` reading back **223** (the unsigned ladder keeps it a machine word
			// without filling one), a `bool` element for which `v` and `not v` were BOTH true, and an ENUM
			// element on which the program compiled and then HUNG on an ordinal no arm names. The stride is
			// therefore no longer a precondition: it is an INPUT to the one rule
			// (`ProgramSignatures.elementAdmitsEverySlotValue`), which asks `rangeHoldsEveryByte` at a
			// one-byte slot and the slot's own value set above it.
			//
			// ⚠⚠ **THAT IS NOT E3117 WIDENED, AND WIDENING IT IS THE MEASURED WRONG ANSWER IN THE OTHER
			// DIRECTION.** "Holds every byte" asked at a wider slot refuses `Array with Int` — three
			// `managed-memory-methods` cases stopped compiling when A4a's first cut did exactly that,
			// `IntArray.create()` staging a low byte told about a range a machine word obviously holds. E3117
			// keeps the two fills that ARE byte-per-element by contract (`parseByteView`, whose fill is
			// element-wise at whatever stride, and `__ManagedFile.read`, whose argument gate has already
			// established a byte-packed buffer); this site left it because its construct never asked that
			// question except at one width.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE PREREQUISITE THIS ROW WAITED ON IS SETTLED ONE DOOR AWAY**: a
			// `__ManagedMemory.create(n, 1)` in a file whose `Byte` is two bytes was TYPED stride 2 over a
			// record stamped stride 1 (`set(0, value: 300)` read back **44**) while the same file's `b"…"`
			// literal was already refused for the identical incoherence. Both producers now go through
			// `requireBytePackedRecordStridesLikeThisFilesByte`, so no receiver reaching here disagrees with
			// its own record about its stride — which is what makes a slot question answerable at all.
			//
			// ⚠ Its `byteAt` dual two arms up is untouched whatever the slot — it yields a plain `int`,
			// never the element — which is the one place this rule and E3110's pair part company; see
			// `requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement`.
			try self.requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot(giid, methodTok: methodTok)

			// The RECEIVER is argument 0, so the two-int list's array is prepended to rather than rebuilt —
			// the arg-list protocol stays in its one home and the receiver is added where it belongs.
			var args = try self.parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, firstParam: ManagedMemoryOffsetParamName, secondParam: ManagedMemoryValueParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			args.insert(0, value: arr)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedSetByteName, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'setByte'

		// ⭐ **`grow(newCapacity)` — THE BUFFER'S CAPACITY RAISER (R4.4).** Void and throwing, so it belongs
		// with these two and takes `emitRuntimeCall` for their reason: under a `try`, `emitArrayVoidCall` would
		// make the try's value the owned array record and the reconciliation would act on it (measured as an
		// access violation, see above).
		//
		// ⚠ It is NOT `reserve` under another name: `reserve` GROWS by the doubling policy and never
		// complains, `grow` raises the capacity to EXACTLY what it is asked for and THROWS `invalidCapacity`
		// when asked to lower it. `specs/managed-memory-builtin.md:grow` asserts the exact capacity.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryGrowMethod) 'grow'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			let newCapacity = try self.parseOneBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, paramName: ManagedMemoryNewCapacityParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedMemGrowName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: newCapacity), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'grow'

		// ⭐⭐ **`fill(start, count:, value:)` — THE WHOLE-WINDOW ELEMENT WRITE, AND THE ONE BUFFER MEMBER THAT
		// ANSWERS A `bool` (EC1).** `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `refill`/`growFilled` call it and keep their
		// per-element loop as the arm it DECLINES; see `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedFillName` for why a
		// managed element is declined rather than served, and `buildManagedFill` for the window's bounds.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THE VALUE IS TYPE-CHECKED AND THEN LEFT ALONE — `moveElementIntoContainer` MUST NOT RUN HERE,
		// AND CALLING IT WOULD BE A LEAK ON THE ONE PATH THIS MEMBER EXISTS FOR.** Every other element-store
		// door moves the value in, because the container becomes its owner; this one stores it only when the
		// element is TRIVIAL, which owns nothing. For a managed or opaque element the move-in would take a
		// reference (`coOwnBorrowedOpaque` inside the shared `Array` body, W58) that the runtime's decline
		// then never stores — and the value the caller still holds is the one the fallback loop is about to
		// take its own references through.
		//
		// ⚠ `containerElementWord` still runs, and it is the half that is NOT ownership: a `float` element
		// travels as its 64 bits, and the read back (`emitContainerElementAccessor`) reverses exactly this
		// conversion. Skipping it would put a double in a slot every reader takes for a bit pattern.
		//
		// ⚠ **NO MANAGED-ELEMENT GATE, unlike `set`'s E3109.** That refusal is about a write landing in the
		// UNOWNED staging region `[length, capacity)`; this window is bounded by the LIVE length and stores
		// nothing at all for a managed element, so there is no ownership for it to get wrong — and `refill`
		// on an `Array with String` is precisely the program that has to reach here.
		//
		// ⚠ It takes `emitRuntimeCall` rather than `emitArrayVoidCall` for the reason `setLength` states, and
		// needs no `requireVoidMethodIsStatement`: the call HAS a value, and it is the answer the caller must
		// read.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryFillMethod) 'fill'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
			let start = try self.parseBuiltinIntegerArg(func, paramName: ManagedMemoryStartParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consumeArrayArgLabel(ManagedMemoryCountParamName, required: false)
			let count = try self.parseBuiltinIntegerArg(func, paramName: ManagedMemoryCountParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consumeArrayArgLabel(ManagedMemoryValueParamName, required: false)
			let valueStart = self.pos
			let raw = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
			let checked = try self.requireContainerElementType(func, giid: giid, value: raw, valueStart: valueStart, methodTok: methodTok)
			try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
			let element = self.containerElementWord(checked, giid: giid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(self.tokenAt(valueStart)))
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedFillName, args: quadArgs(arr, second: start, third: count, fourth: element), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'fill'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE RELOCATORS — void, throwing, two integer arguments each, and BUFFER-SURFACE ONLY.**
		// Neither reference gives `Array` any of these spellings: the corpus reaches them through
		// `managed.swap`/`managed.shiftRight` from inside `stdlib/Array.maxon` and
		// `stdlib/helpers/sort/smallSort.maxon`, and the `Array` methods built ON them (`insert`, `remove`,
		// `Array.swap`) are the surface a program is meant to see.
		//
		// ⚠ **THEY TAKE `emitRuntimeCall` FOR `grow`'s MEASURED REASON**: under a `try`, `emitArrayVoidCall`
		// would make the try's value the owned array record and the reconciliation would act on it (an access
		// violation — see the note above `setLength`).
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **NO MANAGED-ELEMENT GATE, AND THAT IS THE GROUP'S DEFINING PROPERTY RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**
		// `set` is refused for a managed element (E3109) because it takes OWNERSHIP of a value it was handed,
		// into a slot above `length` that nobody owns; `setByte`/`byteAt` are refused (E3110) because they read
		// or write a pointer as though it were data. A relocator does neither: it moves words the buffer
		// ALREADY OWNS between slots the buffer already owns, so the live multiset — and every element's
		// refcount — is invariant across it and the element's kind never enters the question.
		// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedMemSwapName`'s header carries that argument in full, and it is the same one that
		// makes `swap` unbuildable out of `get` + `set`.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemorySwapMethod) 'swap'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			// The RECEIVER is argument 0, so the two-int list is prepended to rather than rebuilt — the
			// arg-list protocol stays in its one home, exactly as `setByte` above does it.
			var swapArgs = try self.parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, firstParam: ManagedMemorySwapFirstParamName, secondParam: ManagedMemorySwapSecondParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			swapArgs.insert(0, value: arr)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(ManagedMemSwapName, args: swapArgs, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'swap'
		if isManagedMemory and (methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryShiftRightMethod) or methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryShiftLeftMethod)) 'shift'
			try self.requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed, methodTok: methodTok)
			// ⚠ ONE ARM FOR THE PAIR, because they differ ONLY in the DIRECTION — same two parameters, same
			// labels, same capacity bound, same thrown case. The direction IS the callee, so it is chosen here
			// and nothing else in the arm branches on it; two arms would have been one argument protocol
			// written twice.
			let shiftCallee = ManagedMemShiftRightName if methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryShiftRightMethod) else ManagedMemShiftLeftName
			var shiftArgs = try self.parseTwoBuiltinIntegerArgList(func, firstParam: ManagedMemoryIndexParamName, secondParam: ManagedMemoryCountParamName, methodTok: methodTok)
			shiftArgs.insert(0, value: arr)
			return self.emitRuntimeCall(shiftCallee, args: shiftArgs, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'shift'

		// ⭐⭐ **`createCursor()` — THE BUFFER'S ONE PRODUCER OF A `__ManagedMemoryCursor with Element`.**
		// Throwing (an EMPTY buffer has no valid position to start at) and OWNED: the cursor is its own
		// `__mm_alloc` box holding a RETAINED source, so it is tracked as an owned temp and drops at scope exit
		// through `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` → `__managed_cursor_decref`, exactly as a `.create()`d container
		// drops through `__managed_decref`.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE RESULT'S ELEMENT IS DERIVED FROM THE RECEIVER'S INSTANCE, NOT RE-INFERRED**
		// (`managedMemoryCursorInstanceFor`), so a `current()` hands back the element the buffer actually
		// holds and the two cannot disagree about what that is.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE CURSOR IS NOT MARKED AS THE BUFFER SURFACE, and that is the one thing a reader of the
		// `slice` arm above might expect.** A slice of a buffer IS a buffer — same record, same members — so
		// its surface rides the value. A cursor is a DIFFERENT record with a disjoint member list, and it
		// carries its surface in its TYPE (`MemberSurface.cursor`, reached off the instance) rather than in
		// the provenance table, because unlike the buffer it does not share a `GenericInstanceId` with anything.
		if isManagedMemory and methodTok.value.equals(ManagedMemoryCreateCursorMethod) 'createCursor'
			try self.consumeEmptyArgList()
			let cursor = self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(ManagedCursorCreateName, args: singletonArgs(arr), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: self.signatures.managedMemoryCursorInstanceFor(giid), range: range)

			// ⭐⭐ **A CURSOR IS A STANDING BORROW OF ITS SOURCE, AND E3070 IS WHERE THAT IS ENFORCED.** ⚖ USER
			// RULING (PLAN `S2l`, 2026-08-06): *"an array cursor locks its source."* The retain in
			// `__managed_cursor_create` keeps the record ALIVE, which is a different guarantee from keeping it
			// UNCHANGED — MEASURED on this tree before this line: `let c = try xs.managed.createCursor()`
			// followed by `xs.clear()` and `c.current()` compiled clean and faulted with **0xC0000005**, because
			// `clear` releases every element and zeroes its slot while the cursor still names position 0. The
			// trivial-element twin does not crash and is just as wrong — it reports the zero left behind as an
			// element the array no longer has.
			//
			// ⭐ **IT IS THE BORROW SET AND NOT A FOURTH LOCK, and the borrow set is the STRONGER of the two
			// here.** The lock a `for … in` takes is LEXICAL — it holds for a syntactic body — where a borrow
			// holds until the borrower's LAST USE (`attachPendingBorrows` seeds `lastUseToken`). For a cursor
			// that is the more precise extent in both directions: a cursor BINDING outlives any body, so a
			// lexical lock keyed on a block would expire while the cursor was still readable; and
			// `let c = …; print(c.current()); xs.clear()` is SAFE and a lexical lock would refuse it. Nothing
			// new is built, and a cursor answers the same E3070 an outstanding `get` borrow already does.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS MINTED WHATEVER THE ELEMENT TYPE, where `emitContainerElementAccessor` mints only for a
			// MANAGED one.** That gate is right for an element read — a trivial element is a copied WORD that
			// cannot dangle — and wrong here, because what the cursor holds is a position into the CONTAINER,
			// and a container can be emptied under a position whatever its elements are.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **THE LOCK USED TO BE ONE HOP DEEP AND IS NOT ANY MORE (ARR-borrow).** This block read *"a
			// value read OUT of a cursor borrows the CURSOR, and the borrow set does not compose — so after the
			// cursor's own last use its lock on the source expires while that value still points inside it:
			// `let s = c.current()` then `xs.clear()` then `print(s)` faults"*, and called it a rung rather than
			// a line. It was right about the fault — **0xC0000005, reproduced on that exact program before the
			// cure** — and the rung is done: a borrow minted on storage that is ITSELF borrowed now mints one on
			// its base too, up to the root (`BorrowCheck.composePendingBorrowOntoBases`, called from the ONE
			// mint). Nothing here changed; the cursor's borrow simply became a link in a chain rather than the
			// end of one. `borrow-liveness.error.a-borrow-taken-out-of-a-borrow-holds-the-root` is that program
			// and `.a-composed-borrow-expires-at-its-own-last-use` is its over-rejection guard.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE NESTED-CONTAINER SHAPE THE OLD NOTE CITED AS THE SAME FAULT IS WRITABLE AGAIN SINCE G18**,
			// which gave a managed-element container its per-instance cloner and retired the refusal that had
			// made this note's *"cannot be written today"* true. The composition rule was already in front of it
			// when it landed, which is what that note was banking on; nothing here changed to let it through.
			//
			// ⚠ **A SOURCE NOTHING CAN NAME MINTS NOTHING, AND THAT IS SAFE FOR A SECOND REASON.**
			// `makeBuffer().managed.createCursor()` has no subject to key a borrow on — and needs none: no
			// other expression can reach that record to write it, and the retain is what stops it being freed
			// (`managed-memory-cursor.the-cursor-keeps-its-source-alive` measures exactly that, across 500
			// intervening allocations).
			if not borrowSubjectName.isEmpty() 'sourceIsNameableStorage'
				self.mintPendingBorrow(func, subjectName: borrowSubjectName, borrowValue: cursor, range: range)
			end 'sourceIsNameableStorage'

			return cursor
		end 'createCursor'

		// ⚠⚠ **NO RECEIVER OF EITHER SURFACE CAN REACH HERE, AND THIS IS THE CHECK THAT SAYS SO** (D11b for
		// the buffer, A2k for the `Array`). The roster gate at the top admitted only the names
		// `surfaceMemberNames` lists, and each of those matches an arm on its own spelling and at most on the
		// surface that gate had already established — so arriving here means a roster gained a member and the
		// dispatch did not. That is D11's defect one level up, and the wrong answer it would otherwise give is
		// unusually convincing: the reader would be handed the roster refusal for a member the roster
		// PROVIDES. It cannot be caught earlier than the call that reveals it (an arm is a control-flow
		// position, not an entry a table can enumerate at install time), so it is caught here, loudly, rather
		// than described in a comment nobody re-reads.
		// ⭐ **VERIFIED RED, once per roster**: pushing `ManagedMemoryCreateMethod` onto the buffer roster and
		// writing `mm.create()` reached this panic in D11b's review; pushing a bogus name onto
		// `arraySurfaceMemberNames` and calling it reached it in A2k's — each naming the member and both
		// remedies, not the roster refusal.
		let drift = rosterDriftMessage("dispatchArrayMethod", member: methodTok.value, roster: roster, rosterFuncs: "`arraySurfaceMemberNames`/`bufferSurfaceMemberNames`")
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'dispatchArrayMethod'

	// ⭐⭐ **REFUSE A RECEIVER-WRITING METHOD ON AN IMMUTABLE RECEIVER (E3019) — ONE RULE, ONE HOME, FOR
	// EVERY CONTAINER.** A read-only method on either kind of receiver, and any method on a writable
	// one, pass straight through.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS SHARED BY `String`, `Array` AND `Set` BECAUSE THE THREE HAD ALREADY DRIFTED APART.**
	// `parseStringAppend` asked `not binding.mutable`, `dispatchArrayMethod` asked `mutable or
	// isParameter`, and the then-existing `dispatchSetMethod` asked NOTHING — three premises for one rule, and two of them
	// disagreed with the runnable oracle, MEASURED:
	//
	//   • `function grow(s String) … s.append("XY")` — the oracle ACCEPTS it (a parameter is a borrowed
	//     reference to the caller's record, exactly as for an array). The `mutable`-only premise made it
	//     a FALSE REJECTION, because a parameter is never `mutable`.
	//   • `let s = IntSet.create() … s.insert(1)` / `s.remove(1)` — the oracle REFUSES both with E3019.
	//     With no premise at all, shv2 accepted them.
	//
	// What stays per-container is only WHICH METHOD NAMES WRITE THE RECEIVER, which is a fact about that
	// container's method table and nothing else; the caller passes it as `mutatesReceiver`. What is
	// shared — the writability of the binding, and the diagnostic — lives here, so a fourth container
	// cannot invent a fourth answer to the same question.
	//
	// ⚠ **`immutableReceiverName` IS ONE `ByteArray`, NOT A UNION, AND THAT IS MEASURED.** The obvious
	// shape is `mutable | immutable(name)` — and it was written that way first — but this runs at every
	// container method call in the program, and Maxon heap-BOXES a union case, INCLUDING a payload-free
	// one. `scale-test` billed it at exactly one box per array-method call site
	// (+120/+240/+480/… across the doubling ladder). This is the same reading, and the same fix, that
	// `BuiltinConformer`'s header records for the per-method-call classifier one rung earlier. It is a
	// `ByteArray` rather than the `VarInfo` for a second reason the array path forces: a receiver that is
	// not a binding at all (`[10,20,30].get(0)`) has no `VarInfo` to hand over.
	//
	// **EMPTY IS NOT A SENTINEL, it is the literal truth**: a writable receiver has no immutable binding
	// for a diagnostic to blame — a `var`, a parameter and a temporary all have nothing to name. The
	// diagnostic REQUIRES a name, so "immutable" and "nameable" are the same state by construction.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT ALSO RECORDS THE WRITE IT PERMITS, AND THE TWO HALVES ARE ONE DECISION.** A write through a
	// receiver that denotes a PARAMETER's record is a mutation of that parameter, which is exactly what the
	// call-site half of E3019 (`SemanticCheck.buildParamMutationSummary`) must know about the enclosing
	// function. Refusing the write and recording it are the same question asked of the same receiver at the
	// same instant — `mutatesReceiver` is the per-container authority's answer and is read ONCE — so they
	// are answered together rather than by two passes that would then have to agree for ever. A summary
	// built by re-reading the LOWERED callee names (`__managed_push`, `__managed_append`, `__list_append`) would be
	// precisely that second list, three tiers away from the one the parser already consults.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT ANSWERS E3019 AND W117, AND IT DELIBERATELY NO LONGER ANSWERS E3070** — see `IrFunction`'s
	// table, which is where the columns are defined. `receiverOwner` is E3019's ("does passing an immutable
	// binding here make it an error?", NO for a self-field write, the ruling `self-keyword.md:self-with-params`
	// pins); `receiverStorage` is which parameter's record this receiver denotes, which W117's union below
	// needs and which E3019 must not read.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE E3070 COLUMN USED TO BE SET HERE, OFF `mutatesReceiver`, AND THAT WAS THE ROOT OF A WHOLE
	// FAMILY OF USE-AFTER-FREES.** E3070's two halves — the same-body site and the cross-function
	// `storageWrittenParamMask` — were then recorded at two doors off two different flags, so a surface whose
	// *"writes the receiver"* and *"invalidates an outstanding borrow"* answers DIFFER got one half and not
	// the other. Both halves now live at `noteBorrowSubjectWrite`, which every caller of this function
	// already calls with the E3070 answer; its header carries the measurement.
	function noteReceiverWrite(func IrFunction, immutableReceiverName ByteArray, receiverOwner ParamPositionMask, receiverStorage ParamPositionMask, methodTok Token, mutatesReceiver bool) throws ParseError
		if not mutatesReceiver 'readOnlyMethod'
			return
		end 'readOnlyMethod'

		if not immutableReceiverName.isEmpty() 'immutableReceiver'
			throw ParseError.immutableMutatingCall(String.from(immutableReceiverName), functionName: String.from(func.name), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'immutableReceiver'

		func.writtenParamMask = func.writtenParamMask or receiverOwner
		// ⭐ **W117's COLUMN TAKES THE UNION OF BOTH**, because it asks the widest of the three questions —
		// "does this body write the record this parameter denotes, at all?" — and each of the other two is
		// narrowed away from it in a different direction (see `IrFunction.writesThroughParamMask`). Both
		// answers are already in hand here, decided about the same receiver at the same instant, so the union
		// costs nothing and cannot fall out of step with either. ⚠ It is keyed on `mutatesReceiver` and NOT on
		// the narrower E3070 answer: `ml.insertLast(x)` frees no node but does write the record, which is
		// exactly the question this column asks.
		func.writesThroughParamMask = func.writesThroughParamMask or receiverOwner or receiverStorage
	end 'noteReceiverWrite'

	// ⭐ **WHICH OF THE ENCLOSING FUNCTION'S PARAMETERS THIS RECEIVER'S RECORD BELONGS TO** — the bits
	// `noteReceiverWrite` sets when the write goes ahead, and 0 for a receiver that denotes storage no
	// parameter owns (a local, a temporary, a top-level global).
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS IS A QUESTION ABOUT THE VALUE, WHERE `VarInfo.isParameter` IS ONE ABOUT THE BINDING, AND THE
	// TWO MUST NOT BE UNIFIED.** `immutableReceiverNameOf` needs `isParameter` because `let a = p` is a
	// `let` even though it carries `p`'s ValueId (E3019, measured). Here the opposite is wanted: `var b = p`
	// followed by `b.push(9)` writes the CALLER's array, so the mutation belongs to `p`. The parameter
	// ValueId convention (`bindParameters` reserves 0..paramCount-1 and `mintValue` starts above it) is what
	// makes "this value IS parameter k" decidable, and it is the right test for THIS question only.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A SELF-FIELD ALIAS OWNS NOTHING HERE, AND THAT IS A RULING, NOT AN OMISSION.** A method writing
	// its own receiver's data does NOT make the CALLER's `let` an error in shv2:
	//
	//   *"A `let` on a struct binding refuses a REBIND (`acc = other`) and a direct field write through it
	//   (`acc.total = 1`, E2013); it does not reach inside the type's own methods."*
	//
	// — the rule `parseSelfFieldAssignment` already states, pinned by `specs-shv2/self-keyword.md`'s
	// `self-with-params`: `let acc = Accumulator.create(0)` then `acc.add(10)` expects **42**, and both
	// compilers deliver it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS A MEASURED DIVERGENCE FROM THE ORACLE, AND THE ORACLE DISAGREES WITH ITSELF.** On the same
	// program with a `let` receiver, `self.total = self.total + value` is ACCEPTED (42) while
	// `total = total + value` is **E3019** — one write, two spellings, two verdicts, because its analysis
	// matches op TYPES and only the bare spelling emits the `MaxonAssignOp` its self-field check inspects.
	// shv2 has exactly ONE self-field store (`emitCheckedSelfFieldStore`, deliberately — see its header on
	// what v1's split cost), so it cannot reproduce that split and must choose one answer for both. It
	// chooses the one the corpus pins.
	//
	// The same ruling covers a CONTAINER held in a field (`items.push(v)` / `self.items.push(v)`, which the
	// oracle refuses through a `let` receiver): there is no principled line between writing `self.total` and
	// writing the array `self.items` points at, and drawing one at the spelling is the very inconsistency
	// above. What a `let`-bound struct still refuses is unchanged — a rebind, a direct field write through
	// it, and a receiver-writing method on it AS a container (E3019, the receiver half, which still refuses
	// a `let` FIELD: `push-on-let-self-field-array-error`).
	//
	// ⚠ The arm is EXPLICIT rather than left to the value test below, because a self-field alias's
	// `boundValue` is 0 — `createSelfField` leaves it so — and 0 is `self`'s own id, so the value test would
	// mark parameter 0 for the wrong reason, off the very coincidence `requireStructBase`'s header records
	// three measured miscompiles from.
	//
	// The value test itself is exact rather than approximate: `bindParameters` reserves ids
	// `0..paramCount-1` for the parameters and `mintValue` starts above them, so no body value can collide
	// with a parameter's id. ⭐ It is `paramMaskOfValue` and no longer inline: E3070's `receiverStorageMask`
	// asks the identical question of the identical value, and so does the self-field STORE door, so what had
	// exactly ONE asker now has three.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE ONE SELF-FIELD THE RULING ABOVE DOES NOT COVER: A FUSED WRAPPER'S INLINE `managed` (W49
	// wave 8).** The ruling rests on a `let`-bound struct HOLDING a record — `acc` names an `Accumulator`
	// box, `b` names a `Bag` box — so a write inside the type reaches storage that box owns and the caller's
	// `let` never promised anything about it. **A fused wrapper has no such box.** `type String`'s `managed`
	// is the 40-byte `__ManagedMemory` EMBEDDED WHOLE at offset 0 — the ENVELOPE COLLAPSE
	// (`StructLayout.isInlineManagedField`), whose whole point is that **a String IS its own
	// `__ManagedMemory`** — so `managed.append(…)` inside `String.append` writes the very record the
	// CALLER's binding denotes, byte for byte. That is not "reaching inside the type's own methods"; it is
	// the write `let s = "hello"` exists to refuse, and refusing it is what keeps `s.append(" world")` off
	// an `.rdata` literal and off an array element some other name still borrows.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE LINE IS THE ENVELOPE COLLAPSE, NOT "THE FIELD IS MANAGED" — THAT WIDER RULE WAS THE
	// PLANNED CURE AND A COMMITTED CASE REFUTES IT.** `items.push(v)` inside an ordinary `type Bag` is a
	// managed self-field write too, and `parameter-mutation:let-struct-with-array-field-to-mutating-method-ok`
	// requires `let b = Bag.create(); b.add(1)` to COMPILE and answer 1. `Bag.items` is an 8-byte POINTER to
	// storage the Bag owns; `String.managed` is the record itself. Keyed on managed-ness this arm would
	// redden that case; keyed on the collapse it cannot see `Bag` at all, because `Bag` declares no builtin
	// literal marker. **The four conformers are `String`, `Character`, `Array` and `Vector`** — exactly the
	// types whose `let` discipline `specs-shv2/immutable-method-call.md` documents by name.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT SETS E3019's COLUMN ONLY. `receiverStorageMask` below is unchanged and still answers for
	// E3070**, which is the same independence `IrFunction`'s table already records for a `String` receiver
	// that is a parameter: every borrow E3070 tracks is a reference INTO A CONTAINER — an element, a chain
	// node handle, a cursor — and a `String` write frees none of them because a `String` is not a container
	// and hands out no such reference. (It read *"no ARRAY element, and an array element is the only borrow
	// E3070 tracks"* until spec-port `list` — true when the `Array` was the only surface handing out element
	// borrows, and false once a `List`, a `__ManagedList` and a `Vector` each did. The claim this arm
	// actually needs is unaffected: a `String` is not a container in any reading.)
	// `borrow-liveness:method-writing-a-string-or-set-field-is-not-a-conflict` is the case
	// that fails the instant the two are fused, and it was watched green before, during and after this arm.
	function receiverOwnerMask(func IrFunction, binding VarInfo, baseTok Token) returns ParamPositionMask throws ParseError
		if binding.isSelfField 'ownedByTheTypeNotTheCaller'
			// ⛔ **BOUND FIRST, NEVER `if try …` — MEASURED, AND IT IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.** Written as
			// `if try self.selfFieldIsInlineManaged(…) 'label'` this arm fired for `type Bag`'s plain
			// `items` field, on which the predicate is provably FALSE (probed: `conforms=false`,
			// `fieldIndex=0`, `inlineIdx=1`) — so every `let`-bound struct with a container field was
			// refused with E3019. `dispatchMethodOnBinding`'s own use of this predicate already states the
			// rule — *"which needs a `try` and so cannot join a condition chain"* — and this is the
			// measurement behind it.
			let theRecordIsTheReceiver = try self.selfFieldIsInlineManaged(binding, baseTok: baseTok)
			if theRecordIsTheReceiver 'theFieldIsTheReceiversOwnRecord'
				return self.enclosingSelfParamMask(func)
			end 'theFieldIsTheReceiversOwnRecord'

			return NoParamPositions
		end 'ownedByTheTypeNotTheCaller'

		return paramMaskOfValue(func, value: binding.boundValue)
	end 'receiverOwnerMask'

	// ⭐ **THE ENCLOSING METHOD'S RECEIVER, AS A MASK** — shared by the two masks above and below, which both
	// reach it for a self-field alias and would otherwise spell one lookup and one panic twice. The
	// parameter INDEX is DERIVED (`self` resolved through the scope) rather than assumed to be 0: the
	// convention that binds it there lives in `bindParameters`, and restating it here would be a second copy
	// of it.
	function enclosingSelfParamMask(func IrFunction) returns ParamPositionMask
		return paramMaskOfValue(func, value: try self.scope.lookupValue(SelfParamName) otherwise panic("enclosingSelfParamMask: a self-field alias is in scope but `self` is not — `declareSelfFieldAliases` and `bindParameters` both run at method entry before a body token is read, and neither other path can hold an alias in THIS scope without a receiver (a closure body gets a FRESH scope carrying no alias — a bare self-field name inside one resolves through the CAPTURE path instead, off the enclosing scope; a `static function` installs no alias at all)"))
	end 'enclosingSelfParamMask'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE E3070 TWIN OF THE MASK ABOVE: WHICH PARAMETER'S STORAGE A WRITE THROUGH THIS RECEIVER
	// REACHES.** It differs from `receiverOwnerMask` in EXACTLY ONE ARM — a SELF-FIELD alias — and that one
	// arm is the whole point of there being two masks. See `IrFunction`'s table for the pair, and
	// `receiverOwnerMask` above for why E3019 must keep answering NO there.
	//
	// `items.clear()` inside a method of `Bag` writes storage the CALLER can reach, because the caller
	// handed `b` over as the receiver: `let s = try b.items.get(0) otherwise ""` then `b.wipe()` frees the
	// element `s` holds. MEASURED before this arm existed — the oracle prints 44 (it RETAINS), shv2 ran and
	// printed **4557430888798830399** out of freed memory, with no diagnostic anywhere.
	//
	// The receiver's parameter index is DERIVED rather than assumed to be 0 — see `enclosingSelfParamMask`,
	// the one home that lookup and its panic now have.
	function receiverStorageMask(func IrFunction, binding VarInfo) returns ParamPositionMask
		if binding.isSelfField 'fieldOfTheEnclosingReceiver'
			return self.enclosingSelfParamMask(func)
		end 'fieldOfTheEnclosingReceiver'

		// ⚠ **MODULE STORAGE REACHES NO PARAMETER, AND THE ARM IS EXPLICIT FOR `receiverOwnerMask`'s OWN
		// REASON.** A top-level `var` is addressed by `.data` label, never handed to this body — a callee
		// that writes it writes it BY NAME, which is a site recorded in the callee, not a fact about any
		// argument. The stand-in's `boundValue` is 0 and 0 is `self`'s parameter id, so falling through to
		// the value test would mark parameter 0's storage written off that coincidence and refuse every
		// call handing it on: a false rejection with no compile error. See `VarInfo.namesModuleStorage`.
		if binding.namesModuleStorage 'moduleStorageNotTheCallers'
			return NoParamPositions
		end 'moduleStorageNotTheCallers'

		return paramMaskOfValue(func, value: binding.boundValue)
	end 'receiverStorageMask'

	// The shared arm of the two masks above: the parameter `value` IS, as a mask, or none when it is not a
	// parameter at all. The parameter-ValueId convention (`bindParameters` reserves 0..paramCount-1) is what
	// makes the test a comparison rather than a lookup — see `receiverOwnerMask`'s ⚠ block for why this is
	// a question about the VALUE and `VarInfo.isParameter` is a different one about the BINDING.
	function paramMaskOfValue(func IrFunction, value ValueId) returns ParamPositionMask
		if value >= func.paramCount 'notAParameter'
			return NoParamPositions
		end 'notAParameter'

		return maskWithParam(NoParamPositions, param: value)
	end 'paramMaskOfValue'

	// ⭐ **THE E3070 STORAGE MASK FOR A WRITE NAMED BY ITS SUBJECT** — the same answer `receiverStorageMask`
	// gives, reached through the ONE subject derivation (`borrowSubjectNameAt`) that every E3070 door and the
	// P1.8 iteration lock already share. It is what lets an array method reached through a FIELD CHAIN
	// (`bag.items.clear()` inside `function wipe(bag Bag)`) record the write against `bag`, which the
	// receiver-value path has no binding to find.
	function subjectStorageMask(func IrFunction, subjectName ByteArray) returns ParamPositionMask
		if subjectName.isEmpty() 'noNamedStorage'
			return NoParamPositions
		end 'noNamedStorage'

		return self.receiverStorageMask(func, binding: self.borrowSubjectBindingOf(subjectName))
	end 'subjectStorageMask'

	// Is this DECLARED type an `Array` instance? Asked by `arrayManagedFieldAt`, the `x.managed` ROUTING
	// question, which holds a declared `MaxonType` rather than a value.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TAG TEST IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND LEAVING IT OUT IS A WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A LOOSE ONE.**
	// `TypeNameId` and `GenericInstanceId` share a numeric space, so `isArrayInstance` asked of a name that
	// is not an instance at all can answer TRUE by coincidence — MEASURED: without it an `Integer` field's
	// store marked the whole receiver written, and `b.add(42)` was refused while `b.items` was borrowed, a
	// program the oracle compiles and runs (it prints `44 42`). It is the same two-part test
	// `parseFieldChainCall` makes of a receiver's VALUE, asked here of a type.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT WAS THE TWO STORE DOORS' E3070 GATE UNTIL W-BORROW, AND THAT WAS A USE-AFTER-FREE** — see
	// `typeOwnsBorrowableStorage` below, which is the question those doors were actually asking.
	function typeIsArrayInstance(t MaxonType) returns bool
		return self.signatures.isArrayInstanceAt(maxonTypeTag(t), nameId: typeNameIdOf(t))
	end 'typeIsArrayInstance'

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **CAN DROPPING A RECORD OF THIS TYPE FREE STORAGE AN E3070 BORROW POINTS INTO? — THE ONE HOME OF
	// THE GATE ON BOTH FIELD-STORE DOORS (W-BORROW).** `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` (`items = <fresh>`) and
	// `parseFieldAssignment` (`b.items = <fresh>`) both DROP the record the field held, so what they can
	// invalidate is every borrow into anything that record transitively owns — which is a question about the
	// FIELD'S TYPE and has exactly two wrong answers, both of which shv2 has now shipped:
	//
	//   • **`typeIsArrayInstance`, which is TOO NARROW and was a measured 0xC0000005 twice.** A `List` field
	//     dropped under a live `first()` borrow, and a nested STRUCT field whose drop cascades to the `Array`
	//     inside it. "An array element is the only borrow E3070 tracks" was true only while the `Array` was
	//     the only surface handing one out, and a store does not free an ELEMENT in any case.
	//   • **bare `typeIsManaged`, which is TOO WIDE and is a measured false rejection.** A `String` field
	//     store — `name = "another tag"` inside a method — was refused *"cannot mutate 'b' via 'retag'"*
	//     while `b.items` was borrowed, on a program the runnable oracle compiles and runs (`44 another tag
	//     entirely`). It also contradicts a committed case one door over:
	//     `borrow-liveness:method-writing-a-string-or-set-field-is-not-a-conflict` states this very fact for
	//     the METHOD door, and one fact may not have two answers.
	//
	// ⇒ **EVERY BORROW E3070 TRACKS IS A REFERENCE INTO A CONTAINER** — an element (`Array`, `List`,
	// `Vector`, through `emitContainerElementAccessor`), a chain NODE handle (`emitManagedListNodeHandle`) or
	// a CURSOR (`createCursor`). So the question is *"is this type, or does it own, a container"*, and the
	// two managed types that are neither are `String` and `Character`: each owns a BYTE BUFFER and nothing
	// else, and no door in the language mints a tracked borrow into one (`s.byteAt(i)` copies a word, and
	// `arrayManagedFieldAt` will not route `.managed` off a non-instance binding, so no cursor can be taken
	// on either). Every other managed type is an aggregate or a container and is admitted.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CARVE-OUT IS THE DANGEROUS DIRECTION AND IS DELIBERATELY THE SHORTEST POSSIBLE ONE.** A type
	// wrongly EXCLUDED here is a use-after-free; a type wrongly included is only a refusal. These two are
	// excluded on the same claim `IrFunction`'s mask table already makes about a `String` RECEIVER — a
	// String write frees no container element — and nothing else is.
	function typeOwnsBorrowableStorage(t MaxonType) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.typeIsManaged(t, from: self.artifact.typeNames) 'ownsNoHeapAtAll'
			return false
		end 'ownsNoHeapAtAll'

		let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
		return tag != ValueTypeTag.string and tag != ValueTypeTag.character
	end 'typeOwnsBorrowableStorage'

	// ⭐ **THE DENSE-GROWTH GATE for `resize` (E3106).** `__managed_resize` lengthens by publishing a longer
	// `length@8` over slots `__managed_grow` left ZEROED, and a zero is an ELEMENT only while the element lives
	// INLINE in the buffer. A MANAGED element lives there as an 8-byte POINTER (`ManagedArrayElementBytes`),
	// so a zeroed slot is NULL — an absence, not a value — and Maxon has no default constructor to make one.
	// The array that comes back answers `count()` = N while `get(0)` takes the empty-slot branch of
	// `emitLoadElemAndOkReturn`: a `count()` that does not agree with `get()`, which is a silent wrong answer
	// rather than a missing feature. `push(value)` is the growth that supplies the element.
	//
	// An OPAQUE-`T` element is NOT gated, and that is the rule's scope rather than a gap in it: one shared
	// generic body serves every instantiation, so there is no element type to name and no single answer to
	// give — and a deliberately SPARSE slot table (a hash map's key column, which tracks occupancy in a
	// parallel state column and never reads a slot it did not write) is built through exactly that path.
	// `containerElementIsManaged` already answers false for a type parameter, so the carve-out needs no second
	// test written here.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A SECOND THING NOW DEPENDS ON THIS REFUSAL, IN ANOTHER FILE (P1.8 slice A).**
	// `__managed_get_unchecked` — the element read a `for x in arr` loop makes — DROPPED the empty-slot arm
	// `__managed_get` carries, and is safe only because no index in `[0, count)` can hold a NULL managed slot.
	// That is true precisely because this rule refuses the one operation that would create one. If this
	// refusal is ever relaxed, that read becomes a null deref in a function whose own text still looks
	// correct: `ManagedMemoryRuntime.maxon`'s `ManagedGetUncheckedName` header states the obligation from its end,
	// and this note is the pointer back, so neither side can be changed without meeting the other.
	function requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'trivial'
			return
		end 'trivial'
		throw ParseError.arrayResizeManagedElement(self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BUFFER `set` MUST OWN WHAT IT STORES, SO A MANAGED ELEMENT IS REFUSED (E3109, R4.6 review).**
	// ⚖ USER RULING, 2026-07-30: the buffer surface's `set` is bounded by CAPACITY, which is what makes the
	// stage-then-`setLength`-to-publish idiom spellable — and what puts a write into `[length, capacity)`, a
	// region that carries NO OWNERSHIP. For a TRIVIAL element that is exactly the intent. For a MANAGED one
	// the staged element is owned by nobody until a `setLength` publishes it, and by nobody at all if one
	// never comes: MEASURED at exit **101** by two programs that break no stated contract — `reserve(4)` then
	// `set(0, "staged")` and return; and stage, then `push` over the staged slot (`__managed_push` stores AT
	// `length` without destroying the occupant, because before the ruling that slot was PROVABLY NULL).
	//
	// ⚖ **RULED A REJECTION RATHER THAN A FIX (user, 2026-07-30), and the reasons are worth keeping because
	// they are what a future "why not just make ownership follow?" has to answer.** Making the staged slot
	// owned means a destructor gate on every managed `push` — a hot path — and it makes the
	// `[length, capacity)`-reads-ZERO invariant CONDITIONAL, which `push`, `insert`, `append`, the grow
	// realloc and `__managed_cow_detach` all currently rely on. Refusing costs neither, and costs no capability:
	// `[0, length)` is precisely what the ARRAY surface's own length-bounded `set` covers, which owns what it
	// stores and is untouched — so the message names it as the spelling to use.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS COARSER THAN THE RULE IT ENFORCES, DELIBERATELY, AND THE COST WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN
	// ASSUMED.** The rule is about the INDEX (`>= length`), which is a runtime expression no parser holds, so
	// a parse-time refusal has to refuse the whole managed-element buffer `set` including the `[0, length)`
	// half that leaks nothing. A census of the corpus found every `.managed.set` site is a TRIVIAL element
	// bar the two this rung had just added, and the harmless half has an exact replacement one word shorter
	// (`arr.set(i, value: x)`). Refusing at compile time makes the leak IMPOSSIBLE where a runtime throw
	// would only make it reported — and buys the runtime setter no check at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO OPAQUE-`T` CARVE-OUT IS NEEDED, and that is checked rather than inherited from
	// `requireArrayResizeHasZeroElement`'s note** (which does need one). `.managed` is not reachable inside a
	// shared generic body: `self.items.managed` is refused a step earlier as a field access through a
	// non-struct, so every site this rule can see has a CONCRETE element and a statically known answer.
	// `containerElementIsManaged` answering false for a type parameter is therefore not what makes this sound.
	function requireBufferSetOwnsWhatItStores(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'trivial'
			return
		end 'trivial'

		throw ParseError.bufferSetManagedElement(self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireBufferSetOwnsWhatItStores'

	// ⭐⭐ **A MANAGED ELEMENT'S BYTES ARE A POINTER, NOT DATA, SO RAW BYTE ACCESS IS REFUSED (E3110, R4.6
	// review).** ⚖ USER RULING, 2026-07-30. `setByte` writes into a live heap ADDRESS — corruption in place,
	// after which the element it named is unreachable and unreleasable (MEASURED: exit **101** writing offset
	// 0 of an `Array with String`). `byteAt` READS one and hands the fragment back as though it were a value
	// (MEASURED: returned NONZERO for offsets 0 and 1 of a live `String` pointer) — a silent wrong answer, and
	// an address disclosure.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **`byteAt` IS REFUSED ALONGSIDE `setByte`, NOT AFTER IT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF DOING BOTH
	// AT ONCE.** It returns a value: it corrupts nothing, trips no leak gate, and raises no error, so it is
	// exactly the half that stays green forever if only the writer is fixed. The reader was found only
	// because the writer's fix prompted someone to ask what its dual did.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT SHARES `requireBufferSetOwnsWhatItStores`'s PREDICATE AND MUST NOT SHARE ITS MESSAGE.** The
	// question ("is this element managed?") is one fact and is asked once, through
	// `signatures.containerElementIsManaged`. The ANSWER's consequence is two different facts: `set` is refused
	// for OWNERSHIP and only past the published length, so it has a harmless half and an exact replacement
	// (`arr.set(i, value:)`) the message names; these two are refused for REPRESENTATION, at every offset,
	// with no replacement — byte access to a pointer is not something a correct program wants. Folding the
	// two messages would put a rationale on one refusal that is true only of the other, which is the failure
	// this review already corrected once on `emitBufferAccessGuard`'s `isWrite`.
	//
	// ⚠ TRIVIAL elements are untouched, and that is the load-bearing half of the rule rather than an
	// exemption: `__ManagedMemory.create` can only ever yield a trivial element, so string building and the
	// NUL terminator `stdlib/File.maxon` writes at the length boundary go through here unchanged.
	function requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'trivial'
			return
		end 'trivial'

		throw ParseError.bufferByteAccessManagedElement(String.from(methodTok.value), elementType: self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireBufferBytesAreNotAPointer'

	// ⭐⭐ **A RAW-BYTE FILL, ONE BYTE PER ELEMENT, NEEDS AN ELEMENT THAT ADMITS EVERY BYTE (E3117, A4a).**
	// ⛔ Two constructs write bytes into an `Array with Byte` that exist only at RUN TIME, and both were a
	// silent wrong answer under a `Byte` too narrow to hold them:
	//
	//   • the BYTE VIEW (`parseByteView` — `String.toByteArray()`, `String.bytes()`, `Character.bytes()`),
	//     whose `__str_to_bytes` blits the receiver's UTF-8. MEASURED on `origin/main` with A3r's fix already
	//     in: `takes("ß".toByteArray())` into `takes(b Array with Byte)` under `typealias Byte = int(0 to 100)`
	//     returned **195**, exit 195, no diagnostic. `.bytes()` returned the same 195 through the same emitter.
	//   • `__ManagedFile.read(managed, size)`, which blits a file's contents into a caller's buffer.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A THIRD SITE — THE BUFFER SURFACE'S `setByte` — REACHED THIS RULE AT A4a AND NO LONGER DOES
	// (A4c).** It addresses a byte OFFSET rather than an element, so what its write leaves behind is any bit
	// pattern of the element's whole SLOT, and *"admits every byte"* is that question's answer at ONE width
	// and no other. It asks `requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot` (E3118) instead, at every stride. The two
	// remaining sites are exactly the ones whose fill is byte-per-element BY CONTRACT, which is why neither
	// needs — or may have — a stride test of its own; see the note below.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT IS PER-TYPE AND A3r's `requireByteStringBlobFitsItsElement` IS PER-VALUE, AND THEY MAY NOT BE
	// MERGED.** A3r inspects the blob it holds, so it can accept `b"abc"` under `int(0 to 100)` — which it
	// MUST, and its own header says why. There is nothing here to inspect: the fill is one runtime blit, not
	// a run of stores that could be narrowed one at a time, so the only question left is about the ELEMENT.
	// Asking A3r's question here would accept every one of the readings above; asking this one there would
	// refuse `b"abc"` and re-break the rung this one follows.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE OBVIOUS CURE — RETYPE THE BYTE VIEW ONTO `__ManagedByte` AND LET `byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`
	// DECIDE — IS WRONG, AND IT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED.** A byte view is NOT a buffer of the
	// `__ManagedByte` kind: `emitArrayCreateOp` stamps its `element_size@24` from the very instance it is
	// typed as, so a wide `Byte` gives a genuinely stride-2 record and `__str_to_bytes` fills it at THAT
	// stride — `b0=97 b1=98 b2=99` under `typealias Byte = int(0 to 1000)`, correct today. `__ManagedByte`
	// strides 1 always (`undeclaredNamedElementSlot`), so a retyped view would be refused by that
	// door's STRIDE test at every declared byte-array position: measured on the tree as
	// `E3005 … expected 'Array_Byte$0_1000', got 'Array___ManagedByte'` for the buffer that already wears the
	// element. ⇒ the interning `parseByteView` does is deliberate and it is RIGHT; only the BOUNDS were never
	// asked. `bytearray-element-size.a-wide-byte-still-materializes-a-byte-view` holds it shut.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RAW-BYTE READER IS DELIBERATELY UNTOUCHED, AND UNLIKE E3110's PAIR THAT IS NOT THE HALF THAT
	// STAYS GREEN FOREVER.** `byteAt` emits `resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer` with `UnnamedTypeId` — a plain
	// unranged `int`, never the element — so a byte read back is honest whatever `Byte` was declared to be.
	// E3110 refuses its reader because a managed element's bytes are an ADDRESS at every offset, which is a
	// fact about the REPRESENTATION and true in both directions; a declared RANGE constrains only what may be
	// STORED. Refusing the read as well would cost a capability to close nothing.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE BOUNDS QUESTION AND NOTHING ELSE, AND NEITHER REMAINING SITE MAY GROW A STRIDE TEST.**
	// Folding one in would open the byte view's own negative-`low` case: a `Byte` whose bounds cannot be
	// trusted takes the machine word (`rangedAliasStorageBytes`), so the view would stop LOOKING byte-packed
	// and the rule would stop asking, on exactly the element it least trusts. The construct that genuinely
	// needs the stride — `setByte` — does not have it as a precondition either: it feeds the stride INTO its
	// rule (`requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot`), which is the difference between a boundary and an answer.
	//
	// ⚠ A raw-negative `low` is REFUSED here, and it is the same conservative direction
	// `rangeHoldsEveryByte`'s header argues for: bounds ride as raw 64-bit patterns, so a negative `low` and
	// a bound above `i64.max` are the same bits. This door ADMITS a run of raw bytes into a declared element,
	// so the ambiguous answer has to be "no". `int(-5 to 300)` would in fact hold every byte and is refused
	// with the rest; that is the price of the ambiguity, and it is paid in the direction that cannot produce
	// a wrong answer.
	//
	// `undeclared` is the program that declares no `Byte` at all — the literal's own instance
	// (`SignatureIndex.undeclaredNamedElementSlot`), where no declared range exists for a byte to
	// fall outside of.
	function requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.arrayElementAliasOf(giid, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'element'
			undeclared then return
			declared(alias) then try self.requireElementAdmitsEveryByte(alias, methodTok: methodTok)
		end 'element'
	end 'requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement'

	// The rule above's test, split out so the rule reads as its two cases — `requireEveryByteInRange`'s shape,
	// for its reason. The predicate is `RangedAliasRegistry`'s own `rangeHoldsEveryByte`, which the boundary
	// door already asks one quantifier up (`holdsEveryByteInEveryFile`), so a byte and a raw buffer cannot come
	// to mean two different things at the two doors.
	function requireElementAdmitsEveryByte(alias RangedTypeAlias, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if rangeHoldsEveryByte(alias.low, high: alias.high) 'holdsEveryByte'
			return
		end 'holdsEveryByte'

		throw ParseError.rawByteFillNarrowElement(String.from(methodTok.value), aliasName: String.from(alias.name), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: aliasIsFloat(alias), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireElementAdmitsEveryByte'

	// ⭐⭐ **A RAW-BYTE WRITE NEEDS AN ELEMENT THAT ADMITS EVERY BIT PATTERN OF ITS SLOT (E3118, A4c).** The
	// rule above's neighbour and NOT its widening: this one is asked by the single construct that addresses a
	// byte OFFSET, so the value its write leaves in the element is an arbitrary unsigned value of the whole
	// slot, and the width is part of the question rather than a gate in front of it. The predicate is
	// `ProgramSignatures.elementAdmitsEverySlotValue`, whose one-byte rung IS `rangeHoldsEveryByte` — so at
	// the width where the two rules coincide they cannot come to disagree, and above it only this one speaks.
	//
	// ⚠ The SLOT is read from `arrayElementSize` — the sole producer of `element_size@24`, so the width this
	// refusal names is the width the runtime will actually stride, not a second derivation of it.
	function requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.elementAdmitsEverySlotValue(giid, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'fillsItsSlot'
			return
		end 'fillsItsSlot'

		throw ParseError.rawByteWriteSlotDomain(String.from(methodTok.value), elementDisplay: self.slotElementDisplay(giid), slotBytes: self.signatures.arrayElementSize(giid), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireRawByteWriteFitsItsSlot'

	// The element as the refusal above must NAME it: the display name, plus the DECLARED RANGE when there is
	// one, because a sentence about which values an element admits is unreadable without the values. The
	// choice between the two shapes is made HERE and not in the renderer, because only the parser holds the
	// signatures — `valueDisplayTypeName` feeding `builtinArgumentType`'s `got:` is the same split.
	function slotElementDisplay(giid GenericInstanceId) returns String
		return match self.signatures.arrayElementAliasOf(giid, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'element'
			undeclared gives "'{self.signatures.arrayElementDisplayName(giid)}'"
			declared(alias) gives rangedAliasValueSetDisplay(String.from(alias.name), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: aliasIsFloat(alias))
		end 'element'
	end 'slotElementDisplay'

	// ⭐ **THE COPY GATE for `slice`/`clone`/`append`, routed by element KIND (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b).** An OPAQUE-`T`
	// element (a generic body's shared `Array with <type parameter>`) copies through the descriptor's `copyFunc@32`
	// (`requireOpaqueArrayCopyable`); a CONCRETE element copies through its statically-known cloner
	// (`requireArrayElementCopyable`). One home so the three copy methods pick the right gate identically.
	function requireArrayCopyMethodSupported(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaque'
			try self.requireOpaqueArrayCopyable(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
			return
		end 'opaque'
		try self.requireArrayElementCopyable(giid, methodTok: methodTok)
	end 'requireArrayCopyMethodSupported'

	// ⭐ **THE COPY GATE for an OPAQUE-`T` array (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b-β).** An opaque `Array with <type parameter>`
	// inside a generic body copies each element through the enclosing instance's descriptor `copyFunc@32`, which
	// can hold only a SINGLE `(box) -> newBox` cloner (`__managed_*_opaque` branches on it: a byte-blit for a
	// trivial element, a deep clone for a managed one). It REJECTS (E2015) only when SOME instantiation of the
	// enclosing generic type substitutes a managed type nothing can deep-clone — an OS handle, a base-struct-less
	// generic instance with no runtime copy of its own (a `Vector`), an existential, or a type that owns one of
	// those — because such an element has no `copyFunc` and its copy would byte-blit a managed pointer and
	// double-free it. The reject is per-INSTANTIATION (the shared body's element is opaque), so it enumerates the
	// enclosing type's instances through `genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation`, mirroring the concrete gate's
	// `arrayElementSupportsDeepClone`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A MANAGED-ELEMENT CONTAINER USED TO BE ON THAT REJECT LIST AND IS NOT ANY MORE (G18).** It was there
	// for an ARITY reason and never for an impossibility: an `Array with (Array with String)` element copies
	// through a 2-argument `__managed_clone_managed(box, funcAddr(__str_clone))`, which `callIndirect(fn, elem)`
	// could not reach. A per-instance one-argument thunk now can
	// (`SignatureIndex.managedElementRecordCopy`), so this gate has nothing left to refuse there.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND UNTIL IT DID, THIS GATE REFUSED PROGRAMS THAT COPY NOTHING — which is the shape G18 was found by,
	// not a hypothetical.** The refusal fires where `stdlib/Array.maxon:145`'s `managed.slice(0, len)` IS, and
	// that body is compiled once for the whole program, so ANY program reaching the corpus `Array` at all (a
	// `for … in`, an `isEmpty()`, a `clear()`) answered for `clone` on behalf of every instantiation in the
	// file. Curing THAT by making the demand reachability-driven was measured and rejected as the wrong cure:
	// the instantiation that would be exempted still needs a `copyFunc@32`, and a 0 there is the shape 25
	// measured `0xC0000005`s came out of (see `ProgramSignatures.managedOpaqueArrayElementOf`). The cloner was
	// the missing piece, not the demand.
	function requireOpaqueArrayCopyable(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⚠ The declaring type is NOT paired in here from `self.enclosingType` any more (W14): the element's
		// token names its own declaration, so the gate resolves both halves from one fact — see
		// `instanceWithTypeArgKind` for the hand-pairing that removes.
		match self.signatures.genericTypeFieldUncopyableInstantiation(typeNameIdOf(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))) 'uncopyable'
			noInstance then return
			// ⭐ The offending INSTANTIATION is what the user wrote, so it is what this refusal is blamed at
			// when the body raising it is the library's — see `blameLibraryRefusalOnInstantiation`.
			instance(offender) then self.blameLibraryRefusalOnInstantiation(offender)
		end 'uncopyable'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(methodTok.value)}` COPIES each element of an `Array with <type parameter>` field, but this generic type is instantiated with a type whose managed element cannot be deep-cloned — a compiler-owned aggregate or a base-struct-less generic instance with no runtime copy of its own (`__ManagedFile`, a `Vector`), a value held at an interface type, or a generic instance that owns one of those. String / struct / boxed-union / container (`Array with int`, `List with String`, `Array with (Array with String)`) / trivial instantiations, and a declared generic's instance whose own substituted fields are all deep-cloneable (`Box with String`), ARE supported (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b, W162, W173, G18).", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireOpaqueArrayCopyable'

	// ⭐⭐ **BLAME A LIBRARY-BODY REFUSAL ON THE INSTANTIATION THAT FORCED IT (BLAME).**
	//
	// This gate is contingent on the INSTANCE REGISTRY — it refuses `Array.clone`'s own
	// `managed.slice(0, len)` because SOME instantiation of the enclosing generic type substitutes an
	// element with no single `copyFunc`. When the body it refuses is `stdlib/`'s, the line it names is a
	// line no user wrote; the instantiation is the user's own construct, and blaming it there restores both
	// halves of what the relocation cost — the user's line, and (because the line IS `typealias Holders =
	// Array with Holder`) the user's element.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY FOR A LIBRARY BODY.** In a USER generic body the refusal already lands on a line its author
	// wrote, and re-pointing it at the instantiation would move a diagnostic that was never mis-blamed.
	// `isStdlibSource` is the right bit and not `livesUnderStdlibDirectory`: the question is whose code the
	// author is reading, which is exactly the visibility partition (`maxon-shv2 build stdlib/URL.maxon`
	// makes those files the user's own, and there the refusal must stay where it is raised).
	//
	// An instance the program never WROTE — one substituted into a generic body's inner alias — is followed
	// back to the enclosing instantiation that minted it, and one with no written origin at all simply keeps
	// the raise site. Both are `ProgramSignatures.instantiationSiteOf`'s business, argued there.
	function blameLibraryRefusalOnInstantiation(offender GenericInstanceId)
		if not self.isStdlibSource 'theAuthorWroteThisBody'
			return
		end 'theAuthorWroteThisBody'

		match self.signatures.instantiationSiteOf(offender) 'site'
			unwritten then return
			written(site) then self.libraryBlame = LibraryBlame.userSite(site.filePath, line: site.nameLine, column: site.nameColumn)
		end 'site'
	end 'blameLibraryRefusalOnInstantiation'

	// Emit a VOID array mutator call; return the receiver (what the statement still denotes).
	function emitArrayVoidCall(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, receiver ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		_ = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		return receiver
	end 'emitArrayVoidCall'

	// ⭐ **EMIT A MANAGED-AWARE ELEMENT ACCESSOR** (`get`/`first`, the `Array` cursor's two, and the `List`
	// accessors — P1.7 slice 3 / 3b-vi-a).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS IS NOT "THE ONE DOOR EVERY ELEMENT READ USES" ANY MORE, AND THIS HEADER SAID IT WAS UNTIL
	// `enum-narrow-storage` MEASURED OTHERWISE. THERE ARE THREE DOORS, and each was found by a wrong answer
	// the previous two did not cover.** This one carries the reads the COMPILER emits, and it used to carry
	// `last`/`pop`/`remove` and the `for … in` element read as well — which is why the enumerations below
	// still name them. Those are **CORPUS** members now (`stdlib/Array.maxon` is compiled, and iteration goes
	// through the cursor surface), so they arrive as ordinary calls at `emitCall` and never reach here.
	//
	// ⇒ all three exist because a corpus body is compiled ONCE against an opaque `Element`: it reads a slot
	// with the shared, zero-extending element load and can know nothing the INSTANCE fixes. Where the value
	// GOES from there is what splits the doors:
	//
	//   1. **`emitContainerElementAccessor`** (here) — a read the compiler emits, which holds the instance.
	//   2. **`retypeOpaqueReturnValue`** — a value a corpus body RETURNS through the opaque word; the caller
	//      holds the instance. Two facts cross there, an f64 riding the general-purpose register and a
	//      narrow SIGNED value, and both have a twin here.
	//   3. **`bindParameters`** — a value a corpus body passes INTO user code as an ARGUMENT, through a
	//      function value (`arr.sort(cmp)`) or a witness call (`contains`'s `item == element`). NEITHER
	//      1 NOR 2 STANDS IN FRONT OF IT: nothing returns and nothing is read at the call site — the
	//      callee's own entry is the only place that can see it.
	//
	// All three spell it through the same two helpers (`emitNarrowSignedSlotRead`,
	// `narrowSignedSlotOfLocalType`) so the set cannot drift apart.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A RULE ADDED AT ONE AND NOT THE OTHERS IS A WRONG ANSWER ON THE REST OF THE SURFACE, MEASURED
	// TWICE.** With the sign extension at door 1 alone, on a three-case `Signal { minus = -1 }`: `for s in a`
	// summed **256** where 0 is correct and `a.pop()` answered **255** for `-1`. With doors 1 and 2:
	// `a.sort(cmpSig)` handed the comparator `x=1 y=255` and sorted into the WRONG ORDER. Whatever you add
	// below, ask what the other two owe.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT SERVES A `List` TOO, AND IT NEEDED NO CHANGE TO — which is what identifies it as a CONTAINER
	// door rather than an `Array` one.** Everything below reads the instance through `containerElementType`
	// (managed-ness, opacity, the result's value type) and the ownership rule it states is about the
	// OPERATION: `list.first()`/`list.get(i)` look at an element the list keeps, `list.removeFirst()` takes
	// one out, exactly as `arr.first()` and `arr.pop()` do. A second copy under a list name would have been
	// this whole header — the borrow mint, the buffer-surface mark, the opaque arm — transcribed for a
	// container that answers every one of its questions identically.
	// The result carries the element's VALUE type: for a CONCRETE managed element its usable form — a struct →
	// `structRef` so `first.name` resolves, a boxed union → `named` so `match first` dispatches, a String → text,
	// an instance → `genericInstance`; for an OPAQUE element the element's own `typeParameter(token)` (the shared
	// body has no concrete type, so the tag stays opaque and its drop is descriptor-gated — slice 3b-vi-a); or
	// `integer` for a TRIVIAL concrete element (slice 1, unchanged). Ownership:
	//   • get/first/last yield a BORROW (`ContainerElementReference.borrowed`): the array keeps the element, so
	//     the result is NOT tracked owned and is dropped by nothing here — the array's `__managed_decref` walk
	//     destroys it. A borrow merged with an OWNED `otherwise` fallback is reconciled by `finishValueTry` (the
	//     fallback drops on the error edge; see there).
	//   • pop/remove MOVE the element OUT (`movedOut`): the runtime NULLS the slot it hands back (so the
	//     array walk skips it), so the caller becomes the sole owner and the result is tracked owned — dropped by
	//     the concrete destructor (concrete element) or the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param` (opaque element,
	//     inert for a trivial instantiation). The enclosing method reserves the descriptor for the opaque drop —
	//     `scanDescriptorNeedsInto` seeds it off any `.pop()`/`.remove()` move-out in the shared body.
	//   • `__ManagedListNode.value()` on a concrete managed element RETAINS (`retained`): the node keeps the
	//     element and the caller takes a reference of its own, because `setValue` releases what the node held.
	//     See `ContainerElementReference` for why a borrow and a move-out are both wrong there.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE AN E3070 BORROW IS MINTED (`BorrowCheck`), off the `reference` answer that already
	// answers the question.** `reference` IS *"does the caller take the element OUT, look at it in place, or keep
	// a reference of its own?"*, so
	// `pop`/`remove` are excluded STRUCTURALLY rather than by a second hand-maintained method list — v1 keeps
	// one (`MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon:42-44`) and shv2 deliberately does not port it, because
	// `arrayMethodMutatesReceiver` is already the ONE such list and answers a different question.
	//
	// `borrowSubjectName` is the storage the element came out of, EMPTY when the receiver is anonymous (an
	// array literal, a call result — nothing else can name that record, so no later statement can free it).
	//
	// ⭐ **THE `for … in` ELEMENT ENROLS TOO, and that is a USER RULING** (`parseForStatement` passes
	// `borrowSubjectNameAt(sourceStart)` here). P1.8 Slice A's lock refuses every write that NAMES the
	// iterated array; it structurally cannot refuse one that hands the array to a CALLEE
	// (`wipe(b.items)`), and shv2 read the free-poison byte 0x3F on exactly that program. The oracle
	// ACCEPTS it — it retains the element — so this refuses a program the oracle compiles, deliberately and
	// in the only direction that is sound for a compiler that does not retain. See
	// `bindingIsIterationLocked`'s ⚠⚠ block, which is what this closes, and `attachPendingBorrows`'s
	// `liveUntil` for why the loop element's borrow is LEXICAL rather than last-use.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AN OPAQUE `T` ELEMENT MINTS A BORROW LIKE ANY OTHER, AND FOR THREE RUNGS IT DID NOT (W61).** This
	// block used to read *"an opaque `T` element mints no borrow, deliberately: a shared generic body cannot
	// know whether the instantiation's element owns heap … the managed instantiation's hazard is real and
	// unclosed"*. It was, and it was a **USE-AFTER-FREE THE WHOLE SUITE WAS GREEN OVER** — found by hand, not by
	// a test. `containerElementIsManaged` answering FALSE for a `typeParameter` is a LOSSY PROJECTION: in a
	// shared body it means *"no static answer"*, never *"owns nothing"*, and read as the latter it hid every
	// write through an opaque container from the one rule that guards this hazard. Measured `0xC0000005` on
	// `let a = managed.get(0)` + `let b = managed.get(1)` + `set(0, value: b)` + `set(1, value: a)` over
	// `Array with String`, and `stdlib/helpers/sort/smallSort.maxon:24-32` names that exact program as the
	// reason `swap` routes through the raw `managed.swap` builtin. The refusal is that comment enforced;
	// `specs-shv2/generic-opaque-element-borrow-liveness.md` is its corpus.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE COST THE OLD NOTE NAMED IS REAL AND IS PAID DELIBERATELY: the TRIVIAL instantiation of a body
	// this refuses is refused with it.** A shared body is compiled ONCE, so the refusal cannot be conditioned on
	// the element a caller chose, and the oracle — which monomorphizes — accepts what this rejects. That is the
	// same conservatism `referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement` already applies to a concrete-typed value in a shared
	// body, taken in the same direction, and the alternative is a body that is a use-after-free for one
	// instantiation with nothing anywhere saying so.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND RETAINING AT THE READ IS NOT THE CHEAPER ANSWER — IT IS A DIFFERENT PROGRAM.** The reference an
	// opaque value takes is `retainFunc@64`, which for a byte record is `__str_clone`, a DEEP CLONE rather than
	// an incref (`SignatureIndex.retainCalleeForProtocol`, and its reason is that a literal's record is immortal
	// `.rdata`). So a retain here would copy the record on every element read and hand the reader a DIFFERENT
	// record from the one the slot holds. The USER RULING two paragraphs down settled the same question for the
	// container's own lifetime: it is the BASE that is promoted, not the element that is retained.
	function emitContainerElementAccessor(func IrFunction, callee ByteArray, container ValueId, args ValueIdArray, giid GenericInstanceId, reference ContainerElementReference, borrowSubjectName ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		// A TRIVIAL CONCRETE element (int / Byte / ranged-int / bool) is a bare inline word that owns nothing, so
		// it is NEVER tracked owned, read or moved out (a decref of an integer would corrupt the count). An
		// OPAQUE element is NOT trivial here — `typeIsManaged` is false for a `typeParameter`, but a managed
		// instantiation's element DOES own heap — so it falls through to the opaque-aware path below.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE ELEMENT'S DECLARED TYPE, NOT A BARE `integer`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A TYPE
		// IDENTITY (P1.8 Slice E).** The rule this arm states is about OWNERSHIP, and typing the result
		// `integer` was a second claim riding on it — that a `Byte` read out of an `Array with Byte` is
		// indistinguishable from an `int`. It is not: an array LITERAL picks its instance from its first
		// element's tag (`arrayLiteralInstanceFor`), so `[b.get(0), b.get(1)]` over a byte array built an
		// `Array with integer` — an 8-byte stride over values that came out of a 1-byte one — and the byte
		// array could not be rebuilt from its own elements. MEASURED before this line, on
		// `String.from([x, y, z])` over a `b"Hi!"`: `E3005 requires a Array with Byte, but its argument is
		// Array_int`, a false reject of a program both references accept. `containerSlotValueType` is the one
		// function that answers "what type does an element READ as", and it answered here already for a
		// managed element; a trivial one now asks the same question of the same function.
		if not self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) and not self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'trivial'
			let trivialType = self.containerSlotValueType(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))
			// ⭐ **A FLOAT ELEMENT COMES BACK AS ITS BITS AND IS REINTERPRETED HERE** — the read half of
			// `containerElementWord`, and it may not simply TAG the call's result `float`: the runtime returns
			// in a general-purpose register, so a float-tagged result would make the allocator colour the same
			// value into two register files and panic the emitter. The call is therefore typed honestly as an
			// integer and the bitcast is a real op.
			if self.containerElementIsFloatWord(giid) 'floatElement'
				let bits = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
				return self.emitUnaryOp(bits, op: MaxonUnaryOp.bitsToFloat, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.float, range: range)
			end 'floatElement'

			let slotValue = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(trivialType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(trivialType), range: range)

			// ⭐⭐ **A NARROW SIGNED SLOT IS SIGN-EXTENDED HERE (`enum-narrow-storage`).** The shared element
			// load ZERO-EXTENDS — one compiled byte loop, strided by the record's runtime `element_size@24`,
			// ending in a shift and a width mask (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitLoadElemAt`) — and the record carries no
			// signedness for it to consult. So an `i8`-slotted `Signal.minus` comes back as **255** and not
			// `-1`, which is the wrong answer `specs/enum-narrow-storage.md:enum-rdata-i8-array` describes in
			// its own prose and catches twice.
			//
			// ⚠⚠ **THIS DOOR IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, AND BELIEVING IT WAS COST TWO MEASURED WRONG ANSWERS.**
			// This function's own header calls itself the door "every element read in the language uses", and
			// that was true when `last`/`pop`/`remove` and the iteration read were compiler-emitted. They are
			// CORPUS members now (`stdlib/Array.maxon`, and `for … in` goes through the cursor surface), and a
			// corpus body is compiled ONCE against an opaque `Element` — so it cannot know the sign and this
			// door never sees the call. MEASURED on a three-case `Signal { minus = -1 }`: `for s in a` summed
			// to **256** where 0 is correct, and `a.pop()` answered **255** for `-1`, while `get`/`first`/
			// `match` through here were right. The corpus half is `retypeOpaqueReturnValue`'s, off the SAME
			// `narrowSignedSlotOf` question — see `emitNarrowSignedSlotRead` for why asking it twice is one
			// derivation and not two.
			return self.emitNarrowSignedSlotRead(slotValue, valueType: trivialType, range: range)
		end 'trivial'

		let resultType = self.elementAccessorResultType(giid)
		let result = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(resultType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(resultType), range: range)
		self.markElementReadSurface(result, container: container)

		// ⭐⭐ **A RETAINED READ — THE CONTAINER KEEPS THE ELEMENT AND THE CALLER TAKES A REFERENCE OF ITS
		// OWN.** It is the `movedOut` arm plus the `+1` that makes it honest, and NOT a borrow: a borrow's
		// safety rests on the container not releasing the element, which is precisely what
		// `__ManagedListNode.setValue` does. The incref goes through `emitIncref` for `emitManagedListNodeHandle`'s
		// stated reason — that door is where *"after this line the allocation has two owners"* is recorded and
		// where the retain is kept off a block-form `try`'s handler edge.
		//
		// ⚠ **NO BORROW IS MINTED AND THE CONTAINER IS NOT SCOPE-PROMOTED, because neither is owed.** The
		// value holds its own reference, so nothing about the container's lifetime can dangle it — which is
		// the whole difference from the arm below and the reason a third answer had to exist.
		if reference == ContainerElementReference.retained 'retainedRead'
			self.emitIncref(result, range: range)
			self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
			return result
		end 'retainedRead'

		if reference == ContainerElementReference.movedOut 'moveOut'
			// ⛔ **CO-OWNED: REMOVING AN ELEMENT PROVES THE CONTAINER NO LONGER HOLDS IT, NOT THAT NOBODY DOES.**
			// `append`/`push` of a BORROWED aggregate co-owns it (`moveManagedValueInto`'s borrowed arm), so the
			// element handed back here may be a reference the CALLER still shares — MEASURED as 0xC0000005 on
			// `list.append(<a borrowed union>)` + `removeFirst()` + `match`. ⭐ The `Array` spelling of that same
			// program always passed, because `Array.remove` is a CORPUS member reaching `enrolOwnedCallTemp`,
			// which is co-owned; this compiler-emitted door was the one still claiming sole, and two spellings of
			// one operation must not disagree. See `OwnedHeapExclusivity`.
			self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.coOwned)
		end 'moveOut' else 'borrowsInPlace'
			// ⭐⭐ **A CONTAINER THE STATEMENT ALONE OWNS IS HELD TO THE SCOPE'S EXIT, BECAUSE THE ELEMENT JUST
			// HANDED OUT POINTS INTO IT (A3h).** ⚖ USER RULING, 2026-08-01: extend the TEMPORARY's lifetime.
			// `let n = try make().get(0) otherwise …` used to free `make()`'s array at the statement's end
			// (`drainPendingTemps`, or the release `desugarTry` places on an abandoning path) while `n` still named an element
			// inside it — measured **`4557430888798830399`** = `0x3F3F3F3F3F3F3F3F`, `__mm_free`'s poison byte
			// read back as a `count()`. **A WRONG ANSWER in every channel this project has**: not a crash, not
			// a refusal, and no leak (the free is legitimate), so the suite was green over it for as long as it
			// existed and only a hand-probe found it.
			//
			// ⭐ It is stated HERE, at the one door every borrow THIS FUNCTION mints leaves through, rather than at the
			// five arms that reach it — the same argument `markElementReadSurface` makes one line up. So `get`,
			// `first`, `last`, the `for … in` element read and the list's two accessors are covered by one line
			// and a sixth arm cannot miss it. ⚠ **`last`/`pop`/`remove` AND THE LOOP READ ARE NOT AMONG THEM
			// ANY MORE**, and that list named them until `enum-narrow-storage`: they are CORPUS calls now, so
			// their lifetimes are `emitCall`'s. See this function's ⛔⛔ header block.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT IS THE BASE THAT IS PROMOTED, NOT THE ELEMENT THAT IS RETAINED** — the ruling's other
			// half. Retaining here would make `get` mean two different things depending on the RECEIVER's
			// provenance, which is exactly the shape the roster family has already cost this project three
			// rungs. Both references extend the lifetime instead: v1 as a per-value liveness EXTENSION over SSA
			// (`StdLiveness.maxon:236-267`, "interior-borrow liveness extension (the UAF fix)" — `baseOf[result]
			// = receiver`, every read of a borrow counting as a read of its base), the bootstrap by retaining on
			// STORE, which is its universal discipline and not one shv2 shares. shv2 has no liveness pass at
			// drop-insertion time (it emits drops at parse), so it takes the coarser SCOPE, which is the
			// direction v1's own note calls safe: *"the worst case is a slightly-later decref … never an early
			// free."*
			//
			// A TRIVIAL concrete element returned from the arm above and never reaches here, so the promotion
			// costs nothing for the `Array with Integer` case that borrows nothing. An OPAQUE element does reach
			// here and is promoted: the shared body cannot know whether the instantiation's element owns heap,
			// and holding the container is inert for a trivial one and necessary for a managed one.
			self.giveTemporaryScopeLifetime(container, name: BorrowSourceBindingName)

			// ⭐ **NO ELEMENT TEST HERE, BECAUSE THE ARM ABOVE ALREADY MADE IT.** The trivial arm returns unless
			// the element is managed OR opaque, so everything reaching this line is one or the other — and both
			// borrow (see this function's ⭐⭐ block on the opaque half). A second `containerElementIsManaged`
			// test here would be that one question asked twice, and its second answer was the wrong one.
			//
			// ⭐ The subject comes through `receiverBorrowSubjectName`, the SAME derivation the write door
			// reads, so a bare `get(0)` on the enclosing `self` and a bare `set(0, …)` on it key alike.
			let elementSubject = self.receiverBorrowSubjectName(borrowSubjectName, receiver: container)
			if not elementSubject.isEmpty() 'borrowsANamedSourcesElement'
				self.mintPendingBorrow(func, subjectName: elementSubject, borrowValue: result, range: range)
			end 'borrowsANamedSourcesElement'
		end 'borrowsInPlace'
		return result
	end 'emitContainerElementAccessor'

	// ⭐⭐ **READ A VALUE OF `valueType` THAT MAY HAVE COME OUT OF A NARROW SIGNED SLOT — the ONE door both
	// halves of `enum-narrow-storage`'s read side go through**, so the direct element accessor above and the
	// opaque-word retype at `emitCall` cannot come to disagree about which types extend or how wide.
	//
	// `narrowSignedSlotOf` answers `notASignExtension` for everything else, so this is a pass-through for
	// every value in every program that has no narrow signed enum — which is why the callers ask it
	// unconditionally rather than guarding first and asking twice.
	// The value a parameter's NAME binds to: the slot itself, or the sign-extended reading of it when the
	// parameter is declared at a type stored in a narrow signed slot — see `bindParameters` for why the
	// callee's entry is the only place the third door can be. The extension lands in the ENTRY block, which
	// is current at both call sites and dominates every use of the parameter.
	function narrowSignedParamValue(slot ValueId, paramType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if not self.buildsBodies 'noBodyToReadIt'
			return slot
		end 'noBodyToReadIt'

		return self.emitNarrowSignedSlotRead(slot, valueType: paramType, range: range)
	end 'narrowSignedParamValue'

	function emitNarrowSignedSlotRead(value ValueId, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		match self.narrowSignedSlotOfLocalType(valueType) 'slot'
			fromBytes(slotBytes) then return self.emitSignExtendFromSlot(value, slotBytes: slotBytes, valueType: valueType, range: range)
			notASignExtension then return value
		end 'slot'
	end 'emitNarrowSignedSlotRead'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NARROW SIGNED SLOT `valueType`'s VALUES OCCUPY — asked of a type interned in THIS FILE's
	// table, which is why the NAME crosses and the id does not.** `ProgramSignatures` classifiers must be
	// asked in the INDEX's space (`slotTypeThroughReceiver`'s ⚠ block, OPEN #56); both callers here hold a
	// type the parser re-interned (`containerSlotValueType`, `slotTypeThroughReceiver`), so the id is this
	// file's and only the name means the same thing on both sides.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED — the id form of this call was a WRONG ANSWER that depended on an unrelated declaration.**
	// `let o = [Offset.behind, Offset.here, Offset.ahead]` then `o.first()` over a three-case enum at
	// `-200 … 200` answered `-200` in a program that also declared `typealias Offsets = Array with Offset`
	// and **65336** in one that did not — the extra declaration only changed which order the two interners
	// assigned ids in.
	function narrowSignedSlotOfLocalType(valueType MaxonType) returns SignExtendSlot
		if maxonTypeTag(valueType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'onlyANamedLeafCanBeNarrowAndSigned'
			return SignExtendSlot.notASignExtension
		end 'onlyANamedLeafCanBeNarrowAndSigned'

		return self.signatures.narrowSignedSlotOf(self.localTypeName(valueType, asker: "narrowSignedSlotOfLocalType"))
	end 'narrowSignedSlotOfLocalType'

	// ⭐ **THE NAME BEHIND A LEAF TYPE'S ID, READ THROUGH THIS FILE'S INTERNER** — the one spelling of a
	// lookup that had grown three verbatim copies whose `otherwise` clauses said the same thing three ways.
	// A MISS is an invariant break and not a case: every id reaching a caller of this was minted by
	// `intern` against this same table or adopted into it (`adoptType`), so the panic names the ASKER rather
	// than guessing at which of those two routes failed.
	//
	// ⚠ Sites whose `otherwise` carries a REAL derivation — `fieldStorageType`, `readTypeAliasDeclaration`,
	// `requireThrownTypeFitsEnclosing` — deliberately keep their own message: the
	// explanation is the value there, and collapsing three different invariants onto one sentence would be
	// deduplication that loses the thing being deduplicated.
	function localTypeName(t MaxonType, asker String) returns ByteArray
		return try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise panic("{asker}: a leaf type's id ({typeNameIdOf(t)}) is not in this file's interner — every id reaching here was interned into that table or adopted into it")
	end 'localTypeName'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS ARGUMENT AN ENUM VALUE? — the gate that keeps `enum-narrow-storage`'s constant enum tags
	// out of the range-check ARG SITES.** Recording a case's tag as a known constant (`emitEnumTagLiteral`)
	// was needed so a constant enum literal could become a `.rdata` blob, and it had a second reacher:
	// `recordConstantArgRangeChecks` admits on `constIsKnown`, so EVERY enum-valued argument in EVERY
	// program would have started allocating a `RangeCheckArgSite`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SITE COULD NEVER PRODUCE A CHECK, which is why this is a gate and not a trade.**
	// `InsertRangeChecks.paramAliasOf` resolves an arg site against the CALLEE's ranged PARAMETERS, and a
	// parameter declared at an enum type is never among them (an enum has no `int(lo to hi)` declaration for
	// `paramSites` to hold). So the site was a parse-time allocation per call site with a structurally
	// guaranteed `ParamAliasLookup.none` at the other end — the `+N per call site, parse only` shape
	// `docs/optimization-log.md` has caught three times. This restores the pre-tick reach exactly.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks whether the name is a DECLARED ENUM and not merely whether the tag is `named`, because a
	// ranged-alias value is `named` too (`7 as Byte`) and its site is the one this door exists for.
	function argIsAnEnumValue(argValue ValueId) returns bool
		let argType = self.typeOf(argValue)
		if maxonTypeTag(argType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedLeaf'
			return false
		end 'notANamedLeaf'

		return self.signatures.containsEnum(self.localTypeName(argType, asker: "argIsAnEnumValue"))
	end 'argIsAnEnumValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **SIGN-EXTEND A VALUE READ OUT OF A NARROW SIGNED SLOT — ONE compiler-only unary op.** The width
	// comes from the slot the storage ladder chose, mapped by `signExtendFromSlotBytes`, so the op and the
	// stride are one derivation; the expansion into shifts is the Std tier's
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerSignExtend`), and `MaxonUnaryOp`'s header carries why it may not be shifts here.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RESULT KEEPS THE VALUE'S OWN TYPE, not `integer`.** It IS the element — `v.rawValue` and
	// `match v` are what happens next — and typing it `integer` would be the same lost identity
	// `emitContainerElementAccessor`'s trivial arm already measured for a `Byte` (`E3005 requires a
	// Array with Byte`, a false reject of a program both references accept). That is why it does not go
	// through `emitUnaryOp`, which mints at a bare TAG and would drop the enum's interned name.
	function emitSignExtendFromSlot(value ValueId, slotBytes ByteOffset, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValueOfType(valueType)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.unaryOp(result, operand: value, op: signExtendFromSlotBytes(slotBytes)), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitSignExtendFromSlot'

	// ⭐ **AN ELEMENT READ OFF AN ARRAY OF BUFFERS IS A BUFFER (A2m).** Placed at the accessor's ONE mint
	// rather than at the five arms that reach it (`get`, `first`, `last`, `pop`, `remove` — plus the `for … in`
	// loop's unchecked read), because every one of them funnels through that line and a per-arm mark would be
	// the enumeration that misses the sixth. A trivial element never arrives here at all: an `Array with
	// Integer` returns from the early arm above, and no trivial element can be a buffer.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE CONTAINER IS PASSED IN, AND IT USED TO BE RECOVERED FROM `args.get(0)` ON THE ARGUMENT THAT
	// "argument 0 is the container, which is the runtime ABI".** That premise held for every `__managed_*`
	// accessor — `__managed_get(arr, i)`, `__managed_pop(arr)`, `__managed_remove(arr, i)` — and it is FALSE for the list's
	// loop read: `__list_node_value(node)` takes the NODE the value sits in, so `args.get(0)` there is a node
	// pointer, and asking a buffer-surface question of it answers `false` by accident rather than by
	// derivation. A premise that has broken is not a premise, so the value comes from the caller, which holds
	// the container in every arm regardless of where the ABI happens to put it.
	function markElementReadSurface(result ValueId, container ValueId)
		if self.valueElementIsBufferSurface(container) 'containerHoldsBuffers'
			self.markBufferSurface(result)
		end 'containerHoldsBuffers'
	end 'markElementReadSurface'

	// The VALUE type an element accessor's result carries — the opaque element's own `typeParameter(token)` for an
	// OPAQUE array (the shared body keeps the element opaque; its drop is descriptor-gated, `decrefCalleeFor`'s
	// `typeParameter` arm → `__drop_type_param`), else the CONCRETE managed element's usable value form
	// (`containerSlotValueType`). Split so `emitContainerElementAccessor` reads ONE type either way; the opaque
	// `typeParameter` carries a POSITION, not an interned name, so it needs no interner adoption.
	function elementAccessorResultType(giid GenericInstanceId) returns MaxonType
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaque'
			return self.signatures.containerElementType(giid)
		end 'opaque'
		return self.containerSlotValueType(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))
	end 'elementAccessorResultType'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE DOOR FOR "A RUNTIME CALL WHOSE RESULT IS A FRESH OWNED HEAP VALUE".** The result carries the
	// value type the caller names and is enrolled as a statement-scoped OWNED temporary (`trackOwnedTemp`), so
	// an UNBOUND result is dropped at statement end and a bound one transfers to the binding; its scope-exit
	// drop then routes by TAG through `decrefCalleeFor` (`__managed_decref` for an instance, `__str_decref` for a
	// String), which is why the tag is the caller's to state and nothing here needs to know the family.
	//
	// ⚠ **SINGLE-SOURCED BECAUSE IT HAD FOUR INDEPENDENT COPIES and nothing made them agree** — the whole-array
	// `slice`/`clone` (P1.7 slice 4), `Set from […]` (P1.7b), a subprocess line read (P1.5 slice 1b) and the
	// String byte methods (P1.8 Slice C) each spelled `emitRuntimeCall` + `trackOwnedTemp` + return. They are
	// the same two-step ownership protocol, so a future change to what enrolling an owned temporary costs (an
	// escape mark, a second registry, a different drop door) would have had to be made in four places that each
	// read as correct on its own — the failure mode being a LEAK or a double-free in whichever copy was missed,
	// never a compile error.
	//
	// The callers that pass `genericInstance` + the receiver's own `giid` get the P1.7 slice 4 behaviour
	// unchanged: `sub.get(0)`/`sub.count()` dispatch on the result because it is the SAME instance type as the
	// receiver. `slice` is THROWING, so this must stay the last op it emits — the enclosing `try` rewrites it
	// to a `tryCall`, and `finishValueTry` reconciles the OWNED result with an OWNED `otherwise` fallback (both
	// drop once, on their own edge).
	// ⚠ **`sole` IS ABOUT THE RECORD THIS RETURNS, WHICH IS THE ONLY THING A DROP OR A DESTRUCTIVE WRITE
	// TOUCHES.** `__str_bytes_view` is the one entry here that hands back a record VIEWING bytes another
	// allocation owns — but the record itself is freshly minted and has exactly one owner, and a byte record has
	// no payload or field slot for a move-out to null. See `OwnedHeapExclusivity`.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THIS IS THE ONE `sole` IN THE FILE THAT RESTS ON THE SET OF CALLEES RATHER THAN ON AN OP — SO THE
	// FRESHNESS IS THE CALLEE'S TO EARN, NOT THIS DOOR'S TO ASSUME.** Every runtime entry reaching here today
	// mints its result, so the claim holds; but it holds by ENUMERATION, and this tree's enumerated doors rot.
	// FOUR of them already return a `structRef` (`__file_open`, `__dir_open_search`, `__charset_make`, and the
	// per-type deep-cloner `__clone_<T>` a source `p.clone()` emits — whose first act is an `__mm_alloc`, so
	// `sole` is exact for it), and a
	// struct box CAN serve as `lastFieldReadBox` for a `throw box.unionField` — so a later entry that handed
	// back a SHARED record would inherit `sole` here in silence and re-arm the destructive write
	// `OwnedHeapExclusivity` documents three segfaults for. ⇒ **Adding a runtime entry whose result is not
	// freshly minted means passing `coOwned` here, not extending this list.** (Found by W55's review, which
	// classified it as the enumerated-doors shape rather than as transitive soleness — a different bug class,
	// and not live today.)
	function emitOwnedRuntimeResult(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultNameId TypeNameId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: resultNameId, range: range)
		self.trackOwnedTemp(result, exclusivity: OwnedHeapExclusivity.sole)
		return result
	end 'emitOwnedRuntimeResult'

	// `slice(start, end)` — both positional; the `endIndex:` label the `Array` spelling required is OPTIONAL on
	// this surface (see `consumeArrayArgLabel`), and since ARR3a this surface is the only one that reaches
	// here. Emits the throwing `__managed_slice(arr, start, end)` as the LAST op so the enclosing `try` rewrites
	// it; returns the fresh owned array. Neither argument is a managed move-in (both are plain indices).
	//
	// ⭐ **A SLICE OF A BUFFER IS A BUFFER, WHICH IS WHY THE MARK REACHES THE RESULT AND NOT ONLY THE LABEL
	// (R4.4).** `specs/managed-memory-builtin.md:slice` reads `s.length()` off the result and
	// `managed-memory-methods.bounds-slice-oob` does the same, so the surface must survive the operation — which
	// it does by riding the VALUE (`markBufferSurface`), the only carrier available when both surfaces share one
	// `GenericInstanceId` (see `Parser.bufferSurfaceValues`).
	function parseArraySlice(func IrFunction, arr ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let start = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.consumeArrayArgLabel(EndIndexArgLabel, required: false)
		let endIndex = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		let sliced = self.emitArraySliceCall(giid, arr: arr, start: start, endIndex: endIndex, range: range)
		self.markBufferSurface(sliced)
		// And a slice of an array OF buffers is an array of buffers — the same rule one container out, which is
		// why the two live side by side rather than in one test: a value can be either, and never both.
		return self.inheritArrayElementSurface(sliced, source: arr)
	end 'parseArraySlice'

	// ⭐ **A COPY OF AN ARRAY OF BUFFERS HOLDS BUFFERS (A2m).** A whole-array producer hands back a value of the
	// receiver's own instance, and the element spelling cannot ride that instance (`Array with __ManagedMemory`
	// and `Array with ByteArray` are one id) — so it rides the value here. MEASURED before this line:
	// `bufs.clone().get(0).length()` was refused as an unknown `Array` method while `bufs.get(0).length()`
	// answered.
	//
	// ⚠ **ITS CALLERS WERE THE `clone` AND `slice` ARMS; SINCE ARR3a IT IS ALSO EVERY CORPUS `Self` RETURN**
	// (`retypeInstanceMethodResult`), because the `Array`-surface `slice` is `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s declaration
	// now and the same fact has to reach it. One helper, asked from the synthesized side and from the corpus
	// side, rather than a second answer on each. ⇒ **`bufs.clone()` above is now served by that CORPUS route
	// (ARRH struck `clone` from the roster), so the measurement it records is answered by the `Self`-return
	// caller rather than by an arm of its own — which is the retirement working, not the case going away.**
	function inheritArrayElementSurface(result ValueId, source ValueId) returns ValueId
		if self.valueElementIsBufferSurface(source) 'sourceHoldsBuffers'
			self.markBufferSurfaceElements(result)
		end 'sourceHoldsBuffers'

		return result
	end 'inheritArrayElementSurface'

	function emitArraySliceCall(giid GenericInstanceId, arr ValueId, start ValueId, endIndex ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaqueSlice'
			return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(ManagedSliceOpaqueName, args: tripleArgs(arr, second: start, third: endIndex), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
		end 'opaqueSlice'
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'managedSlice'
			return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(ManagedSliceManagedName, args: quadArgs(arr, second: start, third: endIndex, fourth: self.arrayElementCloneValue(giid, range: range)), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
		end 'managedSlice'
		return self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(ManagedSliceName, args: tripleArgs(arr, second: start, third: endIndex), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: giid, range: range)
	end 'emitArraySliceCall'

	// `append(other)` — append `other`'s elements to `self`. `other` is BORROWED (read-only, not moved) and
	// PRESERVED (`append-preserves-originals`). Its element must be one the receiver's admits every value of,
	// which the rule all three spellings share enforces (`ProgramSignatures.arrayAppendArgAdmits`); only the
	// SENTENCE is per-surface, which is what `bufferSurface` selects — see the caller. A TRIVIAL receiver
	// block-copies `other`'s bytes (`__managed_append`); a CONCRETE MANAGED receiver (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b)
	// DEEP-CLONES each `other` element into `self` via `__managed_append_managed`, stamped with the element's
	// `funcAddr(__clone_<E>)`, so both arrays end up sole owners of independent elements. Returns the receiver
	// (what the statement still denotes).
	function parseArrayAppend(func IrFunction, arr ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token, bufferSurface bool, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let other = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.requireAppendArg(other, arr: arr, giid: giid, methodTok: methodTok, bufferSurface: bufferSurface)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaqueAppend'
			return self.emitArrayVoidCall(ManagedAppendOpaqueName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: other), receiver: arr, range: range)
		end 'opaqueAppend'
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'managedAppend'
			return self.emitArrayVoidCall(ManagedAppendManagedName, args: tripleArgs(arr, second: other, third: self.arrayElementCloneValue(giid, range: range)), receiver: arr, range: range)
		end 'managedAppend'
		return self.emitArrayVoidCall(ManagedAppendName, args: pairArgs(arr, second: other), receiver: arr, range: range)
	end 'parseArrayAppend'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ARGUMENT RULE ALL THREE `append` SPELLINGS MAKE, AND THE TWO SENTENCES IT SPEAKS IT IN.** The
	// whole VERDICT is `ProgramSignatures.arrayAppendArgAdmits` — one record, one operation, one precondition,
	// stated where the element types live so no door may respell it. What lives here is which sentence a
	// refusal gets, and that is a property of the SURFACE the author wrote rather than of the rule:
	//   • the BUFFER's parameter is declared `__ManagedMemory`, so its mismatch names that type and its `other`
	//     label (`builtin-type-checking.error-managed-memory-append-int` pins the wording);
	//   • `Array.append`/`appendMemory` name the RECEIVER's own instance instead, and read the parameter name
	//     off the spelling the author wrote (`appendArgParamName`), so `appendMemory` is never told to fix an
	//     `other` it does not declare.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT REPORTS E3005 AND USED TO REPORT E2015, AND THE OLD CODE WAS A FALSE CLAIM** — A3r's argument,
	// applied to a fourth site. E2015 reads *"a language construct this compiler does not implement yet"*, and
	// `append` is implemented: the PROGRAM is wrong, exactly as it is at the declared parameter this argument
	// position is the ninth sibling of. Reporting what the other eight report (`argTypeMismatchSentence`,
	// naming both types) is also what lets the receiver's element be READ OFF the message — the three
	// hand-written sentences it replaces named neither type, so `expected 'Array_Byte', got 'Array_Small'`
	// could not be said at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH ANCHOR ON THE METHOD NAME, NOT ON THE ARGUMENT, AND THAT IS THE REFERENCE'S ANCHOR.**
	// `specs/type-checking.md`'s `method-call-wrong-self-type` pins column 6 of `	arr.append("hello")` — the
	// `append` token — and its `array-of-different-element-types` pins the `append` of `	ints.append(strings)`
	// the same way. shv2 pointed at the argument on the `Array` side while pointing at the callee at every
	// DECLARED call (`SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType` anchors on the call op) and at the method on the buffer
	// side — one refusal, three anchors, decided by which door the value happened to enter. The two sides were
	// still two functions with one body until the 2026-08-07 fold, which is how they could differ at all.
	function requireAppendArg(other ValueId, arr ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token, bufferSurface bool) throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.arrayAppendArgAdmits(giid, argTag: self.tagOf(other), argNameId: self.valueNameId(other), readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'admitted'
			return
		end 'admitted'

		if bufferSurface 'bufferSentence'
			throw ParseError.builtinArgumentType(String.from(ManagedMemoryOtherParamName), expected: String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName), got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(other), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'bufferSentence'

		throw ParseError.builtinArgumentType(String.from(appendArgParamName(methodTok.value)), expected: self.valueDisplayTypeName(arr), got: self.valueDisplayTypeName(other), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
	end 'requireAppendArg'

	// ⭐ **THE COPY GATE for `slice`/`clone`/`append` on a CONCRETE-element array (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b).** A
	// TRIVIAL element passes (its copy is a correct byte blit / COW view). A CONCRETE MANAGED element (String /
	// struct / boxed union) now passes too — its copy is a per-element DEEP CLONE (`arrayElementCloneValue` stamps
	// the element's `funcAddr(__clone_<E>)` into the `_managed` runtime call), shv2's move-only answer to the
	// reference compilers' incref. What still REJECTS (E2015) is a managed element whose nested type NOTHING can
	// clone: a base-struct-less generic instance with no runtime copy of its own (a `Vector` — W162 gave a
	// DECLARED generic's instance its per-instance cloner, W173 gave the chain its runtime walk, and a
	// layout-less one with neither still has none), an OS handle, or an existential.
	//
	// ⭐ **A CONTAINER WHOSE OWN ELEMENTS ARE MANAGED (an array-of-managed-arrays, a `List with String`) WAS ON
	// THAT LIST UNTIL G18 AND IS NOT NOW.** Its copy is a 2-argument
	// `<containerClone>(box, funcAddr(elementClone))`, which `callIndirect(fn, elem)` cannot reach — an ARITY
	// bound on the entry point, never an impossibility — and a per-instance one-argument thunk closes it
	// (`SignatureIndex.managedElementRecordCopy`).
	//
	// An OPAQUE-`T` element (a generic body) is routed to `requireOpaqueArrayCopyable`
	// by `requireArrayCopyMethodSupported` instead, so only CONCRETE elements reach here.
	function requireArrayElementCopyable(giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if not self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'trivial'
			return
		end 'trivial'
		if not self.signatures.arrayElementSupportsDeepClone(giid) 'unsupportedNested'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(methodTok.value)}` on an array whose managed element has a nested type this rung cannot deep-clone — a `Vector` or other base-struct-less generic instance with no runtime copy, an OS handle, or a value held at an interface type. String / struct / boxed-union / declared-generic-instance / container elements, including nested String, struct, union, Array, chain and instance fields and a managed-element container of any depth, ARE supported.", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'unsupportedNested'
	end 'requireArrayElementCopyable'

	// ⭐ **THE ELEMENT-CLONER `funcAddr` STAMP (P1.7 slice 3b-vi-b)** — the DUAL of `arrayElementDestroyValue`. A
	// CONCRETE managed element knows its `(box) -> newBox` clone callee at this site (`__str_clone` / `__clone_<T>`
	// / `__managed_clone` for a trivial-element nested array), so the address rides the P1.5-A1 `funcAddr` code→register
	// path — the `_managed` runtime helper `callIndirect`s it per element. `requireArrayElementCopyable` has already
	// guaranteed the strategy is `direct` (an element cloner must satisfy `callIndirect(fn, elem)`); a `managedElements`/
	// `unsupported` strategy at the TOP element is a gate drift, not a valid program.
	function arrayElementCloneValue(giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		match self.signatures.containerElementCloneStrategy(giid) 'strategy'
			direct(callee) then return self.emitRuntimeFuncAddr(callee, range: range)
			managedElements then panic("arrayElementCloneValue: a top-level managed array element resolved to `managedElements` — requireArrayElementCopyable gates this to a `direct` element cloner")
			unsupported then panic("arrayElementCloneValue: a top-level managed array element resolved to `unsupported` — requireArrayElementCopyable rejects it at the front end")
		end 'strategy'
	end 'arrayElementCloneValue'

	// The VALUE type an element is loaded as and checked against — the element's declared type retyped to its
	// usable value form (a struct → `structRef`, a boxed union → its `named(<union>)`, a String → text, a
	// nested Array / instance → `genericInstance` so `retrieved.count()` dispatches, a trivial element → its
	// own declared type, alias name intact so the shift rule keeps reading its low bound).
	//
	// It is TWO shared steps and nothing of its own. `ProgramSignatures.declaredSlotType` resolves what the
	// declaration sweep could not yet name — a generic alias (`ByteArray` = `Array with Byte`), a function
	// alias, a float alias — and adopts the id into this file's interner (a ranged alias that shifted the
	// interning order would otherwise misresolve, OPEN #52); `classifyUnionPayload`/`payloadValueTypeOf` then
	// give the same value form a union payload and a struct field read. The alias→instance step used to be
	// spelled here as well as there, which is exactly how the FUNCTION-alias element went missing: an
	// `Array with <function alias>` element stayed `named`, i.e. an int.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FLOAT ARM BELOW IS WHAT KEEPS A FLOAT ELEMENT OFF `payloadValueTypeOf`'s PANIC — NOT E2062,
	// WHICH NEVER HELD IT SHUT (A4d).** The line here used to say the type ARGUMENT gate caught every float
	// element one step earlier. It did not, in EITHER direction: a diagnostic recorded at a `typealias` is
	// RECORDED, not thrown, so the file's bodies are walked anyway and the accessor is minted regardless;
	// and `[1.5, 2.5]`'s instance is MINTED by the compiler and never passes a type-argument gate at all.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE SLOT'S DECLARED TYPE, NOT A `giid`, because a `Map`'s VALUE column asks the identical
	// question of a different slot.** Keyed on the instance it would have had to re-derive "which argument is
	// the slot" per container, which is the enumeration a two-column container breaks; keyed on the TYPE, an
	// `Array` element and a `Map` value are one question with one answer.
	function containerSlotValueType(slotType MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		let declared = self.resolvedSlotType(slotType)
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(declared)
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return declared
		end 'instance'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.named 'named'
			// ⛔ **A FOURTH COPY OF THE GENERIC-ALIAS ARM STOOD HERE AND WAS PROVABLY DEAD (A4k review).** It read
			// `isGenericAlias(name)` and re-tagged to the instance — but `declaredSlotType` one line up already
			// asked that, over `denotedSlotType`, and its hit RETURNS a `genericInstance` the `instance` arm above
			// takes. Reaching this line with tag `named` therefore MEANS the name is not a generic alias, and the
			// two lookups cannot disagree: `adoptType` re-interns by the SAME name string, so the artifact table's
			// spelling and the index's are one. A live-looking copy of an arm the shared door owns is what A4k was
			// about; it may not survive one door down from the door that fixed it.
			let name = self.localTypeName(declared, asker: "containerSlotValueType")
			// ⭐⭐ **A RANGED-ALIAS ELEMENT KEEPS ITS NAME, AND THAT IS A TYPE-IDENTITY FIX, NOT A REFINEMENT
			// (P1.8 Slice E).** `classifyUnionPayload` below answers a STORAGE question — what width does this
			// payload occupy — and its answer for a ranged int alias is the bare `integer` the alias erases
			// to. That is right for a union payload and WRONG for an array element read, because the value it
			// types is handed straight back to source, where the NAME is what decides an array literal's
			// instance (`arrayLiteralInstanceFor` reads the tag): reading three `Byte`s out of an
			// `Array with Byte` and writing `[x, y, z]` produced an `Array with integer` — an 8-byte stride
			// over values that came out of a 1-byte one — so a byte read back through a literal was no longer
			// a byte. MEASURED before this line, on `String.from([x, y, z])` over a `b"Hi!"`'s own elements:
			// `E3005 requires a Array with Byte, but its argument is Array_int`, a false reject of a program
			// both references accept. It is also what makes an array read AGREE with a function return, which
			// never erased: `function pick(v Byte) returns Byte` already yields a `named`-tagged value, so
			// `[pick(72)]` was an `Array with Byte` while `[bytes.get(0)]` was not.
			if let underlying = try self.signatures.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'rangedAlias'
				// A FLOAT-underlying alias resolves to `float` exactly as it does everywhere else
				// (`resolveFloatAliasType`) — the `named` tag cannot carry a float VALUE, whose class is XMM.
				return MaxonType.float if tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(underlying)) else declared
			end 'rangedAlias'
		end 'named'
		// ⭐ **A BARE FLOAT SLOT IS A FLOAT, and it is answered HERE rather than by the payload classifier
		// below, which has no arm for one.** `classifyUnionPayload` reports `floatPayload` and
		// `payloadValueTypeOf` PANICS on it — *"a float payload has no lowerable value type"* — because a
		// union payload's float is refused before any load reaches it. A container ELEMENT's is not refused:
		// it rides the slot as its 64 bits (`containerElementWord`) and reads back through the inverse
		// reinterpretation, so the type it reads as is `float`. MEASURED before this arm, on
		// `Array with Real` + `.get(0)`: that panic, with a stack trace and no `error E….`
		if tagIsFloat(tag) 'floatSlot'
			return MaxonType.float
		end 'floatSlot'
		return payloadValueTypeOf(classifyUnionPayload(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: declared, slotScopeFilePath: self.filePath), declared: declared)
	end 'containerSlotValueType'

	// ⭐ **WHAT A SLOT'S STORED TYPE ACTUALLY DENOTES, adopted into THIS file's interner** — a generic alias to
	// its instance (`ByteArray` = `Array with Byte`), a FUNCTION alias to `function`, a float alias to `float`.
	// The `into`/`slotScopeFilePath` pair is the same at every slot door in this file and was spelled out at
	// each of them; a slot type that shifted the interning order otherwise misresolves (OPEN #52), which is a
	// silent wrong type rather than a diagnostic, so the triple is written once.
	//
	// ⚠ It is the FIRST STEP of `containerSlotValueType` above and not a synonym for it: that one goes on to
	// retype the result into the value form a READ hands back (a union payload, a struct field). The doors that
	// only need to know what the declaration NAMES — the column type checks — stop here.
	function resolvedSlotType(slotType MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		return self.signatures.declaredSlotType(slotType, into: self.artifact.typeNames, slotScopeFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'resolvedSlotType'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN ELEMENT ARGUMENT MUST AGREE WITH THE INSTANCE'S ELEMENT TYPE.** The `Array` mutators are
	// PARSER-INTRINSIC — `push`/`set` emit `__managed_push`/`__managed_set` directly, so they
	// have no `FuncSignature` and never reach `SemanticCheck.validateCall` — and until this existed nothing
	// asked the question at all: `moveElementIntoContainer` asked only about OWNERSHIP. All three symptoms were
	// MEASURED, and none of them was a diagnostic:
	//
	//   • `Array with String` + `push(42)` — compiled clean, then the array's `__managed_decref` walk freed `42`
	//     as a String pointer. A WILD FREE (0xC0000005).
	//   • `Array with Integer` + `push("hello")` — compiled clean and printed the record's raw address as an
	//     integer. A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.
	//   • `Array with Integer` + `push(1.5)` — reached the backend and panicked there on an xmm→gpr move,
	//     an internal error naming no source position, for a conversion the language refuses everywhere else.
	//
	// **IT IS `requireFieldType`'S BODY, AND DELIBERATELY SO**: an array element is a storage slot of a
	// container exactly as a struct field is, so it takes the SAME classifier and the SAME two verdict doors
	// — `assignableValue` (the coercion-site rule `checkDeclaredType` plus the widening an `ok` verdict owes,
	// in one call) for a trivial element, `requireManagedValueMatches` (the matching heap kind, plus the
	// aggregate NAME two `structRef` pointers share a tag over) for a managed one. No sentence is composed
	// here: the array door and an ordinary assignment cannot come to explain one rule two ways.
	//
	// It is NOT folded into `requireFieldType` itself for one measured reason: that function names its site
	// with `fieldSiteName`, a `String`, built INSIDE its managed arm. A shared core taking a pre-built name
	// would make every SCALAR field pay an allocation it does not use today — the cost `FunctionTypeDoor`'s
	// own header records at +1,852 in `phase:parse` for exactly this shape. What is written twice is a
	// three-line composition; the RULE is single-homed.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE MANAGED/TRIVIAL ARM IS CHOSEN BY THE CLASSIFIER AND BY NOTHING ELSE, AND THAT COSTS A MEASURED
	// ALLOCATION WORTH PAYING.** `ProgramSignatures.containerElementIsManaged` answers the same question without
	// boxing anything, and asking it here instead saves exactly one `PayloadClass` box per trivial-element
	// `push`/`set`/`insert` SITE (measured on the ladder: 32 allocations at rung 0, doubling to 1,024 at
	// rung 5 — 0.007% of the compile). It is NOT taken. The two predicates are SEPARATE implementations of
	// "does this own heap?" — they already disagree on a generic-ALIAS `named` type, which `typeIsManaged`
	// calls managed and `classifyUnionPayload` calls `undeclaredName` — and they agree at THIS door only
	// because `declaredSlotType` resolves that alias first. A rung whose entire subject is two things
	// disagreeing about a type does not buy 0.007% with a second opinion whose failure mode is the wild free
	// it exists to stop.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RUNS BEFORE THE MOVE-IN, NOT AFTER**, and that ordering is the fix rather than an ordering
	// preference: `moveElementIntoContainer` PROMOTES a borrowed record by the ELEMENT's protocol
	// (`tagIsByteRecord`), so a struct pushed into a `String` array would be copied as a fused byte record
	// before any check could see it. It hands back the value the slot wants, so the caller rebinds — the
	// widening is unreachable here (`Array with Float` is E2062 at the type argument), but a caller that
	// stored the value it already held is precisely the bug the shared door exists to make unwriteable.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN OPAQUE `T` ELEMENT IS NOT CHECKED, and that is the same ruling every other opaque door makes.**
	// A shared generic body compiles ONCE, so `self.items.push(item)` has no concrete element type to compare
	// against; the instantiation's argument is checked in the CALLER's frame, where the type is concrete.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARRAY LITERAL IS NOT A CALLER, and does not need to be.** `[a, b, c]` has no DECLARED element
	// type to violate — its first element FIXES the instance (`arrayLiteralInstanceFor`) and every later one
	// is compared against it by `requireArrayLiteralElementMatches`. It also has no method token to name.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN INTEGRAL ELEMENT SLOT COERCES A CHARACTER LITERAL BEFORE IT COMPARES (A5m-ab).** `a.push('e')`
	// into an `Array with Character` needs nothing — the literal already IS a Character — but pushing one into
	// an array of a ranged integer alias reaches the same codepoint conversion every other declared slot makes
	// (`integerizedOperand`). It needs the value's TOKEN SPAN, which is why the callers hand over `valueStart`
	// (the same position `moveElementIntoContainer` reads).
	function requireContainerElementType(func IrFunction, giid GenericInstanceId, value ValueId, valueStart TokenIndex, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaqueInSharedBody'
			return value
		end 'opaqueInSharedBody'

		let declared = self.resolvedSlotType(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))
		// ⭐ **A `Promise` ELEMENT IS THE ONE SLOT WHOSE VALUE CHANGES TYPE ON THE WAY IN (P1.5-B2c)** — a
		// spawn's handle is minted `integer` and the storage names `Promise with (T[, E])`, so the door that
		// checks the element is also the door that retypes it. It is `assignableValue`'s "verdict and
		// coercion in ONE call" shape, which is why it is asked HERE and not by the two arms below: they both
		// compare a tag the promise deliberately does not carry.
		if typeIsPromiseStorage(self.signatures, t: declared) 'promiseElement'
			return try self.storedPromiseValue(func, value: value, storage: typeNameIdOf(declared), anchor: methodTok)
		end 'promiseElement'

		let element = try self.integerizedOperand(value, expected: maxonTypeTag(declared), start: valueStart, pastEnd: self.pos)
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: declared, slotScopeFilePath: self.filePath)
		if payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'managed'
			try self.requireManagedSlotValueMatches(class, declared: declared, value: element, placeDoor: FunctionTypeDoor.containerElement, placeName: methodTok.value, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.containerElementArgument, anchor: methodTok)
			return element
		end 'managed'

		// The `field` door, because an element IS a slot of the container's storage and the noun a function
		// value's E3005 needs is the container-slot one. A fourth noun would have to be minted in
		// `FunctionTypeDoor`, and there is no fourth RULE here to justify one.
		//
		// The VOICE is the other axis and is the mutator's own: `push` is a METHOD and its element is that
		// method's ARGUMENT, so the type refusal is the argument sentence rather than the assignment one.
		// See `SlotMismatchVoice`, which records what calling `push` a "variable" was measured to say.
		let stored = try self.assignableValue(methodTok, value: element, declared: maxonTypeTag(declared), declaredNameId: typeNameIdOf(declared), door: FunctionTypeDoor.field, placeOwner: EmptySourceText, voice: SlotMismatchVoice.containerElementArgument)
		// ⭐⭐ **THE ELEMENT'S DECLARED RANGE BINDS IT AT RUNTIME TOO (A1f-arrayelem) — AND THE GUARD GOES
		// HERE, AT THE STORE, WHICH IS THE LAST PLACE THAT KNOWS THE ELEMENT TYPE.** This door owed only
		// the compile-time half until this rung, on the argument the call-argument door owed it: the element
		// travels as `__managed_push`/`__managed_set`'s third argument, and the callee it travels
		// into is a SHARED `Array` body whose parameter is the OPAQUE element type — so A1f's cure, a guard
		// at the callee's entry, has no narrowed parameter to stand behind and structurally cannot reach it.
		//
		// ⭐ **BUT THE OTHER HALF OF THAT ARGUMENT — "a guard would land PAST the call" — WAS NEVER TRUE
		// HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE.** A general call argument has no alias at parse time (the
		// parameter belongs to a callee that may be in another file — `RangeCheckArgSite`), so it can record
		// no positioned site at all. An element's alias is RIGHT HERE, in the instance's declared element
		// type, and this runs BEFORE the store call is emitted — so `recordRangeCheckSite`'s
		// `opRefs.count()` is the ordinal the `__managed_*` call is about to take, and the split lands the
		// cascade immediately in front of it. It is the ordinary alias-named site every other door records.
		//
		// ⚠ **WHAT IS STILL UNENFORCED IS NOT A DOOR** — it is the zero-initialized slot `Array.resize`
		// EXPOSES, which no value ever crosses into, so there is nothing for a store guard to stand in front
		// of. Pinned by `specs-shv2/safety.md`'s `divide-by-zero-fault-through-a-resized-array-slot`.
		self.recordRangeCheckSite(stored, targetAlias: self.declaredAliasOfType(declared), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		return stored
	end 'requireContainerElementType'

	// ⭐⭐ **STORE A SPAWN'S HANDLE IN A `Promise with (T[, E])` SLOT (P1.5-B2c).** The value that comes back
	// is the SAME machine word — shv2 stores the green-thread handle rather than boxing it (see
	// `PromiseType.maxon`) — retyped at the storage instance, which is what carries the result type and the
	// error type past this frame.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE THREE COMPARISONS ARE THE WHOLE FEATURE.** A `Promise` type is a CLAIM about the thunk, and
	// every downstream answer — what `await` yields, what `(e)` binds, what `try` propagates — is read off
	// the claim rather than off the callee, because the callee is unreachable from a container. So the claim
	// is checked once, HERE, at the only door a promise can enter storage through, and after that no
	// consumer needs to re-derive anything:
	//
	//   • the RESULT type, or `await` types its value at a type the thunk never returned — a `String`-typed
	//     read of an `Integer` handle is a wild pointer;
	//   • THROWS-NESS, both ways: a throwing thunk in a `Promise with T` erases the error (the erasure this
	//     whole slice exists to make unrepresentable), and a non-throwing thunk in a `Promise with (T, E)`
	//     hands `try await` a `gt.threw` word the trampoline never wrote — `AsyncReleaser.maxon`'s header
	//     records that it is GARBAGE for a non-throwing callee;
	//   • the ERROR type itself, or one enum's ordinals are decoded under another's name.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND THE CONTAINER ADOPTS THE THREAD.** `consumePromiseThread` is the same door `await` uses: the
	// spawn's handle leaves this frame's ownership, so the statement-end drain does not `__gt_promise_drop` a
	// thread the array now holds. What the container does NOT gain is a drop — `specs/async-await.md`'s
	// *"Known boundary"* paragraph says a promise that escapes into storage is untracked.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND "UNTRACKED" MEANS NOTHING RECLAIMS IT. THERE IS NO EXIT DRAIN.** This door does not hand the
	// thread to a later reclaimer — `consumePromiseThread`'s FIRST statement is `removeFromPendingTemps`, so
	// it REMOVES the thread from the only reclaimer this frame had (the statement-end `drainPendingTemps`) —
	// and no program-exit walk of live GTs exists to take over: `__gt_live_count` is a leak COUNTER read once
	// by `__mm_leak_check`, never a worklist anything drains. So a promise stored in a container and never
	// awaited LEAKS its GT struct, and since A2 it leaks with it the references the spawn took to the
	// callee's managed arguments (`Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon` — the releaser runs at the await or at the
	// drop, and this promise reaches neither).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE EXIT GATES DO SEE IT, WHICH IS THE ONE MERCY — IT IS A REPORTED LEAK, NOT A SILENT ONE.**
	// MEASURED: a stored, never-awaited promise over a SCALAR-argument callee exits **75**
	// (`RuntimeAbort.greenThreadLeak`, the `__gt_live_count != 0` gate); over a `String`-argument callee it
	// exits **101** (`leakDetected` — the heap gate is tested first and the argument's outstanding reference
	// trips it). Closing this needs a drop the CONTAINER owes for its promise elements, which is the
	// documented boundary's own rung and not this door's to invent.
	function storedPromiseValue(func IrFunction, value ValueId, storage GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// A promise read back OUT of storage and put into another slot of the same type carries its claim
		// already — it was checked when it was first stored, and there is no `asyncCall` left to re-check it
		// against. Same instance means same claim, so this is an identity rather than a second verdict.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **BUT "READ BACK OUT OF STORAGE" IS THE OWNERSHIP MARK, NOT THE TYPE — THE TYPE IS THE ONE THING A
		// STORE SITE OVERWRITES.** `retypeValue` below stamps the storage instance onto the SPAWN's own ValueId,
		// so a spawn stored through TWO doors — `push` on one arm of a branch, `set` on the other — arrives at
		// whichever door the parser reads SECOND already wearing the type this test reads. Taking the identity
		// path there skips `consumePromiseThread`, so that path never records the move; `reconcileMovesAtMerge`
		// then finds the binding live on that edge and emits `__gt_promise_drop`, which CANCELS the thread the
		// container is now holding. The slot keeps a handle that never completes, and a non-blocking poller
		// (`Testing/SpecWorkerPool.drainHasAnswered`) waits on it forever.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED: THAT IS WHAT WEDGED THE 🚩 PHASE 1 GATE.** `SpecWorkerPool.sendAndDrain` arms a slot with
		// `push` on its first dispatch and with `set` on every dispatch after, so the shv2-compiled harness
		// completed EXACTLY ONE job per worker and then stalled on a cancelled drain — deterministically, with
		// the watchdog's `drain complete: no` naming the symptom and nothing naming the cause.
		//
		// `valueOwnsGreenThread` is the question actually meant here (its own header: *"does THIS frame owe a
		// `__gt_promise_drop`?"*): the spawn sets it, a container read never does, and no store site rewrites
		// it — so unlike the type it answers the same at the first door and at the fifth.
		// `specs-shv2/async-promise-drop.md`'s two `branch-store-into-a-container-*` cases pin both orders.
		if not self.valueOwnsGreenThread(value) and self.tagOf(value) == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and self.valueNameId(value) == storage 'alreadyStored'
			return value
		end 'alreadyStored'

		let callee = try self.spawnCalleeStoredHere(func, value: value, storage: storage, anchor: anchor)
		try self.requirePromiseStorageDescribesCallee(callee, storage: storage, anchor: anchor)
		self.consumePromiseThread(value)
		self.retypeValue(value, valueType: MaxonType.genericInstance(storage))
		return value
	end 'storedPromiseValue'

	// The callee whose spawn `value` is — the one thing a storage claim can be checked against. Anything that
	// is not an `async` spawn's handle is refused here rather than by the tag doors below, whose sentence
	// would compare a `Promise with …` against an `int` and tell the author nothing about why.
	function spawnCalleeStoredHere(func IrFunction, value ValueId, storage GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) returns ByteArray throws ParseError
		if self.valueOwnsGreenThread(value) 'aSpawn'
			match self.asyncCallCalleeOf(func, promise: value) 'callee'
				found(callee) then return callee
				none then break 'callee'
			end 'callee'
		end 'aSpawn'

		throw ParseError.promiseStorageMismatch("only an `async` spawn can be stored in '{String.from(self.signatures.instanceDisplayName(storage))}' — the value here is {self.valueDisplayTypeName(value)}", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'spawnCalleeStoredHere'

	// The three comparisons `storedPromiseValue`'s header sets out, in the order an author most wants to hear
	// them: what it returns, then whether it throws at all, then what it throws.
	function requirePromiseStorageDescribesCallee(callee ByteArray, storage GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		try self.requirePromiseStorageResult(callee, storage: storage, anchor: anchor)
		try self.requirePromiseStorageError(callee, storage: storage, anchor: anchor)
	end 'requirePromiseStorageDescribesCallee'

	// The RESULT half. Both sides are adopted into THIS file's interner before they are compared — the
	// storage's argument through `resolvedSlotType` (which is what `requireContainerElementType` resolves the
	// element itself through) and the callee's return type through `calleeReturnType` (`returnTypeOf(…, into:
	// self.artifact.typeNames)`) — so the `(tag, nameId)` pair is a comparison of two types and not of two
	// tables' numbering. A VOID callee cannot arrive: `Promise with <nothing>` is unspellable, so a void
	// spawn has no storage type to be stored in and reaches the sentence below naming its own absence.
	function requirePromiseStorageResult(callee ByteArray, storage GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let claimed = self.resolvedSlotType(self.signatures.substitutedInstanceArg(storage, position: PromiseResultArgPosition))
		match self.calleeReturnType(callee) 'r'
			void then throw ParseError.promiseStorageMismatch(self.promiseStorageResultDetail(storage, claimed: claimed, actual: "nothing (it returns no value)"), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
			value(returned) then try self.requirePromiseStorageResultAgrees(returned, claimed: claimed, storage: storage, anchor: anchor)
		end 'r'
	end 'requirePromiseStorageResult'

	// The comparison itself, split out because the arm above is a `match` arm and this needs a labelled `if`.
	function requirePromiseStorageResultAgrees(returned MaxonType, claimed MaxonType, storage GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if maxonTypeTag(returned) == maxonTypeTag(claimed) and typeNameIdOf(returned) == typeNameIdOf(claimed) 'agrees'
			return
		end 'agrees'

		throw ParseError.promiseStorageMismatch(self.promiseStorageResultDetail(storage, claimed: claimed, actual: "'{self.displayTypeNameOf(maxonTypeTag(returned), nameId: typeNameIdOf(returned))}'"), line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requirePromiseStorageResultAgrees'

	function promiseStorageResultDetail(storage GenericInstanceId, claimed MaxonType, actual String) returns String
		return "'{String.from(self.signatures.instanceDisplayName(storage))}' names the result type '{self.displayTypeNameOf(maxonTypeTag(claimed), nameId: typeNameIdOf(claimed))}', but this promise's function returns {actual}"
	end 'promiseStorageResultDetail'

	// The THROWS half — three outcomes, and the two mismatched ones are different mistakes with different
	// cures, which is why they are two sentences on one code.
	function requirePromiseStorageError(callee ByteArray, storage GenericInstanceId, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let storageName = String.from(self.signatures.instanceDisplayName(storage))
		match promiseStorageThrows(self.signatures, gid: storage) 'claimed'
			// `Promise with T` — the storage names no error, so the thunk must throw none. A throwing one
			// stored here is THE erasure: `try await` would have no type to bind `e` at, no static payload
			// type to release, and nothing to check the enclosing `throws` against.
			none then try self.requirePromiseStorageErrorless(callee, storageName: storageName, anchor: anchor)
			throwing(claimedError) then try self.requirePromiseStorageErrorMatches(callee, claimedError: claimedError, storageName: storageName, anchor: anchor)
		end 'claimed'
	end 'requirePromiseStorageError'

	function requirePromiseStorageErrorless(callee ByteArray, storageName String, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let clause = self.signatures.throwsOf(callee)
		if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(clause) 'agrees'
			return
		end 'agrees'

		let thrown = try throwsClauseName(clause) otherwise panic("requirePromiseStorageErrorless: a clause `throwsClauseIsThrowing` accepted has a name")
		throw ParseError.promiseStorageMismatch("cannot store a promise from a function that throws '{thrown}' in '{storageName}': it names the result type only, which would erase the error type — declare the storage as 'Promise with (T, {thrown})' so 'try await' can bind and release the error", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requirePromiseStorageErrorless'

	// The storage names an error type, so the thunk must throw exactly it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE NON-THROWING ARM IS NOT A COURTESY.** `gt.threw` is written unconditionally by the trampoline
	// and is GARBAGE for a non-throwing callee (`Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon`'s header), so a `try await`
	// admitted on this claim would branch on that garbage and hand its `otherwise (e)` a fabricated error.
	function requirePromiseStorageErrorMatches(callee ByteArray, claimedError String, storageName String, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let clause = self.signatures.throwsOf(callee)
		if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(clause) 'calleeThrowsNothing'
			throw ParseError.promiseStorageMismatch("'{storageName}' names the error type '{claimedError}', but this promise's function does not throw — store it in a 'Promise with T' instead", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'calleeThrowsNothing'

		let thrown = try throwsClauseName(clause) otherwise panic("requirePromiseStorageErrorMatches: a clause `throwsClauseIsThrowing` accepted has a name")
		if thrown.equals(claimedError) 'agrees'
			return
		end 'agrees'

		throw ParseError.promiseStorageMismatch("'{storageName}' names the error type '{claimedError}', but this promise's function throws '{thrown}'", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requirePromiseStorageErrorMatches'

	// ⛔⛔ **`elementAccessCallee` STOOD HERE, AND THE BOUNDS-CHECK ELISION WENT WITH THE MEMBERS IT SERVED
	// (W190).** ⚖ USER RULING, 2026-08-06: *"we should be able to add an optimization to remove the bounds
	// check when using static indexes on a vector (fixed size)"* — and it was built, at `S2m-b`: a second
	// runtime entry (`__managed_get_in_bounds` / `__managed_set_in_bounds`) picked when the container carried
	// its SIZE in its TYPE and the index was a compile-time constant inside it.
	//
	// ⭐ **WHAT RETIRED IT IS NOT A CHANGE OF MIND ABOUT THE OPTIMIZATION — IT IS THAT ITS SUBJECT LEFT.** The
	// proof is a fact about the CALL SITE (this index, this size); `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `get`/`set` are ONE
	// shared body reached by a CALL, taking `index` as a runtime parameter, and no callee can see whether its
	// caller wrote a literal. The elision therefore had exactly one live pair of call sites — the vector's own
	// dispatch arms — and every other container declined by itself (`genericInstanceFixedSize` answers
	// `NoFixedSize`), so with those arms gone this router could only ever return its argument.
	// `specs-shv2/vector-static-index.md` keeps all eighteen of its cases, which pin the ANSWERS, and states
	// what is no longer true of the emitted code.
	//
	// ⚠ **RECOVERING IT LATER IS AN INLINER'S JOB AND NOT THIS DOOR'S**: once `Vector.get`'s body is inlined
	// at a constant index, the test is a comparison of two constants and the ordinary folding removes it — one
	// mechanism instead of a second runtime entry per accessor.

	// `( expr )` — a single positional array-method argument (`push(x)`, `get(i)`, `reserve(n)`).
	function parseArrayOneArg(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let arg = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		return arg
	end 'parseArrayOneArg'

	// The ` , <label> : ` separating one of a builtin method's positional arguments from the next —
	// `set`/`insert`'s `value:`, `slice`'s `endIndex:`, `sendFrom`'s `offset:` and `length:`. ONE home for that
	// shape, so a builtin method names its label and inherits the same "must be labelled `<label>:`"
	// diagnostic; the cursor is left on the token that begins that argument's value.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A TWO-ARGUMENT RULE, AND THE DIAGNOSTIC SAID IT WAS.** `__ManagedSocket.sendFrom` is the
	// compiler's first THREE-argument builtin method and calls this TWICE, so the throw below may not name an
	// arity or a position: it once read "the second argument of a two-argument builtin method", which told an
	// author about the second argument of a two-argument method while complaining about the third of a
	// three-argument one. The label is the only thing this reader knows and the only thing it now says.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE LABEL IS REQUIRED ON AN `Array` AND OPTIONAL ON THE `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER, AND BOTH HALVES ARE
	// MEASURED (R4.4).** `stdlib/Array.maxon` declares `set`/`slice` as ordinary methods, so E2053's "second and
	// subsequent arguments must be named" applies — the bootstrap answers exactly that for `arr.set(0, 99)` on an
	// `Array with Int`. A `__ManagedMemory` member is a compiler BUILTIN, and the bootstrap accepts both
	// spellings for one: `mm.set(0, 99)` and `mm.set(0, value: 99)` both reach lowering there, and a WRONG label
	// is `E3003 unknown parameter name`. The corpus needs both directions —
	// `specs/managed-memory-builtin.md` writes `set(0, value: 11)` and `slice(1, endIndex: 4)` while
	// `specs/managed-memory-methods.md` writes `set(10, 99)` and `slice(0, 10)` — so "optional, and checked when
	// present" is the only rule that admits the corpus, and it may not leak onto `Array`.
	//
	// ⇒ The requirement rides in from the ONE surface answer (`dispatchArrayMethod`'s `isManagedMemory`), so
	// there is no second derivation of which spelling a receiver takes.
	function consumeArrayArgLabel(label ByteArray, required bool) throws ParseError
		if not required and not self.atArgLabel(self.pos) 'positionalSecondArgument'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
			return
		end 'positionalSecondArgument'

		let labelTok = try self.consumeArgLabel()
		if not labelTok.value.equals(label) 'wrongLabel'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("this builtin method's argument must be labelled `{String.from(label)}:` (got '{String.from(labelTok.value)}:')", line: labelTok.line, column: labelTok.column)
		end 'wrongLabel'
	end 'consumeArrayArgLabel'

	// `, <name> :` — the token shape an argument LABEL is spelled as, and the NAME token it carries.
	// Written once because a reader's VERDICT is its own while the SHAPE is shared: `consumeArrayArgLabel`
	// asserts the ONE label it expects, and this returns the token for a caller that decides for itself. A
	// String's `slice` DISPATCHED on which of its two labels it got until W49 wave 4 retired it; that reader
	// is gone, and the split is kept because a second copy of "this is what a label looks like" is a copy
	// that can come to disagree about it.
	function consumeArgLabel() returns Token throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.comma)
		let labelTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.colon)

		return labelTok
	end 'consumeArgLabel'

	// Is the ` , <name> : ` label shape at `index`? The LOOKAHEAD half of the consumer above — asked where a
	// label is OPTIONAL, so a `,` that is not followed by one is read as a POSITIONAL argument rather than
	// reported as a missing label (R4.4). It is the same relationship `memberCallFollows` has with the chain walk
	// it stops: a predicate and a consumer over one shape, spelled once each and next to each other, so neither
	// can come to admit a token the other rejects.
	function atArgLabel(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.comma and self.kindAt(index + 1) == TokenKind.identifier and self.kindAt(index + 2) == TokenKind.colon
	end 'atArgLabel'

	// ⭐ **`( index , value: X )` — THE INDEXED ELEMENT STORE, whichever member spells it**: the index
	// positional, the value labelled `value:` on every surface but the buffer (see `consumeArrayArgLabel`).
	// Emits the `__managed_set` / `__managed_mem_set` / `__list_insert` call and returns its result. The value
	// argument is parsed AFTER the index so the call is the last op the enclosing `try` rewrites.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO SERVE `insert` TOO, ON A `throwing` FLAG, AND ARR1 TOOK BOTH AWAY.** The `Array`'s
	// `insert` left the roster and is served from `stdlib/Array.maxon`, which was the flag's only `false`.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE SPELLINGS IT SERVES ARE NOW ONE CALL SITE (W190).** `List.insert` went with the list's
	// surface at W153 and `Vector.set` with the sized container's here, so what is left is
	// `dispatchArrayMethod`'s single `set` arm serving BOTH of its receivers — which is also what keeps
	// `secondArgLabelled` live rather than a branch nothing can reach: it is `not isManagedMemory`, `true` for
	// the array surface's `set(i, value: x)` and `false` for the buffer's positional one.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY SPELLING OWES THE THROW PATH'S HALF OF THE MOVE-IN CONTRACT.** `moveElementIntoContainer`
	// below runs BEFORE the call is emitted, so a callee that refuses owns the element and must destroy it —
	// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitDestroyRejectedElement`, which every entry reached through here calls from its
	// reject block. Shipping a fourth spelling without it is a leak, measured twice now (R4.6 on `__managed_set`,
	// and again on `__list_insert` in the review of spec-port `list`).
	function parseArraySet(func IrFunction, arr ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, callee ByteArray, methodTok Token, secondArgLabelled bool, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let index = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		try self.consumeArrayArgLabel(ArrayValueLabel, required: secondArgLabelled)
		// The value is a managed-element STORE, so it is handled exactly as `push` is —
		// `moveElementIntoContainer` promotes a borrowed String, co-owns an owned value a live binding keeps,
		// adopts an owned temporary, and co-owns a borrowed aggregate. `__managed_set` destroys the LIVE occupant it overwrites (its element_destroy walk of one
		// slot), so no leak. The TYPE check precedes the move-in for the reason `requireContainerElementType` states.
		let valueStart = self.pos
		let raw = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		let checked = try self.requireContainerElementType(func, giid: giid, value: raw, valueStart: valueStart, methodTok: methodTok)
		let value = try self.moveElementIntoContainer(checked, giid: giid, valueStart: valueStart)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// ⭐⭐ **THE THROWING `set` IS VOID AND ITS TAG MUST SAY SO — the guard the void mutators call cannot
		// reach here, and the check that CAN reads only this tag.** A `try` target is parsed with
		// `resultUsed: false` by design (`parseTryCallTarget`), so the arm's `requireVoidMethodIsStatement` is
		// blind to `let x = try arr.set(…) otherwise …` — the spelling the corpus actually writes, since a
		// throwing call wants a `try`. There `parseTry`'s `voidInValue` decides, and it reads NOTHING but
		// `tagOf(result)`. Tagged `integer`, that check was told there is a value, and the
		// program COMPILED, LINKED AND RAN (exit 0) with `x` bound to the ok-return's literal `0`
		// (`emitElementStoreAndOkReturn`) — while the runnable oracle refuses it, `E3059 type mismatch:
		// ''stdlib.Array.set' does not return a value'`. Found in review of D11: the arm is the THIRTEENTH
		// void one, and the only one whose refusal is DERIVED from the tag rather than written in the arm.
		// ⚠ The Std entry still ok-returns `(0, flag)` — a throwing `void` runtime function is normal
		// (`__managed_set_length`, `__managed_set_byte` and `__managed_mem_grow` are all declared `StdReturnType.void`
		// and all throw); what this tag decides is whether MAXON believes the call yields something.
		return self.emitRuntimeCall(callee, args: tripleArgs(arr, second: index, third: value), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
	end 'parseArraySet'

	// `( expr )` — a single element-VALUE argument (`push(x)`), TYPE-CHECKED against the instance's element
	// type and then MOVED into the array (P1.7 slice 3): a managed element is consumed so the array owns it
	// (its decref walk destroys it), a trivial element passes through. The value's start position is captured
	// so a bare-local source can be poisoned (E3102).
	function parseContainerElementArg(func IrFunction, giid GenericInstanceId, methodTok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let valueStart = self.pos
		let value = try self.parseExpression(func, block: self.currentBlock)
		// The move-in MUST run before the `)` is consumed: `rhsWasBareLocalReference` reads `[valueStart, self.pos)`
		// to decide whether the arg is a bare local to POISON, and a consumed `)` would widen that window past a
		// single-identifier arg (`push(node)`) — the store would silently take NO reference and the container
		// would hold a slot it does not own, released a second time when the binding drops at scope exit (a
		// double-free / over-release, exit 101). The ordering requirement is unchanged by the 2026-08-12
		// ruling; only the op that goes missing is — an `__mm_incref` where it used to be a move poison.
		let checked = try self.requireContainerElementType(func, giid: giid, value: value, valueStart: valueStart, methodTok: methodTok)
		let moved = try self.moveElementIntoContainer(checked, giid: giid, valueStart: valueStart)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		return moved
	end 'parseContainerElementArg'

	// ⭐ **STORE A PARSED ELEMENT INTO A MANAGED-ELEMENT ARRAY (P1.7 slice 3).** The array holds exactly ONE
	// reference per slot (its `__managed_decref` walk releases it), so the source is handled exactly as a
	// struct-field / union-payload store is (`moveManagedValueInto`): a borrowed String literal is promoted to
	// an owned copy, an owned value a LIVE BINDING keeps is CO-OWNED (the binding stays readable and releases
	// its own reference at scope exit — the 2026-08-12 ruling), an owned TEMPORARY is ADOPTED (de-listed from
	// the pending drops), and a borrowed struct/union is CO-OWNED too (no clone exists, so the container takes
	// a second reference to the same record rather than refusing — the transitive-consume ruling of
	// 2026-08-04). A TRIVIAL-element array's value owns no heap and passes through unchanged. Shared by
	// push/set/insert. `valueStart` locates the value's first token — its bare-local source and the diagnostic
	// anchor both.
	function moveElementIntoContainer(value ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, valueStart TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **AN OPAQUE-`T` ARRAY MOVE-IN IN THE SHARED BODY (P1.7 slice 3b-v, W10).** The shared generic body has
		// no static type for the element (`containerElementIsManaged` is false — the element is a `typeParameter`
		// word), so it can neither promote a borrow nor decide the element's managed-ness here. The array becomes
		// the element's OWNER whatever the instantiation — `__managed_decref` destroys each slot exactly once through
		// the descriptor's `destroyFunc@40` — so the value moved in must be one this frame OWNS, and
		// `moveOpaqueFeedArg` transfers it (poisoning a bare-local source, draining an owned temporary).
		// The CONCRETE call site's own consume (3b-iii/3b-iv) is untouched — it runs in the caller's frame on the
		// concrete argument.
		if self.signatures.containerElementIsOpaque(giid) 'opaque'
			// ⭐⭐ **A BORROWED ELEMENT TAKES A REFERENCE, BECAUSE IT HAS NONE TO GIVE UP (W60).** The container
			// releases exactly one per slot, so the store must hand it one it owns — and where this frame owns
			// nothing there is only one way to supply that: `retainFunc@64`, through the enclosing instance's
			// descriptor. See `referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement`, which is where the refusal this replaced lived.
			if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value) 'borrowed'
				return try self.referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement(value, valueStart: valueStart)
			end 'borrowed'

			// ⭐⭐ **INSIDE A LOOP THE FRAME TAKES A NEW REFERENCE INSTEAD OF GIVING UP ITS OWN (W58).** A move
			// is the right and cheaper discharge only while this frame will not read the value again, and
			// across a back edge it provably will: the next iteration stores the same value into the next
			// slot. A `movedFrom` bit is set once, at the store's TEXT, and cannot say "moved on iteration 3,
			// live on iteration 4" — which is why the move door refuses this outright
			// (`poisonOwningBinding`). Taking a reference needs no such distinction: N iterations take N
			// references, the binding is never poisoned, and its own one is released at scope exit exactly as
			// it would have been had the loop not been written.
			//
			// ⚠ **AND THE ZERO-ITERATION CASE IS WHY THIS IS A CORRECTION AND NOT ONLY A WIDENING.** A move
			// decides at parse time that the store happened; a loop whose body never runs then leaks the
			// reference its consuming caller handed over, with the binding marked moved and nothing left to
			// release it. `a-loop-that-never-runs-still-releases-the-value` is that program.
			//
			// ⚠ **THIS ARM IS ABOUT AN OWNED VALUE ONLY, AND SINCE W60 THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT REACHES IT.** A
			// BORROW is served one branch up, by the same `coOwnBorrowedOpaque`; the two arms differ in what
			// happens to the SOURCE, not in what the container gets. An owned source keeps its own reference and
			// releases it at scope exit, and a borrowed one never had one.
			if self.moveOfValueEscapesLoop(value) 'referenceRatherThanMove'
				return self.coOwnBorrowedOpaque(value, range: SourceRange.ofToken(self.tokenAt(valueStart)))
			end 'referenceRatherThanMove'

			try self.moveOpaqueFeedArg(value, valueStart: valueStart)
			return value
		end 'opaque'
		if not self.signatures.containerElementIsManaged(giid) 'trivial'
			return self.containerElementWord(value, giid: giid, range: SourceRange.ofToken(self.tokenAt(valueStart)))
		end 'trivial'
		let anchor = self.tokenAt(valueStart)
		// ⚠ `tagIsByteRecord`, NOT `tagIsText`: this flag selects the fused-record COPY protocol (promote a
		// borrowed record into a fresh owned one) against the aggregate protocol (refuse — a struct has no
		// clone), which is a question about the element's REPRESENTATION. A `Character` element answered
		// `false` here and `a.push('é')` was refused as *"moving a borrowed struct/union value into durable
		// storage … a struct/union has no `clone`"* — on a value that has exactly the clone that message
		// says it lacks, while the identical literal into a struct FIELD was accepted (that door asks
		// `classifyUnionPayload`, which already answers `isString: true` for a Character).
		let isString = tagIsByteRecord(maxonTypeTag(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid)))
		return try self.moveManagedValueInto(value, argBareLocal: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart), isString: isString, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'moveElementIntoContainer'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE MACHINE WORD A TRIVIAL ELEMENT IS STORED AS — the FLOAT boundary, and the only element type
	// where the value and its slot live in different register files.**
	//
	// Every `__managed_*` mutator takes its element as an ordinary integer parameter, so it arrives in a
	// general-purpose register; a float value is born in XMM. Handed over unconverted it reached
	// `X64Backend.emitRegRegMove` and PANICKED — *"a register-to-register move from xmm0 to rdx crosses
	// register files"* (measured on `Array with Real` + `push(2.5)`, which is exactly the crash
	// `generic-types.md:error.float-type-arg` describes from the other side). A REINTERPRETATION is the
	// correct conversion and not a widening: the slot holds the f64's 64 bits, so `bitcastF64ToI64` in and
	// `bitcastI64ToF64` out (`containerSlotValueType`'s float arm) round-trip every value including NaNs and
	// the two zeros, where a numeric convert would not.
	//
	// ⚠ **ASKED OF THE INSTANCE'S ELEMENT, NOT OF THE VALUE'S TAG.** A `Byte` element assigned an int literal
	// carries an `integer` tag and must pass straight through; a float element handed an int literal has
	// already been promoted by `requireContainerElementType`, so the tag agreeing with the element is the
	// precondition rather than the test. Reading the element is what makes this one question with one answer
	// per instance.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE TEST IS `containerElementIsFloatWord` BELOW, AND BOTH SIDES ASK IT — see that predicate for the
	// measured wrong answer a bare `tagIsFloat` on the raw element type gives.**
	function containerElementWord(value ValueId, giid GenericInstanceId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if not self.containerElementIsFloatWord(giid) 'wordAlready'
			return value
		end 'wordAlready'

		return self.emitUnaryOp(value, op: MaxonUnaryOp.floatToBits, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, range: range)
	end 'containerElementWord'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES CONTAINER `giid`'s ELEMENT TRAVEL AS ITS 64 BITS — the ONE home of the float-element question,
	// and it has to be one home because its two askers are the two ENDS of a round trip.**
	// `containerElementWord` writes the bits in; `emitContainerElementAccessor` reads them back out. Spelled
	// separately, the two are free to disagree about what counts as a float element, and a disagreement is not
	// a diagnostic — it is a value that goes in through one door and comes out of another.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RESOLVES THROUGH `containerSlotValueType`, NOT OFF THE RAW ELEMENT TYPE, AND THAT IS THE MEASURED
	// PART.** The stored element of a RANGED FLOAT ALIAS is `named("Float")`, which answers FALSE to a bare
	// `tagIsFloat`. With the raw type on the write side only, `Vector with 2 Float` over
	// `typealias Float = float(f64.min to f64.max)` stored its element unconverted and panicked the x64
	// emitter — *"a register-to-register move from xmm0 to rax crosses register files"* — while the
	// bare-float `Vector from [1.5, 2.5]`, whose element IS `float`, was already correct. Exactly the
	// asymmetry one shared door makes unrepresentable.
	function containerElementIsFloatWord(giid GenericInstanceId) returns bool
		return tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(self.containerSlotValueType(self.signatures.containerElementType(giid))))
	end 'containerElementIsFloatWord'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE REFERENCE A BORROWED OPAQUE-`T` ELEMENT OWES THE CONTAINER IT IS STORED INTO (W60)** — the
	// container twin of `moveOpaqueValueInto`'s borrowed arm, and the two discharge one obligation for the two
	// durable slot kinds. The container destroys each element exactly once through the enclosing instance's
	// `destroyFunc@40`, so a store must hand it a reference IT owns; a borrow has none to give up, so one is
	// TAKEN through the same descriptor's `retainFunc@64`. The two words are stamped from ONE
	// `typeParamOwnershipProtocol` answer and are therefore non-zero together, which is what makes that pairing
	// a fact rather than a hope — `managedOpaqueArrayElementOf` records what their disagreeing cost, twice.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS DOOR SPENT THREE RUNGS AS A FLAT REFUSAL OF EVERY BORROW, AND EVERY REASON IT GAVE HAS SINCE
	// BEEN CLOSED SOMEWHERE ELSE.** Both of its recorded measurements are kept, because both are still true of
	// what they measured and neither is about this arm any more:
	//
	//   • *"widening this door to co-own a borrow UNCONDITIONALLY was tried and measured worse — a feed
	//     parameter is enrolled OWNED, so `emitScopeDrops` releases it at the method's exit and that release
	//     cancels the retain, read off the emitted x64 as `__retain_type_param` … `__managed_set` …
	//     `__drop_type_param`, with the `Map` still segfaulting at 13."* That is an argument about an OWNED
	//     value, and an owned value cannot reach here — `moveElementIntoContainer` routes it to the move, or
	//     across a loop back edge to its own co-own, one branch up. The `Map.grow()` hole it was measured on
	//     is a BORROW CROSSING A CALL, which no store door can see and which `handleEscapingBorrowFeed` closed
	//     at the only frame that can: the caller.
	//   • *"reserving the descriptor such a co-own needs turns `feed-into-borrowing-user-push-rejected` from a
	//     clean REFUSAL into a compiling program that LEAKS (exit 101) — the descriptor it gains has a ZERO
	//     `destroyFunc@40`."* Recorded STALE at W58: both words come off one protocol answer now, and that
	//     program is `generic-type-nested-array-typealias.feed-into-a-borrowing-user-push-releases-what-it-took`.
	//
	// ⇒ What the refusal was left guarding is the ONE shape whose reference no other frame can take, because
	// the borrow is produced INSIDE this body: `items.push(try other.get(i) otherwise …)`, an opaque field
	// read, a `for` element. The rule the frames keep is unchanged — *whoever hands a value to an opaque sink
	// hands over a reference IT owns; if it does not own one, it takes one first* — and this is the half where
	// the taking happens here. `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `appendMemory` is that program, and it could not compile.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE DESCRIPTOR IS RESERVED BY A TOKEN PRE-SCAN (`storesContainerElement`), so a body this door
	// serves and a body the scan missed disagree — and the disagreement is REFUSED with a position
	// (`requireDescriptorForOpaqueStores`) rather than emitted.** `coOwnBorrowedOpaque` records the absence.
	//
	// ⚠ **A VALUE WHOSE TAG IS NOT THE OPAQUE PARAMETER IS STILL REFUSED, AND THAT IS A TYPE ANSWER RATHER
	// THAN AN OWNERSHIP ONE.** `items.push("literal")` in a shared body has no caller frame to check the
	// element against — `requireContainerElementType` declines for an opaque element precisely because the
	// instantiation's argument is checked where it is CONCRETE — so accepting it would let a `Bag with Int`
	// store a String record through a zero retain word: a silent wrong answer where today there is a position.
	// The OWNED concrete case is accepted by the move arm above and carries that same hole; it is pre-existing
	// there and deliberately not widened here.
	function referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement(value ValueId, valueStart TokenIndex) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let anchor = self.tokenAt(valueStart)
		if self.tagOf(value) != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'notTheElementsOwnParameter'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("storing a borrowed value of a CONCRETE type into an `Array with <type parameter>` in a shared generic body — the body compiles once for every instantiation, so nothing here can check the value against the element type the caller chose, and the reference the container needs would be taken through a descriptor written for a different type. Store a value of the element's own type parameter (a `get`/`first`/`last` read, an opaque field read, a `for` element, or a parameter the method consumes), or write the store in a method of a concrete instantiation", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'notTheElementsOwnParameter'

		// A generic no `with` ever names has no `T` to reference, and this is the SAME predicate the
		// reservation read (`genericTypeIsInstantiated`, asked here of the value's own token) — one question,
		// two askers, so the descriptor the store wants and the descriptor the seed reserved are one decision.
		// `moveOpaqueValueInto` declines on the identical answer and records why the weaker question is the
		// only one both ends can ask.
		if not self.signatures.typeParamOwnerIsInstantiated(self.valueNameId(value)) 'noInstantiationToReference'
			return value
		end 'noInstantiationToReference'

		// ⚠ **A `constructedValue` FEED PARAMETER IS *NOT* EXEMPTED HERE, WHERE `moveOpaqueValueInto` EXEMPTS
		// IT — and the difference is which SLOT the concrete call site paid for.** There the store IS the
		// `Self{…}` that call site consumed its argument for, so a second reference would leak the caller's. A
		// container is a DIFFERENT durable slot with an element walk of its own, so it owes its own reference;
		// passing the value straight through would give that walk one nobody ever took.
		return self.coOwnBorrowedOpaque(value, range: SourceRange.ofToken(anchor))
	end 'referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement'

	// ⭐ **MOVE AN OPAQUE OWNED FEED VALUE INTO ITS NEW OWNER (P1.7 slice 3b-v, W10).** The fed value (an
	// `items.push(item)` element, or a `self.N(item)` forward argument) is transferred so its new owner (the
	// array, or the consuming sibling) is its SOLE owner and this frame does not drop it too. Both transfer
	// forms run, and each is a no-op on the other's case — exactly as `transferConsumedArg`'s opaque arm pairs
	// them, which is the same transfer one call further out:
	//   • a bare reference to a LIVE owned binding is POISONED (a second use → E3102). `poisonMoveSource` is
	//     value-keyed and matches only a live owned binding, and the bare-local gate keeps it off the hot path
	//     (the same gate `moveManagedValueInto` uses).
	//   • an owned TEMPORARY is drained from the statement's pending drops. ⚠ Without this,
	//     `self.items.push(self.items.pop())` — the one shape that produces an owned opaque temporary — freed
	//     the moved-out element at statement end AND again through the array's own element walk, a double free
	//     on a live record with no diagnostic anywhere (measured: `0xC0000005`).
	function moveOpaqueFeedArg(value ValueId, valueStart TokenIndex) throws ParseError
		if self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart) 'bareLocal'
			try self.poisonMoveSource(value, anchor: self.tokenAt(valueStart))
		end 'bareLocal'
		self.removeFromPendingTemps(value)
	end 'moveOpaqueFeedArg'

	// ⭐⭐ **A VOID CONTAINER METHOD HAS NO VALUE, so a value-position call is refused exactly as a void
	// free call is (`voidCallResultUsed`) — AND EVERY CALLER IS INSIDE THE ARM ITS OWN NAME MATCHED,
	// WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF THIS FUNCTION'S SCOPING.** It tests `resultUsed` and nothing about the name,
	// so it can only ever be as name-scoped as its call sites are.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO BE SCOPED POSITIONALLY — one call between the value-yielding arms and the void ones
	// — and that is a scope an UNKNOWN name shares (D11).** A name matching no arm falls past every one
	// of them, so `let x = arr.frobnicate()` reached the check and was told *"Function 'frobnicate' does
	// not return a value"*: a sentence asserting that the method EXISTS and is VOID, about a name the
	// type has never heard of, where the identical call in statement position correctly reported the
	// roster refusal. The old comment here named `push`/`reserve`/`resize`/`clear`/`insert` as the
	// methods it guarded, which the code did not check and could not — a guarantee only the call sites
	// can make. `specs-shv2/void-call-result.md`'s four red cases and three controls are that pair of
	// properties: an unknown name reaches the ROSTER, and a real void mutator still reaches this.
	//
	// ⇒ **A NEW VOID ARM MUST CALL THIS AS ITS FIRST STATEMENT**, before it consumes an argument list, so
	// the refusal keeps beating the argument diagnostics it used to beat.
	function requireVoidMethodIsStatement(resultUsed bool, methodTok Token) throws ParseError
		if resultUsed 'valueUsed'
			throw ParseError.voidCallResultUsed(String.from(methodTok.value), line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column)
		end 'valueUsed'
	end 'requireVoidMethodIsStatement'

	// The SCOPE name a base token denotes. `self` is a keyword, so it carries no binding name of its
	// own; it names the receiver parameter, which is bound under `SelfParamName`. Every other base
	// token names itself.
	//
	// One home, because `p.x`, `p.m()` and their `self` spellings all have to answer it the same
	// way — and because it is the ONLY place the `self` token and the receiver parameter's name are
	// connected. A second copy is a second chance to look the receiver up under the word the source
	// happened to write.
	function baseBindingName(tok Token) returns ByteArray
		return SelfParamName if tok.kind == TokenKind.self else tok.value
	end 'baseBindingName'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES A DOTTED BASE NAME A VALUE IN SCOPE — a local BINDING or a closure CAPTURE?** The one
	// answer to the "a value outranks a type name" question, and therefore the one place the shadowing
	// rule is written. `methodCallsAt` (the STATEMENT door) and `parseDottedPrimary` (the EXPRESSION
	// door) each used to spell these same two tests out for themselves, in the same order, with a
	// comment on one of them asking that the other be kept in step BY HAND — one fact written twice,
	// where a clause added to either copy alone re-points a name at a different declaration and earns
	// no compile error anywhere.
	//
	// ⚠ **`Self` IS EXCLUDED, AND THAT EXCLUSION *IS* THE RULE THAT `Self` NAMES A TYPE AND NEVER A
	// VALUE (D9 review).** It is not a shortcut for a lookup that would have missed: a declaration CAN
	// bind the name — `function twin(Self Num)` is admitted, because a parameter's name may be spelled
	// with a keyword (D8, `consumeNameToken`) — and the scope test would then find it and hand
	// `Self.make(v)` to `parseMethodCall` on that parameter. Measured before this guard: `Self.make(41)`
	// inside `twin(Self Num)` reported *"'int' has no method named 'make'"*, `Self.on` inside an `enum`
	// reported *"a field access on 'Self', which is declared 'int' and not a struct type"*, and
	// `Self{n: 42}` in the SAME body still meant the type — because `parsePrimary`'s `selfType` arm
	// claims `{` ahead of any scope test. The reference bootstrap resolves every one of those to the
	// TYPE and leaves the parameter unreadable (`E3012 unused variable: 'Self'` when nothing reads it,
	// `E3003 'Gate' is a type and cannot be used directly as a value` when something does), so this is
	// the oracle's rule and not a new one.
	//
	// ⭐ Excluded HERE rather than refused at the declaration, and that placement is the argument: the
	// oracle ADMITS `function twin(Self Num)`, and `enum Kw { Self }` is a legal, useful case name both
	// compilers run (`Kw.Self`). Reserving the word at `requireUnreservedName` would refuse both. What
	// is wrong is not the declaration — it is a base position consulting the VALUE namespace about a
	// token that cannot be in it.
	function baseNamesAValueInScope(tok Token) returns bool
		if tok.kind == TokenKind.selfType 'alwaysAType'
			return false
		end 'alwaysAType'

		let name = self.baseBindingName(tok)
		return self.scope.contains(name) or self.captureBinds(name)
	end 'baseNamesAValueInScope'

	// ⭐ **DOES THE BASE AT `index` NAME A VALUE A FIELD CHAIN CAN START FROM?** `baseNamesAValueInScope`
	// plus the one base that is neither a local nor a capture: a TOP-LEVEL MANAGED BINDING (spec-port
	// `map-struct-bytearray`) — `db.sourceFiles.contains(key)`, where `var db = Database.create(…)` is a
	// struct global. Without it the chain fell past every arm to the SIZED-BOUND reading and was refused as
	// `Expected ''min' or 'max'' but got 'sourceFiles'`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE GLOBAL IS ASKED LAST, AND EVERY TYPE-BASED READING OF THE SHAPE IS ASKED BEFORE IT** — the
	// precedence `staticCallClaimsBase` already fixes for a one-hop call: a name that is both a declared type
	// and a global keeps its type reading. That is why the expression door's arm sits BELOW the enum,
	// namespace-call and static-call arms rather than beside the local one. A LOCAL, by contrast, outranks a
	// type name (the rule `baseNamesAValueInScope` carries), and the two orders differ for a reason: a local
	// is a declaration the reader can see on the page, while a global's spelling can collide with a type's
	// from another file.
	//
	// It costs one hash of bytes already in hand when the scope test declines — `managedTopLevelBaseAt`'s
	// measured filter — and allocates nothing.
	function chainBaseNamesAValue(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.baseNamesAValueInScope(self.tokenAt(index)) or self.managedTopLevelBaseAt(index)
	end 'chainBaseNamesAValue'

	// `p.x` — read field `x` out of the box `p` points at.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT RIDES `loadIndirect`, THE OP A GLOBAL'S READ ALREADY USES** (`emitGlobalLoad`), with a
	// real offset where that one passes `GlobalSlotOffset`. There is no `fieldLoad` op and there is
	// not going to be one: the Std memory band is the GENERAL pair, and P1.0d.5b's comments said so
	// while this rung was still called P1.1 ("the offset field is not dead weight — it is what
	// `point.x` will carry, through this same op").
	//
	// The type comes off the BINDING, not off the value column's tag alone: `VarInfo.varType` is a
	// `structRef` carrying the struct's interned name id, and that name is what the layout registry
	// is keyed by. So `p.x` resolves through the same two steps a human does — which type is `p`,
	// and which slot does that type call `x`.
	function parseFieldAccess() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let step = try self.resolveFieldChain()
		let index = try self.requireFieldIndex(step.layout, fieldTok: step.lastFieldTok)
		try self.requireFieldAccessible(step.layout, index: index, fieldTok: step.lastFieldTok)

		return self.emitFieldLoad(step.box, layout: step.layout, index: index, range: SourceRange.between(step.baseTok, last: step.lastFieldTok))
	end 'parseFieldAccess'

	// ⭐ **RESOLVE A FIELD-ACCESS CHAIN `base.f1.f2.…fN` to its FINAL box + layout + last field token** —
	// the shared spine of a chained READ (`o.inner.x`) and a chained WRITE (`o.inner.x = 42`). It resolves
	// the base binding to its box and layout (`requireStructBase`, which also enforces the live-binding
	// guard), then for every INTERMEDIATE field (all but the last) LOADS that field — which must be a struct
	// — as the next box and threads its layout onward. A borrowed load: the loaded struct box is a borrow of
	// the owning struct's field, valid while the base binding lives, so no ownership is taken and nothing is
	// dropped. The cursor is left on the token AFTER the last field name (the `=` of a write, or wherever the
	// read's expression continues).
	// ⭐ **`t.0` NAMES THE FIELD `_0` — THE ONE REWRITE FROM A POSITIONAL MEMBER TOKEN TO A FIELD-NAME TOKEN
	// (P1.10).** A tuple's fields ARE ordinary struct fields, called `_0`, `_1`, … (`tupleFieldName`), so
	// nothing downstream learns about tuples: the rewritten token flows into `requireFieldIndex` /
	// `requireFieldAccessible` / `emitFieldLoad` exactly as `p.x`'s own token does, and `t._0` — which
	// `specs-shv2/tuples.md` spells in five cases — resolves through the identical `indexOfField`.
	//
	// The synthesized token keeps the ORIGINAL's line and column, so a diagnostic still points at what the
	// author wrote; it carries no source SPAN, because its text is not in the source buffer (`t.0` holds no
	// `_0`) and a span is a slice of that buffer. It is `identifier`-kinded for the same reason: the token is
	// a NAME now, and no later reader may re-decide it was a number.
	//
	// Both references perform the identical rewrite (v1 `computeFieldNameForMemberToken`,
	// `Parser.maxon:14303-14312`; bootstrap `2-Parser.cs:17809-17819`).
	function consumeMemberNameToken() returns Token throws ParseError
		let member = try self.memberNameTokenAt(self.pos)
		self.advance()
		return member
	end 'consumeMemberNameToken'

	// The member-name token at `index` WITHOUT moving the cursor — `consumeMemberNameToken` minus the
	// advance, and the reason the rewrite above is not written inline there. `dispatchMethodOnBase` has to
	// READ the member to decide whether it is a field or a method and may not consume it: the method path
	// hands the cursor to `parseCallNamed`, which consumes the name itself. Peeking with the rewrite absent
	// would make a tuple's positional member (`makePair().0`) invisible to the field lookup on the one door
	// that cannot reach it through the chain walk.
	function memberNameTokenAt(index TokenIndex) returns Token throws ParseError
		let tok = self.tokenAt(index)
		// A KEYWORD after a `.` is a member NAME (D8) — `Box.from(7)`, `p.to()`. A `.` is followed by a member
		// name and by nothing else in this grammar, which is the same lookbehind `keywordIsAName` already draws
		// for the block-extent scans; so the reader admits exactly what those scans already exclude.
		if tokenCanBeAName(tok) 'namedMember'
			return tok
		end 'namedMember'
		if tok.kind != TokenKind.intLiteral 'notAMemberName'
			throw ParseError.unexpectedToken(TokenKind.identifier.rawValue, got: tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'notAMemberName'

		return Token.create(TokenKind.identifier, line: tok.line, column: tok.column, value: tupleFieldName(tok.value))
	end 'memberNameTokenAt'

	// **The token kinds that may NAME a member after a `.`** — anything `tokenCanBeAName` admits (an identifier,
	// or a KEYWORD, which after a `.` is a name — D8), or the int literal of a tuple's positional access.
	// Spelled once, so the two shapes built on it — the chain HOP (`fieldChainEndAfter`, which every field-store
	// and chained-call dispatcher now walks through) and the call TAIL (`memberCallFollows`, which every
	// `<base> . <member> (` dispatcher now asks) — and the consumer above cannot come to disagree about where a
	// chain's hop ends. They already MUST agree: `fieldChainEndAfter`'s header states that a divergence
	// between its two askers is a use-after-free rather than a compile error.
	function namesMemberAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.intLiteral or tokenCanBeAName(self.tokenAt(index))
	end 'namesMemberAt'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TOKEN SHAPE THAT ROUTES A PRIMITIVE-TYPE KEYWORD INTO A DOTTED EXPRESSION (A1s-prim)** —
	// `<int|float|bool> . <member> (`, and nothing shorter. It is the ONE gate both expression doors ask
	// (`parsePrimary`, `parseTryCallReceiver`), so the two positions cannot come to disagree about which
	// keyword shapes open a dotted form — the asymmetry that left `try int.fromString(s)` reporting
	// *"`try` must be applied to a call … (got 'int')"* while the same call in a `let` had no arm at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE WHOLE FOUR-TOKEN SHAPE ON PURPOSE, NOT JUST `<primitive> .`.** A primitive keyword has
	// no OTHER dotted meaning to fall into — there is no `int.max` bound in this language, and the oracle
	// says so too (`E3003 Undefined variable 'int'`) — so a two-token gate would route `int.max` to
	// `parseTypeQualifiedBoundExpression` and answer it with a sized-type complaint in place of the
	// `expectedExpression` it reports today. Claiming exactly the call shape moves no diagnostic.
	//
	// `byte` is deliberately NOT here: it is a plain identifier, so `byte.fromString(…)` is already routed
	// by the identifier arms and joins the primitive reading further down, at `parseQualifiedCall`. See
	// `namesAPrimitiveType` for why one door is not enough and neither reference compiler has both.
	function primitiveStaticCallAt(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return keywordNamesAPrimitiveType(self.tokenAt(index)) and self.memberCallFollows(index + 1)
	end 'primitiveStaticCallAt'

	// **Is the `.member` at `index` a CALL — `. <member> (`?** The one question that tells a field-chain HOP
	// from the chain's END: a member followed by `(` is a method (or a call through a function-typed field),
	// which is not a field the walk may consume, and the value it is called ON is the chain's result.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE WALK'S STOP CONDITION, NOT THE STATEMENT DISPATCHER'S — and those are two derivations,
	// which is worth saying because it reads like one.** `resolveFieldChainFrom` asks THIS, hop by hop, of
	// the cursor it is standing on; `fieldChainMethodCallAt` asks `fieldChainEndAfter` for the index just
	// past the WHOLE chain and then tests `(` there. They agree today, and the agreement is structural
	// rather than shared: `fieldChainEndAfter`'s loop consumes a `.member` only while the NEXT tokens are
	// again `. <member>`, so the hop it stops before is exactly the one this predicate stops before. Both
	// spell the member half through `namesMemberAt`, which is the piece that genuinely has one home — teach
	// a new member-naming token to that and both walks learn it at once, which is the divergence
	// `fieldChainEndAfter`'s own header is about.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS ALSO THE ONE SPELLING OF THE THREE-TOKEN TAIL `. <member> (` THAT EVERY `<base> . <member> (`
	// DISPATCH PREDICATE ASKS (A1s-prim review).** `isSelfMethodCallAt`, `methodCallsAt`, `voidStaticCallsAt`
	// and `primitiveStaticCallAt` each claim a four-token shape whose last three tokens are exactly this
	// question; all four used to write `kindAt(i+1) == dot and namesMemberAt(i+2) and kindAt(i+3) == leftParen`
	// out by hand, so the tail was FIVE copies of one syntactic fact with nothing making them agree. That is
	// the shape this project keeps paying for: teach the language a new call tail (a generic method call
	// `.m<T>(…)`, say) and a copy left behind does not fail to compile — it silently stops claiming a shape,
	// and the token walk and the statement dispatcher part company. The distinction the paragraph above draws
	// is between two DERIVATIONS of where a chain ends; it was never about the tail's spelling, which is one
	// fact and now has one home. What each predicate still owns is its BASE half — a keyword `self`, a bound
	// name, a plain identifier, a primitive-type keyword — which is where they genuinely differ.
	function memberCallFollows(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.kindAt(index) == TokenKind.dot and self.namesMemberAt(index + 1) and self.kindAt(index + 2) == TokenKind.leftParen
	end 'memberCallFollows'

	// Consume a field-chain base token — the receiver `self` (a keyword) or a local identifier. `self` names the
	// receiver parameter (resolved by `baseBindingName` to `SelfParamName`); every other base names itself.
	function consumeChainBaseToken() returns Token throws ParseError
		if self.at(TokenKind.self) 'selfBase'
			let tok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			return tok
		end 'selfBase'
		// A KEYWORD-named binding may be the base (D8) — `type.n` where `type` is a struct-typed parameter.
		// It resolves through the same `scope.contains` test an identifier base does; a keyword that names
		// nothing reaches the same `undefinedVariable` below.
		return try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)
	end 'consumeChainBaseToken'

	function resolveFieldChain() returns FieldChainStep throws ParseError
		// The base's INDEX, taken before the token is consumed: the top-level arm below is keyed on it, because
		// deciding whether a global claims this base needs the MEMBER name too (`managedTopLevelBaseAt`).
		let baseIndex = self.pos
		// The base is a local identifier (`b.ops.push`) OR the receiver `self` (`self.nodes.push` — P1.7 slice 3):
		// `self` is a keyword, not an identifier, and it names the receiver parameter bound under `SelfParamName`,
		// which `baseBindingName` maps. A single-hop `self.field` read/write resolves elsewhere; this is the 2+-hop
		// chain and the chain-method-call spine, which `fieldChainMethodCallAt` already admits a `self` base to.
		let baseTok = try self.consumeChainBaseToken()

		// ⭐ **THE GATE IS THE SHARED PREDICATE; THE ARMS INSIDE IT DO THE RESOLVING.** This door needs more
		// than a bool — a local base wants its `VarInfo`, a captured one its enclosing `Scope` — so it cannot
		// simply BE `baseNamesAValueInScope`. It can still ASK it, which is what keeps the "a value outranks a
		// type name" rule single-sourced across all three doors that read it: the statement door
		// (`methodCallsAt`), the expression door (`parseDottedPrimary`) and this one, which RESOLVES a chain
		// rather than merely recognizing the shape of one.
		//
		// ⚠ **`Self` IS THEREFORE EXCLUDED HERE TOO (D9 review), AND IT WAS THE LAST DOOR STILL SHADOWABLE.**
		// `Self.n = 5` inside `bad(Self Gate)` resolved to the PARAMETER and reported *"cannot assign to
		// immutable variable: 'Self'"*; it now falls to the tail and reports what the `Gate.n = 5` spelling
		// reports on the same token. No wrong answer was reachable through it — every binding a store could
		// have succeeded against is refused elsewhere, since only a PARAMETER may be named `Self` and a
		// parameter is immutable — but the rule held at two doors of three, and a rule that holds at two doors
		// of three is the shape this project keeps paying for.
		//
		// The gate cannot pass a base the arms then drop: `captureBinds` true means `captureScopeOf` answers
		// `found` or throws its nested-frame error, never `notCaptured`.
		if self.baseNamesAValueInScope(baseTok) 'valueBase'
			let baseName = self.baseBindingName(baseTok)
			if self.scope.contains(baseName) 'localBase'
				let baseBinding = self.scope.getInfo(baseName)
				let base = try self.requireStructBase(baseBinding, baseTok: baseTok)

				// ⚠ **THE BINDING THREADED ON IS THE MATERIALIZED ONE, NOT THE ONE THE SCOPE HANDED BACK (W66).**
				// It is what `parseFieldAssignment`'s instance rank asks (`requireWritableInstance`), and for a
				// self-field alias the scope's binding answers that question `false` whatever the field declares
				// — `mutable: false` by construction and not a parameter. `baseBindingOf` re-derives it from the
				// box this door already loaded, so nothing is emitted twice and the WRITE door and the METHOD
				// door read one column (`selfFieldIsWritable`) about one field.
				return try self.resolveFieldChainFrom(base.box, layout: base.layout, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: try self.baseBindingOf(baseBinding, baseTok: baseTok, box: base.box))
			end 'localBase'

			// A CAPTURED enclosing struct is the base (P1.5-A2b-2): `level.rawValue` inside a closure that captures
			// `level`. The base is not in the closure's own scope; `resolveCapturedFieldChain` reads the box out of
			// the env and resolves its layout the same way `requireStructBase` does.
			match try self.captureScopeOf(baseName, anchor: baseTok) 'captured'
				found(enclosing) then return try self.resolveCapturedFieldChain(baseTok, enclosing: enclosing)
				notCaptured then break 'captured'
			end 'captured'
		end 'valueBase'

		// ⭐ **A STATIC MEMBER IS THE FOURTH BASE (W65)** — `Cache.head.n = 42`, where `Cache.head` is module
		// storage keyed by its declaring type. `consumeChainBaseToken` has taken the TYPE name; the `.` and the
		// member are consumed here, and what the chain walks from is the SYNTHETIC qualified token — so the rest
		// of this door, `requireStructBase` and the immutable-write rule included, sees exactly the global base
		// the arm below already serves.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE READ SIDE ALREADY WORKED AND THE WRITE SIDE DID NOT, which is the asymmetry this closes.**
		// `Cache.head.n` in expression position is `parseDottedPrimary`'s static arm plus `parsePostfix`;
		// `Cache.head.n = 42` reaches THIS door, where the base `Cache` is neither a value nor a global and fell
		// to the tail as **`E2004: Undefined variable 'Cache'`** (measured) — a name that is defined, and the
		// "sends the reader hunting for a typo" noun `top-level-let-scalar-reassign-error` exists to argue
		// against.
		//
		// Asked with the type-based readings and BEFORE the managed-global arm, the precedence
		// `chainBaseNamesAValue` states: a name that is both a declared type and a global keeps its type reading.
		if self.namesStaticMemberAt(baseTok, memberIndex: self.pos + 1) 'staticMemberBase'
			try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
			let nameTok = self.staticMemberNameToken(baseTok, memberTok: try self.consumeMemberNameToken())
			match self.managedReceiverBindingOf(nameTok) 'binding'
				// A managed CONSTANT (a `String`, a `b"…"`) and an INLINED scalar have no field to chain
				// through, so both take the `undefinedVariable` an unknown base does — the arm below's own
				// choice, spelled at the QUALIFIED name because that is what this chain is rooted at.
				constant then break 'binding'
				global(label, valueType, mutable) then return try self.resolveGlobalFieldChain(nameTok, label: label, valueType: valueType, mutable: mutable)
				none then break 'binding'
			end 'binding'

			throw ParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'staticMemberBase'

		// ⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL MANAGED GLOBAL IS THE THIRD BASE** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`) —
		// `db.sourceFiles.contains(key)`. The slot's occupant is LOADED and wrapped in the same synthetic
		// `VarInfo` a global METHOD receiver already wears (`dispatchOnManagedReceiver`), so the layout
		// resolution, the live-binding guard and the immutable-write rule are the ones every other base gets;
		// nothing about a chain over a global is decided a second time here.
		//
		// The loaded pointer is BORROWED — the slot owns it, and `__maxon_global_cleanup` releases it at exit
		// — which is exactly the property an intermediate field load already has, so the chain walk below
		// needs no ownership bookkeeping it did not already have.
		if self.managedTopLevelBaseAt(baseIndex) 'managedTopLevelBase'
			match self.managedReceiverBindingOf(baseTok) 'binding'
				// A managed CONSTANT is a `String` or a `b"…"`, neither of which has a field, so it takes the
				// same `undefinedVariable` an unknown base does rather than an arm that could only refuse.
				constant then break 'binding'
				global(label, valueType, mutable) then return try self.resolveGlobalFieldChain(baseTok, label: label, valueType: valueType, mutable: mutable)
				none then break 'binding'
			end 'binding'
		end 'managedTopLevelBase'

		throw ParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(baseTok.value), line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column)
	end 'resolveFieldChain'

	// Resolve a field chain whose base is a top-level managed global. Split out so the arm above stays one
	// line and the synthetic binding is built once — `VarInfo.createValueBinding` is the same constructor
	// `dispatchOnManagedReceiver` uses, and the two must not come to describe one global differently.
	function resolveGlobalFieldChain(baseTok Token, label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, mutable bool) returns FieldChainStep throws ParseError
		let binding = VarInfo.createValueBinding(baseTok.value, varType: valueType, mutable: mutable, boundValue: self.emitGlobalLoad(label, valueType: valueType, range: SourceRange.ofToken(baseTok)))
		let base = try self.requireStructBase(binding, baseTok: baseTok)
		// No `baseBindingOf` here, and it would be a no-op if there were: `createValueBinding` cannot mint a
		// self-field alias, so a global base always holds its own value.
		return try self.resolveFieldChainFrom(base.box, layout: base.layout, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: binding)
	end 'resolveGlobalFieldChain'

	// ⭐ **RESOLVE A FIELD-ACCESS CHAIN ON A CAPTURED STRUCT BASE (P1.5-A2b-2).** `level.rawValue` inside a
	// closure that captures the enclosing struct `level`. The box is READ OUT OF THE ENV (`emitCaptureRead` —
	// a borrowed pointer copy, one `loadIndirect`), and its layout is resolved from the ENCLOSING binding's
	// struct type, identical to a local base. The enclosing binding is threaded as `baseBinding` so a WRITE
	// through a captured base (`level.x = …`) is correctly refused: a captured `let` is immutable (E2013).
	function resolveCapturedFieldChain(baseTok Token, enclosing Scope) returns FieldChainStep throws ParseError
		let base = try self.capturedStructBase(baseTok, enclosing: enclosing)
		// The enclosing binding is threaded as `baseBinding` so a WRITE through a captured base (`level.x = …`)
		// is correctly refused — a captured `let` is immutable (E2013). A read never reads it.
		return try self.resolveFieldChainFrom(base.box, layout: base.layout, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: enclosing.getInfo(self.baseBindingName(baseTok)))
	end 'resolveCapturedFieldChain'

	// Consume one `.field` on `(box, layout)`. The final field (no `.` after) is RETURNED unread for the
	// caller to read or write; an INTERMEDIATE field (a `.` follows) must be a struct — it is LOADED as the
	// next box (a borrow of the owning struct's field) and the walk recurses. Recursive rather than a `var`
	// loop deliberately: threading a `StructLayout` through a reassigned `var` trips the struct-aliasing
	// policy (E3078), while each recursion binds a fresh `let`. Chains are a handful of hops deep.
	function resolveFieldChainFrom(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, baseTok Token, baseBinding VarInfo) returns FieldChainStep throws ParseError
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let fieldTok = try self.consumeMemberNameToken()
		if not self.at(TokenKind.dot) 'lastField'
			return FieldChainStep.create(box, layout: layout, lastFieldTok: fieldTok, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: baseBinding)
		end 'lastField'

		let index = try self.requireFieldIndex(layout, fieldTok: fieldTok)
		try self.requireFieldAccessible(layout, index: index, fieldTok: fieldTok)

		// ⭐⭐ **A `.member(` AFTER THIS FIELD ENDS THE CHAIN — THIS FIELD IS THE RECEIVER.** The chain walks
		// FIELDS; a member that opens a CALL is not one, so the walk stops here and returns this field as the
		// chain's last, leaving the cursor on the `.`. The caller loads it and `dispatchMethodOnReceiver`
		// decides what the member is, off the loaded value's own type — which is the same decision it makes
		// for `[1,2].get(0)` and for `s.clone().byteLength()`, reached through the same door.
		//
		// ⭐ **IT REPLACES TWO SPECIAL CASES WITH THE RULE THEY WERE BOTH INSTANCES OF.** An ARRAY field
		// (`b.ops.push(1)`, P1.7 slice 1) and a TYPE-PARAMETER field (`self.item.digest()`, P1.7a slice 2b-v)
		// each had an arm here saying "this kind of field has no addressable sub-fields, so stop" — and both
		// are only ever followed by a call, because that is the only thing the language lets you write after
		// them. Every other field kind fell through to `structLayoutOfField`, which is why a STRUCT field's
		// method (`o.inner.get()`) was `E3018 type 'Inner' has no field named 'get'` — the walk had consumed
		// the METHOD NAME as a field — and a scalar field's (`o.count.hash()`) was "not a struct".
		//
		// A member with NO `(` after it is still a FIELD and the walk continues, so `o.inner.x` is unchanged
		// and a chain THROUGH a non-struct field is still refused by `structLayoutOfField` below.
		if self.memberCallFollows(self.pos) 'receiverField'
			return FieldChainStep.create(box, layout: layout, lastFieldTok: fieldTok, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: baseBinding)
		end 'receiverField'

		// ⭐ **A `T`-TYPED HOP IS SUBSTITUTED BEFORE THE WALK ASKS WHAT IT ADDRESSES (A4j).** `pb.value.x` on a
		// `Box with Point` hops through a field the shared generic body typed `T`, and an unsubstituted
		// `typeParameter` reads as an INTEGER (`tagIsIntegral`) — so the hop door refused it as "not a struct"
		// on a program the oracle runs. This is the SAME substitution `emitFieldLoad` applies to the loaded
		// VALUE one line below, under the same shared condition; it has to happen here as well because the
		// walk decides where to go NEXT from the type, before any value exists.
		let declaredFieldType = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
		let fieldType = self.slotTypeThroughReceiver(box, declared: declaredFieldType)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN ENUM ACCESSOR AFTER THIS FIELD ENDS THE CHAIN TOO — the third instance of the rule the
		// `.member(` arm above states, and it arrived the way that arm predicts one will.** `b.c.name` on a
		// field declared `as Color` is not a hop THROUGH `c`: `.name` is a member of the enum VALUE, so the
		// walk stops here, the caller loads `c`, and `parsePostfix` reads the accessor off the loaded value
		// through the same `emitEnumInstanceAccessor` the other two spellings use. Left to fall through, the
		// walk consumed `name` as a field and `structLayoutOfField` refused it — *"a field access through
		// 'Box.c', which is declared 'int' and not a struct"*, a message that is true of the ERASURE and
		// false of the program, on source the bootstrap compiles and shv2's own `Lexer.tokenDisplayText`
		// (`token.kind.rawValue`) is written in.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT IS THE ONE GATE HERE THAT MUST ASK THE DECLARED TYPE RATHER THAN A VALUE**, because no value
		// exists yet — the load is what this decision gates. `enumAccessorFollowsFieldTyped` is that reading,
		// and it reaches the same lookup `enumMatchFor` does so the two cannot disagree.
		if self.enumAccessorFollowsFieldTyped(fieldType) 'enumAccessorField'
			return FieldChainStep.create(box, layout: layout, lastFieldTok: fieldTok, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: baseBinding)
		end 'enumAccessorField'

		let nextLayout = try self.structLayoutOfField(layout, fieldTok: fieldTok, fieldType: fieldType)
		let nextBox = self.emitFieldLoad(box, layout: layout, index: index, range: SourceRange.between(baseTok, last: fieldTok))
		return try self.resolveFieldChainFrom(nextBox, layout: nextLayout, baseTok: baseTok, baseBinding: baseBinding)
	end 'resolveFieldChainFrom'

	// Does an enum accessor hop follow the cursor, off a field whose DECLARED type is `fieldType`? The
	// type-keyed twin of `enumAccessorHopOn`, and it exists because the field-chain walk has to decide
	// whether to continue BEFORE it loads anything — there is no value to classify yet. Past the `named`
	// test the two read the identical lookup (`enumOfTypeNameId`) and the identical admission rule
	// (`atEnumInstanceAccessor`), so a member one admits is a member the other admits.
	//
	// A `named` tag is what an enum-typed field carries (`typeTagName` prints it as `int`, which is what
	// made the old refusal read as a claim about an integer); every other tag declines here and the walk
	// carries on to `structLayoutOfField` exactly as before.
	function enumAccessorFollowsFieldTyped(fieldType MaxonType) returns bool
		if maxonTypeTag(fieldType) != ValueTypeTag.named 'notNamed'
			return false
		end 'notNamed'

		// ⚠ The tag test above filters almost none of the hops this runs at — a STRUCT-typed field is `named`
		// too — so the allocation guard on the shared tail is what keeps this off the ladder.
		return self.atEnumAccessorHopOfType(self.internedName(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)))
	end 'enumAccessorFollowsFieldTyped'

	// The `StructLayout` of a field whose already-resolved type `fieldType` is passed in — used to continue a
	// field chain THROUGH a struct-typed field (`o.inner.x`). The type is resolved to a `structRef`
	// (`named(Inner)` → `structRef`) and must carry a struct layout; a chain through a scalar / String / array
	// field is refused (an array is handled by the caller before this), since there is nothing to address a
	// further field on. `fieldType` is threaded IN (the caller already computed it) so it is not read twice.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT ASKS `structLayoutOfType` RATHER THAN ANSWERING FOR ITSELF (A4j), AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FIX.**
	// The BASE door has known `genericInstance` since P1.6-B1 and this HOP door did not, so a chain through a
	// generic-instance-typed field — an ordinary `type Outer` with `export var b as IntBox`, no type parameter
	// anywhere — was refused by a message that contradicted itself: `typeTagName(genericInstance)` prints
	// `"struct"`, so it read *"declared 'struct' and not a struct"*. The oracle compiles that program and
	// prints 7.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE ASK IS THE POINT, NOT A TIDY-UP: `structLayoutOfType` CARRIES A MEMORY-SAFETY GATE THIS
	// DOOR WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE HAD TO REMEMBER.** An instance may not ask `structOf` alone — see that
	// function's ⚠⚠ block, which records `a.value` on an `Array with Integer` printing the element buffer
	// POINTER and `a.value = 4242` overwriting it, with no diagnostic and exit 0. A second copy of the gate is
	// exactly the shape this compiler keeps paying for; one door means it cannot be forgotten here.
	// `specs-shv2/generic-types.md`'s `error.a-field-chain-through-a-builtin-instance-field-is-still-refused`
	// is what says it came across.
	//
	// The `baseTok` `structLayoutOfType` names in its diagnostics is the FIELD token here — for a hop, the
	// field IS what the reader must fix.
	function structLayoutOfField(baseLayout StructLayout, fieldTok Token, fieldType MaxonType) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		let resolved = self.resolveNamedStruct(fieldType)
		let tag = maxonTypeTag(resolved)
		if not self.tagCarriesStructLayoutHere(tag) 'notAStruct'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a field access through '{String.from(baseLayout.name)}.{String.from(fieldTok.value)}', which is declared '{typeTagName(tag)}' and not a struct — only a struct field can carry a further field access", line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column)
		end 'notAStruct'

		return try self.structLayoutOfType(BaseAccessKind.field, tag: tag, nameId: typeNameIdOf(resolved), baseTok: fieldTok)
	end 'structLayoutOfField'

	// ⭐⭐ **A SELF-FIELD READ — `count` AND `self.count`, ONE PATH.**
	//
	// Both spellings mean the same thing, so both resolve to the same `(layout, index)` and hand it
	// to `emitFieldLoad` — **the very routine `p.x` uses**, emitting the same `loadIndirect` at the
	// same offset with the same storage type. The two spellings differ in exactly one step, and it
	// is the step they *have* to differ in: `self.count` names its field with a TOKEN, so it asks
	// `requireFieldIndex` (and can report E3018); a bare `count` came off an alias that
	// `declareSelfFieldAliases` minted by enumerating the layout, so its index is already known and
	// provably valid.
	//
	// ⚠ **v1 SPLITS THIS AND SHV2 MUST NOT — the split is what this rung exists to not inherit.**
	// There, a bare `x` gets dedicated `selfFieldLoad`/`selfFieldStore` ops carrying the receiver
	// out-of-band with slot 0 hardcoded in lowering, while `self.x` gets an ordinary `varLoad` +
	// `fieldLoad`. Two ops, two lowerings, one fact — and it cost v1 twice over:
	//   • its E3014 walker inspects only `fieldLoad`/`fieldStore`, so a bare-name access is
	//     STRUCTURALLY invisible to field visibility (TypeResolution.maxon:8183-8188);
	//   • inside a closure there is no receiver slot, so the hardcoded slot 0 *"would load
	//     garbage"* and the closure path must re-route to the explicit form
	//     (Parser.maxon:18179-18189).
	// Neither is reachable here, and not because they were fixed — because the second path that
	// would have to be kept in step does not exist. `Project.maxon`'s `VarInfo.isSelfField` states
	// the rule; this is where it is obeyed.
	function parseSelfFieldRead(nameTok Token, binding VarInfo) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		return self.emitFieldLoad(try self.requireReceiverValue(nameTok), layout: try self.enclosingLayout(nameTok), index: binding.fieldIndex, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
	end 'parseSelfFieldRead'

	// `count = <expr>` — write a field of the enclosing type through the receiver, named bare. The
	// mirror of `parseSelfFieldRead`, landing on `emitFieldStore` exactly as `p.x = 5` does.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER'S OWN MUTABILITY IS NOT ASKED, AND THAT IS THE LANGUAGE'S RULE, NOT AN
	// OMISSION.** `parseFieldAssignment` demands a mutable INSTANCE because `let p` refuses a write
	// through it; here the instance is `__self`, a parameter, and every parameter is bound
	// immutable — so asking would report E2013 against `self.n = self.n * 2` in every mutating
	// method the corpus has. The corpus is also what settles that this is right rather than
	// convenient: `let acc = Accumulator.create(0)` followed by `acc.add(10)` expects **42**
	// (specs/self-keyword.md, `self-with-params`). A `let` on a struct binding refuses a REBIND
	// (`acc = other`) and a direct field write through it (`acc.total = 1`, E2013 — structs.md pins
	// it); it does not reach inside the type's own methods.
	//
	// What IS asked is the FIELD's mutability — `layout.fieldIsMutable`, the same column and the
	// same diagnostic `c.version = 2` gets, because it is the same question. Anchored at the name
	// token, which is the only token there is.
	function parseSelfFieldAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, nameTok Token, binding VarInfo) throws ParseError
		try self.emitCheckedSelfFieldStore(func, block: block, receiver: try self.requireReceiverValue(nameTok), layout: try self.enclosingLayout(nameTok), index: binding.fieldIndex, fieldTok: nameTok, range: SourceRange.ofToken(nameTok))
	end 'parseSelfFieldAssignment'

	// **THE ONE SELF-FIELD WRITE**, shared by the two spellings: the bare `count = 1` and the
	// explicit `self.count = 1`. Everything a write decides lives here — whether the field accepts
	// one, when the right-hand side is parsed, what type it must have, and the store itself — so
	// the two spellings cannot come to disagree about any of it. They differ only in how the index
	// was found and which tokens the span covers, which is all either caller passes in.
	//
	// The right-hand side is parsed AFTER the rejection, so a refused program emits no op —
	// `emitCheckedGlobalStore`'s discipline, and its name, for its reason.
	function emitCheckedSelfFieldStore(func IrFunction, block IrBlock, receiver ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, fieldTok Token, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		// ⭐⭐ **AN ITERATED FIELD REFUSES THE STORE WHATEVER IT WAS DECLARED (P1.8 slice A)** — this is the
		// FIFTH door of the iteration lock, and the rung shipped without it. `for it in items 'l' items =
		// IntArray.create() … end` drops the record the loop is still walking, and the header re-reads
		// `__managed_count` off it on the very next trip: MEASURED 0xC0000005 on all four spellings of one
		// program (`items`/`self.items` as the source × `items = …`/`self.items = …` as the write), where
		// the local twin `arr = other` was already E2013 through `requireMutableBinding`. Asked FIRST, and
		// keyed on the INDEX rather than a binding, because that is what BOTH callers hold and neither
		// carries an alias — see `selfFieldIsIterationLocked`.
		//
		// The same E2013 the local spelling gets, and the same code and wording the runnable oracle emits
		// for the bare form (measured: `cannot assign to immutable variable: 'items'`). It is one rule
		// about one storage; it must not answer differently for the way the source spelled it.
		if self.selfFieldIsIterationLocked(index) 'iteratedField'
			throw assignToImmutableError(fieldTok)
		end 'iteratedField'

		if not layout.fieldIsMutable(index) 'immutableField'
			throw ParseError.assignToImmutableField(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(fieldTok.value), line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column)
		end 'immutableField'

		// ⭐ E3070's rebind door ONE INDIRECTION OUT — the same write as `arr = other`, reached through
		// `items = other` / `self.items = other`, which both converge here. This is the ONE door that cannot
		// ask `borrowSubjectNameAt`: it holds a field token and a layout index and no chain base at all
		// (`self.items = x`'s base token is `self`). It needs no re-derivation either — `iterationSubjectNameAt`
		// already rules that a self-field chain's subject is the FIELD's name and never the receiver, and
		// `fieldTok` IS that token in both spellings. The `isSelfField` test is only for a store reached outside
		// an instance method, where no alias is in scope and no borrow could have named one.
		if self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) 'mayConflictWithABorrow'
			self.noteBorrowSubjectRebind(func, subjectName: fieldTok.value if self.scope.isSelfField(fieldTok.value) else EmptySourceText, anchor: fieldTok)
		end 'mayConflictWithABorrow'

		// ⭐ **AND IT IS A WRITE OF THE RECEIVER'S STORAGE, WHICH A CALLER CAN SEE** — `items = <fresh>` inside
		// `b.wipe()` drops the record a caller borrowed an element out of, exactly as `items.clear()` does. It
		// records ONLY the E3070 mask: `writtenParamMask` deliberately stays untouched here, because making a
		// caller's `let b` an E3019 for a method that assigns its own field is the very ruling
		// `self-keyword.md:self-with-params` pins (see `IrFunction`'s table).
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **THIS GATE WAS `typeIsArrayInstance` AND IT WAS A MEASURED USE-AFTER-FREE, TWICE OVER
		// (W-BORROW).** The line read *"gated on the field being an ARRAY, because an array element is the only
		// borrow E3070 tracks"* — and the store does not free an ELEMENT, it drops the RECORD, so what it can
		// invalidate is every borrow into anything that record transitively owns. Two shapes measured on the
		// tip, both compiling clean and faulting **0xC0000005** where the bootstrap oracle prints the value:
		//
		//   • a `List`-typed field — `let s = try b.items.first() otherwise "none"` across a `b.reset()` whose
		//     body is `items = StringList.create()`. A `List` hands out element borrows through the very same
		//     `emitContainerElementAccessor` an `Array` does, so "not an array" was never the question.
		//   • a nested STRUCT field — `let s = try b.inner.items.get(0) otherwise "none"` across a `b.reset()`
		//     whose body is `inner = Inner.create()`. Dropping `inner` cascades to the `Array` IT owns, and
		//     that array's element is a borrow of the most canonical kind.
		//
		// ⇒ **THE QUESTION IS "CAN DROPPING THIS FIELD'S RECORD FREE ANYTHING A TRACKED BORROW POINTS INTO",
		// AND `typeOwnsBorrowableStorage` IS ITS ONE HOME** — shared with the sibling store door
		// (`parseFieldAssignment`), which had the identical hole. That predicate's header carries both widths
		// this gate has been measured wrong at, in both directions. A TRIVIAL field frees nothing at all,
		// which is what the original narrowing was actually for: ungated, `total = total + v` marked its whole
		// receiver written and a caller holding `let s = try b.items.get(0) otherwise ""` was refused
		// `b.add(42)` (`borrow-liveness:method-writing-a-non-array-field-is-not-a-conflict`). The field type is
		// read through the one accessor the type check below already asks.
		if self.typeOwnsBorrowableStorage(self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)) 'freesBorrowableStorage'
			func.storageWrittenParamMask = func.storageWrittenParamMask or paramMaskOfValue(func, value: receiver)
		end 'freesBorrowableStorage'

		// ⭐ **W117's COLUMN IS NOT GATED AT ALL, and that is the difference between the two questions.** E3070's
		// column is gated because a store that frees NOTHING cannot invalidate a borrow; W117 tracks whether a
		// `let` global's record is written, and `total = total + v` on a scalar field of a global's box writes
		// it exactly as a managed store does. Unconditional for that reason, off the same receiver value.
		func.writesThroughParamMask = func.writesThroughParamMask or paramMaskOfValue(func, value: receiver)

		let valueStart = self.pos
		let parsed = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)
		// The COERCED value is what the store writes — `count = 3` into a `float` field stores the widened
		// f64. Same door as the struct literal and the `p.v = 3` spelling.
		let value = try self.requireFieldType(layout, index: index, fieldType: self.storedFieldType(receiver, layout: layout, index: index), value: parsed, anchor: fieldTok)

		// Routed through `emitFieldWrite`, exactly as `parseFieldAssignment` is: a MANAGED self-field
		// (`name = n`) must MOVE the new value in and only THEN drop the value it displaces (a borrowed String
		// is promoted, a borrowed struct is co-owned), not a raw store that leaks the old value and dangles
		// behind a borrow. ⚠ The acquire-before-release order is load-bearing — `emitFieldWrite` says why.
		try self.emitFieldWrite(func, box: receiver, layout: layout, index: index, value: value, argBareLocal: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart), anchor: fieldTok, range: range)
	end 'emitCheckedSelfFieldStore'

	// `self.x` / `self.m(…)` — the receiver named explicitly. `self` is its own KEYWORD token, so it
	// cannot ride the identifier paths; what it can do is resolve to the same two destinations they
	// do, which is what this routine is for.
	//
	// The `(` at +3 splits them, exactly as `parseDottedPrimary` splits `p.x` from `p.m(…)` — one
	// lookahead, written the same way, because it is the same question about the same three tokens.
	//
	// ⭐ **A BARE `self` — not followed by a dot — IS THE RECEIVER AS A VALUE, AND IT IS ONE (S5).** It used
	// to be a positioned `unsupported` claiming the receiver's type needed "the ownership ruling struct-typed
	// parameters and returns are deferred to" — a ruling those had already had for rungs (`stdlib/FilePath`
	// alone declares 13 signatures taking and returning `FilePath`, all of which compiled while `return self`
	// did not). What was actually missing was nothing: the receiver is parameter 0, a BORROWED managed value
	// of the enclosing type, so handing it back here does not create an ownership case — it stops hiding one,
	// and every rule that already governs a borrowed struct/union parameter reaches it unchanged. The
	// load-bearing one is the HAND-OFF promotion: `return self` and `f(self)` are the `return p` / `f(p)` of
	// a `p Point` parameter, decided by `emitOwnedValueReturn` and the ordinary argument rules, in ONE place.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **`resultUsed` IS THE CALLER'S, NOT `true` — AND HARDCODING IT REFUSED A LEGAL `try self.m()`.** This
	// is reached from a VALUE position (`parsePrimary`) and from a `try` TARGET position
	// (`parseTryCallReceiver`), which is a STATEMENT: `try self.advance()` runs a void throwing method for its
	// effect, exactly as `try advance()` and `try other.advance()` do. With the flag pinned `true`, the void
	// arm of `mintDeclaredCallResult` fired instead — `E2004: Function 'C.advance' does not return a value`,
	// about a call whose value nobody asked for. MEASURED against the bootstrap, which compiles and runs the
	// same program. The `self` arm was the ONE arm of `parseTryCallReceiver` not threading the caller's flag;
	// every sibling arm there already passed `false`, which is why the two other spellings were unaffected.
	function parseSelfPrimary(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let selfTok = self.current()

		// A receiver is required for EVERY destination, so its EXISTENCE is established once, here, before
		// the split — which is what keeps `self` in a `static function` out of `parseMethodCall`, whose
		// binding lookup would panic rather than report. It is the existence and NOT the value: the two
		// dotted arms resolve the receiver for themselves, and inside a closure a resolve EMITS an env load,
		// so resolving here as well would leave a dead one behind. See `requireReceiverInScope`.
		try self.requireReceiverInScope(selfTok)

		// ⭐ **ONE ARM FOR EVERY RECEIVER KIND, and the two used to be split for a reason that was never real
		// (D1 opened the enum half, S5 closed the struct half).** An enum's `self` is its i64 TAG or a pointer
		// to its box; a struct's is a pointer to its box; a generic instance's is a pointer to its box — and
		// each is exactly what a PARAMETER of that type holds, which `self` is. So there is nothing here to
		// decide: the value goes back, and whichever rule governs a borrowed value of that type governs it.
		if self.peekKind() != TokenKind.dot 'bareSelf'
			let receiver = try self.requireReceiverValue(selfTok)
			self.advance()
			return receiver
		end 'bareSelf'

		if self.methodCallsAt(self.pos) 'methodCall'
			return try self.parseMethodCall(func, resultUsed: resultUsed)
		end 'methodCall'

		return try self.parseExplicitSelfFieldRead()
	end 'parseSelfPrimary'

	// `self.x` in value position. It asks `requireFieldIndex` where the bare spelling reads the
	// alias's index — see `parseSelfFieldRead` for why that is the one legitimate difference — and
	// converges on the same `emitFieldLoad`.
	function parseExplicitSelfFieldRead() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let selfTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.self)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let fieldTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)

		let layout = try self.enclosingLayout(selfTok)
		return self.emitFieldLoad(try self.requireReceiverValue(selfTok), layout: layout, index: try self.requireFieldIndex(layout, fieldTok: fieldTok), range: SourceRange.between(selfTok, last: fieldTok))
	end 'parseExplicitSelfFieldRead'

	// `self.x = <expr>`. The explicit spelling, resolving its field token to an index and then
	// handing over to the one shared write above — so both spellings ask one mutability rule and
	// emit one store.
	function parseExplicitSelfFieldAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		let selfTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.self)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.dot)
		let fieldTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.identifier)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)

		// The receiver first, so `self.x = 1` in a `static function` reports what is actually wrong
		// rather than tripping over the enclosing type's layout.
		let receiver = try self.requireReceiverValue(selfTok)
		let layout = try self.enclosingLayout(selfTok)
		try self.emitCheckedSelfFieldStore(func, block: block, receiver: receiver, layout: layout, index: try self.requireFieldIndex(layout, fieldTok: fieldTok), fieldTok: fieldTok, range: SourceRange.between(selfTok, last: fieldTok))
	end 'parseExplicitSelfFieldAssignment'

	// `p.x = <expr>` — write field `x` of the box `p` points at. The exact mirror of
	// `parseFieldAccess`: the same three tokens resolved through the same two steps (which type is
	// `p`, which slot does that type call `x`), landing on `storeIndirect` where that one lands on
	// `loadIndirect`. There is no `fieldStore` op for the same reason there is no `fieldLoad`.
	//
	// ⭐ **TWO THINGS MUST BE WRITABLE AND THEY ARE TWO DIFFERENT FACTS**: the INSTANCE (`let p` is
	// not writable through) and the FIELD (`export let version` is not writable). Both report E2013
	// — one code, because it is one defect from two sides: a write to something declared not to
	// accept one. Both anchor at the ROOT VARIABLE token and not at the field, which is measured
	// from the bootstrap and pinned by `structs.md`'s two `error.*` cases at `:16:2`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE INSTANCE RANK IS NOT `requireMutableBinding` — A PARAMETER PASSES IT AND STILL REFUSES A
	// REBIND** (rung PBR-1). `let p` is refused here exactly as before; a parameter is not, because it
	// BORROWS the caller's box and a borrow's pointee fields are writable. The two doors and the ruling
	// behind the split are at `requireMutableBinding` / `requireWritableInstance`.
	//
	// ⭐ **RESOLVE, THEN PERMIT — AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE DIAGNOSTIC CONTRACT.** The checks below
	// run in two bands, and the band boundary is what makes every message name a change that
	// actually fixes the program:
	//
	//   1. RESOLUTION — is `p` a struct, and does it have a field `x`? Asked FIRST, and asked in the
	//      same order and through the same two helpers `parseFieldAccess` asks them, so a field
	//      WRITE and a field READ can never come to disagree about what `p.x` even DENOTES.
	//   2. PERMISSION — may this write happen? The instance, then the field.
	//
	// ⚠ A PERMISSION message can only ever advise "declare it `var`", so asking permission about a
	// write that does not RESOLVE names a change that does not work: `let n = 5` then `n.x = 1` once
	// reported *"cannot assign to immutable variable: 'n'"*, sending the reader to write `var n` —
	// which changes nothing, because an `int` has no fields. It now reports the not-a-struct error
	// the READ path always reported for the same three tokens. Same defect, same message, whichever
	// way the program touches the field.
	//
	// ⚠ **WITHIN the permission band, THE INSTANCE STILL OUTRANKS THE FIELD, and that too is a
	// decision.** When both fail — a `let` instance holding a `let` field — telling a reader to
	// write `var version` would not fix `let p`. In the bootstrap this ranking is a positional
	// ACCIDENT (`ResolveStructVariable(requireMutable: true)` happens to throw before
	// `RequireMutableField` runs) and nothing there declares it. `struct-field-assign-precedence.md`
	// pins BOTH ranks, because a rule stated only in the order of two `if`s is a rule the next edit
	// reorders.
	//
	// ⭐ **VISIBILITY (E3014) LANDS BETWEEN THE TWO, AND THE WHOLE BAND IS ONE CHAIN: EACH RANK IS
	// THE FIX THAT ACTUALLY WORKS NEXT.** The instance, then whether the field can be SEEN, then
	// whether it can be WRITTEN — because `export private` does not fix `let p`, and `var private`
	// does not fix a field the caller cannot reach at all. All three ranks are MEASURED against the
	// bootstrap, not inferred:
	//
	//   • `var v` + `let` unexported field  → **E3014** (visibility outranks field mutability)
	//   • `let v` + `var` unexported field  → **E2013 'v'** (the instance outranks visibility)
	//   • `v.nosuch`                        → **E3018** (resolution outranks all of it)
	//
	// The READ path asks the same chain minus its middle: it has no instance to write through, so
	// resolution is followed directly by visibility (`parseFieldAccess`).
	function parseFieldAssignment(func IrFunction, block IrBlock) throws ParseError
		// The base's own token index, taken before the chain walk moves the cursor, so the E3070 subject below
		// comes through the shared `borrowSubjectNameAt` — ONE derivation of "which storage does this write go
		// through", asked identically at every door (`parseAssignment` captures it for the same reason).
		let baseIndex = self.pos
		// `resolveFieldChain` reads `base.f1.…fN` (a single `p.x` or a chain `o.inner.x`), resolving every
		// intermediate struct field and leaving the cursor on the `=`. Its `requireStructBase` already
		// enforced the base's live-binding guard.
		let step = try self.resolveFieldChain()
		try self.consume(TokenKind.equals)
		let index = try self.requireFieldIndex(step.layout, fieldTok: step.lastFieldTok)

		try self.requireWritableInstance(step.baseBinding, nameTok: step.baseTok)
		try self.requireFieldAccessible(step.layout, index: index, fieldTok: step.lastFieldTok)

		if not step.layout.fieldIsMutable(index) 'immutableField'
			throw ParseError.assignToImmutableField(String.from(step.layout.name), fieldName: String.from(step.lastFieldTok.value), line: step.baseTok.line, column: step.baseTok.column)
		end 'immutableField'

		// ⭐⭐ **E3070's TWO DOORS, WHICH THIS STORE OWED THE MOMENT ITS INSTANCE COULD BE A PARAMETER
		// (PBR-1).** `b.items = <fresh>` DROPS the record the field held (`emitFieldWrite`'s decref), freeing
		// every element some other name still borrows — the same write `items = <fresh>` performs one
		// indirection in, which is why `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` has carried both of these all along and
		// this door is the twin that had nothing to guard: until PBR-1 no write here could reach a caller's
		// record, because a parameter base was refused outright. **MEASURED on the permission change alone:**
		// `let s = try b.items.get(0) otherwise ""` + `wipe(b)` whose body is `b.items = StringArray.create()`
		// compiled clean and faulted **0xC0000005**, where the oracle reports the conflict.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THE SUBJECT IS THE CHAIN BASE `b`, NOT THE FIELD — WHICH IS WHY THE TWO SEEDS BELOW ARE GATED
		// DIFFERENTLY AND THE SIBLING DOOR'S TWO ARE NOT.** `iterationSubjectNameAt` names the FIELD only for a
		// `self`-rooted chain, so `emitCheckedSelfFieldStore` gets per-field subjects for free: a write to
		// `n` cannot collide with a borrow taken out of `items`, because they are two subjects. Here EVERY
		// field of `b` collapses onto one subject, so an ungated note makes every store through `b` conflict
		// with every live borrow rooted at `b` — including stores that free nothing. **MEASURED (review):**
		// `let s = try b.items.get(0) otherwise ""` + `b.n = 5` was refused *"cannot mutate 'b' via '='"*
		// where the oracle compiles it and prints `hello`. `borrow-liveness:a-scalar-field-store-is-not-a-
		// conflict` is that program, and it is the store door's twin of the METHOD door's own over-rejection
		// guard (`method-writing-a-non-array-field-is-not-a-conflict`).
		//
		// Both seeds are keyed on ONE subject derivation and ONE field-type read, bound here rather than
		// spelled at each — and since W-BORROW they are keyed on ONE GATE as well.
		//
		// ⛔ **ONE GATE FOR BOTH SEEDS, AND THE MASK'S HALF OF IT USED TO BE `typeIsArrayInstance` — WHICH WAS A
		// USE-AFTER-FREE (W-BORROW).** `b.inner = other` frees a nested STRUCT and cascades to the arrays that
		// struct owns, and a borrow taken through `b.inner.items.get(0)` carries this very same subject `b`; a
		// `List`-typed field frees the nodes a `first()` borrow names. MEASURED: `let s = try b.items.first()
		// otherwise "none"` + `wipe(b)` whose body is `b.items = StringList.create()` compiled clean and ran to
		// **0xC0000005**. The two seeds are the SAME-BODY and CROSS-FUNCTION halves of one rule and had drifted
		// to two different gates; `typeOwnsBorrowableStorage` is the one question both were asking, and its
		// header carries the two widths this has been measured wrong at. A scalar field frees nothing at all
		// and a `String` field frees no container, which is the whole of what it excludes.
		//
		// `writtenParamMask` (E3019) deliberately stays untouched beside both — the row `IrFunction`'s table
		// already carries for the field-chain write whose base is a parameter: **no / yes**. Setting E3019's
		// column here would make `let state = …` + `clearAll(state)` an error, which is the very program
		// `array-managed-field-reassign:reassign-empty-array-field` pins at exit 0.
		let fieldType = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(step.layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
		let subjectName = self.borrowSubjectNameAt(baseIndex)
		let storeFreesBorrowableStorage = self.typeOwnsBorrowableStorage(fieldType)

		if self.functionHoldsABorrow(func) and storeFreesBorrowableStorage 'mayConflictWithABorrow'
			self.noteBorrowSubjectRebind(func, subjectName: subjectName, anchor: step.baseTok)
		end 'mayConflictWithABorrow'

		if storeFreesBorrowableStorage 'freesBorrowableStorage'
			func.storageWrittenParamMask = func.storageWrittenParamMask or self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: subjectName)
		end 'freesBorrowableStorage'

		// ⭐⭐ **W117's COLUMN, AND THIS IS THE DOOR NEITHER OF THE OTHER TWO RECORDS AT ALL.** `bump(b Box)`
		// whose body is `b.n = 99` writes the caller's record and, before this line, was summarised as writing
		// nothing — so `let G = Box.make()` + `bump(G)` compiled and MUTATED a global the program declared
		// `let` (measured on both compilers, `g 99`). Neither existing mask could be widened to catch it:
		// E3019's would refuse `let a = Box.make()` + `bump(a)`, which both references ACCEPT, and E3070's is
		// scoped to fields whose drop frees something, on purpose. Ungated by field type, for the reason the sibling door above
		// carries: a scalar field of a `let` global's box is as much the global as an array field is.
		func.writesThroughParamMask = func.writesThroughParamMask or self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: subjectName)

		// Parsed AFTER both rejections, so a refused program emits no op — `emitCheckedGlobalStore`'s
		// discipline, for its reason. The value's start is captured so `emitFieldWrite` can decide, for a
		// MANAGED field, whether the new value is a bare-local reference to poison.
		let valueStart = self.pos
		let parsed = try self.parseExpression(func, block: block)
		// The COERCED value is what the store writes — `p.v = 3` into a `float` field stores the widened
		// f64. Same door as the struct literal and the bare self-field spelling.
		let value = try self.requireFieldType(step.layout, index: index, fieldType: self.storedFieldType(step.box, layout: step.layout, index: index), value: parsed, anchor: step.lastFieldTok)

		try self.emitFieldWrite(func, box: step.box, layout: step.layout, index: index, value: value, argBareLocal: self.rhsWasBareLocalReference(valueStart), anchor: step.lastFieldTok, range: SourceRange.between(step.baseTok, last: step.lastFieldTok))
	end 'parseFieldAssignment'

	// ⭐ **WRITE A FIELD, DROPPING THE OLD MANAGED VALUE AND MOVING THE NEW ONE IN** (P1.4a wave 2) — the
	// assignment counterpart of the construct-time `emitStructLiteral` move. A SCALAR field is a plain store.
	// A MANAGED field (a `String` or a `struct`) first MOVES the new value in (`moveInPayload` — a borrowed
	// literal is promoted, an owned value transfers, a borrowed struct is CO-OWNED) and only THEN drops the
	// value it displaces (`emitDecref`, tag-routed), so the field owns exactly one value across the write and
	// the old one is freed exactly once. Mirrors the `var`-String reassignment drop model, one indirection out.
	// ⛔ **THE ACQUIRE-THEN-RELEASE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND WAS THE OTHER WAY ROUND UNTIL 2026-08-12** — see
	// the measured use-after-free at the emission site.
	// ⚠ **A FIELD WRITE DELIBERATELY RECORDS NO PARAMETER MUTATION (E3019, the call-site half)** — and since
	// PBR-1 that is a RULING rather than a vacuous truth. It used to be vacuous: the only box a write could
	// reach was the enclosing `self`, because a field write through a PARAMETER was refused outright (E2013),
	// and a method writing its own receiver's data does not make the caller's `let` an error in shv2 (see
	// `receiverOwnerMask` for that ruling, the corpus case that pins it and the measured oracle inconsistency
	// it settles). A parameter's box is now reachable, and the answer is the SAME `no` — it is the row
	// `IrFunction`'s table already carries for a field-chain write whose base is a parameter, and
	// `array-managed-field-reassign:reassign-empty-array-field` is the case that goes red the moment E3019's
	// column is fed from here. **E3070's column IS fed**, at the two doors `parseFieldAssignment` states.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE TYPE A WRITE THROUGH *THIS BOX* ACTUALLY STORES — the ONE derivation, read by the TYPE CHECK
	// and by the STORE.** A field's declared type is the SHARED layout's, so an opaque `export var value as T`
	// answers `typeParameter` however concrete the receiver is; only the box says what `T` stands for. A
	// `genericInstance` box binds it (`slotTypeThroughReceiver`, the same door `emitFieldLoad` retypes a READ
	// through), a `structRef` box is the shared generic body and leaves it opaque.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS A HELPER BECAUSE THE STORE ALREADY DID THIS AND THE CHECK DID NOT, AND THAT SPLIT WAS A WILD
	// WRITE (BATCH32 review).** `emitFieldWrite` resolved `T` → the instance's argument before classifying the
	// slot, while `requireFieldType` — one line earlier at the very same doors — was handed the UNSUBSTITUTED
	// `fieldTypeOf`. So the verdict was taken against `type parameter`, which admits everything, and the store
	// was then performed against `Label`. **MEASURED 2026-08-06:** on `LabelBox = Box with Label`,
	// `b.value = Other.create(99)` COMPILED, wrote an `Other` box into the `Label` slot, and reading
	// `b.value.text` exited **0xC0000005** — where the bootstrap refuses it (`E3005 cannot assign a value of
	// type 'Other' to field 'value' of 'LabelBox', which holds 'Label'`). Two derivations of one fact, and
	// nothing made them agree; now there is one and both read it.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS THE READ'S DOOR VERBATIM SINCE W25**, where it used to carry its own `typeParameter`-only
	// copy of the condition. That copy had the read's blind spot exactly: a field typed through an inner
	// `typealias Items = Array with T` is a `genericInstance`, so the store's verdict was taken against
	// `Array with T` for a box that holds `Array with Num` — the same two-derivations-of-one-fact shape the
	// paragraph above records, one declaration form over.
	function storedFieldType(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns MaxonType
		return self.slotTypeThroughReceiver(box, declared: self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames))
	end 'storedFieldType'

	function emitFieldWrite(func IrFunction, box ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ValueId, argBareLocal bool, anchor Token, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		// ⭐⭐ **E3064's SEED, SET AT THE ONE EMITTER BOTH FIELD-ASSIGNMENT DOORS FUNNEL INTO.** `self.n = 5`,
		// `n = 5` and `p.x = 5` all arrive here, and every one of them writes an aggregate that already existed —
		// which is an effect the function's RETURN type cannot express. Set HERE and not at the two doors, for
		// the reason the loop-phi predicate is one predicate: a third assignment spelling would otherwise have to
		// remember. ⚠ Deliberately NOT on `emitFieldStore`, which also fills a FRESH box — see
		// `IrFunction.assignsIntoAnExistingAggregate` for what setting it there would cost.
		func.assignsIntoAnExistingAggregate = true

		// ⭐ **ESCAPE (P1.5-A2b): a capturing closure may not be stored in a struct FIELD** — a struct box is
		// heap memory that outlives the frame, so the field would hold a closure whose env is gone. Positioned
		// at the field name (`anchor`).
		try self.rejectEscapingClosure(value, message: "cannot store a closure that captures in field '{String.from(anchor.value)}' of '{String.from(layout.name)}': {CapturingClosureEscapeSuffix}", tok: anchor)

		let fieldType = self.storedFieldType(box, layout: layout, index: index)

		// ⭐ **A BARE OPAQUE `T` FIELD REASSIGNMENT (P1.7 Finding A).** The field is typed opaque `T`
		// (`typeParameter`) in the shared layout; how its OLD value drops depends on the box:
		//   • the enclosing SELF (a `structRef`, the shared generic body) keeps the field opaque, so a MANAGED
		//     instantiation's old value drops through the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param` and the new value
		//     transfers in — `emitOpaqueFieldReassign`, which also handles the trivial-inert store.
		//   • a CONCRETE instance (`b.saved = …`, a `genericInstance`) knows its argument, so the field was
		//     RETYPED to that substituted concrete type above (as `emitFieldLoad` retypes a READ) and never
		//     reaches this arm — a managed concrete field drops the old through its own decref (`__str_decref` /
		//     `__destruct_<T>`, Finding B) and moves the new in; a trivial one reclassifies to a scalar store.
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **"AS `emitFieldLoad` RETYPES A READ" IS NOW LITERALLY THE SAME CALL, AND THE DAY IT WAS ONLY A
		// COMMENT IT WAS A READ-AFTER-FREE (A4i review).** This line spelled `adoptType(substitutedInstanceArg(…))`
		// — the exact body the READ door (now `slotTypeThroughReceiver`) had before A4i routed it through
		// `declaredSlotType` — so the
		// READ door was fixed and its declared twin was not. What the copy handed on was a bare `named`, and the
		// consumer here is `classifyUnionPayload`, which resolves a `named` through its OWN cascade: it has a
		// struct arm (so a `Box with Point` write kept working, which is why the split hid) and NO generic-alias
		// arm, so a `Holder with IntBox` write fell through to `undeclaredName`, took the SCALAR store, and
		// neither dropped the old box nor moved the new one in.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED**, `var h = BoxHolder.hold(IntBox.create(7)); h.item = IntBox.create(8); h.item.value`:
		// the temporary was freed at the statement and the field kept pointing at it, so the read printed
		// **4557430888798830399** — `0x3F3F3F3F3F3F3F3F`, the free poison — where the oracle prints 8. A4i is
		// what made it reachable: before it, reading the field back was `E3011 Unknown type 'IntBox'`, so the
		// program could not be written at all and a clean refusal became a silent wrong answer.
		//
		// Sharing the door rather than re-spelling it is the cure: a re-tag added to `declaredSlotType` now
		// reaches the WRITE the same day it reaches the READ, which is exactly what nothing made true before.
		if maxonTypeTag(fieldType) == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueTypeParamField'
			let tag = self.tagOf(box)
			if tag != ValueTypeTag.structRef 'unexpectedBox'
				panic("emitFieldWrite: a `typeParameter` field write has a box of tag {tag.name} — only the enclosing self (a structRef) or a concrete generic instance (`b.f = …`) reaches an opaque field, and `storedFieldType` has already substituted the latter")
			end 'unexpectedBox'

			try self.emitOpaqueFieldReassign(box, layout: layout, index: index, value: value, fieldType: fieldType, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, anchor: anchor, range: range)
			return
		end 'opaqueTypeParamField'

		let class = classifyUnionPayload(self.signatures, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, fieldType: fieldType, slotScopeFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
		if not payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'scalarField'
			self.emitFieldStore(box, layout: layout, index: index, value: value, range: range)
			return
		end 'scalarField'

		// ⛔⛔ **THE SHARED BODY CANNOT NAME THE DROP FOR AN INSTANCE OVER ITS OWN PARAMETERS, AND STORING OVER
		// IT WOULD LEAK WHAT THE ARGUMENT BROUGHT.** See the door below for the whole argument and the
		// measurement; refused there, positioned at the field name, rather than emitted here.
		try self.requireSharedBodyReassignHasAStaticDrop(box, layout: layout, index: index, fieldType: fieldType, anchor: anchor)

		let old = self.emitFieldLoad(box, layout: layout, index: index, range: range)

		// ⛔⛔ **ACQUIRE THE NEW VALUE BEFORE RELEASING THE OLD ONE — THE OTHER ORDER IS A USE-AFTER-FREE WHEN
		// THEY ARE THE SAME RECORD.** This door emitted the release FIRST until 2026-08-12, which made an
		// INDIRECT self-assignment free the value and then incref the freed memory.
		//
		// ⚠ **MEASURED**, `function assign(dst Blk, src Blk)` doing `dst.args = src.args`, called as
		// `assign(a, src: a)`: a struct is a pointer to a shared heap box, so both parameters name ONE box and
		// the store's two operands are the same `Array` record. Release-first took its refcount 1 → 0 (freed),
		// the acquire then touched freed memory and the program exited **0xC0000005**. Acquire-first makes the
		// same store a net no-op (1 → 2 → 1), which is what it must be. The bootstrap gets this WRONG too — it
		// prints the element as `0` and reports `mm_decref: refcount underflow` — so it is not the oracle here.
		//
		// ⚠ **NO STATIC CHECK CAN STAND IN FOR THIS.** The LEXICAL no-op `self.args = self.args` is refused by
		// E3067 (`rejectDegenerateAssignment`), and that refusal is exactly what hid the defect: it masks the
		// spelling while the aliasing route through two parameters is a genuine store whose operands only
		// coincide at RUN TIME. The store protocol itself has to be safe, so the fix is the order, not a wider
		// refusal. Pinned by `specs-shv2/managed-field-store-order.md`.
		//
		// This is the order the other DURABLE-STORAGE writes in this file already use and state for themselves —
		// `reassignCellBinding` ("the release must come AFTER the new value is settled and BEFORE the store"),
		// `emitCheckedGlobalStore`, and `reassignBindingValue` in an inline comment rather than its header.
		// ⚠ **ONE DOOR STILL RELEASES FIRST AND IS DELIBERATELY LEFT THAT WAY: `emitOpaqueFieldReassign`**, this
		// same function's other arm. It is safe only because of a PRECONDITION, not because it follows the rule
		// — the reason is written at its own decref, and a widening that gives it a real acquire must move that
		// decref down. Do not read this paragraph as "every other door is ordered correctly".
		//
		// An opaque `T` field is settled above by `emitOpaqueFieldReassign`, so the class here is always a
		// concrete managed one and the `owner` is never read — it is passed because the box IS the owner of
		// whatever lands in the slot, which is the fact the parameter names.
		let stored = try self.moveInPayload(class, arg: value, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, owner: box, anchor: anchor, range: range)

		self.emitDecref(old, range: range)
		self.emitFieldStore(box, layout: layout, index: index, value: stored, range: range)
	end 'emitFieldWrite'

	// ⭐⭐ **WOULD THIS WRITE DISPLACE A VALUE THE BODY CANNOT NAME A DROP FOR? — `emitOpaqueFieldReassign`'s
	// question ONE LEVEL OF NESTING UP, and the level at which the descriptor has no answer.**
	//
	// An opaque `T` field is reassignable in a shared body because `__drop_type_param` reads `T`'s destructor out
	// of the enclosing instance's layout descriptor at run time. A field typed `Cell` inside `type Holder uses T`
	// is a `Cell with Holder.T` — an INSTANCE over that parameter — and the descriptor describes the PARAMETERS,
	// not the instances built over them: it holds `String`'s `__str_decref` and nothing that names
	// `__destruct_Cell_String`. So the drop the body would emit is whatever the NON-CONCRETE instance answers
	// (`__mm_decref`, since `Cell with Holder.T`'s only field is opaque and so owns nothing), which frees the
	// cell's box and strands everything the argument put inside it.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED on this tree before the refusal**: `Holder.replace(next Cell)` doing `self.cell = next`, over
	// `Holder with String`, printed its answer and exited **101** — the leak gate, no diagnostic. The
	// CONCRETE-receiver form of the same assignment (`h.cell = StrCell.make(…)`) is correct and stays legal:
	// `slotTypeThroughReceiver` substitutes the receiver's argument, so that write drops through the concrete
	// `__destruct_Cell_String`.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TEST IS DIVERGENCE, NOT NON-CONCRETENESS**, and that is what keeps an all-trivial program legal.
	// `Holder with Integer`'s field really is released by the `__mm_decref` the shared body picks, so a program
	// with no managed instantiation is untouched; the refusal fires exactly when the one callee the shared body
	// can emit is the wrong one for some instantiation the program actually interns.
	//
	// A CONCRETE receiver never reaches the registry walk: its box is a `genericInstance` and its field type was
	// already substituted, so the two cheap gates below settle it. Only a write through the enclosing `self` of a
	// GENERIC type gets that far, which is what keeps an O(instances) question off every field write.
	//
	// It THROWS rather than answering, so the one callee the refusal names is the one the divergence was
	// measured against — a predicate would have had the message recompute it, which is the same fact derived
	// twice at the one site where the two must agree.
	function requireSharedBodyReassignHasAStaticDrop(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, fieldType MaxonType, anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if maxonTypeTag(fieldType) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAnInstanceField'
			return
		end 'notAnInstanceField'

		if layout.typeParamNames.isEmpty() or self.tagOf(box) != ValueTypeTag.structRef 'notTheSharedBody'
			return
		end 'notTheSharedBody'

		let sharedCallee = self.signatures.genericInstanceBoxDropCallee(typeNameIdOf(fieldType))
		if not self.signatures.instanceFieldDropDivergesAcrossInstantiations(layout.name, index: index, sharedCallee: sharedCallee) 'oneCalleeServesEveryInstantiation'
			return
		end 'oneCalleeServesEveryInstantiation'

		throw ParseError.unsupported("reassigning '{String.from(anchor.value)}' of '{String.from(layout.name)}', whose type is a generic instance over this type's OWN parameters — the shared generic body compiles once for every instantiation, so the drop for the value being displaced is not one callee: it is `{String.from(sharedCallee)}` here and something else at some instantiation. The box's own destructor releases the field correctly, so reassign it through a CONCRETE receiver instead; a descriptor slot carrying a nested instance's per-instantiation destructor is a later slice", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireSharedBodyReassignHasAStaticDrop'

	// ⭐ **REASSIGN A BARE OPAQUE `T` FIELD IN THE SHARED GENERIC BODY (P1.7 Finding A)** — the opaque twin of the
	// concrete managed field write. The new value MUST be an OWNED feed parameter: the shared body cannot COPY an
	// opaque `T`, so ownership transfers — `markOpaqueFieldReassignFeed` recorded the reassigned parameter as a
	// feed, so `bindParameters` enrolled it owned and the concrete call site transferred it. The field's OLD value
	// drops ONLY when some instantiation makes it MANAGED: then through the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param`
	// (`emitDecref`'s `typeParameter` arm, whose descriptor the method reserved via `scanDescriptorNeedsInto`'s
	// `reassignsOpaqueField` signal, inert for a trivial instantiation whose `destroyFunc@40` is 0). An ALL-TRIVIAL
	// field owns no heap, so no drop and no descriptor — the store is byte-identical to the pre-slice plain store.
	// The new value then ALWAYS transfers into the field (a bare-local source is poisoned, E3102 on later use), so
	// this frame does not also drop it AND the enrolled-owned parameter is never left live to drop at a scope exit
	// (which would need a descriptor a trivial method does not carry). A non-owned RHS (aliasing one opaque field
	// into another, whose descriptor-gated copy is a later slice) is refused cleanly rather than storing a borrow.
	function emitOpaqueFieldReassign(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ValueId, fieldType MaxonType, argBareLocal bool, anchor Token, range SourceRange) throws ParseError
		// ⛔⛔ **THE ARRAY MOVE-IN MADE THIS SAME DEMAND UNTIL W60, AND IT NO LONGER DOES — THE TWO DOORS HAVE
		// DIVERGED, AND THIS RECORDS *WHY* SO NEITHER IS READ AS AN OVERSIGHT.** A borrowed opaque `T` stored
		// into a CONTAINER now takes a reference through the descriptor's `retainFunc@64`
		// (`referenceBorrowedOpaqueElement`), because a container store adds a slot and nothing else: the
		// container's element walk releases exactly what the store took.
		//
		// ⚠ **A FIELD REASSIGNMENT IS NOT THAT SHAPE — IT ALSO HAS TO RELEASE THE VALUE IT DISPLACES**, and
		// that half is what is missing here rather than the retain. The old-value drop and the descriptor-need
		// seed both read `genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation` (the CONSUME boundary — see the next
		// paragraph but one), so a co-owned-trivial instantiation emits no drop and reserves no descriptor to
		// drop through; a retain widened in on its own would store over a value nothing releases. So the demand
		// stays here, and the two doors are one rule about the RETAIN and two rules about the DISPLACEMENT.
		//
		// ⚠ **BOTH DOORS ASK OWNERSHIP OF THE VALUE AT THE STORE, AND A PARAMETER ANSWERS "OWNED" WHATEVER ITS
		// CALLER DID — SO ONE METHOD HOP USED TO DEFEAT BOTH (W41-fix).** That is closed at the CALLER rather
		// than here: `handleEscapingBorrowFeed` takes a real reference for a borrowed opaque argument at a
		// callee-storage feed position, so the "owned" answer this door reads is TRUE by construction.
		if not self.valueIsOwnedHeap(value) 'notOwned'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("reassigning the opaque type-parameter field '{String.from(anchor.value)}' of '{String.from(layout.name)}' from a value that is not an owned parameter — the shared generic body cannot copy an opaque `T` value, so assign it from a parameter the method consumes; aliasing one opaque field into another needs the descriptor-gated copy a later slice adds", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'notOwned'

		// ⭐ A CO-OWNED TRIVIAL STRUCT instantiation (`Box with Point`, the retain-escaping rung) cannot be
		// reassigned in a shared body: the box CO-OWNS its trivial `T` field (retained at the constructor feed,
		// dropped by its destructor), but this opaque reassignment has no way to drop the field's OLD co-owned
		// value — a trivial struct's `__mm_decref` cannot be emitted from the opaque body. Storing the new value
		// over the old would LEAK it. Rejected cleanly rather than shipping the leak. A managed instantiation
		// reassigns through the descriptor as before, and a scalar is inert — neither is a co-owned trivial, so
		// this fires on exactly the co-ownership gap.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE REASON IS NOW THE *SITE'S* WIRING, NOT THE DESCRIPTOR'S CONTENTS, AND THIS SAID OTHERWISE
		// (W41-trivial).** It read *"the descriptor-gated `__drop_type_param` is inert for a trivial
		// instantiation (`destroyFunc@40` is 0)"* — and that word is no longer 0: it is stamped for every
		// argument owning a record, co-owned trivial aggregates included, which is what makes the container
		// column below releasable. What is still missing here is the two gates BELOW this one: the old-value
		// drop and the descriptor-need seed (`isManagedOpaqueTypeParamField`) both read
		// `genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation`, the CONSUME boundary, so a co-owned-trivial instantiation
		// emits no drop and reserves no descriptor to drop through. Widening those two is what would lift this
		// refusal; it is a separate change with three committed `error.` cases pinning this message, and it is
		// REPORTED rather than taken here. The refusal remains TRUE as written — a positioned diagnostic, never
		// a leak — but a reader must not re-derive its old rationale from a zero that has moved.
		if self.signatures.genericTypeFieldHasCoOwnedTrivialInstantiation(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) 'coOwnedTrivialReassign'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("reassigning the type-parameter field '{String.from(anchor.value)}' of '{String.from(layout.name)}' in a shared generic body, where a trivial-struct instantiation co-owns the field — the box retains a co-owned trivial struct at construction and drops it once at destruction, but a shared-body reassignment cannot drop the old co-owned value (the descriptor-gated single-value drop for a trivial struct is a later slice); reassign the field on a concrete instance, or use a managed element type", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'coOwnedTrivialReassign'

		// ⛔ **THIS DOOR RELEASES BEFORE IT "ACQUIRES", THE OPPOSITE OF `emitFieldWrite`, AND IT IS SAFE ONLY
		// BECAUSE OF THE `valueIsOwnedHeap` REFUSAL ABOVE.** That refusal means the acquire here is
		// `transferOwnedInto`, which is pure compile-time bookkeeping — it poisons the move source and de-lists
		// a temp, and emits NO op that reads the record. So there is nothing for a freed record to be read by,
		// and aliasing cannot underflow it either: an owned RHS already carries a reference of its own, so if
		// the value IS the displaced occupant the record is at refcount ≥ 2 when this decref runs.
		// ⚠ **THE MOMENT THIS DOOR TAKES A REAL REFERENCE, THIS ORDER IS THE USE-AFTER-FREE `emitFieldWrite`
		// FIXED ON 2026-08-12 — MOVE THE DECREF BELOW THE ACQUIRE THEN.** That widening is already scheduled:
		// the `notOwned` refusal above says in its own message that "aliasing one opaque field into another
		// needs the descriptor-gated copy a later slice adds", and the container twin has already widened a
		// borrowed opaque element to a real retain through `retainFunc@64`. See
		// `specs-shv2/managed-field-store-order.md` for what the ordering costs when it is wrong.
		if self.signatures.genericTypeFieldHasManagedInstantiation(typeNameIdOf(fieldType)) 'managedDrop'
			let old = self.emitFieldLoad(box, layout: layout, index: index, range: range)
			self.emitDecref(old, range: range)
		end 'managedDrop'

		try self.transferOwnedInto(value, argBareLocal: argBareLocal, anchor: anchor)
		self.emitFieldStore(box, layout: layout, index: index, value: value, range: range)
	end 'emitOpaqueFieldReassign'

	// Declare every field of the enclosing type as a self-field alias, so a bare `width` inside
	// `function area()` of `type Rect` resolves to `self.width`. Called at instance-method entry
	// only; `anchor` is the method's name token, which the layout lookup's diagnostic points at.
	//
	// The alias carries the field's INDEX and its declared TYPE, both read straight out of the
	// layout being enumerated — so the alias cannot name a field the type does not have, which is
	// what lets the read and write paths skip the "does this type have this field?" question that
	// `self.x` and `p.x` must ask.
	//
	// The type comes through `fieldTypeOf`, not off the layout's column directly, because that is
	// the door that RE-INTERNS a `named` type into this file's own interner — the same door
	// `emitFieldLoad` reads a field's type through. Reading the column raw would bind aliases to
	// type-name ids from the SWEEP's table, which this file's interner cannot resolve.
	function declareSelfFieldAliases(anchor Token) throws ParseError
		let layout = try self.enclosingLayout(anchor)
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let fieldName = try layout.fieldNames.get(i) otherwise panic("declareSelfFieldAliases: fieldNames.get OOB at {i} for `type {String.from(layout.name)}`, which declares {layout.fieldCount()}")
			self.scope.declareSelfField(fieldName, varType: self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: i, into: self.artifact.typeNames), fieldIndex: i)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'declareSelfFieldAliases'

	// The `StructLayout` of the type whose body encloses the cursor. The self-field twin of
	// `requireStructBase`: that one resolves the layout of a BINDING's declared type, this one
	// resolves the layout of the enclosing DECLARATION, which is what a receiver and a bare field
	// name are both typed by.
	//
	// Both misses panic rather than report, and for `requireConstructible`'s reason: every caller
	// is inside an instance method, so an enclosing type exists by construction, and the
	// declaration sweep recorded every `type` in the program before this file's parse began.
	function enclosingLayout(anchor Token) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		if self.enclosingType.isEmpty() 'noEnclosingType'
			panic("enclosingLayout: asked for the enclosing type's layout at {anchor.line}:{anchor.column}, but the cursor is at file scope — every caller is inside an instance method, whose body only `parseTypeDeclaration` reaches")
		end 'noEnclosingType'

		// ⛔ **AN `enum`/`union` HAS NO LAYOUT TO ASK FOR (D1)** — and this is the one door the RECEIVER-FIELD
		// family has to say so at, because both spellings of a receiver field access come through it
		// (`parseExplicitSelfFieldRead`
		// and `parseExplicitSelfFieldAssignment`; the bare-name spellings cannot arrive, since `parseFunction`
		// declares no self-field aliases for an enum). Without it, `self.reason` inside a `union` method — the
		// first thing a reader tries on a payload-bearing case — reached the `undeclared` panic below and took
		// the compiler down while blaming the declaration sweep for a disagreement that never happened.
		//
		// A positioned refusal, because the source can perfectly well write it: a case's PAYLOAD is a
		// pattern binding, not a member. (The oracle answers `E4006 has no property or method named 'reason'`;
		// shv2 says the same thing as the E2015 the rest of this rung refuses with, and names the cure.)
		if self.enclosingTypeIsEnum 'enumHasNoFields'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a field access through `self` in a method of `enum`/`union` `{String.from(self.enclosingType)}` — an enum/union declares no fields; a case's PAYLOAD is bound by a pattern (`match self 'k' … fail(reason) then …`), never read through the receiver", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'enumHasNoFields'

		// ⛔ **A PRIMITIVE HAS NO LAYOUT EITHER (W133), AND IT IS THE ARM ABOVE'S TWIN FOR A DIFFERENT REASON:**
		// an enum has fields nowhere to *put*, and a primitive IS its value — `self` inside `extension int` is
		// the integer, not a box, so there is nothing a `.field` could name. `parseFunction` installs no
		// self-field aliases for such a body (its `⚠` states the measured abort), so the only spelling that
		// reaches here is the EXPLICIT `self.x`, which is exactly the door the enum arm was added at.
		//
		// Positioned rather than panicking for that arm's reason: the source can perfectly well write it.
		if primitiveTagOfTypeName(self.enclosingType) != ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'aPrimitiveHasNoFields'
			throw ParseError.unsupported("a field access through `self` in a method of `extension {String.from(self.enclosingType)}` — a primitive IS its value and declares no fields, so `self` here is the {String.from(self.enclosingType)} itself and has no members to read", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
		end 'aPrimitiveHasNoFields'

		match self.signatures.structOf(self.enclosingType) 'declared'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("enclosingLayout: `type {String.from(self.enclosingType)}` is being parsed right now, but the declaration sweep never recorded it — `recordScannedType` and `parseTypeDeclaration` disagree about what opens a type declaration")
		end 'declared'
	end 'enclosingLayout'

	// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER'S SSA VALUE — READ BACK, NEVER ASSUMED.** It is parameter 0, so it is
	// ValueId 0; but that is a CONSEQUENCE of the Std-ABI seeding `parseFunction` performs
	// (`ValueIdRef.create(paramCount)` reserves 0..paramCount-1 and `bindParameters` binds
	// parameter `i` to id `i`), not an independent fact. A literal `0` here would be that
	// convention written down a second time, in the one place that would keep compiling — and
	// silently reading the wrong value — the day the seeding changed.
	//
	// **IT IS ALSO THE ONE PLACE `self` IS REFUSED WHERE THERE IS NO RECEIVER**, because that is
	// the same question: the receiver is bound iff `parseFunction` pushed one, so a failed lookup
	// IS "this is not an instance method". A `static function` is the interesting case — it has an
	// enclosing type but no instance — and file scope has neither. Both report here, positioned,
	// rather than reaching `enclosingLayout` and panicking on an enclosing type that is absent or
	// irrelevant.
	function requireReceiverValue(anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The existence question first, so the two arms below can be a straight dispatch: whichever one
		// answers, the other is provably empty, and there is no third outcome to invent a sentinel for.
		try self.requireReceiverInScope(anchor)

		// ⭐⭐ **INSIDE A CLOSURE THE RECEIVER IS A CAPTURE, AND IT IS THE ORDINARY ONE.** A lifted closure has
		// no receiver of its own — its ValueId 0 is its first PARAMETER — so the enclosing `self` reaches the
		// body the way every other enclosing binding does: one env slot holding the receiver POINTER, read
		// back with one `loadIndirect`. Captured under `SelfParamName`, which is what makes ALL THREE
		// spellings share it: `self.x`, a bare self-field name (`parseSelfFieldRead`) and `self.m(…)` each
		// resolve the receiver through this door, `captureSlotFor` dedupes by name, so a body naming four
		// fields still allocates ONE slot.
		//
		// Before this, the gate here REFUSED the capture — and the refusal was load-bearing, because the
		// enclosing scope is still visible from a closure body: a `lookupValue(SelfParamName)` that reached
		// past it would hand back ValueId 0 and read the closure's own first parameter as a struct box.
		// MEASURED then: `apply(function(k int) gives k + n, x: 1)` inside a method of a `type Counter` with
		// `n = 3` returned **25** — the box pointer's low byte — with no diagnostic anywhere. That cannot
		// arise now for the reason it never arose for an ordinary capture: the closure's OWN scope is fresh
		// and binds no `__self`, so the receiver is found in the enclosing scope or nowhere, and the value it
		// yields is the env load rather than an id from a different function's SSA space.
		match try self.captureScopeOf(SelfParamName, anchor: anchor) 'capturedReceiver'
			found(enclosing) then return try self.emitCaptureRead(SelfParamName, enclosing: enclosing, anchor: anchor)
			notCaptured then break 'capturedReceiver'
		end 'capturedReceiver'

		return try self.scope.lookupValue(SelfParamName) otherwise panic("requireReceiverValue: `requireReceiverInScope` passed and no enclosing scope captured the receiver, so it is bound in this function's own scope — but `lookupValue` missed it. The two are reading one `Scope` and disagree.")
	end 'requireReceiverValue'

	// ⭐ **IS THERE A RECEIVER HERE AT ALL — bound as this function's own parameter 0, or reachable as a
	// CAPTURE of the enclosing method's?** The one home of that rule and of the E2001 it raises, asked by
	// `requireReceiverValue` before it resolves and by `parseSelfPrimary` before it splits on the token
	// after `self`.
	//
	// ⚠ **`parseSelfPrimary` NEEDS THE CHECK WITHOUT THE VALUE, and that is why this is separate.** Its
	// two dotted arms re-resolve the receiver for themselves (`parseMethodCall` from the cursor,
	// `parseExplicitSelfFieldRead` after consuming the field token), so resolving it up front would emit a
	// SECOND env load inside a closure body that nothing reads. Outside a closure the same call was free —
	// which is why the split only became necessary when the capture landed.
	function requireReceiverInScope(anchor Token) throws ParseError
		if self.scope.contains(SelfParamName) or self.captureBinds(SelfParamName) 'haveReceiver'
			return
		end 'haveReceiver'

		throw ParseError.selfOutsideInstanceMethod(anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
	end 'requireReceiverInScope'

	// **The declaration index `fieldTok` names in `layout`, or E3018** — asked by every one of the
	// three places a field is named by a TOKEN: the struct literal's `x:` label, the field READ,
	// and the field WRITE. One home, because it is one question, and the three must not come to
	// disagree about what "this type has no such field" means or where the diagnostic points (the
	// FIELD token — the token that is wrong, never the value it is read from).
	//
	// A BARE self-field name does not ask it: its index came off the alias, which
	// `declareSelfFieldAliases` minted by enumerating the layout, so the field provably exists. A
	// bare name that is NOT a field is not a self-field alias at all and never reaches a field
	// path — it is an undefined variable, and reports as one.
	//
	// It is also the one place `indexOfField`'s "not found" convention is READ. That answer is
	// `fieldCount()` — an index one past the end — and every caller comparing against it by hand was
	// a caller that could get the comparison subtly wrong (`>` rather than `>=`) and index a
	// neighbouring field's slot instead of reporting an error. Now the convention is decoded once.
	function requireFieldIndex(layout StructLayout, fieldTok Token) returns FieldIndex throws ParseError
		let index = layout.indexOfField(fieldTok.value)
		if index >= layout.fieldCount() 'noSuchField'
			throw ParseError.unknownField(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(fieldTok.value), line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column)
		end 'noSuchField'
		return index
	end 'requireFieldIndex'

	// ⭐ **E3014 — A FIELD NOT DECLARED `export` IS REACHABLE ONLY FROM ITS OWN TYPE'S BODY.**
	//
	//     accessible  ==  the field is exported  OR  this code is inside the declaring type
	//
	// **THE GATE IS THE TYPE, NOT THE FILE**, and that is measured rather than inherited: `type B`
	// reading `type A`'s unexported field is E3014 on the bootstrap **in the same file**. BOTH
	// reference registries call it "another file" and both are wrong — v1's CODE agrees with the
	// measurement (it gates on `structName == enclosingType`, TypeResolution.maxon:8204) and only
	// its prose does not. `docs/error-codes.txt`'s doc line is corrected to match.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THAT SENTENCE IS PINNED BY A SPEC, BECAUSE FOR ONE REVIEW-ROUND IT WAS NOT.** As
	// first written this comment asserted the type-vs-file rule while **nothing in the suite could
	// tell the two apart**: every corpus case reaches the field from `main()`, which is inside no
	// type, so they pin only *"outside ANY type ⇒ reject"*. Weakening the test below to
	// `not self.enclosingType.isEmpty()` — a compiler where every method reads every type's private
	// state — left `specs-shv2` at **392/0**, both corpus E3014 cases included (measured, not
	// argued). That is OPEN.md #27's shape exactly: the reason written down enforced nothing while
	// the property held for a reason nobody had written. `specs-shv2/field-visibility-is-type-scoped.md`
	// is the sabotage's red, kept: two cases that fail under that edit, plus the positive companion
	// (a type reaching its OWN private field through a LOCAL, which the self-field path never sees)
	// so the rule is pinned in the ADMIT direction too. **Do not delete it to make a refactor green.**
	//
	// **ONE HOME, TWO CALLERS** — the field READ and the field WRITE. It is one rule; written at
	// both sites, a future answer to what "accessible" means (a `module`-visible field, a friend
	// type) taught to one and not the other would let a private field be WRITTEN where reading it
	// correctly reports. An accepted access that should have been refused is a wrong answer, not a
	// compile error.
	//
	// `namesEnclosingType` is the SAME predicate `requireConstructible` (E3076) and
	// `parseTypeReference` ask — "am I inside type T's body?" — so visibility, construction and
	// `Self` cannot come to disagree about where a type's body ends.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT ASKED AT THE STRUCT-LITERAL SITE, AND THE REASON IS THE SPEC'S, NOT E3076'S.**
	// Initialization is exempt outright: *"All fields (exported or not) can be initialized in
	// struct literals, since the type itself determines what fields exist. The visibility only
	// affects access after construction"* (specs/export-var-fields.md). In shv2 that exemption is
	// VACUOUS today — E3076 already refuses every literal written outside its own type's body, so
	// no out-of-type literal survives to ask — but it is the SPEC's rule that makes the absence
	// correct rather than merely unreachable, and it is written here so the day E3076 relaxes does
	// not silently take the exemption with it. (v1 needs the rule live, and spells it `isInit`.)
	//
	// ⚠ **NOR IS IT ASKED ON THE SELF-FIELD PATH**, where it could not fail: a bare `count` and
	// `self.count` both resolve through `enclosingLayout`, so `namesEnclosingType(layout.name)`
	// holds by construction. The rule is asked wherever it can be broken, which is wherever the
	// layout came from a BINDING's type rather than from the enclosing declaration.
	function requireFieldAccessible(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, fieldTok Token) throws ParseError
		// Asked FIRST because it rescues BOTH tiers below: inside the declaring type's own body the reader is
		// the declaring file, so neither the type's visibility nor the field's can refuse it.
		if self.namesEnclosingType(layout.name) 'insideDeclaringType'
			return
		end 'insideDeclaringType'

		// ⭐⭐ **THIS FILE REQUIRES `layout.name` VISIBLE, AND THIS IS THE ONE LINE THAT KNOWS IT.** Everything
		// below decides whether the access is ALLOWED; the export audit needs the prior fact that it was
		// ASKED FOR, because a file that reaches a type only this way never writes its name and therefore
		// leaves no trace in `usedTypeNames`. Recorded after the enclosing-type rescue and not before it: the
		// rescued access is by construction to this file's OWN declaration, which credits nothing.
		self.recordMemberAccessedTypeName(layout.name)

		// ⭐⭐ **E4006 — THE TYPE'S OWN VISIBILITY OUTRANKS THE FIELD'S (N1a), and v1 orders the two exactly
		// this way** (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/TypeResolution.maxon:8215-8224`, the only implementation of
		// this rule in either reference): if the reader may not name the type at all, "this field is not
		// exported" is a sentence about a type they cannot see. The message says "Unknown type" for the same
		// reason, and it is the reference's own wording.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE FIELD ACCESS IS THE ANCHOR, NOT THE `Type.` THAT PRODUCED THE VALUE**, and that is measured
		// rather than chosen: the reference reports `error.non-exported-type-cross-file` at `p.x` and not at
		// the `InternalPoint.create(42)` two lines above. ⇒ **A hidden type's `export`ed STATIC stays
		// callable**, which both references also permit — what a caller cannot do is reach into the value.
		if not self.signatures.isVisibleFrom(layout.visibility, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'typeHidden'
			throw ParseError.hiddenTypeName(String.from(layout.name), line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column)
		end 'typeHidden'

		if self.fieldReachableFromOutsideItsType(layout, index: index) 'wideEnough'
			return
		end 'wideEnough'

		throw ParseError.unexportedFieldAccess(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(fieldTok.value), line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column)
	end 'requireFieldAccessible'

	// ⭐ **THE THREE FIELD TIERS, AND THE ONE THAT IS NOT THE ORDINARY MEANING OF ITS NAME (N1a).** A field's
	// DEFAULT tier answers `false` here and is then rescued by the caller's enclosing-type test, so `file`
	// means "this type's own body" for a field where it means "this file" everywhere else — which is the
	// measurement `requireFieldAccessible`'s header records, not a choice made here. The two WIDER tiers mean
	// exactly what they mean for every other declaration, and `module` therefore has to ask about the FILE the
	// type was declared in, which is why `StructLayout` carries one.
	//
	// ⚠ It is a separate predicate rather than an inlined `match` because the caller's rescue must apply to
	// the `module` tier too: a type's own body may read its `module` field from OUTSIDE the declaring
	// subtree, since the body IS the declaring file.
	function fieldReachableFromOutsideItsType(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns bool
		return match layout.fieldTier(index) 'tier'
			file gives false
			module gives self.signatures.isVisibleFrom(Visibility.module, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
			global or public gives true
		end 'tier'
	end 'fieldReachableFromOutsideItsType'

	// ⭐ **A MOVED-FROM BINDING IS DEAD AT EVERY USE, AND THIS IS THE ONE PREDICATE THAT SAYS SO.** Its
	// owned heap value was transferred to another binding by a `let u = t` / `s = t` whose RHS was a
	// bare reference to it (`poisonMoveSource` set `movedFrom`), so the value now belongs to that new
	// owner and any further USE of the source — a bare read, a field read or write THROUGH it, a method
	// call ON it — is use-after-move (E3102), anchored on the USE token. Only a FULL reassignment
	// `t = <expr>` revives it (`reassignBindingValue` clears the bit); a field store `t.x = …` is itself
	// a use of `t`, not a revive of it, so it must route through here and NOT through the reviving path.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE HOME, TWO ENFORCING CALLERS**: the bare read (`parseVariableReference`) and every base of
	// a `.` — field read (`parseFieldAccess`), field write (`parseFieldAssignment`) and method receiver
	// (`parseMethodCall`), which ALL funnel through `requireStructBase`. Written at only the bare read,
	// a moved-from struct read or written through its fields was an UNCHECKED use-after-move: `let q = p`
	// then `p.x = 99` mutated `q`'s aliased box and `return q.x` returned 99 — a wrong answer for a
	// program that must be rejected (OPEN.md #42). The rule cannot be split across use sites: a site
	// taught it beside a site not taught it IS that bug, and it is the same "one predicate, one enforcing
	// home" the move gate itself is written to.
	//
	// A SELF-FIELD ALIAS is EXCLUDED: a bare `count` inside a method reads a fresh `loadIndirect` through
	// the receiver rather than aliasing an owned value, so it is never moved-from (`poisonMoveSource`
	// gates on `rhsWasBareLocalReference`, which excludes self-fields). `parseVariableReference` routes
	// self-fields away before it reaches this guard; the exclusion here is what keeps the guard safe on
	// the struct-typed self-field base that DOES reach `requireStructBase` (which since W66 materializes it
	// rather than refusing it, so the guard runs on the alias for real and not merely on its way to an error).
	function requireBindingLive(binding VarInfo, nameTok Token) throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE NON-LEXICAL HALF OF E3070 IS WRITTEN HERE, and this is why it is written HERE.** This is the
		// one chokepoint every USE of a local binding funnels through — the bare read, a field read or write
		// through it, a method call on it — which is exactly the set of events a borrow's lifetime ends at. A
		// second site asking "was this name read?" would be the same defect the guard below documents, one
		// question later: a use taught to one site and not the other lets a borrow expire while it is still
		// live, and a mutation past it becomes a use-after-free the compiler waved through.
		//
		// Written BEFORE both early returns: a self-field alias and a scalar binding are uses too, and the
		// exclusions below are about a MOVE, not about whether the name was read.
		//
		// ⚠ **ONLY EVER FORWARD.** The cursor is monotone through a function body, so the test is a no-op
		// for every ordinary read — it is here for the ONE binding whose last use is seeded AHEAD of the
		// parse, a `for … in` element's (`attachPendingBorrows`'s `liveUntil`). Without it the first read of
		// the loop variable inside the body would pull that lexical extent back to itself and expire a
		// borrow the iteration is still holding: a use-after-free.
		if self.pos > binding.lastUseToken 'laterThanAnyUseSoFar'
			binding.lastUseToken = self.pos
		end 'laterThanAnyUseSoFar'

		// ⭐ **E3012's EVIDENCE, WRITTEN AT THE SAME CHOKEPOINT AND FOR THE SAME REASON.** "Was this name
		// read?" is the question this routine already exists to be the single home of; the unused check asks
		// it of the whole function instead of of one moment. It is a SEPARATE bit from `lastUseToken` because
		// that field is SEEDED ahead of any read by `attachPendingBorrows` — see `VarInfo.referenced`.
		// Written before the early returns below for the reason the line above is: a self-field alias and a
		// scalar binding are reads too, and the exclusions are about a MOVE.
		self.markBindingReferenced(binding)

		if binding.isSelfField 'selfFieldAlias'
			return
		end 'selfFieldAlias'

		// ⭐ **A SCALAR-VALUED BINDING IS ALWAYS SAFE TO READ (P1.5-B2 #88).** `movedFrom`/`partiallyMoved`
		// forbid a read because a MOVED HEAP value now belongs to another owner; a scalar is never moved that
		// way, so a move bit on a scalar-valued binding means something else entirely. Two such cases exist,
		// both from #88: a CONSUMED promise (`movedFrom` = "an `await` reclaimed the green thread" — a second
		// use is caught by the more specific linear-await E3100 at SemanticCheck, which needs the read to PARSE
		// first, and a re-arm revives it), and a promise binding reassigned to a bare scalar (`movedFrom` =
		// "owns nothing droppable"). A promise handle is integer-tagged, so this one predicate admits both.
		if tagOwnsNoHeapValue(self.tagOf(binding.boundValue)) 'scalarBinding'
			return
		end 'scalarBinding'

		// Both move states are dead at every read. `movedFrom` (a `let u = t` / `s = t` handed the value
		// to another binding) and `partiallyMoved` (a binding-match moved a managed payload OUT of this
		// union, nulling its slot) differ only in whether the binding still owes a DROP — a use is
		// forbidden for both, and this is the one predicate that says so.
		if binding.movedFrom or binding.partiallyMoved 'moved'
			throw ParseError.useAfterMove(String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column)
		end 'moved'
	end 'requireBindingLive'

	// The box `binding` denotes and the `StructLayout` describing it — the two-step every field
	// access takes: the binding's declared type must BE a struct, and that struct must be one some
	// file declares.
	//
	// The second step cannot fail for a `structRef` the parser itself minted (it interned the id from
	// a name the sweep had already recorded), so it panics; the first very much can — `let n = 5`
	// then `n.x` — and reports. `baseTok` is only the token those diagnostics point at.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS THE ONE READER OF A BASE BINDING'S BOX, AND THAT IS WHY IT RETURNS THE BOX AND NOT
	// JUST THE LAYOUT.** A SELF-FIELD ALIAS HAS NO SSA VALUE: `createSelfField` leaves
	// `boundValue` 0 because a read of the alias is a `loadIndirect` through the receiver, not a
	// reference to a value — **and ValueId 0 IS THE RECEIVER**. So a caller that proved the layout
	// here and then reached for `binding.boundValue` on its own silently addressed `__self`. What this
	// door hands back is `baseBoxOf`'s answer, which is that `loadIndirect` for an alias and the
	// binding's own value for everything else.
	//
	// That is not a hazard this comment is imagining; it was MEASURED, on the three sites that did
	// exactly that. In a self-referential `type Node` (`var next as Node`, which
	// `parseTypeReference` types `structRef` through its `namesEnclosingType` arm):
	//   • `return next.a`      compiled to a single `loadRegBaseDisp r8, [rcx + 0]` — that is
	//                          `return self.a`, off the receiver, at the wrong field's offset.
	//   • `return next.readA()` compiled to a bare `callDirect Node.readA` with NO argument setup —
	//                          the receiver was already in `rcx`, so it is `self.readA()`.
	//   • `next.a = 99`        reported E2013 "cannot assign to immutable variable: 'next'", which
	//                          is simply false of an `export var` field.
	// The first two exit 0 with no diagnostic anywhere. `parseVariableReference` guards this same
	// fact for the bare-name path and its comment states it exactly; the fact was written down
	// once and needed in four places. Handing the box back from here is what makes the other three
	// unable to get it wrong.
	function requireStructBase(binding VarInfo, baseTok Token) returns StructBase throws ParseError
		// The base binding must be LIVE before anything about it is resolved — a moved-from base is dead,
		// so `p.x`, `p.x = …` and `p.m()` on it are use-after-move (E3102), reported here at the base
		// token before the field/method is even looked up. This is the shared guard's second enforcing
		// home; `parseVariableReference` is the first. See `requireBindingLive`.
		try self.requireBindingLive(binding, nameTok: baseTok)

		// A struct box OR a generic INSTANCE (`Sizer with bool`, P1.6-B1) can be a method/field base — both
		// resolve to a struct layout (the instance's base struct, its methods compiled once against the
		// opaque `T`). Every other tag has no fields.
		let baseTag = maxonTypeTag(binding.varType)
		let baseTypeNameId = typeNameIdOf(binding.varType)
		if not self.tagCarriesStructLayoutHere(baseTag) 'notAStruct'
			// ⚠ **A TYPE THE PROGRAM DOES NOT HAVE IS NOT "declared 'int'", AND IT IS NOT THIS ACCESS'S
			// FAULT EITHER** — asked BEFORE the not-a-struct refusal below, because a name that denotes
			// nothing has no struct-hood to be wrong about. See `requireDeclaredBaseTypeName`.
			try self.requireDeclaredBaseTypeName(baseTag, nameId: baseTypeNameId, tok: baseTok)

			throw ParseError.unsupported("a field access on '{String.from(baseTok.value)}', which is declared '{typeTagName(baseTag)}' and not a struct type (only a struct has fields)", line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column)
		end 'notAStruct'

		// ⚠ **A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD USED AS A BASE IS MATERIALIZED, AND REFUSED ONLY WHEN NO VALUE OF ITS
		// TYPE COULD EXIST (W66)** — `inner.x` inside `type Outer`'s own method loads the field's box out
		// of the receiver and addresses THAT; `next.a` inside `type Node` is still E2015, because
		// `var next as Node` closes a cycle and no program can put a `Node` there to observe the load
		// being right. That is the `mintPhi` trap, and it is the whole of what the original refusal was
		// arguing for — see `requireConstructibleSelfFieldBase`, which the METHOD door asks too.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ARM STAYS BELOW THE `structRef` GATE, and moving it above is the tidy-looking edit that
		// is wrong.** A SCALAR field's alias (`count.x`, where `count` is an `Integer`) is not a struct
		// base either, but it is not a struct-typed field and must not be told it is;
		// `self-field-struct-typed.md`'s `error.scalar-field-base-is-not-a-struct` goes red on exactly
		// that reordering while the cycle cases stay green.
		if binding.isSelfField 'structTypedField'
			try self.requireConstructibleSelfFieldBase(baseTag, nameId: baseTypeNameId, fieldName: baseTok.value, anchor: baseTok)
		end 'structTypedField'

		// The layout is proved BEFORE the box is loaded, so a base this door is going to refuse emits no op
		// — `emitCheckedGlobalStore`'s discipline, and `structLayoutOfType` can still throw for a generic
		// instance whose base carries no layout of its own.
		let layout = try self.structLayoutOfType(BaseAccessKind.field, tag: baseTag, nameId: baseTypeNameId, baseTok: baseTok)

		let box = try self.baseBoxOf(binding, baseTok: baseTok)

		// ⚠⚠ **NEITHER NAME COLUMN OF THE RECORD HAS A READER ON THIS DOOR'S PATH, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF
		// THE TWO CALLERS RATHER THAN AN OMISSION — CENSUSED (W66 review).** `resolveFieldChain` and
		// `resolveGlobalFieldChain` take only the box and the layout off the `StructBase`; `immutableName`'s
		// ONE reader is `dispatchMethodOnBase`, and the only producers that reach it are
		// `structBaseOfReceiver` and `capturedStructBase`, never this one. **Do not read either column here
		// in a later rung without moving its derivation** — a field CHAIN answers both questions elsewhere
		// and HAS to: its E3019 blame name is `parseFieldChainCall`'s `iterationLockNameOf`, its E3070
		// subject that same `borrowSubjectNameAt`, and its WRITE permission `parseFieldAssignment`'s
		// `requireWritableInstance`, asked of the binding `resolveFieldChain` threads on. All three are keyed
		// on the base's TOKEN INDEX or on that binding, because only those can tell a `self`-rooted chain's
		// FIELD subject from the receiver; a name answered from HERE could not, which is the whole reason
		// `subjectName` is empty rather than derived.
		//
		// ⚠ `immutableName` is still filled with the TRUE answer instead of that same empty, and the
		// asymmetry is deliberate: `EmptySourceText` in THAT column MEANS "writable" (`immutableReceiverNameOf`),
		// so a future reader would be told the permissive thing by a door that never asked. Asking the
		// MATERIALIZED binding is what keeps the value right if one ever arrives — every self-field alias is
		// built `mutable: false` and is not a parameter, so asking the ALIAS would report "immutable" against
		// an `export var` field, the false E3019 `baseBoxOf`'s header measures.
		return StructBase.create(layout, box: box, immutableName: self.immutableReceiverNameOf(try self.baseBindingOf(binding, baseTok: baseTok, box: box), baseTok: baseTok), subjectName: EmptySourceText)
	end 'requireStructBase'

	// The `StructLayout` a struct-typed (tag, nameId) pair names — the guarded layout half of
	// `requireStructBase`, keyed on the TYPE rather than on a binding so all three base resolutions share it:
	// a local binding (`requireStructBase`), a CAPTURED base (whose box is the value read out of the env, not
	// `binding.boundValue` — `resolveCapturedFieldChain`), and a VALUE receiver bound to no name at all
	// (`structBaseOfReceiver`). Assumes the `structRef`/`genericInstance` gate above already held.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TWO TAGS THAT GATE REACH HERE DIFFER IN WHETHER A MISSING LAYOUT IS POSSIBLE, AND THAT IS THE
	// WHOLE OF THIS FUNCTION.** A `structRef` was interned by the parser itself from a name the declaration
	// sweep had already recorded, so its layout cannot be absent and an absence is an invariant break — it
	// panics. A `genericInstance`'s base is whatever a `with` NAMED, and the instance is interned for it
	// either way, so a missing layout is an ordinary property of a program a user can write and must be a
	// positioned diagnostic. Both were one panic, and both were reachable: `arr.managed` on an `Array with
	// Int` and `t.foo` on a parameter typed `Nonexistent with Int` each died with a stack trace and no
	// `error E….` at all.
	//
	// ⇒ So the two tags do not ask the same question, and only the `structRef` half may ask `structOf`. The
	// instance half asks the SAME walk gate the four `SignatureIndex` field walks open with, for the reason
	// the gate below states: a name being declared is not the same fact as this instance's fields living
	// there, and where they came apart the compiler read a heap pointer out as user data.
	function structLayoutOfType(access BaseAccessKind, tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, baseTok Token) returns StructLayout throws ParseError
		let typeName = self.baseStructNameOfType(tag, nameId: nameId, baseTok: baseTok)

		// ⚠⚠ **AN INSTANCE ASKS `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`, NEVER `structOf` ALONE — THE TWO DISAGREE,
		// AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS MEMORY-UNSAFE.** They answer different questions: `structOf` says "is a
		// type of this NAME declared anywhere", the gate says "does THIS INSTANCE have a layout its fields
		// actually live at". For a BUILTIN base they part company the moment a program writes `type Array
		// uses T` — the declaration registers, so `structOf` hands back ITS field table, while the VALUE in
		// hand is the runtime's synthesized 48-byte record, because every other door (`create`, the method
		// router, `genericInstanceBoxSize`, `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee`) is already routed to the builtin
		// by `isArrayInstance`. The field offsets then address the record, and MEASURED, with no diagnostic
		// anywhere and a compile that exits 0: `a.value` on an `Array with Integer` printed 7077984 — the
		// element buffer POINTER read as an Integer — and `a.value = 4242` overwrote that pointer, taking an
		// access violation (0xC0000005) in the teardown. `Set` hands out its record's count the same way.
		//
		// So the walk gate is the ONE door here too, exactly as it is at the four `SignatureIndex` walks: an
		// instance whose base has no layout OF ITS OWN never reads fields, whoever else declares the name.
		//
		// ⭐⭐⭐ **EXCEPT FOR THE ONE SHAPE WHERE THE DECLARATION *IS* THE RECORD, AND THAT IS NOT A WEAKENING —
		// IT IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME ARGUMENT (W59).** The paragraph above refuses a declared `Array`
		// because `structOf` hands back a field table the VALUE does not have. `declarationIsTheManagedRecord` is
		// exactly the test for the case where it does: the base is `Array`, the declaration has ONE field, and
		// that field is the buffer — so field 0 at offset 0 IS the record, which is why `emitFieldLoad`'s
		// inline-`managed` arm already answers it with the surface flip rather than a load. A second field is
		// what makes the two disagree, and that declaration is refused at construction
		// (`requireArrayIsItsOwnRecord`) rather than admitted here.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **THIS DELIBERATELY DOES *NOT* MOVE `genericInstanceHasBaseLayout` ITSELF, AND THAT CHOICE WAS
		// MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED.** The rung was briefed on the belief that the corpus fall-through and
		// that gate are ONE change, because the gate also decides the FIELD WALK the drop cascade reads. It is
		// not: widening the gate to admit a declared `Array` base — the whole prescription, applied on this
		// tree — was **INERT**, 551 `--filter=array` cases, 0 failures. The reason is that the walk it opens
		// would enumerate exactly ONE field, the buffer, whose drop callee is `__managed_decref` — the very callee
		// `genericInstanceBoxDropCallee` already gives the record itself — and both cascade readers filter a
		// non-`__destruct_` leaf out (`MmRuntime.addNestedInstanceDestructors`, `declareInstanceFieldDropNeeds`).
		//
		// ⇒ So the gate is not what was refusing the member, and moving it buys nothing to offset its REACH: it
		// is read by four `SignatureIndex` field walks and by every caller of this function, including the
		// WRITE path and the field-chain walk. The two are the preconditions of two different queries — a
		// layout to NAME a member, and a layout to ENUMERATE fields — exactly as that gate's own header says of
		// its neighbour, so the admission is spent at the one door that needs it and nowhere else.
		// ⚠ **THE SIZED CONTAINER IS ADMITTED BY THE SAME ARGUMENT AS THE ARRAY RECORD (W189)** — with
		// `stdlib/Vector.maxon` listed, a `Vector`'s ONE declared field IS the record, so `structOf` and the
		// value agree about it exactly as they do for `Array`. **MEASURED without this: 375 cases refused at
		// `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon:229`'s `self.createIterator()` inside `extension Iterable`, with
		// `missingBaseLayout`'s *"shv2 reads no stdlib"* sentence — a refusal whose every clause had stopped
		// being true for this type the moment the module was listed.**
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance and not (self.signatures.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout(nameId) or self.signatures.declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord(typeName)) 'noBaseLayout'
			throw self.missingBaseLayout(access, typeName: typeName, baseTok: baseTok)
		end 'noBaseLayout'

		match self.signatures.structOf(typeName) 'declared'
			declared(layout) then return layout
			undeclared then panic("structLayoutOfType: '{String.from(baseTok.value)}' resolves to base struct '{String.from(typeName)}' but no file declares that type — a struct base is only minted for a name the declaration sweep recorded (Parser.parseTypeReference), and an INSTANCE base was gated by genericInstanceHasBaseLayout above")
		end 'declared'
	end 'structLayoutOfType'

	// Which of the two ways a generic instance's base has no `StructLayout` this one is, worded for a reader
	// who needs opposite things from them — the diagnostic half of `ProgramSignatures.genericInstanceHasBaseLayout`,
	// whose header states the split. Only an instance reaches here; a `structRef` with no layout is an
	// invariant break and panics at the call site above.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS TOLD WHICH ACCESS IT WAS ASKED ABOUT, BECAUSE IT USED TO CALL EVERY ONE OF THEM A FIELD
	// (W190).** This door serves BOTH the field walk and the member-dispatch fall-through, and its own
	// neighbour's header recorded the consequence in so many words — *"a declared `Array.size()` got as far as
	// `missingBaseLayout` and was refused 'a field access … `Array` is a BUILTIN whose runtime record shv2
	// synthesizes', about a METHOD"*. That stayed theoretical while every synthesized surface caught its own
	// members first; retiring `dispatchVectorMethod` sent the first member call here for real, and
	// `array-declared-record.error.a-conformer-named-for-a-container-that-constructs-nothing-…` MEASURED it:
	// `v.push(5 as Num)` reported as a field access on `v`. A refusal that misnames the construct it refuses
	// sends the reader to look at the wrong line.
	function missingBaseLayout(access BaseAccessKind, typeName ByteArray, baseTok Token) returns ParseError
		// ⚠ **DO NOT SAY THE FIELD DOES NOT EXIST — IT VERY LIKELY DOES.** shv2 reads no stdlib and SYNTHESIZES
		// the `Array`/`Set` runtime record instead of compiling the declaration, so no `type` exists here to
		// carry a field table at all — the field is missing from this compiler, not from the language. E2015 —
		// a construct this compiler does not implement YET — is exactly the register that carries, and it is
		// the same one the sibling `n.x` guard above reports in.
		//
		// ⚠ **`Array`'s OWN `managed` FIELD NO LONGER REACHES HERE (R4.4).** It used to be the reason this arm
		// existed — 12 committed spec cases write `arr.managed` — and it is now routed to the array method
		// dispatcher before the field machinery is entered (`arrayManagedFieldAt`), because that field is an
		// IDENTITY on the record rather than a slot inside it. What still reaches here is every OTHER field of a
		// synthesized builtin record, for which this refusal remains the true answer.
		//
		// The wording names the stdlib file rather than a field, because the two builtins declare DIFFERENT
		// fields (`Array` its `managed`, `Set` its `elements`/`states`/`hashes`) and a message that named one
		// of them would be false about the other.
		if self.signatures.isBuiltinGenericBaseName(typeName) 'synthesizedBuiltin'
			return ParseError.unsupported(builtinRecordAccessRefusal(access, typeName: typeName, baseName: baseTok.value), line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column)
		end 'synthesizedBuiltin'

		// The base is not declared AT ALL, so it has no fields and never will under this spelling. The
		// `with` that named it is the actual error and `checkGenericInstance` records E2055 against it — but
		// a thrown ParseError discards this file's artifact diagnostics (`abortedParseArtifact`), so this
		// message has to carry the cause on its own rather than defer to one the user will never see.
		return ParseError.unsupported("a field access on '{String.from(baseTok.value)}': its type instantiates '{String.from(typeName)}', which no file declares as a generic `type`, so it has no fields — the `with` that names it is the error", line: baseTok.line, column: baseTok.column)
	end 'missingBaseLayout'

	// The synthesized-record refusal, worded for the access that met it. Two sentences and not one with a noun
	// swapped, because they say genuinely different things: a FIELD is missing from this compiler and can be
	// reached through the members instead, while a MEMBER of a declaration this name also carries is not
	// missing at all — it belongs to a type the value is not. `containerStaticSurfaceSentence`'s per-container
	// router is the same shape for the same reason.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE "shv2 READS NO STDLIB" CLAUSE IS GONE FROM BOTH, AND IT WAS FALSE RATHER THAN MERELY DATED.**
	// `stdlib/Array.maxon` and `stdlib/Vector.maxon` are both LISTED, so this compiler has read the very file
	// the old sentence said it does not. What is true is narrower and is what the reader needs: the RECORD is
	// synthesized, so a value of the instance carries that record's words whatever any declaration of the name
	// says.
	function builtinRecordAccessRefusal(access BaseAccessKind, typeName ByteArray, baseName ByteArray) returns String
		return match access 'access'
			field gives "a field access on '{String.from(baseName)}': `{String.from(typeName)}` is a BUILTIN whose runtime record shv2 SYNTHESIZES rather than compiling a declaration for, so a value of it carries that record's own words and not the fields `stdlib/{String.from(typeName)}.maxon` declares. The field is missing from this compiler, not from the language; reach the contents through the methods"
			member gives "a member access on '{String.from(baseName)}': `{String.from(typeName)}` is a BUILTIN whose runtime record shv2 SYNTHESIZES rather than compiling a declaration for, so a `{String.from(typeName)} with …` is this compiler's own instance and its members are the container's — not the ones a `type {String.from(typeName)}` declared here carries. Rename the declaration to reach its own members"
		end 'access'
	end 'builtinRecordAccessRefusal'

	// ⭐⭐ **A MEMBER-ACCESS BASE MAY NOT BE REFUSED IN TERMS OF A TYPE THE PROGRAM DOES NOT HAVE** — the
	// guard the same three doors `structLayoutOfType` above serves stand behind: a local BINDING
	// (`requireStructBase`, serving both the read and the write path), a CLOSURE CAPTURE
	// (`capturedStructBase`) and a VALUE receiver with no binding to name (`structBaseOfReceiver`). It
	// raises `ParseError.unknownTypeName` — E3011, the authority's own code and its own wording; see that
	// arm for why a parse-time door must report it at all.
	//
	// It THROWS rather than handing the name back for each door to throw with, so the ask-and-refuse is
	// written once instead of three times and there is no "no undeclared name" value to represent. ⚠ It is
	// deliberately NOT the `throw self.helperReturningAParseError(…)` shape
	// `structTypedSelfFieldBaseMessage`'s header measures as leaking two allocations: nothing returns a
	// `ParseError` here, it is constructed and thrown inside, exactly as the sibling `requireBindingLive`
	// does at the top of `requireStructBase`.
	//
	// MEASURED rather than reasoned, because that header's rule was measured too: all seven programs that
	// reach this guard still exit **1**, never 101. That is a real check and not a vacuous one — the shv2
	// binary is emitted by the bootstrap, whose `mm_leak_check` sits on the SINGLE unconditional exit path
	// taking main's return value as its argument and returning it unchanged only when the live allocation
	// count is zero (`X86CodeEmitter.cs:562-572`). A failing compile returns from `main` like any other run,
	// so the gate sees it; two leaked allocations would have come back as 101 instead of 1.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE ALL THREE DOORS ASSERTED A TYPE THE COMPILER INVENTED.** Each worded its
	// refusal around `typeTagName(<the base's tag>)`, and a `named` tag PRINTS AS `int` (TypeRules'
	// `typeTagName`, whose `named` arm is right for the ranged INT alias it was written for — and wrong for
	// a ranged FLOAT one, which still reads `int` on the DECLARED branch this rung leaves alone). So a parameter
	// declared with a name no registry declares was reported as *"'h', which is declared 'int'"* — a
	// statement about the source that the source does not make, and the kind of diagnostic that sends a
	// reader to the wrong line. MEASURED on `function takes(h NoSuchTypeAtAll)` + `return h.v`: all three
	// doors said `int`, in three different sentences. The runnable oracle answers the same program
	// `Unknown type: NoSuchTypeAtAll`, blaming the DECLARATION.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE FIX IS THE VERDICT, NOT A BETTER SENTENCE.** The first cut of this kept each door's
	// "a field access on 'h', …" framing and merely swapped the invented type for the real name — which
	// left one fact with a second wording, under E2015, beside the E3011 the authority gives it. One
	// authority, one code, one text, three anchors is the arrangement that cannot drift.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT ASKS `denotedNamedType`, AND ASKING ANYTHING ELSE IS THE WHOLE HAZARD HERE.** A `named` tag
	// covers every spelling `parseTypeReference` settles at parse time and no further — a declared `type`
	// named from OUTSIDE its own body, a declared `enum`/`union`, a ranged int alias, a qualified inner or
	// per-instance alias, five of the six compiler-owned names, and a name nothing declares at all. Only the
	// last is undeclared, and the list of the rest is exactly `denotedNamedType`'s cascade: the ONE place
	// that knows what a `named` type denotes, asked here for the same reason `denotedCastTargetTag` and
	// `checkGenericArgType` ask it, so a member-access base and the resolution pass cannot come to disagree
	// about what a name means. A re-derivation ("is it in the struct registry, the enum registry, …") is how
	// this refusal would come to reject a name the authority accepts — and it would do it by asserting the
	// name is undeclared, which is the very false statement this guard exists to remove.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE `isCompilerOwnedTypeName` GATE IS HERE FOR A NAME THE CASCADE CANNOT ANSWER FOR, AND IT IS
	// WRITTEN OVER THE WHOLE LIST RATHER THAN OVER ONE NAME.** The cascade answers `ExitCode` by its own arm,
	// `HashValue`/`Codepoint` through `isSynthesizedIntAliasName`, `Ordering` and `ArrayError` because
	// `createEnumRegistry` seeds them, and `CharSet` because `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon:19`'s own
	// `typealias CharSet = Set with Character` folds into `genericAliases` like any other declared alias (the
	// compiler upserted its own copy of that entry until W129 — the seed moved, the reservation did not);
	// `String` and `Character` never arrive here at all, because `parseTypeReference`
	// settles both syntactically and neither ever wears a `named` tag.
	//
	// ⚠ **`CharacterSet` WAS THE MEASURED CASE AND IT IS NO LONGER ONE (W115).** It answered NOWHERE while
	// its layout lived under the RESERVED spelling `__CharacterSet` and `containsStruct("CharacterSet")` was
	// FALSE, and without this gate `function hasIt(s CharacterSet, …)` + `s.contains(c)` reported
	// **`E3011: Unknown type 'CharacterSet'`** for a type the compiler shipped. `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`
	// is listed now, so `recordStruct` files the corpus layout under the BARE name and the cascade answers it
	// like any other declared struct. ⇒ **the gate is no longer load-bearing for THIS name, and it stays for
	// the LIST**: `isCompilerOwnedTypeName` still carries names no registry cascade reaches, and writing the
	// gate as the list rather than as a count is what kept it correct across the two rungs that moved a name
	// off it. Deleting it needs a measurement per remaining name, not this paragraph's expiry.
	//
	// ⇒ **A NAME THE COMPILER RESERVES IS NEVER THE USER'S UNDECLARED NAME**, so this gate cannot swallow a
	// genuine typo: `requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` refuses any user declaration that would bind a name on
	// that list, which is what makes "reserved" and "the program's own missing type" disjoint by
	// construction — and it is stated as *the list* rather than as a COUNT of it on purpose. The count was
	// written down here as "five of six", and it had already gone stale once (at `ArrayError`) before this
	// paragraph was reread at the BATCH2 slice 2 review, which added two more. A count is a copy of a list
	// that lives somewhere else, and this file has the rule in so many words one registry over
	// (`BuiltinConformanceRuntime`: "no count written down, because a count is a copy that goes stale on the
	// next rung").
	// The pre-existing refusal then stands unchanged for the names still on the list, and it is the RIGHT one:
	// a reserved name is real, so reporting it undeclared would be a false statement.
	// ⚠ **THE CLAUSE THAT SAID `CharacterSet` "merely cannot be NAMED at a parameter YET" IS RETIRED, AND SO
	// IS THE PIN THAT WAS CITED FOR IT (W129 review).** The `yet` expired at W17 and the layout it pointed at
	// (`CharacterSetTypeName`) went at W129 — `field-declared-unknown-type`'s case is
	// `compiler-reserved-base-type-is-nameable-at-a-parameter` and pins the OPPOSITE, that the name IS
	// nameable. The `error.compiler-reserved-base-type-is-not-undeclared` this cited pins nothing: **no test
	// of that name exists**, only an orphaned golden on all four lanes (board row `W111`, which owns the
	// class sweep). ⇒ this guard's live witness is the `-nameable-` case; treat it as the one to redden
	// before touching the gate.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING IS SUBSTITUTED FOR THE BASE'S TYPE — only the throw is the point.** `resolveTypes` erases a
	// `named` signature type and the parser deliberately does not (a `named` value's tag carries the alias
	// NAME the shift rule and the per-instance identity checks read — see `emitFieldLoad`'s header), so
	// resolving the base here would drop both. This is a DIAGNOSTIC query and nothing else.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONLY EVER CALLED ON A PATH THAT IS ALREADY THROWING**, which is what makes it free: it costs a name
	// lookup and a registry cascade per REFUSED member access, never one per field read. It also means it
	// cannot false-reject — the decision to refuse was taken before it was asked, and it only chooses which
	// refusal is reported.
	function requireDeclaredBaseTypeName(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, tok Token) throws ParseError
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'notANamedType'
			return
		end 'notANamedType'

		let name = self.internedName(nameId)
		if isCompilerOwnedTypeName(name) 'compilerReserved'
			return
		end 'compilerReserved'

		// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS CHECKED RATHER THAN `_ =`-DISCARDED, AND THE REASON IS A BOOTSTRAP RULE, NOT A
		// TASTE.** `denotedNamedType` reads registries and writes nothing, so `PurityAnalysisPass` calls it
		// PURE — and E3064 refuses `_ = <pure call>` as dead code. This call is not dead: what this site wants
		// is the FAILURE, and the purity rule does not model the `throws` channel (a function that can throw
		// has an observable effect its RETURN type cannot express). It became pure at N1b, when
		// `splitQualifiedName` stopped routing through `String.split` in order to lift its two-segment cap.
		//
		// ⭐ So the answer is spent on the ONE claim its own header makes and nothing enforced: that the
		// cascade ERASES — every arm returns `exitCode`, `structRef`, `integer` or a ranged alias's
		// `int`/`float` underlying, and an arm that handed the `named` REFERENCE back would put this routine
		// (and `denotedCastTargetTag`, which reads a tag off the same call) into an unbounded resolve.
		let denoted = try denotedNamedType(self.signatures, name: name, id: nameId) otherwise throw ParseError.unknownTypeName(String.from(name), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		if maxonTypeTag(denoted) == ValueTypeTag.named 'cascadeDidNotErase'
			panic("requireDeclaredBaseTypeName: `denotedNamedType` answered `named` for '{String.from(name)}' — every arm of its cascade erases to a concrete type or throws, so a `named` answer means a new arm hands its own input back")
		end 'cascadeDidNotErase'
	end 'requireDeclaredBaseTypeName'

	// The BASE struct name a method/field base resolves to: the struct itself for a `structRef`, or the
	// generic instance's base struct for a `genericInstance` (`Sizer with bool` ⇒ `Sizer`, P1.6-B1) — the
	// SHARED body its methods were compiled once against. The caller's `requireStructBase` has already
	// gated the tag to one of these two.
	function baseStructNameOfType(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, baseTok Token) returns ByteArray
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'instance'
			return self.signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(nameId)
		end 'instance'
		// ⭐ A BYTE RECORD carries no name id of its own (`String` and `Character` are their own `MaxonType`
		// arms, not `named`), so its layout is found under the CORPUS's declaration name — which is the one
		// name a declaration of it can wear, since both are on `isCompilerOwnedTypeName`.
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.string 'aString'
			return StringConformerName
		end 'aString'
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.character 'aCharacter'
			return CharacterConformerName
		end 'aCharacter'
		return try self.artifact.typeNames.nameOf(nameId) otherwise panic("baseStructNameOfType: '{String.from(baseTok.value)}' carries a structRef whose name id is not in this file's interner — every structRef the parser mints is interned from a name in the same table")
	end 'baseStructNameOfType'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS VALUE HAVE A STRUCT LAYOUT *IN THIS PROGRAM*? — `tagCarriesStructLayout` PLUS THE ONE
	// QUESTION A TAG CANNOT ANSWER ON ITS OWN (W17).** A `String`/`Character` is a byte record whose FIELDS
	// exist only when the corpus that declares them is loaded: with `stdlib/String.maxon` listed, `managed`
	// and `singleByteGraphemesFlag` are real fields at real offsets; without it there is no layout and the
	// old refusal is still the right answer. So the admitted set is the static one WIDENED by a lookup,
	// never a second static list.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT WIDEN WHAT USER CODE MAY REACH, AND THAT IS A DIFFERENT GATE'S JOB.** Neither corpus
	// field is exported, so `"abc".managed` outside `type String` is E3014 exactly as any unexported field
	// is — the visibility check runs after this one and is unchanged. What this changes is only WHICH
	// diagnostic an inaccessible field gets, and it moves it from "not a struct type" (false, and useless)
	// to the accurate one.
	function tagCarriesStructLayoutHere(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
		if tagCarriesStructLayout(tag) 'staticallyAggregate'
			return true
		end 'staticallyAggregate'
		if not tagIsByteRecord(tag) 'notAByteRecord'
			return false
		end 'notAByteRecord'
		return match self.signatures.structOf(self.byteRecordLayoutName(tag)) 'corpusDeclaresIt'
			declared gives true
			undeclared gives false
		end 'corpusDeclaresIt'
	end 'tagCarriesStructLayoutHere'

	// The corpus name a byte-record tag's layout is declared under. Split from `baseStructNameOfType` only
	// because that one needs a token to panic with and this one is asked before any error is possible; the
	// two must answer the same, so the second reads the first's two constants rather than restating them.
	function byteRecordLayoutName(tag ValueTypeTag) returns ByteArray
		if tag == ValueTypeTag.character 'aCharacter'
			return CharacterConformerName
		end 'aCharacter'
		return StringConformerName
	end 'byteRecordLayoutName'

	// `loadIndirect <box> <offset>` — the one op a field read is.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE VALUE'S TAG AND THE OP'S `valueType` ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT THINGS**, and reading
	// this as a redundancy is how it would get broken:
	//
	//   • the VALUE is minted with the field's DECLARED type — `named(Integer)` for a ranged alias —
	//     so the tag column keeps what the source said. THE SHIFT RULE reads it (`valueIsUnsigned`
	//     looks the alias's LOW BOUND up by name), so a field of `int(0 to u64.max)` right-shifts
	//     zero-filled, exactly as a PARAMETER of that type does.
	//   • the OP carries the field's STORAGE type — the resolved primitive — because
	//     `maxonTypeToStdType` PANICS on a `named`, and it panics on purpose: an op's type must be
	//     concrete by lowering, and `resolveTypes` rewrites signatures only, never op types.
	//
	// A parameter declared `Integer` already lives with exactly this split (`valueTagToStdType`'s
	// `named` arm exists for it), so this is that arrangement reaching one op further, not a new one.
	function emitFieldLoad(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		// ⭐⭐ **READING A FUSED WRAPPER'S INLINE `managed` IS NOT A LOAD — THE FIELD *IS* THE RECEIVER (W17).**
		// `type String`'s `managed` is the `__ManagedMemory` embedded whole at offset 0, so its ADDRESS equals
		// the record pointer; an `emitIndirectLoad` at offset 0 would read `buffer@0` and hand back a raw byte
		// pointer where a buffer RECORD is wanted. Both references settle it the same way — *"a bare `managed`
		// reference IS `self`"* (`MaxonToStandardConversion.cs:1600`).
		//
		// ⭐ **AND THE CORPUS ALREADY HAS A NAME FOR THIS VALUE: `addressableBytes()`, whose entire body is
		// `return managed`** (`stdlib/String.maxon:181`). shv2 serves that through `__str_bytes_view`, which
		// since W157 CO-OWNS the receiver's own record and hands it straight back — so the ownership is the
		// debugged one rather than a second answer invented here, and the two spellings of one question land
		// in one producer. Handing back the receiver's own `ValueId` UNCOUNTED is the double free
		// `bufferSurfaceOf`'s header measures; what this door hands back is counted.
		//
		// ⭐⭐⭐ **THIS USED TO BE A HEAP ALLOCATION, AND WHAT KEPT IT OFF THE HOT PATH WAS THE DOOR ABOVE
		// IT.** A BYTE RECORD's `managed` read that is immediately CALLED never arrives here at all —
		// `dispatchMethodOnBinding`'s `inlineManagedServesTheRecord` hands the receiver's own record to the
		// buffer entry (W49 wave 7 cure). What still reaches this line is `managed` used as a VALUE: bound,
		// returned (`addressableBytes()`'s whole body), passed as an argument, or stored into a `String{…}`
		// literal — every one of which may outlive the statement, and for which handing back the receiver's
		// own `ValueId` uncounted is the double free `bufferSurfaceOf`'s header measures. ⭐ **W157 MADE THE
		// REMAINING COST A REFCOUNT PAIR RATHER THAN A 48-BYTE RECORD**: a String record now IS a buffer
		// record, so there is nothing left to mint — the whole of `__str_bytes_view` is an `__mm_incref`.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **W194 BRIEFLY ROUTED EVERY NON-BYTE-RECORD `managed` CALL THROUGH HERE, AND THE PRICE OF
		// THAT IS THE REASON THE FUSED DOOR STILL EXISTS FOR ALL FOUR CONFORMERS.** Its first cure turned the
		// fused record door away from `Array` and `Vector`, so a bare `managed.byteAt(i)` inside a container
		// body took `coOwnAggregateAsTemp` like any other value read. **That is not a user-code-only cost**:
		// `Array.hash` and `Array.equals` are NOT on `arraySurfaceMemberNames`, so unlike `count`/`get` they
		// are not served by a synthesized arm — the corpus bodies are what a program actually calls, and each
		// is a loop over `try managed.byteAt(i) otherwise panic(…)` (`stdlib/Array.maxon:670-688`), so the
		// pair was paid once PER BYTE. A/B on identical source, this rung's RED baseline `6af975e7b6` against
		// the routed tip, both built by the same `bin/maxon.exe`, a program hashing a 64-element
		// `Array with Int` 200,000 times: **327 ms -> 624 ms** (review, min of three) and **413 ms -> 684 ms**
		// (coordinator, min of four), 6,160 -> 6,224 code bytes. `Character.hash` is the same loop over the
		// same member one type away and was UNCHANGED — a Character IS a byte record and kept the fused door
		// — which is what identified the re-route as the cause rather than anything else in the rung.
		// ⇒ The routing was withdrawn: the fused door hands each wrapper its OWN buffer-surface instance
		// (`bufferSurfaceInstanceOf`) instead of turning any of them away, which fixes the same three
		// mis-served members and emits nothing. **`Array.hash` is what every `Set` and `Map` keyed on an
		// array runs**, and this is the 34.3x `hashString` shape of the table below one container over —
		// which is why it was worth a second cure rather than a filed row.
		//
		// ⛔ **WHY THAT SPLIT EXISTS, MEASURED — IT WAS 86.7x.** `String.byteLength`'s corpus body is
		// `return managed.length()`, and before the cure that ONE expression emitted `__str_bytes_view` (a
		// 48-byte `Array` record + `__mm_incref`), then `__managed_count`, then `__managed_decref` — a heap allocation
		// and two refcount operations to read `length@8`. `String.byteAt` was the same shape once PER BYTE.
		// min-of-7 interleaved, same source, base / wave-7-uncured / cured:
		//
		//     s.byteLength()  4,000,000 trips      3.33 ms -> 278.69 ms -> 11.13 ms   83.7x -> **3.34x**
		//     s.isEmpty()     4,000,000 trips      5.05 ms -> 287.48 ms -> 17.22 ms   56.9x -> **3.41x**
		//     hashString(s)   524,288 bytes x 8    8.82 ms -> 302.39 ms -> 19.74 ms   34.3x -> **2.24x**
		//     short.clone()   4,000,000 trips    251.03 ms -> 1370.27 ms -> 841.54 ms  5.5x -> **3.35x**
		//     integer control                     5.89 ms ->   5.83 ms ->  5.88 ms    1.0x ->   1.00x
		//
		// Never a leak (`__managed_decref` ran, no run exits 101) and never a wrong answer — every mode returns a
		// bit-identical accumulator on all three binaries, which is what says the cure deleted no work.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ **`clone()` WAS THE MEMBER THE WAVE-7 CURE COULD NOT REACH, AND W157 REACHED IT.** Its body goes
		// through `sliceBytes` -> `managed.slice(...)`, and `slice` was the ONE buffer member that could not
		// take a String record: `__managed_slice` -> `emitRecordView` LOADS `@40` and STORES it into the fresh
		// view, so a String's `singleByteGraphemesFlag` became that view's `element_destroy` and the first
		// `__managed_decref` `callIndirect`ed address 1. The view mint was not overhead there — it was what
		// supplied a correct `TrivialDestructor`. It improved anyway at wave 7 (5.5x -> 3.35x) because
		// `sliceBytes` asks `byteLength()`, which the cure made cheap; W157 then removed the mint itself,
		// because a String record's `@40` IS `TrivialElementDestroy` and this line no longer allocates. See
		// `fusedManagedMemberTakesTheRecord` for the census as it now reads.
		if layout.fieldIsTheInlineManagedRecord(index) 'inlineManagedIsTheReceiver'
			// ⭐⭐⭐ **AN `Array`'s BUFFER IS THE `Array`, SO THERE IS NO VIEW TO MINT (W57)** — the same
			// answer `bufferSurfaceOf` gives for the SPELLED `arr.managed`, reached here by the implicit-`self`
			// read inside `type Array`'s own body. It is not an optimization of the line below; that line is
			// **WRONG** for this wrapper in two independent ways, and both are silent:
			//   • **THE ELEMENT.** `internSynthesizedByteInstance` is the BYTE buffer, which is exactly right
			//     for `String` and `Character` — their record IS a byte buffer — and is a stride lie for an
			//     `Array with Element`, whose slots are 8 bytes wide. `__str_bytes_view` mints a record
			//     stamped `element_size@24 = 1`.
			//   • **THE TYPE PARAMETER.** With the element read back as a concrete byte, the copy lowering
			//     asks which of the enclosing declaration's `uses` names that element is and there is no
			//     answer — **MEASURED as `panic … opaqueTypeParamPosition: asked for the `uses`-list position
			//     of a `int`, which names no type parameter`** on a `clone()` whose body is
			//     `managed.slice(0, len)`.
			// One record with two surfaces is the whole of this wrapper, so the read is the surface flip and
			// nothing else — which also means it allocates nothing where the String path allocates a record.
			// ⚠ **AND THE SIZED CONTAINER TAKES THIS ARM TOO (W189)** — a `Vector`'s value IS the same fused
			// record, so `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s `managed` is the surface flip and not a `__str_bytes_view`.
			// Both silent wrongs the paragraph above enumerates apply to it word for word: the byte view's
			// stride is 1 and a vector's slots are its element's, and the element read back as a concrete byte
			// leaves `opaqueTypeParamPosition` with no `uses` name to answer.
			if self.signatures.declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord(layout.name) 'theRecordISTheBuffer'
				return self.bufferSurfaceOfDeclaredRecord(box, layout: layout, range: range)
			end 'theRecordISTheBuffer'

			// ⚠ **THE MINTED VIEW WEARS THE INSTANCE `bufferSurfaceInstanceOf` NAMES, AND NO LONGER A CONSTANT
			// SPELLED HERE (W194 second review).** This is the THIRD door asking *"which instance does this fused
			// wrapper's buffer surface wear?", and it answered by writing `internSynthesizedByteInstance()` out
			// again — while that function's FIRST ARM answers the identical thing behind the identical predicate,
			// `declarationsValueIsTheManagedRecord`, which is the very test the arm above just made. Provably the
			// same value, so this is a spelling removed and not a behaviour changed; what it buys is that a second
			// byte-record instance can never be taught to two of the three doors and missed at the third.
			let view = self.emitOwnedRuntimeResult(StrBytesViewName, args: singletonArgs(box), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, resultNameId: self.bufferSurfaceInstanceOf(box, layout: layout), range: range)
			self.markBufferSurface(view)
			return view
		end 'inlineManagedIsTheReceiver'

		// A FUNCTION-typed field (`export var op as UnaryOp`) arrives from `fieldTypeOf` already re-tagged
		// `function` (it functionizes over the whole-program function-alias registry, since the SWEEP that
		// recorded the layout ran before the alias was known), which is what makes the loaded value carry
		// the `function` tag so `h.op(21)` reaches an indirect call (P1.5-A1).
		let declared = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)

		// ⭐⭐ **READING A PROMISE'S `inner` IS NOT A LOAD EITHER — THE HANDLE *IS* THE VALUE (G17).** The
		// same shape as the fused-wrapper arm above and settled the same way: shv2 does not box a promise
		// (`PromiseType.maxon` carries the argument), so a `Promise with T` value IS the green-thread
		// pointer, and the line below would DEREFERENCE it. `inner` sits at offset 0, which is `GtOffSp` —
		// so `p.inner` answered the thread's saved STACK POINTER, never faulting and never crashing, just
		// handing back a plausible number. `PromiseType.maxon`'s header carries the measurement and the
		// paragraph it replaced.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT MINTS RATHER THAN HANDING `box` BACK, AND THE MINT IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF.** Returning the
		// receiver's own ValueId would make `let h = p.inner` a SECOND NAME for the promise: it carries
		// `valueOwnsGreenThread`, so the binding enrols a second owner and `__gt_promise_drop` runs twice, and
		// it answers `valueIsPromise`, so `await h` is a second await the linearity check cannot see as one.
		// `emitRetaggedCastValue` is the compiler's existing spelling of "the same bits, a fresh identity,
		// the target type" — the field's DECLARED type here, which is what makes the handle arrive at
		// `__Builtins.gtIsComplete` as the machine word its operand rule wants.
		//
		// ⚠ The tail below is skipped and owes nothing: `inner` is declared `int`, so there is no interface
		// witness to pair, no instance substitution to apply (the field's type names no type parameter), no
		// `named` throw-source to record and no buffer surface; and it is a `let`, so the rebindable-slot
		// mark is unreachable twice over (immutable, and not managed heap).
		if fieldReadIsThePromiseHandle(self.signatures, receiverType: self.valueMaxonType(box), layout: layout, index: index) 'handleIsTheValue'
			return self.emitRetaggedCastValue(box, targetType: declared, range: range)
		end 'handleIsTheValue'

		let offset = layout.offsetOfField(index)
		let result = self.emitIndirectLoad(declared, box: box, offset: offset, storageType: self.fieldStorageType(declared), range: range)

		// ⭐⭐ **AN INTERFACE-TYPED FIELD READS AS TWO LOADS, AND THE SECOND IS THE WITNESS
		// (P1.7a-existentials slice 2).** The field's carve-out holds both halves, so the witness comes out of
		// the box beside the value rather than being re-derived — which is the only thing that can be right:
		// a fat pointer's dynamic type is a run-time fact, and the box is where the write recorded it. Paired
		// here rather than at the six doors that reach this mint (`h.t`, a bare `t` in a method, a chain hop,
		// …) for the reason `markDeclaredSurface` sits here: every one of them funnels through this line, and
		// a per-door pairing is the enumeration that misses the seventh. Without it, passing `h.t` at an
		// interface argument position panics in `witnessOfValue` with "no witness paired".
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialField'
			self.pairInterfaceWitness(result, witness: self.emitIndirectLoad(MaxonType.integer, box: box, offset: offset + InterfaceWitnessHalfOffset, storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
		end 'existentialField'

		// ⭐ **RETYPE AT A CONCRETE FIELD READ (P1.6-B2, widened by W25).** A `b.value` read on a concrete
		// `Box with String` loads a field the shared body typed opaque `T`, and a `b.items` read on a
		// `HoldA with Num` loads one it typed `Array with T`. Retype the result to what the instance FIXES it to
		// (`String`, `Array with Num`) so it is usable as what it actually is — but ONLY the type moves: the read
		// is a BORROW (`emitFieldLoad` never marks it owned-heap), so the box keeps its +1 and drops the value at
		// ITS scope exit; a `let s = b.value` re-reads a live pointer and is never double-dropped. The
		// substitution ends in `adoptType`, which re-interns any named argument into this file's interner.
		let fixedByTheInstance = self.slotTypeThroughReceiver(box, declared: declared)
		if not maxonTypesDenoteSame(fixedByTheInstance, b: declared) 'concreteSlotRead'
			self.retypeValue(result, valueType: fixedByTheInstance)
		end 'concreteSlotRead'

		// ⭐ **RECORD A BOXED-UNION-CANDIDATE FIELD READ**, so a THROW of it can MOVE it out of the container
		// (`emitThrowError` nulls this slot — shv2's only transfer for a borrowed error). Gated on the `named`
		// tag, which a boxed union carries (as do a ranged-int alias and a scalar enum — the throw path
		// filters those out via `throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion`); a scalar/String/struct field is not `named` and is
		// never a thrown error, so the common field read leaves this slot untouched.
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.named 'namedFieldRead'
			self.lastFieldReadValid = true
			self.lastFieldReadValue = result
			self.lastFieldReadBox = box
			self.lastFieldReadOffset = offset
		end 'namedFieldRead'

		// ⭐ **A FIELD DECLARED `__ManagedMemory` READS AS THE BUFFER SURFACE (A2j)** — the declared SPELLING,
		// which `declared` above cannot carry (the alias resolves to the same `genericInstance` a `ByteArray`
		// field does), taken off the layout's own column. It sits at THIS mint rather than at the nine doors
		// that reach it — `h.buf`, `self.buf`, a bare `buf` in a method, a chain hop, a compound assignment's
		// read — because every one of them funnels through this line, and a per-door mark would be the
		// enumeration that misses the tenth.
		self.markDeclaredSurface(result, declared: self.signatures.fieldSurfaceOf(layout, index: index))

		// ⭐ **AND THE SPELLING THE RECEIVER'S INSTANCE ARGUMENT CARRIES, WHICH THE FIELD'S OWN DECLARATION
		// CANNOT.** A field declared `var slot as U` names no buffer anywhere — the spelling belongs to the
		// `Cell with __ManagedMemory` being read THROUGH, not to the shared body's declaration. Asked here
		// because this is where the same crossing already happens for the TYPE (`slotTypeThroughReceiver`, ten
		// lines up), and the two halves must not be able to come apart.
		self.markDeclaredSurface(result, declared: self.slotSurfaceThroughReceiver(box, declared: declared))

		// ⭐ **AND THE PER-VALUE SLOT MASK, WHICH THE LAYOUT CANNOT CARRY (A2m).** When `box` is a TUPLE, its
		// layout is SHARED with every other tuple of the same canonical element types — `(__ManagedMemory, Int)`
		// and `(ByteArray, Int)` are one registration — so the buffer-ness of slot `index` is a fact about THIS
		// VALUE and about nothing the layout knows. Read here in ADDITION to the column above rather than
		// instead of it: the column stays authoritative for a real struct field, whose declaration is its own.
		//
		// This one line is what serves `p.0`, `let (m, n) = pair()` and `h.p.0` alike — every read of a tuple
		// element in the language funnels through this mint.
		self.markDeclaredSurface(result, declared: self.valueSlotSurface(box, slot: index))

		// ⭐⭐ **A MANAGED VALUE OUT OF A WRITABLE FIELD OWES A REFERENCE THE MOMENT IT IS BOUND TO A NAME
		// (W41).** The retype note above states this read's ownership rule — "the read is a BORROW
		// (`emitFieldLoad` never marks it owned-heap), so the box keeps its +1 and drops the value at ITS
		// scope exit" — and that is still true of the READ. What it does not survive is the field being
		// REBOUND while a name still holds the value: `emitFieldWrite` drops the old occupant, so `let old =
		// items` followed by `items = fresh` leaves `old` naming freed memory (MEASURED 0xC0000005 where the
		// oracle prints `sum 10`). The mark rides the VALUE to `declareInitializedBinding`, which promotes it
		// there — see `rebindableSlotReads` for why the mark and not a token re-walk, and why the promotion
		// belongs at the BINDING rather than here (a receiver load, an argument, a chain hop are all transient
		// and owe nothing).
		//
		// It sits at THIS mint rather than at the doors that reach it, for `markDeclaredSurface`'s reason
		// stated two lines up: every field read in the language funnels through this line, and a per-door mark
		// is the enumeration that misses one. Gated on the field being WRITABLE (an immutable field's occupant
		// cannot be replaced — E2013) and on the value being MANAGED (a scalar is freed by nothing), so the
		// set stays empty for the overwhelming majority of reads.
		if layout.fieldIsMutable(index) and self.valueIsManagedHeap(result) 'writableManagedSlot'
			self.markRebindableSlotRead(result)
		end 'writableManagedSlot'

		return result
	end 'emitFieldLoad'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A SLOT DECLARED IN A SHARED GENERIC BODY ACTUALLY HOLDS WHEN IT IS READ THROUGH A CONCRETE
	// INSTANCE — the ONE answer, for the two doors that each need it at a different moment:** `emitFieldLoad`
	// retypes the loaded VALUE, and `resolveFieldChainFrom` needs the TYPE one step earlier, to decide what the
	// next hop addresses (A4j). Written out twice it was one condition with two homes.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT USED TO BE A PREDICATE OVER THE BARE `typeParameter` TAG ALONE (W25), and that is exactly the tag
	// an inner `typealias Items = Array with T` does NOT carry.** A field typed `Items` is a `genericInstance`,
	// so `b.items` on a `HoldA with Num` kept the declaration view's `Array with T` — refused where it should
	// have been accepted (`E3005: expected 'HoldANum.Items', got 'HoldA.Items'`), and accepted where a
	// `HoldA with String`'s items should have been refused. The question is not "is this the body's `T`", it is
	// "does this instance FIX this type", and only the substitution can answer that.
	//
	// It returns `declared` UNCHANGED — the same object — when the receiver is not a concrete instance or when
	// the instance fixes nothing in it, so a caller distinguishes "no instance view" by asking
	// `maxonTypesDenoteSame` of what it passed in. That is one fact (the substitution) read two ways, rather
	// than a predicate that has to agree with a substitution it does not call.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CROSSING BACK IS `resolvedSlotType` AND NOT A BARE `adoptType` (A4i) — AN INSTANCE'S TYPE
	// ARGUMENT IS A SLOT LIKE ANY OTHER, RECORDED BEFORE THE ALIAS REGISTRIES EXISTED.** The DECLARATION SWEEP
	// interns `Box with Point`'s argument before anything can say what `Point` denotes, so it comes back here as
	// a bare `named` for exactly the reason a struct FIELD's and an `Array` ELEMENT's declared types do — and
	// `adoptType` re-interns the NAME while leaving the tag alone. That is not a cosmetic loss: `named` reads as
	// an INTEGER (`tagIsIntegral`), so this door — the ONE place a `T`-typed read learns what `T` is — would
	// hand back a type that says "int" for a value that is a POINTER.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, THREE FACES OF THIS ONE LINE, against the runnable oracle** (which compiles and runs all
	// three): `let q = b.value` on a `Box with Point` then
	//   • `q + 0` **COMPILED AND PRINTED THE RAW HEAP ADDRESS** — arithmetic accepted against a pointer, and
	//     the worst of the three because it is the one that does not refuse (the oracle says E2004);
	//   • `q.x` was `E2015 … 'q', which is declared 'int' and not a struct type`;
	//   • with a GENERIC-ALIAS argument (`Holder with IntBox`) it was `E3011 Unknown type 'IntBox'`, said of
	//     a name the program declares one line above.
	// One decision, one cause, one door — the struct arm and the generic-alias arm of `declaredSlotType` are
	// literally the two the three faces split along.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SUBSTITUTION ITSELF IS ASKED IN THE INDEX'S SPACE, and only the ANSWER crosses.** `typeIsManaged`
	// and every other `ProgramSignatures` classifier must be asked of an index-space type, never of one already
	// re-interned into this file's table — the two interners assign ids in their own insertion orders, so such
	// an answer is right exactly while they coincide and silently wrong when they drift (OPEN #56). That is why
	// the two steps stay two.
	function slotTypeThroughReceiver(box ValueId, declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		if self.tagOf(box) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAConcreteInstance'
			return declared
		end 'notAConcreteInstance'

		// ⭐ **A TUPLE CROSSES AS A NAME, NOT AS AN ID** — `substituteTupleThroughInstance` states why the
		// interner-free substitution cannot answer for a `structRef`, and why a tuple's canonical name is the
		// currency that can. The composition of the two lives in `substituteThroughInstanceInterned`, beside the
		// substitutions it composes, because the ARGUMENT CHECK needs the identical rule in the PROJECT's
		// interner — and this header used to say the crossing was owned here, which stopped being true the
		// moment a second door needed it.
		let substituted = self.signatures.substituteThroughInstanceInterned(declared, id: self.valueNameId(box), interner: self.artifact.typeNames)
		if maxonTypesDenoteSame(substituted, b: declared) 'theInstanceFixesNothingHere'
			return declared
		end 'theInstanceFixesNothingHere'

		// ⚠ **ONLY THE NON-TUPLE ANSWER IS RESOLVED, and the gate reads the DECLARED side rather than the
		// substituted one.** `resolvedSlotType` repairs a sweep-recorded `named` leaf (see the A4i note above); a
		// tuple came back already interned as a `structRef` by the crossing, and handing THAT to the slot
		// resolver would ask the alias registries about a name no alias declares. A `structRef` INPUT that
		// reached this line can only be a moved tuple — every other `structRef` leaves through the
		// denotes-same return one line up — whereas a `structRef` ANSWER is also what a `typeParameter` whose
		// argument is a struct substitutes to, and that one still needs resolving.
		return substituted if tagIsStructRef(maxonTypeTag(declared)) else self.resolvedSlotType(substituted)
	end 'slotTypeThroughReceiver'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT SURFACE A SLOT DECLARED IN A SHARED GENERIC BODY WEARS WHEN IT IS READ THROUGH A CONCRETE
	// INSTANCE — the twin of the door above, and it must be asked WHEREVER THAT ONE IS.** The type and the
	// surface are two halves of one crossing: the receiver's instance fixes what a `returns Element` IS, and the
	// receiver's ARGUMENT SPELLING fixes which member roster the value that comes back may reach. The door above
	// can never carry the second half — `Array with __ManagedMemory` and `Array with ByteArray` are one
	// `GenericInstanceId` by design — so a caller that retypes and does not also re-surface hands back a value
	// whose roster is the wrong one.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED, which is what says the two halves are not merely symmetric but both required:** inside
	// `function peek(c BufCell)` over `typealias BufCell = Cell with __ManagedMemory`, `c.get().length()` was
	// **E2015 … `Array` member 'length'** — the buffer roster's first member refused on a value whose argument
	// the author spelled `__ManagedMemory`, while `c.get().count()` on the `Cell with ByteArray` spelling
	// answered. The roster, exactly inverted, at the one declared position A2j / A2m / BATCH22 had not reached.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE RECEIVER'S MARK THAT CARRIES THE SPELLING, AND NOTHING ELSE CAN.** `bufferSurfaceElements`
	// says *argument `BufferSurfaceArgPosition` of this value's instance was spelled the buffer*; it is written
	// from a declared position (`markDeclaredSurface`), from a builtin `Array` factory
	// (`emitOwnedContainerCreate`) and from a declared generic's factory
	// (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`) — the three ways a value of such an instance is minted.
	//
	// A receiver with no mark returns the shared empty tree before anything is asked of the index, which is
	// every receiver in a program that never writes `__ManagedMemory`.
	function slotSurfaceThroughReceiver(box ValueId, declared MaxonType) returns DeclaredSurface
		if self.tagOf(box) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'notAConcreteInstance'
			return sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
		end 'notAConcreteInstance'

		if not self.valueElementIsBufferSurface(box) 'itsArgumentNamesNoBuffer'
			return sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
		end 'itsArgumentNamesNoBuffer'

		return surfaceOfRootBits(self.signatures.surfaceBitsThroughInstanceArg(declared, id: self.valueNameId(box), interner: self.artifact.typeNames, readerFilePath: self.filePath))
	end 'slotSurfaceThroughReceiver'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE `loadIndirect` EMIT** — and it now IS the one, which it was not when it first claimed
	// to be. A field read (`p.x`), a union tag load, a union payload read, a capturing closure's
	// env-slot read (`emitCaptureRead`), a String record's field (`emitLoadStringField`) and a global's
	// slot (`emitGlobalLoad`) all reach here. One op shape, so the value's SOURCE type (`declared`,
	// minted onto the tag column so a nested `match` or the shift rule reads what the source said) and
	// its STORAGE type (the concrete primitive the op carries, because `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a
	// `named`) are split in exactly one place. See `emitFieldLoad`'s header for why the two differ.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CALLER THAT PASSES THE SAME TYPE TWICE IS STILL A CALLER, NOT AN EXCEPTION.** The String
	// record and the global do exactly that, each for its own stated reason — and each used to own a
	// raw `artifact.emitOp` instead, which is what made the claim above false. The hazard is not the
	// duplicated line; it is that the split between declared and storage would then have had two more
	// homes free to disagree with this one, with no compile error to say so.
	function emitIndirectLoad(declared MaxonType, box ValueId, offset ByteOffset, storageType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintValueOfType(declared)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.loadIndirect(result, addr: box, offset: offset, valueType: storageType), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitIndirectLoad'

	// `storeIndirect <box> <offset> <value>` — the one op a field write is, and the exact mirror of
	// `emitFieldLoad` (same offset, same storage type, same reason for each).
	//
	// **THE ONE STORE SITE**, shared by the two paths that write a field: the `Self{…}` loop filling
	// a fresh box (`emitStructLiteral`) and the assignment statement writing a live one
	// (`parseFieldAssignment`). They were one line apart from being two copies of the offset-and-
	// width derivation, which is the pair that must never disagree — a literal that stored `y` at
	// the width the layout says and an assignment that stored it at another would corrupt the box
	// silently.
	function emitFieldStore(box ValueId, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ValueId, range SourceRange)
		let declared = self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)
		let offset = layout.offsetOfField(index)
		self.emitIndirectStore(box, offset: offset, value: value, storageType: self.fieldStorageType(declared), range: range)

		// ⭐ **THE WITNESS HALF, STORED BESIDE THE VALUE (P1.7a-existentials slice 2)** — the exact mirror of
		// `emitFieldLoad`'s second load, at the same offset, and the write that makes the read's answer a fact
		// about the box rather than a guess. Both the `Self{…}` fill and the assignment statement land here,
		// so a literal that recorded the conformer and an assignment that did not is unrepresentable.
		if maxonTypeTag(declared) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialField'
			self.emitIndirectStore(box, offset: offset + InterfaceWitnessHalfOffset, value: self.witnessOfValue(value), storageType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		end 'existentialField'
	end 'emitFieldStore'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE `storeIndirect` EMIT**, the mirror of `emitIndirectLoad` above and true on the same
	// terms. A field write, a union tag store, a union payload store, a union's unused-slot zero-fill, a
	// capturing closure's env-slot fill (`buildClosureEnv`), a String record's field
	// (`emitStoreStringField`) and a global's slot (`emitGlobalStore`) all reach here — one op shape, so
	// the offset-and-width derivation a box's stores must agree on lives in one place (a literal storing
	// `y` at one width and an assignment storing it at another would corrupt the box silently — see
	// `emitFieldStore`).
	// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE A BINDING CURRENTLY HOLDS — THE ONE DOOR EVERY CONSUMER OF A BINDING'S VALUE ASKS
	// (PBR-2).**
	//
	// For nearly every binding this is `boundValue`, the SSA value the name aliases. For a CELL-resident one
	// it is a fresh `loadIndirect`, because `boundValue` holds the cell's ADDRESS and handing that to a
	// consumer would give it a pointer where the source asked for a value.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS THE SAME CENSUS THE SELF-FIELD ALIAS ONCE OWED, AND THAT ONE COST THREE WRONG ANSWERS.**
	// `VarInfo.boundValue`'s header records them: three readers took an alias's unset 0 for a real id, and
	// each was a silent miscompile rather than a compile error — `return count` compiling to `return self`.
	// A cell binding is the same hazard with the opposite sign: `boundValue` is a REAL id, so nothing panics;
	// the consumer simply acts on the box instead of on what is in it. Every site that wants a binding's
	// VALUE goes through here. The sites that legitimately want `boundValue` itself are the ones asking about
	// OWNERSHIP or IDENTITY — the drop at scope exit, the owned-binding scans, the move/alias searches — and
	// for a cell binding the thing this frame owns and the thing another name could alias IS the cell.
	//
	// ⚠ The loop-phi and merge-snapshot writers (`boundValue = phiId`) are not in either group and need no
	// arm: a cell binding is never pushed into `mutableVars` (`declareCellLocal`), so no phi machinery can
	// reach one.
	function bindingValue(binding VarInfo, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if binding.isCellBinding 'livesInACell'
			return self.emitCellLoad(binding.varType, box: binding.boundValue, range: range)
		end 'livesInACell'

		return binding.boundValue
	end 'bindingValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **READ A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING (PBR-2)** — the one load, paired with the one store below. A cell
	// holds exactly one value at offset 0, so unlike an env slot there is no index to get wrong; what MUST
	// agree is the WIDTH, which is why both sides ask `wordSlotStorageType` rather than spelling a width.
	//
	// The declared type goes through `resolveFloatAliasType` for `emitCaptureRead`'s reason: a ranged INT alias
	// must stay `named` (its NAME carries the signedness `valueIsUnsigned` reads), while the one alias whose
	// VALUE is a different machine class is collapsed here so the load is minted at the right register class.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A CELL IS THE THIRD REBINDABLE SLOT, AND IT WAS THE ONE W41 NEVER REACHED.** `rebindableSlotReads`
	// names its producers as *"the TWO ways a managed value comes out of storage a later statement can
	// overwrite"* — a mutable FIELD and a `var` GLOBAL. A cell is a third: `emitCellStore` decrefs the
	// occupant it displaces (`reassignBindingValue`'s cell arm), so `let old = n` + `n = Node.create(99)` on a
	// by-reference parameter leaves `old` naming freed memory for word-for-word W41's reason. MEASURED before
	// this line existed: `refcount-reassigned-slot-borrow/byref-param-reassign-then-read-borrow` returned
	// **1061109567** (`0x3F3F3F3F`, the freed-slab fill) where the spec pins 5.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS MARKED AT THE LOAD, NOT AT THE PARAMETER**, because that is where its two siblings mark and
	// because the parameter is not the only cell: a `var` LOCAL promoted to a cell (`declareCellLocal`, so a
	// callee can write it) is rebindable by this frame's own store and dangles identically. One door covers
	// both, which is the census argument `bindingValue` makes directly above.
	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE CELL'S TYPE AND ITS BOX, NOT THE `VarInfo` THEY CAME OFF** — the two facts it ever
	// read, and the difference is not tidiness. `emitScopeDrops` reads its binding out of `ownedBindings`,
	// which makes it a BORROW of that array, and this function EMITS — so handing the record in kept the
	// borrow live across a write to the parser's own storage (E3070, on this compiler's own source). Two
	// scalars cannot dangle.
	function emitCellLoad(cellVarType MaxonType, box ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let cellType = self.resolveFloatAliasType(cellVarType)
		let loaded = self.emitIndirectLoad(cellType, box: box, offset: CellContentOffset, storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(cellType), range: range)
		if self.valueIsManagedHeap(loaded) 'managedOccupant'
			self.markRebindableSlotRead(loaded)
		end 'managedOccupant'

		// ⭐⭐ **W117's MARK COMES BACK OUT OF THE CELL (review).** A cell holding an immutable global's record
		// hands that same record to every reader, so the load inherits the mark the STORE put on the cell. Its
		// absence was a wrong answer and not a cosmetic gap: a `var` that a closure captures becomes
		// cell-resident, and MEASURED on the rung tip that alone laundered the taint — `grow(b)` in the very
		// frame that owns `b` compiled and mutated a `let` global (exit 8) purely because a closure elsewhere
		// in the body had turned `b` into a cell, while the identical program without that closure is refused.
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: box, to: loaded)
		return loaded
	end 'emitCellLoad'

	// ⭐⭐ **WRITE A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING (PBR-2)** — the rebind, which for this binding kind is a store and
	// not a `Scope.setValue`. That is the whole of what makes a caller observe a callee's `x = 99`.
	function emitCellStore(binding VarInfo, value ValueId, range SourceRange)
		self.emitIndirectStore(binding.boundValue, offset: CellContentOffset, value: value, storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(self.resolveFloatAliasType(binding.varType)), range: range)
		// ⭐ **THE CELL ITSELF CARRIES W117's MARK (review)** — put on the BOX rather than on the occupant,
		// because the occupant is a value this frame may never name again while the cell is what every later
		// read, every capture and every write door (`bindingAliasesAnImmutableGlobal` reads `boundValue`, which
		// for a cell binding IS the cell) has in hand.
		//
		// ⚠ **MONOTONE ON PURPOSE: a later clean store does not CLEAR it.** A cell is written by reference, so
		// the tainted store and the clean one may reach any given read in either order at run time; keeping the
		// mark costs a false REJECT on a rebound-then-written cell and dropping it would cost a silent
		// acceptance, which is the direction this whole rule refuses to err in.
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.immutableGlobalReads, from: value, to: binding.boundValue)
	end 'emitCellStore'

	function emitIndirectStore(box ValueId, offset ByteOffset, value ValueId, storageType MaxonType, range SourceRange)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.storeIndirect(box, offset: offset, value: value, valueType: storageType), range: range)
	end 'emitIndirectStore'

	// The CONCRETE `MaxonType` a SLOT holding a `declared`-typed value is accessed as — what the
	// `loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` carries, and the only type in the compiler that says how wide
	// the access is.
	//
	// TWO kinds of slot ask it, and they are the same question: a struct/union FIELD, whose declared
	// type comes from the layout, and a capturing closure's ENV SLOT, whose declared type comes from
	// the captured binding. The name predates the second caller; it is left alone because four files
	// outside the parser cite it by name (`Project`, `TypeResolution`, `SignatureIndex`, `PLAN.md`).
	//
	// It resolves a `named` alias through `signatures.aliasOf`, the SAME whole-program door THE
	// SHIFT RULE already asks (`valueIsUnsigned`), for the same reason: an alias declared in another
	// file is still an alias here, and this file's own table would answer for neither it nor a
	// forward reference.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS THE SECOND STATEMENT OF "WHAT A `named` TYPE DENOTES", AND `TypeResolution.
	// resolveNamedType` IS THE FIRST.** That is not a duplication this rung introduced — it is the
	// standing shape of the two registries (see `ProgramSignatures.structTypes`): the sweep answers
	// the PARSER, the project registry answers the AUTHORITY, and the alias half has been written in
	// both places since ranged typealiases landed. What is new is that a slot's type has to be
	// resolved at PARSE time at all, and that is forced by the op: `resolveTypes` never rewrites an
	// op's type, so the parser is the last tier that can make it concrete.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE CASCADE ITSELF LIVES IN `SignatureIndex` (W116), BECAUSE A SECOND FAMILY OF ASKER ARRIVED
	// WITHOUT A PARSER.** A top-level `static var origin = Pair{a: 1}` resolves its box's slot widths on the
	// INDEX side — `SynthesizedFunction` holds neither an interner nor the alias registry — and a slot stored
	// at one width by `__module_init` and loaded at another by a body is a corrupted box no diagnostic can
	// report. The two facts only a `Parser` holds are the interner an id was minted in and the READER's file
	// path, so those are what this supplies; everything else is `slotStorageType`'s, once. That is exactly the
	// arrangement `denotedSlotType` states for the id-keyed vs name-keyed halves of the type cascade.
	function fieldStorageType(declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		return slotStorageType(self.signatures, declared: declared, typeNames: self.artifact.typeNames, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'fieldStorageType'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE UNION-PAYLOAD REJECTION MESSAGE, so the construct and the extract site report a refused
	// payload identically.** The REJECT itself is `requirePayloadLowerable`'s — deferred to the construct and
	// extract sites rather than taken at the declaration, exactly as a struct-typed field's is refused at its
	// use site (`requireStructBase`): a rejection at declaration would `throw` in the tolerant enum sweep and
	// truncate the layout (see `readEnumCaseInto`'s payload arm).
	//
	// ⚠ **IT RETURNS THE STRING, and the caller throws `ParseError.unsupported(…)` INLINE** — never
	// `throw self.helperReturningAParseError(…)`, which LEAKS 2 allocations under the C#-emitted refcount
	// runtime: a freshly-owned union returned across a call and immediately re-thrown is not reconciled (the
	// family OPEN.md records for a `throw` of a call result and for the owned-value ternary). Building the
	// message via a call that returns a String and throwing the ParseError inline is the non-leaking shape,
	// and it is the shape three other refusals in this file cite this one for.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT HAD A SIBLING — `nestedUnionPayloadMessage`, DELETED WITH ITS REFUSAL (W55).** That message
	// claimed a nested payload-bearing union payload "needs its own destructor cascade"; the cascade was
	// generic all along and what the payload path lacked was a slot-width derivation, so both the refusal and
	// the sentence explaining it are gone rather than reworded. A message describing a refusal that no longer
	// exists is a defect this project has paid for repeatedly.
	function floatPayloadMessage(field PayloadField, layout EnumLayout) returns String
		return "payload field '{String.from(field.name)}' on `{layout.kindWord()} {String.from(layout.name)}`: a `float` payload is not lowered yet (a scalar-only union is int-tagged, so a float must be re-encoded on the way into the slot and decoded on the way out, and neither the construct nor the extract does that)"
	end 'floatPayloadMessage'

	// A type-qualified numeric bound used as a VALUE (`let x = u16.max`, `if v == u64.max`).
	// The same three tokens a range declaration's endpoint is built from, resolved through
	// the same table — so `int(0 to u64.max)` and `v == u64.max` can never disagree about
	// what `u64.max` is. It materializes an ordinary integer literal.
	function parseTypeQualifiedBoundExpression() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let boundTok = self.current()
		let bound = try self.parseTypeQualifiedBound(false)
		return self.emitLiteral(bound.value, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: SourceRange.ofToken(boundTok))
	end 'parseTypeQualifiedBoundExpression'

	// Resolve a variable reference to the SSA value its binding aliases — M2's
	// value-substitution model emits NO load op; the reference simply IS the bound
	// value. A name with no local binding may still be a TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT (which inlines) or a
	// TOP-LEVEL `var` (which loads); only a name that is none of the three is a positioned parse
	// error (undefinedVariable).
	//
	// LOCAL FIRST. A local binding SHADOWS a top-level constant of the same name — `let OFFSET = 10`
	// at file scope and `let OFFSET = 2` inside a function means 2 in that function, which is what
	// every language with both scopes does and what the bootstrap does (its constants are consulted
	// only once a bare name has failed to resolve as a local).
	//
	// **CONSTANT BEFORE GLOBAL, and the order costs nothing**: the two are disjoint by construction —
	// one storage key holds ONE decl, and `constantValueOf`/`globalDeclFor` each answer only for
	// their own kind — so at most one of the two probes can ever hit. The order is readability, not
	// precedence.
	// ⚠ **A SELF-FIELD ALIAS IS TESTED BEFORE `lookupValue`, AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER
	// THAN TIDY.** An alias aliases no SSA value — a read of it is a `loadIndirect`, so there is
	// nothing for `boundValue` to hold and it is left 0. `lookupValue` would hand that 0 back as if
	// it were the answer, and ValueId 0 is the RECEIVER: `return count` inside a method would
	// silently compile to `return self`, reading a box pointer as an integer. A wrong answer with
	// no diagnostic anywhere.
	// ⭐ **IT ALSO PUBLISHES WHAT THE NAME RESOLVED TO, for the ONE consumer no ValueId can serve** —
	// `lastBareBindingImmutableName`, the E3019 blame name of a bare-identifier call ARGUMENT (see
	// `bareArgImmutableName`). Each arm that can answer sets it, through the same `immutableReceiverNameOf`
	// a receiver asks, so an argument and a receiver naming the same binding get the same verdict.
	function parseVariableReference() returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// Captured before the cursor leaves it: a by-reference call argument identifies the ONE read that may
		// take a cell's address by the token it stands on (`cellArgumentTokenIndex`).
		let nameIndex = self.pos
		// A KEYWORD-named binding is read here too (D8) — `return type` inside `function identity(type Integer)`.
		// `parsePrimary`'s keyword arm has already established that the name RESOLVES, so this consume never
		// admits a keyword the resolution below would then have to reject.
		let tok = try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)

		if self.scope.isSelfField(tok.value) 'selfField'
			// A self field's writability is the FIELD's own `var`/`let`, exactly as it is for a self-field
			// method receiver (`baseBindingOf`) — the receiver is a parameter and is never `mutable`.
			let binding = self.scope.getInfo(tok.value)
			let writable = try self.selfFieldIsWritable(binding, nameTok: tok)
			self.publishBareBindingName(tok, immutable: not writable)
			return try self.parseSelfFieldRead(tok, binding: binding)
		end 'selfField'

		// A LOCAL binding: reject a read of one that has been MOVED FROM through the shared liveness guard
		// (`requireBindingLive`), the SAME predicate the field/method base sites ask. This is the bare
		// READ site — a WRITE target routes through `parseAssignment`, which REVIVES the binding, so the
		// check must not run there. A name that is NOT a local (a top-level constant or `var`) is never
		// owned and never moved, so it stays on the original resolve path.
		if self.scope.contains(tok.value) 'local'
			let binding = self.scope.getInfo(tok.value)
			try self.requireBindingLive(binding, nameTok: tok)
			self.lastBareBindingImmutableName = self.immutableReceiverNameOf(binding, baseTok: tok)

			// ⭐⭐ **A CELL-RESIDENT NAME ALIASES NO SSA VALUE (PBR-2), so its read is a LOAD** — the same shape
			// the self-field alias two arms up already takes, and for the same reason: `boundValue` holds the
			// CELL, and handing that back would give the caller a pointer where the source asked for a value.
			// Reading through memory at every use is exactly what makes a write from another frame visible here.
			if binding.isCellBinding 'livesInACell'
				// ⭐⭐ **A BY-REFERENCE ARGUMENT ASKED FOR THE CELL ITSELF, so this read hands over the ADDRESS
				// and emits no load at all.** Keyed on the token index rather than on a flag — see
				// `cellArgumentTokenIndex` for the `f(x + 1)` wrong answer a flag would give — and CLEARED
				// here, which is also how the call site learns its request was taken.
				if self.cellArgumentTokenIndex == nameIndex 'handOverTheCell'
					self.cellArgumentTokenIndex = NoCellArgumentRequest
					return binding.boundValue
				end 'handOverTheCell'

				return self.emitCellLoad(binding.varType, box: binding.boundValue, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok))
			end 'livesInACell'

			return binding.boundValue
		end 'local'

		// ⭐ **CAPTURE (P1.5-A2b).** The name is not one of the CLOSURE's own bindings (its scope, checked
		// above). If it resolves into an ENCLOSING function's scope it is a CAPTURE: `captureEnclosing`
		// records it in the current closure's capture set and returns the value read out of the env block
		// (one `loadIndirect` — capture is BY VALUE). Checked BEFORE the top-level fall-through so an
		// enclosing local that SHADOWS a same-named global is caught as the capture it is, not silently
		// resolved to the global (a wrong answer). Outside a closure `closureCaptureScopes` is empty and this
		// returns `notCaptured` after one `count()` read.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE SAME ORDER BINDS A METHOD RECEIVER, WHICH IS THE SAME NAME IN THE SAME POSITION.** It is
		// re-encoded in `parseMethodCall`'s arms and in `methodCallsAt`'s membership tests, because neither
		// can route through this function (one needs a receiver binding, the other a side-effect-free bool).
		// `parseMethodCall` had them the other way round until the P1.7-slice-2a review and carries the
		// measurement of what that cost; a change here is a change there.
		// A CAPTURE publishes nothing, so it reads "writable" — capture is BY VALUE (`loadIndirect` out of
		// the env block), and writing through the loaded copy does not reach the enclosing binding. A
		// captured managed CONTAINER, whose value IS the shared record, is refused outright at this rung
		// (`capturedStructBase` admits only a struct), so the case the answer would matter for cannot arise.
		match try self.captureEnclosing(tok) 'capture'
			captured(value) then return value
			notCaptured then break 'capture'
		end 'capture'

		return try self.topLevelBindingReference(tok)
	end 'parseVariableReference'

	// Publish the blame name of the bare identifier just resolved, or clear it when the binding may be
	// written through — the one shape the arms that do not hold a `VarInfo` (a self field, a top-level
	// binding) use to reach `lastBareBindingImmutableName`'s "empty is writable" convention.
	function publishBareBindingName(tok Token, immutable bool)
		self.lastBareBindingImmutableName = tok.value if immutable else EmptySourceText
	end 'publishBareBindingName'

	// ⭐ **CAPTURE AN ENCLOSING BINDING INTO THE CLOSURE'S ENVIRONMENT (P1.5-A2b) — the real env handling
	// that replaces A2a's E2015 refusal.** A name is a capture iff it resolves into one of the enclosing
	// scopes a closure body is nested in (`closureCaptureScopes`) — an enclosing local or parameter.
	// References to GLOBALS, top-level `let`/`var`, other FUNCTIONS, and the closure's OWN parameters are NOT
	// captures (none lives in an enclosing `Scope`), so they fall through as `notCaptured`.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **CAPTURE IS BY VALUE — a thesis-forced divergence from v1 / the bootstrap's by-REFERENCE model.**
	// v1 stores the ADDRESS of each captured stack slot (`sys.stackAddr`) and reads a capture through TWO
	// indirect loads, so a read sees later mutations of the captured `var`. shv2 has NO addressable stack
	// slots — locals are SSA values in registers, there is no `stackAddr` and no mem2reg — so the env stores
	// the captured VALUE and a read is ONE `loadIndirect`. The corpus captures only immutable `let`s, so the
	// two models AGREE on every test; a captured-then-mutated `var` would differ, and no test exercises that.
	//
	// The env slot is assigned in first-seen order and DEDUPED by name (a capture read twice occupies one
	// slot); the enclosing binding's value + type are recorded now, read after the body parse by
	// `parseClosureExpression` to fill the env. The capture read loads through the reserved `__env` parameter
	// (`closureEnvParamId`). A capture from a NON-immediate enclosing frame (a nested capturing closure) needs
	// an env chain this slice does not build, so it is refused cleanly (E2015 → A2b-2).
	function captureEnclosing(tok Token) returns CaptureResult throws ParseError
		let name = self.baseBindingName(tok)
		match try self.captureScopeOf(name, anchor: tok) 'scope'
			found(enclosing) then return CaptureResult.captured(try self.emitCaptureRead(name, enclosing: enclosing, anchor: tok))
			notCaptured then return CaptureResult.notCaptured
		end 'scope'
	end 'captureEnclosing'

	// Does `name` resolve to a CAPTURE — a binding in any enclosing scope this closure body is nested in? A
	// cheap membership predicate that neither emits a capture nor raises the nested-frame error (the actual
	// capture through `captureScopeOf` does both). Used by `parseDottedPrimary` to route `capturedStruct.field`
	// to field access, and by `requireReceiverInScope` to answer "is the enclosing `self` reachable from
	// here?". False outside a closure (`closureCaptureScopes` empty).
	//
	// ⚠ It takes the BINDING NAME, not the token: the receiver is bound under `SelfParamName` and no token
	// spells that (`baseBindingName` is the one place the `self` keyword and `__self` are connected). A
	// `tok.value` here would look the receiver up under the word the source happened to write and miss it.
	function captureBinds(name ByteArray) returns bool
		for enclosing in self.closureCaptureScopes 'each'
			if enclosing.contains(name) 'bound'
				return true
			end 'bound'
		end 'each'
		return false
	end 'captureBinds'

	// ⭐ **THE CAPTURE-SCOPE WALK (P1.5-A2b) — the ONE place that answers "is this name a capture, and from
	// which enclosing frame?"** Shared by the bare read (`captureEnclosing`) and the field/method base
	// (`resolveFieldChain`/`methodBaseCapture`), so they cannot disagree about what is captured. Walks the
	// enclosing scopes innermost-first — a name resolves to the nearest scope that binds it, exactly as an
	// ordinary lookup would (`popScope`'s shadowing rules), so the FIRST hit walking down is the capture
	// source. A hit in a NON-immediate enclosing frame is a nested capturing closure, refused cleanly (the env
	// chain this slice does not build). Outside a closure `closureCaptureScopes` is empty and this returns
	// `notCaptured` after one `count()` read.
	//
	// ⚠ It keys on the BINDING NAME rather than the token, for `captureBinds`'s reason: the enclosing
	// RECEIVER is bound under `SelfParamName`, which no token spells (`baseBindingName` maps the `self`
	// keyword to it). `anchor` is the token the nested-frame diagnostic points at, and only that.
	function captureScopeOf(name ByteArray, anchor Token) returns CaptureScopeResult throws ParseError
		let depth = self.closureCaptureScopes.count()
		var i = depth
		while i > 0 'eachEnclosing'
			i = i - 1
			let enclosing = try self.closureCaptureScopes.get(i) otherwise panic("captureScopeOf: closureCaptureScopes.get OOB at {i}")
			if enclosing.contains(name) 'captured'
				if i != depth - 1 'nested'
					throw ParseError.unsupported("a closure that captures a variable from a non-immediate enclosing frame (nested capturing closures — P1.5-A2b-2)", line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column)
				end 'nested'
				return CaptureScopeResult.found(enclosing)
			end 'captured'
		end 'eachEnclosing'

		return CaptureScopeResult.notCaptured
	end 'captureScopeOf'

	// Assign (or reuse) the env slot for a captured name and emit its BY-VALUE read: `loadIndirect __env,
	// slot*8`. The slot column (`captureNames`) is env-array order; the parallel `captureValues`/`captureTypes`
	// carry the enclosing value to store and the type to read back, filled in lockstep on FIRST sight only so
	// a name captured twice shares one slot and one env store.
	//
	// ⚠ Keyed by the BINDING NAME, not the token — the enclosing RECEIVER captures under `SelfParamName`,
	// which no token spells. `anchor` positions the read and the diagnostics, and only that.
	function emitCaptureRead(name ByteArray, enclosing Scope, anchor Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// `var`, because the read is RECORDED on the binding below — a `let` here is a store through an
		// immutable local (E2013), the same rule `declareInitializedBinding` binds its own `VarInfo` under.
		var binding = enclosing.getInfo(name)

		// ⭐ **A CAPTURE IS A READ OF THE ENCLOSING BINDING, and this is the ONE read door that does not run
		// through `requireBindingLive`** (which owns the move guard a capture is deliberately not subject
		// to). The enclosing function's E3012 drain happens after this closure has been parsed inline, so
		// without this line `let x = 5` + `function(n Integer) gives n + x` would report `x` unused — a
		// false rejection of a program both reference compilers accept.
		//
		// ⚠ The NAME half of this mention lands in the CLOSURE's `referencedNames`, which the restore
		// discards — so `parseClosureExpression` pushes the capture names back into the enclosing function's
		// list at the restore, exactly as the oracle re-adds `captures` to `_referencedVars`. The bit above
		// excuses THIS binding; the re-push is what excuses another binding of the same name.
		self.markBindingReferenced(binding)

		// ⭐⭐ **A SELF-FIELD ALIAS IS NOT A CAPTURABLE BINDING — IT HOLDS NO VALUE, AND `boundValue` 0 IS THE
		// RECEIVER.** `createSelfField` leaves `boundValue` 0 because a read of the alias is a `loadIndirect`
		// through the receiver; capturing it therefore stored the enclosing method's `self` BOX in the env
		// slot and handed it back as the field. MEASURED: `apply(function(k int) gives k + n, x: 1)` inside a
		// method of a `type Counter{ var n as int }` holding 3 compiled clean and returned **25** — the box
		// pointer, low byte — where the answer is 4, with no diagnostic anywhere. With `n` declared through a
		// ranged alias the same capture instead PANICKED in lowering, because the env slot's type is filled
		// from `binding.varType` at parse time and a `named` reaches `maxonTypeToStdType` unresolved. One
		// defect, two faces.
		//
		// ⭐ **SO THE BARE NAME IS NOT CAPTURED — THE RECEIVER IS, AND THE FIELD IS READ THROUGH IT.** That is
		// exactly what the alias MEANS (`n` is `self.n`), so it routes to `parseSelfFieldRead`, the one
		// self-field read door, whose `requireReceiverValue` resolves the enclosing receiver as the capture it
		// is. The env therefore holds ONE slot — the receiver — however many fields the body names, and both
		// spellings of a field read emit the identical `loadIndirect` off it. This is the THIRD reader of
		// `boundValue` that took 0 for a real id — `dispatchMethodOnBinding` and
		// `builtinConformanceReceiverValue` were the other two, and the census says it is the last: every
		// other reader either guards on `isSelfField` first (`receiverOwnerMask`, and `baseBoxOf` for the
		// field-chain base W66 opened), or cannot see an alias at
		// all (`parseVariableReference`'s local arm runs under `not isSelfField`; `consumedArgName` and the
		// move/alias searches scan `ownedBindings`, which a `trivial`-ownership alias never joins).
		//
		// ⚠ It cannot recurse: the receiver's OWN binding is a parameter, so the capture this delegation
		// triggers reaches this same routine with `isSelfField` false.
		if binding.isSelfField 'capturedSelfField'
			return try self.parseSelfFieldRead(anchor, binding: binding)
		end 'capturedSelfField'

		// ⭐ **DECLARED vs STORAGE, at an env slot.** `captureType` is what the SOURCE said, and it is
		// what the read's value is minted as — a ranged INT alias stays `named` on purpose, because its
		// NAME is what carries the signedness `valueIsUnsigned` reads (a captured `int(0 to u64.max)`
		// must still `shr` zero-filling), and `resolveFloatAliasType` has already collapsed the one
		// alias whose VALUE is a different machine class. The OP, though, carries a WIDTH, and a `named`
		// reaches `maxonTypeToStdType` unresolved — so the slot's type goes through `fieldStorageType`,
		// the same door a struct field's width comes from and the same one `buildClosureEnv`'s store
		// asks. That the two ask ONE routine is the invariant, not a tidiness: a slot filled at one
		// width and read at another corrupts the environment silently.
		let captureType = self.resolveFloatAliasType(binding.varType)
		let range = SourceRange.ofToken(anchor)

		// ⭐⭐ **A CELL-RESIDENT BINDING IS CAPTURED BY ITS CELL, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES CAPTURE BY REFERENCE
		// (PBR-2).** The env slot holds the cell's ADDRESS rather than a snapshot of its contents, so the read
		// is TWO loads deep — out of the env, then out of the cell — and sees whatever the enclosing frame has
		// written since. This is exactly v1's model (`sys.stackAddr` into the env, two indirect loads back);
		// what differs is only WHERE the storage lives, since shv2 has no addressable locals.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE ENV SLOT'S TYPE IS THE POINTER'S, NOT THE CONTENT'S**, and `buildClosureEnv`'s store reads
		// back the same recorded type — so a captured `float` cell stores and loads its ADDRESS as a machine
		// word and only the second load is float-typed. Recording the content type here would have the env
		// store put a pointer through an f64 slot.
		//
		// ⚠ It costs nothing for every OTHER capture: a binding that is not cell-resident is unchanged, which
		// is every capture in the corpus — `closure-capture-by-ref`'s `let x = 42` included, because a `let`
		// never needs a cell.
		// ⭐⭐ **A CAPTURE OF A TAINTED BINDING IS TAINTED (W117 review).** Both arms mint a value for a record
		// the ENCLOSING frame already knows may alias a `let`-declared global, so the mark is re-minted in the
		// CLOSURE's own SSA space — which is what every write door inside the body is keyed by. Without it the
		// closure LAUNDERED the taint: measured, `callThunk(function() gives grow(b))` mutated the global and
		// exited 3 where the same call spelled directly is refused E3019. The source is the ENCLOSING frame's
		// mark set, which `parseClosureExpression` hands over rather than dropping.
		//
		// ⚠ **BOTH ARMS, and the CELL one is not the odd case.** A cell-resident capture is captured BY
		// REFERENCE, so its content is if anything more certainly the same record; the two arms differ in how
		// many loads deep the value sits, never in what it points at.
		if binding.isCellBinding 'capturedByItsCell'
			let cell = self.emitIndirectLoad(MaxonType.integer, box: self.closureEnvParamId, offset: envSlotOffset(self.captureSlotFor(name, enclosingValue: binding.boundValue, captureType: MaxonType.integer)), storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(MaxonType.integer), range: range)
			let occupant = self.emitIndirectLoad(captureType, box: cell, offset: CellContentOffset, storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(captureType), range: range)
			self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.capturedImmutableGlobalReads, from: binding.boundValue, to: occupant)
			return occupant
		end 'capturedByItsCell'

		let slot = self.captureSlotFor(name, enclosingValue: binding.boundValue, captureType: captureType)
		let captured = self.emitIndirectLoad(captureType, box: self.closureEnvParamId, offset: envSlotOffset(slot), storageType: self.wordSlotStorageType(captureType), range: range)
		self.carryImmutableGlobalMark(self.capturedImmutableGlobalReads, from: binding.boundValue, to: captured)
		return captured
	end 'emitCaptureRead'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE SPELLING OF "THIS VALUE DENOTES THE SAME RECORD AS THAT ONE, SO IT INHERITS THE MARK"
	// (W117 review).** Four sites move a may-alias-an-immutable-global record from one SSA name to another
	// without going through a promotion — the two capture reads above, and the cell store/load pair — and each
	// of them is a place the taint was silently DROPPED before this existed. One function so that a fifth such
	// carrier is a call rather than a fourth copy of the test.
	//
	// ⚠ **`marks` IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE THE SOURCE SET IS NOT ALWAYS THIS FUNCTION'S.** A capture reads the
	// ENCLOSING frame's marks (a closure body's ids are its own SSA space); a cell load reads this one's. The
	// DESTINATION is always this function's, which is why only the source is passed.
	//
	// ⚡ Both sets are the shared empty one for every program that declares no aggregate `let` global, so this
	// is a membership test on an empty set.
	function carryImmutableGlobalMark(marks ValueMarkSet, from ValueId, to ValueId)
		if marks.contains(from) 'sourceMayAliasAnImmutableGlobal'
			self.markImmutableGlobalRead(to)
		end 'sourceMayAliasAnImmutableGlobal'
	end 'carryImmutableGlobalMark'

	// The env slot for `name`, appending a fresh one (recording the enclosing value + type to store) on first
	// sight and reusing it thereafter. Dedup keeps `multiple-captures` (`x + a + b`) at one slot per distinct
	// name and, more importantly, keeps a name read twice from storing — and later dropping — two slots.
	function captureSlotFor(name ByteArray, enclosingValue ValueId, captureType MaxonType) returns EnvSlotIndex
		for k in 0 upto self.captureNames.count() 'existing'
			let seen = try self.captureNames.get(k) otherwise panic("captureSlotFor: captureNames.get OOB at {k}")
			if seen == name 'reuse'
				return k
			end 'reuse'
		end 'existing'
		self.captureNames.push(name)
		self.captureValues.push(enclosingValue)
		self.captureTypes.push(captureType)
		return self.captureNames.count() - 1
	end 'captureSlotFor'

	// A bare name that is not a local: a top-level constant, a top-level `var`, or nothing.
	//
	// A top-level CONSTANT is a `let`, so it is immutable wherever it is read — and for a MANAGED one that
	// is not a formality: `let G = "hi"` materializes the ONE immortal `.rdata` record every read of those
	// bytes shares, so a callee that appended through it would write read-only memory (measured: exit 139,
	// where the oracle raises E3019). It publishes its name for the same reason a `let` local does.
	function topLevelBindingReference(tok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.constantValueOf(tok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'constant'
			found(payload, tag, typeName) then return self.recordImmutableConstantRead(tok, value: self.emitFoldedConstant(payload, tag: tag, typeName: typeName, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok)))
			foundManaged(bytes, kind) then return self.recordImmutableConstantRead(tok, value: try self.emitManagedConstant(bytes, kind: kind, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok)))
			notFound then break 'constant'
		end 'constant'

		return try self.topLevelGlobalRead(tok)
	end 'topLevelBindingReference'

	// Publish a top-level constant read's blame name and hand the value straight back —
	// `recordGlobalReadValue`'s shape, for its reason: the two arms above differ only in how the value is
	// materialized, and a `let` is immutable in both.
	// ⭐⭐ **MATERIALIZE A FOLDED CONSTANT AT A USE — and an ENUM CASE takes the door the BODY spelling takes.**
	// `Reg.rdx` written in a body goes through `emitEnumTagLiteral`, which mints a value TAGGED `named(Reg)`
	// while the op it emits carries `integer`; that split is the whole shape of a payload-free enum in this
	// compiler — the value knows its enum, the storage is an i64.
	//
	// ⛔ **EMITTING THE LITERAL AT `MaxonType.named` INSTEAD IS A COMPILER PANIC, MEASURED:**
	// `maxonTypeToStdType: a `named` type must be resolved to a primitive before lowering`. The op's
	// valueType is a STORAGE question and `named` is not an answer to it. Routing here rather than widening
	// `constantValueType` is what keeps the two spellings of `Reg.rdx` — in a body and out of a top-level
	// `let` — one mechanism.
	function emitFoldedConstant(payload ParsedInt, tag ValueTypeTag, typeName ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'aPlainScalar'
			return self.emitLiteral(payload, valueType: constantValueType(tag, typeNameId: 0), range: range)
		end 'aPlainScalar'

		return self.emitEnumTagLiteral(typeName, tag: payload, range: range)
	end 'emitFoldedConstant'

	// The `MaxonType` a folded constant denotes, interned into THIS parser's table. Only a `named` tag has a
	// name to intern, so every other constant read pays nothing — which matters because this is on the path
	// of every top-level constant reference in the program.
	function constantValueTypeOf(tag ValueTypeTag, typeName ByteArray) returns MaxonType
		if tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'namesNoType'
			return constantValueType(tag, typeNameId: 0)
		end 'namesNoType'

		return constantValueType(tag, typeNameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(typeName))
	end 'constantValueTypeOf'

	function recordImmutableConstantRead(tok Token, value ValueId) returns ValueId
		self.publishBareBindingName(tok, immutable: true)
		return value
	end 'recordImmutableConstantRead'

	// ⭐ **A USE OF A TOP-LEVEL MANAGED CONSTANT MATERIALIZES ITS LITERAL** (P1.7a) — the managed twin of
	// the `literal`-op inline directly above, and the same storage model: the declaration reserves
	// nothing, and the READ is where the value comes from.
	//
	// It emits the very ops the literal itself emits, so a managed constant's storage class is the one
	// its literal already has and shv2 grows NO second immortality mechanism (the oracle's
	// `MmImmortalRefcount` sentinel is deliberately not imported — see `ARCHITECTURE.md`):
	//
	//   • a **String** literal is a wholly IMMORTAL `.rdata` record (`lowerStringLiteral`), and
	//     `registerRdataConstant` dedupes it by bytes — so every read of `let name = "Ada"` in the whole
	//     program shares ONE record, materialized once, never allocated and never freed. This is exactly
	//     "materialized once, shared by every read, immortal", reached with no new mechanism at all.
	//   • a **byte string** literal is an owned `Array with Byte` RECORD over an immortal `.rdata` BLOB
	//     (`lowerConstArrayLiteral`). The payload is still materialized once and shared — the blob dedupes
	//     by bytes — but the 48-byte record is heap and per-read, because an Array is a MUTABLE container
	//     whose record its own methods rewrite (a `push` detaches the buffer by writing `buffer@0`,
	//     `capacity@16` and `length@8`), and a `.rdata` record cannot be written. Making the RECORD
	//     immortal too needs two things shv2 does not have — enforcement that a `let`-bound array is never
	//     mutated, and a real COPY promotion for `var b = <borrowed array>` (which today increfs the box,
	//     and an rdata box has no refcount word to incref). Both are named in OPEN.md; neither is invented
	//     here, because a SECOND immortality mechanism is precisely what this design exists to avoid.
	function emitManagedConstant(bytes ByteArray, kind ManagedConstKind, range SourceRange) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		return match kind 'k'
			string gives self.emitStringLiteral(bytes, range: range)
			byteArray gives try self.emitByteStringLiteralValue(bytes, range: range)
		end 'k'
	end 'emitManagedConstant'

	// ⭐ **A USE OF A TOP-LEVEL CONSTANT INLINES ITS VALUE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE STORAGE MODEL.**
	//
	// The constant was folded to a number by the declaration sweep, so the reference materializes as
	// an ordinary `literal` op carrying it — indistinguishable, from here down, from a program that
	// wrote the number out. Nothing is loaded, no `.data` slot is reserved, no relocation is emitted
	// and no backend knows this feature exists: `return ANSWER` compiles to exactly what `return 42`
	// compiles to.
	//
	// Both reference compilers do precisely this. v1 rewrites the read op in place into a `literal`
	// (`rewriteUnresolvedRead`, TypeResolution.maxon:2915-2925) and its `.data` emitter walks
	// `topLevelVars` only — a constant is in a different map and the emitter never sees it. The
	// bootstrap mints a fresh literal per use (`EmitConstantLiteral`, 2-Parser.cs:16870-16900), whose
	// comment says it outright: "a top-level `let` reaches expression position already folded, as a
	// literal". Storage is what a top-level `var` needs, and that is P1.0d.5b.
	//
	// It goes through `emitLiteral`, so an inlined INTEGER constant lands in the parser's constant
	// column (`recordConst`) exactly as a written-out literal does — which is what keeps `let SHIFT =
	// 1` … `x shl SHIFT` folding, and THE SHIFT RULE seeing a count it can check.
	//
	// **A TOP-LEVEL `var` IS THE OTHER HALF OF THAT STORY, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE ONE** — see
	// `topLevelGlobalRead` directly below. It is what P1.0d.5b added: the read that does NOT
	// disappear.

	// ⭐ **A USE OF A TOP-LEVEL `var` LOADS FROM ITS `.data` SLOT.** The exact inverse of a
	// constant's read: nothing is folded, a slot IS reserved, a relocation IS emitted, and the
	// backend very much knows this feature exists.
	//
	// It emits the GENERAL PAIR — `globalAddr` then `loadIndirect` — rather than a fused
	// `globalLoad`, per the Std memory band's contract: the same two ops a struct field (P1.1) and a
	// heap value (P1.2) read through. The address op is its own value, so a function that reads one
	// global twice materializes the address twice and the ordinary CSE that arrives with the Std
	// optimization passes will fold them — `globalAddr` declares `isPure: true` precisely so that it
	// can.
	//
	// ⚠ The load is NOT hoisted out of a loop that writes the same global, and that is not this
	// function's doing — it is `loadIndirect`'s `isPure: false` (see StdDialect). Get that wrong and
	// this emits perfectly correct IR that a later pass silently breaks.
	function topLevelGlobalRead(tok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.globalDeclFor(tok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'global'
			// A read itself does not care whether the slot may be WRITTEN — it borrows the occupant either
			// way. What the MUTABILITY decides is whether the borrowed value may later become a mutable
			// ALIAS, which is `recordGlobalReadValue`'s business (P1.7 slice 2b).
			found(label, valueType, mutable) then return self.recordGlobalReadValue(tok, value: self.emitGlobalLoad(label, valueType: valueType, range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok)), mutable: mutable)
			notFound then return try self.emitFunctionRef(tok)
		end 'global'
	end 'topLevelGlobalRead'

	// Mark a global read that came out of an IMMUTABLE slot, and hand the value straight back (P1.7 slice 2b).
	// It is only the READ path that marks: a receiver load (`parseMethodCall`'s stored-binding arm) is
	// consumed by the dispatch on the spot, where `noteReceiverWrite` already refuses a write through
	// an immutable binding, and it can never reach a `var`'s initializer.
	function recordGlobalReadValue(tok Token, value ValueId, mutable bool) returns ValueId
		// The blame name rides the same mutability answer (a top-level `let` refuses a write through it
		// exactly as a `let` local does), published here so the constant and the slot-backed halves of a
		// top-level read reach `lastBareBindingImmutableName` through one decision each.
		self.publishBareBindingName(tok, immutable: not mutable)

		// ⭐ **THE WRITABLE ARM IS NOT "NOTHING TO RECORD" (W41).** A `var` global's slot is rebindable
		// storage exactly as a `var` field is, and `emitCheckedGlobalStore` decrefs the old occupant on the
		// way past — so `let old = G` + `G = fresh` dangles for the identical reason and died with the
		// identical 0xC0000005 (the oracle prints `sum 3`). The two arms now carry the two halves of one
		// question — can this slot be REBOUND — and the answer feeds the binding promotion rather than the
		// aliasing refusal. See `rebindableSlotReads`.
		if mutable 'writableSlot'
			if self.valueIsManagedHeap(value) 'managedOccupant'
				self.markRebindableSlotRead(value)
			end 'managedOccupant'

			return value
		end 'writableSlot'

		self.markImmutableGlobalRead(value)
		return value
	end 'recordGlobalReadValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `immutableGlobalReads`** — the read that mints a mark and the merge that
	// PROPAGATES one both come through here (P1.7 slice 2b), because the copy-on-write off the shared empty
	// set is the half of this that is dangerous to write twice: a second insert site that skipped the
	// identity test would insert into `sharedEmptyImmutableGlobalReads` itself, and that value is MODULE
	// level and shared across the per-file parse fan-out — every other function in every other file would
	// then see a mark naming an unrelated value in its own SSA space, and a program would be refused for a
	// binding it never wrote. One home makes that unwritable rather than merely unwritten.
	function markImmutableGlobalRead(value ValueId)
		self.immutableGlobalReads = privateValueMarkSet(self.immutableGlobalReads, anchor: sharedEmptyImmutableGlobalReads)
		self.immutableGlobalReads.insert(value)
	end 'markImmutableGlobalRead'

	// ⭐⭐ **DID THE WHOLE-PROGRAM SWEEP PUBLISH THIS NAME AS ONE WHOSE RESULT MAY ALIAS A `let`-DECLARED
	// GLOBAL'S RECORD? (W117)** — THE one composition, spent at three doors: the declaration being entered
	// (`beginFunction`), the synthesized default helper being drained (`parseDefaultHelperBody`), and the
	// CALLEE at the one line every direct, method and static call passes (`emitCall`).
	//
	// ⛔ **THE THREE MUST AGREE, BECAUSE THEY ARE TWO HALVES OF ONE RULE.** The entry doors decide whether
	// `ownedFormOfBorrowedValue` REFUSES a returned global's record, and the call door decides whether the
	// result that return produced is MARKED. A spelling that drifted between them would either refuse a
	// function whose callers already mark its result, or — the costly direction — admit one whose callers do
	// not, which is a global mutated silently.
	//
	// ⚡ The whole-program flag is asked FIRST and is false for every program that declares no such function,
	// which is nearly all of them: one bool where the second half costs a hash.
	function functionMayReturnAGlobalAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.signatures.programReturnsAnImmutableGlobalAlias() and self.signatures.returnsImmutableGlobalAlias(name)
	end 'functionMayReturnAGlobalAlias'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `mergeImmutableBlame`** — `markImmutableGlobalRead`'s twin, for the identical
	// copy-on-write reason and through the same one home for it. An EMPTY name is not recorded: absence is
	// what "this merge blames nothing" means, and a stored empty would make the reader answer the same
	// question twice.
	function recordMergeBlame(phi ValueId, blamed ByteArray)
		if blamed.isEmpty() 'nothingToBlame'
			return
		end 'nothingToBlame'

		self.mergeImmutableBlame = privateMergeBlameMap(self.mergeImmutableBlame, anchor: sharedEmptyMergeBlames)
		self.mergeImmutableBlame.upsert(phi, value: blamed)
	end 'recordMergeBlame'

	// The blame a merge phi carries, or empty. Gated on the table being non-empty so a program with no
	// blamed merge — nearly every program — answers with one field read and never hashes.
	function mergeBlameOf(value ValueId) returns ByteArray
		if self.mergeImmutableBlame.count() == 0 'noMergeBlamedAnything'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'noMergeBlamedAnything'

		return try self.mergeImmutableBlame.get(value) otherwise EmptySourceText
	end 'mergeBlameOf'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `rebindableSlotReads` (W41)** — `markImmutableGlobalRead`'s twin, for the
	// identical copy-on-write hazard and through the same one home for it (`privateValueMarkSet`).
	//
	// Its THREE producers are the three ways a managed value comes out of storage a later statement can
	// overwrite: `emitFieldLoad` (every `self.items`, `h.items` and chain hop funnels through it),
	// `recordGlobalReadValue`'s WRITABLE arm (the exact complement of the immutable arm one function up), and
	// `emitCellLoad` (PBR-2's by-reference parameter and cell-resident `var`, whose store decrefs the
	// occupant it displaces exactly as the other two do). All three gate on the value being MANAGED, so a
	// scalar field — which owns no heap and can be freed by nothing — never enters the set and never costs a
	// hash.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LIST WAS "TWO" FOR A RUNG AND A HALF WHILE THE THIRD SLOT KIND EXISTED AND DANGLED.** It is
	// DERIVED by grepping `self.markRebindableSlotRead(` rather than remembered — the census
	// `markBufferSurface` keeps two functions down, for this reason.
	function markRebindableSlotRead(value ValueId)
		self.rebindableSlotReads = privateValueMarkSet(self.rebindableSlotReads, anchor: sharedEmptyRebindableSlotReads)
		self.rebindableSlotReads.insert(value)
	end 'markRebindableSlotRead'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `bufferSurfaceValues` (R4.4)** — `markImmutableGlobalRead`'s twin, for the
	// identical copy-on-write reason, through the same one home for it (`privateValueMarkSet`).
	//
	// Its EIGHT call sites in SEVEN functions, DERIVED by grepping `self.markBufferSurface(` rather than
	// remembered — the six producers of a buffer VALUE:
	//   • `__ManagedMemory.create` (`parseManagedMemoryStaticCall`);
	//   • a `slice` taken THROUGH the buffer surface (`parseArraySlice`);
	//   • `__ManagedDirectory.filename`/`currentPath` (`emitOwnedManagedMemoryResult`, R4.3);
	//   • a FUSED WRAPPER's inline `managed` read (`emitFieldLoad`) — which since W49 wave 7 is what serves
	//     the corpus's own `String.addressableBytes()`, whose entire body is `return managed`;
	//   • an ELEMENT read out of an array whose elements are buffers (`markElementReadSurface`, A2m) —
	//     the one producer that reads a DIFFERENT column (`bufferSurfaceElements`) to decide;
	//   • `arr.managed` in VALUE position (`bufferSurfaceOf`, BATCH2 slice 6), which is TWO call sites in
	//     one function because its two ownership arms mark two different values;
	// — plus `markDeclaredSurface` below, whose own header carries ITS list (eight call sites in six
	// functions, and NOT all of them a declared type any more: three of them read the receiver's INSTANCE
	// ARGUMENT, which no declaration in the callee's file spells at all).
	// ⚠⚠ **THIS SENTENCE HAS NOW BEEN WRONG FIVE TIMES, AND THE FIFTH WAS THE FIRST WHERE THE *NUMBER* WAS
	// RIGHT AND THE *LIST* WAS NOT** — TWO while there were three, THREE while there were four, FIVE while
	// there were six (`markElementReadSurface` had never been counted), SIX while there were eight, and then
	// EIGHT while there were NINE: `emitFieldLoad` had been minting and marking a view since W17 and was
	// never listed, while `dispatchStringMethod`'s `addressableBytes` arm was listed until wave 7 retired it.
	// The two errors cancelled in the count and not in the list, so a reader checking the total would have
	// been reassured by it. **Count them, do not copy this sentence — and read the NAMES off the grep too**;
	// and count CALL SITES, not producers: `bufferSurfaceOf` is one producer with two of them.
	// ⚠ The CHAINED `.managed` (`arr.managed.setLength(2)`) is still the producer that mints NO value to mark
	// — D11b measured it as one — and it passes the surface along the dispatch instead (`viaManagedField`).
	// Only the VALUE form has a value to mark, and it mints one for exactly that reason.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE MARK IS A FACT ABOUT A `ValueId`, SO IT DOES NOT SURVIVE TYPE ERASURE — AND A *DECLARED*
	// SPELLING IS THEREFORE AN ENTRANCE A SWEEP OVER VALUE PRODUCERS STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE.** That is the
	// generalisable lesson of A2j, and it is why D11b's enumeration missed it. `__ManagedMemory` is registered
	// as a generic ALIAS of `Array with Byte` (`ProgramSignatures.registerManagedFileType`, whose own note
	// calls it "reaches the ordinary `Array` paths with nothing special-cased"), so a parameter, a struct field
	// or a return type SPELLED `__ManagedMemory` resolves to a type indistinguishable from `ByteArray`.
	// MEASURED on the tree before A2j: inside `function f(m __ManagedMemory)`, `m.push(7)`/`m.count()`
	// compiled, linked and RAN, while `m.length()` — the buffer roster's FIRST member — was refused as an
	// unknown `Array` method. The roster, exactly inverted, at all three spellings.
	// ⇒ Each of the three now carries a *declared-as-the-buffer-alias* bit from its declaration site to the
	// site that binds the value, and marks it here. There is no second surface mechanism: this mark stays the
	// one authority, and `dispatchArrayMethod` needed no change for it.
	function markBufferSurface(value ValueId)
		self.bufferSurfaceValues = privateValueMarkSet(self.bufferSurfaceValues, anchor: sharedEmptyBufferSurfaceValues)
		self.bufferSurfaceValues.insert(value)
	end 'markBufferSurface'

	// ⭐ `markBufferSurface`'s inverse — the value no longer denotes a buffer because the record it denoted no
	// longer exists (W35, `foldByteViewIntoStreamWrite`). It is CLEARED rather than left to be unreachable by
	// construction: "no integer-tagged value ever reaches a buffer-surface question" is exactly the kind of
	// premise this compiler keeps finding false, and the mark is one `remove` to retire honestly. No
	// copy-on-write is needed — a value can only be UNMARKED in a function that already marked one, so the
	// set is this function's own by then.
	function unmarkBufferSurface(value ValueId)
		// The removal's "was it there?" answer is not a fact worth asking: the caller has just proved the
		// value IS a byte view, and this door exists to make the mark's absence true, not to report on it.
		_ = self.bufferSurfaceValues.remove(value)
		// ⚠ **AND THE SIZED COLUMN WITH IT, BECAUSE THE INVARIANT IS "EVERY SIZED VALUE IS ALSO A BUFFER
		// VALUE" AND A ONE-COLUMN REMOVAL WOULD BREAK IT (W192 second review).** Not reachable today — the
		// only caller folds a `String.addressableBytes` view, which no sized container's record can be — so
		// this line changes no program. It is here because the two columns describe ONE value and the
		// alternative is the premise this compiler keeps finding false: a value marked sized and NOT marked
		// as a buffer would be refused a length change while being served the `Array` roster one line down,
		// which is the exact disagreement `markSizedContainerBuffer`'s door invariant exists to prevent.
		_ = self.sizedContainerBufferValues.remove(value)
	end 'unmarkBufferSurface'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `bufferSurfaceSlots` (A2m)** — `markBufferSurface`'s twin for a TUPLE value,
	// through the same one home for the copy-on-write (`privateValueSlotSurfaceMap`) and with the same hazard:
	// the anchor is module-level, so a write into it names slots of an unrelated function's SSA space.
	// `error.tuple-slot-mark-does-not-leak-across-functions` is what holds that shut.
	//
	// Only a NON-PLAIN mask is stored, so a program whose tuples name no buffer allocates no table.
	function markBufferSurfaceSlots(value ValueId, surface DeclaredSurface)
		if surfaceSlotCount(surface) == 0 'notATupleWithSlots'
			return
		end 'notATupleWithSlots'

		self.bufferSurfaceSlots = privateValueSlotSurfaceMap(self.bufferSurfaceSlots, anchor: sharedEmptyBufferSurfaceSlots)
		self.bufferSurfaceSlots.upsert(value, value: surface)
	end 'markBufferSurfaceSlots'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `bufferSurfaceElements` (A2m)** — `markBufferSurface`'s twin for an ARRAY whose
	// ELEMENTS are buffers, on the same discipline and against the same anchor hazard.
	function markBufferSurfaceElements(value ValueId)
		self.bufferSurfaceElements = privateValueMarkSet(self.bufferSurfaceElements, anchor: sharedEmptyBufferSurfaceElements)
		self.bufferSurfaceElements.insert(value)
	end 'markBufferSurfaceElements'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE WRITER OF `sizedContainerBufferValues` (W192)** — `markBufferSurface`'s twin for the one
	// receiver whose record the TYPE also describes, on the same discipline and against the same anchor
	// hazard.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT TAKES THE BUFFER MARK TOO, AND THAT IS THE INVARIANT RATHER THAN A CONVENIENCE.** The refusal
	// reads THIS column alone (`dispatchArrayMethod`), so a value marked sized and not marked as a buffer
	// would be refused a length change while being served the `Array` roster one line down — two surfaces
	// answering about one value, which is the disagreement the buffer mark exists to prevent. Every caller
	// today already holds a value the buffer mark is written on — each of them marks it through
	// `markDeclaredSurface` or through `bufferSurfaceOf` a line earlier — so this line adds nothing to any
	// path that exists; it is what makes the invariant a property of the DOOR instead of a rule each caller
	// remembers, which is the same argument `privateValueMarkSet` makes one level down. ⚠ **DELIBERATELY NOT
	// A CALL-SITE LIST**: `markBufferSurface`'s header records that exact sentence being wrong five times.
	function markSizedContainerBuffer(value ValueId)
		self.markBufferSurface(value)
		self.sizedContainerBufferValues = privateValueMarkSet(self.sizedContainerBufferValues, anchor: sharedEmptySizedContainerBufferValues)
		self.sizedContainerBufferValues.insert(value)
	end 'markSizedContainerBuffer'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DECISION: A TYPE SPELLED `__ManagedMemory` DENOTES THE BUFFER (A2j).** ⚖ **USER RULING,
	// 2026-07-31** — D11b's ("the buffer's legitimate surface is EXACTLY what its roster names",
	// `bufferSurfaceMemberNames`) applied to the spelling that NAMES the buffer. The alternative — a declared
	// `__ManagedMemory` deliberately exposing the `Array` surface — would make the roster message false at
	// three spellings, which is the defect this batch removes.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF THE TOKEN, NOT OF THE RESOLVED TYPE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.**
	// `parseTypeReference`'s generic-alias arm folds the spelling into `genericInstance(Array with Byte)`, so
	// once a `MaxonType` exists there is nothing left to test — a `__ManagedMemory` annotation and a
	// `ByteArray` one are then the same type. Every caller therefore asks BEFORE `parseTypeReference` runs.
	// ⚠ A token test is also robust where a resolved-type test could not be: the tolerant declaration SWEEP
	// asks this too, and at sweep time the alias may not be registered yet (`registerManagedFileType` is the
	// sweep's own write), so `isGenericAlias` can still answer no there. A spelling is the same fact in both
	// passes — the property `parseOptionalReturnType` is shared for.
	//
	// `identifierNameAt` answers the empty source text for anything that is not an identifier — a `returns
	// int`, a missing clause, a token past the end — so every non-identifier type position answers "no".
	// ⭐⭐ **AND AN ALIAS FOR ONE DENOTES IT TOO (W43)** — `typealias KeyMemory = __ManagedMemory with Key`,
	// then `function init(initKeys KeyMemory)`. The alias FOLDS the spelling away (`canonicalGenericBaseName`
	// interns it as an `Array` instance, which is the binding R4.4 exists to keep), so the declaration that
	// wrote `__ManagedMemory` is the only place the fact survives — `recordGenericAlias` files it, and this is
	// where a position spelling the alias reads it back. Without it `initKeys.length()` is refused as an
	// unknown `Array` member on `stdlib/Map.maxon`'s `init`, which is the exact inversion A2j measured for the
	// bare spelling.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH KEYS, because a nested alias is keyed `<Type>.<member>` and SPELLED bare.** `Map.KeyMemory` is
	// what `qualifiedInnerGenericAlias` files; `KeyMemory` is what the parameter writes. The bare probe comes
	// first — a file-scope alias is the common case and `enclosingType` is empty at file scope, where the join
	// would build a string for a key that cannot exist.
	function typeSpellingAtDenotesBuffer(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		return self.spellingDenotesBufferSurface(self.identifierNameAt(index))
	end 'typeSpellingAtDenotesBuffer'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHERE DOES THE TYPE WRITTEN AT `index` NAME `__ManagedMemory` (A2m)?** THE one reader every
	// declaration door asks — a parameter, a struct field, a return clause — and the generalisation of the
	// single-token test above from a BIT to a whole `DeclaredSurface` TREE:
	//
	//   • `m __ManagedMemory`              ⇒ the root's outright bit
	//   • `a BufArray`                     ⇒ the root's element bit, `BufArray = Array with __ManagedMemory`
	//   • `t (__ManagedMemory, Int)`       ⇒ slot 0's outright bit
	//   • `t (BufArray, Int)`              ⇒ slot 0's ELEMENT bit
	//   • `t ((__ManagedMemory, Int), Int)` ⇒ slot 0's slot 0's outright bit
	//
	// The last three are ONE rule, not three: a tuple slot is a declared position, so it gets whatever a
	// declared position gets, by the same function calling itself. Nothing here knows how deep it is.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF THE TOKENS, BEFORE `parseTypeReference` RUNS, FOR THE REASON A2j STATES ABOVE** —
	// and the tuple depth is why the tree cannot be recovered afterwards even in principle: two tuple types
	// that differ only in a slot's spelling canonicalize to ONE interned name with ONE layout, so by the time
	// a `MaxonType` exists the difference is not merely erased, it does not exist.
	//
	// ⚠ **A PLAIN POSITION ALLOCATES NOTHING** — the cheap whole-spelling test runs first and hands back the
	// shared empty tree, so the overwhelming majority of declared positions in any program cost one token
	// test and one `Set.contains` on an empty set. Only a position that actually names the buffer builds.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE ELEMENT BIT IS THE ONE ARM THAT CONSULTS A REGISTRY, AND AT A TUPLE SLOT THAT REGISTRY MAY BE
	// HALF-BUILT — WHICH IS WHAT `slotElementsFromAliases` IS FOR.** The tolerant declaration SWEEP reads this
	// while `bufferElementAliases` is still filling, so a slot's element bit read there is not a fact about
	// the program, it is *how far the sweep had got*; and the sweep's copy is the one the whole-program index
	// stores. ⇒ A door the sweep also feeds (a struct FIELD, a RETURN clause) passes `false`; a door only the
	// REAL parse reaches (a parameter — the sweep skips a parameter list wholesale through
	// `skipBalancedParens`) passes `true`, where the index is complete and `bindParameters` consumes the
	// answer in the same pass.
	//
	// ⚠ **MEASURED, REVIEW OF A2m** — the identical TWO-FILE program compiled when the file declaring
	// `typealias BufArray = Array with __ManagedMemory` sorted FIRST and was refused E2015 when it sorted
	// LAST: `function make() returns (BufArray, Int)` with `make().0.get(0).length()`. A member roster that
	// depends on file order is a wrong answer in one of the two orders, whichever one that is.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AND THE ROOT IS THE EXACT OPPOSITE CASE — IT ALWAYS ASKS, BECAUSE ITS ANSWER IS REPAIRED AT EVERY
	// READ DOOR BY A DERIVATION THAT IS COMPLEMENTARY BY CONSTRUCTION.** `ProgramSignatures.fieldSurfaceOf`,
	// `declaredReturnSurface` and `payloadSurfaceOf` all OR in `derivedAliasSurfaceBits`, which reads the alias
	// NAME off a swept type that is still `named` — and a swept type is still `named` EXACTLY when
	// `parseTypeReference`'s generic-alias arm found no alias, i.e. exactly when this token test finds none
	// either. Both conditions are `recordGenericAlias`'s one write (`genericAliases`, `bufferElementAliases`
	// and `bufferSurfaceAliases`, adjacent lines), so the two halves cover the two orders between them and
	// their UNION is order-independent. ⚠ Suppressing the root here — which this review did, briefly — breaks
	// that pair and makes `var bufs as BufArray` / `returns BufArray` themselves order-dependent; measured, the
	// same two-file program then flipped the other way. A SLOT has no such second half, which is the whole of
	// why the two positions answer differently.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **BOTH ROOT BITS RIDE THAT PAIR, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM DID UNTIL W49.** The read-door derivation
	// carried the ELEMENT bit alone, so a root spelled with an alias FOR the buffer —
	// `typealias ByteBuffer = __ManagedMemory with Byte`, then `var buf as ByteBuffer` — had only the stored
	// token half, which is empty for every alias its own file declares (`recordGenericAlias` runs at
	// `foldFile`). MEASURED: the field, the RETURN clause and the union PAYLOAD each refused `length`, the
	// buffer roster's first member, while a PARAMETER spelled with the same alias compiled and RAN — because a
	// parameter's surface is read in the REAL parse, where the registry is complete. The token test below is
	// unchanged; what changed is that its read-door twin now answers for the same two bits it does.
	function declaredSurfaceAt(index TokenIndex, slotElementsFromAliases bool) returns DeclaredSurface
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.leftParen 'notATuple'
			return self.rootLeafSurfaceAt(index)
		end 'notATuple'

		if not self.tupleNamesBufferAt(index, slotElementsFromAliases: slotElementsFromAliases) 'namesTheBufferNowhere'
			return sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
		end 'namesTheBufferNowhere'

		var surface = DeclaredSurface.create()
		self.appendSurfaceNodesAt(surface, index: index, slotElementsFromAliases: slotElementsFromAliases)
		return surface
	end 'declaredSurfaceAt'

	// The whole surface of a NON-TUPLE declared position: one node, or the shared empty tree when it names the
	// buffer nowhere. It always reads the alias registry — see `declaredSurfaceAt`'s ⚠⚠ block for why the ROOT
	// may and a SLOT may not.
	function rootLeafSurfaceAt(index TokenIndex) returns DeclaredSurface
		return surfaceOfRootBits(self.leafSurfaceNodeAt(index, elementFromAliases: true))
	end 'rootLeafSurfaceAt'

	// Does the TUPLE type written at `open` name the buffer anywhere inside it? The cheap gate that keeps a
	// plain declaration allocation-free, and it is EXACT rather than an over-approximation — it asks the very
	// leaf test the builder below asks, under the very same `slotElementsFromAliases`, so a tree of all-clear
	// nodes can never be built and stored as a non-plain surface (which would spuriously refuse an overload
	// set through `requireOverloadableName`).
	//
	// It scans the whole balanced parenthesized range at once rather than walking element boundaries: the
	// question is "anywhere", so where the boundaries fall does not matter and the scan needs no bookkeeping
	// and no allocation.
	function tupleNamesBufferAt(open TokenIndex, slotElementsFromAliases bool) returns bool
		var depth = 0
		var at = open
		while at < self.tokens.count() 'scanTuple'
			let kind = self.kindAt(at)
			if kind == TokenKind.leftParen 'openNested'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'openNested'

			if kind == TokenKind.rightParen 'closeNested'
				depth = depth - 1
				if depth == 0 'endOfThisTuple'
					return false
				end 'endOfThisTuple'
			end 'closeNested'

			if self.leafSurfaceNodeAt(at, elementFromAliases: slotElementsFromAliases) != 0 'namesIt'
				return true
			end 'namesIt'

			at = at + 1
		end 'scanTuple'

		return false
	end 'tupleNamesBufferAt'

	// Does the token at `index` name an array-of-buffers alias, AND is this a position that may ask? THE one
	// consultation of `bufferElementAliases` from a declared spelling, so the gate and the leaf builder cannot
	// come to hold different opinions about a position's right to the registry — which is exactly the shape
	// that made a slot's answer file-order dependent (see `declaredSurfaceAt`).
	// ⚠ **`allFilesFolded` JOINS `elementFromAliases` AS A SECOND GATE (W49)**, and it says the same thing one
	// scope wider: `elementFromAliases` refuses the registry at a sweep-fed SLOT, and this refuses it in the
	// sweep at ALL positions. Both exist because a registry read during the sweep reports how far the sweep had
	// got; see `spellingDenotesBufferSurface` for the measurement and for why the root can now afford it.
	function spellingAtNamesBufferElements(index TokenIndex, elementFromAliases bool) returns bool
		if not elementFromAliases or not self.signatures.allFilesFolded 'sweepFedSlotOrSweepItself'
			return false
		end 'sweepFedSlotOrSweepItself'

		return self.signatures.aliasElementIsBufferSurface(self.identifierNameAt(index), readerFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'spellingAtNamesBufferElements'

	// Append the pre-order nodes of the type written at `index` to `out`. A tuple pushes its own node — whose
	// payload is only its SLOT COUNT, since a tuple record denotes nothing itself — and then each element's
	// whole subtree, in order. Everything else is one leaf node carrying the two spelling bits.
	//
	// Every leaf it reaches is a tuple SLOT — `declaredSurfaceAt` answers a non-tuple position without coming
	// here at all — which is what makes `slotElementsFromAliases` the right question to carry down.
	function appendSurfaceNodesAt(out DeclaredSurface, index TokenIndex, slotElementsFromAliases bool)
		if self.kindAt(index) != TokenKind.leftParen 'leafType'
			out.push(self.leafSurfaceNodeAt(index, elementFromAliases: slotElementsFromAliases))
			return
		end 'leafType'

		let starts = self.tupleElementStartsAt(index)
		out.push(tupleSurfaceNode(starts.count()))
		for start in starts 'eachElement'
			self.appendSurfaceNodesAt(out, index: start, slotElementsFromAliases: slotElementsFromAliases)
		end 'eachElement'
	end 'appendSurfaceNodesAt'

	// The two spelling bits of a non-tuple type position, in one node. A node of 0 is "names the buffer
	// nowhere", which is what both the gate above and `rootLeafSurfaceAt` test for — one decoder, so the gate
	// and the builder cannot disagree about what counts.
	function leafSurfaceNodeAt(index TokenIndex, elementFromAliases bool) returns DeclaredSurfaceNode
		var node = 0 as DeclaredSurfaceNode
		if self.typeSpellingAtDenotesBuffer(index) 'spelledTheAlias'
			node = node or SurfaceBitIsBuffer
		end 'spelledTheAlias'

		if self.spellingAtNamesBufferElements(index, elementFromAliases: elementFromAliases) 'namedAnArrayOfBuffers'
			node = node or SurfaceBitElementIsBuffer
		end 'namedAnArrayOfBuffers'

		return node
	end 'leafSurfaceNodeAt'

	// The token index each top-level element of the tuple type at `open` starts at. It counts PAREN DEPTH, so
	// a nested tuple's own commas belong to it and not to this list — which is exactly what makes the builder
	// above recurse correctly rather than flattening a nested tuple into its parent's slots.
	//
	// A malformed or unterminated type is not this walk's to report: it stops at the matching `)` or at the
	// end of the token array, and `parseTupleTypeReference` reports what is actually wrong a moment later.
	function tupleElementStartsAt(open TokenIndex) returns TokenIndexColumn
		var starts = TokenIndexColumn.create()
		starts.push((open + 1) as TokenIndex)
		var depth = 0
		var at = open
		while at < self.tokens.count() 'scanTuple'
			let kind = self.kindAt(at)
			if kind == TokenKind.leftParen 'openNested'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'openNested'

			if kind == TokenKind.rightParen 'closeNested'
				depth = depth - 1
				if depth == 0 'endOfThisTuple'
					return starts
				end 'endOfThisTuple'
			end 'closeNested'

			if kind == TokenKind.comma and depth == 1 'nextElement'
				starts.push((at + 1) as TokenIndex)
			end 'nextElement'

			at = at + 1
		end 'scanTuple'

		return starts
	end 'tupleElementStartsAt'

	// The RETURN clause's spelling, asked with the cursor ON `returns` — so the type token is the next one.
	// ⚠ **ONE reader, because BOTH passes ask it**: the tolerant declaration sweep (`recordScannedSignature`,
	// whose answer reaches every call site through the whole-program index) and the real parse
	// (`parseFunction`). That is exactly why `parseOptionalReturnType` is shared, and a second hand-written
	// peek beside it would be free to disagree the moment a return clause grows a second shape — silently,
	// since a wrong SURFACE is a wrong answer and not a compile error.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE BARE NAME THE `returns` CLAUSE WROTE — the CALL RESULT's half of P1.6-C's provenance.**
	// `returns DenseColumn` gives `DenseColumn`; `returns Array with Integer`, a primitive, or no clause at
	// all gives EMPTY, because none of those names an alias. Read at the same moment as
	// `returnDeclaredSurface` and for the same reason: the clause has not been consumed yet.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT ASKS NOTHING ABOUT THE NAME, and that is deliberate.** Whether the spelling is a generic alias
	// is a WHOLE-PROGRAM question and this runs inside the declaration sweep that answers it — asking here
	// is R-1's own first mistake one layer up. `recordCallResultInstanceAlias` asks, at parse time.
	// ⭐⭐ **A CALL RESULT CARRIES THE ALIAS ITS CALLEE'S SIGNATURE NAMED (P1.6-C, extended at R-1).**
	// `filledColumn(…) returns DenseColumn` hands back a gid that `DurationNanosArray` and 26 other stdlib
	// aliases share, so without this a diagnostic about the result can only fall back to the element RANGE.
	// It is `markDeclaredSurface`'s neighbour at the same door and reads the same kind of per-callee fact.
	//
	// ⚠ **PAID FOR ONLY WHERE IT CAN BE READ**, exactly as the container door's stamp is: the provenance is
	// consulted only when the gid has more than one author spelling, which R-1 settled before this parse
	// began. **AND ONLY HERE IS IT SAFE TO ASK WHETHER THE SPELLING IS AN ALIAS** — the sweep that answers
	// that is complete, which is why `returnAliasSpelling` below records the token's text and asks nothing.
	function recordCallResultInstanceAlias(result ValueId, callee ByteArray)
		if self.tagOf(result) != ValueTypeTag.genericInstance 'theResultIsNotAnInstance'
			return
		end 'theResultIsNotAnInstance'

		if not self.signatures.instanceIsMultiplyAliased(self.valueNameId(result)) 'oneAliasSpellsItForEveryone'
			return
		end 'oneAliasSpellsItForEveryone'

		let aliasName = self.signatures.returnAliasNameOf(callee)
		if not self.signatures.isGenericAlias(aliasName) 'theClauseNamedNoGenericAlias'
			return
		end 'theClauseNamedNoGenericAlias'

		self.recordValueInstanceAlias(result, aliasName: aliasName)
	end 'recordCallResultInstanceAlias'

	function returnAliasSpelling() returns ByteArray
		if not self.at(TokenKind.returns) 'noReturnClause'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'noReturnClause'

		let named = self.tokenAt(self.returnTypeTokenIndex())
		if not tokenCanBeAName(named) 'theClauseDoesNotOpenWithAWrittenNAME'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'theClauseDoesNotOpenWithAWrittenNAME'

		return named.value
	end 'returnAliasSpelling'

	function returnDeclaredSurface() returns DeclaredSurface
		if not self.at(TokenKind.returns) 'noReturnClause'
			return sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
		end 'noReturnClause'

		// `slotElementsFromAliases: false` — BOTH passes ask this and the SWEEP's copy is the one the
		// whole-program index stores, so a SLOT's element bit read here would be how far the sweep had got,
		// and a call's member roster would depend on file order. The ROOT's is safe and is left asking; see
		// `declaredSurfaceAt`'s two ⚠⚠ blocks for why the two positions differ.
		return self.declaredSurfaceAt(self.returnTypeTokenIndex(), slotElementsFromAliases: false)
	end 'returnDeclaredSurface'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE BRIDGE FROM A PRE-ERASURE SPELLING TO THE VALUE MARKS.** Several separate registries carry
	// the surface — a parameter's, a return's, a field's, a payload's — so several storage COLUMNS are
	// inherent. Several copies of the *decision* are not, and this is what keeps them at one.
	//
	// A2m made the surface a WORD, and this is where the word is unpacked onto the three carriers — so a door
	// that gains a depth gains it here, once, rather than at each binder.
	//
	// Its EIGHT call sites in SIX functions, DERIVED by grepping `self.markDeclaredSurface(` and not
	// remembered — `markBufferSurface`'s header records what copying such a sentence has cost five times:
	//   • a PARAMETER (`bindParameters`);
	//   • a union PAYLOAD binding (BATCH22);
	//   • a declared generic's FACTORY, from the alias name the call site wrote
	//     (`retypeGenericAliasConstructorResult`);
	//   • a FIELD READ (`emitFieldLoad`), which is THREE of them in one function — the field's own declared
	//     surface, the receiver's instance-argument spelling, and the per-value tuple-slot mask;
	//   • a CALL RESULT's declared return (`emitCall`);
	//   • a CALL RESULT's receiver instance-argument spelling (`retypeOpaqueReturnValue`).
	//
	// ⚠ **THE LAST TWO OF THOSE ARE NOT A DECLARED SPELLING AT ALL**, which is why this header no longer says
	// "declared": `Cell with __ManagedMemory` and `Cell with ByteArray` are one `GenericInstanceId`, so a
	// `returns U` read through either has NO declaration in the callee's file that could carry the answer.
	// See `slotSurfaceThroughReceiver`.
	function markDeclaredSurface(value ValueId, declared DeclaredSurface)
		if surfaceIsBuffer(declared) 'declaredTheAlias'
			self.markBufferSurface(value)
		end 'declaredTheAlias'

		if surfaceElementIsBuffer(declared) 'declaredAnArrayOfBuffers'
			self.markBufferSurfaceElements(value)
		end 'declaredAnArrayOfBuffers'

		self.markBufferSurfaceSlots(value, surface: declared)
	end 'markDeclaredSurface'

	// Does `value` denote a `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER rather than the `Array` around it? The read side of the
	// mark above — asked once per array-method dispatch, and `Set.contains` on the shared zero-capacity set
	// returns before it hashes anything, so a program that never names `__ManagedMemory` does not pay to ask.
	function valueIsBufferSurface(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.bufferSurfaceValues.contains(value)
	end 'valueIsBufferSurface'

	// Does `value` denote a SIZED container's own record (W192)? The read side of `markSizedContainerBuffer`,
	// asked once per array-method dispatch beside the line above and on the same terms: `Set.contains` on the
	// shared zero-capacity set returns before it hashes anything, so a program that names no sized container's
	// buffer does not pay to ask.
	function valueIsASizedContainersBuffer(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.sizedContainerBufferValues.contains(value)
	end 'valueIsASizedContainersBuffer'

	// ⭐ **THE SURFACE OF ELEMENT `slot` OF THE TUPLE `value` — A WHOLE SURFACE, NOT A BIT (A2m).** The read
	// side of `markBufferSurfaceSlots`, asked at `emitFieldLoad`, which every `p.0`, every destructured name
	// and every chain hop funnels through. Because a slot's payload is a surface like any other, `p.0` of a
	// `((__ManagedMemory, Int), Int)` binds a value carrying ITS slots — the recursion needs no case here, it
	// is the same line running again one container in.
	//
	// A value with no entry is plain, which is every tuple in a program that never writes the alias.
	function valueSlotSurface(value ValueId, slot FieldIndex) returns DeclaredSurface
		let surface = try self.bufferSurfaceSlots.get(value) otherwise sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
		if slot >= surfaceSlotCount(surface) 'noSuchMarkedSlot'
			return sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface
		end 'noSuchMarkedSlot'

		return surfaceSlot(surface, slot: slot)
	end 'valueSlotSurface'

	// Do the ELEMENTS of the array `value` denote buffers (A2m)? The read side of
	// `markBufferSurfaceElements`, asked at `emitContainerElementAccessor`.
	function valueElementIsBufferSurface(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.bufferSurfaceElements.contains(value)
	end 'valueElementIsBufferSurface'

	// ⭐ **A BARE FUNCTION NAME USED AS A VALUE → a `functionRef`** (P1.5-A1), the last resort a name
	// falls to after it is not a local, a self-field, a top-level constant or a `var`. It is what makes
	// `let f = double`, `apply(triple, x: 10)` and `return double` bind a function VALUE — a code pointer.
	//
	// It resolves through the WHOLE-PROGRAM signature index, so a FORWARD reference (a function declared
	// LATER in the file, the `.forward-reference-*` cases) resolves here at parse time exactly as a
	// backward one does — the sweep recorded every declaration before any file was parsed, which is the
	// shv2 thesis (no `unresolvedRead` placeholder, no TypeResolution rewrite pass). A name NO file
	// declares as a function is a genuine undefined variable (E2004), the answer this path used to give
	// unconditionally.
	//
	// A THROWING function is refused here (E3101): a function type has no throws clause, so the binding
	// would drop it and `indirectCall` has no error channel to carry it back. Refused at the point the
	// name becomes a value, with the callee's `throws E` type named.
	//
	// The value's type is `function(intern(name))` — the FUNCTION's own name governs its signature, which
	// is how an indirect call on it (`f(21)`) recovers the return type (`returnTypeOf(name)`).
	// ⭐ **THE RETURN HALF OF "A FUNCTION VALUE CARRIES ONE RESULT" (P1.7a slice 2)** — asked at the TWO
	// producers of a function value whose return type nothing declares: a named function referenced by name
	// (`emitFunctionRef`) and a closure literal, whose return type is INFERRED from its `gives` expression and
	// settled the moment that expression is parsed. Both are refused at the value's own line rather than at
	// the call, so the author is shown where the type came from — and both would otherwise reach the SAME
	// panic, `witnessOfValue` on a `callIndirect` result nothing could pair (MEASURED on both routes).
	function requireFunctionValueReturnHasRoom(returnType MaxonReturnType, tok Token) throws ParseError
		match returnType 'declaredReturn'
			void then break 'declaredReturn'
			value(inner) then try self.requireStorableDeclaredType(inner, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.functionValueReturnType, line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'declaredReturn'
	end 'requireFunctionValueReturnHasRoom'

	// ⭐ **MAY THE NAME `tok` SPELLS STAND AS A FUNCTION VALUE? — the whole of what separates a function
	// reference from an undefined variable, asked in one place.** Split out of `emitFunctionRef` because a
	// SECOND site asks the identical question about a name it will not immediately emit: a function-backed
	// enum case (`Parser.checkFunctionBackedCase`), which must refuse an unusable target at the DECLARATION
	// even in a program that never reads the enum. The gates are the header's, unchanged.
	function requireNameIsUsableAsFunctionValue(tok Token) throws ParseError
		match self.signatures.returnTypeOf(tok.value, into: self.artifact.typeNames) 'callee'
			// ⭐ **A FUNCTION VALUE CARRIES ONE RESULT, SO A FUNCTION THAT USES THE SECONDARY RETURN SLOT CANNOT
			// BE ONE** — both disjuncts of `functionUsesSecondaryReturnSlot`, refused here at the one door a
			// named function becomes a value through. The THROWING half has been refused just below since P1.4b;
			// the INTERFACE-RETURNING half is the same ABI fact (P1.7a slice 2) and would otherwise reach
			// `witnessOfValue` with a call result nothing paired — MEASURED, `let f = make` then `f(42)`.
			declared(returnType) then try self.requireFunctionValueReturnHasRoom(returnType, tok: tok)
			undeclared then throw ParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(tok.value), line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
		end 'callee'

		match self.signatures.throwsOf(tok.value) 'throwsClause'
			throwing(errorType) then throw ParseError.throwingFunctionAsValue(String.from(tok.value), errorType: errorType, line: tok.line, column: tok.column)
			none then break 'throwsClause'
		end 'throwsClause'
	end 'requireNameIsUsableAsFunctionValue'

	function emitFunctionRef(tok Token) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **THE EXPRESSION-POSITION HALF OF E2060 (W72).** This is the LAST-RESORT door for a bare name
		// READ — not a local, not a captured binding, not a global, not a constant — so a `__line__` whose
		// form is a plain identifier READ arrives here and nowhere earlier, and a name that DID bind is never
		// asked. Placed here rather than at the top of the primary parse for exactly that reason: the check
		// then costs nothing on the path every identifier in every program takes, and runs only on a program
		// already destined for a diagnostic.
		//
		// ⚠ **"A BARE NAME READ" IS NARROWER THAN "AN ORDINARY EXPRESSION", WHICH THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY
		// (W72 review).** `parsePrimary` splits the four forms an identifier can open one token before this
		// door, so the CALL, DOTTED and STRUCT-LITERAL forms never reach it and are refused under their own
		// codes. See `requireNotACallerLocationDefault`'s header for the measured table and for why the codes
		// are not brought into line here.
		try requireNotACallerLocationDefault(tok.value, at: tok)
		try self.requireNameIsUsableAsFunctionValue(tok)

		let result = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.function, nameId: self.artifact.typeNames.intern(tok.value))
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.functionRef(result, funcName: tok.value), range: SourceRange.ofToken(tok))
		return result
	end 'emitFunctionRef'

	// ⭐ **PARSE A CLOSURE LITERAL, LIFTING A NON-CAPTURING ONE TO A TOP-LEVEL FUNCTION (P1.5-A2a).**
	//
	// `function(p1 T1, p2 T2) gives <expr>` is an anonymous function written inline. A closure that
	// captures NOTHING is semantically a plain function reference — it binds no environment to the
	// enclosing frame — so it lifts to an ordinary top-level function `<enclosing>$closure_<k>` and yields
	// the SAME `functionRef` value A1 mints for a named function. Everything downstream (the indirect-call
	// lowering, `funcAddr` → `leaRegFunc`/`callReg`) is then A1's, unchanged — this rung adds NO IR op and
	// NO backend path. A closure that captures an enclosing local is refused (A2b); the DETECTION is built
	// here as the seam (`parseVariableReference`/`parseSelfPrimary` check `closureCaptureScopes`).
	//
	// The body parses into a FRESH function context — its own scope, value numbering, blocks and type
	// columns — swapped in around the parse and restored verbatim after, so the enclosing function's parse
	// is undisturbed. The lift is v1's `parseClosure` (Parser.maxon:17203) shorn of its env/capture
	// machinery (`closureCreate`/`closureEnvLoad`/`__env`), which is A2b's; the state save/restore is v1's,
	// adapted to shv2's field set (its `currentFunc`/`inClosure`/`closureOuterScope` become the swapped
	// per-function columns plus the `closureCaptureScopes` stack, and the `funcCount()`-keyed ValueOrigin
	// needs the `currentFuncIndex` re-stamp that v1's index model did not).
	// A closure parameter declared at an interface type, refused through the SAME door the other seven
	// positions come through. It finds the offending parameter through the SAME scan the interface-requirement
	// reader uses — the two walked the identical `(paramTypes, paramRanges)` pair with their own copy of "is
	// this an existential", which agreed only by coincidence: widen what counts as one (an alias resolving to
	// an interface, a tuple holding one) and one arm would have kept firing while the other went quiet.
	function requireNoInterfaceClosureParam(paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, paramRanges SourceRangeArray) throws ParseError
		if let found = try firstExistentialParam(paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges) 'existentialParam'
			try self.requireStorableDeclaredType(found.declaredType, position: DeclaredStoragePosition.closureParameter, line: found.anchor.line, column: found.anchor.column)
		end 'existentialParam'
	end 'requireNoInterfaceClosureParam'

	function parseClosureExpression(func IrFunction) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// ⭐ **TAKE the call position's parameter-type offer — read it AND clear it (see `closureParamHint`).**
		// Clearing here is what scopes the offer to THIS literal: a closure nested inside this one's body sees
		// `none`, so `xs.map(function(x) gives apply(function(y) gives y, to: x))` cannot silently type `y` as
		// the OUTER container's element. The clear runs one line after the read, with nothing between that can
		// throw, so the alias discipline the save block below states holds here too.
		let paramTypeHint = self.closureParamHint
		self.closureParamHint = ClosureParamTypeHint.none

		let functionTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.function)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)

		// Parse the parameters through the shared `parseFunctionParameters` — `name Type` pairs, the
		// no-default rule and the reserved-name check all apply identically. (The ARGUMENT-COUNT ceiling is
		// NOT among them and deliberately so: only a caller knows how many hidden slots its own ABI adds, so
		// each asks `requireAbiParamCount` itself — see that routine's header.) `_` is
		// a legal identifier the check accepts (the corpus's `function(_ Integer) gives …`). An UNTYPED
		// parameter (`function(p) gives …`) is typed from `paramTypeHint` when the call position supplied one
		// and refused with a positioned diagnostic when it did not — `parameterTypeOrHinted`, which is where
		// the one position that supplies one is named.
		var paramNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		var paramTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		var paramRanges = SourceRangeArray.create()
		// A closure parameter may spell `__ManagedMemory` exactly as a function's may (A2j), and may be declared
		// at a parameterized-interface type exactly as a function's may (W57) — it reaches the same
		// `parseFunctionParameters` and the same `bindParameters`, so neither needs separate treatment.
		var paramSurfaces = DeclaredSurfaceArray.create()
		var paramTypeStarts = TokenIndexColumn.create()
		try self.parseFunctionParameters(paramNames, paramTypes: paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges, paramSurfaces: paramSurfaces, paramTypeStarts: paramTypeStarts, owner: ParamDefaultOwner.closureLiteral, paramTypeHint: paramTypeHint)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)
		// A struct parameter is re-tagged `structRef` here, exactly as `parseFunction` does — the whole-program
		// struct registry is complete in the real parse (see `resolveNamedStruct`).
		paramTypes = self.resolveNamedStructAll(paramTypes)
		// ⭐⭐ **A CLOSURE PARAMETER MAY NOT BE DECLARED AT AN INTERFACE TYPE, AND IT PANICKED THE PARSER**
		// (P1.7a-existentials slice 1, fourth review round). A value held at an interface is a two-word fat
		// pointer, and the second word travels as an ADJACENT HIDDEN ARGUMENT that only a real function's
		// signature reserves. A lifted closure's parameters are bound before any witness is paired, so
		// `witnessOfValue` found none and PANICKED with no source position at all — measured on
		// `function(t Shape) gives t.area()`, tip and control alike.
		// Round 3 turned three sibling producers into positioned refusals and this was the fourth, missed
		// because a closure's parameters are parsed by a different door than a function's. Refused rather than
		// paired: the uniform `(userargs, env)` indirect ABI carries one machine word per argument, so a fat
		// pointer would need a second slot the shape has nowhere to put.
		try self.requireNoInterfaceClosureParam(paramTypes, paramRanges: paramRanges)
		// The lifted closure's ABI count is its own parameters PLUS the trailing `__env` slot reserved below —
		// so the ceiling is asked of `+ 1`. Asked HERE rather than in the shared `parseFunctionParameters` for
		// the reason `requireAbiParamCount`'s header gives: only the caller knows how many hidden slots its own
		// ABI adds, and a check that did not would be the weak second count all over again.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ AND IT IS ASKED **BEFORE** THE CONTEXT SAVE BELOW, WHICH IS A HARD REQUIREMENT, NOT AN ORDERING
		// PREFERENCE. Each of the ~25 `savedX` locals ALIASES the field it was read from — one object, two
		// names, no incref — until that field is re-pointed at a fresh object. A call that can THROW while an
		// alias is live gives the enclosing frame a failure edge through it, and the drop the throw edge owes
		// for the alias releases an object the field still holds. Placed there, this check made every closure
		// program die compiling with `mm_decref: refcount underflow` inside `__destruct_Parser` — a compiler
		// crash, on programs with SIX parameters, from a check about sixty-five.
		//
		// ⛔ **THE PROTECTED WINDOW IS FIRST SAVE → LAST REASSIGNMENT, *NOT* SAVE → RESTORE.** Getting that
		// boundary right is the whole of the rule, and the two are not the same span: `bindParameters`,
		// `parseExpression` and `emitOwnedValueReturn` all sit between the saves and the restore and all
		// throw — they MUST, since that is how a syntax error inside a closure body is reported. They are
		// safe because by then every managed field has been re-pointed (`self.scope = Scope.create()` and
		// the block below it), so each `savedX` uniquely owns its object and the throw edge releases it
		// exactly once. **So: add nothing that can throw between the first `let savedX =` and the last
		// `self.X =` of that block — and when adding a new saved field, put its reassignment inside that
		// block rather than after the parse.** The fields with no reassignment (`lastFieldRead*`,
		// `closureEnvParamId`, `artifact.currentFuncIndex`) are scalars: copying one aliases nothing.
		try requireAbiParamCount(paramNames.count() + 1, anchor: functionTok)
		let givesTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.gives)

		// The lifted name — `<enclosing>$closure_<k>`. `$` is unwritable in a Maxon identifier, so the name
		// can collide with no user function, alias, or OTHER closure (the counter is per enclosing
		// function). Post-increment so sibling closures get 0, 1, 2, … in source order.
		let k = func.closureSubCounter
		func.closureSubCounter = k + 1
		let closureName = "{String.from(func.name)}$closure_{k}".toByteArray()

		// --- Switch into the lifted function's own parse context. Every per-function column is captured
		//     here and restored verbatim below; the closure gets fresh ones so the enclosing function's SSA
		//     numbering, scope, drop sets and blocks are untouched. ---
		let savedScope = self.scope
		let savedValueIds = self.valueIds
		let savedMutableVars = self.mutableVars
		let savedMutableVarShadowed = self.mutableVarShadowed
		let savedMutableVarInnermost = self.mutableVarInnermost
		let savedLoops = self.loops
		let savedMatches = self.matches
		// ⚠ **SAVED AND RESTORED, NOT MERELY RESET — a closure is the ONE re-entrant door into this reset
		// block.** `parseFunction` and `parseDefaultHelperBody` reach a fresh function from a drained
		// top level, so a reset with no restore is right there; this routine resumes the ENCLOSING body
		// afterwards, and a closure written INSIDE a block-form `try` body would otherwise return to it
		// with an empty stack — the body's remaining throwing calls stop routing and its `end` panics on
		// the pop. `loops`/`matches` two lines up carry exactly this pairing for exactly this reason.
		let savedTryBlocks = self.tryBlocks
		let savedRoutingSuppressed = self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed
		// A closure body is a DIFFERENT `IrFunction`, so an enclosing handler's error-union binding and its
		// E3084 obligation must not be visible inside it — a `match e` there would name a fused phi from the
		// function the closure was written in. See the fields for the measured shape this neighbourhood has.
		let savedErrorUnionBindings = self.errorUnionBindings
		let savedTryBindingName = self.tryBindingName
		let savedTryBindingMatched = self.tryBindingMatched
		let savedCurrentBlock = self.currentBlock
		let savedValueTags = self.valueTags
		let savedValueNameIds = self.valueNameIds
		let savedValueConsts = self.valueConsts
		let savedValueConstDomains = self.valueConstDomains
		let savedValueOwnsHeap = self.valueOwnsHeap
		let savedValueOwnsHeapAlone = self.valueOwnsHeapAlone
		let savedValuePromiseMarks = self.valuePromiseMarks
		let savedNodeHandleMintedFrom = self.nodeHandleMintedFrom
		let savedPhiAliasUnprovenMarks = self.phiAliasUnprovenMarks
		let savedOwnedBindings = self.ownedBindings
		let savedUnusedCandidates = self.unusedCandidates
		let savedMintedValueAliases = self.functionMintedValueAliases
		let savedReferencedNames = self.referencedNames
		let savedMutabilityExercisedNames = self.mutabilityExercisedNames
		let savedPendingTempDrops = self.pendingTempDrops
		// The last-field-read record is ONE fact spread over FOUR fields, and ALL FOUR are saved/restored as
		// a unit — not `lastFieldReadValid` alone. The closure body opens with `lastFieldReadValid = false`,
		// but if it reads a `named` field it overwrites `lastFieldReadValue`/`Box`/`Offset` with ids in the
		// CLOSURE's SSA space; restoring only `Valid` would leave the enclosing function believing its live
		// field read is a value/box the CLOSURE numbered. The mechanism's whole safety argument (an equal
		// value id can only have come from this exact `(box, offset)`) holds only WITHIN one function's SSA
		// numbering, which a nested closure breaks — so a `throw <named field>` after the closure could
		// mis-fire `moveOutThrownField` on a wrong slot (a closure id that collides with an enclosing one).
		let savedLastFieldReadValid = self.lastFieldReadValid
		let savedLastFieldReadValue = self.lastFieldReadValue
		let savedLastFieldReadBox = self.lastFieldReadBox
		let savedLastFieldReadOffset = self.lastFieldReadOffset
		let savedInMatchArmBody = self.inMatchArmBody
		let savedFuncIndex = self.artifact.currentFuncIndex
		// The two closure columns and the capture-collection set are per-function and per-CLOSURE, so they
		// swap with the rest of the context — a nested closure collects its own captures without disturbing
		// the enclosing closure's.
		let savedValueIsCapturingClosure = self.valueIsCapturingClosure
		let savedValueClosureEnv = self.valueClosureEnv
		let savedValueWitnessIds = self.valueWitnessIds
		let savedValueInstanceAlias = self.valueInstanceAlias
		let savedValueExistentialInstance = self.valueExistentialInstance
		let savedValueDeclaredAlias = self.valueDeclaredAlias
		// ⛔⛔ **THE CHARACTER-LITERAL OP MAP SWAPS WITH ITS SIBLINGS, AND IT WAS RESET WITHOUT BEING SAVED
		// (BATCH23 review).** It was added to the RESET below and to `parseFunction`'s two resets, but not to
		// this save/restore pair — so a closure erased the ENCLOSING function's filed op indices, and its own
		// entries were left behind in their place. Both halves of that are wrong answers on legal programs,
		// MEASURED:
		//
		//   • `'0' + apply(function(n Integer) gives n + 1, x: 1)` — the outer literal is parsed BEFORE the
		//     closure and coerced AFTER it (`parseBinary` reads the left operand's span once the right is in
		//     hand), so `retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint` found no entry and PANICKED the compiler.
		//   • `'A' + apply(function(n Integer) gives n + '0', x: 1)` — worse, because a closure's value ids are
		//     a FRESH SSA space that COLLIDES with the enclosing function's: the lookup HIT the closure's entry,
		//     rewrote the CLOSURE's op, and left the outer literal a `stringLiteral`. It printed 5368717386 —
		//     the record's `.rdata` ADDRESS added as a number — where 114 is correct, with no diagnostic.
		//
		// The invariant is the one `CharLiteralOpMap`'s header already states ("SWAPPED across a closure body,
		// exactly like the sibling value columns"); this is what makes it true.
		let savedCharLiteralOps = self.charLiteralOps
		let savedStringBytesViewOps = self.stringBytesViewOps
		// ⛔⛔ **THE SAME DEFECT, TWICE MORE — FOUND BY SWEEPING THE RESET LIST AGAINST THIS ONE (BATCH23
		// review).** Both are RESET below, correctly, and neither was restored, and each is a compiler PANIC
		// on an ordinary program. They are listed here rather than beside their resets because the reset is
		// not what was wrong.
		//
		//   • `iterationLockedBindings` is the enclosing function's `for..in` lock STACK, popped by
		//     `releaseIterationSource` with a panic when it is empty. Emptied by a closure, the enclosing
		//     loop's own release had nothing to pop — MEASURED: `for x in a` with ANY closure in the body,
		//     *"the iteration lock stack is empty"*.
		//   • `declaringWitnessParamBase`/`Count` (then spelled `current*`) say where the enclosing function's
		//     `where`-constraint witness parameters sit. Zeroed by a closure, the first witness dispatch AFTER
		//     it found none — MEASURED inside `type Holder uses T where T is Named`: a closure then
		//     `self.item.label()` panicked in `witnessParamValueId`, while the SAME two statements in the other
		//     order compile and print 42. The ORDER being the whole of it is what kept this invisible.
		//
		//     ⭐⭐ **THAT PAIR HAS SINCE LEFT THIS TRIPLE ENTIRELY (W58), and the SECOND fix is the interesting
		//     one: adding them here cured the dispatch AFTER a closure and left the dispatch INSIDE one still
		//     panicking, because a reset is the wrong answer to "what witnesses does a closure body see?".
		//     They now describe the DECLARING function throughout, are written only where one begins
		//     (`parseFunction` / `parseDefaultHelperBody`), and a closure body inherits its table through its
		//     ENVIRONMENT instead (`witnessValueForConstraint`). Nothing reachable from an expression writes
		//     them, so there is nothing here to save. See the reset block below, and the field header.**
		//
		// ⚠ **AND THE ROOT CAUSE IS THAT THIS LIST IS WRITTEN THREE TIMES.** The columns that make up one
		// function's parse context are spelled in the `Parser.create` initializer, in `parseFunction`'s reset,
		// in the synthesized-helper reset, and in this save/reset/restore TRIPLE — four places, with nothing
		// that makes them agree. Every miss is silent at the compiler's own build and shows up only as a
		// panic or a wrong answer on a user program, which is how three of them shipped at once.
		let savedIterationLockedBindings = self.iterationLockedBindings
		let savedImmutableGlobalReads = self.immutableGlobalReads
		let savedMayReturnAGlobalAlias = self.enclosingFunctionMayReturnAGlobalAlias
		let savedCapturedImmutableGlobalReads = self.capturedImmutableGlobalReads
		let savedMergeImmutableBlame = self.mergeImmutableBlame
		let savedRebindableSlotReads = self.rebindableSlotReads
		let savedConstructorFeedParams = self.constructorFeedParams
		let savedRecordsOwningOpaqueSlots = self.recordsOwningOpaqueSlots
		let savedBufferSurfaceValues = self.bufferSurfaceValues
		let savedBufferSurfaceSlots = self.bufferSurfaceSlots
		let savedBufferSurfaceElements = self.bufferSurfaceElements
		let savedSizedContainerBufferValues = self.sizedContainerBufferValues
		let savedCaptureNames = self.captureNames
		let savedCaptureValues = self.captureValues
		let savedCaptureTypes = self.captureTypes
		let savedInheritedWitnessCaptures = self.inheritedWitnessCaptures
		// ⛔⛔ **THE LAYOUT-DESCRIPTOR TRIPLE WAS IN NONE OF THE THREE LISTS, AND THAT WAS A COMPILER PANIC
		// (W58).** `functionCarriesLayoutParam` is "does the function being EMITTED carry the hidden
		// `sizeof(T)` descriptor parameter" — the layout twin of the witness question one door over — and it
		// stood at the ENCLOSING method's answer for the whole of a closure body. A lifted closure reserves no
		// descriptor whatever its author reserved, so `coOwnBorrowedOpaque` and `emitDecref` skipped the W10
		// disagreement they exist to record and emitted `__retain_type_param` / `__drop_type_param` into a body
		// with no descriptor to read.
		// **MEASURED: `panic at LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:2163: appendTypeParamDescriptor: `__retain_type_param` in
		// 'Box.pick$closure_0' but the function carries no layout descriptor parameter`**, on a `pick()` whose
		// only sin was reading `sizeof(T)` (which is what reserves the descriptor) beside
		// `function(v T) gives v`. Without the `sizeof` the SAME program is refused with a position, so the
		// enclosing method being BETTER served is what turned the refusal into a crash.
		// The two accumulator flags swap with it because they are its answer: they are per-function facts read
		// at a function's end, and the closure now asks its own question at its own end (below).
		let savedCarriesLayoutParam = self.functionCarriesLayoutParam
		let savedDroppedOpaqueWithoutDescriptor = self.functionDroppedOpaqueWithoutDescriptor
		let savedStoredOpaqueWithoutDescriptor = self.functionStoredOpaqueWithoutDescriptor
		let savedClosureEnvParamId = self.closureEnvParamId
		// PBR-2: the closure body computes its OWN cell-resident local names (`collectCellLocalNames`, below), so
		// the enclosing function's set has to come back with the rest of its parse state — otherwise every `var`
		// declared after the closure would be tested against the closure's answer.
		let savedCellLocalNames = self.cellLocalNames
		// ⭐⭐ **THE RANGE-CHECK ACCUMULATORS SWAP TOO, AND THEY DID NOT UNTIL THE P1.9 REVIEW.** They are
		// per-function for the reason every column above is: a site names a ValueId AND a BlockId in the
		// SSA/CFG space of the function being parsed, and a closure gets its OWN `IrFunction` with its own
		// dense numbering. Left shared, a `x as Small` inside a closure body appended to the ENCLOSING
		// function's list, and `InsertRangeChecks` then resolved that site inside the ENCLOSING function —
		// where the same numbers name completely unrelated things.
		//
		// ⚠ MEASURED, both directions, against `maxon-sharp`: `apply(function(n Wide) gives (n - j) as Small,
		// x: 605)` with `j = 600` is a program in which every value is in range — the bootstrap prints `r=5`
		// and exits 0 — and shv2 PANICKED, because the guard landed in `main` and read `main`'s `j` (600)
		// through the id the closure had numbered. A correct program killed at run time. The dual is worse in
		// a quieter way: the closure's OWN cast got no guard at all, so an out-of-range value inside a closure
		// passed unchecked where the bootstrap panics `in _$closure_0`.
		//
		// The field header's note that the mis-attribution was "unreachable today" was true of a corpus with
		// two doors and is not a property of the mechanism; P1.9 took it to six, and a struct literal or a
		// field store inside a closure body is ordinary code.
		let savedRangeCheckSites = self.rangeCheckSites
		let savedRangeCheckArgSites = self.rangeCheckArgSites
		let savedRangeCheckReturnSites = self.rangeCheckReturnSites
		let savedRangeCheckReturnAlias = self.rangeCheckReturnAlias
		let savedRangeCheckParamSites = self.rangeCheckParamSites

		let paramCount = paramNames.count()
		// ⭐ **RESERVE THE TRAILING `__env` PARAMETER'S ValueId UP FRONT (P1.5-A2b).** A capturing closure
		// receives its environment as a trailing parameter (`closureEnvParamId = paramCount`), and every
		// capture read loads through it — so its id must be fixed BEFORE the body parses, which is BEFORE we
		// know whether the closure captures. So the slot is always reserved: value numbering starts at
		// `paramCount + 1` and the value columns carry one extra entry for it. Whether the closure actually
		// captures is settled AFTER the body parse (`captureNames` non-empty), and only then is the reserved
		// slot promoted to a real parameter (`lifted.paramCount` patched). A non-capturing closure leaves the
		// slot an unused hole — no op references it, so it is dead and the lifted body is A2a-identical.
		//
		// A FRESH scope (not `scope.reset()`) so the enclosing one survives in `savedScope` to be restored;
		// every other per-body column is re-created exactly as `parseFunction` re-creates it (a stray entry
		// carried into the closure body would name a value in the ENCLOSING body's SSA space). This mirrors
		// `parseFunction`'s reset rather than sharing a helper with it: extracting the reassignments into a
		// method that stores into ~11 aliased `self` array-fields tripped a bootstrap ownership crash
		// (`mm_decref` in the compiler itself), so the two copies stay inline and the reviewer's
		// duplication note is answered here rather than by a helper the bootstrap cannot compile.
		self.scope = Scope.create()
		self.valueIds = ValueIdRef.create((paramCount + 1) as ParamCount)
		self.mutableVars = VarInfoArray.create()
		self.mutableVarShadowed = sharedEmptyMutableVarShadowed
		self.mutableVarInnermost = sharedEmptyMutableVarInnermost
		self.ownedBindings = VarInfoArray.create()
		// A closure declares its own parameters and its body sees none of the enclosing function's bindings,
		// so it owes its own E3012 accounting — drained under its own name below, before the restore.
		self.unusedCandidates = UnusedBindingCandidateArray.create()
		// Cleared for the same reason: whether the ENCLOSING body aliased a value says nothing about whether
		// this closure's own moves need the alias walk, and its SSA values are a separate space anyway.
		self.functionMintedValueAliases = false
		// Fresh for the same reason, and the CLEAR is the point: a closure's private local named `i` may not
		// excuse an `i` the ENCLOSING function declared and never mentioned. What DOES cross the boundary is
		// what the closure captured, pushed back at the restore below.
		self.referencedNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		// Cleared, and NOT re-seeded at the restore: capture is by VALUE, so a write inside the closure
		// reaches a copy and is no reason for the enclosing binding to stay a `var` — see the field.
		self.mutabilityExercisedNames = ByteArrayArray.create()
		self.pendingTempDrops = ValueIdArray.create()
		self.lastFieldReadValid = false
		self.loops = LoopContextArray.create()
		// Fresh per function, with its companion flag — see the `tryBlocks` field declaration for why.
		self.tryBlocks = TryBlockContextArray.create()
		self.tryBlockRoutingSuppressed = false
		self.matches = MatchContextArray.create()
		// ⚠ Balanced by construction — pushed and popped around each `for` body — EXCEPT on the error path,
		// where a `ParseError` thrown inside a body skips the release. So the reset is real; it points back
		// at the shared empty rather than allocating (see `sharedEmptyIterationLocks`).
		self.iterationLockedBindings = sharedEmptyIterationLocks
		self.valueTags = ValueTypeTagArray.create()
		self.valueNameIds = TypeNameIdArray.create()
		self.valueConsts = ParsedIntArray.create()
		self.valueConstDomains = ConstPayloadDomainArray.create()
		self.valueOwnsHeap = BoolArray.create()
		self.valueOwnsHeapAlone = BoolArray.create()
		self.valuePromiseMarks = BoolArray.create()
