	// `mintIndirectCallResult` types an indirect call's SSA RESULT from the same `functionAliasReturnType`, so
	// the result VALUE became `exitCode`-tagged too and a capture of it reached this store — W1 guarded the
	// SIGNATURE column and the VALUE column came along with it. The OTHER entrance is older:
	// `interfaceReturnMaxonType` has minted `exitCode` results since P1.7a, so an interface method
	// `returns ExitCode` whose result is captured panicked before W1 as well. Fixing either DOOR would have
	// left the other live, which is why the fix is at the SINK. Probed: struct-field assign, array push,
	// loop-carried var, plain return and direct call never reach a width door with a value's own tag.
	//
	// Pinned by `closure-capture.capture-exitcode-from-alias-call`, `.capture-exitcode-from-interface-method`
	// and `.capture-exitcode-wide-value` — the last because the first two carry `9`, which survives even a
	// 1-byte slot, so they pin THAT the capture works and not the width it works at.
	function wordSlotStorageType(declared MaxonType) returns MaxonType
		let stored = self.fieldStorageType(declared)

		return MaxonType.integer if maxonTypeTag(stored) == ValueTypeTag.exitCode else stored
	end 'wordSlotStorageType'

	// `globalAddr <label>` then `loadIndirect <addr> 0` — the two ops a global's read is.
	//
	// OFFSET 0, ALWAYS: each global is its OWN labelled slot, so the label already names the exact
	// address and there is nothing to add to it. The offset field is not dead weight — it is what
	// P1.1's `point.x` will carry, through this same op.
	//
	// ⭐ **A MANAGED GLOBAL'S LOAD IS A BORROW** (P1.7 slice 2a): the result is the record's ADDRESS, and
	// the `.data` slot keeps owning it, so the value is minted WITHOUT `markValueOwnsHeap` and nothing
	// drops it. That is not an omission — a scope-exit drop of a borrowed global would free the program's
	// only copy while the slot still points at it, and the cleanup after `main` would then double-free.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE SLOT'S DECLARED AND STORAGE TYPES DO *NOT* COINCIDE, AND THIS PAIR NOW ASKS FOR BOTH — the
	// moment S2j's header predicted, arrived.** That note recorded the history: it once claimed a global's
	// type is INFERRED FROM ITS INITIALIZER and so is never `named`, which expired when a top-level FACTORY
	// CALL landed; what kept the single type correct after that was a REFUSAL rather than an inference
	// (`requireFactoryReturnsARecord` rejects exactly the `named` return types,
	// `GlobalFactoryReturn.unbridgedRecord`), and it closed: *"If that refusal is ever lifted, this pair is
	// one of the doors that must start asking `fieldStorageType`."*
	//
	// ⇒ **The refusal was not lifted — a SECOND ROUTE opened past it.** A boxed union's payload-free case
	// written at file scope is MATERIALIZED by the compiler (`ConstFoldedValue.boxedUnionCase`), never
	// returned by a factory, so that refusal never sees it and its `named(<union>)` arrives here regardless.
	// The consequence was the one the note names: `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named` outright, because
	// the tag is the value's IDENTITY and its storage is a machine word. So the ACCESS width is now asked of
	// `fieldStorageType` — the split every other slot in the language already makes — while the VALUE stays
	// minted at its declared type, which is what lets a `match` on the loaded global recognize its union.
	//
	// It is byte-neutral for every type that reached here before: `slotStorageType` answers `declared` for a
	// String, a struct, an instance and every scalar, so only the arm that used to panic moves. The other two
	// doors the note lists — `ModuleInit`'s synthesized store and the cleanup's load — take
	// `declaredAggregateSlotWidth()` directly, which is the same answer reached from a stronger fact: a
	// MANAGED global's slot is a pointer whatever it points at.
	function emitGlobalLoad(label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let addr = self.emitGlobalAddr(label, range: range)
		return self.emitIndirectLoad(valueType, box: addr, offset: GlobalSlotOffset, storageType: self.fieldStorageType(valueType), range: range)
	end 'emitGlobalLoad'

	// `globalAddr <label>` then `storeIndirect <addr> 0 <value>` — a global's write, the mirror of
	// `emitGlobalLoad`. It defines no value: a store is a statement.
	function emitGlobalStore(label ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, value ValueId, range SourceRange)
		let addr = self.emitGlobalAddr(label, range: range)
		self.emitIndirectStore(addr, offset: GlobalSlotOffset, value: value, storageType: self.fieldStorageType(valueType), range: range)
	end 'emitGlobalStore'

	// The `globalAddr` half both a read and a write open with. A POINTER, so its value is typed
	// `integer` — the Maxon tier has no pointer type today, and an address is a machine word; the
	// Std tier it lowers into is typeless anyway, and nothing dispatches on this value's tag (it is
	// read only by the load/store that follows).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE A LAZY STATIC'S INITIALIZER IS FORCED** (spec-port `lazy-static`).
	// `specs/lazy-static.md` says a static field's initializer runs *"the first time the static field is
	// accessed"*, and this function is, exactly, every access: `emitGlobalLoad` and `emitGlobalStore` are its
	// only two callers and every read and every write of every global goes through one of them. Putting the
	// guard here rather than at either of them — still less at the six call sites above them — is what makes
	// "every access forces it" a property of the code instead of a claim about a list.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **A WRITE FORCES IT TOO — AND THAT IS A SEMANTIC CHOICE, NOT A CONSEQUENCE** (§6 review). It has
	// one mechanical half and one genuinely chosen half, and they should not be confused:
	//
	//   * **Mechanically required.** `State.set(s)` assigns a `static var` whose initializer may never have
	//     run, and the assignment path RELEASES the old value before storing the new one
	//     (`emitCheckedGlobalStore`, whose unconditional decref is safe BECAUSE of this guard — see its
	//     header). Without forcing, a first-access assignment decrefs a null slot.
	//   * **CHOSEN.** That the initializer's SIDE EFFECTS therefore run, for a static that is only ever
	//     written and never read, is shv2 reading `specs/lazy-static.md`'s *"the first time the static field
	//     is accessed"* as covering a write — canonical draws no read/write distinction, and a store IS an
	//     access to the field. A compiler could instead skip the initializer and store into the null slot
	//     without releasing; that would be a defensible reading too, and it is NOT the one shv2 takes.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ORACLE WAS MEASURED AND CANNOT ARBITRATE IT.** On the write-only-static program the C# bootstrap
	// does not compile at all: `error E9001: Unresolved global: Box.slot.__initialized`, out of
	// `X86CodeEmitter.ResolveGlobals` — an internal error, i.e. a defect on its own write-only path. So this
	// choice rests on the sentence, not on agreement with a reference. `specs-shv2/lazy-static-observable.md`
	// pins it as a choice.
	function emitGlobalAddr(label ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		self.emitLazyStaticInitGuard(label, range: range)
		let addr = self.mintValue(ValueTypeTag.integer, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.artifact.emitOp(self.currentBlock, op: MaxonOp.globalAddr(addr, label: label), range: range)
		return addr
	end 'emitGlobalAddr'

	// ⭐ **ONE `call __lazy_init$<label>` AHEAD OF THE ACCESS, OR NOTHING AT ALL.**
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE GUARD IS OUT OF LINE — THE BRANCH LIVES IN THE ROUTINE, NOT AT THE ACCESS — AND THAT IS A
	// DEPARTURE FROM BOTH REFERENCES, TAKEN ON THE EVIDENCE OF THE SPEC'S OWN PROSE.** v1 and the bootstrap
	// each emit the test INLINE at every load, and `specs/lazy-static.md`'s `two-loads-in-one-function` exists
	// because that shape produced an **endless loop** the second time one function read one static: its
	// already-initialized edge is a fall-through, so an init block laid between two guards branches back into
	// the first. Out of line, that failure mode does not exist — there is one branch per STATIC instead of one
	// per ACCESS, so it cannot be got wrong at the Nth site — and an access costs one op rather than a
	// diamond, which is also why this moves goldens by a single `call`.
	//
	// It buys an unconditional call where the references have a predicted-not-taken branch. That is a
	// constant-factor cost on a correctness-critical path, and it is the optimizer's to reclaim (inlining
	// `__lazy_init$…` restores the reference shape with the guard still written once).
	function emitLazyStaticInitGuard(label ByteArray, range SourceRange)
		match self.signatures.lazyStaticInitCalleeFor(label) 'lazy'
			none then break 'lazy'
			callee(name) then _ = self.emitRuntimeCall(name, args: ValueIdArray.create(), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'lazy'
	end 'emitLazyStaticInitGuard'

	// --- The top-level constant evaluator (P1.0d.5a) ---
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE GRAMMAR, TWO OUTPUTS.** Below is a second walk over the same expression syntax
	// `parseBinary` walks — and it is the minimum irreducible duplication of this rung, so it is worth
	// being exact about what is and is not shared.
	//
	// SHARED, because every one of them is a DECISION: the precedence table (`infixPrecedence`), the
	// operator maps (`infixMaxonOp`, `infixCmpOp`, `comparisonOperator`), the operand rules
	// (`requireBinOpOperands`), the result types (`binOpResultTag`, `wordOpResultTag`), the folds
	// (`foldIntBinOp`, `foldIntUnaryOp`, `foldIntCompare`, `foldLogicalNot`), THE SHIFT RULE
	// (`shiftFillsWithSign` → `shiftOpcodeFor` → `evalShift`), the `not` reading (`notOperatorFor`),
	// the cursor, the literal decoders, and the bound table. A constant expression and a runtime one
	// therefore cannot come to MEAN different things — which is the failure that matters, because
	// `let X = 1 shl 64` folding to 1 while `x shl 64` computes 0 is a silent wrong answer, not a
	// compile error.
	//
	// NOT SHARED: the climb loop itself, because the two produce different things. `parseBinary`
	// EMITS OPS and yields a `ValueId`; this yields a NUMBER. They cannot be one function, and the
	// reason is not the return type — it is `and`/`or`. Over bools those are CONTROL FLOW in a
	// function body (`emitShortCircuit` mints blocks and a merge phi, and the phi is by construction
	// not a constant), while a top-level `let BOTH = true and true` must be the value `true`. The op
	// path is *right* to branch and this path is *right* to fold, so the fork is real. Both reference
	// compilers keep the same two walks for the same reason (2-Parser.cs's `EvalConstOr` ladder;
	// v1's `evaluateUnresolvedConstExpr`).
	//
	// The evaluator lives on `Parser` because everything it reads is a Parser's: the token cursor,
	// `intLiteralValue`, `parseTypeQualifiedBound`, `decodeCharLiteral`. It is driven from
	// `ProgramSignatures.walkConstInitializer`, which builds a throwaway Parser per declaration and
	// keeps the ARENA and the recursion. It emits no ops and touches no artifact.

	// Fold one declaration's initializer to its value. The cursor is seated on the recorded token
	// range, and the walk must consume ALL of it — see `requireInitializerConsumed`.
	//
	// ⭐ **A MANAGED LITERAL IS THE WHOLE INITIALIZER OR IT IS NOT A CONSTANT AT ALL** (P1.7a). The
	// split is made HERE, once, on the token range — never inside the fold — and that is what keeps
	// this ONE mechanism rather than v1's four. v1 dispatches a top-level initializer into four
	// structurally different implementations chosen by the RHS's syntax (`Parser.maxon:3251-3347`): a
	// const-folded string rematerialized at every read, an eagerly-built array in a synthesized
	// `__module_init_<n>`, the same again for `T.from`, and a LAZILY-built struct behind a first-read
	// guard. Here there is one arena, one memoized DFS, one outcome union and one use-site inline; a
	// managed constant differs from a scalar one in exactly what its VALUE is (bytes, not a number).
	//
	// The runnable oracle makes the same split at the same place and for the same reason — its
	// `IsComplexInitializer` (2-Parser.cs:2101-2143) routes a bare managed literal away from the scalar
	// evaluator before a single operator is read.
	export function evalConstInitializer(decl TopLevelDecl, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstInitializerValue throws ConstEvalError
		self.pos = decl.initStart
		self.seatInDeclaringTypeBody(decl)

		// ⭐⭐ **A NODE — A USER STATIC FACTORY CALL, OR A STRUCT LITERAL — IS THE ONE INITIALIZER THAT IS NOT A
		// FOLD** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`; W116) — `var db = Database.create(EntryMap.create(),
		// sourcePaths: …)`, `static var origin = Pair{a: 1, b: 2}`. Nothing about either is evaluated here: what
		// the walk produces is WHAT to build and WHAT to put in it, and the record is built before `main` by
		// `__module_init` (or on first access by `__lazy_init#<label>`), exactly as a top-level array literal's
		// already is.
		//
		// Both are claimed on a LOOKAHEAD, and for one reason: the scalar walk reads `<identifier> .` as a
		// RANGED-TYPE BOUND and a bare `<identifier>` as a reference to another top-level `let`, and has no
		// other reading of either — so a call surfaced as *"Expected 'min' or 'max'"* and a struct literal as
		// **E2004 `Undefined constant 'Pair'`**, a message about a `let` the author never wrote. That is the
		// identical misattribution the `CharSet from […]` and `ItemArray.create()` arms below were added for.
		if self.atConstInitializerCall() or self.atConstStructConstruction() or self.atConstStringLiteralInit() or self.atConstMapLiteral() 'deferredNodeInitializer'
			var calls = ConstFactoryCallArray.create()
			let root = try self.evalConstInitializerNodeInto(calls, stack: stack, nested: false)
			try self.requireInitializerConsumed(decl, deferred: true)

			return ConstInitializerValue.factoryCall(calls, root: root)
		end 'deferredNodeInitializer'

		let folded = try self.evalConstFoldedValue(stack)
		// ⭐ **ONE CONSUMED CHECK FOR EVERY FORM.** It used to be spelled once per arm below, four identical
		// copies of the rule that an initializer is the WHOLE token range — and a fifth arm that forgot it
		// would silently truncate `var xs = [1, 2] junk` to `[1, 2]`.
		try self.requireInitializerConsumed(decl, deferred: false)

		return ConstInitializerValue.direct(folded)
	end 'evalConstInitializer'

	// ⭐ **EVERYTHING A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CAN FOLD TO WITHOUT RUNNING USER CODE** — and therefore,
	// exactly, everything a factory ARGUMENT may be. The two callers are the initializer entry point above
	// and `evalConstFactoryArgument`, which is what makes the admitted argument set a consequence of the
	// grammar rather than a second list that could drift from it.
	//
	// ⚠ It does NOT check that the token range is exhausted: an initializer is bounded by its declaration and
	// an argument by the `,` or `)` that follows it, so the two callers own that check and it is not one rule.
	function evalConstFoldedValue(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstFoldedValue throws ConstEvalError
		// ⭐ **THE OPENING TOKEN IS THE MANAGED TEST, and whatever FOLLOWS the literal is rejected by the one
		// shared `requireInitializerConsumed`** — the array-literal arm's arrangement below, for its reason.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT USED TO REQUIRE THE INITIALIZER TO BE EXACTLY ONE TOKEN, AND THAT MISATTRIBUTED EVERY
		// TRAILING FORM.** A longer initializer fell to the scalar walk, whose `evalConstPrimary` has no
		// string-literal arm at all, so `let B = "critsplit".toByteArray()` was **E2004 "Expected expression
		// but got 'string literal'" AT THE LITERAL** — blaming the one part of the line that IS a constant,
		// for the fault of the part that is not. Measured identically on `"a" + 1`, `"a" "b"` and
		// `"cs".byteLength()`. Now the literal folds and the tail is reported where it is, as the E2045 the
		// canonical corpus pins (`specs/byte-string-literal.md`: *"is not a constant expression:
		// '.toByteArray()' cannot be evaluated at compile time"*).
		//
		// An INTERPOLATION reaches the same answer by the same route rather than by a special test: the
		// lexer splits `"a{x}b"` into `stringLiteral("a") stringInterpStart … stringInterpEnd`, so the
		// leading chunk folds and the interpolation machinery is the non-constant tail — which is what it
		// is, its value being a heap String built from a runtime expression.
		//
		// The two arms are written out HERE rather than behind a probe that answers "which kind?",
		// because that probe's answer is a union and this walk runs once per top-level binding in the
		// program: it would be a heap box per declaration, allocated only to be matched on one line
		// later. The token test IS the kind test, read once.
		if self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'stringLiteral'
			let decoded = try self.decodeConstStringLiteral(self.consumeManagedLiteral())

			return ConstFoldedValue.managed(decoded, kind: ManagedConstKind.string)
		end 'stringLiteral'
		if self.at(TokenKind.byteStringLiteral) 'byteStringLiteral'
			let byteStringTok = self.consumeManagedLiteral()
			// DECODED FIRST, because the rule is about the blob's BYTES and not only about its element's
			// stride (A3r) — the escapes have to be resolved before there is a byte to range-check. The
			// expression door has always asked in this order, and now the two doors ask one question.
			let decoded = try self.decodeConstByteStringLiteral(byteStringTok)
			try self.requireConstByteStringBlobFitsItsElement(decoded, tok: byteStringTok)

			return ConstFoldedValue.managed(decoded, kind: ManagedConstKind.byteArray)
		end 'byteStringLiteral'

		// ⭐ **AN ARRAY LITERAL IS THE THIRD ANSWER, AND IT IS DECIDED ON THE OPENING TOKEN** (P1.7 slice
		// 2b). It cannot ride the single-token test above — `[1, 2]` is five tokens — and it must not fall
		// to the scalar walk, whose `evalConstPrimary` has no `[` and would report `Expected expression but
		// got '['` (which is what it did before this slice, in every position). The walk stops at the closing
		// `]`, so a trailing `var xs = [1, 2] junk` is caught by the caller's consumed check.
		if self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'arrayLiteralInitializer'
			return try self.evalConstArrayLiteral(stack)
		end 'arrayLiteralInitializer'

		// ⭐⭐ **`<ContainerAlias>.create()` IS THE FOURTH ANSWER, AND IT IS DECIDED ON THE OPENING TOKEN**
		// (spec-port `ownership`) — `var globalArr = ItemArray.create()`, a top-level container every
		// function in the program pushes into. It is claimed HERE for the array literal's reason: the scalar
		// walk below reads `<identifier> .` as a RANGED-TYPE BOUND (`i64.min`) and has no other reading of
		// it, so this shape surfaced as **E2010 `Expected ''min' or 'max'' but got 'create'`** — a diagnostic
		// about a grammar the author was not writing in.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE TEST IS THAT THE NAME IS A GENERIC-INSTANCE ALIAS, NOT THAT THE MEMBER IS `create`.** A
		// ranged alias and a generic alias live in different registries, so `IntArray.` can only be this
		// form — and claiming the whole shape is what lets the arm below say what a container's static
		// surface IS, rather than leaving `ItemArray.push` to the bound reader's nonsense message.
		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot and self.signatures.isGenericAlias(self.current().value) 'containerFactoryInitializer'
			return try self.evalConstContainerCreate()
		end 'containerFactoryInitializer'

		// ⭐⭐ **`CharSet from ['a', 'e']` IS THE FIFTH ANSWER** (spec-port `lazy-static`), claimed on the head
		// the body and the declaration sweep already share a predicate for. It must be claimed HERE rather than
		// left to the scalar walk, whose `evalConstNamed` reads a bare identifier as a reference to another
		// top-level `let` and reported **E2004 `Undefined constant 'CharSet'`** — a message about a `let` the
		// author never wrote.
		if self.atSetFromConstruction() 'setLiteralInitializer'
			return try self.evalConstSetFrom(stack)
		end 'setLiteralInitializer'

		// ⭐⭐ **A BOXED UNION'S PAYLOAD-FREE CASE IS THE SIXTH ANSWER** — `let NoPayloadStorage =
		// MaxonType.unresolved`, `let sharedUnreadParamType = MaxonType.unresolved`, the shape the compiler's
		// own source uses to keep ONE sentinel box for a whole compile instead of one allocation per site.
		//
		// ⭐ **IT IS CLAIMED HERE AND NOT BESIDE THE PAYLOAD-FREE ENUM FOLD, because the two produce different
		// KINDS of value.** An unboxed case's tag IS its value, so it folds into a `ConstValue` down in the
		// scalar walk; every case of a payload-bearing union is a heap BOX, and only this tier can answer with
		// a record. Left to the scalar walk it fell to that walk's dotted refusal, which read `<identifier> .`
		// as a ranged-type bound and then explained that a heap object cannot be a constant — a rule the
		// runnable oracle has never had (it builds the box in its own `__module_init`).
		//
		// ⚠ Disjoint from every arm above by construction: a union is not a generic-instance alias
		// (`isGenericAlias`), declares no `Type.method` for `atConstInitializerCall` to claim, and is not a
		// `<alias> from [` head — so no spelling can satisfy two of these, whichever order they are asked in.
		if self.atConstBoxedUnionCase() 'boxedUnionCaseInitializer'
			return self.evalConstBoxedUnionCase()
		end 'boxedUnionCaseInitializer'

		// ⚠⚠ **`CharacterSet.whitespacesAndNewlines()` WAS A SIXTH ARM HERE, AND W115'S REVIEW DELETED IT.** It
		// existed because *"shv2 has no `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` to declare the eleven accessors, so
		// `atConstInitializerCall` cannot claim them"* — and W115 listed that module, which is exactly what
		// gives `atConstInitializerCall` a declaration to claim them BY. See the deleted pair's headstone below
		// for the measurement that the arm had become unreachable and its refusal false.

		return ConstFoldedValue.scalar(try self.evalConstBinary(stack, minPrec: OrPrecedence))
	end 'evalConstFoldedValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE CURSOR SIT ON A CALL A GLOBAL'S INITIALIZER DEFERS TO `__module_init` — either
	// `<TypeName>.<method>(` or a bare `<function>(`?** One question in two spellings, and the decisive
	// token is the LAST of the lookahead: what makes either form recognizable is that some file DECLARES a
	// function under that name, which is exactly the question the call site asks
	// (`Parser.staticCallClaimsBase`) and exactly the key the signature index files a declaration under.
	// Asking anything weaker — "does the head start with a capital?", "is the member `create`?" — would be a
	// second, weaker reading of what a call IS.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS TEST IS ASKED *BEFORE* THE CONTAINER ARM (`evalConstInitializer`), AND THE TWO ARE DISJOINT
	// ANYWAY.** A generic-instance alias lives in a different registry and declares no `Type.method`, so no
	// spelling can satisfy both — which is what keeps the container's own surface sentence saying what a
	// container has, whichever order the two are asked in.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **THE BARE FORM ADDITIONALLY REQUIRES A RECORD RETURN, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE**
	// (spec-port `lazy-static`). The two shapes differ in what ELSE already claims them:
	//
	//   * `<Type>.<method>(` has NO other reading in a constant initializer — the scalar walk reads
	//     `<identifier> .` as a ranged-type bound (`i64.min`) and fails at the `(` — so claiming it whole and
	//     then EXPLAINING the refusal (`requireFactoryReturnsARecord`) is strictly better than any message
	//     the bound reader could give.
	//   * a bare `<name>(` is already owned, precisely worded, by the fold's own call rejection
	//     (`evalConstNamed` → E2045 *"Function calls are not allowed in global variable initializers"*),
	//     which BOTH reference compilers emit and which `specs/top-level-let.md` and
	//     `specs/static-variables.md` pin verbatim on `compute()` and `getDefault()`. Both of those return a
	//     SCALAR, and a scalar global's value is written into the `.data` image before any code runs — so the
	//     canonical sentence is exactly true of every bare call this test declines, and claiming those here
	//     would only re-word a refusal two canonical specs already fix.
	//
	// ⇒ **The admitted set is the same in both shapes** — a declared callee returning a record — and only
	// the REFUSAL differs, which is the same split `requireFactoryReturnsARecord`'s sentence table already
	// makes between the slot position and the argument position.
	function atConstInitializerCall() returns bool
		if not self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'notANameAtAll'
			return false
		end 'notANameAtAll'

		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'qualified'
			if self.kindAt(self.pos + 2) != TokenKind.identifier or self.kindAt(self.pos + 3) != TokenKind.leftParen 'notACall'
				return false
			end 'notACall'

			return self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(self.current().value, methodName: self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2).value))
		end 'qualified'

		if self.peekKind() != TokenKind.leftParen 'notACall'
			return false
		end 'notACall'

		return self.signatures.globalFactoryReturnsARecord(self.current().value)
	end 'atConstInitializerCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **SEAT THIS THROWAWAY EVALUATOR IN THE BODY THE DECLARATION WAS WRITTEN IN (W116).** The evaluator is
	// a `Parser` built per DECL over a token range (`ProgramSignatures.walkConstInitializer`), so it starts at
	// file scope however the binding was declared — and a `static var origin = Pair{…}` is written INSIDE
	// `type Pair`, which is the whole of why that literal is legal there and E3076 anywhere else. Pointing the
	// evaluator at the owning type is therefore not a licence, it is the truth about where those tokens are,
	// and it is what lets the construction door reach `requireConstructible` VERBATIM instead of restating a
	// location rule.
	//
	// ⭐ **BOTH REFERENCES DO EXACTLY THIS.** The runnable oracle re-points `_currentTypeName` at the owning
	// type while it generates a static's lazy initializer (`2-Parser.cs:2973-2984`, whose own comment names
	// `CharacterSet`'s statics as the motivating case), and v1 saves and restores `currentTypeName` around its
	// `parseLazyStaticStructDecl` (`Parser.maxon:3795-3797`). What shv2 does NOT take from either is the block
	// surgery around it: this seats a scope and emits nothing.
	//
	// The KIND rides with the name because `enterTypeScope` takes both and they are one fact — a `static`
	// inside an `enum` body must reach the enum's own refusal rather than a `structOf` miss (`requireConstructible`).
	function seatInDeclaringTypeBody(decl TopLevelDecl)
		match decl.scope 'declaredIn'
			fileScope then break 'declaredIn'
			typeMember(typeName) then self.enterTypeScope(typeName, isEnum: self.signatures.containsEnum(typeName))
		end 'declaredIn'
	end 'seatInDeclaringTypeBody'

	// ⭐ **IS THE CURSOR ON `<TypeName>{` — A STRUCT LITERAL?** (W116) The two-token shape
	// `atEnumStructRawValue` already reads for the same question one construct over. It needs nothing more:
	// `{` follows an identifier in no other constant-expression form, and WHICH type it names — declared,
	// visible, constructible from here — is `requireConstructible`'s to answer, with the diagnostics it
	// already has. Asking any of that here would be a second copy of a rule the body path owns.
	function atConstStructConstruction() returns bool
		return self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftBrace
	end 'atConstStructConstruction'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THE CURSOR ON `<TypeName> from "…"` — A STRING-LITERAL `init` CONSTRUCTION?** The THIRD
	// initializer that builds a record without folding one, and it reads the identical token shape its body
	// twin does (`atStringLiteralInitConstruction`) through the identical `structOf` test — one question, two
	// positions, so a type this admits in a body is a type it admits here.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT WAS MISSING, AND SHV2'S OWN `Project.maxon:407` IS THE PROGRAM:**
	// `export let CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath = FilePath from ""`. Unrecognised, the scalar walk read the bare
	// `FilePath` as a reference to another top-level `let` — its only other reading of an identifier — and
	// answered **E2004 `Undefined constant 'FilePath'`**, a message about a constant nobody wrote. That is
	// the SAME misattribution the struct-literal and factory-call arms beside it were added for, a third
	// time, and it cost four errors: the declaration plus the three files that read the name it declares.
	function atConstStringLiteralInit() returns bool
		if not (self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.from and self.kindAt((self.pos + 2) as TokenIndex) == TokenKind.stringLiteral) 'notTheShape'
			return false
		end 'notTheShape'

		return match self.signatures.structOf(self.current().value) 'declared'
			declared gives true
			undeclared gives false
		end 'declared'
	end 'atConstStringLiteralInit'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS THE CURSOR ON `[` … `:` — A MAP LITERAL?** The FOURTH initializer that builds a record without
	// folding one, and the only one of the four whose shape cannot be settled from a fixed number of tokens: a
	// map literal and an array literal open identically, and what separates them is a `:` an arbitrary
	// expression away. Both reference compilers say why no bounded lookahead finds it — `[f(a, b): 1]` has a
	// `,` at depth 1 before the colon, `[m.get(k): 1]` has a whole call in front of it.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BODY DECIDES THIS BY PARSING THE FIRST ELEMENT AND LOOKING AT WHAT FOLLOWS, AND THIS WALK CANNOT.**
	// `parseArrayLiteral` can, because both of its forks emit into the same block and the element it parsed is
	// usable either way. Here the forks produce DIFFERENT THINGS — an array literal folds to a
	// `ConstFoldedValue`, a map literal is an arena NODE — so the fold would have to be run and thrown away,
	// and its element rule refuses a key outright before it could be: a `"a"` standing before a `:` is not
	// `arrayElementIsSingleToken`, so `var m = ["a": 1]` reported **E2004 `Expected expression but got 'string
	// literal'`**, blaming the one part of the line that IS a constant (MEASURED, and the identical
	// misattribution the three arms above were each added to cure).
	//
	// ⭐ **SO THE SHAPE IS READ OFF THE TOKENS, AND THAT IS SOUND HERE FOR THE REASON IT IS NOT IN A BODY: a
	// constant initializer's brackets are BALANCED AND BOUNDED.** The scan stops at the `]` closing the `[` it
	// started on, and inside those brackets a `:` at depth 1 has no other reading — a labelled argument's colon
	// is inside `(`…`)` and a struct literal's field colon inside `{`…`}`, both of which this counts. It is
	// linear in the literal and asked once per bracketed initializer.
	function atConstMapLiteral() returns bool
		if not self.at(TokenKind.leftBracket) 'notABracketedLiteral'
			return false
		end 'notABracketedLiteral'

		var depth = 0 as ParsedInt
		var i = self.pos
		while i < self.tokens.count() 'eachToken'
			let kind = self.kindAt(i)
			if kind == TokenKind.leftBracket or kind == TokenKind.leftParen or kind == TokenKind.leftBrace 'opens'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'opens'

			if kind == TokenKind.colon and depth == 1 'theMapColon'
				return true
			end 'theMapColon'

			if kind == TokenKind.rightBracket or kind == TokenKind.rightParen or kind == TokenKind.rightBrace 'closes'
				depth = depth - 1
				if depth == 0 'closedWithoutAColon'
					return false
				end 'closedWithoutAColon'
			end 'closes'

			i = i + 1
		end 'eachToken'

		// An UNCLOSED bracket is not a map literal, and saying so is what leaves the diagnostic to the walk
		// that reads the tokens rather than to a lookahead that only counted them: the array-literal fold
		// reports the missing `]` at the token it stopped on.
		return false
	end 'atConstMapLiteral'

	// ⭐ **WALK WHICHEVER NODE IS HERE INTO THE ARENA** — the ONE fork between the two ways a top-level
	// initializer produces a record without folding one (W116), so a whole initializer and a nested
	// argument/field admit exactly the same pair rather than two lists that could drift.
	//
	// ⚠ **`nested` REACHES ONLY THE CALL ARM, AND THAT IS BECAUSE ONLY IT HAS A VERDICT TO WORD DIFFERENTLY**
	// (`requireFactoryReturnsARecord`'s return-refusal table). A construction's admissibility is decided by
	// WHERE it is written, which is the same answer in slot position and in argument position — so there is
	// nothing for the flag to reach, and threading it into the construct arm would invite a rule that differs
	// by position, which is exactly what that table's header warns against.
	function evalConstInitializerNodeInto(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, nested bool) returns InitializerCallId throws ConstEvalError
		if self.atConstStructConstruction() 'structLiteral'
			return try self.evalConstStructConstructionInto(calls, stack: stack)
		end 'structLiteral'

		if self.atConstStringLiteralInit() 'stringLiteralInit'
			return try self.evalConstStringLiteralInitInto(calls)
		end 'stringLiteralInit'

		if self.atConstMapLiteral() 'mapLiteral'
			return try self.evalConstMapLiteralInto(calls, stack: stack)
		end 'mapLiteral'

		return try self.evalConstFactoryCallInto(calls, stack: stack, nested: nested)
	end 'evalConstInitializerNodeInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **`FilePath from ""` AS AN INITIALIZER — recorded as the CALL it already is.** The body form is a
	// call to the type's own `init` static (`parseStringLiteralInitConstruction`, whose callee is
	// `qualifiedMethodName(<Type>, methodName: StringInitMethod)`), so nothing new is evaluated here: this
	// walk states WHICH function to call and WHAT to pass it, and `__module_init` builds the record before
	// `main` exactly as it does for `Database.create(…)`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENT IS THE DECODED LITERAL, THROUGH `decodeConstStringLiteral` — the same decoder the
	// scalar walk's own string arm uses**, so an escape means here what it means there. It is `folded`
	// rather than a nested `call` because a literal runs no user code; that is the whole of the difference
	// between this arm and the one below it.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **IT ASKS WHETHER THE `init` EXISTS, AND DELIBERATELY NOT WHETHER IT IS EXPORTED.**
	// `requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible` — the door the qualified factory form takes — was tried here first
	// and REFUSED shv2's own `Project.maxon:407` with `E3008 function 'FilePath.init' is not exported`, on a
	// program the bootstrap runs. That is the right question for `Database.create(…)`, where the author NAMED
	// the method, and the wrong one here: this construction reaches `init` through the type's CONFORMANCE and
	// never spells it, which is exactly why the body form (`parseStringLiteralInitConstruction`) does not ask
	// it either. Asking existence keeps the arm honest about what it is recording — a call `__module_init`
	// will emit — without inventing a visibility rule the body spelling does not have.
	function evalConstStringLiteralInitInto(calls ConstFactoryCallArray) returns InitializerCallId throws ConstEvalError
		let typeTok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.from)

		// ⛔⛔ **THE CONFORMANCE IS CHECKED HERE, AND LEAVING IT OUT WAS A FALSE ACCEPT.** The body spelling's
		// conformance is decided by `checkLiteralInitConformance`, which walks `project.literalInitSites` —
		// a store the REAL PARSE fills and this phase cannot reach, so a top-level construction recorded
		// nothing and was checked by nobody. **MEASURED with the check absent**: a `type Tag` with an `init`
		// static and NO `implements InitableFromStringLiteral` compiled and RAN, where the bootstrap answers
		// `E3005 Type 'Tag' does not conform to InitableFromStringLiteral`. Accepting what the oracle refuses
		// is the costly direction, and this arm created it.
		//
		// ⭐ It asks the SAME predicate through the SAME pair `checkLiteralInitConformance` asks it through
		// (`typeDeclaresInterface` over a declared-conformance map and the interface map), reading the SWEEP's
		// own store — which is exactly the substitution `sweptConformanceIndex`'s header exists for: two
		// stores of the declarations at two moments, one map type and one predicate, so what "conforms" MEANS
		// is stated once. The wording is the oracle's, verbatim.
		if not typeDeclaresInterface(self.signatures.sweptConformanceIndex(), interfaces: self.signatures.interfaceMap(), typeName: typeTok.value, interfaceName: InitableFromStringLiteralName) 'doesNotConform'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.literalInitDoesNotConform(String.from(typeTok.value), interfaceName: String.from(InitableFromStringLiteralName), line: typeTok.line, column: typeTok.column))
		end 'doesNotConform'

		let callee = qualifiedMethodName(typeTok.value, methodName: StringInitMethod)

		let decoded = try self.decodeConstStringLiteral(self.consumeManagedLiteral())

		var args = ConstFactoryArgumentArray.create()
		args.push(ConstFactoryArgument.create(EmptySourceText, value: ConstFactoryArgumentValue.folded(ConstFoldedValue.managed(decoded, kind: ManagedConstKind.string))))

		let id = calls.count()
		calls.push(ConstFactoryCall.call(callee, args: args))

		return id
	end 'evalConstStringLiteralInitInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Database.create(EntryMap.create(), sourcePaths: StringArray.create())` — A GLOBAL BUILT BY THE
	// PROGRAM'S OWN CODE** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). The cursor sits on the type name;
	// `atConstInitializerCall` has already established that some file declares this callee.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS IS THE ONE INITIALIZER FORM THAT RUNS USER CODE, AND EVERYTHING ELSE ABOUT IT FOLLOWS FROM
	// THAT.** `evalConstNamed` refuses `f()` because a call's answer needs the program to be RUNNING — still
	// true, and still nothing is folded here. What the walk produces is the CALL, which `__module_init`
	// makes before `main`, so the answer exists by the time any user code can read the slot.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FACTORY MAY NOT READ ANOTHER GLOBAL — and that is a property of the ARGUMENTS, not a rule this
	// walk enforces on the body.** `__module_init` fills the slots in arena order, so a body that read a
	// later global would read the null the image left there. The reason no ordering pass is needed is the
	// same one `ModuleInit`'s header already states for the literal kinds: a managed initializer cannot NAME
	// another global (`ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl` answers `notFound` for every record-valued decl), and
	// the admitted argument set below inherits that unchanged — every argument is a constant, a fresh empty
	// container, or another NODE. What a factory BODY does is not bounded here, and
	// `specs/static-variables.md` does not bound it in any compiler in the tree.
	//
	// ⚠ **W116's STRUCT CONSTRUCTION INHERITS THAT BOUND WHOLE, AND IT IS A REAL LIMIT RATHER THAN AN
	// ARTEFACT OF THE NEW ARM.** MEASURED, all three spellings alike: `Holder{name: NAME}`,
	// `Holder.make(NAME)` and `Holder{xs: SEEDS}` are each `E2004 Undefined constant '…'` against a
	// top-level managed `let` the runnable oracle reads happily — because ITS static initializer is
	// ordinary code in a lazy-init function, where naming a global is a LOAD and not a fold. Admitting
	// that here means an initializer value that READS another slot, which is the ordering invariant this
	// paragraph rests on; it is one feature for all three positions and belongs to whichever rung takes it.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **WALK ONE CALL INTO THE ARENA AND ANSWER WHICH ENTRY IT BECAME** — the recursion, and the whole of
	// what admitting a nested call costs. Its ARGUMENTS are walked first, so an inner call has already taken
	// its slot by the time this one takes its own; the id handed back is therefore stable the moment it is
	// minted, and nothing ever rewrites an entry.
	//
	// ⚠ **`nested` DECIDES WHICH RETURN RULE APPLIES, AND ONLY THAT.** Both positions demand a record and
	// neither demands anything else — the visibility of the type, the visibility of the callee and the
	// E3062 name recording are the same three checks wherever the call is written, which is why they are
	// asked here once rather than by two callers.
	function evalConstFactoryCallInto(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, nested bool) returns InitializerCallId throws ConstEvalError
		let callee = try self.consumeConstInitializerCallee()

		// THE CALLEE'S OWN VISIBILITY, anchored at the name the author wrote. The rule itself is
		// `requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible`'s — see there for why this position has to ask it at all.
		try self.requireGlobalInitCalleeIsVisible(callee.name, anchor: callee.anchor) otherwise (err) 'hiddenFactoryMethod'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'hiddenFactoryMethod'

		try self.requireFactoryReturnsARecord(callee, nested: nested)

		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.leftParen)
		var args = ConstFactoryArgumentArray.create()
		if not self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'hasArguments'
			args.push(try self.evalConstFactoryArgument(calls, stack: stack))
			while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreArguments'
				try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.comma)
				args.push(try self.evalConstFactoryArgument(calls, stack: stack))
			end 'moreArguments'
		end 'hasArguments'
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// The slot this call is about to occupy. Read BEFORE the push and AFTER the arguments, which is what
		// makes it this call's own index: every nested call the walk above found has already appended itself.
		let id = calls.count()
		calls.push(ConstFactoryCall.call(callee.name, args: args))

		return id
	end 'evalConstFactoryCallInto'

	// ⭐⭐ **READ THE NAME A TOP-LEVEL CALL IS MADE THROUGH, IN EITHER SPELLING** — the one reader, so the
	// registry key, the diagnostic spelling and the blame anchor are derived together and cannot drift.
	// `atConstInitializerCall` has already established that some file declares whichever name is here.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO SHAPES DIFFER IN EXACTLY ONE CHECK, AND IT IS THE ONE ONLY THE QUALIFIED FORM HAS A
	// SUBJECT FOR**: a TYPE name, which must be visible here (the door every other type-named form asks,
	// `parseDottedPrimary`'s hidden-base guard — a file that may not NAME `Database` may not build one at
	// file scope either) and which is a USE of that name for the E3062 unused-typealias check. A bare call
	// names no type, so it has neither; the CALLEE's own visibility is asked by the caller for both.
	function consumeConstInitializerCallee() returns ConstInitializerCallee throws ConstEvalError
		let headTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)
		if not self.at(TokenKind.dot) 'aFreeFunction'
			return ConstInitializerCallee.create(headTok.value, display: String.from(headTok.value), anchor: headTok)
		end 'aFreeFunction'

		// The type name is a USE of it, recorded for the same reason and at the same point the container
		// factory's alias is: this walk is the only reader of a top-level initializer's tokens, so it is the
		// only thing that can tell the E3062 check the name was written here.
		self.signatures.recordConstInitializerTypeName(headTok.value, declFilePath: self.filePath)
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.dot)
		let methodTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)

		if not self.typeNameVisibleHere(headTok.value) 'hiddenFactoryType'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.undefinedVariable(String.from(headTok.value), line: headTok.line, column: headTok.column))
		end 'hiddenFactoryType'

		return ConstInitializerCallee.create(qualifiedMethodName(headTok.value, methodName: methodTok.value), display: "{String.from(headTok.value)}.{String.from(methodTok.value)}", anchor: methodTok)
	end 'consumeConstInitializerCallee'

	// ⭐⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL FACTORY MUST HAND BACK A RECORD, WHEREVER THE CALL WAS WRITTEN — ONE MATCH, TWO
	// SENTENCE TABLES.** A `.data` slot holds either a baked VALUE or a POINTER filled before `main`, and only
	// the second can be the result of a call: a scalar global's number is written into the image, which
	// happens strictly before anything runs. So a factory returning `int`/`bool`/`float` — or returning
	// nothing at all — is refused here, rather than reaching `globalStdType`'s width table with a tag it has
	// no slot shape for. A NESTED call is refused on the same five arms for a reason of its own, which the
	// sentence table carries (`nestedFactoryReturnRefusal`).
	//
	// ⭐ **`nested` REACHES THE MESSAGE AND NEVER THE VERDICT, and that is the invariant this shape ENFORCES
	// rather than asserts.** The admitted set cannot differ by position because there is only one place that
	// decides — this match — and it cannot see `nested` at all; the flag is passed straight through to the
	// wording. It was two matches for one commit, and the failure that shape invited is not a sixth arm (that
	// breaks both loudly) but someone admitting an existing arm in ONE position: nested→admitted is a panic
	// three passes later, which is precisely what `unbridgedRecord` exists to prevent.
	function requireFactoryReturnsARecord(callee ConstInitializerCallee, nested bool) throws ConstEvalError
		match self.signatures.globalFactoryReturn(callee.name) 'returns'
			record then return
			undeclared then throw factoryUndeclaredRefusal(callee, nested: nested)
			scalar(typeName) then throw factoryScalarRefusal(callee, typeName: typeName, nested: nested)
			unbridgedRecord(typeName) then throw factoryUnbridgedRefusal(callee, typeName: typeName, nested: nested)
			void then throw factoryVoidRefusal(callee, nested: nested)
		end 'returns'
	end 'requireFactoryReturnsARecord'

	// ⭐ **ONE ARGUMENT OF A TOP-LEVEL FACTORY CALL — its optional `name:` LABEL and a value that is anything
	// an initializer may be, INCLUDING another call.** Neither admitted set is a list written here: the
	// folded half is `evalConstFoldedValue`, the very walk the initializer itself goes through, and the call
	// half is `evalConstFactoryCallInto`, the very walk the initializer's own call goes through — so an
	// argument is admitted by the same two doors that admit a whole initializer, and neither can drift from
	// what a top-level `var` accepts.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A NESTED CALL IS AN ARENA ENTRY, NOT A VALUE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM** (spec-port
	// `module-level-struct-var`). It used to be refused here, because an argument that could itself be a call
	// makes the folded-value union RECURSIVE and Maxon forbids a recursive type outright (E4014, `type 'X'
	// contains a reference cycle`). The tree is now held FLAT — the inner call appends itself to the
	// initializer's `ConstFactoryCallArray` and this argument keeps only its INDEX — and an integer names a
	// call without the type naming itself. The recursion that remains is the WALK's, which the language has
	// never had a problem with; only the TYPE had to be flattened.
	function evalConstFactoryArgument(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstFactoryArgument throws ConstEvalError
		let label = self.consumeConstArgumentLabel()

		return ConstFactoryArgument.create(label, value: try self.evalConstNodeValue(calls, stack: stack))
	end 'evalConstFactoryArgument'

	// ⭐ **THE VALUE HALF OF AN ARGUMENT, WHICH IS ALSO THE WHOLE OF A FIELD (W116)** — the thesis
	// `ConstFactoryCall` states, made operative: a struct literal's field is a labelled argument minus its
	// label, so both positions admit one set through one walk. `Outer{inner: Inner.create(7)}` and
	// `Pair.create(Pair{a: 1, b: 2})` both fall out of this, in both directions, without either door knowing
	// the other exists.
	function evalConstNodeValue(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstFactoryArgumentValue throws ConstEvalError
		// ⚠ **THE MAP LITERAL JOINS THE NODE TEST HERE AS WELL AS AT THE INITIALIZER, AND THE SYMMETRY IS THE
		// POINT rather than a widening taken for its own sake.** This function's own thesis is that an argument,
		// a field and a whole initializer admit ONE set through ONE walk; a form claimed at the initializer and
		// not here would make that sentence false and leave `Holder{entries: [k: v]}` to fall to the folded walk,
		// which reads the `[` as an ARRAY literal and reports `Expected ']' but got ':'` — a message about a
		// grammar the author was not writing in, which is the exact misattribution every arm of this fork was
		// added to cure.
		if self.atConstInitializerCall() or self.atConstStructConstruction() or self.atConstMapLiteral() 'nestedNode'
			return ConstFactoryArgumentValue.call(try self.evalConstInitializerNodeInto(calls, stack: stack, nested: true))
		end 'nestedNode'

		return ConstFactoryArgumentValue.folded(try self.evalConstFoldedValue(stack))
	end 'evalConstNodeValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **`[k0: v0, k1: v1, …]` AS A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER — the SAME desugar `parseMapLiteralBody` reads
	// inside a function, walked into the arena instead of emitted:** `Map.create()` plus one `Map.upsert(k,
	// value: v)` per pair, on the instance the FIRST pair's two column types fix. One desugar written at the
	// two positions a map literal can be written, so a literal-built map is one representation and one
	// instance wherever it stands.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS A NODE AND NOT A FOLD, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON IT LIVES HERE.** `ConstFoldedValue` is
	// *"everything an initializer can be WITHOUT running user code"*, and this runs the program's own
	// `Map.create` and `Map.upsert` before `main` — and a VALUE may be a further call, which is
	// `var keywordMap = [b"if": KeywordInfo.create(…), …]`, the shape this compiler's own lexer is written in.
	//
	// ⚠ **NO OWNERSHIP BOOKKEEPING, for `evalConstArrayLiteral`'s reason one container over**: a pair's halves
	// are CONSTANTS, so there is no binding to poison and no temp to de-list. Who FREES each half after the
	// `upsert` is decided from the callee's own consume bits at the synthesis
	// (`ProgramSignatures.globalMapEntry`) — the ordinary call machinery's answer, and deliberately not a
	// second parse-time move, which is exactly the leak `parseMapLiteralBody`'s header records.
	function evalConstMapLiteralInto(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns InitializerCallId throws ConstEvalError
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		self.skipNewlines()

		var entries = ConstMapEntryArray.create()
		let firstKeyTok = self.current()
		let first = try self.evalConstMapEntry(calls, stack: stack, columns: ConstMapColumns.open)
		entries.push(first)
		let keyType = self.constMapColumnType(first.key, calls: calls)
		let valueType = self.constMapColumnType(first.value, calls: calls)
		let columns = ConstMapColumns.fixed(keyType, value: valueType)

		// A newline at each pair boundary, exactly as `evalConstArrayLiteral` allows one at each element's:
		// a multi-line literal is the same literal, and `keywordMap` is 62 pairs written one per line.
		self.skipNewlines()
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'morePairs'
			try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
			entries.push(try self.evalConstMapEntry(calls, stack: stack, columns: columns))
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'morePairs'
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.rightBracket)

		let giid = self.signatures.builtinInstanceForColumns(MapBuiltinBaseName, key: keyType, value: valueType)
		// ⭐ **THE LITERAL RECORDS ITS OWN INSTANTIATION** — `gateMapLiteralColumns`' rule for the body's
		// literal, and it binds here for the identical reason: this is the one door a map is born through with
		// no `with (K, V)` to anchor an E3017 on, so a key implementing NEITHER of `Map`'s constraints would
		// otherwise be admitted in silence. What differs is only WHERE the site is filed — on the INDEX, not on
		// a parse artifact, because the constant evaluator's `Parser` is a throwaway whose artifact is never
		// committed (`ProgramSignatures.constInferredInstantiationSites`).
		self.signatures.recordConstInferredInstantiationSite(GenericInstantiationSite.create(giid, nameLine: firstKeyTok.line, nameColumn: firstKeyTok.column, filePath: self.filePath))

		// The slot this node is about to occupy. Read BEFORE the push and AFTER the pairs, which is what makes
		// it this node's own index — `evalConstFactoryCallInto`'s invariant, for its reason: every nested node
		// the walk above found has already appended itself.
		let id = calls.count()
		calls.push(ConstFactoryCall.mapLiteral(giid, entries: entries))

		return id
	end 'evalConstMapLiteralInto'

	// ONE `k: v` pair. Both halves go through `evalConstNodeValue` — the very walk a factory ARGUMENT and a
	// struct-literal FIELD go through — so a map column admits exactly what an initializer admits, another
	// CALL included, and no third list of admitted forms can drift from the other two.
	//
	// ⚠ **EACH HALF IS BLAMED AT ITS OWN TOKEN**, which is why the column check lives here rather than in the
	// caller's loop: the tokens are gone by the time the pair is returned, and a mismatched VALUE reported at
	// the KEY's position points at the half that was right.
	function evalConstMapEntry(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, columns ConstMapColumns) returns ConstMapEntry throws ConstEvalError
		let keyTok = self.current()
		let key = try self.evalConstNodeValue(calls, stack: stack)
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.colon)
		self.skipNewlines()
		let valueTok = self.current()
		let value = try self.evalConstNodeValue(calls, stack: stack)

		try self.requireConstMapColumnIsNameable(key, columnNoun: HashTableKeyColumnNoun, tok: keyTok)
		try self.requireConstMapColumnIsNameable(value, columnNoun: HashTableValueColumnNoun, tok: valueTok)

		match columns 'expected'
			// The FIRST pair FIXES both columns, so there is nothing yet to hold it to.
			open then break 'expected'
			fixed(keyType, valueType) then try self.requireConstMapColumns(key, value: value, calls: calls, keyType: keyType, valueType: valueType, keyTok: keyTok, valueTok: valueTok)
		end 'expected'

		return ConstMapEntry.create(key, value: value)
	end 'evalConstMapEntry'

	// Hold one later pair to the columns the first fixed — BOTH halves, from one `match` arm, which is why the
	// pair is a function rather than two lines at the caller. The body walk's rule, restated here because the
	// body's is enforced by `parseHashTableColumnArg`, which reads a `ValueId` out of the parser's value
	// columns — and a top-level initializer mints no values at all. What is compared instead is the TYPE each
	// half will be MINTED at (`ProgramSignatures.constNodeValueType`), which is the very type the synthesis
	// mints it at, so a pair this admits is a pair the emitted `Map.upsert` can take.
	function requireConstMapColumns(key ConstFactoryArgumentValue, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, keyType MaxonType, valueType MaxonType, keyTok Token, valueTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		try self.requireConstMapColumn(key, calls: calls, column: keyType, columnNoun: HashTableKeyColumnNoun, tok: keyTok)
		try self.requireConstMapColumn(value, calls: calls, column: valueType, columnNoun: HashTableValueColumnNoun, tok: valueTok)
	end 'requireConstMapColumns'

	// ⛔⛔ **AN ENUM CASE CANNOT BE A TOP-LEVEL MAP LITERAL'S COLUMN, AND THE LIMIT IS THE INTERNER RATHER THAN
	// THE GRAMMAR.** A payload-free enum case folds to a `named`-tagged SCALAR whose type is carried as BYTES
	// (`ConstValue.typeName`) because the evaluator is a throwaway parser; a `Map with (K, V)`'s type argument
	// has to be a `MaxonType`, and the only id this tier can mint is one in `ProgramSignatures`' own table —
	// which `SemanticCheck` does not speak. `ProgramSignatures.foldedMintType`'s header carries the measured
	// panic, and the wrong ANSWER the same shape gives when the name is dropped instead.
	//
	// ⇒ **REFUSED, AND POINTED AT THE SPELLING THAT WORKS.** A body's literal has a real parse artifact whose
	// ids are remapped at merge, so the identical program written inside a function is correct today — and
	// refusing here is what keeps this door from being the one that answers `int` where the author wrote an
	// enum, or panics three passes later.
	function requireConstMapColumnIsNameable(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, columnNoun String, tok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		match constScalarOf(value) 'scalar'
			notAScalar then return
			scalar(folded) then try self.requireConstMapScalarColumnIsNameable(folded, columnNoun: columnNoun, tok: tok)
		end 'scalar'
	end 'requireConstMapColumnIsNameable'

	// The tail of the question above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one. A `named` tag is the
	// whole test: every other folded scalar (`integer`, `boolean`, `float`, `exitCode`) rebuilds its type from
	// the tag alone and needs no id, and a BOXED union's case is not a scalar at all.
	function requireConstMapScalarColumnIsNameable(folded ConstValue, columnNoun String, tok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		if folded.tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'namesNoType'
			return
		end 'namesNoType'

		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("a `{String.from(folded.typeName)}` case as a top-level map literal's {columnNoun} — a `{String.from(MapBuiltinBaseName)}`'s column type has to be nameable from the whole-program index, and an enum case folded at file scope carries its enum's name as BYTES rather than as an id this tier can put in `{String.from(MapBuiltinBaseName)} with (…)`. Build the map inside a function, where the literal's instance is interned from the file's own parse artifact", line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
	end 'requireConstMapScalarColumnIsNameable'

	// One half against its column. The VERDICT and both spellings are the index's — see
	// `ProgramSignatures.constMapColumnCheck` for why neither can be produced on this side — so all this door
	// owns is where the refusal is BLAMED, which is the half only a token walk can see.
	function requireConstMapColumn(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, calls ConstFactoryCallArray, column MaxonType, columnNoun String, tok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		match self.signatures.constMapColumnCheck(column, value: value, calls: calls, declName: MapBuiltinBaseName, elementFilePath: self.filePath) 'verdict'
			agrees then return
			mismatch(got, expected) then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported(constMapColumnMessage(columnNoun, expected: expected, got: got), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'verdict'
	end 'requireConstMapColumn'

	// The type one column half is minted at, in the index's own interner. It is the SYNTHESIS's own producer
	// (`constNodeValueType`), so the instance this literal is interned at and the values that fill it cannot
	// disagree about what the columns are.
	//
	// The `declName` it takes is only ever spent on a panic message naming what held the value; a map literal's
	// answer to that is the container, exactly as a struct literal's door answers with its layout's name.
	function constMapColumnType(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, calls ConstFactoryCallArray) returns MaxonType
		return self.signatures.constNodeValueType(value, calls: calls, declName: MapBuiltinBaseName, elementFilePath: self.filePath)
	end 'constMapColumnType'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Pair{a: 1, b: 2}` AS A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER OR AS A FIELD/ARGUMENT OF ONE (W116)** — the SAME
	// construction `parseStructLiteral` reads inside a function, walked into the arena instead of emitted.
	// Its shape is that walk's, step for step, and every refusal it can raise is that walk's OWN:
	//
	//   • **E3072 / E3076 / the enum refusal** — `requireConstructible`, called with the cursor on the `{`
	//     exactly as the body's door is, so its brace-interior anchor lands on the token the oracle blames.
	//   • **E3018** (`requireFieldIndex`), **E3006** (a repeated field) and **E3086**
	//     (`requireAllFieldsInitialized`) — the same three deciders, in the same order.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOTHING HERE RESTATES A FIELD ROSTER OR A CONSTRUCTIBILITY RULE**, which is the point: those six
	// refusals were the whole of the gap between this door and the oracle, and a second copy of any of them is
	// this project's signature bug in the place it would be hardest to see, because both copies would "work".
	function evalConstStructConstructionInto(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns InitializerCallId throws ConstEvalError
		let typeTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)
		// This walk is the ONLY reader of a top-level initializer's tokens, so it is the only thing that can
		// tell the E3062 check the name was written here — `evalConstContainerCreate`'s hook, and recorded
		// BEFORE the refusal below for its reason: a name about to be rejected is still a mention.
		self.signatures.recordConstInitializerTypeName(typeTok.value, declFilePath: self.filePath)

		let layout = try self.requireConstructible(typeTok) otherwise (err) 'notConstructibleHere'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'notConstructibleHere'

		// Parallel to the LAYOUT, not to the source — `parseStructLiteral`'s columns, for its reason: entry `i`
		// belongs to the field the type declares at `i`, whatever position the literal wrote it in. The seeded
		// value is a placeholder for a slot nothing has written yet (the body seeds `0 as ValueId` for the same
		// instant), and `filled` is what keeps it from ever being read: a slot neither the literal nor a
		// declared default fills is E3086 below.
		var fields = ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray.create()
		var filled = BoolArray.create()
		for _ in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'initSlots'
			fields.push(ConstFactoryArgumentValue.folded(ConstFoldedValue.scalar(ConstValue.create(0, tag: ValueTypeTag.integer))))
			filled.push(false)
		end 'initSlots'

		try self.evalConstStructFields(calls, stack: stack, layout: layout, fields: fields, filled: filled)
		// Defaults BEFORE the check, so an omitted-but-defaulted field is legal without teaching
		// `requireAllFieldsInitialized` a second rule — `parseStructLiteral`'s ordering, for its reason.
		try self.applyConstFieldDefaults(calls, layout: layout, fields: fields, filled: filled, anchor: typeTok)
		try self.requireAllFieldsInitialized(layout, filled: filled, anchor: typeTok) otherwise (err) 'unfilledSlot'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'unfilledSlot'

		// The slot this construction is about to occupy. Read BEFORE the push and AFTER the fields, which is
		// what makes it this node's own index — `evalConstFactoryCallInto`'s invariant, for its reason: every
		// nested node the walk above found has already appended itself.
		let id = calls.count()
		calls.push(ConstFactoryCall.construct(layout.name, fields: fields))

		return id
	end 'evalConstStructConstructionInto'

	// The `name: value` pairs inside the braces, into the layout-parallel columns — `parseStructLiteralFields`
	// without the ops, and with its newline rule unchanged: a literal's shape must not depend on where its
	// author pressed return. The caller is on the type token; this consumes `{` through `}`.
	function evalConstStructFields(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, layout StructLayout, fields ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray, filled BoolArray) throws ConstEvalError
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.leftBrace)
		self.skipNewlines()
		while not self.at(TokenKind.rightBrace) 'eachField'
			let nameTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)
			try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.colon)

			let index = try self.requireFieldIndex(layout, fieldTok: nameTok) otherwise (err) 'noSuchField'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
			end 'noSuchField'
			let alreadyFilled = try filled.get(index) otherwise panic("evalConstStructFields: filled.get OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
			if alreadyFilled 'duplicate'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.duplicateFieldInitializer(String.from(layout.name), fieldName: String.from(nameTok.value), line: nameTok.line, column: nameTok.column))
			end 'duplicate'

			try fields.set(index, value: try self.requireConstFieldValueFits(calls, layout: layout, index: index, value: try self.evalConstNodeValue(calls, stack: stack), fieldTok: nameTok)) otherwise panic("evalConstStructFields: fields.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
			try filled.set(index, value: true) otherwise panic("evalConstStructFields: filled.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")

			self.skipNewlines()
			if not self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'lastField'
				break
			end 'lastField'
			try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'eachField'
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.rightBrace)
	end 'evalConstStructFields'

	// ⭐ **A SLOT THE LITERAL DID NOT WRITE TAKES ITS DECLARED DEFAULT** — `applyFieldDefaults` without the ops,
	// and it produces the same two things that one does: a folded LITERAL, or a CALL to the nullary helper the
	// declaration synthesized (`FieldDefault.helper`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE HELPER BECOMES AN ORDINARY ARENA ENTRY, WHICH IS WHAT ROOTS IT.** `factoryInitializerCallees`
	// walks this arena to name everything a top-level initializer calls, and a helper reached any other way
	// would be filed unreachable, never lowered, and met as an empty function by the call `__module_init`
	// makes — `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`'s failure, exactly as the union header describes for a
	// nested factory. That is also why the defaults are applied HERE and not on the index side.
	//
	// Both kinds go through the same `requireConstFieldValueFits` a WRITTEN value does, so a `= 3` meeting a
	// float slot widens and a `= 500` meeting `int(0 to 100)` is refused identically whichever way the slot
	// came to have a value — the property `fillFieldDefault` states for the body path.
	function applyConstFieldDefaults(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, layout StructLayout, fields ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray, filled BoolArray, anchor Token) throws ConstEvalError
		for i in 0 upto layout.fieldCount() 'eachField'
			let alreadyFilled = try filled.get(i) otherwise panic("applyConstFieldDefaults: filled.get OOB at {i} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
			if alreadyFilled 'literalWins'
				continue
			end 'literalWins'

			match layout.fieldDefault(i) 'declared'
				// The literal did not fill this slot and the declaration offers nothing. That is E3086, and
				// `requireAllFieldsInitialized` is what says so — not this function.
				none then continue
				literal(payload, tag) then try self.fillConstFieldDefault(calls, layout: layout, index: i, value: ConstFactoryArgumentValue.folded(ConstFoldedValue.scalar(ConstValue.create(payload, tag: tag))), fields: fields, filled: filled, anchor: anchor)
				helper(helperName) then try self.fillConstFieldDefault(calls, layout: layout, index: i, value: ConstFactoryArgumentValue.call(self.pushConstNullaryCall(calls, callee: helperName)), fields: fields, filled: filled, anchor: anchor)
			end 'declared'
		end 'eachField'
	end 'applyConstFieldDefaults'

	// Record one default as slot `index`'s value and mark the slot filled — the last two lines of BOTH ways a
	// declared default becomes one, exactly as `recordFieldDefaultValue` is for the body path, with the fit
	// check in front so neither way can install a value the slot cannot hold.
	function fillConstFieldDefault(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, fields ConstFactoryArgumentValueArray, filled BoolArray, anchor Token) throws ConstEvalError
		try fields.set(index, value: try self.requireConstFieldValueFits(calls, layout: layout, index: index, value: value, fieldTok: anchor)) otherwise panic("fillConstFieldDefault: fields.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
		try filled.set(index, value: true) otherwise panic("fillConstFieldDefault: filled.set OOB at {index} — the columns are sized to the layout's field count")
	end 'fillConstFieldDefault'

	// Append a no-argument call to the arena and answer which entry it became — the field-default helper's
	// whole shape. It takes its slot the same way every other node does (after its arguments, of which it has
	// none), so nothing about the arena's numbering is special-cased for it.
	function pushConstNullaryCall(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, callee ByteArray) returns InitializerCallId
		let id = calls.count()
		calls.push(ConstFactoryCall.call(callee, args: ConstFactoryArgumentArray.create()))

		return id
	end 'pushConstNullaryCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **MAY THIS CONSTANT FILL THIS SLOT — and if it may only after a widening, WHICH VALUE ACTUALLY GOES
	// IN** (W116). The VERDICT is `ProgramSignatures.constStructFieldCheck`'s, because both halves of the
	// comparison resolve against the index's own interner and registries; what is decided here is only where
	// it is BLAMED, which is the half only a token walk can see.
	//
	// ⛔ **WITHOUT IT `Pair{a: "hi"}` STORED A STRING POINTER IN AN `int` SLOT** — a wrong answer on a program
	// the oracle refuses (MEASURED: `E3005 … cannot assign a value of type 'struct' to field 'a' of 'Pair',
	// which holds 'int'`). shv2's own body path refuses the same program as `E3005 cannot assign 'String' to
	// variable 'a' of type 'int'`, and this raises that same arm so the two spellings of one mistake agree.
	//
	// The site is named `<Struct>.<field>` (`fieldSiteName`) rather than the bare field name: this ONE door
	// covers scalar and managed slots alike, where the body path splits them across two, and the more precise
	// of the two names is the one that is right for both.
	function requireConstFieldValueFits(calls ConstFactoryCallArray, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, fieldTok Token) returns ConstFactoryArgumentValue throws ConstEvalError
		match self.signatures.constStructFieldCheck(layout, index: index, value: value, calls: calls, declName: layout.name, elementFilePath: layout.declFilePath) 'verdict'
			agrees then break 'verdict'
			widensToFloat then return ConstFactoryArgumentValue.folded(ConstFoldedValue.scalar(ConstValue.create(self.constFloatBits(self.constScalarFilling(value, layout: layout, index: index)), tag: ValueTypeTag.float)))
			mismatch(got, expected) then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.assignTypeMismatch(assignedPlacePhrase(FunctionTypeDoor.field, name: self.fieldNameAt(layout, index: index), owner: layout.name), got: got, expected: expected, line: fieldTok.line, column: fieldTok.column))
		end 'verdict'

		try self.requireConstFieldValueInRange(layout, index: index, value: value, fieldTok: fieldTok)

		return value
	end 'requireConstFieldValueFits'

	// ⭐ **THE RANGE HALF, WHICH THE TAG COMPARISON ABOVE CANNOT SEE** — `Pair{a: 500}` against
	// `var a as Percent`, where `typealias Percent = int(0 to 100)`. A top-level initializer is not a function,
	// so it records no site for `InsertRangeChecks` to fold, and the compile-time E3005 a body's literal gets
	// from that pass has to be raised by the evaluator instead — which is exactly the arrangement
	// `checkConstCastRange` already makes for `let X = 5 as T`, reached here through its own decider so the two
	// positions cannot word or judge one refusal differently. MEASURED: both compilers report
	// `Value 500 is outside the range of 'Percent' (int(0 to 100))` for the body spelling, anchored at the
	// FIELD NAME, which is where this anchors too.
	//
	// A non-scalar value has no number to compare and simply is not asked; a float alias resolves to a bare
	// `float` (`denotedSlotType`'s ranged arm) and so denotes no alias here, which is the same unchecked
	// promotion `reportConstCastIfOutOfRange` declines for the body.
	function requireConstFieldValueInRange(layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, fieldTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		match constScalarOf(value) 'scalar'
			notAScalar then return
			scalar(folded) then try self.requireConstScalarSuitsFieldRange(folded, layout: layout, index: index, fieldTok: fieldTok)
		end 'scalar'
	end 'requireConstFieldValueInRange'

	// The tail of the question above, split out because a `match` arm may not open one.
	function requireConstScalarSuitsFieldRange(folded ConstValue, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex, fieldTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		match self.declaredAliasOfType(self.signatures.fieldTypeOf(layout, index: index, into: self.artifact.typeNames)) 'alias'
			undeclared then return
			declared(alias) then try self.reportConstCastIfOutOfRange(folded, alias: alias, anchor: fieldTok)
		end 'alias'
	end 'requireConstScalarSuitsFieldRange'

	// The number a `widensToFloat` verdict is about. The verdict is returned only for an integer-tagged folded
	// scalar, so anything else here is the index and this walk disagreeing about what was compared.
	function constScalarFilling(value ConstFactoryArgumentValue, layout StructLayout, index FieldIndex) returns ConstValue
		match constScalarOf(value) 'scalar'
			scalar(folded) then return folded
			notAScalar then panic("Parser.constScalarFilling: field {index} of `{String.from(layout.name)}` was told to widen to float, but its value is not a folded scalar — `ProgramSignatures.constStructFieldCheck` answers `widensToFloat` only for an integer constant")
		end 'scalar'
	end 'constScalarFilling'

	// The `name:` an argument was written with, or the EMPTY bytes that mean "positional" — the same "no
	// label" spelling `MaxonOp.call`'s `argLabels` uses, so what this walk records and what a body's call
	// site records are one encoding. A label is an identifier followed by `:`; anything else is the value.
	function consumeConstArgumentLabel() returns ByteArray
		if not (self.at(TokenKind.identifier) and self.peekKind() == TokenKind.colon) 'positional'
			return EmptySourceText
		end 'positional'

		let labelTok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		self.advance()
		return labelTok.value
	end 'consumeConstArgumentLabel'

	// ⭐ **`[e0, e1, …]` AT TOP LEVEL — the constant twin of `parseArrayLiteral`**, and the same three rules
	// read off the same grammar: an EMPTY literal has no element type to infer, the FIRST element fixes the
	// element type, and every later element must match it. What differs is only what the walk produces —
	// folded VALUES here, ops there — which is the fork `evalConstInitializer`'s header states for the whole
	// evaluator. The two REJECTIONS are worded once and shared (`MixedArrayLiteralElementsMessage`) or
	// deliberately not (`EmptyConstArrayLiteralMessage` — see there).
	//
	// ⚠ **NO OWNERSHIP BOOKKEEPING, and that is a property of the position rather than an omission.** A
	// literal in a function body must move each element into the array (poisoning a bare local, de-listing an
	// owned temp) because its elements can be values something else already owns. A top-level initializer's
	// elements are CONSTANTS — there is no binding to poison and no temp to de-list — so the only thing that
	// carries over is that a String element must reach the array OWNED, which `ModuleInit` does by cloning
	// the literal exactly as `moveElementIntoContainer` promotes a borrowed one.
	function evalConstArrayLiteral(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstFoldedValue throws ConstEvalError
		let openTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		self.skipNewlines()
		if self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'empty'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported(EmptyConstArrayLiteralMessage, line: openTok.line, column: openTok.column))
		end 'empty'

		var elements = ConstArrayElementArray.create()
		let first = try self.evalConstArrayElement(stack)
		let element = constArrayElementKindOf(first)
		elements.push(first)

		// A newline at each element boundary, exactly as the body walk allows one (`finishArrayLiteral`) — a
		// multi-line literal is the same literal, and the two positions must not disagree about that.
		self.skipNewlines()
		while self.at(TokenKind.comma) 'moreElements'
			try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.comma)
			self.skipNewlines()
			let elemTok = self.current()
			let elem = try self.evalConstArrayElement(stack)
			// ⚠ **`constArrayElementsAgree` AND NOT `constArrayElementKindOf(…) != element`, because a KIND
			// STOPPED BEING THE WHOLE IDENTITY when enum elements arrived.** `[Reg.rcx, CharClass.other]` is two
			// enums and one kind: compared on kind alone it would be accepted, and then typed from whichever
			// element the walk happened to see first. The comparison is against the FIRST element for that
			// reason — it is the one the instance is built from.
			if not constArrayElementsAgree(elem, b: first) 'mixed'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported(MixedArrayLiteralElementsMessage, line: elemTok.line, column: elemTok.column))
			end 'mixed'
			elements.push(elem)
			self.skipNewlines()
		end 'moreElements'
		try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.rightBracket)

		return ConstFoldedValue.arrayLiteral(elements, element: element)
	end 'evalConstArrayLiteral'

	// ⭐⭐ **`ItemArray.create()` AT TOP LEVEL — AN EMPTY CONTAINER AS A GLOBAL** (spec-port `ownership`).
	// The cursor sits on the alias; `evalConstInitializer` has already established that it names a
	// generic instance.
	//
	// ⭐ **THIS IS NOT A FUNCTION CALL THE EVALUATOR HAS BEEN TALKED INTO ALLOWING.** `evalConstNamed`
	// refuses `f()` because a call's answer needs the program to be RUNNING, and that is still true: nothing
	// is folded here. What the fold produces is the INSTANCE — a type, known at compile time — and the
	// record is built at run time by `__module_init`, exactly as a top-level array literal's already is
	// (`ModuleInit.builtArrayRecord`). A container factory is the array literal's empty case, so it takes
	// the same slot, the same init and the same `__maxon_global_cleanup` drop.
	//
	// ⭐ **EVERY BUILTIN CONTAINER, not just `Array`** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). The `Array`-only
	// limit this door used to carry was a property of `__module_init`, which knew one `create`; the routing
	// now lives on the instance registry (`ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall`), so a `Set` / `Map` /
	// `List` / `Vector` global is built from the same description an `Array` global is.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND EVERY DECLARED GENERIC (W41)** — `var g = StrMap.create()` once `stdlib/Map.maxon` is listed and
	// `Map` is an ordinary user generic. That widening cost this door ONE routed gate and the emitter NOTHING:
	// a builtin's `create` is a runtime entry needing stamps and a declared generic's is a static needing none,
	// which `ContainerCreateCall` was already the description of. What remains refused is a `create` this
	// position genuinely cannot call — absent, invisible from here, or not returning `Self` — plus arguments,
	// which no `containerCreate` outcome carries. See `requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate`.
	function evalConstContainerCreate() returns ConstFoldedValue throws ConstEvalError
		let aliasTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)
		// This walk is the ONLY reader of a top-level initializer's tokens, so it is the only thing that can
		// tell the E3062 check the alias was named here — the cast target's door, one shape over, and
		// recorded BEFORE the refusals below for the same reason: a name about to be rejected is still a
		// mention, and a file must not be told about two unrelated mistakes at once.
		self.signatures.recordConstInitializerTypeName(aliasTok.value, declFilePath: self.filePath)
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.dot)
		let methodTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)

		if not methodTok.value.equals(CreateMethod) 'notTheFactory'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(aliasTok.value)}.{String.from(methodTok.value)}` as a top-level initializer — a global container is declared with `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()`, the one static a constant initializer can name; every other method needs the program to be running", line: methodTok.line, column: methodTok.column))
		end 'notTheFactory'

		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.leftParen)
		// ⚠ **THE EMPTY RECORD IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT THIS OUTCOME DESCRIBES**, for a builtin container and a
		// declared generic alike: `ConstFoldedValue.containerCreate` carries an INSTANCE and nothing else, which
		// is exactly what lets it serve a factory ARGUMENT as well as a global's own slot. An argument would
		// have to travel in the outcome, and the arm that carries arguments (`factoryValue`) types its slot from
		// the callee's DECLARED return — the BASE for a generic, not this instance — so it cannot take this
		// shape either. See `requireDeclaredGenericGlobalCreate` for why the instance identity is load-bearing.
		if not self.at(TokenKind.rightParen) 'hasArguments'
			let argTok = self.current()
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("arguments to `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` in a top-level initializer — a global declared through a generic-instance alias holds the EMPTY record that alias's `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` builds, and that is the whole of what its slot description carries. Build a populated one inside a function", line: argTok.line, column: argTok.column))
		end 'hasArguments'
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.rightParen)

		let giid = self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(aliasTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath)
		// The gate for whichever kind of `create` this instance has — a builtin container's is the SAME
		// function a body's `create()` passes, so a container this index refuses cannot become admissible by
		// being written at file scope; a declared generic's is the top-level door's own, because a body's call
		// site has no `.data` slot and no `__module_init` to satisfy. Only the error TYPE differs — a constant
		// initializer's refusals travel as `ConstEvalError` — which is one wrap and not a second decider.
		try self.requireGlobalContainerIsCreatable(giid, aliasTok: aliasTok) otherwise (err) 'inadmissibleContainer'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'inadmissibleContainer'

		// ⚠ **AN ARRAY OF BUFFERS IS REFUSED, because the SURFACE would not survive the slot.** Which
		// surface an element reads as is a mark the parser carries on the VALUE
		// (`markBufferSurfaceElements`, asked of the ALIAS NAME at `parseArrayStaticCall`), and a global is
		// re-loaded at every use, so no mark reaches the read. Admitting it would hand back an ARRAY-surfaced
		// element where the author wrote a buffer's alias — a refusal at the first buffer method, blaming the
		// method rather than the declaration.
		if self.signatures.aliasElementIsBufferSurface(aliasTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'elementsAreBuffers'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("a top-level `{String.from(aliasTok.value)}.{String.from(CreateMethod)}()` — its elements are `{String.from(ManagedMemoryTypeName)}` buffers, and a global is re-loaded at every use, so an element read out of it would carry the `{String.from(self.signatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName())}` surface instead. Declare an array of buffers inside a function", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column))
		end 'elementsAreBuffers'

		return ConstFoldedValue.containerCreate(giid, elements: sharedEmptyConstArrayElements)
	end 'evalConstContainerCreate'

	// ⭐⭐ **`CharSet from ['a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u']` AS A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER** (spec-port `lazy-static`) —
	// the SAME construction `parseSetFromConstruction` reads inside a function, folded to its instance and its
	// members instead of emitted.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE HEAD IS WHAT THE BRACKETS CANNOT SAY, WHICH IS WHY ONLY THE `from` FORM IS ADMITTED HERE.** A
	// bare `['a', 'e']` at file scope is refused and stays refused: a character literal is not a constant
	// (`evalConstCharLiteral`), and even for an integer element list the brackets name an `Array`, never a
	// `Set`. The alias states both the container and the key type, which is the whole of what this arm adds.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE HEAD IS CLAIMED BY `atSetFromConstruction`, THE VERY PREDICATE THE BODY AND THE DECLARATION
	// SWEEP SHARE.** That predicate exists because the sweep must PRE-INTERN whatever instance a `Set from`
	// mints, or the parser's own read becomes the first write to the shared registry mid-parse — see there.
	// Asking a fourth, local question about the head would have re-opened exactly that hole for the one
	// position that reaches the registry EARLIEST.
	function evalConstSetFrom(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstFoldedValue throws ConstEvalError
		let aliasTok = try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.identifier)
		// This walk is the only reader of a top-level initializer's tokens, so it is the only thing that can
		// tell the E3062 check the alias was named here — `evalConstContainerCreate`'s hook, for its reason.
		self.signatures.recordConstInitializerTypeName(aliasTok.value, declFilePath: self.filePath)
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.from)

		// A BARE `Set from […]` mints its instance from the elements, and a top-level element list cannot
		// supply one: the members are folded, not emitted, so there is no value whose type could be read back.
		// The alias form is the only one that STATES the instance, which is what this arm needs.
		if not self.signatures.isGenericAlias(aliasTok.value) 'noInstanceStated'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(aliasTok.value)} from […]` as a top-level initializer — a global set is declared through a generic-instance alias, which is what states the key type its members are checked against (`typealias {String.from(CharacterMemberSetAliasName)} = {String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} with Character`). A bare `{String.from(SetBuiltinBaseName)} from […]` infers its instance from the elements, which a folded member list cannot supply", line: aliasTok.line, column: aliasTok.column))
		end 'noInstanceStated'

		let giid = self.signatures.genericAliasInstanceFrom(aliasTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath)

		return ConstFoldedValue.containerCreate(giid, elements: try self.evalConstSetMembers(stack, giid: giid, aliasTok: aliasTok))
	end 'evalConstSetFrom'

	// The `[…]` half — one member per element, each held to the SET'S OWN KEY TYPE. The bracket walk is the
	// array literal's (newlines are layout, a trailing token is the caller's `requireInitializerConsumed`);
	// what differs is that the members are checked against a type the head STATED rather than against the
	// first element's kind, which is why this cannot simply be `evalConstArrayLiteral`.
	//
	// ⚠ **AN EMPTY MEMBER LIST IS ADMITTED, unlike an empty ARRAY literal.** `[]` is refused there because
	// nothing can say what its element type is; here the alias already did, and `CharSet from []` denotes the
	// empty character set exactly as `CharSet.create()` does.
	function evalConstSetMembers(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) returns ConstArrayElementArray throws ConstEvalError
		try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.leftBracket)
		self.skipNewlines()
		var members = ConstArrayElementArray.create()
		while not self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'eachMember'
			members.push(try self.evalConstSetMember(stack, giid: giid, aliasTok: aliasTok))
			self.skipNewlines()
			if not self.at(TokenKind.rightBracket) 'moreMembers'
				try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.comma)
				self.skipNewlines()
			end 'moreMembers'
		end 'eachMember'
		try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.rightBracket)

		return members
	end 'evalConstSetMembers'

	// ⭐ **ONE MEMBER, ADMITTED BY THE SET'S KEY TYPE** — the folding twin of `parseSetMemberList`, which holds a
	// body's `insert` argument to the same type off the same `setKeyType`. Without it a `Set with String`
	// declared from `[1, 2]` would build a set whose slots hold integers and whose teardown walk calls
	// `__str_decref` on them.
	//
	// A CHARACTER key takes a character literal — the one position at module scope where one is admitted, and
	// it is admitted because the value being built is a RECORD in a container rather than a folded constant
	// (see `ConstArrayElement.character`). Every other key type folds through the array literal's own element
	// reader, so a `Set with Integer` accepts exactly what an `Array with Integer` does.
	function evalConstSetMember(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, giid GenericInstanceId, aliasTok Token) returns ConstArrayElement throws ConstEvalError
		let keyType = self.signatures.setKeyType(giid)
		let memberTok = self.current()
		if maxonTypeTag(keyType) == ValueTypeTag.character 'characterKeyed'
			if not self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'notACharacterLiteral'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.builtinOperandType("{String.from(aliasTok.value)} from", required: typeTagName(ValueTypeTag.character), got: tokenDisplayText(memberTok), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column))
			end 'notACharacterLiteral'
			self.advance()
			// DECODED here, so a malformed literal is named for what is wrong with IT rather than reported as a
			// key-type mismatch — `evalConstCharLiteral`'s discipline, one arm over.
			let bytes = try self.charLiteralBytes(memberTok) otherwise (err) 'malformed'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
			end 'malformed'

			return ConstArrayElement.character(bytes)
		end 'characterKeyed'

		let element = try self.evalConstArrayElement(stack)
		if not constSetMemberMatchesKeyType(element, keyType: keyType) 'wrongKeyType'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.builtinOperandType("{String.from(aliasTok.value)} from", required: typeTagName(maxonTypeTag(keyType)), got: tokenDisplayText(memberTok), line: memberTok.line, column: memberTok.column))
		end 'wrongKeyType'

		return element
	end 'evalConstSetMember'

	// ⛔⛔ **`atCharacterSetStaticCall` AND `evalConstCharacterSetStatic` STOOD HERE AND W115'S REVIEW DELETED
	// THEM, BECAUSE THE RETIREMENT LEFT THEM DEAD IN THE FOLD AND FALSE IN THE REFUSAL.** They claimed
	// `CharacterSet.<name>(` at a top-level initializer and folded the eleven presets out of
	// `characterSetPresets`, in the since-deleted `Runtime/CharacterSetRuntime.maxon` (W129) — a SECOND
	// transcription of what `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon`
	// now declares, consulted at a door the corpus was supposed to own.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE FOLD WAS ALREADY UNREACHABLE, MEASURED.** `atConstInitializerCall` is asked FIRST
	// (`evalConstInitializer`) and claims any `<Type>.<method>(` some file DECLARES — which, since the module
	// is listed, is all eleven. Proven rather than read: seeding the corpus's `cachedWhitespaces` with `'x'`
	// moved a TOP-LEVEL `let ws = CharacterSet.whitespaces()` from `false` to `true`, so the value came from
	// the corpus body and not from this table.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THE ONLY EXIT LEFT WAS A REFUSAL STATING A FACT THIS RUNG DELETED** — *"a global `CharacterSet`
	// is one of the eleven predefined sets, whose members the compiler holds as data"*. MEASURED on
	// `let bogus = CharacterSet.inverted()`: E2015 with that sentence, while the SAME name inside a function
	// answers *"'CharacterSet' has no static method named 'inverted' — the compiler owns that type name, so a
	// static of its own can only come from the module that declares it"*. Two doors, one question, and the
	// stale one was the confident one. With the arm gone the top-level door answers exactly as it does for any
	// other declared type's undeclared static (`Foo.nope()`), which is the whole point of the retirement.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT WENT WITH IT: `CharacterSet.from(…)` AT A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER NOW GETS THE GENERIC SENTENCE**,
	// because `atConstInitializerCall` tests the member position for `identifier` and `from` lexes as
	// `TokenKind.from`. That is a property of that predicate and of every keyword-named static, not of this
	// type — it is pinned as its own case below, so widening it is a decision with a witness rather than a
	// silent side effect of this deletion.

	// ONE element of a top-level array literal: a STRING literal, or anything the scalar evaluator folds to
	// an integer. The managed/scalar split is `evalConstInitializer`'s, applied between two separators —
	// a String element is exactly one token, so `["a{x}b"]` (several tokens) and `["a" as T]` fall to the
	// scalar walk and are rejected there, which is what they are.
	//
	// A non-integer SCALAR is refused here rather than silently stored: `[true, false]` folds perfectly well
	// and would then be built as an `Array with Integer` of 1s and 0s, which is not the type the program
	// wrote. shv2 rejects a bool element in a function body too (`arrayLiteralInstanceFor`), so this is the
	// same boundary at the same width — a top-level literal must never accept more than a body does.
	function evalConstArrayElement(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstArrayElement throws ConstEvalError
		if self.at(TokenKind.stringLiteral) and self.arrayElementIsSingleToken(self.pos) 'stringElement'
			return ConstArrayElement.string(try self.decodeConstStringLiteral(self.consumeManagedLiteral()))
		end 'stringElement'

		let tok = self.current()
		let value = try self.evalConstBinary(stack, minPrec: OrPrecedence)

		// ⭐ **A PAYLOAD-FREE ENUM CASE IS AN ELEMENT** — `[X64Register.rcx, X64Register.rdx, …]`, which is how
		// three of shv2's own backends write their register tables. The scalar walk already folded it to its
		// TAG and tagged the value `named` (see `evalConstNamed`), so nothing is decided here beyond keeping
		// the enum's spelling with the element: the array it makes is an `Array with <the enum>`, and
		// `constArrayValueType` reads that name off element 0.
		if value.tag == ValueTypeTag.named 'enumCaseElement'
			return ConstArrayElement.enumCase(value.payload, typeName: value.typeName)
		end 'enumCaseElement'

		if value.tag != ValueTypeTag.integer 'notAnInteger'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("a top-level array literal element of type '{typeTagName(value.tag)}' — a constant array's elements are integers or String literals", line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'notAnInteger'

		return ConstArrayElement.integer(value.payload)
	end 'evalConstArrayElement'

	// Is the element STARTING at `index` exactly one token — i.e. is the next SIGNIFICANT token the `,` or `]`
	// that ends it? The element-level twin of `evalConstInitializer`'s `initEnd == initStart + 1`, and it is
	// there for the identical reason: one token is the whole managed test, so an interpolation (several tokens)
	// and every trailing form stay out of the String arm and reach the walk that rejects them.
	//
	// ⭐ **BOTH array-literal walks ask it, and they ask the SAME question about DIFFERENT token kinds** — the
	// top-level one about a `"…"` (is this element a bare String literal?), the body one about an int literal
	// (is this element a bare integer, so the record may be created before it is evaluated?). Each caller has
	// already matched the kind; what neither can settle alone is whether the token is the WHOLE element, and
	// that is this. A second copy of it would be a second answer to "where does an element end".
	function arrayElementIsSingleToken(index TokenIndex) returns bool
		let after = self.kindAt(self.indexPastNewlines(index + 1))
		return after == TokenKind.comma or after == TokenKind.rightBracket
	end 'arrayElementIsSingleToken'

	// Take the managed-literal token the caller has just recognized and step past it. It cannot be
	// `consumeConst(kind)`: the caller has already matched the kind, and this is used in ARGUMENT
	// position, where a two-statement read-then-advance cannot be written inline.
	function consumeManagedLiteral() returns Token
		let tok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		return tok
	end 'consumeManagedLiteral'

	// ⭐ The two decoders below are the ones the EXPRESSION path uses (`decodeEscapes`,
	// `decodeByteStringLiteral`), so a literal means the same thing in a `let` as it does in a function
	// body — the property the scalar evaluator's header states for numbers, applied to bytes. Each is
	// wrapped for the evaluator's error type, for `consumeConst`'s reason: a `ParseError` raised here is
	// a defect in THIS initializer and must be recorded on its declaration, not thrown at whatever file
	// happens to be parsing.

	function decodeConstStringLiteral(tok Token) returns ByteArray throws ConstEvalError
		match decodeEscapes(tok.value) 'decoded'
			bytes(buf) then return buf
			unknownEscape(text) then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("the escape sequence '{text}' in a string literal is not a recognized escape", line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
			badDigitEscape(text, offset, marker) then throw ConstEvalError.defective(invalidDigitEscapeError(text, kind: LiteralEscapeKind.stringInterpolation, offset: offset, marker: marker, tok: tok))
		end 'decoded'
	end 'decodeConstStringLiteral'

	function decodeConstByteStringLiteral(tok Token) returns ByteArray throws ConstEvalError
		return try decodeByteStringLiteral(tok.value, tok: tok) otherwise (err) 'rejected'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'rejected'
	end 'decodeConstByteStringLiteral'

	// ⚠ A PARTLY-FOLDED INITIALIZER IS AN ERROR, NOT A TRUNCATION. The walk stops at the first token
	// it cannot fold; without this check the rest of the line is silently DISCARDED and the constant
	// quietly becomes whatever the prefix came to. It is the one failure mode the token-range model
	// has that a tree model does not, and the bootstrap guards it in the same place and words
	// (`ExpectConstInitializerFullyConsumed`, 2-Parser.cs:4979-4987).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **ONE RULE, TWO SENTENCES — and `deferred` reaches the WORDING and never the verdict**, exactly
	// as `nested` does at `requireFactoryReturnsARecord`. The rule is that an initializer is its WHOLE token
	// range, and it cannot differ by path; what the leftover MEANS does:
	//
	//   * a FOLDED initializer has already produced its value, so the leftover is the part that could not be
	//     folded and the complaint is about CONSTANTNESS (`specs/byte-string-literal.md` pins
	//     *"'.toByteArray()' cannot be evaluated at compile time"*).
	//   * a DEFERRED one folds nothing — the whole of it is a call `__module_init` will make — so there is no
	//     constant to complain about. Its grammar simply ENDED, and the leftover is a token standing where
	//     the line should have. `specs/lazy-static.md` and `specs/top-level-let.md` both pin that sentence,
	//     and the runnable oracle emits it from the same position (MEASURED: `static var b = Box.create() zzz`
	//     is `E2010 Expected 'end of global initializer' but got 'zzz'` there).
	function requireInitializerConsumed(decl TopLevelDecl, deferred bool) throws ConstEvalError
		if self.pos >= decl.initEnd 'fullyConsumed'
			return
		end 'fullyConsumed'

		let tok = self.current()
		if deferred 'runsBeforeMain'
			// The phrase is written out here exactly as its sibling `"end of default value"` is
			// (`parseParameterDefaultHelper`): `Lexer.expectationDisplayText`'s header names these two and
			// `'interpolation end'` as canonical's three hand-typed expectation PHRASES, each with one call
			// site. A named constant would be a second spelling of a sentence nothing else says.
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unexpectedToken("end of global initializer", got: tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'runsBeforeMain'

		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.nonConstantInitializer(String.from(decl.name), leftover: self.tokenTextFrom(self.pos, endIndex: decl.initEnd), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
	end 'requireInitializerConsumed'

	// The source text of a token range, as the diagnostic prints it — **laid out at the COLUMNS the author
	// wrote them at**, so `.toByteArray()` prints as `.toByteArray()` and `1 + 2` prints as `1 + 2`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT USED TO SPACE-JOIN UNCONDITIONALLY, AND THAT IS NOT WHAT THE DIAGNOSTIC PROMISES.** The
	// message names the text it could not evaluate, and `. toByteArray ( )` is not text anybody wrote —
	// the canonical corpus pins `'.toByteArray()'` (`specs/byte-string-literal.md`), which the reference
	// produces by slicing its source buffer. shv2's parser holds TOKENS and no source buffer, and its
	// punctuation tokens carry no byte offsets (`Token.startByte` is 0 for every punctuator), so the
	// column is the only positional fact available for every token — and it is enough: writing each
	// token's spelling at its own column reproduces the line.
	//
	// ⚠ The cursor advances by the SPELLING's length, and that is the source width for every token —
	// including a quoted literal, whose delimiters `tokenSourceSpelling` restores. Reading `value`
	// directly used to under-advance a string literal by two and pad the next gap two spaces wider.
	//
	// ⚠ **A TOKEN'S `value` IS ITS LEXEME ONLY WHERE THE KIND DOES NOT ALREADY IMPLY ONE.** The lexer
	// fills it for words and literals — keywords included — and leaves it EMPTY for punctuation, whose
	// spelling is `TokenKind.rawValue` (the same text `consume` puts in a mismatch). Reading `value`
	// alone therefore printed NOTHING for a leftover made of punctuation — `var xs = [1]]` and
	// `var a = 1)` both reported `''`, a diagnostic that promises to name the text it could not
	// evaluate and then names nothing at all.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS ALSO THE MATCH-PATTERN DIAGNOSTICS' RENDERER**, which is why it is not private to the
	// const evaluator: a duplicate pattern and a single-value range both name a span of source
	// (`-5`, `'a' upto 'b'`), and a second loop over the same tokens with its own spelling rule is the
	// drift `tokenDisplayText`'s header warns about — it does not fail loudly, it quietly names the
	// offending token something the source never wrote.
	function tokenTextFrom(start TokenIndex, endIndex TokenIndex) returns String
		var text = ""
		var cursor = self.tokenAt(start).column
		for i in start upto endIndex 'each'
			let tok = self.tokenAt(i)
			// A token on a LATER line restarts the layout: one space stands in for the newline, and the
			// cursor is re-seeded from that token so the rest of its line lays out against its own columns.
			if tok.column < cursor 'wrappedToANewLine'
				text = "{text} "
				cursor = tok.column
			end 'wrappedToANewLine'
			while cursor < tok.column 'padToItsColumn'
				text = "{text} "
				cursor = cursor + 1
			end 'padToItsColumn'

			let spelling = tokenSourceSpelling(tok)
			text = "{text}{spelling}"
			cursor = cursor + spelling.count()
		end 'each'

		return text
	end 'tokenTextFrom'

	// The precedence-climbing core, mirroring `parseBinary`'s shape over values instead of ops. It
	// reads the SAME precedence table, so `1 + 2 * 3` is 7 in a constant for the same reason it is 7
	// in a function.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **BECAUSE IT READS THAT TABLE, EVERY TOKEN `infixPrecedence` ADMITS NEEDS AN ARM IN *BOTH*
	// CLIMBERS — AND ADDING `is` TO THE TABLE WITHOUT ONE HERE PANICKED THE COMPILER ON ORDINARY SOURCE.**
	// MEASURED: `let X = 1 is 2` reached `foldConstBinOp`, whose `comparisonOperator` test is false for `is`
	// by design, and fell to `infixMaxonOp`'s
	// *"token 'is' is not a supported binary operator — infixPrecedence must gate this call"*. The panic
	// message named the invariant, and the invariant is this: **`infixPrecedence` is a shared decision with
	// two readers, so a token admitted there is a claim made about two loops.** Before the table changed,
	// `is` returned `NotAnInfixOperator`, this loop stopped, and the author got a positioned error.
	function evalConstBinary(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray, minPrec InfixPrecedence) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		var left = try self.evalConstUnary(stack)

		while true 'climb'
			let opTok = self.current()
			let prec = infixPrecedence(opTok.kind)
			if prec == NotAnInfixOperator or prec < minPrec 'stop'
				break
			end 'stop'

			// ⭐ **REFERENCE IDENTITY HAS NO CONSTANT READING, AND IT IS REFUSED AT THE SAME POINT IN THE CLIMB
			// `parseBinary` HANDLES IT** — so the two loops special-case the token in the same place and cannot
			// drift again about which tokens the shared table admits.
			//
			// The sentence is the emitting path's own E3068, which is exact rather than convenient: a top-level
			// constant folds integers, bools and floats and nothing else (`constantValueType`), so EVERY operand
			// that can reach here is a value with no object identity — precisely what that diagnostic says. The
			// `not` is PEEKED rather than consumed, only so the message names the form the author wrote.
			if identityOperator(opTok.kind) 'referenceIdentity'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.refIdentityOnPrimitive(referenceIdentityOpSpelling(self.peekKind() == TokenKind.not), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
			end 'referenceIdentity'

			self.advance()
			let rhsStart = self.pos
			let right = try self.evalConstBinary(stack, minPrec: prec + 1)
			left = try self.foldConstBinOp(left, right: right, opTok: opTok, countStart: rhsStart)
		end 'climb'

		return left
	end 'evalConstBinary'

	// Apply one binary operator to two constants, under the very rules the emitting path applies.
	//
	// **`and`/`or`/`xor` NEED NO SPECIAL CASE HERE, AND THAT IS NOT LUCK.** A bool's payload IS 0 or 1
	// (TypeRules' `BoolTrueValue`), so the BITWISE fold of two bools is already the LOGICAL answer —
	// `1 and 1` is 1, `0 or 1` is 1 — and `wordOpResultTag` supplies the `boolean` tag that says which
	// reading it was. The bootstrap writes the two readings out separately (`lhs is bool lb && rhs is
	// bool rb ? lb && rb : ll & rl`, 2-Parser.cs:4997-5031) and computes the identical answer both
	// ways. What is lost by folding rather than branching is only the ELISION, and a constant has no
	// side effect to elide.
	function foldConstBinOp(left ConstValue, right ConstValue, opTok Token, countStart TokenIndex) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		if comparisonOperator(opTok.kind) 'comparison'
			try self.requireConstComparable(left, right: right, opTok: opTok)
			let cmpOp = infixCmpOp(opTok.kind)

			// A float comparison folds with the host's native f64 (`foldFloatCompare`), NEVER over the
			// raw bit patterns: two negatives order opposite to their patterns, so an integer compare of
			// `-1.0 > -2.0` would answer backwards. Both operands are float here — `requireConstComparable`
			// rejects a mixed int/float compare — so `operandsAreFloat` true means BOTH sides carry bits.
			if operandsAreFloat(left.tag, right: right.tag) 'float'
				return ConstValue.create(foldFloatCompare(cmpOp, lhs: left.payload, rhs: right.payload), tag: ValueTypeTag.boolean)
			end 'float'

			return ConstValue.create(foldIntCompare(cmpOp, lhs: left.payload, rhs: right.payload), tag: ValueTypeTag.boolean)
		end 'comparison'

		let op = infixMaxonOp(opTok.kind)
		try self.requireConstOperands(op, left: left, right: right, opTok: opTok)

		if shiftOperator(op) 'shift'
			return try self.foldConstShift(left, count: right, op: op, countStart: countStart)
		end 'shift'

		// Float arithmetic (`+ - * /`) folds with the host's native f64 (`foldFloatBinOp`), an INT
		// operand of a mixed `1 + 2.0` first PROMOTED to its f64 value (`constFloatBits`). `mod` and the
		// bit ops are integral-only, so `requireConstOperands` has already turned a float away from them
		// above — a float pair here is always one of the four SSE-backed operators, and its result is
		// always a `float` (`arithResultTag` promotes), so the tag is not re-asked of `binOpResultTag`.
		if operandsAreFloat(left.tag, right: right.tag) 'float'
			match foldFloatBinOp(op, lhs: self.constFloatBits(left), rhs: self.constFloatBits(right)) 'f'
				folded(value) then return ConstValue.create(value, tag: ValueTypeTag.float)
				// A float `/ 0`. shv2's own `divsd` yields ±inf/nan at run time, but a CONSTANT folds with
				// the host's f64 — which faults on `/ 0` — and this rung does no software IEEE, so the
				// constant is rejected. A DOCUMENTED divergence from the runtime, and its OWN message
				// rather than the integer `constantDivisionByZero` one, whose "no run time to trap in"
				// would tell a user who expected inf the wrong story (a float `/0` does not trap).
				notFoldable then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("`/` by zero in a float constant initializer: a constant is folded with the host's f64, which faults on division by zero (shv2's own float division would yield an infinity or NaN at run time), so the constant has no value", line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
			end 'f'
		end 'float'

		match foldIntBinOp(op, lhs: left.payload, rhs: right.payload) 'folded'
			folded(value) then return ConstValue.create(value, tag: binOpResultTag(op, left: left.tag, right: right.tag))
			// `foldIntBinOp` declines exactly one family here — a division or modulo whose answer is a
			// TRAP rather than a number (`1 / 0`, `i64.min / -1`); the shifts it also declines are
			// handled above (`shiftOperator`). A runtime `x / 0` panics, but a top-level constant has no
			// run time to panic IN: it is folded before the program exists, so there is no answer to
			// give and no later pass that could give one.
			notFoldable then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.constantDivisionByZero(opTok.kind.rawValue, line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
		end 'folded'
	end 'foldConstBinOp'

	// ⭐ **THE COMPILE-TIME `cvtsi2sd`** — the f64 BIT PATTERN of a constant the parser is holding, and the
	// CONSTANT half of the `TypeRules.widensIntToFloat` obligation (its SSA half is `promoteToFloat`). A
	// float carries its bits already; an INT is promoted to its f64 value first, so `1 + 2.0` folds to 3.0
	// and not to a summed bit pattern, and a `as float = 3` field default fills its slot with 3.0 rather
	// than with eight bytes of two's complement. `0.0 + payload` is the host's own int→f64 conversion;
	// `floatToBits` captures the result.
	//
	// Idempotent, exactly as `promoteToFloat` is, so neither caller — the float FOLD or the field DEFAULT —
	// has to work out which side it is holding.
	function constFloatBits(value ConstValue) returns ParsedInt
		if tagIsFloat(value.tag) 'alreadyFloat'
			return value.payload
		end 'alreadyFloat'

		return __Builtins.floatToBits(0.0 + value.payload)
	end 'constFloatBits'

	// ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE, asked of a constant. Every part of the answer comes from TypeRules — the fill,
	// the opcode, the legality of the count, and the fold — so a shift in a `let` and a shift in a
	// function body cannot disagree. `1 shl 64` is 0 in both, and `1 shl -1` is E2054 in both.
	//
	// `leftOperandIsUnsigned` is FALSE, and asked rather than assumed. A top-level constant carries no
	// ranged type — its initializer is a bare expression and shv2 infers `int` or `bool`, nothing else
	// — so its left operand is signed by construction. The rule is still routed through
	// `shiftFillsWithSign` so that the day a constant CAN be unsigned, this answers it the same way
	// the expression parser does instead of having quietly hard-coded the fill. The bootstrap makes
	// exactly this call, and says so at 2-Parser.cs:5062-5068.
	function foldConstShift(left ConstValue, count ConstValue, op MaxonBinOp, countStart TokenIndex) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		if shiftCountIsNegative(count.payload) 'negative'
			let anchor = self.shiftCountAnchor(countStart, countEnd: self.pos)
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.shiftCountNegative(count.payload, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column))
		end 'negative'

		let shiftOp = shiftOpcodeFor(op, signFills: shiftFillsWithSign(op, leftOperandIsUnsigned: false))

		// The TAG is ASKED of `binOpResultTag` — the same decider, handed the same rewritten opcode,
		// that `emitShift`'s `emitBinOp` asks — and not restated as a literal `integer` here. It
		// answers `integer` for every shift today, so this is not a bug being fixed; it is the second
		// decider not being created. `emitShortCircuit` records what restating one costs: a word
		// operator's result type ended up with one decider per path and nothing making them agree,
		// and the day the rule moves, the path that ASSERTS goes on asserting. A wrong tag is not a
		// compile error, it is a wrong answer at whatever reads the tag next.
		return ConstValue.create(evalShift(left.payload, count: count.payload, op: shiftOp), tag: binOpResultTag(shiftOp, left: left.tag, right: count.tag))
	end 'foldConstShift'

	// A comparison's operands must AGREE — the same class-strict rule `emitCompare` enforces, asked of
	// the same `typesAgree`, and reported under the same code and text.
	// The COMPARISON rule, asked of two constants — `comparableOperands`, the same decider
	// `emitCompare` asks, so `let X = 3.5 > 2` and `f > i` in a body cannot come to disagree about
	// whether a float may be compared with an int. It used to ask `typesAgree`, which admits the
	// pair (float and int agree, and must), and so would have folded the mixed compare that the
	// expression path rejects.
	function requireConstComparable(left ConstValue, right ConstValue, opTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		if comparableOperands(left.tag, right: right.tag) 'comparable'
			return
		end 'comparable'

		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.comparisonTypeMismatch(typeTagName(left.tag), right: typeTagName(right.tag), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
	end 'requireConstComparable'

	// Every other binary operator's operand rule, asked of `requireBinOpOperands` — the one classifier
	// the emitting path asks. It throws a `ParseError`; the evaluator's failures are wrapped, so the
	// two paths reject `let X = 4 + true` and `y = 4 + true` with the identical diagnostic.
	function requireConstOperands(op MaxonBinOp, left ConstValue, right ConstValue, opTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		try requireBinOpOperands(op, left: left.tag, right: right.tag, opTok: opTok) otherwise (err) 'rejected'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'rejected'
	end 'requireConstOperands'

	// A prefix `-` or `not`, mirroring `parseUnary` over values. Both readings of `not` come from
	// `notOperatorFor`, so a constant `not` complements exactly what a runtime `not` complements.
	function evalConstUnary(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		if self.at(TokenKind.minus) 'neg'
			let opTok = self.current()
			self.advance()

			// ⭐ A leading `-` on a FLOAT LITERAL is part of the literal — a sign flip (`negatedFloatBits`),
			// NOT `0.0 - x`, which would lose `-0.0`'s sign. This mirrors `parseUnary`'s negated-literal
			// fold and shares the ONE sign-flip spelling, so `-2.5` means the same in a `let` and a body.
			// Trailing `as` casts then apply exactly as to a primary (as `parseCast` wraps `parseUnary`).
			if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'negatedFloatLiteral'
				let tok = self.current()
				self.advance()
				return try self.applyConstCasts(try self.evalConstFloatLiteral(tok, negated: true))
			end 'negatedFloatLiteral'

			let operand = try self.evalConstPostfix(stack)
			if not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.numeric, tag: operand.tag) 'notANumber'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.nonNumericNegation(typeTagName(operand.tag), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
			end 'notANumber'

			if tagIsFloat(operand.tag) 'float'
				// A NON-literal float negation (a float const REFERENCE, or `-(expr)`) is `0.0 - x` — the
				// subtract-from-zero the runtime's `emitFloatNegation` emits (x64 has no XMM negate),
				// routed through the ONE float sub fold so the constant and the codegen cannot disagree.
				// `-(0.0)` is therefore `+0.0`, exactly as `emitFloatNegation` and the bootstrap give it.
				// A subtract never divides, so the fold never declines.
				match foldFloatBinOp(MaxonBinOp.sub, lhs: FloatZeroBits, rhs: operand.payload) 'negated'
					folded(value) then return ConstValue.create(value, tag: ValueTypeTag.float)
					notFoldable then panic("evalConstUnary: foldFloatBinOp declined `0.0 - x` — only division declines, and negation is a subtraction")
				end 'negated'
			end 'float'

			return ConstValue.create(try self.foldConstUnary(MaxonUnaryOp.neg, operand: operand.payload, opTok: opTok), tag: operand.tag)
		end 'neg'

		if self.at(TokenKind.not) 'not'
			let opTok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			let operand = try self.evalConstUnary(stack)
			return try self.foldConstNot(operand, opTok: opTok)
		end 'not'

		return try self.evalConstPostfix(stack)
	end 'evalConstUnary'

	// A constant primary followed by any trailing `as <Type>` casts. A cast to a ranged-int alias or a
	// primitive is a compile-time NO-OP on the value's bytes (`0 as Integer` is `0`), so the target type
	// is consumed and the value passes through unchanged — the same shape the emitting path uses for a
	// numeric range cast. ⚠ NO-OP ON THE BYTES IS NOT UNCHECKED: `applyConstCast` checks the target's
	// KIND (`requireConstCastTargetKind` — E3011 for a name that denotes nothing, E3009 for a pair with
	// no conversion) and its RANGE (`checkConstCastRange` — `let X = 256 as Octet` is E3005), through the
	// same deciders the emitting path uses. The value passing through unchanged is the CODEGEN, not the
	// verdict.
	function evalConstPostfix(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		return try self.applyConstCasts(try self.evalConstPrimary(stack))
	end 'evalConstPostfix'

	// Consume any trailing `as <Type>` casts on an already-evaluated constant. Split out so the
	// negated-float-literal path applies the same casts a primary gets (mirroring `parseCast`, which
	// wraps `parseUnary`'s negated literal in the identical cast loop).
	function applyConstCasts(value ConstValue) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		var result = value
		while self.at(TokenKind.as) 'cast'
			result = try self.applyConstCast(result)
		end 'cast'
		return result
	end 'applyConstCasts'

	// Consume one `as <Type>` cast and return the value (unchanged on the bytes; see `evalConstPostfix`).
	// The target is a named alias or a primitive keyword — a single token at this rung (a ranged
	// `int(low to high)` cast is a follow-up). When the target is a ranged INT alias and the value is an
	// integer LITERAL, the range is checked HERE — a top-level `let` is not a function, so it records no
	// site for `InsertRangeChecks` to guard, and the compile-time E3005 that a body cast gets from that
	// pass must be raised by the evaluator instead.
	//
	// ⚠ **E3010 IS NOT ASKED HERE, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.** The unneeded-cast rule
	// needs the SOURCE's declared alias NAME on both sides (`Parser.reportUnneededCast`), and this evaluator
	// carries a constant as a `(payload, tag)` pair with no name in it — it emits no ops, touches no
	// artifact and has no interner of its own, which is the same reason `requireConstCastTargetKind` is
	// handed the filler `UnnamedTypeId`. Closing it means carrying an alias name through the const arena,
	// beside the payload and the tag, at every fold. The other two verdicts a body cast gets ARE asked, and
	// through the body path's own deciders, so the two spellings agree on everything a tag can settle.
	function applyConstCast(value ConstValue) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		let asTok = self.current()
		try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.as)
		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) or self.at(TokenKind.int) or self.at(TokenKind.bool) or self.at(TokenKind.float) 'targetType'
			let targetTok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			// This walk is the ONLY reader of a top-level initializer's tokens — the real parse skips
			// them — so it is the only thing that can tell the E3062 check the alias was named here.
			// Recorded before the kind gate below, so a target that is about to be REJECTED still counts
			// as a mention and the file is not told about two unrelated mistakes at once.
			self.signatures.recordConstInitializerTypeName(targetTok.value, declFilePath: self.filePath)
			try self.requireConstCastTargetKind(value, targetTok: targetTok, asTok: asTok)
			try self.checkConstCastRange(value, targetTok: targetTok, asTok: asTok)
			return value
		end 'targetType'
		let tok = self.current()
		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.nonConstantInitializer("cast target", leftover: String.from(tok.value), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
	end 'applyConstCast'

	// ⭐ **`castTargetTag`'S CONST TWIN — THE SECOND PLACE A CAST TARGET IS WRITTEN.** A top-level
	// `let X = 5 as T` never reaches the expression path: it is folded here, by a walk driven from
	// `ProgramSignatures.evaluateInitializers` before any file is parsed. Checking only `applyCast`
	// would have left `let SENTINEL = 5 as Bogus` evaporating exactly as it always had — half a fact,
	// which is worse than none, because the half that works makes the other half look tested.
	//
	// It asks the identical authority and raises the identical two diagnostics, anchored on the same
	// `as` token, so the two spellings of one mistake are reported identically.
	//
	// A PRIMITIVE-KEYWORD target (`int`/`bool`/`float`) denotes itself and is never `named`, so only
	// the identifier form has anything to look up. ⚠ The id handed to the authority is the filler
	// `UnnamedTypeId`: this walk STORES no type — it reads only the target's KIND — and the id rides
	// exactly one arm (a declared `type`'s `structRef`), whose TAG is all this gate reads. It is also
	// the only id available: the const evaluator "emits no ops and touches no artifact", so it has no
	// interner of its own, and minting into the signature index's would mutate a memo mid-build.
	function requireConstCastTargetKind(value ConstValue, targetTok Token, asTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		if targetTok.kind != TokenKind.identifier 'primitiveKeyword'
			return
		end 'primitiveKeyword'

		// The VISIBILITY half, asked first for the reason `requireRepresentableRange` orders its two
		// tests: a name this file may not see is a fact about the name, and every question below it is
		// about a type the file was never entitled to reach. It THROWS where the body cast records —
		// see `Parser.recordHiddenCastTarget` for why the two deliveries differ and what they share.
		if self.aliasHiddenFromThisFile(self.signatures.aliasOf(targetTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath)) 'hiddenAlias'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.hiddenCastTargetType(targetTok.line, column: targetTok.column))
		end 'hiddenAlias'

		// Both halves are the EMITTING path's own deciders and both raise a `ParseError`; `defective` is
		// the one wrapper every parser diagnostic reaches this walk's `ConstEvalError` channel through, so
		// the const and the body spelling of one mistake cannot report differently. Exactly
		// `requireConstOperands`' shape, for exactly its reason.
		let targetTag = try self.denotedCastTargetTag(targetTok.value, id: UnnamedTypeId, asTok: asTok) otherwise (err) 'undeclared'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'undeclared'

		try self.requireCastKindsConvert(value.tag, targetTag: targetTag, asTok: asTok) otherwise (err) 'incompatibleKinds'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'incompatibleKinds'
	end 'requireConstCastTargetKind'

	// Compile-time range check for a constant `as` cast — the top-level `let` half of the narrowing
	// E3005, mirroring `InsertRangeChecks.needsRuntimeGuard`'s literal path. It fires only for a
	// NUMERIC literal cast into a ranged alias: a bare `int`/`bool`/`float` primitive target has no
	// range, and a bool const carries no number to test. The out-of-range verdict is `literalInRange`'s
	// — the same decider the body path uses, so the const and runtime casts cannot disagree — and the
	// message is the shared one, anchored at the `as` token as the body cast is.
	function checkConstCastRange(value ConstValue, targetTok Token, asTok Token) throws ConstEvalError
		if targetTok.kind != TokenKind.identifier or not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.numeric, tag: value.tag) 'notCheckable'
			return
		end 'notCheckable'

		match self.signatures.aliasOf(targetTok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath) 'a'
			undeclared then break 'a'
			declared(alias) then try self.reportConstCastIfOutOfRange(value, alias: alias, anchor: asTok)
		end 'a'
	end 'checkConstCastRange'

	// Raise the E3005 when `value` is outside `alias`.
	//
	// ⚠ THE CONST AND THE ALIAS MUST BE IN THE SAME DOMAIN. A float const's payload is an IEEE-754 bit
	// pattern and an integer const's is a number, so testing one against the other's bounds compares
	// numbers that were never the same kind of thing. A MISMATCH IS NOT A DEFECT to report here — an
	// int const into a float alias is a promotion, which is legal and simply unchecked — so the
	// mismatched pair is skipped, exactly as `InsertRangeChecks.needsRuntimeGuard` falls through to the
	// runtime guard for the same pair, and exactly as the bootstrap does.
	//
	// ⚠ The anchor is the CAST's `as` token for a cast and the FIELD NAME for a struct-literal slot (W116) —
	// each is where its own compiler reports the same refusal — so the parameter names the ROLE and not one
	// caller's token.
	function reportConstCastIfOutOfRange(value ConstValue, alias RangedTypeAlias, anchor Token) throws ConstEvalError
		let isFloat = aliasIsFloat(alias)
		if tagIsFloat(value.tag) != isFloat 'domainMismatch'
			return
		end 'domainMismatch'

		if literalInRange(value.payload, low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat) 'inRange'
			return
		end 'inRange'

		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.constantCastOutOfRange(value.payload, aliasName: String.from(alias.name), low: alias.low, high: alias.high, isFloat: isFloat, line: anchor.line, column: anchor.column))
	end 'reportConstCastIfOutOfRange'

	// Consume `kind` in the constant evaluator, wrapping a mismatch as a `ConstEvalError`.
	function consumeConstToken(kind TokenKind) throws ConstEvalError
		if not self.at(kind) 'mismatch'
			let tok = self.current()
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unexpectedToken(kind.rawValue, got: tokenDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'mismatch'
		self.advance()
	end 'consumeConstToken'

	// Which `not` this is, and the fold for it — one question, asked of `notOperatorFor`.
	//
	// `logicalNot` cannot come through `foldIntUnaryOp`, which declines it: that folder's domain is
	// the INTEGERS, where a logical not is a parser bug. Over the bool domain it is meaningful and
	// total, which is `foldLogicalNot`. The two live side by side in TypeRules, and this is the split
	// they exist for.
	function foldConstNot(operand ConstValue, opTok Token) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		match notOperatorFor(operand.tag) 'which'
			op(kind, resultTag) then return ConstValue.create(try self.foldConstUnary(kind, operand: operand.payload, opTok: opTok), tag: resultTag)
			// Unreachable from a constant: `unresolved` is a call to a function no file declares and
			// `void` a call to one returning nothing, and NO call reaches this evaluator at all — a call
			// in an initializer is rejected as E2045 before its type is ever asked for. It is kept as a
			// case rather than a panic because it is the truthful answer if a second route to an untyped
			// operand ever appears.
			unknownOperandType then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.notOnUnknownType(opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
			// `not 3.14` — reachable here, unlike the two above: a float literal IS a constant.
			// Same rule and same message as the expression path's, because it is the same rule.
			nonIntegralOperand then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.nonIntegralOperands(opTok.kind.rawValue, operand: typeTagName(operand.tag), line: opTok.line, column: opTok.column))
		end 'which'
	end 'foldConstNot'

	// The unary folds, both routed through TypeRules. `neg` and `bitNot` are `foldIntUnaryOp`'s;
	// `logicalNot` is `foldLogicalNot`'s (see `foldConstNot`).
	function foldConstUnary(op MaxonUnaryOp, operand ParsedInt, opTok Token) returns ParsedInt throws ConstEvalError
		if op == MaxonUnaryOp.logicalNot 'logical'
			return foldLogicalNot(operand)
		end 'logical'

		match foldIntUnaryOp(op, operand: operand) 'folded'
			folded(value) then return value
			// `foldIntUnaryOp` declines only `logicalNot`, which the guard above already took. Reaching
			// here means a unary operator was added to the enum without being given a fold.
			notFoldable then panic("Parser.foldConstUnary: TypeRules declined to fold '{op.rawValue}' over the integer {operand} at {opTok.line}:{opTok.column} — only `logicalNot` is declinable there, and it is handled above")
		end 'folded'
	end 'foldConstUnary'

	// A constant PRIMARY: an integer literal, `true`/`false`, a character literal, a parenthesized
	// sub-expression, a type-qualified bound (`u16.max`), or a reference to another top-level
	// constant. Every literal decoder is the one the emitting path uses, so a constant reads a
	// literal exactly as a function body reads it.
	function evalConstPrimary(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		if self.at(TokenKind.intLiteral) 'intLit'
			return try self.evalConstIntLiteral()
		end 'intLit'

		if self.at(TokenKind.floatLiteral) 'floatLit'
			let tok = self.current()
			self.advance()
			return try self.evalConstFloatLiteral(tok, negated: false)
		end 'floatLit'

		if self.at(TokenKind.true) or self.at(TokenKind.false) 'boolLit'
			return self.evalConstBoolLiteral()
		end 'boolLit'

		if self.at(TokenKind.charLiteral) 'charLit'
			return try self.evalConstCharLiteral()
		end 'charLit'

		if self.at(TokenKind.leftParen) 'group'
			return try self.evalConstGroup(stack)
		end 'group'

		if self.at(TokenKind.identifier) 'namedPrimary'
			return try self.evalConstNamed(stack)
		end 'namedPrimary'

		let tok = self.current()
		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.expectedExpression(expressionOperandDisplayText(tok), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
	end 'evalConstPrimary'

	function evalConstIntLiteral() returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		let tok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		let value = try self.intLiteralValue(tok, negated: false) otherwise (err) 'overflow'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'overflow'

		return ConstValue.create(value, tag: ValueTypeTag.integer)
	end 'evalConstIntLiteral'

	// A float literal in a constant initializer — `let PI = 3.14`, `var zeroA = 0.0`, and the operands
	// a fold like `let X = 1.0 + 2.0` computes on. It carries the IEEE-754 bit pattern in the same
	// `ParsedInt` payload every other constant uses, exactly as `parseFloatLiteral` does in an
	// expression, through the same `floatLiteralBits` — so the bits of a float in a `let` and a float
	// in a body cannot differ.
	//
	// `negated` folds a leading `-` INTO the literal (`negatedFloatBits`, a sign flip), the same way
	// `parseNegatedNumericLiteral` does — the reason `-0.0` keeps its sign where `0.0 - x` would lose
	// it. `evalConstUnary` is its only negated caller; every other float literal reads un-negated. The
	// caller has already consumed `tok`, mirroring `evalConstIntLiteral`'s call to `intLiteralValue`.
	function evalConstFloatLiteral(tok Token, negated bool) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		let bits = try self.floatLiteralBits(tok) otherwise (err) 'rejected'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'rejected'

		return ConstValue.create(negatedFloatBits(bits) if negated else bits, tag: ValueTypeTag.float)
	end 'evalConstFloatLiteral'

	function evalConstBoolLiteral() returns ConstValue
		let isTrue = self.at(TokenKind.true)
		self.advance()
		if isTrue 'true'
			return ConstValue.create(BoolTrueValue, tag: ValueTypeTag.boolean)
		end 'true'

		return ConstValue.create(BoolFalseValue, tag: ValueTypeTag.boolean)
	end 'evalConstBoolLiteral'

	// ⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL `let c = 'x'` IS REFUSED, WHATEVER THE CHARACTER'S WIDTH.** A module-scope binding
	// folds to a compile-time constant, and a `Character` is a heap-shaped record the constant evaluator has
	// no representation for (`SignatureIndex.globalStdType` says the same from the other end). EVERY character
	// literal is one (`parseCharLiteral`), so the one-byte case that used to fold to its byte folds no longer
	// — and that is the ruling's own consequence rather than a narrowing: the value the initializer denotes
	// is a record, and it read as an integer only because the literal did.
	//
	// ⚠ The refusal is PERMANENT, so it is E2004 and not E2015: nothing about this is a feature pending
	// implementation — a module-scope initializer IS a folded constant by definition, and a Character record
	// can never be one. E2015 said "not yet", which was the one thing the verdict does not mean. Both
	// reference compilers refuse a single-quoted top-level initializer under E2004 with this exact sentence
	// (v1 `Parser.maxon:3277`; MEASURED on the oracle, `let DASH = '-'` is *"Expected constant expression,
	// got '-'"*), so this agrees with them on the verdict and not merely on the wording.
	//
	// It still DECODES the literal first, so a malformed one is named for what is wrong with it rather than
	// swept into the constant complaint.
	function evalConstCharLiteral() returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		let tok = self.current()
		self.advance()
		try self.charLiteralBytes(tok) otherwise (err) 'malformed'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'malformed'

		throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.expectedConstantExpression(String.from(tok.value), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
	end 'evalConstCharLiteral'

	// `( … )` — grouping, re-entering the climb at the LOOSEST precedence exactly as
	// `parseParenthesizedExpression` does, so a parenthesis restores every operator.
	function evalConstGroup(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.leftParen)
		let value = try self.evalConstBinary(stack, minPrec: OrPrecedence)
		try self.consumeConst(TokenKind.rightParen)

		return value
	end 'evalConstGroup'

	// An identifier in a constant: a CALL (rejected), a type-qualified bound (`u16.max`), or a
	// reference to another top-level constant. The same two one-token lookaheads `parsePrimary`
	// splits these three forms with.
	function evalConstNamed(stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		let tok = self.current()

		// ⚠ REJECTED BEFORE THE CALL IS READ, not after. A call's answer needs the program to be
		// RUNNING, and a top-level `let` is folded before any of it exists — so there is nothing to
		// fold and no later pass that could supply one. Both reference compilers reject it here with
		// this exact text.
		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.leftParen 'call'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.functionCallInConstant(String.from(tok.value), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'call'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE SIZED-BOUND READING IS CLAIMED ONLY BY A SIZED TYPE, AND THAT ORDER IS THE FIX FOR A
		// DIAGNOSTIC THAT DESCRIBED A DIFFERENT PROGRAM** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`). `<ident> .`
		// used to enter `parseTypeQualifiedBound` unconditionally, so ANY dotted name in a constant —
		// `Database.create()`, `Colour.green`, `lib.inner.pick()` — was read as `i64.min` and refused with
		// **E2010 `Expected ''min' or 'max'' but got 'create'`**, a message about a grammar the author was
		// not writing in. The bound's base is a fixed roster of ten spellings (`sizedTypeBound`), so asking
		// FIRST whether the head is one of them costs one comparison and makes the misreading unreachable:
		// everything else now gets a sentence naming the real gap, which is that a constant initializer
		// cannot reach a member of a user type.
		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot and isSizedTypeName(tok.value) 'qualifiedBound'
			let bound = try self.parseTypeQualifiedBound(false) otherwise (err) 'badBound'
				throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
			end 'badBound'
			return ConstValue.create(bound.value, tag: ValueTypeTag.integer)
		end 'qualifiedBound'

		if self.peekKind() == TokenKind.dot 'dottedNonBound'
			let memberTok = self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2)

			// ⭐⭐ **A PAYLOAD-FREE ENUM CASE *IS* A CONSTANT — its value is its TAG, a number fixed at
			// declaration.** `let JumpTableBaseReg = X64Register.r10` (`Targets/X64/X64Backend.maxon:1144`) and
			// `let calleeSavedOrder = [X64Register.rbx, …]` are what the refusal below cost, six times across
			// shv2's own backends, on source the bootstrap compiles. Nothing here runs: `tagOfCase` reads the
			// layout the sweep already built.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE VALUE CARRIES THE ENUM'S NAME, because the number alone is a wrong answer about it.**
			// A bare `integer` cannot be passed where the enum is declared — MEASURED, `argument type mismatch
			// for 'r': expected 'Reg', got 'int'` — so the fold is tagged `named` and `ConstValue.typeName`
			// carries the spelling for the use site to intern (see that field for why bytes and not an id).
			//
			// ⛔⛔ **A BOXED UNION'S CASE IS NOT THIS ARM'S BUSINESS, AND THAT IS A TIER BOUNDARY RATHER THAN A
			// REFUSAL.** `isBoxed` means SOME case carries a payload, and then EVERY case — payload-free ones
			// included — is a heap box rather than a bare tag (`EnumLayout`'s own header), so its value is a
			// RECORD and this walk, which answers with a `ConstValue`, has no way to spell it. It is claimed one
			// tier up instead, by `evalConstFoldedValue`'s `atConstBoxedUnionCase` arm.
			//
			// ⚠ **THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY THE EXCLUSION WAS "THE RULE RATHER THAN A LIMIT OF THIS ARM" — that
			// a heap object cannot be a constant at all — and it was MEASURED FALSE.** The runnable oracle
			// compiles `let x = Boxed.plain` and runs it, building the box in its own `__module_init`; shv2's
			// own `Parser.sharedUnreadParamType` and `Project.NoPayloadStorage` are that shape. The claim was
			// false of shv2 too the moment an empty container and a `create()` factory were admitted — both are
			// heap objects — which is the tell that it described a tier, not a rule.
			if self.constEnumCaseOfLayout(tok, memberTok: memberTok, boxed: false) 'payloadFreeEnumCase'
				let layout = requireEnumLayout(self.signatures.enumOf(tok.value))
				self.advance()
				try self.consumeConstToken(TokenKind.dot)
				self.advance()

				return ConstValue.create(layout.tagOfCase(memberTok.value), tag: ValueTypeTag.named, typeName: tok.value)
			end 'payloadFreeEnumCase'
			// ⚠ **"FOLDED BEFORE ANY CODE RUNS" WAS THE OLD WORDING AND IT CONTRADICTED ITS OWN ADMITTED LIST.**
			// An empty container and a `create()`-style factory are HEAP RECORDS built by `__module_init`, so
			// nothing about them is folded and code does run — and the sentence was then used to justify
			// refusing a boxed union's case for being a heap object, which the oracle had compiled all along.
			// What a constant actually is, is SETTLED BEFORE `main`: folded to a number, or materialized by the
			// module initializer. The boundary this now describes is the real one.
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.unsupported("`{String.from(tok.value)}.{tokenDisplayText(memberTok)}` in a constant initializer — a constant is settled before `main`, either folded to a number or materialized by the module initializer, so it can name another top-level `let`, a literal, an empty container, a payload-free enum or union case, a `{String.from(CreateMethod)}()`-style factory at the TOP of an initializer, or a sized type's `.{String.from(MinBoundField)}`/`.{String.from(MaxBoundField)}`, and nothing else", line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'dottedNonBound'

		self.advance()
		return try self.evalConstNameRef(tok, stack: stack)
	end 'evalConstNamed'

	// ⭐ **DOES `<tok>.<memberTok>` NAME A CASE OF A DECLARED ENUM OR UNION WHOSE LAYOUT MATCHES `boxed`?**
	// The admission rule BOTH constant-initializer readers share, split out so a test and its fold cannot come
	// to disagree about which members exist — the shape `enumAccessorFieldAt` and its dispatch already use one
	// construct over.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO READERS DIFFER IN EXACTLY THIS BIT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A PARAMETER AND NOT A SECOND COPY.**
	// The scalar walk (`evalConstNamed`) asks `boxed: false` — an unboxed case's TAG *is* its value, so it
	// folds to a number. The record arm (`evalConstFoldedValue`) asks `boxed: true` — every case of a
	// payload-bearing union is a heap BOX, so its value is built by `__module_init` instead. Everything else
	// about the question is identical, and two near-copies of this lookup are precisely how the two would come
	// to admit different member sets.
	//
	// It asks the SWEEP's registry (`signatures.enumOf`), which is the only store that exists this early and
	// is the same one every other constant-time enum question in this evaluator would ask.
	function constEnumCaseOfLayout(tok Token, memberTok Token, boxed bool) returns bool
		let namedEnum = self.signatures.enumOf(tok.value)
		if not enumMatchIsDeclared(namedEnum) 'notAnEnum'
			return false
		end 'notAnEnum'

		let layout = requireEnumLayout(namedEnum)
		if layout.isBoxed() != boxed 'theOtherReadersBusiness'
			return false
		end 'theOtherReadersBusiness'

		return layout.hasCase(memberTok.value)
	end 'constEnumCaseOfLayout'

	// Does the cursor sit on `<BoxedUnion>.<case>`? The token shape AND the layout question, asked together so
	// the arm that claims the form and the walk that consumes it cannot part company about what was claimed.
	function atConstBoxedUnionCase() returns bool
		if not self.at(TokenKind.identifier) or self.peekKind() != TokenKind.dot or self.kindAt(self.pos + 2) != TokenKind.identifier 'notAQualifiedName'
			return false
		end 'notAQualifiedName'

		return self.constEnumCaseOfLayout(self.current(), memberTok: self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2), boxed: true)
	end 'atConstBoxedUnionCase'

	// Walk `<BoxedUnion>.<case>`, already claimed by the test above — which is why the three tokens are simply
	// stepped over rather than re-checked, and why this cannot fail.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE TWO SPELLINGS AND DERIVES NOTHING FROM THEM.** The tag's number, the box's size and
	// the slots to zero are read off the layout where the registry lives
	// (`ProgramSignatures.boxedUnionRecordOf`), so one declaration is read ONCE and a fold cannot hand the
	// synthesis a stale box shape. Bytes rather than interned ids, for `ConstValue.typeName`'s reason: this
	// evaluator is a throwaway parser built per declaration.
	function evalConstBoxedUnionCase() returns ConstFoldedValue
		let typeTok = self.current()
		let caseTok = self.tokenAt(self.pos + 2)
		self.advance()
		self.advance()
		self.advance()

		return ConstFoldedValue.boxedUnionCase(typeTok.value, caseName: caseTok.value)
	end 'evalConstBoxedUnionCase'

	// **THE FORWARD REFERENCE, AND THE CROSS-FILE ONE.** A name in an initializer resolves through the
	// whole-program arena, which recursively evaluates whatever it finds — so declaration ORDER does
	// not decide what a program means, and a constant in another file is as reachable as one on the
	// line above. The cycle detection is the arena's (`ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl`), which is
	// the only place that can see one.
	function evalConstNameRef(tok Token, stack TopLevelDeclIndexArray) returns ConstValue throws ConstEvalError
		match self.signatures.evaluateNamed(tok.value, readerFilePath: self.filePath, stack: stack) 'resolved'
			value(payload, tag, typeName) then return ConstValue.create(payload, tag: tag, typeName: typeName)
			// It exists and has no value. Its own declaration carries the diagnostic, so this one stays
			// quiet — see `TopLevelConstant.poisoned`.
			failed then throw ConstEvalError.dependencyFailed
			notFound then throw ConstEvalError.defective(ParseError.undefinedConstant(String.from(tok.value), line: tok.line, column: tok.column))
		end 'resolved'
	end 'evalConstNameRef'

	// `consume`, wrapped for the evaluator's error type. The evaluator cannot use `consume` directly:
	// it throws `ParseError`, and a `ParseError` raised HERE is a defect in THIS initializer that must
	// be recorded on its declaration rather than thrown at whatever file happens to be parsing.
	function consumeConst(kind TokenKind) returns Token throws ConstEvalError
		return try self.consume(kind) otherwise (err) 'unexpected'
			throw ConstEvalError.defective(err)
		end 'unexpected'
	end 'consumeConst'

	// ⭐ **#65 — IS `name` A METHOD OF THE ENCLOSING TYPE?** A bare call `name(…)` inside a method body resolves
	// to the sibling method `name` rather than a free function — and the method WINS when a free function of the
	// same name also exists (oracle-verified), which is why the free-call path asks this FIRST. False at file
	// scope, where there is no enclosing type and every call is free.
	//
	// ⭐⭐⭐ **A SIBLING IS ANY METHOD THE ENCLOSING TYPE HAS, AND A TOKEN WALK CANNOT SEE THEM ALL.**
	// `ensureSiblingReceivers` reads ONE declaration's member list, which is the right list only when every
	// method of the type is written in it. An `extension` breaks that in both directions, and both were
	// MEASURED as `E3004 call to undefined function` on programs whose `self.<name>()` spelling compiled:
	//
	//   • inside an `extension` body the walk starts at the EXTENSION's members, so the CONFORMER's own
	//     methods are invisible — a different declaration, possibly in a different file;
	//   • inside the TYPE's own body the walk sees only what the type wrote, so a method an `extension`
	//     published onto it is invisible the other way round.
	//
	// ⇒ the walk's miss falls through to the whole-program index, which is the only reader that has every
	// declaration under one `<Type>.<method>` key however many declarations contributed them. The walk keeps
	// its PRIORITY: it answers for the declaration the author is looking at, and it is the one that can tell
	// an interface REQUIREMENT (which the index does not file under the conformer at all) from a method.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PROBE IS ON THE MISS PATH ONLY**, and that is a cost decision rather than a semantic one: this
	// is asked at every bare call in the program, the join `qualifiedMethodName` allocates, and inside a type
	// body the overwhelming majority of bare calls hit the walk. A miss is a call to a free function, which
	// then pays one join and one map probe — linear in call sites, and the answer it buys is the difference
	// between resolving and E3004.
	function isImplicitSelfMethod(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if self.enclosingType.isEmpty() 'notInType'
			return false
		end 'notInType'
		self.ensureSiblingReceivers()
		if self.siblingReceivers.contains(name) 'declaredInTheBodyBeingParsed'
			return true
		end 'declaredInTheBodyBeingParsed'

		return self.signatures.declaresCallee(qualifiedMethodName(self.enclosingType, methodName: name))
	end 'isImplicitSelfMethod'

	// Is this bare call a member of the roster the enclosing declaration's OWN record serves? See `parseCall`'s
	// arm for why it outranks the implicit-self resolution. It must decide WITHOUT resolving anything: a name
	// the roster does not carry answers no, and only then is a receiver materialized.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE RECEIVER TEST IS PART OF THE QUESTION AND NOT AN OPTIMISATION.** A `static` method of `type
	// String` writing a bare `byteLength()` has no instance to dispatch on, and the answer there is the arity
	// error the ordinary resolution words, not a dispatch on nothing.
	//
	// Written once because the two surfaces below differ only in WHICH roster the enclosing type names — and
	// a second copy of "self is in scope and the roster carries it" is exactly how one of them comes to admit
	// a member the other refuses.
	function implicitSelfTakesTheRoster(surface MemberSurface, calleeTok Token) returns bool
		if not (self.scope.contains(SelfParamName) or self.captureBinds(SelfParamName)) 'noReceiver'
			return false
		end 'noReceiver'
		return namesInclude(self.surfaceMemberNames(surface), name: calleeTok.value)
	end 'implicitSelfTakesTheRoster'

	// The BYTE RECORD half of the test above — `String` and `Character`, told from every other enclosing type
	// by the tag their name denotes.
	function implicitSelfTakesTheByteRecordRoster(calleeTok Token) returns bool
		let enclosing = byteRecordTagOfTypeName(self.enclosingType)
		if enclosing == ValueTypeTag.unresolved 'notInsideAByteRecord'
			return false
		end 'notInsideAByteRecord'
		return self.implicitSelfTakesTheRoster(byteRecordSurface(enclosing), calleeTok: calleeTok)
	end 'implicitSelfTakesTheByteRecordRoster'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE `Array` HALF (W59) — THE SAME RULE ONE GENERICITY UP.** The enclosing declaration is told from
	// every other by the SHAPE that makes its record the compiler's (`declarationIsTheManagedRecord`), never by the
	// name alone: `Array` is not a name the compiler reserves, and a user container of that name declaring
	// anything else has no synthesized roster to win with.
	function implicitSelfTakesTheArrayRoster(calleeTok Token) returns bool
		if not self.signatures.declarationIsTheManagedRecord(self.enclosingType) 'notInsideTheOwnRecordArray'
			return false
		end 'notInsideTheOwnRecordArray'
		return self.implicitSelfTakesTheRoster(MemberSurface.array, calleeTok: calleeTok)
	end 'implicitSelfTakesTheArrayRoster'

	// `self`, shaped as the receiver the roster dispatchers take. It carries no immutable name and no borrow
	// subject for `implicitSelfReceiver`'s stated reason — a bare sibling call names no ONE storage — and the
	// anchor is the callee token, which is what a diagnostic inside the arm points at.
	function implicitSelfRosterReceiver(calleeTok Token, tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns MethodReceiver throws ParseError
		let value = try self.requireReceiverValue(calleeTok)
		return MethodReceiver.create(value, tag: tag, nameId: nameId, anchor: calleeTok, immutableName: EmptySourceText, subjectName: EmptySourceText, owner: NoParamPositions, selfFieldName: EmptySourceText)
	end 'implicitSelfRosterReceiver'

	// The receiver a bare call to the sibling `name` carries. An INSTANCE method gets the enclosing `self`
	// prepended at parameter 0 — resolved EXACTLY as an explicit `self.name(…)` resolves it, so the two spellings
	// emit byte-identical calls in every context. A `static` sibling gets none. Two contexts make the self
	// resolution more than a plain scope read, and BOTH are shared with the explicit path so they cannot drift:
	//   • inside a CLOSURE, `self` is a CAPTURE — the receiver comes out of the env block, through the very
	//     door `parseSelfPrimary` resolves it through, so `doubled()` and `self.doubled()` still emit the
	//     same call from inside a closure body as they do outside one;
	//   • in a `static` method there is no `self` at all, so the resolve fails and the sibling resolves
	//     receiver-less, failing the arity check on the hidden `self` parameter (E3036) — the same rejection the
	//     oracle gives ("missing argument for parameter 'self'"). That fallback is why this door swallows
	//     `requireReceiverValue`'s E2001 rather than propagating it: "there is no instance" is not an error
	//     HERE, it is the answer, and the arity check words it in terms of the call the author actually wrote.
	// The caller (`parseCall`) has already established `name` IS a sibling (`isImplicitSelfMethod`), so the map
	// is built and this `ensureSiblingReceivers` is a memo hit.
	function implicitSelfReceiver(name ByteArray, anchor Token) returns ReceiverArg throws ParseError
		self.ensureSiblingReceivers()
		if not self.siblingTakesReceiver(name) 'staticSibling'
			return ReceiverArg.none
		end 'staticSibling'
		// `self` is a PARAMETER — a borrowed reference to the caller's record — so it is writable and has no
		// blame name, which is exactly what keeps a sibling call inside a mutating method legal.
		// ⭐ The empty subject is not an exemption here — it is what makes `self` reach ALL of them. A bare
		// sibling call names no ONE storage, and `noteReceiverBorrowSubjects` turns that plus the receiver's
		// own value into one site per borrowed field of the enclosing receiver. See there.
		// ⚠ DECIDE EXISTENCE WITHOUT THE THROWING RESOLVER. `requireReceiverValue` fails for two structurally
		// different reasons — there is no receiver at all (a static function), and the receiver exists but is
		// only reachable through a NON-IMMEDIATE enclosing frame (A2b-2's nested-capture refusal, general to
		// every capture). A blanket `otherwise` here caught both and turned the second into `none`, so a
		// sibling call from a doubly-nested closure reported the arity error `'T.helper' expects 1 argument(s)
		// but 0 were provided` — the receiver counted as the missing argument — instead of the honest
		// "unsupported nested capture". Measured; the predecessor `requireNotInSelfCapture` threw
		// unconditionally and could not swallow it.
		if not (self.scope.contains(SelfParamName) or self.captureBinds(SelfParamName)) 'noReceiver'
			return ReceiverArg.none
		end 'noReceiver'
		// The anchor IS the callee token here — an implicit-self sibling call writes no receiver, so the
		// only token either half of the call can point at is the name the author wrote.
		return ReceiverArg.value(try self.requireReceiverValue(anchor), immutableName: EmptySourceText, subjectName: EmptySourceText, anchor: anchor)
	end 'implicitSelfReceiver'

	// Does the sibling `name` carry a hidden `self`? — the receiver half of the resolution `isImplicitSelfMethod`
	// admitted, and it answers from the SAME two sources in the SAME priority, which is the whole reason it is
	// written here rather than inline: an admission that came from the index and a receiver read off the walk
	// would be two readings of one declaration, and the pair that disagreed would call a `static` on an instance.
	// The caller has already established that one of the two answers exists.
	function siblingTakesReceiver(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if let takesReceiver = try self.siblingReceivers.get(name) 'declaredInTheBodyBeingParsed'
			return takesReceiver
		end 'declaredInTheBodyBeingParsed'

		return self.signatures.declaredMethodTakesReceiver(qualifiedMethodName(self.enclosingType, methodName: name))
	end 'siblingTakesReceiver'

	// Populate `siblingReceivers` (the members of the DECLARATION BEING PARSED, bare name -> takesReceiver)
	// unless it is already built for this type. It is not the whole sibling set — a method declared by another
	// declaration of the same type is reached through the index instead (`isImplicitSelfMethod`) — because a
	// token walk can only read the body it is standing in. LAZY so a type whose methods make no calls — the
	// whole scale corpus — never builds it and allocates nothing (the field keeps its
	// `sharedEmptySiblingReceivers` placeholder). A file-local token walk of the type body, so it sees every
	// sibling regardless of declaration order: a forward reference resolves exactly as a backward one, and
	// recursion sees the method itself. It is the ONE source of both "is this a sibling?" (`contains`) and
	// "does it take a receiver?" (the value) FOR THE MEMBERS IT CARRIES, so those two facts cannot drift
	// apart — and the index behind it is asked for both in the same one place (`siblingTakesReceiver`), for
	// that same reason.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **AN `enum`/`union` BODY'S MEMBER LIST IS NOT BLOCK STRUCTURE, AND A CASE MAY BE SPELLED WITH A
	// KEYWORD (D1).** `enum … end  while  if …` declares three cases, and `opensBlockAt`/`closesBlockAt`
	// read those three tokens as an `end`, a `while` and an `if`. MEASURED, before the depth-0 arm below
	// existed: a case named `end` closed the walk early, so a bare sibling call to a method declared after
	// it reported `E3004 call to undefined function 'inner'`; a case named `while` did the opposite,
	// over-counting so the walk ran PAST the enum's own `end` and adopted a LATER type's `helper` as a
	// sibling — turning a bare call to a free `helper()` into `E3004 ... 'Sib.helper'`. Both are legal
	// programs, refused.
	function ensureSiblingReceivers()
		if self.siblingReceiverScanType.equals(self.enclosingType) and self.siblingReceiverScanBody == self.enclosingTypeBodyStart 'memoHit'
			return
		end 'memoHit'

		// Read once: it names the DECLARATION this walk is over, and it cannot change while the walk runs.
		let inEnumBody = self.enclosingTypeIsEnum
		var map = SiblingReceiverMap.create()
		var depth = 0 as BlockDepth
		var q = self.enclosingTypeBodyStart
		while self.kindAt(q) != TokenKind.eof 'walk'
			// A member method is `function <name>` — a closure is `function(` (no name, and no `end`), and no
			// other construct pairs `function` with a NAME, so this cannot mistake one for a method. It
			// opens the method's own block, which `opensBlockAt` does NOT count (it knows if/while/match, not
			// `function`), so counting it here is the +1 that balances the method's `end`.
			let isMethod = self.functionDeclarationAt(q)
			if isMethod 'method'
				// A method with no `static` modifier takes the hidden `self` receiver — the SAME token-level test
				// the descriptor sweep uses (`functionDeclIsStatic`), so the two scans agree by construction.
				map.upsert(self.tokenAt(q + 1).value, value: not self.functionDeclIsStatic(q))
			end 'method'

			// At an ENUM's OWN level every token that is not a method declaration belongs to a CASE — its name
			// (possibly a keyword), its `= raw`, its payload parens, or the label of the method whose `end` just
			// brought the depth back here. None of it is block structure, and the body's terminator is told from
			// a case named `end` by the one shared rule the real parse and the sweep use.
			if inEnumBody and depth == 0 and not isMethod 'enumMemberLevel'
				if self.enumBodyEndsAt(q) 'typeEnd'
					break
				end 'typeEnd'

				q = q + 1
				continue
			end 'enumMemberLevel'

			if isMethod or self.opensBlockAt(q) 'opens'
				depth = depth + 1
			end 'opens' else 'notOpener'
				if self.closesBlockAt(q) 'closes'
					if depth == 0 'typeEnd'
						break
					end 'typeEnd'
					depth = depth - 1
				end 'closes'
			end 'notOpener'

			q = q + 1
		end 'walk'

		// ⭐ **AN EXTENSION BODY'S SIBLINGS ARE ITS INTERFACE'S REQUIREMENTS TOO, and no token walk can find
		// them** — they are declared on the CONFORMER, in another declaration and possibly another file. Added
		// AFTER the walk so an extension method of the same name as a requirement keeps the walk's answer
		// (the extension is what this body is, and it is the declaration the author is looking at). Every
		// requirement takes a receiver: an interface method's `self` is implicit and never declared, and a
		// `static` requirement is not reachable through `self` at all — so a `static` one is skipped rather
		// than filed as receiver-less, which would resolve `make()` to a call with no instance to make it on.
		// The transitive list, so a requirement inherited through `extends` is a sibling exactly as an own one
		// is (`requirementSlotsOf`, memoized, and the SAME list a witness dispatch resolves against).
		if not self.extensionInterfaceName.isEmpty() 'inAnExtensionBody'
			for slot in self.requirementSlotsOf(self.extensionInterfaceName) 'eachRequirement'
				if slot.method.isStatic 'staticRequirement'
					continue
				end 'staticRequirement'
				if map.contains(slot.method.name) 'shadowedByAnExtensionMethod'
					continue
				end 'shadowedByAnExtensionMethod'
				map.upsert(slot.method.name, value: true)
			end 'eachRequirement'
		end 'inAnExtensionBody'

		self.siblingReceivers = map
		self.siblingReceiverScanType = self.enclosingType
		self.siblingReceiverScanBody = self.enclosingTypeBodyStart
	end 'ensureSiblingReceivers'

	// Parse a call expression `callee(args…)` and return the ValueId its result defines. The
	// callee name is kept as identifier TEXT (a `ByteArray` slice per the MaxonOp value/name
	// split); the arguments are parsed in source order with their `name:` labels. Call-site
	// diagnostics anchor on the callee-through-`)` span.
	//
	// The call op lands in `currentBlock` AFTER the arguments are parsed, because an argument
	// can short-circuit (`f(a or b)`) and move it. Emitting into the block the call STARTED in
	// would put the call on a path its own arguments do not reach.
	// `resultUsed` names the ONE thing the two call positions differ by, and the grammar has exactly
	// two of them: a bare-call STATEMENT (`greet()` on a line of its own — the result is evaluated
	// for effect and discarded) and a call inside an EXPRESSION (`let x = f()`, `f() + 1`,
	// `if ready()`, `g(f())` — the result IS the expression). The parser is therefore the only tier
	// that can tell a legal void call from an illegal use of nothing, which is why the check lives
	// here and not in a later pass.
	function parseCall(func IrFunction, resultUsed bool) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		let calleeTok = self.current()
		// #65 — a bare call to a sibling method of the enclosing type resolves to that method under its
		// qualified name, prepending `self` for an instance method. The method takes precedence over a
		// same-named free function; a name that is NOT a sibling stays the free/direct call below (E3004 if it
		// names nothing declared).
		// ⭐⭐⭐ **A BARE SIBLING CALL INSIDE A BYTE RECORD TAKES THE ROSTER, EXACTLY AS `s.<member>()` DOES
		// (W17).** The roster-wins order `dispatchMethodOnReceiver` states was written for the EXPLICIT
		// spelling and silently did not cover the implicit-self one — so inside `type String` a bare
		// `addressableBytes()` resolved to the corpus module function while `s.addressableBytes()` reached the
		// arm that mints `__str_bytes_view` **and marks the result a buffer surface**.
		//
		// ⚠ **THE MARK IS THE WHOLE POINT, AND ITS ABSENCE IS A WRONG ANSWER RATHER THAN A SLOWER ONE.**
		// `String.replace` writes `let selfBytes = addressableBytes()` and then `selfBytes.slice(pos, matchPos)`;
		// unmarked, `dispatchArrayMethod` read `selfBytes` as an `Array`, and the `Array`-surface `slice` — which
		// DEMANDED the `endIndex:` label the buffer surface makes optional — refused the corpus inside its own
		// file: `E2010 Expected ':' but got ')'` at `String.maxon:542`, 1002 of the 1158 failures listing the
		// module produced. ⚠ The mark still decides the SURFACE (ARR3a made `slice` buffer-only, so an unmarked
		// value is now refused outright rather than mis-labelled); what it decides is unchanged.
		//
		// ⇒ Asked BEFORE the implicit-self arm and gated to the roster, so it can only ever catch a member the
		// arm below would have sent to a corpus body that duplicates one. A member the roster does NOT serve
		// falls through and reaches the corpus exactly as it should — the same split
		// `memberBelongsToTheCorpus` makes one door over, from the other side.
		if self.implicitSelfTakesTheByteRecordRoster(calleeTok) 'byteRecordRosterMember'
			let recv = try self.implicitSelfRosterReceiver(calleeTok, tag: byteRecordTagOfTypeName(self.enclosingType), nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
			if recv.tag == ValueTypeTag.character 'aCharacter'
				return try self.dispatchCharacterMethod(recv)
			end 'aCharacter'
			return try self.dispatchStringMethod(func, recv: recv)
		end 'byteRecordRosterMember'

		// ⭐⭐⭐ **AND THE SAME RULE FOR AN `Array` WHOSE RECORD THIS COMPILER OWNS (W59).** Everything the
		// header above argues for `String` is true here one genericity up, and it was measured in two places
		// at once. Inside `type Array`'s own body the bare spelling reached the DECLARATION while
		// `self.get(i)` reached the synthesized arm, so one member meant two different bodies depending on
		// how it was written. Inside an `extension Array` body it reached NOTHING — the sibling walk sees
		// only the extension's own methods, so `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `contains(sequence)` was
		// `E3004 call to undefined function 'get'`, a diagnostic no reader ever saw: the untyped result then
		// raised a THROWN `E3005` about the `!=` two lines later, and a thrown `ParseError` discards the
		// file's recorded diagnostics (`abortedParseArtifact`). The visible complaint named the comparison.
		if self.implicitSelfTakesTheArrayRoster(calleeTok) 'arrayRosterMember'
			let recv = try self.implicitSelfRosterReceiver(calleeTok, tag: ValueTypeTag.genericInstance, nameId: self.signatures.ownRecordSelfInstance(self.enclosingType))
			return try self.dispatchArrayMethod(func, arr: recv.value, giid: recv.nameId, resultUsed: resultUsed, immutableReceiverName: recv.immutableName, borrowSubjectName: recv.subjectName, receiverOwner: recv.owner, receiverStorage: self.subjectStorageMask(func, subjectName: recv.subjectName), viaManagedField: false)
		end 'arrayRosterMember'

		// `resolvedTypeQualifier`: the author wrote the bare member and NO qualifier at all — the head is the
		// enclosing declaration's name, which under a stdlib/user shadow is this compile's own mint (F31).
		if self.isImplicitSelfMethod(calleeTok.value) 'implicitSelfMethod'
			let receiver = try self.implicitSelfReceiver(calleeTok.value, anchor: calleeTok)
			return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: qualifiedMethodName(self.enclosingType, methodName: calleeTok.value), anchor: calleeTok, receiver: receiver, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.resolvedTypeQualifier)
		end 'implicitSelfMethod'

		// A free call has no receiver and constructs no generic instance: there is no value to its left, and
		// a generic type is instantiated only through a `Type.method` qualified call.
		return try self.parseCallNamed(func, callee: self.freeCallCallee(calleeTok.value), anchor: calleeTok, receiver: ReceiverArg.none, resultUsed: resultUsed, constructorInstance: ConstructorInstance.none, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted)
	end 'parseCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **A BARE FREE CALL TO A CONTESTED NAME MEANS THIS DIRECTORY'S DECLARATION, IF THERE IS ONE (N1c)** —
	// the "local namespace wins" tier both references have, and the reason a file in `alpha/` goes on calling
	// `duplicate()` unqualified after `beta/` declares one too.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS APPLIED AT THE FREE-CALL DOOR AND NOT INSIDE `parseCallNamed`, WHICH IS WHERE IT WAS FIRST
	// WRITTEN.** That funnel also carries a sibling method (already mangled `Type.method`), a static call, a
	// primitive-static mint and a receiver call, and a rewrite there is one condition away from moving one of
	// them. Here the input is a bare identifier the author wrote with nothing to its left — the only shape
	// this rule is about.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE FIRST VERSION ASKED THE WRONG INDEX AND WAS MEASURED WRONG.** It guarded on
	// `not declaresCallee(bare)` — "rewrite only a name that resolves to nothing" — but the SWEEP's bare entry
	// SURVIVES a contest by design (`refileContestedFreeFunction` copies rather than moves, so E3095 can be
	// told from E3004), while the name a call must actually reach is decided by the MERGE registry. So the
	// guard read `true` and the rewrite never ran: a bare `pick()` inside `alpha/`, next to `alpha/`'s own
	// `export function pick`, was reported AMBIGUOUS. One fact, two indexes, two answers.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PROBE IS ONE LEVEL AND DOES NOT WALK UP THE DIRECTORY CHAIN**, which is v1's behaviour too
	// (`qualifyName(currentNamespace, bare)`, a single probe): a file in `alpha/sub/` does not see `alpha/`'s
	// contested declaration bare and must qualify. Widening that is a resolution rule neither reference has
	// and no case pins.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A ROOT READER'S LOCAL NAME IS THE BARE NAME, SO BOTH ARMS RETURN THE SAME BYTES — AND THAT IS
	// PRECISELY WHY THE BARE ENTRY HAS TO BE THE ROOT DECLARATION'S (BATCH2 slice 6).** This line used to
	// end *"…and the stale bare entry cannot change the answer"*, which read the coincidence as a licence.
	// It is the opposite: a root reader has no other key to be routed to, so every sweep fact this call
	// site then reads — its return type, its `throws` clause, its parameter defaults — comes off the bare
	// key, and a contest that left another directory's declaration sitting there answered a call it never
	// selected. `SignatureIndex.refileContestedFreeFunction` is where that is now guaranteed; here there is
	// nothing to do about it, because there is nothing to rewrite.
	function freeCallCallee(bareName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
		if not self.signatures.isContestedFreeFunction(bareName) 'oneMeaningEverywhere'
			return bareName
		end 'oneMeaningEverywhere'

		let local = self.signatures.freeFunctionRegistrationName(bareName, moduleDirectory: self.signatures.moduleDirectoryOf(self.filePath))
		return local if self.signatures.declaresCallee(local) else bareName
	end 'freeCallCallee'

	// The call parser proper, taking the callee's NAME rather than reading it — because two call
	// syntaxes produce one call, and only the name differs. `greet()` is its own token's text;
	// `Point.create(…)` is `Point.create`, mangled by `parseQualifiedCall` from two tokens plus a
	// dot. Everything after the name — the argument grammar, the `name:` label rules, the builtin
	// check, the result typing, the emit — is identical, so it is written once.
	//
	// The CURSOR is on the callee's final name token (`greet`, `create`) with `(` behind it; the
	// caller has already consumed any qualification. `anchor` is the token diagnostics point at, and it is
	// the callee's FINAL NAME token for every spelling: `greet` for a free call, `bump` for `bx.bump(…)`,
	// and — since W81 — `create` for `Box.create(…)` rather than the `Box` that qualifies it.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE QUALIFIED DOOR POINTED AT THE TYPE NAME, AND EVERY DIAGNOSTIC ABOUT SUCH A CALL LANDED ONE
	// QUALIFIER TOO FAR LEFT.** The instance door already anchored at the method (`parseMethodCall` hands
	// over the method token), so the two spellings of one call disagreed about where the call IS. MEASURED
	// against the bootstrap oracle on the identical source: `Box.take(1.5, b: 2.5)` is E3009 at the `t` of
	// `take` there and was at the `B` of `Box` here; `bx.bump(1.5)` was already right in both. It is two
	// canonical cases in one spec — `testing-assertions`' `error.float-has-no-equal` (E3009 at the `equal`
	// of `Expect.equal`) and `error.forgotten-try` (E3057 at the same token) — and one fix, because both
	// read the CALL OP's range and this is where that range starts.
	//
	// `spelling` is threaded straight through to `emitCall`'s reserved-space door and read nowhere else:
	// every caller but ONE hands it the author's own bytes, and the exception is the primitive-static mint
	// (`parseQualifiedCall`). See `CalleeMint`.
	function parseCallNamed(func IrFunction, callee ByteArray, anchor Token, receiver ReceiverArg, resultUsed bool, constructorInstance ConstructorInstance, mint CalleeMint) returns ValueId throws ParseError
		// The callee's final name token may be a KEYWORD (D8) — `FilePath.from(path)`, `c.to()`, `from(41)`.
		// The `callee` bytes the caller passed were read off this very token, so refusing it here would have
		// refused every call to a name the declaration now accepts.
		try self.consumeNameToken(NamePosition.declaration)
		try self.consume(TokenKind.leftParen)

		// ⭐ **THE TWO UCD TABLE LOADS ARE PARSED, NOT EMITTED FROM PARSED ARGUMENTS** — their first
		// argument is a compile-time LABEL that selects a linked blob, so it must be read off its token
		// rather than resolved as a value. Taken here, before `parseCallArgs`, because that is the only
		// point at which the label's token is still the cursor. See `parseUcdTableLoad`; the label RULE it
		// enforces on the second argument is `consumeArgLabel`'s, not a second copy.
		//
		// A receiver cannot have been prepended: `__Builtins` is a compiler TYPE, so both these callees
		// arrive through `parseQualifiedCall` with `ReceiverArg.none`.
		//
		// ⚠ Each arm states WHICH TABLE and nothing else: the label it must be given, the leaf it lowers to
		// and its second parameter's name are all derived from that one value (`ucdTableStemOf` and its two
		// siblings). Passing the four separately let a call site pair an intrinsic with the other table's
		// stem — a load of the wrong blob at the wrong stride, out of a build with no error in it.
		if callee.equals(BuiltinsUcdByteAtName) 'ucdByteAt'
			return try self.parseUcdTableLoad(func, callee: callee, load: UcdTableStride.byteIndexed, anchor: anchor)
		end 'ucdByteAt'

		if callee.equals(BuiltinsUcdI64AtName) 'ucdI64At'
			return try self.parseUcdTableLoad(func, callee: callee, load: UcdTableStride.packedEntry, anchor: anchor)
		end 'ucdI64At'

		// ⭐ **THE THIRD INTRINSIC WHOSE ARGUMENT IS A NAME RATHER THAN A VALUE**, taken here for the two
		// above's reason and one of its own: the name is not merely known at compile time, it must never
		// become a value, because a `stringLiteral` op would mint the `.rdata` String record and the runtime
		// allocation the zero-alloc trace tier exists to avoid. See `DebugStreamNameIdCallee`.
		if callee.equals(DebugStreamNameIdCallee) 'debugStreamNameId'
			return try self.parseDebugStreamNameId(func, anchor: anchor)
		end 'debugStreamNameId'

		var args = ValueIdArray.create()
		var argLabels = ByteArrayArray.create()
		var argRanges = SourceRangeArray.create()
		var argImmutableNames = ByteArrayArray.create()

		// ⭐ **THE RECEIVER GOES IN FIRST, AS AN UNLABELLED ARGUMENT, AND THEN IT IS JUST AN
		// ARGUMENT.** Pushed BEFORE the source's own arguments are parsed, so it occupies index 0
		// and everything downstream slots it there without being told: `slotCallArgs` puts an
		// unlabelled argument at index `i` into parameter position `i`, and parameter 0 is
		// `SelfParamName`. The two ends of the convention never speak to each other.
		//
		// It does NOT disturb the label rules. `parseCallArgs` counts the SOURCE's arguments from
		// zero on its own counter, so `acc.add(10)`'s `10` is still "the first argument" and is
		// still required to be positional — the receiver is not something the user wrote and must
		// not shift what they did.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT DOES DISTURB THE ARITY COUNTS, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THAT CAN SAY SO.** Every
		// E3036 against a method now counts the receiver on BOTH sides, so both numbers are one
		// higher than anything the source wrote: `a.add()` — zero arguments written — reports
		// *"'Adder.add' expects 2 argument(s) but 1 were provided"* (measured). The count is
		// internally consistent and the program is correctly refused; the numbers are just not the
		// user's. Every method call is affected, through both callers — `parseMethodCall`'s
		// `a.add()` and `parseQualifiedCall`'s receiverless `Adder.add(5)` — which is exactly why
		// the note lives HERE, at the prepend that causes it, rather than being written once per
		// caller and drifting. The bootstrap dodges it by reporting per-parameter ("missing argument
		// for parameter 'value'") instead of by count. The honest fix is a diagnostic that speaks in
		// the SOURCE's arity, and no corpus case covers one — so it is recorded, not invented here.
		//
		// Its span is the receiver EXPRESSION's OWN anchor (`acc`, or the `self` keyword), which is the
		// token a diagnostic about the receiver has to point at — and it is the receiver's rather than this
		// function's `anchor` because a method call anchors the CALL at the method name. See `ReceiverArg`.
		// ⚠ **BOTH TOKENS TRAVEL, AND THEY WERE ONE UNTIL ARR1** — the ARGUMENT span is the receiver's, and
		// E3070's write site is the METHOD's. See `prependReceiverArg`.
		match receiver 'prependReceiver'
			none then break 'prependReceiver'
			value(id, immutableName, subjectName, receiverTok) then try self.prependReceiverArg(func, callee: callee, callAnchor: anchor, receiverTok: receiverTok, id: id, immutableName: immutableName, subjectName: subjectName, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames)
		end 'prependReceiver'

		// ⭐ THE ONE DOOR WHERE A BUILTIN — BARE OR `__Builtins.`-QUALIFIED — CAN ARRIVE, so it is the one
		// door that asks which label regime the callee takes. The question is asked HERE, from the name
		// alone, because the builtin check below runs only AFTER the arguments are parsed — and the label
		// rule fires during that parse. See `argLabelRuleForCallee`.
		try self.parseCallArgs(func, callee: callee, anchor: anchor, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: argRanges, argImmutableNames: argImmutableNames, rule: argLabelRuleForCallee(callee))

		let rparenTok = try self.consume(TokenKind.rightParen)

		// A BUILTIN is recognized by name, AFTER its arguments are parsed — so it takes the
		// ordinary call syntax and the ordinary argument rules, and only the emit differs. The
		// check sits here rather than in `emitCall` because a builtin emits no `MaxonOp.call` at
		// all: it has no signature to slot arguments against and no symbol to link.
		//
		// A QUALIFIED callee can never match: `Point.trunc` is not `trunc`, so a type may declare a
		// static method of that name and it is linked, not shadowed. The reservation the builtin
		// does impose — a free `function trunc` is unreachable — is unchanged and still undiagnosed
		// (see `emitTrunc`).
		//
		// ⭐⭐ **THE MATH INTRINSICS ENTER THROUGH THEIR ROSTER, NOT THROUGH SEVEN `equals` OF THEIR OWN.**
		// `calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly` is the ONE list of that family, and gating the emit on it is what
		// makes the list and the emit impossible to disagree — see `emitPositionalMathBuiltin` for the two
		// directions this closes and for the `max` tick that would otherwise have paid for it.
		if calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly(callee) 'mathBuiltin'
			return try self.emitPositionalMathBuiltin(args, callee: callee, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'mathBuiltin'

		// `spawnReadLine(cmd)` — the IOCP overlapped-read PROBE (P1.5 slice 1a-ii): `__gt_io_read` spawns the
		// command with stdout → a fresh overlapped pipe, issues a YIELDING overlapped read and returns the byte
		// count. NON-throwing (a plain integer, not the dual-register error ABI), so a bare call needs no `try`.
		if callee.equals(SpawnReadLineBuiltinName) 'spawnReadLineBuiltin'
			return try self.emitCommandLineRuntimeCall(args, runtimeName: IoReadName, builtinName: SpawnReadLineBuiltinName, arity: SpawnReadLineArity, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'spawnReadLineBuiltin'

		// The seven streaming subprocess builtins (P1.5 slice 1b). Each is recognized by name after its arguments
		// are parsed, exactly like `spawnReadLine`, and routes to a `__gt_subp_*` runtime entry.
		// `subpSpawn(cmd)` — `__gt_subp_spawn` creates three pipes, spawns the child and returns a
		// non-negative handle (or -1), which the six `subp*` handle doors below take as their argument.
		if callee.equals(SubpSpawnBuiltinName) 'subpSpawnBuiltin'
			return try self.emitCommandLineRuntimeCall(args, runtimeName: SubpSpawnName, builtinName: SubpSpawnBuiltinName, arity: SubpSpawnArity, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpSpawnBuiltin'

		if callee.equals(SubpReadLineBuiltinName) 'subpReadLineBuiltin'
			return try self.emitSubpReadLine(args, runtimeName: SubpReadLineName, builtinName: SubpReadLineBuiltinName, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpReadLineBuiltin'

		if callee.equals(SubpReadErrLineBuiltinName) 'subpReadErrLineBuiltin'
			return try self.emitSubpReadLine(args, runtimeName: SubpReadErrLineName, builtinName: SubpReadErrLineBuiltinName, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpReadErrLineBuiltin'

		if callee.equals(SubpWriteLineBuiltinName) 'subpWriteLineBuiltin'
			return try self.emitSubpWriteLine(args, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpWriteLineBuiltin'

		if callee.equals(SubpCloseStdinBuiltinName) 'subpCloseStdinBuiltin'
			return try self.emitSubpHandleOp(args, runtimeName: SubpCloseStdinName, builtinName: SubpCloseStdinBuiltinName, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultUsed: resultUsed, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpCloseStdinBuiltin'

		if callee.equals(SubpWaitBuiltinName) 'subpWaitBuiltin'
			return try self.emitSubpHandleOp(args, runtimeName: SubpWaitName, builtinName: SubpWaitBuiltinName, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultUsed: resultUsed, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpWaitBuiltin'

		if callee.equals(SubpReleaseBuiltinName) 'subpReleaseBuiltin'
			return try self.emitSubpHandleOp(args, runtimeName: SubpReleaseName, builtinName: SubpReleaseBuiltinName, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultUsed: resultUsed, calleeTok: anchor)
		end 'subpReleaseBuiltin'

		// ⚠ **THE MATH-INTRINSIC DISPATCH NO LONGER STANDS HERE, AND THIS PARAGRAPH IS KEPT FOR ITS
		// MEASUREMENT.** It used to be a run of `if callee.equals(…)` rows at this point, one per
		// intrinsic; they were folded into the ONE roster read at the top of this chain
		// (`calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly` ⇒ `emitPositionalMathBuiltin`), which is what makes the label
		// rule and the emit unable to disagree. The count in this paragraph read "four" while the roster
		// named EIGHT — a prose copy of a roster, wrong for the reason this chain's other prose copies
		// were (see `calleeTakesPositionalArgsOnly`); corrected at the W35 review, which is also where
		// the sentence stopped naming a table that had moved.
		//
		// ⚠⚠ A `union MathIntrinsic{notAnIntrinsic, op(MaxonUnaryOp)}` returned by a name→op resolver
		// would read better and was written first. It was REVERTED ON MEASUREMENT: **a payload-FREE
		// union case still heap-allocates**, so returning `notAnIntrinsic` cost ONE allocation per
		// PARSED CALL — +85/+153/+289/+561/+1105/+2193 allocations across the six scale rungs, a
		// linear constant factor on the hottest path in the parser, for a call that is not an
		// intrinsic 99.99% of the time. A plain `equals` allocates nothing and `scale-test` returns
		// to bit-for-bit parity with main. (The `flat union` rule in ARCHITECTURE.md is about NESTING
		// costing a box per level; this is the same accounting one level down.)

		// The `__Builtins.*` intrinsic table (see `BuiltinsQualifierPrefix`). Gated behind ONE prefix compare
		// so a call to anything else pays two bytes and no allocation; an unrecognized member falls through to
		// `emitCall`'s reserved-callee rejection, which is this table's "unknown intrinsic" arm.
		if isBuiltinsIntrinsicCallee(callee) 'builtinsIntrinsic'
			if callee.equals(BuiltinsCurrentTimeNanosName) 'currentTimeNanos'
				return try self.emitClockIntrinsicCall(args, name: callee, runtimeName: GtNowNsName, calleeTok: anchor)
			end 'currentTimeNanos'

			if callee.equals(BuiltinsCurrentTimeMsName) 'currentTimeMs'
				return try self.emitMonotonicMs(args, calleeTok: anchor)
			end 'currentTimeMs'

			if callee.equals(BuiltinsCurrentUnixTimeSecondsName) 'currentUnixTimeSeconds'
				return try self.emitClockIntrinsicCall(args, name: callee, runtimeName: WallClockNowUnixSecondsName, calleeTok: anchor)
			end 'currentUnixTimeSeconds'

			// ⭐ **THE ONLY SPELLING OF `sleep` THE PARSER RECOGNIZES — STDLIB'S OWN FLOOR.** A source
			// `sleep(ms)` is an ordinary call to `stdlib/Sleep.maxon`'s declaration, which the loader
			// brings into every compile and whose one-line body is exactly this intrinsic; there is no
			// bare-name `sleep` builtin and there must never be a second one, or a call and a declaration
			// of the same name would reach the scheduler by two routes that could come to disagree.
			if callee.equals(BuiltinsSleepName) 'sleep'
				return try self.emitSleep(args, calleeTok: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
			end 'sleep'

			// `Runtime.yield()`'s floor, on the same terms as `sleep` above: the surface is
			// `stdlib/Runtime.maxon`'s declaration and this is the only spelling the parser knows.
			if callee.equals(BuiltinsYieldName) 'yield'
				return try self.emitVoidZeroArgIntrinsic(args, builtinName: BuiltinsYieldName, runtimeName: GtReschedName, arity: YieldIntrinsicArity, calleeTok: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
			end 'yield'

			// ⭐ **THE CPU-PARALLEL CHECKPOINT — `yield`'s CALL CONTRACT AND THE OPPOSITE RUNTIME.** It marks
			// a hand-written CPU-bound function as a deliberate `async` target (E3073) and hands the
			// processor to nobody; `__parallel_boundary`'s body is a void return. See
			// `BuiltinsParallelBoundaryName` and `ParallelBoundaryRuntime`.
			if callee.equals(BuiltinsParallelBoundaryName) 'parallelBoundary'
				return try self.emitVoidZeroArgIntrinsic(args, builtinName: BuiltinsParallelBoundaryName, runtimeName: ParallelBoundaryName, arity: ParallelBoundaryArity, calleeTok: anchor, resultUsed: resultUsed)
			end 'parallelBoundary'

			// ⭐ **THE THIRD ARM THAT HANDS OVER THE PROCESSOR, and the one with no declaration above it.**
			// `sleep` and `yield` are stdlib's floor; this one is the whole surface, because
			// `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` reaches a child by a different mechanism entirely (see
			// `BuiltinsRunProcessName`). It was a BARE name until G17 and the harness's own `runProcess` is
			// what that cost.
